N2  - Introduction: Lemierre's syndrome is a Septic Thrombophlebitis of Internal Jugular Vein, the common causes of which include Fusobacterium, Bacteroides, other anaerobes and Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus. We describe an unusual presentation of Lemierre's syndrome where Mycobacterium Tuberculosis was identified as the etiological agent in a young female. Case Report: A 26-year-old lady presented with fever, left sided painful neck swelling since 3 months associated with odynophagia, pain and swelling over left cheek, productive cough, loss of weight and appetite and low grade fever since 3 months. Neck Sonography revealed bilateral cervical and axillary lymphadenopathy (L > R) of which FNAC was inconclusive. CT thorax revealed presence of a thrombus in the Left Internal Jugular Vein, consolidation of right middle lobe and mediastinal, cervical and axillary lymphadenopathy. Her USG Neck Vein Doppler revealed Bilateral Internal Jugular Venous (IJV) thrombosis with significant luminal compromise (L > R) with a conglomerated lymph node compressing the Left IJV. Deep Vein Thrombosis scan of bilateral lower limbs showed no Thrombus. Hemogram and coagulation profile were normal except for the presence of microcytic hypochromic anemia and leukocytosis. Broncho Alveolar Lavage and the CBNAAT of the BAL washings detected the presence of Mycobacterium Tuberculosis sensitive to Rifampicin. Patient was started on Anti-TB Therapy of Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol (HRZE) from NTEP and Low Molecular Weight Heparin for 5 days, switched to oral Rivaroxaban, to which the clinical improvement was observed in terms of reduction in cervical lymph node swelling, resolution of fever and cheek pain, increased appetite and she was discharged with advice for follow-up. Conclusion: Tuberculosis is widely prevalent in the Indian Subcontinent and unusual and bizarre clinical presentations are often encountered at tertiary care centers as seen in this case of Disseminated Tuberculosis with Septic Thrombophlebitis of Bilateral Internal Jugular Veins with Pulmonary and Extrapulmonary Tuberculosis.
