N2  - Introduction: Anterior mediastinal masses encompass a diverse group of neoplastic and nonneoplastic lesions located within the mediastinum, presenting a diagnostic challenge due to their varied etiologies and potential for serious complications. Tuberculosis presenting solely as an isolated mediastinal mass, as observed in our patient without parenchymal lesions, is rare. Case Report: A 25-year-old male presented with dry cough and right sided chest pain for the past 6 months. On initial evaluation the patient had a palpable mass of 3x2 cm below the clavicle. Chest radiograph shows illdefined heterogeneous opacity over the right middle and lower zone silhouetting the right heart border. Computed tomography showed hypo dense soft tissue density in anterior mediastinum on right side obscuring right heart border and ascending aorta. USG guided anterior chest wall biopsy showed caseous necrosis with adjacent fibrocollageous tissue showing epithelioid granuloma and gene Xpert indicated very low Mycobacterium tuberculosis detection without rifampicin resistance. He was started on Anti-tubercular therapy and currently is on follow up. Conclusions: Anterior mediastinal mass in this case exemplifies the diverse and often enigmatic nature of thoracic pathologies, requiring comprehensive and multidisciplinary diagnostic approach. The atypical manifestation challenges conventional diagnostic paradigms, highlighting the necessity for a high degree of clinical vigilance. The integration of advanced imaging modalities, coupled with histopathological and microbiological evaluation, remains paramount in establishing accurate diagnosis. Early recognition and prompt initiation of targeted therapy are crucial in mitigating potential complications and ensuring favorable outcomes.
