N2  - Introduction: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a heterogenous lung condition characterised by chronic respiratory symptoms (dyspnea, cough, sputum production and/exacerbation) due to abnormalities in airways (bronchitis, bronchiolitis) and/or alveoli (emphysema) that cause persistent, often progressive, airflow obstuction. The chronic inflammation of COPD is due to recurrent colonization or due to recurring inflammatory process as an aftermath of bacterial colonization. WBC and its subtypes are well known markers of inflammation. During periods of stress the neutrophil levels go up and the lymphocyte levels come down, this is the basis behind calculating the NLR ratio. There are other inflammatory markers which have been under scrutiny including ESR, CRP, which need to be correlated with NLR. Measurement of FEV1 is a reliable marker to assess the degree of obstruction and disease severity. But this value does not signify the level of inflammation. Moreover, pulmonary function testing and exercise related tests cannot be performed in many COPD patients who are in exacerbation, hence at these junctures, biomarkers can help in risk and severity assessment and treatment. Aim: To analyse the Neutrophil-Lymphocyte Ratio in blood among COPD patients as a marker of disease severity. The objectives of the study included: 1. To analyse the neutrophil-lymphocyte ratio in blood among COPD patients, 2. To find out the correlation of NLR ratio with severity of obstruction, RDW and see if NLR can be used to assess disease severity comparing with BODE index. Methods: In a cross sectional design, 80 patients were recruited. Patients with acute exacerbation of COPD admitted in the respiratory medicine ward and also stable COPD patients attending the Respiratory Medicine OPD were chosen as study subjects. Data like MMRC grade, BMI, PFT, 6MWT, ESR, CRP, RDW, Absoute Neutrophl count and absolute lymphocyte count are collected. Also a chest xray is taken to rule out other pathologies. Results: NLR has positive correlation with MMRC, RDW and BODE index. Thus NLR can be used as a marker of disease severity in COPD. According to ROC curve analysis of NLR, with 4.1 as cut off, NLR can be used to predict an exacerbation in COPD patients. NLR and RDW are increased among the exacerbated COPD patients compared to the stable patients. Hence both these markers can be used to denote the severity of the disease and also an exacerbated state. Also RDW is positively correlated to NLR, hence both these simple, cost effective markers can be used in routine practice to grade the severity of COPD. CRP, another acute phase reactant protein is also found to have a statistically significant association with COPD and is found to be elevated more in the exacerbated COPD patients compared to the stable ones. Conclusion: NLR is a cost effective, easy, quick marker derived from routine CBC. It can be used as a marker of disease severity among COPD patients, and also to predict an exacerbation.
