N2  - Introduction: Breast carcinoma is increasingly affecting lower age groups over the last decade giving an age adjusted rate for Indian females of 25.8/ lakh women. Breast carcinoma can metastasise to the following organs in the frequency of bones-51%, lungs-17%, brain-16%, and liver-6%. Cancers other than breast carcinoma that can metastasize to the lungs include colorectal, testicular, thyroid malignancies and osteosarcoma. Case Report: A 40 year female, with left breast carcinoma underwent MRM. Histopathology-immunohistochemistry showed TNM stage pT2N3a with ERpositive, PRpositive and HER2positive. Treated with 8 cycles of chemotherapy - Endoxan, Doxorubicin and Steroids (no targeted therapy given), 15 cycles of 3DCRT radiotherapy and was discharged on tamoxifen. Two years later, presented with bilateral ovarian complex cysts. CA125/ROMA values negative. Underwent trans-abdominal hysterectomy, bilateral salpingo-oopherectomy and left ovarian cystectomy. Histopathology ruled out malignancy and was suggestive of inactive endometrium consistent with tamoxifen therapy with chronic papillary endocervicitis. After seven months, presented with an acute cough, breathlessness and mild fever at D.Y.PATIL HOSPITAL. With a differential-diagnosis of fungal/bacterial LRTI, tuberculosis and metastatic lung disease, bronchoscopy performed. BAL negative for cytology, KOH mount, Gramstain and GeneExpert. Serial chestxrays showed rapid worsening, suggestive of infectious etiology. HRCT showed bilateral mild pleural effusions and consolidation. Appropriate antibiotics and antifungals administered, with little improvement. PET-CT showed lung and bone metastasis, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, bilateral pleural effusion and consolidation in the lungs indicating metastatic lung disease with concomitant lung infection. Started on targeted chemotherapy with paclitaxel and trastuzumab. Serial chest xrays showed improvement. Conclusion: (1) Early detection by spreading awareness of self breast examination is mandatory. (2) Metastatic disease in CA breast reduces the 5 year survival rate from 96% to a horrific 21%. (3) HER2+: Targeted therapy with Monoclonal Antibodies (Trastuzumab and Pertuzumab) + IV Hyaluronic acid prevents metastasis. 'Being Proactive and Not Just Reactive' helps prevent progression and metastasis. 'To Be Fore-warned Is To Be Fore-armed'.
