N2  - Introduction: Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA), traditionally seen in immunocompromised individuals, is increasingly found in immunocompetent patients with atypical presentations. This prospective study aimed to examine the clinical features, diagnostic approaches, and treatment outcomes of IPA, focusing on posaconazole as an alternative to voriconazole. Methods: Conducted over six months at Dr. Vasantrao Pawar Medical College, Nashik, the prspective observation study included 18 IPA patients diagnosed via positive bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL)/serum galactomannan, Aspergillus smear/culture, or PCR. Demographic data, immune status, risk factors, clinical presentation, radiological findings, and treatments were analyzed. Outcomes included mortality rates, treatment response, hospital stay, and complications. Results: The study revealed diverse IPA presentations, with half of the patients (9/18) showing atypical forms, such as secondary IPA following bacterial sepsis (5 cases), endobronchial mass, unstable angina, concurrent tuberculosis, and even a case of IPA after deep cleaning in an immunocompetent individual. Common risk factors included immunocompromised states and sepsis. Main symptoms were fever, cough, and breathlessness, with 40% of patients also reporting weight loss and decreased appetite. Radiological findings primarily showed consolidations with ground-glass opacities (GGOs), while only 25% presented with cavitary lesions. Diagnosis was confirmed by BAL galactomannan (14), fungal cultures (5), histopathology (2), and PCR (1). Treatment mainly included posaconazole, with voriconazole/caspofungin used for critically ill patients. Outcomes included 5 complete resolutions, 9 partial recoveries under ongoing treatment, and 3 deaths. Concluson: This study underscores the importance of BAL galactomannan testing and fungal cultures in non-resolving pneumonia cases, as IPA can present atypically. Early diagnosis and prompt treatment with azoles like posaconazole significantly improve outcomes, emphasizing the need to consider IPA in the differential diagnosis.
