N2  - Background: Nocardia, a genus of Gram-positive bacteria, thrives in aerobic conditions. Infection typically occurs through inhalation into the respiratory tract. Often referred to as 'the great masquerader,' Nocardia has a remarkable ability to mimic tuberculosis in both clinical symptoms and imaging characteristics. The situation becomes even more complex when co-infection with Nocardia and MTB occurs, presenting a heightened challenge that demands careful attention and prompt intervention. Case Study: A 60-year-old male presented with a cough with sputum and SOB for the past 3 months, along with a headache for the last 2 days. The patient has a history of pulmonary tuberculosis and was on ATT for 18 months. He has been using inhalers for 3 years. chest revealed bilateral fibronodular opacities. An MRI of the brain showed multiple conglomerate ring-enhancing lesions in the left occipital lobe, hippocampus, and right temporooccipital lobe, with perilesional edema. Bronchoscopy was performed, BAL sent for modified ZN stain revealed Nocardia species and CBNAAT positive for MTB. Discussion: Approximately two-thirds of all primary Nocardia spp. Infections affect the lungs .In our case, the patient initially presented with respiratory symptoms, followed by the onset of headache. Inhalation was likely the route of infection. CNS is the second most frequent site of infection, typically through dissemination from the primary pulmonary focus. The importance of microscopic examination cannot be overstated, as the presence of branched, unevenly colored Gram-positive bacilli is strongly indicative of Nocardia sspp. Advanced diagnostic techniques like MALDI-TOF MS and 16S rRNA gene analysis are instrumental in confirming the presence of Nocardia. Given that clinical signs are often non-specific, distinguishing pulmonary nocardiosis from tuberculosis can be challenging. A confirmed diagnosis of tuberculosis helps eliminate other potential pathogens. Conclusion: Patients face higher morbidity and mortality following opportunistic infections caused by pulmonary pathogens like Nocardia and MTB. Consequently, the early detection of these pathogens, coupled with the prompt selection of appropriate antimicrobial therapy, can drastically improve patient outcomes and prognosis.
