N2  - Background: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a common cause of atypical pneumonia, often presents with varied respiratory and extrapulmonary manifestations, posing diagnostic challenges. This study aims to phenotype patients presenting to the hospital with respiratory symptoms to predict Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection based on clinical features, prior illnesses, and laboratory and radiological findings. Methods: Eight cases (all inpatients) of Mycoplasma pneumoniae confirmed via PCR testing were analysed. Patients, aged 6-46 years, included adults (37.5%) and children (62.5%), all of whom were fully immunized and belonged to a single demographic area. Clinical history, physical findings, and radiological and laboratory data were reviewed. Results: All patients presented with fever and cough (100%), while dyspnoea (37.5%), diarrhoea (25%), ear pain (12.5%), and sore throat (12.5%) were also observed. Desaturation was noted in 25% patients. Prior illnesses included atopy (50%), bronchial asthma (25%), hypertrophied adenoids (12.5%), tuberculosis (12.5%), and tonsillitis (12.5%). Radiological findings included bilateral lung involvement (37.5%), minimal bilateral pleural effusion (12.5%), and unilateral pleural effusion (25%), which was exudative in nature. Targeted management with macrolides (azithromycin) and Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics resulted in clinical and radiological improvement in all cases. Discussion and Conclusion: The clinical and radiological features of Mycoplasma pneumoniae often overlap with other forms of pneumonia. Awareness of this condition and empirical coverage with appropriate antibiotics in regions with high prevalence are crucial for effective management.
