N2  - Case Report: A 51-year-old male, non-smoker, who was working as an IT professional presented to us with history of attacks of chronic intermittent cough and copious sputum production over the last 7 years. It was also associated with recurrent pneumonia and hospital admissions. Had progressive dyspnea now increased to grade 3-4 mmrc dyspnoea scale. No significant history of hemoptysis, difficulty of swallowing, or chocking was reported. Systemic examination was unremarkable. He used inhalers and cough suppressants as required. He is married and is having 1 child. No other relevant family history. Examination: The patient was thin built and malnourished. Pan digital clubbing, bilateral pedal edema was present, Dyspneic, tachypneic, Spo2-86% room air, Bilateral midinspiratory coarse crackles noted, more in lower hemithorax areas, Other system examination were normal except for loud p2. Chest X-ray: Enlongated opacity along paravertebral region from c7 spine extending below diaphragm with tapering towards left near the fundus of the stomach.Small cystic changes with peribronchial thickening noted on right midzone, lower zone and left lower zone [Figure 1]. Work Up: Initial blood investigation showed increased inflammatory markers Absolute eosinophil count, serum Ige levels, Aspergillus specific Igg, Ige were normal Immunoglobulin profile was normal except for Igg that was high Abg showed type 2 respiratory failure ANA profile was negative Spirometry-Severe obstruction with no reversibility Renal function test was deranged. In view of elevated Igg and acute kidney injury, serum electrophoresis was sent which showed monoclonal M band. Hemato oncology opinion was sought and bone marrow biopsy reported as multiple myeloma-light chain deposition disaese.He was treated with CyBroD and Bortezomib of 6 doses and is on follow up. Bronchoscopy was done in intubated state during hospital stay.Thick copious purulent secretions noted in bilateral bronchial tree and thorough bronchial toileting done. Bal culture showed MDR pseudomonas. Echo showed mild dilatation of RA, RV with severe PAH. Provisional Diagnosis: • Primary achalasia with pneumoesophagus causing bilateral bronchiectasis with type 2 respiratory failure and corpulmonale. • Multiple myeloma-light chain deposition disease. CT Chest-2020 [Figure 2]: • Confirmed the presence of the TEF at the level of sternoclavicular joint along with dilated esophagus. Also, there was evidence of cystic, cylindrical, and varicose bronchiectatic changes in both the right upper, middle lobes, and in the lingula • Unfortunately Ct finding of trachea esophageal fistula was missed during follow up in local hospital for the last 5 years. • We repeated CT chest and it showed progression of bronchiectasis and features of pulmonary hypertension with v/q mismatch.H type trachea esophageal fistula at D3 level. CT Chest-2021 [Figure 3]: Further Investigations: Esophageal manometry showed aperistaltic esophagus. Upper GI endoscopy revealed the presence of smooth tracheoesophageal opening (fistula) in the upper 3rd of the anterior wall of the esophagus at 25 cm from the incisor teeth with dilated esophagus [Figure 4], however, biopsy and wash aspiration from this fistula was negative for tuberculosis TB panel. Medical gastroenterology opinion was sought for possibilities of closure of tef and they suggested over the scope clip closure [Figure 5]. Over-the-scope clip closure of tef and pneumatic dilatation of achalasia cardia done. Final Diagnosis: Figure 2: CT Chest in 2020 • Primary achalasia cardia with congenital H type tef • Chronic aspiration and bilateral cystic bronchiectasis with coexisting multiple myeloma. Management: Post procedure oral contrast study showed no evidence of leak with persisting achalasia cardia. During discharge FEES done showing no evidence of aspiration and he was started on diltiazem thrice daily for achalasia cardia reatment of exacerbation with antibiotics, bronchodilators and mucolytics Airway clearance and anti inflammatory treatment Bipap and supplemental oxygenation atleast 15 hours per day Pneumococcal and influenza vaccination Pulmonary rehabilitation and nutrition. Conclusion: This case highlights the importance of identifying rare congenital anomalies like H-type TEF and achalasiacardia in adults presenting with chronic respiratory symptoms. Delayed diagnosis led to severe bilateral bronchiectasis, respiratory failure, and cor pulmonale. The coexisting multiple myeloma further complicated the clinical picture. A multidisciplinary approach combining interventional, medical, and supportive therapies was key to stabilizing the patient and improving quality of life.
