N2  - Background: Infection Prevention works to create a healthcare environment where the risk of contagious pathogens is minimized. Potential exposure to Tuberculosis (TB) by patients, families, visitors and staff is a failure in our standard of care to maintain a safe work environment. It is also recognized that exposure investigations may cause anxiety to those potentially exposed. In response to a potentially sizable TB outbreak due to the index patient’s extended length of stay (3months) at a large academic medical center, the Infection Prevention (IP) team classified the infectivity level of exposure using a risk-stratified approach of a “stone in the pond”. This principle has been utilized in England and Singapore for TB contact tracing. Methods: Infectivity of the index case was determined by using a point system of low, medium and high-risk based on the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention “Guidelines for the Investigation of Contact of Persons with Infectious Tuberculosis.” They include infectivity factors such anatomical disease site, patient symptoms, radiological findings, diagnostic tests and other factors. Then using the “stone in the pond” principle IP focused on high-level exposures first, then extended to lesser exposures based on proximity, activity and duration of the exposure until the prevalence of infection approximated that of the local community. High-level exposures were determined to be any staff present during an aerosol-generating procedure without the use of a respirator and any patients in the same room for a cumulative >8 hours without the use of a respirator. Results: Using the point system methodology, the index patient was categorized to meet low risk infectivity for TB. Twenty-three staff and eleven patients met criteria for high-risk exposure. There was no occurrence of transmission identified. Conclusions: This scaled approach significantly decreased unnecessary investigations which saved time and resources while allowing IP to focus on individuals with the highest risk.
