N2  - Objectives: Cough swabs (CS) are commonly used in paediatric CF clinics for microbiological surveillance, especially in non-expectorating children with CF (cwCF). However, reliability of CS in detecting lower respiratory tract infection is questionable. In this study we aimed to compare utility of CS and sputum samples in identifying respiratory pathogens in cwCF. Methods: Over a 12-month period paired CS and sputum samples were collected, during regular outpatient CF clinics from cwCF aged five to 16 years (median age 11.6years) who could spontaneously expectorate. Both CS and sputum samples were cultured in accredited NHS Trust laboratory by conventional standards. Pathogen detection rates were compared between CS and sputum samples. Results: Around 300 respiratory samples were collected over 12 months. Paired CS and sputum samples were possible in only 12.6% (38) cases due to children's inability to expectorate. 52.63% (20/38) sputum samples identified pathogens as compared to 7.89% (3/38) CS. The three positive CS results came from cwCF already colonised with the detected bacteria (Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa). 15 paired samples were taken from symptomatic cwCF. In symptomatic cwCF 86.67% (13/15) sputum samples identified a pathogen whereas no CS was positive. 34.78% (8/23) sputum samples identified pathogen in asymptomatic cwCF. Overall, sputum samples identified 12 clinically relevant pathogens, including Haemophilus influenzae, S.aureus and P.aeruginosa. Conclusion: Consistent with previous results from other studies, in our cohort, sputum samples were more sensitive than CS in detecting respiratory pathogens in cwCF, however, obtaining a sputum sample from cwCF remains challenging. While CS offers a less invasive alternative, its reduced sensitivity limits its clinical utility, particularly for guiding therapy. Sputum sampling, particularly in symptomatic cwCF, should be prioritised to improve care and reduce uncertainty.
