N2  - Objectives: to better understand the practices of microbiological monitoring of respiratory samples of people with CF across European Cystic Fibrosis Society Clinical Trials Network (ECFS CTN) centres. Methods: an online survey was conducted in 2024, n=32 centres responded from a total of n=57. The following monitoring practices were assessed: Pre-analytical steps: assessment of types of respiratory samples accepted; Analytical steps: assessment of cytological quality of samples, systematic fluidization of sputum samples, mycobacteria testing of samples, use of MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry, PCR or amplicon sequencing to target specific pathogens, profiling of bacterial microbiomes; Post-analytical steps: microbial species assessed for antimicrobial resistance, differentiation of specificities for S. aureus, adherence to European recommendations for antimicrobial susceptibility testing, laboratory accreditation/ for CF samples, use of regional or country-level reference laboratories/centres, participation in multidisciplinary consultation meetings? Results: A wide variety of respiratory samples are accepted including spontaneous sputum, induced sputum, oropharyngeal (OP), nasal & cough swabs, nasal lavage, bronchial aspiration and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). The majority of centres test every sample for mycobacteria (91%). Microbial species commonly assessed for antimicrobial resistance include H. influenzae, Enterobacterales, S. aureus, P. aeruginosa, Achromobacter spp., S. maltophilia, Burkholderia spp., mycobacteria, fungi, and respiratory viruses. 44% of respondent labs had a process for lab accreditation specifically for analysis of CF microbiological samples. Conclusion: Ultimately, ECFS CTN labs play a crucial role in advancing CF research and clinical care, serving as a cornerstone in our efforts to monitor, protect, and improve the lives of our patients. Acknowledgements: with many thanks to the ECFS CTN survey respondents.
