N2  - Introduction: Cystic fibrosis is the most common severe genetic disease in Caucasian population. The high consanguinity rate of 25% in Morocco contributes to the high prevalence of this autosomal recessive condition. The respiratory impairment is responsible for morbidity, mortality, and over 90% of deaths. The decline of lung function is progressive, aggravated by episodes of superinfection with potential chronic colonisation by Pseudomonas aeruginosa and progression towards chronic respiratory failure. Objectives: The objective is to describe the various epidemiological, clinical, bacteriological and evolution aspects of the respiratory impairment in cystic fibrosis. Methods: This is a retrospective descriptive study conducted on 42 children followed for cystic fibrosis for a period of over nine years from 1 of January 2015 to 31 December 2024. Results: The male to female ratio (M/F) was 0.9. The average age was 42 months. The delay in diagnosis 39 months. The respiratory symptoms were dominated by chronic cough (65%) and recurrent respiratory infections (74%). P. aeruginosa was found in 67% of the cases, with chronic colonisation in 23%. An association with tuberculosis (9%). Imaging has revealed bronchial dilation in 40% of cases. Pulmonary function tests performed on 24 patients showed a severe obstructive syndrome in 4,4% and moderate obstructive syndrome 13%. The disease progression was marked by chronic respiratory failure (40%) and death in (10%). Conclusion: Our study is characterised by the high frequency of severe forms, an elevated rate of chronic colonisation with P. aeruginosa and the association with pulmonary tuberculosis. Cystic fibrosis is underdiagnosed in Morocco. Its management is largely insufficient, hence the need for neonatal testing and the establishment of a reference and expertise center for cystic fibrosis.
