N2  - Introduction: Routine respiratory sampling and a proactive approach to detecting and treating respiratory pathogens is a recommended standard of care for paediatric patients with cystic fibrosis (CF). National and international guidelines advise collecting respiratory samples at every clinic visit and during exacerbations. However, the recent expansion of virtual consultations, offered within our service to stable patients, has placed the responsibility for sample collection and mailing largely on families, potentially reducing the number of samples received for culture. Methods: Retrospective data from CF clinic reviews in 2023 were analysed. Microbiology samples were matched with the dates of clinic reviews, which were categorised as either in-person or virtual. For virtual consultations, samples collected within a two-week window before or after the virtual appointment were included. Results: A total of 443 patient visits occurred in 2023, of which 78 (18%) were virtual. Among the 78 virtual consultations, only 23 respiratory samples (29%) were received, including 3 sputum samples and 20 cough swabs. Of these, 6 (26%) grew CF-related pathogens, leading to treatment according to local guidelines. In contrast, all patients attending in-person clinics submitted samples. Conclusion: The marked reduction in respiratory sample collection during virtual consultations raises concerns about potential under-detection of CF pathogens. While stable patients may have fewer exacerbations, the absence of routine sampling in virtual settings could compromise timely identification and management of infections. Future work should explore barriers to sample submission and evaluate whether mandatory respiratory sampling—regardless of consultation format—improves clinical outcomes. This study highlights the need to adapt CF care models to preserve high-quality surveillance in an era of increasing virtual healthcare delivery.
