N2  - Objectives: Surveillance of respiratory infections remains an important component of cystic fibrosis (CF) disease management. Historically, it was based on culture of expectorated sputum, but many people with CF (pwCF) stopped producing sputum after the introduction of CFTR modulators. In 2023, the Czech CF Working Group wrote national guidelines on surveillance strategy. In principle, they emphasise the need for at least one lower respiratory tract sample per year (expectorated or induced sputum), supplemented by cough swabs in between, to fulfil the rule of having a sample at every clinic visit. This study aims to assess the level of implementation of the guidelines in each of 5 national CF centres. Methods: For the period of 01-11/2024, the following data were collected: the number of cough swabs and sputum inductions performed, the number of paediatric and adult pwCF with at least one guideline-recommended sample tested and their 2023 infection status, culture positivity for Staphylococcus aureus (Sa) and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa). Results: A total of 1512 cough swabs from 423 pwCF (59% of the total CF population) and 100 induced sputum samples from 100 pwCF (13.9%) were analysed. The majority (87.5% cough swabs and 88% induced sputum) were collected from paediatric patients. Cough swabs were Sa positive in 62 of 161 pwCF who had been known to be chronically infected with Sa (38.5%), while induced sputum was positive in 25 of 39 Sa infected pwCF (64.1%). Pa was detected by cough swab in 14 of 44 pwCF with chronic Pa infection (31.8%) and by induced sputum in 3 of 13 pwCF (23.1%). Conclusions: The implementation of the national guidelines is more successful in paediatric than in adult parts of CF centres, but overall low numbers of induced sputum suggest a need for further promotion and staff training. As expected, induced sputum allows to detect more infections than cough swabs, although this cannot be confirmed by Pa data due to the low number of samples.
