N2  - Why did you do this work? Empyema thoracis refers to the collection of pus in the pleural cavity. Empyema is an increasingly frequent clinical problem worldwide and substantial morbidity and mortality is reported in resourced limited settings. Aim of our study was to analyze the clinical characteristics, microbiological features, and management of empyema at Lady Ridgeway Hospital for children (LRH), Sri Lanka. What did you do? A record based retrospective descriptive study was conducted at LRH, Sri Lanka. Data on clinical characteristics, microbiological features, and management of empyema of all the children admitted with empyema from February 2022 to February 2023 were extracted from patient records. Permission was obtained from relevant authorities to conduct the study. Data was analysed using SPSS version 22. What did you find? A total of 24 children were admitted with empyema. 58.3% were females. Mean age was 6 years and 54% of the patients were between 5 years to 13 years. All had community-acquired empyema and 54% were right-sided. Clinical features were predominantly fever and cough 22 (92%), upper abdominal pain 7 (30%) and chest pain 2 (8%). About 85% had ESR of >100 mm/h. Microbiological diagnosis was obtained in 7 (30%) patients and included streptococci (57%), staphylococci (14%), coliform (14%), and mycobacteria (14%). Mycobacterium tuberculosis was considered as an etiological agent in 3 (12.5%) patients and the etiology of remaining patients were bacterial in origin. Findings of the ultra sound scan and contrast CT scan of the chest were uninoculated 2 (8%), multiloculated 18 (75%) and pleural thickening (100%). 21 (87.5%) cases received intercostal tube drainage (ICD) and broad-spectrum antibiotics as primary management. Intra venous (IV) antibiotic was given in all the children for 2-3 weeks followed by oral antibiotics for a total duration of 4 to 6 weeks. Out of 24 cases, 6 (33%) recovered with medical treatment alone, and 18 (67%) required Video Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery (VATS) with decortication. 9 (37.5%) patients had necrotizing pneumonia and 4 (16.7%) had with broncho pleural fistula. There was a death due to streptococcus pneumoniae causing bilateral severe necrotizing pneumonia in our series. What does it mean? Pneumococcal infection is the predominant cause of morbidity and mortality in pyogenic empyema in Sri Lanka. Microbiological diagnosis was only achieved in just 1/3 of cases, and tuberculosis (1/8) was a notable causative organism. Even though, all the cases received broad spectrum IV antibiotics, substantial proportion of the children subsequently required VATS with decortication. VATS with decortication should be reserved for ICD failure cases. Availability of intrapleural urokinase and routine pneumococcal vaccines in the national immunization schedule of Sri Lanka could improve the morbidity and mortality of the children with empyema.
