N2  - Pulmonary actinomycosis is a rare presentation of actinomycosis that commonly affects the cervicofacial region in immunocompromised hosts or those with tissue/mucosal injury. Diagnosis is challenging because the presentation mimics malignant, infectious and inflammatory conditions alike, and a multimodality diagnostic approach is warranted. We present one such case with an extensive diagnostic pathway, eventually leading to successful diagnosis and management.A 47- year-old male with a past medical history of tobacco dependence and severe COPD (noncompliant with prescribed Ipratropium-albuterol inhaler) was referred by his pulmonologist to our interventional pulmonary (IP) clinic for persistent cough with green sputum and intermittent hemoptysis for two years. Associated symptoms included fatigue with progressive dyspnea now present even at rest, and consequent functional decline. Previous pulmonary workup included a series of chest x-rays that demonstrated a progressive left upper lobe (LUL) opacity over the past three years. CT Chest by referring pulmonologist two months prior showed a left upper lobe masslike consolidation with volume loss and traction bronchiectasis along with borderline subpectoral supraclavicular, and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Following failed empiric antibiotic therapy with malignancy suspicion, bronchoscopy by primary pulmonologist was negative for malignancy and infections. Subsequent PET scan demonstrated strongly avid LUL mass. Despite large lesion size due to prior non-diagnostic results, we performed robotic bronchoscopy biopsies to target more avid areas and avoid necrosis, and EBUS to sample the mediastinal lymphadenopathy. The mass was extremely hard in consistency and yielded small biopsy pieces. Again, bronchoscopy was nondiagnostic including microbiology, cytology and tissue pathology results. IP service then performed an ultrasound-guided transthoracic (TruCut) needle biopsy to obtain large tissue pieces which demonstrated aggregates of bacteria compatible with actinomyces. The patient was thereafter referred to infectious diseases for treatment with follow-up CT chest in three months demonstrating significantly improved left upper lobe mass and lymphadenopathy. This case reiterates how convincingly actinomycosis mimics malignant and typical infectious etiologies, causing lifethreatening diagnostic delays. It is hard to culture and the consequent reactive fibrosis makes smaller bronchoscopy biopsies including cryobiopsy (due to low water content) often nondiagnostic necessitating larger/surgical biopsies. Patients at times undergo lobectomy in nondiagnostic cases due to high suspicion of malignancy. Mindfulness about pulmonary actinomycosis can facilitate early diagnosis and treatment, preventing significant morbidity and mortality.
