N2  - INTRODUCTION: Ulcerative colitis (UC) is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) often requiring immunomodulatory therapy. This report presents a case of severe pneumonia in a UC patient treated with infliximab, an anti-tumor necrosis factoralpha agent. It highlights the diagnostic challenges in distinguishing between pulmonary extra-intestinal manifestations (EIM) of UC, infectious or autoimmune-related pneumonia, and drug-induced organizing pneumonia (OP). CASE PRESENTATION: A 40-year-old non-smoking nun with no significant respiratory history had UC, which was recently well-managed with infliximab. Despite endoscopic remission, she developed a non-productive cough and a persistent fever unresponsive to antibiotics, leading her to the emergency department. A chest CT scan revealed bilateral multifocal pulmonary consolidations predominantly subpleural, with associated peribronchial involvement. Admitted to the medical ward, she received broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics. Extensive investigations, including bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage for common and uncommon bacterial, viral, and fungal pathogens, as well as autoimmune panels, ruled out infections and autoimmune causes. A contrast-enhanced chest CT scan performed due to severe respiratory failure excluded pulmonary embolism but revealed worsening parenchymal consolidations, indicative of rapidly progressive pneumonia. Persistent negative findings and the temporal association with infliximab led to a suspicion of drug-induced OP. Consequently, infliximab was discontinued, and high-dose intravenous prednisone (1 mg/kg per day) was initiated, resulting in gradual improvement of symptoms, gas exchange, and radiological findings. After ten days, the patient was discharged asymptomatic on room air, with a gradual tapering plan for steroids. Three months later, vedolizumab was started due to new intestinal symptoms. Follow-up CT scans at six months showed significant resolution of lung consolidations. The patient discontinued prednisone, continued vedolizumab for UC, and remained symptomfree. DISCUSSION: The development of pneumonia in patients with UC undergoing infliximab treatment presents significant challenges. The absence of other EIMs, lack of intestinal symptoms, and endoscopic remission can nearly exclude a pulmonary EIM of UC. Infliximab-induced OP is a rare adverse reaction with unclear pathophysiology and variable onset. Management involves excluding infections and other causes, discontinuing infliximab, and initiating steroid therapy, typically with a gradual taper over several months. Close clinical and radiological follow-up is crucial, along with gastroenterological monitoring for UC relapse, considering alternative therapies like vedolizumab. This case underscores the importance of careful evaluation and comprehensive investigations to guide diagnosis and treatment in patients with IBDs on immunomodulatory therapy. Early detection, prompt management, multidisciplinary collaboration, and regular follow-up are essential to optimize outcomes and facilitate recovery in this complex clinical scenario.
