N2  - Background: Lactobacillus rhamnosus is a gram-positive, facultative anaerobe commonly present as a commensal organism in the gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts and is widely used in probiotics. Although generally non-pathogenic, L. rhamnosus has been implicated in severe infections among immunocompromised individuals, including bacteremia, endocarditis, and aspiration pneumonia. However, reports of L. rhamnosus-induced pleural empyema are exceedingly rare, with one previously documented case linked to esophageal carcinoma. This case illustrates an atypical presentation of L. rhamnosus-associated empyema in an immunocompromised patient with colorectal cancer, highlighting the need for clinical vigilance and adaptive therapeutic strategies in such high-risk cases. Case Presentation: A 61-year-old female with a history of colorectal cancer, post-partial colectomy with colostomy, and ongoing chemotherapy, presented with a 2-day history of dyspnea, productive cough, and rightsided pleuritic chest pain. Initial evaluation at an outside hospital indicated acute respiratory failure, leukocytosis, and elevated lactic acid. Imaging revealed a large right-sided pleural effusion prompting transfer to our institution. On admission, the patient was afebrile with stable vital signs and an oxygen saturation of 92% on room air. Physical examination revealed diminished breath sounds at the right lung base. Laboratory tests demonstrated marked leukocytosis (WBC count of 32,910/mm3). Broad-spectrum antibiotics were initiated. Right-sided thoracentesis drained 1200 mL of purulent fluid, with analysis showing a pleural fluid WBC count of 252,917/mm3 (79% neutrophils), LDH >7500 U/L, and protein concentration of 3.2 g/dL. Cultures revealed Lactobacillus rhamnosus, leading to de-escalation to ceftriaxone 2 g daily. Persistent effusion on follow-up imaging required chest tube placement, draining an additional 2.6 L of purulent fluid. Repeat cultures confirmed L. rhamnosus, with negative results for fungi, Nocardia, and Actinomyces. Intrapleural tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) and deoxyribonuclease (DNase) were administered twice daily for three days yielding an additional 1.8 L of drainage and facilitating resolution of the multiloculated empyema. Over four days, the WBC count decreased to 14,100/mm3, with follow-up CT imaging showing significant empyema reduction. Discussion: This case underscores the potential for L. rhamnosus to cause severe pleural infections, including empyema in immunocompromised patients. The patient's cancer and chemotherapy likely contributed to bacterial translocation emphasizing the importance of comprehensive microbiological evaluation in pleural infections among high-risk groups. In this case, adjunctive intrapleural tPA and DNase therapy enhanced drainage resolving the complex empyema. This case highlights the need to consider atypical pathogens and employ tailored therapeutic strategies for immunocompromised patients presenting with rare and severe respiratory infections.
