N2  - Introduction Legionella pneumonia is increasing in the general population and has high mortality in immunocompromised patients. We present a case of a patient on adalimumab with legionella pneumonia requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO). Case Summary A 37-year-old male with a history of psoriasis on adalimumab (recently switched from methotrexate), G6PD deficiency, and active tobacco use presented to the emergency room with one week of breathing difficulty, fever, chills, lightheadedness, and cough. Review of systems revealed hemoptysis, confusion, and watery diarrhea. Initially, he was febrile to 40.4°C, tachycardic to 142, tachypneic to 28, and hypoxic to 85% on room air, with stable blood pressure. Lab work showed leukopenia, transaminitis, hyponatremia, and lactic acidosis. Broad-spectrum antibiotics and systemic steroids were initiated. HIV screening and a respiratory viral panel were negative. Antibiotics were narrowed to levofloxacin after a urine Legionella antigen was detected. Despite treatment, he developed severe acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) with a PaO2/FiO2 (P:F) ratio of 66, requiring intubation, after which the P:F ratio improved to 147; however, his FiO2 requirement later increased to 100%. With a Murray score of 3.3 and no evidence of cardiac dysfunction on echocardiogram, veno-venous ECMO (VV-ECMO) was initiated. Antibiotics were adjusted to include rifampin. Respiratory status and lab abnormalities improved, and the patient was decannulated after 10 days on ECMO and discharged. Discussion Legionella pneumonia is an atypical community-acquired pneumonia, often presenting with high fever (above 39.4°C), relative bradycardia, nonproductive cough, and diarrhea. Its incidence is rising, with mortality rates as high as 20-50% in immunocompromised patients. Those on adalimumab, a TNF-α antagonist, are considered immunocompromised; this patient also had additional risk factors of G6PD deficiency and smoking for severe legionella pneumonia. Our literature review identified cases of severe legionella pneumonia in TNF-α antagonist users, but none required ECMO. Standard treatment for Legionella pneumonia, as well as for severe cases, includes fluoroquinolones or macrolides. Despite limited evidence, rifampin might be added cautiously for immunocompromised or significant comorbidities. ECMO is an established intervention for severe respiratory failure, with survival rates as high as 71-80%. VV-ECMO is a potential tool when the P:F ratio falls below 150 on FiO2 > 90% and/or with a Murray score of 2-4 as a bridge to recovery from severe infection. As immune-modulating therapies become more common, the possibility of more severe infections requiring advanced interventions like VV-ECMO may increase as well.
