N2  - Introduction Streptococcus pyogenes is recognized as a rare cause of community-acquired pneumonia, accounting for approximately 2- 5% of bacterial pneumonia cases documented in the early 20th century. Despite occasional case reports, recent literature on this condition remains limited. Pleural effusions and empyema are common in streptococcal pneumonia, in clinical presentations pleuritic chest pain was a predominant symptom, suggesting a significant association with the disease. Recent reviews highlight the role of Group A Streptococcus virulence factors, underscoring the need for increased clinical awareness.Case reportWe report the case of a 19-year-old female with a history of vertical gastrectomy performed as a treatment for obesity. Following the surgery, she developed malnutrition, further complicated by eating disorders. She presented to the emergency department with pleuritic chest pain and abdominal pain after sustaining a blunt thoracoabdominal trauma during a physical aggression. A computed tomography (CT) scan identified a cavitated pulmonary nodule in the left lower lobe, along with a right pleural effusion and subpleural lesions. As a differential diagnosis, pulmonary laceration or contusion caused by atypical pathogens was considered given the absence of immunocompromised status. During her hospitalization, she experienced intermittent fever and developed a dry cough. Initial blood work showed leukocytosis (20,000 cells/mm3) with lymphopenia, thrombocytosis, and elevated C-reactive protein (CRP), which gradually decreased with beta-lactam and penicillinase inhibitor therapy. Serial smears, polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests for Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and cultures for common pathogens were negative, examination for fungus was also negative. The patient underwent right thoracoscopic decortication and wedge resection. To identify the causative agent bronchoalveolar lavage was performed, which included polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing for microorganisms confirming the involvement of Streptococcus pyogenes.Discussion The Streptococcus pyogenes infections have been associated with rapid progression and mortality, even in previously healthy individuals. However, in this young patient with multiple comorbidities, certain clinical conditions and prior surgical interventions serve as recognized risk factors that heighten susceptibility to this pathogen. Given the substantial costs and complex challenges during hospitalization, early recognition and prompt diagnosis of Streptococcus pyogenes infection are essential for enhancing patient outcomes and optimizing healthcare resource management.
