N2  - A 40-year-old Vietnamese female nail salon worker with no significant medical history presented with a persistent, dry cough lasting approximately four months. Initial treatments targeting possible allergic rhinitis, including intranasal corticosteroids (Flonase) and later cetirizine, were ineffective. An additional trial of omeprazole for suspected gastroesophageal reflux also failed to alleviate her symptoms. A chest X-ray revealed extensive bilateral pulmonary opacities across both lungs, prompting further evaluation with a computer tomography (CT) scan, which showed widespread patchy reticulonodular infiltrates, some with mass-like features, and bilateral hilar adenopathy. Given these findings, the patient was referred to pulmonology for further workup, with her diagnosis remaining unclear. Despite the lack of familial or personal autoimmune history or history of tuberculosis, her occupational exposure over 24 years as a nail salon worker raised concerns for an environmental trigger. Although she was advised to wear an N95 mask consistently at work to minimize exposure, the patient was not compliant with this and instead only intermittently used a surgical mask. Follow-up imaging showed no improvement compared to the previous CT scan. Interventional Pulmonology performed a robotic bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). The procedure was complicated by bleeding periprocedurally and a pneumothorax requiring admission for chest tube management. Histologic analysis of biopsy samples showed non-caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells, fibrosis, and early calcifications, with negative stains for acid-fast bacteria, negative testing for fungal elements, and negative for evidence of malignancy. Extensive autoimmune and hypersensitivity pneumonitis panels were also negative. After review by the pulmonology department, sarcoidosis was considered the most probable diagnosis, likely related to her occupational exposure in the nail salon. She was started on prednisone with plans for close followup in the near future. This case highlights a potential, previously underrecognized link between nail salon occupational exposure and pulmonary granulomatous disease, specifically sarcoidosis. Although anecdotal reports suggest respiratory conditions including sarcoidosis among nail salon workers, there are no published case studies directly linking this exposure to sarcoidosis. This case underscores the need for further awareness and research into occupational health risks faced by nail salon workers and may inform future guidelines to reduce respiratory risk in similar work environments in attempts to reduce occupational lung disease in these populations.
