N2  - Rationale: Bacterial culture is the gold standard for identifying pathogens in bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) samples but can be time-consuming, potentially delaying crucial treatment decisions. Rapid diagnostic tests, including polymerase chain reaction (PCR)-based methods, offer faster results but their impact on clinical management remains unclear. Current literature on PCR testing in BAL primarily focuses on adult patients with critical illnesses or immunocompromised status, with limited evidence on its role in pediatrics. This study aims to compare the frequency and types of microorganisms identified by the Pneumonia Panel PCR (PPCR), a test that includes 18 bacterial microorganisms, versus bacterial culture and to evaluate the impact on treatment initiation and response. Methods: This is a retrospective study of data from patients aged 1 day to 21 years who underwent flexible bronchoscopy and BAL with PPCR from January 2022 to July 2024. The study was approved by the local Institutional Review Board. Data analysis was performed using SAS software version 9.4. The level of significance was set at 0.05. Results:One hundred and ninety-five unique patients met inclusion criteria, 115(59%) were male, 80(41%) were female; 54(28%) were immunocompromised, 24(12%) post-transplant status and total of 213 bronchoscopies were performed, with 18 patients undergoing multiple procedures. Mean age was 7.1 years(SD ± 5.5). The primary indication for bronchoscopy was chronic cough. Haemophilus influenzae was the most frequently detected organism by both PPCR and bacterial culture. The kappa statistic for PPCR and bacterial culture agreement was.44 (95% CI [.34,.55]). PPCR resulted on average within 4.71 hours, while culture results took 73.6 hours. Of the BAL samples, detection rates were 57.07% for PPCR and 30.73% for culture, 41% were negative on both tests, 31% positive on both, <1% negative on PPCR and positive on Culture, and 27% positive on PPCR and negative on culture. Antibiotics were administered prior to bronchoscopy in 54.72% of cases, with post-bronchoscopy antibiotics prescribed in 63%. Immediate treatment initiation based on PPCR occurred in 25.5% of cases, while 20.5% waited for final bacterial culture results. For 25.4%, the same antibiotics were continued as started pre-results. Conclusion: PPCR provides earlier and higher organism detection rates compared to traditional culture. However, clinical decision-making varies, with some providers initiating antibiotics based on PPCR alone, while others await culture results. While rapid diagnostic testing can guide treatment decisions, the evaluation of the clinical condition and traditional culture methods remain key components in the care of pediatric patients.
