N2  - Introduction: Smoking-related interstitial lung diseases (ILD) are a spectrum of diseases, including desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP). Here, we present a case of DIP diagnosed by clinical presentation and radiographic pattern. Case presentation: A 56-year-old male with an active 25-pack-year smoking history presented to the hospital with several weeks of dyspnea and dry cough. Exposure history was negative for pneumotoxic drugs, occupational hazards, farm animals/birds, mold, or chemicals. On initial evaluation, he was profoundly hypoxic, saturating 80% on room air requiring high-flow nasal cannula (70%/40 LPM). Initial laboratory testing demonstrated elevated C-reactive protein (324 mg/mL) and erythrocyte sedimentation rate (145 mm/hr). Extensive autoimmune and infectious serologies were negative. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed diffuse ground glass opacities (GGO) with interspersed rare cysts (Figure 1 A-D). The patient was empirically initiated on ceftriaxone, doxycycline, and pulmonology was consulted. Differential diagnoses included DIP, acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis, diffuse alveolar hemorrhage (DAH), and infectious pneumonitis. Subsequently, flexible bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was performed, revealing 93% neutrophils, and negative for DAH, viral, bacterial, or fungal etiology. With a preliminary diagnosis of DIP, he was initiated on 1 mg/kg of methylprednisolone, followed by oral prednisone (40 mg daily) tapered over 6-weeks. Over the next three days, he was weaned to 3 L/min nasal cannula and discharged home. At 8-week follow-up, he had improvement in symptoms with repeat CT chest demonstrating complete resolution of the GGOs with remnant scattered cysts and basilar linear scarring (Figure 1 E-H). He has quit smoking and remained symptom-free to date. Discussion: Exposure history and chest imaging are essential for proper diagnosis of smoking-related ILDs. Microcystic changes within areas of diffuse GGOs are a unique to DIP, whereas flexible bronchoscopy may demonstrate eosinophilia and neutrophilia1,2. Treatment includes smoking cessation, and steroids may be considered in severe cases. Smoking-related ILDs, including DIP are a diagnosis of exclusion.
