N2  - Introduction Melioidosis is often referred to as the great mimicker since it can resemble diseases like tuberculosis or malignancy. It is caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei, a resilient Gramnegative bacterium, thrives in tropical and subtropical regions, enduring extreme conditions. This ensuing case discusses a rare presentation of melioidosis with a paratracheal cyst, initially mimicking tuberculous lymphadenitis, highlighting the challenges in diagnosis and management. Case Report A 60-year-old female with diabetes, ischemic heart disease, and hypertension presented with a 12-day history of fever, dry cough, shortness of breath, and pleuritic chest pain, but no hemoptysis. She had no recent soil or river water exposure, travel, prolonged immobilization, or relevant lifestyle risk factors. On examination, she was febrile and hemodynamically stable, with coarse crepitations in the right lung. Blood work showed neutrophilia and elevated inflammatory markers, and the chest X-ray revealed a right middle lobe opacity. She was initially treated with IV piperacillin-tazobactam and clindamycin for 10 days without improvement. Tests for tuberculosis retroviral studies, and cultures were negative. A CT chest scan showed a 3.4 x 3.2 x 3.2 cm cystic lesion in the right paratracheal region with an irregularly enhancing wall, suggesting differential diagnoses of Tuberculous lymphadenitis or central bronchial carcinoma (Figure 1). Given the high prevalence, anti-tuberculous chemotherapy was initiated but the patient showed minimal improvement. Melioidosis was subsequently diagnosed with an antibody titer of 1:1280 and a positive culture. She was successfully treated with intravenous meropenem, followed by trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for 3 months. The post-treatment follow up CT revealed nearcomplete resolution. Discussion This case underscores the rarity of paratracheal cyst associated with melioidosis, as paratracheal cysts are more common with tuberculosis or malignancy. Therefore, in patients with prolonged fever and pulmonary symptoms, it is important to consider melioidosis alongside primary differentials like tuberculosis and malignancy, especially in regions with high melioidosis prevalence and among immunocompromised patients. Early diagnosis is vital, as mortality can reach up to 50%. While body fluid cultures are the gold standard for diagnosis, their sensitivity and predictive value are limited, often requiring repeat cultures from suspected sites when clinical suspicion is high. Melioidosis treatment is divided into an intensive phase to reduce the mortality and an eradication phase to prevent relapses, with the total duration of antibiotics determined by the infection's severity. This report highlights the potential relationship between melioidosis and paratracheal cyst formation, and to improve clinical management strategies for atypical presentations of melioidosis.
