N2  - INTRODUCTION: Blastomycosis, a systemic fungal infection caused by Blastomyces dermatitidis, is endemic to the Ohio and Mississippi River Valleys and Great Lakes region. Clinical presentation can vary significantly between the acute and chronic phases, often mimicking conditions such as infectious pneumonia, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, malignancy, and other fungal infections. This case highlights pulmonary Blastomycosis presenting as organizing pneumonia, with features suggestive of malignancy. CASE: A 30-year-old male with no significant medical history presented with a two-month history of persistent cough, blood-tinged sputum, and fever. Chest computed tomography (CT) revealed a 6.9 x 7.3 x 8.3 cm ill-defined mass-like consolidation in the left upper lobe, extending toward the left hilar region with pleural involvement. The differential diagnosis included infection versus a primary neoplastic process (Fig. 1A). He was started on empiric treatment for community-acquired pneumonia with Augmentin and Azithromycin. Outpatient workup included three negative sputum acid-fast bacilli tests and negative cultures. Five months after symptom onset, the patient returned to the emergency department with persistent cough, worsening dyspnea on exertion, night sweats, and additional weight loss. A repeat chest CT showed progression of the left upper lobe consolidation, now involving the entire lobe, along with new nodules in the left lower lobe, highly concerning for primary lung carcinoma with satellite lesions (Fig. 1B). HIV, serum Blastomyces antibody, urine Legionella antigen, ANCA, and QuantiFERON Gold tests were negative; however, urine Histoplasma antigen was positive. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial biopsy of the left upper lobe was performed, revealing a histological pattern compatible with organizing pneumonia, and no malignancy findings. Due to the clinical concern for a fungal etiology, therapy was deferred until final cultures resulted, which ultimately confirmed Blastomyces dermatitidis. He was started on Itraconazole 200 mg twice daily and established follow-up with pulmonology and infectious disease specialists. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary Blastomycosis presents with a wide range of radiographic features. Airspace consolidations are the most common finding, often leading to misdiagnosis as bacterial pneumonia. Organizing pneumonia can present as either a complication or lung manifestation of other diseases. Secondary organizing pneumonia is much more common, as seen in this case. Mass-like lesions, which can raise concern for lung malignancy, are the second most common pulmonary Blastomycosis presentation. Early diagnosis and treatment is crucial to prevent complications and reduce mortality of Blastomyces. This case highlights the importance of a comprehensive diagnostic work-up, including consideration of fungal infections, in patients from endemic regions for Blastomyces.
