N2  - The main methods for diagnosing Legionella infection include nucleic acid detection using polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and urine antigen tests (UAT) since the organism cannot be easily cultured. UAT is often preferred due to its accessibility, but it is limited to detecting Legionella pneumophila serotype 1. This report presents a case of an immunocompromised patient diagnosed with Legionella non-pneumophila pneumonia. A 61-year-old male with a history of autologous stem cell transplant for multiple myeloma 3 months prior presented with fever, dry cough, and dyspnea persisting for two weeks. A resident of New York State, he had not traveled out-of-state recently and had no history of chronic lung disease or smoking. He was receiving daily Pomalidomide and weekly dexamethasone. Upon admission, he was febrile but otherwise hemodynamically stable and without signs of respiratory distress on room air. Initial laboratory results revealed pancytopenia baseline kidney function, normal liver enzymes and an elevated lactate dehydrogenase at 292 U/L. Chest radiography showed dense consolidation in the right upper lobe, and a follow-up computed tomography (CT) of the chest demonstrated bilateral consolidations, more pronounced in the right upper lobe. Empiric treatment was started with Meropenem, Linezolid, Voriconazole, and Azithromycin. The infectious workup which included tests for a respiratory viral panel, blood cultures, serum (1-3)-ß-D-Glucan, serum galactomannan, urine Legionella antigen, tick-borne infection serology, and MRSA surveillance culture yielded negative results. After four days, he developed persistent fevers and worsening leukopenia, thus Meropenem and Voriconazole were continued but Azithromycin and Linezolid were stopped. He continued to have no significant hypoxemia. Bronchoscopy with BAL was performed, bacterial and fungal cultures from which returned negative. However, Legionella PCR was positive for non-pneumophila Legionella, leading to the resumption of Azithromycin for an additional seven days. The patient improved clinically and was discharged. A follow-up CT scan one month later showed complete resolution of previous consolidations. Timely detection and appropriate antibiotic therapy enhance the prognosis of Legionella infections. Non-pneumophila Legionella infections often originate from outdoor activities such as gardening and mowing lawns, which can expose individuals to contaminated soil. Conversely, Legionella pneumophila is typically transmitted through tainted water systems. In the United States, cases of non-pneumophila Legionella are rarely reported. This is likely attributable to underdiagnosis, resulting from the widespread use of the Legionella UAT. Legionella PCR should be performed in all patients with negative UAT, especially those who are immunocompromised, if concern for legionellosis persists.
