N2  - Introduction:Invasive pulmonary candidiasis, while uncommon, poses a high mortality risk particularly in immunocompromised or critically ill patients. Typical chest computed tomography (CT) findings in such cases usually show bilateral nodules and air-space consolidation. Here, we present a rare and unique case of Candida infection with a cavitary lesion in the lung, an unusual presentation for this pathogen [1].Case Presentation:A 68-year-old male with a history of heavy cigarette smoking and uncontrolled diabetes, initially presented with stroke-like symptoms, a productive cough, and hemoptysis. Chest CT [Image 1] revealed a cavitary lesion in the left upper lobe, initially suspected to be either an atypical infection or malignancy. A CT-guided biopsy confirmed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with Candida species. Initially treated with fluconazole, the patient was later switched to itraconazole due to intolerance. Follow-up chest CT [Image 2] showed partial improvement, but the persistence of the symptoms warranted a thoracic surgery evaluation with the possibility of resection as an alternative plan if no noticeable clinical improvement is reached. Discussion:Fungal cavitary lung lesions are more commonly associated with Aspergillus, Cryptococcus, and Mucor species, while Candida typically causes infections in other forms [2]. Primary Candida pneumonia often arises from aspirated esophageal or gastric contents, while secondary cases are due to hematogenous spread [3]. Risk factors for Candida colonization include smoking, diabetes, gastric aspiration, and extended use of antibiotics or steroids. However, cavitary lung lesions due to Candida are typically very rare and mostly seen in immunocompromised patients, such as those with neutropenia or organ transplants. In this case the patient's smoking and diabetes increased his risk of Candida colonization, infection, and cavitary lesion formation, though his overall immunocompetence would typically lower his susceptibility to invasive infection [4]. This case highlights the rarity of Candida pneumonia presenting as a cavitary lesion in an immunocompetent individual, confirmed by histopathology. Although uncommon, Candida infections should be considered in the differential diagnosis for fungal lung cavitary lesions, even in patients without immunosuppression.Conclusion:This appears to be the first documented case of a Candida-induced lung cavitary lesion in an otherwise immunocompetent patient without complex clinical course or advanced co-morbidities underscoring the importance of recognizing atypical presentations in clinical practice. Image 1: Left Upper Lobe Cavitary lesion Image 2: Left Upper Lobe Cavitary lesion, decreased in size after anti-fungal therapy.
