N2  - INTRODUCTION Interstitial lung disease (ILD) associated with lung cancer (LC) has been known to have a worse prognosis when compared to ILD from other causes. We present a unique case of ILD associated with rare pulmonary malignancy complicated by non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) infection. CASE PRESENTATION An 83-year-old female with a prior 60- pack-year smoking history presented with a dry cough and 20-lb weight loss. Chest CT at the time revealed a 7 mm right middle lobe (RML) lung nodule, which demonstrated a gradual increase in size over one year to 1.2 x 1.4 cm. CT-guided core biopsy demonstrated high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma. A PET-CT showed increased metabolic activity within the RML mass and right lower lung (RLL) peribronchial and subcarinal lymph nodes. A mediastinal nodal staging with EBUS did not demonstrate malignant nodal involvement. Wedge resection of the RML lung mass revealed a heterogenous appearance, with few areas showing a well-developed organoid growth and a carcinoid-type morphology, transitioning into areas with cytologic features of small-cell lung carcinoma (SCLC). Ki-67 was highly variable, ranging from less than 1% in carcinoid regions to >40% in areas with small cell morphology. This categorized the tumor into an emerging but rare pulmonary neuroendocrine tumor with features between carcinoids and SCLC. She completed four cycles of adjuvant chemotherapy comprising carboplatin and etoposide, with good tolerance. A follow-up CT chest after one month was negative for any recurrence but showed new-onset interstitial changes along the peripheral lung fields, which progressed over the next few months to show diffuse areas of ground glass opacities bilaterally with lower lobe predominance, multiple subpleural nodular opacities, and diffuse bronchiectasis. She was diagnosed with severe diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, most likely in the setting of her recent encounter with high-grade lung malignancy, and was started on prednisone. She developed a new-onset cough with increased oxygen demand when a new chest CT showed the interim development of multiple thin-walled upper lobe cysts in the background of severe ILD (Figure 1). Fungitell and the autoimmune workup were non-contributory. She was diagnosed with NTM infection after sputum testing was positive for Mycobacterium avium complex. She was initiated on rifampin and ethambutol along with azithromycin, resulting in minimal symptomatic improvement. DISCUSSION ILD is most commonly associated with squamous cell lung cancer. Although uncommon, the development of ILD after SCLC has been thought to be both a prognostication factor and not. Further studies are needed to evaluate this association.
