N2  - Introduction: Diagnosis and management of adult patients with primary deficiencies of the innate or adaptive immune systems can often be challenging and should be revisited as technology advances. This case study discusses a patient previously diagnosed with common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) who was later found to have primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD). PCD is rare, affecting 1/7,500 to 1/20,000 individuals globally, while CVID has a prevalence of approximately 1/75,000. Co-occurring humoral immunodeficiency (HID) has been noted in 6.5% of PCD cases, suggesting a potentially novel aspect of the disease's natural history. Case presentation: The patient is a 33-year-old non-smoking female with a history of recurrent Pseudomonas pneumonia, lobar bronchiectasis status post segmentectomy of the left lung base, chronic sinusitis, asthma, infertility, and CVID. At age 11, she was diagnosed with CVID, and was started on immunoglobulin replacement. Due to logistical challenges, these infusions became less frequent over the last 2 years. While her laboratory results from the time of diagnosis are not available, flow cytometry testing of peripheral blood obtained in 2014 (while on immunoglobulin replacement) revealed deficiencies of both memory and switched B cells. However, recent testing after being off immunoglobulin replacement for 6 months revealed normal quantities of IgG, IgA, and IgM, with targeted antibodies to pneumococcal antigens all detected, suggesting an appropriate vaccine response, contradicting her original diagnosis of CVID. Further investigation revealed homozygous pathogenic variants in RSPH4A, which is associated with autosomal recessive PCD. No other pathogenic variants among 429 genes associated with primary immunodeficiency were detected. Current therapy consists of an ICS/LABA/LAMA inhaler, oral theophylline, prophylactic azithromycin, dupilumab, tobramycin, and as needed albuterol and vest therapy. Her symptoms are characterized by chronic cough and dyspnea, and her exam is notable for diffuse wheezes and rhonchi at baseline. Flares consist of productive cough, chills, and lethargy. Discussion: Traditionally, primary antibody deficiencies like CVID require lifelong immunoglobulin supplementation. However, this patient's normal immunoglobulin levels, including specific antibodies to pneumococcal antigens, challenge her original diagnosis. While a causal link between PCD and antibody deficiencies remains unproven, proposed mechanisms include dysfunction of immune synapse formation or impaired antigen presentation by ciliary epithelial cells. Advancements in genetic understanding may shed light on these rare diseases. As such, it is appropriate to revisit diagnoses as these tools become available. This patient's current therapies have resulted in stabilization of disease, however recurrent assessment of her immunoglobulin levels may also be appropriate.
