N2  - Introduction: Purpureocillium lilacinum, previously known as Paecilomyces lilacinus, is a saprobic fungus commonly found in soil and decaying vegetation. It can infect both healthy and immunocompromised individuals, particularly causing eye infections in contact lens users and serious invasive infections, including pneumonia and bloodstream infections. We present a case of P. lilacinum infection resulting in cavitary lung disease. Case Presentation: A 60-year-old male presented to the clinic for a follow-up visit for his mild chronic nonproductive cough accompanied by weight loss. His past medical history included severe COPD, smoking, diabetes mellitus, pulmonary nodules, bronchiectasis, and hyperlipidemia. A yearly low-dose computed tomography (LDCT) scan revealed a significant worsening of bronchiectasis, with a new spiculated consolidative cavitary lesion in the left upper lobe. His Quantiferon TB Gold test was negative, and he was started on Augmentin after initially refusing bronchoscopy and sputum sampling. Due to persistent symptoms, new oxygen requirements, and ongoing weight loss, he ultimately underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) of the left upper lobe. Cytology, gram stain, and AFB culture results were negative, but the fungal stain was positive, leading to the identification of P. lilacinum in two separate samples. The patient was referred to infectious disease and initiated on voriconazole 200 mg twice daily for four weeks. Surveillance CT scan after treatment indicated radiographic improvement. Discussion: Once thought to be merely a contaminant, P. lilacinum is now increasingly acknowledged as a pathogenic agent in both immunocompromised and immunocompetent individuals. Sprute et al analyzed 101 cases of invasive P. lilacinum infection. They found that 31 cases (30.7%) with hematologic and neoplastic disease, 27 (26.7%) with steroid therapy, 26 (25.7%) with solid organ transplantation, and 19 (18.8%) with diabetes mellitus, with the skin being the most common site of infection (36.6%); fever, cough, and dyspnea were the most common clinical symptoms of a pulmonary infection; overall mortality of 21.8%. Imaging findings on the CT chest include nodular infiltrate consolidation and cavitary lesions. Currently, no standardized antifungal regimens exist for treating P. lilacinum infections. However, isavuconazole, posaconazole, and voriconazole are considered the most effective agents with the lowest MIC. Conclusion: P. lilacinum can cause rare pulmonary infections with atypical symptoms and imaging findings, complicating diagnosis and treatment due to antifungal resistance. In our patient, voriconazole was effective, but further research is needed to determine optimal treatment. Increased awareness of P. lilacinum as a significant pathogen is essential for timely diagnosis and management.
