N2  - Introduction: Echinococcosis, a rare disease caused by the Echinococcus tapeworm, forms hydatid cysts primarily in the liver and lungs. While many cases of pulmonary cysts are asymptomatic for years, they can become clinically significant due to mass effect or rupture. Diagnosis typically relies on imaging, as eosinophilia is rare (less than 15% of cases). Though more prevalent in endemic regions, echinococcosis remains rare in the US. Case Report: A 47-year-old male from Ecuador with a history of pulmonary tuberculosis treated decades prior presented with a several-week history of worsening cough and pleuritic chest pain. He developed hemoptysis on the day prior to admission. On physical examination, scattered wheezes and coarse breath sounds were noted in the right lower lobe. A chest CT scan revealed a fluid- and air-filled mass-like consolidation in the right lower lobe with adjacent alveolar infiltrate and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Laboratory tests showed eosinophilia of 3300 cells/μL. Initially, tuberculosis was suspected due to the patient's medical history. However, further evaluation by infectious disease and pulmonary specialists favored a pyogenic lung abscess, as the imaging findings were more consistent with this diagnosis and acid-fast bacillus cultures were negative. An abscess would not cause significant eosinophilia, therefore paragonimus and paracoccidiomycosis were also considered. In addition, the presence of a hypoattenuating calcified liver lesion raised concern for a hydatid cysts and serology for echinococcus was obtained. The patient was discharged on a course of amoxicillin-clavulanic acid with close follow-up. Upon follow-up, echinococcal serology was positive, eosinophilia had resolved, and albendazole therapy was initiated for suspected echinococcal cyst. The patient's symptoms improved significantly. A followup CT scan showed a persistent cystic lesion in the lung, but the adjacent alveolar opacities and adenopathy had resolved. Discussion: The patient's response to treatment, as evidenced by the normalization of eosinophilia, resolution of wheezing, and improvement on CT scans, is consistent with a diagnosis of pulmonary echinococcal cyst with superimposed bacterial infection and subsequent spillage. Peripheral eosinophilia can be a helpful diagnostic tool when evaluating for cyst spillage, as it is not typically expected in uncomplicated cysts nor pyogenic abscesses. This case highlights the importance of considering rare parasitic infections, even in non-endemic regions, as increased immigration can lead to increased prevalence of tropical diseases, even when initial diagnoses seem straightforward.
