N2  - Bordetella bronchoseptica is a gram-negative coccobacillus that is transmitted from mammals with tracheobronchitis. Rare case reports of B. bronchoseptica causing bronchitis and pneumonia have been reported in patients with immunocompromise. We present a patient with B. bronchoseptica on biopsy of cavitary lung nodules. An 82 year old former smoker with T1cN0M0 adenocarcinoma of left lower lobe treated in 2017 with radiation therapy presented for evaluation after CT chest showed increased size and extent of known left lower lobe adenocarcinoma with new cavitations and increased size of multiple solid and cavitated satellite lesions in the left lower lobe. Review of systems was positive for cough, which she had endorsed for years. PET/CT showed new/increased solid and cavitary FDG avid nodules in the left lower lobe at the site of the previously radiated adenocarcinoma without evidence of FDG avid lymph nodes or extrathoracic disease. She underwent navigational bronchoscopy to the left lower lobe and transthoracic and cryobiopsy were performed in both solid and cavitary areas. Specimens were without evidence of malignancy. Culture from fine needle aspiration (FNA) was positive for B. bronchoseptica. At the time results were delivered she noted that her cough had worsened and that her dog had also been coughing. She was treated with levofloxacin for a 2 week course, and repeat imaging is pending. B. bronchoseptica is a pathogen that commonly affects the airways of dogs and is commonly referred to as kennel cough. Few cases of B. bronchoseptica are documented, and those cases occur primarily in patients with immunocompromise. Other risk factors include active malignancy and underlying lung disease. Isolation of B. bronchoseptica has not been documented from transbronchial needle biopsy; prior cases report culture positivity from sputum samples, airway brushing, and bronchoalveolar lavage. Symptoms range from mild bronchitis to respiratory failure from pneumonia. Patients with B. bronchoseptica should be treated with antibiotic therapy, typically a fluoroquinolone, for two to four weeks depending on disease severity.
