N2  - Introduction: Alcaligenes faecalis (A. Faecalis) is an aerobic nonfermenting gram-negative bacteria that typically infects immunocompromised hosts. Although rare, infections can be life threatening. We present a unique case of an A. faecalis-induced cavitary lung lesion in an immunocompetent patient.Case Report: A 54 year old male presented with shortness of breath, cough, and failure to thrive with generalized weakness over four weeks. Patient was hemodynamically stable with a leukocytosis of 22 and hemoglobin of 7.3. Imaging revealed right upper lobe necrotic mass measuring 7.3 x 6.0 cm. Cultures were positive for Alcaligenes faecalis and Aspergillus, although latter was considered a contaminant given a negative Fungitell assay. Immunocompromised status was excluded through extensive workup. On day 4 of hospitalization, bronchoscopy and endobronchial ultrasound-guided biopsy confirmed A. Faecalis and showed no malignancy. The patient was treated with piperacillin-tazobactam, azithromycin, and meropenem showing minor lesion improvement (6.1 x 5.0) on a 2-week follow up CT. Patient was discharged on cefpodoxime and metronidazole, and later transitioned to amoxicillin clavulonate for a total of 6 weeks. However, five months post-discharge, the lesion had expanded to 7.6 x 6.8 cm. Nine months from initial presentation, the patient was readmitted due to septic shock and worsening oxygen requirement. CT imaging showed a worsening lung lesion measuring 13.9 cm x 12cm. Invasive drainage was attempted to stabilize the patient but resulted in acute respiratory and hemodynamic deterioration, necessitating intubation and vasopressor support. Decision was made to perform right upper lobectomy day 4 of admission. Despite emergency lobectomy and broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy, the patient succumbed to septic shock five days later. Discussion: A faecalis, typically a bacteria of gastrointestinal commensals, can cause severe and often fatal infections. This can occur in both immunocompromised hosts, and, as demonstrated here, immunocompetent hosts. Reported infections range from bacteremia and meningitis to peritonitis, pneumonia, and cavitary lung lesions. A key challenge lies in distinguishing A. faecalis as a pathogen rather than a contaminant, given its potential for significant morbidity and multidrug resistance. Effective management requires tailored antibiotic therapy guided by sensitivity testing. However, rising resistance rates, including to carbapenems, complicate treatment and underscore the need for aggressive, prolonged therapeutic strategies.
