N2  - Chronic occult pulmonary aspiration is rarely considered in the initial differential for multiple lung nodules as they typically do not present that way. It typically presents radiographically with multilobar centrilobular nodules, tree-in-bud changes, and sometimes airway thickening and fibrosis. Pathologically, we tend to see exogenous lipoid pneumonia, poorly formed granulomas, and foreign body type multinucleated giant cells with or without foreign material. Here, we describe a case of chronic occult aspiration that presented with multiple large pulmonary nodules. A 58-year-old man was seen in our office with an abnormal chest computed tomography (CT) scan. Radiographic findings had raised suspicion of metastatic cancer in his lungs. He complained of a mucopurulent cough for the preceding 4 to 5 months with occasional fever, fatigue, and exertional dyspnea with associated weight loss of about 24 pounds in the last year. He also had some epigastric and retrosternal pain, worse in the supine position. The lung nodules were found as his cough and weight loss were being investigated. He had no prior history of cancer and was up-to-date on his age-appropriate cancer screening. He worked for a construction company and in chemical plants, paper mills, and steel mills. He was a former smoker, having smoked 1.5ppd for 4 years and quit over 30 years ago. He had a history of Osteoarthritis, Bell's Palsy, Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), Obstructive Sleep Apnea, Hypertension, and Hypercholesterolemia. His physical examination was unremarkable except for obesity. Initial testing, including sputum analysis and biopsy, was negative for bacteria, mycobacteria, and malignancy, revealing only a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with granulomas. Work-up for autoimmune disease showed a positive ANA but no evidence of active disease. Given differential considerations of lymphomatoid granulomatosis and bronchiolitis, a surgical lung biopsy was performed. It revealed granulomatous inflammation, multinucleated giant cells, and polarizable material, suggesting chronic aspiration as the most likely diagnosis. Treatment involved 60mg oral prednisone daily for 2 weeks followed by a gradual taper by 10mg biweekly. Patient was noted to have Los Angeles Grade A GERD after an esophagogastroduodenoscopy. He was started on proton pump inhibitors and instructed on lifestyle modifications. After 8 weeks, a repeat chest CT scan showed complete resolution of the lung nodules, and he became asymptomatic. Our case underscores the need for confirmatory tissue diagnosis in evaluating atypically appearing lung nodules. Although rare, chronic pulmonary aspiration can present as multiple lung nodules and should also be a possible differential.
