N2  - Introduction: Silicosis and sarcoidosis are both well-characterized, non-caseating granulomatous diseases, with overlapping radiological features, including hilar lymphadenopathy and noted pulmonary parenchymal nodules. We present a rare case of a patient diagnosed with sarcoidosis while having underlying history of both silicosis and tuberculosis. Epidemiological data indicates; that the likelihood of encountering such a patient is extraordinarily low, estimated at 0.0000000000004%. Case Presentation: A 46-year-old male with a history of prolonged exposure to sandblasting in construction and a 12.5-pack-year smoking history was initially diagnosed with silicosis in 2019 on a lung wedge biopsy, for which he was treated with corticosteroids. Concurrently, the patient underwent therapy for latent tuberculosis. He reestablished care with our pulmonology service in May 2023 due to worsening exertional dyspnea related to his silicosis. He was managed symptomatically with fluticasone/salmeterol and albuterol rescue inhalers with advice to notify his pulmonologist of any acute changes. By November 2023, the patient reported persistent dyspnea on exertion, and a CT scan revealed bilateral pulmonary micronodules with worsening severity compared to 2019, along with previously noted hilar lymphadenopathy. Upon further history, we learned that in late 2021, he was evaluated by gastroenterology for intermittent nausea and emesis. By late 2023, further evaluation identified periportal lymphadenopathy on MRI. A biopsy of the periportal lymph nodes revealed non-caseating granulomas. In May 2024, the patient presented with symptoms of cough, phlegm production, night sweats, and worsening dyspnea. He underwent a bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL), and cultures were negative for coccidioidomycosis, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, acid-fast bacilli, and common viral and bacterial pathogens. Given the persistence of pulmonary and gastrointestinal symptoms, a repeat biopsy of the periportal lymph nodes was performed in August 2024, confirming the diagnosis of sarcoidosis, with non-caseating granulomatous disease and siderosis. Prednisone therapy was initiated with a scheduled pulmonology follow up. Discussion: Silicosis and sarcoidosis are distinct diseases which can have pulmonary and extrapulmonary manifestations, making differential diagnosis critical for appropriate management. As demonstrated in this case, though exceedingly rare, it is possible for a patient to concurrently have multiple conditions. Silicosis and sarcoidosis are some diseases capable of mimicking the radiographic and clinical characteristics of each other alongside a host of other conditions. This underscores the importance of not ruling out one diagnosis when the other is present. Comprehensive evaluation, avoidance of anchoring bias and vigilant investigation are essential for guiding informed clinical decisions for the management of any noted phenomenon.
