N2  - Case History and Background: We present a case of a middle aged female who presented with acute history of progressive shortness of breath, cough, chest discomfort, fatigue and was febrile over 4 days. She had been using some inhalers in the past with no definitive diagnosis and had no other co-morbidities.She was found in severe respiratory distress, dyspneic with oxygen saturation of 66 % RA, was confused and becoming drowsy, hence she was intubated and was put on mechanical ventilation. Examination:Auscultation - B/L bronchial breath sounds in ISA/IAA areas, HR-140/Min , RR- 38/min , BP-158/94 mm of Hg.ABG-Type 1 respiratory failure, Pa02/Fio2-89.Laboratory exam:Hb-10.4 g/dl, TLC- 3.14 10-/ul CRP- 415.17 mg, PCT- 0.2ng/mL (0-0.5), Urine exam- No RBCs/Protien/NitriteECHO- No RWMA, No PAH, Normal LV, NT-proBNP-969 pg/ml (0-125), ANA Profile/ANCA/Anti CCP/Hypersensitive Pneumonitis Panel-Negative, AFB Rapid MGIT-Negative, BAL fluid -No pathogens grown/No evidence of pulmonary hemorrhage/Cytology- Negative for malignacy. Radiology: Pre and Post treatment CXR AP: Bilateral mid and lower zone hazy opacity showing improvement, Transbronchial Lung Biopsy showing proliferation of fibroblasts suggesting Acute intersitial pneumonia (AIP). Results and treatment: Initially treated with emperical broad spectrum antibiotic therapy with Meropenem, Teicoplanin and Levofloxacin along with low dose hydrocortisone , anticoagulants and other supprotive management with IV fluids, oxygen etc and was maintained on PRVC mode of mechanical ventilation. She was started on methylprednisolone 240mg/day in divided doses after the diagnosis of Acute interstitial pneumonia (AIP) with slow tapering.Significant reduction in Fio2 and radiological improvement over 3 days.Conclusions and discussion: AIP is a rare and fulminant form of diffuse lung injury that generally occurs in previously healthy individuals1.The main treatment is supportive care and more commonly invasive ventilation is required, as mostpatients develop respiratory failure. With the diagnosis of AIP, high dose systemic steroids are initiated and continued for days and tapered over weeks to months, although supportive data is limited2. Alternative immunosuppressive therapies like azathioprine, cyclophoshamide, cyclosporin, vincristine have been tried with limited sucess3,4.In-patient mortality from AIP is high (>50%), those surviving initial illness may have substantial or complete recovery of lung functions or may experience recurrence of AIP5,6.Sudden onset acute hypoxic respiratory failure in a patient without pre-existing lung disease could suggest the presence of interstitial pneumonias. AIP should be considered in the differential diagnosis of ARDS, when the etiology remains unclear. Although early glucocorticoid or immunosuppressive therapy has been reported to improve the clinical outcomes, its efficacy is yet to be proven.
