N2  - RATIONALE & OBJECTIVES: Chronic cough is commonly evaluated by pediatric pulmonologists and is associated with high healthcare resource utilization as well as anxiety for families. Flexible bronchoscopy (FB) and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) are tools frequently used for diagnosis and management of chronic cough. BAL results commonly reveal bacteria on gram stain and culture, and clinicians must determine if these results are reflective of airway flora or colonization versus an active bacterial infection that might benefit from antibiotics. Unfortunately, there are no evidencebased guidelines available to guide clinicians prescribing antibiotics following FB/BAL in children with chronic cough and dysphagia. Our study aims to 1) describe how often antibiotics are prescribed to children with chronic cough following FB/BAL, 2) determine what factors are associated with antibiotic prescription, and if possible, 3) determine if antibiotic prescription is associated with improved clinical outcomes following FB/BAL compared to no antibiotic prescription. METHODS : This retrospective, single center cohort study looked at children ages 6 months to 7 years who underwent an outpatient FB with BAL between October 1 st 2020 and May 24th 2024 and had at least one Pulmonary or Aerodigestive clinic visit. Children with primary oncologic processes (including receipt of or preparation for bone marrow transplant) or with cystic fibrosis were excluded. RESULTS: To this point we have reviewed 241 charts of children who underwent a total of 289 bronchoscopies with BAL. The median age at time of bronchoscopy was 38 months and 64.7% of our cohort was male. Diagnosis of dysphagia was present at time of bronchoscopy in 90% of cases (261/289). Tracheo- or bronchomalacia were reported at time of bronchoscopy in 24.6% and 19% of cases, respectively. The three most commonly isolated bacteria were Streptococcus viridans (237/289), Neisseria species (184/289), and Haemophilus non-influenzae (137/289). Antibiotics were prescribed in response to BAL results in 16.2% (n=47) of cases. Children with ≥17% polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMN) on cytology had a 27 times higher odds (95% CI [9.5, 76.21]) of receiving antibiotics. CONCLUSIONS: Thus far, our data demonstrate that a diagnosis of dysphagia is common among children with chronic cough undergoing outpatient bronchoscopy and BAL. Our data also show that antibiotic use following bronchoscopy with BAL is seen in 16.2% of cases at our institution and that antibiotic prescriptions are associated with higher PMN count.
