N2  - Introduction: Pneumocystis pneumonia (PJP) is an opportunistic infection that can be lifethreatening in immunocompromised individuals such as those with HIV/AIDS, hematologic or solid organ transplant, or on chronic immunosuppressive therapies including corticosteroids. In the setting of high-dose corticosteroid therapy, PJP prophylaxis is traditionally initiated with a threshold of ≥ 20 mg of prednisone daily for at least 2-4 weeks. Case Description: A 74-year-old male presented with a subacute dry cough and new onset atrial fibrillation. His past medical history included severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), coronary artery disease, carotid artery stenosis with prior carotid endarterectomy, and recent preliminary diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica treated with prednisone 15 mg daily for 3 months without PJP prophylaxis. Chest radiograph revealed new left upper lobe consolidation, and intravenous ceftriaxone and azithromycin were initiated. Over the next 24 hours, his oxygen requirements escalated requiring heated high flow nasal canula (HHFNC) and transfer to the ICU. CT chest revealed bilateral interstitial opacities on background severe emphysema. Bronchoscopy was deferred due to severe hypoxemia. In consultation with ID, empiric treatment for PJP was added with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and prednisone while awaiting sputum and serologic infectious testing. His HHFNC escalated to 95% oxygen and 40 L of flow and diuresis was performed in additional to antimicrobial coverage. The initial PJP sputum polymerase chain reaction testing was positive and beta-D-glucan (Fungitell) assay was negative, while subsequent confirmatory testing with silver staining was positive for PJP. Other non-invasive microbial testing, including acid fast bacteria, viral and fungal pathogens, were negative. His oxygen requirements improved over the next five days, and he was ultimately discharged on 3L of oxygen. His course was complicated by acute renal failure secondary to pre-renal insult, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and contrast-induced nephropathy. Discussion: This patient's presentation was classic for PJP given subacute to chronic dry cough, progressive oxygen requirements and multifocal parenchymal opacities on lung imaging. He had several potential risk factors for opportunistic infections including prednisone therapy, an autoimmune condition, and structural lung disease; however, he did not meet the typical threshold for initiating PJP prophylaxis. This case highlights the importance of holistic consideration of all risk factors when determining need for prophylaxis in patients at risk for opportunistic infections, such as PJP who do not meet traditional clinical parameters.
