N2  - Introduction: Sarcoidosis is a systemic granulomatous disease of unknown etiology with multiorgan affections; commonly the lungs, skin, and joints. Extrapulmonary findings are not unusual such the cardiac or gastrointestinal organ. It is a diagnosis of exclusion in a broad differential of granulomatous disease with characteristic histological features of non-caseating granuloma. This case underscores the diagnostic challenge of identifying sarcoidosis amidst benign mimics, such as respiratory symptoms attributed to a Haemophilus parahaemolyticus contaminant and cutaneous manifestations of granuloma annulare, in a Caucasian female from Arizona. Case Report: A 78-year-old female presented with a persistent dry cough, night sweats, and a progressive rash that started on her upper extremities and spread to her trunk, accompanied by occasional gastric discomfort. Her personal and family history were unremarkable, and she denied any history of asthma or tuberculosis exposure. Laboratory findings revealed hypoalbuminemia (albumin 2.8 g/dL) with mildly reduced total protein (6.1 g/dL) and mild hypercalcemia. Coccidioides serologies (IgG and IgM) and the ANCA panel were negative, and the Complement Fixation (CF) titer was <1:2. An elevated gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) level of 200 U/L raised suspicion of hepatic involvement. Initial CT imaging of the abdomen revealed mild pericardial effusion. A follow-up CT chest scan identified multiple small clusters of nodules in the right upper, left upper, and right middle lobes, along with a calcified granuloma in the right lower lobe and diffuse bronchial wall thickening (Figures). These granulomatous lesions were biopsied and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was performed. Tissue samples were negative for malignancy and mycobacteria but tested positive for Haemophilus parahaemolyticus, deemed a contaminant. While initially part of our differential diagnosis, the final pathology report revealed granulomas and multinucleated giant cells, narrowing our focus to sarcoidosis, atypical mycobacterial infection, or coccidioidomycosis.The absence of necrosis and negative ANCA testing made certain vasculitides, particularly granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), less likely. Based on these findings, the multidisciplinary team reached a consensus to manage the case as sarcoidosis. Notably, the patient's skin biopsy confirmed granuloma annulare rather than annular sarcoidosis. Discussion: Sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of exclusion and can be challenging when complicated by conditions with similar presentations. In our case, Haemophilus parahaemolyticus was identified but classified as a contaminant, while skin biopsy confirmed granuloma annulare. Although sarcoidosis and granuloma annulare share granulomatous pathology, the latter is an isolated dermatologic condition without systemic inflammation, underscoring the need for distinct diagnostic pathways.
