N2  - IntroductionLung nodules are common findings in respiratory clinics, often requiring thorough investigation. While causes range from benign conditions like infections to malignancies, rare benign aetiologies, such as necrobiotic nodules, should be considered, especially when malignancy risk factors are absent.Case PresentationA 20-year-old man presented with four days of worsening per rectal bleeding and abdominal pain, reporting nine episodes of bloody stools in a single day. He had a history of ulcerative colitis (UC) treated with mesalazine and two recent courses of oral steroids with limited effect. Additionally, he experienced a dry cough and weight loss, attributed to poor oral intake. There was no haemoptysis, night sweats, or significant smoking history; he was a current vaper.Examination showed a clear chest, normal heart sounds, a soft abdomen with mild leftsided tenderness, and rashes on the elbows and knees. Blood tests revealed elevated inflammatory markers, while a chest X-ray showed well-defined, round opacities in both lungs. Initially treated as UC flare and referred to respiratory team for lung nodules. Further evaluation with CT scan confirmed multiple bilateral cavitary nodules, the largest measuring 1.5 cm, with no lymphadenopathy.Initial differential diagnoses included metastasis, infections, and vasculitis. Infectious disease (ID) consultation led to a workup for tuberculosis, aspergillosis, cytomegalovirus (CMV), and atypical bacterial infections. A multidisciplinary team (MDT) discussion involving ID, Gastroenterology and Surgical team recommended starting infliximab for the UC flare and empirical antibiotic coverage for the nodules. However, all tests for infections and vasculitis were negative.A literature review suggested necrobiotic nodules as a rare extraintestinal manifestation of UC. The clinical and radiological findings supported this diagnosis. As the patient had missed several follow-ups, including a bronchoscopy, It was decided to follow up him with a Chest X-Ray a month later, which showed complete resolution of the nodules with just treatment for UC flare with infliximab.DiscussionPulmonary manifestations in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are varied, with necrobiotic nodules being extremely rare. The classical radiology finding, as in this case, is of multiple bilateral nodules which are between 8 and 30mm in size, with a subpleural distribution.ConclusionNecrobiotic nodules should be considered in UC patients presenting with lung nodules during a flare, especially when no risk factors for malignancy are present. These cases can be managed conservatively, with monitoring and follow-up. Biopsy Of nodules is usually not required.
