N2  - Introduction: Legionnaires' disease (LD), or Legionella pneumonia , is a notable cause of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP), accounting for 4.6% of cases, with 20-40% requiring ICU admission. The most common radiologic manifestation is lobar consolidation. While pleural effusion occurs in 15-50% of cases, spontaneous pneumothorax is extremely rare. Case Presentation: We report a case of LD in a 57-year-old male recently diagnosed with IgG4 interstitial nephritis. He presented one week after starting corticosteroids and rituximab, with fever, malaise, productive cough, and left-sided chest pain. Labs showed leukocytosis (15.3) with 96% neutrophils, bandemia, elevated procalcitonin (6.77), worsening kidney function, and hyponatremia (128). Imaging revealed left upper lobe infiltrates and consolidation. The patient was admitted and started on vancomycin and Zosyn. Legionella pneumophila DNA was detected in sputum, and the urinary Legionella antigen was positive. Methylprednisolone was discontinued, and treatment was de-escalated to azithromycin. Six days post-admission, the patient experienced acute dyspnea and left-sided chest pain. A CT scan revealed a large pneumothorax necessitating chest tube placement. Pleural fluid analysis showed exudative effusion (LDH 2768, neutrophil count 2840/mm3), with no organisms. Imaging later revealed consolidation with cavity formation and trace pleural effusion. The chest tube was removed after one week, and the patient was discharged after 14 days of azithromycin with symptomatic improvement and negative follow-up cultures and PCR. One month later, the patient returned with recurrent left-sided chest pain. Imaging showed a large left-sided pneumothorax, consolidation, ground-glass opacities, and partial improvement of the cavitary lesion. Repeat cultures and PCR were negative, with no leukocytosis or bandemia. A chest tube provided only partial improvement, further suggesting a bronchopleural fistula. The patient was referred to another hospital for further interventions. Discussion: Necrotizing pneumonia with secondary cavitary lesions complicated with pneumothorax is a recognized complication associated with Staphylococcus aureus and Klebsiella pneumonia. However, cavitary lesions are uncommon in LD and spontaneous pneumothorax has been documented in only a few cases. Our patient's case involved rapidly progressing multilobar pneumonia, which led initially to hydropneumothorax and later on to lung tissue necrosis and cavitation. This cavitation contributed to recurrent and persistent pneumothorax, suggesting a potential bronchopleural fistula. Cases like this often involve some form of immunodeficiency; in our patient, one possible factor was the recent one-week course of methylprednisolone. Although uncommon, clinicians should consider LD in the differential diagnosis of lung cavitation, especially in immunocompromised patients, but also in those without overt immunodeficiency.
