N2  - Introduction Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by CFTR gene mutations, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport in epithelial cells. This results in thick, viscous mucus in various organs, particularly the lungs, pancreas, and gastrointestinal tract. CF presents with chronic respiratory infections, progressive lung disease, pancreatic insufficiency, and malnutrition. While individuals with two pathogenic CFTR variants display classic CF symptoms, heterozygotes generally do not meet CF diagnostic criteria. However, recent studies indicate that carriers may face increased risk for CF-related complications, such as bronchiectasis and respiratory infections, due to partial CFTR dysfunction. The most common mutation, Phe508del, causes protein misfolding and degradation, severely reducing functional chloride channels (Class II mutation). About 90% of CF patients are homozygotes or compound heterozygotes for Phe508del and are eligible for CFTR modulator therapy, whereas carriers with a single Phe508del mutation are currently ineligible.Case descriptionA 68-year-old female former smoker with nontuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) infections, including Mycobacterium avium complex and Mycobacterium scrofulaceum, and bronchiectasis, presented to our clinic. She was diagnosed in 2016 after hospital admission for persistent cough, chest pain, hemoptysis, and weight loss. Initially treated for NTM, she discontinued therapy due to side effects. Since 2022, she experienced more than 10 bronchiectasis exacerbations with hemoptysis, prompting a restart of azithromycin and ethambutol. Sequence analysis and deletion/duplication testing of 574 genes in the Inborn Errors of Immunity and Cytopenias Panel revealed heterozygosity for the pathogenic CFTR Phe508del variant. Given recent evidence linking CFTR heterozygosity with bronchiectasis and NTM infection, SYMDEKO (tezacaftor/ivacaftor and ivacaftor) was initiated in January 2024. Post-therapy, the patient reported marked improvement, including reduced cough and sputum, stabilized weight, and enhanced wellbeing, with no exacerbation.Discussion Traditionally, CFTR carriers are considered asymptomatic, but emerging evidence suggests they are susceptible to pulmonary complications like NTM infection and bronchiectasis. An estimated 10 million CF carriers live in the U.S. Studies indicate that partial CFTR dysfunction may impair mucociliary clearance, contributing to chronic airway infections and lung damage in heterozygotes. SYMDEKO is approved for patients homozygous for the Phe508del mutation or compound heterozygous with at least one responsive mutation. In this case, a single Phe508del mutation likely contributed to the patient's NTM infection and bronchiectasis. SYMDEKO therapy led to significant clinical improvement, suggesting that CFTR modulators may benefit symptomatic carriers. CFTR modulators represent a potential therapeutic approach for adult CF with one Phe508del mutation presenting with recurrent bronchiectasis exacerbation and/or NTM infection.
