N2  - Introduction: People with cystic fibrosis (CF) in regions without access to newborn screening, multidisciplinary CF care, and CF registries often have delayed diagnosis and worse outcomes. Bias in medical education that CF prevalence is low outside of non-Hispanic white populations can exacerbate these disparities. Here, we describe a case of delayed CF diagnosis and associated complications. Case: A 4-year-old Hispanic/Latina female presented to the emergency department with 2 weeks of cough, rhinorrhea, and difficulty breathing. Exam findings included fatigue, moderate respiratory distress, and bilateral diffuse crackles. Evaluation revealed negative viral testing, elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and peribronchial thickening and bilateral airspace opacities on chest x-ray. History included full term birth in Guatemala, asthma, environmental allergies, constipation, and growth faltering requiring unspecified enzyme treatment. Social history included recent undocumented immigration from El Salvador, lack of housing, and Spanish language preference. Asthma directed therapy was given without improvement; thus, the patient was started on ceftriaxone and admitted for multifocal pneumonia. Further history revealed multiple hospitalizations for bronchiolitis, pneumonia, and sinusitis, and family history of a sister who died in infancy from pulmonary infection. Repeat examination identified barrel chest and clubbing. CF evaluation was initiated, and positive sweat test (97/101) confirmed CF diagnosis. Sputum cultures grew Achromobacter, mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus. Broadened antibiotics, airway clearance, and pancreatic enzymes were started. At discharge 1 month later, forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1) was 51% predicted; chest computed tomography revealed cystic bronchiectasis in the right lower lobe, right middle lobe, and lingula (Figure 1). Genetic analysis identified F508del and c.489+1G>T splice variant mutations. After protracted health insurance enrollment and prior authorization, she was started on elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor (ETI) 1 month following discharge. 1 year later, FEV1 improved to 78% predicted and weight improved from the 20th to the 65th percentile. She had one CF exacerbation treated outpatient, no further hospitalizations, and is thriving in school. Discussion: Patients of Hispanic origin with CF have increased morbidity and mortality compared to non-Hispanic white patients; socioeconomic status and increased risk of Pseudomonas acquisition have been implicated. Undocumented immigration status also presents a significant barrier to CF care, insurance coverage, and ETI approval and places disparate social emotional stressors on families navigating the dynamic medicolegal system. This case highlights the increased morbidity associated with delayed CF diagnosis and treatment, yet offers hope that improvement can be achieved when access to multidisciplinary CF care is realized. (Figure Presented).
