N2  - Introduction: Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) is characterized by an extensive skin rash, visceral organ involvement, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, and atypical lymphocytosis. Rare instances of DRESS with anti-tubercular therapy have been reported. We present a unique case of DRESS likely precipitated by reactivation of latent human herpes virus (HHV) infection in a patient on multidrug therapy for Mycobacterium abscessus. Description: A 72-year-old man with a history of Pseudomonas pneumonia presented with pleuritic pain. Sputum cultures grew M. abscessus. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed mild nodular lung disease involving bilateral upper lobes. Treatment was planned with inhaled liposomal amikacin, clofazimine, and minocycline but was not started at the time due to patient reluctance. The patient presented again 2 years 9 months later with worsening cough, night sweats and mild hypoxemia requiring supplemental oxygen. Imaging demonstrated interval worsening of diffuse bilateral solid and tree-in-bud nodules with upper-lobe predominance. Bronchoscopy confirmed growth of M. abscessus intracellulare. Based on sensitivities, treatment was started with doxycycline, cefoxitin, inhaled liposomal amikacin, and clofazimine. However, within 3 weeks, the patient developed an extensive skin rash, lymphadenopathy, and peripheral eosinophilia, most likely from cefoxitin. All medications were stopped, and the patient was treated with intravenous corticosteroids and immunoglobulin with improvement in systemic signs and symptoms. Interestingly while investigating DRESS, HHV-6 was detected in the patient's serum. Eventually the patient was managed solely with doxycycline, linezolid, and clofazimine with symptom improvement. Discussion: Atypical mycobacterial infections are notoriously drug resistant and difficult to treat, often necessitating prolonged and complex regimens leading to significant side effects, including DRESS. The mortality rate of patients with DRESS is estimated to be 8%, especially among those with liver involvement; thus, early recognition and treatment are paramount. The presence of HHV-6 DNA suggests a potential reactivation of latent infection, which may have contributed to the patient's immune response and exacerbated his condition. Clinicians should remain vigilant for signs of DRESS and consider viral infections in patients receiving prolonged antimicrobial therapies.
