N2  - RATIONALE: Omadacycline is an FDA-approved IV/PO treatment for adults with community-acquired bacterial pneumonia and acute bacterial skin and skin-structure infections that demonstrates potent in vitro and intracellular activity and in vivo efficacy in mice versus Mycobacterium abscessus complex (MABc). Treatment of nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (NTM-PD) caused by MABc is challenging, with no approved therapies. Further investigation of omadacycline was warranted. METHODS: Phase 2, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, monotherapy, multicenter study in adults with NTM-PD caused by MABc (NCT04922554). Eligible participants were stratified by prior antibiotic treatment for MABc PD and randomized 1.5:1 to receive omadacycline 300mg orally once daily or placebo for 84 days. Patients were eligible if they met diagnostic criteria for NTM-PD and did not require guideline-directed antibiotic therapy for MABc for the next 3 months per the investigator. Select exclusion criteria were antibiotic treatment for NTM within prior 6 months, any systemic/inhaled antibiotic therapy (except chronic macrolides) within prior 4 weeks, cystic fibrosis, cavitary disease, or extrapulmonary NTM disease. Primary endpoints were clinical response (improvement in symptom severity at Day 84 [D84] for at least half of baseline symptoms), clinical response plus no deterioration of baseline symptoms, and safety assessments. Secondary and exploratory endpoints included Qualityof- Life-Bronchiectasis (QoL-B), Patient Global Impressions (PGI-C), Clinician Global Impressions (CGII), semi-quantitative sputum culture score, and negative sputum culture. RESULTS: Sixty-six patients were randomized (41 omadacycline, 25 placebo). Baseline characteristics were similar across treatment groups; 68% female, median age 72 years, 88% had history of bronchiectasis, 14% had prior treatment for MABc. At baseline, cough, fatigue, mucous production, shortness of breath, and throat clearing were the most bothersome and common symptoms (68.2-95.4% of patients) and mostly mild to moderate severity. M. abscessus subspecies abscessus was the most common pathogen (85% omadacycline, 80% placebo); all were multi-drug resistant. Compared to placebo, omadacycline was consistently favorable across symptom-based efficacy endpoints and global assessments. Microbiological endpoints were decreased semi-quantitative culture score at D84, 76.5% and 45.8%, and negative sputum cultures at D84/EOT, 56.4% and 29.2%, for omadacycline and placebo, respectively. (TABLE) The most frequent omadacycline treatmentemergent adverse events (TEAEs) were gastrointestinal, particularly nausea; 4 patients discontinued therapy due to a TEAE. CONCLUSION: In this randomized, controlled trial of patients with pulmonary MABc, omadacycline monotherapy demonstrated consistent and favorable results across multiple outcomes at D84 versus placebo and was well-tolerated. Consistent with prior studies of oral therapy, nausea was the most frequent TEAE. Further investigations are warranted.
