N2  - Introduction:Mycobacterium Paragordonae (MPG), a Non-tuberculosis mycobacterium, is an emerging cause of acute pulmonary infections in humans. There are cases associated with environmental factors such as contaminated water or exposure to reptiles. Due to difficulty in culturing this organism, it can be challenging to make the diagnosis. Case presentation:A 49-year-old male was admitted to the hospital with three weeks of progressive cough and shortness of breath. The patient had no significant past medical history, was a non-smoker but did have a pet turtle. Caring for the turtle required changing out of water and bedding on a weekly basis. One week into his illness he was diagnosed with outpatient community acquired pneumonia and was treated with amoxicillin and levofloxacin. Despite antibiotics his illness progressed to include worsening shortness of breath, cough and night sweats. On admission workup was notable for a Chest CT with bilateral multifocal infiltrates, worse in the right middle lobe, consistent with an atypical pneumonia. Viral panel, blood cultures and sputum cultures were all initially negative. He was treated with cefepime and vancomycin. He developed hypoxia, transiently requiring low flow nasal oxygen. Lab studies, including TB interferon gamma release assay, MRSA nasal swabs, HIV and ANA were all negative. Vancomycin was discontinued after negative MRSA testing. Due to his failure to improve his antibiotic regimen was altered to include a macrolide. On cefepime and azithromycin he gradually made clinical progress. Given the negative culture data at discharge, he was sent home on Cefuroxime 500 mg twice daily for 7 more days. Over the next several weeks he made a steady gradual recovery. Six weeks after sputum collection, multidrug resistant Mycobacterium Paragordonae was cultured. All other cultures remained negative. It was resistant to fluoroquinolones, doxycycline and trimethoprim. It was sensitive to macrolides and rifabutin. At three months post hospitalization he had a minimal residual cough and complete resolution of his pneumonia on Chest CT. Discussion: Mycobacterium Paragordonae is an emerging cause of acute pulmonary disease that has been associated with exposure to contaminated water sources. Turtles are known to be reservoirs for mycobacterium species including Mycobacterium Paragordonae. This case demonstrates the importance of taking a careful social history and considering all exposures as potential sources of infection.
