N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary mucormycosis is a rare but serious opportunistic fungal infection, particularly in immunocompromised individuals. The infection is associated with high morbidity and mortality rates, necessitating rapid diagnosis and intervention. Misdiagnosis can occur due to overlapping symptoms with other pulmonary infections, particularly in patients with prior history of lung disease and immunosuppressive conditions. We present a case of 43-year-old woman with a history of bronchiectasis, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), and recurrent Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection, who presented with respiratory distress due to mucormycosis Description: A 43-year-old woman presented to the advanced lung disease clinic with increased shortness of breath, fatigue, and productive cough with green sputum. Her medical history included bronchiectasis, systemic lupus erythematosus managed with prednisone and hydroxychloroquine, and recurrent MAC infections previously treated with azithromycin, ethambutol, and rifampin with MAC disease in remission. Chest CT revealed persistent bilateral multifocal tree-in-bud patchy opacities and nodularity consistent with chronic MAC infection. In addition, new infiltrates were observed in the right middle lobe and lingula, indicative of potential infection. Given the new radiographic changes and the patient's drop in lung function, bronchoscopy with alveolar lavage was performed to investigate the abnormal lobes. The results of the bronchoalveolar lavage showed growth of Scedosporium and Penicillium species, consistent with previous findings related to the MAC infection. Importantly, newly identified Rhizopus growth was detected, confirming the presence of mucormycosis. Acid-fast staining was negative, effectively ruling out an active MAC infection as the cause of her symptoms. Treatment regimen consisting of posaconazole and inhaled amphotericin B was initiated. Within a week, her respiratory symptoms improved significantly, and follow-up CT scans indicated a reduction in opacities compared to previous imaging. Repeat BAL cultures were negative for Rhizopus. Discussion: Pulmonary mucormycosis (PM) is a rare cause of fungal pneumonia associated with high mortality rates, particularly in immunocompromised patients. Nonspecific radiological and clinical findings can overlap with other pulmonary conditions. High-dose liposomal amphotericin B is the recommended first-line treatment, with intravenous isavuconazole and posaconazole as subsequent options. This case highlights the importance of considering pulmonary mucormycosis in patients with complex lung diseases and the efficacy of targeted antifungal therapy. Continued vigilance in diagnosis and treatment can significantly improve outcomes in this vulnerable patient population.
