N2  - RATIONALE: Exacerbations are key events in the natural course of bronchiectasis (BE) and are associated with poor clinical outcomes. However, information on symptoms and disease progression in patients with infrequent exacerbations is limited. Thus, we conducted this retrospective study using data from the U.S. Bronchiectasis and Non-Tuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM) Research Registry (BRR). METHODS : Adult patients with non-CF bronchiectasis were included. Baseline data were retrospectively analyzed, where the baseline data collection period refers to the 2-years prior to consent. Patients were grouped by annualized exacerbation rate at baseline, defined as 0/year, >0-1/year, >1-2/year, and >2/year. Demographics, clinical characteristics, bronchiectasis severity, possible causes of bronchiectasis and symptoms were evaluated. The analysis included descriptive statistics only. RESULTS: Between January 1, 2013, through December 31, 2023, we identified 6183 patients, enrolled across 24 centres in the US, of which 5616 had baseline exacerbation data. Table 1 summarizes the clinical characteristics, etiology of BE, and symptoms for all sub-groups. Milder severity as measured by the modified Bronchiectasis Severity Index (mBSI) was associated with lower number of annual exacerbations. Patients with zero annual exacerbation at baseline had better lung function (FEV1), less dyspnea, were less frequently chronically infected with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA), and experienced less BE-related hospitalization compared with groups that had higher annual exacerbations at baseline. The presence of NTM was associated with a lack of exacerbations. Previous history of smoking did not appear to influence exacerbation risk. Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) was the leading co-existing diagnosis and potential cause of BE across all baseline exacerbation groups, and this was more pronounced in patients with > 2 annual exacerbations. Proportion of patients with symptoms including fatigue, cough, hemoptysis, chest pain and wheezing tend to increase with the annual exacerbation frequency. However, despite an absence of exacerbations, a high number of patients experienced symptoms, particularly cough (71.4%) and fatigue (43.4%). CONCLUSIONS: Consistent with the literature, our findings show disease severity and poorer symptoms are associated with exacerbations. We provide new insights of a high symptom burden in the low exacerbator phenotype. This highlights symptomatic patients with mild to moderate controlled disease may represent an underrecognized area of unmet need. Unlike asthma or COPD, in our population of primarily non-smokers, exacerbations are more closely linked with chronic PA infection. Early recognition of BE and role of chronic infection may result in better outcomes and slow down disease progression. (Figure Presented).
