N2  - Introduction: Pleural infections involving fungal pathogens are rare, particularly in immunocompetent patients. Co-infection with Candida and Aspergillus species in the pleural space presents a diagnostic and therapeutic challenge due to limited evidence guiding management. This case report describes a unique instance of mixed fungal empyema in an elderly patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Case Description: We present a case of 75-year-old male with a history of coronary artery disease, heart failure with improved ejection fraction, COPD on 4L nasal cannula at baseline, and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation presented with worsening shortness of breath and a progressive cough over two weeks. Initial evaluation showed hemodynamic stability, and computed tomography angiography (CTA) of the chest ruled out pulmonary embolism but revealed bilateral basilar pleural effusions, more prominent on the right with partial loculations. Due to worsening respiratory distress, a right-sided chest tube was placed, draining a minimal amount of thick, bloody, pus-like fluid. Analysis revealed an exudative pleural effusion with glucose of 28 mg/dL and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) of 5310 U/L. Pleural fluid cultures identified Candida albicans and Aspergillus species, and blood cultures subsequently grew Candida glabrata. The patient's condition deteriorated, progressing to septic shock and necessitating vasopressor support. Antifungal therapy with anidulafungin and voriconazole was initiated, and TPA/Dornase was administered for loculated empyema, leading to gradual improvement in respiratory and hemodynamic status. Discussion: This case underscores the importance of considering fungal infections, including mixed infections, in patients with chronic lung disease who present with pleural effusions and clinical deterioration. Mixed Candida and Aspergillus pleural infections are extremely uncommon and can rapidly progress to septic shock. Diagnosis requires a high index of suspicion, especially when pleural fluid analysis reveals exudative characteristics with elevated LDH and low glucose levels. Optimal management of mixed fungal pleural infections is not well established but appears to require aggressive antifungal therapy and hemodynamic support.
