N2  - Introduction: Pericarditis Tuberculosis (PTB) is a complex condition, often challenging to diagnose and treat. It can mimic other heart conditions. Timely diagnosis and treatment are crucial to prevent severe consequences. This paper aims to report a rare case, accounting for 1-2% of all TB infections. Case: A 19-year-old male, living in a small town, in poor condition, came to the hospital with chief complaint of shortness of breath for a month. The patient exhibited classic symptoms of TB including a month-long chronic cough with difficulty in expelling phlegm, chest pain, dyspnea, fever, night sweats, decrease in appetite, weight loss, no prior TB medication, non-HIV, and smoker. A chest X-ray revealed fibroinfiltrate at the upper lung, left pleural effusion and Erlenmayer-shaped heart. Contrast Chest CT scan concluded lung TB and pericardial effusion. Echocardiography indicated massive pericardial effusion at left and right lateral, 4.47 cm, and 2.51 cm respectively, no signs of tamponade. Pericardial effusion showed exudative, polymorphonuclear dominant, adenosine deaminase 55 U/L, Xpert MTB/RIF: Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) detected, rifampicin sensitive. The same outcome in Xpert MTB/RIF sputum. The patient was subsequently administered anti-tuberculosis drugs (ATD); rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol. Additional therapy of corticosteroids injection and colchicine oral to prevent severe sequelae TB. Chest tube was placed for left pleural effusion. A pigtail catheter was inserted for pericardial effusion, however inadequate and turned into empyema. A multidisciplinary team performed surgical intervention by pericardiostomy with video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery pericardial window to remove the thickened pericardium, preventing future fluid accumulation and alleviating pressure on the heart. Substantial purulent pericardial effusion was drained in total 1.7 L, and culture confirmed the presence of MTB with secondary bacterial infection. Postoperative and ATD treatment for ten months leading to significant clinical improvement and negative acid-fast bacilli sputum at the end of treatment. Discussion : This case report highlights PTB survivor in one of the TB endemic countries. Early detection and appropriate treatment are crucial in managing this case. Despite the late admission to the hospital, the patient received a multimodal treatment including ATD, anti-inflammatory drugs, antibiotics, and surgical intervention thus indicating a comprehensive treatment strategy. Significant factors that contributed to the patient outcome were compliance to the treatment, young age, and no comorbidity. This also suggests the importance of a thorough evaluation in suspected case because of the potential for misdiagnosis or delayed diagnosis due to atypical presentations.
