N2  - Introduction: E-cigarette and vaping product use-associated lung injury (EVALI) is a growing phenomenon that has gained traction over the last 5 years, first investigated by the Center for Disease Control in 2019. Despite its rise in prevalence, little is known about the pathophysiology and appropriate management of EVALI. Here, we present a case of a healthy 26-year-old male with rapid onset of hypoxia and severe lung injury. We outline his clinical course and highlight his treatment regimen, which may help guide the management of future cases. Case: The patient is a 26-year-old male without significant medical history but 7-year history of daily vape use. He was in his usual state of health until three days prior to arrival to the emergency department. He reported progressive myalgias, chills, fever, shortness of breath, cough, and initially presented hypertensive and febrile to 39.4 C. Lab testing revealed a normal white blood cell count and elevated lactate. CTA demonstrated multifocal ground-glass and consolidative opacities bilaterally. Patient's severe lung disease on imaging, his hypoxia, and progressive tachypnea were concerning for viral or bacterial community acquired pneumonia (CAP) versus severe upper respiratory infection in an immunocompromised host. HIV testing, recent STI testing, and respiratory viral panel were negative. The patient was admitted to medicine service and treated with empiric antibiotics for CAP. His negative infectious workup and extensive lung pathology on CTA was concerning for EVALI. He completed a full course of empiric antibiotics and high dose systemic steroids. He was successfully weaned-off of oxygen and discharged home with a prednisone taper. Discussion: The growing prevalence of electronic cigarette use warrants further consideration of EVALI as a diagnosis in cases where other diagnoses have been ruledout. Moreover, there has yet to be a consensus on an optimal treatment regimen for EVALI, thus more attention should be paid to treatment regimens-specifically the role of steroids-in treating acute cases of EVALI. Conclusions: This case outlines the importance of considering EVALI as a diagnosis in situations where the patient's clinical picture is not clearly explained by any findings in the initial diagnostic work-up. The patient's radiologic findings were more severe than expected in a healthy, young adult. His history of extensive vaping and significant improvement with systemic steroids corresponds with the clinical picture of EVALI, a diagnosis of exclusion that warrants further investigation to optimize guidelines for treating this type of lung injury for future patients.
