N2  - Introduction Hairy Cell Leukemia (HCL) is a rare B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder characterized by neoplastic B cells with cytoplasmic projections, giving them a 'hairy' appearance. It affects 600-800 individuals annually in the U.S., predominantly middle-aged men. HCL typically involves the bone marrow, spleen, and blood, but can infiltrate other organs, presenting with symptoms such as splenomegaly, pancytopenia, and increased infection risk. In rare cases, HCL may manifest as lung infiltrates resembling pneumonia. This case report discusses a patient with an atypical presentation of hairy cell leukemia that appeared as pneumonia. Case Presentation A 57-year-old male presented to the emergency department with worsening shortness of breath over one week, accompanied by a productive cough, pleuritic chest pain, fever, chills, diarrhea, and lethargy. He reported significant weight loss and poor appetite over several months. Vital signs indicated fever, tachycardia, and hypoxia. Chest X-ray revealed right lower lobe consolidation suggestive of pneumonia. Laboratory tests showed bicytopenia, with elevated procalcitonin and mild transaminitis. Despite antibiotic treatment for atypical and community-acquired pneumonia, the patient experienced persistent fevers and worsening hypoxia. Additional workup, including a CT scan, revealed extensive ground-glass and denser opacities, along with hepatosplenomegaly. Bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage returned negative for carcinoma. Subsequent bone marrow aspiration, biopsy, and flow cytometry confirmed hairy cell leukemia, leading to scheduling for induction chemotherapy with cladribine. Discussion Pulmonary infiltrates can be categorized as infectious and non-infectious in etiology. Infectious agents include bacteria, viruses, and fungi, leading to inflammation, immune cell accumulation, and fluid in lung tissues, which can manifest as lobar or diffuse infiltrates. There are a plethora of non-infectious causes of pulmonary infiltrates, and the differential is extremely broad, particularly in immunocompromised patients. Malignancies, such as lymphoma or leukemia, can also infiltrate lung parenchyma, mimicking pneumonia. Pulmonary infiltrates can arise from various conditions, and the onus is on the clinician to assimilate the clinical presentation, radiologic manifestations, and lab data associated with each. While rare, pulmonary manifestations of hairy cell leukemia can mimic pneumonia as seen in this case. In such circumstances, clinicians should consider leukemic infiltrates as a possibility to ensure timely and appropriate treatment tailored to the underlying etiology. Conclusion Accurate diagnosis is crucial to differentiate between infectious and non-infectious pulmonary infiltrates, as treatment strategies differ significantly. This case report highlights the importance of timely diagnosis in a patient with an atypical presentation of pneumonia.
