N2  - Introduction Actinobaculum schaalii is a facultative anaerobic, Gram-positive, non-motile coccoid rod belonging to the Actinobacteriaceae family that is typically normal flora of the skin and genitourinary tract mucosa. It has been increasingly recognized as an opportunistic pathogen, particularly in immunocompromised and elderly individuals. Although it is primarily associated with urinary tract infections, we believe this is the first reported case of empyema due to Actinobaculum schaalii. Case Presentation A 66-year-old male with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, hepatocellular carcinoma, and hypertension presented to the emergency department with progressive dyspnea, bilateral lower extremity swelling, and a non-productive cough for one week. On physical examination, decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion were noted in the left lower lung field. Vital signs included tachypnea (24 breaths/min), tachycardia, and mild hypoxia (SpO2 91%). Blood work revealed leukocytosis and elevated inflammatory markers. CTA showed a large loculated left-sided pleural effusion, measuring 11 cm x 5.5 cm x 9.2 cm. Thoracentesis yielded 120 mL of thick, grey, foulsmelling fluid. Pleural fluid analysis showed turbid appearance, elevated white blood cell counts with neutrophilic predominance, and elevated protein and LDH indicating an exudative complicated parapneumonic effusion or empyema. Cardiothoracic surgery performed left video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with decortication encountering 350 mL of frank pus in the thoracic cavity. Culture revealed Actinobaculum schaalii. Antimicrobial therapy included piperacillin/tazobactam for seven days, followed by Amoxicillin/clavulanate potassium for two weeks. Discussion The most common pathogens responsible for empyema include Streptococcus pneumoniae (20-50% of cases), Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Escherichia coli. Diagnosis is confirmed through imaging and pleural fluid analysis. Treatment generally involves targeted antibiotics, drainage, and, in more complicated cases, surgical procedures. In immunocompromised individuals the risk of infection with less common pathogens, such as A. schaalii, is increased. Our patient's underlying medical conditions, including COPD, CHF, and hepatocellular carcinoma, likely contributed to the development of this severe infection. The detection of A. schaalii in the pleural fluid culture was a critical step, as this pathogen is not typically included in routine testing for pleural space infections. Treatment of A. schaalii infections is generally effective with β-lactam antibiotics, as supported by studies involving other infections. Fluoroquinolones and cotrimoxazole should be avoided due to high minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) and reported treatment failures.
