N2  - Introduction: The rise in black lung cases has been seen in Appalachia region due to the mining industry. The scarring and inflammation caused by the mineral dust, may set the stage for colonization of the nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM). The clinical and radiological similarities between black lung with Progressive Massive Fibrosis (PMF), and M kansasii, makes it a diagnostic challenge. Case Presentation: A 67-year-old man with history of PMF, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) on 2L home oxygen presented with five days of yellowishgreen blood-tinged sputum production. It was associated with chronic dyspnea, cough for the past 3 years. Chest X ray showed bilateral upper lung consolidative changes/masses with cavitation in the right upper lobe. CT chest (figure) showed cavitation in the right upper lobe, numerous masses throughout the lungs with diffuse nodular interstitial thickening, calcified mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy on both lungs. Sputum culture was negative. He tested negative for autoimmune workup and fungal workup. Patient refused bronchoscopy. Sputum for AFB was positive for Mycobacterium. TB QuantiFERON test was negative. Culture grew Mycobacterium kansasii 6 weeks later. He was receiving treatment with rifampin 600 mg daily, ethambutol 1200 mg daily, azithromycin 500 mg q. daily for the last 6 months with improvement in his symptoms. Discussion: PMF is the most advanced form of black lung disease. A total of 4,679 PMF cases out of 314,176 miners who applied for Federal Black Lung Program benefits from 1970 to 2016. The coal mine employment has declined from 250,226 miners in 1979 to 81,485 miners in 2016 but the total number of PMF cases continues to increase. 83.9% of PMF cases were from West Virginia, Kentucky, Pennsylvania, Virginia, or Tennessee. The incidence of PMF cases increased most in Virginia (31.5%) followed by Kentucky (16.6%) and West Virginia (16.8%). Different subspecies of NTM organisms or fungal infections present as cavitary lung lesions. Conspicuous bronchiectasis and nodules accompanying thin walled cavitary disease of limited extent should suggest the possibility of NTM infection. ATS recommends 12 months of treatment. Conclusion: This patient with underlying COPD and PMF made him more susceptible to M. kansasii. The presence of any cavitary lesion in black lung with PMF needs further evaluation with mycobacterial infections. Early detection could reduce a lot of disease burden on the patient and reduce the progression of lesions or its complications.
