N2  - Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are mycobacterial species other than Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex (MTB) and the organisms that cause leprosy. They can cause pulmonary, central nervous system, lymph-node, joint, catheter-related as well as disseminated infection (Griffith, 2007). NTM pulmonary disease (NTM-PD) occurs when NTM infection causes progressive inflammatory lung damage. NTM-PD is increasing in both incidence and prevalence (Haworth et al., 2017). Mycobaterium szulgai, is an uncommon, slow-growing NTM (Martín-Casabona, 2004). M. szulgai primarily causes pulmonary infections which present like MTB pulmonary infections (Sánchez-Alarcos et al., 2003). Due to low prevalence there are no standardised treatment guidelines for the management of M. szulgai infection (Haworth et al., 2017; Sánchez-Alarcos et al., 2003). We describe a case of M. szulgai pulmonary infection in diabetic man in his fifties who presented with productive cough, dyspnoea, weight loss, fatigue and night sweats. Computed tomography (CT) showed three thick walled cavities in the right lung, with consolidation, emphysema and adenopathy, thought to be reactive. Sputum samples were positive for acid alcohol fast bacilli (AAFB) but MTB PCR testing using Xpert MTB/RIF (Cepheid® GeneXpert® systems) was negative. Sputum cultures grew M. szulgai. He was initiated on Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Ethambutol and Azithromycin for a minimum duration of 12 months. The patient improved significantly following initiation of anti-mycobacterial treatment. The patient’s clinical presentation, radiological findings of upper lobe cavitating lesions and characteristics; male, over 50 years old, immunosuppressed with underlying lung disease, are similar to reported cases.4 Evidence to guide therapy is lacking and treatment duration in the literature varies from six to 39 months.5 Consensus guidelines recommend that NTM should be treated for at least 12 months from the first negative sputum culture (Lange, 2022). A combination of at least three susceptible drugs should be used, with rifampicin, ethambutol and azithromycin or clarithromycin recommended first line (Lange, 2022).
