N2  - Purpose: Antimicrobial stewardship (AMS) in the emergency department (ED) is often challenging due to the fast-paced environment and diagnostic uncertainty. At this community hospital, there is a robust inpatient AMS program, however, antibiotic prescribing in the ED is not consistently reviewed. Joint Commission has established new AMS accreditation standards, which require greater efforts from ED providers. The main purpose of this medication use evaluation was to assess the appropriateness of broad-spectrum antibiotics for the treatment of suspected community-acquire pneumonia (CAP) in the ED based on risk for multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs). Methods: This medication use evaluation was conducted at a 391-bed acute care community hospital. A report was generated in the electronic health record for patients over the age of 18 who received meropenem, piperacillin/tazobactam, cefepime, levofloxacin, vancomycin or linezolid for CAP or sepsis secondary to CAP in the ED from 6/1/2024 - 7/31/2024. CAP was defined as having pulmonary infiltrates on chest imaging and at least one respiratory symptom (cough, increased sputum, dyspnea, or pleuritic chest pain). Risk for MDROs was defined by at least 1 of the following criteria: hospitalization and receipt of IV antibiotics within the last 3-months, history of MDROs isolated within 1 year or failure of outpatient therapy. Organisms considered to be MDROs were Pseudomonas aeruginosa, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, extended-spectrum beta-lactamase- producing organisms, carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae species, Acinetobacter baumanii or Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. Data was collected on the patient's point of origin, categorized as home or congregated living facility, ED discharge disposition, risk for MDROs, ventilator status, antimicrobial(s) administered and past microbiological results.
