N2  - Background: Klebseilla pneumoniae is a gram-negative organism that can cause cavitating pneumonia with extrapulmonary manifestations. Patients with severe pneumonia, especially those with gram-negative organisms, may present with acute respiratory distress and have an increased risk for thrombogenicity triggered by inflamation. We report a rare case of Klebseilla pneumonia presenting as rapidly large cavitating left-sided pneumonia that was accompanied by a intracardiac left ventricular thrombus and pulmonary embolism of the main pulmonary artery. We also discuss and address the treatment challenges in this situation. Methods and Results: A 36-year-old male with no known co morbidities presented to our hospital with a history of chest pain and breathing difficulty on exertion for 3 days, a low-grade fever with a cough, and minimal sputum expectoration for the last 2 days. He is an alcoholic, smoker, and cannabis abuser. There is no past history of pulmonary tuberculosis. On general physical examination, the patient was afebrile with a pulse rate of 108 beats/min, a blood pressure of 120/70 mmHg, and a spo2 of 96% at room air. Cardiac examination - heart sounds were normal and there was no murmur. Respiratory system examination revealed normal vesicular breath sounds and crepitations in the left mammary area. Laboratory investigations showed hemoglobin: 15.2 g/dL, hematocrit: 42%, raised white blood cell count -17,200 cells/mm3, platelet count -233,000 cells/mm3, raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) -80mm at 1 h, and random blood sugar: 180 mg/dL. Blood investigations for viral markers were negative. The chest X-ray taken on the day of admission was normal. Ecg showed sinus tachycardia and a right-axis deviation with an RV strain pattern. A 2D transthoracic echocardiogram on admission showed mild RA, RV dilatation with RV dysfunction (TAPSE -14 mm), TRPG of 40 mmHg. Normal LV function, Ejection fraction of 60%, with a clot of size 1.8* 1.4 cm in left ventricle, polypoid in shape attached to left ventricular apex. CT pulmonary angiography revealed a filling defect in the left main pulmonay artery, causing near total occlusion extending to the upper lingula and lower lobar arteries, segmental, and sub-segmental arteries. A thin-walled, small cavity in the apicoposterior segments of the left upper lobe with surrounding ground glass opacities was also noted in the CT imaging. Ultrasound abdomen and venous doppler of both lower limbs were normal. Samples for blood, urine, and sputum cultures were taken. The patient was started on an inj. Heparin infusion and empirical antibiotic therapy with inj. Ceftriaxone. In spite of the above treatment, the patient had worsening breathlessness and chest discomfort, with two episodes of hemoptysis on the third day of admission. So Inj. Heparin was withheld and laboratory test for APTT, PT/INR was sent. Repeat CXR was done which showed a thick-walled cavity with air fluid levels in the upper and middle zones so a pulmonologist opinion was taken. Blood investigations WBC counts and ESR were on an increasing trend (122 mm at 1 h). A repeat CT Thorax was done on day 3 of admission, which showed thick irregular cavitating consolidation with air fluid level and surrounding ground glass opacities. Meanwhile, sputum for AFB and CBNAT for MTB were negative. The sputum culture turned out to be positive for Klebsiella pneumoniae (K. pneumoniae), which was sensitive to Inj. Piperacillin, Inj.Tazobactam, and Inj. Amikacin, so the patient was switched over to the above antibiotics. Inj. Heparin was restarted after 48 hours as there were no fresh episodes of hemoptysis. The patient's condition improved symptomatically. A repeat 2D transthoracic echocardiogram showed resolution of the left ventricular clot with improvement of right ventricular function (TAPSE-18mm). The basic hypercoagulable workup panel was reported to be negative. The patient was discharged on oral anticoagulant T. Apixabin and asked to follow up at OPD. Radiological resolution of cavitating consolidation was achieved after 3 months. The patient is currently able to carry on with his everyday routines and has returned to work. Conclusion: This patient presented with acute respiratory distress due to rapidly progressive cavitating Klebsiella pneumonia, with coexisting thrombotic manifestations like pulmonary embolism and intracardiac thrombus, which can clinically conceal and exacerbate one another. Seldom is there information in the literature about this rare presentation of K. pneumoniae with a rapidly progressing large cavitating pneumonia linked to various site sepsis related thrombotic events such as left ventricular thrombus in normally functioning Left ventricle and acute pulmonary embolism. This case highlights the difficulties in treating patients who have both significant cavitating pneumonia and a concurrent pulmonary embolism, as well as the link between K.pneumoniae, a gram-negative bacteria, and an inflamatory prothrombotic condition that results in extrapulmonary complications. Patient with a diagnosis with klebsiella pneumonia, especialy those who are deteriorating should be evaluated for sepsis related thrombotic complications and treated with appropriate antibiotics, anticoagulation, and supportive treatment. Key words: Klebsiella pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, left ventricular thrombus, cavitating consolidation. [Formula presented]
