N2  - Background: We present a case of hypercalcemia that worsened following BCG intravesical bladder therapy in a patient subsequently found to have concurrent sarcoid and disseminated tuberculosis (TB) Case description: 79-year-old male with a complex medical history including remote pulmonary tuberculosis, bladder cancer, and warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) treated with glucocorticoids (GC) for over 10 years. He developed hypercalcemia initially during an outpatient visit after GC taper, with corrected calcium (cCa) 11.9. He reported subjective weight loss, with no fevers, chills, night sweats or cough. Testing showed 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (1,25OHD) 69 ng/mL, phosphorus 3.8 mg/dL, PTH 11 pg/mL, PTHrP 6 pg/mL. He subsequently underwent treatment for bladder cancer including BCG intravesical instillations. Two months later, he experienced acute decompensation of AIHA requiring splenectomy, rituximab, and high-dose GC. After GC taper, he developed hypercalcemia with cCa 11.4, PTH 7 pg/mL, PTHrp 9 pg/mL, 1,25OHD 89 ng/mL, 25-hydroxyvitamin D 29 ng/mL, TSH 0.73, negative SPEP/light chains. One out of five AFB sputum samples tested positive; Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) PCR and cultures were negative. Hypercalcemia worsened and he was admitted with cCa 13.6, treated with IV fluids, and IV bisphosphonate. No GC were given due to concerns about systemic TB. Biopsy of a newly evident hepatic lesion demonstrated non-necrotizing hepatic granulomas which were also evident on retrospective examination of prior splenectomy specimen, leading to a diagnosis of sarcoid. Biopsy of a right-sided prostate lesion demonstrated prostate adenocarcinoma and necrotizing granulomatous prostatitis; PCR analysis showed MTB complex, no cultures were completed. ACE levels were in the upper normal limit range. Empiric RIPE therapy for disseminated MTB vs BCGosis was initiated. Patient's hypercalcemia has been refractory to attempts to taper high-dose GC despite frequent IV bisphosphonate and administration of methotrexate. Conclusion: Our case demonstrates refractory 1,25OH D mediated hypercalcemia with concurrent infectious and non-infections granulomatous disorders including systemic BCG-osis, sarcoid, and disseminated TB. Treatment in this case is challenging due to refractory nature of hypercalcemia, difficulty distinguishing disseminated TB from BCG-osis, and concerns about systemic GC and immunosuppressive therapy in the setting of a disseminated infectious disease.
