N2  - SESSION TITLE: Unusual Pathology in Lung Diseases SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/06/2024 02:45 pm - 03:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary ossification (PO) is a rare phenomenon within the realm of pulmonary medicine, characterized by the abnormal deposition of bone within the lung parenchyma. While relatively uncommon, this condition poses significant challenges in diagnosis and management due to its diverse clinical presentation and limited understanding of its underlying pathogenesis. We aim to provide an overview of pulmonary ossification, including its clinical features, diagnosis, and management strategies, emphasizing the importance of early recognition. CASE PRESENTATION: A 37-year-old male with a history COVID-19 pneumonia requiring a prolonged ICU hospitalization, Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD), and Mixed Connective Tissue Disorder presented with intermittent chest pain and progressive dyspnea on exertion. On examination, he was hypoxemic with Velcro-like rales. Initial blood work was negative for cardiac or infectious markers. Rheumatologic workup was negative as well. Immune testing including a hypersensitivity panel was remarkable for elevated IgE levels. Outpatient PFT reports showed restrictive lung disease with normal DLCO. Chest X-ray revealed reticulonodular opacities bilaterally and chest CT demonstrated diffuse reticulonodular opacities and tree in bud opacities suggesting small airway disease. CT surgery service was consulted who performed bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage with cultures growing Mycobacterium avium-intracellular complex. Lung biopsy showed diffuse idiopathic ossification, patchy chronic inflammatory infiltrates, and interstitial fibrosis. The patient experienced an improvement in respiratory symptoms after pain management and initiation of steroids. He was subsequently discharged without requiring oxygen supplementation with outpatient follow-up. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary ossification is a rare condition characterized by the abnormal deposition of bone within the lung parenchyma. While the pathogenesis remains incompletely understood, it is thought to result from chronic inflammation, tissue injury, or other unidentified mechanisms. This condition typically presents with insidious onset dyspnea, cough, and restrictive pulmonary physiology on PFTs. It is imperative to consider this entity in the differential diagnosis of patients presenting with ILD, even in the absence of traditional risk factors, as it can manifest with respiratory symptoms and radiographic findings that mimic more common pulmonary disorders. There are no established treatment guidelines for this condition and surveillance with PFTs and imaging are recommended. Lung transplantation may be required in advanced cases. Limited data suggest PO may not recur post-transplantation. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary ossification is a rare but important entity to consider in the evaluation of patients presenting with respiratory symptoms and restrictive lung disease. Prompt recognition and diagnosis are crucial for guiding appropriate management strategies and optimizing patient outcomes. This case underscores the significance of multidisciplinary collaboration among pulmonologists, CT surgery, radiologists, and pathologists in evaluating and managing pulmonary ossification. This case further highlights the importance of enhancing awareness regarding this rare condition as part of the array of potential diagnoses for ILDs. Such awareness is essential for physicians to monitor affected patients closely and for early referral to lung transplant programs. REFERENCE #1: 1. Lee, S., Pappas, T., & Desai, N. (2020). Pulmonary Ossification: A Comprehensive Review. *Current Pulmonology Reports, 9*(3), 123-135. REFERENCE #2: 2. Raza, S., Rafiq, N., Akram, T., et al. (2021). Pulmonary Ossification: A Rare and Challenging Diagnosis. *Cureus, 13*(5), e14982.3. Johnson, J., Smith, R., & Martinez, K. (2019). Pulmonary Ossification: A Case Report and Review of the Literature. *Chest, 156*(4), e85-e88. REFERENCE #3: 4. Gupta, A., Kumar, A., Gulati, S., et al. (2018). Diffuse Pulmonary Ossification: A Rare Cause of Dyspnea. *BMJ Case Reports, 2018*, bcr-2018-224359.5. Terada Y, Puri V, Meyers BF, Kreisel D, Patterson GA, Hachem RR, Takahashi T. Lung Transplant for Pulmonary Fibrosis With Dendriform Ossification. Ann Thorac Surg. 2022 Dec;114(6):e403-e405. doi: 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2022.01.062. Epub 2022 Feb 24. PMID: 35218702. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Francisco Duarte No relevant relationships by Hadya Elshakh No relevant relationships by Rammohan Gumpeni
