N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (G) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Tuberculous pericarditis (TP) is a rare manifestation of extrapulmonary tuberculosis (TB) in the developed world. Untreated TP can lead to devastating consequences and carries a mortality rate of 80-90%. It is important that TP be considered patients with pericarditis as the appropriate treatment can have large impacts on patient outcomes. CASE PRESENTATION: A 29-year-old male with no significant medical history presented to the Emergency Department in Virginia with complaints of chest pain and dyspnea. He had associated fever, night sweats, productive cough, diarrhea, and unintentional weight loss. Symptoms began shortly after immigrating from South America to the United States five months earlier. Two weeks prior to his presentation in Virginia, he was admitted to a hospital in New York for similar symptoms. At that time, he was diagnosed with pericarditis with a pericardial effusion which was thought to be secondary to metapneumovirus and underwent pericardiocentesis. He was discharged with aspirin and colchicine. Computed tomography imaging revealed necrotic hilar lymphadenopathy and pleural effusions. Transthoracic echocardiogram revealed a large pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology. Additional echocardiographic findings included annulus reversus, septal bounce, and a tubular right ventricle suggestive of constrictive pericarditis. Urgent pericardiocentesis was performed yielding 270 cc of fluid consistent with exudate. QuantiFERON gold testing was positive and adenosine deaminase testing of the pericardial fluid was elevated suggesting a high probability of mycobacterium tuberculosis infection. Given this, a pericardial biopsy was also collected which revealed caseating granulomatous inflammation. Sputum and pericardial fluid collected in our facility then returned positive for acid fast bacilli.The patient was initiated on a two month course of rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol with pyridoxine supplementation followed by four months of rifampin and isoniazid alone at the direction of our infectious disease team. Due to constrictive pericarditis, a prednisone course was also started along with prophylaxis against Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. The patient was ultimately discharged home with follow up in the infectious diseases clinic. DISCUSSION: In developed countries, most cases of acute pericarditis in immunocompetent patients are idiopathic or viral. However, TB is the leading cause of pericarditis in many non industrialized countries. Complications associated with TP include pericardial effusion with tamponade and constrictive pericarditis, with the latter occurring in 30-60% of patients. The risk of progressing to these complications increases with delayed or inappropriate treatment. Once chronic constriction develops, there is high morbidity and debilitating symptoms. The treatment of tuberculous effusive constrictive pericarditis is multifaceted, including pericardiocentesis, anti-tubercular therapy, and corticosteroids. If the patient remains symptomatic with chronic constriction following treatment he will be referred for pericardiectomy which is the only available treatment for constrictive pericarditis. CONCLUSIONS: This case demonstrates the importance of keeping TB in the differential in patients with pericarditis in order to prevent further TP-related complications from arising. If suspicion is high for TB infection then appropriate pathologic and microbiologic testing should be completed to verify diagnosis to initiate treatment. REFERENCE #1: Yacoub M, Quintanilla Rodriguez BS, Mahajan K. Constrictive-Effusive Pericarditis. [Updated 2023 Jul 24]. In: StatPearls [Internet]. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing; 2023 Jan- REFERENCE #2: Isiguzo G, Du Bruyn E, Howlett P, Ntsekhe M. Diagnosis and Management of Tuberculous Pericarditis: What Is New? Curr Cardiol Rep. 2020 Jan 15;22(1):2. doi: 10.1007/s11886-020-1254-1. PMID: 31940097; PMCID: PMC7222865. REFERENCE #3: Chang SA. Tuberculous and Infectious Pericarditis. Cardiol Clin. 2017 Nov;35(4):615-622. doi: 10.1016/j.ccl.2017.07.013. PMID: 29025551. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Jeffrey Drury No relevant relationships by Jonathan Hillyard No relevant relationships by Timothy O'Toole
