N2  - SESSION TITLE: Uncommon Presentations of Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Legionella bacteria are aerobic, gram-negative, intracellular pathogens typically transmitted via inhalation of aerosols from contaminated water or soil. The two common clinical conditions associated with legionella bacteria are Legionnaires disease (legionella pneumonia), and Pontiac fever (self-limited febrile illness). Old age (>50 years), travel history, diabetes mellitus, and immunocompromise, particularly impaired cell-mediated immunity are the risk factors associated with Legionella pneumonia. We present a case of Legionella pneumonia in a 35-year-old male who is a heavy cannabis smoker, leading to the need for hospital admission. This presentation in a young male without diabetes mellitus, history of travel, or immunocompromise suggests a potential association with cannabis use. CASE PRESENTATION: Our patient is a 35-year-old male presenting with abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, bizarre behaviour, and abnormal breathing pattern. The patient, a heavy cannabis user, smoked 4-5 joints daily, and has been smoking cannabis for the last 10 years. Blood workup revealed hypernatremia, transaminitis, and elevated Creatine Kinase. Chest X-ray showed a left lower lobe consolidation. Urinary antigen for legionella was positive. Oral Azithromycin and IV fluids were given. Rhabdomyolysis resolved with the IV fluid and mental status improved with the antibiotic treatment. The patient remained afebrile for 24 hours and was discharged home to complete 10 days of oral antibiotic treatment. DISCUSSION: Cannabis is the most extensively used illicit substance globally. With its legalization in the United States, it is important to increase our understanding of the potential health consequences of using cannabis. The demographics of various diseases may shift, emphasizing the need for further research. Δ(9)-tetrahydrocannabinol (Δ9-THC), a key cannabis component, acts as an agonist for cannabinoid receptors CB1 which are primarily present in neural cells and CB2 primarily present on immune cells. The presence of CB receptors on immune cells suggests that cannabis use may have potent immunomodulatory effects. (5) The bacteria enter lungs through inhalation and micro aspiration. They then attach to alveolar macrophages, initiating host phagocytosis. They release effector proteins that impair phagosome-lysosome fusion, allowing survival within the host cell. (13) In humans, alveolar macrophage (AM) counts from bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) are notably higher in cannabis smokers compared to non-smokers. (6) AM retrieved from the lungs of marijuana smokers exhibited an impaired ability to eliminate bacteria and tumor cells. (7) These AMs exhibited reduced ability to utilize nitric oxide as an antibacterial effector molecule and produced lower levels of tumor necrosis factor–alpha, granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor, and interleukin-6. This suggests that marijuana exposure diminishes the function and/or cytokine production of AMs in the lungs. (7,8,9) Exposure to Δ9-THC alters phagocytosis related specific cytoskeletal components, inhibiting macrophage-mediated phagocytosis. (10) CONCLUSIONS: This case report along with others reported by Nguyen et al (2010) suggest that individuals who smoke cannabis are at higher risk of developing Legionnaires disease. The risk may be cumulative with tobacco use, potentially explaining the increased severity of infection and the younger age of the affected individual without any additional risk factors. (12) Testing cannabis users for legionella pneumonia, despite lacking typical risk factors, is prudent due to the potential elevated risk. REFERENCE #1: 1) Ruiz M, Ewig S, Marcos MA, et al. Etiology of communityacquired pneumonia: impact of age, comorbidity, and severity. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 1999; 160 (2): 397-405. 2) Den Boer JW, Nijhof J, Friesema I. Risk factors for sporadic community-acquired Legionnaires' disease. A 3-year national case-control study. Public Health. 2006; 120 (6): 566-571. 3) Che D, Campese C, Santa-Olalla P, et al. Sporadic communityacquired Legionnaires' disease in France: a 2-year national matched case-control study. Epidemiol Infect. 2008; 136 (12): 1684-1690. 4) Bagaitkar J, Demuth DR, Scott DA. Tobacco use increases susceptibility to bacterial infection. Tob Induc Dis. 2008; 4: 12-22. 5) Klein TW, Newton C, Larsen K, et al. The cannabinoid system and immune modulation. J Leukoc Biol. 2003; 74 (4): 486-496. 6) Barbers RG, Gong H Jr, Tashkin DP, Oishi J, Wallace JM. Differential examination of bronchoalveolar lavage cells in tobacco cigarette and marijuana smokers. Am Rev Respir Dis. 1987; 135 (6): 1271-1275. 7) Baldwin GC, Tashkin DP, Buckley DM, Park AN, Dubinett SM, Roth MD. Marijuana and cocaine impair alveolar macrophage function and cytokine production. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 1997; 156 (5): 1606-1613. 8) Roth MD, Baldwin GC, Tashkin DP. Effects of delta9-tetrahydrocannabinol on human immune function and host defense. Chem Phys Lipids. 2002; 121 (1-2): 229-239. 9) Shay AH, Choi R, Whittaker K, et al. Impairment of antimicrobial activity and nitric oxide production in alveolar macrophages from smokers of marijuana and cocaine. J Infect Dis. 2003; 187 (4): 700-704. 10) Tang JL, Lancz G, Specter S, Bullock H. Marijuana and immunity: tetrahydrocannabinol-mediated inhibition of growth and phagocytic activity of the murine macrophage cell line, P388D1. Int J Immunopharmacol. 1992; 14 (2): 253-262. 11) Springs AE, Karmaus PW, Crawford RB, Kaplan BL, Kaminski NE. Effects of targeted deletion of cannabinoid receptors CB1 and CB2 on immune competence and sensitivity to immune modulation by Delta9-tetrahydrocannabinol. J Leukoc Biol. 2008; 84 (6): 1574-1584. 12) Nguyen, Lan Tien; Picard-Bernard, Valérie; Perriot, Jean (2010). Legionnaires Diseasei in Cannabis Smokers. Chest, 138(4), 989–991. doi:10.1378/chest.10-0410. 13) Edelstein PH and Roy CR. Legionnaires' Disease and Pontiac Fever. In: Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 8, Bennet JE, Dolin R and Blaser MJ (Eds), Elsevier, Pennsylvania 2015. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Prachi Bapat No relevant relationships by Arsh Chowdhary No relevant relationships by Asli Sucu
