N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Malignancies II Clinical Case Puzzlers SESSION TYPE: Case Puzzlers PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 08:00 am - 09:00 am HISTORY: A 51-year-old man, with no significant medical history, presented with a persistent cough productive of sputum for the past year, along with worsening shortness of breath over the last six months and a three-week history of fever. He reported a weight loss of 15 pounds within the past six months. Despite initial treatment with antibiotics, mucolytics, and nebulizers, his symptoms worsened and he also had chest tightness on exertion. He experienced an episode of hemoptysis on the third day of hospitalization and disclosed having had 3-4 previous episodes of hemoptysis in the past four months. The patient denied significant risk factors such as tuberculosis (TB), chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), or smoking history. Additionally, he had no established diagnosis of asthma. There was no history of sick contact exposure, but the patient reported occupational exposure related to farming. PHYSICAL FINDINGS: Temperature 101.3°F, respiratory rate of 23/min, heart rate of 90/min, SpO2 92% on room air. BMI 22. Inspiratory crackles are noted in the left lower lung field (LLL). Occasional diffuse wheezing on exertion or when the patient had shortness of breath. DISCUSSION: The clinical presentation, radiological findings, and laboratory results strongly suggested allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). High-resolution CT (HRCT) revealed characteristic features such as a "finger in glove" appearance in the right lower lobe, tree-in-bud appearance in bilateral lung fields, and central bronchiectatic changes, highly indicative of ABPA. Moreover, the presence of high attenuation mucus, pathognomonic of ABPA, was observed in the left lobe. In resource-limited settings where testing for IgE levels or skin testing for Aspergillus may not have been feasible, maintaining a high index of suspicion was crucial for diagnosis. Additionally, the patient's positive response to steroids and antifungals further supported the diagnosis of ABPA. ENUMERATION OF DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS AND FINAL DIAGNOSIS: Bacterial pneumoniaTuberculosisAllergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA)Eosinophilic PneumoniaParasitic InfectionsChurg-Strauss Syndrome (CSS)Invasive Fungal InfectionsBronchiectasisLung CancerGranulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)The patient was initially admitted for suspected pneumonia and commenced on antibiotics. However, diffuse wheezing also made us think about undiagnosed underlying asthma, as COPD was less likely as the patient was a nonsmoker. Later on, worsening symptoms during hospitalization and hemoptysis led to further evaluation with a CT scan, which revealed classic findings of Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA) on HRCT. Subsequently, he was started on Voriconazole and IV methylprednisolone, along with inhalers. The patient responded well to antifungal and steroid therapy, resulting in discharge on the 7th day of hospitalization with oral antifungals and steroids. The patient was also referred to a pulmonologist for further evaluation of underlying asthma/cystic fibrosis and management. PERTINENT LAB DATA: Leukocytosis with a white blood cell count of 24,000/microL, 62% neutrophils, 25% lymphocytes, 10% monocyte, and 3% eosinophils (absolute eosinophils 720 cells/microL). Tuberculosis testing negative. TEACHING POINTS: 1. High Attenuation Mucus (HAM) is a pathognomic of ABPA in patients with Asthma.2. In resource-limited settings, ABPA can be diagnosed based on clinical and radiological findings for initial treatment until further investigations are done. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Anunaya Aashish No relevant relationships by Dr. Shruti Baradkar No relevant relationships by Naman Kumar No relevant relationships by Dhruvangkumar Modi No relevant relationships by Anum Mubasher No relevant relationships by Mandvi Pandey
