N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (A) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: A cavitary lesion is a gas filled area with a pulmonary consolidation, mass, or nodule. One can have many causes including bacterial, fungal, mycobacterial, parasitic, or embolic. Today we present a case describing an immunocompetent patient with cavitary lesions that were originally suspected to be secondary to a bacterial or mycobacterial infection that was revealed to be a fungal infection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 66 y.o. African American female with COPD and tobacco use presented for cough, weakness, fatigue, and headache for 2 weeks. She had no sick contacts or recent travel. She had no skin lesions. She was afebrile but had a WBC of 32.1. CT chest showed multiple noncalcified cavitary lesions throughout both lungs in addition to mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy. Broad spectrum antibiotics were started. On day 3 of hospitalization, Pulmonology performed a BAL and lung biopsy. She was transferred to ICU for post procedure pulmonary insufficiency. QuantiFERON was positive on admission, but TB was ruled out by AFB sputum cultures. Blood and sputum cultures showed no growth since initial presentation. Despite appropriate antibiotic therapy, she continued to have daily fevers. Infectious Disease was consulted. Histoplasma antigen obtained on admission resulted positive after 3 days. Amphotericin B was initiated. HIV was negative. Lung biopsy was negative for malignancy. Patient started having worsening ventilator requirements, pressor requirements, and mental status; therefore, brain imaging and lumbar puncture were obtained. CSF studies revealed elevated opening pressure, protein, glucose, and RBC count but a negative Meningitis panel. Despite antibiotic and antifungal therapy, patient continued to deteriorate. She was palliatively extubated and expired after a 19-day hospital course. Seven weeks after initial BAL, fungal cultures grew Blastomyces dermatitidis. DISCUSSION: Blastomycosis is caused by dimorphic fungi most seen in the Mississippi and Ohio River basins. It is often compared to histoplasmosis (histo). They have a similar distribution in North America, can both be detected by the histoplasma antigen test, and both cause opportunistic pneumonia. Histo tends to primarily remain the lungs while Blasto tends to have extrapulmonary manifestations such as skin lesions and osteomyelitis. Galactomannan will often cross-react with blasto but not with histo. Fungal cultures are the gold standard of diagnosis of blasto. Culture sensitivity is 75% on a single sample. Disseminated Blasto is treated with Amphotericin B for 4-6 weeks, followed by a year of fluconazole or itraconazole. According to the CDC, the incidence rate is state dependent and only reportable in five states with a total blastomycosis incidence of 0.8 cases per 100,000 population. Whereas, the incidence of TB was a rate of 2.7 cases per 100000 population. CONCLUSIONS: Suspecting Blasto requires a high clinical index of suspicion given its rarity and difficulty of obtaining a timely fungal culture. If the patient has been in endemic area, physicians should consider a diagnosis of Blasto if the patient has one of the following: pneumonia of unknown etiology not responding to empiric antibiotics, pneumonia associated with skin lesion or subcutaneous nodules, or granulomas on pathology. Early consultation of Infectious Diseases and initiation of antifungals is recommended. REFERENCE #1: Hansell DM, Bankier AA, MacMahon H, McLoud TC, Muller NL. Remy J. Fleischner Society: glossary of terms for thoracic imaging. Radiology. 2008; 246:697-672. REFERENCE #2: US Department of Health and Human Services. (13 Sept. 2023). "Blastomycosis Statistics." Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 13 Sept. 2023, www.cdc.gov/fungal/diseases/blastomycosis/statistics.html. REFERENCE #3: US Department of Health and Human Services. "Incidence of Tuberculosis in 2019." Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 13 Nov. 2023, www.cdc.gov/tb/statistics/indicators/2019/incidence.htm#:~:text=In%202019%2C%20overall%20TB%20incidence,(27%20cases%20per%20million). DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Toluwani Adebayo No relevant relationships by Ana Bonetti Lugo No relevant relationships by Virginia Overton No relevant relationships by Kristen Roberts
