N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (D) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium conceptionense is a nonpigmented, rapidly growing, nontuberculous mycobacterium, first isolated in France in 2006. Less than 25 cases of infection with Mycobacterium conceptionense have been reported in humans, with only 7 involving pulmonary disease. We report a case of mediastinal lymphadenopathy caused by M. conceptionense infection. The patient was cured with azithromycin and doxycycline. CASE PRESENTATION: A 58-year-old woman, originally from Ecuador, was referred to our clinic due to a one-year history of cough productive of clear to occasionally green sputum, accompanied by a 30-pound weight loss. Medical history was notable for right lower lobe pneumonia one year prior, Diabetes Mellitus II, epilepsy, and depression. Six months preceding her visit, she experienced a second episode of right lower lobe pneumonia, which was treated with antibiotics; however, the cough persisted. Upon consultation with her primary care provider, a CT scan of the Chest revealed mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy, with clear bilateral lung fields. Consequently, she was referred to our clinic for further evaluation of lymphadenopathy.Endobronchial Ultrasound examination of hilar and mediastinal lymph nodes revealed numerous aggregates of non-caseating granulomata, suggestive of sarcoidosis. Additionally, Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) cultures of right lower lobe bronchoalveolar lavage demonstrated the presence of Mycobacterium conceptionense. A therapeutic regimen consisting of doxycycline and azithromycin was initiated. At the one-month follow-up visit, cough and sputum resolved. A CT scan of the Chest conducted three months after initiation of treatment demonstrated resolution of the previously identified mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy. DISCUSSION: Nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) encompass over 180 species, categorized into rapid and slow-growing types. Rapidly growing mycobacteria (RGM) comprise six main groups: Mycobacterium fortuitum, M. chelonae/M. abscessus, M. smegmatis, M. mucogenicum, M. mageritense/M. wolinskyi, and pigmented RGM. The M. fortuitum group includes M. fortuitum, M. peregrinum, M. senegalense, M. porcinum, M. neworleansense, M. boenickei, M. houstonense, M. brisbanense, M. septicum, and M. setense. Species within the Mycobacterium fortuitum group are emerging opportunistic pathogens. Mycobacterium conceptionense, belonging to the Mycobacterium fortuitum group, was initially isolated in France in 2006 from a patient with posttraumatic osteitis. It shares 99.7% 16S rRNA and 97.0% rpoB gene sequence similarity with M. porcinum, the closest species. Less than 25 cases of M. conceptionense infection have been reported in humans, with only a few instances of pulmonary involvement. The optimal treatment approach remains uncertain; however, in vitro susceptibility testing indicates susceptibility to imipenem, doxycycline, azithromycin, amikacin, ciprofloxacin, and amoxicillin-clavulanate, while it is resistant to penicillin and vancomycin. Considering our patient's baseline medications for epilepsy and depression, which exhibit significant interactions with quinolones, azithromycin and doxycycline were selected. Resolution of the patient's symptoms and lymphadenopathy on repeat imaging following the initiation of antibiotic therapy suggests that findings of non-caseating granulomas on lymph node biopsy were likely a consequence of infection rather than sarcoidosis. CONCLUSIONS: Mycobacterium conceptionense is an uncommon cause of pulmonary mycobacterial infections. Non-caseating granulomas do not always represent sarcoidosis or inflammatory etiologies. Clinicians should maintain a high index of suspicion for infectious etiologies especially when there is clinical and radiological improvement following appropriate antibiotic therapy. REFERENCE #1: 1. Adékambi, T., Stein, A., Carvajal, J., Raoult, D., & Drancourt, M. (2006). Description of Mycobacterium conceptionense sp. nov., a Mycobacterium fortuitum group organism isolated from a posttraumatic osteitis inflammation. Journal of clinical microbiology, 44(4), 1268–1273. https://doi.org/10.1128/JCM.44.4.1268-1273.2006. REFERENCE #2: 2. Kim, S. Y., Kim, M. S., Chang, H. E., Yim, J. J., Lee, J. H., Song, S. H., Park, K. U., Song, J., & Kim, E. C. (2012). Pulmonary infection caused by Mycobacterium conceptionense. Emerging infectious diseases, 18(1), 174–176. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1801.110251. REFERENCE #3: 3. Michienzi, S. M., Burgos, R. M., & Novak, R. M. (2019). Mycobacterium conceptionense Pneumonitis in Patient with HIV/AIDS1. Emerging infectious diseases, 25(10), 1986–1988. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2510.190444.4. Brown-Elliott BA.Philley JV. 2017. Rapidly Growing Mycobacteria. Microbiol Spectr 5:10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0027-2016. https://doi.org/10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0027-2016 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Javier Blanco Jimenez No relevant relationships by Arjun Madhavan No relevant relationships by Anum Qadri
