N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (F) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Nontuberculous Mycobacterium (NTM) is a challenging infection implicated in both normal and diseased lungs. Cystic fibrosis (CF) is a strong risk factor for NTM, and often thought of as a disease of childhood. The US CF registry between 1995-2005 demonstrated 7-9% of new CF diagnosis was made in adults [1]. In addition, among CF pathogens NTM has a prevalence of 6-13% with the majority of species being M. Abscessus (16-68%) [2]. Due to the high burden of treatment on the patient and that refractory disease is associated with severe morbidity and mortality, it is critical to gain further understanding in what underlying pathologies and their associated microbiology are implicated in a rare presentation of late-onset disease process. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old female presented with multiple complaints consisting of a chronic productive cough, body aches, fatigue, and pooling of sputum in the back of her throat. She reported that a previous physician advised her that she may have chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) based on a chest X-ray. She was a lifelong nonsmoker with a history of childhood exposure to secondhand smoke, seasonal allergies, and pet ownership.Diagnostic evaluation included a CT chest showing bibasilar tree in bud opacities, bronchiectasis, and mucous plugging [Figure 1]. Pulmonary function tests (PFTs) demonstrated a non-obstructive pattern with a FEV1/FVC of 92% and FEV1 of 89%. However, RV/TLC was 127%, indicating air trapping. Given imaging and function testing, the diagnostic process progressed to bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage, which revealed growth of Mycobacterium Kansasii, Mycobacterium Abscessus, and Mycobacterium Avium Complex (MAC) [Figure 2] on acid fast bacilli cultures. Treatment was initiated based on this culture information. The patient's clinical course showed delayed improvement in her respiratory symptoms with treatment for these mycobacterial infections, and she experienced side effects and challenges related to her medication regimen. Subsequent evaluations included high sweat chloride levels and testing for cystic fibrosis, revealing one mutation; delta 508, which lead to the diagnosis of late onset CF. Further treatment modifications were made to manage her CF, and her respiratory symptoms continued to improve. DISCUSSION: This diagnostic process demonstrates how to utilize our understanding of the predisposing pathologies associated with NTM infection and how to incorporate the underlying pathology in the NTM treatment journey. CONCLUSIONS: This case is significant as there are no known publications on triple atypical NTM co-infections specifically with Mycobacterium Kansasii, Mycobacterium Abscessus, and Mycobacterium Avium Complex. The case is particularly noteworthy given its association with the rare diagnosis of late-onset cystic fibrosis. REFERENCE #1: 1. Jain R. Diagnosing Cystic Fibrosis in Adults: Better Late Than Never. Ann Am Thorac Soc. 2018 Oct;15(10):1140-1141. doi: 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201806-432ED. PMID: 30272501. REFERENCE #2: 2. Martiniano SL, Nick JA, Daley CL. Nontuberculous mycobacterial infections in cystic fibrosis. Clin Chest Med 2016; 37: 83–96. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Jeannette Crew No relevant relationships by Jeffrey Kopita No relevant relationships by Kenji Yoshino
