N2  - SESSION TITLE: Rare Pulmonary Manifestations of Systemic Disease SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/06/2024 02:45 pm - 03:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis (PPFE) is a rare clinical entity, characterized by fibrosis in the upper lobes visceral pleura and subpleural fibroelastosis(1). The etiology of PPFE remains elusive, with idiopathic cases being commonly considered(2). CASE PRESENTATION: A 56-year-old woman with relapsing polychondritis, diagnosed in 2008, and ulcerative proctitis, diagnosed in 2019, presented with progressive dyspnea and dry cough since 2020. Her medication included prednisolone 2.5 mg/day, cyclophosphamide 50 mg weekly, and mesalazine rectal suppositories. Physical examination revealed platythorax with suprasternal notch deepened. CT chest revealed fibrotic bands, traction bronchiectasis, and architectural distortion with bilateral pleural thickening in both upper lobes. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid showed a predominance of neutrophils with no identifiable pathogen. Cyclophosphamide was discontinued due to the inability to exclude drug-induced interstitial lung fibrosis. Her pulmonary function tests showed FVC of 1.11 L (44.2% predicted), TLC of 69.3% predicted, DLCO of 30% predicted, and RV/TLC of 58.3%. One year later, she experienced bilateral spontaneous pneumothorax. Besides the pneumothorax, HRCT showed progression of lung fibrosis and pleural thickening in both upper lobes. Additionally, there were ground-glass opacities with multifocal consolidation in the peripheral areas of both basal lungs, with subpleural sparing, which were compatible with non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) and organizing pneumonia (OP) patterns. She underwent left upper lobe wedge resection, along with surgical pleurodesis. The histopathology showed the characteristic features of pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis. She was treated with prednisolone 30 mg/day. Initially, her clinical condition was stable, and follow-up HRCT showed an improvement of NSIP and OP patterns in both basal lungs. However, during the 2-year follow-up, there was a progression of fibrotic changes and pleural thickening in both upper lobes, along with progressive loss of lung function. She ultimately died from chronic respiratory failure. DISCUSSION: According to the criteria established in 2012, this patient confirmed a definite PPFE diagnosis(3). Various etiologies of PPFE have been proposed, including post-hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, connective tissue diseases, and chemotherapy. In this patient, respiratory symptom onset occurred during cyclophosphamide treatment. However, cyclophosphamide is less likely to be the cause of PPFE, as it was administered at a very low dose, and the patient developed a progression of PPFE and a new onset of NSIP and OP after discontinuation of cyclophosphamide. Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is associated with various parenchymal lung diseases, with the most common being OP and NSIP (4). The final diagnosis of this patient, based on multidisciplinary discussion, was PPFE with the overlap of NSIP and OP, associated with ulcerative colitis. There have been a few case reports of PPFE which was associated with IBD with various clinical courses and prognoses (5-7). There have been no guidelines on the treatment of PPFE, whether idiopathic or secondary. Corticosteroids with or without immunosuppressive agents have been recommended on an individualized basis. CONCLUSIONS: The present case report highlights the rare interstitial pneumonia that occurred in a patient with ulcerative colitis. PPFE is a condition with a highly variable presentation and progression, underscoring the importance of early recognition and a multidisciplinary approach to diagnosis and management. REFERENCE #1: 1. Frankel SK, Cool CD, Lynch DA, Brown KK. Idiopathic pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis: Description of a novel clinicopathologic entity. Chest. 2004;126(6):2007-13. 2. Chua F, Desai SR, Nicholson AG, Devaraj A, Renzoni E, Rice A, et al. Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis. A review of clinical, radiological, and pathological characteristics. Ann Am Thorac Soc. 2019;16(11):1351-9. 3. Reddy TL, Tominaga M, Hansell DM, Thusen Jvd, Rassl D, Parfrey H, et al. Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis: A spectrum of histopathological and imaging phenotypes. European Respiratory Journal. 2012;40(2):377-85. REFERENCE #2: 4. Black H, Mendoza M, Murin S. Thoracic manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease. Chest. 2007;131(2):524-32. 5. Amado C, Ferreira PG. Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis associated with crohn's disease: A new aetiology? Eur J Case Rep Intern Med. 2020;7(12):002017. REFERENCE #3: 6. Kinoshita Y, Ikeda T, Miyamura T, Ueda Y, Yoshida Y, Kushima H, et al. A proposed prognostic prediction score for pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis. Respir Res. 2021;22(1):215. 7. Singh R, Sundaram P, Joshi JM. Upper lobe fibrosis in ulcerative colitis. J Assoc Physicians India. 2003;51:515-7. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Supparerk Disayabutr No relevant relationships by Chakorn Lapanan No relevant relationships by Ruchira Ruangchira-urai No relevant relationships by Krittika Teerapuncharoen No relevant relationships by Suwimon Wonglaksanapimon
