N2  - SESSION TITLE: Infections and Inflammation SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 02:35 pm - 03:05 pm INTRODUCTION: It has been demonstrated that chronic bacterial and viral infections can induce epigenetic changes that alter host function (3). We present the case of a 71-year-old-male who presents with chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis. Despite treatment, the patient had unexplained persistent fevers with night sweats and nonspecific rash of the face and extremities. Genetic workup would confirm a pathogenic heterozygous variant of Familial Mediterranean Fever. We postulate that the patient's chronic infection led to the activation of the genetic variant. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71-year-old-male presents with a medical history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia presents with the chief complaint of right sided chest pain, cough with tan colored sputum, and self reported fevers of 102 degrees fahrenheit. He was previously prescribed a macrolide antibiotic by his primary care physician without relief of symptoms. He was also seen at a previous hospital and found to have pseudomonas pneumonia a month prior. Imaging at that time showed a right apical cavitary lung lesion. Repeat computed tomography (CT) of the chest on this admission showed a persistent right apical cavitary lung lesion (Figure 1). Bronchoscopy was performed which revealed Aspergillus niger. Treatment with Voriconazole was started and the patient was discharged. The patient's respiratory symptoms significantly improved, but one year later he began developing cyclic fevers with night sweats. Pulmonary function testing at that time showed a restrictive pattern with reduced diffusion capacity (Figure 2). Due to his complex history of chronic pulmonary aspergillosis he was referred for autoimmune work up. Genetic testing would eventually reveal that the patient had the MEFVc.2177T>C (p.Val726A1A, V726A) heterozygous variant for Familial Mediterranean fever. The patient was referred to rheumatology for treatment. DISCUSSION: Familial Mediterranean fever (FMF) is an autoinflammatory disease caused by mutations in the MEFV gene. MEFV is responsible for producing the protein Pyrin which is involved with the regulation of inflammation. Symptoms of FMF include fever, abdominal pain, pleurisy, myalgia, and rash. Fever is present in 96% of inflammatory episodes. Traditionally, FMF is an autosomal recessive disease requiring two mutated alleles of the gene for penetrance, but studies have shown that up to 30% of FMF cases have only one mutation (2). The V726A variant found in our patient, is present in just 2.5% of studied cases (1). As the patient had no prior episodes of FMF before his chronic aspergillosis, it is believed that his infection may have caused an epigenetic event that unmasked the MEFV variant causing FMF. CONCLUSIONS: Studies have shown how viruses and bacteria alter their host to ensure replication and transmission. Chronic infections have been known to cause permanent changes to cellular function and compromise the immune response (3). One such example is with Helicobacter pylori which is known to cause changes in DNA methylation that persist after its eradication causing chronic inflammation (3). It is possible that this case represents another example. This brings to light the need for further research into the relationship between pathogens and the epigenetic changes they can induce. REFERENCE #1: Bagheri M, Rad IA. Analysis of the Most Common Three MEFV Mutations in 630 Patients with Familial Mediterranean Fever in Iranian Azeri Turkish Population. Maedica (Bucur). 2017 Sep;12(3):169-173. PMID: 29218063; PMCID: PMC5706755. REFERENCE #2: Booty MG, Chae JJ, Masters SL, Remmers EF, Barham B, Le JM, Barron KS, Holland SM, Kastner DL, Aksentijevich I. Familial Mediterranean fever with a single MEFV mutation: where is the second hit? Arthritis Rheum. 2009 Jun;60(6):1851-61. doi: 10.1002/art.24569. PMID: 19479870; PMCID: PMC2753538. REFERENCE #3: Fischer N. Infection-induced epigenetic changes and their impact on the pathogenesis of diseases. Semin Immunopathol. 2020 Apr;42(2):127-130. doi: 10.1007/s00281-020-00793-1. PMID: 32318841; PMCID: PMC7174260. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Zuhair Ali No relevant relationships by Rene Franco No relevant relationships by Jorge Garza No relevant relationships by Ethan Goldberg No relevant relationships by Arjun Kamath No relevant relationships by Ahsan Syed
