N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Global Case Reports Posters SESSION TYPE: Global Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 01:45 pm - 02:30 pm INTRODUCTION: Tuberculosis presents a significant global health challenge, particularly in regions where it is widespread, often proving challenging to manage effectively. The complexity of treatment further intensifies when compounded by coinfections. Pulmonary fungal infections, although infrequent, is characterized by elevated morbidity and mortality. Their impact is most pronounced among immunocompromised individuals, especially those with underlying coinfections. CASE PRESENTATION: A 38-year-old female with a medical history significant for diabetes mellitus and recent admission for diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), severe pneumonia, and hemorrhagic stroke presents with a 5-day history of fever, purulent expectorant cough, dyspnea on exertion, and lethargy. Physical examination revealed sinus tachycardia, hypoxia with an SpO2 of 84% on room air, vesicular murmur on the left lung, and known right hemiplegia. CT chest showed an irregular, heterogeneous, thick-walled cavitary lesion in the left lingula and superior lower lobe with concerning features suggestive of tuberculosis versus necrotic bacterial infection. Bronchoscopy revealed a large necrotic cavity affecting the superior and lower bronchi of the left lung with hemopurulent aspirations. Cultures confirmed positivity for mucormycosis and Aspergillus fumigatus. Gene Xpert testing was positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The patient underwent left pneumonectomy and was initiated on treatment with liposomal amphotericin, carbapenem, rifampicin, isoniazid, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide. Patient has tolerated treatment well and follow-up sputum cultures have been negative for mycobacterium, mucormycosis and aspergillus. DISCUSSION: By altering immunity, diabetes mellitus predisposes individuals to opportunistic infections. The clinical and radiological presentation of mycoses may resemble tuberculosis, thus complicating the diagnosis of coinfections, especially in tuberculosis-endemic regions. The recommended antimycotic therapy for mucormycosis and aspergillosis is liposomal amphotericin B. Coinfection with tuberculosis should be addressed concurrently.Treatment rests on three pillars: early antifungal therapy, surgical intervention, and management of comorbidities. Roden et al. demonstrated through multivariate analysis that combining antifungal therapy with surgery significantly increased survival rates (69%), contrasting with a 97% mortality rate among untreated patients. This finding is supported by reports such as that of Sipsas et al. This is further exemplified in our patient who has successfully undergone treatment. CONCLUSIONS: Due to the clinical similarity between tuberculosis and mycoses, timely diagnosis is often delayed. It is crucial to maintain a high index of clinical suspicion in patients with risk factors for fungal infection who show clinical deterioration despite broad-spectrum antibacterial therapy. REFERENCE #1: 1. Iñiguez-García M, Del Villar-Trujillo A, Cardona-Infante V, Carrillo-Ponce C, Téllez-Becerra J. Diagnóstico y tratamiento de la mucormicosis pulmonar Reporte de un caso. Revista de la Facultad de Medicina UNAM. 2016;59(2):28–37. 2. Aggarwal D, Chander J, Janmeja AK, Katyal R. Pulmonary tuberculosis and mucormycosis co-infection in a diabetic patient. Lung India. 2015;32(1):53–5. 3. Cornely OA, Alastruey-Izquierdo A, Arenz D, Chen SCA, Dannaoui E, Hochhegger B, et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of mucormycosis: an initiative of the European Confederation of Medical Mycology in cooperation with the Mycoses Study Group Education and Research Consortium. Vol. 19, The Lancet Infectious Diseases. 2019. p. e405–21. REFERENCE #2: 4. Skiada A, Lass-Floerl C, Klimko N, Ibrahim A, Roilides E, Petrikkos G. Challenges in the diagnosis and treatment of mucormycosis. Vol. 56, Medical Mycology. 2018. p. S93–101. 5. Martín M, Salavert M. Mucormycosis: Current and future management perspective. Vol. 38, Revista Iberoamericana de Micología. 2021. p. 91–100. 6. Roden MM, Zaoutis TE, Buchanan WL, Knudsen TA, Sarkisova TA, Schaufele RL, et al. Epidemiology and outcome of zygomycosis: a review of 929 reported cases. Clin Infect Dis. 2005;41(5):634-53. 7. Skiada A, Lass-Floerl C, Klimko N, Ibrahim A, Roilides E, Petrikkos G. Challenges in the diagnosis and treatment of mucormycosis. Med Mycol. 2018;56(suppl 1):93-101. DISCLOSURES: No disclosure on file for Maria del Rosario Castro Soto No relevant relationships by Maria Consuelo Duran Mejia No relevant relationships by Edwin Mármol Cazas No relevant relationships by Irene Riestra Guiance
