N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (E) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Human Metapneumovirus(HMPV) was first identified in 2001 in the Netherlands[1]. It is an enveloped RNA virus classified in the Paramyxovirus family[1]. HMPV is distributed worldwide and tends to strike in the late winter and early spring[2]. HMPV infections in adults varied between 3%–7.1% in four consecutive winters[1]. HMPV is thought to be transmitted by direct or close contact with contaminated saliva, droplets, or large particle aerosols[2]. Adults with an HMPV infection might be asymptomatic or have mild upper respiratory tract symptoms to severe pneumonia[2]. HMPV infection presents more often with wheezing than other viruses[2]. Elderly and immunocompromised patients are at higher risk for severe manifestations[2]. We present a case of an HIV/AIDS patient who contracted HMPV and subsequently developed moderate ARDS, a rare manifestation of HMPV. CASE PRESENTATION: A 47-year-old female with a medical history of poorly controlled HIV and diabetes presented to the hospital with 1-week of fatigue and cough productive of white-green phlegm. Her physical exam was significant for wheezing and hypoxemia, requiring supplemental oxygen with 40L and 50% FiO2 high-flow nasal cannula. Her labs revealed severe metabolic derangements consistent with a hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, and an arterial blood gas showed a PaO2 of 93 mmHg. A PCR respiratory viral panel returned positive for HMPV. A chest CT scan showed scattered ground glass opacities(GGO) and interstitial infiltrates. Her HIV viral load and CD4 count were 9,630 copies/ml and 17 cells/uL, respectively. She was admitted to the ICU and was started on insulin, antiretroviral therapy, and empiric antibiotics. She later underwent a bronchoscopy, and alveolar lavage samples were collected to rule out opportunistic infections. Lavage cultures, gram stain, Pneumocystis Jiroveci PCR, and Beta-D Glucan returned negative. She was diagnosed with ARDS secondary to HMPV. Supportive care continued during her course; she was discharged after 14 days. DISCUSSION: Our case highlights the clinical and radiological manifestations of HMPV in patients with HIV/AIDS. In Canada, viruses accounted for 64% of acute respiratory illness in HIV-infected adults, with HMPV being the second most isolated pathogen[3]. Adult HIV patients who contract HMPV are more likely to be hospitalized than HIV-uninfected patients[4]. In such a population, bacterial coinfection was seen in 7-12% of patients, with Streptococcus pneumonia being the most common pathogen[3,4]. Bacterial coinfection was associated with poor outcomes. Accordingly, a significant proportion of these hospitalizations may be prevented by vaccination with pneumococcal conjugate vaccine[5]. CT chest manifestations of HMPV among immunocompetent patients most commonly include non-specific GGO and nodular consolidation described in fewer patients[6]. Immunocompromised patients were more likely to develop asymmetric GGO and parenchymal airspace consolidations accompanied by signs of bronchitis[7], as seen in our case. Ribavirin may exhibit antiviral activity in vitro against HMPV[8]. There is no clinical data on the efficacy of ribavirin for HMPV infections; therefore, it is not recommended. CONCLUSIONS: Amongst HIV/AIDS patients, HMPV remains a common respiratory viral illness that can lead to prolonged hospitalization and ARDS. Physicians should be aware of the clinical and radiographic manifestations of HMPV to provide appropriate supportive care and vaccination and avoid overprescribing antibiotics. REFERENCE #1: 1. Walsh EE, Peterson DR, Falsey AR. Human metapneumovirus infections in adults: another piece of the puzzle. Arch Intern Med. 2008 Dec 8;168(22):2489-96. doi: 10.1001/archinte.168.22.2489. PMID: 19064834; PMCID: PMC2783624. 2. Haas LE, Thijsen SF, van Elden L, Heemstra KA. Human metapneumovirus in adults. Viruses. 2013 Jan 8;5(1):87-110. doi: 10.3390/v5010087. PMID: 23299785; PMCID: PMC3564111. 3. Klein MB, Yang H, DelBalso L, Carbonneau J, Frost E, Boivin G. Viral pathogens, including human metapneumovirus, are the primary cause of febrile respiratory illness in HIV-infected adults receiving antiretroviral therapy. J Infect Dis. 2010 Jan 15;201(2):297-301. doi: 10.1086/649587. PMID: 19951142; PMCID: PMC7109862. REFERENCE #2: 4. Groome MJ, Moyes J, Cohen C, Walaza S, Tempia S, Pretorius M, Hellferscee O, Chhagan M, Haffejee S, Dawood H, Kahn K, Variava E, Cohen AL, Gottberg Av, Wolter N, Venter M, Madhi SA; South African Severe Acute Respiratory Illness (SARI) Surveillance Group. Human metapneumovirus-associated severe acute respiratory illness hospitalization in HIV-infected and HIV-uninfected South African children and adults. J Clin Virol. 2015 Aug;69:125-32. doi: 10.1016/j.jcv.2015.06.089. Epub 2015 Jun 18. PMID: 26209394; PMCID: PMC9134797. 5. Madhi SA, Ludewick H, Kuwanda L, Niekerk Nv, Cutland C, Little T, Klugman KP. Pneumococcal coinfection with human metapneumovirus. J Infect Dis. 2006 May 1;193(9):1236-43. doi: 10.1086/503053. Epub 2006 Mar 17. PMID: 16586360. 6. Keske Ş, Gümüş T, Köymen T, Sandıkçı S, Tabak L, Ergönül Ö. Human metapneumovirus infection: Diagnostic impact of radiologic imaging. J Med Virol. 2019 Jun;91(6):958-962. doi: 10.1002/jmv.25402. Epub 2019 Feb 1. PMID: 30659630; PMCID: PMC7166911. REFERENCE #3: 7. Syha R, Beck R, Hetzel J, Ketelsen D, Grosse U, Springer F, Horger M. Human metapneumovirus (HMPV) associated pulmonary infections in immunocompromised adults--initial CT findings, disease course and comparison to respiratory-syncytial-virus (RSV) induced pulmonary infections. Eur J Radiol. 2012 Dec;81(12):4173-8. doi: 10.1016/j.ejrad.2012.06.024. Epub 2012 Jul 15. PMID: 22795844. 8. Wyde PR, Chetty SN, Jewell AM, Boivin G, Piedra PA. Comparison of the inhibition of human metapneumovirus and respiratory syncytial virus by ribavirin and immune serum globulin in vitro. Antiviral Res. 2003 Sep;60(1):51-9. doi: 10.1016/s0166-3542(03)00153-0. PMID: 14516921. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ali Al-Tarbsheh No relevant relationships by Daniel Chacon No relevant relationships by Ramez Halaseh No relevant relationships by Lidice Hernandez No relevant relationships by Maekhila Koppikar No relevant relationships by Ashley Nguyen No relevant relationships by Jared Piotrkowski No relevant relationships by Alice Sonnino
