N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (V) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Endobronchial lesions caused by MAC infections are very rare even in an immunocompromised host. Mycobacterium Avium Complex a rare condition in immunocompetent individuals CASE PRESENTATION: We report a case of a 38-year-old woman with 6-month history of productive cough of white to yellow color sputum followed by emesis episodes, low grade fever, headaches, shortness of breath, fatigue and unintentional weight loss of 21 lbs in 6 months. Reports multiple visits to her doctor who prescribed oral antimicrobials including Amoxicillin, Levofloxacin and multiple courses of antitussives with mild improvement. Medical history of Diabetes Mellitus type 2, Hypertension, and a stray bullet wound in the right mid back 3 years prior requiring exploratory laparotomy. Imaging showed minimal free intraperitoneal air, laceration of right psoas muscle, and right lower lung contusion. Due to patient's lack of improvement she underwent CT scan of chest that showed multiple central and peripheral nodules showing variable sizes localized in the right mid and lower lung zones, large thick-walled cavitation in right lower lobe superior segment around 9.6 x4.5 x 6.8 cm, extending to the pleural surface. Also thickening of bronchial walls, focal bronchial dilation. Due to imaging findings she was sent to ED. Evaluation's vital signs were within normal limits. Physical examination had decreased breath sounds in the right lower lobe with inspiratory crackles. Bloodwork was within normal limits. Patient was admitted with airborne isolation for MTB and was started on empiric antimicrobial therapy with Cefepime, Vancomycin and Metronidazole alongside Infectious Diseases evaluation. Initial AFB sputum stains, routine sputum and blood cultures were negative. HIV, PPD and Immunological workup were negative. Bronchoscopy showed mild right basal erythema; cultures and biopsy were obtained. Isolation was discontinued and was discharged with outpatient follow up. Bronchoscopy culture grew Group B Streptococcus; patient was treated with Augmentin outpatient without improvement. In the following week, bronchoscopy lavage PCR was positive for M. avium; 2/3 AFB samples were positive for the same organism. Unfortunately, MAC susceptibility was not performed at the Department of Health of Puerto Rico as it's not performed in non-tuberculous mycobacteria. Antimicrobial therapy with Azithromycin, Rifampin, and Ethambutol was started empirically given patient's persistence of symptoms. Nebulized Amikacin was not started as it was not available. Patient's symptoms started improving over the next months. AFB sputum cultures were repeated at 4 months of therapy where 1/3 cultures were positive for MAC. Follow up Chest CT at 6 months showed minimal improvement. AFB sputum cultures obtained after 8 months of therapy were negative. Patient has tolerated therapy without side effects. Patient is completing at least a year of antimicrobials after negative culture. DISCUSSION: MAC, known to cause severe disease in those with HIV and cystic fibrosis. It presents as a disseminated disease. NTM are generally found in the water and soil. Person-to-person transmission is rare; therefore, isolation is not required. In US, Mycobacterium avium and M kansasii are the most common causes of NTM. CONCLUSIONS: This case highlights the importance to look for further causes when a patient does not improve despite appropriate therapy. REFERENCE #1: Charles L Daley, Jonathan M Iaccarino, Christoph Lange, Emmanuelle Cambau, Richard J Wallace, Claire Andrejak, Erik C Böttger, Jan Brozek, David E Griffith, Lorenzo Guglielmetti, Gwen A Huitt, Shandra L Knight, Philip Leitman, Theodore K Marras, Kenneth N Olivier, Miguel Santin, Jason E Stout, Enrico Tortoli, Jakko van Ingen, Dirk Wagner, Kevin L Winthrop, Treatment of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Pulmonary Disease: An Official ATS/ERS/ ESCMID/IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline, Clinical Infectious Diseases, Volume 71, Issue 4, 15 August 2020, Pages 905–913, https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciaa1125 REFERENCE #2: Chitty, Stephen A., and Juzar Ali. "Mycobacterium avium complex pulmonary disease in immunocompetent patients." Southern Medical Journal, vol. 98, no. 6, June 2005, pp. 646+. Gale Academic OneFile, link.gale.com/apps/doc/A134075630/AONE? u=anon~560e54c&sid=googleScholar&xid=e329d54f. Accessed 14 Nov. 2023. REFERENCE #3: Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease in immunocompetent patients: radiography–CT correlation C Wittram and G L WeisbrodThe British Journal of Radiology 2002 75:892, 340-344. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Jesse Aleman No disclosure on file for Mailyn Figueroa-Borrero No relevant relationships by Dariel Irizarry No relevant relationships by Carmen Reyes-Jimenez
