N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (D) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Talaromycosis caused by Talaromyces marneffei is a rare fungal infection predominantly affecting immunocompromised individuals, particularly those with HIV/AIDS. Although native to Southeast Asia's tropical regions, nonendemic cases have increased in individuals with autoimmune diseases undergoing immunosuppressive therapy. In non-endemic areas, HIV-negative patients often present atypically, leading to diagnostic delays, misdiagnoses, and, ultimately, poor outcomes [2,3]. We present a case of nonendemic pulmonary talaromycosis in an HIV-negative patient with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) on immunosuppressant therapy CASE PRESENTATION: An 82-year-old Hispanic woman with a history of severe RA, limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis, RA-related interstitial lung disease (RA-ILD), and lifelong nonsmoker presented to the Pulmonology office with a 6-month history of worsening exertional dyspnea, nonproductive cough, and unintentional weight. Her medical history included ongoing rheumatologic management with low-dose prednisone, leflunomide, hydroxychloroquine, and etanercept, complemented by annual CT chest scans for RA-ILD monitoring. 2 weeks before her presentation, a routine high-resolution CT scan revealed multiple new small solid and cystic nodules and tree-in-bud nodularity in the left upper lobe. These findings were deemed more consistent with a fungal infection than rheumatoid nodules, as the patient's RA was controlled.A bronchoscopy was recommended but postponed due to the patient's travel plans to Puerto Rico. 3 months later, she returned with worsening symptoms. A follow-up CT scan reported multiple new small to medium-sized, predominantly cavitating lung nodules in both the upper and lower lobes. Tests for HIV, tuberculosis, Aspergillus, Histoplasma, and blood cultures were negative. Subsequent bronchoscopy and cultures identified Talaromyces species, confirming a diagnosis of talaromycosis. The patient was admitted to the hospital and started induction therapy with liposomal amphotericin B for 2 weeks, followed by 10-week consolidation therapy with oral itraconazole, during which her RA treatment was discontinued to mitigate the risk of disseminated fungal infection. Her clinical course was complicated by severe RA flares that required multiple intra-articular steroid injections. Ultimately, her treatment stabilized on hydroxychloroquine and low-dose methotrexate, effectively managing her arthritis symptoms while minimizing the risk of aggravating the fungal infection. After completing her antifungal regimen, her respiratory symptoms improved markedly. The management plan includes regular follow-ups with pulmonology, rheumatology, and infectious disease specialists, maintenance therapy with oral itraconazole, and periodic CT scans to monitor her pulmonary condition and to adjust her treatment as needed. DISCUSSION: This case of pulmonary talaromycosis in an HIV-negative, immunosuppressed RA patient underscores the complexity of diagnosing and managing opportunistic infections in immunosuppressed individuals, particularly in non-endemic regions. Pulmonary nodules in such patients might be misattributed to RA. Therefore, maintaining a broad differential diagnosis and considering opportunistic infections, even without typical epidemiological risk factors, is paramount. Additionally, managing RA during a severe infection like talaromycosis poses a considerable therapeutic challenge, requiring a careful balance between reducing infection exacerbation and managing autoimmune activity, as evidenced by the worsening of RA symptoms after adjusting therapy. CONCLUSIONS: Talaromycosis is a serious infection with an increasing incidence in non-endemic areas and among HIV-negative populations. High clinical vigilance is crucial, particularly in immunocompromised individuals with rheumatologic conditions, where management can become particularly complex. REFERENCE #1: Cao, C., Xi, L. & Chaturvedi, V. Talaromycosis (Penicilliosis) Due to Talaromyces (Penicillium) marneffei: Insights into the Clinical Trends of a Major Fungal Disease 60 Years After the Discovery of the Pathogen. Mycopathologia 184, 709–720 (2019). https://doi.org/10.1007/s11046-019-00410-2 REFERENCE #2: Cunwei Cao, S2.2c Talaromycosis in HIV-negative patients: challenges and counter-measures, Medical Mycology, Volume 60, Issue Supplement_1, September 2022, myac072S22c, https://doi.org/10.1093/mmy/myac072.S2.2c REFERENCE #3: Le, T., Kinh, N. V., Cuc, N. T. K., Tung, N. L. N., Lam, N. T., Thuy, P. T. T., Cuong, D. D., Phuc, P. T. H., Vinh, V. H., Hanh, D. T. H., Tam, V. V., Thanh, N. T., Thuy, T. P., Hang, N. T., Long, H. B., Nhan, H. T., Wertheim, H. F. L., Merson, L., Shikuma, C., … Wolbers, M. (2017). A Trial of Itraconazole or Amphotericin B for HIV-Associated Talaromycosis. In New England Journal of Medicine (Vol. 376, Issue 24, pp. 2329–2340). Massachusetts Medical Society. https://doi.org/10.1056/nejmoa1613306 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Shannay Bellamy No relevant relationships by Khurram Malik No relevant relationships by Valentin Marian No relevant relationships by Andreina Martinez Paulino No relevant relationships by FNU Roshan No relevant relationships by Reza Samad
