N2  - SESSION TITLE: TB or NOT To Be SESSION TYPE: Original Investigations PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 09:15 am - 10:15 am PURPOSE: Pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) is diagnosed by isolation of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) from respiratory samples. Culture conversion, defined by two negative consecutive sputum cultures, is an important microbiologic benchmark that informs total treatment duration, and persistent culture positivity has traditionally signified a higher burden of disease and greater risk of relapse. Nontuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) species are found in soil and water and can cause significant pulmonary disease in certain patients. In Ontario, the prevalence of NTM is very high. Co-isolation of NTM in patients with pulmonary TB can result in persistent sputum positivity, and the clinical significance of co-isolation of NTM is not well understood. We sought to characterise these patients and determine if co-isolation of NTM during treatment of pulmonary TB results in a longer time to sputum conversion or total treatment duration, persistent symptoms at the end of TB treatment, or a difference in treatment outcomes. METHODS: We conducted a retrospective cohort study of all patients with pulmonary TB treated at our institution between January 2010 and December 2020. Patients were excluded if they had extrapulmonary TB with no pulmonary involvement, or if they had multidrug-resistant/rifampin-resistant TB. Patient demographics, clinical data, and outcomes were abstracted from electronic charts. Patients who co-isolated any NTM species during treatment for pulmonary TB were compared to those who cultured MTB alone. RESULTS: 771 patients were included in the study. 284 patients (37%) co-isolated TB and NTM. 41% were female, and 85% were fully sensitive to first line TB medications. Patients who co-isolated TB and NTM were significantly older (median age 58, IQR 37-78) compared to those with TB alone (median age 49, IQR 30-71, p=0.003), and were more likely to have concurrent diabetes mellitus (28.5% vs. 18.7%, p=0.002). Patients with TB-NTM co-isolation had significantly more sputa collected during treatment (median 10, IQR 7-14, vs. 7, IQR 4-11, p<0.001), were less likely to achieve sputum culture conversion (70.7% vs. 79.6%, p=0.01), and had a longer time to culture conversion (median 56 days, IQR 32-82 vs. 46 days, IQR 24-70, p=0.001). There was no significant difference in total treatment duration (median 9 months in both groups, p=0.8), and slightly improved treatment outcomes in patients who co-isolated TB-NTM compared to those with TB alone (successful treatment in 76% vs. 68%, p=0.002). Patients who co-isolated TB-NTM were more likely to have persistent cough (23.9% vs. 13.9%, p=0.003) and sputum (7.8% vs. 3.6%, p=0.03) at the end of treatment for pulmonary TB. CONCLUSIONS: NTM co-isolation is common in patients with pulmonary TB, and more likely to occur in patients who are older and have concurrent diabetes mellitus. Patients with TB-NTM co-isolation are less likely to culture convert, have a longer time to sputum conversion, and are more likely to have persistent pulmonary symptoms at the end of TB treatment. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: NTM co-isolation during treatment of pulmonary TB makes it more difficult to follow microbiologic parameters of treatment response. Further research is needed to better understand this group, as they are more likely to have persistent symptoms at treatment completion. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mantaj Brar No relevant relationships by Sarah Brode No relevant relationships by Henry Mah No relevant relationships by Natasha Sabur
