N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (AE) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Blastomycosis is caused by inhalation of the thermally dimorphic fungus Blastomyces dermatitidis or Blastomyces gilchristill. The clinical presentation of blastomycosis varies from asymptomatic, pneumonia, and extrapulmonary disease, with up to 50% of the cases being asymptomatic. While blastomycosis often causes lung infections, it can disseminate to other organs such as skin and bone, in immunocompromised patients. Patients may present with nonspecific symptoms and early treatment is critical. We present a case of disseminated blastomycosis in a 23 year old immunocompetent male. CASE PRESENTATION: A 23 year old male, who emigrated from Togo 7 years ago, presented to ED with a two week history of left sided chest pain, fatigue, dry cough, post-tussive vomiting, and loss of appetite. Past medical history for untreated latent TB diagnosed five years back. He denied any recent travel, trauma, or sick contacts. On presentation, blood pressure was 123/70 mmHg, pulse was 115/min, afebrile, and maintaining saturation on room air. Physical examination revealed coarse breath sounds with decreased air movement on the left side. Lab work was significant for leukocytosis (WBC 18.57), and elevated D-dimer (4.39) raising concerns for pulmonary embolism. CT angiography chest ruled out pulmonary embolism, but revealed a large area of consolidation in the left lower lobe with air bronchograms and lucencies suggestive of cavitation. There were additional areas of tree-in-bud distribution nodularity in the left upper and lower lobes. The patient was initiated on IV antibiotics and bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was performed by the pulmonologist revealing copious, purulent exudate and moderate mucosal thickening in the lateral basal segment of the left lower lobe. Results of testing for Legionella, pneumococcal antigen, HIV, Epstein Barr Virus, procalcitonin, Streptococcus, MRSA, Adenovirus, Coronavirus, Influenza, Rhinovirus, Enterovirus, Respiratory Syncytial Virus, Bordetella Pertussis, Chlamydia Pneumoniae, and Mycoplasma Pneumoniae were all negative. Blastomyces serology and antigen came back high positive and the patient was initiated on itraconazole. BAL fungal cultures and Karius digital also resulted with Blastomyces dermatitidis consistent with dissemination. Patient was discharged on itraconazole with improvement noted on one month follow-up visit at Infectious Disease clinic. Latent TB will require treatment after completion of blastomycosis treatment. DISCUSSION: The diverse manifestations of disseminated blastomycosis can complicate its timely diagnosis and treatment. Both acute and chronic blastomycosis mimics other diseases making the diagnosis challenging. Acute pulmonary blastomycosis can present as bacterial or viral pneumonia and chronic is often similar to malignancy and tuberculosis. In our case, the history of latent TB was baffling the diagnosis. Immunosuppression has not been associated with increased risk of dissemination hence it can be seen in immunocompetent patients. Thus, it is crucial to perform a comprehensive evaluation and rule out other etiologies that present similarly to blastomycosis. CONCLUSIONS: Disseminated blastomycosis, even in immunocompetent individuals, can present a significant diagnostic and therapeutic challenge due to the nonspecific symptoms and potential for severe outcomes if left untreated. REFERENCE #1: Saccente M, Woods GL. Clinical and Laboratory Update on Blastomycosis. Clinical Microbiology Reviews. 2010;23(2):367-381. doi:https://doi.org/10.1128/cmr.00056-09 REFERENCE #2: Klein BS, Vergeront JM, Disalvo AF, Kaufman L, Davis JP. Two Outbreaks of Blastomycosis Along Rivers in Wisconsin: Isolation of Blastomyces dermatitidis from Riverbank Soil and Evidence of its Transmission Along Waterways. The American review of respiratory disease. 1987;136(6):1333-1338. doi:https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm/136.6.1333 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ashley Ceniceros No relevant relationships by Daniel Cheah No relevant relationships by Mobeen Haider No relevant relationships by Yousra Khalid No relevant relationships by Amir Khan No relevant relationships by Rachael Wong
