N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (T) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: TNF-Alpha inhibitor are known to be associated with tuberculosis (TB), and this is the diagnosis that comes to mind in a patients on this medication presenting with night sweats. CT scan findings of TB can vary but miliary TB presents with a diffuse micronodular pattern. We describe a case where a diagnosis of miliary TB was presumed but ultimately ruled out with transbronchial biopsies and cultures and confirmed to be pulmonary histoplasmosis instead. CASE PRESENTATION: A 32-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis on golimumab for 15 years presented with night sweats for two weeks refractory to two courses of oral antibiotics. He had recent travel to Colombia where he hiked and attended large gatherings. His chest CT was significant for a right lower lobe (RLL) consolidation, right hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly (Fig 1). He underwent bronchoalveolar lavage, transbronchial biopsy of the RLL, and endobronchial ultrasound guided sampling of enlarged lymph nodes with unremarkable results. Acid fast sputum (AFB) cultures were sent and 1 of 3 were positive for Mycobacterium abscessus complex, though this was thought to be colonizer. He continued antibiotics but had a recurrence of symptoms with repeat CT chest showing diffuse micronodular pattern (Fig 2) concerning for military TB. He was admitted for repeat transbronchial biopsy. Transbronchial biopsy and AFB cultures were unremarkable. Urine histoplasmosis antigen and antibody were positive, so he was treated with itraconazole with significant improvement in symptoms. DISCUSSION: TNF-alpha inhibitors play a large role in the treatment of ankylosing spondylitis. With the introduction of this class of medication, there is a known increased risk of opportunistic infections especially active and latent TB [1]. CT findings of random micronodules in a diffuse distribution are most characteristic of miliary tuberculosis but can also be a result of less commonly seen in alternate diagnoses like fungal infections [2]. Histoplasmosis is the most common opportunistic fungal infection with patients on TNF-alpha inhibitors when located in an area where it is considered endemic like South America where our patient visited prior to his symptom onset [3], Subacute and acute presentations of histoplasmosis include fever, headaches, chills, dry cough, and malaise [4]. The most common imaging findings for subacute histoplasmosis are focal opacities associated with hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy [4]. Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis is when clinical symptoms do not improve within 3 weeks and there is progression to extrapulmonary tissues such as hepatosplenomegaly, extrapulmonary lymphadenopathy, and skin lesions [4]. Symptoms include fevers, fatigue, and weight loss with very limited pulmonary symptoms present despite chest imaging findings of diffuse miliary pattern [4]. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary histoplasmosis is a diagnosis that should be considered in patients on TNF-alpha inhibitors, particularly those who have visited an endemic region. Imaging findings of pulmonary histoplasmosis can be falsely mistaken for miliary TB. REFERENCE #1: Gardam MA, Keystone EC, Menzies R, et al. Anti-tumour necrosis factor agents and tuberculosis risk: mechanisms of action and clinical management. Lancet Infect Dis. 2003;3(3):148-155. doi:10.1016/s1473-3099(03)00545-0 REFERENCE #2: Gruden JF, Naidich DP, Machnicki SC, Cohen SL, Girvin F, Raoof S. An Algorithmic Approach to the Interpretation of Diffuse Lung Disease on Chest CT Imaging: A Theory of Almost Everything. Chest. 2020;157(3):612-635. doi:10.1016/j.chest.2019.10.017 REFERENCE #3: Cipolat MM, Rodrigues DRR, Brenol CV, Pasqualotto AC, Falci DR. Incidence of histoplasmosis in patients receiving TNF-alpha inhibitors: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Medicine (Baltimore). 2023;102(49):e36450. doi:10.1097/MD.0000000000036450 Barros N, Wheat JL, Hage C. Pulmonary Histoplasmosis: A Clinical Update. J Fungi (Basel). 2023;9(2):236. Published 2023 Feb 10. doi:10.3390/jof9020236 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Zein Kattih No relevant relationships by Sonica Patel No relevant relationships by Ashwin Varkey
