N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (A) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Legionella is a gram-negative, intracellular pathogen that causes community-acquired and hospital-acquired pneumonia. We present a case of Legionella pneumonia complicated by rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury. This case was successfully treated with hydration and appropriate antibiotics. CASE PRESENTATION: A 29-year-old African American male presented to the Emergency Department with complaints of malaise, generalized weakness, and dark-colored urine. Review of system was notable for cough, nausea, non-bloody non-bilious vomiting, and chills. He reported marijuana use but denied smoking, cocaine and opiate drug use. He had been residing in a shelter and had a history of anxiety and depression, for which he was prescribed fluoxetine and olanzapine. On physical examination, the patient did not have a fever and had normal vital signs. He had Left lower lobe crackles and tenderness in the lower back was observed. Initial laboratory tests were significant for creatinine kinase of 481,200 U/l and AST and ALT of 2,956 and 661 U/l respectively, with normal alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin levels. Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine were 50 and 4.1 mg/dl respectively. Alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin levels were within normal range. Chest x-ray showed a homogenous opacity in the left lower and middle lung fields suggestive of pneumonia. The urine legionella test was initially positive for serotype 1 but became negative after antibiotic treatment. HIV, hepatitis B, and hepatitis C serologies were negative, as were viral swabs for influenza and SARS-CoV-2. The urine pneumococcal antigen test was also negative. The urine drug screen yielded negative results. The patient received aggressive hydration and levofloxacin at a dosage appropriate for patients with renal impairment. His urine output improved, and his laboratory values showed a downward trend. He was discharged home in stable condition. DISCUSSION: This case demonstrates the potential for atypical presentations of Legionnaires disease with myalgia, dark color urine and weakness. Rhabdomyolysis is a rare but increasingly recognized complication of Legionella pneumonia. The triad of Legionnaires' disease, rhabdomyolysis, and acute renal failure has been well-documented since 1980s (1). The presence of this triad increases the fatality rate of Legionnaires' disease by up to 40% highlighting the importance of early recognition (2). Prompt diagnosis and treatment with antibiotics, intravenous fluids, and dialysis, if necessary, can lead to favorable outcomes (2,3). Although the exact mechanism of rhabdomyolysis is not certain it may be related to direct invasion of the muscle by the bacteria and endotoxin induced muscle damage. CONCLUSIONS: This case report highlights a distinct presentation of legionnaires disease associated rhabdomyolysis and acute renal failure and the need to maintain a high degree of suspicion for legionella pneumonia even in patients without classic symptoms like fever, especially for high-risk individuals like those residing in shelters. Our patient was successfully treated with aggressive hydration and appropriate antibiotics and didn't require initiation of hemodialysis, emphasizing the need for early recognition and prompt initiation of treatment. REFERENCE #1: 1. Williams ME, Watanakunakorn C, Baird IM, Gerald SE. Legionnaires' disease with acute renal failure. Am J Med Sci. 1980;279(3):177-183. doi:10.1097/00000441-198005000-00008 REFERENCE #2: 2. Soni AJ, Peter A. Established association of Legionella with rhabdomyolysis and renal failure: A review of the literature. Respir Med Case Rep.2019; 28:100962. doi: 10.1016/j.rmcr.2019.100962 REFERENCE #3: 3. Shah A, Check F, Baskin S, Reyman T, Menard R. Legionnaires' disease and acute renal failure: case report and review. Clin Infect Dis Off Publ Infect Dis Soc Am. 1992;14(1):204-207. doi:10.1093/clinids/14.1.204 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Bisrat Hussien Adal No relevant relationships by Temesgen Edea No relevant relationships by Bronson Benno Joseph Raja
