N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (X) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Silicosis significantly elevates the risk of Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (MTB) infection, second only to HIV. Individuals with silicosis are 2.8 times more likely to develop pulmonary tuberculosis and up to 3.7 times more likely to contract extra-pulmonary tuberculosis than those without silicosis [1]. Silica exposure impairs dendritic cell activation and suppresses Toll-like receptor 2, increasing vulnerability to bacterial infections, notably Mycobacterium Tuberculosis [2]. CASE PRESENTATION: We report a case of a 43-year-old male, with a twenty-year history of silica exposure from working in a tile grinding factory in India. He sought care for symptoms including dry cough, shortness of breath, and a ten-pound weight loss. Examination revealed bilateral diffuse crackles and pulmonary function tests indicated mild restrictive impairment and a slight reduction in diffusing capacity. Chest CT scans showed diffuse bilateral micronodules and calcified mediastinal lymph nodes, raising concerns for miliary MTB. Despite a positive QuantiFERON TB gold test a year earlier, initial sputum cultures and bronchoalveolar lavage were negative for Acid Fast Bacillus (AFB). Nevertheless, a lymph node biopsy at that time showed necrotizing granulomas. He received treatment for latent TB based on these findings, with his condition attributed to pulmonary silicosis. Upon his current evaluation, due to persistent concerns of active TB, further testing revealed positive results for Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) in sputum samples. Given that the patient's symptoms and chest imaging were not consistent with MAC infection, additional investigations including repeat bronchoscopy, bronchoalveolar lavage, and biopsy were undertaken. We confirmed MTB infection via AFB positivity and PCR analysis. The patient was treated with the Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (RIPE) regimen and has shown considerable improvement. DISCUSSION: The overlap of silicosis with active tuberculosis poses substantial diagnostic complexities, often requiring extensive and repeated evaluations to identify underlying infections. This case highlights the complex relationship between occupational lung diseases and tuberculosis, underscoring the importance of comprehensive diagnostics in patients with latent TB and a history of silica exposure. CONCLUSIONS: Silicosis profoundly increases the risk of tuberculosis while complicating its diagnosis. Our case illustrates the necessity of vigilant and ongoing assessment for TB in patients with silicosis, even after initial negative testing. REFERENCE #1: Lanzafame M, Vento S. Mini-review: Silico-tuberculosis. J Clin Tuberc Other Mycobact Dis. 2021 Feb 2;23:100218. doi: 10.1016/j.jctube.2021.100218. PMID: 33598569; PMCID: PMC7868994. REFERENCE #2: Beamer G.L., Seaver B.P., Jessop F., Shepherd D.M., Beamer C.A. Acute exposure to crystalline silica reduces macrophage activation in response to bacterial lipoproteins. Front Immunol. 2016;7:49. doi: 10.3389/fimmu.2016.00049 REFERENCE #3: Suresh C. Singh, Pankaj Yadav, Awadesh Kumar, Arun Kumar, Harendra S. Bhoj, Amit Gupta, Amit Verma, Harsh K. Singh, Sonal Mishra, Rajeshwar P. Sinha, SILICOSIS: A CURSE THAT NEEDS IDENTIFICATION, INDIAN JOURNAL OF APPLIED RESEARCH, 10.36106/ijar/0714387, (61-65), (2023) DISCLOSURES: Content reviewer relationship with Ubie Pte Please note: June 2022 - Dec 2022 by Saqib Baig, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Regeneron Please note: Nov 2022 by Saqib Baig, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Verona Please note: March 2023 by Saqib Baig, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Rhythmic health Please note: 2023 Added 11/29/2023 by Saqib Baig, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Saikou Saidy
