N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (P) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Nocardiosis is an uncommon opportunistic infection with over two thirds occurring in those who are immunocompromised [1] or who have undergone solid organ transplants [2]. Here, we present a unique case of pulmonary Nocardiosis complicated by superimposed Pseudomonas infection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 57-year-old M with CAD s/p CABG, ESRD and T1DM post kidney-pancreas transplant on oral tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil, and prednisone presented with somnolence and intermittent coughing. Per family, the patient was non-compliant with home prophylactic medications. His vital signs were normal while physical exams were only notable for significant somnolence. Laboratory workups revealed diabetic ketoacidosis in the setting of pancreatic transplant failure as well as acute kidney injury. Chest x-ray and CT chest were notable for a large cavitary mass-like opacity/consolidation in the left upper lobe concerning for possible malignancy versus infection. CT head was notable for right parietal encephalomalacia. As there was substantial concern for opportunistic infection, he was started on empiric coverage using vancomycin, cefepime, amphotericin B, valganciclovir, and one-dose IV pentamidine. Further infectious workups were initially unrevealing with negative blood culture, sputum AFB and fungal culture, and lumbar puncture. As the patient recovered from his initial diabetic ketoacidosis and acute kidney injury, he was transferred to his original transplant center. His bronchoscopic wash sputum culture grew modified acid-fast positive branching rods; ultimately confirmed to be Nocardia abscessus plus Pseudomonas aeruginosa. His broad-spectrum coverage was de-escalated to IV sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim and meropenem. To re-evaluate for disseminated Nocardia infection given encephalomalacia, the patient underwent a repeat lumbar puncture and MRI brain that were negative. At this time, the patient was diagnosed with isolated pulmonary Nocardia abscessus with Pseudomonas superinfection. He was initiated on IV sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim and meropenem while inpatient and continued upon discharge. DISCUSSION: The combination of pulmonary Nocardia and Pseudomonas aeruginosa remains rare, lacking a published series. As in this case, both are more prevalent in patients who have undergone solid organ transplants and have medication induced T-cell immunity impairment [4]. Nocardiosis requires up to 12 months treatment in immunocompromised hosts due to its relapsing nature [3]. Even in those who have received appropriate therapy, the one-year mortality rate after Nocardiosis can reach up to 22 percent [6] and represents up to ten times increase in those with solid organ transplant [5]. When presenting with mild-to-moderate isolated pulmonary Nocardiosis, sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim has been considered the standard empiric therapy while combination therapy with at least two antimicrobials that is commonly consist of sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim, imipenem, and amikacin has been utilized [6]. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary Nocardia is a rare entity with the potential to resemble pulmonary malignancy. Suspicion should rise in an immunocompromised male patient presenting with cough and abnormal chest imaging with air-space disease as well as central necrosis. The presence of immunosuppression should raise concern for multiple infections, as in this case with both Nocardia and Pseudomonas. The presence of ring-enhancing brain abscesses or meningitis should prompt concern for CNS Nocardiosis, as it is the most common site of disseminated infection and carries elevated mortality. REFERENCE #1: 1. Beaman BL, Beaman L. Nocardia species: host-parasite relationships. Clin Microbiol Rev. 1994 Apr;7(2):213-264. doi: 10.1128/CMR.7.2.213. PMID: 8055469; PMCID: PMC358319.2. Lafont E, Conan PL, Rodriguez-Nava V, et al. Invasive Nocardiosis: Disease Presentation, Diagnosis and Treatment - Old Questions, New Answers? Infect Drug Resist. 2020 Dec 22;13:4601-4613. doi: 10.2147/IDR.S249761. PMID: 33376366; PMCID: PMC7764858. REFERENCE #2: 3. Restrepo A, Clark NM. Infectious Diseases Community of Practice of the American Society of Transplantation. Nocardia infections in solid organ transplantation: Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Community of Practice of the American Society of Transplantation. Clin Transplant. 2019 Sep;33(9):e13509. doi: 10.1111/ctr.13509. Epub 2019 Mar 19. PMID: 30817024.4. Wilmes D, Coche E, Rodriguez-Villalobos H, et al. Bacterial pneumonia in kidney transplant recipients. Respir Med. 2018 Apr:137:89-94. doi: 10.1016/j.rmed.2018.02.022. Epub 2018 Mar 6. REFERENCE #3: 5. Lebeaux D, Freund R, van Delden C, et al. European Study Group for Nocardia in Solid Organ Transplantation; European Study Group for Nocardia in Solid Organ Transplantation. Outcome and Treatment of Nocardiosis After Solid Organ Transplantation: New Insights from a European Study. Clin Infect Dis. 2017 May 15;64(10):1396-1405. doi: 10.1093/cid/cix124. Erratum in: Clin Infect Dis. 2017 Oct 15;65(8):1431-1433. PMID: 28329348; PMCID: PMC10941331.6. Paige EK, Spelman D. Nocardiosis: 7-year experience at an Australian tertiary hospital. Intern Med J. 2019 Mar;49(3):373-379. doi: 10.1111/imj.14068. PMID: 30091232. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kevin Liu Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with Edwards Lifesciences Please note: 2017 - Present by Herbert Patrick, value=Honoraria Consultant relationship with Edwards Lifesciences Please note: 2017 - Present by Herbert Patrick, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Samuel Shulimson
