N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (R) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Legionella pneumonia, caused by the gram-negative bacterium Legionella pneumophila, is a severe and potentially life-threatening respiratory infection. While Legionella pneumonia typically presents with fever, cough, and dyspnea, it can lead to severe complications, including Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS). Although Legionella infection is commonly associated with exposure to contaminated water sources such as hot tubs, cooling towers, and air conditioning systems, there are instances where patients develop Legionella pneumonia without a clear history of exposure, underscoring the importance of considering Legionella as a potential pathogen in severe cases of pneumonia. This case report describes a patient with Legionella pneumonia who developed cardiac arrest and ARDS, highlighting the critical nature of the disease and the challenges in its management. CASE PRESENTATION: 50-year-old male with a history of stroke was brought via EMS to the ED with 1 week history of fever, cough, and progressive dyspnea that now had progressed to altered mentation. He did not elicit any exposure to hot tubs, cooling towers, and air conditioning systems. Upon admission, he was oriented to self and location however exhibited confused behavior. Auscultation revealed diffuse crackles bilaterally. Laboratory tests indicated leukocytosis, hyponatremia 128, elevated creatinine 2.8, and elevated inflammatory markers. Urinary streptococcal and legionella antigen sent. Chest x-ray showed right lower lobe consolidation. He was treated with ceftriaxone and azithromycin for community-acquired pneumonia. A few hours after admission, he experienced a sudden cardiac arrest with pulseless electrical activity. Immediate cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) was initiated, and after multiple rounds of CPR, administration of vasopressors, and endotracheal intubation spontaneous circulation was achieved. He was moved to the ICU, however his condition continued to worsen, and he developed refractory hypoxemia despite maximal ventilatory support. Repeat chest imaging demonstrated worsening bilateral infiltrates consistent with ARDS. A diagnosis of Legionella pneumonia was confirmed based on positive Legionella urinary antigen (Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1) testing. Echocardiogram showed diffuse mild hypokinesis with reduced systolic ejection fraction 40%. Despite aggressive supportive measures, including antibiotic therapy and hemodynamic support, the patient's condition continued to deteriorate, and he eventually succumbed to multiorgan failure. DISCUSSION: Legionella pneumonia is a severe form of pneumonia associated with significant morbidity and mortality, particularly in immunocompromised individuals and those with underlying comorbidities. While respiratory failure is a common complication, cardiac involvement leading to cardiac arrest is less frequently reported but can occur, as demonstrated in this case. Legionella can progress to ARDS, multiorgan failure, and death. Additionally, the development of ARDS further underscores the severity of Legionella pneumonia. Greater than 70% survival rates are reported for patients with Legionella on Veno-Venous Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (VV-ECMO). CONCLUSIONS: Prompt recognition, early initiation of appropriate antibiotics, and aggressive supportive care, including mechanical ventilation and hemodynamic support, are crucial in managing such critically ill patients. This case highlights the importance of considering Legionella pneumonia in patients presenting with severe pneumonia, even in the absence of classic risk factors, and underscores the potential for life-threatening complications such as cardiac arrest and ARDS in the course of the illness. REFERENCE #1: 1 Bell H, Chintalapati S, Patel P, Halim A, Kithas A, Schmalzle SA. Legionella longbeachae pneumonia: Case report and review of reported cases in non-endemic countries. IDCases. 2021 Jan 14;23:e01050. doi: 10.1016/j.idcr.2021.e01050. PMID: 33511033; PMCID: PMC7817369. REFERENCE #2: 2 Grotberg JC, Schulte L, Schumer E, Sullivan M, Kotkar K, Masood MF, Pawale A. Venovenous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation after cardiac arrest for acute respiratory distress syndrome caused by Legionella: a case report. J Cardiothorac Surg. 2024 Jan 28;19(1):27. doi: 10.1186/s13019-024-02492-6. PMID: 38281956; PMCID: PMC10822179. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Akash Dodia No relevant relationships by Asma Mohammadi
