N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (AB) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Nontuberculous myobacteria (NTM) refer to member of the myobacterium group that are not part of the Myobacterium Tuberculosis complex and also exclude myobacterium leprae. Myobacterium avium complex is classified under NTM and is a free living bacterium found in water, soil and dust and route of transmission is through inhalation or ingestion. NTM infection of the lung will occur in individuals with preexisting lung disease especially patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 64 year old male with a history of COPD, asbestos exposure and myobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection 6 years prior, presented to the hospital after a fall. The patient had a prior emergency room (ER) visit 3 weeks ago for atrial fibrillation (AF) with rapid ventricular response requiring cardioversion. The patient reported a productive cough for the past 6 months and generalized weakness, weight loss and dyspnea. Patient was treated with oral antibiotics for concern for pneumonia at his prior ER visit. Computed tomography showed worsening of the bilateral multifocal cavitating lesions with extensive traction bronchiectasis, thick walled cavitary lesions with air-fluid level occupying the right upper chest and smaller cavitating thick-walled lesions throughout the right middle and lower lobes. Solid nodules in the perilymphatic distribution throughout the lingula and left lower lobe were also found.Patient was followed by infectious disease and completed treatment for MAC with azithromycin, rifampin and ethambutol and had been doing well till 6 months ago. Given his symptoms and worsening imaging findings, there was concern for recurrence of MAC. Sputum cultures were obtained which were positive and MAC treatment was initiated. His hospital course was complicated with difficult to treat AF which required cardioversion. DISCUSSION: To determine the precise frequency of the disease is challenging given NTM reporting is not mandatory which makes documenting the cases of recurrence even harder. The incidence of lung disease caused by NTM has been increasing worldwide. Per a study in South Korea that investigated the recurrence after initial treatment of lung disease caused by MAC, the treatment success was found to be unsatisfactory with failure rate being about 20-40%. Recurrence rate was higher in patients with nodular bronchiectasis than those with fibrocavitary disease while our patient was found to have both radiographic findings. It was unclear if the recurrence of NTM disease had any association with AF. NTM infections cause chronic pulmonary inflammation by inducing changes in the inflammatory cells and cytokines which can be associated with increased risk of cardiovascular events. Another potential explanation is that NTM infections have similar risk factors such as old age, smoking and COPD that are also associated with AF. There have been reports of NTM associated with myocardial infarction and heart failure with higher association with AF especially with progressive NTM vs indolent NTM. CONCLUSIONS: This is a case of isolated recurrent MAC lung disease presenting as resistant AF despite prior completion of treatment, likely due to his multiple comorbidities including COPD and asbestosis exposure in addition to high recurrence rates of MAC. REFERENCE #1: Cowman S, van Ingen J, Griffith DE, Loebinger MR. Non-tuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease. European Respiratory Journal. 2019;54(1):1900250. doi:https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.00250-2019. Abaleka FI, Nigussie B, Onal O, Al-Zakhari R, Yimer E. A Case of Isolated Pulmonary Mycobacterium Avium Complex Being the First Presentation of a Newly Diagnosed HIV/AIDS. Cureus. Published online July 16, 2020. doi:https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.9223 REFERENCE #2: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, et al. An Official ATS/IDSA Statement: Diagnosis, Treatment, and Prevention of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Diseases. American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine. 2007;175(4):367-416. doi:https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.200604-571st. Lee BY, Kim S, Hong Y, et al. Risk Factors for Recurrence after Successful Treatment of Mycobacterium avium Complex Lung Disease. Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy. REFERENCE #3: Park CS, Choi EK, Kim B, et al. Association between Atrial Fibrillation, Myocardial Infarction, Heart Failure and Mortality in Patients with Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infection: a nationwide population-based study. Scientific Reports. 2019;9(1). doi:https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-51801-w DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Natasha Maqsood No relevant relationships by Saad Tariq
