N2  - SESSION TITLE: Management of Commonly Encountered Pneumonic Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Original Inv PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am PURPOSE: Amikacin liposome inhalation suspension (ALIS, Arikayce®) with multidrug regimen (MDR) is the only therapy approved for treatment of Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease (MACLD) in patients who do not achieve culture conversion (CC) after ≥6 consecutive months of MDR. In the phase 3 clinical trial, ALIS + MDR resulted in higher rates of CC than MDR alone. Higher rates of adverse events including dysphonia, cough, and dyspnea with ALIS resulted in some patients discontinuing treatment prematurely. Prescription refills from claims databases provide information on medication use that reflects a combination of real-world prescribing patterns and patient adherence to therapy. We describe real-world use of ALIS, and association with hospitalizations and emergency room (ER) visits in a US patient population using claims. METHODS: A retrospective study was conducted using the Merative® MarketScan® claims database. Patients who initiated ALIS between October 2018 and March 2021, with ≥3 months continuous eligibility before, and ≥12 months after index date (i.e., date of first ALIS prescription) were included. ALIS use was defined as total months supply of ALIS dispensed over a 12-month post-index period, dichotomized into longer use (i.e., ≥6 months supply) and shorter use (i.e., <6 months supply) groups. Patients were followed-up for hospitalizations (all-cause, respiratory-related) and ER visits from index date through March 2022. Kaplan-Meier curves with log rank tests were used descriptively to assess the association between ALIS use group and hospitalizations and ER visits. RESULTS: 103 ALIS-initiated patients were included, with 54 (52.4%) and 49 (47.6%) patients comprising longer and shorter use groups, respectively. The mean (SD) follow-up period was 25.4 (9.3) and 25.3 (7.7) months in the longer and shorter use groups, respectively. Patients in the longer use group received a mean (SD) of 10.4 (2.6) months supply of ALIS over a 12-month period compared to 2.9 (1.7) months in the shorter use group. The longer use group had a higher proportion of patients with baseline (i.e. 3-month period before index date) dyspnea, cough and pneumonia; the shorter use group had a higher proportion of baseline asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cardiovascular disease and diabetes. Kaplan-Meier curves showed that the longer use group was less likely to experience hospitalizations (all-cause, respiratory) and ER visits at follow-up compared to the shorter use group (all with p <0.05). The cumulative probability of an all-cause hospitalization at month 6, 12, and 24 from index date in the longer use group was 15%, 22%, and 31% versus 22%, 31%, and 57% in the shorter use group. CONCLUSIONS: Patients with longer use of ALIS use during the first 12 months following initiation were less likely to experience hospitalization or ER visits at follow-up than patients with shorter use. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: The health care resource utilization outcomes observed with longer ALIS use in this study is worth considering when tailoring treatment plans for patients with refractory MACLD. However, more work is needed to understand severity of disease and other confounding factors that may impact ALIS use and outcomes. DISCLOSURES: Former Employee relationship with Merck Please note: ~1995 - present Added 03/21/2024 by Leona Markson, source=Web Response, value=Stock from when employed Former Employee relationship with Merck Please note: Oct 2019-present Added 03/21/2024 by Leona Markson, source=Web Response, value=Retiree benefits Former Employee relationship with Pfizer Please note: June 2023 - present Added 03/21/2024 by Leona Markson, source=Web Response, value=Stock & retiree benefits Employee relationship with Pfizer Please note: July 2019-June 2023 Added 03/21/2024 by Leona Ma