N2  - SESSION TITLE: Mycobacterial Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Tuberculosis (TB) remains a persistent challenge due to the complexity of diagnosing active TB infections. Patients typically present with fever, cough, malaise, and weight loss. Chest imaging findings can vary depending on the stage of the disease. Primary TB can present with consolidation, cavitary lesions, and hilar lymphadenopathies whereas reactivation TB can manifest in branching linear and nodular opacities (tree-in-bud), patchy or lobular consolidation, pleural thickening and calcification, and traction bronchiectasis [A]. Artificial intelligence (AI) tools are being used to improve the diagnosis of TB [B]. We present a case of TB discovered in an incidental lung nodule (ILN) follow-up program assisted by natural language processing. CASE PRESENTATION: A 66-year-old male non-smoker with a history of atrial fibrillation on apixaban after ablation presented to the lung nodule clinic for an ILN noted on a computed tomography (CT) coronary angiogram captured by the ILN program. The CT demonstrated a semi-solid 9mm left upper lobe nodule (LUL) with a 3mm solid component for which a 3-month follow-up was indicated. Repeat CT chest showed a right upper lobe (RUL) 5mm solid nodule, tree-in-bud and nodular opacities, bronchiectasis (image 1) none of which had been seen in the original CT, and the 7mm part-solid LUL nodule (image 2). On presentation, the patient was asymptomatic, with no history of mycobacterial infection, recent pneumonia, incarceration, and exposure to mold or asbestos. Laboratory studies were negative for QuantiFERON-Gold TB Test (QF-G), negative for human immunodeficiency virus and showed normal interleukin-2 and C-reactive protein levels. Bronchoscopy with bronchioalveolar lavage (BAL) and fluoroscopy-guided biopsy of the RUL lesion showed non-caseating granulomas on histopathology, normal CD4:CD8 ratio, with positive acid-fast bacilli (AFB) testing and positive culture for pan-susceptible Mycobacterium tuberculosis, prompting initiation of pyrazinamide, ethambutol, rifampin, and isoniazid. DISCUSSION: This case presents the diagnosis of an active TB infection using natural language processing (NLP) in the setting of an ILN program. Of note, this patient was asymptomatic and had no risk factors for TB. Despite not having cough at the time of presentation, the positive AFB stain and culture in the BAL suggest that he was contagious. Moreover, the QF-G test (sensitivity: 94.1%, specificity: 97.3%, negative likelihood ratio: 0.029 [C]) was negative. Despite the atypical presentation and microbiologic workup, the use of the NLP-assisted program avoided a delayed diagnosis, hence reducing the spread of this disease among the community. There is growing evidence in using AI for chest radiography and tomography in TB detection [D, E], many of which are still in the developmental stage. This case illustrates one of the many utilizations that the ILN program has, exemplifying the importance of pulmonary nodule follow-up, not only for early detection of malignancy, but also for other pulmonary conditions such as infections including TB or non-tuberculosis mycobacterium. CONCLUSIONS: AI-assisted programs are useful tools that can assist healthcare providers in the follow-up of incidental radiological findings, promoting individual patient benefit through timely diagnosis and treatment of multiple conditions, including malignancy and infections. REFERENCE #1: [A] Jeong YJ, Lee KS. Pulmonary tuberculosis: up-to-date imaging and management. AJR Am J Roentgenol. 2008 Sep;191(3):834-44. doi: 10.2214/AJR.07.3896. PMID: 18716117. REFERENCE #2: [B] Kulkarni S, Jha S. Artificial Intelligence, Radiology, and Tuberculosis: A Review. Acad Radiol. 2020 Jan;27(1):71-75. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2019.10.003. Epub 2019 Nov 20. PMID: 31759796. [C] Yancey JR, Melchert VE. QuantiFERON-TB Gold+ for the Diagnosis of Mycobacterium tuberculosis Infection. Am Fam Physician. 2021 Feb 1;103(3):177-178. PMID: 33507050. REFERENCE #3: [D] Okada K, Yamada N, Takayanagi K, Hiasa Y, Kitamura Y, Hoshino Y, Hirao S, Yoshiyama T, Onozaki I, Kato S. Applicability of artificial intelligence-based computer-aided detection (AI-CAD) for pulmonary tuberculosis to community-based active case finding. Trop Med Health. 2024 Jan 2;52(1):2. doi: 10.1186/s41182-023-00560-6. PMID: 38163868; PMCID: PMC10759734. [E] Harris M, Qi A, Jeagal L, Torabi N, Menzies D, Korobitsyn A, Pai M, Nathavitharana RR, Ahmad Khan F. A systematic review of the diagnostic accuracy of artificial intelligence-based computer programs to analyze chest x-rays for pulmonary tuberculosis. PLoS One. 2019 Sep 3;14(9):e0221339. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0221339. PMID: 31479448; PMCID: PMC6719854. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sascha Adler No relevant relationships by Nour Ayoub No relevant relationships by Shasha Chen No relevant relationships by Steven Lim No relevant relationships by Sara Luby No relevant relationships by Junyu Qi No relevant relationships by Agostina Velo No relevant relationships by Hong Yu Wang Advisory Committee Member relationship with Median Technologies Please note: January 2022-present Added 04/01/2024 by Javier Zulueta, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Consultant relationship with HeartLung Technologies Please note: 1/2022 - Present Added 04/01/2024 by Javier Zulueta, source=Web Response, value=Ownership interest Consultant relationship with OncoSwab Please note: 1/2024 - present Added 04/01/2024 by Javier Zulueta, source=Web Response, value=Ownership interest
