N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (C) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary cavitary lesions arise from underlying infectious, inflammatory, or malignant origins. Among infectious causes, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, fungal infections, and certain gram-positive organisms are the most frequently implicated. Pneumonia from Escherichia coli has been increasing in prevalence, thought to be from microaspiration of secretions, and typically occurs in patients with some kind of immunocompromised state; however it is not known to cause severe necrotizing pneumonia. In this report, we discuss a case of worsening cavitary E. coli pneumonia. CASE PRESENTATION: A 51-year-old female with severe malnutrition (BMI 12.4 kg/m2) in the setting of eating disorder, history of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) and gastroparesis, presented with one-week-history of dyspnea and cough. She was hypoxic to 90%, with signs of protein-calorie malnutrition. Labs were remarkable for WBC of 15 x 103/uL, and computed tomography (CT) of chest revealed a large left apical cavitary lesion measuring 6.8 x 7.5 x 4.8 cm, consistent with necrotizing pneumonia. Microbiology was positive only for sputum culture growing resistant Escherichia coli, with serum quantiferon, (1,3)-beta-D-glucan, and sputum acid-fast bacilli (AFB) all testing negative.She received intravenous ertapenem and discharged with plan for prolonged antibiotic therapy, however returned three days later with worsening dyspnea, with CT chest showing mildly increased size of the cavitary lesion. No changes were made to her antibiotics. One month later, repeat CT showed worsening of the cavity and a new left lower lobe infiltrate. Antibiotics were changed to meropenem and vancomycin, however repeat sputum culture showed no growth. She underwent bronchoscopy with unremarkable bronchial washings and negative cultures. Within the following three months, the left lower lobe consolidation gradually decreased with residual scar while the apical cavity remained unchanged, with reported improvement of symptoms. DISCUSSION: Necrotizing pneumonia is a severe complication of community-acquired pneumonia, which develops over a few days and can present with severe sepsis and escalate to pulmonary gangrene, with a high mortality rate [1]. In adults, necrotizing pneumonia is more commonly caused by community-acquired Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Klebsiella pneumoniae through suppurative necrosis of pyogenic lung abscesses [2]. Pulmonary gangrene is more common in gram-negative organisms, particularly Klebsiella and Pseudomonas.Our patient presented with necrotizing pneumonia that produced sputum cultures positive for E. coli. The incidence of E. coli necrotizing pneumonia is low and management is difficult due to the lack of guidelines [2]. This resulted in a prolonged clinical course with our patient for many months. Our patient was treated with intravenous carbapenems and vancomycin, which led to improvement in clinical status and imaging. While the underlying cause remained unclear, reports of E. coli necrotizing pneumonia have been associated with microaspiration of secretions [3]. Our patient's history of gastroparesis and SIBO, coupled with malnutrition leading to relative immunocompromise, were thought to contribute to necrotizing pneumonia. CONCLUSIONS: While E. coli is a rising culprit for pulmonary infections, it is an uncommon cause of necrotizing pneumonia. This case highlights that E. coli can cause large pulmonary cavities, and recognizing its severity is important to initiate prompt targeted antimicrobial therapy. REFERENCE #1: Gadkowski LB, Stout JE. Cavitary pulmonary disease. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2008 Apr;21(2):305-33, table of contents. doi: 10.1128/CMR.00060-07. PMID: 18400799; PMCID: PMC2292573. REFERENCE #2: Krutikov, M., Rahman, A. & Tiberi, S. (2019). Necrotizing pneumonia (aetiology, clinical features and management). Current Opinion in Pulmonary Medicine, 25 (3), 225-232. doi: 10.1097/MCP.0000000000000571. REFERENCE #3: Harsha NS, Sandeepa HS, Hemantha Kumar S, Prakash B, Jayalakshmi K. A rare cause of cavitatory pneumonia. Respir Med Case Rep. 2016 Aug 27;19:125-7. doi: 10.1016/j.rmcr.2016.08.011. PMID: 27672553; PMCID: PMC5030368. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Angila Bag No relevant relationships by Shim Roh No relevant relationships by Stephanie Rosales No relevant relationships by Devon Scott No relevant relationships by David Shiu
