N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Manifestations Case Reports Posters (Q) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Spontaneous pneumothorax is a complication of septic pulmonary emboli that is commonly reported in immunocompromised patients, intravenous drug abusers, and recently, due to the increased use of indwelling catheters and implantable devices [1,2]. Septic pulmonary emboli (SPE) are nonthrombotic emboli that arise from an infectious site and embolize into the pulmonary artery causing pulmonary nodular/ cavitary lesions. Right-sided endocarditis is a common source of the SPE, however, this case demonstrates a sternal infection, as a source of septic pulmonary emboli and possibly right-sided endocarditis in a patient with no history of intravenous drug abuse. CASE PRESENTATION: A 52-year-old man presented with dyspnea, fever, and productive cough. He has a past medical history of cocaine abuse (denies IV drug use), bipolar disorder, and a recent hospitalization one month ago, for chest pain, two weeks following blunt trauma to the chest during an altercation, a computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed a non-displaced upper sternal fracture with evidence of a small sternal abscess (Figure 3 with otherwise unremarkable findings (Figure 1A, 2A). A 2-D echo showed no vegetations, valvular abnormalities, and normal left ventricular ejection fraction. The patient was readmitted with the above symptoms to the intensive care unit, a CT chest revealed, moderate left-sided pneumothorax and diffuse bilateral cavitations representing a septic embolic event, and mixed lytic sclerotic lesion with cortical destruction and surrounding soft tissue representing an infection (Figure 1B, 2B). A chest tube was placed. Laboratory studies for tuberculosis were negative, blood cultures were positive for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA). Treatment was initiated with oxacillin. A repeat 2-D echocardiogram revealed a small vegetation in the septal leaflet of the tricuspid valve flailing into the right atrium (RA), with a possible source being the sternal infection. The patient was unable to pursue further investigative tests such as a transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE). Blood cultures were repeated until negative, the patient was later discharged to a skilled nursing facility to complete 6 weeks of IV oxacillin. DISCUSSION: A PubMed search for spontaneous pneumothorax from SPE from April 2000 yielded less than 20 patients. To our knowledge, this is the first case reported of septic pulmonary emboli from a sternal abscess/ infection. SPE requires prompt diagnosis and treatment owing to its mortality rate of 20-30% [3], and complications such as pneumothorax. Radiological findings of SPE may range from the most common finding of multiple nodular opacities (66%), cavitary lesions (that may indicate infarction), pleural effusions, and peripheral wedge-shaped lesions (17%) [4], occasionally a feeding vessel sign that indicates a hematogenous source may be present. [5]. Treatment includes long-term broad-spectrum antibiotics and where indicated, source control i.e. removal of the intravascular catheter, abscess drainage, etc. CONCLUSIONS: Sternal infection as a source of SPE and tricuspid valve endocarditis is a rare finding. In a patient with risk factors, presentation with fever, dyspnea, new multiple infiltrates in the lung, and bacteremia are highly suspicious features of SPE. This case report aims to emphasize early detection and treatment owing to its high mortality rate. REFERENCE #1: 1. Okabe M, Kasai K, Yokoo T. Pneumothorax Secondary to Septic Pulmonary Emboli in a Long-term Hemodialysis Patient with Psoas Abscess. Intern Med. 2017 Dec 1;56(23):3243-3247. doi: 10.2169/internalmedicine.9050-17. Epub 2017 Oct 11. PMID: 29021472; PMCID: PMC5742401. 2. Prasad, R.M., Raziq, F., Kemnic, T. and Abubaker, A., 2021. Implantable port developing septic pulmonary emboli and secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. Am J Med Case Rep, 9, pp.35-9. REFERENCE #2: 3. Chou DW, Wu SL, Chung KM, Han SC, Cheung BM. Septic Pulmonary Embolism Requiring Critical Care: Clinicoradiological Spectrum, Causative Pathogens and Outcomes. Clinics (Sao Paulo). 2016 Oct 1;71(10):562-569. doi: 10.6061/clinics/2016(10)02. PMID: 27759843; PMCID: PMC5054769. REFERENCE #3: 4. Ye R, Zhao L, Wang C, Wu X, Yan H. Clinical characteristics of septic pulmonary embolism in adults: a systematic review. Respir Med. 2014 Jan;108(1):1-8. doi: 10.1016/j.rmed.2013.10.012. Epub 2013 Oct 17. PMID: 24183289.5. Karadeniz, Gülistan, et al. "Septic pulmonary embolism." (2017): 153-156. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Percy Thomas No relevant relationships by Rishika Trivedi No relevant relationships by Elisa Velez
