N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (R) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Upadacitinib is a Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor used for induction and maintenance therapy in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).(1) Adverse effects of upadacitinib include increased infection risk.(2) Here we report a case of cryptococcal pneumonia in a man recently started on upadacitinib. CASE PRESENTATION: A 64-year old man with Crohn's and ankylosing spondylitis presented with progressive positional dyspnea, productive cough, and right sided pleuritic chest pain. He denied hemoptysis, fevers, or other systemic symptoms. Medical history included proctocolectomy at age 19. He was left with draining enterocutaneous fistulas which chronically required antibiotic courses. Fifteen weeks prior, he switched from vedolizumab (Entyvio) and ustekinumab (Stelara) to upadacitinib (Rinvoq) which had improved his Crohn's symptoms. Initial plans included a 45mg daily induction dose of upadacitinib for 12 weeks, followed by a 30mg daily maintenance dose. Temperature, blood pressure, and pulse oximetry were unremarkable. Auscultation revealed right lower lobe crackles, coarse breath sounds, and no notable wheezes or rhonchi. Labs were significant for leukocytosis of 24.9 x10^3/mcL, CRP 17.5 mg/L, and D-Dimer 2946 FEU ng/ml. CT angiogram showed no pulmonary thromboembolism but consolidations in lower lobes and lingula consistent with community acquired pneumonia. He completed 7 days of amoxicillin/clavulanic acid, 3 days of azithromycin, and stopped upadacitinib but symptoms failed to improve. One week later, opacification in the left mid and lower regions worsened on CXR. Labs showed leukocytosis of 22x10^3/mcL and increased CRP to 199.9 mg/L. Bacterial pneumonia was felt to be unlikely but both fungal and organizing pneumonia were considered in the differential and further infectious workup was ordered. Lower respiratory culture showed normal flora and calcofluor white fungal stain was negative. Fungitell (1,3)-beta-D-glucan assay returned >500 pg/mL and cryptococcal antigen titer serum returned positive at 1:160. Once cryptococcal testing returned positive, the patient began 6 months of fluconazole treatment. One week later, he had a new flat affect and resting hypoxemia (SpO2 85% on room air) and was evaluated for cryptococcal meningitis. CSF studies returned with total protein 29 mg/dL (ref 15-45 mg/dL), cell count 4 nucleated cells (ref 0-5/mcl) and negative for cryptococcal antigen. Blood cultures returned negative. At one month his pneumonic symptoms and chest radiology improved and his upadacitinib regimen was restarted at a lower daily 30mg dose. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary cryptococcus is a "not to miss" diagnosis which can be mistaken clinically or radiographically for lung cancer, pulmonary tuberculosis, other mycotic infections, or bacterial pneumonia(3). However, disseminated cryptococcal infection should remain high on the differential for any immunocompromised patient on immunosuppressive medications such as JAK inhibitors, anti TNF alpha drugs, or other similar therapies presenting with respiratory symptoms. Our patient in the scenario above was ultimately diagnosed and treated appropriately when cryptococcal antigen serology returned positive. The prognosis for cryptococcal pneumonia is variable, and if left untreated, morbidity and mortality risk are high. CONCLUSIONS: JAK inhibitor therapies are safe and well-tolerated by most, but in patients who may be immunosuppressed at baseline due to chronic illness and poor nutrition, extra care should be taken when prescribing these medications at higher doses. REFERENCE #1: 1. Solitano V, Vuyyuru SK, MacDonald JK, Zayadi A, Parker CE, Narula N, et al. Efficacy and Safety of Advanced Oral Small Molecules for Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. J Crohns Colitis. 2023 Nov 1;17(11):1800–16. REFERENCE #2: 2. Olivera PA, Lasa JS, Bonovas S, Danese S, Peyrin-Biroulet L. Safety of Janus Kinase Inhibitors in Patients With Inflammatory Bowel Diseases or Other Immune-mediated Diseases: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Gastroenterology. 2020 May 1;158(6):1554-1573.e12. REFERENCE #3: 3. Setianingrum F, Rautemaa-Richardson R, Denning DW. Pulmonary cryptococcosis: A review of pathobiology and clinical aspects. Med Mycol. 2019 Feb 1;57(2):133–50. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Graham Atkins No relevant relationships by Kaitlin McGowan speaker for CME activities relationship with Abbvie Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee grant funding relationship with BMS Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee grant funding, speaker for CME programs relationship with Janssen Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Lilly Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee grant support, speaker for CME activity relationship with Pfizer Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee licensing fees, grant support relationship with Takeda Please note: current Added 03/18/2024 by Corey Siegel, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Adele Soutar
