N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (N) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) refers to infections caused by Mycobacterium avium and Mycobacterium intracellulare. MAC comprises a group of ubiquitous environmental organisms and is an important cause of disease in immunocompromised patients. Pulmonary MAC infections predominantly afflict those with underlying lung conditions such as COPD, bronchiectasis, or immune deficiencies. Classically, high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) reveals bilateral reticulonodular opacities and bronchiectasis. However, MAC infections can, occasionally, deviate from this norm, presenting as solitary pulmonary nodules mimicking malignancies. We report a case of a patient with a history of smoking and pre-existing COPD, exhibiting an atypical radiographic presentation of MAC infection—a spiculated right upper lobe lung nodule, initially raising concerns of lung cancer. CASE PRESENTATION: A 56-year-old male with a 40 pack-year smoking history and known COPD was referred to pulmonary clinic for evaluation of chronic lung disease. He reported progressive exertional dyspnea and cough for 5 years, along with nocturnal cough. He had been diagnosed with COPD and reported "lung nodules" previously but had not follow up on it since first being notified. On exam, patient was not in respiratory distress. Pulmonary function tests showed moderate obstructive ventilatory impairment without significant bronchodilator response and hyperinflation. Recent CT chest showed new severe upper lobe predominant bullous emphysematous changes since prior imaging. A new spiculated 1.6 x 1.5 cm right upper lobe lung nodule was noted, suspicious for malignancy. PET scan demonstrated increased uptake with a maximum Standardized Uptake Value (SUV) of 3.8. CT-guided biopsy yielded rare, atypical cells, interpreted as favoring organizing pneumonia. The decision was made for surgical excision given concern for cancer. Right upper lobectomy was per-formed. Final surgical pathology revealed necrotizing granulomas with rare acid-fast bacilli. Cultures grew MAC. The patient was treated with a macrolide and ethambutol. On follow up he demonstrated clinical improvement on this regimen. DISCUSSION: While pulmonary nodules and mass lesions are infrequent manifestations of MAC infections, there are case reports documenting MAC infections presenting as solitary pulmonary nodules that mimic malignancy. Typical HRCT findings include bilateral bronchiectasis and tree-in-bud opacities. A solitary pulmonary nodule on imaging is rare for MAC. Our patient had severe emphysematous changes on imaging and a history of tobacco abuse, putting him at risk for both non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) infections and lung cancer. The initial suspicion of lung cancer, backed by a positive PET scan and spiculated morphology, highlights the potential for misinterpretation and underscores the role of thorough diagnostic evaluation. The unexpected identification of MAC infection post-resection not only adds to the existing literature but also emphasizes the need for a comprehensive approach in managing patients with atypical presentations. CONCLUSIONS: Furthermore, the case serves as a reminder that while classic radiographic features of MAC include bilateral reticulonodular opacities and bronchiectasis, the spectrum of presentations can be broader. This recognition has significant implications for clinical practice, prompting clinicians to maintain a high index of suspicion for MAC even in cases where the radiographic findings deviate from the norm. REFERENCE #1: n/a DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ailia Ali No relevant relationships by Aqeel Khanani
