N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (V) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Mucormycosis, a severe fungal infection caused by Mucorales, affects immunocompromised individuals and uncontrolled diabetics, with high mortality in pulmonary cases. Formerly known as zygomycosis, mucormycosis manifests in various syndromes, notably rhino-orbital-cerebral and pulmonary infections, with a risk of disseminated disease in 40 to 50 percent of cases (1). Patients with hematologic malignancies, particularly post-hematopoietic cell transplantation, face higher risks, with pulmonary mucormycosis being more prevalent in this group. The ubiquitous nature of Mucorales, found in soil and decaying vegetation, and the genera Rhizopus, Mucor, and Rhizomucor are most commonly implicated in human infection, while Apophysomyces is less common. The incidence and severity of mucormycosis have escalated among patients with hematologic malignancies, attributed to factors like voriconazole prophylaxis and increased immunosuppressive therapy (2). CASE PRESENTATION: A 69-year-old male with a history of high-risk chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) on Pirtobrutinib, hypogammaglobulinemia receiving regular IVIG infusions, bronchiectasis, and a complex infectious history presented with gross hematuria, a worsening cough, and new skin lesions. He had a history of respiratory infections, including Mycobacterium avium complex, Scedosporium, and Aspergillus, with a recent switch from Ibrutinib to Pirtobrutinib for CLL management. He was admitted for sepsis, presenting with tachycardia and leukocytosis, and suspicion of skin, pulmonary, and urinary tract infections. Urinalysis showed significant pyuria and hematuria. Diagnostic imaging revealed right-sided pyelonephritis and right middle lobe pneumonia, prompting the initiation of empirical antibiotic therapy with Rocephin and Azithromycin. Further evaluation through bronchoscopy confirmed a Rhizopus infection, a notable finding given the patient's extensive infectious history and immunocompromised state. Comprehensive testing excluded other pathogens, including acid-fast bacilli, pneumocystis, and viral infections. Due to the active infection, the medical team held the patient's CLL treatment with Pirtobrutinib following discussions with hematology. During the hospitalization, he developed progressive enlarging skin lesions for which he underwent debridement and biopsy. These procedures revealed an angioinvasive fungal infection characterized by filamentous fungi in the dermis and subcutis with associated tissue necrosis. Initial biopsies were demonstrative of Apophomyces, and subsequent pathology confirmed its angioinvasive nature. Concurrently, the growth of coccidioidomycosis in the urine culture indicated a disseminated infection, necessitating empirical antifungal therapy with Amphotericin B (Ambisome) and Isavuconazole (Cresemba). However, due to deteriorating renal function, Ambisome was discontinued and continued on Cresemba. He was eventually discharged on Cresemba for 12 weeks with multidisciplinary outpatient follow-up. DISCUSSION: This case highlights the importance of aggressive surgical debridement and the early initiation of antifungal therapy in managing mucormycosis. The utilization of Ambisome as the cornerstone of initial treatment with the transition to salvage therapy with Cresemba underscores the tailored approach necessary for these high-risk infections. Despite evolving treatment strategies, including monotherapy and combination therapies, the survival benefit remains closely tied to early intervention and personalized management plans based on the patient's response and tolerance to treatment (3). CONCLUSIONS: Effective mucormycosis management in high-risk CLL patients demands a responsive strategy, adjusting antifungal therapy for renal function and ensuring thorough clinical and radiographic monitoring. This case exemplifies the challenges mucormycosis poses and the need for meticulous, coordinated care in immunocompromised individuals. REFERENCE #1: Roden MM, Zaoutis TE, Buchanan WL, et al. Epidemiology and outcome of zygomycosis: a review of 929 reported cases. Clin Infect Dis 2005; 41:634. REFERENCE #2: Kontoyiannis DP, Lionakis MS, Lewis RE, et al. Zygomycosis in a tertiary-care cancer center in the era of Aspergillus-active antifungal therapy: a case-control observational study of 27 recent cases. J Infect Dis 2005; 191:1350. REFERENCE #3: Cornely OA, Alastruey-Izquierdo A, Arenz D, et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of mucormycosis: an initiative of the European Confederation of Medical Mycology in cooperation with the Mycoses Study Group Education and Research Consortium. Lancet Infect Dis 2019; 19:8405. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Natasha Dyal No relevant relationships by Clive Miranda No relevant relationships by Sasikanth Ravi No relevant relationships by Srekar Ravi
