N2  - SESSION TITLE: Right in the Middle: Mediastinal Disorders SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: A lung mass presents a limited list of differential diagnoses, with infectious or malignant processes being the primary concerns. Here, we present a case of a lung mass where the final diagnosis was only confirmed after a lobectomy and tissue samples were obtained, despite multiple biopsies. CASE PRESENTATION: A 57-year-old male ex-smoker presented with an intermittent dry cough and left-sided chest pain persisting for many months. Computed tomography (CT) imaging revealed a left upper lobe lung mass extending into the mediastinum, accompanied by skin erythema (Figure 1). The patient underwent a CT-guided core biopsy which showed reactive cells consistent with chronic inflammation and no definitive evidence of malignancy. Despite antibiotic treatment, the patient's symptoms persisted, prompting an endobronchial ultrasound with transbronchial needle aspiration. The subsequent biopsy revealed inflamed granulation tissue and abscess formation, with light growth of Actinomyces odontolyticus on bronchial washing. Following three weeks of Augmentin, repeat imaging showed an increase in mass size and worsening skin drainage (Figure 2). A punch biopsy was attempted, revealing chronic inflammatory infiltrate with numerous neutrophils. The patient was started on a six-week course of penicillin G without symptom improvement. Follow-up imaging revealed the abscess had now nearly completely occluded the left upper lobe and lingula, and there was a new pleural effusion (Figure 3). Meanwhile, his white blood cell counts continued to climb and the mean corpuscle volume decreased. Additionally, the cough and shortness of breath worsened. The patient lost 15 pounds in two months. On repeat bronchoscopy, the left main stem and subsegments appeared inflamed with edematous and erythematous mucosa, along with thick mucopurulent secretions. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and pleural fluid from a thoracentesis were sent for gram stain, cultures, cytology, mycology, and cell count, all of which were negative. Eventually, the patient underwent a left upper lobectomy, during which the left upper lobe was found to be boggy and necrotic with purulent fluid. Immunostains from the excised lung tissue were positive for CD30 and CD15, consistent with classical Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL). Once the patient recovered from his lobectomy, he was initiated on chemotherapy. DISCUSSION: HL is a B-cell cancer that usually involves lymph nodes but may affect other organs such as the liver, lungs, and bones1. HL manifesting as a large lung lesion makes it difficult to distinguish from infection by opportunistic organisms such as anaerobic bacteria, tuberculosis, or fungi. Chest wall abscess is also an unusual presentation of HL and is mostly described only in case reports2. Diagnosis often relies on small specimens obtained from sputum culture, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL), or bronchoscopic biopsy3. Radiographic evidence can be nonspecific and mimic other infectious or malignant processes. Due to the unusual presentation of HL, the definitive diagnosis in this case remained elusive, and it was not until eight months after the patient first presented to a healthcare facility that the final diagnosis was made. CONCLUSIONS: This case underscores the challenge of reaching a definitive diagnosis and emphasizes the importance of considering HL in the differential diagnosis when encountering a refractory lung abscess. REFERENCE #1: Matsumoto T, Otsuka K, Funayama Y, Imai Y, Tomii K. Primary pulmonary lymphoma mimicking a refractory lung abscess: A case report. Oncol Lett. 2015;9(4):1575-1578. doi:10.3892/OL.2015.2929/HTML REFERENCE #2: Khalbuss WE, Grigorian S, Mignone J, D'Agostino HJ. Chest wall abscess: An unusual presentation of Hodgkin's lymphoma. Diagn Cytopathol. 2005;33(1):33-35. doi:10.1002/DC.20220 REFERENCE #3: Drent M, Wagenaar SS, Mulder PGH, Van Velzen-Blad H, Diamant M, Van den Bosch JMM. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid profiles in sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and non-Hodgkin's and Hodgkin's disease: An evaluation of differences. Chest. 1994;105(2):514-519. doi:10.1378/chest.105.2.514 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Anh Nguyen
