N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (K) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Aspergillosis is caused by inhalation of the ubiquitous fungus Aspergillus. It can manifest with a range of diseases, mostly determined by the immune status of the host. The most feared manifestation is invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA), which usually occurs in severely immunocompromised hosts, traditionally seen after chemotherapy or solid-organ transplantation. Newer case reports of IPA after COPD and asthma exacerbations suggest short courses of steroids as a rare but under-recognized risk factor for this infection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 78-year-old woman with a history of asthma presented with one week of worsening shortness of breath and cough. On arrival, she had an oxygen saturation of 70% on room air. Her exam was notable for diffuse expiratory wheezes, and CT of the chest showed scattered areas of ground glass opacities and bibasilar mosaicism. The patient was intubated and started on high dose steroids for status asthmaticus. Three weeks into her hospitalization, the patient remained afebrile, but unable to be weaned off mechanical ventilation. A CTA chest was obtained due to persistent tachycardia and hypoxemia. This showed a large cavitation with air-fluid level in the right lower lobe as well as bilateral irregular solid nodules. Drainage was performed, with pleural fluid cultures growing Aspergillus fumigatus. Bronchoalveolar lavage cultures also grew Aspergillus fumigatus, as well as Klebsiella pneumoniae and Staphylococcus lugdunensis. Repeat CT one week later showed a worsening pattern of endobronchial spread with increasing cavitation. Beta-D-glucan was elevated to over 500pg/ml. The patient was initially treated with caspofungin, then isavuconazole. Ceftriaxone and metronidazole were added to cover a possible concurrent pulmonary abscess. This led to a resolution of her infiltrates, the large cavity decreased in size two weeks later. After tracheostomy, the patient was weaned off the ventilator and discharged to long-term care on tracheostomy collar. DISCUSSION: PA is a rapidly progressive disease with mortality ranging from 30 to 95% with increasing incidence over the last decades. It is thought to be one of the most frequently missed diagnoses in the ICU, often only found on autopsy. Recognition of IPA hinges on identification of host risk factors and evidence of invasive infection. Diagnosis is supported by isolation of Aspergillus from sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage or biopsy and determination of galactomannan and beta-D-glucan blood levels. However, only biopsy can prove invasion. Recently, non-traditional risk factors, such as COPD and severe viral infections have been identified, with limited data pointing towards influenza infection as an independent risk factor. This is thought to be due to changes in lung architecture, impaired mucociliary clearance, and frequent steroid usage. Our case suggests that even limited steroid use in asthma exacerbations should be considered a risk factor for invasive fungal infections. CONCLUSIONS: A high index of suspicion is needed to diagnose IPA, and it remains underdiagnosed. Growing use of steroids for bacterial and COVID-19 pneumonia may increase the risk of invasive fungal infections in patients lacking traditional risk factors. REFERENCE #1: Jaumally BA, Salem AM, Sagar AE, Pyrgos G. Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in a steroid-dependent asthmatic. Respir Med Case Rep. 2017 Feb 16;20:156-159. doi: 10.1016/j.rmcr.2017.02.007. PMID: 28275535; PMCID: PMC5328747. REFERENCE #2: Tejerina EE, Abril E, Padilla R, Rodríguez Ruíz C, Ballen A, Frutos-Vivar F, Lorente JÁ, Esteban A. Invasive aspergillosis in critically ill patients: An autopsy study. Mycoses. 2019 Aug;62(8):673-679. doi: 10.1111/myc.12927. Epub 2019 Jun 9. PMID: 31177621. REFERENCE #3: Thompson GR 3rd, Young JH. Aspergillus Infections. N Engl J Med. 2021 Oct 14;385(16):1496-1509. doi: 10.1056/NEJMra2027424. PMID: 34644473. Schauwvlieghe AFAD, Rijnders BJA, Philips N, Verwijs R, Vanderbeke L, Van Tienen C, Lagrou K, Verweij PE, Van de Veerdonk FL, Gommers D, Spronk P, Bergmans DCJJ, Hoedemaekers A, Andrinopoulou ER, van den Berg CHSB, Juffermans NP, Hodiamont CJ, Vonk AG, Depuydt P, Boelens J, Wauters J; Dutch-Belgian Mycosis study group. Invasive aspergillosis in patients admitted to the intensive care unit with severe influenza: a retrospective cohort study. Lancet Respir Med. 2018 Oct;6(10):782-792. doi: 10.1016/S2213-2600(18)30274-1. Epub 2018 Jul 31. PMID: 30076119. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Jan Hoffmann No relevant relationships by Klaus Meinhof No relevant relationships by Dan Mozell No relevant relationships by Marcella Christina Sousa Barreto
