N2  - SESSION TITLE: Unusual Causes of Chest Pain SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Epicardial adipose tissue (EAT) is the visceral fat between the myocardium and visceral pericardium, with known physiological roles in protecting myocardium against hypothermia and adiponectin secretion from epicardial adipocytes which promote coronary circulation. EAT necrosis is a rare benign cause of chest pain. Herein, we present a 36-year-old female who presented with this unique diagnosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old female with medical history of bipolar disorder, diabetes mellitus, hypertension, morbid obesity status post gastric bypass surgery, obesity hyperventilation syndrome and pulmonary emboli complicated by chronic ventilator dependence and tracheostomy tube placement in 2015, presented to the emergency room (ER) with chief complaint of shortness of breath, chest pain and fever. She described her chest pain as sharp, 7/10 in intensity, constant in nature, with worsening chest pain on leaning forward and improvement with lying down. She denied any relation of pain to cough or deep breathing. She denied any history of smoking or family history of premature coronary artery disease (CAD). Patient was noted to be afebrile and hemodynamically stable. Her labs showed normal WBC count of 7900, lactate of 0.8 and CRP of 1. Troponin and BNP were normal. CT chest ruled out pulmonary emboli. Moderate to large sized right sided pleural effusion was noted with airspace opacities, interlobular septal thickening and a prominent right EAT pad with high attenuation measuring 9.4 x7.9 centimeter (Image 1 and 2). Cardiology team was consulted for concern of EAT necrosis. Echocardiogram revealed normal left ventricular size and function and normal wall motion. Given its' usually self-limiting prognosis, decision was made to treat patient with NSAIDs. However, given her history of gastric bypass and risk with NSAIDs, she was started on short course of steroids, Prednisone 40 milligrams daily for total duration of five days. Patient was additionally started on empiric antibiotics with intravenous vancomycin, cefepime and metronidazole for pneumonia with tracheobronchitis. Cardiothoracic surgery was consulted for placement of chest tube for possible large parapneumonic right sided pleural effusion. A 28 French chest tube was placed, and 800 ml of serosanguinous output was noted. Pleural pH noted to be 7.7 with negative fluid cultures. Sputum culture grew Pseudomonas sensitive to Cefepime. Vancomycin and Metronidazole were discontinued. Her tracheostomy tube was changed and chest tube removed. Patient clinically improved and was discharged with plan for outpatient follow up with cardiology to reassess epicardial fat. DISCUSSION: EAT necrosis is a benign, uncommon or underdiagnosed condition which usually presents with chest pain. It can be mistaken with acute coronary syndrome (ACS) and differentiated with negative cardiac enzymes. CT or MRI are the non-invasive imaging modalities of choice with findings of rounded fat stranding, with or without central calcification/necrosis and thickening of adjacent pericardium. Pleural effusion is a common finding due to inflammation. EAT is self-limited and is treated conservatively with NSAIDs, rarely surgical resection is necessary. CONCLUSIONS: EAT is considered a new cardiovascular therapeutic target because of it's relation with CAD. EAT necrosis although benign, usually mimics ACS, and can be managed with NSAIDs or steroids with good improvement. REFERENCE #1: 1. Nagy E, Jermendy AL, Merkely B, Maurovich-Horvat P. Clinical importance of epicardial adipose tissue. Arch Med Sci. 2017 Jun;13(4):864-874. doi: 10.5114/aoms.2016.63259. REFERENCE #2: 2. Braescu L, Gaspar M, Buriman D, Aburel OM, Merce AP, Bratosin F, Aleksandrovich KS, Alambaram S, Mornos C. The Role and Implications of Epicardial Fat in Coronary Atherosclerotic Disease. J Clin Med. 2022 Aug 12;11(16):4718. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ibrar Atiq No relevant relationships by Afsha Aurshina No relevant relationships by Rasha Aurshiya No relevant relationships by Abdelhamid Ben Selma No relevant relationships by Hanan Iftikhar No relevant relationships by Ali Javed No relevant relationships by Assad Marwat No relevant relationships by Priyanka Singh
