N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Manifestations Case Reports Posters (F) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Lymphocytic Interstitial Pneumonia (LIP) is a rare disease characterized by the diffuse infiltration of lymphocytes and plasma cells into the lung interstitium. LIP is an uncommon complication in HIV patients, and is more commonly a sequela of autoimmune disorders[1]. We present a 43-year-old male Haitian immigrant with progressively worsening respiratory symptoms found to have LIP and newly diagnosed HIV. CASE PRESENTATION: A 43-year-old Haitian male presented to the emergency department with a two-year history of progressive dyspnea and productive cough. Further history revealed recurrent episodes of pneumonia, dyspnea on exertion, leg swelling, and wheezing. Oxygen saturation remained in the low nineties on room air, and he was in noticeable respiratory distress. Initial labs were significant for positive sputum cultures for staphylococcus aureus for which the patient was started on sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim (TMP-SMX). Chest X-ray and subsequent CT chest revealed bilateral infiltrates, pleural effusions, reticular opacities with areas of subpleural sparing, bronchiectasis, and intralobular septal thickening suggestive of interstitial lung disease (Fig.1+2). A subsequent bronchoscopy with cryobiopsy revealed prominent chronic lymphoplasmacytic infiltration and expansion to the alveolar interstitial walls with peribronchiolar distribution, and patchy lymphoid aggregation. Trichrome stain differentiated the final diagnosis of LIP from nonspecific interstitial pneumonia with little fibrin and collagen deposition (Fig. 3). Autoimmune and infectious workup returned positive for HIV with a CD4 count of 204 and viral load of 87,000. Treatment included highly active antiretroviral therapy (HAART) along with TMP-SMX prophylaxis with close follow up in the pulmonology and infectious disease clinics. DISCUSSION: LIP is most commonly linked to autoimmune disorders as well as immunodeficiency syndromes, suggesting an interplay between immune system dysregulation and various triggering stimuli, such as chronic viral exposure in those with HIV. The pathogenesis of LIP remains unclear, with theories proposing direct immune responses to HIV in lymphoreticular cells or chronic antigenic stimulation from viral antigens and cytokines[2]. Diagnosing LIP presents significant challenges due to its overlapping clinical and radiological features with other interstitial lung diseases. Radiographic criteria of LIP per the American Thoracic Society and European Respiratory Society consensus are ground-glass opacities, bilateral cystic airspaces, and centrilobular nodules. Histologically, LIP is distinguished by diffuse interstitial infiltrates predominantly composed of lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages, that are primarily distributed along the alveolar septa[3]. The management of LIP lacks a standardized treatment protocol, with therapeutic strategies tailored to individual patient characteristics, disease severity, and the ability to treat underlying conditions. While systemic corticosteroids are frequently employed, their long-term efficacy remains uncertain. In refractory cases or those associated with hematologic malignancies, such as lymphomas, cytotoxic drugs such as cyclophosphamide and azathioprine may be considered3. CONCLUSIONS: Despite its rarity, LIP should be considered in patients presenting with progressive respiratory symptoms, particularly in the context of HIV infection or autoimmune disorders. Close collaboration between pulmonologists, infectious disease specialists, and pathologists is essential for accurate diagnosis and appropriate management. Further research is needed to reveal the pathogenesis and prognosis of LIP, as well as to establish treatment protocols to improve patient outcomes. REFERENCE #1: Fraune, C., Churg, A., Yi, E. S., Khoor, A., Kelemen, K., Larsen, B. T., Butt, Y. M., Smith, M. L., Gotway, M. B., Ryu, J. H., & Tazelaar, H. D. (2023). Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia (LIP) revisited. American Journal of Surgical Pathology, 47(3), 281–295. https://doi.org/10.1097/pas.0000000000002014. van Zyl-Smit, R. N., Naidoo, J., Wainwright, H., Said-Hartley, Q., Davids, M., Goodman, H., Rogers, S., & Dheda, K. (2015). HIV associated lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia: A clinical, histological and radiographic study from an HIV endemic resource-poor setting. BMC Pulmonary Medicine, 15(1), 1–1. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12890-015-0030-2 Das, S., & Miller, R. F. (2003). Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis in HIV infected adults. Sexually Transmitted Infections, 79(2), 88–93. https://doi.org/10.1136/sti.79.2.88 Swigris, J. J., Berry, G. J., Raffin, T. A., & Kuschner, W. G. (2002). Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia. Chest, 122(6), 2150–2164. https://doi.org/10.1378/chest.122.6.2150 REFERENCE #2: Sherani, K., Vakil, A., Sharma, A., Cervellione, K., & Thurm, C. (2016). Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia: An uncommon interstitial lung disease commonly seen in HIV. Chest, 149(4). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chest.2016.02.200Ilhan, S., & Ozkan, S. (2016). Idiopathic lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia. Istanbul Medical Journal, 17(1), 35–38. https://doi.org/10.5152/imj.2016.51078 Persaud, K., Harris, C., & Ramsahai, S. (2013). Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia—another consideration for recurrent bacterial pneumonia in an HIV positive patient (a case report). Case Reports in Clinical Medicine, 02(01), 76–80. https://doi.org/10.4236/crcm.2013.21021 Louza, G. F., Nobre, L. F., Mançano, A. D., Hochhegger, B., Souza Jr., A. S., Zanetti, G., & Marchiori, E. (2020). Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia: Computed tomography findings in 36 patients. Radiologia Brasileira, 53(5), 287–292. https://doi.org/10.1590/0100-3984.2019.0107 REFERENCE #3: Das, S., & Miller, R. F. (2003). Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis in HIV infected adults. Sexually Transmitted Infections, 79(2), 88–93. https://doi.org/10.1136/sti.79.2.88Sverzellati, N., Lynch, D. A., Hansell, D. M., Johkoh, T., King, T. E., & Travis, W. D. (2015). American thoracic society–european respiratory society classification of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias: Advances in knowledge since 2002. RadioGraphics, 35(7), 1849–1871. https://doi.org/10.1148/rg.2015140334 Johkoh, T., Müller, N. L., Pickford, H. A., Hartman, T. E., Ichikado, K., Akira, M., Honda, O., & Nakamura, H. (1999). Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia: Thin-section CT findings in 22 patients. Radiology, 212(2), 567–572. https://doi.org/10.1148/radiology.212.2.r99au05567 Fraune, C., Churg, A., Yi, E. S., Khoor, A., Kelemen, K., Larsen, B. T., Butt, Y. M., Smith, M. L., Gotway, M. B., Ryu, J. H., & Tazelaar, H. D. (2023). Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia (LIP) revisited. American Journal of Surgical Pathology, 47(3), 281–295. https://doi.org/10.1097/pas.0000000000002014 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Allen George No relevant relationships by Nikhil Madan No relevant relationships by Magrian Munetsi
