N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (X) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary histoplasmosis is a fungal lung infection caused by Histoplasma capsulatum spores. It is endemic to specific regions, particularly in the Americas, where it thrives in soil enriched with bird or bat droppings[1]. While often asymptomatic or self-limiting, severe cases can present with progressive respiratory symptoms and disseminated disease[2]. Here, we report a case of pulmonary histoplasmosis with mediastinal involvement, highlighting diagnostic challenges in unraveling complex clinical presentation and therapeutic considerations. CASE PRESENTATION: A 28-year-old female, previously in good health, presented with a two-week history of difficulty breathing in, cough, tiredness, and sharp chest pain lasting ten days. Initial evaluation revealed a mediastinal mass and presumed pneumonia. Subsequent deterioration necessitated hospital transfer. Upon transfer to our institution due to worsening symptoms, she exhibited tachypnea and was diaphoretic. Laboratory investigations showed anemia, elevated inflammatory markers, and impaired renal function. Further evaluation revealed a mediastinal mass on imaging, prompting concern for malignancy or infection. CT angiography confirmed a pre-tracheal and subcarinal mediastinal mass measuring 4.9 x 5.1 cm, exerting a minor mass effect on the right pulmonary artery, along with a nodular lesion in the right lower lobe. The patient's condition deteriorated despite initiating empiric antibiotics targeting bacterial pneumonia, with one blood culture growing Gram-positive cocci clusters. The serum fungal antibody panel and Histoplasma urine antigen tests were negative. Subsequent development of a large pericardial effusion with early tamponade physiology necessitated emergent pericardiocentesis, yielding purulent fluid consistent with an infectious process. The patient's complexity prompted ICU transfer.Repeat imaging studies highlighted the progression of the mediastinal pathology and the emergence of gas within the pericardial space, likely secondary to the procedural intervention. Ultimately, we pursued video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery, and histopathological analysis revealed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with positive staining for fungus, consistent with histoplasmosis. We added a complement fixation assay, which resulted in a titer of 1:8. Notably, the patient's husband disclosed the presence of bats in their home's attic, suggesting a potential environmental source of the fungal infection. We initiated treatment with liposomal Amphotericin B and then long term itraconazole. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary histoplasmosis presents with nonspecific respiratory symptoms, a challenging diagnosis[3]. This case emphasizes considering fungal etiologies due to atypical clinical presentations, especially in endemic areas. Histoplasmosis mimics malignancy or tuberculosis on imaging, warranting invasive biopsy for definitive diagnosis[2-3]. Blood cultures may yield false negatives, and serologic tests have variable sensitivity[4]. Despite initial suspicion of bacterial pneumonia, our patient's deterioration necessitated further investigation, leading to Histoplasma capsulatum identification. Environmental history, like bat exposure, aids in diagnosis[1]. Prompt initiation of antifungal therapy improves outcomes[4]. Complement fixation titers of 1:32 or higher are highly suggestive of acute infection. However, one should not disregard titers of 1:8 or 1:16, as they occur in about one-third of cases with active disease[4]. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary histoplasmosis should be considered in patients with respiratory symptoms and mediastinal involvement, especially in endemic regions. A high index of suspicion, coupled with multimodal diagnostic approaches and consideration of environmental exposures, is crucial for timely diagnosis and optimal management of this potentially life-threatening infection[5]. REFERENCE #1: 1. Kauffman CA. Histoplasmosis: a clinical and laboratory update. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2007 Jan;20(1):115-32. doi: 10.1128/CMR.00027-06. PMID: 17223625; PMCID: PMC1797635. REFERENCE #2: 2. Wheat LJ. Approach to the diagnosis of the endemic mycoses. Clin Chest Med. 2009 Jun;30(2):379-89, viii. doi: 10.1016/j.ccm.2009.02.011. PMID: 19375642. 3. Wheat LJ, Conces D, Allen SD, Blue-Hnidy D, Loyd J. Pulmonary histoplasmosis syndromes: recognition, diagnosis, and management. Semin Respir Crit Care Med. 2004 Apr;25(2):129-44. doi: 10.1055/s-2004-824898. PMID: 16088457. REFERENCE #3: 4. Hage CA, Ribes JA, Wengenack NL, Baddour LM, Assi M, McKinsey DS, et al. A multicenter evaluation of tests for diagnosis of histoplasmosis. Clin Infect Dis. 2011;53(5):448-454. 5. Hage CA, Azar MM, Bahr N, Loyd J, Wheat LJ. Histoplasmosis: Up-to-Date Evidence-Based Approach to Diagnosis and Management. Semin Respir Crit Care Med. 2015 Oct;36(5):729-45. doi: 10.1055/s-0035-1562899. Epub 2015 Sep 23. PMID: 26398539. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sruthi Samala No relevant relationships by Deepak Taneja
