N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (L) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Nocardiosis is rare disorder, generally seen in immunocompromised patients with depressed cell-mediated immunity and can present as cutaneous, pulmonary, or disseminated disease. Here we present a case of pulmonary Nocardiosis in an immunocompetent patient. CASE PRESENTATION: 69-year-old male active smoker with COPD who initially presented to pulmonary clinic for evaluation of shortness of breath. He had baseline shortness of breath for years but for last few weeks his shortness of breath was getting worse. He has cough on and off with some sputum production. He denied any fevers, weight loss or hemoptysis. He has been on long-acting inhalers but does not seem to help much. He denied any recent sick contacts, denied any TB risk factors. He has been treated for possible pneumonia and bronchitis as outpatient few times before this hospital admission. He had chest x-ray done which showed right upper lobe opacity. CT scan of the chest done for further evaluation which showed right upper lobe mass-like consolidation with cavitary lesion.His TB testing was negative. He underwent bronchoscopy, Biopsies were negative for malignancy BAL culture grew nocardia species. Patient was admitted to the hospital and required IV antibiotics per ID recommendations, he felt better and was discharged to home with prolonged course of oral antibiotics with Infectious disease and pulmonary outpatient follow up. DISCUSSION: Actinomycetes are Gram-positive, weakly acid-fast, filamentous aerobic bacteria. Infection occurs from direct inoculation of skin or soft tissues or via direct inhalation of Nocardia species[1]. Risk factors include steroids therapy, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis[2]. Diagnosis of Nocardiosis can be challenging as it is difficult to culture Nocardia, Microbiology lab technicians need to know that nocardia is in differential diagnosis. Molecular techniques such as polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing, restriction enzyme analysis, and 16s ribosomal-DNA sequencing have enhanced the identification of the different Nocardia species. Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole is a treatment of choice and usual treatment duration is between 6-12 months. Mortality related to nocardiosis is around 15% and up to 40% in immunocompromised patients. Brain Imaging should be considered in patients with disseminated disease as mortality is even higher. CONCLUSIONS: Although pulmonary nocardiosis is usually seen in immunocompromised patients but should be considered in immunocompetent patients as well especially with COPD, as early diagnosis and treatment can prevent morbidity and mortality. REFERENCE #1: J.W. Wilson, "Nocardiosis: updates and clinical overview," Mayo Clinic Proceedings, vol. 87, no. 4, pp. 403–407, 2012. REFERENCE #2: Rivière F, Billhot M, Soler C, Vaylet F, Margery J. Pulmonary nocardiosis in immunocompetent patients: can COPD be the only risk factor? Eur Respir Rev. 2011 Sep 1;20(121):210-2. doi: 10.1183/09059180.00002211. PMID: 21881150; PMCID: PMC9584118. REFERENCE #3: R. Mart´ınez Tomas, R. Menendez Villanueva, S. Reyes Calzada et al., "Pulmonary nocardiosis: risk factors and outcomes," Respirology, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 394–400, 2007 DISCLOSURES: No disclosure on file for Akram Eraky No relevant relationships by Zaigham ul Islam No relevant relationships by Mark Jarosz No relevant relationships by Adnan Khan No relevant relationships by Sana Khan
