N2  - SESSION TITLE: Mycobacterial Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Tuberculous pleural effusion, also known as pleural tuberculosis (pTB), is one of the prevalent manifestations of extrapulmonary tuberculosis. It may present as a simple effusion that resolves spontaneously or purulent TB empyema that can evolve into severe chronic sequela. Diagnosing this paucibacillary disease poses a challenge; most patients are initially misdiagnosed with bacterial pneumonia. Pleural microbiology or histopathology is the gold standard for pTB diagnosis. PCR enhances diagnosis and tests susceptibilities. Typical presentations include fever, pleuritic chest pain, and non-productive cough, often accompanied by night sweats, chills, dyspnea, and weight loss. It can manifest insidiously, particularly in immunocompromised patients. Anti-tumor necrosis factor inhibitors (Anti-TNF) are known to increase the risk of latent TB (LTB) reactivation. Herein, we present a patient with a history of TB exposure without previous treatment for LTB, who was found to have left-sided pleural tuberculosis after years of treatment with etanercept for rheumatoid arthritis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old female with a history of rheumatoid arthritis and psoriasis presented to the emergency department with fever, worsening chronic cough, exertional shortness of breath, and urinary symptoms. She was on long-term weekly etanercept and methotrexate. She is a nonsmoker and had moved from the Philippines to the United States years ago. Vitals revealed low blood pressure, elevated heart rate, and respiratory rate with fever. Examination indicated suprapubic tenderness and decreased air entry on the left lung base. Lab tests revealed leukocytosis, thrombocytopenia, and lactic acidosis. Urine and blood cultures were positive for Escherichia coli. Chest x-ray showed left lower lobe opacities with a small effusion. A subsequent computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed pleural nodularity and thickening on the left side, and multiple right upper pulmonary nodules with progression in noncalcified pleural and mediastinal deposits from 3 years ago, suggestive of granulomatosis or asbestos exposure. Further inquiry revealed past tuberculosis exposure and a positive tuberculin skin test. CT-guided left pleural biopsy showed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation, but Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) culture was negative. Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (MTB) PCR and the QuantiFERON TB Gold test were positive for TB. After sepsis resolved, she was started on anti-TB medications and discharged with infectious disease follow-up, and etanercept was discontinued after discussions with her rheumatologist. DISCUSSION: Biologic agents such as TNF-α inhibitors, used for immune-mediated inflammatory conditions, increase the risk of LTB reactivation. Adalimumab and infliximab carry the highest risk, with etanercept also posing a potential risk. This patient was not previously treated for LTB, given that her previous x-ray did not show a pulmonary disease; however, TB was reactivated as pleural TB after years of receiving anti-TNF injections without regular TB screening tests. CONCLUSIONS: Diagnosis of pleural tuberculosis might be challenging due to the paucibacillary nature of pTB and its insidious course. Pleural microbiology and/or histopathology are the gold standard. This case report highlights the importance of treating patients for latent TB before initiating anti-tumor necrosis factor inhibitor (anti-TNF) therapy. For individuals with a high-risk profile and a history of exposure, annual tuberculosis testing while on biologics is essential. REFERENCE #1: McNally E, Ross C, Gleeson LE. The tuberculous pleural effusion. Breathe 2023; 19: 230143 [DOI: 10.1183/20734735.0143-2023] REFERENCE #2: Dobler CC. 2016. Biologic Agents and Tuberculosis. Microbiol Spectr. 4:10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0026-2016.https://doi.org/10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0026-2016 REFERENCE #3: Shaw J, Kouejenelberg A. Pleaural Tuberculosis. Clinics in Chest medicine. Volume 42, Issue 4, December 2021, Pages 649-666.https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccm.2021.08.002 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ihab Ahmed No relevant relationships by A B M Nasibul Alam No relevant relationships by Ruba Ghalayni No relevant relationships by Yuliana Petryshyn
