N2  - SESSION TITLE: Rare Swelling: Anaphylaxsis, Angioedema, Vasculitis and Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Chronic cough is a common manifestation of severe persistent asthma. High dose of inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) may result in hoarseness. We present a case of a patient who was referred for chronic cough and hoarseness on high dose inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) and biologic therapy where a comprehensive workup demonstrated Endobronchial Nocardiosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 54-year-old female with a known history of gastroesophageal reflux disorder (GERD), irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), allergic rhinitis, and severe persistent allergic asthma since childhood presented with chronic cough and hoarseness. There was no history of immune deficiency. Her neurological exam was unremarkable. She was maintained on a high dose inhaler regimen with dual bronchodilators (LAMA + LABA) and an ICS; cumulative daily dose of the latter being fluticasone furoate 200 mcg, fluticasone propionate 880 mcg, beclomethasone dipropionate 320 mcg and nebulized budesonide 1 mg. She was also on biologic therapy with dupilimumab 200 mg every two weeks. She had a normal eosinophil count (historically elevated) and her pulmonary function tests including exhaled nitric oxide were normal. Laryngoscopy revealed normal vocal fold motion and severe vocal erythema suggestive of laryngitis. Her CT Chest revealed normal lung parenchyma without any evidence of bronchiectasis yet there was tracheal wall thickening with possible endotracheal lesion (Figure 1). A Flexible bronchoscopy confirmed endotracheal lesions (Figure 2). An endobronchial biopsy of the lesions demonstrated filamentous bacteria on Gorcott's Methanamine Silver (GMS) stain (Figure 3). Microbiological culture grew Nocardia abscessus. She was placed on oral Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole. An MRI of the brain was normal. Three months into the treatment, she had marked improvement in her cough with a significant reduction in her ICS requirement. In addition, her hoarseness completely resolved, and her laryngoscopy demonstrated improvement in laryngeal erythema. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary Nocardiosis is a rare condition that most commonly affects individuals with structural lung disease, or those who are immunocompromised. Endobronchial Nocardiosis is even rarer. Mortality of 20-30% has been reported in retrospective case series, which is influenced by their underlying comorbidities. [1,2] We report a rare case of a patient without structural lung disease, whose main predisposing condition was extremely high dose of ICS. CONCLUSIONS: High dose ICS can predispose patients to endobronchial as well as parenchymal infections from opportunistic organisms. As the most benefit from ICS is seen at low-medium dose, efforts must be made to rule out the complications of the therapy if the higher doses are required. [3] REFERENCE #1: Martinez Tomas R, Menendez Villanueva R, Reyes Calzada S, Santos Durantez M, Valles Tarazona JM, Modesto Alapont M, Gobernado Serrano M. Pulmonary nocardiosis: risk factors and outcomes. Respirology. 2007 May;12(3):394-400. REFERENCE #2: Kurahara Y, Tachibana K, Tsuyuguchi K, Akira M, Suzuki K, Hayashi S. Pulmonary nocardiosis: a clinical analysis of 59 cases. Respiratory investigation. 2014 May 1;52(3):160-6. REFERENCE #3: Husta B, Raoof S, Erzurum S, Mehta AC, Special Features: Tracheobronchopathy from Inhaled Corticosteroids, CHEST, 2017, 152(6):1296-1305 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sobia Farooq No relevant relationships by Colin Gillespie No relevant relationships by Atul Mehta No relevant relationships by Sairam Raghavan
