N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (G) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Legionella pneumophila, a rare gram-negative coccobacillus, often leads to severe conditions including Legionnaire's disease. Common symptoms include coughing, shortness of breath, fever, headache, disorientation, myalgias, and gastrointestinal symptoms. Transmission can be due to aspiration and inhalation of the infectious aerosol from contaminated water sources, and possibly person-to-person transmission. Urinary antigen test has proven most useful for confirmation, although its utilization is not widespread due to the unexpected nature of legionella occurrences. Radiographic findings may not correlate with severity of clinical condition. Common radiologic manifestations typically include patchy consolidations with pleural-based opacities or interstitial patterns. Our literature search revealed one other case manifested as single lobar pneumonia. We are reporting an unconventional case of legionella pneumonia with atypical lobar radiographic manifestation and no known environmental exposures. CASE PRESENTATION: A 76-year-old Hawaiian-Caucasian male with smoking history, atrial fibrillation, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and transurethral resection of prostate presented with coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath, and generalized weakness. One week prior to admission, patient presented with malaise, chills, and lightheadedness during golf. Chest x-ray revealed bibasilar opacities. Patient refused complete work-up and was discharged to follow-up with cardiology. Patient was started on amoxicillin during telehealth visit 2 days later, but due to worsening pulmonary status, was admitted to our facility. Chest x-ray revealed diffuse left lung patchy opacities. Chest computerized tomography (CT) scan showed left upper lobe pneumonia. Patient was unable to generate sufficient sputum for culturing. Procalcitonin and c-reactive protein levels were elevated. Urinary legionella antigen tested positive; urinary Streptococcal pneumoniae antigen was negative. Mycoplasma IgG was 0.12 U/L and IgM was normal. Patient required supplemental oxygen; azithromycin and ceftriaxone were initiated. Patient's overall condition improved as he was able to breathe comfortably on room air and reported less coughing episodes; he was discharged 2 days after admission. Follow-up chest x-ray showed complete resolution of opacities. DISCUSSION: Legionella pneumophila are found in warm freshwater environments, such as showerheads, sink faucets, and large plumbing systems. High risk factors include smoking history, chronic lung disease, and other immunocompromised states. Our patient was high risk due to his underlying COPD, atrial fibrillation, and smoking history. No known exposures to communal or environmental sources elicited. Urinary legionella antigen test is the most common diagnostic tool used to confirm such pneumonia. Given our patient's unusual chest x-ray findings, legionella was not suspected, but diagnosis was confirmed with the urinary legionella antigen test. Legionella infections commonly manifest on chest x-ray with diffuse interstitial and/or patchy consolidations. Our patient's x-ray revealed a lobar pneumonia, which is more commonly seen in Streptococcus pneumoniae, Klebsiella pneumoniae, or Staphylococcus pneumoniae. Most severe cases require aggressive antibiotic therapy and hospitalization. Legionella is typically treated with macrolides and fluoroquinolones. Our patient was successfully treated with azithromycin and ceftriaxone. CONCLUSIONS: This is the first known case of legionella pneumonia presented as unusual lobar consolidation with unknown source of exposure. High index of suspicion, good environmental history, and routine urinary legionella antigen testing can guide medical providers to accurate diagnosis and therapy. REFERENCE #1: Plaschke, M., Ströhle, A., Bergh, F. et al. Neurologische und psychiatrische Symptomatik bei der Legionellainfektion Fallbericht und Übersicht über das klinische Spektrum. Nervenarzt 68, 342–345 (1997). REFERENCE #2: Winn, W. C. (1995). Legionella. Manual of clinical microbiology, 7, 572-85. REFERENCE #3: Correia AM, Ferreira JS, Borges V, et al. Probable person-to-person transmission of Legionnaires' disease. N Engl J Med. 2016;374:497–8. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Elizabeth Fischer No relevant relationships by Takkin Lo
