N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (S) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) is a potential complication of highly active antiretroviral therapy (HAART). It is characterized by an exaggerated inflammatory reaction against latent infections that occur within a week to several months after initiating HAART. We present a case of life-threatening IRIS that benefitted from ECMO. CASE PRESENTATION: A 29-year-old male, newly diagnosed with human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immune deficiency syndrome (HIV/AIDS) with CD4 count of 10 and pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, presented to the emergency room with worsening cough, shortness of breath, and generalized weakness 10 days after being started on HAART for newly diagnosed HIV. On arrival, he had a temperature of 102°F, heart rate 128 bpm, respiratory rate 28 breaths/min, BP 117/66 mm Hg and saturation of 97% on room air. Lung auscultation revealed diffusely coarse breath sounds. Laboratory tests showed leukopenia with white blood cell of 2.97, mild normocytic anemia with hemoglobin of 10.2 mg/dl, and lactic acidosis (3.13). Chest x-ray showed diffuse infiltrates. The patient was started on IV fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics cefepime and vancomycin, in addition to trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole for pneumocystis pneumonia and HAART. During hospital course, patient developed acute respiratory failure with increasing oxygen requirement of 4L/min, which was potentially attributed to parainfluenza virus pneumonia, paradoxical worsening of Pneumocystis pneumonia possibly due to immune reconstitution syndrome [IRIS] or superimposed bacterial infection. With a baseline CD4 count of 10, IRIS was concerning. The patient underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage which yielded positive CMV, broadening our differential CMV pneumonitis as the cause for respiratory failure. He continued to deteriorate, necessitating expanded antibiotic coverage with meropenem and valganciclovir alongside ventilatory support. Further decline led to veno-venous ECMO requirement. After several weeks of ECMO support along with broad spectrum antibiotics and ventilatory support, the patient's condition stabilized, and he was successfully decannulated. DISCUSSION: IRIS, a phenomenon frequently linked with HIV treatment, manifests within six months of starting antiretroviral therapy, impacting approximately 30% of individuals. Noteworthy risk factors encompass the initiation of therapy in younger males, low CD4 counts (<100), existing opportunistic infections, and swift CD4 elevation or HIV RNA suppression post-HAART. In our scenario, our patient was male and was recently initiated on HIV treatment while grappling with Pneumocystis Pneumonia and an ongoing CMV infection. Early initiation of Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO) in a patient with severe acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) complicated by IRIS presents a challenging yet potentially life-saving intervention. CONCLUSIONS: Early initiation of ECMO in ARDS offers improved oxygenation, lung protection, reduced ventilator-induced lung injury and enhanced survival rates. REFERENCE #1: 1. Walker NF, Scriven J, Meintjes G, Wilkinson RJ. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome in HIV-infected patients. HIV AIDS (Auckl). 2015 Feb 12;7:49-64. doi: 10.2147/HIV.S42328. PMID: 25709503; PMCID: PMC4334287. REFERENCE #2: 2. Boatman JA, Baker JV, Emery S, Furrer H, Mushatt DM, Sedláček D, Lundgren JD, Neaton JD; INSIGHT START Study Group. Risk Factors for Low CD4+ Count Recovery Despite Viral Suppression Among Participants Initiating Antiretroviral Treatment With CD4+ Counts > 500 Cells/mm3: Findings From the Strategic Timing of AntiRetroviral Therapy (START) Trial. J Acquir Immune Defic Syndr. 2019 May 1;81(1):10-17. doi: 10.1097/QAI.0000000000001967. PMID: 30664075; PMCID: PMC6456414. REFERENCE #3: 3. Singh SP, Hote MP. Ventilatory management of patients on ECMO. Indian J Thorac Cardiovasc Surg. 2021 Apr;37(Suppl 2):248-253. doi: 10.1007/s12055-020-01021-z. Epub 2020 Aug 12. PMID: 33967448; PMCID: PMC8062618. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Prabina Ghimire No relevant relationships by Esha Patel No relevant relationships by Vishesh Paul No relevant relationships by Saad Ur Rahman No relevant relationships by Danish Thameem
