N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (BA) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Brain abscesses present a significant challenge in developing countries but are rare in developed regions. Mortality rates have decreased to 5% to 15% with antibiotics and advanced imaging. However, untreated abscesses can lead to severe neurological issues. While streptococci are common bacteria, their involvement in multifocal brain abscesses is rarely reported. This case underscores the importance of considering Streptococcus intermedius as a potential cause of such abscesses, particularly in immunocompetent individuals. CASE PRESENTATION: A healthy 37-year-old male presented to the emergency department with a worsening dry cough lasting for 2 weeks. Shortly after, he developed sudden new onset slurred speech, blurred vision, and dyspnea, prompting his arrival to the ED. Vitals showed a temperature of 102.8°F, pulse of 109, and normal blood pressure. On exam, he appeared ill, confused, and in acute distress with a Glasgow Coma Scale of 15 with notable dysarthria, and binocular diplopia worsening on lateral gaze. His head CT without contrast revealed areas of low attenuation, prompting an MRI Brain which demonstrated multiple ring-enhancing lesions with diffusion restrictions above and below the tentorium. Labs showed leukocytosis with 13.5 white blood cells (WBC) with left shift 85% and elevated inflammatory markers including a procalcitonin of 1.76 and elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) of 81. Blood cultures were sent, and the patient was started on broad-spectrum antibiotics due to concern for multiple brain abscesses, as well as seizure prophylaxis with levetiracetam. Serologic studies for Toxoplasma, Histoplasma, syphilis, Coccidioides, tuberculosis, and HIV were negative. A lumbar puncture was obtained with normal cerebrospinal fluid study and no growth on culture. The patient was admitted to the intensive care unit (ICU) for close monitoring of his neurological status and subsequently required intubation for 3 days for airway protection. An echocardiogram was performed, which did not show any vegetations. To identify the source of infection, neurosurgery performed a left-sided temporal craniotomy, and tissues grew Streptococcus intermedius. Antibiotics were weaned down to IV Ceftriaxone 2 gm every 12 hours, which he completed for 6 weeks. A follow-up MRI was conducted showing complete resolution of previous abscesses. The patient regained full neurological function. DISCUSSION: Cerebral abscesses, if untreated promptly, can be fatal. Around 20% of patients upon admission exhibit the classic triad of headache, fever, and neurological deficits. Despite common risk factors, up to 25% of cases lack an identifiable cause, as observed here. Given the typically polymicrobial nature of these abscesses, early empiric antibiotic treatment is universally recommended, with adjustments based on microbiological findings. Early ICU admission is advisable for patients with comorbidities, severe neurological symptoms, or significant cerebral edema causing mass effect. CONCLUSIONS: Despite advancements in detection and treatment, there remains a scarcity of published data regarding the involvement of Streptococcus intermedius in brain abscesses. Observational studies suggest that the overall prognosis has improved but mortality rates remain high. While Streptococcus are frequently identified as a cause of solitary brain abscess, the presentation of multifocal brain abscesses should also lead to the consideration of Streptococcus intermedius as a possible cause of infection. REFERENCE #1: https://journals.lww.com/co-infectiousdiseases/abstract/2022/06000/brain_abscess_in_immunocompetent_patients__recent.9.aspx REFERENCE #2: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10096-012-1782-8 REFERENCE #3: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0035378719307301?via%3Dihubhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9412100/ DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Saif Al-Deen Alattal No relevant relationships by Harith Ghnaima No relevant relationships by Adolfo Martinez Salazar No relevant relationships by Georgette Nader No relevant relationships by Rajit Pahwa No relevant relationships by Rand Sabanci No relevant relationships by Moiz Saeed No relevant relationships by Dina Shaban No relevant relationships by Kevin Watat No relevant relationships by Ramy Zughul
