N2  - SESSION TITLE: Bronchiectasis and CF Case Reports Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive condition characterized by defects in the CFTR gene leading to multiorgan dysfunction, although lung disease is the main determinant of morbidity and mortality. Infection with Mycobacterium abscessus is particularly associated with high mortality. Classically associated with white patients, CF has been diagnosed with increasing frequency in the Hispanic population, although significant disparities exist with regards to disease diagnosis, management, and outcomes. We present the case of a young Latino man with CF complicated by M. abscessus infection, whose illness course has been complicated by multiple barriers to care. CASE PRESENTATION: Our patient is 21-year-old gentleman who immigrated to the United States from Ecuador two years ago. No past medical records are available, but he was diagnosed with CF as a child and was on various antibiotics and inhalers throughout the years, although specific microbiologic culture data is unknown. He presented to the hospital within days of arriving to the US, where imaging noted the presence of diffuse multifocal cystic bronchiectasis and multifocal pneumonia. Sputum cultures were positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Escherichia coli, and Mycobacterium abscessus. He was treated for pneumonia and discharged into the community with airway clearance measures and outpatient follow up. Over the next two years, he was hospitalized for recurrent pulmonary infections with P. aeruginosa and Stenotrophomonas. AFB cultures were consistently positive for M. abscessus, although directed therapy deferred given repeated clinical improvement with treatment of other organisms. Genetic testing revealed CTFR mutation for which there are no available therapies. Outpatient care has been complicated by patient's lack of insurance, leading to delays and/or interruptions in therapy. Last month, patient presented with worsening cough, sputum production, and radiographic progression of his pulmonary disease despite optimal airway clearance measures and treatment of his Pseudomonas and Stenotrophomonas. Treatment was initiated for Mycobacterium abscessus infection with intravenous antibiotics. He remains hospitalized pending approval of charity care so that he can receive this prolonged therapy in the community, and efforts are ongoing to help the patient in obtaining health insurance in hopes of optimizing his care and preparing for more advanced therapies, including lung transplant. DISCUSSION: CF remains difficult to treat, particularly when complicated by M. abscessus infection, given this organism's intrinsic resistance to most antibiotics and requirement of protracted courses of multiple intravenous drugs. While advances in therapy such as CFTR modulators have improved life expectancy, Hispanic patients are less likely to benefit as their CTFR mutations are less likely to be amenable to available therapies. Additional barriers such as lack of insurance and ability to access guideline-directed therapy contribute to these disparities in outcomes as well, adding an additional layer of complexity to treating this complicated disease. CONCLUSIONS: Infectious complications of CF, particularly with M. abscessus, remain difficult to treat. These challenges are worsened by delays in diagnosis, access to care, and more limited treatment options frequently encountered by Hispanic patients. Reducing health disparities remains a crucial component in improving survival and quality of life in all patients with CF. REFERENCE #1: Griffith DE, Daley CL. Treatment of Mycobacterium abscessus Pulmonary Disease. Chest. 2022 Jan;161(1):64-75. doi: 10.1016/j.chest.2021.07.035. Epub 2021 Jul 24. PMID: 34314673. REFERENCE #2: Januska MN, Marx L, Walker PA, Berdella MN, Langfelder-Schwind E. The CFTR variant profile of Hispanic patients with cystic fibrosis: Impact on access to effective screening, diagnosis, and personalized medicine. J Genet Couns. 2020 Aug;29(4):607-615. doi: 10.1002/jgc4.1271. Epub 2020 Mar 30. PMID: 32227567. REFERENCE #3: Recchia D, Stelitano G, Stamilla A, Gutierrez DL, Degiacomi G, Chiarelli LR, Pasca MR. Mycobacterium abscessus Infections in Cystic Fibrosis Individuals: A Review on Therapeutic Options. Int J Mol Sci. 2023 Feb 27;24(5):4635. doi: 10.3390/ijms24054635. PMID: 36902066; PMCID: PMC10002592. REFERENCE #4: Rho J, Ahn C, Gao A, Sawicki GS, Keller A, Jain R. Disparities in Mortality of Hispanic Patients with Cystic Fibrosis in the United States. A National and Regional Cohort Study. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2018 Oct 15;198(8):1055-1063. doi: 10.1164/rccm.201711-2357OC. PMID: 29742360; PMCID: PMC6221571. DISCLOSURES: Consultant relationship with Insmed Please note: 2018-2022 Added 11/29/2023 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Principal investigator in clinical trial relationship with Baxter Please note: 2020-2022 Added 11/29/2023 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Advisory Committee Member relationship with Zambon Please note: 2021 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Advisory Board relationship with Insmed Please note: 2018-2022 Added 11/29/2023 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Advisory Board, PI in clinical study relationship with Hill-Rom Please note: 2019-2022 Added 11/29/2023 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Physio-Assist Please note: 2021 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Dymedso Please note: 2021 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Zambon Please note: 2021 by Ashwin Basavaraj, value=Consulting fee Consultant relationship with Tactile Please note: 2023 Added 03/25/2024 by Ashwin Basavaraj, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Bhaskara Garimella No relevant relationships by Alexandria Imperato No disclosure on file for Angeliki Kazeros No relevant relationships by Nathaniel Nelson No disclosure on file for Rudra Ramanathan
