N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Reports Posters (C) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), formerly known as Wegener's granulomatosis (WG), is an immunologically mediated systemic small-vessel vasculitis that is pathologically characterized by an inflammation in the upper and lower respiratory tracts and kidneys. Here we present an interesting case of GPA that was initially worked up as a malignancy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 45-year-old man with no significant medical history presented to the Emergency room for a non productive cough of 1 week duration. He also reported generalized fatigue and myalgias that had been going on for a few months. He had no fever, chills, chest pain or shortness of breath. He did not smoke tobacco and worked as a furniture cleaner. Vitals and physical examination were unremarkable including clear lung fields on auscultation. Investigations obtained were significant for a leukocytosis of 14k/ul and chest x-ray had bilateral prominent perihilar pulmonary vascular markings. This was followed by a CT scan of chest, abdomen and pelvis that showed innumerable bilateral pulmonary nodules with hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy, most consistent with pulmonary and nodal metastases, CT abdomen was unremarkable. The site of primary malignancy was yet to be determined. Additional investigations obtained including blood culture, sputum culture, sputum acid fast bacilli (AFB), sputum fungal culture, sputum Mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR, urine histoplasma, urine blastomyces antigen were all negative. However, ESR and CRP were elevated at 50mm/hr and 14.27mg/dl respectively, raising concern for a possible vasculitic process. ANA was obtained which was positive along with PR3 (ANCA). Prednisone was initiated and he reported significant improvement in his symptoms. He underwent Video Assisted Thoracic Surgery (VATS) for lung tissue biopsy which demonstrated necrotizing granulomatous inflammation consistent with vasculitis. He was followed up in pulmonary and rheumatology clinics and started on rituximab. DISCUSSION: GPA is an autoimmune condition that is triggered by environmental factors in genetically susceptible patients and is characterized by necrotising granulomatous lesions of the respiratory tract, vasculitis and glomerulonephritis. It can affect the upper respiratory tract, lung and kidneys. The upper respiratory tract; usually the nose and paranasal sinuses are involved in 70–100% of cases of GPA, and the first clinical manifestations are usually classic otorhinolaryngologic symptoms [1]. Lung is involved with necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and tracheobronchial inflammation being defining features [2]. Our case was atypical as the upper respiratory tract was not involved. Diagnosis involves clinical assessment, serological tests for anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA) and tissue diagnosis. The 10-year survival rate is estimated to be 40% when the kidneys are involved and 60–70% when there is no kidney involvement [1]. Immunosuppression and adjuvant therapies improve the prognosis [3]. CONCLUSIONS: High degree of suspicion and early initiation of treatment early is of the essence to improve the prognosis in GPA. This case highlights the importance of considering GPA as a differential in cases with pulmonary nodules even without upper respiratory involvement. REFERENCE #1: Greco A, Marinelli C, Fusconi M, Macri GF, Gallo A, De Virgilio A, Zambetti G, de Vincentiis M. Clinic manifestations in granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Int J Immunopathol Pharmacol. 2016 Jun;29(2):151-9. doi: 10.1177/0394632015617063. Epub 2015 Dec 18. PMID: 26684637; PMCID: PMC5806708. REFERENCE #2: Sacoto G, Boukhlal S, Specks U, Flores-Suárez LF, Cornec D. Lung involvement in ANCA-associated vasculitis. Presse Med. 2020 Oct;49(3):104039. doi: 10.1016/j.lpm.2020.104039. Epub 2020 Jul 7. PMID: 32650042. REFERENCE #3: Trandafir CM, Balica NC, Horhat DI, Mot IC, Sarau CA, Poenaru M. Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)-A Multidisciplinary Approach of a Case Report. Medicina (Kaunas). 2022 Dec 13;58(12):1837. doi: 10.3390/medicina58121837. PMID: 36557039; PMCID: PMC9785195. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Maria Cristina Cuartas-Mesa No disclosure on file for Vaishali Deenadayalan No relevant relationships by Swati Dhungel
