N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Involvement of Systemic Disease II SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: STAT3 hyper-IgE syndrome (HIES) is a primary immunodeficiency syndrome characterized by elevated serum IgE, eczema, and recurrent infections of the skin and respiratory tract. Recurrent pneumonias damage lung tissue and ultimately leads to the formation of abscesses, empyemas, pneumatoceles and, subsequently, chronic lung disease with bronchiectasis. Pulmonary complications are a major contributor for the high morbidity and mortality rates in patients with STAT3-HIES. CASE PRESENTATION: SL is a 12-year-old male with a past medical history of unrepaired atrial septum defect, congenital pulmonic valve stenosis, mild persistent asthma, bilateral pneumatoceles status-post wedge resection, and recurrent skin abscess due to Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) who was admitted for respiratory distress. He had been admitted one month prior with an asthma exacerbation and chest x-ray concerning for pneumonia. Since discharge, he continued to have productive cough and intermittent fevers despite antibiotic treatment. He also had decreased oral intake with a weight loss of 3 kg in 2.5 months.In terms of his medical history, he was hospitalized at four weeks old with seborrheic dermatitis and superinfection with MRSA bacteremia. Since then, he was hospitalized multiple times for recurrent scalp abscesses and chalazions requiring intravenous antibiotics and incision and drainage. Immune workup at that time was normal, except for elevated IgE thought to be due to allergic in nature. At four years old, he was hospitalized for bilateral necrotizing pneumonia, complicated by bilateral pneumatoceles. Subsequently, he required resection of both left lower and right lower lobe pneumatoceles. During this admission, he had a CT scan which showed bronchopneumonia and mild bronchiectasis in the right middle lobe, lingula, and bilateral lower lobes. Bronchoscopy with alveolar lavage was performed, which showed diffusely edematous mucosa and thick secretions noted in the right lower lobe and lingula. His lavage culture resulted with isolated MRSA growth. Given these findings, along with his history, immunology was consulted, and he was diagnosed with STAT3-HIES. He was discharged with chest vest therapy and pulmonary follow up, in addition to antibiotic treatment. DISCUSSION: Despite the severity of pulmonary disease in patients with STAT3-HIES, there are very few studies on therapeutic management. One study followed fourteen STAT3-HIES patients for radiologic imaging, microbiology, and therapy assessments overtime. CXR and CT assessments revealed severe lung parenchyma and airway destruction in eleven patients (78%). Throat swabs were positive for S. aureus, H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, as well as Pseudomonas aeruginosa in more than half the patients. Fungal infections are also reported, with rare deaths related to eight Aspergillus fumigatus-associated complications. Therapies including continuous antibiotic therapy, inhalation with hypertonic or isotonic sodium chloride, rhDNase, and chest physiotherapy were also assessed, with varying results. CONCLUSIONS: Patients with STAT3-HIES can present in a variety of ways; therefore, it is crucial to consider immunodeficiency syndromes in any patient that presents with recurrent respiratory infections, particularly those complicated by empyemas or pneumatoceles. The degree of pulmonary involvement in this disease is significant, and studies have shown it is important to monitor respiratory symptoms to detect pulmonary infections before permanent lung damage occurs. REFERENCE #1: Kröner, Carolin, et al. "Lung disease in stat3 hyper-ige syndrome requires intense therapy." Allergy, vol. 74, no. 9, 4 Apr. 2019, pp. 1691–1702, https://doi.org/10.1111/all.13753. REFERENCE #2: Gilje, Elizabeth A., and Jordan K. Abbott. "The pulmonary effects of STAT3 deficiency." Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology, vol. 152, no. 2, Aug. 2023, pp. 368–370, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2023.06.003. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Lusine Unanyan
