N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (R) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Pulmonic valve endocarditis is extremely rare, accounting for only 2% of all cases of endocarditis (1). Typically this type of endocarditis is associated most directly with intravenous drug users, or persons who have central lines, or other devices. Although rare, right sided endocarditis, along with endocarditis in general, is growing and prevalence, most likely attributed to greater interventions and permanent medical devices (2). Endocarditis with no known precipitating factors is still rare, and is worthy of consideration. This case is about a 56-year-old male who presented with a viral-like illness, subsequently being diagnosed with isolated methicillin sensitive Staph Aureus endocarditis and concern for septic emboli with no known risk factors. CASE PRESENTATION: A 56-year-old male with no significant past medical history presented initially after four days of upper respiratory symptoms including cough, shortness of breath, nausea and vomiting. Initial CT chest scans were concerning for pneumonia with nodular consolidative opacities in the bilateral upper lobes, lingula, and right lower lobe. He was febrile, leukocytosis of 14,230, and hyponatremic upon arrival, so he was treated with a course of IV rocephin, azithromycin and fluids. Blood cultures subsequently were positive for methicillin sensitive staph aureus. He was switched to cefazolin, and a TTE was obtained. Initially, imaging showed possible vegetation on the tricuspid valve with mild to moderate tricuspid regurgitation and the patient underwent a TEE for better visualization. TEE revealed a 1.7 cm x 1.1 cm vegetation on the pulmonic valve. Urine drug screen was negative and patient denied any past IV drug use. Cardiothoracic surgery was consulted due to difficulty in clearing blood cultures, and ultimately they decided to not remove the vegetation, given the size. Blood cultures cleared prior to discharge, he was placed with a PICC line, and sent home with a 28 day course of cefazolin. DISCUSSION: This case highlights the importance of imaging and identification of infectious sources, particularly in cases where chest CT may be unclear. In this case, there was concern about source, and whether or not pulmonary chest CT findings might be remarkable for the originating source of infection, or were in fact septic emboli. It is also an important case due to the lack of identifiable risk factors, and the importance of echocardiogram follow up when blood cultures are positive for MSSA. CONCLUSIONS: As increased prevalence of right-sided endocarditis continues to grow, and lack of case reports about pulmonic valve endocarditis remain, it is important to study cases with no known risk factors to better understand clinical course. The patient did fare well given early identification and subsequent treatment with the proper antibiotics. REFERENCE #1: Chan P, Ogilby JD, Segal B. Tricuspid valve endocarditis. Am Heart J. 1989 May;117(5):1140-6. doi: 10.1016/0002-8703(89)90874-0. PMID: 2653011. REFERENCE #2: Shmueli, H., Thomas, F., Flint, N., Setia, G., Janjic, A., & Siegel, R. J. (2020a). Right-sided infective endocarditis 2020: Challenges and updates in diagnosis and treatment. Journal of the American Heart Association, 9(15). https://doi.org/10.1161/jaha.120.017293 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kate Minke
