N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (S) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Antibiotic resistance among Streptococcus pneumoniae species is well described in the literature. Resistance to 3 or more antibiotic classes qualifies as multidrug resistance (MDR). Reports of MDR strains have been rising globally (1).Extensive use of broad-spectrum antibiotics and evolution of pneumococcal vaccines led to growing concern over resistance to known effective antibiotics like cephalosporins, quinolones, and carbapenems. We wish to report a case of invasive streptococcal pneumonia, caused by an unusual MDR strain in an elderly male. CASE PRESENTATION: A 72-year-old male with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and alcohol use presented with a worsening productive cough and shortness of breath. The patient was febrile, tachycardic, and hypotensive. He received intravenous fluids and empiric antibiotics in the emergency room. However, his condition deteriorated, leading to increasing obtundation and hypoxia, necessitating intubation and admission to the intensive care unit. Workup revealed leukocytosis and elevated procalcitonin. Imaging showed extensive consolidation involving the right lower lung. The urine antigen test for streptococcus pneumoniae was positive. The patient developed shock, requiring vasopressors and corticosteroids.Blood and sputum cultures returned positive for Streptococcus pneumoniae. Antimicrobial sensitivities revealed resistance to penicillin, ceftriaxone, and meropenem with a minimum inhibitory complex (MIC of 2, 1, >0.5), respectively. Based on sensitivities, antibiotics were adjusted to vancomycin and cefepime. He was extubated on day 4. The patient improved and left the hospital. Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV-20) was provided before discharge. DISCUSSION: Here we present a case of MDR streptococcus pneumoniae associated with invasive disease. While this patient had no history of MDRs, he had multiple predisposing factors including alcohol abuse, nursing home stay, and recent antibiotic use. Upon analyzing the resistance pattern, meropenem resistance was unique. Carbapenems belong to the beta-lactam antibiotics, and their resistance is mediated by alteration of penicillin-binding proteins, mostly found in penicillin-resistant strains (2). While fairly reported in Asia, with 6.1% of streptococcus pneumoniae isolates detected in 2013 being resistant to meropenem in one Japanese study, rates of resistance in the United States are unclear, and vary by geographic distribution.We used the whole genome sequencing to identify the serotype, which uncovered streptococcus pneumoniae serotype 35B. This isotype was historically commensal, however after the introduction of the PCV-13, and PCV-20, it became a major cause of invasive pneumococcal disease. None of the current pneumococcal vaccines include this serotype. Clonal expansion and serotype switching are two distinctive features that make it refractory to vaccine protection. It is expected to continue to be clinically relevant in the future. CONCLUSIONS: Invasive disease occurs in 25% of patients and is associated with higher mortality. While the prevalence of MDR strains varies significantly, early identification of high-risk patients with careful selection of antibiotic therapy is paramount. Available literature suggests the emergence of serotype 35B as a virulent strain despite vaccine expansion, owing to its unique characteristics. This distinctive feature is highlighted in this case. Further research on this serotype is imperative to develop strategies for mitigating severe invasive disease. REFERENCE #1: 1. Magiorakos AP, Srinivasan A, Carey RB, et al. Multidrug-resistant, extensively drug-resistant and pandrug-resistant bacteria: an international expert proposal for interim standard definitions for acquired resistance. Clin Microbiol Infect. 2012;18(3):268-281. doi:10.1111/j.1469-0691.2011.03570.x REFERENCE #2: 2. Doi A, Iwata K, Takegawa H, Miki K, Sono Y, Nishioka H, Takeshita J, Tomii K, Haruta T. Community-acquired pneumonia caused by carbapenem-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae: re-examining its prevention and treatment. Int J Gen Med. 2014 May 21;7:253-7. doi: 10.2147/IJGM.S63744. PMID: 24899822; PMCID: PMC4038523. REFERENCE #3: 3. Kim GR, Kim EY, Kim SH, Lee HK, Lee J, Shin JH, Kim YR, Song SA, Jeong J, Uh Y, Kim YK, Yong D, Kim HS, Kim S, Kim YA, Shin KS, Jeong SH, Ryoo N, Shin JH. Serotype Distribution and Antimicrobial Resistance of Streptococcus pneumoniae Causing Invasive Pneumococcal Disease in Korea Between 2017 and 2019 After Introduction of the 13-Valent Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine. Ann Lab Med. 2023 Jan 1;43(1):45-54. doi: 10.3343/alm.2023.43.1.45. Epub 2022 Sep 1. PMID: 36045056; PMCID: PMC9467834. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Glenn Fort No relevant relationships by Hossameldeen Mustafa No relevant relationships by Anvitha Rangan
