N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (AC) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Rasmussen's aneurysm is a rare but potentially fatal complication of pulmonary tuberculosis characterized by a focal dilation of a branch of the pulmonary artery due to arterial wall erosion secondary to chronic inflammation within a tubercular cavity. Although the incidence is about 5% in patients with cavitary pulmonary tuberculosis, it poses a significant risk of massive hemoptysis and can be life-threatening. Here, we present a case of Rassmussen's aneurysm presenting with hemoptysis. CASE PRESENTATION: An 18-year-old male, recently immigrated from Haiti without significant past medical history presented with a 10-day history of cough and recent onset of hemoptysis. Other symptoms were fever, chills, and intermittent night sweats for the past 20 days. He does not recall any prior vaccination history or recent screening for tuberculosis He lives with his family without obvious sick contacts. His vital signs were stable at presentation and his physical exam was unremarkable without palpable cervical lymphadenopathy. The laboratory tests showed microcytic anemia with hemoglobin 12.4 g/dl, and MCV 70 fl, otherwise, the rest of the blood tests including serum chemistry were within normal limits. AFB culture and mycobacterium tuberculosis DNA PCR were sent. CT of the chest without contrast showed right apical and upper lobe cavitary lesions with thick cystic walls, measuring up to 4.4 x 4.6 cm with multiple tree-in-bud opacity in bilateral upper lobes with differentials, including infectious processes (mycobacterium disease, invasive fungal infections), autoimmune diseases, and malignancy. Considering his degree of hemoptysis (175 ml), a CT angiography chest was ordered. It identified 4 mm enhancing density consistent with Rasmussen's aneurysm adjacent to the medial wall of the main cavitary lesion. The patient was transferred to a tertiary care center where he had angiography of the right pulmonary artery revealing no pseudoaneurysm. Angiography of the right bronchial artery revealed a pseudoaneurysm which was embolized, in addition to the embolization of the right intercostal artery. The quantiferon test was positive initially, subsequently, AFB culture was noted positive. The patient was started on RIPE therapy (rifamycin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) and was discharged home with follow-up. DISCUSSION: Tuberculosis can lead to various complications, including hemoptysis, pneumothorax, bronchiectasis, extensive pulmonary destruction, fistula formation, and malignancy. Aspergillosis can also develop in the cavitary lesions from pulmonary tuberculosis [1]. Massive hemoptysis commonly occurs from the erosion of pulmonary, bronchial, or intercostal arteries. While multidetector row CT angiography is important for early localization of the bleeding source, arterial trans-catheter embolization prevents fatal outcomes associated with this rare complication [2]. This case underscores the importance of angiographic imaging in patients with tuberculosis presenting with massive hemoptysis. CONCLUSIONS: Rasmussen's aneurysm, though rare, is important to recognize in a patient with pulmonary tuberculosis presenting with massive hemoptysis. Early identification is critical for timely intervention for embolization to prevent ongoing hemoptysis. REFERENCE #1: Shebani A, Hnish M, Elmelliti H, Lutfe Abdussalam A. Massive Hemoptysis in Pulmonary Tuberculosis From Rasmussen Pseudoaneurysm. Cureus. 2022;14(10):e30117. Published 2022 Oct 10. doi:10.7759/cureus.30117 REFERENCE #2: Neelakantan S, Anandarajan R, Swamy AK. Rare cause of massive haemoptysis in pulmonary tuberculosis: Rasmussen's aneurysm. BMJ Case Rep. 2016;2016:bcr2016216652. Published 2016 Oct 21. doi:10.1136/bcr-2016-216652 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Asude Hasanoglu No relevant relationships by Marina Khan No relevant relationships by Kamran Manzoor No relevant relationships by Muhammad Usama Zafar Mirza No relevant relationships by M. Talha Ugurlu
