N2  - SESSION TITLE: Diffuse Lung Disease Case Reports Posters (N) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia is characterized by bronchial and peripheral eosinophilia, lung infiltrates, and progressive respiratory symptoms lasting over a month. Known causes include parasites, drugs, and autoimmune conditions. Idiopathic chronic eosinophilic pneumonia (ICEP) is most often seen in middle-aged women and takes months to diagnose. ICEP presents with a range of parenchymal abnormalities on imaging. Prolonged treatment with steroids yields dramatic improvement. Here, we present a case of chronic eosinophilic pneumonia in a daily marijuana smoker. CASE PRESENTATION: A 51-year-old obese Hispanic woman with depression presented with progressive dyspnea. She had no history of lung conditions, occupational exposures, or recent travel, smoked one tobacco cigarette and marijuana daily. Pulmonary function testing showed severe obstruction, and she was prescribed triple therapy. Despite maximal therapy for months, symptoms worsened. Chest x-ray (image 1) showed diffuse parenchymal disease. Computed tomography of her chest showed diffuse tree-in-bud opacities (image 2). She had peripheral eosinophilia. Bronchoalveolar lavage contained 54% eosinophils. Bacterial, viral, fungal, mycobacterial, autoimmune, and hypersensitivity studies were negative. Histopathology did not reveal malignant cells. The patient was started on 1 mg/kg of ideal body weight of prednisone for chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. Chest x-ray (image 3) obtained five days later showed remarkable improvement. She refused to stop smoking marijuana. She remained on the same dose of prednisone for three months, after which it was very slowly tapered. Once the dose was dropped below 20 mg/day, she had an exacerbation which improved once steroids were increased. DISCUSSION: Eosinophilic pneumonia presents with respiratory symptoms, pulmonary opacities on imaging, and eosinophilia (1). Known causes include drugs, parasites, and autoimmune conditions. Idiopathic cases are differentiated based on duration and severity of symptoms. ICEP is characterized by at least 2-4 weeks of dyspnea, cough, and chest pain (1). It predominates in women in their fourth decade (1, 2, 3). Bronchoalveolar lavage with at least 40% eosinophils and blood eosinophilia prior to starting steroid therapy aids in diagnosis. ICEP's characteristic chest x-ray finding of "photographic negative pulmonary edema" is found in 25% of patients (3), but bilateral peripheral alveolar opacities are almost always present (2, 3). On lung function testing, there is an even representation of obstructive and restrictive ventilatory defects (3). Treatment with corticosteroids is fundamental, and 80% of patients have obvious improvement on imaging within 2 weeks (2). A long taper of steroids (6-12 months) seems necessary in these patients, and more than half of patients relapse when decreasing daily dose below 10-15 mg (2). Fortunately, relapses also respond dramatically to increased steroid (2). CONCLUSIONS: The time frame, lab work up, rapid response to treatment, and relapse are consistent with ICEP. The patient's list of home medications were cross-checked on The Drug-Induced Respiratory Disease database and not found to have any causative association. This work up excluded infection, autoimmune conditions, and prescription drug exposure, which begs further questioning of marijuana in the etiology of this patient's respiratory disease. At this time, there are a handful of acute eosinophilic pneumonias associated with marijuana use, but no chronic cases with the same association. REFERENCE #1: Cottin V, Cordier JF. Eosinophilic pneumonia. Orphan Lung Diseases: A Clinical Guide to Rare Lung Disease. 2014:227-251. REFERENCE #2: Marchand E, Reynaud-Gaubert M, Lauque D, Durieu J, Tonnel AB, Cordier JF. Idiopathic chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. A clinical and follow-up study of 62 cases. The Groupe d'Etudes et de Recherche sur les Maladies "Orphelines" Pulmonaires. Medicine (Baltimore). 1998. 77(5):299-312. REFERENCE #3: Jederlinic PJ, Sicilian L, Gaensler EA. Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. A report of 19 cases and a review of the literature. Medicine (Baltimore). 1988. 67(3):154-162. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kenneth Iwuji No relevant relationships by Joscilin Mathew No relevant relationships by Divya Vangipuram Wyatt
