N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (O) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Nocardiosis is a rare, opportunistic infection with annual incidence of 500-1000 cases in the United States. Caused by a gram-positive, partially acid-fast, catalase-positive, filamentous, rod-shaped bacteria, Nocardia typically affects lungs, brain, joints, and skin via hematogenous dissemination. T-cell immunodeficient hosts are at an increased risk for multisystem Nocardia infection. Pulmonary nocardiosis can present as acute necrotizing pneumonia with cavitation, or as an indolent, gradually enlarging pulmonary nodule with empyema. We describe a case of rapidly progressive pulmonary Nocardiosis with cavitating pulmonary nodules in an immunocompetent host. CASE PRESENTATION: A 47-year-old male with history of asthma and smoking (10 pack-years) presented to the emergency room (ER) with 1 month history of fever, productive cough, dyspnea, malaise, night sweats, and left elbow pain. The patient was never incarcerated, worked in healthcare, nor use drugs; he had moved from Indiana to Arizona a few years ago and works in dusty areas. He was febrile on presentation, tachycardic, and tachypneic with bilateral wheezing. Initial workup was remarkable for elevated procalcitonin, ESR, CRP and D-dimer. Chest imaging showed multiple bilateral speculated upper lobe nodules with central cavitation. Of note, he previously presented to ER twice within one month and was prescribed antibiotics with inhaled and systemic corticosteroids. The patient was now admitted for interval worsening of symptoms and imaging findings of markedly increased number and size of nodules within 2 weeks (Figure 1), associated with 20-pound weight loss. Further serologic evaluation was negative for infectious, autoimmune, and vasculitis differentials. Echocardiogram was unremarkable, and UDS screen was negative. He was empirically treated with azithromycin and fluconazole. The patient underwent diagnostic bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsies of the right upper lobe. He endured post-procedural bronchospasm and required bilevel positive airway pressure ventilation for 2 days. Eventually, BAL grew positive for Nocardia species assay, and TMP-SMX therapy was started. The patient was weaned off oxygen and discharged home. On follow-up, he improved on antibiotic therapy. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary nocardiosis in an immunocompetent individual is extremely rare, but still possible1. Given our geographic location in southwestern US and the patient's aerosolized exposure to soil, coccidioides was high on our differential. However, our differential broadly included pulmonary abscess, septic emboli, atypical infection, aspergillosis, inflammatory disease states (i.e., sarcoidosis, Churg-Strauss syndrome) and less likely malignancy. Distinguishing subacute pulmonary nocardiosis from other lower respiratory tract infections can be challenging due to common presentations and diagnostic delays are prevalent. Additionally, Nocardia species are partially acid-fast on account of the mycolic acid present in the cell wall and typically grow slowly, resulting in the multiple negative sputum cultures in the initial search as opposed to using the more highly sensitive PCR assay. CONCLUSIONS: Having a high index of suspicion in cases with refractory pulmonary cavitary lesions is paramount, and neuroimaging to rule out metastatic disease is warranted. Choosing the correct treatment is vital even in the setting of an immunocompetent host. REFERENCE #1: Fujita T, Ikari J, Watanabe A, Tatsumi K. Clinical characteristics of pulmonary nocardiosis in immunocompetent patients. J Infect Chemother. 2016;22(11):738-743. doi:10.1016/j.jiac.2016.08.004 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Christine Firth No disclosure on file for Heather Ledterman No relevant relationships by Robert Lee No relevant relationships by Deepika Razia No relevant relationships by Alexander Sipe
