N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Case Reports Posters (G) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: With the introduction of antiretroviral therapy (ART), there has been an increase in non-AIDS- defining malignancies (NADMs) [1-3]. Among NADMs found in people living with human immunodeficient virus (PLHIV), lung cancer is among the most prevalent [1,3-5]. A recent population-based registry showed adenocarcinoma was the most common histological subtype prevalent in the United States 6. Common radiological manifestations in lung adenocarcinoma range from a spectrum of solid to part-solid nodules and ground glass opacities (GGO) [7,8]. We present a case of progressive lung fibrosis later found to be lung adenocarcinoma in a HIV patient. CASE PRESENTATION: 73-year-old male with history of HIV (on ART), hypertension, prostate acinar adenocarcinoma status post radical prostatectomy, remote alcohol use disorder and prior smoking history (quit in 1990). Patient initially presented for chronic cough since a year. CXR revealed multiple cavitary lesions. Chest CT revealed multiple cavitary lesions, bilateral consolidations, bronchiectasis, tree in bud appearance in bilateral lung fields and lung fibrosis. Underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage and Endobronchial Ultrasound-guided biopsy that was negative for malignancy and more suggestive of acute infection, however extensive work up was negative for bacteria, fungi, and AFB. Cough did not respond to trials of PPI, antihistamines, albuterol inhalers or discontinuing lisinopril. Repeat bronchoscopy performed was negative except for fibrosis noted on pathology. Empirically started on steroids and Bactrim for pneumocystis jiroveci prophylaxis. Later, high resolution computed tomography (HRCT) performed showed worsening lung fibrosis and interstitial disease, increase in size of lung consolidations bilaterally and few new pulmonary nodules. Around this time, he sought another opinion. PET scan obtained elsewhere showed increased metabolic activity in multiple areas in the lung. Robotic ion bronchoscopy with utilization of cryotherapy confirmed lung adenocarcinoma. Currently, patient is being treated for stage IV lung adenocarcinoma with palliative chemotherapy. However, he developed pneumonitis after first round of chemotherapy and immunotherapy (Keytruda) was discontinued. Unfortunately, surveillance CT scan continue to show worsening lung consolidations and fibrosis despite therapy. Repeat bronchoscopy was performed to rule out worsening malignancy versus infection versus inflammation. Biopsies of consolidations only showed persistent adenocarcinoma. DISCUSSION: This case report emphasizes two key aspects. First, rarely, adenocarcinoma can masquerade as lung fibrosis [9]. Concomitant pulmonary fibrosis and lung cancer is a difficult entity to both diagnose and manage. Several studies have shown the risk of pneumonitis is higher with systemic chemotherapy and immunotherapy in interstitial lung disease [10-13], as is evidenced in our case as well. Second, the risk of lung cancer is higher among HIV patients compared to the general population [6]. Lung cancer among PLHIV tends to present at a younger age, is at an advanced stage and carries a worse prognosis given its aggressive progression [1,6,14,15]. Current lung cancer screening guidelines do not address PLHIV. It would be beneficial to address this in the future given the surge in lung cancers in this cohort. CONCLUSIONS: We aim to highlight the increase of lung cancers in PLHIV and bring attention to an uncommon presentation of adenocarcinoma that precluded early detection. REFERENCE #1: 1. Berman R, van Blydenstein A, Graham A. A comparison of lung cancer in HIV-positive and HIV-negative populations. Afr J Thorac Crit Care Med. 2022;28(2). 2. Shiels MS, Engels EA. Evolving epidemiology of HIV-associated malignancies. Current Opinion in HIV and AIDS. 2017;12(1):6-11. 3. Shiels MS, Pfeiffer RM, Gail MH, et al. Cancer Burden in the HIV-Infected Population in the United States. JNCI: Journal of the National Cancer Institute. 2011;103(9):753-762. 4. Winstone TA, Man SFP, Hull M, Montaner JS, Sin DD. Epidemic of Lung Cancer in Patients With HIV Infection. Chest. 2013;143(2):305-314. 5. Januszewski AP, Bower M. Lung cancer in people living with HIV. Lancet HIV. 2022;9(10):e670-e672. 6. Haas CB, Engels EA, Horner MJ, et al. Trends and risk of lung cancer among people living with HIV in the USA: a population-based registry linkage study. Lancet HIV. 2022;9(10):e700-e708. 7. Kuriyama K, Yanagawa M. CT Diagnosis of Lung Adenocarcinoma: Radiologic-Pathologic Correlation and Growth Rate. Radiology. 2020;297(1):199-200. 8. Cohen JG, Reymond E, Jankowski A, et al. Lung adenocarcinomas: correlation of computed tomography and pathology findings. Diagnostic and Interventional Imaging. 2016;97(10):955-963. 9. Mehic B, Duranovic Rayan L, Bilalovic N, Dohranovic Tafro D, Pilav I. Lung adenocarcinoma mimicking pulmonary fibrosis-a case report. BMC Cancer. 2016;16(1):729. 10. Frank AJ, Dagogo-Jack I, Dobre IA, et al. Management of Lung Cancer in the Patient with Interstitial Lung Disease. Oncologist. 2023;28(1):12-22. 11. Kenmotsu H, Yoh K, Mori K, et al. Phase II study of nab-paclitaxel + carboplatin for patients with non-small-cell lung cancer and interstitial lung disease. Cancer Science. 2019;110(12):3738-3745. 12. Asahina H, Oizumi S, Takamura K, et al. A prospective phase II study of carboplatin and nab-paclitaxel in patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer and concomitant interstitial lung disease (HOT1302). Lung Cancer. 2019;138:65-71. 13. Kanai O, Kim YH, Demura Y, et al. Efficacy and safety of nivolumab in non-small cell lung cancer with preexisting interstitial lung disease. Thoracic Cancer. 2018;9(7):847-855. 14. Bhikoo R, Allwood BW, Irusen EM, Koegelenberg CFN. Lung Cancer Presents at a Younger Age and Is Less Likely to be Curable in People Living with HIV. Respiration. 2024;103(1):47-50. 15. Baluku JB, Bogere N, Namiiro S, et al. HIV-related lung cancer in Uganda: a cohort study. Infect Agent Cancer. 2022;17(1):24. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kenneth Iwuji No relevant relationships by Joscilin Mathew No relevant relationships by Divya Vangipuram Wyatt No relevant relationships by Pitchaporn Yingchoncharoen
