N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (P) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a short rod-shaped bacterium with no cell wall, not visible on gram stains, and not routinely cultured due to its special culture media requirement. This unique bacteria has been flying under the radar due to most cases being asymptomatic and/or atypical signs and symptoms; chest auscultations can even present as normal. Associated symptoms may include headaches, nagging cough, and wheezing. A hallmark feature is absence of a productive cough. Common radiologic findings include reticulo-nodular pattern or patchy areas of consolidation; it is rare that Mycoplasma pneumonia presents as a solitary lung nodule. We are reporting a case of a patient with right lower lobe solitary lung nodule, atypical symptoms, and discordant radiographic images of Mycoplasma pneumonia mimicking bronchogenic carcinoma. CASE PRESENTATION: A 65 year old male with history of smoking a pack of cigarettes a day for seven years presented with symptoms of upper respiratory tract infection consistent with fever, congestion, right sided chest pain, weight loss, and eventual nonproductive coughing. Both the chest X-ray and chest computerized tomography (CT) scan was suggestive of a right lower lobe pneumonia. He was started on a course of levofloxacin with no improvement. A repeat chest x-ray two weeks later revealed increasing size of the opacity. He was prescribed amoxicillin-clavulanic acid. Chest CT scan five days later showed worsening right-sided pleurisy with increasing size of the lesion. The patient underwent a transthoracic needle aspiration biopsy, which was negative for malignancy, but an elevated Mycoplasma IgG level. The Nodify® test evaluation of possible bronchogenic carcinoma was also negative for tumor related autoantibodies; as such bronchoscopy was not performed. He was prescribed a high dose course of azithromycin for 10 days which led to an eventual full recovery. DISCUSSION: Solitary lung nodules are an uncommon presentation of Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Disease prevalence for this bacterium is scarce due to unreliable rapid diagnostic techniques. This typically innocuous pathogen is of increasing concern due to its adept ability to disguise as other pulmonary diseases, including that of bronchogenic carcinoma. Our patient also had the unusual symptom of pleuritic chest pain, not commonly associated with Mycoplasma pneumonia. The new Nodify CDT® and Nodify XL2® are proteomic biomarker blood tests that potentially identify patients with malignant lung nodules by measuring autoantibodies to tumor-associated antigens (PS3, CAGE, NY-ESO-1, GBU4-5, MAGE A4, SOX2, and HuD). In our case, it obviated the need for the invasive bronchoscopy. Once diagnosed, treatment of Mycoplasma pneumoniae includes azithromycin, macrolides, doxycycline, or fluoroquinolones. CONCLUSIONS: We are reporting a novel case of Mycoplasma pneumoniae mimicking as a bronchogenic carcinoma exhibiting discordant radiographic images and the rare symptom of pleurisy. The new Nodify® proteomic biomarker blood test contributed to the workup and averted the need for unnecessary invasive procedures. REFERENCE #1: FISCHER, ELIZABETH, and TAKKIN LO. "A RARE and UNUSUAL CASE of CAVITARY MYCOPLASMA PNEUMONIA: A NEW ENIGMA for the ELDERLY." Chest, vol. 164, no. 4, 1 Oct. 2023, pp. A1142–A1142, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chest.2023.07.826. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Takkin Lo No relevant relationships by Jaylene Lum
