N2  - SESSION TITLE: Infections Mimicking Cancer SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Curvularia is a type of fungus that inhabits soil and plants, with over 80 species known to exist. Curvularia lunata is the most commonly reported species in clinical cases, with lung infections being the most frequently observed. Cases of keratitis, brain abscess, and endocarditis have also been reported. In this particular case, a middle-aged female patient presented with a cough, and initial suspicion was of nontuberculous mycobacterium (NTM). However, further examination via bronchoscopy revealed an endobronchial lesion that raised concerns for malignancy. Cultures revealed that the patient had a Curvularia infection, and malignancy was ruled out. The patient showed noticeable improvement after treatment with Posaconazole. CASE PRESENTATION: A 55-year-old female nonsmoker was referred to pulmonary clinic by her infectious disease physician. She had history of mild intermittent asthma but had developed a persistent cough for 12 months with an evaluation by an allergist and ENT and treatment with nasal sprays proving ineffective. As her cough progressively worsened, a chest CT showed multifocal reticulonodular infiltrates with concern for nontuberculous mycobacterium. Treatment with levofloxacin did not resolve her symptoms. A comprehensive work up including IgG levels, alpha-1-antitrypsin, CF screen and aspergillus IgE was nondiagnostic. She was otherwise healthy and only had secondhand tobacco exposure as a child. There was no exposure to chemical fumes, asbestos, or radon. Physical examination was unremarkable. And given persistence of the cough affecting her quality of life, she was referred for a bronchoscopy for improved sampling. Prior respiratory cultures had been negative.On bronchoscopy, she was noted to have bronchiectasis, and narrowing of the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Given this lesion appeared malignant in nature, brushings were obtained. Endobronchial biopsies were performed with right middle lobe BAL and washings. EBUS assessment of the lymph nodes showed enlarged station 7, station 10R and station 11Ri nodes, which were sampled. Pathology was negative for malignancy. Biopsy of the endobronchial mass showed benign bronchial mucosa, with acute and chronic inflammation & necroinflammatory debris. The brushings were negative for mycobacterial organisms on acid fast stain. GMS stain of the brushings & mixed wash showed scattered fungal hyphae with acute angle branching & septation.Fungal cultures on multiple samples from washings and cytology grew only Curvularia species. She was started on 6 weeks of Posaconazole. A repeat bronchoscopy was performed with demonstrable improvement in her endobronchial lesion. DISCUSSION: While Curvularia rarely causes infection in an immunocompetent host, these ubiquitous fungi can be a source for significant bronchopulmonary infections with a variable presentation that ranges from sinusitis to allergic bronchopulmonary disease and in this case an endobronchial mass concerning for malignancy. Given the rarity and lack of serologic and antigenic testing, culture remains the primary means to a diagnosis. Treatment while not well established, is primarily an azole antifungal guided by sensitivities. Duration of therapy is guided by clinical improvement and symptom resolution. Follow up bronchoscopy is helpful to document the same. CONCLUSIONS: Infective mimics such as Curvularia should be considered when assessing for malignancy in relatively healthy younger nonsmokers with endobronchial lesion. REFERENCE #1: Rinaldi MG, Phillips P, Schwartz JG, Winn RE, Holt GR, Shagets FW, Elrod J, Nishioka G, Aufdemorte TB. Human Curvularia infections. Report of five cases and review of the literature. Diagn Microbiol Infect Dis. 1987 Jan;6(1):27-39. doi: 10.1016/0732-8893(87)90111-8. PMID: 3802744. REFERENCE #2: Madrid H, da Cunha KC, Gené J, Dijksterhuis J, Cano J, Sutton DA, Guarro J, Crous PW. Novel Curvularia species from clinical specimens. Persoonia. 2014 Dec;33:48-60. doi: 10.3767/003158514X683538. Epub 2014 Jul 29. PMID: 25737593; PMCID: PMC4312937. REFERENCE #3: Daoud N, Gulati N. Curvularia lung infection mimics malignancy. Heliyon. 2023 Apr 25;9(5):e15773. doi: 10.1016/j.heliyon.2023.e15773. PMID: 37215835; PMCID: PMC10192402. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by John Allonce No relevant relationships by Kshitij Mehrotra No disclosure on file for Vinay Patel No relevant relationships by Keegan Plowman No relevant relationships by Branden Wilson
