N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (AI) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Tuberculosis (TB) is the second leading cause of infectious mortality worldwide [1]. Despite global initiatives to address this public health crisis, it remains widely prevalent. Although tuberculosis is most associated with its pulmonary form, extrapulmonary manifestations are a growing concern and gaining recognition. Here, we describe a case of peritoneal tuberculosis in a cirrhotic patient, which we diagnosed after having high index of suspicion based on atypical presentation of peritonitis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 54-year-old male with a history of hypertension, diabetes and alcohol-induced cirrhosis presented to the hospital with complaints of abdominal distension and dyspnea on exertion. He denied infectious symptoms such as fevers, chills, or cough; his last alcoholic drink was one week ago. Initially, the patient was well appearing, however, two days into admission, he developed a fever to 38.9° C, tachycardia to 130 beats/min, acute encephalopathy, and an up-trending lactate. Broad antibiotic coverage was started, and a diagnostic paracentesis was performed for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP). Ascitic fluid studies were notable for lymphocyte predominance and high serum to ascites albumin gradient (SAAG). Computed tomography pulmonary angiography (CTPA) imaging (Fig 1) showed evidence of mediastinal nodal disease, and computed tomography (CT) imaging of the abdomen and pelvis (Fig 2) showed evidence of peritonitis. Given the lymphocyte predominance, there was suspicion of peritoneal tuberculosis, and patient was placed on a communicable tuberculosis rule out. His Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex polymerase chain reaction (MTB-PCR) from the sputum returned positive, while his MTB-PCR of the peritoneal fluid returned negative. The patient was then started on a liver sparing anti-tuberculous regimen. Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) was cultured from the peritoneal fluid after 20 days, and from sputum culture after 40 days. The patient had a complicated hospital course, eventually developing multiorgan failure, and ultimately discharged to hospice care on hospital day 87. DISCUSSION: Peritoneal TB accounts for 6.3% of all extrapulmonary TB cases [2]. Pathogenesis involves spread from latent pulmonary TB through reactivation of dormant organisms that lie in mesenteric lymph nodes, hematogenous spread during active TB, or extension through adjacent organs [3]. Risk factors include alcoholic liver disease, end-stage renal disease, diabetes, and malignancy [4]. In this patient, the diagnosis of peritoneal TB proved to be a diagnostic challenge, as cases typically present with lymphocyte predominant ascites with low SAAG [5]. With his cirrhosis, the high SAAG confounded our picture, and hepatic-related illness seemed more likely. Additionally, MTB-PCR testing of the ascitic fluid itself was negative. However, given the lymphocyte predominance, the patient was evaluated for communicable pulmonary TB. Fortunately, sputum MTB-PCR testing returned positive, allowing us to secure a diagnosis early in the course. Had this not returned positive, there would need to be further testing, likely necessitating a peritoneal biopsy [6]. CONCLUSIONS: TB peritonitis is a rare manifestation of TB. The diagnosis can be elusive with routine testing, as evidenced in our case with negative ascitic PCR testing. A high index of suspicion should be maintained, and further evaluation be explored despite negative testing. REFERENCE #1: [1] World Health Organization. (2023, November 7). Tuberculosis (TB). World Health Organization. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis#:~:text=Worldwide%2C%20TB%20is%20the%20second,all%20countries%20and%20age%20groups.[2] Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. (2023, November 15). Tuberculosis Cases and Percentages by Site of Disease: United States, 2010–2022. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. https://www.cdc.gov/tb/statistics/reports/2022/table19.htm REFERENCE #2: [3] Kapoor VK. Abdominal tuberculosis. Postgrad Med J. 1998 Aug;74(874):459-67. doi: 10.1136/pgmj.74.874.459. PMID: 9926119; PMCID: PMC2360888. [4] Vaid U.Kane GC. 2017. Tuberculous Peritonitis. Microbiol Spectr 5:10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0006-2016. https://doi.org/10.1128/microbiolspec.tnmi7-0006-2016 REFERENCE #3: [5] SANAI, F. M., & BZEIZI, K. I. (2005). Systematic review: tuberculous peritonitis – presenting features, diagnostic strategies and treatment. In Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics (Vol. 22, Issue 8, pp. 685–700). Wiley. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2036.2005.02645.x [6] Huang B, Cui DJ, Ren Y, Han B, Yang P, Zhao X. Comparison between laparoscopy and laboratory tests for the diagnosis of tuberculous peritonitis. Turk J Med Sci. 2018 Aug 16;48(4):711-715. doi: 10.3906/sag-1512-147. PMID: 30114349. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Patrick Chan No relevant relationships by Alex Duong No relevant relationships by Grace Manchala
