N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (AC) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Streptococcus anginosus (S. anginosus) is a gram-positive anaerobe that can be found in the upper respiratory tract. It is part of the streptococcus anginosus group (SAG), which are usually associated with purulent infections and abscess formation. We discuss a rare presentation of S. anginosus as a cause of bronchomediastinal fistula formation. CASE PRESENTATION: A 17-year-old male with a history of asthma presented with two weeks of worsening chest pain, productive cough, and shortness of breath. Found to have WBC of 21.8, elevated ESR and CRP. CTA chest notable for ill-defined, large, subcarinal lymphadenopathy, causing severe stenosis of the left main bronchus. Extensive infectious workup was pursued. HIV and EBV were negative. Endobronchial Ultrasound guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) lymph node was performed. Purulent bronchial secretions were visualized along with a left main stem medial wall exophytic fistula entering into the subcarinal mediastinal space. Initial gram stain of bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) culture was notable for gram-positive cocci and gram-positive rods raising concern for post-obstructive pneumonia. Tissue pathology was notable for acute inflammation without evidence of malignancy and BAL cultures ultimately grew Streptococcus Anginosus. He was initially treated with Ampicillin/Sulbactam then switched to Amoxicillin/Clavulanic acid and ultimately completed 4 weeks of IV Ceftriaxone followed by two weeks of Amoxicillin with resolution of his symptoms. Follow up chest CT scan six weeks later showed complete resolution of the mass and fistula. DISCUSSION: Tracheomediastinal fistula is a rare complication caused by multiple conditions that includes lymphoma, radiation, lung cancer undergoing chemotherapy especially Bevacizumab. SAG is known to cause life threatening infection that includes abscesses and empyema. To our knowledge this is the first case of reported bronchomediastinal fistula caused by S. anginosus. Patient presented with mediastinal adenopathy with suspicion of granulomatous disease or lymphoma. He underwent bronchoscopy which revealed purulent secretions and a defect in the left main bronchus. Cultures identified S. anginosus, with no evidence of malignancy but significant inflammation. This led to a diagnosis of bronchomediastinal fistula caused by S. Anginosus likely through aspiration of oral secretions in an immunocompetent patient. The case highlights the emerging pathogenicity of S. anginosus as an opportunistic infection capable of causing severe and life-threatening conditions. CONCLUSIONS: To our knowledge, this is the first reported case that highlights Streptococcus anginosus as the main pathogen leading to the formation of bronchomediastinal fistula. It is important to recognize that SAG can lead to severe and life threatening infections even in immunocompetent hosts. REFERENCE #1: 1. Jiang S, Li M, Fu T, Shan F, Jiang L, Shao Z. Clinical Characteristics of Infections Caused by Streptococcus Anginosus Group. Sci Rep. 2020;10(1):9032. Published 2020 Jun 3. doi:10.1038/s41598-020-65977-z. 2. Whiley RA, Beighton D, Winstanley TG, Fraser HY, Hardie JM.Streptococcus intermedius, Streptococcus constellatus, and Streptococcus anginosus (the Streptococcus milleri group): association with different body sites and clinical infections. J Clin Microbiol 30: 1992 https://doi.org/10.1128/jcm.30.1.243-244. REFERENCE #2: 3. Iyer H, Anand A, Sryma PB, et al. Mediastinal lymphadenopathy: a practical approach. Expert Rev Respir Med. 2021;15(10):1317-1334. doi:10.1080/17476348.2021.1920404. 4. Pilarczyk-Zurek M, Sitkiewicz I, Koziel J. The Clinical View on Streptococcus anginosus Group - Opportunistic Pathogens Coming Out of Hiding. Front Microbiol. 2022;13:956677. Published 2022 Jul 8. doi:10.3389/fmicb.2022.956677 REFERENCE #3: 5. Thawani R, Thomas A, Thakur K. Tracheomediastinal Fistula: Rare Complication of Treatment with Bevacizumab. Cureus. 2018;10(4):e2419. Published 2018 Apr 3. doi:10.7759/cureus.2419. 6. Pilarczyk-Zurek M, Sitkiewicz I, Koziel J. The Clinical View on Streptococcus anginosus Group - Opportunistic Pathogens Coming Out of Hiding. Front Microbiol. 2022;13:956677. Published 2022 Jul 8. doi:10.3389/fmicb.2022.956677 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Stephanie Baltaji No relevant relationships by Danai Khemasuwan Royalties - authorship relationship with UpToDAte Please note: $1001 - $5000 by Ray Shepherd, value=Royalty grant for industry study relationship with Olympus Spiration Please note: $1001 - $5000 by Ray Shepherd, value=Grant/Research Support Removed 03/24/2024 by Ray Shepherd, source=Web Response grant clinical trial relationship with GALA therapeutics Please note: 2022 to present by Ray Shepherd, value=Grant/Research Support Grant support for industry research relationship with Steris Medical Please note: 2023 to present Added 03/24/2024 by Ray Shepherd, source=Web Response, value=Grant/Research Support No relevant relationships by Jaasrini Reddy Vellore
