N2  - SESSION TITLE: Uncommon Presentations of Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary abscesses caused by Legionella pneumophila are uncommon, typically encountered in immunocompromised individuals. We present a distinctive case of a pulmonary abscess attributed to Legionella pneumophila in an immunocompetent patient. This rarity underscores the importance of recognizing atypical presentations of Legionella infection, even in individuals without underlying immunosuppression, and emphasizes the significance of prompt diagnosis and tailored management strategies to prevent complications and ensure favorable outcomes. CASE PRESENTATION: A 57-year-old male with a history of daily smoking and alcohol use presented with shortness of breath and cough for 1 week duration. This was associated with hemoptysis. Patient denied any history of immunosuppression. There was no history of recent sick contacts, travel or tuberculosis. Patient was febrile and tachypneic on presentation necessitating admission. Physical examination was unrevealing. Laboratory data showed leukocytosis with decreased serum sodium (121 mmol/L), elevated AST of 411 units/L, and an elevated ALT of 133 units/L. Chest imaging showed right upper lobe cavitation (Figure 1A). Bronchoscopy cultures were unrevealing. Urinary antigen was positive for Legionella. Patient was discharge on oral ciprofloxacin therapy. Repeat chest imaging after 4 weeks showed complete resolution of cavitary lesion (Figure 1B). DISCUSSION: Legionella pneumophila frequently affects adults 50 years or older that have been exposed to contaminated water sources. This species is derived from its growth within lung macrophages after aerosols are inhaled from contaminated water sources. Interest in this bacterium stems from its ability to manipulate host cell vesicular-trafficking pathways and establish a membrane-bound replication vacuole, making it a model for intra-vacuolar pathogens. This pathophysiology leads to a manipulation of the host cell function and intracellular invasion, allowing for increased bacterial growth. Clinical presentation includes non-specific non-productive cough, generalized weakness, and diarrhea. The disease course generally leads to resolution with limited lasting complications and medical concerns. It has been reported that immunocompromised patients suffer additional sequela of the disease such as abscess formation at the site of the initial infections, as well as an overall more insidious course. More recently, risk factors such as chronic lung disease and heavy tobacco use have also been reported to lead to legionnaires disease, but no reports have shown abscess formation in such cases. With an estimated incidence of 12 cases per 100,000 per year, only 40 cases of abscess formation have been described. In this report, we highlight a rare case of abscess formation after a diagnosis of legionella pneumophila in a relatively healthy, non immunocompromised patient. In previous reports, immunocompromised patients have experienced worsening manifestations of the disease. CONCLUSIONS: This case underscores the importance of considering Legionella pneumonia in immunocompetent individuals with pulmonary abscess, despite uncommon presentations. Early recognition, appropriate diagnostic testing, and prompt initiation of targeted therapy are crucial for favorable outcomes. Clinicians should maintain a high index of suspicion for atypical pathogens, especially in patients with persistent respiratory symptoms and predisposing risk factors such as smoking and alcohol use, even when the patient is otherwise immunocompetent. REFERENCE #1: Dow GC, Chow AW. Legionella-associated lung abscess: Critical pathogen or minor isolate? Can J Infect Dis. 1992 Nov;3(6):315-8. doi: 10.1155/1992/985398. PMID: 22346408; PMCID: PMC3250737. REFERENCE #2: Yu H, Higa F, Koide M, Haranaga S, Yara S, Tateyama M, Li H, Fujita J. Lung abscess caused by Legionella species: implication of the immune status of hosts. Intern Med. 2009;48(23):1997-2002. doi: 10.2169/internalmedicine.48.2647. Epub 2009 Dec 1. PMID: 19952481. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Zuhair Ali No relevant relationships by Ethan Goldberg No relevant relationships by Ahsan Syed No relevant relationships by Abhay Vakil
