N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pediatrics Case Reports Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disorder in which thoracic involvement is the most common presentation. In pulmonary sarcoidosis, bilateral hilar adenopathy is the most common radiologic finding, followed by interstitial lung disease.(1) The prompt recognition of sarcoidosis is critical to provide appropriate therapeutic interventions and prevent potentially fatal pulmonary fibrosis or cardiac sarcoidosis. The objective of this case report is to equip other providers to prevent missed diagnosis of pulmonary sarcoidosis, given that it can often present with subtle inconsistencies in clinical presentation as shown in our case. CASE PRESENTATION: A 17-year-old previously healthy male presents with acute hypoxic respiratory failure after one month of progressively worsening dyspnea on exertion, non-productive cough, intermittent low-grade fevers, and 4.4 kg weight loss. He was initially discharged with a working diagnosis of cryptogenic organizing pneumonia and a six-month steroid taper but was readmitted after 4.5 weeks for acute hypoxic respiratory failure. Vitals were significant for tachypnea, tachycardia, and hypoxia to lowest 88%, requiring bilevel positive airway pressure respiratory support. Abnormal findings on the physical exam included diminished breath sounds in all lung fields and scattered bibasilar crackles. Extensive laboratory workup including serum angiotensin converting enzyme level and hypersensitivity pneumonitis panel were normal. Computed tomography (CT) chest scan revealed diffuse centrilobular ground glass opacities and a 4-mm solid right lower lobe pulmonary nodule. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid was negative for bacteria, fungus, and acid-fast bacilli and showed minimal neutrophilia (8%) and lymphopenia (13%), as well as a decreased CD4/CD8 ratio of 0.91 and 1.51 on two separate samples. On patient's second admission, he underwent a video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) procedure with segmental resection of lingula. Pathology showed non-necrotizing granulomatous inflammation in the interstitium of the lung parenchyma, airways, and blood vessels, consistent with sarcoidosis. Specifically, the centrilobular opacities without lymph node involvement on CT imaging suggests that this patient has stage III thoracic sarcoidosis.(2) DISCUSSION: Both the initial diagnosis of cryptogenic organizing pneumonia and sarcoidosis are considered diffuse parenchymal lung diseases, but differentiating among the different etiologies requires a dynamic process between clinician, radiologist, and pathologist, particularly when histologic diagnosis requires an invasive, costly VATS biopsy. Clinically, our patient presented with the classic pulmonary symptoms of sarcoidosis such as non-productive cough and dyspnea but lacked the pathognomonic hilar lymphadenopathy or any extrapulmonary manifestations. To our knowledge this is the first case report of pediatric pulmonary sarcoidosis with centrilobular ground glass opacities lacking any lymphadenopathy, skin, or neurologic manifestations. This pediatric presentation is additionally unique in a 17-year-old male, as juvenile disease, while rare, usually presents from ages 13-15.(3) CONCLUSIONS: In the pursuit of high-value care and shared decision-making, the patient was discharged during his first admission with a presumptive diagnosis that was ultimately proven incorrect on subsequent readmission with invasive testing. It is our hope that by modeling this approach and describing a rare presentation of pediatric sarcoidosis presenting as a diffuse parenchymal lung disease, we equip other providers to recognize the subtle features that resulted in delayed diagnosis for our patient. REFERENCE #1: Criado E, Sánchez M, Ramírez J, Arguis P, de Caralt TM, Perea RJ, Xaubet A. Pulmonary sarcoidosis: typical and atypical manifestations at high-resolution CT with pathologic correlation. Radiographics. 2010;30(6):1567-1586. REFERENCE #2: Scadding JG. Prognosis of intrathoracic sarcoidosis in England. A review of 136 cases after five years' observation. Br Med J. 1961;2(5261):1165-1172. REFERENCE #3: Baculard A, Blanc N, Boulé M, Fauroux B, Chadelat K, Boccon-Gibod L, Tournier G, Clement A. Pulmonary sarcoidosis in children: a follow-up study. Eur Respir J. 2001;17(4):628-635. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Diane Choi No relevant relationships by Carolyn Garcia No relevant relationships by Katherine Harer No relevant relationships by Denzil Reid No relevant relationships by Julia Small
