N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (K) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Cavitary lung lesions are gas-filled spaces within a consolidation, mass, or nodule. They are commonly associated with infectious, malignant, or rheumatological processes with various clinical and radiologic presentations [1].We present a case of rapidly evolving pulmonary cavitary lesion in an immunocompromised host caused by resistant Pseudomonas. Awareness of Pseudomonas and presence of resistant Pseudomonas, as a potential cause, especially in immunocompromised hosts, is paramount to ensure appropriate antimicrobial therapy. CASE PRESENTATION: 46-year-old female with a history of bilateral tonsillar cancer, Systemic Lupus Erythematosus, antiphospholipid syndrome, immune thrombocytopenia, hypertension, bipolar disorder, and hypothyroidism, presented to the emergency room complaining of malaise and productive cough for three days. On examination, the patient was hypotensive, tachycardic, tachypneic, and febrile. Laboratory findings revealed leukocytosis with a white blood cell count (WBC) of 22,000 cells/mL. Chest CT revealed right upper lobe consolidation. Meropenem and Linezolid were started targeting sputum cultures from a prior admission showing Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus; cultures were sensitive to both meropenem and linezolid respectively. During this admission sputum cultures were unable to obtain owing to scant sputum production and deemed not critical since the patient had clinical improvement. After a 10-day course of antibiotics, she was afebrile with decreased cough, and leukocytosis had resolved (WBC decreased to 6,000 cells/mL). Four days after discharge, the patient presented to a different hospital with worsening productive cough and fever. Repeat CT scan of the chest revealed cavitation of previous consolidation in the right upper lobe. A bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was negative for M. tuberculosis DNA, fungal culture, and malignant cytology. Sputum cultures and (BAL) cultures revealed moderate carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The pathogen was sensitive to ceftolozane-tazobactam, which was initiated for a 14-day course with clinical improvement., and a follow-up CT scan is planned after completion of treatment. DISCUSSION: Pseudomonas, an aerobic gram-negative rod, is often isolated in patients with hospital-acquired pneumonia. Risk factors include immunosuppression or structural lung abnormalities. Rarely, Pseudomonas infection is implicated as a causative agent in cavitary lung lesions: notably advanced HIV disease [3][4], and chemically immunosuppressed patients [2]. Some of these reports show a predominance of upper lung lobe cavitations, and it may hint at the inherent aerobic metabolism of Pseudomonas species.Our patient, immunocompromised owing to chemotherapy and long-term steroid use, also had right upper lobe cavitations caused by Pseudomonas. We expect her recovery to continue. CONCLUSIONS: This case emphasizes the importance of considering Pseudomonas aeruginosa as a potential etiology in the differential diagnosis of rapidly progressing upper lobe pulmonary cavitary lesions particularly in immunocompromised patients. REFERENCE #1: [1] Gadkowski LB, Stout JE. Cavitary pulmonary disease. Clin Microbiol Rev [Internet]. 2008 Apr [Cited March 23, 2024];21(2):305-333. Available at: DOI: 10.1128/CMR.00060-07. PMID: 18400799; PMCID: PMC2292573. REFERENCE #2: [2] Salciccioli JD, Woodcock H, Darmalingam M. Pseudomonas aeruginosa as an unusual cause of cavitating lung lesion. BMJ Case Rep. 2017 Jul 14;2017:bcr2017220527. doi: 10.1136/bcr-2017-220527. PMID: 28710242; PMCID: PMC5535126. REFERENCE #3: [3] Gallant JE, Ko AH. Cavitary pulmonary lesions in patients infected with human immunodeficiency virus. Clin Infect Dis. 1996 Apr;22(4):671-82. doi: 10.1093/clinids/22.4.671. PMID: 8729207. [4] Traill ZC, Miller RF, Ali N, Shaw PJ. Pseudomonas aeruginosa bronchopulmonary infection in patients with advanced human immunodeficiency virus disease. Br J Radiol. 1996 Dec;69(828):1099-103. doi: 10.1259/0007-1285-69-828-1099. PMID: 9135463. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Fernando Enrique Amador Fiallos No relevant relationships by Francisco Gallegos Koyner No relevant relationships by Lisa Glass No relevant relationships by Nehemias Guevara No relevant relationships by Franc Hodo No relevant relationships by Maria Jaquez Duran No relevant relationships by Elffy Munoz No relevant relationships by Daviel Rodriguez No relevant relationships by Adisalem Teferi
