N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (A) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Bordetella bronchiseptica is a rare respiratory pathogen in humans. While many infections are reported in immunocompromised patients, it is rare for this organism to cause infection in an immunocompetent patient. We present a 76-year-old male who had a recent history of exposure to a sick cat. He developed a left upper lobar pneumonia due to Bordetella bronchiseptica, and a full course of antibiotics was completed. However, he presented with hemoptysis secondary to a left upper lobe lung abscess. The choice of antibiotics causes a dilemma, as this bacterium, compared to other species, does not respond to erythromycin. The patient had to undergo a lobectomy which only performed in 10% of cases. This case highlights the rarity of respiratory disease caused by this pathogen in humans, emphasizing the importance of early clinical suspicion in the appropriate clinical setting. CASE PRESENTATION: A 76-year-old male, with a complex medical history including hypertension, hyperlipidemia, coronary artery disease, diabetes, prior tobacco abuse, and chronic pancreatitis, presented with dyspnea, productive cough, and unintentional weight loss. He recently adopted a cat with cold-like symptoms. Initial workup revealed leukocytosis and left-mid upper lung zone pneumonia (fig.1), progressing to multiple cavitations (fig.2) despite antibiotic treatment. Bronchoscopy confirmed Bordetella bronchiseptica infection. Despite adequate antibiotic treatment his follow-up revealed a large cavitary abscess(fig.3) with active contrast extravasation (fig.4). Patient was admitted to the intensive care unit (ICU) and was given inhaled tranexamic acid. The patient was started on cefepime, metronidazole and doxycycline. Later on, underwent surgical intervention with left upper lobe lobectomy and thoracic lymph node dissection. He was discharged on a three-week course of oral Augmentin and doxycycline upon discharge. DISCUSSION: Bordetella bronchiseptica, typically found in the respiratory tracts of mammals, rarely infects humans, mainly affecting immunocompromised individuals or those with predisposing conditions. Our patient, with comorbidities including diabetes and coronary artery disease, was exposed to a sick cat, suggesting infection. Reported manifestations range from acute respiratory distress syndrome to bronchitis. The patient presented with hemoptysis, resolving within 24-48 hours, revealing a left upper lobe pulmonary artery aneurysm on CT angiogram with signs of contrast extravasation. Though bronchial artery embolization was considered, conservative management sufficed. Pulmonary artery aneurysms typically develop in the late phase of abscess. B. bronchiseptica exhibits specific antibiotic sensitivities, with resistance to some antibiotics but sensitivity to others. Lobectomy was chosen due to associated hemoptysis, abscess size, and prolonged sepsis despite antibiotics, aligning with indications for surgical resection in 10% of cases. CONCLUSIONS: This case underscores the importance of considering rare pathogens like Bordetella bronchiseptica in patients presenting with pneumonia, particularly when there's a history of exposure to symptomatic animals. Despite its rarity, B. bronchiseptica infection can lead to severe complications, such as pulmonary artery aneurysms and hemoptysis, especially in individuals with predisposing conditions. Additionally, it emphasizes the importance of thorough patient history-taking and a high index of suspicion for unusual pathogens, particularly in immunocompromised patients or those with underlying comorbidities. REFERENCE #1: ucours M, Rispal P, Danjean MP, Imbert Y, Dupont E, Traissac EM, et al. Bordetella bronchiseptica infection. Médecine et Maladies Infectieuses. 2017;47(7):453-8. REFERENCE #2: Yacoub AT, Katayama M, Tran J, Zadikany R, Kandula M, Greene J. Bordetella bronchiseptica in the immunosuppressed population - a case series and review. Mediterr J Hematol Infect Dis. 2014;6(1):e2014031. REFERENCE #3: Clements J, McGrath C, McAllister C. Bordetella bronchiseptica pneumonia: beware of the dog! BMJ Case Rep. 2018;2018. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Omer Ahmed No relevant relationships by Jason Lyons No relevant relationships by Sanjana Ramakrishnan No relevant relationships by Xin Ya See
