N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Manifestations Case Reports Posters (L) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: MAC associated IRIS is a known, though poorly characterized, phenomenon in people with HIV who receive ART. However, there is limited literature on MAC-IRIS in other immunosuppressed populations. CASE PRESENTATION: An 89 year-old man with past medical history of OSA and aortic stenosis presented with fever, malaise, and rash. He had been admitted to an outside hospital one week prior for neutropenic fever and ground glass opacities on chest imaging with unrevealing infectious work-up. He received antibiotics for possible atypical pneumonia and Granix for neutropenia with improvement in his ANC. He had no prior hematologic, oncologic, or rheumatologic history. Prior to this, he had a 1-2 month history of dyspnea and generalized fatigue. Social history negative for smoking; no known environmental or occupational exposures. At presentation, he was febrile. Physical exam was significant for oral thrush and diffuse maculopapular rash. Labs were notable for pancytopenia and neutropenia. CT chest with contrast showed widespread tree-in-bud nodularity in all five lobes with mild mosaic attenuation, patchy ground glass opacities, and no frank consolidations. CT of the abdomen and pelvis was unremarkable. He was started on antibiotics and antifungals. Broad infectious work-up including investigation for atypicals, tick-borne, and viral illnesses was unremarkable. His EBV IgG was positive, but IgM was negative. He underwent bone marrow biopsy, which later resulted MDS, IPSS-M low-intermediate risk.Patient developed hypoxia and underwent bronchoscopy; airways appeared grossly normal, and cytology, bacterial cultures, fungal cultures, and pneumocystis testing was negative. He was treated with diuretics and discharged on a steroid taper. AFB culture subsequently resulted as positive for mycobacterium species. By the time of this result, symptoms of dyspnea and cough had resolved. Mycobacterium speciation and treatment plans are ongoing. DISCUSSION: IRIS refers to a hyperinflammatory response state that often presents in HIV+ individuals initiating ART; the hallmark includes either worsening of a known disease or development of a new illness due to dysregulation of the immune system in response to subclinical pathogens and residual antigens. IRIS should be considered when a known infection worsens, or unknown infection is identified in a patient with improved immunologic function; in this patient, as his neutropenia improved, he developed symptoms of rash and cough. Although IRIS has been identified with tuberculosis as an opportunistic infection, MAC-IRIS is a rare entity (Xerri et al. 2021). In HIV+ individuals, MAC-IRIS may present with fever, lymphadenitis, osteomyelitis, granulomatous hepatitis, brain abscesses, or worsening lung infiltrates after ART initiation. Management includes continuing ART, treating the opportunistic infection, and glucocorticoids (Phillips et al. 2005). MAC-IRIS in non-HIV+ individuals is a less common presentation; review of the literature notes a case report that identified MAC-IRIS in an individual with known MAC and interferon deficiency who received immunosuppressives post allogenic bone marrow transplant and had worsening of MAC disease. The patient improved with steroids (Dimitrova et al. 2018). CONCLUSIONS: MAC-IRIS can be seen in patients who are immunocompromised due to conditions other than HIV. Our patient had MDS causing neutropenia. Steroid treatment may be appropriate in cases of MAC-IRIS in non-HIV+ individuals. REFERENCE #1: Xerri T, Borg J, Casha R, Fsadni C. Complexity of Mycobacterium avium complex immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (MAC-IRIS) in a patient with HIV. BMJ Case Rep. 2021 Sep 3;14(9):e241813. doi: 10.1136/bcr-2021-241813. PMID: 34479876; PMCID: PMC8420731. REFERENCE #2: Phillips, Peter, et al. "Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Immune Reconstitution Syndrome in HIV-Infected Patients: Spectrum of Disease and Long-Term Follow-Up." Clinical Infectious Diseases, vol. 41, no. 10, 2005, pp. 1483–97, https://doi.org/10.1086/497269. REFERENCE #3: Dimitrova, Dimana, et al. "Mycobacterium Avium Complex (MAC)-Associated Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS) Following Allogeneic Bone Marrow Transplantation (alloBMT)." Biology of Blood and Marrow Transplantation, vol. 24, no. 3, 2018, pp. S385-, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbmt.2017.12.475. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ethan Bernstein No relevant relationships by Nicolette Pennington No relevant relationships by Angela Quental
