N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (Q) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Non-tuberculosis mycobacteria (NTM) are widely found in our environment, but NTM lung infections occur infrequently compared to mycobacterium tuberculosis. The data from the USA suggest that the current prevalence of NTM-positive culture ranges from 1.4/100,000 individuals to 6.6/100,000 individuals.The lung is the most involved organ, and the NTM-pulmonary disease (NTM-PD) occurs frequently in patients with pre-existing lung disease.These organisms can be found in soil, air, dust and plants. Significant NTM include M. avium complex (MCC), Mycobacterium fortuitum, and M. kansasii. Mycobacterium shimondai is a rare cause of lung infection. Less than 100 cases are reported worldwide. Due to its rarity, there is no clear treatment. CASE PRESENTATION: 72-year-old Caucasian female with background of severe diffuse emphysema and seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, who never left the United States, presented with complaints of difficulty breathing and nonproductive cough.She was treated for COPD exacerbation and underwent CT chest which showed upper lobe cavitary lesion, followed by bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL).Acid-fast bacilli culture (AFB) was positive for mycobacterium Shimoidei using 16 S rRNA sequence.The isolate was not considered clinically significant, and no treatment was administered. Six months later, her symptoms worsened and she experienced more shortness of breath. A new lung CT showed an increase in size of right upper lobe cavitary lesion.A new BAL specimen was strongly positive 3+ AFB with neg. M. tuberculosis probe, and neg. MAC probe. Repeat sputum cultures grew M. shimoidei in three separate cultures; therefore, this NTM was deemed pathologic.Treatment initiated with rifabutin, ethambutol and clarithromycin for six months with 3 months regular follow-ups with pulmonologist and infectious disease specialist. After 3 months, regression of symptoms, radiological improvement and 1+ AFB. At 6 months, sputum negativization and BAL culture conversion were obtained. The patient was followed for 1 year after treatment completion. Smears and sputum cultures remained negative, and no symptoms relapsed. The same therapy with rifabutin, clarithromycin and ethambutol was prolonged up to 18 months of treatment. DISCUSSION: Mycobacterium shimoidei was first isolated in Japan in 1968. Worldwide, only a couple of cases have been reported since.This case is the first in the US in immunocompromised female with underlying lung disease; in 2022, a case was reported in USA in a healthy young male who presented with productive cough and weight loss and imaging showed large right upper cavitary lesion.Reported cases of mycobacterium shimoidei most commonly associated concurrent conditions were COPD and bronchiectasis in older male. Common symptoms were cough or sputum production, weight loss, dyspnea, fevers, or sweat. Radiology demonstrated cavitating disease in approximately 70% of patients. The infection route for humans is unknown.Previous cases suggest combinations of rifabutin and ethambutol due to apparent resistance rifampicin. In this case, clarithromycin was added as a third antimycobacterial agent. CONCLUSIONS: Due to its rarity, further investigation and reports for better understanding of the risk factors, pathogenicity and treatment are required. ​Our case highlights the efficacy of the drug regimen offered. REFERENCE #1: Thomson RM; NTM working group at Queensland TB Control Centre and Queensland Mycobacterial Reference Laboratory. Changing epidemiology of pulmonary nontuberculous mycobacteria infections. Emerg Infect Dis. 2010;16:1576–83. 10.3201/eid1610.091201 REFERENCE #2: Galizzi N, Tortoli E, Gori A, Morini F, Lapadula G. 2013. A Case of Mild Pulmonary Disease Due to Mycobacterium shimoidei with a Favorable Outcome. J Clin Microbiol 51:.https://doi.org/10.1128/jcm.01028-13. Patel JB, Leonard DG, Pan X, Musser JM, Berman RE, Nachamkin I. Sequence-based identification of Mycobacterium species using the MicroSeq 500 16S rDNA bacterial identification system. J Clin Microbiol. 2000 Jan;38(1):246-51. doi: 10.1128/JCM.38.1.246-251.2000. PMID: 10618095; PMCID: PMC88703. REFERENCE #3: Tsukamura M, Shimoide H, Shaefer WB. A possible new pathogen of group iii Mycobacteria. J Gen Microbiol. 1975;88:377–80. 10.1099/00221287-88-2-377. SHENDE, T., LARSEN, T. and M EL-BABA, F. (2022) 'Mycobacterium shimoidei: A rare nontuberculous infection in a US patient', Chest, 162(4). doi:10.1016/j.chest.2022.08.263. Nagano H. Mycobacterium shimoidei, a rare non-tuberculous mycobacteria pathogen identified by matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight mass spectrometry. Respirol Case Rep. 2019 Apr 17;7(5):e00428. doi: 10.1002/rcr2.428. PMID: 31019716; PMCID: PMC6470063.Thomson RM; NTM working group at Queensland TB Control Centre and Queensland Mycobacterial Reference Laboratory. Changing epidemiology of pulmonary nontuberculous mycobacteria infections. Emerg Infect Dis. 2010;16:1576–83. 10.3201/eid1610.091201 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Farah Majdoub
