N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (B) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycoplasma Pneumoniae has been classically considered a respiratory tract pathogen, however numerous extrapulmonary phenomena have been described (1). Cardiac complications associated with M. Pneumoniae infections have been documented in pediatric and adult patients, frequently myocarditis and pericarditis (2,3). However, to our knowledge, M. Pneumoniae associated conduction abnormalities are exceedingly rare in adults. We present a case of an adult patient presenting with syncope and cough, who was subsequently diagnosed with pneumonia and high grade atrioventricular block. CASE PRESENTATION: An 82-year-old male presented to the Emergency Department for recurrent syncopal episodes, dry cough, and malaise, which began 1 week prior while on cruise ship. He had a history of Diabetes, no pre-existing cardiac disease, and did not take atrioventricular nodal blocking agents. The patient was tachypneic and hypoxic requiring 3L/min via nasal cannula. Physical exam was remarkable for mild respiratory distress and bibasilar crackles. An electrocardiogram revealed a first-degree atrioventricular block at a heart rate of 65 beats per minute. A chest CT found biapical, subpleural, patchy opacities (Image 1). He was treated with intravenous fluids, ceftriaxone, and azithromycin. The following morning, the patient complained of faintness. Simultaneously, the telemetry monitor revealed a 10 second period with 12 non-conducting p-waves and ventricular asystole, consistent with high grade heart block (Image 2). The patient remained hemodynamically stable during the episode, subsequently converted back to normal sinus rhythm, and was transferred to the Intensive Care Unit. A transthoracic echocardiogram exhibited normal left ventricular size and function, mildly reduced right ventricular function, and no pericardial effusion or valvular abnormalities. On day four of admission, the patient received a dual-chamber permanent pacemaker. Serologic analysis detected the presence of M. Pneumoniae IgM antibodies in the serum. The patient completed a seven-day course of Azithromycin, demonstrated significant clinical improvement, and was discharged home. DISCUSSION: The incidence of cardiac involvement during M. Pneumoniae infection has been estimated at 4.5% (4). A review of the literature identified 11 cases, including pediatric and adult, of varying degrees of atrioventricular block in the setting of M. Pneumoniae infection (5-13). Of the aforementioned cases, only three occurred in adult patients (11-13). The pathogenesis of M. Pneumoniae infection associated cardiac manifestations have been theorized to be a result of direct bacterial invasion or an auto-immune mechanism. The former has been evidenced by isolating M. Pneumoniae via culture of pericardial fluid and tissue during episodes of pericarditis. The latter theory gained momentum by researchers elucidating a correlation between extrapulmonary phenomena, the presence of antiphospholipid antibodies, and other inflammatory markers, such as TIM-1 (1,3,14). CONCLUSIONS: In regards to clinical management, 82% of the identified cases from literature review were second or third-degree heart block. Given these conduction abnormalities often lead to permanent pacemaker implantation, physicians should consider utilizing a telemetry monitor for patients who test positive for M. Pneumoniae when admitted (15). REFERENCE #1: 1. Narita M. Classification of Extrapulmonary Manifestations Due to Mycoplasma pneumoniae Infection on the Basis of Possible Pathogenesis. Front Microbiol. 2016 Jan 28;7:23. doi: 10.3389/fmicb.2016.00023. PMID: 26858701; PMCID: PMC4729911. 2. Gordon O, Oster Y, Michael-Gayego A, et al. The Clinical Presentation of Pediatric Mycoplasma pneumoniae Infections-A Single Center Cohort. Pediatr Infect Dis J. 2019;38(7):698-705. doi:10.1097/INF.0000000000002291. 3. Paz A, Potasman I. Mycoplasma-associated carditis. Case reports and review. Cardiology. 2002;97(2):83-88. doi:10.1159/000057677. 4. Pönkä A. Carditis associated with mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. Acta Med Scand. 1979;206(1-2):77-86. doi:10.1111/j.0954-6820.1979.tb13473.x. 5. Agarwala BN, Ruschhaupt DG. Complete heart block from mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. Pediatr Cardiol. 1991;12(4):233-236. doi:10.1007/BF02310573 REFERENCE #2: 6. Friedli B, Renevey F, Rouge JC. Complete heart block in a young child presumably due to mycoplasma pneumoniae myocarditis. Acta Paediatr Scand. 1977;66(3):385-388. doi:10.1111/j.1651-2227.1977.tb07912.x 7. Tsai YG, Ou TY, Wang CC, Tsai MC, Yuh YS, Hwang B. Intravenous gamma-globulin therapy in myocarditis complicated with complete heart block: Report of one case. Acta Paediatr Taiwan. 2001;42(5):311-313. 8. Wang JN, Tsai YC, Lee WL, Lin CS, Wu JM. Complete atrioventricular block following myocarditis in children. Pediatr Cardiol. 2002;23(5):518-521. doi:10.1007/s00246-002-0129-0. 9. Chien SJ, Liang CD, Lin IC, Lin YJ, Huang CF. Myocarditis complicated by complete atrioventricular block: nine years' experience in a medical center [published correction appears in Pediatr Neonatol. 2009 Feb;50(1):39]. Pediatr Neonatol. 2008;49(6):218-222. doi:10.1016/S1875-9572(09)60014-0. 10. Youssef D, Britton P, Mohammad S, Mervis J, G. Gnanaapa, S. Yusoff. 647 A Rare Case Report of Complete Heart Block and Transient Parkinsonism in a Child Secondary to Mycoplasma Pneumoniae With Normal Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI). Heart, Lung and Circulation. 2020;29:S327-S328. doi:https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hlc.2020.09.654 REFERENCE #3: 11. O Feizi, Grubb C, Skinner JI, M Constandou, Henderson WG. Unusual complications of primary atypical pneumonia due to M. pneumoniae. The BMJ. 1971;4(5789):751-751. doi:https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.4.5789.751-b. 12. Umapathi KK, Schmidt H, Salazar N, Sosnowski C, Wilkerson MK, Logan LK. A Previously Healthy 18-Year-Old Male With Fever, Arrhythmia, and Shock. Pediatrics. 2021;147(5):e2020017624. doi:10.1542/peds.2020-017624. 13. Chergui K, Fourme T, Veillard-Baron A, Loubieres Y, Voyer C, Jardin F. Mycoplasma pneumoniae and second-degree heart block. Clin Infect Dis. 1998;27(6):1534-1535. doi:10.1086/517739. 14. Fan Q, Meng J, Li P, Liu Z, Sun Y, Yan P. Pathogenesis and association of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection with cardiac and hepatic damage. Microbiol Immunol. 2015;59(7):375-380. doi:10.1111/1348-0421.12267. 15. Kusumoto FM, Schoenfeld MH, Barrett C, et al. 2018 ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline on the Evaluation and Management of Patients With Bradycardia and Cardiac Conduction Delay: A Report of the American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association Task Force on Clinical Practice Guidelines and the Heart Rhythm Society [published correction appears in J Am Coll Cardiol. 2019 Aug 20;74(7):1016-1018]. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2019;74(7):e51-e156. doi:10.1016/j.jacc.2018.10.044 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Asad Abbas No relevant relationships by Nathaniel Allison No relevant relationships by Philip Hall No relevant relationships by Sashi Wettimuny
