N2  - SESSION TITLE: Occupational and Environmental Case Reports Posters (A) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Silicosis has been on the rise over the past few decades with a global estimate of 138,965 new cases annually. Despite being the most common pneumoconiosis, silicosis is still a challenging disease to identify in its early stages as patients often do not present until they have dyspnea and significant lung fibrosis, frequently requiring multiple medical encounters to reach a final diagnosis. Silicosis often presents after 10-15 years of exposure though recent case studies suggest shorter latency periods among individuals who worked with engineered stone, which can have 3-30 times higher silica content compared to natural stone. Here we present a case of accelerated silicosis after only 5 years of occupational exposure to engineered stone and despite reported N95 usage, with a delay in diagnosis due to initial attribution of symptoms to mycobacterial infection alone. CASE PRESENTATION: A 53-year-old male first presented to his local emergency room in 2018 due to uncontrolled hypertension and was incidentally found on chest x-ray to have multiple pulmonary nodules. He had grown up in Mexico and therefore was empirically treated for active tuberculosis with rifampin, isoniazid, and ethambutol. AFB smears were ultimately negative for mycobacterium tuberculosis but were positive for mycobacterium avium complex, so the patient still completed a course of the above medications. The patient was then seen at our institution's pulmonary clinic in 2020 for ongoing productive cough and dyspnea and underwent a CT of the chest which showed bilateral innumerable diffuse sub-centimeter centrilobular nodules. Coccidioides and histoplasma serologies were negative. Upon further investigation, it was revealed that the patient had worked as a mason cutting engineered stone for countertops from 2014 to 2018, concerning for silicosis. Pulmonary function testing showed progression to moderate diffusion and restrictive ventilatory defects over the next two years, with the patient eventually requiring home oxygen. In 2023, the patient was hospitalized for hypoxia requiring high flow oxygen and underwent a lung transplant evaluation. His workup was notable for pulmonary hypertension with an mPAP of 52 mmHg and PVR of 6 Woods units. He was also found to have positive ANA, Scl-70, SSA, Smith, and RNP antibodies suggestive of scleroderma and was started on hydroxychloroquine. He completed a course of prednisone and was weaned back to his home oxygen settings and currently awaits lung transplant. DISCUSSION: The initial differential for this patient included tuberculosis, fungal infection, sarcoidosis, and metastatic malignancy. This case, however, highlights the importance of a thorough social and occupational history, particularly with regard to exposure to engineered stone, which clinched the diagnosis of accelerated silicosis and helped to initiate the patient's lung transplant evaluation. Accelerated silicosis has been found to be associated with non-tuberculous mycobacterial infections as well as autoimmune conditions, both of which were present in this patient, likely in the setting of macrophage activation and immunomodulatory dysfunction. CONCLUSIONS: Silicosis can be a life-threatening illness, particularly in the accelerated form, highlighting the need for occupational protections for workers especially those handling engineered stone, adequate screening for at-risk populations, and close clinical surveillance. REFERENCE #1: Liu, X., Jiang, Q., Wu, P., Han, L. & Zhou, P. Global incidence, prevalence and disease burden of silicosis: 30 years’ overview and forecasted trends. BMC Public Health 23, (2023). REFERENCE #2: Fazio, J. C. et al. Silicosis Among Immigrant Engineered Stone (Quartz) Countertop Fabrication Workers in California. JAMA Intern Med 183, 991–998 (2023). REFERENCE #3: Leso, V., Fontana, L., Romano, R., Gervetti, P. & Iavicoli, I. Artificial stone associated Silicosis: A systematic review. International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health vol. 16 Preprint at https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph16040568 (2019). DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Jenny Chen No relevant relationships by Nida Qadir No relevant relationships by Jing Ren
