N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Reports Posters (H) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) is the tenth leading cause of death worldwide [1]. In recent decades, increased movement of people between countries has led to a change in the epidemiological profile of TB. In the U.S., most MTB cases are among the non-U.S.-born population and likely acquired before entry to the U.S [2, 3]. Studies have shown that lymph nodes are the second most common site of TB infection after the lungs [4]. Generalized lymphadenopathy in TB can mimic lymphoma. Differentiation between infectious and malignant lymphadenopathies remains a challenge in healthcare because of different treatment approaches. We are presenting a case of a 53-year-old Albanian man with MTB and generalized lymphadenopathy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 53-year-old Albanian male, former smoker (25 pack year), with a medical history of inflammatory bowel disease presented with two weeks of intermittent, non-exertional, pressure-like, substernal chest pain. He denied shortness of breath, fever, chills, cough, night sweats, or weight loss. The electrocardiogram (ECG), chest x-ray (CXR), transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE), and cardiac enzymes were normal. The coronary computed tomography angiography (CCTA) showed incidental findings of mediastinal, hilar and abdominal lymphadenopathy. The patient denied fever, chills, cough, night sweats, or weight loss. The result of positron emission tomography (PET) scan was suggestive of lymphoproliferative disease. The patient underwent endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) guided transbronchial needle aspiration, which was non-diagnostic and bronchoscopy derived acid-fast bacilli staining and culture were negative. Secondary to the importance of ruling out lymphoma, the patient then underwent robotic video-assisted thoracoscopic (VATS) mediastinal excisional lymph node biopsy by thoracic surgery. Lymphoma was ruled out both through pathology and flow cytometry. Mycobacterium tuberculosis was isolated from acid-fast tissue culture, and the patient started on "RIPE” treatment for tuberculosis (Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol) with appropriate response. DISCUSSION: Tuberculosis is one of the most common infectious diseases with heterogeneous clinical manifestations causing a wide range of differential diagnoses from benign to malignant etiologies. Diagnostic dilemmas can delay appropriate therapy, favoring mycobacterium tuberculosis transmission. We reported a case of MTB in an immunocompetent, Albanian 53-year-old man who presented with generalized lymphadenopathy (thoracic and abdominal) and later was diagnosed with MTB through mediastinal lymph node biopsy. In low MTB incidence countries, the majority of patients with TB and lymph node involvements do not show active pulmonary TB on chest x-rays [5]. Diagnostic delay and inappropriate therapy represent the most relevant shortcomings of TB care in low TB incidence countries, frequently associated with atypical presentation of the TB as well as poor physicians’ knowledge on TB disease. CONCLUSIONS: This case report shows clinical and radiological complexity of MTB with generalized lymph node involvement. Furthermore, pathologies with generalized lymphadenopathy can pose challenging problems in terms of differential diagnosis with lymphomas. Comprehensive awareness and knowledge of atypical TB manifestations can help with the early diagnosis, prompt initiation of the therapy, and decrease in transmission rate. REFERENCE #1: 1- Salinas JL, Mindra G, Haddad MB, Pratt R, Price SF, Langer AJ. Leveling of Tuberculosis Incidence - United States, 2013-2015. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2016 Mar 25;65(11):273-8. doi: 10.15585/mmwr.mm6511a2. PMID: 27010173. 2-Talwar A, Tsang CA, Price SF, Pratt RH, Walker WL, Schmit KM, et al. Tuberculosis: United States, 2018. Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2019;68:257–262. REFERENCE #2: 3-Yuen CM, Kammerer JS, Marks K, Navin TR, France AM. Recent Transmission of Tuberculosis - United States, 2011-2014. PLoS One. 2016 Apr 15;11(4):e0153728. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0153728. PMID: 27082644; PMCID: PMC4833321. 4- Lowbridge C, Christensen A, McAnulty JM. EpiReview: tuberculosis in NSW, 2009-2011. N S W Public Health Bull. 2013 Jul;24(1):3-9. doi: 10.1071/NB12115. PMID: 23849020. REFERENCE #3: 5-Claudia Collu, Alessandro Fois, Paola Crivelli, Gianni Tidore, Claudio Fozza, Giovanni Sotgiu, Pietro Pirina. A case-report of a pulmonary tuberculosis with lymphadenopathy mimicking a lymphoma. International Journal of Infectious Diseases, Volume 70, 2018, Pages 38-41, ISSN 1201-9712, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2018.02.011. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mallika Pradhan No relevant relationships by Griwan Rajbhandari No relevant relationships by Mahsa Sedaghati
