N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (G) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB) remains a significant global health threat. We present a case of a 31-year-old male who presented with MDR-TB shortly after immigrating to the United States. His clinical course was complicated by pneumothorax and significant malnutrition. CASE PRESENTATION: This is a 31-year-old male with no past medical history who presented to our hospital with a month-long history of shortness of breath and productive cough. Three months prior, he immigrated from Pakistan to the United States (U.S.). Two months after immigration, he began experiencing persistent cough and progressive weight loss. He sought care at an outside clinic where he tested negative for TB. Due to the persistence of his cough, he went to a different clinic where his sputum culture was positive for TB, and his chest x-ray was notable for bilateral interstitial opacities with cavitation and an apical pneumothorax. He subsequently started Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (TB) therapy for active TB with Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (RIPE). Several days after initiating RIPE therapy, he was brought to our hospital by Emergency Medical Services (EMS) due to progressive shortness of breath. Further workup was notable for acid-fast bacilli on AFB smear, TB on AFB culture resistant to Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Streptomycin. Chest imaging was notable for a large right pneumothorax and multiple multifocal cavitating consolidations. He subsequently started second-line TB therapy with Bedaquiline, Pretomanid, Linezolid, and Moxifloxacin (BPaLM). His hospital course was complicated by the persistence of right-sided pneumothorax requiring multiple chest tubes for months, malnutrition requiring gastric tube feeds, and total parental nutrition (TPN). He achieved AFB sputum culture conversion one month after initiation of therapy, though smears remained positive for acid-fast bacilli. He was discharged to complete a total of 9 months of intensive therapy with BPaLM at a long-term acute care hospital (LTACH). DISCUSSION: Despite low incidence rates and prevalence in the U.S., TB remains a major public health concern globally, as it is one of the leading causes of death by an infectious organism.1 This global public health concern is further highlighted in drug-resistant forms of TB as cases of MDR-TB continue to rise, and as cure rates are lower in MDR-TB (approximately 40%) compared to non-resistant TB (approximately 60-80%).² Early time to sputum culture conversion is a significant predictor of treatment success, and in our patient with culture conversion in one month, it portends a better prognosis.³ Pneumothorax is a well-known complication of TB and was a major complication in our patient. It is thought to be driven by the rupture of cavities or blebs into the pleural space.⁴ Management of pneumothorax is dependent on the size and clinical stability of the patient, with unstable or large pneumothorax (≥3 cm apex-to-cupola distance) requiring placement of a chest tube. Persistent air leaks can be managed with pleurodesis or with surgery.⁵ CONCLUSIONS: This case highlights the challenges in managing MDR-TB, especially when complicated by pneumothorax and nutritional issues. MDR-TB requires a comprehensive approach, addressing not only drug resistance but also associated complications. REFERENCE #1: 1. Schildknecht KR, Pratt RH, Feng PI, Price SF, Self JL. Tuberculosis -united states, 2022. 2023.2. Seung KJ, Keshavjee S, Rich ML. Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis and extensively drug-resistant tuberculosis. Cold Spring Harb Perspect Med. 2015;5(9). doi: 10.1101/cshperspect.a017863. REFERENCE #2: 3. Alene KA, Viney K, Yi H, et al. Comparison of the validity of smear and culture conversion as a prognostic marker of treatment outcome in patients with multidrug-resistant tuberculosis. PLoS ONE. 2018;13(5). doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0197880. 4. Blanco-Perez J, Borden J, Pineiro-Amigo L, Roca-Serrano R, Abal-Arca J, Bord& J. Pneumothorax in active pulmonary tuberculosis: Resurgence of an old complication? REFERENCE #3: 5. Baumann MH, Strange C, Heffner JE, et al. Management of spontaneous pneumothorax* an american college of chest physicians delphi consensus statement. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Uyioghosa Evbayiro
