N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (F) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary nocardiosis is an opportunistic infection that rarely affects immunocompetent individuals. With highly variable culture results and no serological tests available, it is imperative that clinicians have a high clinical suspicion for this atypical infection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 67-year-old female with no past medical history presented to pulmonary clinic for bothersome cough and fatigue for past 3 months. She had been diagnosed with several URI and bronchitis episodes and was treated with multiple 5-day courses of antibiotics including augmentin, azithromycin, cefdinir, doxycycline, and levofloxacin. Symptoms improved while on antibiotics but worsened shortly after stopping. CT chest was notable for tree-in-bud opacities and mild bronchiectasis throughout both lungs. Airway clearance regimen was initiated but sputum samples were unable to be collected. Bronchoscopy was preformed and infectious work-up was negative. Patient endorsed resolution of her symptoms with airway clearance alone. Several months later, patient became ill with bronchitis while traveling. Increasing airway clearance and antibiotics had no effect on symptoms. CT chest showed increasing tree- in-bud parenchymal disease in the right and left lower lobes concerning for atypical infection. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed and initial infectious testing was negative. Three weeks later, Nocardia was isolated on BAL AFB culture. Patient was started on bactrim and referred to Infectious Disease. Patient is currently doing well with continued improvement in her symptoms. She is currently on bactrim and follows with pulmonology and infectious disease outpatient. DISCUSSION: Nocardia is an opportunistic infection that can affect almost every organ with the most frequent presentation being Pulmonary Nocardiosis (PN) (2,5,6). PN is typically seen in immunocompromised individuals, but it can uncommonly affect immunocompetent individuals (6). Risk factors include HIV, chronic steroid use, immunosuppressants, organ transplant, and diabetes (6). COPD and bronchiectasis may also increase risk of Nocardia infection (1,2,4,6). Symptoms are nonspecific including chronic cough, sputum production, dyspnea as well as fever, lethargy, and weight loss (7). Imaging is also nonspecific and can include consolidations, nodules, cavitary masses, and pleural effusions (3,5). Infection with Nocardia can mimic NTM and has been called Lady Windermere-like syndrome (7). Nocardia is an aerobic, gram-positive, and weakly acid-fast bacilliform (4). There are no serological testing methods, so diagnosis is dependent on culture (3). Culture, however, is difficult to obtain and typically requires weeks to grow (2,3,6). Gram stain showing delicate beaded, branching filaments, can be useful when Nocardia is the suspected pathogen (1,3). Bactrim is first line treatment and length of treatment can be as long as 12 months (5,6). Approximately 25% of Nocardia are resistance to Bactrim; these species are typically harder to grow on culture making treatment extremely difficult (1,3). Early diagnosis is imperative due to potential of poor prognosis. (1,3). Mortality rate ranges from 40% to as high as 60% in disseminated cases (3). Due to absence of serological testing as well as difficulty and delay with culture, it is important to have high clinical suspicion (7). CONCLUSIONS: Our case highlights the importance of considering atypical infections including Nocardia in the differential for refractory cough in an immunocompetent patient. REFERENCE #1: 1. Dong, J., Guan, W., Hu, A., & Luo, Q. (2023). Mild Pulmonary Nocardiosis Caused by Nocardia terpenica in an Immunocompetent Patient: A Case Report. Internal Medicine, 62(19), 2911–2917. https://doi.org/10.2169/internalmedicine.9740-22. 2. Ito, Y., Miwa, S., & Shirai, M. (2023). Pulmonary nocardiosis following nodular Bronchiectatic Mycobacterium Avium Complex pulmonary disease in an immunocompetent patient. Case Reports, 16(11), e256007. https://doi.org/10.1136/bcr-2023-256007. 3. Kato, K., Noguchi, S., Naito, K., Ikushima, I., Hanaka, T., Yamasaki, K., Kawanami, T., & Yatera, K. (2019). Pulmonary Nocardiosis Caused by Nocardia exalbida in a Patient with Lung Cancer and Radiation Pneumonitis: A Case Report and Literature Review. Internal Medicine, 58(11), 1605–1611. https://doi.org/10.2169/internalmedicine.2177-18 REFERENCE #2: 4. Li, Z., Li, Y., Li, S., Li, Z., Mai, Y., Cheng, J., Su, D., Zhan, Y., Zhong, N., & Ye, F. (2022). Identification of a novel drug-resistant community-acquired Nocardia spp. in a patient with bronchiectasis. Emerging Microbes & Infections, 11(1), 1346–1355. https://doi.org/10.1080/22221751.2022.2069514. 5. Lopes, K., Montenegro, C., Vìlchez, J., Camacho, M. E., & Marques, H. (2022). IMAGES: Nocardia pneumonia – A surprising and rare pulmonary infection mimicking lung cancer. Pulmonology, 28(5), 415–417. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pulmoe.2022.03.013. 6. Yagi, K., Ishii, M., Namkoong, H., Asami, T., Fujiwara, H., Nishimura, T., Saito, F., Kimizuka, Y., Asakura, T., Suzuki, S., Kamo, T., Tasaka, S., Gonoi, T., Kamei, K., & Betsuyaku, T. (2014). Pulmonary nocardiosis caused by Nocardia cyriacigeorgica in patients with Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease: two case reports. BMC Infectious Diseases, 14(1). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-014-0684-z REFERENCE #3: 7. Zeng, P., Zhang, X., Wu, H., Wang, L., Nan, X., & Mu, X. (2023). Bronchiectasis with Nocardia infection was designated as Lady Windermere-like syndrome: a retrospective study. Chinese Medical Journal, 136(15), 1864–1866. https://doi.org/10.1097/cm9.0000000000002553 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sailaja Allamneni No relevant relationships by Sarah Cheyney No relevant relationships by Rameez Rao No relevant relationships by Tulsi Shah
