N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (G) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Toxic shock syndrome is classically associated with staphylococcus aureus and less commonly with Group A Streptococcal (GAS) infections. Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (STSS) is more often associated with necrotizing fasciitis and severe soft tissue infections, yielding a high mortality rate of 30 – 70%. This mechanism is not fully understood and is suspected to involve superantigen activity leading to rapidly progressive multi-organ involvement. This report describes the clinical course of STSS, a rare but serious complication of Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis, and the importance of prompt diagnosis for life-saving treatment. CASE PRESENTATION: A 29-year-old male with history of heavy alcohol use presented to the emergency department for dyspnea. Five days prior to arrival, the patient had a severe sore throat with a fever of 102°F, cough, and diarrhea. Upon admission, lab values were pertinent for white blood cell count of 1.6 k/uL, bicarbonate of 9 mmol/L, lactate of 17.8 mmol/L, procalcitonin of 172 ng/mL, glucose of 34 mg/dL, and venous blood gas revealing pH 7.16 and pCO2 26 mmHg. On exam, he had diffuse bilateral rhonchi, without dermatologic findings. He was febrile to 104°F, tachycardic to 160, tachypneic to 48, for which he was emergently intubated. Given the patient's history, septic shock from alcohol-induced aspiration pneumonia was assumed and treated empirically with ceftriaxone, metronidazole, and azithromycin. He developed refractory shock requiring several vasopressors and a persistent high-grade fever that was unresponsive to internal and external cooling measures. The patient also had refractory acidosis, pH < 7, despite maximal ventilator settings and multiple intravenous infusions of sodium bicarbonate. Due to persistent febrile neutropenia, antibiotic coverage was broadened to vancomycin, cefepime, meropenem and micafungin. Within 24 hours of admission, the patient's multiorgan failure continued to worsen with laboratory studies consistent with disseminated intravascular coagulopathy and worsening renal and liver function. Microbiology studies revealed pneumonia PCR panel which isolated only Streptococcus pyogenes, and respiratory viral panel was negative. Preliminary blood cultures from admission grew gram positive cocci in chains, and at that point there was concern for STSS. Given his penicillin allergy, clindamycin and intravenous immunoglobulin therapy were promptly initiated for antitoxin effect. Due to the patient's significant hemodynamic instability and refractory acidosis, he expired shortly after treatment began and before further diagnostic imaging. Final blood cultures subsequently grew beta-hemolytic streptococcus pyogenes. DISCUSSION: There are few cases in the literature that describe STSS. The high mortality rate associated with STSS is due to superantigen production and that the diagnosis is often delayed or missed. This case highlights the importance of prompt recognition, source control, and aggressive treatment of GAS infections due to its potentially fatal complications. Due to the patient's rapid decompensation, he expired prior to source identification and control. CONCLUSIONS: This clinical case brings attention to the importance of the current and ongoing research on the virulence factors of GAS, improving diagnostic and treatment strategies, as well as potential vaccine development. Meanwhile clinicians should include STSS in their differential diagnosis for rapidly progressing septic shock refractory to aggressive resuscitation and broad-spectrum antimicrobial coverage. REFERENCE #1: Nelson GE, Pondo T, Toews KA, et al. Epidemiology of invasive group A streptococcal infections in the United States, 2005–2012. Clin Infect Dis. 2016;63(4):478–86. REFERENCE #2: Avire NJ, Whiley H, Ross K. A Review of Streptococcus pyogenes: Public Health Risk Factors, Prevention and Control. Pathogens. 2021 Feb 22;10(2):248. doi: 10.3390/pathogens10020248. PMID: 33671684; PMCID: PMC7926438. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Emily Boone No relevant relationships by Melissa Dominguez No relevant relationships by Fady Youssef
