N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (AC) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary hydatid cyst (PHC) is a rare parasitic infection prevalent in developing nations with high rates of animal domestication and limited healthcare resources, with limited occurrences in North America. While many cases are asymptomatic, the slow progression of cystic echinococcus (CE) or hydatid cysts can result in prolonged undetected infections, posing challenges for early recognition in non-endemic areas. This case emphasizes the importance of maintaining a high index of suspicion in individuals with a travel history and potential exposure, particularly when presenting with persistent pulmonary symptoms. CASE PRESENTATION: A 25-year-old woman, an active military member with a travel history to Africa, South America, and the Middle East, was hospitalized in the Middle East in 2022 for an ovarian cyst rupture and was incidentally found to have a left lower lobe nodule of unknown size. She reported exposure to various environmental hazards,including black mold, asbestos, poor sanitation, and demolition activities, during her deployment. The patient was readmitted six months later for ovarian rupture and pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). CT scan revealed a dime-sized fluid collection in the left lung. The patient experienced night sweats, chronic cough, and underwent various infectious tests, all negative except for strongyloidiasis antibodies. She was treated with Augmentin, doxycycline for PID, and ivermectin. Follow-up CT scans showed a 4.0 x 3.1 cm left lung rim-enhancing cystic lesion with thick nodular wall and central hypodensity and resolved surrounding consolidation, leading to a referral to pulmonary services. The patient underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage. Cultures revealed Group C Streptococcus and Enterobacter aerogenes, likely contaminants, and Echinococcus serology tested positive. Additionally, the patient was discovered to be 6 weeks pregnant. A week later, she was admitted for worsening symptoms and subsequent CT chest scan unveiled a complex left lower lobe mass with central cavitation, calcification, and fluid measuring 4.3 x 3.8 x 4 cm, accompanied by adjacent bronchiectasis. Clinical symptoms improved with appropriate antibiotics. A multidisciplinary care plan was proposed, involving pre-operative albendazole, thoracotomy with left lower lobectomy, and postoperative albendazole therapy for 3-6 months, coordinated among infectious disease, obstetrics and gynecology, and thoracic surgery. Due to the theoretical risk of teratogenicity in the first trimester with albendazole, the patient opted to terminate the pregnancy. Surgical pathology also confirmed Echinococcus infection. DISCUSSION: PHC can mimic various conditions, such as bacterial abscesses or malignancies, leading to diagnostic challenges. Positron emission tomography or computed tomography (PET/CT) may be utilized to rule out malignancy in complex cysts. While specific signs like the "doughnut sign" exist for hepatic hydatid cysts, no distinct sign is defined for PHC. Bronchoscopy, though used in some instances, carries a risk of disease dissemination; thus, serology plays a crucial role in diagnosis. Treatment strategies, guided by the World Health Organization's classification of cystic echinococcosis (CE), encompass surgical intervention, percutaneous management, drug therapy, and observation. A surgical approach, whether open or endoscopic, is recommended based on cyst characteristics and patient factors. CONCLUSIONS: Clinicians should maintain a high index of suspicion for PHC in at-risk patients and promptly employ serological testing for confirmation. REFERENCE #1: 1- Halezeroglu S, Okur E, Tanyü MO. Surgical management for hydatid disease. Thorac Surg Clin. 2012 Aug;22(3):375-85. doi: 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2012.04.004. Epub 2012 Jun 12. PMID: 22789600. 2- Yoldaş B, Gürsoy S, Budak E, Gülmez B, Ceylan KC, Çırak AK, Susam S, Güldaval F, Gayaf M, Şanlı B, Yazgan S, Sevinç S. FDG PET/CT signs of proven pulmonary hydatid cyst: is there any clue? Jpn J Radiol. 2022 Nov;40(11):1194-1200. doi: 10.1007/s11604-022-01296-9. Epub 2022 Jun 21. PMID: 35727457. 3- Mathew JL, Kumar K, Singh M. Pulmonary Hydatid Cyst Diagnosed by Flexible Fiberoptic Bronchoscopy. J Bronchology Interv Pulmonol. 2021 Apr 1;28(2):150-152. doi: 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000742. PMID: 33753704. 4- Beacom S, Nand P, Singh P, Harrison A. Isolated pulmonary hydatid cyst. BMJ Case Rep. 2023 Aug 4;16(8):e254876. doi: 10.1136/bcr-2023-254876. PMID: 37541696; PMCID: PMC10407385. 5- Center for diseases Control and Prevention. Parasites Echinococuss; https://www.cdc.gov/parasites/echinococcos (Accessed March 17,2024). 6- Moro PL. Echinococcus Treatment: UpToDate (Accessed March 17,2024). DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ioana Amzuta No relevant relationships by Lubaba Dahab No relevant relationships by Ifreah Usmaiel
