N2  - SESSION TITLE: Infections in Immunocompromised SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/06/2024 11:00 am - 12:00 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (TB) aortitis is an underrecognized complication of disseminated TB, rapid progression and fatal complications make early diagnosis and effective management necessary. No clear treatment guidelines exist, due to the rarity of the condition, however, prompt surgical and prolonged medical treatment is recommended. We present a case of a 49-year-old woman with TB aortitis complicated by dissection to increase awareness and provide a database for future research. CASE PRESENTATION: A 49-year-old woman, with a history of SLE, chronic kidney disease, hypertension and hypothyroidism was initially admitted for initiation of dialysis due to uremic symptoms. During her hospital stay, she reported having progressively worsening weakness, decreased appetite, generalized fatigue, cough, and unintentional loss of 15kg in the last 2 years. A screening QuantiFERON test came back positive given her presentation and high-risk travel history a CT chest was ordered followed by confirmatory CT angiogram which showed evidence of saccular aneurysmal dilation of the descending thoracic aorta measuring approximately 5.0 cm in caliber with a penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer, new since prior CT Chest done 8 years ago. CT chest also showed T9-T10 osteomyelitis, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and pulmonary nodules concerning for chronic mycobacterial infection. Differentials included infectious etiologies, including disseminated tuberculosis, malignancy, and autoimmune disease. Initial confirmatory tests such as AFB smears with PCR testing, AFB stain on vertebral body and mediastinal lymph node biopsy and WBC scintigraphy of thoracic aorta were all negative. However, given the high suspicion of disease the patient was started on RIPE therapy. The vascular team initially planned for intervention after two months of TB treatment. Unfortunately, following two weeks of treatment, the patient developed an aortic aneurysm rupture and underwent urgent endovascular repair. Subsequently sputum cultures, vertebral body aspiration cultures and hilar lymph aspirates cultures grew drug susceptible mycobacterium tuberculosis. DISCUSSION: TB aortitis is a rare diagnosis that can occur either due to intravascular or extravascular extension of infection1 with the most common mechanism of extension being direct extension from surrounding structures2, such as lymph nodes or bones1. Diagnosis is made by proven disseminated TB infection in the presence of an aortic aneurysm, the presence of contiguous infected structures like lymph nodes or vertebral bodies supports the diagnosis2. Additionally, chest CT Angiogram demonstrating aortic ulcers combined with intramural hematoma is supportive of TB aortitis1. Treatment is a combination of antimicrobials and surgical treatment, one study showed a survival rate of 87% in patients who received combination therapy, while none of the other patients who received only one of the therapy options survived[9]. Indicating a high index of suspicion and prompt initiation of treatment to be essential. The size of the aneurysm does not appear to influence the need for surgery6.Duration of treatment is controversial, with some studies suggesting lifelong medical treatment especially if endovascular repair is attempted[3,4,5]. Some studies also suggest the addition of steroids to reduce inflammation[7,8]. CONCLUSIONS: The rarity of the condition, in association with the rapid progression and the possibility of fatal complications, highlights the importance of early recognition and management. REFERENCE #1: (1)https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1942484/?page=7 (2) https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/327927 (3)https://www.annsaudimed.net/doi/full/10.5144/0256-4947.2006.56 REFERENCE #2: (4)https://www.jvascsurg.org/article/0741-5214(89)90006-2/fulltext (5)https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8377242/ (6) https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1628999/?page=3 REFERENCE #3: (7)https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0741521417300058 (8)https://www.thelancet.com/journals/a/article/PIIS1473-3099(12)70321-3/abstract (9) https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012369215505990 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Nana Yaa Ampaw No relevant relationships by Shruthi Kumar No relevant relationships by Seba Qaddorah No relevant relationships by Matthew Stutz
