N2  - SESSION TITLE: Polymicrobial Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis (CPA) is an occasional complication of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA), and the transitional mechanisms remain poorly understood. In some cases, CPA that complicates ABPA exhibits distinctive radiological features, including bronchiectasis, which is the most widely recognized complication of ABPA. This case report highlights an asthmatic patient initially diagnosed with ABPA. Despite receiving treatment, our patient progressed to CPA and experienced severe lung damage. In this case report, we explore the intricate interplay between ABPA and the development of CPA. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old man with a complex medical history including COPD, severe asthma, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA), and diffuse cystic bronchiectasis presented to the advanced lung disease clinic with complaints of difficulty breathing along with low-grade fever, night sweats, productive cough, and wheezing. He had previously been stable on a regimen of prednisone, fluticasone, umeclidinium, and omalizumab along with Mucinex and albuterol as needed. A chest CT revealed mid- and lower-lobe predominant bronchiectasis with tree-in-bud opacities. Omalizumab, previously discontinued, was restarted since the patient had previously responded to the medication. Six months later, the patient returned with wheezing, hoarseness, and a persistent cough with green-colored sputum that did not respond to antibiotic therapy. Levaquin was initiated, and testing revealed that his serum IgE level was elevated to 7000 UI/mL. CT at this time revealed features of CPA with cavitation and no obvious fungal ball. Further investigation revealed Pseudomonas, Aspergillus, and Acinetobacter in bronchoalveolar lavage cultures, and the patient started experiencing body aches, palpable fevers with chills, and night sweats. Treatment with voriconazole, levofloxacin, inhaled tiotropium, and budesonide led to remarkable recovery, and the patient currently remains stable with follow-up. DISCUSSION: Persistent cavitation lasting a minimum of 3 months is a defining feature of CPA. These cavities may or may not harbor a fungal ball (i.e., aspergilloma). While elevated Aspergillus immunoglobulin G (IgG) serology is nearly ubiquitous in patients with CPA, it is also present in patients with ABPA and Aspergillus bronchitis. Therefore, detecting increased Aspergillus IgG alone lacks specificity for CPA in this context, necessitating radiological or other criteria for a precise diagnosis. Furthermore, the prolonged presence of Aspergillus in the airways may contribute to the development of bronchiectasis. Patients with ABPA typically have airways colonized by Aspergillus to which they are sensitized, often accompanied by markedly elevated serum IgE levels, as seen in our patient. The reasons why some individuals develop complicating pulmonary nodules, cavitation, and pleural thickening while most do not remain unclear. Our observations indicate ABPA complicated by CPA may exhibit distinct radiological features. Characteristic findings include bronchiectasis, nodules—some with cavitation, lobar shrinkage with indrawing of extrapleural fat, aspergillomas, and bilateral pleural thickening. CONCLUSIONS: Preventing further lung damage involves starting antifungal therapy and minimizing corticosteroid use, as increased corticosteroid treatment may compromise the immune response and promote Aspergillus growth. REFERENCE #1: Dogra V, Sinha AK, Saxena R, Talwar D. Aspergillus march: from ABPA to aspergilloma to subacute invasive aspergillosis. Allergy Asthma Clin Immunol. 2016;12:64. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ashwini Arjuna No relevant relationships by Hesham Mohamed No relevant relationships by Ariba Moin No relevant relationships by Deepika Razia No relevant relationships by Wali Zaidi
