N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (K) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Pembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) that blocks the interaction between PD-1 on T cells and its ligand PD-L1 on cancer cells thereby enhancing the anti-tumour T-cell response. In 2021, pembrolizumab received FDA approval as a neoadjuvant treatment for high-risk, early-stage triple-negative breast cancer. Although side effects involving multiple organs like Gastrointestinal, endocrine, infusion-related, skin and liver have been most commonly reported, lung involvement is potentially rare and lethal with manifestations ranging from pneumonitis to interstitial lung disease. Case reports of pneumonitis induced after 4-11 cycles of ICI therapy for metastatic melanoma and Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) are reported. To our knowledge, we report the first case of Pembrolizumab-induced pneumonitis occurring within a day of chemotherapy for early-stage breast cancer. CASE PRESENTATION: A 59-year-old lady with a history of triple-negative breast cancer presented to our emergency department with shortness of breath for 1-day duration. Noteworthy to mention that she received the first cycle of Pembrolizumab, Paclitaxel and Carboplatin one day before the symptom's onset. On examination, she was distressed, tachypnic, was using accessory muscles for breathing and her O2 saturation was 82% on a non-rebreather mask. On auscultation, she had decreased air entry and crackles bilaterally. As she was being assessed, she became unresponsive and pulseless. After 2 cycles of CPR, she regained ROSC and was intubated on-site. On laboratory workup, sodium was 115, ABG on 100% FIO2 showed: pH 6.8, PCO2 29.1, PaO2 269, HCO3 5.9, Lactic Acid was 6.7, Legionella antigen in the urine was negative, Mycoplasma IGM was <770, respiratory pathogen panel was negative. Chest X-ray showed extensive airspace reticular infiltrates in both lung fields. Chest CT scan showed extensive ground glass parenchymal opacification and interstitial septal thickening of bilateral lung parenchyma. Given the acuity of her presentation, and the symptom onset a day after receiving Pembrolizumab, pneumonitis was diagnosed. Thus she was started on methylprednisone and within 2 days of treatment, she was able to be extubated, and a follow up chest x-ray showed clearance of the previously seen infiltrates. DISCUSSION: Monoclonal antibodies have revolutionized cancer treatment, but their side effect profile remains poorly understood. Therefore, vigilant monitoring, early diagnosis and prompt intervention for any adverse reactions are crucial. Several studies showed that Pneumonitis was more common in asthma/COPD patients and prior history of thoracic radiation in comparison to others. Patients with NSCLC have a higher incidence of immunotherapy-induced pneumonitis (4.7%) compared to patients with other solid organ malignancies receiving immunotherapy (2.92%). While steroids continue to be the primary treatment for pneumonitis induced by pembrolizumab, immunosuppressants such as infliximab or mycophenolate may be considered in advanced stages if symptoms do not improve or worsen. CONCLUSIONS: Increasing utilization of immunotherapy in oncology practice has transformed pneumonitis from a rare into a more common event. Diagnosing this disease can be challenging due to the variability in onset, clinical severity and radiological presentations, hence identification of common symptoms like cough and dyspnea and rapid intervention is important to reduce mortality. Special attention should be given to patients with underlying lung disease. REFERENCE #1: Tumeh PC, Harview CL, Yearley JH, et al. PD-1 blockade induces responses by inhibiting adaptive immune resistance. Nature. 2014;515(7528):568-571. doi:10.1038/nature13954 REFERENCE #2: Garon EB, Rizvi NA, Hui R, et al. Pembrolizumab for the treatment of non-small-cell lung cancer. N Engl J Med. 2015;372(21):2018-2028. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1501824 REFERENCE #3: Joohee Sul, Gideon M. Blumenthal, Xiaoping Jiang, Kun He, Patricia Keegan, Richard Pazdur, FDA Approval Summary: Pembrolizumab for the Treatment of Patients With Metastatic Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer Whose Tumors Express Programmed Death-Ligand 1, The Oncologist, Volume 21, Issue 5, May 2016, Pages 643–650, https://doi.org/10.1634/theoncologist.2015-0498 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Himaja Dutt Chigurupati No relevant relationships by Praneeth Reddy Keesari No relevant relationships by Sivaram Neppala No relevant relationships by Hamid Shaaban
