N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Reports Posters (A) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Penicillium is a fungi species that has classically been reported to cause hypersensitivity pneumonitis in cheese factory workers ("cheese worker's lung"), as well as with sausage production. Although rare, it can also present as a lung nodule. We present a case of penicillium in a patient found to have lung nodules and was later diagnosed with immunoglobulin deficiency CASE PRESENTATION: 67-year-old woman with a history of ulcerative colitis and chronic cough was investigated with a computed tomography (CT) of the chest due to her persistent cough, present for the past 10 years, producing thick yellow phlegm. CT revealed a centrally located solid nodule in the left lower lobe measuring up to 1.4 cm, with an endobronchial component involving basilar segmental branches. Positron emission tomography (PET) scan showed a low positivity for the nodule. Pulmonary function tests indicated mild obstruction and a slight reduction in DLCO, prompting a referral to thoracic surgery for planned left lower lobectomy. During the procedure, no endobronchial tumors were found, but prominent secretions were noted, raising concerns about an active infection possibly related to the mass, potentially a granuloma. The lobectomy plan was postponed, and acid-fast bacilli (AFB) cultures, along with bacterial and fungal cultures, were sent. A post-procedure CT showed nodule resolution with a small groundglass area and streaky atelectasis. Positive fungal cultures indicated penicillium species, possibly linked to the patient's gardening habits. Given the resolution of the nodule and symptom improvement, no treatment was initiated initially. However, a follow-up CT after four months revealed multiple pulmonary nodules, prompting a course of azithromycin and prednisone for three months. Subsequent CT showed a decreased groundglass nodule and signs of bronchiectasis with bronchial wall thickening and mucous plugging. Although initial treatment with azithromycin and prednisone provided some relief, the cough and sputum returned. Sputum cultures were sent for AFB, routine, and fungal testing, and the patient was restarted on azithromycin and prednisone, along with hypertonic saline nebs mixed with albuterol, to alleviate symptoms. Additionally, the patient had been diagnosed with IgA and IgG deficiency, considering immunoglobulin therapy, which she declined. DISCUSSION: We believe the patient's lung disease is a granuloma secondary to chronic exposure to penicillium species from her gardening, which exposed her to various inhaled antigens, forming a solitary nodule. Two prior cases in the literature report similar findings, with a Penicillium granuloma presenting as a solitary nodule, and Penicillium marneffei in an HIV patient presenting with a solitary pulmonary nodule – however, neither patient had a primary immunodeficiency. Our patient was found to have IgA and IgG deficiency, likely predisposing her to this infection, with coexisting bronchiectasis. Although prior studies have reported penicillium infection in patients with primary immunodeficiency, they presented with systemic manifestations, as opposed to solely pulmonary symptoms such as in our case. CONCLUSIONS: Clinicians should be mindful that if patients found to have lung nodules secondary to Penicillium species, underlying immunodeficiency conditions should be investigated. REFERENCE #1: Campbell, J A et al. "Cheese worker's hypersensitivity pneumonitis." The American review of respiratory disease vol. 127,4 (1983): 495-6. doi:10.1164/arrd.1983.127.4.495 REFERENCE #2: Liebler, G A et al. "Penicillium granuloma of the lung presenting as a solitary pulmonary nodule." JAMA vol. 237,7 (1977): 671. REFERENCE #3: Chang, K C et al. "Penicillium marneffei infection and solitary pulmonary nodule." Hong Kong medical journal = Xianggang yi xue za zhi vol. 4,1 (1998): 59-62.Santos, P E et al. "Penicillium piceum infection: diagnosis and successful treatment in chronic granulomatous disease." Medical mycology vol. 44,8 (2006): 749-53. doi:10.1080/13693780600967089 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ramsha Abbas No relevant relationships by Aiza Abbas No relevant relationships by Samia Nadeem No relevant relationships by Kenneth Wojnowski
