N2  - Background: Mycobacterium abscessus species (MABS) are nontuberculosis mycobacteria commonly isolated from the environment. MABS consists of three subspecies: M. abscessus subsp. abscessus (M. abscessus), M. abscessus subsp. bolletii (M. bolletii), and M. abscessus subsp. massiliense (M. massiliense). Of these, M. bolletii is rarely isolated, and its characteristics are very similar to M. abscessus, so the two are often evaluated together. In Japan, M. abscessus and M. massiliense are isolated at a ratio of about 6:4, making MABS the third most frequently isolated nontuberculosis mycobacteria (2%-3%) after Mycobacterium avium and Mycobacterium intracellulare. MABS infections are particularly common in the southwestern region of Japan, with Okinawa being a significant hotspot. Globally, multiple clones of both M. abscessus and M. massiliense are widespread. While most cases, overseas are from cystic fibrosis (CF) patients; cases in Asia, including Japan, primarily involve non-CF patients. Yoshida et al. reported that some clones are localized in Asia. Human infection is well documented, but the details of the infection mechanism remain unknown. Discussion: A survey conducted by Morimoto et al. in the residential environment of a patient infected with M. massiliense reported identical genomes of M. massiliense isolated from the environment (bathroom), bioaerosols in the environment, and patients, indicating the possibility of infection through bioaerosols. Interestingly, an experiment to recover M. massiliense from bioaerosols in the bathroom environment found almost no M. massiliense in the bioaerosols when the bathtub was filled with hot water, but a large amount during the hot water supply. This suggests that M. massiliense present in the pipeline may have been discharged by the hot water supply, with the large amount of bioaerosols generated by stirring playing a role. The patient in question was able to prevent reinfection by stopping the use of the bathtub after being treated for M. massiliense. There have been several reports of MABS infection in severely ill children and adults and patients with intractable neurological diseases in Japan. The published studies are by the National Hospital Organization Nishibeppu Hospital (Komiya et al.) and the Osaka Developmental Comprehensive Rehabilitation Centre (Takemoto et al.). Both are cases of infection (or colonization) in the hospital environment. In the former case, MABS was isolated only from bioaerosols around the patient, whereas in the latter case, MABS was isolated from various areas around the patient. Conclusion: Regarding human-to-human transmission, a whole genome analysis of 52 strains of M. massiliense isolated from patients at Nishibeppu Hospital found that they basically formed a single cluster, suggesting transmission within the hospital. Yoshida et al. reported that MAS-I has been associated with outbreaks in the past, but the Nishibeppu case belongs to MAS-II, indicating possible transmission for this clone as well. In environmental surveys conducted to date, MABS, including M. massiliense, have not been isolated from patient rooms, shared facilities, staff, etc., making it unclear whether the infection route is airborne through bioaerosols or cross-transmission through humans. There is no conclusive evidence that MABS can be transmitted directly from human to human. However, circumstantial evidence suggests the possibility of environmental-to-human and human-to-human transmission through bioaerosols. Further, field studies are necessary to clarify these transmission routes.
