N2  - Objectives Induced Sputum (IS) using nebulised hypertonic saline (HTS) is noted to be potentially useful to resume effective sputum sampling in people with CF (PwCF) who are unable to readily expectorate, e.g. those on highly effective modulator therapy. Following successful 2022 Virtual IS trials at Blackpool Adult Cystic Fibrosis Service (BACFS) we embedded IS as routine sputum surveillance for all who do not expectorate.We performed impact analysis, key focus being new and previous pathogen growth. Methods All selected were placed on at least 1 of 3 Induced Sputum Pathways determined by their current microbiological status: Non Pseudomonas (Non PsA), PsA/Other Chronic Pathogen (OCP) or Nontuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM). Frequency of IS within 12 months was 2 for Non PsA, 4 for PsA, and 3 for NTM. We compared individual patient 2022/23 IS results with their 2021/22 routine sputum/cough swab results. Results 30 PwCF (51% of population) placed on BACFS 12 month IS pathway completed 47 procedures by December 2023. Positive cultures obtained in 40% (n = 19) with bacterial regrowth in 90% (n = 17) of those; 47% (n = 8) PsA, 41%(n = 7) Staphylococcus Aureus, 6% (n = 1) MRSA and 6% (n = 1) Achromobacter. Mean number of days since last growth when compared with 2021/22 sputum/cough swab results is 540 (range 152– 1343). New pathogen isolation in 10%(n = 2) of the positive cultures; 1 PsA and 1 Mycobacterium Abscessus. We were also able to declassify 1 PwCF with long standing NTM following 4 consecutive clear cultures within 12 months. Conclusion Our study demonstrates that Induced Sputum is a robust way to identify newpathogens, regrowth of pathogens not grown for some time and for declassification. BACFS will continue to embed this Induced Sputum Pathway as routine sputum surveillance for those that cannot readily expectorate.
