N2  - Introduction Routine and exacerbation respiratory cultures are essential in managing children with Cystic Fibrosis (CF). However, patients on highly effective modulator therapy often struggle to expectorate sputum. Traditional induced sputum procedures are time consuming and require additional appointments, burdening patients and their families. This study evaluates the efficacy of a novel sputum collection method involving saliva accumulation in the mouth followed by a lowto high lung volume huff and cough, compared to routine cough swabs. Method Over a period of seven months, 70 children and young people (CYP) aged 6–18 years provided 100 samples via the newsputum collection method and cough swabs. All sputum samples exceeded 0.3mls although four were rejected by the microbiology lab for lacking mucoid or purulent content. All samples were cultured according to our enhanced CF protocol. Results The new sputum collection method yielded CF pathogens not detected in cough swabs in 17 cases. In three symptomatic patients, this led to a change in antibiotic treatment. Among 14 asymptomatic patients, 10 commenced antibiotics based on pathogen identification. Conversely, four asymptomatic patients had positive cough swabs but negative sputum results; two of these received treatment. Seven patients showed identical pathogens in both sample types. Pseudomonas aeruginosa was identified in four sputum samples and in two of these, it corresponded with cough swab results. Chi-square analysis showed a significant difference in bacterial detection between the new method and cough swab tests (χ2 = 5.03, p = 0.025), indicating superior detection rates with the saliva-based tests. Discussion Our technique for obtaining lower respiratory samples for culture was easy to teach and quick to perform in this age group. This approach has the potential to significantly improve respiratory sample collection for CYP with CF, minimizing patient burden while enhancing diagnostic accuracy.
