N2  - Objectives Since the initiation of CFTR modulators we have noticed a decline in the number of sputum samples we were able to obtain from CF patients during clinic visits and annual reviews. We have also noticed a general decrease in the number of pseudomonas positive samples. As a result of this we wondered if the positive effect the modulators were having on altering the properties of the mucus on patient’s lungs was also affecting our ability to sample patients effectively. We wanted to asses whether Induced sputum’swere a more effectiveway of obtaining accurate bacteria growth than a routine cough swab in the real world. Methods We started a small audit comparing the bacterial growth from a routine cough swab to the bacteria growth on an induced sputum performed on the same patient on the same day. In order to do this, we obtained a routine cough swab initially and then performed an induced sputum on patients over the age of six during their annual review appointment. Results To datewe have conducted 15 induced sputum’s, 12 of thesewere effective in producing a sputum sample. Out of these 12, 6 have grown a bacterium from the sputum sample but not from the cough swab. One patient grew a bacterium from the sputum sample and from the cough swab. Conclusion Our audit thus far is proving the effectiveness of obtaining Induced sputum samples from patients to facilitate the effective detection of bacterial growth and therefore optimise effective antibiotic treatment.
