N2  - Objective Mycoplasma pneumoniae (Mp) is an infrequently reported pathogen in Cystic Fibrosis (CF). Little is known about its prevalence and clinical significance in CF patients. Our objective was to review the incidence, epidemiology and clinical significance of Mp infection at the Northern Ireland Regional Paediatric CF Centre (211 patients). Method Retrospective review of respiratory samples from Jan’22-Jan’24. Data were collected from all Mp-positive patients. Results Mp was confirmed by PCR from nasopharyngeal swabs (median CT value 33.75) in 8 children. All reports were between Aug’23–Jan’24. Of these,1 had concomitant RSV infection and 1 adenovirus (within 3weeks). None had concomitant other bacterial infection. Epidemiologically, Mp was reported in 4F & 4M, median age 7yrs 2 month (range 1yr-12yrs 4 months). Genetics: 4 F/F, 1 F/R117H(5T), 1 F/D1152H(CF-SPID), 1 F/G551D & 1 N1303K/G542X. Six patients were on CFTR modulators (4 ETI & 2 Ivacaftor). Clinically, all patients were symptomatic including cough, coryzal, pyrexia, increased sputum production. There was 20% or more reduction in FEV1 in 3 patients. One patient was admitted for intravenous antibiotics and oxygen. FEV1 at end of admission was 7% below baseline. Radiologically, 5/8 showed X-ray changes, 5/5 showing bronchial wall thickening and 4/5 consolidation in the left lung. All were treated with azithromycin for 5 days, with 1/8 given azithromycin 1st line. Conclusion Mp infection has increased in the last 6 months, with most infections in the last 2 months. All patients have been symptomatic, with most having significant radiological changes. Our data mirrors that of increased incidence of Mp in children in Europe. It is important to consider Mp infection if a CF patient is not responding to standard 1st line therapies. Early detectionwith extended respiratory testing is key, as is PCR testing to confirm macrolide susceptibility. Long-term sequelae from these infections is yet to be determined.
