N2  - Objectives In response to improved health observed after initiating CFTR modulators in 2020, Cystic Fibrosis (CF) patients (Pts) questioned the necessity of nebulized antibiotics (NA), leading many to cease this treatment independently. Methodology We conducted a retrospective review of 22 CF Pts with chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) pre-CFTR modulators and from 2020 to 2023 who stopped or were highly non-compliant. Data included spirometry, microbiology results, bronchoscopy (bronch) findings, and Intra Venous antibiotic (IVA) usage. Results • Of the 22 Pts who stopped NA five subsequently re-isolated Pa during a follow-up period of 6–24 months. Three of these required an induced sputum (IS) to confirm regrowth. • In the 4 Pts who had over 2 years follow-up after stopping NA outcomes varied in Pa presence. One Pt had persistent Pa growth (6 Pa positive (+ve) sputum (S) and a Pa +ve bronch), one Pt had 4 S and 4 cough swab (CS) negative (−ve) for Pa and then one Pa +ve sample and two Pts remained -ve despite multiple samples being tested (8 −ve CS and 2 −ve S, and 5 −ve CS and 7 −ve S). • For the five Pts with Pa +ve samples thesewere obtained between 6–16 months after stopping NA (one Pt did not provide any samples in the interim year, other after 8 -ve samples). • The majority of Pts (19 out of 22) did not have any more IVA than they had required in previous years. • Among isolated Pa cases, two Pts chose not to restart NA despite advice to do so and three resumed NA. • We have not detected any significant fall in lung function in any Pts although there has only been a short period of follow-up. Conclusion Pts initiating CFTR modulators often decide independently to halt NA, with clinical deterioration notably absent. IS, proved valuable in confirming re-isolation in Pts previously testing -ve on S or CS. To date no demonstrated clinical decline in those opting to discontinue NA, even with persistent or re-isolated Pa but continue to monitor closely.
