N2  - Objective CF-HomeSpIT (ISRCTN86523335) is a prospective internallycontrolled interventional trial comparing pathogen yield from unsupervised early morning sputum-induction (SI) performed at home, with same day physiotherapy-supervised SI in clinic. In this preliminary qualitative analysis, we explore acceptability, tolerability, and success rate in producing a mucoid sample with home SI sampling, in the first 50 children recruited to date. Methods The CF-HomeSpIT Study Protocol and instruction leaflets can be found at [CF-HomeSpIT website]. In brief, on clinic day, patients are asked to produce an early morning SI sample before breakfast, using 4mls 7% hypertonic saline nebuliser during their normal morning physiotherapy session. To optimise the CoughClear in an unsupervised setting at home, patients are asked to COUGH, HUFF, COUGH, CLEAR THROAT, SPIT repeating this sequence at least 3 times during nebuliser treatment. The sample is brought to clinic. Patients/parents fill in a qualitative questionnaire. Results Median age was 11.4 yrs. Median FEV1% was 91%. 92% were on modulator therapy. 20% had a wet cough (see Table 1). [Formula presented] 46/50 (92%) patients produced a sample from home SI. Median duration for the home procedure was 10 minutes (range 5–20). 43/50 (86%) samples were described as sputum and 3/50 (6%) as salivary by the microbiology lab. 29/50 (58%) were additionally described as mucoid, mucopurulent or purulent. Tolerabilitywas high with no reported side effects. On a Likert scale of 0–10 (10 = difficult), fitting the procedure into the morning schedulewas rated at mean 3.1/10 (range 0–8) and 48/50 (96%) families were willing to do this regularly, with a median chosen frequency of 4/year from a given range of 1–6. Conclusion Unsupervised home sputum-induction on clinic day is acceptable to patients and could be incorporated into routine care. It is well tolerated, and successful in producing sputum in non-productive children age 6–18 years on modulator therapy.
