N2  - Objective Lower airway sampling has become increasingly challenging in the post-modulator era, particularly in paediatric patients in whom induced sputum or bronchial alveolar lavage sampling are considerably more invasive procedures than in adult patients. Consequently, we are reliant on oropharyngeal sampling or cough swabs for routine surveillance to identity Cystic Fibrosis (CF) respiratory pathogens. In this single centre study, we aimed to determine if cough swabs could reliably identify CF pathogens as compared to induced sputum. Method Paired cough swab and induced sputum sampleswere performed on paediatric patients aged 6 years and over, using a standard operating procedure. Patients were eligible if they were non-productive of sputum, previously isolated Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA), had bronchiectasis or in whom there was clinical concern. Results Paired samples were attained in 39 patients over a 12-month period, mean age 10.4 years (range 6–17). 15 organisms were identified on cough swab: 6×Staphylococcus aureus (SA), 5 × H. Influenzae, 2 × PA, 1 × Aspergillus fumigatus, 1x Group A streptococcus. 10 organisms were identified from Induced sputum samples: 5 × SA, 4 × PA and 1x Group B haemolytic streptococcus. Cough swab failed to detect 6 significant pathogens 3 × PA and 3 × SA [sensitivity 60%, specificity 81%; PPV 56.2%]. Conclusion In this study we show that cough swab has low sensitivity for detection of significant lower airway pathogens as compared to induced sputum. Unlike other similar studies, this study has shown a higher yield for bacterial pathogens on cough swab compared with induced sputum indicating a high rate of oropharyngeal contamination. Rigorous testing procedures and surveillance are essential for early detection and treatment of clinically significant organisms. Both methods of sputum sampling have their place and sample methods used must be chosen on a case-by-case basis.
