N2  - Objectives Research suggests sputum samples are more sensitive than cough swabs in detecting pathogens for children with Cystic Fibrosis (CF)1. Subsequently, local CF guidelines have been updated stipulating a sputum sample following a period of eradication of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) or Burkholderia cepacia complex (Bcc) if cough swabs are negative. Due to recent advances, children with CF are not always productive thus, obtaining a sputum sample can be difficult and may require induction2. Performing an induced sputum can be a traumatic experience for a child therefore, it is important to evaluate the need for it in practice. The aim of this retrospective review is to determine the sensitivity of sputum samples in confirming eradication of Pa or Bcc in children in comparison with routine sampling. Methods Children that had undergone eradication treatment for Pa or Bcc between June 2022 - November 2023 and required sputum sampling were included in this retrospective review. All non-productive children provided an induced sputum sample. Routine sampling was followed up for comparison. All samples were completed by a physiotherapist. Results 10 children were included in the review (male = seven), age range 16 months - 12 years (median five years three months). All 10 sputum samples were induced. Positive sputum samples were obtained for three children, of which two routine cough swabs were subsequently negative. Negative sputum samples were obtained for seven children, of which two routine cough swabs were subsequently positive. Conclusion The data determines that sputum sampling offers additional sensitivity in testing for eradication. However, false negatives have been identified, therefore sputum sampling should not be used in isolation. As induced sputum sampling can be traumatic for the child, future research is required to ascertain if alternative less traumatic methods for non-productive children are available in establishing eradication of Pa or Bcc.
