N2  - Introduction Detecting infection in people with (pw) CF based on culturing expectorated sputum poses new challenges, particularly in children and those on CFTR modulators (CFTRm). The James Lind Alliance identified this as the second top research priority, underscoring the urgency for prompt and effective treatment initiation. In our ongoing study, we seek to optimize sample collection for bacterial and fungal infections, initially comparing methods suitable for non-expectorating people in those who can produce sputum. Methods We prospectively collect and analyse eight different samples (saliva, cough swab, cough plate, throat swab, nasal swab, huff tube, gargles, and induced or expectorated sputum) from pwCF during routine clinic appointments. Sputum samples are processed on general and selective media in the Royal Brompton Hospital lab based on current CF standards; other samples are processed in a similar fashion in the research lab. Here, we focus on the detection of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in saliva; saliva is considered Pa+ if colonies are observed on pseudomonas isolation agar and test oxidase positive. All samples are stored for later accurate identification with MALDI TOF. Clinical datawere extracted from electronic databases. Results To date, we have recruited 30 pwCF (5–63 yrs; 14 male & 16 female). 19 (63%) were reported to have chronic Pa, with 15 (80%) on chronic inhaled anti-pseudomonal agents. From 24 sputum samples, the lab reported Pa growth in 9 (37.5%). From these 9 pwCF, salivawas Pa+ in 5; of the 15 people with negative sputum cultures, 2 were saliva Pa+ (chi2p < 0.05). Conclusion The era of CFTRm and reduced sputum expectoration demands a more thorough investigation of alternative sampling techniques. These preliminary data on saliva show some promise although more data are needed. This study is ongoing; results from larger subject numbers and across sample types will be available at the conference.
