N2  - Background-Aim: Tuberculosis is the most common cause of death in adults from a single infectious agent in the world. The most common agent is M.tuberculosis, but M.bovis is responsible for approximately 3 % of tuberculosis cases because is a zoonosis with a wide range of mammalian hosts (bovin pathogen). Primary infection in humans can occur by inhalation of aerosols, ingestion or handling of contaminated, unpasteurized milk. Objetive: To detect human tuberculosis cases due to M.bovis in Asturias during 2007-2023 to identify the agent and establish early treatment. Methods: Review of the clinical history of the cases reported to the Epidemiological Surveillance Service of Asturias during the years 2007-2023 because tuberculosis is an obligatory communicable disease. It was established as a case, the patients with symptoms or radiological data compatible with tuberculosis in any location and isolation in culture or detection of nucleic acid of a microorganism of the M.tuberculosis complex in a clinical sample with positive bacilloscopy. Results: In this period, were detected 36 cases of M.bovis from the total of 2748 cases of tuberculosis notificated in Asturias. 24 men and 12 women aged between 19-88 years, only 10 were linked with agriculture and livestock. Spoligotyping showed that these human cases were caused by 15 different strains which all the patterns except one, appear registered in the databases of cases detected in bovine cattle afected in central and northern Spain. 11 patterns were orphans in human spoligotyping database of Asturias and 4 strains originate human clusters with different sizes Conclusions: -Its important to eliminate the bovine and caprine TB by identifying and slaughtering animals that test positive because the control of the disease in livestock and the pasteurization of milk has drastically reduced the incidence of M.bovis infection in industrialized countries. -It would be significant to study the transmission of this pathogen through wild/domestic animals in urban environments and in patients with respiratory symptoms. -Urgent measures must be adopted in the event of a case and its contacts to prevent the spread of the disease to the rest of the community and a serious public health problem.
