N2  - Introduction: Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans (Aa) is a Gram negative coccobacillus native to the oral cavity and has been known to cause local infections such as periodontitis and other systemic infections such as infective endocarditis, soft tissue abscesses, and osteomyelitis. This case highlights the relevancy of considering disseminated infection from oral flora such as Aa when working up suspected malignancy, and validates prior reports of extraoral isolation of Aa in cases masquerading as malignancy, both radiographically and clinically.Case Report:A 49 year-old man with a history of cirrhosis presented from clinic for a mass on his back rapidly increasing in size over the preceding month. CT imaging revealed a right upper lobe consolidation that crossed the fissure within two adjacent lateral segments of the right middle lobe and superior segment of the right lower lobe (Figure 1). Patient was admitted to oncology service for expedited workup. On admission, patient was afebrile, normotensive, and saturating normally on room air. Laboratory evaluation was largely unremarkable aside from a slightly elevated alkaline phosphatase of 142. Exam was notable for a 7x5 cm firm, mobile mass on the patient's right scapula without palpable axillary or cervical lymphadenopathy, along with dental caries and significant plaque noted on oral exam. Biopsy and culture of the right periscapular mass was positive for Aa. Bronchoscopy with lavage and endobronchial ultrasound with lymph node biopsies was performed, all of which were negative for malignancy. Patient was discharged on Augmentin 875 mg BID for six months with follow up in pulmonary clinic scheduled for six weeks. Figure 1 Discussion: Pulmonary actinomycosis represents a diagnostic challenge, with differential diagnoses often including malignancy, tuberculosis, and pulmonary aspergillosis. In this case, the patient was admitted to oncology ward service for expedited workup of suspected malignancy. There are several cases published in the literature showing pulmonary actinomyces masquerading as lung malignancy, however this patient is unique in presenting with a periscapular soft tissue mass. The right upper lobe consolidation was an incidental finding seen on imaging as the patient oddly did not have unexplained weight loss or respiratory symptoms, such as cough, dyspnea, or hemoptysis. Several risk factors for Aa have been identified, including poor oral hygiene, which can be seen in this case. In summary, although overall global incidence of actinomyces is decreasing, pulmonary actinomyces continues to present clinicians with a unique diagnostic challenge and should not be overlooked. (Figure Presented).
