N2  - Introduction: Mediastinal lymphadenopathy is often associated with community-acquired and other forms of pneumonia. However, asymptomatic bacterial mediastinal lymphadenitis is relatively uncommon. Case: A 59-year-old female with a past medical history of developmental delay, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, obesity (BMI of 45), and SVC syndrome status-post stenting presented to the emergency department with complaint of increased swelling and fullness in the right side of her neck. She denied symptoms of chest pain, shortness of breath, fevers, chills, cough, sputum production, diaphoresis, or weight loss. White blood cell count was elevated at 13.87 x10∧3/μL. She underwent a CT angiogram of the chest which showed cystic parenchymal changes in the right upper lobe and enlargement of mediastinal lymph nodes in the right paratracheal region with the largest node measuring 4.2 x 2.3 centimeters. Subsequently, she underwent evaluation with bronchoscopy and endobronchial ultrasound. Bronchoscopy demonstrated moderate amount of purulent secretions mainly in the right lower lobe. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was performed in the right lower lobe with return of purulent fluid. Ultrasound examination of the right paratracheal space at lymph node station 4R demonstrated a large lymph node measuring approximately two centimeters in the transverse diameter. Multiple tissue samples were collected from this lymph node via transbronchial needle aspiration. The BAL culture grew more than 10,000 CFU/mL of Streptococcus pneumoniae. There was no growth of fungi or acid-fast bacilli (AFB). The lymph node tissue samples had light growth of Streptococcus pneumoniae and no growth of fungi or AFB. Cytologic examination of the lymph node specimens demonstrated acute inflammation but showed no evidence of malignancy. Notably, there was an increased CD4:CD8 ratio at 10:1. Further flow cytometry showed normal immunophenotyping results. The patient was eventually referred to the infectious diseases department and was started on treatment with amoxicillinclavulanate. Discussion: This case demonstrates a unique phenomenon of microbiologic evidence of bacterial pneumonia and mediastinal pyogenic lymphadenitis in the absence of associated symptoms or known immunodeficiency. It is worth noting that our institution also encountered a similar case of mediastinal bacterial lymphadenitis with methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus. The increasing prevalence of such cases may be partly attributed to widespread antibiotic use. Patients in such cases should also be thoroughly evaluated for underlying immunodeficiency as is currently being done for our patient. (Figure Presented).
