N2  - Pasteurella multocida, a Gram-negative bacillus found in the oral flora of felines and canines. It is well-known for causing cutaneous and soft tissue infections as a result of animal bites or scratches. While rare, inhalation of airborne contaminants from infected animal nasopharyngeal secretions can lead to primary lung infections. Invasive cases are more prevalent among individuals with underlying conditions such as chronic liver or lung diseases, active malignancies, and the elderly. Clinical signs range from coughing with hemoptysis to respiratory failure, with the illness displayed as pneumonia, tracheobronchitis, lung abscess, and empyema. Significantly, P. multocida infections often masquerade as symptoms caused by other pathogens, necessitating a high level of suspicion for accurate diagnosis and appropriate treatment due to the disease's severity and high morbidity rates. A 55-year-old male, previously a smoker and currently dependent on oxygen due to chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, arrived at the emergency department complaining of breathlessness over the past two days. He also reported feeling generally unwell and having a nonproductive cough. Upon examination, he displayed hemodynamic instability, severe respiratory distress, and diffuse bilateral rhonchi, although no cyanosis was observed. Due to the impending respiratory failure, he required intubation. A chest X-ray revealed significant bilateral opacities indicative of pneumonia. Arterial blood gas analysis indicated severe hypoxemia, CO2 retention, and acidosis. Despite testing negative for viral causes, his blood work showed mild leukocytosis, left shifting, and elevated inflammatory markers. LDH levels were within normal limits. A CT scan unveiled extensive consolidative and ground glass opacities, tree-in-bud nodules, and traction bronchiectasis in the lower lobes, along with large irregular thick-walled cystic changes in the lung bases, forming abscesses. Tuberculosis test and blood cultures came back negative. The patient responded well to empiric treatment, leading to extubation and subsequent nighttime BiPAP therapy. Sputum analysis identified Pasteurella multocida, which was sensitive to beta-lactam therapy, despite the lack of animal exposure at home. Immunoglobulin tests were conducted due to suspicions of atypical organisms, but the results were negative. Additionally, HIV screening yielded a non-reactive result. Under infectious diseases supervision, the patient completed the prescribed antibiotics, showing both clinical and hemodynamic stability. He was discharged home with plans for close monitoring. Pasteurella multocida pneumonia has been shown to cause severe pneumonia when discovered as a single or in combination with other pathogens. Management of Pasteurella multocida infectious disease requires collaboration among specialists to provide the best course of action for these patients.
