N2  - Introduction: Adenovirus is a common and self-limiting cause of upper respiratory infections and gastroenteritis in children and rarely leads to significant disease in immunocompetent adults. We present a case of adenovirus respiratory infection in an immunocompetent host complicated by acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) requiring extracorporeal life support (ECMO), systolic cardiac dysfunction and kidney dysfunction requiring renal replacement therapy. Case description: A 57-yearold female with hypertension presented to a local hospital with one week of fever, cough, and headaches. She resided with her son who had a self-limiting upper respiratory infection two weeks prior. On examination, she was tachypneic, confused, with bilateral crackles on lung auscultation. Initial workup revealed mild acute kidney injury, elevated inflammatory markers and multifocal lung opacities prompting admission for antibiotics with presumed community acquired pneumonia. On day 3 of hospitalization, a respiratory viral swab resulted positive for adenovirus, and she rapidly developed worsening hypoxemic respiratory failure and ARDS necessitating intubation and mechanical ventilation, along with new systolic heart failure and cardiovascular shock requiring vasopressor support. Owing to persistent hypoxemia and multiorgan failure, she was started on veno-venous ECMO with concomitant continuous renal replacement therapy and transferred to a tertiary care facility. CT chest revealed bilateral diffuse ground glass and consolidative opacities with bilateral pleural effusions. Bronchoalveolar lavage detected adenovirus and no other bacterial or fungal pathogens. On day 7 of hospitalization, adenovirus serum testing revealed >10 million copies/mL, thus prompting administration of one dose of cidofovir 2 mg/kg in the setting of CRRT. By day 14 of hospitalization, she underwent a tracheostomy and was switched to pressure support ventilation and was noted to have recovery of her systolic function. Repeat adenovirus testing revealed significant decrease with 9,000 copies/mL - but in concert with her persistent ECMO dependence, a second dose of cidofovir 2 mg/kg was administered. After 2 weeks on ECMO, she was successfully decannulated and discharged on day 35 of hospitalization on room air. At her one-month follow-up appointment, she remained on room air and was no longer requiring renal replacement therapy. Discussion: Adenovirus should be considered as part of the differential diagnosis of immunocompetent adults presenting with acute respiratory infection complicated with ARDS. Herein we describe the successful use of ECMO and cidofovir in management of severe adenovirus infection in an immunocompetent adult. Further study is warranted as there remains a paucity of data to guide usage of cidofovir in this population. (Figure Presented).
