N2  - By current conventions, the intronic 7T variant in the CFTR gene is not expected to cause cystic fibrosis (CF) when occurring intrans with a second disease-causing variant. The exception to this is when 7T is present on the same allele as the R117H variant, it is considered a variant of varying clinical consequence (VVCC). In contrast, a variant of the same polythymidine tract, 5T, is a VVCC when identified alone, and its classification can be altered when in-cis with R117H or certain TG-tract variants. In this study, we conducted a retrospective chart review of patients presenting to the adult pulmonology clinic with clinical features concerning for CF but with full CFTR analysis returning non-diagnostic. Within this cohort, cases were included if poly-T analysis revealed 7T homozygous, 5T/7T, or 7T/9T. This resulted in a group of 21 patients, with the majority being female (n=18) and white/Caucasian (n=18). The average age was 52 years old. All patients had either equivocal (n=14) or positive (n=7) sweat chloride tests. A majority of patients had adult-onset of chronic lung disease (n=17), which manifested as either frequent lower respiratory infections requiring antibiotic treatment and/or chronic cough and sputum production, and a subset of these patients had evidence of bronchiectasis on chest imaging (n=13). A majority had history of positive respiratory cultures (n=17), with colonization by Pseudomonas aeruginosa (n=4), non-tuberculous mycobacteria (n=6), Staphylococcus aureus (n=6), and Acinetobacter species (n=3), amongst others. Only two patients were being treated for pancreatic insufficiency, and two other patients had a history of pancreatitis. Overall, this study reveals a large cohort of patients who are either homozygous or heterozygous for the 7T poly-T polymorphism, a majority of whom had no other CF-causing mutations identified through full CFTR analysis. Within this group, a majority of patients have clinical features consistent with previously reported mild presentations of cystic fibrosis. These findings present contrasting data to challenge the current classification of the 7T variant as benign, but further work is necessary to elucidate the causality, such as in-vitro CFTR functional studies of 7T in the presence of CFTR modulators. Such studies could also guide utility of modulators as treatment in this group. Future work would also be necessary to explore if any CFTR dysfunction is attributable to 7T alone or if this association alludes to the existence of other cryptic variants occurring in-cis, such as in the situation of 5T and the TG-tract.
