N2  - INTRODUCTION: AGR-2 protein plays a vital role in the production of mucins, which are gel-forming molecules essential for regulating viscosity and elasticity of mucus. They are expressed mainly in the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts. Rare pathogenic mutations of AGR-2 gene can cause mucus barrier dysfunction leading to protein losing enteropathy. It can also cause airway mucociliary dysfunction leading to cystic fibrosis-like airway disease. We report a case of AGR-2 mutation in a child with severe gastrointestinal and respiratory symptoms who died early in life due to chronic respiratory failure. CASE REPORT: A 5- year-old boy who was diagnosed during infancy with failure to thrive and protein losing enteropathy thought to be due to infantileonset inflammatory bowel disease, which did not respond to treatment. Extensive evaluation, including whole exome sequence analysis revealed a homozygous variant in AGR-2 gene (c.349C>T; p.H117Y). No CFTR mutations were detected. The patient had an older sibling with the same mutation who died at 7 years of age with respiratory failure. Our patient was initially referred to pulmonology clinic because of chronic productive cough. He had pectus carinatum and diffuse crackles on physical examination. Chest CT showed bilateral bronchiectasis (Figure). He was treated with mucolytic agents, airway clearance, and azithromycin. Subsequently, he required numerous hospitalizations and PICU stays, due to recurrent episodes of respiratory distress and hypoxemia. One hospitalization was with necrotizing pneumonia and mediastinal fluid collection confirmed by chest CT scan (Figure). He was treated with broad-spectrum IV antimicrobial agents and drainage of the fluid collection with good response. Sputum culture was positive for pseudomonas and klebsiella pneumonia. He was hospitalized again with an acute adenovirus infection. Repeated chest CT showed stable bronchiectasis but worsening hyperinflation with mosaic attenuation suspicious for bronchiolitis obliterans (Figure). He was treated with high dose pulse steroid with minimal improvement. The patient's clinical status deteriorated steadily with worsening tachypnea, retractions, hypoxemia, and hypercapnia. He was started on BiPAP support. His respiratory status continued to deteriorate prompting the decision to perform tracheostomy for long term mechanical ventilation. However, the patient continued to have increased work of breathing with progressively elevated CO2 level up to 100 mmHg. Patient eventually died at 5 years of age with cardiopulmonary arrest. CONCLUSION: Rare AGR-2 gene mutations can result in severe protein losing enteropathy and chronic airway disease mimicking cystic fibrosis. Suspicion of AGR-2 related disorder should be raised in cases with negative CFTR mutations.
