N2  - Introduction: A variety of conditions are associated with bronchiectasis in children, including congenital anomalies, primary immunodeficiencies, post-infectious processes, and cystic fibrosis. Here, we present a young female with bronchiectasis and chronic sinopulmonary disease, ultimately found to have a pathogenic variant in the tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor 3 (TRAF3) gene, which has recently been described to cause an immune dysregulation syndrome.1 Case: A 17-year-old unimmunized female with history of post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans, bronchiectasis, and severe scoliosis presents to pulmonary clinic with chronic wet cough after being lost to follow up for 7 years. History is significant for multiple hospitalizations from 3-4 years of age due to recurrent pneumonia with empyema and respiratory failure, chronic cough, and failure to thrive. Family history is significant for death due to pneumonia illness in her mother and younger sister, and two brothers with chronic sinopulmonary disease. Physical examination was significant for scoliosis, fingernail clubbing, and poor growth. Pulmonary function testing showed severe obstructive lung disease without bronchodilator responsiveness. She previously had extensive work-up including normal sweat chloride, normal ciliary biopsy, normal laboratory evaluation with exception of mildly elevated IgG, and non-diagnostic primary ciliary dyskinesia. Chest CT was notable for scattered bronchiectasis and air trapping consistent with bronchiolitis obliterans. She was started on aggressive airway clearance, along with FAM therapy (inhaled Fluticasone, thrice weekly Azithromycin, and daily Montelukast). Symptom-driven exome sequencing revealed a heterozygous loss of function mutation in TRAF3, consistent with a diagnosis of autosomal dominant TRAF3-related immunodeficiency. Six of seven additional family members tested positive, two of whom are symptomatic for chronic sinopulmonary disease and bronchiectasis on chest imaging. Discussion: TRAF3 is a central regulator of immunity. In 2022, Rae et al described a novel immune dysregulation syndrome of recurrent bacterial infections, autoimmunity, systemic inflammation, B cell lymphoproliferation, and hypergammaglobulinemia due toTRAF3 haploinsufficiency. Reduced TRAF3 expression can lead to immune dysregulation with a predisposition to recurrent sinopulmonary infections and bronchiectasis. Though our patient's initial genetic testing was nondiagnostic, this panel was limited to 14 genes that had been identified at that time to cause chronic lung disease. Broad testing many years later revealed the diagnosis of TRAF3-related immune dysregulation, which has resulted in changes in care and management for the entire family. Therefore, reexamination of previous genetic testing and broadening the scope of genetic testing are key when familial disease and an underlying genetic cause are suspected.
