N2  - Mediastinal abscesses are extremely rare, evolve acutely and are potentially life-threatening if not aggressively treated (10-40% mortality). They are even more rare when atraumatic and isolated. We present a case of isolated, atraumatic mediastinal abscess in an otherwise healthy young male. 24-year-old male teetotaler and never-smoker without PMH, presented with 6-days of right-sided chest pain, worse with movement, exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, non-productive cough, chills and fever. Two-months prior, he travelled to Cancun, remaining at the resort without wildlife interaction. He denied prior fever, ill-contacts, weight-loss, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhea, headache or lymphadenopathy. Daily swinging pyrexia was noted (101-1030F), WBC 19.8, ESR 109, CRP 35.7 and negative blood cultures. CT chest (figure 1) demonstrated right upper and mid anterior mediastinal masses, bilateral lower lobe infiltrative/atelectatic changes with small pleural effusions, enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes and borderline splenomegaly. He completed a 5-day-course of Ceftriaxone/Azithromycin for community acquired pneumonia. IR-guided fine-needle aspiration (FNA) was negative for malignant cells but predominantly neutrophils and necrotic debris, indicating an abscess. Flow cytometry was negative and bronchoscopy without significant abnormality. Right anterior thoracotomy revealed fibrotic tissue at the location of the “mass” which was debrided, biopsied and abscess drained (100cc purulent material). Biopsies returned with fibroconnective tissue with acute/chronic inflammation, fibroblastic reaction and inflammatory exudate from the “mass,” while confirming inflammatory exudate from the abscess. Intraoperative cultures returned positive for Methicillin Sensitive Staphylococcus Aureus (MSSA) and he completed 6 weeks of cefazolin. Transthoracic, trasnoesophageal echocardiogram and oesphagram were negative as sources. Convalescent CT (figure 2) chest showed postsurgical/post-inflammatory changes and no new fluid collection. Mediastinal abscesses can be classified based on;a.Primary (direct extension) vs secondary (introduction via viscus perforation)b.Compartment (anterior, middle, posterior) c.Traumatic from chest trauma with aerodigestive tract perforation (iatrogenic, spontaneous or traumatic) vs non-traumatic (2 subtypes)i.Mediastinal extension: peri-visceral cervical-mediastinal fasciae ii.Blood or lymphatic dissemination They rarely develop without a traceable source of infection. Risk factors include; immunocompromised states, diabetes mellitus, for which common organisms are aspergillus, mycobacteria, nocardia, syphilis and staphylococcus (most common), however these factors weren't identified in our case. MSSA was identified raising concerns for skin/oropharyngeal origin, however examination and investigations did not support this.This case provided a unique learning experience to suspect mediastinal inflammatory involvement when no apparent origin can be identified. This should be considered when exhaustive investigations prove unremarkable as early diagnosis and treatment with antibiotics and surgical debridement significantly reduces mortality resulting in survival rates upwards of 80%. (Figure Presented).
