N2  - RATIONALE: An exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (ECOPD) is identified by a rapid onset of worsened dyspnea, cough, and increased sputum within 14 days. It commonly correlates with local and systemic inflammation, often triggered by infections or airway irritants. The airway microbiome contributes to the pathogenesis of neutrophilic inflammation in COPD. Previous research indicated alterations in the airway microbiome during exacerbations, yet further data is required. This study aims to investigate alterations in the airway microbiome during an ECOPD and assess their correlation with clinical outcomes. METHODS: Sputum samples were collected from COPD patients at Gangnam Severance Hospital between November 1, 2018, and May 31, 2023. Participants were divided into two groups as stable disease (SD) and exacerbation of COPD (E1). For the exacerbation group, additional samples were collected after two weeks post-recovery (E2) for microbiome analysis. RESULTS: Over the 54-month period, 38 participants were enrolled (17 in the SD group and 21 in the E group). No significant differences were observed between the groups regarding sex, age, BMI, or underlying diseases. Additionally, there were no significant differences in the z-scores of FEV1, FVC, or FEV1/FVC in the pulmonary function tests. In the 16S-rRNA amplicon sequence variant analysis, a total of 310 bacterial species across 131 genera were identified, displaying diverse distributions among individuals in both groups. A noticeable decrease in the strains of normal flora was observed in ECOPD patients (Figure A). The percentage of Neisseria genus exhibited notable distinctions among the groups (SD, E1, and E2; median [lower quartile; upper quartile]; 17.1 [3.6; 38.4], 2.6 [0.0; 7.4], and 0.3 [0.0; 12.1]; p = 0.015; respectively; Figure B). Specifically, N. perflava within Neisseria demonstrated a pronounced difference (6.1 [1.6; 37.9], 0.6 [0.0; 2.4], and 0.0 [0.0; 1.1]; p = 0.004; respectively; Figure C). Regarding the overall proportion of the Haemophilus genus, no significant differences were observed (6.0 [4.5; 9.0], 1.7 [0.0; 9.9], and 2.6 [0.5; 7.4]; p = 0.175). However, H. parainfluenzae notably displayed a significantly higher rate in the stable disease group (6.0 [4.4; 7.9], 0.8 [0.0; 2.1], and 1.9 [0.1; 7.4]; p = 0.032). CONCLUSION: Disturbance in the normal flora, diminishing non-pathogenic organisms, potentially contributes to ECOPD. Specifically, the interaction of normal flora such as Neisseria spp. and H. parainfluenzae with other pathogens could significantly impact the course of ECOPD. Understanding the biological mechanisms is crucial to grasp their influence on disease progression within the microenvironment. (Figure Presented).
