N2  - Pulmonary tuberculosis (TB), caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis, primarily affects the lungs but can also affect the CNS and cause bone disease. Though TB incidence has decreased in the US, challenges persist, especially in vulnerable populations and specific geographic regions. This report details a case of TB reactivation, encompassing its clinical presentation, diagnostic challenges, and treatment strategies. A 58 y/o male of Filipino descent with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus presented to the ED with a productive cough and night sweats for 6 months. One week before symptom onset, he returned from vacationing in Ohio. He also admitted to breeding pigeons as a hobby. He completed an outpatient antibiotic regimen for suspected pneumonia but with no improvement. In the ED, vitals showed 97% oxygen saturation on room air. Initial labs were remarkable for a white blood cell count of 11 and procalcitonin 4.88. He was admitted for pneumonia and started on IV ceftriaxone. Imaging studies revealed multiple bilateral cavitary nodules. He subsequently underwent bronchoscopy with resultant positive acid-fast bacilli (AFB) and fungal sputum cultures. His antimicrobial therapy was transitioned to rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for pulmonary TB as well as amphotericin B for suspected fungal infection. His hospital course was complicated by ARDS requiring intubation with mechanical ventilation. Further workup for HIV and viral hepatitis was negative. Over several days of treatment, his respiratory status improved. Reactivation of TB occurs in 5-10% of patients with latent TB and no underlying medical issues. This risk is significantly increased in patients with HIV or immunocompromised disease. Given our patient's unremarkable medical history, we suspect his illness to be a reactivation from a prior infection during his time in the Philippines. His initial travel history and pet exposure raised concern for blastomycosis and histoplasmosis infection. However, due to his pathognomonic CT findings in conjunction with a positive AFB smear, he was able to begin his multi-drug regimen early on during hospitalization. The AFB smear is a rapid and cost-effective TB test. Its sensitivity is reduced in patients with HIV primarily due to low organism burden. Alternative causes of cavitary lung lesions including cancer, mycotic infection, autoimmune disorders, and sarcoidosis must be investigated. Although our patient tested negative for HIV, his clinical picture was consistent with active TB infection. Further respiratory decompensation while on appropriate therapy would have warranted further testing with nucleic acid amplification for multi-drug resistance.
