N2  - INTRODUCTION Pericardial effusions occur with accumulation of excessive fluid in the fibroelastic sac surrounding the heart with risk of tamponade and compromised cardiac output. Causes include infectious (especially viral, bacterial, and fungal), inflammatory or auto-immune, malignant, traumatic, uremic, and idiopathic, among others. Without identification of causative factors, management may be limited to fluid removal. Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) can cause pericardial effusions but presents diagnostic challenges given its rarity in the United States. We present a case of MTB pericarditis in an immunocompetent individual without known TB history that eluded initial diagnosis. This case demonstrates the importance of maintaining vigilance for this infectious cause. Especially in persons with MTB risk factors. CASE PRESENTATION A 44-yearold African man residing in the US for seven years presented with two weeks of persistent malaise, fever, and abdominal pain but without weight loss, night sweats, chronic cough, or known MTB infection. Despite daily febrile episodes (> 103°F), vital signs remained normal and blood cultures and parasitic investigations were negative. An abdominal CT revealed a pericardial effusion; subsequent echocardiography showed preserved biventricular function (ejection fraction, EF, 60%) and a moderate pericardial effusion. Pericardiocentesis removed 500 ml of serosanguineous fluid. Repeat echocardiograms showed no re-accumulation of fluid but cardiac function worsened with moderate hypokinesis (EF 35%). Various infectious investigations were non-revealing, except for mildly elevated adenosine deaminase. Despite treatment for idiopathic pericarditis, febrile episodes persisted. Given his positive QuantiFERON result, anemia, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and unresponsiveness to prior antibiotics (ceftriaxone and doxycycline), empiric anti-TB medications (Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol) were initiated. Subsequent tests were positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis in sputum and pericardial fluid. He received a complete MTB treatment course and on follow-up he remained stable with a recovered cardiac function (EF 56%). DISCUSSION Pericardial effusions occur from various causes (above). In African endemic areas, MTB accounts for 70% of pericarditis cases. However, most US cases are idiopathic (>50%) with only 8% of cases from all infectious causes, mostly viral, and very few from MTB. Since the MTB infection rate is higher in migrant US populations versus US native born, these populations are at higher risk of MTB-induced pericarditis, yet this is not always appreciated during clinical presentations. Our case highlights the clinical challenge of diagnosing MTB-induced pericarditis and reinforces the importance of maintaining clinical suspicion in patients with MTB risk factors, especially since timely diagnosis improves treatment and reduces complication risks.
