N2  - Rationale: Patients with COPD-bronchiectasis overlap syndrome often have chronic productive cough, airway mucus plugging and pathogenic infections. We sought to describe clinical characteristics of patients with COPD-bronchiectasis overlap in comparison to those with idiopathic bronchiectasis enrolled in the US Bronchiectasis and NTM Research Registry (BRR), and determine if CFTR dysfunction is associated with the overlap cohort.Methods: We identified bronchiectasis participants from BRR with concomitant COPD as discerned by the treatment team at enrollment, and sweat chloride data availability. Participants with comorbid congenital and autoimmune etiologies of bronchiectasis diagnosis were excluded. Cohorts were stratified by 1) idiopathic bronchiectasis vs. COPD-bronchiectasis overlap and 2) COPD-bronchiectasis overlap with known sweat chloride levels. Demographics, clinical characteristics, and concomitant medications were compared across the strata using the Kruskal-Wallis and t-tests for continuous variables and chi-square and Fisher's exact tests for categorical variables.Results: Among 5872 consenting patients, 14.5% (494) exhibited COPD-bronchiectasis overlap, in comparison to 85.5% (2910) with idiopathic bronchiectasis. Those with overlap were older (70.5±10 vs. 67.6±12.3years), more often women (69.2% vs. 83%), with higher percentage of current or former smokers (69 vs 35%, median pack years 25 vs 10, p<0.0001), and no racial or ethnic differences. The overlap cohort displayed lower baseline FEV1 (62.0±22.0% vs. 79.0±20.7%, p<0.0001), greater bronchiectasis severity indices (8.8±4 vs. 7±3.7, p<0.0001), higher frequency of exacerbations especially exceeding four annual exacerbations (12% vs. 8%, p=0.005), and higher prevalence of Pseudomonas aeruginosa growth during exacerbations (27.3% vs. 19.4%, p=0.0002). No significant distinctions were observed in the presence of non-tuberculous mycobacterium, Staphylococcus, or Hemophilus between the two groups. The overlap cohort exhibited increased use of inhaled corticosteroids and anti-muscarinic agents, while no variations were noted in the usage of supplemental oxygen, mucoactive agents, or chronic macrolides. Additionally, only 17 participants in the overlap cohort had available sweat chloride data, of which six individuals had levels exceeding 30mmol/L, a value previously associated with acquired CFTR dysfunction, and none displayed pathogenic CFTR variants.Conclusion: Patients with COPD-bronchiectasis overlap exhibit heightened disease severity, frequent exacerbations, reduced lung function, and presence of pathogenic organisms, suggesting a phenotypic resemblance to patients with CF. This data represents one of the largest assessments of clinical characteristics of overlap syndrome to date, and confirms prior evidence of COPD-bronchiectasis endotype severity. The paucity of CFTR outcome data in the registry precluded further analysis to better understand the pathophysiological mechanism governing the development of overlap, but this remains a future direction of the work.
