N2  - Rationale Patients with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (bronchiectasis) are at risk of recurrent pulmonary exacerbations and progressive lung damage. Multiple comorbidities in bronchiectasis are common, contributing to increased mortality, exacerbations, and hospitalization. This study aimed to characterize the association between exacerbations and comorbidities among patients with bronchiectasis. Methods This study used deidentified claims data from the STATinMED Real World Data Insights database (1-Jan-2014 to 31-Oct-2022). Eligible patients were adults (≥18 years) with a bronchiectasis diagnosis (≥2 outpatient claims with a bronchiectasis diagnosis code dated ≥30 days apart, ≥1 inpatient claim with bronchiectasis as primary diagnosis, or ≥1 chest computed tomography scan followed by ≥1 inpatient claims or ≥2 outpatient claims for bronchiectasis). Patients with cystic fibrosis, pulmonary fibrosis, or sarcoidosis were excluded. Patients had ≥12 months of continuous data capture before and after the index date (earliest claim used to identify each patient with bronchiectasis). Exacerbations were any inpatient claim with a primary/admitting diagnosis of bronchiectasis, or any outpatient claim with intravenous antibiotic code or pharmacy claim for oral antibiotic (14-day supply). Associations between bronchiectasis cases with exacerbations and comorbidities compared with bronchiectasis patients without exacerbations or comorbidities were analyzed using logistic regression. Ordinal logistic regression estimated the odds of a bronchiectasis case having ≥1 exacerbation events; exacerbation frequency was categorized into 4 groups: 0, 1, 2, or ≥3 events. Analyses were conducted after controlling for pre-defined baseline characteristics. Results The study included 117,718 patients with bronchiectasis. After controlling for baseline variables, odds of higher exacerbation frequency were 2.0-3.5 times higher in patients with cough, primary ciliary dyskinesia, graft versus host disease (GVHD), history of immune deficiency, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease [COPD], or pulmonary tuberculosis compared with patients with bronchiectasis without the respective symptom or comorbidity (Table; all P<0.0001). Odds of higher exacerbation frequency were 1.8 times higher in patients with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (P<0.0001). Additionally, compared with those without exacerbations, patients with any exacerbations had higher odds of having COPD (odds ratio [95% CI] 2.76 [2.69- 2.83]), alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (3.89 [3.10-4.89]), cough (3.08 [3.01-3.15]), GVHD (3.95 [2.96-5.27]), and pulmonary tuberculosis (4.26 [3.62-5.02]). Certain comorbidities were associated with >3.0 times higher odds of exacerbations leading to hospitalization, particularly AAT deficiency, GVHD, and pulmonary tuberculosis (all P<0.0001). Conclusions For patients with bronchiectasis, cough and hemoptysis symptoms, and comorbidities including COPD and history of immune deficiency, were associated with a higher frequency and incidence of exacerbations, including those that involved a hospitalization.
