N2  - Introduction: Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage (DAH) is a life-threatening condition which occurs with increased frequency in children with Trisomy 21 (T21). There is paucity of data on DAH in the T21 population, but several case reports suggest the most common diagnosis is idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis. We describe the case of DAH in a child with T21 which resulted in the diagnosis of non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). Case: A nine-year-old female with T21 with history of atrial and ventricular septal defect repair in infancy presented with hemoptysis after two weeks of cough. She developed hypoxemic respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation. Her hemoglobin was 7.5 g/dL with diffuse pulmonary opacities on initial imaging consistent with DAH. Her physical exam was notable for prominent clubbing of all digits, raising suspicion for underlying cardiac or lung disease. Results were significant for Rhinovirus on a viral panel and Haemophilus influenzae on blood cultures. She was treated with IV cefepime and vancomycin. A comprehensive rheumatologic workup and intravenous immunoglobulin therapy was initiated. She showed limited improvement despite these treatments and failed extubation on hospital day fifteen with worsening pulmonary bleeding, prompting additional workup. Echocardiogram was normal, but a CT scan of the chest demonstrated concern for DAH on a background of long-standing interstitial lung disease (ILD). Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) studies grew methicillin-susceptible staphylococcus aureus (10,000 - 100,000 cfu/mL) in both lungs. Given return of fevers and persistence of bleeding despite targeted antibiotics, a lung biopsy was planned to evaluate for capillaritis. Lung biopsy showed diffuse lymphocytic inflammation with moderate fibrosis favoring a diagnosis of NSIP (Figure 1). Simultaneous infectious workup revealed herpes simplex virus (HSV) IgG in the serum and HSV on BAL which was thought to be reactivation, but she was treated for HSV pneumonitis. She responded well to a prolonged course of steroids and ultimately was extubated on hospital day 29. Discussion: This case presented challenges from a diagnostic and management perspective. The acuity of presentation raised suspicion for an infectious etiology, but the limited response to multiple targeted antimicrobials along with the degree of clubbing suggested an underlying chronic lung disease was present. The pathologic diagnosis of NSIP supports the suspicion of an immunodeficiency or immune dysregulation associated ILD, which has a higher frequency in T21. This diagnosis in combination with her numerous infectious etiologies complicated the timing and safety of steroid therapy and warranted further work up with pursuit of histopathologic diagnosis.
