N2  - Introduction: Children's interstitial lung disease (chILD) encompasses genetic, infectious and inflammatory disorders that present with respiratory symptoms and diffuse abnormalities on lung imaging. We describe a case of an infant with chILD, who presented diagnostic and management challenges, and ultimately was diagnosed with an immunodeficiency. Case Report: A male infant born prematurely at 34 4/7 weeks via C-section for severe preeclampsia had a 27-day NICU course during which he required CPAP for 1 week. He had intermittent tachypnea which resolved with furosemide dose. CXRs showed subsegmental atelectasis, echocardiography was normal and he was discharged home on room air. At home, he developed worsening cough leading to ED presentation at 4.5 months with fever and tachypnea. Lungs were clear to auscultation, SpO2 was 89%, CXR showed bilateral hazy pulmonary opacities, WBC counts were elevated and respiratory viral panel was negative. He was admitted to the PICU and treated with high flow nasal cannula 8 LPM FiO2 40% and antibiotics. His course was notable for tachypnea, and poor weight gain despite 150-170 kcal/kg/day. ABGs showed adequate ventilation and minimal A-a gradient. Respiratory support escalated to non-invasive neurally adjusted ventilatory assist (NAVA). Chest CT showed bilateral diffuse hazy ground glass opacities with lower lobe predominance[figure1]. Lymphocyte subset analysis and quantitative immunoglobulins were within normal limits. Hydroxychloroquine, anti-inflammatory dose azithromycin and pulse steroid therapy were started for presumed chILD. Flexible bronchoscopy was unremarkable but bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid grew P. aeruginosa. Lung biopsy did not reveal alveolar structure or surfactant protein abnormality but showed pneumonia with chronic remodeling. Genetic testing for chILD (Invitae) was negative. Given atypical organism cultured from BAL, an oxidative burst assay was performed (DHR) and revealed absent neutrophil function. Whole exome sequencing (WES) detected pathogenic variant in CYBB gene, confirming chronic granulomatous disease (CGD). Hence, prophylaxis with bactrim and itraconazole was started. Due to poor weight gain and persistent tachypnea a tracheostomy was performed. Discussion: This infant had signs and symptoms suggestive of chILD. However, when bronchoalveolar lavage grew P. aeruginosa, suspicion for immunodeficiency arose and CGD was confirmed by DHR and WES. Pulmonary manifestations are commonly seen in CGD and secondary interstitial lung disease often develops later due to recurrent pneumonia and inflammation. Our presentation was atypical due to its early onset and severity, requiring persistent non-invasive support and ultimately tracheostomy and chronic mechanical ventilation. Immunodeficiencies should be considered in infants with chILD presentation and negative genetic testing.
