N2  - Tuberculosis (TB) remains a global health concern and is a leading cause of death due to infectious diseases worldwide. Tuberculous pleural effusion (TPE) is the second most common extra-pulmonary presentation of TB. In this report, we present an unusual case of TPE with negative adenosine deaminase (ADA) levels and a predominantly neutrophilic pleural effusion, diagnosed through pleural fluid Mycobacterium tuberculosis polymerase chain reaction (MTB-PCR). This case is notable for its non-resolving pleural effusion following TB treatment. A 30-year-old male from Venezuela, with a history of latent syphilis and HIV (CD4: 283) on antiretroviral therapy, presented with intermittent dry cough and dyspnea on exertion for 3 weeks. He denied fevers, chills, night sweats, or weight loss. No sick contacts or recent travel were reported, with no history of prior tuberculosis treatment. On arrival, he exhibited tachycardia (heart rate: 105) and decreased breath sounds on the left side. Laboratory evaluation revealed a white blood cell count of 7,000 cells/ml, and the basic metabolic panel was within normal limits. Chest x-ray and computed tomography (CT) revealed a left-sided pleural effusion and pleural fluid (PF) analysis met the criteria for an exudative effusion (PF protein: 5.6, LDH 573, Glucose 14; Serum protein: 8.4, LDH: 125), with neutrophilic predominance (WBC 2,739, PMN 89.6 %). The serum QuantiFERON test returned positive. Although PF ADA levels were normal, PF MTB-PCR was performed due to a high suspicion of TPE and yielded a positive result. Sputum smears did not reveal any acid-fast bacilli (AFB), and no mycobacterial growth was observed in blood or pleural fluid cultures. The patient completed 9 months of RIPE therapy; however, upon follow-up, the left pleural effusion persisted despite the appropriate treatment. Consequently, the patient was referred to thoracic surgery for pleuroscopy, and underwent left pleural biopsy which revealed dense fibrotic tissue with chronic inflammation and extensive necrosis. Cytology tests were negative for malignancy, and AFB staining showed no mycobacteria. Non-resolving pleural effusion following adequate tuberculosis treatment poses a diagnostic and therapeutic challenge. In this case, the pleural effusion did not resolve as expected despite receiving appropriate therapy. This case serves as a reminder that tuberculosis can manifest with atypical responses that require careful evaluation. It's essential to acknowledge the limitations of the initial diagnosis, which relied solely on pleural MTB-PCR. The prognosis of our patient remains uncertain, and close monitoring may be necessary due to concerns about drug resistance or superimposed infections. (Figure Presented).
