N2  - Introduction: Immunocompromised patients face an increased risk of invasive fungal infections. Rhizopus can lead to severe pulmonary infection, necessitating potent antifungals and surgical debridement. We present a case of Rhizopus-associated right middle lobe occlusion in a lung transplant patient, managed with bronchoscopic 1.1 mm cryoprobe debridement. Case Presentation: A 67-year-old male with a history of double lung transplant for nonspecific interstitial pneumonia, presented to the post-lung transplant clinic for an 8-week follow-up. He underwent a CT chest for a persistent productive cough, which showed a right perihilar middle lobe consolidation and peripheral peribronchovascular nodularity. On admission, he received meropenem and linezolid due to concern for bacterial pneumonia. Isavuconazole was changed to voriconazole and micafungin, and other prophylaxis was continued. His tacrolimus dose was reduced, and mycophenolate was held. Bronchoscopy revealed pseudomembrane with eschar formation in the RML lateral segment and a focal area of pseudomembrane in the RLL lateral segment. BAL cultures grew Rhizopus species. Bronchoscopic biopsy of the RML showed evidence of mucormycosis. Thus, voriconazole was changed back to Isavuconazole, and Amphotericin was added. CT Chest angiogram on day 12 showed no changes from previous scans. Repeat bronchoscopy, on days 14 and 17 revealed a black pseudomembrane with white-and-black tissue occluding the RML. The Interventional Pulmonology team was consulted and a 1.7 mm cryoprobe was used to debride the pseudomembrane area. Despite these interventions, repeat CT scans revealed worsening consolidation. The culture of the RML tissue eventually grew ESBL E. coli and VRE. Consequently, Linezolid and ertapenem were added to the existing antifungal regimen. Since then, the patient has improved and his clinical status has been relatively stable, with repeat imaging showing improvement in the RML opacification. Discussion: Pulmonary Rhizopus, a severe fungal infection that frequently occurs in immunosuppressed patients. Accurate diagnosis can be challenging, requiring intricate tissue sampling. Treatment involves administering IV antifungals and conducting surgical debridement. In our case, both diagnosis and debridement were successfully conducted using bronchoscopic techniques. It is crucial to consider mucor and other fungal infections in immunosuppressed individuals presenting with pneumonia-like symptoms and concerning CT results. Early bronchoscopic debridement can enhance outcomes and potentially avert the need for surgical intervention.
