N2  - Introduction: Anti-synthetase Syndrome is uncommon and an underrecognized cause of interstitial lung disease (ILD). It is characterized by ILD, myositis, and arthritis in addition to antibodies against anti-aminoacyl t-RNA synthetase. The clinical presentation can be variable, and diagnosis is often delayed due to specialized testing needed to identify auto-antibodies. We present a rare case of anti-synthetase syndrome that presented as a rapidly progressive ILD in a 50-year-old female associated with anti-PL-12 antibodies. Case: A 50-year-old Spanish-speaking female presented with a month of dyspnea on exertion and cough. A chest X-ray revealed bilateral airspace opacities. She was diagnosed with atypical pneumonia and prescribed a course of azithromycin without improvement. She re-presented 3 weeks later with worsening symptoms and was hospitalized. Additionally, she reported fatigue, proximal muscle weakness, sicca symptoms, and joint pain. Her initial labs were remarkable for an elevated D-dimer to 0.954 mcg/ml, CK of 43 U/L, aldolase of 4.9 U/L, CRP of 1.9 mg/dL and ESR of 53 MM/HR. A CT chest showed diffuse ground glass opacities bilaterally. A broad infectious work-up was sent for bacterial, viral, and fungal respiratory illnesses which returned unremarkable. Auto-immune work-up was also sent. An echocardiogram showed a normal ejection fraction. Bronchoscopy was discussed, but the decision was made to defer while a non-invasive work-up was pending. She was discharged on home oxygen while the autoimmune work-up was pending, and pulmonary follow-up was scheduled. After discharge, the results of her testing revealed a cytoplasmic titer elevated to 1:1280 in a speckled pattern, an elevated SSA (Ro Ab) at 184 AU/mL, and a positive PL-12 (alanyl-tRNA synthetase) antibody on extended myositis panel. A CT scan performed one-month post-discharge showed basilar fibrosis and persistent ground glass opacities. Unfortunately, she has not attended the scheduled follow-up pulmonology appointment to initiate treatment. Discussion: Anti-synthetase syndrome associated with anti-PL-12 antibodies is rare and only occurs in 2% of cases of myositis. Lung involvement is more common in this subtype, compared to Anti-Jo-1 related anti synthetase syndrome, and patients can present with an isolated rapidly progressive ILD. A high clinical suspicion is needed to prompt an extended myositis panel to make a timely diagnosis and prevent irreversible pulmonary fibrosis. This case highlights healthcare disparities for non-English speaking patients who are lost to follow-up in the diagnostic stages due to diagnostic delays and communication barriers.
