N2  - Introduction: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP) is a type of pneumonia caused by the unicellular eukaryote P. jirovecii, often affecting immunocompromised individuals. PJP can present with various radiographic manifestations, most commonly as diffuse ground-glass opacities in a bilateral and predominantly perihilar distribution. Other radiographic features can include interstitial markings, thin-walled cysts, and unilateral pulmonary infiltrate with areas of lung condensation mimicking bacterial pneumonia. In this case, we present an asymptomatic patient found to have PJP on biopsy from a solitary lung nodule with no additional abnormalities on chest imaging. Case Presentation: 71-year-old male with history of NASH cirrhosis complicated by hepatocellular carcinoma, status post orthotopic liver transplant (CMV-/-, EBV +/+) on Myfortic and Tacrolimus, IgA nephropathy complicated by chronic kidney disease, and hypertension initially seen in pulmonary clinic for evaluation of pulmonary nodules. He underwent routine computed tomography (CT) as part of HCC surveillance that demonstrated a left upper lobe mixed ground glass and nodular opacity that was initially identified one year prior but had rapidly increased in size, now measuring up 2.7 cm. Patient denied any respiratory symptoms including dyspnea, cough, fevers or chills. He underwent navigational bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsies of the left upper lobe nodule as well as bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) of the left upper lobe. Pathology from transbronchial samples demonstrated dense histiocytic infiltration with GMS, PAS and mucin stains showing multiple organisms, many of which demonstrate cup like shape with morphology consistent with pneumocystis jirvoveci. Cytology from BAL did not show pneumocystis but possible fungal yeast forms. Following diagnosis, the patient was treated with atovaquone for three weeks followed by trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole prophylaxis which had been discontinued in the post-transplant period secondary to the development of cytopenia. The patient underwent follow up CT chest imaging two months later that showed decrease in size of the left upper lobe nodule. Discussion: In immunodeficient patients, PJP classically presents as bilateral, diffuse ground glass opacities with airspace filling and uncommonly presents as a single lung nodule on CT. There are case reports of lung nodules in patients with a granulomatous response to PJP. In our patient, his initial radiographic presentation of a mixed ground glass and semi-solid nodule increasing in size over time was most concerning for malignancy, especially in a liver transplant recipient. This case highlights the need for clinicians to consider atypical infections as a cause of lung nodules in certain populations.
