N2  - Introduction: Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) is a rare genetic disease characterized by diminished mucociliary clearance and recurrent sino-oto-pulmonary infections. Neutrophil Extracellular Traps (NETs), web-like structures composed of DNA, histones, and antimicrobial peptides, are released by neutrophils during bacterial infections. While NETs play a critical role in trapping and neutralizing pathogens, their excessive release can also contribute to tissue damage and inflammation, highlighting the complex dual nature of these structures in the host defense response. Previous studies suggest that neutrophils from PCD patients exhibit reduced capacity for NETs release and a diminished antimicrobial capacity. However, these observations are primarily in response to a non-physiological stimulus commonly employed but not representative of relevant NETs triggers during bacterial infections, such as Phorbol 12-myristate 13-acetate (PMA). Case Description: We present the case of a 55-year-old male with a history of recurrent pulmonary infections dating back to childhood. The presenting symptoms included a chronic cough, sinusitis, and hearing loss. Cultures revealed multi-drug-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonization. Pulmonary function testing showed an FEV1 of 42%, indicating compromised lung function, and chest CT showed bilateral moderate to severe bronchiectasis. Genetic analysis identified a homozygous RSPH4A gene mutation (c.921+3-921+6del, intronic), with baseline nasal nitric oxide (nNO) levels at 32 nL/min, all consistent with PCD diagnosis. Circulating neutrophils were isolated from the patient, cultured, and stimulated using varying concentrations of PMA or lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Live neutrophil imaging was used to quantify NETs in real-time. Surprisingly, the patient with PCD exhibited significantly elevated levels of NETs release in response to LPS compared to a sex-age-matched healthy control (Figure 1A, P = 0.001). Conversely, consistent with previous studies, when stimulated with PMA, the healthy control exhibited significantly higher NETs release compared to the patient with PCD (Figure 1B, P = 0.03). Discussion: These findings suggest that the previously described diminished NETs release capacity in PCD may be stimulus-specific, as observed in response to PMA, rather than reflective of a widespread dysfunction in neutrophil antimicrobial capacity among PCD patients. Moreover, the patient's neutrophils exhibited amplified responsiveness to LPS, a relevant bacterial stimulus. Aligning with the disease's pathophysiology, excessive NETs release can potentially contribute to lung damage, increased inflammation, and mucus thickness, possibly contributing to the clinical presentation of PCD in this case. Therefore, our data suggest that gaining a comprehensive understanding of neutrophil functional responses in PCD may be crucial for effective management and the development of innovative therapeutic strategies.
