N2  - IntroductionTreatment failure in tuberculosis (TB) patients is a critical concern, often attributed to factors like non-adherence, drug resistance and medical co-morbidities such as diabetes mellitus (DM). Here, we present two cases of TB treatment failure where whole-genome sequencing (WGS) provided valuable insights into the underlying causes. Both patients had cavitary pan-sensitive TB and newly diagnosed uncontrolled DM, and were initiated on Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Ethambutol, and Pyrazinamide (RHEZ), with treatment compliance under direct-observed therapy (DOT).Case Report 1:A 46-year-old Javanese female was diagnosed with TB and DM (HBA1C 10.6%) on preemployment screening. She initially sputum-converted but at month 5, sputum cultures turned positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex (MTC) with resistance to rifampicin and isoniazid WGS revealed a genetic match (0 SNPs apart) between the baseline MTC strain and the resistant strain, indicating within-host evolution with acquired resistance.Case Report 2:A 52-year-old Burmese male was diagnosed with TB on CXR following symptoms of cough and incidentally diagnosed with DM (HbA1C 11.9%). His cough recurred at month 5 despite initial sputum conversion. Sputum testing showed rifampicin and isoniazid resistant MTC. On WGS, the MTC strains did not match, and there was no heterogenous infection at baseline. This suggested a new infection with multidrug-resistant (MDR) strain rather than within-host evolution. Discussion:WGS is a useful tool in understanding the complexities and underlying mechanisms of TB treatment failure.Within-Host Evolution: The first case highlighted the emergence of drug resistance within the same patient despite DOT compliance. WGS showed a genetic match between the initial and resistant strains signifying within-host evolution of drug resistance. This underscores the importance of close monitoring in diabetic patients.Exogenous Reinfection: In the second case, WGS revealed a new MDR strain of infection. This emphasized the risk of exogenous reinfection in DM patients and the potential need for stricter infection control measures, especially in TB-prevalent settings.DM has been shown to increase intra-patient MTC mutation rate, contributing to drug resistance Additionally, it can heighten the risk of exogenous reinfection. Cavitary disease is also a recognized risk factor for treatment failure.In conclusion, these cases highlighted the diverse mechanisms of TB treatment failure in DM patients and the role of WGS. The findings emphasize the need for individualized treatment approaches, stringent infection control measures, and heightened vigilance when managing TB in patients with DM .
