N2  - Background: Acinetobacter pittii (A. pitti) is a rare pathogen typically associated with nosocomial infections. (1) We present an unusual case of community-acquired cavitary pneumonia caused by A. pitti in an immunocompetent patient. Case Presentation: A 60-year-old immunocompetent Caucasian male with a history of benign adrenal adenoma presented to the hospital with a nonproductive cough and night sweats lasting for three weeks. Initial investigations revealed a normal white cell count, procalcitonin level, and c-reactive protein. A chest X-ray demonstrated bilateral round opacities, raising concern for metastatic disease. Computed tomography scan of the chest revealed multiple bilateral cavitatory lesions. Bronchial lavage analysis later confirmed the presence of A. pitti. All other diagnostic tests, including vascultic screen, acid-fast bacilli, atypical pneumonia screen, blood-borne virus screen, immunoglobulins, aspergillus markers, as well as blood and urine cultures, were negative. The patient improved significantly after receiving eleven days of antimicrobial treatment with piperacillin/tazobactam, followed by seven days of oral co-amoxiclav. Follow-up chest X-ray after six weeks showed significant improvement. Discussion: A.pittii is increasingly recognized as a causative agent of nosocomial infections, commonly observed in intensive care units and associated with aspiration pneumonia, catheter-associated bacteremia, soft tissue, and urinary tract infections. (2) However, its occurrence in community-acquired infections, particularly leading to cavitary pulmonary disease, is rare. Our patient's diagnosis was supported by positive bronchial lavage results for A. pitti with clinical and radiological improvement following beta-lactam therapy. The first reported case of community-acquired A. pitti-induced cavitary pneumonia was documented in 2017, involving a smoking and immunocompromised patient. (1) In contrast, our patient was immunocompetent, and all other potential causes of cavitary lung disease were thoroughly ruled out. Conclusions: This case highlights the importance of considering A. pittii as a potential causative pathogen, even in immunocompetent individuals with cavitary community-acquired pneumonia. Early and accurate diagnosis, along with appropriate antimicrobial therapy, can lead to favourable clinical outcomes. References: 1. Larcher R, Pantel A, Arnaud E, Sotto A, Lavigne JP. First report of cavitary pneumonia due to community-acquired Acinetobacter pittii, study of virulence and overview of pathogenesis and treatment. BMC Infect Dis. 2017 Jul 6;17(1):477. doi: 10.1186/s12879-017-2589-0. PMID: 28683724; PMCID: PMC5500973. 2. Wong D, Nielsen TB, Bonomo RA, Pantapalangkoor P, Luna B, Spellberg B. Clinical and Pathophysiological Overview of Acinetobacter Infections: a Century of Challenges. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2017 Jan;30(1):409-447. doi: 10.1128/CMR.00058-16. PMID: 27974412; PMCID: PMC5217799.
