N2  - Introduction: Flexible bronchoscopy has evolved into an indispensable diagnostic and therapeutic tool in the field of pediatric respiratory medicine. This study aims to describe the characteristics of children who underwent flexible bronchoscopy, the indications for the procedure, microbiological and cytopathological findings, complications, post procedure diagnoses, and the impact of the procedure on patient care. Methods: This IRB-approved single center retrospective cohort study included children (0-20 years old) who underwent flexible bronchoscopy at Arkansas Children's Hospital between 01/01/2017 and 12/31/2022 and had relevant clinical and evaluation information (imaging, cytology, pathology, and microbiology) available. Results: 394 children (58.6% male) with median age of 97.8 months (IQR 29-174) were included. Most studies were done in hospitalized children (224/56.9%) and in the operating room (274/69.5%). Most subjects had a chest-X-ray (351/89.1%) with 277/70.3% showing abnormal findings. A CT scan of the chest was done in 204/51.8% subjects with 186/47.2% showing abnormal findings. Most common indications for flexible bronchoscopy included: recurrent lower respiratory infections (139/35.3%), abnormal radiological findings (133/33.8%), chronic cough (108/27.4%), and failed antibiotic treatment (97/24.6%). In addition to flexible bronchoscopy, some children underwent additional procedures, such as bronchoalveolar lavage (326/82.7%) with 167/42.1% performed on multiple sites and 159/40.3% performed on a single site, rigid bronchoscopy (58/14.7%), brush biopsy (55/14%), esophagogastroduodenoscopy (27/6.9%), and PEEP titration (4/1%). Bronchoalveolar lavage was most performed in the left lower lobe (95/24.1%) and right middle lobe (81/20.6%). Cytopathological findings revealed that 273/69.3% were suggestive of inflammation, 49/12.4% were normal, 16/4.1% exhibited eosinophilia, and 9/2.3% had increased lipid-laden macrophages. Bronchoalveolar lavage culture results included: 163/41.4% no growth, 81/20.6% gram-negative bacilli, 73/18.5% gram-positive cocci, 26/6.6% fungal growth, 25/6.3% gram-negative cocci, 16/4.1% gram-positive bacilli, and 1/0.3% non-tuberculous mycobacterium. Complications within the first 48 hours included: increased cough (67/16.8%), increased oxygen requirement (58/14.5%), increased fever (28/7%), wheezing (23/5.8%), hospitalization (7/1.8%), and acute respiratory failure (3/0.8%). The most common post-bronchoscopy diagnoses, based on review of culture, pathology, radiological, and macroscopic findings, included: pneumonia (88/22.3%), normal airways (64/16%), protracted bacterial bronchitis (61/15.5%), atelectasis (44/11.2%), tracheobronchomalacia (41/10.3%), fungal pneumonia (32/8.1%), and cystic fibrosis exacerbation (25/6.3%). The impact of flexible bronchoscopy was significant, with diagnostic contributions in 342/86.8% cases, medical treatment changes in 215/54.6% cases, therapeutic lavage in 37/9.4% cases, and decision-making for surgical intervention in 24/6.1% cases. Conclusion: This study reaffirms the invaluable role of the flexible bronchoscope in the diagnosis and treatment of pediatric airway disorders, emphasizing its safety and effectiveness in the clinical setting.
