N2  - Introduction Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) from blastomycosis is rare but dangerous, even in immunocompetent individuals, due to the fungal pathogen Blastomyces. We examine a 66-year-old immunocompetent woman who developed ARDS secondary to blastomycosis, highlighting the need for early diagnosis and treatment. Case presentation A 66-year-old woman with obstructive sleep apnea and chronic pulmonary disease presented with a persistent cough, dyspnea, and headaches. Despite outpatient treatment with antibiotics and steroids, she worsened, showing hypotension, tachycardia, rapid breathing, and low oxygen saturation. Chest imaging revealed bilateral lung opacities; however, a head CT was unremarkable. Elevated lactic acid and procalcitonin levels were noted without leukocytosis. With negative cultures, she received broadspectrum antibiotics but progressed to acute respiratory failure, requiring intubation. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was conducted, and liposomal amphotericin B treatment began. Despite intensive care and support for septic shock and kidney injury, she passed away on day two. Postmortem, urine antigens indicated Blastomyces and Histoplasma, with BAL cytology confirming Blastomyces dermatitidis, indicating blastomycosis pneumonia. DiscussionSevere pulmonary manifestations of blastomycosis are commonly seen in patients with compromised cell-mediated immunity, but even individuals with no apparent health issues can be overwhelmed by aggressive forms of the infection. Blastomycosis primarily affects the lungs in 90% of cases and can lead to ARDS in roughly 10% of hospitalized patients, where mortality rates range between 50% and 90%. The challenge in diagnosis is exacerbated by nonspecific radiographic signs, such as pneumonitis, nodules, or mass-like infiltrates, which can resemble tuberculosis, bacterial pneumonia, or other fungal infections. B. dermatitidis can be swiftly identified by its large size (8- 15 μm), thick cell wall, and characteristic budding pattern of the same size, enabling prompt diagnosis through microscopic analysis of respiratory smears or tissue samples. Liposomal amphotericin B is the preferred initial treatment for severe pulmonary disease, with a switch to itraconazole considered upon clinical stabilization. This case underscores the importance of vigilant clinical observation, particularly in regions endemic to blastomycosis. Prompt and precise diagnosis, along with aggressive antifungal therapy, is essential for improving patient outcomes. (Figure Presented).
