N2  - INTRODUCTION: Acute eosinophilic pneumonia (AEP) is an uncommon, potentially fatal lung disease. Antibiotics are a possible cause of drug induced eosinophilic lung disease. We present a case of Daptomycin induced AEP. CASE PRESENTATION: This is a 75-year-old male with a past medical history of chronic kidney disease stage IIIb presenting with one week of fevers, dry cough and worsening shortness of breath. Two weeks prior, he was diagnosed with right metatarsal osteomyelitis secondary to methicillin resistant staphylococcus aureus. He was discharged on intravenous Daptomycin and Ceftriaxone. On admission, he was hypoxic with oxygen saturation of 75% on room air. His blood work did not show peripheral eosinophilia. His clinical exam did not show signs of extrapulmonary of eosinophilic disease. His initial chest radiography was concerning for right greater than left bilateral scattered opacifications and ground glass infiltrates. CT Chest was obtained showing dense bilateral consolidations in the upper lobes and scattered ground glass opacites throughout both lungs. He was admitted to the medical intensive care unit. His daptomycin was stopped. He was started on antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia. Despite three days of antibiotic coverage, his oxygen requirement escalated to high flow nasal cannula requiring 60% FI02. Thus, he was started on empiric steroids. Within three days of receiving intravenous steroids, his chest radiograph showed considerable improvement with de-escalating oxygen requirement. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed showing eosinophil count of 11%. Patient was discharged on a prednisone taper with plans to repeat his CT chest in 4 weeks with pulmonary follow up. His repeat CT chest was considerably improved at the end of his steroid taper. DISCUSSION: Daptomycin-induced AEP remains an uncommon, lifethreatening condition that requires prompt diagnosis and treatment. BAL cell differential showing greater than 25% eosinophilia is consistent with the diagnosis. This patient's BAL cell differential showed 11% eosinophilia after three days of steroid administration and was not consistent with infection. He otherwise met radiographic and clinical criteria for AEP. Treatment includes cessation of the daptomycin with possible administration of steroids. He did not have peripheral eosinophilia or evidence of extrapulmonary eosinophilia on clinical examination. Uppal et al. noted peripheral eosinophilia in 77% of Daptomycin- AEP cases. It is theorized that daptomycin's preferential binding within pulmonary surfactant results in a release of IL-5 cytokines inducing the migration of pulmonary eosinophils. However, the absence of peripheral eosinophilia in this case raises more questions into the mechanism of Daptomycin induced AEP. (Figure Presented).
