N2  - RATIONALE: Chronic P. aeruginosa pneumonia is difficult to eradicate in individuals with structural lung disease, such as Cystic Fibrosis (CF). During homeostasis, macrophages interact with the alveolar environment and soluble mediators to effect bacterial clearance through phagocytosis. Phagocytosis is initiated by pathogens, in part through pattern recognition receptors, such as the toll-like receptors (TLRs) via MyD88-dependent and independent mechanisms. P. aeruginosa virulence factors, like flagellin, cause lung injury and stiffening of lung tissue that can be sensed by macrophages through mechanosensitive ion channels, such as Piezo1. However, the mechanism by which Piezo1 regulates bacterial clearance in the chronically stiffened and infected lung remains unexplored. METHODS: Wild-type (WT) and MyD88 knock-out (KO) bone marrow derived macrophages (BMDMs) were plated on polyacrylamide gels of pathophysiologic range lung stiffness (1 kPa = normal lung, 8-25 kPa stiffened/infected lung) or standard tissue culture conditions ± P. aeruginosa (Invivogen, 250 ng/mL) ± the Rac1 inhibitor, (EHT 1864, 10 μM). Piezo1 Ca2+ channel activity was measured using fluorescent Ca2+ dye (FLIPR 5 Kit, Molecular Devices) ± the Piezo1 specific agonist, Yoda1 (1-200 μM). Knockdown TLR5 and Piezo1 was achieved using targeted siRNA (Origene). Piezo1 total cellular protein and surface expression were measured by immunoblot and flow cytometry, respectively. Particle engulfment and phagolysosome acidification was measured using pHrodo Zymosan Bioparticles (Invitrogen) and quantified with microscopy. Human alveolar macrophages were isolated from a rejected donor lung. RESULTS: Piezo1 Ca2+ channel activity (at Yoda1 EC50,10 μM) is increased 2-fold by P. aeruginosa flagellin in a TLR5- dependent manner. Piezo1 activity was dependent on increased matrix stiffness in the pathophysiologic range (≥ 25 kPa). Flagellin increased Piezo1 total protein by 30% and surface expression by more than 4-fold in WT BMDMs, an effect which was largely abrogated in MyD88 KO BMDMs. Piezo1 agonism (Yoda1, 5 μM) increased flagellin-induced phagolysosome maturation (engulfment and phagolysosome acidification) in murine BMDMs and human alveolar macrophages by 144% and 64%, respectively. Concordantly, loss of Piezo1 function (siRNA) blunted the increase in flagellin-induced phagolysosome maturation by 33%. The finding that Piezo1 agonism increased Rac1 protein expression prompted further analysis of Rac1 function in phagolysosome maturation. Loss of Rac1 function (EHT 1864, 10 μM) abrogated phagolysosome maturation. CONCLUSION: Piezo1 induces phagolysosome maturation in response to P. aeruginosa flagellin in a TLR5- MyD88-Rac1 and matrix stiffness (≥ 25 kPa)-dependent manner. Taken together, Piezo1 is a potential therapeutic target against P. aeruginosa pneumonia in the chronically infected/stiffened lung.
