N2  - Introduction: Nontuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM) are emerging human pathogens that have been increasing in incidence and prevalence worldwide. Mycobacterium Abscessus may be found in concomitant with Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) but upon extensive review of the published literature there were no previous cases of NTM in concomitant with Mycobacterium Shimoidei. We are reporting a very rare case of an immunocompetent patient with mixed NTM-lung infection. Case Report: A 58-year-old immunocompetent female with history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who presented to our office for evaluation of chronic productive cough and abnormal chest X-ray showing large left upper lobe (LUL) cavitary lesion. CT scan of the chest revealed 6.7cm left upper lobe cavitary lesion with multilobar nodular densities. Multiple sputum acid fast bacilli (AFB) cultures grew M.Shimoidei. QuantiFERON in addition to Aspergillus and histoplasma galactomannan tests were negative. Patient was treated with Azithromycin, Ethambutol, Amikacin, and rifampin. Due to persistent symptoms and lack of significant radiological improvement, the patient underwent left thoracotomy with resection of the LUL cavitary lesion. The patient was seen one year later with intermittent hemoptysis. Repeat CT scan of the chest showed new groundglass opacities surrounding a new cavitary lesion in the left lower lobe. Multiple sputum AFB cultures returned positive for MAC and patient was treated with Rifampin, Ethambutol, and Azithromycin. Following completing treatment course patient continued to report daily productive cough. Follow up CT scan of the chest showed stable bilateral nodular opacities with new nodularity in the upper lobes. New AFB cultures were sent which returned positive for Mycobacterium Abscesses. Discussion: Our patient met the American Thoracic Society (ATS) and Infectious diseases Society of America (IDSA) criteria for the diagnosis of NTM but data is limited regarding NTM-lung disease caused by mixed NTM infections. Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is the most common etiology of NTM lung disease worldwide. Mycobacterium Abscessus is the third most frequently recovered NTM respiratory pathogen in the United Status and account for approximately 80% of rapidly growing mycobacteria respiratory disease isolates. Mycobacterium Shimoidei infection is very rare. A study in 2017 reported only 17 patients described in the literature. M.Shimoidei affects predominately patients with immunosuppression or patients with pre-existing pulmonary disease including smoking-related emphysema, occupationally acquired lung disease (silicosis), previous TB, and lung cancer. To our knowledge, this is the first reported case in literature with mixed NTMlung including concomitant MAC, M.Abscessus, and M.Shimoidei lung infection.
