N2  - Introduction:Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infections have become a significant concern in healthcare, particularly among immunocompromised individuals. MAC comprises a group of slowgrowing, nontuberculous mycobacteria, with Mycobacterium avium being one of its members. These infections can result in various clinical manifestations, including bronchiectasis, cavitary lung lesions, lymphadenitis, and disseminated infection. When MAC presents as a cavitary lesion, it is associated with a high mortality rate. Here we demonstrate a case of deadly cavitary MAC due to challenges from delayed initiation of therapy due to lengthy diagnostic testing. Description:68-yearold man with history of alcohol use and prior incarceration presented with productive cough and hemoptysis for two months. CT showed 6.9x8.2x8.9 cm right upper lobe cavitary lesion with rightsided hydropneumothorax. Due to acute respiratory failure, repeat scan revealed significant worsening of pneumothorax with bronchopleural fistula. Chest tube was placed with studies performed. Cardiology was consulted with EF of 25-30%, infectious disease, cardiothoracic surgery were all consulted during stay. Evaluation included: MTB PCR negative x2, sputum with mixed flora, histoplasma, blastomyces, cocci, crytococcus, brucella, ANA, Aspergillus Ag, CMV, nocardia, negative. Elevated IgE 9,295 with Aspergillus IgE 2.36 (class III/VI). Bronchoscopy was performed twice during hospitalization without significant results, until 14 days later when AFB returned positive. Patient was intubated and continued to decompensate during hospitalization resulting in a family decision to transition to comfort measures at the end of patient's 20 day hospital stay. Shortly after patient's death, AFB finalized speciation Mycobacterium, aviumintracellulare group. Discussion:This case underscores the complexities of managing cavitary MAC infections with delayed culture results due to the slow growth of MAC in culture. Timely initiation of treatment is crucial for optimizing patient outcomes, but is hindered by several factors, including the extended incubation period required for mycobacterial cultures, which poses the question of whether to pursue empiric treatment for cavitary MAC with triple or quadruple therapy with high index of suspicion. Cavitary MAC infections are challenging and can be further complicated by bronchopleural fistula formation or hydropneumothorax. Delayed diagnosis impedes targeted management and further disease progression. In conclusion, this case emphasizes the importance of early diagnosis and timely therapy initiation with MAC infections. Lengthy diagnostic studies present a significant obstacle, and clinicians should be aware of this challenge when managing similar cases. Strategies to expedite diagnostic results while maintaining sensitivity and specificity should be considered to ensure prompt and targeted treatment, to improve outcomes.
