N2  - INTRODUCTION: The differential of a cavitary lesion in a patient with HIV/AIDS is extensive and includes TB, nontuberculous mycobacteria, bacterial pneumonia, or abscess, fungal pneumonias that include cryptococcus and endemic mycoses, nocardiosis and noninfectious causes including Kaposi sarcoma, squamous cell cancer, and lymphoma. Aspergillus has become an increasingly common pathogen among immunocompromised patients but a relatively uncommon pathogen with HIV infection. CASE REPORT: A 54-year-old male with a history of alcohol abuse for 30 years recently quit presenting with generalized fatigue, night sweats, weight loss, chills for 2 weeks associated with some hemoptysis and dry cough. Denies IV drug abuse. Upon initial presentation he was febrile, tachycardic, normotensive, rales at left mid-lower lung zone. Work up showed leukopenia, subsequently came positive for HIV with Viral load 5 and CD4 count 16. CT scans show Left thorax empyema as well as several other foci of pneumonia in the right middle and upper lobe and other fluid collections at the bases. Infectious workup was negative, however Beta- D glucan was Positive. BAL positive for aspergillus and the patient started on Voriconazole. Repeat chest CT showed interval improvement of pneumonia compared to prior, abscess on left decreased in size, right sided opacities decreased consolidation but increased cavitation. Later patients discharged with Pulmonary and Infectious disease follow up as outpatients.DISCUSSION: Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA) occurs primarily in patients with severe immunodeficiency. Neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count less than 1,000), the most commonly reported predisposing risk factor for aspergillosis in AIDS patients. Two distinct forms of pulmonary aspergillosis reported in patients with AIDS, invasive obstructing bronchial aspergillosis. Invasive aspergillosis was included as an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection in the initial definition developed by the Centers for Disease Control. The gold standard in the diagnosis of IPA is histopathological examination of lung tissue obtained by thoracoscopic or open-lung biopsy. A new broad-spectrum triazole, voriconazole, has been approved as the initial treatment of invasive aspergillosis and is currently considered the treatment of choice in many patients with IPA. CONCLUSION: Overall, the clinical outcome of HIV-infected patients with pulmonary aspergillosis is dismal. 56 % of patients died from aspergillosis either due to widespread pulmonary or systemic infection or due to fatal hemoptysis. A late diagnosis of this condition is related to therapeutic failure and progression of the disease, ultimately leading to complications and death of the patient. (Figure Presented).
