N2  - RATIONALE. The world is experiencing more frequent extreme climate conditions, which are driving an increase in severe weather. The unusual number of devastating hurricanes/storms, unprecedented waves of summer heat, and destructive flooding events have become more frequent and more life-threatening. These climate extremes may force the adaptation of human and animal pathogens including air-, soil-, and water-borne bacteria. This scenario becomes more impactful when the bacteria are inhaled or acquired through compromised skin or respiratory barriers, as in the case of the nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM). NTM are an aerosol-, soil-, dust-, and watercolonizing group of bacteria that can cause an under-diagnosed, recalcitrant respiratory infection in humans. We hypothesize NTM are climate sensitive, environmentally adaptive pathogens. To test this hypothesis, we pair complementary microbiological and isolate data with environmental and atmospheric data available through the National Ecological Observatory Network (NEON) to perform NTM ecological research on a continental-scale platform. METHODS. To begin to define a new understanding of climate-related features that influence species abundance, diversity, morphology, and pathogenicity, we studied NTM across varied continental spaces and multiple time scales using NEON resources including the Surface Water Microbe Community Composition data product, DP1.20141.001 and the Soil Microbe Community Composition data product, DP1.10081.001. Then, to interrogate mycobacteria, we used published NEON 16S gene sequences and taxonomic information to query 6,343 surface water and soil samples collected by NEON between 11-01-2015 and 10-31-2018. RESULTS. Across this 3-year period, there were 633 site-days on which environmental samples were collected for 16S gene sequencing. We found 81.8% (n=5,189/6,343) were positive for Mycobacteria, with higher positivity rates at NEON terrestrial sites compared to surface water sites (Table 1). We are confident that the “Mycobacteria” detected are NTM since Mycobacterium tuberculosis is not readily found in exogenous environments. We are currently characterizing viable NTM from these samples and will integrate this new information with new data and new samples from a prospective sampling study at NEON sites to build a mechanistic model of eco-evolutionary features of NTM in response to climate changes. CONCLUSIONS. Leveraging NEON resources, we are opening new doors to elucidate the climatic features that may inadvertently serve as selection pressures for pathogens, and we continue to study bacterial trade-off mechanisms that modulate NTM pathogenicity during ongoing climate change while improving model predictions on a continental scale. (Table Presented).
