N2  - Introduction: Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (AATd) is a rare genetic disease that affects 1 to 5 in every 10,000 in the US. Panlobular lower lobe emphysema is pathognomonic for AATd in the lungs. There are rare instances where cystic bronchiectasis has been described in this population, but causal links have not been established. We present a case of a 66-year-old woman who presented with bronchiectasis without obstruction caused by AATd with underlying mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection. Case: Our patient was a non-smoking female with a history of gastroesophageal reflux disease, who initially presented to pulmonary clinic with complaints of dry cough for 18 months. History is significant for secondhand smoke exposure with positive family history of MAC infection with bronchiectasis in both mother and maternal grandmother. CT chest at the time of presentation showed findings consistent with cystic bronchiectasis involving bilateral lower lobes. Initial work-up for bronchiectasis showed negative autoimmune work-up, negative cystic fibrosis testing and normal immunoglobulin levels. Bronchoscopy and bronchioalveolar lavage were unremarkable with endobronchial ultrasound guided biopsy of mediastinal lymph nodes was negative for malignant cells. Further testing showed positive AATd with PiZZ genotype and sputum culture positive for M. Abscessus. She was initiated on treatment for M. abscessus and for airway clearance techniques for bronchiectasis. She did not meet criteria for Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) augmentation therapy initially and anti-mycobacterium therapy was discontinued early due to drug intolerance. Disease progression was noted on PFTs with progressive loss of FEV1 by 500cc over 4.5 years and worsening bronchiectasis on CT chest. Subsequently, she started AAT augmentation therapy which was well tolerated with biannual monitoring. Discussion: Studies have shown that bronchiectasis was identified in 10-20% of those with AATd and more so (25-30%) in those with the PiZZ genotype which is the most severe AATd genotype. AATd lowers host immunity against pathogens and there is an association between AATd and nontuberculous mycobacterial infections. Our case presents the importance of diagnosing the underlying cause in cases of bronchiectasis and recognizing that reduced host defenses in AATd and structural changes to the lung parenchyma increase the likelihood of indolent infections and progression to bronchiectasis. There is evidence that AAT replacement therapy alters the course of disease progression further highlighting the importance of the correct diagnosis of this potentially modifiable cause of bronchiectasis. Serial follow up of patients with AATd and bronchiectasis is recommended to decrease morbidity from the disease. (Figure Presented).
