N2  - Introduction: The most common pathogen in nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (NTM-PD) is Mycobacterium Avium Complex (MAC), which may cause fibrocavitary and nodular bronchiectatic forms. In cases of cavitary lung disease from macrolide-susceptible MAC NTM-PD, guidelines recommend a standard three-drug oral regimen, along with parenteral aminoglycoside for 2-3 months at treatment initiation. Case: A 62-year-old female with history of mild gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), hyperlipidemia, and sinusitis, presented with persistent cough and production of yellowish-green sputum but no dyspnea or hemoptysis. Chest CT scan revealed scattered tree-in-bud nodular infiltrates in the right lung, bronchiectasis in the right middle lobe, and a 3.6cm cavitary lesion in the right lower lobe (figure 1). CT at 6 weeks showed a persistent cavity, sized 3.0cm. Autoimmune serologies, ANCA, HIV Ag/Ab-4th generation, and interferon (IFN)-gamma release assay (IGRA) for Tuberculosis all yielded negative results, but she was found to be Heterozygous for the c.3469-17T>C CFTR Variant. Three sputa grew MAC in AFB culture, which was determined to be susceptible to amikacin (MIC 16 ug/ml) and clarithromycin (MIC 2 ug/ml). Subsequently, the patient started a treatment regimen consisting of azithromycin 250 mg daily, ethambutol 800 mg daily and rifabutin 300 mg daily. The patient tolerated treatment well, and after six months of therapy, a significant improvement in the cavity was observed, characterized both by filling and shrinkage (figure 2).Discussion: Recommendations for the addition of parenteral aminoglycoside in cavitary MAC NTM-PD treatment are based on limited literature. One study found better microbiological responses in patients who received parenteral streptomycin alongside standard three-drug regimen initially. However, clinical symptoms, radiographic findings, and relapse rates were similar between groups. Given the significant side effect profile of antibiotics for MAC, there is substantial interest in decreasing the number of antibiotics, when able. Aminoglycosides may confer significant ototoxicity, even with intermittent dosing. Our case suggests clinical and radiographic improvement is possible with a three-drug regimen. However, this limited approach is likely only appropriate for a subset of patients. A two-drug regimen for noncavitary MAC NTM-PD is currently being investigated (MAC 2v3 clinical trial).Conclusion: We demonstrate a case in which cavitary MAC NTM-PD improved substantially on three-drug oral therapy, without using parenteral aminoglycoside. Further research may help to stratify patients with cavitary MAC into those in whom it is appropriate to wait for initial treatment response without parenteral aminoglycoside vs. others in whom such a delay risks disease progression. (Figure Presented).
