N2  - RATIONALE: Pneumonia is the 4th leading cause of death and first among infectious causes of death. More than half a million adults are hospitalized each year with pneumonia.This study is carried out to understand the contributing factors and comorbidities which lead to ICU admission in Pneumonia and its clinical profile. An attempt has been made to find the association of Disease severity, Treatment outcomes and Contributing factors towards mortality.METHODS: A retrospective analysis of 50 ICU admissions with Pneumonia was done. Their demographics, presenting complaints, comorbidities, causative organisms, oxygen requirement, Ventilatory assistance, treatment outcomes and contributing factors towards death were studied and analysed.RESULTS: The mean age of study population was 72 years (range 29-92 years), maximum number of patients were in the age group 50-59 years. There were 30 males and 20 females. Predominant complaints were cough with expectoration -78%,breathlessness -78% and fever 22%.68% of the patients had severe Pneumonia and 32% had moderate pneumonia.42% patients required NIV(BiPAP) support, 22% were managed with Mechanical Ventilation. Those with moderate pneumonia had an average length of hospitalisation of 7 days (range 3-10days) and severe pneumonia patients were hospitalised for 9 days(range 3-21days).Diabetes Mellitus was the most common comorbidity identified in 27cases (54%) followed by structural lung disease -34%, smoking-30%, systemic hypertension in 30%, and chronic alcoholism in 10%, CAD-8% and CKD-8%, CVA-4%, recent travel history - 4%.The overall microbiological isolation was achieved in 37(74%) cases of pneumonia- Sputum 83%, Endotracheal tube aspirate-18% , BAL-2%.The most common organism isolated was Klebsiella pneumoniae(10),followed by Pseudomonas Aeruginosa(8),E.coli (7), Acinetobacter(4),Staphylococcus aureus(3),Enterobacter(2), Candida albicans(2), Hemophilus influenza(2) Streptococcus pneumoniae (1), Citrobacter (1), MRSA(1). One was a case of viral pneumonia (H1N1 confirmed by PCR) and one was a case of fungal pneumonia(Mucormycosis confirmed by HPE) . Out of the 50, a total of 43 patients were treated successfully and discharged while 7 patients succumbed (5 male, 2 female).The factors contributing towards mortality were - Diabetes mellitus, COPD, Alcoholic liver disease. Most common cause of death were Type 2 respiratory failure, ARDS, Aspiration or necrotising pneumonia, sepsis.Using Fischer's exact test 'p' value ≤ 0.05, which implies comorbidities have significant association with mortality.CONCLUSIONS: Comorbidities when not under control contributed significantly towards severity of pneumonia. Diabetes mellitus, pre-existing structural lung disease, COPD, systemic hypertension were associated with pneumonia severity. Type 2 respiratory failure, ARDS, Aspiration or necrotising pneumonia and sepsis contributed to mortality.
