N2  - Introduction: A lung abscess is a pulmonary cavity containing necrotic debris or pus caused by microbial infection. CT results and symptomatic similarities between lung cancer and lung abscesses can cause confusion and pose a diagnostic challenge. In this case, we present a patient with nonspecific symptoms and abnormal lung masses, who was eventually found to have multiple lung abscesses. CASE REPORT: A 76-year-old male with the past medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease(COPD), hypertension, and a former smoker (16 packs -year) was admitted to the hospital for left-sided pleuritic chest pain associated with dyspnea, chest tightness, exercise limitation, night sweats, and white scant sputum with cough. Patient was admitted to the hospital after several weeks of chest pain. He denied fever, chills, weight loss, loss of appetite, bloody sputum, and periodontal disease. Upon physical examination, the patient had diminished breath sounds and coarse inspiratory crackles in the lung bases. Blood work showed leukocytosis and high erythrocyte sedimentation rate with negative blood cultures and negative results for HIV infections, bacteria and fungi, and Quantiferon gold testing. CT chest showed a 3.5 x 3 cm peripheral mass-like consolidation of the left lung lingula, a 3.5 x 2.5 area of masslike consolidation is seen in the right lower lobe, and 2 additional masses are seen at the lower lobes measuring 3.5 x 2 cm on the right and 2 x 1.7 cm on the left lung. The pulmonary function tests were mildly obstructive. Based on his clinical presentation and imaging findings, lung abscess was the most probable diagnosis so broad spectrum antibiotics were prescribed. He was referred to a cardiothoracic surgeon for further evaluation to rule out malignancy. A CT-guided lung biopsy displayed benign bronchial tissue with severe acute inflammation and underlying organizing pneumonia with abscess formation. PET-CT shows the decreased size of bilateral pulmonary infiltrates after taking antibiotics and no significant evidence of metastatic disease. The diagnosis of Lung abscess was confirmed. After treatment with antibiotics, there was a significant improvement in patient symptoms and follow-up CT demonstrated a remarkable decrease in the size of lung consolidations. DISCUSSION: Old age and smoking increase the chances of lung abscess formation but through prompt diagnosis and treatment lung abscess can be managed accurately. A delay in the treatment can cause complications like empyema, bronchopulmonary fistula, sepsis, and trapped lung.
