N2  - Introduction Aspergillosis and Penicillosis are filamentous fungal infections, occurring mainly in immunocompromised patients. Pulmonary co-infection with both species is very rare and carries a high mortality rate. To our knowledge, this is the first report of an uncommon co-infection of Penicillium and Aspergillus species. Case Presentation A 64-year-old man with a history of diabetes mellitus (DM) and chronic lymphocytic leukemia presented with 2-months of a productive cough, night sweats, 30-lb weight loss, and hemoptysis. He denied fever, dyspnea, and chest pain. He worked in a broiler, and his symptoms started 4 days after exposure to an oil spill in a moldy basement. A month prior he was admitted for pneumonia, and imaging at that time was reportedly normal. Upon this admission, he was nontoxic but tachycardic to 124 bpm and had crackles bilaterally. Labs showed leukocytosis of 34.25 K/uL. CT chest revealed large, multi-focal, thick-walled cavitary lesions within the bilateral upper and right middle lung lobes. Blood cultures, HIV serology, and sputum for acid-fast bacilli were negative. Beta-D-glucan and Aspergillus antibodies were positive, but antigen testing was negative. Sputum cultures grew quinolone-resistant P. aeruginosa. Fungal blood cultures were negative. He was then treated with cefepime. Interval CT scans at 4 and 8 months showed enlarging cavitations, and a subsequent bronchoscopy with BAL grew Penicillium spp. He underwent a VATS with left upper lung wedge resection. Ensuing tissue and blood cultures grew Aspergillus fumigatus. He was ultimately started on voriconazole. Discussion Penicillium spp causing invasive pulmonary disease is rare and the prevalence of Chronic Pulmonary Aspergillosis [CPA] is only 1 in 100,000. Risk factors for both include DM and moderate immunosuppression. Our patient presented with a chronic cough and cavitary lesions despite a normal CT chest a month prior. Given his occupational history, fungal serologies were done and suggestive of prior exposure. Due to non-resolution of his cavitations, we pursued a bronchoscopy with BAL isolating Penicillium spp, which is exceedingly rare. Based on current guidelines for suspected CPA with aspergilloma, surgical resection was performed which confirmed Aspergillus. Limited guidelines exist for invasive Penicillium management; however, voriconazole has been shown to treat both pathogens. This case emphasizes the importance of suspecting CPA in immunocompromised patients with an upper lobe cavitation. Timely diagnosis and prolonged anti-fungal treatment can significantly decrease morbidity and mortality. Given the co-infection with Penicillium spp, our case is even more rare than an isolated infection of aspergillosis.
