N2  - Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is a highly heterogeneous inherited disease of impairment in mucociliary clearance with equal sex prevalence a mean age of onset 22 years in adults (5 in children) in the US. The pathophysiology includes mutations in genes encoding components of cilia with more than 50 genes identified to date. Here we present a multiparous female with bronchiectasis and situs inversus totalis from PCD.A 38-year-old multiparous immigrant from Afghanistan presented with shortness of breath on exertion and a productive cough with sputum, worse in the mornings. Of note, the patient was receiving courses of antibiotics frequently. She denied smoking and exposure to inhalants or animal dander. An exam revealed bilateral rales and labs showed mild leukocytosis. Chest X-ray revealed right lobar consolidation. Persistent symptoms led to a CT chest that revealed sinus inversus totalis with lingula and right middle lobe bronchiectasis. Further questioning elicited a history of chronic sinusitis and dextrocardia. Quantiferon and sputum studies were negative for various infections including TB and Pseudomonas. Repeat sputum cultures showed a heavy growth of Haemophilus influenza. Immunoglobulin quantification showed normal levels except for an elevated IgG3. The patient received a diagnosis of primary ciliary dyskinesia, however, additional evaluation is ongoing.Clinical presentations of PCD are highly variable and hence pose a diagnostic challenge. The presence of situs inversus initiated our evaluation as this can be seen up to 50 percent of patients with PCD. Although infertility is common in patients with PCD, our patient was multiparous with 7 children, alive and healthy. Radiological findings include bronchial wall thickening with or without hyperinflation, segmental atelectasis, consolidation and cylindrical or saccular bronchiectasis with a predilection for the anatomic middle lobe, some of which was seen in our patient. These findings suggest a clinically mild variant.Patients who receive a diagnosis of PCD need lifelong proactive bronchial hygiene regimens to preserve lung function, reduce symptoms, and help prevent typical pulmonary infections. Measurement of nasal nitric oxide and high-speed videomicroscopy analysis are some tests available and electron microscopy of nasal or bronchial biopsy can also be confirmatory. Genetic testing should also be considered after cystic fibrosis is ruled out. The patients with variants leading to absent inner dynein arm, central apparatus, and microtubular disorganization seem to have a worse prognosis. Early diagnosis is key for these patients and referral to specialists for further management prevents morbidity associated with the disease.
