N2  - Background Takotsubo cardiomyopathy (TCM) is a complex pathophysiological phenomenon thought to result from chemical and neuromuscular mechanisms such as catecholamine excess, microvascular dysfunction, and coronary artery spasm. The impact of surgical denervation of the transplanted heart on susceptibility to this phenomenon is poorly understood and reports of occurrences are rare. Case A 69 year-old male heart and kidney transplant recipient presented with dry cough, shortness of breath, and wheezing four years after uncomplicated transplant. Initial workup was suggestive of atypical bacterial pneumonia with no pathogen isolated. Admission echocardiogram revealed normal LVEF with no evidence of graft dysfunction. Two days into admission, the patient demonstrated acute respiratory distress and increasing oxygen requirements. Repeat chest X-ray revealed new evidence of pulmonary vascular congestion and subsequent limited echocardiogram revealed an acute decline in LVEF to 20% with new focal apical akinesis, prompting workup for acute graft rejection or new ischemic insult. Decision-making Through an extensive series of diagnostics including right and left heart catheterization, endomyocardial biopsy, strain echocardiography, and cardiac MRI, etiologies for acute, precipitous LV function decline such as cellular and antibody-mediated rejection, cardiac allograft vasculopathy, acute ischemia, and viral myocarditis were systematically ruled out. Wall motion and strain patterns on cardiac MRI and strain echocardiography demonstrated an apical ballooning, base-sparing pattern consistent with likely TCM. The patient received routine supportive care and standard immunosuppressive management with follow-up echocardiogram two weeks after hospital discharge demonstrating full LVEF recovery and resolution of focal wall motion abnormalities. Conclusion We present a case of TCM in a transplanted heart likely precipitated by atypical pneumonia. Though rare in this population, this phenomenon warrants consideration in a decompensated heart transplant patient, and its occurrence may shed further light on a yet incompletely understood pathophysiologic process.
