N2  - Introduction: The incidence of mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) in lung transplant recipients ranges from 0.8-10%; most cases being reactivation of untreated latent tuberculosis infections (LTBI), due to T cell suppression. We present a case of disseminated MTB diagnosed few weeks post transplant. Case Report: A 51-year-old male with IPF underwent bilateral lung transplantation. Induction immunosuppression (ISN) included basiliximab, mycophenolate (MMF) & solumedrol followed by maintenance ISN of tacrolimus (FK,10-12 ng/ml), MMF, & prednisone. Hospital stay was uneventful & patient was discharged on POD 16. 1-month later patient endorsed fever, chills, productive cough & dyspnea. Labs were notable for leucopenia & neutropenia. CT Chest showed mediastinal lymphadenopathy and loculated right pleural effusion. Bronchoscopy with EBUS, BAL & transbronchial biopsies were performed. The subcarinal lymph node was necrotic with frank pus on aspiration and AFB smear was positive. Subsequently blood, pleural fluid, BAL, and lymph nodes were all positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex. The patient was treated with Rifabutin, Isoniazid/B6, Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide (RIPE) for 4 months followed by 8 months of Isoniazid/B6 & rifabutin. ISN was adjusted for drug-drug interactions (DDI's). FK goal was lowered to 8-10 ng/ml, MMF dose was reduced. Prednisone dose was 20 mg a day while on rifabutin. INH dose was adjusted based on the levels. 2 months into RIPE therapy, cultures were negative. Patient is now 19 months post-transplant, on 3 drug ISN with good graft function. We reexamined the donor samples & the explanted lungs. AFB stains were negative & no signs of MTB were noted, however cultures were not done on these samples. Summary: MTB can be successfully treated in transplant patients however it is crucial to adjust & monitor the ISN given the DDI's. Rapid onset & dissemination of MTB 1 month after the transplant makes our case unique. Donor was a young female, who moved from Mexico. At the time of organ offer, CT Chest did not show any concerning features & per the donor data, there was no obvious concern for LTBI or MTB. Our patient's QuantiFERON was negative on pre-evaluation testing. This brings up the inquiry of whether & how to screen the donors for LTBI. The limitation would be both TST & IGRA might not be feasible due to time constraints and AFB cultures on BAL if sent, will be finalized after organ is transplanted.
