N2  - Case Report: Lemierre's syndrome is a rare disease characterized by invasive bacterial infection of head or neck, often initiated as an oropharyngeal or tooth infection complicated by septic thromboembolism and/or thrombophlebitis of regional vessels. The development of aneurysms as a sequela of Lemierre's syndrome is a rare finding. We present two cases of Lemierre's syndrome both with subsequent development of internal carotid artery aneurysms. The causative agent of Lemierre's syndrome in the first patient was pansusceptible Staphylococcus aureus, while our second patient had Fusobacterium necrophorum, which is the most likely causative agent of Lemierre's syndrome. Our first patient was 6 months old, and he presented with tachycardia, tachypnea, ascites, hepatomegaly, elevated liver function tests, and hypoalbuminemia. MRI showed retropharyngeal, parapharyngeal, and carotid space abscesses with extension to the mediastinum, as well as thrombophlebitis of the right internal jugular vein and abscess involvement of the right carotid artery. He also had septic right pulmonary emboli, pneumonia, empyema and systemic inflammation. Repeat MRI showed a pseudoaneurysm arising from the distal cervical segment of the right internal carotid artery at the base of the skull measuring 1.28 × 1.28 cm, as well as right sigmoid sinus thrombosis. He underwent intravascular repair and coiling of right internal carotid artery and incision and draining of the abscesses, and showed improvement as he completed a 6-week antibiotic course. The second patient was 16-year-old male who had headache, fatigue, rhinorrhea, cough and fever of 5 days duration then developed altered mental status and was admitted with diagnosis of meningoencephalitis in the setting of acute sinusitis and left periorbital swelling with discharge. He subsequently developed agitation, slurred speech, headache, photophobia, left eye with injected conjunctiva, dizziness, unequal pupils, expressive aphasia and neck rigidity. Repeat MRI showed sphenoid sinusitis, left cavernous sinus thrombosis, left medial temporal lobe focal empyema, and left infraorbital abscess, which causing cranial nerve 6 palsy. Around 10 weeks after completion of a 3-week course of antibiotics, a routine follow up MRI showed a left cavernous internal carotid artery mycotic aneurysm measuring 16 × 16 mm, in the setting of holocraneal headaches and imbalance. He underwent coil embolization of a left cavernous internal carotid artery dissecting aneurysm and carotid artery sacrifice. Anticoagulation is controversial but its use has increased over the past years. Both of our patients were placed on anticoagulation therapy. Our patients had different presentations on admission. Lemierre's syndrome can have a spectrum of presentations, and we discuss the different presentations described in the literature. $F$
