N2  - Case Report: Pleural effusions are prevalent clinical condition frequently encountered in the hospital. Common etiologies encompass congestive heart failure, pneumonia, cancer, and pulmonary embolism1. Thoracocentesis assist with narrowing the etiology of these pleural effusions. Lung cancer, leukemia/lymphomas, and breast cancer constitute the predominant causative factors of malignant pleural effusions (MPE)2. A minority of MPE are categorized as cancer of unknown primary (CUP). We present a unique case of isolated recurrent pleural effusion as initial presenting feature, ultimately diagnosed as a malignant pleural effusion with an obscure primary cancer source. A 71-year-old male with history of COPD and OSA presented with recurrent right-sided pleural effusion s/p thoracocentesis twice in 3 weeks prior to his admission along with oneweek history of treatment with an unknown antibiotic. The patient reported malaise, fatigue, and shortness of breath. He reported associated non-bloody cough for 3 weeks and substantial weight loss of 10 lbs in one month. Upon admission patient exhibited hemodynamic stability but needed 6L O2 via NC to maintain saturation >90%. Computed tomography revealed loculated right sided pleural effusion vs empyema measuring 9.4 cm. Chest tube was placed and pleural fluid analysis revealed exudative pleural fluid with elevated LDH (2948 units/L) with lymphocytic predominant cells. Pleural fluid culture remained negative for any bacterial or fungal growth. Immunohistochemical analysis of pleural fluid indicated positivity for CK7, IMP3 and HMWCK which suggested possible pancreato-biliary primary. Importantly, lung primary was effectively ruled out by negative TTF1 and Napsin A stains. CT abdomen with pancreatic protocol did not show any evidence of pancreatic mass or lesion. Notably, tumor marker CA 19-9 was normal 27 units/mL, while CEAwas significantly elevated at 93.1 ng/mL. Chest tube was removed once clinically indicated and follow up chest x-ray ruled out reaccumulation of fluid. Due to constraints inherent to inpatient diagnostic capabilities and availability, the patient was referred to a community oncologist for further evaluation through positron emission tomography (PET) scanning to elucidate the primary cancer source. Cancer of unknown primary account of 4–5% of all invasive cancers. The presentation of isolated CUP within a MPE is an exceedingly rare clinical scenario. The incidence of CUP in context of MPE is close to 11% with extremely poor prognosis2. Immunohistochemical analysis played a pivotal role in pinpointing the source of malignancy to a pancreato-biliary origin, effectively eliminating lung cancer as the presumed primary source for the malignant pleural effusion. This case represents a unique manifestation of an intra-abdominal malignancy, as indicated by immunohistochemical markers, which metastasized to pleural fluid without the identification of an overt primary tumor through available diagnostic modalities.
