N2  - BACKGROUND: Respiratory infections are a severe risk to CKD patients and can be fatal if not promptly addressed. Understanding the microorganisms involved aids in initiating empirical treatment while awaiting confirmatory tests. Due to similarities in symptoms and imaging a more accurate explanation of dyspnea in CKD patients is crucial. Diagnostic methods include sputum testing chest X-rays HRCT scans bronchoscopy and thoracic biopsies. AIM OF THE STUDY: To study the clinical features microbiological spectrum and the role of various diagnostic modalities and outcome of pulmonary infections in renal patients. METHODS: A cross-sectional study was conducted at Pushpawati Singhania Hospital and Research Institute's Department of Nephrology. It included CKD and post-renal transplant patients with respiratory infections attending the outpatient department over two years from April 2021 to April 2023. Patients with end-stage renal disease who were bedridden or in poor general condition were excluded. Comprehensive clinical evaluations were conducted including physical examinations and detailed patient histories. In addition various laboratory tests radiographic evaluations and specialized examinations were performed to identify the causative organisms. Sputum specimens were examined for bacterial and fungal elements using Gram stain and Ziehl-Nielsen stain for acid-fast bacilli. Additional procedures such as bronchoalveolar lavage highresolution CT scans and culture sensitivity tests were also carried out. CONCLUSION: The predominant symptoms reported in this study were cough, fever, and generalized weakness. The CT scans revealed common findings such as cavitations, nodules, lymph node involvement, and pleural effusion. Bacterial infections were the most prevalent among the reported cases. The most frequently identified organisms in the study were Klebsiella, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, SARSCoV-2 virus. Mortality rate was 9.78%.
