N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Nocardia is an opportunistic gram-positive organism primarily affecting immunocompromised patients. Cerebral nocardiosis is rare, accounting for 2% of all intracranial abscesses. Here, we report a patient who suffered from Nocardia farcinica brain abscess successfully managed with antibiotics and neurosurgical intervention. CASE PRESENTATION: A 38 year old male suffering with polysubstance use on methadone, homelessness, and treated Hepatitis C presented with seizures and was intubated for airway protection. The patient experienced cough, chills, and fatigue for two days prior to admission and tested positive for Influenza A. Initial labs were notable for leukocytosis of 11 K/mm3 and lactic acidosis of 12 mmol/L. Computed tomography (CT) head showed vasogenic edema concerning for posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome versus abscess and dexamethasone and empiric meningitis treatment were initiated. Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain demonstrated multiloculated right posterior parietal abscess. A right parietal craniotomy with abscess drainage was performed revealing purulent fluid with gram stain demonstrating branching gram positive rods. Further speciation resulted in Nocardia farcinica and the patient was started on Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX), Imipenem, and Amikacin and successfully extubated on post operative day two. HIV, AFB and Hepatitis panels were negative with the exception of a reactive Hepatitis C Ab. DISCUSSION: Nocardia is an opportunistic gram positive branching rod found in soil primarily causing pneumonia in affected individuals. Cerebral Nocardiosis is a rare complication thought to be a result of hematogenous spread of the bacteria. N. farcinica has the highest predilection for brain abscess formation of the 16 different Nocardia species. Nocardia species carry the highest morbidity and mortality of all brain abscesses necessitating prompt detection and intervention. Identification of Nocardia can be difficult due to the prolonged incubation time for culture. Microbiologic evaluation demonstrates gram positive, beaded, branching filaments that are partially acid-fast and form right angles when exposed to thioglycollate medium. Nocardia is the only gram positive branching bacteria staining partially acid fast, however, the sensitivity of this test is low. 16s rDNA sequencing may be necessary in some cases. Radiologically, Norcardia brain abscesses demonstrate single or multiloculated lesions similar to a "bunch of grapes" shape with restricted diffusion and perilesional vasogenic edema. Neurosurgical intervention with open craniotomy and abscess drainage is critical in identification. Management of Nocardia brain abscesses is complicated due to a high antimicrobial resistance profile4 and predilection to relapse despite therapy. Typical treatment is with TMP-SMX for 6 weeks to 1 year. Due to resistance, Amikacin and Imipenem are typically added as with this patient. Moxifloxacin has also shown to be effective. Patients with a single abscess have a mortality rate of 33%, while multiple abscesses carry a mortality rate of 66%. Outpatient management can include TMP-SMX, Minocycline, and Augmentin. CONCLUSIONS: Cerebral Nocardiosis is often secondary to primary lung infection, however, this was not identified in our patient. The patient's immunocompromised state with Hepatitis C reinfection most likely led to dissemination and formation of cerebral nocardiosis. Total surgical excision and prolonged antibiotic therapy are the mainstays of treatment and resulted in successful management in this case. REFERENCE #1: Baldawa S, Nayak N, et al. Cerebral nocardiosis. Asian Journal of Neurosurgery. 2014; 9(4). REFERENCE #2: Jiangqin Song, Lian Dong, Yan Ding, Junyang Zhou. A case report of brain abscess caused by Nocardia farcinica. European Journal of Medical Research. 2021;26(1):1-7. doi:10.1186/s40001-021-00562-2. REFERENCE #3: Spelman, D. (n.d.). Nocardia infections: Epidemiology, clinical manifestations, and diagnosis. UpToDate. Retrieved February 6, 2023, from https://www.uptodate.com/contents/nocardia-infections-epidemiology-clinical-manifestations-and-diagnosis. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Matthew McAuliffe No relevant relationships by Jonathan Pickos
