N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Report Posters 7 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) is a small-medium vessel necrotizing vasculitis that is rare to find and is part of the anti-neutrophil-cytoplasmic-antibody (ANCA) associated vasculitides (AAV) [1]. With the most common manifestations of GPA being generalized and pulmonary symptoms, it can be difficult to distinguish between viral and bacterial illnesses versus vasculitides in the initial workup. Imaging can go on to reveal pulmonary nodules and infiltrates. Finally, renal involvement is also found in the majority of patients after years of the disease. We present an unusual case of a cavitary lesion GPA. CASE PRESENTATION: A 77-year-old male with a past medical history of prior myocardial infarction, abdominal aortic aneurysm s/p repair, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and arthritis who presented to the emergency department (ED) after a fall at home. He endorsed that he fell while transferring from his chair to his walker in his kitchen. He also endorsed having recurrent falls lately. He denied any lightheadedness, dizziness, loss of consciousness, or head and neck trauma. He was also recently diagnosed with pneumonia and was influenza A positive and had a persistent cough despite completing a course of oseltamivir. The patient endorsed chills, fatigue, decreased oral intake, productive cough, and nasal congestion. Chest x-ray revealed hazy opacity in the lateral right mid to upper lobes. CT of the chest showed a 2.4 cm right upper lobe cavitary lesion with surrounding reticulonodular ground glass opacities and an 18 mm right lower lobe pulmonary nodule. Labs revealed creatinine 2.21 mg/dL and were positive for c-ANCA, decreased C3 79 mg/dL (Ref. range 82-185 mg/dL), and C4 9 mg/dL (Ref. range 15-53 mg/dL), urine protein: creatinine ratio 1047.5 mg/g, C-reactive protein 129.72 mg/L (Ref. range 0 - 3.00 mg/L). Pulmonary was consulted and the patient underwent bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound with biopsies taken from subcarinal and right hilar regions via fine needle aspiration and bronchoalveolar lavage. The procedure showed paratracheal adenopathy as well as bleeding which was controlled by epinephrine and cold saline. Cultures from the procedure had no growth. Pathology showed bronchial cells, pulmonary macrophages, lymphocytes, multinucleated giant cells, and eosinophils. He was given amoxicillin-clavulanate for presumed post-viral pneumonia and was discharged home with a close follow-up with Pulmonology. On follow-up admission CT, the patient now had resolution of the previous cavitary lesion but revealed bilateral atelectasis, scarring, fibrosis, and new and increasing pulmonary nodules measuring up to 18 mm, as well as increasing peripheral reticulonodular opacities. DISCUSSION: Diagnosis of GPA is confirmed through biopsy and supported by positive ANCA serologies as well as classic symptoms of upper and lower respiratory tract involvement [1]. Even before testing, there can be a higher suspicion of GPA based on imaging such as cavitary lesions and pulmonary nodules, however, other differentials such as malignancy, infection, abscess, emboli, and alternate vasculitides should also be considered. CONCLUSIONS: While challenging to diagnose clinically, GPA is rare vasculitis that can present in a multitude of ways and requires biopsy for final diagnosis. Cavitary lesions and pulmonary nodules can be presenting signs of GPA. REFERENCE #1: Comarmond C, Cacoub P. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener): clinical aspects and treatment. Autoimmun Rev. 2014;13(11):1121-1125. doi:10.1016/j.autrev.2014.08.017 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ahmad Abu Homoud No relevant relationships by Remi Ashkar No relevant relationships by Eric Costanzo No relevant relationships by Steven Imburgio No relevant relationships by Anmol Johal No relevant relationships by Rushi Patel No relevant relationships by Medin Tafa No relevant relationships by Ndausung Udongwo
