N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Report Posters 13 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Rapidly enlarging fibrocavitary lesions caused by non tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) can be seen in patients who are immunocompromised. At times, an opportunistic infection may precede the diagnosis of an underlying malignancy. We present a case of rapidly progressive Mycobacterium Abscessus (MA) cavitary lesions mimicking primary lung disease, in a patient diagnosed with peripheral T cell lymphoma via endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) and transbronchial needle aspiration (TBNA.) CASE PRESENTATION: A 75 year old male presented with abdominal pain. CT abdomen showed nonspecific inflammation of the right small bowel. Chest X-ray (CXR) showed no acute pathology. The patient was managed conservatively as an inpatient for 3 days. He presented 2 months later with chest pain and cough. CXR showed a right upper lobe (RUL) opacity. CT chest showed a 5.4 cm lesion in the posterior and a 3.6 cm lesion in the anterior segment of the RUL, which was treated as pneumonia. One month later, CT chest showed multiple cavitary lesions. The largest measured 7.7 x 8.2 x 9.7 cm in the apical and posterior segments of the RUL. Sputum acid fast bacilli (AFB) x 3, and HIV were negative. Quantiferon was positive. The patient had worsening dyspnea and hemoptysis, leading to hospitalization. An EBUS and TBNA was scheduled and performed. Biopsies of the RUL showed atypical T cell infiltrate compatible with peripheral T cell lymphoma of intestinal origin. The patient also developed diffuse abdominal pain. CT abdomen showed pneumoperitoneum. An exploratory laparotomy revealed perforation of more than 50% of the distal ileum. Pathology of the small intestine showed monomorphic epitheliotropic intestinal T cell lymphoma. Sputum stain for AFB was negative, but subsequently grew MA in the culture. Post operative course included multiorgan failure with transition to palliative care and comfort measures. DISCUSSION: MA is the second most common cause of NTM lung disease in America. It belongs to a species of rapidly growing mycobacteria that affect patients with pre-existing lung conditions or immunocompromised states. Clinical manifestations may vary, from slow growing lesions to rapidly decompensating hemodynamics. When compared to other mycobacterium species, MA appears as thin walled apical cavities with pleural involvement. Diagnostic criteria include positive AFB on at least 3 sputum samples, radiographic changes and exclusion of other entities. Our patient initially presented with abdominal pain (likely secondary to his undiagnosed intestinal T cell lymphoma) and later developed cough and hemoptysis due to rapidly progressing MA. CONCLUSIONS: Although initial AFB stains from the sputum may show no growth, mycobacterial species such as MA should still be considered when evaluating cavitary lesions in patients with immunocompromised states. In our case, the patient initially had an undiagnosed intestinal T cell lymphoma which may have facilitated the rapid progression of MA. The role of bronchoscopy, EBUS and TBNA remains fundamental in diagnosing pulmonary cavitary lesions and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. REFERENCE #1: Johansen MD, Herrmann JL, Kremer L. Non-tuberculous mycobacteria and the rise of Mycobacterium abscessus. Nat Rev Microbiol. 2020 Jul;18(7):392-407. doi: 10.1038/s41579-020-0331-1. Epub 2020 Feb 21. PMID: 32086501. REFERENCE #2: Prevots DR, Shaw PA, Strickland D, Jackson LA, Raebel MA, Blosky MA, Montes de Oca R, Shea YR, Seitz AE, Holland SM, Olivier KN. Nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease prevalence at four integrated health care delivery systems. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2010 Oct 1;182(7):970-6. doi: 10.1164/rccm.201002-0310OC. Epub 2010 Jun 10. PMID: 20538958; PMCID: PMC2970866. REFERENCE #3: Henkle E, Winthrop KL. Nontuberculous mycobacteria infections in immunosuppressed hosts. Clin Chest Med. 2015 Mar;36(1):91-9. doi: 10.1016/j.ccm.2014.11.002. Epub 2014 Dec 23. PMID: 25676522; PMCID: PMC4710582. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Fareeha Nasir No relevant relationships by Padmaja Sajja No relevant relationships by Sabnam Shakya No relevant relationships by Mantoj Singh No relevant relationships by Kim Moi Wong Lama
