N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters 27 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Acute severe mitral regurgitation (MR) often presents with hemodynamic compromise, dyspnea and bilateral pulmonary edema due to sudden increases in left atrial and ventricular filling pressures. In rare cases, this can manifest as unilateral pulmonary edema (UPE) and hemoptysis, which due to its atypical nature can lead to delays in diagnosis and worsened prognosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 83 year-old male presented with 5 days of cough and hemoptysis. His medical history was significant for coronary artery disease with CABG and recent radiation therapy for left upper lobe non-small cell lung cancer. Three weeks prior he was evaluated in the emergency department with a few days of hemoptysis and one year of dyspnea on exertion, but was ultimately discharged after a chest CT showed only evolving radiation changes. On current presentation, he was afebrile and hemodynamically stable but was hypoxic requiring 10L by nasal cannula. Chest CT showed mild diffuse interstitial pulmonary edema, small bilateral pleural effusions and dependent ground glass and consolidative opacities in the right upper and lower lobes suspicious for superimposed pneumonia or aspiration. Labs were significant for NT-proBNP 2233 pg/mL, troponin 173 ng/L, and normal coagulation profile. Initial transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) demonstrated severe mitral annular calcification, mild calcific mitral stenosis and mild-moderate anteriorly directed MR. The leading concern was hemorrhage from the prior radiation site in the left lung versus pneumonia. He was given diuretics, antibiotics and nebulized tranexamic acid. Broad infectious and immunologic workup included high inflammatory markers (ESR 90, CRP 21) along with positive p-ANCA with negative ANA. His skin and urine studies revealed no evidence of small vessel vasculitis; subsequent PR3 and MPO antibodies were normal. Microbial evaluation was ultimately negative for fungal and bacterial causes and only positive for rhinovirus. Bronchoscopy and dedicated CTA of bronchial arteries were deferred initially due orthopnea and progressive hypoxemia. His respiratory status continued to decline and he was intubated on hospital day 10. Subsequent bronchoscopy revealed remote appearing scant bloody secretions with no evidence of active hemorrhage. Due to ongoing concern for cardiogenic pulmonary edema as primary etiology, transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) was performed showing severe anteriorly directed MR due to flail P3 segment with systolic flow reversal of the right upper pulmonary vein. With this finding, he was aggressively diuresed and was ultimately extubated on hospital day 13 with plans for mitral valve repair with mitraclip once medically optimized. DISCUSSION: UPE and hemoptysis are unique presentations of acute severe MR due to increased capillary hydrostatic pressures from an eccentrically directly MR jet into the right pulmonary veins. In our case, many confounding factors such as prior lung cancer with radiation therapy, possible infectious or autoimmune causes, and only mild-moderate MR described on initial TTE likely contributed to delay in diagnosis. TEE, which can provide better characterization of MR, can be helpful in early diagnosis. CONCLUSIONS: UPE is usually associated with severe MR and increased mortality compared to patients with bilateral pulmonary edema. Treatment of severe MR often involves surgical repair or replacement and therefore early recognition is vital. REFERENCE #1: Attias D, Mansencal N, Auvert B, Vieillard-Baron A, Delos A, Lacombe P, N'Guetta R, Jardin F, Dubourg O. Prevalence, characteristics, and outcomes of patients presenting with cardiogenic unilateral pulmonary edema. Circulation. 2010 Sep 14;122(11):1109-15. doi: 10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.109.934950. Epub 2010 Aug 30. PMID: 20805429. REFERENCE #2: Castello R, Fagan L Jr, Lenzen P, Pearson AC, Labovitz AJ. Comparison of transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography for assessment of left-sided valvular regurgitation. Am J Cardiol. 1991 Dec 15;68(17):1677-80. doi: 10.1016/0002-9149(91)90328-i. PMID: 1746471. REFERENCE #3: Stout KK, Verrier ED. Acute valvular regurgitation. Circulation. 2009 Jun 30;119(25):3232-41. doi: 10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.108.782292. PMID: 19564568. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Pedram Fatehi No relevant relationships by Serena Hua
