N2  - SESSION TITLE: Latest Advances in Management of Obstructive Lung Disease SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Original Inv PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm PURPOSE: Neutrophil serine proteases (NSPs) such as neutrophil elastase (NE) are activated in the bone marrow by dipeptidyl peptidase-1. High levels of NSPs are associated with increased exacerbations (Ex) in patients with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (BE). Brensocatib is a reversible dipeptidyl peptidase-1 inhibitor that prolonged the time to first Ex vs placebo in patients with BE in the phase 2 WILLOW trial (NCT03218917). A subset of patients who experience consistently high Ex rates in BE has been recognized, termed “the frequent exacerbator phenotype”. The present analysis compares patient characteristics and outcomes in WILLOW subgroups based on history of Ex frequency in the year prior to study enrollment. METHODS: Adults with BE in the WILLOW study, treated with once-daily brensocatib (10 mg or 25 mg) or placebo were stratified by history of 2 or ≥3 Ex in the prior year. Endpoints included time to first Ex, annualized Ex rate, changes in sputum NE levels and treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs). RESULTS: Consistent with greater disease severity, a greater proportion of patients with ≥3 Ex in the prior year (n=84) had higher BE Severity Index (BSI) scores, received maintenance macrolides or had P. aeruginosa cultured from sputum at baseline (BL), than patients with 2 Ex (n=171). Having ≥3 Ex at BL was also associated with higher levels of NE at BL than patients with 2 Ex. Brensocatib treatment resulted in prolonged time to first Ex and reduced Ex rates across both Ex groups. The hazard ratios vs placebo (95% CI) for time to first Ex in the subgroup with history of 2 Ex were 0.53 (0.30–0.93) and 0.59 (0.33–1.07); and for the ≥3 Ex group were 0.60 (0.21–1.71) and 0.78 (0.33–1.83), for 10 mg and 25 mg brensocatib, respectively. Annualized Ex rates in the brensocatib groups were lower than those in the placebo groups (38% lower with the 10 mg dose and 22% lower with the 25 mg dose for the 2 Ex group; 45% lower with the 10 mg dose and 26% lower with the 25 mg dose for the ≥3 Ex group). Brensocatib treatment also resulted in greater median reductions of sputum NE levels than the placebo group at Week 24: 2 Ex group: 76.3%, 94.4% and 98.7%; and ≥3 Ex group: 79.4%, 97.7% and 98.1% for placebo, 10 mg and 25 mg brensocatib, respectively. The most common TEAEs (≥10%) in the pooled brensocatib arms were cough, dyspnea and headache in the 2 Ex group; and cough, headache and sputum increase in the ≥3 Ex group. CONCLUSIONS: Consistent with overall WILLOW study results, brensocatib treatment prolonged time to first Ex and reduced Ex rates and sputum NE levels as compared with placebo in both Ex subgroups. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Results support potential of brensocatib as a treatment for patients with BE experiencing Ex. The phase 3 ASPEN trial (NCT04594369) is a larger study that is currently ongoing to further evaluate these findings in BE. DISCLOSURES: Researcher relationship with Insmed Please note: 2022-2023 Added 04/06/2023 by James Chalmers, source=Web Response, value=Grant/Research Support Consultant relationship with Insmed Please note: 2022-2023 Added 04/06/2023 by James Chalmers, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by carlos fernandez Employee relationship with Insmed Inc. Please note: 06/29/2020-current Added 04/04/2023 by Sebastian Fucile, source=Web Response, value=Salary Employee relationship with InsmedInc. Please note: 06/29/2020-current Added 04/04/2023 by Sebastian Fucile, source=Web Response, value=Stocks Removed 04/04/2023 by Sebastian Fucile, source=Web Response Employee relationship with Insmed Incorporated Please note: current Added 03/29/2023 by Melanie Lauterio, source=Web Response, value=Salary Consultant relationship with 30 Technology Please note: past 36 months Added 03/29/2023 by Michael Loebinger, source