N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 25 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: 'Non-resolving pneumonia’ refers to cases of presumed community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) that slowly or incompletely resolve despite antibiotic therapy. This may be due to incorrect diagnosis, inadequate antibiotic therapy, atypical organisms, antimicrobial resistance, impaired host immune response or non-infectious etiologies. In these cases, an in-depth workup including CT scan or bronchoscopy may be warranted to aid in correct diagnosis and direct treatment accordingly. CASE PRESENTATION: A healthy 19-year-old female presented to the emergency department (ED) with a one-month history of non-resolving pneumonia. She initially presented to the ED at symptom onset with cough and shortness of breath. Respiratory viral panel was negative. Chest x-ray showed significant infiltrates in the right middle and lower lung fields. CT chest was comparable with evidence of right lower lobe consolidation. She received a course of levofloxacin for CAP. Due to unresolving symptoms and persistent infiltrates on chest x-ray, patient received steroids as well as an additional course of antibiotics. Despite this, she continued to have fever, productive cough, wheezing, shortness of breath and fatigue. She denied hemoptysis or weight loss. She reported smoking hookah but denied any vaping, alcohol or drug use. She did report a prior history of tuberculosis exposure from her mother in 2014 and completion of latent tuberculosis treatment with isoniazid. On admission to the hospital, CT chest showed interval increase of large focal consolidation of the right lower lobe extending from the hilum to the posterior basilar segments with narrowing of the right lower lobe bronchus. There were multiple small focal infiltrates and nodular densities in the right upper and middle lobes with enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes in the right paratracheal and hilar regions. She was started on broad-spectrum antibiotics with Infectious Disease and Pulmonology consultations. Patient had a bronchoscopy which showed significant narrowing of the distal trachea and right mainstem bronchus due to a combination of extrinsic compression and severely edematous and indurated mucosa with areas of necrosis. Biopsy pathology revealed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with acid-fast bacilli (AFB), confirming pulmonary Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Bronchoalveolar lavage was also positive for AFB. Treatment for tuberculosis was initiated with the standard four-drug regimen. HIV testing was negative. Information from the Department of Health revealed that the patient did not actually complete treatment for latent tuberculosis infection. DISCUSSION: While the incidence of tuberculosis within the United States is low, the number of cases with a delayed diagnosis has increased. Tuberculosis may present as acute pneumonia and be misdiagnosed as CAP. Due to the low prevalence of tuberculosis in the United States, it is often not considered in the initial differential diagnosis. A history of prior tuberculosis exposure, poor response to standard antibiotic therapy for CAP or transient improvement with fluoroquinolones, which have anti-tuberculous effects, should point physicians toward the possibility of tuberculosis. CONCLUSIONS: In cases of non-resolving pneumonia, it is important to keep other etiologies such as tuberculosis on the differential to prevent diagnostic delays, unintentional spread and increased morbidity and mortality. REFERENCE #1: Chaudhuri, Arunabha D., et al. "A Study on Non-Resolving Pneumonia with Special Reference to Role of Fiberoptic Bronchoscopy." Lung India, vol. 30, no. 1, 2013, p. 27., https://doi.org/10.4103/0970-2113.106130. REFERENCE #2: Miller, Aaron C., et al. "Missed Opportunities to Diagnose Tuberculosis Are Common among Hospitalized Patients and Patients Seen in Emergency Departments." Open Forum Infectious Diseases, vol. 2, no. 4, 2015, https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofv171. REFERENCE #3: Pinto, Lancelot M., et al. "Pulmonary Tuberculosis Masquerading as Community Acquired Pneumonia." Respiratory Medicine CME, vol. 4, no. 3, 2011, pp. 138–140., https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmedc.2010.11.004. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Marina Dolina No relevant relationships by Alexandra Myers No relevant relationships by David Nguyen
