N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Report Posters 17 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary sequestration occurs due to a congenital malformation of the primitive foregut, likely due to the formation of an accessory lung bud under the normal lung bud, causing intralobular and extra lobar sequestrations. It is most commonly diagnosed before the second decade of life due to patients presenting with recurrent pneumonia symptoms. We herein present a rare case of late identification of pulmonary sequestration in an elderly patient with a confirmed diagnosis by chest tomography and bronchoscopy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71-year-old male with long-standing secondhand smoking exposure during childhood and noninsulin dependent diabetes mellitus presented to the pulmonary clinic with persistent productive cough with dark brown sputum for over three months. An initial chest radiograph showed a right lower lobe consolidation suggestive of infiltrative bronchopneumonia. The patient was initially treated with a course of azithromycin without improvement of his symptoms. A noncontrasted chest CT scan was done and showed a multilobulated right lower lobe mass with multiple internal cavitations for which tissue sampling was consideried but it was recommended that old images are reviewed first to assure this mass was not a sequestration. A review of an old CT abdomen with contrast done 3 years prior indeed showed an opacity in the medial right lung base consistent with pulmonary sequestration with its own arterial supply, along with cystic changes within the right basilar lung parenchyma. A follow up contrasted chest CT scan confirmed the finding of right lower lobe pulmonary sequestration with pulmonary venous drainage, along with numerous air-fluid levels consistent with infection of the sequestration.Follow-up bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was negative for bacterial and fungal cultures. Nevertheless, the patient completed 4 weeks of amoxicillin-clavulanate prior to surgical consideration.The patient underwent a robotic thoracoscopic resection of the sequestration and partial lobectomy of the right lower lobe, with pathology consistent with intrapulmonary sequestration and no malignancy. The patient's symptoms resolved and he had no postoperative complications. DISCUSSION: Although up to 6.4% of all congenital lung malformations are caused by pulmonary sequestration and around 40% of these abnormalities are identified after the second decade of life, less than five cases in the elderly have been described in the literature in the United States. This mostly occurs because most intra-lobar sequestrations are often asymptomatic. Therefore, sequestration should be suspected in patients with lung abscesses, hemoptysis, and recurrent pneumonia. Lung sequestration diagnosis remains challenging. Chest tomography may not provide a definite diagnosis due to the variable presentation of lung sequestration, which may include cystic lesions, masses, encapsulated lesions, atelectasis, or bronchiectasis. Biopsy should be avoided due to the high risk of bleeding from aberrant vessels. This case also highlights the importance of reviewing old imaging prior to pursuing a biopsy of a lung mass. CONCLUSIONS: This case report highlights a rare congenital pulmonary sequestration presentation in the elderly which was successfully resected by Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery without any complications. REFERENCE #1: Van Raemdonck D, De Boeck K, Devlieger H, et al. Pulmonary sequestration: a comparison between pediatric and adult patients. Eur J Cardiothorac Surg. 2001;19:388. REFERENCE #2: Gezer S, Taştepe I, Sirmali M, et al. Pulmonary sequestration: a single-institutional series composed of 27 cases. J Thorac Cardiovasc Surg. 2007;133:955. REFERENCE #3: Alsumrain, M., Ryu, J.H. Pulmonary sequestration in adults: a retrospective review of resected and unresected cases. BMC Pulm Med 18, 97 (2018). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12890-018-0663-z DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Fatemeh Abbasi No relevant relationships by Nour Assad No relevant relationships by Stefani Garcia
