N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 6 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Burkholderia pseudomallei is a gram-negative bacteria that causes melioidosis, which can manifest as a life-threatening infection in the form of sepsis. Although it is endemic in Southeast Asia and Northern Australia, it is rarely seen in the United States [1]. We report a case of melioidosis in an immunocompetent adult who presented with severe sepsis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 61-year-old female with a history of thyroid cancer presented to the hospital with one month of fatigue. She recently traveled to Algeria, Tunisia, and Mauritius and returned home three weeks before admission. One week before returning home, she developed shortness of breath, nonproductive cough, watery diarrhea, nausea, and fevers. During her trip, she took precautions, including drinking only bottled water and eating fully-cooked food. However, she stayed at several rural campsites during her trip, swam in freshwater, and hiked barefoot. On presentation, she was febrile to 102.4F and hypotensive to 80/50. She had a leukocytosis of 20,400/uL, hyponatremia of 125 mEQ/L, and transaminitis with aspartate transaminase of 53 IU/L. A chest x-ray showed a dense left upper lobe (LUL) opacity. Given her clinical presentation and radiographic findings, she was started on ceftriaxone and azithromycin for the treatment of community-acquired pneumonia. Despite this, she was persistently hypotensive requiring norepinephrine and admission to the medical intensive care unit. A Computed Tomography (CT) of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis was significant for multilobar, dense airspace opacities in the LUL. The dense consolidation on the CT scan, along with hyponatremia, gastrointestinal symptoms, and transaminitis raised concern for Legionnaires Disease; therefore, antibiotics were changed to levofloxacin. Initial blood cultures grew gram-negative rods in the blood; however, urine legionella antigen testing came back negative. Bloodborne parasite testing, C. difficile, and stool PCR panel were negative. Although the patient showed some signs of improvement, she continued to remain hypotensive, requiring vasopressor therapy. On day four of hospitalization, blood cultures speciated to Burkholderia species. She was transitioned to meropenem, and within 24 hours, was tapered off vasopressors. Further analysis confirmed the presence of B. pseudomallei. She completed a four-week course of meropenem and subsequently transitioned to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to complete a three-month course. DISCUSSION: B. pseudomallei is typically found in contaminated soil and surface groundwater in endemic areas. The clinical presentation of melioidosis depends on the route of bacterial entry: inhalation, ingestion, or skin penetration. Pneumonia is the most common clinical syndrome, and patients with underlying lung disease, diabetes, or alcoholism are more prone to severe infections. Eradication is often difficult and treatment regimens involve prolonged antibiotic therapies with high rates of relapse [2]. Treatment involves an intensive phase, followed by a continuous phase for two to three months [1]. Although many cases have been seen in Southeast Asia and Australia, there has been an emergence of cases of melioidosis described in Mauritius [3]. CONCLUSIONS: Melioidosis has the potential to cause fulminant disease and is an important differential diagnosis to be considered for patients that present with severe pneumonia in the setting of travel history to endemic areas. REFERENCE #1: Shenoy, Shabari M., Ankith Vaidya, and Chethan Subramanya. "Melioidosis: a case series from coastal Karnataka, India." (2019) REFERENCE #2: Wiersinga, W., van der Poll, T., White, N. et al. Melioidosis: insights into the pathogenicity of Burkholderia pseudomallei. Nat Rev Microbiol 4, 272–282 (2006). REFERENCE #3: Bundhun, C. D., Gokhool, H., & Issack, M. I. (2005). Melioidosis in Mauritius. Emerging Infectious Diseases, 11(1). DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sathya Alekhya Bukkuri No relevant relationships by Henry Mayo-Malasky No relevant relationships by Mangalore Amith Shenoy
