N2  - SESSION TITLE: Biotechnology Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: The Karius Test utilizes microbial cell-free DNA next generation sequencing (NGS) to detect a variety of pathogens via blood sample. This case report highlights the utility of Karius Testing in the detection of Legionnaires disease (LD) not detected on legionella urine antigen test (UAT) or bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) culture. CASE PRESENTATION: A 25-year-old obese male with no known history presented with productive cough, fever, and malaise and was admitted for community acquired pneumonia managed with ceftriaxone. Initial respiratory infectious workup was negative (serum Mycoplasma Antibody(Ab), urine Strep Pneumoniae Ag, Legionella UAT, Chlamydia Ag, Aspergillus Ag, Coccidioidomycosis Ab, Fungitell, and sputum culture). Initial CXR showed a faint LLL opacity (figure 1A). On day 3, he developed rapidly progressive acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation and paralysis. Antibiotics were broadened to azithromycin, zosyn, and vancomycin. Repeat CXR and CT chest showed bilateral multifocal airspace opacities consistent with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) (figure 1B). On day 5, veno-venous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (VV ECMO) was initiated (figure 1C). Repeat infectious workup and bronchoscopy with BAL, including BAL legionella culture and UAT, remained negative. Serum Karius Test confirmed the diagnosis of LD. He was decannulated from VV ECMO on day 20 and liberated from mechanical ventilation 3 days later. He continued to improve on azithromycin and was discharged on day 30 (figure 1D). DISCUSSION: Respiratory secretion culture is the gold standard for diagnosis of LD but UAT is more commonly used as it is rapid and affordable. Culture on selective buffered charcoal yeast extract media has a sensitivity of 80%, specificity of 99% and is able to detect many legionella species however yield decreases with antibiotic exposure.1 Cultures often result within 3-5 days but can take up to 2 weeks leading to delays in patient care. UAT can result within minutes to 2 days however it has a lower sensitivity (~ 70%) and is only able to detect Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1.2 The Karius Test is a non-invasive and prompt tool that can detect many infections. The ability to test for hundreds of pathogens in a single blood test allows for early diagnosis and management of severe infections. Though cfDNA NGS is traditionally considered expensive, increased use and availability of the tool may allow it to become more affordable.3 Karius may prove to be a cost effective diagnostic tool in cases where invasive diagnostics (i.e. bronchoscopy) and multiple pathogen specific assays are required. It may also help eliminate the time dissociation between diagnosis and treatment as the turnaround time is typically 2-4 days. Studies show Karius has outperformed other microbiology tests in diagnostic yield with a sensitivity around 95% and specificity around 99%. In this case, both BAL culture on selective media and UAT failed to detect active LD however Karius confirmed the diagnosis ultimately leading to a favorable patient outcome. CONCLUSIONS: The Karius Test utilizing cfDNA NGS has proven to have utility in detecting infections of unknown origin. Clinicians should consider routine use of Karius as it may ultimately lead to decreased morbidity and mortality. REFERENCE #1: Pierre, D. M., Baron, J., Yu, V. L., & Stout, J. E. (2017). Diagnostic testing for Legionnaires' disease. Annals of Clinical Microbiology and Antimicrobials, 16(1). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12941-017-0229-6 REFERENCE #2: Harris, A. M., Bramley, A. M., Jain, S., Arnold, S. R., Ampofo, K., Self, W. H., Williams, D. J., Anderson, E. J., Grijalva, C. G., McCullers, J. A., Pavia, A. T., Wunderink, R. G., Edwards, K. M., Winchell, J. M., & Hicks, L. A. (2017). Influence of Antibiotics on the Detection of Bacteria by Culture-Based and Culture-Independent Diagnostic Tests in Patients Hospitalized With Community-Acquired Pneumonia. Open Forum Infectious Diseases, 4(1). https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofx014 REFERENCE #3: Murdoch, D. R. (2003). Diagnosis ofLegionellaInfection. Clinical Infectious Diseases, 36(1), 64–69. https://doi.org/10.1086/345529 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mohamed Fayed No relevant relationships by Jasmine Nudanu
