N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 29 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Pasteurella small gram-negative coccobacilli commensurate bacteria in animals and are known zoonotics that cause a variety of infections in humans. The most common disease-causing species is Pasteurella multocida[1]. Most common infections are soft tissue infections and cellulitis transmitted from animal bites or scratches. Pasteurella related pneumonias are less common in immunocompetent patients. Immunosuppression and immunomodulation increase risk for Pasteurella infection. Denosumab, a receptor activator of nuclear factor κ-B ligand (RANKL), is known to have immunomodulatory effects[2]. CASE PRESENTATION: 80 year old female was referred to outpatient pulmonary clinic for an abnormal CT Chest. Patient has a prior 55 pack-year smoking history, hypothyroidism and osteoporosis treated with denosumab. Patient reported a chronic cough for more than two years. She worked as a secretary and denied occupational exposures. She reported dogs in the household. Initial CT Chest revealed changes within the bilateral lower lobes with mucous plugging. Patient declined complete pulmonary function test (PFT). Repeat CT Chest 8 months later showed minimal improvement. Patient reported new-onset dyspnea on exertion. She continued to decline testing and therapy. Follow-up CT Chest 21 months after initial scan showed severe emphysema and worsening patchy opacity in the left lung base. Patient reported congested cough but declined PFT and bronchoscopy. Final CT Chest 27 months after initial presentation revealed patchy infiltrative densities in the left lung base and peripheral atelectasis/consolidation. Patient continued to have dyspnea on exertion and chronic congested cough. A sputum culture was obtained and was positive for Pasteurella canis. She did report dogs licking her face. Amoxicillin/Clavulanic acid was prescribed for 10 days. Three-month follow-up post-therapy, Patient reported improvement in her symptoms. Radiographic findings improved. Complete PFT showed severe obstructive ventilatory defect with reversibility. DISCUSSION: Pasteurella species are commensal flora in dogs and cats but known to cause zoonotic infections in humans. Skin and soft tissue infections are the most common, typically from bites. Respiratory infections are less common[3]. Our case demonstrates respiratory infection via salivary transfer. COPD alone would not be considered a factor that increases risk for atypical pathogens. Bronchiectasis and various immunodeficiencies were ruled out. Upon review of her medication list, the patient was prescribed Denosumab for osteoporosis. A human monoclonal antibody that binds to RANKL, Denosumab inhibits the function and survival of osteoclasts. Additionally, osteoprotegerin (OPG) is a decoy receptor for RANKL. There is emerging evidence that the OPG/RANK/RANKL system has immunomodulatory effects[2]. Meta-analysis demonstrated higher incidence of serious adverse events of infection in patients treated with denosumab. However, the infections with the highest relative risk were skin and soft tissue[2]. CONCLUSIONS: This patient displayed a unique presentation with atypical pneumonia and atypical subspecies of Pasteurella. Though Pasteurella is a known zoonotic, infections are uncommon. It is likely that the immunomodulatory effects of Denosumab increased the patient's risk. Severe COPD, denosumab, and canine exposure were likely the precipitating factors for infection. This case highlights the importance of detailed history of environmental exposures and the importance of medication reconciliation when patients are diagnosed with pulmonary infections from uncommon pathogens. REFERENCE #1: 1. Bhat S. A Case of Lower Respiratory Tract Infection with Canine-associated Pasteurella canis in a Patient with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease. JCDR. Published online 2015. doi:10.7860/JCDR/2015/13900.6351 REFERENCE #2: 2. Diker-Cohen T, Rosenberg D, Avni T, Shepshelovich D, Tsvetov G, Gafter-Gvili A. Risk for Infections During Treatment With Denosumab for Osteoporosis: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. The Journal of Clinical Endocrinology & Metabolism. 2020;105(5):1641-1658. doi:10.1210/clinem/dgz322 REFERENCE #3: 3. Faceira A, Póvoa S, Souteiro P, Ceia F, Ferreira S. Human infection by Pasteurella canis – A case report. Porto Biomedical Journal. 2017;2(2):63-65. doi:10.1016/j.pbj.2017.01.005 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sean Dawes No relevant relationships by Sam Hamade No relevant relationships by Christine Plichta
