N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 26 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: This case reviews the diagnosis of chest wall blastomycosis in a patient who presented to a community medical center in Chicago with dry cough and shortness of breath. CASE PRESENTATION: Patient is a 40-year-old male with a history of thalassemia trait and tobacco dependence who presented with 6 weeks of non-productive cough and dyspnea. Initial evaluation by primary care with a chest radiograph revealed a large left-sided pleural effusion. He was treated with amoxicillin/clavulanic acid without clinical improvement, and subsequently referred to pulmonology. Worsening symptoms prompted a presentation to the ED.Ultrasound revealed a loculated pleural effusion, so a chest tube was placed with 1500cc of exudative amber fluid removed. Cytology, cytometry, and cultures were negative. He was treated with three days of tPA/DNase therapy and six days of antibiotics. CT chest after chest tube placement revealed a small residual effusion and lytic lesion of the 9th rib.CT chest 4 weeks post-discharge showed resolution of the pleural effusion, a new 4x7cm mass along the posterior left chest wall, splenomegaly, and enlarged right cardiophrenic lymph node, unchanged lytic lesion. A PET-CT revealed hypermetabolism along the left chest wall and epigastric region of abdomen. Subsequent IR-guided biopsy aspirated frank pus from the chest wall. Pathology showed inflammation and granulomas with fungal forms consistent with blastomycosis. Fungal cultures identified Blastomycosis dermatitidis, so oral itraconazole was started. DISCUSSION: Blastomycosis is a pyrogranulomatous infection resulting from inhalation of Blastomyces dermatitidis or Blastomyces gilchristii. The lungs are the most common area of infection, but other infection sites are possible(1). Most extrapulmonary infections are secondary to a primary pulmonary infection.This blastomycosis infection along the chest wall posed a unique presentation as pleural fluid studies did not yield growth on cultures nor diagnostic cytology. IR-guided biopsy of the chest wall mass for malignancy workup yielded purulent fluid and identified Blastomycosis dermatitidis. While consolidation and pleural effusion have been identified as known manifestations of blastomyces infections, discrete chest wall lesions are not commonly identified on CT imaging(2). This case highlights the need for prompt tissue sampling and a broad differential when evaluating PET-positive chest wall lesions, as our initial concern was for bacterial osteomyelitis or lymphoma. Malignancy was unlikely after pleural fluid cytology and flow cytometry were negative. Without biopsy, the patient would have sustained significant morbidity due to delayed diagnosis and treatment.Manifestation and treatment of blastomycosis varies. Pulmonary manifestations may present asymptomatically, as pneumonia, or in severe cases ARDS (3). It is well known to mimic processes such as malignancy and chronic pulmonary infections such as tuberculosis. Disease is staged as mild, moderate, or severe, and these distinctions are based largely on clinical judgment, immunocompetency being a significant factor. Disease severity guides selection of a treatment regimen. This case was classified as mild disseminated disease in an immunocompetent host, so plans were made to continue itraconazole treatment for 6 months. CONCLUSIONS: Blastomycosis commonly mimics other processes. Pulmonary symptoms account for a great majority of initial presentations. Without continued surveillance and definitive species identification, appropriate treatment may be delayed. REFERENCE #1: Endemic blastomycosis in Mississippi: epidemiological and clinical studies. Chapman SW, Lin AC, Hendricks KA, Nolan RL, Currier MM, Morris KR, Turner HR SO Semin Respir Infect. 1997;12(3):219. REFERENCE #2: Ronald S, Strzelczyk J, Moore S, Trepman E, Cheang M, Limerick B, Wiebe L, Sarsfield P, Macdonald K, Meyers M, Embil JM. Computed tomographic scan evaluation of pulmonary blastomycosis. Can J Infect Dis Med Microbiol. 2009 Winter;20(4):112-6. doi: 10.1155/2009/763018. PMID: 21119802; PMCID: PMC2807245. REFERENCE #3: Meyer KC, McManus EJ, Maki DG. Overwhelming pulmonary blastomycosis associated with the adult respiratory distress syndrome. N Engl J Med. 1993 Oct 21;329(17):1231-6. doi: 10.1056/NEJM199310213291704. PMID: 8413389. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Maribe Bangayan No relevant relationships by Kelsey Berry No relevant relationships by Jasper Marc Bondoc No relevant relationships by Jalal Damani No relevant relationships by Kyle Henn No relevant relationships by Matthew Nemoy
