N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 37 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium Paraffinicum (M.P) is a rare, slow-growing scotochromogenic nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) with unique metabolic capabilities. It was initially isolated from soil in 1956 and finally defined as distinct species in 2010 after 5 decades of taxonomic dilemma [1]. M.P clinical reports are limited, with only three cases reported [2, 3]. Here we report a 3rd case in the United States that will contribute to the literature and help understand clinical course and regimen selection. CASE PRESENTATION: antiretroviral therapy (undetectable viral load), chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), restrictive lung disease, tuberculosis (T.B), and aspergilloma complicated by massive hemoptysis requiring bronchial artery embolization and right upper lung lobectomy, and recent mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection treated with two-drug regimen azithromycin and ethambutol without rifampicin presented to the emergency department with progressive shortness of breath for one week associated with dry cough, decreased appetite and fatigue without fever, chills and chest pain. The patient was tachycardic (HR 127), normotensive, afebrile, respiratory rate 18, and saturating at 97% on 4L of intranasal oxygen. The physical exam was notable for cachexia (BMI 20), respiratory distress using accessory muscles, and diffuse bilateral wheezing. Labs were unremarkable except venous blood gas suggested compensated chronic respiratory acidosis. High-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest in Fig. 1 shows calcified granuloma consistent with prior granulomatous disease (blue arrow), cystic bronchiectasis (yellow arrow), and scattered granulomatous nodules (red arrow). Fig. 2 shows the post-lobectomy cavity communicating with the right upper lobe bronchus (blue arrow) and bullae (orange arrow). Fig. 3 shows extensive traction bronchiectasis (yellow arrow), widespread fibrosis (green arrow), and volume loss scarring likely secondary to prior T.B.The patient was managed for COPD exacerbation and continued with a MAC regimen with azithromycin; however, due to persistent symptoms, repeat acid-fast bacillus grew Mycobacterium Paraffinicum, which was consistent with a fibro-cavitary form of nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) lung disease. DISCUSSION: M.P is a rare cause of clinical NTM infection, which uses paraffinic hydrocarbons as a source of inorganic carbon rather than methane, which might explain its distinct antibiotic susceptibility patterns. It is notorious for exhibiting resistance to ethambutol and susceptibility in vitro to amikacin, rifabutin, azithromycin, and linezolid [2]. Like M.P, many slow-growing mycobacteria (SGM) species like mycobacterium scrofulaceum demonstrate susceptibility to macrolides, and these agents are now a cornerstone of most SGM regimens as in our case; however, different susceptibility profiles have been noticed in limited literature, which supports susceptibility tests for each isolate to verify the in vivo behavior of drugs and thus aid in the choice of regimen. CONCLUSIONS: We emphasize the consideration of M.P in elderly with advanced bronchiectasis and limited pulmonary reserve. Moreover, selecting an initial treatment regimen can significantly impact the quality of life, and unlike other mycobacterial infections, its resistance to ethambutol should be appreciated. Furthermore, we emphasize close pulmonology follow-up to monitor relapse and progressive pulmonary disease. REFERENCE #1: Toney, N., et al., Revival and emended description of 'Mycobacterium paraffinicum’ Davis, Chase and Raymond 1956 as Mycobacterium paraffinicum sp. nov., nom. rev. Int J Syst Evol Microbiol, 2010. 60(Pt 10): p. 2307-2313. REFERENCE #2: Chan, A.W., et al., Mycobacterium paraffinicum causing symptomatic pulmonary infection. J Clin Microbiol, 2014. 52(4): p. 1281-3. REFERENCE #3: Barretto, A.R., et al., A case of pulmonary infection due to Mycobacterium paraffinicum from the Amazon Region. IDCases, 2017. 10: p. 1-3. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Bisrat Hussien Adal No relevant relationships by Melissa Berges Morales No relevant relationships by Fnu Vikash No relevant relationships by Sindhu Vikash
