N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 2 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: The incidence of non-tuberculoid mycobacterial (NTM) lung disease has steadily increased [1]. Despite this, there remains gaps in our understanding of the full spectrum of NTM disease. Mixed infections involving multiple NTM species are poorly represented in epidemiological data despite reports present in the literature, with up to four isolates in some cases [2]. Here we present a case of mixed NTM lung infection with dual isolation of Mycobacterium avium intracellulare (MAI) AND Mycoabcterium kansasii. CASE PRESENTATION: A 74 y/o smoking male presented to his primary care physician with several weeks of fever, night sweats, weight loss and productive cough. He had a history right upper lobe infiltrate and positive PPD treated at an outside institution several years prior with 2 months of RIPE therapy and 2 months of isoniazid and rifampin. The treatment was discontinued when biopsy resulted negative. He was prescribed amoxicillin – clavulanic acid and A CT of the chest was ordered. Imaging demonstrated biapical cavitary lesions and he was referred to our emergency room for further evaluation and isolation. After initial sputum AFB smears resulted negative, bronchoscopy was pursued. AFB smear obtained was positive for many organisms and negative for MTB PCR. Transbronchial biopsy was pursued with results consistent with cavitary wall. Due to patient's clinical stability, he was discharged home while awaiting results. MAI and M. Kansasii were identified in both BAL and original expectorated sputum cultures. Based on susceptibility testing a regimen of azithromycin, rifampin,and ethambutol was initiated. Isoniazid was added due to its efficacy in M. Kansasii mediated disease and two months of IV amikacin was administered at treatment initiation in the setting of MAI w cavitation. Patient reported significant improvement with associated increases in exercise tolerance, weight gain, and resolution of productive cough. Surveillance BAL about 5 months later showed persistence of M. kansasii and clearance of MAI. Treatment was continued with plans for repeat bronchoscopy. DISCUSSION: Mixed NTM infections are underrepresented in the literature due to inherent difficulties in NTM isolation and identification. As diagnostics for NTM identification become more advanced, the prevalence of immunosuppression increases, and countries face an increasingly ageing population, characteristics of NTM infection are expected. Although mixed infection has been reported in up to 29% patients there is little date on prevalence or implications on outcomes and treatment. A unique aspect of this case is the persistent concomitant isolation while other reports refer to mixed disease as the isolation of multiple species in quick succession. Additionally. current data suggest that the combination of MAI and M. kansassii mixed infections is unusual with mixed infections with MAI and M. abscessus being by far the most common [3]. CONCLUSIONS: More research is required to determine the prevalence of mixed NTM disease as well as its effects on disease severity and treatment response. REFERENCE #1: Ratnatunga, C. N., Lutzky, V. P., Kupz, A., Doolan, D. L., Reid, D. W., Field, M., Bell, S. C., Thomson, R. M., & Miles, J. J. (2020). The rise of non-tuberculosis mycobacterial lung disease. Frontiers in Immunology, 11. https://doi.org/10.3389/fimmu.2020.00303 REFERENCE #2: Masoumi, Morteza, et al. "Mixed Pulmonary Infection with Four Isolates of Nontuberculous Mycobacteria: A Case Report of Mycobacterium Bacteremicum Infection." Pneumonia, vol. 14, no. 1, 2022, https://doi.org/10.1186/s41479-022-00100-6. REFERENCE #3: Kim Jeung Sook MD, et al. "Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infection: CT Scan Findings, Genotype, and Treatment Responsiveness." Chest, Elsevier, 24 Dec. 2015, https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S001236921549628X. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Daniel Acosta No relevant relationships by Adolfo Alvarez No relevant relationships by Nicholas Camps No relevant relationships by Cynthia Espinosa No relevant relationships by Jason Kovacevic No relevant relationships by Laura Mendez Morente
