N2  - SESSION TITLE: Challenging Chest Infections Cases SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Actinomyces are a class of gram positive, filamentous, non-acid fast staining, microaerophilic organisms that normally colonize the mouth and GI tract. While extremely rare, some of its more common presentations include cervicofacial and pelvic infections. Lesser considered pulmonary manifestations are estimated to account for only 15% of all human Actinomycosis. Among these, presentation is variable as cases may manifest as nodules, consolidations, masses or effusions. As a result, it may imitate chronic lung diseases, malignancy or other infections. Here we present two cases of pulmonary Actinomycosis with endobronchial lesions later identified as squamous metaplasia on pathology. CASE PRESENTATION: Case 1: A 67-year-old male on peritoneal dialysis presented to clinic for recurrent pneumonias and a non-productive cough that had been ongoing for nearly a year. He had no associated shortness of breath, wheezing, DOE or B symptoms. However, he did describe some difficulty swallowing. He had never previously smoked and had no personal or family history of malignancies. He also denied any potentially significant chemical exposures. Chest CT showed a wedge-shaped density within the RUL and subsequent PET scan showed an interval increase in right upper lobe consolidation. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy revealed a polypoid lesion at the ostium of the anterior segment of the right upper lobe (Figure 1). Forceps biopsy and brushings were obtained, and pathology showed squamous metaplasia with filamentous organisms. Cultures resulted positive for Hafnia alvei and Actinomycosis. He was treated with one week of ertapenem before transitioning to a prolonged course of doxycycline. Case 2: A 61-year-old Caucasian female with a history of nicotine dependence presented to the hospital for pleuritic lower chest pains, cough and hemoptysis. She described a choking spell on food that had occurred three months prior. CXR was concerning for a right middle lobe, post-obstructive pneumonia from an endobronchial lesion. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy showed a polypoid lesion at the ostium of the RML (Figure 2). Pathology was also consistent with squamous metaplasia and filamentous organisms. She was started on an extended course of high dose amoxicillin and repeat CT showed significant improvement. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary Actinomycosis is a rare bacterial infection that presumably results from the aspiration of normal flora into the respiratory tract. Diagnosis may be challenging as both imaging and presenting symptoms are largely nonspecific. Furthermore, as illustrated in the above cases, it may even result in the development of squamous metaplasia and endobronchial lesions. CONCLUSIONS: Based upon review of previous cases to date, partial occlusions resembling submucosal tumors or exophytic masses may mimic malignancy or other more prevalent infections. As with any infectious process, early detection is crucial as it has been well established that these patients do have an excellent prognosis. Although current guidelines recommend treatment with high dose IV penicillin for 2-6 weeks followed by either oral penicillin or another oral beta-lactam for an additional 8-12 months, it appears that most cases seem to resolve in less than 6 months. Thus, similar reports with well documented follow up results are also important to help better determine the expected response and possibly alter existing recommendations. REFERENCE #1: Valour F, Sénéchal A, Dupieux C, et al. Actinomycosis: Etiology, clinical features, diagnosis, treatment, and management. Infection and drug resistance. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4094581/. Published July 5, 2014. Accessed March 20, 2023. REFERENCE #2: Farrokh D, Rezaitalab F, Bakhshoudeh B. Pulmonary Actinomycosis with endobronchial involvement: A case report and literature review. Tanaffos. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4153276/. Published 2014. Accessed March 20, 2023. REFERENCE #3: Oikonomidis P, Fousekis F. A case of pulmonary Actinomycosis presented with endobronchial involvement. Respiratory Medicine Case Reports. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213007119302473. Published August 29, 2019. Accessed March 20, 2023. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Fahid Alghanim No relevant relationships by William Arvan No relevant relationships by Daniel Carpin No relevant relationships by Alexa Krebs
