N2  - SESSION TITLE: Images in Respiratory Failure SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome (MKS) or tracheobronchomegaly is a rare clinical and radiologic diagnosis characterized by dilatation of the trachea due to loss of longitudinal elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells. MKS often manifests as recurrent hospitalizations due to lower respiratory tract infections. We present a patient with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung, chronic respiratory failure and idiopathic bronchiectasis caused by MKS. CASE PRESENTATION: A 78 year old male with Hypertension, Diabetes Mellitus, Dyslipidemia and Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) on home oxygen, presented with a 1 day history of shortness of breath and a productive cough. Of note, he had multiple prior hospitalizations due to COPD exacerbations secondary to recurrent respiratory tract infections. In the ED, he was hypoxic at 76% on room air and afebrile for which oxygen was started. On examination, he had poor respiratory effort, crackles at bilateral lung bases which were more prominent on the left side and scattered expiratory wheezes throughout. His chest xray revealed emphysematous and fibrotic changes, bronchiectasis and an ill-defined pulmonary parenchymal opacification of the left lower lobe. A computerized tomography (CT) of the thorax further demonstrated saccular and traction bronchiectasis with consolidation in the left lower lobe (LLL) and lingula, tracheomalacia, and emphysematous changes in the lung apices. Pneumonia panel was positive for Staph aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and non-covid coronavirus and he was treated with Antibiotics, steroids and inhalers for copd exacerbation secondary to pneumonia. He was discharged on a Prednisone taper with plan for a follow-up CT thorax in 3 months. The CT was significant for dense consolidation of the LLL with multiple nodular opacities concerning for neoplasm, which was later confirmed as squamous cell carcinoma. Severe emphysematous changes with a 30.87 mm cross-sectional tracheal diameter, a 24.08 mm diameter of the right main bronchus, and 23.09 mm of the left main bronchus, was also noted, meeting radiological criteria for MKS. DISCUSSION: MKS often manifests as recurrent lower respiratory tract infections due to ineffective mucociliary clearance with resultant secretion accumulation, though patients can also be asymptomatic. A tracheal diameter of >30 mm, right main bronchial diameter of > 20 mm and left bronchial diameter of > 18 mm on CT distinguishes the diagnosis. Tracheobronchoscopy and Pulmonary function tests demonstrating a restrictive pattern can serve as confirmatory tests. Treatment is mainly supportive and consists of pulmonary rehabilitation to facilitate mucociliary clearance and antibiotics to address co-existing infection however silicone stenting and tracheobronchoplasty to achieve tracheal stabilization have also been described. CONCLUSIONS: Given the high mortality from complications of recurrent respiratory infections such as respiratory failure, and death, MKS should be considered as a differential diagnosis in patients presenting with idiopathic bronchiectasis and similar findings. REFERENCE #1: Schmitt P, Dalar L, Jouneau S, Toublanc B, Camuset J, Chatte G, Cellerin L, Dutau H, Sanchez S, Sauvage M, Vergnon JM, Dury S, Deslée G, Lebargy F; Groupe d''Endoscopie de Langue Française (GELF). Respiratory Conditions Associated with Tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome): A Study of Seventeen Cases. Respiration. 2016;91(4):281-7. doi: 10.1159/000445029. Epub 2016 Mar 25. PMID: 27022925 REFERENCE #2: Celik B, Bilgin S, Yuksel C. Mounier-Kuhn syndrome: a rare cause of bronchial dilation. Tex Heart Inst J. 2011;38(2):194-6. PMID: 21494536; PMCID: PMC3066798. REFERENCE #3: Chandran A, Sagar P, Bhalla AS, et alMounier-Kuhn syndromeBMJ Case Reports CP 2021;14:e239876 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Arielle Aiken No relevant relationships by Nida Ansari No relevant relationships by Lefika Bathobakae No relevant relationships by Mourad Ismail No relevant relationships by Sacide Ozgur
