N2  - SESSION TITLE: Diffuse Lung Disease Global Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Global Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Concept in etiologies of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) has evolved to recognize autoimmune basis in secondary PAP. CASE PRESENTATION: A 54-year-old lady was detected diffuse alveolar lung shadow on screening chest radiograph, as a household contact of tuberculosis. She had symptoms of occasional cough with mucoid sputum. She never smokes and has enjoyed good past health. She has worked in construction site laying tiles with cement for a decade with dust exposure, as she had no respiratory protection. CT scan thorax showed scattered areas of ground glass opacities (GGOs) with superimposed interlobular and intralobular thickenings in both lungs, consistent with pattern of crazy paving. Sputum culture for mycobacteria and NAAT for mycobacterium tuberculosis were negative. Bronchoscopy and trans-bronchial lung biopsy were performed, and bronchoalveolar fluid had a milky appearance. Microscopically, fluid smears and lung biopsy showed abundant macrophages, multinucleated giant cells and amorphous eosinophilic materials which were positive for PAS stain (diagram 1). The overall clinical, radiological and histopathological picture was consistent with a diagnosis of PAP secondary to toxic inhalation of cement dust.Her arterial blood gases and A-a gradient were all normal. Pulmonary function tests showed normal spirometry of lung volumes and capacities (=/> 103%) and a marginal DLCO (80%). A qualitative ELISA test for GM CSF IgG auto-antibody was positive.The patient was advised respiratory protection by wearing NIOSH approved N95 at work to prevent further exposure to cement dust. She was asymptomatic on follow-up; repeat CT scan thorax at one year upon respiratory protection at work showed significant reduction in GGOs in both lungs (diagram 2), her DLCO improved to 84% predicted but GM CSF IgG was still positive. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) is a rare syndrome characterized by filling of lung with excessive surfactant due to its defective clearance by impaired alveolar macrophages. Traditionally, amongst the three forms of PAP, i.e. primary, secondary and hereditary, only primary PAP is considered to have autoimmune etiology [1]. However, this concept has been challenged since case reports of detecting GM CSF autoantibody in PAP secondary to inhalation of indium-tin oxide [2]. Subsequently, autoimmune basis in PAP secondary to inhalation of dusts and fumes are more widely recognized [3]. It is hypothesized that dust or fume particles may behave as immunological adjuvants or induce cell apoptosis leading to antigen over-exposure and immune reactivity. CONCLUSIONS: The presence of GM CSF autoantibody in our PAP case secondary to inhalation of cement dust adds to literature evidence of overlap between autoimmune etiology and secondary PAP. Mild PAP cases secondary to inhalation of dust may resolve with time by respiratory protection to avoid further antigen exposure. REFERENCE #1: Suzuki T, Trapnell BC. Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis syndrome. Clinics in Chest Medicine 2016 September; 37 (3): 431-440. REFERENCE #2: Cummings KJ, Donat WE, Ettensohn DB, Roggli VL, Ingram P. Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis in workers at an indium processing facility. American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine Vol 181. Pp 458-464, 2010. REFERENCE #3: Papiris SA, Tsirigotis P, Kolilelas L, Papadaki G, Papaioannou AI et al. Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis: time to shift? Expert Review of Respiratory Medicine 9(3), 337-349. 2015 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Chun Lee Kwok No relevant relationships by Chin Tong Kwok No relevant relationships by Kam-Cheung Wong
