N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 32 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium xenopi was initially reported by Schwabacher in 1959 [1]. They are slow-growing atypical mycobacteria that have been recovered from heated water systems in many countries, including natural waters in the UK. M. xenopi is the second most pathogen of pulmonary non-typical mycobacterial (NTM) infections after Mycobacterium avium complex in Canada, southwest Europe, and the United Kingdom but not in the US where Mycobacterium kansasii is more prevalent. We describe a case of M. xenopi in an elderly patient with long-standing COPD who had a cavitary lung lesion that masqueraded lung cancer clinically and radiologically and posed a diagnostic dilemma. CASE PRESENTATION: A 67-year-old Caucasian heavy smoker (a 55-pack-year) male with a long-standing COPD presented with exertional dyspnea and a productive cough for several weeks that did not improve with courses of antibiotic therapy and bronchodilator therapy. Chest CT scan demonstrated a similar appearance of severe emphysema with a new right upper lobe cavity lesion (3.2X 1.7 cm) (Figure 1). PET scan depicted no significant hyper-metabolic activity in the cavitary lesion. The patient underwent endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) which demonstrated no discrete mass. Bronchial biopsy ruled out malignancy but revealed a non-caseating granuloma. Bronchial cultures grew M. xenopi, which was further confirmed by polymerase chain reaction (PCR). The bronchial PCR was negative for M. tuberculosis and MAC. A triple therapy (azithromycin, rifampin, and ethambutol) was commenced for the atypical mycobacterial infection. The patient's symptoms started gradually to improve and a follow-up CT Thorax was planned after 12 weeks to evaluate radiological response. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary NTM infections primarily by MAC, both globally and in the USA, followed by M. kansasii, particularly in Midwestern USA. M. xenopi was isolated in our patient, a finding that is not much in keeping with the epidemiological pattern in the midwestern USA. The patient had no history of traveling to regions where M. xenopi is more prevalent. M. xenopi is a low-virulent NTM that emerged as an opportunistic pathogen for pulmonary disease and less commonly extrapulmonary infections. The literature described three disease patterns; cavitary disease in patients with chronic lung disease, acute infiltrates in immunocompromised hosts, and rarely solitary nodular disease in immunocompetent patients. Interestingly, M. xenopi infection was reportedly associated with lung malignancy. About 25% of patients with M. xenopi had prior or concurrent lung cancer. The concern of lung malignancy was evident in our M. xenopi patients, considering their suggestive clinical history and the suspicious radiological findings on initial presentation. A diagnosis of an atypical mycobacterial infection with M. xenopi was made based on a combination of a suggestive clinical course with possible radiological findings and confirmatory microbiology. CONCLUSIONS: Our case highlights M. xenopi as an uncommon etiology of cavitary lung disease in an elderly patient with underlying lung conditions. M. xenopi is considered a less frequent culprit of lung disease in the midwestern USA. The infection masqueraded clinically and radiologically as lung malignancy posing a diagnostic dilemma. Physicians should be aware of M. xenopi as a causative pathogen for cavitary pulmonary disease, especially in high-risk patients. REFERENCE #1: Carrillo MC, Patsios D, Wagnetz U, Jamieson F, Marras TK. Comparison of the spectrum of radiologic and clinical manifestations of pulmonary disease caused by Mycobacterium avium complex and Mycobacterium xenopi. Canadian Association of Radiologists Journal. 2014 Aug;65(3):207-13. REFERENCE #2: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, Catanzaro A, Daley C, Gordin F, Holland SM, Horsburgh R, Huitt G, Iademarco MF, Iseman M. An official ATS/IDSA statement: diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases. American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine. 2007 Feb 15;175(4):367-416 REFERENCE #3: Fogla S, Pansare VM, Camero LG, Syeda U, Patil N, Chaudhury A. Cavitary lung lesion suspicious for malignancy reveals Mycobacterium xenopi. Respiratory medicine case reports. 2018 Jan 1;23:83-5. DISCLOSURES: No disclosure on file for HARVEY FREIDMAN No relevant relationships by ELTAIB SAAD
