N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 15 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is a gram-negative environmental pathogen whose importance as a nosocomial agent has been increasing lately due to its pathogenic role in compromised patients. We present a case of recurrent S. maltophilia infections in a patient with non-cystic fibrosis (CF) bronchiectasis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71-year-old female with a history of COPD and non-CF bronchiectasis (FEV1 40%) on 6 litres home oxygen, presented with worsening dyspnea associated with productive cough for two weeks. She had a history of MAC infection 2 years ago and had completed a course of antibiotics. She was admitted to the hospital 6 months prior with stenotrophomonas maltophilia pneumonia treated with bactrim that was later switched to ceftazidime due to myoclonus. Three months later, she was admitted again with s. maltophilia and was treated with ceftazidime. A week prior to presentation, her outpatient sputum culture had grown S. maltophilia once again and she was started on doxycycline. On presentation, she was hypoxic and tachycardic. Computer tomography of the chest showed chronic consolidation in the right lower lobe and multifocal tree-in-bud opacities. She was initially treated with ceftazidime and underwent bronchoscopy which did not show any acid-fast bacilli but did grow S. maltophilia. There was no improvement with intravenous ceftazidime after 10 days, and she was then switched to tigecycline. She improved on this new antibiotic and was discharged on suppressive doxycycline therapy. DISCUSSION: A majority of patients with non-CF bronchiectasis are colonized by bacteria, most commonly Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Haemophilus influenzae and Staphylococcus aureus. S. maltophilia accounts for a minority of cases of chronic colonization but the prevalence has been rising. Poor lung function is the main independent risk factor for single isolation of S. maltophilia. Colonization and chronic infection are associated with the absence of chronic colonization of P. aeruginosa and the number of courses of IV antibiotics. S. maltophilia infections have high morbidity and mortality in severely immunocompromised and debilitated individuals with mortality estimates ranging from 21% to 69%. S. maltophilia exhibits resistance to most classes of antibiotics. The first-line treatment is trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, with the second-line being minocycline and tigecycline, as was successfully used in our case. Other options include ticarcillin-clavulanic acid and ceftazidime. In patients with recurrent infections, chronic suppressive therapy can also be considered. CONCLUSIONS: S. maltophilia is an emerging pathogen in non-CF bronchiectasis and is associated with significant mortality. Patients with poor lung function and absence of P. aeruginosa colonization are at a higher risk. Recurrent infections may require chronic suppressive therapy. REFERENCE #1: Brooke JS. Stenotrophomonas maltophilia: an emerging global opportunistic pathogen. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2012 Jan;25(1):2-41. doi: 10.1128/CMR.00019-11. PMID: 22232370; PMCID: PMC3255966.Mark L. Metersky, R. C. (2022). Stenotrophomonas maltophilia in patients with bronchiectasis: An analysis of the US bronchiectasis and NTM Research Registry, Respiratory Medicine, Volume 193, 106746, Volume 193, ISSN 0954-6111 REFERENCE #2: R Marra, G Sgalla, L Richeldi, E G Conte, A T Hill, Role of Stenotrophomonas maltophilia isolation in patients with non-CF bronchiectasis, QJM: An International Journal of Medicine, Volume 113, Issue 10, October 2020, Pages 726–730, https://doi.org/10.1093/qjmed/hcaa120Soumagne T, Levesque F, Milot J, Godbout K, Lacasse Y, Maltais F. Significance of Stenotrophomonas maltophilia When Detected in Sputum of Ambulatory Patients with COPD. Int J Chron Obstruct Pulmon Dis. 2021 Oct 20;16:2895-2900. doi: 10.2147/COPD.S325419. PMID: 34707354; PMCID: PMC8542894. REFERENCE #3: Said MS, Tirthani E, Lesho E. Stenotrophomonas Maltophilia. [Updated 2023 Feb 1]. In: StatPearls [Internet]. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing; 2023 Jan-. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572123 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Syed Ahmad Hasan Husaini No relevant relationships by Syed Nazeer Mahmood
