N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 22 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: While acid fast bacilli (AFB) smears and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests are used to triage MTB suspects, these tests do not "rule out" MTB, and empiric treatment for tuberculosis should be considered when the index of suspicion is high. Organizing pneumonia (OP) is a diagnosis of exclusion, since about half of cases are cryptogenic and treated empirically with immunosuppressants. CASE PRESENTATION: The patient is a 37-year-old male with anemia presenting with fever and cough for several days. He was born in Colombia and brought to the USA as a child. He denied a history of lung disease, tobacco use, incarceration, or homelessness. SARS-COV-2 PCR was negative and computed tomography (CT) imaging demonstrated a unilateral pleural effusion and multifocal opacities. Sputum AFB smears and MTB PCR were negative. We performed thoracentesis and bronchoscopy for further evaluation, revealing lymphocytic exudate with negative adenosine deaminase and negative bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) AFB smears and bacterial cultures. We treated empirically for community acquired pneumonia and discharged the patient. He remained febrile after completing his antibiotic course and was readmitted. Two weeks after his previous CT, imaging demonstrated a persistent, loculated effusion and progression of nodular opacities in the upper lung fields. Lung and pleural biopsies via video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) revealed evidence of inflammation. We preliminarily diagnosed the patient with cryptogenic OP after autoimmune serologies returned negative and treated him with steroids upon discharge. His symptoms did not resolve, and three weeks after sample collection, the sputum and BAL cultures returned positive for MTB. His symptoms improved with anti-mycobacterial agents. DISCUSSION: Based on the patient's epidemiology, symptoms, pleural lymphocytic exudate, and multifocal opacities, we were concerned for MTB. When his AFB smears and MTB PCR returned negative, and he demonstrated rapid progression of disease atypical for MTB on imaging, this decreased our suspicion for MTB, leading us to pursue VATS for further evaluation. However, three sputum AFB smears with two MTB PCR tests only have approximately 85% sensitivity for culture-positive tuberculosis, potentially missing both culture-positive and culture-negative cases. Culture data is not necessary for a clinical diagnosis of tuberculosis, and with empiric antimycobacterial treatment, the patient's risk of progression could have been reduced, the risk of transmission minimized, and necessity for additional procedures eliminated. CONCLUSIONS: With epidemiologic risk factors and clinical presentation consistent with MTB, empiric treatment of MTB suspects should be considered while awaiting AFB cultures. Even if cultures remain negative at two months, if patients improve clinically or radiologically, a diagnosis of culture-negative pulmonary tuberculosis can be made. This approach is especially important to consider before initiating immunosuppressive therapies for alternative or concurrent conditions. REFERENCE #1: Lewinsohn, David M., et al. "Official American Thoracic Society/Infectious Diseases Society of America/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Clinical Practice Guidelines: Diagnosis of Tuberculosis in Adults and Children." Clinical Infectious Diseases, vol. 64, no. 2, 2017, pp. 111–115., https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw778. REFERENCE #2: Nahid P, Dorman SE, Alipanah N, et al. "Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis." Clinical Infectious Diseases. vol. 63, no. 7, 2016, pp. 147 - 195., https://doi:10.1093/cid/ciw376. REFERENCE #3: King, Talmadge E., and Joyce S. Lee. "Cryptogenic Organizing Pneumonia." New England Journal of Medicine, vol. 386, no. 11, 2022, pp. 1058–1069., https://doi.org/10.1056/nejmra2116777. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Caralee Caplan-Shaw No relevant relationships by Jasmine Corona No relevant relationships by Keshav Mangalick No relevant relationships by Nalinee Srisarajivakul-Klein No relevant relationships by Daniel Sterman
