N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 22 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Streptococcus pyogenes, also known as group A streptococcus (GAS), is a known pathogen to asymptomatically colonize humans. It is also known to cause invasive, as well as non-invasive diseases. Localized infections such as pharyngitis and skin lesions are common. However, invasive disease such as bacteremia, pneumonia, necrotizing fasciitis, and toxic shock syndrome are usually severe and associated with high morbidity and mortality with an estimated 500,000 deaths worldwide. Herein, we report a case of invasive GAS causing necrotizing pneumonia and septic shock in a young patient receiving adalimumab. CASE PRESENTATION: A 33-year-old Caucasian man who presented to our hospital with worsening shortness of breath, cough, chest pain, fever, and chills lasting for four days. Patient had a medical history significant for ankylosing spondylitis, which was being treated with adalimumab. He was never a smoker and did not consume alcohol regularly. Patient reported that his young children were experiencing fever and sore throat few days before he started developing symptoms.On presentation, patient was hemodynamically stable, however, he was febrile 39.3 C (102.7F), and tachypneic with respiratory rate of 37, requiring 0.8 FiO2 and 60L on high flow nasal cannula. Physical examination was significant for pharyngeal erythema, and crackles mostly noted on the right lung fields.Initial laboratory workup revealed white blood cell count of 2.3, procalcitonin of 30.4, and C-reactive protein of 21. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed extensive consolidative and ground-glass opacities throughout the right lung (Figure 1). Streptococcus pyogenes was detected from sputum and blood culture specimen on admission, ampicillin/sulbactam was administered.Patient subsequently developed septic shock on the night of admission, he required intubation and mechanical ventilation. Patient was treated for streptococcal toxic shock syndrome with penicillin G and clindamycin. His respiratory status improved for four days. However, the patient's clinical status worsened on day 5 with deteriorating PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 77 while on PEEP 12 cm H2O and FiO2 1.0. Repeat CT chest revealed cavitary lesions consistent with necrotizing pneumonia (Figure 2). Patient was transferred to a tertiary center for veno-venous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO).The patient underwent percutaneous tracheostomy on day 10. He was subsequently weaned from ECMO and decannulated on day 14. Patient's clinical course continued to improve, he was weaned from the ventilator successfully and tracheostomy tube was removed prior to discharge. DISCUSSION: Group A streptococcus is an uncommon cause for community acquired pneumonia. However, infections caused by this pathogen are known to be associated with high morbidity and mortality. Studies estimate mortality rate ranging between 15 and 43% in invasive lung infections, and exceeding 50% in patients with toxic shock syndrome. Epidemiologic studies done in the United States showed that immunosuppression is a major and independent predictor of death in patients with invasive GAS infections. Early recognition of this pathogen, and initiation of appropriate antibiotic therapy are the cornerstones of successful management of this severe disease. CONCLUSIONS: Our case report highlights the importance of early recognition and treatment of invasive streptococcus pyogenes infections in immunocompromised patients presenting with severe, rapidly progressing pneumonia. REFERENCE #1: Nelson GE, Pondo T, Toews KA, Farley MM, Lindegren ML, Lynfield R, Aragon D, Zansky SM, Watt JP, Cieslak PR, Angeles K, Harrison LH, Petit S, Beall B, Van Beneden CA. Epidemiology of Invasive Group A Streptococcal Infections in the United States, 2005-2012. Clin Infect Dis. 2016 Aug 15;63(4):478-86. doi: 10.1093/cid/ciw248. Epub 2016 Apr 22. PMID: 27105747; PMCID: PMC5776658. REFERENCE #2: Tamayo E, Montes M, Vicente D, Pérez-Trallero E. Streptococcus pyogenes Pneumonia in Adults: Clinical Presentation and Molecular Characterization of Isolates 2006-2015. PLoS One. 2016 Mar 30;11(3):e0152640. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0152640. PMID: 27027618; PMCID: PMC4814053. REFERENCE #3: Barnham M, Weightman N, Anderson A, Pagan F, Chapman S. Review of 17 cases of pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pyogenes. Eur J Clin Microbiol Infect Dis. 1999 Jul;18(7):506-9. doi: 10.1007/s100960050333. PMID: 10482030; PMCID: PMC7102171. DISCLOSURES: No disclosure on file for Adam Friedlander No relevant relationships by Abed Madanieh No relevant relationships by Sameh Madanieh No relevant relationships by Chirin Orabi
