N2  - SESSION TITLE: Cardiovascular Disease: Hearts and Bugs SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Pneumococcal endocarditis develops in less than 1% of native heart valves. Although cases of pneumococcal endocarditis are rare, mortality remains high. We report a case of severe aortic insufficiency secondary to aortic abscess due to disseminated pneumococcal infection. CASE PRESENTATION: This is a case of a 55 year old female with a past medical history of ulcerative colitis, left-sided breast cancer with prior lumpectomy and radiation, alcohol use disorder who presented with a chief complaint of fever, cough and shortness of breath. She was admitted for the management of severe pneumococcal pneumonia with secondary bacteremia. Her hospital course was complicated by rapidly worsening respiratory status. Repeat chest imaging showed worsening pulmonary infiltrates. She underwent a transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) which revealed possible severe aortic insufficiency. A subsequent transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) showed severe aortic regurgitation with a perforated right aortic leaflet and a large mobile vegetation with concerns for aortic root abscess. The patient underwent urgent surgical intervention and was found to have a localized root abscess of the aortic valve and several large vegetations with perforations of two leaflets of the valve. Intraoperatively, she underwent debridement of aortic root abscess and aortic valve replacement with a tissue valve. She remained stable post-operatively and was discharged on a 6-week course of IV antibiotics. DISCUSSION: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a typical example of a highly invasive, Gram positive extracellular bacterial pathogen. Pneumococcal endocarditis often causes extensive valve destruction and paravalvular involvement with a high mortality rate of 50% to 62% without treatment. Classically, an invasive pneumococcal infection presents as meningitis and bacteremia. It remains a clinical challenge for the early recognition of cardiac involvement, especially in patients without typical peripheral features of endocarditis. TTE has diagnostic limitations for diagnosis and a TEE should be performed in all cases as it favors early detection. Valvular indications for early surgery include the presence of large vegetations, paravalvular abscess, valve perforation, or in patients with prosthetic valve endocarditis and relapsing infection. Early surgical intervention in severe pneumococcal infections with invasive pneumococcal endocarditis decreases mortality by about 50%. CONCLUSIONS: In an era of penicillin therapy and pneumococcal conjugate vaccination, diagnosing a disseminated pneumococcal infection is infrequent and rare. Given the rarity and high mortality associated with this invasive disease, this report highlights the importance of maintaining a high index of suspicion for cardiac involvement in a disseminated Streptococcus infection with the significance of early diagnosis and treatment to reduce complications. REFERENCE #1: Bindroo S, Akhter S, Thakur K, Geller C. Austrian Triad Complicated by Septic Arthritis and Aortic Root Abscess. Cureus. 2018 Jul 20;10(7):e3018. doi: 10.7759/cureus.3018. PMID: 30254807; PMCID: PMC6150766. REFERENCE #2: Lacalzada J, Padilla M, de la Rosa A, Laynez I. Infectious endocarditis due to Streptococcus pneumoniae in a cardiac surgery patient: a new form of clinical presentation. Clin Case Rep. 2015 Dec 8;4(2):129-32. doi: 10.1002/ccr3.448. PMID: 26862407; PMCID: PMC4736536. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sumarah Curry No relevant relationships by Fatima Ghazal No relevant relationships by John Hinson No relevant relationships by Shubhi Pandey
