N2  - SESSION TITLE: Obstructive Lung Disease Posters 9 SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm PURPOSE: Chronic airway exposure to harmful substances, such as poisonous gases, cigarette smoke, and fine dust, triggers chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), characterized by impaired lung function and unbridled immune responses. Although several drugs have been developed to treat COPD, long-term intake of these drugs could have adverse effects. As an alternative to these medicines, we suggest that Lactobacillus plantarum KF511 strain (Deposit number: KCCM 12573p) may effectively ameliorate the respiratory diseases symptoms without any side effects. PURPOSE: Chronic airway exposure to harmful substances, such as poisonous gases, cigarette smoke, and fine dust, triggers chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), characterized by impaired lung function and unbridled immune responses. Although several drugs have been developed to treat COPD, long-term intake of these drugs could have adverse effects. As an alternative to these medicines, we suggest that Lactobacillus plantarum KF511 strain (Deposit number: KCCM 12573p) may effectively ameliorate the respiratory diseases symptoms without any side effects. METHODS: in vitro assay: H292, HL-60, and MH-S cells were purchased from ATCC and cultured in RPMI 1640 medium containing 10% FBS and 1% penicillin-streptomycin. The cells were incubated at 37 °C in 5% CO2 for 24 h. after treatment of L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 104-6 cells/mL), MUC5AC and MIP-2 were measured by ELISA and NETosis were detected by SYTOX green dye. in vivo experiments: Male BALB/C mice (5 weeks old, 20 g) were purchased from Orient Bio. Mice were divided into the following four groups: control, PPE/CSE, PPE/CSE and L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 108 cells/head), and 10 mg/kg roflumilast group, as the positive control. To set the COPD model, mice were intranasally treated with 20 μL of PPE (1.2 U/head) once a week and CSE (100%) three times a week from days 7 to 18. The PPE/CSE and L. plantarum KF511 groups were given PBS or L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 108 cells/head) from days 0 to 18. The roflumilast group was given 10 mg/kg roflumilast from days 7 to 18. The mice were sacrificed on day 19. METHODS: in vitro assay: H292, HL-60, and MH-S cells were purchased from ATCC and cultured in RPMI 1640 medium containing 10% FBS and 1% penicillin-streptomycin. The cells were incubated at 37 °C in 5% CO2 for 24 h. after treatment of L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 104-6 cells/mL), MUC5AC and MIP-2 were measured by ELISA and NETosis were detected by SYTOX green dye. in vivo experiments: Male BALB/C mice (5 weeks old, 20 g) were purchased from Orient Bio. Mice were divided into the following four groups: control, PPE/CSE, PPE/CSE and L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 108 cells/head), and 10 mg/kg roflumilast group, as the positive control. To set the COPD model, mice were intranasally treated with 20 μL of PPE (1.2 U/head) once a week and CSE (100%) three times a week from days 7 to 18. The PPE/CSE and L. plantarum KF511 groups were given PBS or L. plantarum KF511 (1 x 108 cells/head) from days 0 to 18. The roflumilast group was given 10 mg/kg roflumilast from days 7 to 18. The mice were sacrificed on day 19. RESULTS: L. plantarum KF511 is a facultative anaerobic, non-sporulating, gram-positive, rod shaped bacterium originally derived from kimchi. The L. plantarum KF511 exhibited various activities to inhibit secretion of MUC5AC in NCI-H292 cells, activation of NETosis in HL-60 cells, and inflammatory cytokine MIP-2 in MH-S cells. Furthermore, the L. plantarum KF511 showed abilities to prevent lung tissue damage, inhibit the infiltration of total cells or immune cells, such as macrophages, lymphocytes, and neutrophils, in both BALF and lung tissues of cigarette smoke extract- and porcine pancreas elastase-induced COPD mouse model. In clinical trial (randomized double blind placebo control study), intake of L. plantarum KF511 for 12 weeks significantly changed the variety respiratory symptoms such as COPD Assessment Test (CAT), St George Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ), Breathlessness, Cough, and Sputum Scale (BCSS), and mMRC dyspnea index. RESULTS: L. plantarum KF511 is a facultative anaerobic, non-sporulating, gram-positive, rod shaped bacterium originally derived from kimchi. The L. plantarum KF511 exhibited various activities to inhibit secretion of MUC5AC in NCI-H292 cells, activation of NETosis in HL-60 cells, and inflammatory cytokine MIP-2 in MH-S cells. Furthermore, the L. plantarum KF511 showed abilities to prevent lung tissue damage, inhibit the infiltration of total cells or immune cells, such as macrophages, lymphocytes, and neutrophils, in both BALF and lung tissues of cigarette smoke extract- and porcine pancreas elastase-induced COPD mouse model. In clinical trial (randomized double blind placebo control study), intake of L. plantarum KF511 for 12 weeks significantly changed the variety respiratory symptoms such as COPD Assessment Test (CAT), St George Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ), Breathlessness, Cough, and Sputum Scale (BCSS), and mMRC dyspnea index. CONCLUSIONS: In conclusion, the effects of L. plantarum KF511 can help to prevent respiratory diseases such as COPD and can be applied as functional foods for ‘Respiratory Health’ certified by Food and Drug Safety. CONCLUSIONS: In conclusion, the effects of L. plantarum KF511 can help to prevent respiratory diseases such as COPD and can be applied as functional foods for ‘Respiratory Health’ certified by Food and Drug Safety. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: The incidence of respiratory diseases is continuously increasing by social and environmental changes, and the respiratory diseases occur in various types such as asthma, allergic rhinitis, COPD, pulmonary fibrosis, and pneumonia. However, despite the threats and risks of these respiratory diseases, there is no countermeasure to protect the respiratory system before the development of disease. We believe that the L. plantarum KF511 found in this study can help to prevent respiratory diseases from environmental harmful factors. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: The incidence of respiratory diseases is continuously increasing by social and environmental changes, and the respiratory diseases occur in various types such as asthma, allergic rhinitis, COPD, pulmonary fibrosis, and pneumonia. However, despite the threats and risks of these respiratory diseases, there is no countermeasure to protect the respiratory system before the development of disease. We believe that the L. plantarum KF511 found in this study can help to prevent respiratory diseases from environmental harmful factors. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Hee Soon Shin
