N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pediatrics Posters SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm PURPOSE: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is the most common multi-system genetic disorder among the Caucasian population. It was thought to be extremely rare or absent in Indian subcontinent countries like Bangladesh but literature and research from different corners of this region show that the disease is not so uncommon as thought previously rather underdiagnosed or missed in the majority of cases. METHODS: This prospective observational study was conducted for a period of 05 years from January 2017 to December 2021 in a tertiary care hospital in Dhaka, Bangladesh, and a total of 500 patients ( N = 500 ) were enrolled in the study following inclusion and exclusion criteria strictly. Sweat collection was done by pilocarpine iontophoresis following Gibson and Cooke method using indigenously developed and validated equipment. Chloride estimation was done by Schales and Schales method. The minimum acceptable sweat volume was 100mg collected after 30 minutes of iontophoresis. The sweat chloride concentration of more than 60 meq/L was considered positive, 30 - 60 meq/L was equivocal and less than 30 meq/L was negative. RESULTS: Among 500 clinically suspected CF patients, Sweat Chloride Test (SCT) was positive in 47 patients ( 9.40% ) among which 32 ( 68.08% ) were male. Age distribution was 10 months to 32 years, mean age was 9.3 years. The common clinical presentations among SCT positive patients were repeated respiratory tract infections (91.48%), failure to thrive (85.10%), chronic cough (82.97%), digital clubbing (46.80%), recurrent pneumonia (34.04%), persistent pneumonia (14.89%), chronic malabsorption (25.53%), steatorrhea (19.14%), rectal prolapse (6.38%), azoospermia (4.25%). Radiological evidence of bilateral bronchiectasis in High Resolution Computed Tomography (HRCT) scanning and growth of pseudomonas aeruginosa in sputum culture was seen in 57.44% and 63.82% SCT positive patients respectively. CONCLUSIONS: Cystic fibrosis is more common in people of Bangladesh origin than previously recognized. A stronger and more structured system for proper diagnosis and effective management is required for this disease. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Cystic fibrosis, the most common monogenetic disorder among the Caucasian population, is very neglected and clinically underdiagnosed in Asian sub-continent countries like Bangladesh with a strong belief that CF is a disease of white people and it's existence is denied strongly as it mimics with common diseases of this region like tuberculosis, pneumonia, chronic malabsorption, etc. Misdiagnosis and hence the mismanagement results in the advancement of the disease with an increase in familial as well as social burden. A high index of suspicion among physicians and an increase in diagnostic availability for early detection of the disease are necessary. The present study introduced the pilocarpine iontophoresis sweat chloride test for the first time in Bangladesh using locally developed low-cost equipment for easy, inexpensive, and early diagnosis of CF. This study also gives an idea about the extent of the existence of CF and it's clinical spectrum in this country which may enhance the need for the development of a country-specific management protocol. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Firoza Akter No relevant relationships by Saifuddin Khaled No relevant relationships by Jahangir Khan No relevant relationships by Khadija Rahman
