N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 22 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Actinomycosis is a granulomatous infection caused by gram-positive anaerobic bacteria found in the human mucosa-like oral cavity. Immunocompromised patients are usually affected; however, immunocompetent patients with poor oral hygiene can be infected with actinomyces odontolyticus. In our case, we highlight actinomyces odontolyticus of periodontal origin as a risk factor for COPD exacerbations. CASE PRESENTATION: A 62-year-old female with past medical history of COPD, OSA on 2L home oxygen, and former tobacco abuse presented with worsening dyspnea, tachypnea, and chest tightness. She had frequent hospitalizations for COPD exacerbations requiring corticosteroids with minimal improvement in symptoms. Blood work did not have any abnormalities but Chest CT showed ground glass nodules and a 9 mm cavitary lesion. Bronchoscopy with BAL was positive for actinomycetes odontolyticus. The patient's symptoms improved with intravenous ampicillin for six weeks. Patient was advised appropriate dental care to avoid further infections. DISCUSSION: Case of Actinomycosis have declined during the last forty years. Actinomyces are gram-positive anaerobic bacteria belonging to the family Actinomyceataceae and are commensals of the human mucosa. Pulmonary actinomycosis occurs when bodily fluid containing Actinomyces spp. is aspirated. Pneumonitis leads to pulmonary necrosis and cavity formation. The most common presentation is a chronic cough and dyspnea unresponsive to conventional treatment. The disease shares many similar clinical features with chronic suppurative lung infections. Due to its indolent growing conditions, confirmation can still be difficult even when clinical suspicion is high. Basic tests reflect the nonspecific inflammatory nature of the illness with mild leukocytosis and normochromic anemia with nonspecific elevations in inflammatory markers such as erythrocyte sedimentation rate and the C‐reactive protein. Imaging can show mediastinal lymphadenopathy with consolidation of the adjacent pleura. Pulmonary infiltrates with air bronchograms denoted as the "air sign", may be more suggestive of thoracic actinomycosis. Although it is now a rare disease with a low mortality rate, early accurate diagnosis will prevent complications and death. Parenteral therapy typically occurs for four to six weeks and oral therapy is possible after significant improvement in the patient's condition. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary actinomycosis is a rare disease and extremely challenging diagnosis. With our case we tried to emphasize the importance of investigation of broad differential diagnosis when patients show lack of improvement with conventional treatment. REFERENCE #1: G.F. Mabeza, J. Macfarlane. Pulmonary actinomycosis. Eur Respir J 2003; 21: 545–551 REFERENCE #2: Xing Ding, Gengyun Sun, Guanghe Fei, Xuexin Zhou, Luqian Zhou, and Ran Wang. Pulmonary actinomycosis diagnosed by transbronchoscopic lung biopsy: A case report and literature review. PMCID: PMC4094581 PMID: 25045274 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Muneer Al Zoby No relevant relationships by Molly Howsare No relevant relationships by Ashma Ul Husna No relevant relationships by chitsimran Mann No relevant relationships by Gregory Polcha
