N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 7 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary Blastomycosis has a wide spectrum of severity ranging from asymptomatic and mild to severe and life threatening. Severe infections are associated with immunocompromisation and significant exposure to risk factors. We present the case of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) due to pulmonary Blastomycosis in an immunocompetent patient with minimal risk factors. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60 year old male without significant past medical history presented with ongoing mild fever and cough for the last 10 days. He was seen in the ED a few days ago, diagnosed with right upper lobe pneumonia and sent home on oral antibiotics. Symptoms never improved and he represented in ED with persistent cough and worsening dyspnea. Repeat CXR revealed significant multifocal pneumonia with possible cavitation in the left midlung field. Intubation was required for refractory hypoxemia and ARDS. Detailed history from wife at this point turned out to be positive for recent travel to Minnesota and symptom onset right after that trip. Urine Legionella, Pneumococcal antigens, HIV, MRSA and respiratory viral PCR testing were all negative. Immunoglobulin levels were within normal limits. Karius test and serum Blastomyces antibody tests were performed and returned positive. Pathology from bronchoscopy and lavage were also suggestive of Blastomycosis. Amphotericin B was started and symptoms improved over weeks with subsequent extubation and discharge to the nursing home on Itraconazole. DISCUSSION: Blastomycosis is a fungal infection caused by inhaling spores of thermally dimorphic fungus Blastomyces dermatitidis. Pulmonary and cutaneous mycoses are primarily encountered in people living in the Midwestern and South Central United States. In most cases, pulmonary infection is asymptomatic, causing acute, self-limited disease that often goes unrecognized. ARDS is a rare complication of pulmonary Blastomycosis that is seen in 10% of cases and has a reported mortality rate of 89% which is disproportionately high when compared to ARDS caused by other entities. To avoid this fatal complication, prompt recognition of disease process with initiation of timely antifungal treatment is imperative. Although immunocompromise is well known to predispose to severe disease, our patient only had the risk factor of recent travel to an endemic area. Interestingly, he did not participate in any outdoor activities or have any exposure to dust or soil. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary Blastomycosis usually presents with nonspecific symptoms and commonly mimics the more common bacterial or viral pneumonias. This makes a missed or delayed diagnosis likely which can in turn lead to progression of infection without appropriate treatment. Our case highlights that identification of risk factors including travel to endemic areas is a helpful clue to clinch diagnosis and perform early life saving intervention. REFERENCE #1: Blastomycosis. Bradsher RW. Clin Infect Dis. 1992;14:82–90. REFERENCE #2: Overwhelming pulmonary blastomycosis associated with the adult respiratory distress syndrome. Meyer KC, McManus EJ, Maki DG. N Engl J Med. 1993;329:1231–1236. REFERENCE #3: The treatment of North American blastomycosis. Ten years’ experience. Lockwood WR, Allison F Jr, Batson BE, Busey JF. Am Rev Respir Dis. 1969;100:314–320. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Faraz Badar No relevant relationships by Muhammad Azaz Cheema No relevant relationships by asma iftikhar No relevant relationships by Muhammad Nawaz
