N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 6 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary tuberculosis and symptomatic Coccidioides may present similarly with nodules, cavity formation, pleural disease, and fistulas. Rarely, these infections co-exist and pharmacokinetic interactions between standard anti-tuberculosis and anti-fungal therapies complicate treatment. CASE PRESENTATION: A 53-year-old man from Honduras with uncontrolled type 2 diabetes mellitus had 1 year of productive cough, dyspnea, night sweats and 40-pounds of unintentional weight loss. Two weeks ago the cough and dyspnea worsened, and he developed severe pleuritic post-tussive chest and back pain with fevers. Outpatient chest X-ray revealed large left hydropneumothorax and he was referred to our hospital.CT chest demonstrated multiple bilateral upper-lobe cavities, tree-in-bud nodules, a large 19x15x6 cm left-sided secondary spontaneous hydropneumothorax and adjacent 3.4 cm left upper lobe cavitary mass (Figure 1). Pleuroscopy revealed diffuse yellow pleural exudates, fibrinous adhesions, empyema, and a left lower lobe alveolopleural fistula (Figures 2-3). Sputum Mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR, culture, and AFB stain returned positive. Pleural biopsy demonstrated yeast forms, pleural fluid cytology grew Coccidiodes, and the patient had positive serologic Coccidioides IgG. Given rifamycin-mediated cytochrome p450 metabolism of fluconazole, amphotericin B was the initial anti-fungal therapy, but then patient was switched to rifabutin (less p450 induction) and high-dose fluconazole to establish therapeutic anti-fungal drug levels. Fluconazole-associated drug-induced liver injury developed. After two weeks of therapy, repeat imaging showed decreased fistula size and improving pneumothorax ex vacuo. The patient was deemed a poor surgical candidate with active infection, large fistula size, and severe malnutrition. The patient was transitioned to rifabutin + IPE and posaconazole, chest tube with Heimlich valve, and specialist follow up. DISCUSSION: Concurrent pulmonary tuberculosis and Coccidioides presents a diagnostic and therapeutic challenge. Rupture of the fungal cavity into the pleural space and development of empyema seems to be the most severe complication, occurring in 3% of cases (Spinello). Our patient had multiple risk factors for severe pulmonary Coccidioides and suffered cavity rupture, empyema and fistula formation (Rosenstein). As a non-surgical candidate, anti-fungal therapy was essential to address these complications, but finding a compatible anti-TB regimen proved difficult. CONCLUSIONS: Severe complications of pulmonary Coccidioides occur in 3% of cases and include fungal cavity rupture, fistula formation, and empyema. Azoles are the standard antifungal treatment. Co-infection with tuberculosis brings another layer of complexity with drug-drug interactions between azoles and rifamycins, so careful monitoring is essential. REFERENCE #1: Spinello IM, Munoz A, Johnson RH. Pulmonary coccidioidomycosis. Semin Respir Crit Care Med. 2008 Apr;29(2):166-73. doi: 10.1055/s-2008-1063855. PMID: 18365998 REFERENCE #2: Rosenstein NE, Emery KW, Werner SB, Kao A, Johnson R, Rogers D, Vugia D, Reingold A, Talbot R, Plikaytis BD, Perkins BA, Hajjeh RA. Risk factors for severe pulmonary and disseminated coccidioidomycosis: Kern County, California, 1995-1996. Clin Infect Dis. 2001 Mar 1;32(5):708-15. doi: 10.1086/319203. Epub 2001 Feb 28. PMID: 11229838. REFERENCE #3: Ayudhya, D.P.N., Thanompuangseree, N. & Tansuphaswadikul, S. Effect of Rifampicin on the Pharmacokinetics of Fluconazole in Patients with AIDS. Clin Pharmacokinet 43, 725–732 (2004). doi: 10.2165/00003088-200443110-00003. PMID: 15301576. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Victoria Brehm No relevant relationships by Vincent Chang No disclosure on file for Elizabeth Guy No relevant relationships by Philip Lavere No disclosure on file for Babith Mankidy
