N2  - SESSION TITLE: Genetic and Developmental Disorders Case Report Posters 3 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Chronic Granulomatous Disease (CGD) is a rare inherited condition of defective phagocytes that precipitates recurrent bacterial and fungal infections, diagnosed mostly between one and three years of age. Due to deficient production of superoxide and related microbial oxidants, these phagocytes ineffectively kill ingested microorganisms, leading to recurrent infections. We report an unusual case of a 71-year-old woman with autosomal-recessive CGD, diagnosed at age 55. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71-year-old woman presented with complaints of cough and progressively worsening dyspnea. She had childhood tuberculosis at age six with reactivation ten years later, along with recurrent bacterial pneumonia, specifically Burkholderia species infections, over time. She was an avid farmer with frequent upper respiratory symptoms with normal spirometry, yet decreased DLCO. Serial Chest CTs demonstrated fleeting migratory infiltrates with bilateral reticulonodular opacities. Past records revealed a lung biopsy at age 55, demonstrating necrotizing and non-necrotizing granulomas. A repeat biopsy displayed BOOP 2 years later. Immunoglobulin levels and lymphocyte panel were normal with a negative autoimmune workup for RA and vasculitis. However, she was detected to have only 15% NADPH oxidative activity in her neutrophils. Despite normal T-Cell quantities, she also exhibited a functional T-Cell defect causing anergy. There was no history of hereditary immunodeficiency. She was diagnosed with the autosomal recessive form of CGD. Bactrim antibacterial prophylaxis along with a cholinergic agonist to increase cGMP production that stimulates superoxide release was initiated. She discontinued both years later because of clinical stability.At presentation, she was hypoxic with bilateral fine crackles on examination. CT chest demonstrated diffuse miliary nodularity with patchy opacities, conglomerate mediastinal and hilar adenopathy, and right segmental pulmonary embolism. She was started on Apixaban and empiric IV steroids for a superimposed pneumonitis flare but antibiotic therapy was initially deferred. Subsequent bronchoscopy revealed erythematous airways with clear secretions and no visible lesions. BAL cultures grew Burkholderia cepacia, necessitating Bactrim therapy. Histoplasma and Aspergillus antibodies were negative. Immunoglobulin levels were normal, including IgE with no peripheral eosinophilia, arguing against hypersensitivity pneumonitis. After a brief deterioration requiring BiPAP, her respiratory status improved and she was discharged on Bactrim prophylaxis with a slow steroid taper. She was advised to avoid farm activities and follow up with an immunologist. DISCUSSION: The most common CGD is X-linked recessive with mutations in gp91-phox gene causing no cytochrome production. The autosomal recessive variant has normal cytochrome b but a mutated p22-phox subunit gene with low levels of oxidase activity. Lungs are mostly affected, with recurrent pneumonia and bronchiectasis caused by catalase-positive organisms. Other manifestations include BOOP, cutaneous abscesses, and suppurative adenitis. Our patient demonstrates the importance of considering CGD in older persons with characteristic infection patterns. It is possible that the low level of residual oxidase function in the neutrophils accounted for her relative longevity. CONCLUSIONS: Patients with inexplicable frequent infections should be examined and tested for underlying hereditary immunodeficiency such as an underlying chronic granulomatous disease, despite the age of presentation. REFERENCE #1: Akyıl FT, Sevim T, Barış S, Aksoy E, Tokyay DA, Bodur Y, Aktaş O. Two Chronic Granulomatous Disease Diagnosed in Adult Age. Turk Thorac J. 2016 Oct;17(4):163-167. doi: 10.5578/ttj.30515. REFERENCE #2: Chung, A.G., Cyr, M.M. & Ellis, A.K. Newly diagnosed chronic granulomatous disease in a 44 year old male presenting with recurrent groin cellulitis and colitis. All Asth Clin Immun 9, 9 (2013). https://doi.org/10.1186/1710-1492-9-9 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Deborah Markowitz No relevant relationships by Sushmita Prabhu No relevant relationships by Arunava Saha
