N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: E-cigarette or Vaping Use-Associated Lung Injury (EVALI) includes range of disease processes with various pathology findings that lead to acute lung injury. Currently, EVALI remains a diagnosis of exclusion which make its management extremely challenging. Herein, we present a case of EVALI in a patient to highlight the diagnostic challenges faced during management of EVALI along with the characteristic rapid response of this disease to steroids. CASE PRESENTATION: A 51-year-old male with history of hypertension, presented to the ED due to two weeks of worsening shortness of breath and intermittent non-productive cough. Further questioning revealed that he had quit smoking cigarettes around 15 months ago. Instead, he had started vaping almost a year ago and endorsed using crystal methamphetamine for the last 2 months. In the ED, the patient was noted to be tachycardic, tachypneic, and hypoxic with oxygen saturation of 76%. Auscultation of the lungs was significant for diffuse fine crackles bilaterally. He was placed on 15L oxygen non-rebreather mask. Investigation in the ED revealed leukocytosis with white blood cell count of 18.37, bilateral opacities on chest X-ray, and bilateral diffuse ground glass opacities on CT angiography of the thorax with no signs of PE. Given the above results, the patient was empirically started on ceftriaxone and azithromycin and was admitted.Further workup revealed elevated CRP and procalcitonin. Additional infectious workup including cultures, MRSA, streptococcus pneumonia and legionella screening tests along with serologies for common vasculitides, all proved to be negative. Within 24 hours of admission, the patient's hypoxia worsened. He required transitioning to 60L flow rate and to 100% FiO2 via HFNC.Due to history of vaping along with negative infectious workup, the differentials for his presenting illness were concluded to be interstitial pneumonia from an underlying EVALI, eosinophilic pneumonia, or diffuse alveolar hemorrhage from recent use of crystal methamphetamine. Bronchoscopy was deferred given heightened risk for requiring mechanical ventilation. Consequently, he was started on 1mg/kg daily of intravenous methylprednisolone. Following initiation of steroids, his symptoms improved, and within 72 hours he was weaned down to 4 liters of oxygen via nasal cannula. He was eventually discharged with home oxygen, prednisone taper, and PJP prophylaxis with follow-up as an outpatient. DISCUSSION: This case highlights the complexity of EVALI diagnosis as there is no specific diagnostic test available to confirm it. Given the timeline of vaping history, pattern of infiltrates on imaging, and severe hypoxemia, with negative infectious workup, the patient was diagnosed as probable EVALI. Bronchoscopy with BAL can be helpful to exclude other etiologies such as acute eosinophilic pneumonia. The optimal treatment is not defined yet; however, corticosteroids remain the cornerstone for therapy based on data thus far. As seen in our patient, in a case series of 98 patients where 78 of them received systemic glucocorticoids, clinical improvement was attributed to steroids use. CONCLUSIONS: EVALI remains a diagnosis of exclusion; bronchoscopy has limited utility and treatment with steroids should not be delayed in severe cases. Counseling and offering support for cessation of vaping is important in preventing these cases. REFERENCE #1: N Engl J Med 2020; 382:960-962DOI: 10.1056/NEJMe1912032 REFERENCE #2: Kalininskiy A, Bach CT, Nacca NE, Ginsberg G, Marraffa J, Navarette KA, McGraw MD, Croft DP. E-cigarette, or vaping, product use associated lung injury (EVALI): case series and diagnostic approach. Lancet Respir Med. 2019 Dec;7(12):1017-1026. doi: 10.1016/S2213-2600(19)30415-1. Epub 2019 Nov 8. PMID: 31711871. REFERENCE #3: Layden JE, Ghinai I, Pray I, Kimball A, Layer M, Tenforde MW, Navon L, Hoots B, Salvatore PP, Elderbrook M, Haupt T, Kanne J, Patel MT, Saathoff-Huber L, King BA, Schier JG, Mikosz CA, Meiman J. Pulmonary Illness Related to E-Cigarette Use in Illinois and Wisconsin - Final Report. N Engl J Med. 2020 Mar 5;382(10):903-916. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1911614. Epub 2019 Sep 6. PMID: 31491072. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Tarek Alsibai No relevant relationships by Bilal Bangash No relevant relationships by Sheetal Maragiri No relevant relationships by Humna Abid Memon
