N2  - SESSION TITLE: Beyond the Lungs: Cases of Disseminated Disease SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Disseminated mycobacterium avium complex is a rare disease, and most commonly presents in HIV patients with CD4 counts <50 cells/mm3 or in individuals with severe cell mediated immunodeficiencies. Here we present a case of a patient chronically immunosuppressed s/p renal transplant, who had longstanding waxing and waning pulmonary nodules, 1 of which was positive on culture for MAC 13 years before she developed disseminated disease. CASE PRESENTATION: This is a 75 year old female, former smoker, with a history of Crohn's Disease, and waxing and waning pulmonary nodules. She initially presented for evaluation of a longstanding left lower lobe nodule in 2009. Biopsy of this nodule illustrated granulomatous inflammation, with cultures positive for MAC. Serial chest CT scans showed stability of the nodule without treatment. She later developed renal failure of unknown cause, underwent a kidney transplant, and was placed on Tacrolimus and Mycophenolate. Major symptoms over the next 8 years were fatigue and intermittent dry cough. In 2017, patient developed Pneumocystis pneumonia while intermittently off prophylaxis, and was successfully treated. In 2022, the patient was found to have Cryptosporidium infection after developing profuse diarrhea. Due to a lack of response to treatment, a colonic biopsy was done for further investigation. Abundant acid-fast bacilli were found on biopsy, with culture positive for MAC, as well as PCR positive for cytomegalovirus. She also developed new pulmonary nodules and skin lesions. Both blood and skin cultures were positive for MAC. Thus, patient was started on triple therapy with azithromycin, ethambutol and rifabutin for disseminated MAC. On treatment, she has now markedly improved with resolution of diarrhea and improvement of cough and fatigue. In addition, there is radiographic improvement in pulmonary nodules on CT Chest and blood cultures are now negative. The plan is to continue triple therapy for at least 1 year after culture clearance. HIV testing is negative and her CD4 count is 465. She has been referred to an Immunologist given her history of recurrent opportunistic infections. DISCUSSION: Disseminated MAC infection is seen in patients with severe defects in cell-mediated immunity. Most commonly it has been reported in HIV patients with severely decreased CD4 counts. Despite chronic immunosuppression, kidney transplant patients rarely develop disseminated MAC. This case represents an unusual presentation of disseminated MAC, with pulmonary, GI and skin manifestations. Given the multiplicity of other opportunistic infections in this patient, other cell mediated immune defects should be considered and investigated. CONCLUSIONS: Here we present an unusual case of disseminated MAC in an uncommon host. Her multiple opportunistic infections were out of proportion to her degree of immunosuppression. These patients pose a challenge to diagnose and further manage, so close vigilance and follow-up is always required. REFERENCE #1: Fadlallah J, Rammaert B, Laurent S, et al. Mycobacterium avium complex disseminated infection in a kidney transplant recipient. Transpl Infect Dis. 2016;18(1):105-111. doi:10.1111/tid.12478 REFERENCE #2: Ingilizova M, Epstein S, Heun Lee D, et al. A rare case of disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex with colitis in a renal transplant recipient. Transpl Infect Dis. 2019;21(1):e13011. doi:10.1111/tid.13011 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Anu Menon No relevant relationships by Gopi Patel No relevant relationships by Diane Stover
