N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 15 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Though not widely studied, there is a known association between mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) and lung cancer, with one study showing 2.0% of lung cancers having positive MAC bronchial cultures. MAC will often present as a chronic cough in immunosuppressed patients, and subsequent CT scans of the chest will reveal pulmonary nodules, cavitations, or consolidations. However, these findings are not specific to MAC, and can be seen in lung cancers as well. We present a case of co-existing MAC infection and squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the lung. CASE PRESENTATION: A 61-year-old gentleman presented to his primary care physician with a chronic cough and a history of 10lb weight loss over one year. Subsequent CT of his chest revealed concern for a right hilar mass with multiple nodules bilaterally, with some cavitary lesions. PET/CT was done, and this revealed a right hilar mass measuring 4.5cm by 3.4cm and numerous cavitated pulmonary nodules. Given the high suspicion for metastatic cancer, biopsy of a left lung nodule was performed. Pathology showed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation. Sputum was positive for acid-fast bacillus (AFB), and he was referred to infectious disease due to concerns for non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection. Treatment was deferred at this time, pending speciation, in order to avoid incomplete treatment and increased drug resistance. Possibility of a concurrent cancer was not ruled out, given the large right hilar mass and significant weight loss, prompting a transbronchial needle aspiration of the right upper lobe, with pathology consistent with squamous cell carcinoma. At this point, oncology evaluated the patient for induction therapy, however, opted to delay until treatment for the AFB was initiated. Eventually, Mycobacterium malmoense was identified, and patient was started on azithromycin, rifampin, and ethambutol. After this, he was started on induction chemotherapy. Since starting treatment for MAC and SCC he has had improvement in his initial symptoms and has been doing well. DISCUSSION: A prior study has shown that older smokers, more commonly females, are more likely to have concurrent MAC infection and lung cancer. There are ongoing discussions about whether chronic infection caused by MAC infections ultimately causes cancer versus if cancer increases the risk of contracting a MAC infection. Prior studies have suggested that pulmonary tuberculosis increases the risk of lung cancer, though no definitive comments can be made regarding MAC and cancer risk. While it is unclear what the increased morbidity and mortality rate is in patients with concurrent MAC infection and lung cancer, a study has shown that lung cancer caused mortality in 12.5% of patients with MAC infection treated after 3 years. CONCLUSIONS: This case presents a unique situation in which the initial clinical and radiographic imaging was most consistent with lung cancer. The coexistence of MAC infection and lung cancer, while rare, is not unheard of and especially in the presence of cavitary lesions, should be considered on the differential. A diagnosis of either condition alone could have explained the patient's presentation, resulting in a prematurely terminated diagnostic investigation, and inadequate therapeutic response, ultimately increasing morbidity and mortality. REFERENCE #1: Tamura A, Hebisawa A, Kusaka K, Hirose T, Suzuki J, Yamane A, Nagai H, Fukami T, Ohta K, Takahashi F. Relationship Between Lung Cancer and Mycobacterium Avium Complex Isolated Using Bronchoscopy. Open Respir Med J. 2016 May 12;10:20-8. doi: 10.2174/1874306401610010020. PMID: 27335625; PMCID: PMC4892172. REFERENCE #2: Hagiya H. Mycobacterium avium complex infection mimicking lung cancer. BMJ Case Rep. 2019 Feb 18;12(2):e228847. doi: 10.1136/bcr-2018-228847. PMID: 30782627; PMCID: PMC6388786. REFERENCE #3: Gupta K, Feinstein MB, Goldman DA, Patail HS, Stover DE. MAC Attack: Clinical Correlates of Mycobacterium avium Complex Infection Among Patients With and Without Cancer. J Clin Med Res. 2020 Mar;12(3):142-149. doi: 10.14740/jocmr4058. Epub 2020 Mar 2. PMID: 32231749; PMCID: PMC7092760. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Vignesh Harish No relevant relationships by Hamza Atta Khan No relevant relationships by Gina Patel No relevant relationships by Mir Ali Asghar Shah No relevant relationships by Matthew Woodford
