N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Manifestations of Systemic Disease Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: GPA is an autoimmune vasculitis classically manifesting as renal, upper and lower airway symptoms. Here we describe a case of biopsy-proven GPA with normal kidney function without renal or nasal manifestations in a patient initially thought to have rheumatoid arthritis (RA). CASE PRESENTATION: A 39-year-old male with presumed diagnosis of RA with treatment regimen of prednisone, methotrexate, and adalimumab, presented to his primary physician for three weeks of worsening shortness of breath, productive cough, and malaise. His primary physician sent him to the emergency department (ED) for further evaluation due to abnormal chest X-ray findings.In the ED, the patient had stable vitals and was on room air. Lab workup showed WBC of 12000 and normal basic metabolic panel. CRP and ESR were elevated. Computed Tomography (CT) of the chest showed multiple bilateral large cavitations. He was started on vancomycin, piperacillin-tazobactam, and azithromycin then was admitted for further workup. He underwent extensive infectious workup including PCR for atypical pneumonia panel and MRSA that resulted negative. Pneumococcal and legionella antigens, fungal serology, sputum samples for acid fast bacilli were all negative. He underwent bronchoscopy with BAL twice with negative infectious investigations and cytology results. However, the second time brushings and transbronchial biopsies were performed and showed non-necrotizing granulomas. Further workup showed positive PR3 and C-ANCA antibodies, P-ANCA and anti-MPO antibodies were negative. Later, he developed worsening pleuritic chest discomfort and lower extremity pain. Lower extremity ultrasound revealed acute deep vein thrombosis and CT angiogram of the thorax showed subsegmental pulmonary embolism and enlarging cavitary lesions. He was started on a heparin drip. subsequently, He underwent VATS with biopsy, pathology findings of necrotizing granulomas with vasculitis consistent with GPA were confirmed. Patient was started on pulse steroids and received one dose of rituximab, then he was transitioned to taper dose of prednisone on discharge with marked improvement. He was also sent home on apixaban.His follow up CT scan showed decreased invitations with clinical improvement. DISCUSSION: GPA and RA have been reported to co-exist. Few cases have been published in literature about co-existing GPA and RA, and to our knowledge, only 6 cases of GPA were reported to have symmetric polyarthritis as initial manifestation. Both GPA and RA share similar characteristics. Arthritis and arthralgia are among the common features of both diseases, usually it affects the large joints with GPA. Our patient was initially diagnosed with seropositive RA, and he presented with arthritis and arthralgia at the time of the diagnosis, and later on he came with symptoms of GPA. At this point, it is unclear if the patient has co-existing GPA and RA, or if it is only GPA that initially manifested as arthralgia and arthritis. In addition, patients with GPA are at higher risk of developing venous thromboembolism (VTE). CONCLUSIONS: There is an overlap that can rarely happen between GPA and RA. GPA should be worked up in patients with an underlying diagnosis of RA who develop signs and symptoms of vasculitis. Additionally, these patients are at increased risk for developing VTE. REFERENCE #1: Lakhani DA, Balar AB, Adelanwa A, Gross A, Mohamed R, Smith KT, Kim C. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: A case report and brief review of literature. Radiol Case Rep. 2021 Sep 5;16(11):3445-3450. doi: 10.1016/j.radcr.2021.08.028. Erratum in: Radiol Case Rep. 2023 Jan 24;18(3):1394-1395. PMID: 34527120; PMCID: PMC8429614. REFERENCE #2: Hassan AS, Edigin E, Patel AR, Manadan A. The Co-existence of Rheumatoid Arthritis and Granulomatosis With Polyangiitis: Two Cases and Review of the Literature. Cureus. 2021 Aug 11;13(8):e17103. doi: 10.7759/cureus.17103. PMID: 34527489; PMCID: PMC8432909. REFERENCE #3: Kısacık B, Önder ME, Sayarlıoğlu M, Onat AM. Symmetric polyarthritis as an initial symptom in granulomatosis with polyangiitis: A report of six cases and review of the literature. Eur J Rheumatol. 2018 Sep;5(3):191-193. doi: 10.5152/eurjrheum.2018.17050. Epub 2018 Apr 19. PMID: 30071923; PMCID: PMC6116851. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sara Akbar No relevant relationships by Tarek Alsibai No relevant relationships by Tyler Kemnic No relevant relationships by Joseph Pitcher
