N2  - SESSION TITLE: Extra-pulmonary and Extra-ordinary Infections SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 01:00 pm - 02:00 pm INTRODUCTION: Disseminated nontuberculous mycobacterial (D-NTM) infections occur rarely, primarily in immunocompromised hosts (1). NTM superinfection has been reported to be a rare complication in patients with exogenous lipoid pneumonia (ELP) (2). Here we describe a case of D-NTM infection in a patient diagnosed with ELP due to aspiration of squalene. CASE PRESENTATION: A 72-year-old male was referred to our institution for four months of recurrent fever, cough, sputum, and dyspnea. He was an ex-smoker (11 pack-years) with hypertension but no history of other pulmonary conditions. He was admitted with an initial impression of aspiration pneumonia.The patient had been regularly inhaling squalene nasally for about six months before admission. Initial chest computed tomography (CT) scans showed multifocal ground-glass opacities with interlobular septal thickenings with crazy paving patterns in the dependent area of both lungs, and a central low-density area, reflecting fat component, suggestive of ELP. The patient was started on corticosteroids for ELP and broad-spectrum antibiotics for combined aspiration pneumonia. Initial acid fast bacilli cultures returned two positive isolates of Mycobacterium abscessus subspecies abscessus (MAB), establishing the diagnosis of NTM lung disease. Three weeks later, MAB was isolated from bronchoalveolar lavage and blood specimens. NTM treatment began with azithromycin, amikacin (intravenous and nebulized), and imipenem. Clofazimine and linezolid were added for persisting MAB sepsis, which resolved after day 39 of NTM treatment. The patient was discharged on day 71, maintaining corticosteroid for ELP.Although treatment for MAB continued, mycobacterial cultures from respiratory tract specimens remained positive. The patient was hospitalized three more times for aggravation of respiratory symptoms. Treatment focused on treating infectious etiologies because the extent of ELP only aggravated slightly on follow-up CT scans. On day 234 of NTM treatment, the patient was admitted for rehabilitation. His condition rapidly deteriorated, eventually resulting in ICU admission due to the aggravation of his pneumonia. On day 268, cerebrospinal fluid studies, conducted for evaluation of poor recovery of mental status, revealed a positive polymerase chain reaction result for NTM. The patient died on day 290. DISCUSSION: D-NTM infections are rare clinical entities that usually occur in immunocompromised hosts, such as patients with HIV infection, hematologic or solid organ transplants, malignancies, or immunosuppressive drug use. The most common pathogen in D-NTM is M. avium complex. Rapid-growing NTM species, including MAB, are also known to cause skin and catheter-related infections and bacteremia in immunocompetent adults. In our patient, NTM infection involved not only the lungs, but also blood and the central nervous system despite intensive treatment. Aspiration of exogenous oils is suggested to impair mucociliary transport and alveolar macrophage function, which may enhance the colonization and growth of NTM in patients with ELP (2). This may help explain the refractory clinical course observed in our case. CONCLUSIONS: We describe a rare case of D-NTM infection in a patient with ELP caused by squalene inhalation. Careful history-taking may help identify conditions that may aggravate clinical courses in patients diagnosed with D-NTM. REFERENCE #1: Henkle E, Winthrop KL. Nontuberculous mycobacteria infections in immunosuppressed hosts. Clin Chest Med. 2015;36(1):91-99. REFERENCE #2: Marchiori E, Zanetti G, Mano CM, Hochhegger B. Exogenous lipoid pneumonia. Clinical and radiological manifestations. Respir Med. 2011;105(5):659-666. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Shihwan Chang No relevant relationships by Young ae Kang No relevant relationships by Song Yee Kim No relevant relationships by Youngmok Park
