N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Global Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Global Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Primary pulmonary lymphoma (PPL) is a rare non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and accounts for less than 1% of all primary pulmonary malignancies. PPL occurs due to clonal lymphoid proliferation within lung parenchyma and/or bronchi. Majority (up to 95%) of primary pulmonary lymphomas are of B-cell origin with very few cases of pulmonary T-cell lymphoma reported. We present a challenging case of primary pulmonary T-cell lymphoma masquerading as bacterial pneumonia, organising pneumonia and pulmonary vasculitis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old woman presented with a 3 weeks' history of non-productive cough, intermittent haemoptysis and dyspnea. Chest radiography showed a mass-like consolidation in the right upper lobe. A bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial lung biopsy was performed, with features favouring bacterial and organising pneumonia. She was treated with antibiotics and prednisolone for 4 weeks, but a repeat CT scan post-treatment showed persistent mass-like consolidation. She was referred for a surgical wedge resection and biopsy of the mass.Histology from the surgical lung biopsy was suggestive of an organizing abscess with no evidence of malignancy. She was treated with prolonged steroids and antibiotics. A follow-up CT scan 3 months later revealed an interval increase in size of the lung mass, with two new nodules in the right lower lobe - these also showed intense FDG avidity on the subsequent PET-scan. A repeat bronchoscopy and transbronchial lung cryobiopsy revealed diffuse inflammation with some evidence of capillaritis. An alternative diagnosis of sero-negative small vessel vasculitis was made.However, she failed to respond to immunosuppression with rituximab and prednisolone. A second surgical procedure of VATS biopsy revealed polymorphous infiltrate of dispersed medium to large atypical lymphoid cells. Immunohistochemical staining revealed positive CD2 T-cell marker, and partial cytoplasmic CD3 staining pattern. CD30 highlighted 5-10% of the large atypical lymphoid cells. The patient underwent 6cycles of Brentuximab vedotin, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, prednisolone (BV-CHP) chemotherapy regimen with near complete resolution of the lung mass and secondary nodules. DISCUSSION: PPLs are difficult to diagnose as clinical and radiological features are non-specific. To the best of our knowledge, only 17 primary pulmonary T-cell lymphoma cases have been reported since 1991, including our patient's. It is critical to diagnose T-cell lymphomas accurately as they are associated with poor outcomes, with only 25% of patients surviving >5years. Our patient was diagnosed with bacterial, organizing pneumonia and sero-negative small vessel vasculitis before making the diagnosis of pulmonary T-cell lymphoma. Our team maintained a high index of suspicion for a rare pulmonary malignancy despite the lack of supporting histological evidence, and advised the patient for repeat surgical lung biopsy. Histological diagnosis of a T-cell lymphoma is also very challenging and requires specialised lymphoma staining. In our case, the initial surgical biopsy failed to reveal PPL due to the intense surrounding inflammation and a second biopsy eventually clinched the correct diagnosis. CONCLUSIONS: We report a unique case of primary pulmonary T-cell lymphoma being a 'great mimicker' of bacterial pneumonia, organising pneumonia and pulmonary vasculitis amongst other uncommon pulmonary conditions. This should be considered as a differential for patients with unresolving radiological changes despite combination therapy. REFERENCE #1: Yang, L., Feng, W., Chen, C., Zhang, X., Zhu, Y., Lei, W., & Huang, J. (2016). Primary pulmonary T-cell lymphoma mimicking pneumonia: A case report and literature review. Experimental and Therapeutic Medicine, 12, 365-368. https://doi.org/10.3892/etm.2016.3287 REFERENCE #2: Takahara, Y., Sakuma, T., Nishiki, K. et al. (2019). Primary pulmonary T-cell lymphoma with lung adenocarcinoma: a case report. J Med Case Reports 13, 199. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13256-019-2091-y REFERENCE #3: Saleh K, Michot JM, Ribrag V. (2021). Updates in the Treatment of Peripheral T-Cell Lymphomas. J Exp Pharmacol. 2021;13:577-591 https://doi.org/10.2147/JEP.S262344 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Deshawn Chong Xuan Tan
