N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 31 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary mucormycosis, although rare, is a challenging infectious disease with a high mortality rate. Most frequently seen in those with hematologic or solid organ malignancies, diabetes or other immunocompromised states, it is not only difficult to diagnose but also to treat. We present a case of a 40-year-old male with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) confirmed to have pulmonary mucormycosis and treated with both antifungal therapy and thoracotomy of the affected lung. We use this case report to highlight the diagnosis, management and outcomes of patients with pulmonary mucormycosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 40-year-old male with T2DM presented to the emergency department with progressive dyspnea accompanied only by non-productive cough and weight loss. He was insulin dependent but had recently immigrated to the United States and was unable to afford insulin for several months, and was additionally found to be in diabetic ketoacidosis at presentation. Computed tomography scan of the chest revealed a left upper lobe cavitary pulmonary infiltrate, with numerous scattered ground-glass and tree-in-bud opacities. After testing for mycobacterium tuberculosis was negative, a bronchoscopy was performed which yielded the diagnosis of mucormycosis. The patient was then treated with antifungals for several weeks without improvement. Thoracic surgery subsequently performed a left thoracotomy in the hopes of achieving definitive treatment, with surgical findings of dense pleural adhesions as well as extensive pulmonary mucormycosis. Our patient initially recovered and remained well at home for several months. Ultimately, he re-presented with a bronchopleural fistula and fatal mucormycosis of his remaining right lung. DISCUSSION: Mucormycosis commonly results in rhino-orbital cellulitis, but can also cause pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, and disseminated infections. Generally seen in immunocompromised individuals, this angioinvasive fungal infection can lead to devastating consequences, and thus a high index of suspicion is needed, especially in those presenting with respiratory complaints who do not respond to antibiotics. It can frequently mimic other fungal infections such as tuberculosis or aspergillosis on imaging and requires histopathological diagnosis, most often accomplished by bronchoscopy. Aggressive management with a combination of medical and surgical therapies is recommended. Despite this, mortality is high, with rates of up to 80% being reported. Amphotericin B is the most commonly used agent, and often patients require a second antifungal agent, such as posaconazole or caspofungin, along with surgical intervention. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary mucormycosis is a rare but frequently deadly disease that clinicians should be aware and suspicious of in patients at high risk. We present a case of a patient with pulmonary mucormycosis that initially responded to surgical intervention after initial treatment with antifungal therapy failed, but unfortunately a few months later developed fatal complications. We highlight the importance of early diagnosis and an aggressive treatment approach, and emphasize the importance of a low threshold for diagnostic bronchoscopy for patients refractory to antibiotic treatment with abnormal imaging. REFERENCE #1: Lin, E., Moua, T. & Limper, A.H. Pulmonary mucormycosis: clinical features and outcomes. Infection 45, 443–448 (2017). https://doi.org/10.1007/s15010-017-0991-6 REFERENCE #2: Salwa O. Mekki, Amal A. Hassan, Afnan Falemban, Nashwa Alkotani, Salem M. Alsharif, Ahmed Haron, Basim Felemban, Mohammad S. Iqbal, Aisha Tabassum, "Pulmonary Mucormycosis: A Case Report of a Rare Infection with Potential Diagnostic Problems", Case Reports in Pathology, vol. 2020, Article ID 5845394, 4 pages, 2020. https://doi.org/10.1155/2020/5845394 REFERENCE #3: Tedder M, Spratt JA, Anstadt MP, Hegde SS, Tedder SD, Lowe JE. Pulmonary mucormycosis: results of medical and surgical therapy. Ann Thorac Surg. 1994 Apr;57(4):1044-50. doi: 10.1016/0003-4975(94)90243-7. PMID: 8166512. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Muhammad Adrish No disclosure on file for Caroline Dooley No relevant relationships by Marisa Reynolds
