N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 19 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Foreign body aspiration (FBA) is rare in adults, with roughly 0.16 to 0.33 cases reported per 1000 individuals. Although most FBA cases can be diagnosed easily and accurately using radiographs and bronchoscopy, some patients are misdiagnosed with endobronchial tumors. Furthermore, it poses a diagnostic challenge in adults due to its nonspecific and non-emergent presentation. We describe a case of FBA that mimicked an endobronchial tumor presenting with post-obstructive pneumonia in an adult. CASE PRESENTATION: A 51-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes, obstructive sleep apnea, and hypertension presented to our hospital for evaluation of chronic dry cough and wheezing for the past six months, unresponsive to medications in the outpatient setting. The patient denied a history of smoking, asthma, or any known allergies. Outpatient pulmonary function testing showed no evidence of obstructive or restrictive lung disease. Chest x-ray was inconclusive. The patient was afebrile on admission, and the labs were significant for leukocytosis with no eosinophilia. Physical examination revealed decreased breath sounds at the right lower lung base, with wheezing limited to the right lower lobe. A computed tomogram (CT) of the chest with contrast was done, which showed narrowing of right lower lobe bronchi with bronchial wall thickening suggestive of inflammatory/neoplastic process with patchy infiltrates in the right lower lobe and mildly enlarged right paratracheal and hilar lymph nodes. The patient was started on ampicillin-sulbactam and nebulized treatments. Flexible bronchoscopy was performed, which revealed a fleshy endobronchial mass in the right lower lobe with purulent secretions and significant edema. Multiple biopsies were obtained, exposing a yellowish color foreign body. The object was extracted using retrieval forceps and sent for histopathology and culture. Histopathology showed vegetable matter consistent with orotracheal aspiration, no malignant cells, and a negative bacterial and fungal culture. The patient was discharged on a 10-day course of amoxicillin-clavulanate. On follow-up examination after one month, the patient reported complete resolution of cough and wheezing. DISCUSSION: Foreign body aspiration is rare in adults. The clinical presentation of FBA differs between adults and children. Children are more symptomatic and present with asphyxiation after a choking episode, whereas adults have subtle and chronic symptoms. Older age, certain medications, neurological disorders impairing cough and swallowing, or alcohol intoxication are considered risk factors for adult FBA. The patients usually present with an unresolved cough (with or without fever), hemoptysis, chest pain, wheezing, or dyspnea. Chest CT can help detect a foreign body and help in procedural planning. Flexible bronchoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic in these cases. Failure to diagnose FBA can lead to complications such as lung abscess, empyema, or pneumothorax. CONCLUSIONS: Foreign body aspiration can lead to post-obstructive pneumonia due to airway obstruction, which can mimic a neoplasm. FBA in adults can have varied clinical presentations, with most adults failing to recall the aspiration event, as seen in our case. The physician should consider FBA as one of the differentials in patients with post-obstructive pneumonia to avoid anchoring in a cancer diagnosis and delaying care of a potentially treatable condition. REFERENCE #1:. S. Sehgal, S. Dhooria, B. Ram, et al., "Foreign body inhalation in the adult population: experience of 25,998 bronchoscopies and systematic review of the literature," Respiratory Care, vol. 60, no. 10, pp. 1438–1448, 2015. REFERENCE #2: A. L. Rafanan and A. C. Mehta, "Adult airway foreign body removal: what's new?" Clinics in Chest Medicine, vol. 22, no. 2, pp. 319–330, 2001. DISCLOSURES: No disclosure on file for Victoria Gonzalez No relevant relationships by VATSAL KHANNA No relevant relationships by Trishya Reddy No relevant relationships by Bernadette Schmidt
