N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 12 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Blastomycosis is caused by Blastomyces dermatitidis. It primarily causes lung infection, however, can disseminate to the other extrapulmonary organs, commonest site been skin. Both immunocompetent and immunocompromised host can acquire it. CASE PRESENTATION: Patient is 58-year-old nonsmoker male presented with complaints of shortness of breath for last 4-5 days. He also had associated generalized fatigue, nonproductive cough and decreased appetite. In Emergency Department (ED), he was hypoxic and was placed on 10 liters of oxygen through nasal cannula. Simultaneously he was in atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response. Computed Tomography (CT) pulmonary embolism demonstrated mixed diffuse bilateral nodular densities and miliary like pattern throughout the entire lung field along with consolidative opacity in left suprahilar region. He was started on broad spectrum antibiotics. On retrospective review he did have non healing back abscess post incision and drainage 3 week prior to presentation in ED. He worked in prison in the management department and has been in contact with inmates. Infectious disease was consulted and empirically started on oral itraconazole given high suspicion of disseminated blastomycosis in setting of clinical and radiographic presentation. Serum and urine Blastomyces antigen were reactive. Histoplasma antigen likely cross reactive confirming diagnosis of disseminated blastomycosis. Tuberculosis QuantiFERON-Plus test was non-reactive. Bronchoscopy for biopsy evidence and to rule out malignancy was not performed by Pulmonology as infectious workup was revealing. His respiratory status improved and was discharged without additional oxygen requirement. Prospectively his back wound cultures grew Blastomyces dermatitidis and serum Blastomyces antibody was positive. DISCUSSION: Disseminated Blastomycosis is common in immunosuppressed condition, however our patient had no underlying immunosuppressive etiology. Tuberculosis and Sarcoidosis were also the differentials given occupational history and radiographic pattern (Bilateral variable size miliary like nodules). It can mimic other infections given nonspecific presentation and can be misdiagnosed especially if cutaneous skin lesions manifest initially. Cutaneous lesion presents in two forms verrucous or ulcerative. Our patient was empirically treated for bacterial cellulitis. The cross reactivity of Histoplasma antigen is around 90 % of cases and should be ordered along with serum and urine Blastomyces antigen. Treatment of moderate to severe blastomycosis is initiated with amphotericin B followed by step down with itraconazole. CONCLUSIONS: This case highlights clinical and radiographic presentation of disseminated blastomycosis. Prompt diagnosis is essential to prevent fatalities and complications such as acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). REFERENCE #1: Abdelfattah AH, Ayub S, Talib M, Dawoud H, Talib U. Disseminated blastomycosis in an immunocompetent patient. Cureus. 14(10):e30391. REFERENCE #2: Hashim S, Barrios C. Disseminated blastomycosis. IDCases. 2020;21:e00786. REFERENCE #3: Abdallah FCB, Bachouch I, Belloumi N, et al. [Pulmonary blastomycosis]. Pan Afr Med J. 2020;36:220. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Omar Al Wahadneh No relevant relationships by Jeel Gala No relevant relationships by Lianghe Gao No relevant relationships by Amogh Nadkarni
