N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 30 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis is diagnosed by systemic and pulmonary symptoms of three years duration accompanied by radiologic findings.1 The incidence is on the rise since an increased number of patients undergo chemotherapy and organ transplants. Most cases are indolent and diagnosis is generally not suspected due to nonspecific symptoms.1 We present a case of a 72 year old female found to have a chronic cavitary lesion growing septate hyphae consistent with pulmonary aspergillosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 72 year old female with renal cell carcinoma s/p left nephrectomy, right kidney with nephrocalcinosis, and distal renal tubular acidosis presented with rust-colored sputum and malaise. CT Chest showed a thick-walled cavitary nodule in the right lower lobe with surrounding ground-glass opacities. Testing including ANA/ANCA/MPO/HIV/Interferon Gamma/beta-D glucan were all negative, but Aspergillus galactomannan was positive. A bronchoscopy with biopsy detected no organisms and the galactomannan was thought to be incidental, so the patient was discharged on levofloxacin and metronidazole for presumed aspiration pneumonia. She presented one week after discharge for hypotension and encephalopathy, and imaging showed a worsening cavitary lesion. A chest tube was placed in the right lower lobe and cultures were positive for Stenotrophomonas, Candida tropicalis, and septate hyphal organism that could not grow in culture but was presumed to be Aspergillus. The patient was treated with voriconazole, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and cefiderocol for presumed bacterial superinfection of an Aspergillus-related cavity. She completed approximately 3 months of voriconazole therapy complicated by hepatotoxicity. Repeat imaging showed improvement in the cavity, but still showed a large lesion in the right lower lobe with air fluid levels. DISCUSSION: Lung abscess are acute or subacute lesions, thick walled, with air fluid levels, typically unilateral, and seen in areas prone to aspiration such as superior segments of the lower lobes and posterior segments of the upper lobes.2 They are associated with diabetes mellitus, alcoholism, seizure disorder, untreated dental infections, and the elderly. Etiology includes necrotizing pneumonia, septic emboli, and aspergillosis. In aspergillosis, a patient may present with prolonged neutropenia, T-cell deficiency, and history of organ transplant. Systemic symptoms recalcitrant to antibiotics may also be present such as fevers, cough, dyspnea, weight loss, and hemoptysis.2,3 Labs will be positive for galactomannan (serum and BAL) and b-D-glucan (serum). On imaging, aspergillosis presents as solitary or multiple nodules with a halo sign, and 1-2 weeks after the halo sign appears, the nodule can cavitate leading to an air crescent sign.1 CONCLUSIONS: The root cause of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis is an inadequate immune response, given how ubiquitous aspergillosis is in the environment. It affects patients with AIDS, hematological malignancies, neutropenia, corticosteroids use (typically in asthma and COPD patients), and transplant recipients on immunosuppressant medications.2 Additionally, invasive aspergillosis is increasingly being described in the ICU setting. None of these medical conditions apply to the patient in question, and this was a unique case of chronic pulmonary aspergillosis superimposed with a bacterial superinfection, further complicated by voriconazole induced hepatotoxicity. There is a high frequency of relapse and treatment may be lifelong.2,3 REFERENCE #1: Fosses Vuong M, Hollingshead CM, Waymack JR. Aspergillosis. [Updated 2023 Feb 22]. In: StatPearls [Internet]. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing; 2023 Jan-. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482241/ REFERENCE #2: Naaraayan A, Kavian R, Lederman J, Basak P, Jesmajian S. Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis - case report and review of literature. J Community Hosp Intern Med Perspect. 2015 Feb 3;5(1):26322. doi: 10.3402/jchimp.v5.26322. PMID: 25656673; PMCID: PMC4318821. REFERENCE #3: Kosmidis C, Denning DWThe clinical spectrum of pulmonary aspergillosisThorax 2015;70:270-277. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Tanya Ray No relevant relationships by Sonali Rodrigues
