N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Organizing pneumonia (OP) is a type of interstitial lung disease (ILD) characterized by the formation of granulation tissue within the small airways, alveolar ducts, and surrounding interstitium [1]. OP is an increasingly recognized manifestation of drug reactions, including infliximab, that is commonly used to treat inflammatory conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). Here, we present a case of OP in a patient with ulcerative colitis (UC) on infliximab. CASE PRESENTATION: A 22-year-old male with a history of UC previously on adalimumab before he was switched to infliximab 400 mg every 8 weeks (his 5th infusion was 10 days prior to presentation) presented with dry cough and dyspnea for the past 2 months. His symptoms persisted despite outpatient antibiotics, and he otherwise denied any gastrointestinal complaints. On presentation, he was tachycardic, tachypneic and hypoxic. Laboratory studies showed elevated inflammatory markers but were otherwise unremarkable. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest showed diffuse coarse interstitial thickening and ground glass opacities with minimal traction bronchiectasis. Infectious work up including bacterial, viral, and fungal etiologies was negative. Differential diagnosis included OP, eosinophilic pneumonia, pneumonitis related to medication, or IBD. Further testing including autoimmune serology was negative. His course was complicated by worsening hypoxia for which he underwent bronchoscopy, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL), and transbronchial biopsies. Cultures were negative and fluid studies of BAL demonstrated a lymphocytic predominance. The biopsy showed an organizing pneumonia injury pattern, as well as foamy pneumocytes and macrophages suggestive of drug related changes. He was diagnosed with infliximab-induced OP and started steroid therapy in addition to discontinuation of infliximab. He had significant improvement in his symptoms and a decrease in oxygen requirements. The patient remains on a prednisone taper and is awaiting repeat imaging with discussion with the gastroenterology team to switch therapy to another biologic agent in the future to control his UC. DISCUSSION: Infliximab-induced OP is a relatively rare adverse reaction with a pathophysiology that is not fully understood. Risk factors for infliximab-induced OP includes age and pre-existing lung disease [2]. In a study of 122 cases of anti-tumor necrosis factor alpha induced ILD, 56 cases were associated with infliximab and the mean time for development of ILD was found to be 26 weeks after initiation of the agent [3]. Differentiating whether OP is an extra-intestinal manifestation of UC or drug induced can be challenging. However, drug-induced OP occurs within weeks to months and usually resolves within weeks of discontinuation of the suspected drug. Management focuses on excluding infection, recognizing other risk factors, discontinuing the offending agent, and appropriate steroid taper (typically over months with monitoring of relapse in symptoms). CONCLUSIONS: It is important to screen for risk factors of infliximab-induced OP prior to initiating therapy. Furthermore, clinicians should remain vigilant about this potential complication in patients who develop respiratory symptoms while on infliximab for prompt recognition and management. REFERENCE #1: Baque-Juston, M., Pellegrin, A., Leroy, S., Marquette, C. H., & Padovani, B. (2014). Organizing pneumonia: what is it? A conceptual approach and pictorial review. Diagnostic and interventional imaging, 95(9), 771–777. 10.1016/j.diii.2014.01.004 REFERENCE #2: Thavarajah K, Wu P, Rhew EJ, Yeldandi AK, Kamp DW. Pulmonary complications of tumor necrosis factor-targeted therapy. Respir Med. 2009 May;103(5):661-9. 10.1016/j.rmed.2009.01.002. REFERENCE #3: Perez-Alvarez R, Perez-de-Lis M, Diaz-Lagares C, Pego-Reigosa JM, Retamozo S, Bove A, Brito-Zeron P, Bosch X, Ramos-Casals M. Interstitial lung disease induced or exacerbated by TNF-targeted therapies: analysis of 122 cases. Semin Arthritis Rheum. 2011 Oct;41(2):256-64. 10.1016/j.semarthrit.2010.11.002. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by ALAA ABU SAYF No relevant relationships by Linda Albusoul No relevant relationships by Christopher Kassab
