N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 20 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Necrotizing pneumonia (N-PNA) is often a severe illness characterized by cough, fever, hypoxia, tachycardia, and may rapidly progress to respiratory failure and septic shock[1]. Though pseudomonas aeruginosa has been known to cause N-PNA, only one case of pneumonia from Pseudomonas fluorescens has been reported in literature[2]. We present a rare case of P. fluorescens causing N-PNA. CASE PRESENTATION: This 33-year-old male with PMH of asthma presented from a correctional facility with pleuritic chest pain and hemoptysis. Vitals were remarkable for mild tachycardia, low-grade fever with Tmax of 100.5 degrees F and normal oxygenation inspiring ambient air. Labs revealed leukocytosis with a neutrophil predominance. Computed tomography angiogram of the chest revealed a 4.7 cm cavitary consolidation in the right upper lobe with right hilar lymphadenopathy, and no pulmonary embolism. Rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol was initiated and transitioned to rocephin and zithromax when mycobacterium polymerase chain reaction returned negative. HIV testing, mycobacterial cultures, cryptococcal antigen, aspergillus galactomannan antigen, myeloperoxidase antibody, β-D-glucan, proteinase 3 antibody, and anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody were all negative. Sputum cultures grew pansensetive Pseudomonas fluorescens on day #4 and the patient was transitioned to levofloxacin. Repeat computed tomography (CT) on day #5 revealed a slight decrease in cavitation size. The patient required no supplemental oxygen throughout the hospitalization and was discharged on levofloxacin. Six week follow up CT is pending at the time of this submission. DISCUSSION: Etiology for cavitary lesions in adults include acute or chronic infection, malignancy, and vasculitides[3]. This case is unique in many aspects including the lack of associated conditions, decreased severity of illness, and a unique causative pathogen. Per Gafoor, workup for cavitary lesions include excluding mimickers of cavitary lesions, acute vs chronic symptoms, clinical presentation, and CT features. Given the acute onset of symptoms, fever, cough and leukocytosis with L shift, etiology is infectious. CT characteristics ruled out pulmonary cysts and bullae, while the size (> 3.5 cm) and enumeration (single cavitary lesion), as well as clinical presentation ruled out septic emboli, nocardiosis, crypotococcus, and coccidiomycosis, and abscess leaving N-PNA as the diagnosis[1]. Our case is unique in that N-PNA was found in a young patient with no significant comorbidities associated with N-PNA including alcoholism, diabetes mellitus, generalized convulsive disorder, drug abuse, older age, dental infections or corticosteroid use[1]. Further research on gene expression of various pseudomonas strains and factors that contribute to N-PNA may help identify the potential for other rare bacteria to cause N-PNA. CONCLUSIONS: Pseudomonas fluorescens is a rare cause of necrotizing pneumonia; this case is unique in many aspects including the lack of associated conditions, decreased severity of illness, and a unique causative pathogen. REFERENCE #1: Gafoor, Khalid, et al. "Cavitary lung diseases: a clinical-radiologic algorithmic approach." Chest 153.6 (2018): 1443-1465. REFERENCE #2: Liu, Xiao, et al. "Pneumonia caused by Pseudomonas fluorescens: a case report." BMC Pulmonary Medicine 21.1 (2021): 1-6. REFERENCE #3: Kim, Na Rae, and Joungho Han. "Pathologic review of cystic and cavitary lung diseases." Korean journal of pathology 46.5 (2012): 407. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Amina Kureshi No relevant relationships by Hary Suseelan
