N2  - SESSION TITLE: Diffuse Lung Disease Case Report Posters 12 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Organizing pneumonia (OP) is a rare lung disease that often mimics community-acquired pneumonia. It is a diffuse interstitial lung disease arising from injury to the alveolar wall. It may be idiopathic or secondary to malignancy, connective tissue disorder, or drugs. Cough, fever, and dyspnea are the most common clinical manifestations. Differential diagnosis of OP is vast, including lung infection, granulomatous conditions, interstitial lung disease, and malignancy. Treatment includes observation for minimal symptoms and steroids with severe symptoms. Few case reports have described the use of macrolides in patients with mild symptoms. CASE PRESENTATION: A 52-year-old male with a history of smoking presented with complaints of headache, fever, confusion, and slurred speech. He was being worked up for newly diagnosed bilateral pulmonary nodules as an outpatient a few weeks earlier. Further evaluation of the nodules by PET scan were suggestive of left lower lobe primary lung neoplasm with additional nodules in the left and right middle lobes. On physical examination, he appeared lethargic with a right facial nerve palsy. Laboratory workup showed a leukocytosis (18,300/ uL) and an elevated ESR and CRP. A brain MRI with contrast showed a 2 cm lesion in the left and an 8mm lesion in the right periventricular deep white matter demonstrating ring enhancement and vasogenic edema. He was started on levetiracetam, steroids, and broad-spectrum antibiotics.Blood cultures were negative for bacteria and fungi, as were the studies for HIV, toxoplasmosis, cryptococcus, hepatitis B and C. Immunologic workup was positive for anti-nuclear antibody 1:40 with negative classic and perinuclear antineutrophilic cytoplasmic antibodies. A CT-guided core needle biopsy of the left lung showed organizing pneumonia without evidence of infection or malignancy. Due to a high suspicion of brain abscess despite the negative infectious and malignancy workup including lung biopsy, he underwent a left frontal stereotactic brain biopsy, which revealed benign brain tissue with no evidence of malignancy, abscess, or AFB. His neurological deficits improved after prolonged treatment with steroids and antibiotics. Follow-up brain MRI showed near-complete resolution of the lesions and the chest CT showed complete resolution of the concerning left lower lobe nodule with decrease in size of other nodules. DISCUSSION: OP is a non-specific inflammatory process, even though the word "pneumonia" is in the name. The clinical symptoms such as fever and weight loss and imaging features such as patchy air space consolidation, nodules, and positive PET often mislead us to differentials like pneumonia or lung tumor. Our case of OP raised the suspicion of an infectious or metastatic process as the cause of the ring-enhancing brain lesion, causing a conundrum in the diagnosis of brain abscess and leading to an extensive invasive procedure and delay. CONCLUSIONS: OP is a rare pulmonary disorder. Clinical features are typically non-specific. Imaging is not always helpful in distinguishing OP from infection or lung cancer, and a lung biopsy is required for a definitive diagnosis. We, therefore, present this case to sensitize the reader to the diagnostic challenge posed by OP. REFERENCE #1: Müller, N. L., Staples, C. A., & Miller, R. R. (1990). Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia: CT features in 14 patients. AJR. American Journal of Roentgenology, 154(5), 983–987. REFERENCE #2: American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society International Multidisciplinary Consensus Classification of the Idiopathic Interstitial Pneumonias. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2002;165:277–304. REFERENCE #3: Radzikowska, E., Nowicka, U., Wiatr, E., Jakubowska, L., Langfort, R., Chabowski, M., & Roszkowski, K. (2007). Organising pneumonia and lung cancer - case report and review of the literature. Pneumonologia i Alergologia Polska, 75(4), 394–397. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Joel Popkin No relevant relationships by REGINA SHRESTHA No relevant relationships by MICHELLE SIEW
