N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Mimics: Not All We See Is Malignant SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: RET-fusions are rare activating mutations that occur with higher frequencies in certain cancers, 1-2% of non-small cell lung cancers: 10-20% of papillary thyroid cancers and 50-90% of sporadic medullary thyroid cancers. (1) Pralsetinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor for RET-positive solid tumors that appears to be well tolerated by patients. To date, no case reports have linked Pralsetinib to Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (MTB). CASE PRESENTATION: A 62-year-old male presented with a persistent dry cough for the past four months. Medical history was significant for stage IVB papillary thyroid carcinoma metastatic to the lung, status post left thyroid lobectomy and radiation therapy, currently on Pralsetinib chemotherapy. CT chest obtained one month after initiation of Pralsetinib demonstrated a significant reduction in size of multiple pulmonary nodules. After nine months of treatment, repeat CT chest demonstrated enlargement of mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes. Fine needle aspiration (FNA) of mediastinal lymph nodes was performed using Endoscopic Bronchoscopic Ultrasound (EBUS). Bronchoscopy demonstrated a right main bronchus endobronchial lesion with necrosis which was biopsied with forceps. Pathology was negative for malignancy. Biopsies demonstrated necrosis with abundant acid fast bacilli. MTB was identified via PCR and later cultures. DISCUSSION: The ARROW trial, a multi-cohort phase 1/2 interventional clinical trial assessed overall response rate and the number of adverse events within thirty days of the last dose of Pralsetinib. (2) Pralsetinib demonstrated increased overall response rate in patients with treatment-naive RET-mutant MTC compared to platinum-based chemotherapy. (2) Common adverse effects included hypertension, neutropenia, and anemia, whereas rare adverse effects included pneumonitis with no report of MTB. (3) A South Korean single-center identified two cases of extra-pulmonary MTB among ten patients being treated with Pralsetinib. (1) Both cases demonstrated enlarged necrotic cervical lymph nodes positive for MTB shortly after initiating Pralsetinib. The pathogenesis of endobronchial MTB is not fully understood and accurate diagnosis can be difficult given atypical clinical characteristics and limited diagnostic utility of chest radiographs. Although use of immunosuppressive therapy is a risk factor for the development of MTB, an association between MTB and Pralsetinib therapy must be considered when starting treatment. CONCLUSIONS: We present the first reported case of endobronchial MTB in a patient receiving Pralsetinib therapy for RET-fusion positive metastatic papillary thyroid carcinoma. It is possible that the development of endobronchial and lymph node MTB in our patient may be a result of new infection or reactivation of latent MTB. The potential association between Pralsetinib and MTB is an important consideration and should be monitored in future studies to determine whether a causal relationship exists. Oncologists should consider testing for latent TB in high risk populations prior to starting therapy with Pralsetinib. REFERENCE #1: Lee YP et al. Extrapulmonary tuberculosis in patients with RET fusion-positive non-small cell lung cancer treated with pralsetinib: A Korean single-centre compassionate use experience. Eur J Cancer. 2021 Dec;159:167-173. doi: 10.1016/j.ejca.2021.09.037. REFERENCE #2: Gainor JF et al. Pralsetinib for RET fusion-positive non-small-cell lung cancer (ARROW): a multi-cohort, open-label, phase 1/2 study. Lancet Oncol. 2021 Jul;22(7):959-969. doi: 10.1016/S1470-2045(21)00247-3. Epub 2021 Jun 9. REFERENCE #3: Subbiah V et al. Pralsetinib for patients with advanced or metastatic RET-altered thyroid cancer (ARROW): a multi-cohort, open-label, registrational, phase 1/2 study. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol. 2021 Aug;9(8):491-501. doi: 10.1016/S2213-8587(21)00120-0. Epub 2021 Jun 9. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Samaneh Bolourchi No disclosure on file for Karan Julka
