N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 14 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: The introduction of biologics has dramatically improved asthma-related symptoms and reduced exacerbations. Injection site reaction and anaphylaxis are biologics’ most described side effects; however, the safety data is constantly evolving. (1) Empyema has never been described with the use of biologics. We present a case of recurrence of empyema in a severe asthma patient on two separate occasions with different biologics. CASE PRESENTATION: Our patient is a middle-aged woman getting hospitalized after being referred from the pulmonology clinic with shortness of breath and a chest x-ray showing bilateral pleural effusion left more than right. She was treated for pneumonia a week earlier with levofloxacin. Imaging at that time revealed bilateral airspace opacities. The patient is otherwise a teacher by profession with no smoking history. The patient has a history of severe persistent asthma and chronic sinusitis on a mometasone-formoterol inhaler for ten years. She was started on dupilumab four years back due to persistent symptoms. A year after initiating dupilumab, she developed right-lung pneumonia and loculated empyema and was treated with total lung decortication after failed attempts to drain the effusion. The bronchoscopy and intraoperative culture revealed methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), and she received an extended course of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. The patient was continued on dupilumab; however, a year before her current hospitalization, dupilumab was switched to benralizumab due to worsening cough and recurrent asthma exacerbations. Of note, she did not have any episode of viral infection on routine testing during her entire course on biologics.During her current hospitalization, she was on room air with decreased breath sounds on the left lung base. A left-sided chest tube was placed on admission, and meropenem and vancomycin were started empirically. A CT chest a day later revealed worsening left loculated pleural effusion with a near complete collapse of the left lung. She underwent a left-sided video-assisted thoracoscopic decortication procedure. Intraoperative cultures were positive for MRSA. She was started on six weeks of vancomycin and amoxicillin-clavulanic acid therapy. The patient was discharged on day 10. DISCUSSION: Benralizumab and dupilumab act on IL-5 receptor alpha (IL-5Rα) and IL-4/IL-13 receptors, respectively, inhibiting the Th2 pathway and eosinophil proliferation. The overall rate of pneumonia with biologics is less than 1% comparable to placebo in randomized trials with no reported case of empyema to date. (2) Drug-induced eosinophilic pneumonia is more common. (3) It is possible that in our patient, concomitant use of steroids, especially mometasone, and inhibition of the Th2 pathway by biologics led to activation of Th1/Th17 mediated inflammatory pathway, which may have increased the risk of recurrent empyema with pneumonia. CONCLUSIONS: Our case illustrates the rare possibility of bacterial pneumonia and empyema with biologics and the risk of recurrence with continued use, irrespective of the class of biologic used. REFERENCE #1: Agache I, Beltran J, Akdis C, et al. Efficacy and safety of treatment with biologicals (benralizumab, dupilumab, mepolizumab, omalizumab and reslizumab) for severe eosinophilic asthma. A systematic review for the EAACI Guidelines - recommendations on the use of biologicals in severe asthma. Allergy 2020;75(5):1023-42. REFERENCE #2: Doroudchi A, Pathria M, Modena BD. Asthma biologics: Comparing trial designs, patient cohorts and study results. Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol 2020;124(1):44-56. REFERENCE #3: Liu W, Ma X, Zhou W. Adverse events of benralizumab in moderate to severe eosinophilic asthma: A meta-analysis. Medicine (Baltimore) 2019;98(22):e15868 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sushan Gupta No relevant relationships by Avani Mohta No relevant relationships by danish thameem
