N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Global Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Global Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is a pathogen that causing the infection in immunocompromised patients. However, community-acquired S. maltophilia infections have been occasionally reported [1] Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is a motile, glucose, non-fermenting, gram-negative bacterium that widely exists in hospital environments [2,3]. It usually causes pneumonia and bacteremia and, less frequently, it can cause infections of the urinary tract, intra-abdominal organs, and wounds [3.4]. CASE PRESENTATION: 80 year-old male patient presented to emergency department with low grade fever and productive cough for 1week associated with dyspnea. On examination, he was drowsy, tachycardiac 110 beat/min, tachypneic 31 breath/min, BP 110/70 mmHg, temperature 38 C and O2 saturation 88% on RA. His inflammatory markers on admission revealed CRP 63mg/dl, ESR 25 1st hour. CBC showed normal leucocyte count and HB level at 9.5 with platelets 131 103/ml. initially all sputum and blood culture were negative. CT chest showed bilateral lung infiltrated (figure_1). He has history hypertension and recurrent urinary tract infections due to BPH.He was admitted to ICU due to high CURB65 score and for close monitoring. Meropenm and vancomycin with azithromycin were started.After 3 days, his condition had been rapidly deteriorated and was connected to MV for 10 days along with radiological worsening (figure_2).FOB was done and TBLBs were taken and revealed negative results for malignancy and granulomas. His fungal along with TB cultures were also negative but BAL bacterial culture revealed stenotrophomonas maltophilia without other concurrent respiratory pathogens, which was sensitive trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole. After 3-4 days from the trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole, his condition started to be improved and he weaned from MV in day 14 and his chest X-ray showed good improvement (figure_3). DISCUSSION: S. maltophilia has the innate capacity to attach to external objects and create a biofilm, providing defense against both host defenses and antimicrobial agents [Its behavior is influenced by its positively charged surface and fimbrial adhesions.[1,5,6,7] In the study done by falgas, et al, community-acquired S. maltophilia infections was diagnosed at 77 patients which 45 had bacteremia, six ocular infections, five respiratory tract infections, four wound/soft tissue infections, two urinary tract infections, one conjunctivitis, one otitis, and one cellulitis; data were not reported for the remaining 12 patients. Comorbidity (such as malignancy, HIV infection, prior hospitalization) was common. Data included in the eight case series regarding the outcome of infection were limited [1] S. maltophilia infections are reported to occur in between 7.1 and 37.7 instances out of every 10,000 discharges.As the number of patients at risk rises, the incidence appears to be rising as well. In particular, improvements in the treatment of cancer, a rise in the use of invasive devices, and widespread use of broad-spectrum antibiotics are likely to be to the liklely casue for the increased incidence [8,9,10] A number of outbreaks of S. maltophilia have been described involving adult ICU patients, hematologic malignancy and bone marrow transplant recipients, hemodialysis patients, and neonates. Patient exposure to contaminated tap water, ice machines, or soap has been the suspected etiology in many outbreaks [11,12]. CONCLUSIONS: Physicians should be aware that S. maltophilia infections are not restricted to hospitalized patients. REFERENCE #1: 1.Falagas ME, Kastoris AC, Vouloumanou EK, Dimopoulos G. Community-acquired Stenotrophomonas maltophilia infections: a systematic review. Eur J Clin Microbiol Infect Dis 2009; 28:719.2. Chen, Y.; Suo, J.; Du, M.; Chen, L.; Liu, Y.; Wang, L.; Liang, Z. Clinical features, outcomes, and risk factors of bloodstreaminfections due to Stenotrophomonas maltophilia in a tertiary-care hospital of China: A retrospective analysis. Biomed Res. Int. 2019,2019, 4931501. [CrossRef] [PubMed]3. Looney,W.J.; Narita, M.; Mühlemann, K. Stenotrophomonas maltophilia: An emerging opportunist human pathogen. Lancet Infect.Dis. 2009, 9, 312–323. [CrossRef]. REFERENCE #2: 4. Garcia Paez, J.; Tengan, F.; Barone, A.; Levin, A.; Costa, S. Factors associated with mortality in patients with bloodstream infectionand pneumonia due to Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. Eur. J. Clin. Microbiol. Infect. Dis. 2008, 27, 901–906. [CrossRef]5.Bottone EJ, Reitano M, Janda JM, et al. Pseudomonas maltophilia exoenzyme activity as correlate in pathogenesis of ecthyma gangrenosum. J Clin Microbiol 1986; 24:995.6.Jucker BA, Harms H, Zehnder AJ. Adhesion of the positively charged bacterium Stenotrophomonas (Xanthomonas) maltophilia 70401 to glass and Teflon. J Bacteriol 1996; 178:5472.7.de Oliveira-Garcia D, Dall'Agnol M, Rosales M, et al. Fimbriae and adherence of Stenotrophomonas maltophilia to epithelial cells and to abiotic surfaces. Cell Microbiol 2003; 5:625.8.Pankuch GA, Jacobs MR, Rittenhouse SF, Appelbaum PC. Susceptibilities of 123 strains of Xanthomonas maltophilia to eight beta-lactams (including beta-lactam-beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations) and ciprofloxacin tested by five methods. Antimicrob Agents Chemother 1994; 38:2317. REFERENCE #3: 9.Giamarellos-Bourboulis EJ, Karnesis L, Galani I, Giamarellou H. In vitro killing effect of moxifloxacin on clinical isolates of Stenotrophomonas maltophilia resistant to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Antimicrob Agents Chemother 2002; 46:3997.10.Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. Performance standards for antimicrobial susceptibility testing: Eighteenth informational supplement [Document M100-S18]. Wayne, PA 2008.11.Verweij PE, Meis JF, Christmann V, et al. Nosocomial outbreak of colonization and infection with Stenotrophomonas maltophilia in preterm infants associated with contaminated tap water. Epidemiol Infect 1998; 120:251.12.Çetin BS, Çelebi S, Özkan H, et al. Stenotrophomonas maltophilia outbreak in neonatal intensive care unit and outbreak management. J Pediatr Inf 2015; 9:147. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Usama Abu Elhassan No relevant relationships by Amal Alamri No relevant relationships by Abdullah Alghamdi No relevant relationships by Saad Alqahtani No relevant relationships by Ahlam Alqahtani No relevant relationships by Eman Alsultan No relevant relationships by mohammed fathy No relevant relationships by Ibrahim Hamdy El Sayed Feteih No relevant relationships by Ibrahim Mahmoud No relevant relationships by Dr. Abdullah Musleh No relevant relationships by Magda Saleh Rezk Abdelwahab
