N2  - SESSION TITLE: Atypical Imaging Presentations of Malignancy SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: The rarity and aggressivity of Pulmonary Sarcomatoid Carcinoma (PSC) as a non-small-cell carcinoma variant limits its characterization in literature. We report a case where cystic lung lesions thought to be infectious or inflammatory were further confounded by negative bronchoscopy findings. Ultimately, lung wedge resection helped ascertain the diagnosis of PSC. CASE PRESENTATION: A 68-year-old man with a 49-pack-year smoking history was referred to Pulmonary clinic by his primary care physician for evaluation of abnormal findings on computed-tomography (CT) of the thorax. Patient had no complaints other than daily morning dry cough. His past medical history was notable for pulmonary emphysema, stable sub-centimeter pulmonary nodules and non-calcified pleural plaques, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and hyperlipidemia. He denied asbestos exposure and personal or family history of cancer. CT thorax followed by High Resolution CT thorax revealed 2 enlarging thick-walled cysts, one in right upper lobe and another in left lower lobe, pulmonary nodules, pleural plaques and numerous bilateral upper lobe predominant thin-walled cysts. Bloodwork including autoantibodies, VEGF-D, alpha-1 anti-trypsin, and fungal serology remained negative. Pulmonary function testing showed moderate diffusion impairment. Bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound, lymph node aspiration and bronchoalveolar lavage were negative for cytology, CD1a, S100, acid fast bacilli, fungi and bacteria. Based on tumor board discussion patient underwent robotic wedge resection of right upper lobe of the lung and biopsy was positive for poorly differentiated Sarcomatoid Carcinoma with positive immunohistochemical staining for CD 3, CD 20, AE1/AE3, and negative metastasis confirming pathologic stage 1A (T1C N0 M0). DISCUSSION: Pulmonary Sarcomatoid Carcinoma accounts for 0.4% of all lung cancers with an average survival of 11-13 months1. Five subtypes identified include: Carcinosarcoma, Giant cell carcinoma, Pleomorphic carcinoma, Pulmonary blastoma, and Spindle cell carcinoma. It has a preponderance for male smokers usually in their 60s except for Pulmonary blastoma that demonstrates an equal sex ratio and has a mean age at diagnosis of 35 years2. It commonly presents as solitary masses located peripherally in upper lobes however, other reported presentations include necrotizing cavitary lesion³. The presentation of PSC in our case was confounded by a background of cystic lung disease with two distinct thick-walled cysts in an active tobacco smoker. Differential diagnosis included malignancy, autoimmune etiology and infection such as tuberculosis for the thick-walled cysts. Negative bloodwork and bronchoscopy necessitated reliance on surgical resection to delineate histology and provide distinction between malignancy from other mimickers. Huang et al narrated a misdiagnosis rate of PSC of >70% preoperatively³ which is understandable for rare tumors like PSC whose diagnosis depends on whether biopsied tissue is representative of the mass². Treatment options include resection for limited disease and adjuvant chemotherapy especially for stage II and III disease, however, recurrence rate after surgery is very high1. Work on targeted therapy and immunotherapy are ongoing although antibodies targeting EGFR mutations have not shown significant improvement in overall survival². CONCLUSIONS: PSC is a rare lung cancer with variable presentations, limited treatment options and poor prognosis. More data is necessary to help refine diagnosis and consequently therapeutic targets. REFERENCE #1: Martin, L. W., Correa, A. M., Ordonez, N. G., Roth, J. A., Swisher, S. G., Vaporciyan, A. A., … & Rice, D. C. (2007). Sarcomatoid carcinoma of the lung: a predictor of poor prognosis. The Annals of thoracic surgery, 84(3), 973-980. REFERENCE #2: Vaidya, G. N., Shah, R., & Dhamoon, A. (2017). Pulmonary sarcomatoid carcinoma presenting as a necrotizing cavitary lung lesion: diagnostic dilemma. JOURNAL OF COMMUNITY AND SUPPORTIVE ONCOLOGY, 15(2), 103-105. REFERENCE #3: Huang, S. Y., Shen, S. J., & Li, X. Y. (2013). Pulmonary sarcomatoid carcinoma: a clinicopathologic study and prognostic analysis of 51 cases. World journal of surgical oncology, 11, 1-5. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sara Akbar No relevant relationships by Stephen Doyle No relevant relationships by Samridhi Gulati No relevant relationships by Tyler Kemnic No relevant relationships by Julieta Osella
