N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 5 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Histoplasmosis is a common endemic mycosis in the United States. Antigen testing of serum or urine are commonly used methods of diagnosis. This case represents biopsy proven pulmonary histoplasmosis with negative antigen testing. CASE PRESENTATION: A twenty-three-year-old woman with a medical history of obesity presented to the hospital for a three-day history of cough with hemoptysis. Her cough was minimally productive of clear sputum with rare streaks of red blood. Her illness started three months prior with a twenty-pound weight loss and a transient palpable right neck mass. She took no medications and endorsed no sick contacts, fevers, night sweats, nose bleeds, hematuria, muscle aches, rashes, abdominal pain, nausea, nor vomiting. Michigan was her birthplace with no travel outside of the state in the past year. Risk factors for immunosuppression including illicit drug use, multiple sexual partners, and exposure to tuberculosis were not present. Her vital signs were only remarkable for sinus tachycardia to 124 beats per minute. Laboratory investigations found a neutrophilic leukocytosis to 12.8 K/uL, anemia to 8.9 g/dL, erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 105 mm/Hr, and c-reactive protein of 11.8 mg/dL. The remainder of laboratory data including testing for HIV, procalcitonin, urinary histoplasmosis antigen, and urinary blastomycosis antigen were negative. A peripheral smear had no evidence of blasts, dysplasia, or schistocytes. Chest imaging demonstrated small pulmonary nodules around a right upper lobe consolidation and a small right pleural effusion. Enlarged lymph nodes were seen in the right paratracheal, right hilar, and subcarinal regions with extrinsic compression of the right mainstem bronchus and bronchus intermedius. Bronchoscopy with lymph node biopsies revealed necrotizing granulomas and fungal elements consistent with histoplasmosis on methenamine silver stain. Flow cytometry on lymph node tissue found no evidence of lymphoma. Itraconazole was initiated with subsequent radiographic and symptomatic improvement. DISCUSSION: Utilization of histoplasmosis antigen or serologic testing is recommended to expedite a diagnosis as fungal culture requires two to eight weeks for growth. Reported sensitivity of antigen testing varies from 65% with acute pneumonia, 88% with chronic pulmonary disease, and greater than 90% with disseminated disease. Combining urine and serum antigen testing as well as combining antigen and antibody testing increased the sensitivity to 93% and 96% respectively in acute histoplasmosis pneumonia. Typical histopathology reveals granulomatous inflammation and yeast elements on staining as seen in this case. CONCLUSIONS: Clinical symptoms and imaging findings of histoplasmosis can overlap with many diseases such as bacterial pneumonia, tuberculosis, and malignancy. Knowledge about imperfect laboratory test characteristics can aid in accurate diagnosis of histoplasmosis. REFERENCE #1: Hage CA, Carmona EM, Epelbaum O, Evans SE, Gabe LM, Haydour Q, et al. Microbiological laboratory testing in the diagnosis of fungal infections in pulmonary and critical care practice: an official ATS clinical practice guideline implementation tools. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019; 200(5):535-550. REFERENCE #2: Araúz AB, Papineni P. Histoplasmosis. Infect Dis Clin North Am. 2021 Jun;35(2):471-491. doi: 10.1016/j.idc.2021.03.011. PMID: 34016287. REFERENCE #3: McKinsey DS, McKinsey JP. Pulmonary histoplasmosis. Semin Respir Crit Care Med. 2011 Dec;32(6):735-44. doi: 10.1055/s-0031-1295721. Epub 2011 Dec 13. PMID: 22167401. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Zachary Bauer
