N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Posters 6 SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm PURPOSE: The American Thoracic Society (ATS)/Infectious Disease Society of America (IDSA) guidelines define pulmonary non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) infection with clinical, microbiological and radiographic criteria. The clinical relevance of positive pulmonary isolates in patients who do not meet these criteria i.e., those with NTM colonization is unknown. The main goal of this study is to characterize pulmonary NTM isolates over a 4-year period in a multihospital health system and identify the predictors of NTM infection and colonization. METHODS: We conducted a retrospective chart review study including all patients with NTM positive pulmonary isolates from 2016-2020. NTM infection was defined as per the ATS/IDSA guidelines. Patients who did not meet the ATS/IDSA criteria were labeled to have NTM colonization. In addition to descriptive analysis, univariate analyses were performed to assess predictors of NTM infection. RESULTS: Data from 510 patients with at least one of two NTM positive sputum samples or an NTM positive BAL, bronchial washing or lung biopsy were analyzed. 164/510 (32%) had pulmonary NTM infection and 346/510 (68%) had NTM colonization. Mycobacterium avium complex (95%) was the most frequently isolated species followed by Mycobacterium abscessus (0.1%). Patients with NTM infection were more likely to be over 65 years old (OR 1.03; 95%CI 1.01-1.04) as compared to those with NTM colonization. While comorbid conditions such as interstitial lung disease (p = 0.01), bronchiectasis (p = 0.01), HIV infection (p = 0.047) and cirrhosis (p = 0.03) were significantly associated with NTM infection, patients on immunosuppressive agents (p = 0.68) or oral steroids (p = 0.53) were not. Patients with NTM infection were more likely to present with weight loss (29% vs 19%, p = 0.01) and cough (73% vs 63%, p = 0.03) compared to those with NTM colonization. More patients with NTM infection died within 2 years of sputum positivity as compared to those with NTM colonization (29% vs 17%, p = 0.004). CONCLUSIONS: The prevalence of pulmonary NTM infection was 32% in our study cohort with Mycobacterium avium complex being the most frequently isolated species. Older patients and comorbidities such as HIV infection, cirrhosis, bronchiectasis and interstitial lung disease were associated with NTM infection. Significant clinical symptoms of NTM infection were weight loss and cough with a higher 2-year mortality compared to NTM colonization. Further logistic regression analyses are ongoing to characterize the risk factors better. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: A significant number of patients with sputum positive for NTM have an NTM infection with an overall poorer prognosis. Larger prospective translational studies are needed to elucidate factors that lead to progression of NTM colonization to NTM infection and to develop targeted preventive strategies. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Melissa Berges Morales No relevant relationships by Hansol Choi No relevant relationships by Ryan Fiter No relevant relationships by Gabriel Hernández Romero No relevant relationships by Rahul Nair No relevant relationships by Divya Reddy
