N2  - SESSION TITLE: Diffuse Lung Disease Case Report Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: CD19+ Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T-cells are effective against CD19+ hematologic malignancies such as high-grade B-cell lymphoma. CAR- T infusion is associated with immediate complications such as cytokine release syndrome(CRS), immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity syndrome(ICANS), pulmonary toxicity, and prolonged cytopenia. CAR-T is a relatively new treatment. All potential adverse effects are not well described. The complete risk profile could not be defined in preapproval trials. We describe a 64 yr-old man with relapsed B cell lymphoma treated with CD-19+ CAR-T therapy and developed secondary organizing pneumonia at 18 months. CASE PRESENTATION: A 64-year man presented to the pulmonary clinic with a history of relapsed Follicular Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma previously treated with chemotherapy and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. He completed CAR-T for recent relapse. He reported worsening cough associated with malaise, weight loss, night sweats, fevers, and dyspnea for four months. Physical exam notable for bibasilar crackles. Computed tomography (CT) scan showed patchy bilateral basilar pneumonia. He received courses of antibiotics and steroids without improvement. Repeat CT showed worsening bilateral lower lobe infiltrates. Lab data were normal including connective tissue serologies and Quantiferon. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and sputum cultures were positive for Beta-lactamase H. influenzae. BAL was negative for galactomannan. Cytology was negative for malignancy. BAL AFB culture was positive growth but negative for Mycobacterium Tuberculosis DNA. Transbronchial biopsy showed Organizing pneumonia. He received Levofloxacin and started on long term prednisone for organizing pneumonia. He had remarkable interval clinical improvement after two weeks. DISCUSSION: CAR-T treatment remains an important option for patients suffering from relapsing disease. There is limited data on its long-term complications. It remains challenging to predict the timelines of unwanted effects because CAR-T cells can persist and expand in vivo. Most pulmonary complications occur within the acute period of treatment. In one series, 80% of first infections were within ten days. Pooled data suggest bacterial infection occurred infrequently after 30 days. Late-onset infections were rare and may reflect prolonged immunoglobulin deficiency. However, one must be cognizant of long-term pulmonary toxicity. In another case series, CT showed findings consistent with organizing pneumonia pattern after three months. Two of the three patients received corticosteroids with clinical and radiological improvement. Studies show that in patients surviving 90 + days after CAR T, late infections occurred in approximately 60%. Of those infections, 60% were bacterial infections, 31% viral, and 9% fungal. Although our patient had evidence of bacterial pneumonia, the chronic nature of his symptoms and lack of improvement despite multiple antibiotic courses suggested that organizing pneumonia contributed to his clinical condition. The causes of organizing pneumonia are broad and include viral and bacterial infections, medications, radiation, and connective tissue disorders. While the secondary organizing pneumonia in our patient may be related to bacterial pneumonia, we postulate CAR -T is a contributing etiology. CONCLUSIONS: While CRS and ICANS are the most common CAR-T toxicities, our case highlights the importance of continued surveillance for pulmonary complications beyond the acute phase of treatment and will help increase awareness on immunotherapy-adverse effects of CAR-T cell therapy beyond one year. REFERENCE #1: CAR T cells-associated delayed lung toxicity – a case seriesLouise Bondeelle, Roberta Di Blasi, Laila Samy, Lorea Aguinaga, Gwenael Lorillon, Sophie Bernard, Catherine Thieblemont, Anne Bergeron Lafaurie. European Respiratory Journal Sep 2020, 56 (suppl 64) 746; DOI: 10.1183/13993003.congress-2020.746 REFERENCE #2: Stewart AG, Henden AS. Infectious complications of CAR T-cell therapy: a clinical update. Therapeutic Advances in Infectious Disease. 2021;8. doi:10.1177/20499361211036773 REFERENCE #3: Sun Z, Xie C, Liu H and Yuan X (2022) CD19 CAR-T Cell Therapy Induced Immunotherapy Associated Interstitial Pneumonitis: A Case Report. Front. Immunol. 13:778192. doi: 10.3389/fimmu.2022.778192Penack O, Koenecke C. Complications after CD19+ CAR T-Cell Therapy. Cancers (Basel). 2020 Nov 19;12(11):3445. doi: 10.3390/cancers12113445. PMID: 33228221; PMCID: PMC7699604. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by N. Stacy Amadife No relevant relationships by Isaac Ikwu No disclosure on file for Vishal Poddar No relevant relationships by Alicia Thomas
