N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 8 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pneumonia in the solid organ transplant patient presents a unique challenge to clinicians. Depending on clinical and radiographic presentation, the differential diagnosis is significantly variable. Co-infection with multiple opportunistic infections is rare and diagnostic testing can often confound the diagnosis and therefore treatment of the truly pathogenic organism. CASE PRESENTATION: This 70-year-old-male renal transplant patient on cyclosporine 100 mg twice daily presented with acute hypoxemic respiratory failure secondary to multifocal pneumonia and acute kidney injury in the setting of chronic kidney disease. His past medical history otherwise significant for AVR and PPM placement. He had increased productive cough, chills at home, and dyspnea on exertion for two weeks. Computed tomography of the chest revealed multifocal ground glass opacities, significantly worse compared to three weeks prior when he was admitted and treated for CMV pneumonia. Bronchoscopy was performed. Cell count with differential revealed 6% neutrophils, 24% lymphocytes, and 70% monocytes. Cultures were unrevealing, silver stain was negative for PJP, and BAL fluid was positive for CMV, MAC and BK. No biopsies were performed. He was ultimately transferred to a dedicated transplant facility while febrile to 100.9 F on 6 liters per minute via nasal cannula. Transplant infectious disease was consulted and despite negative silver stain, they felt the clinical presentation and CT imaging were pathognomonic for PJP. He was started on primaquine, clindamycin, and prednisone, ultimately transitioned to Bactrim once his renal function improved. His oxygenation slowly recovered, requiring 2 liters per minute via NC at rest and 4 liters with exertion. PCR on BAL fluid for PJP ultimately returned positive. He was discharged home on 2 L and Bactrim double strength BID and a 21-day prednisone taper. At his outpatient pulmonary follow up, his radiograph showed significant improvement and he was saturating well on room air with minimal symptoms. DISCUSSION: Solid organ transplant patients on chronic immunosuppression require special care regarding infections, particularly when it comes to pneumonia. While co infections with organisms such as CMV, BK, PJP and Mycobacterium avium in the immunocompromised are rare, there are few reported in the available literature. Bronchoscopy with lavage is critical to diagnosis, as the scope of the differential diagnosis for pneumonia in these patients is remarkably broad. In this case, much of his testing revealed red herring diagnoses, but the astute clinical diagnosis of the infectious disease specialists provided timely and proper treatment for PJP. CONCLUSIONS: Though CMV and BK testing remained positive from the respiratory tract, it was clear there were additional infectious precipitating factors that led to his clinical decompensation and worsening multifocal pneumonia. Chest imaging and acuity or chronicity were key in developing the initial differential. Though ultimately flexible bronchoscopy with lavage served as a crucial component in the diagnosis of this case of PJP, it was the history, presentation, and classic chest imaging that led to the diagnosis and swift timing of treatment in this case. As it is said: "Listen to your patient. They will tell you the diagnosis." REFERENCE #1: Gaborit, B.J., Tessoulin, B., Lavergne, RA. et al. Outcome and prognostic factors of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in immunocompromised adults: a prospective observational study. Ann. Intensive Care 9, 131 (2019). REFERENCE #2: Jaroszewski DE, Webb BJ, Leslie KO. Diagnosis and management of lung infections. Thorac Surg Clin. 2012 Aug;22(3):301-24. doi: 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2012.05.002. PMID: 22789595; PMCID: PMC7106184. REFERENCE #3: Kara S, Sen N, Kursun E, Yabanoğlu H, Yıldırım S, Akçay Ş, Haberal M. Pneumonia in Renal Transplant Recipients: A Single-Center Study. Exp Clin Transplant. 2018 Mar;16 Suppl 1(Suppl 1):122-125. doi: 10.6002/ect.TOND-TDTD2017.P23. PMID: 29528008. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kendall Creed No relevant relationships by Matthew Haltom No relevant relationships by Christian Provenza
