N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 16 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Aspergillus is a ubiquitous saprophytic mold that can present as an opportunistic infection in immunosuppressed patients. We present a case of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis-associated interstitial lung disease on immunosuppressive therapy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 67-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis and associated interstitial lung disease on immunosuppressive therapy with Azathioprine and Hydroxychloroquine presented with two weeks of cough, chills, and dyspnea. Laboratory work-up showed an ESR of 42 mm/hr, CRP of 1.6 mg/L, and a normal absolute neutrophil count. Blood cultures, sputum cultures, HIV testing, urine and serum histoplasma antigen, tuberculosis testing, and procalcitonin were negative. Immunoglobulins were within normal range. β-D-glucan was elevated at > 500 pg/mL. A CT chest showed diffuse tree-in-bud nodularity and ground glass opacities with chronic bronchiectasis and fibrotic changes.She underwent flexible bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). Transbronchial lung biopsy was not obtained. Bacterial, fungal, and AFB cultures, PJP PCR, Aspergillus PCR, and a pneumonia PCR panel of the BAL were negative. BAL Aspergillus galactomannan index was positive at 2.31. Given consistent symptoms, imaging findings, and laboratory testing in the setting of immunosuppression, she was diagnosed with invasive pulmonary aspergillosis and started on Voriconazole. On outpatient follow-up, repeat imaging showed interval improvement and her symptoms had nearly resolved. DISCUSSION: The diagnosis of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis requires evaluation of patient history and clinical context to differentiate colonization from invasive illness. Our patient presented with multiple acute symptoms in the setting of therapeutic immunosuppression for rheumatoid arthritis. Although invasive pulmonary aspergillosis typically occurs in the setting of severe neutropenia, our case highlights that patients with other causes of immunosuppression are also at risk.While definitive diagnosis of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis requires culture of the organism from a sterile site or demonstration of the organism with hyphal invasion on histopathology, fungal culture has limited sensitivity and tissue biopsy is not always safe or available. This case shows that BAL Aspergillus galactomannan can be an accurate diagnostic marker in certain populations, especially as it may have superior sensitivity to other non-invasive tests.A variety of imaging findings can be seen in invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. Nodules and infiltrates with a halo sign are common in neutropenic patients. Non-neutropenic patients can present with nodules, consolidation, ground glass opacities, or tree-in-bud opacities. This case demonstrates the importance of maintaining a high clinical suspicion in appropriate populations and the use of additional diagnostic testing given the non-specific radiographic presentation. CONCLUSIONS: Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis is a challenging diagnosis that can occur in varying causes of immunosuppression. Comprehensive evaluation, including use of non-invasive markers of fungal infection, can lead to improved identification and appropriate treatment. REFERENCE #1: Luong, M.-L., Filion, C., Labbé, A.-C., et al. (2010). Clinical utility and prognostic value of bronchoalveolar lavage galactomannan in patients with hematologic malignancies. Diagnostic Microbiology and Infectious Disease, 68(2), 132–139. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.diagmicrobio.2010.03.017 REFERENCE #2: Heng, S. C., Morrissey, O., Chen, et al. (2013). Utility of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid galactomannan alone or in combination with PCR for the diagnosis of invasive aspergillosis in adult hematology patients: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Critical Reviews in Microbiology, 41(1), 124–134. https://doi.org/10.3109/1040841x.2013.804033 REFERENCE #3: Donnelly, J. P., Chen, S. C., Kauffman, C. A., et al. (2019). Revision and update of the consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease from the European Organization for research and treatment of cancer and the Mycoses Study Group Education and research consortium. Clinical Infectious Diseases, 71(6), 1367–1376. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciz1008 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Adam Highley No relevant relationships by Raul Isern No relevant relationships by Ian Jackson No relevant relationships by Marisa Varghese
