N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 27 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Diabetes Mellitus (DM) can lead to immunosuppression and susceptibility to opportunistic infections including fungal pneumonias. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and biopsy should be considered if clinical suspicion for fungal pneumonia is high as serologic tests are only moderately sensitive (~70-80%). We present an interesting case of a patient with uncontrolled DM who developed necrotizing Aspergillus and Microsporus pneumonia. Prompt diagnostic workup and treatment led to a favorable patient outcome. CASE PRESENTATION: A 58-year-old male with uncontrolled DM (HbA1c 14.5%), HTN, CKD and HLD presented with productive cough and dyspnea. He was admitted for diabetic ketoacidosis and sepsis due to community acquired pneumonia (CAP). Initial imaging showed right middle lobe opacity (Figure 1). Patient was treated with ceftriaxone and azithromycin for CAP. He continued to deteriorate with worsening hypoxic respiratory failure despite escalation of antimicrobials to cefepime, metronidazole and vancomycin. Serum Fungitell and Aspergillus antigen were negative. Patient declined bronchoscopy initially but agreed after clinical deterioration. He required endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation. Bronchoscopy with BAL and endobronchial lung biopsy showed significant bronchial necrosis prompting initiation of antifungals including isavuconazole and micafungin. BAL fungal culture came back positive for Rhizopus microsporus. Histopathology of biopsy specimen showed thin septate hyphae consistent with Aspergillus fumigatus and large ribbon-like non-septate hyphae consistent with Rhizopus microsporus (Figure 2). Patient was successfully extubated on day 14 and discharged on antifungal therapy with outpatient follow up. DISCUSSION: Necrotizing fungal pneumonias including Aspergillus and Rhizopus are devastating infections affecting immunocompromised patients and diabetics. Hyperglycemia leads to reduced neutrophil chemotaxis, impaired phagocytosis, decreased macrophage and complement cascade activation, and immunoglobulin deactivation impairing the innate and adaptive immune response to pathogens.Initial evaluation for opportunistic fungal infection often utilizes serologic markers such as fungitell (assay for 1,3-β-D-glucan, a cell wall component of many fungi) and fungal antigen EIA, however histopathologic evidence of organisms is often required for diagnosis. Our patient had persistently negative fungitell and Aspergillus antigen (BAL and serum) despite having an invasive infection with two fungal organisms. Biopsy showed characteristic elements of Aspergillus (thin septate hyphae with 45 degree angle branching) and Rhizopus (broad ribbon-like non-septate hyphae with 90 degree angle branching. In this case, bronchoscopy was pursued early despite negative serologic findings and empiric antifungal therapy was initiated leading to a favorable outcome. CONCLUSIONS: Early diagnosis and treatment is crucial for favorable outcome in fungal infections. Clinicians should consider fungal pneumonia in differential diagnosis while treating immunocompromised patients, including those with uncontrolled diabetes mellitus. REFERENCE #1: Berbudi, A., Rahmadika, N., Cahyadi, A. I., & Ruslami, R. (2019). Type 2 Diabetes and Its Impact on the Immune System. Current Diabetes Reviews, 16(5). REFERENCE #2: Geerlings, S. E., & Hoepelman, A. I. M. (1999). Immune dysfunction in patients with diabetes mellitus (DM). FEMS Immunology & Medical Microbiology, 26(3-4), 259–265. REFERENCE #3: Tran, T., & Beal, S. G. (2016). Application of the 1,3-β-d-Glucan (Fungitell) Assay in the Diagnosis of Invasive Fungal Infections. Archives of Pathology & Laboratory Medicine, 140(2), 181–185. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Nadia Ali No relevant relationships by Waqas Aslam No relevant relationships by Jasmine Nudanu
