N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 12 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: While hepatic hydrothorax develops in up to 15% of cases of peritoneal ascites, the development of empyema from a liver abscess is rare. Here we present a case of right lung empyema attributed to liver abscess. CASE PRESENTATION: An 86-year-old man with a past medical history of multiple myeloma, lower extremity DVT on Rivaroxaban, and GERD presented with failure to thrive for 1 month, as well as productive cough, shortness of breath, and night sweats for 1 week. He had a history of severe aspiration pneumonia that required a right thoracotomy and decortication 15 years prior to admission. On arrival, he was tachycardic but otherwise hemodynamically stable. His labs were notable for leukopenia 1.4, total bilirubin 1.9, lactate 3.2, and procalcitonin 2.25. Chest x-ray demonstrated a new right pleural effusion. CT chest revealed a mass-like right lower lobe consolidation with loculated pleural effusion and associated irregular pleural thickening thought to be inseparable from the right hemidiaphragm. CT abdomen showed a 4.9x3.2 cm segment 7 hepatic dome abscess in communication with the loculated right pleural fluid collection. The patient underwent catheter insertion and drainage of both the complex pleural space and hepatic abscess. Culture from the pleural fluid grew Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron, Streptococcus constellatus, Clostridium clostridioforme, and Bacteroides fragilis. Blood cultures taken from admission also grew Streptococcus sanguinis. On further questioning, he revealed he had undergone a routine dental cleaning one month prior to symptom onset. He was treated with two months of Ertapenem with complete radiographic resolution of the lung consolidation and pleural fluid and hepatic abscess. DISCUSSION: Pyogenic liver abscesses (PLA) are uncommon, with estimated incidence of 0.5-0.8%. Pleural empyema is a rare complication from PLA, with a prior study estimating a 4.3% incidence. The mechanism is thought to be due to translocation of bacteria across the diaphragm into the pleural space resulting in a polymicrobial empyema. Our patient had risk factors for development of empyema from PLA including location of PLA near the right diaphragm and larger abscess size. While our patient did not have typical risk factors for extra hepatic spread of PLA, which include alcoholism and diabetes, they did have a history of malignancy, which is a known risk factor for development of PLA. CONCLUSIONS: A study found an increased hospital admission LOS and increased complication in patient's with PLA who developed empyema compared to those who did not develop empyema, though effect on mortality is unclear. Antimicrobial therapy and separate percutaneous drainage of both the pleural space and hepatic abscess are the cornerstones of treatment, and were successful in treating this patient. Occasionally surgery may be required if drainage is unsuccessful. REFERENCE #1: Yi E, Kim TH, Lee JH, et al. Evaluation of clinical risk factors for developing pleural empyema secondary to liver abscess. BMC Gastroenterol. 2019;19(1):215. REFERENCE #2: AlGhamdi ZM, Boumarah DN, Alshammary S, Elbawab H. Pleural Empyema as a Complication of Pyogenic Liver Abscess: Can the Minimum Achieve the Optimal? A Comparison of 3 Approaches. Am J Case Rep. 2021 Dec 20;22. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Molly Forster No relevant relationships by Ian Mahoney No relevant relationships by Seema Qayum
