N2  - SESSION TITLE: Obstructive Lung Diseases Case Report Posters 2 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Kartagener syndrome (KS) is a rare autosomal recessive ciliary disorder characterized by a triad of findings that includes situs inversus, bronchiectasis, and chronic sinusitis. The underlying cause is a defective movement of cilia, which leads to recurrent upper and lower respiratory tract infections. CASE PRESENTATION: A 41-year-old male with Kartagener Syndrome presented to our ER after a seven-day history of progressive intense chest pain, shortness of breath, productive cough, and two days of subjective fever and chills. The patient visited ER at the peripheral hospital two times prior to this visit and was discharged home with pain medication under the clinical impression of costochondritis. Findings on physical examination included tachycardia, heart sounds in the right side of his chest, and decreased breath sounds in the left lower lung. Laboratory testing was remarkable for leukocytosis (24 x 10^9/L) with neutrophilia (21 x 10^9/L) and hypoxemia (pO2 39.9 at room air) in arterial blood gases. Chest CTA was notable for situs inversus totalis, focal bronchiectasis in the right lower lobe, and moderate to large loculated left pleural effusion with complete left lower lobe atelectasis. The patient was admitted under the diagnosis of bronchiectasis exacerbation and placed in non-invasive positive pressure ventilation. He was started on Meropenem and Azithromycin. The patient underwent video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) for drainage of loculated pleural effusion, after which he remained in mechanical ventilation with two left chest tubes. He was extubated ten days after the procedure and then was transferred to the Internal Medicine Ward. A follow-up chest CT documented partial resolution of loculated pleural effusions. After completing 16 days of broad-spectrum antibiotics, the patient's symptoms resolved, and he was discharged home with an appointment with his outpatient Pneumologist for appropriate follow-up. DISCUSSION: Bronchiectasis exacerbation is defined as a person with bronchiectasis with deterioration of three or more symptoms lasting 48 hours or more, including cough, sputum volume or purulence, breathlessness, fatigue, and hemoptysis. Bronchiectasis has a high bacterial burden and inflammation, and exacerbations are mostly associated with an increase or change in the pathogen population. Antibiotic therapy is the cornerstone treatment, as early and definitive management of these infections may delay the evolution of bronchiectasis and minimize the progressive loss of lung function in these patients. CONCLUSIONS: Our case illustrates the importance of history taking and physical examination in correlation with the patient's medical history on clinical diagnosis and decision-making. Clinicians should be aware of rare disorders such as KS, and how failure to recognize it can shift the clinical diagnosis, and thus the management, affecting the patient harmfully. REFERENCE #1: Leigh, M. W., Pittman, J. E., Carson, J. L., Ferkol, T. W., Dell, S. D., Davis, S. D., Knowles, M. R., & Zariwala, M. A. (2009). Clinical and genetic aspects of primary ciliary dyskinesia/Kartagener syndrome. Genetics in medicine: official journal of the American College of Medical Genetics, 11(7), 473–487. https://doi.org/10.1097/GIM.0b013e3181a53562 REFERENCE #2: Gruffydd-Jones, K., Keeley, D., Knowles, V., Recabarren, X., Woodward, A., Sullivan, A. L., Loebinger, M. R., Payne, K., Harvey, A., Grillo, L., Welham, S. A., & Hill, A. T. (2019). Primary care implications of the British Thoracic Society Guidelines for bronchiectasis in adults 2019. NPJ primary care respiratory medicine, 29(1), 24. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41533-019-0136-8 REFERENCE #3: Hill, A. T., Haworth, C. S., Aliberti, S., Barker, A., Blasi, F., Boersma, W., Chalmers, J. D., De Soyza, A., Dimakou, K., Elborn, J. S., Feldman, C., Flume, P., Goeminne, P. C., Loebinger, M. R., Menendez, R., Morgan, L., Murris, M., Polverino, E., Quittner, A., Ringshausen, F. C., … EMBARC/BRR definitions working group (2017). Pulmonary exacerbation in adults with bronchiectasis: a consensus definition for clinical research. The European respiratory journal, 49(6), 1700051. https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.00051-2017 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Gerardo Cintron No relevant relationships by Elsie Cruz No relevant relationships by Adriel Gonzalez Rivera No relevant relationships by Edilberto Ocasio-Feliciano No relevant relationships by Marcon Soto Telemaco
