N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 11 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Parvimonas micra & Capnocytophaga have previously been described as rare causes of lung abscess. To our knowledge, this is the first case of a co-infection in an immunocompetent host. CASE PRESENTATION: A 55-year-old female with hypertension and seizure disorder presented to our emergency department with worsening right-sided back pain, intermittent dry cough, occasional yellowish sputum, intermittent night sweats, fatigue, and 10-12 lb unintentional weight loss over four weeks duration. There were no associated hemoptysis, exertional dyspnea, or chest pain. She was a 20-pack-year smoker but denied illicit drug use or recent travel outside the US. On physical examination, she was afebrile with unremarkable vital signs and had decreased air entry in the right lower lobe with dullness to percussion. Her blood work was significant for a white blood cell count of 16k/µL, elevated C-reactive protein of 189mg/L & a negative HIV screen. Chest computed tomography without contrast showed three hypodense lesions in the right lower lung; the largest lesion measured 9.2 x 6 x 7.3 cm (image 1) that crossed the major fissure into the posterior right upper lobe. There was also right-sided mediastinal & hilar lymphadenopathy.She underwent endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) guided biopsy of the largest lesion (image 2), which showed atypical cells in the background of abundant necrotic debris and acute inflammation. Gram stain showed moderate gram-positive cocci & bacillus, and later, anaerobic bacterial cultures grew Parvimonas micra and two colonies of Capnocytophaga. Upon further questioning, she reported having a generalized seizure episode 6 months prior, with the suspicion that she may have aspirated during that episode. She was treated with amoxicillin-clavulanate. Unfortunately, post-hospital discharge, the patient was lost to follow-up. DISCUSSION: Parvimonas micra & Capnocytophaga are anaerobic bacteria, considered normal oral flora, and are associated with periodontal disease. Isolation of either of those organisms from lung abscesses is extremely rare, primarily thought to be due to the lungs’ oxygen-rich environment. Chronic lung abscesses associated with Parvimonas micra have been previously reported to occur more commonly in smokers and are often initially misdiagnosed as lung cancer. In contrast, the reports of Capnocytophaga-related lung abscesses are even more sparse, with only two other reported cases in the literature in immunocompetent hosts. The mainstay treatment used for both organisms is beta-lactam antimicrobials, with serial imaging to guide the duration. Only one reported case of Parvimonas micra required surgical removal due to poor response to antibiotic therapies. CONCLUSIONS: To our knowledge this is the first documented case of a large lung abscess secondary to co-infection with Parvimonas micra & Capnocytophaga. REFERENCE #1: Zhang Y, Song P, Zhang R, Yao Y, Shen L, Ma Q, Zhou J, Zhou H. Clinical Characteristics of Chronic Lung Abscess Associated with Parvimonas micra Diagnosed Using Metagenomic Next-Generation Sequencing. Infect Drug Resist. 2021 Mar 23;14:1191-1198. doi: 10.2147/IDR.S304569. PMID: 33790589; PMCID: PMC8001108. REFERENCE #2: Gosse L, Amrane S, Mailhe M, Dubourg G, Lagier JC. Capnocytophaga sputigena: An unusual cause of community-acquired pneumonia. IDCases. 2019 Jun 11;17:e00572. doi: 10.1016/j.idcr.2019.e00572. PMID: 31275806; PMCID: PMC6587014. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sreeja Biswas Roy No relevant relationships by John Fanous No relevant relationships by Umesh Goswami
