N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 34 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Patients with human immunodeficiency virus infection are at risk for opportunistic infections and must be evaluated with a high degree of suspicion for opportunistic infections. Currently available serologic testing for endemic fungi using antigen assay has the potential for cross reactivity between pathogens and can lead to false positive results. CASE PRESENTATION: A 26-year-old male with a medical history significant for congenital human immunodeficiency virus with previous nonadherence to anti-retroviral therapy initially presented to a rural hospital with nonspecific symptoms of fatigue and syncope. He was found to have a right lung opacity and positive urine histoplasma antigen. Patient was treated with amoxicillin-clavulanic acid for presumed diagnosis of bacterial pneumonia and was also placed of fluconazole for histoplasmosis. Three weeks after initial presentation, he was admitted to our tertiary care center with persistent fever and cough. CT chest with contrast showed right lower lobe necrotic mass measuring 9 x 6.5 x 8.5 cm with internal septation and cystic changes. Patient was started on broad-spectrum antibiotics and his home anti-retroviral therapy. The patient subsequently had endobronchial ultrasound guided fine needle aspiration of right lower lobe mass along with a broncho-alveolar lavage (BAL) of the right lower lobe. BAL showed 39% neutrophils and 60% macrophages. Beta-glucan level was noted to be elevated on BAL fluid. BAL and tissue cultures grew Coccidioides Immitis. He completed a two-week course of amphotericin B and followed by plan for at least one year of fluconazole. DISCUSSION: Coccidioides immitis is a soil dwelling fungus that can cause disseminated infection and is limited in range to the Southwest United States [1]. This patient presents outside of the normal habitat for Coccidioides immitis. Urine histoplasma and Blastomyces testing was positive, however these tests are for a common fungal antigen that will sometimes cross-react with coccidioidomycosis [2]. The patient's home state of Arkansas is not an area known for coccidioidomycosis; however, the patient was making frequent trips to Laredo, Texas for his work which likely the location of exposure. Coccidioidomycosis infections are increasing in frequency and interstate travel can blur the previously established areas of incidence [3]. CONCLUSIONS: Diagnosis of coccidiomycosis requires high index suspicion even outside the expected geographic distribution in appropriate clinical setting. Our case highlights the risk of false positive serologic testing and importance bronchoscopy if the diagnosis remains uncertain. REFERENCE #1: Lockhart SR, Toda M, Benedict K, Caceres DH, Litvintseva AP. Endemic and Other Dimorphic Mycoses in The Americas. J Fungi (Basel). 2021 Feb 20;7(2):151. doi: 10.3390/jof7020151. PMID: 33672469; PMCID: PMC7923431. REFERENCE #2: Kuberski T, Myers R, Wheat LJ, Durkin M, Connolly P, Kubak BM, Bruckner D, Pegues D. Diagnosis of coccidioidomycosis by antigen detection using cross-reaction with a Histoplasma antigen. Clin Infect Dis. 2007 Mar 1;44(5):e50-4. doi: 10.1086/511684. Epub 2007 Jan 25. PMID: 17278049 REFERENCE #3: McCotter OZ, Benedict K, Engelthaler DM, Komatsu K, Lucas KD, Mohle-Boetani JC, Oltean H, Vugia D, Chiller TM, Sondermeyer Cooksey GL, Nguyen A, Roe CC, Wheeler C, Sunenshine R. Update on the Epidemiology of coccidioidomycosis in the United States. Med Mycol. 2019 Feb 1;57(Supplement_1):S30-S40. doi: 10.1093/mmy/myy095. PMID: 30690599; PMCID: PMC6823633. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships Added 03/31/2023 by Shahrukh Arif, source=Web Response, value=Owned stocks, dividends Removed 03/31/2023 by Shahrukh Arif, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jose Caceres No relevant relationships by Tanmay Gandhi No relevant relationships by Mohammad Khrais No relevant relationships by Jake Smith
