N2  - Background: Current treatments for CF based on CFTR gene delivery strategies have been largely unsuccessful. Factors such as buildup of thick mucus and bacterial biofilms surrounding the epithelial cells and presence of many highly pinocytic neutrophils contribute to this problem. We have identified a phenotype of granule-releasing, immunomodulatory, metabolically active (GRIM) neutrophils that has elevated rates of pinocytosis and decreased bactericidal capabilities. Expression of HDAC11 in a naïve airway neutrophil promotes differentiation into a GRIM neutrophil, a fate blocked by HDAC11 siRNA knockdown. There is evidence that anti-poly (ethylene) glycol (and related) antibodies are present in the human population, limiting the effectiveness of repeated doses. Extracellular vesicles (EVs) are a naturally occurring method of cell-to-cell communication of the same size as common lipid nanoparticle (LNP) and viral vector formulations. As such, rejection of EVs via adaptive immune mechanisms such as the formation of antibodies is less likely than rejection of LNPs or viral vectors such as adeno-associated viruses, but EVs are heterogenous and are more difficult to engineer with custom cargo than LNPs and viral vectors. We aim to prevent the formation of GRIM neutrophils and then deliver gene therapy to the epithelium using EVs, which are natural particles that allow cell-to-cell communication and are less likely to be rejected by the immune system. We synthesized two sets of EVs. The first targets neutrophils to prevent induction of the GRIM fate and decrease pinocytosis. When the neutrophil blockade is removed, the second set of engineered EVs is delivered to the epithelium to rescue CFTR function. Methods: We developed a combinatorial EV engineering toolkit that allows for synthesis of EVs with custom cargo and targeting capabilities. We generated two sets of EVs using stably transfected HEK293T cells. The first set (HDAC11 siRNA and single-chain variable fragments against CD14) targets neutrophils to prevent the GRIM phenotype and increase bacteria killing. The second set (CFTRmRNA, CD47 surface protein, and single-chain variable fragments against CD326/Ep-CAM) targets epithelial cells to restore CFTR function. We grew H441 epithelial cells at air-liquid interface (ALI), cocultured them with primary human neutrophils with each of the sets of EVs, and measured delivery to each cell type. Bacterial killing abilities of the neutrophils were assessed with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA01) at a multiplicity of infection of 1 for 1 hour. Results: Airway-like neutrophils that were treated with EVs containing siRNAs against MALAT1 or HDAC11 showed a greater ability to clear bacteria. As a control, including a “don't eat me signal” CD47 on the EVs also prevented the neutrophils from receiving the EVs. Based on this result, we included CD47 on the set of EVs containing CFTR mRNA to be delivered to the ALI epithelium to enhance delivery to the desired cell type. We were able to measure greater CFTR mRNA in the epithelium but have not yet measured protein abundance or function. Conclusions: This study supports a framework to deliver gene therapy to specified cell types. The effectiveness of the treatment in restoring CFTR function in epithelial cells needs further validation, as well as testing in relevant CF animal models.
