N2  - Background: Hormonal fluctuations throughout the menstrual cycle affect pulmonary function, which poses a potential health concern for girls and women with cystic fibrosis (WwCF). Estrogen is posited to increase infection risk, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa mucoid conversion, and pulmonary exacerbations [1,2], whereas progesterone may have a positive bronchodilator impact [3].With hormone stability from oral contraceptive pills (OCPs), WwCF achieve better health-related quality of life scores on the Cystic Fibrosis Questionnaire–Revised [4], which are attributed to fewer hospitalizations, less disease burden, greater vitality, and better physical function [1,4]. Despite perceived positive effects of OCPs on WwCF, there is limited published research in the past 40 years [5], so it is critical to elucidate how birth control modalities affect WwCF in the era of highly effective modulator therapies. Methods: In an ongoing study, 12 WwCFaged 11 and older cared for at our institution have been surveyed about their hormonal contraceptive use and perception of pulmonary health as it relates to their menstrual cycle. A retrospective chart review is conducted after participant assent and parental consent are obtained. Results: Twelve WwCF were recruited (mean age 16, range 11–25). Four of the 11 who had had at least one menstrual period at the time of recruitment (36.4%) had used contraceptives. Of two (18.2%) who reported current use, one had had a pre-menarche hospitalization, and one had had a post-menarche hospitalization. Those who were not on contraceptives averaged 0.9 hospitalizations before menarche and 2.2 after menarche (p = 0.23). Ten (83%) were colonized with P. aeruginosa, with four (40%) being colonized at least once after menarche. Weakness, fatigue, and shortness of breath at rest and with exercise were considered more severe during menses. Only increased work of breathing was reported as more severe during ovulation than menses. Shortness of breath with light work, chest tightness, and changes in cough and sputum were neutral in severity throughout the menstrual cycle. Conclusions: Given the variability of symptoms and severity between menses and ovulation, we suggest that there is merit to asking WwCF more about their menstrual cycles and hormonal contraceptive use during CF clinic. When comparing pre- and post-menarche hospitalizations for those currently using contraceptives, there seems to be a trend of pulmonary stability in those taking hormonal contraception. This could be related to a stabilizing effect on estrogen and progesterone levels from hormonal contraceptive use, helping reduce hospitalizations. Greater P. aeruginosa colonization after menarche warrants further investigation into differences in rates of P. aeruginosa colonization according to biological sex and if there are any differences with puberty. Limitations of this survey include recall bias and small study size, but we are continuing recruitment at our institution and hope to contribute additional data in the upcoming months to elucidate the effect of reproductive health and hormonal contraceptive on pulmonary health of WwCF.
