N2  - Background: Lung inflammation in people with CF leads to onset of colonization with different bacteria, in particular Pseudomonas aeruginosa, but also Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Achromobacter spp, Bulkholderia cepacia complex, and nontuberculous mycobacteria, which are responsible for severe pulmonary exacerbations requiring intravenous antibiotics. The most recent modulator combination, elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor (ETI) should be available for 80% to 95% of people with CF in most countries. ETI improved lung function and reduced sweat chloride concentration within the first month after treatment initiation, often leading to drastic diminution of cough, sputum, exacerbation frequency and severity, and antibiotic use.We aimed to describe the evolution of bacterial lung colonization in French adults with CF treated with ETI. Methods: All 198 patients eligible for ETI regularly seen in our adult CF center of Lyon, France,were included.We selected patients who started ETI between January 2020 and November 2021 and conducted a retrospective study analyzing sputum cultures from January 2017 to November 2022 to obtain microbiological data for a period 2 years before and 1 year after ETI initiation. Data were collected on clinical status (FEV1 and BMI), sputum cultures (types of bacteria and CFU/mL), ETI or not, and associated treatments (nebulized, oral or intravenous antibiotics, physiotherapy). All data were obtained from medical files, and sputum was analyzed in our local microbiological laboratory. Our study obtained local ethical approval. Results: We collected a mean number of sputum cultures per patient per year of 4.25 before and 3.06 after initiation of ETI and at least one per year per patient. The number of species involved in chronic colonization per patient fell by 40% after initiation of ETI, and the number CFU/mL for each patient fell by 52%. The number of chronic P. aeruginosa–colonized subjects fell by 22%, and the number of P. aeruginosa CFU in sputum fell by 30%, as well as other species except Haemophilus influenza (Figure 1). We also observed a 40% drop in the use of inhaled antibiotics recommended for P. aeruginosa. Conclusions: Initiation of ETI fundamentally affects lung bacterial colonization. Our results should be interpreted with caution because of the lack of sputum productivity in patients treated with ETI, but we confirmed the decrease in colonization by usual CF bacteria, in particular P. aeruginosa. Our findings are of interest in considering discontinuation of antibiotics. [Figure presented]
