N2  - Background: Mucociliary clearance (MCC) is a primary airway defense mechanism against inhaled pathogens and particulate matter. It occurs through the complex interaction of the viscoelastic response of airway mucus to the metachronal beating of cilia lining the airways. Although exact mechanisms of this interaction remain the focus of much research, the mucus and the surface of the cilia contain large biopolymeric mucin glycoproteins that may interact with each other in biophysically important ways. Furthermore, the mucus polymer network is prone to experience several biophysical regimes depending on its concentration (dilute [c < c*], overlap, [c ≥ c*], entangled [c ≥ ce]). On the airway surface, mucins are entangled in granules before release and upon secretion must swell and solubilize for functional mucus to be produced. CF pathophysiology arises from airway mucus hyperconcentration and results in muco-stasis alongside recurrent, chronic inflammation and infection. Methods: Airway epithelial cell cultures from donors with and without CF were used to study our hypothesis that muco-stasis may be related to increased or prolonged biophysical or biochemical interaction of secreted mucus with cilia and within the mucus itself. MCCwas assessed by particle tracking, and rheological behavior was assessed via particle tracking microrheology (PTMR). Mucus obtained from endotracheal tubes was employed to study mucus swellability via serial dilution and evaluated via PTMR. Results: CF mucus was hindered in its ability to swell into free solution, which also muted the efficacy of attempts to restore MCC. In idealized mucus model systems, PTMR was used to demonstrate that mucus viscoelasticity is governed by mucin viscoelasticity. Pure mucin viscoelasticity across concentrations in the dilute, overlapping, and entangled regimes was homogeneous, whereas cell culture and native airway mucus viscoelasticity tended to bifurcate near the entanglement concentration (ce), with one subpopulation of measurements reporting viscoelasticity less than expected at ce and the other subpopulation reporting a viscoelasticity greater than expected. Conclusions: We hypothesize that this identification of more- and lessreadily swellable mucus types as a feature of physiology and pathophysiology may have important implications for the MCC apparatus, as well as other recently identified features of pathophysiology in CF such as mucus flakes.
