N2  - Background: The growth rate of bacteria has a profound effect on bacterial physiology and antibiotic sensitivity. It is also a fundamental factor determining whether infection persists in the face of host defenses that clear bacteria, but in vivo bacterial growth rates are extremely difficult to measure. As growth rate increases, the abundance of DNA from chromosomal regions close to the origin of replication increases more than i regions close to the replication terminus because rapidly growing cells initiate serial rounds of replication on nascent chromosomes that have not been completed, producing a higher origin-to-terminus ratio (OTR). Sequencing read depth can measure the OTR and has been used to measure in vivo growth rate, but this approach is challenging in CF sputum because even DNA from high-abundance pathogens such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a small fraction of total sample DNA. We developed a method to measure the OTR in CF sputum that exploits the ability of droplet digital polymerase chain reaction (ddPCR) to make high-sensitivity and absoluteabundance measurements of rare DNA targets. Methods: The method (droplet digital PCR measurement of OTR [ddOTR]) uses ddPCR probes designed to origin and terminus regions. Although origin sequences are conserved in P. aeruginosa, termini vary from strain to strain.We investigated this variation by growing 26 CF P. aeruginosa isolates to exponential phase and used Illumina sequencing to identify regions with lowest read depth (likely the strain-specific termini). We examined more than 480 available P. aeruginosa whole-genome sequences to identify conserved sequences around these termini regions and used this information to design 30 candidate ddPCR terminus probes. For reference, probes were mapped to a region spanning 2.8 MB (47%) of the reference PAO1 chromosome. P. aeruginosa strains of interest were then grown at slow and fast rates, and probes were tested to determine which best reflected strains’ growth rates. Results: We began testing ddOTR using PAO1. Average OTR was 3.04 (SEM = 0.07) for early-log cultures (doubling time (DT) = 29 min), 1.83 (SEM = 0.04) for late-log cultures (DT = 65 min), and 1.34 (SEM = 0.02) for stationary phase cultures (DT >600 min). Most CF clinical isolates exhibited somewhat lower OTR differences in log and stationary phase, but ddOTR accurately reflected the growth rate, and measurements were reproducible between replicates (variation averaged 0.04; SEM = 0.015). Clinical isolates’ slower in vitro log-phase growth accounted for some of the decrease in the OTR range. We then isolated DNA from nine CF sputum samples and used strain-optimal probe sets for ddOTR. In six of nine samples, dd-OTR values were high (growth rate estimated to be 30–60% of the maximum observed mid-log). In three of nine samples, dd-OTR rates were indistinguishable from values obtained in stationary phase cultures. Conclusions: ddOTR exploits empiric tests of a panel of ddPCR terminus probes to measure OTR without prior knowledge of strain-specific terminus regions. ddOTR accurately estimates growth rates of CF clinical isolates in culture. Its use of ddPCR enables analysis of complex samples like CF sputum in which DNA from the organisms of interest are rare. It could be used to identify pathogen growth rate changes in response to treatments such as CFTR modulators and antibiotics.
