N2  - Background: Aspergillus fumigatus is an inhaled mold found in the sputum of people with CF. It is implicated in infection and allergy in this population, yet the exact mechanisms of how it alters the human airway is not clear. It remains unknown whether A. fumigatus is favored in CF epithelia because of abnormal mucus related to CFTR dysfunction or from antecedent bacteria, like Pseudomonas aeruginosa, that alter the immunological response in favor of A. fumigatus germination. We evaluated A. fumigatus growth in differentiated primary human bronchial epithelial cells grown with and without mucus stimulation and isolated mucus derived from those cells, evaluated the role of mucus in A. fumigatus infection isolated from immunologic stimuli, and quantified growth using a unique A. fumigatus detection method. Methods: 16hBE14o–, CFBE41o–, and primary human bronchial epithelial (hBE) cells were grown in air-liquid interface (ALI) culture until terminally differentiated using standardized protocols. Cell culture inserts (Transwell inserts, Corning, Inc.) were apically exposed to pyocyanin, a Pseudomonas spp. virulence factor found to induce mucus secretion, or vehicle control for 24 hours. Cell culture inserts were then infected with dsRed expressing A. fumigatus conidia on the mucus-containing epithelium for 24 hours and imaged with a fluorescent microscope and ImageJ particle tracking. Primary hBE mucus was collected using saline wash in pyocyaninstimulated and nonstimulated conditions. This mucus was diluted (neat, 1:2, 1:4, 1:8) and infected with dsRed A. fumigatus conidia for 24 hours and analyzed via microplate reader. Pyocyanin was also tested in Dr. Leal's minimum inhibitory concentration protocol against A. fumigatus strains. Results: We were able to reproduce A. fumigatus growth in 16hBE, CF bronchial epithelial, primary CF and wt hBE, and primary CF and wt hBE– derived mucus in pyocyanin-exposed and nonexposed environments. Little to no A. fumigatus germinationwas seen in phosphate-buffered saline alone. In wt primary-cell ALI, A. fumigatus–infected Cell culture inserts exposed to pyocyanin had significantly higher hyphae particle area (0.17 square inches vs. 0.07 ± 0.027 square inches, p = 0.02) than unexposed wt cells. When combining particle area and intensity, this trended toward significance, showing higher values in wt cells exposed to pyocyanin (96.0 square inches vs. 27.9 ± 31.71 square inches × intensity, p = 0.098). A. fumigatus infection in pyocyanin-exposed primary CF hBE models resulted in complete monolayer destruction, suggesting severe cytotoxic response in the CF airway. In wt mucus washings, there was higher A. fumigatus red fluorescent protein (RFP) signal in mucus derived after pyocyanin exposure than with no exposure (140.0 relative fluorescent units (RFU) vs. 109.0 ± 20.92 [RFU], p = 0.23, in neat mucus), although the difference was not statistically significant. In CF mucus washings exposed to pyocyanin, therewere generally higher A. fumigatus signals at each dilution. In the final dilution, CF pyocyanin mucus had notably more A. fumigatus signal (107 RFU) than wt pyocyanin (40.5 RFU),wt non-pyocyanin (61.3 RFU), and phosphate-buffered saline only (47.7 RFU), although this was limited by available mucus for repeats and all controls. Furthermore, pyocyanin did not inhibit A. fumigatus germination on MIC testing. Conclusions: Germination of A. fumigatus was observed in infected CF and wt ALI isolated mucus from hBE cells and was quantified using RFP imaging. A. fumigatus can germinate in mucus and epithelial cell models with prior pyocyanin stimulation. We have demonstrated that pyocyanin exposure in wt epithelium promotes A. fumigatus germination as detected by higher area of RFP than in unexposed wt cells, suggesting that altered and increased mucin production promotes A. fumigatus germination in the human airway. Future studies will repeat these studies, evaluate mucus characteristics, investigate cytokine influences, and pursue direct Pseudomonas exposure in A. fumigatus infection.
