N2  - Background: CF is a genetic disorder that disrupts a cell's ability to transport chloride ions because mutations in the CFTR protein result in thick, acidic mucus secretions. These secretions can cause pulmonary exacerbations (PEx), which present as increased cough and sputum production requiring antibiotic therapy and increased pulmonary toilet [1]. Respiratory cultures are critical in guiding antimicrobial therapy because PEx caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa are associated with poorer lung function and quality of life and mortality [1]. Aerosolized anti-pseudomonal antibiotics, used for suppressive and eradication therapy, minimize systemic effects, improve lung function and quality of life in people with CF (PwCF), and reduce hospitalizations [1]. The literature focuses on the safety and efficacy of aerosolized tobramycin formulations, cycle duration, and initiation of suppressive therapy [2]. The optimal inhaled cycled therapy duration is unclear and is often according to provider preference. The purpose of this study is to determine if cycled suppressive therapy can be discontinued successfully in PwCF who have not cultured P. aeruginosa in 2 years. Methods: This is a single-center, prospective-intervention, retrospective chart review examining the relationship between discontinuation of inhaled antipseudomonal suppressive therapy in PwCF and incidence of P. aeruginosa culture regrowth and lung function. Patients were included if they met one of the criteria for the institution's inhaled antipseudomonal antibiotic de-escalation guideline: culture negative for P. aeruginosa in the past 2 years; on continuous alternating therapy (CAT) and stable on a CFTR modulator for 3 months and culture positive for P. aeruginosa without PEx, defined as greater airway clearance or initiation of antibiotics, in the last 6 months; or on CAT and stable on CFTR modulator for 3 months and culture negative for PSA within the last 6 months. Patients were excluded if they were being evaluated for or had received a lung transplant. Endpoints included age, CFTR modulator therapy, baseline and quarterly FEV1 and relevant bacterial organisms, date of antibiotic discontinuation, and incidence and management of PEx. Results: Thirty-five PwCF were included (average age 18, 83% Caucasian, 91% on elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor [ETI]). After inhaled antipseudomonal antibiotic discontinuation, FEV1 did not greatly differ and ranged on average from 81% to 91% with repeat cultures. Four patients (11%) experienced P. aeruginosa regrowth, with 50% experiencing a PEx. Of those that regrew P. aeruginosa, one received inhaled tobramycin eradication therapy resulting in culture clearance, and one remained on CAT (Table 1). 16 patients (46%) experienced a PEx, with 69% requiring oral antibiotics. Conclusions: Patients did not experience a decline in FEV1 after inhaled antibiotic discontinuation. Of the 11% that regrew P. aeruginosa, 50% experienced a PEx and were treated with oral antibiotics. Future directions include larger sample size and longer follow-up to evaluate the long-term implications of discontinuation of inhaled antibiotic therapy in PwCF. [Table presented]
