N2  - Background: Gallium citrate (AR-501) demonstrates antimicrobial activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, has a lowantimicrobial drug resistance profile, and protects against Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia in mouse models via a nebulized aerosol approach. In people with CF participating in a Phase 2a randomized, double-blind, doseascending, multicenter study of inhalational AR-501, we sought to identify blood and sputum markers of response to treatment that correlated with FEV1 and microbiological study endpoints. Methods: For the blood analysis, normalized transcript abundance of a 10- gene panel previously identified to predict reduction in CF pulmonary inflammation was evaluated in paired circulating leukocyte samples taken from subjects at the time of screening and then at a second post-dose timepoint on day 14. Expression of the panel was evaluated using Taqman chemistry by quantitative reverse-transcription polymerase chain reaction, with normalization to housekeeping gene PPIA. The following genes were analyzed: CD36, CD64, CD163, TLR2, PLXND1, HCA112, HPSE, ADAM9, CSPG2 and IL-32α. For the sputum samples, mRNA was isolated from paired sputum specimens at screening and post-dose at day 28. Bulk sequencing was performed on samples using the Ampliseq Human Transcriptome Panel. Within-person fold changes before and after treatment were compared for blood and sputum data using linear mixed models. Results: Twenty-four subjects’ blood leukocytes and 11 subjects’ sputum RNA were evaluated before and after inhaled study drug (placebo or AR- 501); 19 subjects received one of three drug doses, and five received placebo. In the blood analysis, a single gene, TLR2, changed significantly with treatment. Genes were analyzed in the context of clinical response. Study subjects were categorized into FEV1 responder categories (nonresponders had <4% improvement in ppFEV1; responders had ≥4% improvement). In subjects designated as responders, 7 of the 10-gene panel showed significant change in expression after treatment. All 7 genes are expressed in circulating monocytes. In non-responders, no genes showed significant reduction in expression. Given the small sample size and lowsputum RNA quality, therewere no statistically significant changes in sputum gene expression after treatment, although an exploratory ontologic over-representation analysis of genes with significant raw pvalues identified cellular transition metal ion homeostasis as the top GO term, with a 7-fold enrichment ratio (false discovery rate = 0.07). Conclusions: In subjects treated with AR-501 who experienced improvements in FEV1, a CF leukocyte gene signature identified FEV1 responders’ systemic inflammatory pathways and accurately categorized responders and non-responders. Although sputum biomarkers of response were not identified, probably because of the timing of sample collection and RNA quality, the trial highlights novel molecular approaches to identifying markers in clinical trials in the post-modulator era. Given the pressing need for primary endpoints beyond modest FEV1improvement and sputum microbiology (in subjects unable to produce), molecular markers are an important future strategy for understanding trial drug effects in human subjects. Acknowledgements: This work was supported by Aridis Pharmaceuticals.
