N2  - Background: Phage therapy is a century-old treatment approach that has recently been revisited as an alternative to traditional antimicrobials in cases of multidrug-resistant infections. Studies that have evaluated phage therapy to treat chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections in CF have found it to be as effective at reducing bacterial load and improving pulmonary function, but the effects of phages on mammalian cells at the mucosal surface of the airways has been overlooked because of their inability to replicate in nonbacterial cells. We hypothesized that, even though mammalian cells do not have the machinery to allow phage replication, airway epithelial cells detect bacteriophages as pathogenassociated molecular patterns that trigger immune responses. Methods: To test this hypothesis, we used a panel of four P. aeruginosa phages with a wide range of microbiological and physical characteristics. We first evaluated whether CF airway epithelial cells could undergo transcriptomic changes after phage treatment. Phage-treated CFBE41o– cells were sorted into phage-positive and -negative cells, followed by bulk RNA sequencing. To evaluate whether phages induce cytokine secretion, we measured an array of secreted inflammatory cytokines in phage-treated CFBE41o– cells using the Bio-Plex multiplex immunoassay platform. To determine which pattern recognition receptors the panel of phages engage, we screened human toll-like receptors (TLR) and the intracellular nucleic acid receptor cGAS-STING. We used confocal microscopy and assessed particle size and location to further understand interactions between phages and the airway epithelium. Results: We found that phages OMKO1 and lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-5 induce fewer transcriptomic changes than phages PSA04 and PSA34. Pathway analysis revealed that differentially expressed genes belong to immunity-related pathways, with pathogen induced cytokine storm signaling pathway having the highest z-score. The Bio-Plex assay showed that phages PSA04 and PSA34 induce IL-8 and TNFS13F secretion, whereas treatment with phages OMKO1 and LPS-5 does not.We discovered that all phages engage and induce signaling through extracellular TLRs, whereas phage PSA04 can also engage cGAS-STING. Nevertheless, TLR4, an extracellular TLR that binds LPS, protein aggregates, and many other danger-associated molecules, is the only tested receptor whose activation was significantly higher for phages PSA04 and PSA34. In our microscopy imaging, we observed that phages PSA04 and PSA34 display a higher percentage of larger aggregates on the apical side of airway epithelial cells than phages OMKO1 and LPS-5, although each phage shows different degrees of penetration into the airway epithelium. Conclusions: These data indicate that phage interactions with mammalian cells are not homogenous and depend highly on the specific phage. Phages that form larger aggregates correlate with TLR4 activation, larger transcriptional changes, and secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines. We anticipate that more-extensive evaluation of phage–airway interactions is needed before phage therapy becomes the standard of care for treatment of multidrug-resistant infections in CF. Acknowledgements: This work was supported by CFF (ZAMORA20F0, BOMBER21P0, ARMBRU22F5,ARMBRU19F0) and NIH (T32AI060525, T32HL129949, R33HL137077).
