N2  - Background: Mucus in the endocervix serves as a barrier to pathogen entry into the upper female reproductive tract and as a regulator of fertility through hormonal modulation of its biophysical properties throughout the menstrual cycle. This regulation is largely accomplished through hormonesensitive ion channels, including CFTR, in the cervical epithelium that hydrate and thin the mucus as ovulation approaches, opening the fertile window for sperm to enter the upper tract, and subsequently re-thickening the mucus after ovulation, closing the upper tract. Presently, 35% of women with CF report infertility after 1 year of trying to conceive. Accordingly, modulation of the biophysical barrier properties of the cervical mucus by thickening via concentrating or thinning via hydration is a critical target to improved family planning options and treatment of infertility, respectively, particularly in the era of highly effective modulator therapies (HEMTs). A critical shortcoming of reproductive studies on the effects of mucus biophysics on fertility stems from the lack of quantitative clinical endpoints for evaluating such changes. Current clinical standards for mucus appraisal (Insler score) use visual approximation of mucus viscosity. We sought to identify a combination of physiological quantitative metrics of fertility. Methods: We used cervical mucus obtained from rhesus macaques (Macaca mulatta) at necropsy. Based on known data about cervical mucus viscosity throughout the cycle, we hypothesized that greater mucus viscoelasticity would be a marker of less fertility and employed particle tracking microrheology (PTMR) to measure the rheological behavior of mucus samples across several serial dilutions. We also hypothesized that sperm motility would provide a functional readout of fertility status in the same samples assayed by PTMR using an identical platform and workflow. Results: Mucus viscoelasticity and sperm velocity displayed concentrationdependent behavior; viscoelasticity increased as concentration increased, and sperm velocity decreased. Viscoelasticity and sperm velocity were strongly negatively correlated (p < 0.001), indicating that direct assessment of cervical mucus viscosity may be a viable way to estimate fertility status quantitatively and serve as a biomarker of contraceptive efficacy. Conclusions: Sperm tracking, particularly PTMR, provides a quantitative method of assessing the fertility status of cervical mucus. Its use in cell culture models and mucus from women with CF may provide crucial insights into the effects of CF pathophysiology and HEMTs and assist with family planning in this new era of CF care. Acknowledgements: This workwas supported by CFF Grant MARKOV22IO and Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation Grant 23-0501.
