N2  - Background: Although CFTR modulators are available to most people with CF in the United States, access remains limited for individuals harboring rare CFTR variants. Previous work by our group and collaborators demonstrated robust elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor (ETI)-induced functional improvement in a rare CFTR variant, G27V (c.80G >T, p.Gly27Val), using in vitro model systems [1]. To validate these data, we enrolled two subjects homozygous for G27V for N-of-1 clinical phenotyping on ETI therapy. Methods: All studies were approved by the Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center Institutional Review Board. Prior data were used to secure off-label drug coverage for ETI. Clinical data were obtained for 6 weeks before treatment and through a 12-week treatment period. Spirometry, sputum and throat cultures, and weight were obtained biweekly, and imaging, lung clearance index, and sweat chloride were obtained before starting therapy and at the end of 12 weeks. Results: Two boys homozygous for G27V CFTR were enrolled. Bothwere on standard-of-care therapies at baseline. Subject 1, aged 12, had a history of left upper lobectomy because of congenital lobar emphysema, as well as mild bronchiectasis and mild pancreatic insufficiency (iron 121 μg/g). Subject 2, aged 10, had mild air trapping on chest CT and mild pancreatic insufficiency (iron 248 μg/g). Subject 1 had to stop ETI briefly after initiation because of rash but was able to restart and escalate back to full dose over 1 month (values below were obtained on full dosing unless otherwise indicated). Both subjects reported less cough, sputum production, and dyspnea and improvement in lung function, CFTR activity, nutrition, and culture growth during treatment with ETI (Table 1). This included an average improvement in ppFEV1 of 9%, although this was not mirrored in improvement in ventilation defect percentage (measured by hyperpolarized 129Xe MRI) or lung clearance index2.5, both of which were normal at baseline. Sweat chloride normalized in both subjects, and total chloride conductance measured on nasal potential difference became more polarized. Both subjects gained weight on therapy, and vitamin D levels improved. During the baseline period, subject 1 grew Pseudomonas aeruginosa on all four cultures, and Staphylococcus aureus on a single culture. He grewP. aeruginosa on one of two cultures during 50% ETI dosing and had only sterile cultures (5 total) during the full-dose treatment period. Subject 2 grewS. aureus on two of four baseline cultures and on the first two of eight treatment cultures; the final six cultures were all sterile (Figure 1). Conclusions: These paired N-of-1 studies confirm previous in vitro data demonstrating functional rescue of G27V CFTR with ETI therapy. Both subjects noted robust improvement in clinical symptoms, lung function, nutritional outcomes, respiratory culture growth, and in vivo CFTR functional assays. Acknowledgements: This workwas supported by CFF (BREWIN20Y2-OUT and NAREN19R0) and NIH/NHLBI (HL144825-01A1). [Table presented]
