N2  - Background: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an opportunistic gram-negative pathogen that colonizes the lungs of people with CF, resulting in significant morbidity. The prevalence of multidrug-resistant (MDR) P. aeruginosa isolates has increased, precipitating an urgent need for novel antibiotic therapies. To address this need, we synthesized the silver salt of ibuprofen (AgIBU), which combines the broad-spectrum and well-described antimicrobial activity of silverwith the anti-inflammatory activity of ibuprofen. Although AgIBU has demonstrated robust antimicrobial activity in vitro, its hydrophobic nature prompted us to explore delivery via lipid droplets composed of a phospholipid exterior and a triglyceride core, materials that have both been FDA-approved for use in other applications. Methods: We confirmed the structure of free AgIBU using 1HNMR and mass spectrometry and then assessed the antimicrobial activity and resistance profile of free AgIBU in vitro. We determined the minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)90 and minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC)90, the minimum concentrations at which free AgIBU inhibits or kills 90% of the 31 laboratory and clinical CF isolates of P. aeruginosa tested. Next, we determined the time-kill kinetic activity of AgIBU against selected isolates and then characterized the size, zeta potential, loading, release, and stability of lipid droplets loaded with AgIBU. Finally, we evaluated the antimicrobial activity of AgIBU-loaded lipid droplets via MIC, MBC, and time-kill kinetic assays. Results:We demonstrated that free AgIBU has bactericidal activity against all laboratory and clinical MDR P. aeruginosa isolates tested, with an MIC90 of 2 μg/mL and an MBC90 of 4 μg/mL. The AgIBU MIC values for P. aeruginosa did not exhibit significant changes upon evolutionary selection analysis, indicating a low probability of resistance development. The viability of human dermal fibroblasts was not affected by treatment with AgIBUloaded lipid droplets; rather a dose-dependent increase in cell proliferation was observed with treatment. The MIC90 of AgIBU-loaded lipid droplets against resistant laboratory and clinical isolates of P. aeruginosa was 4 μg/ mL, and bactericidal activity was observed by 4 hours in all isolates. Conclusions: This nanoparticle lipid droplet formulation of AgIBU is nontoxic to human cells but bactericidal against CF isolates of MDR P. aeruginosa and, in an evolutionary selection assay, demonstrated a low probability of resistance development. Future studies will focus on development of targeted AgIBU-loaded lipid droplets and confirmation of their antimicrobial activity in vivo. We previously reported the antimicrobial efficacy of hetero-multivalent targeted liposomes functionalized with host cell glycans loaded with ciprofloxacin and demonstrated that treatment of mice infected with P. aeruginosa (PAO1; ciprofloxacin S) with targeted ciprofloxacin-loaded liposomes containing one-tenth the standard dosing resulted in local accumulation of liposomes at the site of infection and longer survival times than in those treated with free ciprofloxacin. Our novel antibiotic, AgIBU, delivered via hetero-multivalent targeted lipid droplets, may improve outcomes in people with CF infected with MDR P. aeruginosa.
