N2  - Background: Nontuberculousmycobacteria (NTM) do not cause disease in healthy people but can cause pulmonary infections (PNTMs) in people with chronic airway diseases, including people with CF (PwCF). PNTMs, caused primarily by Mycobacterium avium and M. abscessus, remain a serious comorbidity in PwCF despite CFTR modulator therapy, yet it is poorly understood why PwCF are at risk for PNTM. Host defense against NTM is thought to require macrophages, but receptors that recognize NTM and intracellular mechanisms required for NTM destruction are not well defined. Dectin-1, a c-type lectin receptor originally identified as a receptor for fungi, has been shown to be a pathogen recognition receptor (PRR) for M. tuberculosis and NTM. Studies demonstrate that Dectin-1 ligation by M. abscessus triggers cytokine release by some macrophage populations. One study found that Dectin-1 is required for macrophage phagocytosis of M. abscessus. We sought to better characterize the role of Dectin-1 in host defense against M. abscessus. Methods: One week after airway infection with M. abscessus strain ATCC 19977, wild-type (wt) and dectin-1 knockout (KO) mice were sacrificed; lungs and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid were plated for bacterial CFUs. BAL cells were quantified using microscopy, and BAL cells and lung homogenate cells were analyzed using flowcytometry to identify immune populations. Human monocyte-derived macrophages (MDMs) were treated with Dectin-1 blocking antibody, control antibody, or laminarin (a glucan known to block Dectin-1 and other PRRs) and exposed to fluorescently labeled M. abscessus or zymosan particles (a fungal antigen). Uptake of M. abscessus or zymosan was quantified by determining intracellular CFUs or using flow cytometry or fluorescence microscopy. Results: Dectin-1 KO mice demonstrated no differences in control of M. abscessus infection from wt mice, and no differences were detected in BAL immune cells numbers or proportions of lung homogenate immune cell populations between wt and KO mice. The only difference detected between mouse strains was lack of Dectin-1 protein on cells from KO mice. Flow cytometry showed that human MDMs expressed Dectin-1. Dectin-1 blocking antibody did not alter ingestion of M. abscessus by MDMs, measured according to intracellular CFU counts, or detection of intracellular green flourescence protein-M. abscessus using flow cytometry or microscopy. Dectin-1 blocking antibody reducedMDMingestion of AF488- zymosan particles (p = 0.001). MDMs pretreated with laminarin ingested less M. abscessus (p = 0.0003) and zymosan (p = 0.002). Conclusions: Our data show that Dectin-1 is not required for murine host defense against pulmonary infection with M. abscessus or for ingestion of M. abscessus by human MDMs. Laminarin decreased MDM uptake of M. abscessus, as previous work has shown, indicating that additional receptors blocked by laminarin participate in recognition and ingestion of M. abscessus by macrophages. These data support two models: there are redundant PRRs used by macrophages to phagocytose M. abscessus, and Dectin-1 is sufficient but not necessary for uptake, and laminarin blocks a different PRR that is essential for macrophage M. abscessus ingestion. We are investigating these models. Better understanding of immune mechanisms that are essential for host defense against M. abscessus will help develop new diagnostics to predict susceptibility to pulmonary NTM and new therapeutics for preventing those most at risk. Acknowledgements: CFF grant HISERT19R3.
