N2  - Background: Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) is an attenuated strain of Mycobacterium Bovis that is often used as adjuvant treatment for non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer. BCG instillations are usually characterized by mild/moderate side-effects (most commonly cystitis, fever and haematuria) [1]. Although rarely reported lung involvement by BCG is possible and usually due to systemic dissemination of the bacillus or his antigens. Methods/Case report: This is the case of a 68 years old man that presented in February 2023 with worsening dyspnea on exertion. He is an ex-smoker (30 pack/year, ceased 3 years ago) and was diagnosed with bladder urotelial carcinoma in 2020. The neoplasm was treated surgically and with adjuvant BCG bladder instillations in the following months. All the previous follow-up visits and imaging showed no signs of recurrent urological disease. The patient started complaining of dry cough since quitting smoking and mild dyspnea on exertion - worsening in the last year. Chest auscultation revealed fine inspiratory crackles bilaterally and his vital signs were within normal limits. A high resolution computed tomography (HRCT) scan revealed diffused subpleural reticulation and subtle centrilobular ground glass micronodules in the upper lobes (Fig 1). Lung function tests showed: Forced expiratory volume in the first second (FEV1) 3.18 L (89%), forced vital capacity (FVC) 3.77L (80%), total lung capacity (TLC) 5.48L (71%), diffusing capacity of the lungs for CO 56%. His laboratory profile showed no alteration in the white blood cell count or formula and normal kidney/liver function. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C reactive protein levels resulted within normal limits. He tested negative for rheumatoid factor, anti-nuclear, anti-extractable nuclear antigen and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies. Lung cryobiopsy and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was then performed. BAL cultures showed no viral, bacterial, mycobacterial or fungal evidence in both PCR tests and cultural exams. BAL cytology showed lymphocytosis (42%) with a CD4/CD8 ratio of 6.4. Histological samples showed the presence of patchy fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, reactive pneumocyte hyperplasia and epithelioid non necrotizing granulomas (Fig.1). The final diagnosis was of histological usual interstitial pneumonia and fibrosing hypersensitivity pneumonia (fHP) due to BCG instillation. The patient ceased BCG instillation and corticosteroid treatment has been initiated. Should the patient show signs of function loss, radiologic progression and/ or worsening respiratory symptoms, anti-fibrotic treatment would be evaluated. Conclusions: BCG instillation can be considered a rare but possible risk factor for fHP that can manifest as progressive lung fibrosis causing severe respiratory function loss. (Figure Presented).
