N2  - Background: Marfan syndrome (MFS) is an autosomal dominant connective tissue disease due to a gene mutation coding for the glycoprotein fibrillin-1(FBN1 gene) causing multi-organ alterations. Among them, cardiovascular, skeletal and ocular damage are common. Only in 10% of cases, the disease can induce a variety of effects on the respiratory system, mainly spontaneous pneumothorax and emphysema. Rarely MFS is associated with bronchiectasis and asthma. Bronchiectasis can develop due to either an intrinsic defect in the bronchial wall (in fibrillin degeneration1), or a chest wall deformity (pectus-excavatum, pectus-carinatum) that promotes the onset of recurrent lung infections. Methods/Case report: A 67-year-old woman with history of Marfan syndrome, bilateral crystalline lens dislocation leading to progressive loss of visual acuity, glaucoma; recurrent bronchitis and pneumonia. She referred to our ambulatory for productive cough and dyspnoea. Physical examination did not reveal pathological noises. Blood tests showed increased indices of inflammation and eosinophilia (E 240/mcl). Lung function tests revealed a severe obstructive deficit with hyperinflation and air trapping (Tiffenau index 52%, Forced expiratory volume in 1 sec (FEV1) 1.39 l (48%), Forced Vital Capacity 2.70 l (79%), reversible with salbutamol 400 mcg (FEV1 +220 ml, +16%). Lung HRCT revealed: “multiple non-calcified nodules in the left apical site attendance; multiple areas of tree-in-bud sign; diffuse bronchiectasis localized mainly in the medial segment of the middle lobe and at the lower lingula level; accentuation of the interstitial pattern; bilateral apical fibrotic remnants; small amount of pericardial effusion”. Cardiac ultrasound demonstrated: “normal left ventricular systolic function; mild-to-moderate mitral regurgitation; mild aortic regurgitation; minimal pericardial effusion.” The specific IgE testing for perennial and seasonal allergens was negative. Microbiological examination of sputum isolated oxacillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. Bronchiectasis with eosinophilic inflammatory endotype and asthma was diagnosed, thus inhaled ICS/LABA therapy was initiated. To improve coughing and breathlessness, the patient underwent respiratory physiotherapy sessions for bronchial unblocking with the aid of a low-frequency vibration device (Physioassist - Simeox, Medicair) and continued inhalation therapy with benefit. At 1- month follow up an improvement in respiratory function (FEV1+ 350 ml) and symptoms was observed. Conclusions: MFS can induce lung parenchymal damage and airway remodelling, with an increased risk of obstructive ventilatory disorder. Eosinophilic endotype of bronchiectasis and asthma can overlap. In our case, both etiologies concurred to the obstructive pulmonary disease of the patient. Although the association between MFS and lung disease is uncommon, further studies are needed to better understand respiratory complications in patients with MFS.
