N2  - Background: Fusobacterium necrophorum (FN) is a commensal, anaerobic, Gram-negative, known to cause Lemierre's syndrome. FN infections are associated with a wide clinical spectrum, ranging from mild to severe systemic illness. Case report: We report a case of an immunocompetent 18-years-old female who was admitted to our hospital with fever (38.4°C), dyspnea, cervical pain, sore throat, and dry cough. Before admission she had taken Clarithromycin for 3 days, with no benefit. Initial laboratory values revealed an inflammatory syndrome with C-Reactive Protein 223 mg/L (< 6 mg/L), leukocytosis (25,000 ×109/L) with neutrophilia, Platelets 91 x109/L, Procalcitonin (PCT) > 100 ng/mL (<0.5 ng/mL). The chest X-ray was normal. Empiric therapy with ceftriaxone was started with no benefit and persistence of PCT > 100 ng/mL. Group A Streptococcus, Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis and all respiratory viruses were excluded. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) VCA-IgM and Serum EBV DNA quantification were positive. On the second day, in addition to physical examination, we performed (LUS) using an ultrasound scanner (MyLab X6, Esaote, GE, Italy) with a 3.5 MHZ convex probe in transversal scan and each hemithorax was divided into seven regions: It documented bilateral B-lines and pleural effusion of 9 mm with bilateral consolidations which appeared isoechoic with the liver. Progressive respiratory failure, persistent high fever, and a follow-up LUS showing increased pulmonary consolidations and bilateral effusion, led us to modify the antibiotic regimen, with escalation to piperacillin/ tazobactam every six hours. 72 hours after admission, the first blood cultures resulted positive for anaerobic Gram- negative bacilli and only after 24 hours FN was identified. There was an increase in pulmonary consolidations and bilateral pleural effusion at follow-up LUS. At the same time, we performed computed tomography (CT) which confirmed bilateral pneumonia complicated by parapneumonic pleural effusion without characteristics of empyema at thoracentesis, which evolved into pulmonary cavitations. Doppler examination and neck-CT were negative for jugular venous thrombosis. She continued antibiotic therapy with progressive reduction of leukocytosis and normalization of PCT after 6 days. Clinical conditions improved with resolution of fever and pleural pain. A prolonged course of antibiotic therapy was carried with a successful recovery. Conclusions: The early use of inflammatory biomarkers remains helpful for initial management but an approach to the patient through Eco Bedside allows a timely stratification of the evolution and a prompt curative adjustment. (Table Presented).
