N2  - Background: Aspergillus Flavus is an opportunistic human pathogen causing aspergillosis diseases with incidence increasing in the immunocompromised population 1. In this paper, we report a case of Aspergillus Flavus in a patient with monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS). To the best of our knowledge, aspergillosis caused by Aspergillus Flavus has not been previously reported in association with MGUS. Case report: 71-year-old former smoker woman (24-packyear) with occupational exposure to asbestos was admitted with severe dyspnoea to our hospital. She had medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, arterial hypertension, moderate aortic stenosis and moderate mitral stenosis-insufficiency, hypothyroidism and monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance. On admission atrial fibrillation with high ventricular response and heart failure were observed. In addition she had increase of inflammatory indices and metabolic alkalosis to arterial blood gas. Her physical examination revealed scattered rhonchi and wheezing. She complained with us for persistent cough, dyspnoea at rest, chest discomfort. She performed contrasted CT scan showing centrilobular nodules in the lower lobes, 5 mm nodule in the left upper lobe and 6-7 mm nodule in the right upper lobe (figure a-b). PET imaging with [F- 18] FDG detected uptake to the upper lobe of the left lung. Dense and stratified yellowish mucopurulent secretions on hyperemic bronchial mucosa were observed at flexible bronchoscopy. So she started beta-blocker and anticoagulant therapy with improvement of the ventricular response and antibiotics and steroid therapy but without improvement of respiratory symptoms. Spirometry showed irreversible moderate bronchial obstruction with necessity of triple bronchodilator therapy. The presence of Aspergillus Flavus was detected on bronchoaspirate, so she started therapy with voriconazole. Questioning in depth the patient, we found out that for some months she had been working in the basement of the house where there were moisture and mold. Three days after the beginning of the therapy, the patient complained with nausea, vomiting, visual hallucinations and asthenia with functional involvement of the muscles of the lower limbs. Therefore she stopped voriconazole and started isavuconazole for one month. At the end of the therapy, the patient reported a significant improvement in dyspnoea. Follow-up chest CT scan showed reduction of the centrilobular nodular component. Conclusions: Patients with monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance have an increased risk of infections due to the immunosuppressive effects of disease 2. The underlying factors in a weakened immune response in MGUS are as yet not fully understood therefore these patients need close monitoring and living in a healthy environment. (Figure Presented).
