N2  - Background: A possible association between mycophenolate mofetil (MMF) and bronchiectasis has been reported in renal transplant patients receiving MMF, due to iatrogenic low IgG levels. Hypogammaglobulinemia and subsequent chronic pulmonary infections concur to bronchiectasis development, resulting in permanent dilatation and destruction of bronchial walls, copious sputum production, secondary infections and deterioration of lung function and quality of life. Case report: A 68-year-old woman underwent kidney transplantation after end-stage renal disease (ESRD) due to chronic glomerulonephritis. Immunosuppression regimen consisted of MMF and tacrolimus. She had not history of lung diseases before transplantation as confirmed by a lung CT scan. After three years she was referred to our ambulatory for worsening dyspnea, cough and recurrent respiratory infections, mainly due to Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. Physical examination revealed bilateral crackles and wheezes. At laboratory test low IgG levels was detected (5.7 g/L, normal values 7.0-16.0). Arterial blood gases showed respiratory failure, so oxygen therapy was administered. Pulmonary function tests revealed moderate obstructive deficit with hyperinflation and air trapping, not reversible with salbutamol. Lung CT scan detected “multiple and diffuse bronchiectasis with mucus plug and multiple areas of tree-in-bud sign”. A broncoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed, showing growth of Aspergillus fumigatus and a Galactomannan index of 2.3. Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in patients with bronchiectasis and iatrogenic immunodeficiency was diagnosed. Patient was hospitalized and treated with voriconazole. Clinical condition improved and at 12-weeks follow up a new lung CT scan showed a clear decrease of tree-in-bud pattern. Antifungal treatment was stopped and patient started a bronchodilator therapy with umeclidinium/vilaterol 55/22 mcg once daily. To prevent future exacerbations and improve her quality of life, she underwent regular respiratory physiotherapy sessions for bronchial unblocking with a Temporary Positive Expiratory Pressure (TPEP) device. Continue replacement immunoglobulin therapy has yet to be considered. Conclusions: Even if an association between congenital immunodeficiency and bronchiectasis is recognized worldwide, the etiology of bronchiectasis in patient undergoing renal transplantation is less clear and limited data are available. This case suggests that bronchiectasis could be considered a rare but significant complication of renal transplantation in patient treated with MMF, mostly because of induced hypogammaglobulinemia. Regular pulmonary screening tests should be performed in all transplanted patients, even in case of negative medical history for lung diseases. (Figure Presented).
