N2  - Background: Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are ubiquitous microorganisms living freely in the environment. In a small percentage of cases, with predisposing conditions, they may cause a wide range of lung diseases. Objective: According to current guidelines, NTM treatment should be started considering of patients' symptoms, immunological status, or radiological severity. Our study wants to compare clinical practice with current guidelines. Methods: We described in detail a population composed of patients with confirmed isolation of NTM in the last year, coming from a single study center (IRCCS San Matteo Respiratory Unit, Pavia). We collected data from 41 patients (31 females and 10 males; mean age 68); the vast majority was diagnosed through bronchoalveolar lavage. M. avium was the most isolated microorganism (23 patients). High-resolution computed tomography of the lungs detected bronchiectasis and/or micronodules and/or tree in bud in 17 patients. Cavitated lesions were observed in 9 cases. Results: In 24 cases complaining of symptoms (dyspnea, cough, sputum production, loss of weight, frequent exacerbations, and haemoptysis) we started target antibiotic therapy. On the other hand, 2 asymptomatic patients were treated because of immunosuppression status. In 4 symptomatic patients another concomitant bacterial isolation was observed and treated with a target antibiotic therapy. 11 immunocompetent and asymptomatic patients were not treated. Conclusions: Our results highlight a reasonable adherence to the current guidelines in terms of management and treatment of NTM. However, collection of datasets from different parts of the world is mandatory to better define future directions concerning with patient management affected by this insidious disease.
