N2  - Case Report: History: A 6-year-old female (Pt1) and a 26-year-old female (Pt2) with cystic fibrosis (CF), respiratory cultures positive for multi-drug resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and advanced lung disease were treated with bacteriophage (phage) directed against P. aeruginosa at Children's National Hospital in Washington, DC. Sputum samples were obtained and were sent to the Center for Phage Biology & Therapy at Yale. Multiple strains of P. aeruginosa were isolated from respiratory cultures and were tested against a library of bacteriophage known to have activity against P. aeruginosa. After receiving an investigational new drug approval from the FDA and local IRB approval, Pt1 was treated in the intensive care unit and received inhaled phage INF BID x 7 days in conjunction with IV meropenem and IV colistin. Pt2's treatment was initiated in the hospital where she received phage INF BID x 2 days, and then was discharged home to complete a total 7 days of INF Qday followed by phage pB Qday x 7 days. These treatments were given in conjunction with IV meropenem. Review of symptoms and physical exam findings: Prior to phage administration, Pt1 was on continuous BiPAP 18/9 with 40% FiO2. After phage administration, Pt1 weaned to high flow nasal cannula 10-12L during the day and BiPAP 18/9 FiO2 40% at night, sputum production decreased and Pt1 showed improved energy. Prior to phage administration, Pt2 was on 2L nasal cannula O2 overnight, had significant dyspnea with exertion with severe lung disease (ppFEV1 19%). After one week of phage treatment, Pt2 reported improved energy, less dyspnea, and decreased productive cough. After two weeks of treatment, Pt2 reported continued dyspnea and productive cough. Repeat PFTs showed a ppFEV1 of 20%. Neither patient experienced bronchospasm while receiving the inhaled phage therapy. Laboratory findings: At baseline, Pt1 had a WBC 6.35, Hgb 10.5, Plt 274, Cr 0.2, ALT 50, and AST 34. Her LFTs stable throughout the treatment course and two months of follow-up. At three months post treatment, Pt1 had an increased AST to 134. At baseline, Pt2 had a WBC of 12.7, Hgb 10.2, Plt 530, Cr 0.49, ALT 24, and AST 15. Her LFTs remained stable throughout the treatment course and for three months of follow-up. Outcomes: Pt1 was listed for lung transplant, but a match was not found, and she passed away from respiratory failure. One week after completing phage therapy, Pt2 was found to have a left apical pneumothorax and required chest tube placement. This was considered a Grade 3 adverse event. Pt2 was listed and received a lung transplant 5 months after completion of treatment. Discussion: Both patients had symptomatic improvement while receiving phage INF and tolerated treatment without bronchospasm or an increase in LFTs during the treatment course. Clinical trials are needed to fully establish safety and efficacy of bacteriophage therapy in persons with CF.
