N2  - Background: Children and young people (CYP) with neurodisabilities and complex respiratory disease are considered 'orphan respiratory diseases'. Respiratory illness remains a significant cause of morbidity and mortality for these children, resulting in frequent hospital admissions and poor quality of life. The importance of physiotherapist's prescribed airway clearance techniques (ACT) has been documented, with recommendations for daily use of adjuncts to reduce respiratory morbidity. Aim: To review paediatric respiratory physiotherapy practice and microbiology management in CYP with neurodisabilities and complex respiratory diseases in Edinburgh, East and Mid Lothian. Objectives: Identify CYP with neurodisabilities and complex respiratory disease receiving physiotherapy care and physiotherapeutic interventions used. Investigate bacteria isolated and treatment received. Methods and Inclusion Criteria: Retrospective audit of 50 CYPs receiving regular community respiratory physiotherapy input (2011-2021). Primary comorbidities include cerebral palsy (GMFCS 4 or 5), DMD, spinal muscular atrophy, undiagnosed neurological conditions, Trisomy-21 and cardiac conditions. Results: Fifty (42%F) patients were identified. All received physiotherapy input. Types of ACT included: positive expiratory pressure (PEP) devices (29%), autogenic drainage belt (23%), manual techniques (22%) and cough assist (5%). Many treatments were used in conjunction. Documented physiotherapy annual review occurred in 58%, however children were reviewed at least twice a year, with some children being reviewed weekly. And 329 +ve respiratory throat swabs were received for bacterial analysis. The 50% of samples (164 of 329) isolated Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) on one or more occasion. Repeat isolates were recorded in 109 out of 329 swabs. And 32% (8 of 25) of patients with chronic PA were on nebulised therapy (colistimethate (7 of 25) and tobramycin (1 of 25); 72% (36 of 50) isolated Staphylococcus aureus, 44% (22 of 50) Haemophillus influenza on one or more occasion. And 32% (16 of 50) were receiving prophylactic antibiotics (azithromycin). Discussion: Physiotherapy input in this population is high with care throughout childhood. Most patients receive PEP in conjunction with other ACT techniques. Whilst all patients were reviewed regularly, documented annual review was lacking. Despite frequent microbiology surveillance, only a small percentage of those with isolated PA received nebulised treatment and less than half received prophylactic antibiotics. Conclusions: A wide variety of evidence-based ACTused in physiotherapy to treat children with neurodisabilities and complex respiratory care exists. Bacterial isolation, such as P. aeruginosa, are highly prevalent in CYP with orphan lung diseases. Increased physiotherapy annual reviews could potentially guide treatment options more thoroughly. There remains a lack of evidence regarding the microbiological prevalence, and management for this patient group compared to CYP with bronchiectasis in need for further research..
