N2  - Objectives Colonization of the respiratory tract with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) or Staphylococcus aureus (SA) causes progressive health decline in people with Cystic Fibrosis (pwCF). Therefore, it is of enormous interest to detect those pathogens rapidly and reliably. Here, we evaluate whether 16S rRNA gene sequencing (16S) can compete conventional microbiology (CM) in detecting Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus in respiratory samples. Methods We analyzed 34 sputum samples and 109 cough swabs of 32 pwCF, collected between September 2020 and May 2022. All samples had CM screening for pathogens and 16S for microbiome characterization. Results The positivity rate for Pseudomonas in sputum was 53% for both 16S and CM. The sensitivity for Pseudomonas detection by 16S sequencing when compared to CM as the gold standard was 77,8% (specificity 75%). The positivity rate for Staphylococcus in sputum was 62% for CM and 60% for 16S rRNA gene sequencing. Compared to CM, sensitivity for Staphylococcus detection by 16S in sputum was 71,4% (specificity 61,5%). Both methods performed equally in detecting Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus in sputum (Mc Nemar: p = 1). The positivity rate for Pseudomonas in cough swabs was 18% for 16S and 17% for CM. The sensitivity for Pseudomonas detection by 16S in cough swabs was 63,2% (specificity 91,1%). The positivity rate for Staphylococcus in cough swabs was 59% for CM and only 17% for 16S. Sensitivity for Staphylococcus detection by 16S sequencing in cough swabs when compared to CM was 18,8% (specificity 84,4%). Both methods performed equally in detecting Pseudomonas in cough swabs, but were significantly different for Staphylococcus detection (Mc Nemar: p = 1.014e-08). Conclusion 16S performs equally to CM for Staphylococcus and Pseudomonas detection in sputum, as well as for Pseudomonas detection in cough swabs.
