N2  - Objectives Regular monitoring of airway microbiology is key in CF management. Standards recommend prompt sample processing. Increasingly, care is delivered remotely with limited evidence for sputum sample quality following postal delivery and absent data for postal acid-fast bacilli (AFB) and cough samples. We evaluated airway samples sent by post in CF patients against those processed the same day. Methods Prospective samples were collected from adult CF outpatients (April 2021 - December 2022). Sputum samples obtained were divided between two specimen containers and sent for standard and AFB culture; alternatively two cough swabs were obtained simultaneously. One sample was processed the same day; the second sample posted via Royal Mail from the patient’s home on the same day and processed on arrival at the laboratory. Results 41 patients (from 43) returned paired samples (21 sputum, 20 cough). 4 patients were included twice. 13 samples were taken during a pulmonary exacerbation. At least one bacteria was cultured from one or both samples in 17 (81%) sputum and 7 (35%) cough swabs. Congruency of culture results between same-day and postal samples cultures were 52% (sputum) and 70% (cough). Postal samples missed 7 (17%) significant infections. 10 infections, including P.aeruginosa (PsA) on 7 (17%) occasions, were cultured from postal but not same-day samples. Results from 20/23 AFB samples were available; 2 patients cultured M.abscessus in both samples. The median (IQR) time for postal samples to lab processing was 4 (2, 6.5) days. Conclusion Postal sputum samples offer an easy method for remote clinical management, but we caution limitations in regards to all culture results. Small numbers and negative cultures limit reassurance of AFB stability and cough sampling with delayed processing. More data is required to fully understand the use of postal sampling in the remote delivery of CF care and explore the positive PsA cultures following delayed processing.
