N2  - Background Respiratory sampling all people with cystic fibrosis (PwCF) is part of national guidelines to monitor for new pathogens and provide early treatment on the basis of long term outcomes. Defining exacerbations in PwCF is difficult but consensus suggests that a decrease of 10% in FEV1, a rise in c-reactive protein (CRP) and new symptoms of increased cough, sputum load and chest pain as well as a decrease in exercise tolerance can support the diagnosis of an acute exacerbation. Aim 1. Are PwCF exacerbating when a new pathogen is identified on respiratory samples? 2. Are PwCF receiving modulator therapy displaying different symptoms to those not receiving a modulator? Methods Retrospective review of clinical documents in 2022 on the day of the positive respiratory sample being obtained from PwCF. A 2-tailed t-test was used to compared the modulator and non-modulator eligible PwCF groups. Results 37 patients grew a new pathogen on respiratory sampling (S. aureus = 17; P. aeruginosa = 7; H. influenzae = 6; S. maltophilia = 3; MRSA = 2; A. xylosoxidans = 2). Discussion Respiratory sampling remains important in our clinic for early identification of new pathogens as PwCF did not always exhibit signs and symptoms of an exacerbation when culturing a new pathogen on respiratory sampling. There was no statistically significant difference between the changes seen in PwCF that receive a modulator or not for objective markers – absolute or percentage change in FEV1 or CRP. [Formula presented]
