N2  - Objectives CTFR Modulator therapy has significantly altered the management of cystic fibrosis. This study aimed to review the effect of highly effective CTFR modulator therapy (HEMT) on sputum sample production and bacterial growth rates within people with CF (PwCF) attending the All Wales Adult CF Centre (AWACFC). Methods Electronic records of PwCF prescribed HEMT (n = 145) were used to assess bacterial growth in culture one year prior and one year post HEMT initiation. PwcF with unknown HEMT start dates, insufficient pre HEMT samples, poor HEMT compliance or who died within the study period were excluded from comparison. Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa), Staphylococcus aureus (S.aureus), Mycobacterium abscessus (M.abs) and Burkholderia cepacia Complex (BCC) isolates were compared. Comparison was made between pre and post HEMT samples with significance assessed by Students’ T test. Results From the 85 PwCF included, 283 microbial samples were obtained in the year prior to HEMT, reducing to 244 samples in the year post (p < 0.01). Cough swabs accounted for 21% (n = 60) of the pre-HEMT samples, rising to 42% (n = 103) of the post HEMT samples. Significant reductions in culture positive isolates for Pa (54% (n = 154) vs 27% (n = 67) p < 0.01) and S.aureus (23% (n = 64) vs 17% (n = 34) p = 0.01) were evident. Fewer positive BCC (6% (n = 16) vs 1% (n = 2) p = 0.32) and M.abs (2% (n = 6) vs 1% (n = 3) (p = 0.07) isolates were recorded in the year post HEMT. Conclusions In this adult cohort, within 12 months, HEMT resulted in significant reductions in the number of microbial samples, with a rise in cough swab sampling. The number of positive isolates reduced, with the most significant reductions in Pa and S.aureus. Further comparison with induced sputum samples is underway to further investigate the relative impact of HEMT and changes in sample type on bacterial growth.
