N2  - Objectives: In CF patients on modulator therapy, sputum volume and pathogen density is significantly reduced, yet complete pathogen eradication is uncommon. This mandates a reassessment of current approaches to infection surveillance. Early morning saliva (emSal) and home sputum-induction (hSI) may be enriched with lower airway pathogens from undisturbed muco-ciliary clearance overnight and present an attractive novel proposition. The CF-Home Sputum Induction Trial (CF-HomeSpIT) is a non-blinded prospective internally-controlled interventional trial comparing emSal and hSI sampling to matched same day samples from clinic sputum-induction (cSI), clinic saliva(cSal) and clinic cough swab(cCS) samples. Methods: CF-HomeSpIT will recruit 100 patients and opened in September 2022. All children with CF at the Children’s Hospital for Wales, aged 5–18 yrs, and already using Hypertonic saline (HS) are eligible for recruitment. Home sampling packs are provided by post and consent and instructions delivered by phone (www.uhwchildren.com/CF-HomeSpIT). [Formula presented] EmSal is obtained using 3 rounds of a standardised cough clearance manoeuvre [COUGH, HUFF, COUGH, CLEAR THROAT, SPIT]. For hSI, patients use HS with their normal physiotherapy regimen, and COUGH, HUFF, COUGH, CLEAR THROAT, SPIT, whenever they feel a rattle. Matched clinic samples are taken by a physiotherapist on the same morning using standard protocols. Results: In an interim analysis, matched samples from the first 14 patients identified 6 pathogens. 4/6 (66%) were isolated on emSal, 3/6 (50%) on hSI, 2/6 (33%) on cCS, 3/6 (50%) on cSal, and 5/6 (87%) on cSI. 5/6 (87%) were isolated on emSal and hSI combined. A second interim analysis (with estimated ~40 patients) will be presented at conference. Conclusion: Results suggest home-sampling is possible, with comparable pathogen yield compared to supervised clinic sampling. Early morning saliva is a novel, quick, practical approach to sampling that warrants further evaluation.
