N2  - Objectives Since the advent of newborn screening in the UK, Cystic Fibrosis Screen Positive Indeterminate Diagnosis (CFSPID) infants have been identified. Many are asymptomatic and long term outcomes are uncertain. Within the UK there is a wide variation in physiotherapy input for these children, ranging from routine reviews to referral only and there is ongoing uncertainty regarding the optimal approach. Aim To ascertain the level of physiotherapy involvement with a group of CFSPID children at a regional paediatric cystic fibrosis unit. Method Retrospective audit of a cohort of CFSPID children attending the specialist CF centre. Data collected from diagnosis to April 2022, including age, sweat chloride, pathogens, x-ray results, admissions, number of patients requiring physiotherapy input, type and level of input. Results 37 patients, mean age 7.86 years (1–15),15 male, mean sweat chloride 35.88 mmol (16–87) were included. X-rays: normal-17, mild bronchial wall thickening-11, lingular changes-1, bilateral lower lobe bronchiectasis-1. Pathogens isolated on cough swabs/sputum specimens included Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Haemophilus influenza, Moraxella cattarhalis and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. 14 patients (38%) were referred to the CF physiotherapy team, mainly due to recurring respiratory symptoms. 13 were taught airway clearance techniques (ACT’s) and 1 developed sinus symptoms. Age appropriate ACT’s were taught including modified postural drainage and percussion, Bubble PEP, ACBT, PEP and acapella. Two patients required a twice daily regime; the remaining 11 used these techniques intermittently when symptomatic and the final patient used nasal washes daily. 6 patients required admission for IV antibiotics. Number of admissions per patient ranged from 1–5. Conclusion Children with CFSPID have a wide variation in their presentation. Physiotherapy input has been tailored to individual need at this centre and appears to be effective.
