N2  - Objectives The I-neb is a handheld portable nebuliser, utilising mesh technology for aerosol production and delivery (‘adaptive aerosol delivery’), enabling faster treatment times. Previous studies have shown it to be well tolerated and a useful tool to monitor adherence in children with CF. There is little reported on patient satisfaction. We present findings of a service evaluation from the national paediatric CF service in Wales, UK; detailing patient experience and clinical effectiveness of using the I-neb. Methods Parents were invited to complete an anonymous patient satisfaction survey. In addition, data regarding growth, spirometry, bacterial swabs, courses of antibiotics was collected from the annual assessments of children pre and post commencing I-neb. Data was analysed using Wilcoxon paired test Results Completed questionnaire data was available from 21 parents (table 1, [values given as n (%)]). Over 75% of parents found the I-neb either very or extremely easy to use and clean. Over 75% of children and parents prefer their I-neb over previous devices. They found the I-neb portable and nebulisation quicker with other benefits being quieter and easier to clean. 80% of parents have reported some difficulty in sourcing medication specific chambers for the I-neb. Clinical data was available for 41 children (table 2, [values given as median and (range)]). There was a trend for increase in FEV1 and FVC percent predicted, reduced need for additional antibiotics and reduced CF pathogens detected between pre and post I-neb commencement. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Conclusion Our data suggests that in children with CF who have been commenced on I-neb there is a clear preference over other devices previously used; despite some difficulties in sourcing medication specific chambers. Whilst our data supports improvements in some clinical parameters pre and post introduction of I-neb, additional work, looking at temporal associations and adherence, would be useful to evaluate this further.
