N2  - Objectives: Non-Cystic Fibrosis Bronchiectasis (NCFB) is a chronic, progressive respiratory disorder characterized by irreversibly dilated airways and recurrent pulmonary infections. Concomitant infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa is associated with greater lung function impairment, more frequent exacerbations, greater hospitalization risk, and mortality. NCFB exacerbations are defined as an increase in daily symptoms including cough, sputum, malaise, fatigue, and difficulty breathing. The cost of NCFB illness with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and frequent acute exacerbations have not been quantified. Methods: This retrospective longitudinal study utilized IQVIA’s PharMetrics® Plus commercial claims database for patients 18-64yo (01/01/2006-12/31/2020) and Medicare’s Limited Data Set (≥65yo; 1/1/2012-12/31/2020). All study patients had ≥2 outpatient claims, or ≥1 inpatient or Emergency Department claim for NCFB (ICD-10-CM: J47.XX or ICD-9-CM: 494.XX) and no cystic fibrosis diagnoses. Patients were observed for 1 year after first NCFB diagnosis. P. aeruginosa patients with NCFB had ≥1 claims for infection (ICD9: 041.7; ICD-10: B96-5) or pneumonia due to P. aeruginosa infection. NCFB patients with exacerbations had ≥1 NCFB hospitalization, ≥1 pneumonia (any), ≥2 exacerbations, or IV antibiotic treatment. Outcomes were patient characteristics and mean per patient annual healthcare costs. Results: Among 36,283 commercially insured NCFB patients, mean age was 53.7±9.9, 63.3% were female, 1,180 (3%) had exacerbations with P. aeruginosa, 18,074 (50%) had exacerbations/no P. aeruginosa, and 16,946 (47%) had neither (P. aeruginosanor exacerbations). Among 22,162 NCFB Medicare patients, mean age was 76.2 ± 7.4, 60.9% were female, 824 (4%) had exacerbations with P. aeruginosa, 10,870 (49%) had exacerbations/no P. aeruginosa, and 10,381 (47%) had neither (P. aeruginosanor exacerbations). Total all-cause commercial insurance and Medicare costs were twice as high for the PA+exacerbations cohort compared to the exacerbations/no P. aeruginosa and neither P. aeruginosa nor exacerbations cohorts ($196,914, $71,589, $24,603 and $112,756, $50,010, $17,169, respectively). Conclusions: NCFB carries a large economic burden, particularly among patients with P. aeruginosa.
