N2  - Introduction: Tuberculosis (TB) is an important public health concern worldwide. Pulmonary TB can present in many distinct radiographic forms ranging from tree-in-bud opacities, lobar pneumonia, cavitation, lymphadenopathy, and/or even distinct nodule(s) and mass(es). Pulmonary pseudotumor TB is a rare presentation of TB that presents as a nodule or mass. We describe a case of pulmonary TB in a patient from a TB endemic country who presented with a solitary pulmonary nodule and negative sputum acid-fast bacteria smear initially presumed to represent a malignancy. Case Report: An 83-year-old woman from Mexico with no known medical history presented with 2 months of fevers, chest pain, cough, dyspnea on exertion, and a 10-pound weight loss. She was admitted for acute hypoxemic respiratory failure. Chest X-ray showed opacities and volume loss in the right lower lobe. Follow-up chest CT was significant for a 2.3 cm right lower lobe nodule and tree-in-bud opacities. Initial infectious workup and sputum AFB smears and culture were negative, but quantitative sputum PCR was positive for rifampin-susceptible Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Directly observed therapy with rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol was begun and over the course of three days, her dyspnea improved, and she was weaned to room air. The patient was discharged and seen in outpatient TB clinic where repeat imaging 4 weeks later showed decrease of her tree-in-bud opacities. Discussion: Pulmonary pseudotumor is an unusual presentation of TB. It poses a diagnostic dilemma in that the mass can be easily confused for malignancy. Up to one third of cases show negative AFB staining, so it is crucial to combine sputum AFB staining with PCR testing for increased sensitivity in identifying it.
