N2  - Introduction: In regions with a high prevalence of pulmonary tuberculosis (TB), diagnosing other diseases with a similar clinical presentation is often difficult and requires clinical suspicion. We present a case of a patient with classic respiratory symptoms, where a radiological sign (Bulging fissure) drew attention to other differential diagnoses. Description of case: female patient, 50 years old, previously carrier of Hypertension, Asthma and Type II Diabetes, admitted presenting recurrent fever that started 14 days before admission, associated with hyporexia and consumptive syndrome, with loss of 27kg in 1 year. She denied nausea, vomiting, and bleeding. She also did not present chronic respiratory symptoms. In admission, a chest X-ray evidenced an alveolar opacity in the upper third of the left lung. Broad spectrum antibiotic was initiated. Her symptoms worse and computed tomography (CT) of the thorax was performed a week later and showed a voluminous expansive excavated lesion located in the posterior apical segment of the left lung. Due to the high suspicion, the pulmonary TB diagnosis was pursued, but all tests come back negative (bacilloscopic and RT-PCR for Mycobacterium tuberculosis). Due to the difficulty in managing the case, he was taken for clinical radiological discussion, where the “bulging fissure” sign was observed on the admission radiograph. The culture result for bacteria was retrieved, and showed growth of Klebsiella pneumoniae, closing the diagnosis. Culture-guided antibiotic therapy was started, and the patient had a good evolution of the lung infection. Thus, antibiotic therapy guided by an antibiogram was initiated, and the patient evolved well, with the resolution of the pneumonic picture, and was discharged for follow-up with Pulmonology. Discussion: K. pneumoniae is a pathogen that has been associated with respiratory tract infection, affecting particularly individuals with risk factors such as diabetes mellitus, alcoholism, and malignancy. It presents with a typical pneumonic clinical picture, with fever, cough, and sputum, in addition to leukocytosis and a new pulmonary infiltrate identified radiologically, with a predilection for involvement of the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Curvature of the fissure downward due to intense consolidation can lead 'Bulging fissure' sign. In countries with a high prevalence of granulomatous disease like Brazil, it can be hard to make the differential between pulmonary TB. We bring you this case to emphasize the importance of performing and properly evaluating chest radiography (a simple, inexpensive, and widely available test) when faced with pulmonary respiratory symptoms.
