N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysm (PAP) is a rare and life-threatening condition. Diagnosis requires a high index of clinical suspicion. We present a case of PAP due to Streptococcus pyogenes lung abscess complicated by empyema. Case Description: A 54-year-old man with type II diabetes, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and bipolar disorder presented to our university hospital with four days of dyspnea and productive cough with foul-smelling sputum. On presentation he was afebrile, tachycardic, and hypoxemic requiring three liters per minute of supplemental oxygen. Physical examination was notable for respiratory distress with increased work of breathing and diminished breath sounds throughout all left-sided lung fields. Chest radiograph demonstrated near-complete opacification of the left hemithorax (Image, Panel A). Computed tomography (CT) angiography of the chest revealed an 8.0 x 6.7 x 9.9 cm left lower lobe abscess containing two pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysms (3.1 and 2.2 cm) and a large left pleural effusion (Image, Panels B and C). Within hours of presentation, the patient was intubated for worsening hypoxemia and respiratory failure. On hospital day 1 he underwent coil embolization of both pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysms followed by placement of a midaxillary chest tube with prompt evacuation of two liters of grossly purulent fluid. Pleural fluid and respiratory cultures grew Streptococcus pyogenes. Following chest tube placement, the patient received six doses of intrapleural fibrinolytics and enzyme therapy for treatment of empyema. The patient was extubated on hospital day eight. He was treated with six weeks of amoxicillin-clavulanate. CT of the chest at follow-up demonstrated resolution of the empyema and significantly decreased size of the pulmonary abscess (Image, Panel D). Discussion: PAP secondary to infection is a rare and life-threatening condition. A 2020 review identified 32 infectious cases in the literature from 2010-2018, only 12 of which were secondary to bacterial infection. Historically, most bacterial PAPs were due to syphilitic destruction of the vasa vasorum. Currently, tuberculosis is the leading infectious cause of PAP worldwide. A PAP associated with a tuberculous cavity may also be referred to as a Rasmussen aneurysm. A pseudoaneurysm differs from an aneurysm in that the latter histologically involves all three layers of the vessel wall, including the intima. CT angiography is the preferred imaging modality for PAP diagnosis. As in our case, management of a PAP often involves a multidisciplinary approach, including endovascular therapy and potentially cardiothoracic surgery in cases of treatment failure.
