N2  - INTRODUCTION: Poncet's disease is a non-erosive inflammatory arthritis, which occurs in presence of active mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB), without concomitant joint infection. It presents as oligo- or polyarthritis with a predilection for lower limb joints. Complete response to anti-tubercular therapy, lack of chronicity and lack of axial involvement distinguishes it from other seronegative arthritides. The diagnosis is not well defined but includes features of inflammatory arthritis with an aseptic joint, in the setting of MTB. We present a patient with reactive arthritis in the setting of active pulmonary tuberculosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old man presented with a two-week history of left sided chest pain, associated with one month of dry cough, bilateral knee and hip pain, and lower extremity paresthesia with weakness. Physical exam was notable for temperature of 37.7°C, decreased breath sounds at the left lung base, knee tenderness with decreased range of motion but without effusion or warmth, no erythema nodosum, and decreased sensation in left anterolateral thigh. Lower extremity muscle strength was 4/5, Achilles tendon insertion sites were tender bilaterally, with normal Babinski and ankle reflexes. Laboratory analysis revealed no leukocytosis but an ESR >110 and C-RP of 126. Chest CT showed bilateral apical multifocal infiltrates; therefore, patient was treated with Unasyn for multifocal pneumonia. After development of acute hematochezia, imaging revealed a thickened ileum raising concern for inflammatory bowel disease with associated arthritis. Knee radiographs and complete spinal MRI were unrevealing. Patient remained febrile despite antibiotics; however, viral respiratory panel, ANA, HIV and urine gonococcus/chlamydia tests were negative. Colonoscopy revealed a 3 cm benign ulcerated mass in terminal ileum. QuantiFERON returned positive on day 9 of admission, thus bronchoalveolar lavage was performed. Subsequently, an MRI of left knee revealed lymphadenopathy in posterior compartment, a small effusion, small subarticular cysts, and an intramuscular cyst in Popliteus muscle. Cyst aspirate cultures were negative for crystals or mycobacteria. The patient was started on rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for empiric MTB treatment. At one-month follow-up, sputum and lavage specimens returned positive for MTB, and patient reported complete resolution of his musculoskeletal complaints. CONCLUSION: Poncet's disease is an elusive diagnosis, especially in areas with low prevalence of MTB. A delayed diagnosis could result in further morbidity, in addition to a potentially inappropriate use of anti-inflammatory or immunosuppressive medications instead of antimicrobials. In patients with arthritis and risk factors for MTB, Poncet's disease should be entertained as part of the differential diagnosis.
