N2  - INTRODUCTION: The incidence of non-tuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (NTM-PD) is on the rise globally. Historically, NTM-PD has mainly been reported in immunocompromised individuals, but cases are now being described in healthy adults. However, the presence of underlying chronic lung disease is usually present in such cases. We report a rare case of respiratory failure secondary to extensive NTM-PD in an immunocompetent 21-year-old with no history of pulmonary disease. CASE PRESENTATION: A 21-year-old male presented to the emergency department with a 4-day history of breathlessness, cough, and intermittent haemoptysis He was found to be febrile, tachycardic and hypoxic. Arterial blood gas sampling confirmed type 1 respiratory failure and admission chest x-ray demonstrated non-specific mottling throughout both lung fields; lab tests showed raised infection markers. The patient had no past medical history and took no regular medication. He was a current smoker and had recently returned from a brief vacation to Western Europe. Broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy was commenced pending sputum culture, blood culture and atypical pneumonia screening results. The patient deteriorated the following day, requiring escalation to carbapenem antimicrobials and high-flow nasal oxygen therapy in the high dependency unit. A computerised tomography scan of the chest showed extensive miliary pulmonary nodules with hilar lymphadenopathy (Figure 1) Based on these findings anti-tuberculosis chemotherapy was commenced. A Bronchoscopy was performed for lavage and sampling; complicated by pneumothorax which was successfully treated via chest drain insertion. Sputum culture, blood culture, acid-fact bacilli culture and tuberculosis polymerase-chain reaction (PCR) were negative. Induced sputum culture grew Mycobacterium avium intracellulare (MAI) in two samples. There was subsequent clinical and radiological improvement with NTM-PD treatment (Rifampicin, Ethambutol, Clarithromycin) and the patient was later discharged from hospital. Currently available immunological testing revealed no deficit in immune function, and follow-up imaging confirmed resolution after 18-months of NTM-PD treatment DISCUSSION: Miliary pulmonary disease is a rare manifestation of MAI infection and especially rare in a young individual with no pre-existing pulmonary disease and no apparent risk factors or immunodeficiency. The complex immune interactions involved in NTM-PD are yet to be characterised, and it may be that certain individuals are at risk of extensive disease due to currently undescribed immunodeficiencies. With the rising incidence of NTM-PD globally, this case highlights the importance of suspecting atypical infection such as NTM in cases of acute respiratory failure Prompt pathogen identification and treatment can be lifesaving and greatly reduces the risk of permanent parenchymal damage.
