N2  - Pulmonary sequestration (PS) is a rare congenital condition. It is a dysplastic, non-functional lung tissue with a separate systemic blood supply and without bronchial tree connection in most cases. At times, the emergence of a superimposed infection can lead to its diagnosis. Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Nocardia asteroids, and Aspergillus species are the most reported causes of infection. Mycobacterium avium-complex (MAC) superimposed disease is exceedingly rare, with less than a handful of cases described. We report a case of a 27-year-old male without known medical illness who presents with a persistent cough for six months despite empiric antibacterial therapy. He is from central Mexico but is incarcerated in the US and has not traveled in four years. A CT chest showed irregular consolidation in the right lower lobe with multiple cystic spaces supplied by a large branch of the aorta (Figure 1). Nodules in the right lower and middle lobes were also noted. A radiological diagnosis of pulmonary sequestration was made, and bronchoalveolar lavage was pursued with no microorganisms on the gram-stain, acidfast or GMS stains. Three weeks later, MAC growth was detected. Notably, carbohydrate antigen 19-9 (CA 19-9) was elevated He was treated with antimycobacterial therapy and lobectomy with clinical improvement and CA19-9 normalization Histopathologic examination revealed extensive, extra-airway, noncaseating granulomata and giant cells (Figure 1). An intraoperative lavage culture further grew MAC. This case illustrates the value of comprehensive microbiological investigation in patients with persistent pneumonia symptoms and imaging features. This is particularly valuable in patients with underlying structural lung disease. Although MAC is an aerobic microorganism, it infected a PS, a less aerated zone of the lung. Additionally although the majority of intralobar sequestrations are isolated from the rest of the parenchyma, twenty percent of cases have an aberrant airway connection. This may explain the route of the sequestered tissue acquiring the organism and later spreading it into the adjacent normal lung. Other routes are through the utilization of Kohn's pores, the pleural lining, or lymphatics. Finally, a prominent finding is the elevation of CA 19-9 that trended down after the lobectomy. CA 19-9 is hypersecreted by respiratory epithelium and hypertrophied glands in chronically inflamed lungs. It was also shown to be elevated in PS cases and a minority of non-tuberculous mycobacterial lung diseases. Observational studies are needed to investigate the utility of CA 19-9 to guide MAC infection duration of therapy.
