N2  - Cardiac involvement in tuberculosis (TB) is rare and has been reported in only 1-2% of cases, typically in immunocompromised patients. We present a rare case involving a young immunocompetent male with TB-induced hemopericardium in the suburbs of New York. The patient was a 24-year-old male, otherwise healthy, who presented with productive cough and unintentional weight loss of 15 pounds over two months. He denied hemoptysis, night sweats, or recent travel. He was born in the United States and reported contact with his parents who traveled to Vietnam. On admission, patient was found to be profoundly tachypneic and tachycardic, on room air with no hemodynamic compromise. Contrast tomography of the chest revealed bilateral pleural effusions and moderate pericardial effusion with radiodensity consistent with hemopericardium. The patient was found to have a positive QuantiFERON test; sputum smears were negative for three consecutive days. Analysis of the pleural fluid demonstrated exudative features with lymphocyte predominance, adenosine deaminase (ADA) levels were within normal limits, and pleural cultures were negative for acid-fast bacilli (AFB). Pleural biopsy confirmed caseating and noncaseating granulomas. The patient was started on isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for eight weeks, followed by isoniazid and rifampin for four months. Sputum cultures eventually grew mycobacterium tuberculosis confirming the underlying diagnosis of TB. The patient responded well to treatment and a repeat echocardiogram revealed no re-accumulation of pericardial fluid. TBinduced pericarditis accounts for <5% of cases among the immunocompetent. The spread of TB to the pericardium can involve a retrograde lymphatic spread from local lymph nodes, direct spread from the lung or pleura, or a hematogenous spread from a primary TB infection. A high level of ADA in pleural fluid is one way to accurately diagnose TB, with reported sensitivity (81-100%) and specificity (83-100%) for activity >40 IU/L. ADA and sputum smears in the case presented were variable, and it took weeks for AFB cultures to eventually grow. Immediate decision-making was limited to quick tests. Given the positive Quantiferon test, we immediately started treating for presumptive TB with improved outcomes. TB can present with fatal cardiovascular manifestations; we ascribe our patient's recovery to the early detection of disease despite variable diagnostic tests. It is therefore imperative to have a higher index of suspicion for TB even among the immunocompetent in non-endemic areas.
