N2  - Introduction Accelerated silicosis in workers exposed to respirable crystalline silica while fabricating engineered stone products is an emerging respiratory health concern worldwide. We present the largest US-based case series of pulmonary silicosis among engineered stone fabrication workers in California. Methods Cases were identified through presentation to a single medical center, statewide hospital-based discharge data, physician reporting to the California Department of Public Health or medical testing following enforcement investigation. Patient medical records were utilized to obtain demographic information, medical history, social history, pulmonary function testing, radiology, and treatment course. Subjects or next-of-kin were contacted to obtain additional occupational and exposure history. Results We identified 48 cases diagnosed between 2010 and 2022, with a prominent uptrend in reporting over the last year, demonstrated in Figure 1. Of these, 47 (97%) were Hispanic foreign-born males and most were underinsured (lacked full Medicaid, Medicare or private insurance) (n=32, 73%). The median age at diagnosis was 45 [interquartile range (IQR) 39 - 49] and median employment tenure in stone fabrication was 15 years (IQR 10 -19). Of 37 patients where extended occupational history was obtained, respirators were used “sometimes” in 22 (59%) and “always” in 8 (21.6%), and were predominantly disposable N-95 filtering face-piece respirators. Symptoms at diagnosis were present in 41 (87%) patients, including shortness of breath (100%), cough (n=34, 72%), chest pain (n=25, 53%), weight loss (n=17, 36%), and fever (n=10, 21%). Five (11%) developed a pneumothorax requiring hospitalization. Initial misdiagnosis occurred in 25 (64%) of which 10 (40%) were diagnosed and treated for bacterial pneumonia, and 7 (28%) for tuberculosis. Median time to diagnosis from presentation (or date of medical screening test, for cases identified through surveillance) was 3 months (IQR 0 - 11 months). Seventeen patients (39%) presented with progressive massive fibrosis. Pulmonary function testing was performed in 45 patients and was abnormal in 33 (72%). Findings were predominantly restrictive in 20 (43%) with overall mean FEV1 of 66.8% predicted (SD 26.2% predicted). Diffusion capacity was decreased in 18 (46%) of patients. Outcomes at time of reporting included need for supplemental oxygen in 7 (15%) and death due to silicosis in 8 (17%). Conclusions Our data demonstrate a rapid increase in reported cases of pulmonary silicosis due to engineered stone fabrication in California. Cases are notable for late presentation of disease, misdiagnosis, and poor outcomes, likely reflecting these workers' immigrant status, poor access to healthcare, and hazardous work environments. (Figure Presented).
