N2  - RATIONALE: Brensocatib, a reversible dipeptidyl peptidase-1 inhibitor that inhibits the activation of neutrophil serine proteases, including neutrophil elastase (NE), is under investigation for the treatment of non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (referred to here as bronchiectasis). In the phase 2 WILLOW trial (NCT03218917), brensocatib prolonged the time to first exacerbation vs placebo in patients with bronchiectasis. We compare the patient characteristics and outcomes in mild, moderate, and severe bronchiectasis subgroups from the WILLOW trial based on the Bronchiectasis Severity Index (BSI). METHODS: Adults with bronchiectasis treated with oral, once-daily brensocatib 10 or 25 mg or placebo were evaluated based on disease severity at baseline, using the BSI. Endpoints were time to first exacerbation (primary), annualized rate of pulmonary exacerbations, change from baseline in forced expiratory volume in 1s (FEV1), and treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs). RESULTS: At baseline, 53 of 256 patients (20.7%) had a BSI score of ≤4 (mild), 89 (34.8%) had 5-8 (moderate), and 114 (44.5%) had ≥9 (severe). Subgroups were mostly balanced in baseline characteristics except that the BSI 5-8 and ≥9 subgroups included older patients. Consistent with greater disease severity, patients with higher BSI scores were more likely to receive inhaled steroids or maintenance macrolides and to have P aeruginosa infection. Disease severity was also associated with greater neutrophilic inflammation, with a clear relationship between BSI score and baseline sputum NE levels (Table). Treatment with brensocatib resulted in a prolonged time to first exacerbation and reduced annualized rate of exacerbations across all levels of severity as defined by the BSI (Table). A somewhat larger effect was seen among patients with a BSI score of ≤4. There was a larger numerical improvement with brensocatib vs placebo on lung function in patients with lower disease severity, as assessed by change in FEV1 from baseline (Table). Incidence of TEAEs was similar across subgroups. The most common TEAEs (≥10%) in the brensocatib arms were headache, cough, and sputum increased in the BSI ≤4 subgroup; cough, sputum increased, and sinusitis in the BSI 5-8 subgroup; and headache and infective exacerbation of bronchiectasis in the BSI ≥9 subgroup. CONCLUSIONS: Brensocatib reduced bronchiectasis exacerbation across all levels of disease severity. In general, brensocatib treatment was safe and well tolerated, and similar across the BSI subgroups. The phase 3 ASPEN trial (NCT04594369) is a larger study that is currently ongoing to further evaluate these findings.(Table Presented).
