N2  - Miliary tuberculosis is a potentially fatal form of disseminated disease due to the hematogenous spread of tubercle bacilli to the lungs, and other organs. It results in the formation of millet seedsized tuberculous foci. Miliary mottling on a chest radiograph and/or in a Chest CT scan is the classical hallmark that supports the diagnosis of miliary tuberculosis. Although the incidence of tuberculosis has been slowly decreasing, the epidemiology has been altered given the advent of chemotherapy, the use of immunomodulators, the HIV epidemic, and immigration patterns. A 53 years old male with a past medical history of hypertension and hypothyroidism was referred for bronchoscopy evaluation due to an abnormal Chest CT scan which raised concern for malignancy. The patient described a progressively worsening nonproductive cough which was mostly nocturnal of several months of evolution. During that same period, he refers had unquantified weight loss, night sweats, weakness, and some respiratory difficulty. He works as a security ward, denies any toxic habit, any past or recent exposure to a person with tuberculosis, and refers all his past tuberculin skin tests have been negative. Chest CT scan showed evidence of multiple mediastinal, hilar and axillary lymphadenopathy as well as bilateral patchy air space opacities and nodules. For this reason, a bronchoscopy and endobronchial ultrasound procedure was performed. Nodes in stations 11R, 7, and 4L were identified, and biopsies were taken and sent for pathology analysis. Also, a Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was collected and sent for microbiological analysis. Weeks later, while awaiting his final results, the patient had a syncopal episode and was admitted for further evaluation. HIV was diagnosed at this time and new chest imaging was concerning for bilateral miliary pattern. Axillary lymphadenopathy was biopsied, which identified the presence of mycobacterium species on pathological analysis. This case highlights the importance of a wide differential diagnosis, specifically for this infrequent pathogen when evaluating patients with nonspecific symptoms even when they mimic a malignancy.
