N2  - Introduction: Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) are a spectrum of clonal myeloid disorders characterized by ineffective bone marrow hematopoiesis and peripheral blood cytopenias. Pulmonary complications may develop in their clinical course, such as organizing pneumonia (OP), which can be primary or secondary. Determining whether OP is secondary is important for better patient management. Case report: Male, 83 years old, retired (former radiology technician), admitted to the emergency room complained of worsening dyspnea and productive cough. Previously COPD, hypertensive, CHF and pulmonary hypertension (mixed groups II and III). He was diagnosed with MDS in December 2021 without the need of specific treatment. In the last year, had 3 hospitalizations for bronchopneumonia, treated with antibiotics and corticosteroids, being discharged with partial improvement. Antibiotic therapy was started due to an infectious clinical condition, altered laboratory tests and a new image of consolidation on chest CT. Cultures (blood, sputum and urine) were negative. We reviewed his medical records and found migratory consolidations pattern in chest CT scans and a hypothesis of OP secondary to MDS was raised. Submitted to bronchoscopy and transbronchial biopsy of a consolidation; histopathology showed the presence of plugs of young connective tissue occluding respiratory bronchioles, favoring the PO pattern. Once other causes of OP were excluded and in view of clinical improvement, prednisone 0.5mg/kg/day was maintained for 8 weeks with progressive weaning. Discussion: Organizing pneumonia (OP), formerly called bronchiolitis obliterans with organizing pneumonia (BOOP), can be idiopathic (cryptogenic) or secondary to specific causes such as bacterial and viral infections, drug and radiation exposures, autoimmune diseases, and hematological. First described in 1901 by the German pathologist W. Lange after evaluating two patients with cough, fever and dyspnea. Histopathologically, OP is characterized by intra-alveolar plugs of granulation tissue. Matsunawa et al. (2021) found in their study that 3.1% of MDS patients developed OP, having bilateral recurrent or migratory lung lesions observed over time. Despite the low incidence, organizing pneumonia should be considered in all patients with hematologic diseases who present with recurrent respiratory symptoms with migratory pulmonary infiltrates. Mokhtari et al. (2002) demonstrated that diffuse parenchymal infiltrates were more common in patients with hematologic malignancies. King et al. (2022) describe that OP is a treatable form of lung disease in patients with hematological diseases, with the use of corticosteroids being the preferred therapy in symptomatic patients. (Figure Presented).
