N2  - RATIONALE: Nebulized Hypertonic saline (HS) is often used in patients with bronchiectasis to aid in mucociliary clearance. Although recommended by international guidelines, data supporting HS use is limited. We sought to describe the bronchiectasis patient population who use HS, utilizing the United States Bronchiectasis and NTM Research Registry. METHODS: Baseline data was used from the United States Bronchiectasis and NTM Research Registry, sponsored by the COPD foundation. Patients with a confirmed diagnosis of bronchiectasis were included in the analysis. Cystic fibrosis patients, as well as those who utilized bronchial hygiene measures other than HS or PEP, were excluded. Eligible patients were assigned to one of four mutually exclusive groups: HS only, PEP only, HS & PEP, or no airway clearance or mucoactive agent. Descriptive statistics were computed for the overall study population and stratified by the four groups. One-way ANOVA and chi-square tests were used to test the difference in the means in continuous variables and the association between categorical variables (respectively) across four groups. RESULTS: A total of 1970 patients were included in the analysis (Table). The mean age of the overall cohort was 67.1 +12.4, predominantly Caucasian (89%), and female (80%). A significant association was observed between the use of PEP and/or HS and experiencing a productive cough (p<0.0052), a bronchiectasis exacerbation in the two years prior to enrollment (p<0.001), NTM presence (p=0.042), and presence of positive Pseudomonas aeruginosa cultures (p<0.001) (Table). Among patients who experienced productive cough in the prior two years (n=887), approximately half (423/887, 48%) used HS and/or PEP. Of those, 38% used HS alone, 22% used PEP alone, and 40% used both PEP and HS. Similarly, approximately half (157/329, 48%) of the patients with Pseudomonas aeruginosa used HS and/or PEP; 48% used HS alone, 13% used PEP only, and 39% used both modalities. CONCLUSION: In patients with bronchiectasis, nebulized hypertonic saline is often used in those with productive cough, and in those with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Further longitudinal analysis to compare differences between groups is needed. (Table Presented).
