N2  - Rationale. Bacterial co-infections are observed in 45-66% of the patients with Mycobacterium avium complex pulmonary disease (MAC-PD). The impact of bacterial co-infection at diagnosis on the clinical course of MAC-PD has not been reported. Methods. Among 558 patients diagnosed with MAC-PD between January 2016 and December 2020, 218 patients who underwent sputum culture tests twice or more within one year before and after diagnosis were included. In this retrospective study, we compared the patient characteristics and disease courses between the patients who detected the same bacterial species twice or more (co-infection group) and those who never cultured bacteria (control). Results. We included 70 patients in the co-infection group and 74 in control. The sputum acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear-positive rate in the co-infection group was lower than in control(35.7% vs. 56.8%, p=0.013). During the median follow-up period of 42 months (interquartile range [IQR]: 16.5-55 months), the patients in the co-infection group were more likely to accomplish spontaneous sputum conversion (18.6% vs. 6.8%, p=0.044). The treatment initiation rate for MAC-PD in the co-infection group was lower than in control (41.4% vs. 67.6%, p=0.003). On multivariate analysis, bacterial co-infection was a negative factor of treatment initiation (HR=0.51; 95%CI 0.28-0.93; p=0.031). Among the patients who administered the treatment for MAC-PD, the culture conversion rate was not different between the co-infection group and control (72.7% vs. 64.9%, p=0.578). In the radiological findings, bacterial co-infections correlate with a high Reiff score (p=0.045) and are not associated with cavitary lesions (p=0.852). The time to the first bronchiectasis exacerbation in the co-infection group was shorter than in control (p=0.015). The patients in the co-infection group were more likely to present with symptoms (80.0% vs. 58.1%, p=0.006), especially cough. These features were more pronounced in cases colonized with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Conclusion. The patients with bacterial co-infection at diagnosis have less disease severity of MAC-PD and are less likely to receive antibiotic treatment for MAC-PD. Bacterial co-infection at diagnosis is a risk of bronchiectasis exacerbation. If the patient with MACPD has a bacterial co-infection at diagnosis, it is important to treat the patient not only as MAC-PD but also as bronchiectasis. (Figure Presented).
