N2  - Introduction: Sirolimus is a macrolide immunosuppressant used to prevent rejection after a solid organ transplant. Here we present a case of sirolimus-induced lung toxicity with a biopsy demonstrating acute fibrinous and organizing pneumonia (AFOP), a histological finding rarely seen as a result of sirolimus toxicity. Case presentation: This case is that of a 67-yearold male with a past medical history of emphysema resulting in a bilateral lung transplant two years before admission, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, chronic kidney disease, diabetes, and hypothyroidism presenting with three days of shortness of breath, nonproductive cough, nausea, vomiting, and non-bloody diarrhea. Three months before the onset of symptoms, he had developed nephrotoxicity as a result of tacrolimus use and was switched to sirolimus. His initial chest CT showed ground glass opacities prompting further testing. An extensive infectious workup including bacterial, viral, and fungal cultures and a donor-specific antibody was negative. Transbronchial lung biopsies of the right lung showed AFOP and extensive type 2 pneumocyte hyperplasia with acute inflammation including neutrophilic capillaritis. The CT and biopsy findings prompted a switch back to tacrolimus from sirolimus due to pulmonary toxicity. These acute findings, in conjunction with his presenting symptoms, required treatment with prednisone via an initial steroid burst and subsequent taper. His hospital course was complicated by a desaturation event requiring six liters of oxygen via nasal cannula. A few days later he developed a rightsided pneumothorax which resulted in the placement of a chest tube and a brief intensive care unit admission. Twenty-one days after his initial presentation, he was discharged home with two liters of oxygen via nasal cannula for ambulation and directed to continue his steroid taper. Since discharge, he has been compliant with outpatient follow-up and has experienced symptomatic improvement. Discussion: AFOP is a rare histological finding that was first recognized in 2002. It can be described as patchy intra-alveolar fibrin that is formed into “balls” along with plugs of organizing pneumonia. Numerous etiologies may cause AFOP including autoimmune diseases, drugs, immunocompromised states, hematologic disorders, and occupational exposures, along with it being idiopathic. After a thorough literature review, we were able to find two cases of probable sirolimus-induced AFOP and one case where the patient was taking sirolimus and developed AFOP, but no further discussion was made. This makes our patient the fourth known case in English literature of probable sirolimusinduced AFOP, and the first case in transplanted lungs.
