N2  - RATIONALE: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a common cause of nosocomial pneumonia. P. aeruginosa injures the lung through multiple virulence factors, including lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and flagellin, leading to progressive lung inflammation and tissue rigidity. Macrophages are key lung effector cells that clear dead cells/debris and invading pathogens. Macrophages are activated by pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), leading to recruitment of MyD88, translocation of NFκB to the nucleus, and pro-inflammatory gene transcription. Emerging data suggests the macrophage activation response can be fine-tuned by lung matrix mechanical properties through mechanosensitive ion channels, such as Piezo1. We hypothesize that activation of Piezo1 by convergent signals from P. aeruginosa virulence factors (LPS, flagellin) and increasing lung stiffness leads to augmented macrophage phagocytosis during P. aeruginosa pneumonia. METHODS: Murine WT and MyD88 KO bone marrow derived macrophages (BMDMs) were stimulated in vitro ± a clinical strain of P. aeruginosa (PA99), flagellin 250 ng/mL, or LPS 1 μg/mL for 24 hours. Piezo1 channel activity was measured using the FLIPR 5 Ca2+ Assay (Molecular Devices) ± the Piezo1 agonist, Yoda1 (1-200 μM) to generate dose-response curves. The effect of matrix stiffness was determined using polyacrylamide gels of pathophysiologic range stiffnesses (1kPa-normal, 8-25kPa injured lung). Phagocytosis was measured ± Piezo1-targeted siRNA using fluorescent E. coli particles (Vybrant Phagocytosis Assay, Molecular Devices) for non-opsonin mediated phagocytosis and IgG-coated fluorescent beads for opsonin-mediated phagocytosis. RESULTS: We now show that a clinical strain of P. aeruginosa (PA99) can sensitize macrophage Piezo1, and thereby increase the intracellular Ca2+ response as compared with the Piezo1 agonist, Yoda1 alone. The Ca2+ response was increased after stimulation of BMDMs with LPS or flagellin by 70% (1750 relative fluorescent units (RFU) to 3000 RFU) at a submaximal dose of Yoda1 (10 μM). This effect was only seen at pathological stiffnesses in the range of inflamed lung (≥ 25 kPa). Sensitization of Piezo1 by flagellin was abrogated in MyD88 knock-out BMDMs, suggesting that sensitization of Piezo1 by flagellin is dependent on the toll-like receptor signaling adaptor protein, MyD88. Down regulation of Piezo1 by siRNA abrogated the flagellin-treatment response in both non-opsonin-mediated phagocytosis of fluorescent E. coli particles (2.8-fold, p = 0.0105) and opsonin-mediated phagocytosis of fluorescent, immunoglobulin-coated beads (2.4-fold, p < 0.0001), as compared with control siRNA. CONCLUSIONS: P. aeruginosa flagellin sensitizes the mechanosensitive ion channel, Piezo1, through MyD88, in a stiffness-dependent manner. By increasing macrophage phagocytosis, Piezo1 is a potential therapeutic target to accelerate pneumonia resolution.
