N2  - Introduction: Adult community acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus Aureus (CA-MRSA) infections carry a high degree of complications, treatment failure, and mortality. Viral infections may induce bacterial superinfections rapidly progressing to MRSA necrotizing pneumonia. We present a successfully treated case of viral pneumonia with superimposed CA-MRSA pneumonia and bacteremia causing acute hypoxic respiratory failure in the intensive care unit (ICU). Description: 54-year-old male with a history of diabetes mellitus and obesity presented with 3 days of progressively worsening blood-tinged cough, generalized weakness and subjective fevers. Endorsed recent laying of drywall in his home, however works as a locksmith. Denied recent sick contact or pets at home. Reported recent travel to Jamaica and Haiti. On physical exam, he was found to be febrile (102.97deg;F), tachycardic (129 bpm), tachypnic (40), and hypoxemic (85% SPO2) requiring max High-Flow Nasal Cannula. Lung exam with Bibasilar rales. Laboratories revealed thrombocytopenia (96 ∧10x3/uL), acute kidney injury, elevated d-dimer, C-reactive protein, lactic acid, and a positive parainfluenza PCR. Chest Radiograph (CXR) revealed bi-basilar consolidations and Chest Computed Tomography (CT) without contrast revealed bilateral confluent airspace opacities surrounded by denser consolidations with reversed halo sign suspicious for cavitation. Despite broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy (vancomycin, cefepime, doxycycline) hypoxemia worsened requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation (Pa/FiO2 <100mmHg). Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and blood cultures returned positive for MRSA. Bacterial superinfection from viral parainfluenza was suspected. Consequently, he was started on IV steroids. Vancomycin was continued ensuring appropriate drug levels. However, due to persistent symptomatology and new leukocytosis, additional tests ordered included- Fungitell 1-3 B-D Glucan assay, Legionella, galactomannan, ANA, and ANCA, all resulted negative. Transthoracic echocardiogram showed no vegetative lesions. Chest CT angiogram revealed an acute pulmonary embolism further complicating his course. Repeat BAL cultures grew Serratia Marcescens warranting treatment for a ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP). Following appropriate treatment, the patient;s overall status improved and was extubated and transferred out of the ICU. Discussion: Our case demonstrates the threat CA-MRSA imposes on public health, even in the immunocompetent hosts. The increased pathogenicity of the disease predisposes to potentially lifethreatening complications, such as bloodstream infections, thrombosis, and necrotizing pneumonia (previously thought to have a low incidence in the immunocompetent host). In addition, this case highlights the potential rapidly and progressing bacterial infection that virus may lead to and most importantly, the series of severe complications related to superinfections. High index of suspicion should prompt early diagnosis and treatment, which are key to avoiding complications. (Figure Presented).
