N2  - RATIONALE: Substantial challenges remain in the diagnosis and treatment of tuberculosis (TB) due to an incomplete understanding of the mechanisms by which Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) modulates host immune responses. Cholesterol metabolism is critical to both the infectious life cycle of Mtb and the optimal immune responses of the host. We previously found that host cholesterol is oxidized to cholestenone during Mtb infection and accumulates to substantial levels in human sputum. The concentration of cholestenone in human sputum distinguished patients with TB from TB-negative patients with symptoms suggestive of TB. However, the role of cholestenone in infection is unknown. During infection of macrophages, we found that the production of cholestenone from host cholesterol depends on the Mtb enzyme 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (3β-Hsd), which is encoded by hsd. Here, we seek to determine whether Mtb infection of mice leads to increases in cholestenone and to understand the significance of host-derived, bacteria-modified cholesterols in the pathogenesis of Mtb. METHODS: To interrogate the role of 3β-Hsd during in vivo infection, C57BL/6 mice were infected with wild-type (WT) Mtb or Mtb lacking 3β-Hsd (Δhsd). Following aerosol infection, lung bacterial burden was measured by plating colony forming units (CFU). We used flow cytometry to compare lung myeloid and lymphoid cell subsets between uninfected mice and mice infected with either WT Mtb or Δhsd. We also measured lung concentrations of cholestenone using mass spectrometry. RESULTS: As we found in humans, mice infected with Mtb had elevated lung cholestenone levels, which increased over time. Mice infected with the Δhsd mutant had reduced control of infection relative to mice infected with WT Mtb, with higher CFU evident as early as 2 weeks post infection (wpi) (Figure 1). At 12 wpi, Δhsd-infected mice exhibited increased monocytes and interstitial macrophages in the lungs relative to mice infected with WT Mtb. Surprisingly, cholestenone levels in the lung were similar between Δhsd-infected mice and WTinfected mice. CONCLUSIONS: Infection of mice with Mtb Δhsd led to increased virulence and altered myeloid cell responses compared to infection with WT Mtb. These experiments support a role for a 3β-Hsd-derived cholesterol metabolite during bacterial pathogenesis. In contrast to infection of macrophages, cholestenone production was not dependent on 3β-Hsd during in vivo infection of mice, suggesting alternative or additional sources of cholestenone in vivo. Future experiments are aimed at defining 3β-Hsd-derived metabolites and identifying the source and importance of cholestenone.
