N2  - INTRODUCTION: Patients with idiopathic acute eosinophilic pneumonia (IAEP) usually present with a fever, cough, dyspnea, and hypoxia. Diffuse pulmonary opacities may be seen on chest radiography. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) routinely shows > 25% eosinophil. Other known causes of similar findings such as drugs, infections, and atopic diseases need to be excluded. Some patients may develop respiratory failure requiring oxygen therapy and even mechanical ventilation. As the presentation is similar to infectious pneumonia, most patients initially get treated with empiric antibiotics. Procalcitonin has been used to differentiate between bacterial and nonbacterial causes of pneumonia. Little is known about its use in IAEP. We aimed to study the utility of procalcitonin in the diagnosis of IAEP. METHODS: We retrospectively identified patients from 2015 to 2022, with a discharge diagnosis of eosinophilic pneumonia, who had a procalcitonin level measured. RESULTS: We identified a total of 13 patients with a discharge diagnosis of eosinophilic pneumonia. Of these, 3 patients had recent exposure to medications implicated in eosinophilic pneumonias (daptomycin, amiodarone, and minocycline). One patient was diagnosed with hypereosinophilic syndrome, and two others were diagnosed with chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. All patients survived to discharge. Seven patients were identified as IAEP. Four patients had symptoms for less than a week, while three had symptoms for 3-4 weeks prior to admission. Symptoms on admission included cough, shortness of breath, and fever. Chest computerized tomography (CT) showed diffuse bilateral patchy ground glass opacities. The average length of stay was 8.6 days, with two patients requiring intensive care and one patient requiring mechanical ventilation. The pulmonary service was consulted immediately in four patients, and after day 3 in three patients. The mean procalcitonin level in IAEP patients was 0.25 (SD=0.20). Four patients had peripheral eosinophilia. The average percentage of eosinophils on BAL was 47%. CONCLUSION: Our small retrospective study shows that the level of procalcitonin may be low in patients with IAEP. Further studies are needed to investigate the utility of this test in the early identification of IAEP.
