N2  - Introduction: Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP) typically presents with subacute symptoms and on average, is diagnosed 2 months from the onset of symptoms. However, it presents as a fulminant disease in select cases with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). We present a clinically challenging case of recurrent fulminant COP.Case Description: 66-year-old female with a medical history of ARDS requiring mechanical ventilation (MV) twice, and mood disorder on fluoxetine was admitted at an outside hospital with fever, productive cough and shortness of breath (SOB) for 4 days. Computed tomography (CT) scan chest showed bilateral ground glass opacities (GGOs) in peribronchiolar and peripheral distribution. Progressive hypoxia necessitated MV on day 2 of hospitalization. She was treated for suspected bacterial pneumonia. Bronchoscopy was unremarkable except neutrophilic predominant bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF). Hypersensitivity pneumonitis panel and detailed autoimmune workup were obtained, which was unrevealing. Patient was started on steroids for ARDS followed by prolonged prednisone for presumed inflammatory pneumonitis. Patient was extubated after 12 days of MV. However, prednisone was stopped after a total of 20 days of therapy at discharge. Patient again presented within 3 weeks of discharge with SOB with severe hypoxia requiring MV. CT chest showed improvement in prior consolidations however, demonstrated some GGOs with bronchiectasis in areas of previous disease, and was noted to have reverse hallow sign (central GGO with surrounding dense consolidation) in right lower lobe. Repeat BALF demonstrated elevated nucleated cells with 90% neutrophilic predominance. Patient underwent video assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) which showed organizing pneumonia (Figure 1). Patient developed iatrogenic pneumothorax, unexplained encephalopathy and ischemic colitis, following which, family pursued comfort measures.Discussion: COP predominantly affects those in 5th and 6th decade of life and has annual incidence of 1 in 100,0000 with no gender or race predilection. Commonly it presents as indolent pneumonic illness. Only a handful cases of acute fulminant COP are reported in literature till date which may present with ARDS. Neutrophilic predominance BALF and interstitial fibrosis (IF) (fibrinous OP variant) may suggest steroid resistance and progressive disease in up to 10-15% of COP cases. Fluoxetine can present with pneumonitis with granulomatous component, eosinophilic pneumonia and typically has lymphocytic predominant BALF, unlike in our case. Recurrent ARDS, with steroid responsiveness should raise the suspicion for acute fulminant COP, hence we suggest timely diagnosis with tissue sampling and administration of steroids with slow prolong taper may prevent morbidity and mortality associated with it. (Figure Presented).
