N2  - Introduction Tuberculous empyema (TE) is characterized by the presence of pus in the pleural cavity as a consequence of infection by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. One-third of patients with TE have failed pleural drainage, and invasive procedures may be necessary. Therefore, the use of intrapleural therapy could be part of the therapeutic arsenal for patients with initial failure of chest drainage. Case reportA 36-year-old male with no known comorbidities and a history of fever and productive cough for 12 months, with a 12kg weight loss. Three sputum samples were tested for acid-fast bacilli smears, nucleic acid amplification tests (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra assay), and cultures, all with negative results. Chest radiography revealed an opacity in the left apex associated with moderate ipsilateral pleural effusion. Due to the high prevalence of pleural tuberculosis in Brazil, a standard regimen with rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol (RIPE) was made for 2 months, followed by maintenance therapy with RI. Despite the clinical resolution of fever, the patient maintained dyspnea, and hyporexia, in addition to the persistence of left pleural effusion. Point-of-care ultrasound showed a moderate pleural effusion with homogeneous echogenecity, internal septations, and swirling echoes with significant pleural thickening. Diagnostic thoracentesis was performed, with a drainage of 60 mL of purulent fluid and the Xpert MTB/RIF was positive. Thoracic drainage was indicated, with an immediate output of 200 mL of purulent fluid, despite a significant reduction on the second day and persistence of pleural effusion on a control radiograph. Opted for intrapleural therapy with 10 mg of tissue plasminogen activator and 5 mg of DNase for 3 days. The patient progressed with a significant increase in pleural output (1,130 mL in 5 days) and rapid clinical and radiological improvement without complications associated with local therapy. The chest tube was removed after the 5 days of treatment, and the patient was discharged after 7 days of hospitalization without the need for additional interventions, with maintenance of oral therapy (RI).Discussion Here, we report the case of a patient diagnosed with TE with persistent symptoms and pleural effusion, despite drug therapy and pleural drainage. In this case, some surgical procedures, could be necessary to resolve this pleural infection. However, the adjuvant intrapleural therapy with fibrinolytics and DNase for 3 days resulted in clinical, laboratory, and radiological improvement associated with a short hospital stay, without the need for a more invasive surgical procedure and without adverse effects. (Figure Presented).
