N2  - Selective immunoglobulin M deficiency (SIgMD) is a very rare form of dysgammaglubulinemia. The disease is characterized by an isolated low level of (IgM). The disorder may occur as a primary or secondary manifestation. Most reported cases were attributed to secondary SIgMD, with a strong association to malignancy, autoimmune diseases, and immunosuppressive treatment. We present a unique case of . The patient is a 63-year-old female with a significant past medical history for rheumatoid arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis. She was admitted to our facility multiple times over the past two years with recurrent sinusitis, productive cough, and worsening dyspnea. An extensive workup was performed during each presentation, including radiological imaging, serological studies, and sputum cultures. Collected cultures revealed pseudomonas, stenotrophomona, pneumocystis jiroveci, and enterococcus. High-resolution computed tomography of the chest demonstrated bilateral bronchiectasis. Despite receiving appropriate antibiotics, she continued to show low responses and frequent reinfections. Infectious diseases service was consulted and has recommended further assessment with bronchoscopy. Therefore, the patient underwent bronchoscopic evaluation with performed biopsy and bronchoalveolar lavage. The procedure showed no evidence of endobronchial masses, bleeding, or lesions. Lung samples were negative for malignancy and positive for Nocardia. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was initiated. Screening for HIV and HCV infections was negative. Total hemolytic complement was within normal. Immunoglobulin levels were checked initially. The patient was found to have significantly lower IgM levels as compared to normal IgG, IgE, and IgA. IgG subclasses were tested and noted to be low in IgG subclass 2, 3, and 4. Immunoglobulin levels were rechecked subsequently after discharge and were consistent with repeatedly low IgM and IgG subclasses. Hence, the patient was diagnosed with SIgMD and concomitant IgG subclasses deficiency. She completed a course of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole antibiotics. In addition, intravenous immunoglobulin, prophylactic antibiotics, pneumococcal and meningococcal vaccinations were initiated, and periodic surveillance follow-up. SIgMD is a challenging diagnosis, owing to the scarce data available in the literature. Therefore, understanding this condition is still preliminary. This is the first case to be reported of pulmonary nocardiosis presenting in SIgMD. However, the late-onset presentation of the disease warrants further evaluation in the setting of coexistence autoimmune disorders. Early recognition of this illness is critical for clinicians to appropriately diagnose, treat, and prevent serious complications.
