N2  - INTRODUCTION: We presented a fatal case of community-acquired Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (CA-MRSA) necrotizing pneumonia that rapidly progressed to acute respiratory distress syndrome, septic shock, multiorgan failure, and eventually death despite aggressive management. CASE PRESENTATION: 51-year-old male presented with left-sided chest pain, dyspnea, and productive cough for 3 days. On arrival, the patient was febrile, tachycardic, hypotensive, and tachypneic with an oxygen saturation of mid-80 % on ambient air. Pulmonary exam was notable for accessory muscle use, diffuse rhonchi, and reproducible tenderness on the left-sided chest wall. A 2-cm painful, erythematous nodule with purulent drainage was noted on the left lower extremity. Initial labs revealed leukocytosis with 45% of band neutrophils, elevated lactate, and creatinine. Urine drug screen (UDS) was positive for cocaine, amphetamine, benzodiazepine, cocaine, and oxycodone. Chest X-ray (CXR) showed an initial left upper lobe consolidation (Figure 1A) that rapidly progressed to complete opacification throughout the left lung within six hours (Figure 1B). Following initial fluid resuscitation and broad-spectrum antibiotics, he continued to deteriorate and was intubated. Multiple vasopressors were initiated for worsening shock. Subsequent microbiology testing was positive for MRSA in blood culture, nasopharyngeal swab, and respiratory culture. On hospital day 2, patient developed left hydropneumothorax (Figure 1C) and a chest tube was inserted with immediate abundant serosanguinous fluid drainage (Figure 1D). The following day, diffuse subcutaneous emphysema throughout the chest wall extending to the neck, abdomen, groin, and scrotum was noted (Figure 1E), leading to the placement of a second chest tube (Figure 1F). Despite the above management, patient developed refractory hypoxia and veno-venous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (VV-ECMO) was initiated. His hospital course was further complicated by atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular rate, oliguric renal failure requiring continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT), and persistent bacteremia despite therapeutic vancomycin level. He continued to deteriorate and ultimately expired after transitioning to hospice care on hospital day 8. DISCUSSION: CA-MRSA strains are most recognized to cause skin and soft-tissue infections, however, reports regarding CA-MRSA causing necrotizing pneumonia in the intravenous drug use (IVDU) patient population remain scarce. Given the aggressiveness and substantial mortality with CA-MRSA pneumonia, and the possibility of increased prevalence in IVDU patients, clinicians should consider CA-MRSA earlier in such patients presenting with severe community-acquired pneumonia on presentation. This is of particular significance in an era of a growing opioid epidemic. Further study is also needed for more effective synergistic antimicrobial coverage. (Figure Presented).
