N2  - RATIONALE: The worldwide estimate of deaths from tuberculosis (TB) had increased with 1.6 million deaths during 2021. Tracheobronchial tuberculosis is Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) infection of the upper airways. Airway mucus represents a first-line defense against luminal pathogens. In the first 10-12 generations of the tracheobronchial tree, submucosal glands are responsible for mucin secretion. Mucous cell differentiation and mucin secretion are controlled by various signaling factors including infectious responses, leukocyte products, and neuronal input. Proinflammatory pathways trigger excessive mucin production and secretion via an increase in mucus cell numbers and the amount of MUC5AC and MUC5B produced. In inflammatory conditions, gland and duct diameter are increased. These alterations are deleterious as effective mucociliary transport requires specific rheologic properties. We hypothesize that there are alterations to mucous production and submucosal mucous gland structure in the upper airways of patients with TB as compared to healthy individuals. METHODS: Upper airway tissue specimens were obtained from a deceased human subject with latent TB and a surgically re sected specimen from a patient with active TB. The patient with latent TB was asymptomatic and expired secondary to myocardial infarction; Mtb was incidentally detected post-mortem as mCT scan of his upper airways revealed tracheal calcification concerning for tuberculosis. A normal control specimen was obtained from a deceased human subject who did not have TB. Specimens were fixed and embedded in paraffin, subsequently stained with H&E, and mounted on glass slides. Large airways were identified by the presence of cartilaginous rings; adjacent submucosal glands were analyzed in comparison to the control specimen. Specimens subsequently underwent immunohistochemistry for the Mtb secreted antigen 85 (Ag85). RESULTS: Compared to a normal control (Figure 1A&B), the latent TB specimen revealed lymphocytic infiltration surrounding the submucosal glands, architectural distortion and evidence of mucous gland metaplasia. The active TB specimen (Figure C) revealed more severe architectural distortion and evidence of increased mucous gland metaplasia and hyperplasia. The active TB specimen also revealed significant lymphocytic infiltration surrounding the submucosal glands and ongoing airway inflammation. Staining of the tissue for Ag85 revealed positivity of mucinous acini within submucosal glands (Figure 1D). CONCLUSIONS: These results suggest that both active and latent TB infection demonstrate varying degrees of airway inflammation and mucous gland dysfunction. The presence of TB secreted Ag85 within submucosal glands suggests the involvement of TB infection within the submucosal glands themselves. Thus, mucin dysregulation and airway irritation may underly the pathophysiology of cough in TB.
