N2  - Introduction: Francisella tularensis causes a potentially life-threatening zoonotic disease with variable presentations. Tularemia is a Category A bioterrorism disease and as few as 10-25 bacteria are enough to cause human disease via cutaneous or inhalation routes(1). The non-specific clinical presentation of Tularemia makes diagnosis challenging. We present the case of pneumonic tularemia presenting with headache and fever. Description of case: The patient is a 56 yr old sewer worker without past medical history who presented with complaints of left temporal headaches, night sweats, fevers, and fatigue for 2 weeks. On exam, the patient exhibited sinus tenderness but was otherwise well. Laboratory evaluation revealed transaminitis and elevated CRP, and CSF analysis was unremarkable. Brain MRI and CT showed mucosal thickening in the ethmoid cells. Given his age and the location of the headache, there was concern for temporal arteritis and the patient was admitted to the hospital. A temporal artery biopsy was found to be negative. In the hospital, he developed a productive cough and acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring nasal cannula O2. Chest imaging revealed bilateral interstitial infiltrates and a right-sided pleural effusion. Thoracentesis removed 800 mL of cloudy, exudative fluid. The patient was empirically started on doxycycline, and a broad infectious workup including HIV, fungal testing, tuberculosis, toxoplasmosis, legionella, and streptococcus was negative. The patient's testing enzyme-linked immunoassay was positive for Francisella tularensis and the diagnosis was confirmed with a positive pleural fluid culture. The patient was diagnosed with pneumonic tularemia complicated by empyema requiring chest tube drainage. The patient's antibiotics were changed to gentamicin and he required long-term antibiotics with outpatient infectious disease follow-up. Discussion: Pneumonic tularemia is caused by inhalation exposure to F. tularensis and has up to 60% mortality despite appropriate treatment with aminoglycosides for severe infection and doxycycline or ciprofloxacin for mild infection(2). Complications of pneumonic tularemia include pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and lung abscess. Extra-pulmonary manifestations of tularemia include kidney failure, meningitis, peritonitis, and rhabdomyolysis. Our patient was likely exposed to the bacteria through his work in the Illinois sewer systems. Tularemia has an incidence of 124-314 cases per 100,000 residents in the US and is most common in the south-central US, the pacific northwest, and parts of Massachusetts(3). Given the variable constellation of symptoms and high mortality rate, tularemia is important to consider in patients presenting with fever of unknown origin.
