N2  - Introduction - Invasive aspergillosis (IA) results from a disseminated infection with aspergillus species. IA primarily affects immunocompromised patients. We present IA in an immunocompetent individual which started as an occult pulmonary infection with subsequent systemic involvement and misled clinicians to a diagnosis of malignancy. Case presentation - We present a 40-year-old male patient who had a history of recurrent pleural effusion and a right pulmonary nodule. Three years prior he had an exhaustive workup including pleural fluid analysis, and fine needle aspiration (FNA), which were negative for malignancy. He had a positive aspergillus antigen level however blood cultures, pleural fluid cultures, and wedge biopsy were negative for infection and malignancy. Immediately prior to presentation, he was discharged after a transesophageal biopsy of a mediastinal mass with a working diagnosis of metastatic malignancy of unknown primary. He then presented to us with a chronic nonproductive cough with fever and lethargy. On exam, vitals were stable except for a fever of 102F. He appeared drowsy with an otherwise normal exam. Investigations revealed a leukocytosis of 20.40 cells/mm3. Blood and sputum cultures were negative. Chest X-ray showed an airspace opacity within the medial right lung. He was started on treatment for pneumonia. Previous mediastinal mass biopsies reported hyphal elements. The patient was treated with Micafungin with improvement in clinical condition. Serology later showed positive aspergillus antigen and (1,3)-β-D glucan. HIV test was negative, and immunoglobulin levels were normal. Bronchoscopy revealed extrinsic compression of the right lower lobe (RLL) airway and an endobronchial “pit” lesion in the medial basal aspect of the RLL. Culture from these lesions grew Aspergillus fumigatus. Subsequently, micafungin was changed to voriconazole. Unfortunately, the patient had complications from IA including intracranial aspergillosis and mycotic aneurysm resulting in a complicated course. Discussion - Aspergillus is a ubiquitous fungal pathogen. The most common portal of entry for aspergillus infection is through inhalation of conidia. IA is rare in immunocompetent patients. Our patient's normal immune status may be the reason for the patient's protracted clinical course. A handful of case reports describe aspergillosis mimicking multiple pulmonary nodules and malignancy. Current guidelines recommend bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) with galactomannan testing as a suitable alternative to histopathology in the event of diagnostic uncertainty. An earlier bronchoscopy with tissue diagnosis might have prevented a delay in diagnosis. Physicians should have IA in their differential when evaluating a young patient with a spurious presentation of metastatic illness.
