N2  - BackgroundThe pulmonary manifestations of Aspergillus fumigatus species include aspergilloma, allergic bronchopulmonary, chronic non-invasive pulmonary, and invasive pulmonary aspergillosis [1]. Pulmonary aspergilloma (PA) is a mass composed of fungal hyphae, inflammatory cells, and tissue debris, growing in a pre-existing lung cavity. Antecedent diseases leading to aspergilloma formation include tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, cavitary lung tumor, lung abscess, and cystic fibrosis. Pulmonary tuberculosis is the most common facilitator [2]. The majority of PA remain asymptomatic. Rarely, massive hemoptysis can be a fatal complication [3]. Case PresentationA 58-year-old man, with a history of active smoking, presented with two days of several episodes of hemoptysis associated with unintentional weight loss and night sweats. He had intermittent episodes of smallvolume, self-resolving hemoptysis over the past few years. He denied fevers, chest pain, shortness of breath, recent travel, or sick contacts. He was incarcerated for >30 years, released several years prior. He was currently living in a shelter and occupied as a maintenance worker. He denied prior positive tuberculosis or HIV tests. Vital signs were normal. Labs without leukocytosis, negative ANCA antibodies, Aspergillus antibodies, fungal markers, and IGRA. CT chest showed right apical cavitary lesion measuring 4 x 6 centimeters and mediastinal/hilar lymphadenopathy. Sputum AFB smears and bacterial cultures were negative. He underwent bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy of the cavitary lesion, BAL of the right upper lobe (RUL), and endobronchial-ultrasound transbronchial needle aspiration of mediastinal lymphadenopathy. His post-procedure course was complicated by cough and hypoxemia concerning for massive hemoptysis requiring reintubation and endobronchial blocker placement within the posterior subsegment of the RUL. He underwent urgent right bronchial artery embolization (BAE) and was successfully extubated the following day. He had minimal further episodes of hemoptysis. Biopsy results of the lesion and BAL were consistent with aspergillus species and he was started on voriconazole. Weeks later he underwent successful RUL wedge resection. Discussion The diagnosis of aspergillus includes mycologic identification, clinical context, and characteristic imaging features. Hemoptysis is a common presentation of PA and is known as one of the most common causes of hemoptysis. Among patients who underwent BAE for hemoptysis, patients with aspergilloma had an increased risk of recurrent bleeding and mortality [4]. Definitive treatment of PA includes antifungal treatment and surgical resection if feasible [5]. Conclusion Pulmonary as per gillosis can occur in a broad range of clinical presentations from asymptomatic colonization and aspergilloma to massive hemorrhage from invasive aspergillosis. Therapy is nuanced with antifungal agents and surgery.
