N2  - Introduction: Approximately 10,000 cases of Legionella occur in the U.S.A per year, with 80% occurring in patients above 50, and with risk factors like immunosuppression and chronic respiratory illnesses. It commonly causes community acquired pneumonia with gastrointestinal upset, and rarely elicits neurologic symptoms. We present a case of Legionella Pneumonia in a young, healthy male presenting with cerebellar dysfunction in the radiographic absence of stroke. Case: A 28-year-old male with no medical history presented with 5 days of slurred speech, ataxia, and confusion. Exam was consistent with dysarthria, expressive aphasia, and left sided dysmetria and paresis. Other findings included cough, fevers, diarrhea, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, severe rhabdomyolysis, and sepsis. CT head and lumbar puncture in the Emergency Department were unremarkable, however, CTA chest depicted a left lobar pneumonia. Based on the presentation, Legionella was suspected and urine antigen returned positive. Given the severity of symptoms, MRI brain and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) testing for extrapulmonary Legionella were completed, both of which were unremarkable. With minimal progress on Azithromycin, Levofloxacin was initiated and resulted in sepsis and rhabdomyolysis resolution. However, cerebellar dysfunction remained, necessitating inpatient rehabilitation at the time of discharge. Discussion: Extrapulmonary manifestations of Legionella Pneumophilia have been described extensively, with neurologic dysfunction dating to its origin at the American Legion Convention in Philadelphia, 1976. Typically, encephalopathy occurs disproportionate to metabolic derangements, and is not associated with an impact on mortality. However, cerebellar dysfunction occurs in 5.7-11.2% of neurologic cases, can precede pulmonary symptoms, and carries an increased mortality rate. Dysarthria and ataxia are most frequently described, but dysmetria, nystagmus and dysdiadochokinesia can present as well. CT and MRI brain are often negative, with some literature stating it is possible for SPECT to detect brain lesions. In one study, autopsy reports and diagnostic evaluations were largely normal or nonspecific, with pathogenesis to cerebellar dysfunction unknown. Literature reviewed proposes direct bacterial invasion of brain parenchyma is unlikely as it is rarely positive in CSF analysis. Immunemediated cytotoxic effects could provide an explanation as it has been previously confirmed in vitro. Encephalopathy from sepsis resolves with antibiotic treatment, yet, cerebellar dysfunction can linger over a year post-infection and often requires rehabilitation. Overall, extrapulmonary Legionellosis with neurologic involvement is rare, often misdiagnosed, and with a protracted course. Treatment is largely supportive, but early intervention is warranted to reduce mortality. Thus, it is important to recognize Legionella as a cause of concomitant cerebellar dysfunction and pneumonia.
