N2  - Introduction: Spiculated masses in immunocompetent patients typically represent malignancies. Here we present a case of a spiculated mass in an immunocompetent host ultimately diagnosed as Nocardia. Case: A 75-year-old man with a history of atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, hypertension and hyperlipidemia presented with weight loss and chronic cough. He was a former smoker with a 25-pack-year history. He had been hospitalized one month prior for a right-sided empyema managed with chest tube drainage. On exam, he was afebrile, normotensive, and oxygenating well on room air. His initial laboratory workup was largely unremarkable. A CT chest revealed a large spiculated mass with areas of calcification concerning for a primary lung malignancy. He underwent bronchoscopy with biopsy of the mass which revealed gram positive filamentous branching rods and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate. Final speciation revealed Nocardia beijingensis. He was treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a total duration of 6 months. Discussion: Nocardia is an aerobic gram-positive rod within the class of actinomycetes. It has branching filaments that are weakly acid-fast. There have been 50 species of Nocardia identified that are implicated in human disease. Nocardia beijingensis is a rare organism that was first isolated from soil in China in 2001. Nocardial infections are rare with 500-1000 new cases in the US each year. Typically, patients with a nocardial infection will have a defect in cell-mediated immunity or decreased immunoglobulins. Other notable risk factors for developing a nocardial infection include concurrent infections, underlying lung disease combined with chronic steroid use, and alcohol use disorder. However, it is important to note that up to one third of patients will have no identifiable immunodeficiency. Our patient had no risk factors and was immunocompetent. The most common site of infection is the lungs and half of these patients will have areas of disseminated disease. The most common site of dissemination is the brain and mortality approaches 50% in these patients. The mortality rate for isolated pulmonary nocardiosis is less than 5%. Therefore, while Nocardia is a rare infection it commonly mimics malignancy and should remain on the differential as it is treatable, and mortality dramatically increases when there is disseminated disease.
