N2  - Introduction: The differential diagnosis of pulmonary cavities is broad and includes infections (Bacterial, Fungal), autoimmune, malignancy. Blastomyces dermatitidis is a thermally dimorphic fungus that causes a systemic pyogranulomatous infection called Blastomycosis. The clinical disease primarily involves the lungs following inhalation of the spores found in the soil. Blastomycosis is most commonly reported in North America, particularly in areas surrounding the Ohio and Mississippi River. Patients may be asymptomatic or present with acute or chronic pneumonia along with extrapulmonary manifestations. Case Presentation: A 24-year-old male with history of asthma, marijuana usage and vaping for past 6 years presented to the ER with cough with blood tinged mucus for a week. Vitals were stable. On auscultation, bronchial breath sounds were heard in the upper lobe. Chest X-ray showed right apical cavitary lesion. Subsequent CT chest confirmed a 5.0 x 5.3cm thin walled cavitary lesion in the right upper lobe. Patient was discharged home with medications for a presumed pneumonia. On a follow-up visit in the pulmonology outpatient clinic 2 days later, further testing was pursued to rule out infectious and autoimmune etiology. Autoimmune workup, TB QuantiFERON, Fungitell, Aspergillus antigen, AFB sputum were all negative. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed later. Bronchoalveolar Lavage cultures initially grew MRSA and patient was started on a course of Linezolid. With further incubation of the lavage specimens, the cultures grew Blastomyces dermatitidis. The patient was started on itraconazole with plan of continuing for 6 months. Discussion: Based on the severity and duration of the problem, patient was diagnosed with isolated chronic pulmonary blastomycosis presenting as a right upper lobe thin walled cavitary lesion. Common presenting symptoms are cough, fever, sputum production, chest pain, shortness of breath, weight loss, night sweats, and hemoptysis. Extrapulmonary manifestations such as skin lesions, osteomyelitis, and involvement of the genitourinary system can be seen. Consolidation, nodules, and masses are most commonly seen in CT chest with cavities less frequently seen when compared to other fungal infections. Differential diagnosis includes Tuberculosis, malignancy, sarcoidosis, fungal infections. Blastomycosis should be considered in patients with cavitary lesions living in endemic areas with cultures being the most sensitive test for detection of infection. Blastomycosis cannot be ruled out based on a negative Blastomyces antibody or antigen test due to its lack of sensitivity. Treatment for pulmonary blastomycosis depends on severity with usage of amphotericin or azoles. Blastomycosis should be kept in mind in cavitary Lung lesions.
