N2  - Rationale: Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is a rare heterogeneous genetic disease resulting in absent or dysfunctional cilia in the respiratory tract. This reduces mucociliary clearance causing mucus accumulation, formation of airway mucus plugs, recurrent bacterial infections, and progressive lung damage. While PCD mucus has high mucin concentrations similar to Cystic Fibrosis, DNA concentrations are lower. In contrast to Cystic Fibrosis, airway clearance in PCD relies solely upon cough clearance, which is determined by mucus rheological properties. Currently, available mucolytics (rhDNase, hypertonic saline, N-acetylcysteine, drypowder mannitol) have demonstrated marginal or no efficacy in improving airway clearance and lung function in PCD. The aim of this study was to characterize PCD sputum rheology and assess the effect of novel inhalable dry-powder microspheres of a dualaction mucolytic (CIL-05) on the rheology of PCD sputum. Method: Sputum samples were collected by spontaneous expectoration from 10 PCD patients following informed consent. Clinical spirometry results (% predicted FEV1, FVC, and absolute FEV1/FVC) were collected. Immediately after collection, saliva was removed and the remaining mucus plugs were homogenized. Sputum was aliquoted, and 0.75-1.5 mM of CIL-05 or water as control was added to each sample. After 30 minutes of incubation and shaking at 37 °C, rheological measurements were performed (Rheomuco). The strain sweep (0.1%-3000%) measurement at a frequency of 1 Hz was used to obtain viscoelastic properties (elasticity (G') viscosity (G') moduli at 10% strain, damping ratio (tan), and critical strain). Results: High patient to patient variability in the viscoelastic properties of PCD sputum was observed (N=10) (Figure). The patient samples' elastic modulus (G') and viscous modulus (G'') values ranged between 0.7-7.9 Pa, and 0.3-2.6 Pa respectively. On average, G' and G' were respectively 3.7X and 3X higher than reported for spontaneous cystic fibrosis sputum and 33X and 30X higher than reported for hypertonic saline-induced healthy sputum (Patarin et al, Sci Rep 2020). There was no significant correlation between the rheological measurements and spirometric values. The effect of the thiolbased mucolytic was assessed in sputum samples from three patients. Viscoelastic properties decreased in all three samples in a dose-dependent manner. Conclusions: While there was significant inter-subject variability, viscoelastic properties of PCD sputum were higher than previously reported values in other pulmonary diseases. CIL-05 reduced viscoelastic properties and has potential as a novel dry-powder mucolytic to improve symptoms and lung function in PCD.
