N2  - Introduction Cough is a common symptom of virtually all respiratory diseases including asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), pulmonary fibrosis, lung cancer, bacterial and viral infections, pneumonia, and most recently, COVID-19. Hence, cough is a significant burden to society deteriorating the physical and mental health of patients. Despite its importance, little is known about the short- and long-term dynamic properties of cough. The aim of this study was to characterize cough over a 14-day period in a small group of patients. Methods We tracked 5 patients with refractory chronic cough using a smartphone-based AI cough monitor (Hyfe Cough Tracker) for 2 weeks. Every cough was separately identified and the time periods between consecutive coughs, the Inter-Cough Intervals (ICIs), was determined. The probability distribution of ICIs was calculated using equal logarithmic bins. Linear regions of the distributions were fitted with a straight line on log-log graphs and the negative slope, or exponent , was used to characterize the ICI time series. Results In all 5 subjects, the distribution of ICI showed similar behavior characterized by a first power law between 1 and 10 s with an exponent . After this power law region, there was a plateau between 10 and approximately 30 s followed by a second power law region stretching out to about 2000 s with a second exponent of (Fig. 1). The complex process underlying cough timing can be described by a random walk analogy. When the random walk has a single absorbing boundary, the distribution of first escape time at the boundary that is equivalent to releasing a cough, is a power law with . These 2 exponents suggest that the short-time scale random walk is supra-diffusive leading to accelerated coughing, whereas the long-time scale random walk is subdiffusive resulting in cough less frequently than a regular random walker escaping through the boundary. We also speculate that may be related to the loss of central inhibitory control that can result in difficulty of stopping coughing once it is provoked. Conclusions Cough displays very complex dynamics. Although random walk analogy can be used to interpret the data, a mechanistic model needs to be developed to better understand the physiological basis of cough dynamics. Figure 1: Top: Inter-Cough Interval (ICI) time series. Bottom: Probability distribution of ICIs (red circles) and the linear fits (blue lines). The exponents, negative slopes of the regression lines, of the two regions are also indicated.
