N2  - Introduction: Cushing's Disease (CD) is caused by pituitary hypersecretion of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH) leading to hypercortisolemia. Cortisol is an immunosuppressive hormone which increases patient susceptibility to opportunistic pulmonary infections. This report highlights the importance of early detection and management of CD to prevent life-threatening opportunistic infection from invasive pulmonary Aspergillosis. Description of Case: A 38yo man with recently diagnosed CD was admitted with chronic cough, hemoptysis and subacute pulmonary infiltrates. He initially presented five months earlier for hypertensive urgency with associated hypokalemia and hyperglycemia. Secondary hypertension workup revealed an elevated cortisol of 46.73ug/dL; however, he wasn't diagnosed with CD. Two months later he developed hemoptysis and was diagnosed with pulmonary coccidioidomycosis. Patient was hospitalized again one month prior to this admission for vertebral compression fractures from T5-T11. Random cortisol was elevated (136 ug/dL) with imaging showing a pituitary adenoma and bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. CD was suspected at the time, but adrenal enzyme inhibitors were not initiated. Chest imaging showed pulmonary infiltrates attributed to his recent coccidioidomycosis diagnosis, and diagnostic work up was deferred. Patient was hospitalized 2 weeks later with hemoptysis and worsened pulmonary infiltrates initially attributed to community acquired pneumonia. Random cortisol was 107ug/dL, and ketoconazole was initiated. He developed acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation and underwent bronchoscopy with fungal cultures positive for Aspergillus fumigatus. Despite treatment with amphotericin, the patient's respiratory status continued to decline and he succumbed to his infection. Discussion: This case highlights the importance of early detection and management of CD. Hypercortisolemia leads to immunosuppression via hyperglycemia- which provides fuel-rich environment for microorganisms, vascular damage- which disrupts the defense barrier allowing pathogen entry, and direct cytokine modification- which inhibits cellular immune response and increases susceptibility to intracellular and opportunistic infections. The degree of hypercortisolemia, including duration and severity of cortisol elevation directly correlates with the risk of opportunistic infections and associated mortality.1,2 This patient's manifestations of CD (obesity, resistant hypertension, hypokalemia, hyperglycemia, and compression fractures) suggests he had long-standing hypercortisolemia increasing his risk for opportunistic infection and death. Definitive CD treatment is pituitary microadenoma resection, but initiation of an adrenal enzyme inhibitor (ketoconazole or metyrapone) is recommended to lower cortisol levels in the interim. Early recognition and management of CD could have improved this patient's outcome not only by reversing the hypercortisolemia but also by having a lower threshold for pursuing an invasive evaluation (e.g. bronchoscopy) to diagnose opportunistic pneumonia.
