N2  - Introduction Serial imaging in cancer patients to detect response to anti-neoplastic therapy at specified images is standard. We describe a patient with prostate cancer who presents with growing opacity concerning for malignant disease or infection. Case report A 70-year-old man, never-smoker with prostate cancer was referred for evaluation of lung opacity detected on serial imaging. He had metastatic castration resistant pancreatic cancer (Gleason 8) status post 6 cycles of docetaxel, currently on androgen deprivation therapy over the past 1.5 years. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest showed lung opacity with a centralized calcified in the right lower lobe with subcarinal and right hilar nodes (figure 1). Patient denied any dyspnea, cough, night chills, fevers, or wheezing. He has no recent upper respiratory infections or hospitalizations for respiratory issues. There was a 10-pound intentional weight loss, as he started riding mountain bicycles daily for an hour. Basic laboratory data was normal.Bronchoscopy revealed a calcified foreign body in the right lower lobe (RLL), lateral segment, likely broncholith with mucous distal to the obstruction. Broncholith was removed with snare and forceps. Pathology revealed calcified granuloma, which was negative for tumor. Quantiferon was positive, and sputum demonstrated Mycobacterium intracellulare. No therapy was started for MAI due to asymptomatic nature of disease. Additional history revealed history of positive PPD since childhood after exposure to a positive TB relative. He declined isoniazid and B6. Patient was told he may have blastomycosis about twenty years ago, but treatment was not given. Antigen levels were negative at the time of evaluation. Repeat CT of the chest 5 months later demonstrated stable scaring in the RLL, as well as sub-centimeter supraclavicular, mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes. Pulmonary function testing revealed normal spirometry, lung volumes and diffusion. Discussion Broncholithiasis notoriously mimics cancer. It is characterized by presence of calcified material within the tracheobronchial tree. Calcification is usually initiated at the lymph nodes as a result of granulomatous process such as tuberculosis, fungal or occupational elements. Migration of the calcified material into the airway is hypothesized to dislodge into the airway. Patients frequently present with cough, hemoptysis, or wheezing. Although many have concomitant tuberculosis, up to a third may not demonstrate underlying disease. Histoplasma capsulatum, Coccidoides immitis and necrotizing granulomatous lymphadenitis have been associated. Management of broncholith depends on the location and symptoms of the broncholith. Although many broncholiths can be removed bronchoscopically, some may need surgical intervention.
