N2  - Background Tuberculous pericardial effusion (TPE) is a rare complication of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) infection. We present an atypical presentation of isolated TPE. Case An 84-year-old male presented with epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting. He did not have night sweats, weight loss, fever, chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, cough, or previous MTB infection. Evaluation for a gastroenterological cause of his pain was unrevealing, however, the CT showed a large pericardial effusion, bilateral pleural effusions, and a consolidation in the right upper lobe of the lung. A TTE showed right atrial and ventricular collapse, which was concerning for tamponade. A pericardial window was performed, where 1100cc of blood-tinged fluid was drained and showed many lymphocytes and RBCs on cytology. Decision-making The patient was discharged on colchicine for pericarditis and empiric antibiotic coverage for aspiration pneumonia. One month later, the pericardial fluid culture speciated MTB complex. The patient was referred to the DOH and started on antituberculous treatment. Early initiation of treatment is imperative to prevent progression to constrictive pericarditis, which carries a mortality rate as high as 85%. This case emphasizes the importance of extra-abdominal differentials for abdominal pain. Conclusion Abdominal pain is a rare manifestation of TPE, highlighting the importance of multimodal cardiac diagnostic imaging in patients who present with atypical or isolated symptoms. [Formula presented]
