N2  - Introduction: HIV related kidney disease has a wide histological phenotype and has been succinctly categorized depending on the kidney compartment affected in the 2018 KDIGO controversies conference paper. There are however various other infective and non-infective associations with HIV infections which can manifest initially or solely in kidney disease. This case serves as a reminder to look closer into underlying aetiology when the clinical presentation and work up raises suspicions of another less likely culprit. Methods: A 28-year-old black male patient presented with a history of backache and generalized body swelling for 5 days. There was no history of weight loss, cough, or night sweats. There was no history of features suggestive of auto-immune disease or a significant family history. He had a 6-year pack year hx of smoking. On clinical examination the BP was 123/87 mmHg, HR 101bpm, temperature 35.9 0C, RR 18 bpm he had grade 2 pitting oedema. The cardiac and abdominal examination was unremarkable. There were no other significant systemic findings. Results: Work-up began with urine analysis, thereafter investigations were done for infective, auto-immune, deposition diseases. An abdominal ultrasound showed 14.5cm and 13.5cm kidney with a mention of loss of cortico-medullary differentiation. There was over 16g of proteinuria and the HIV test was positive with a CD4 count of 572cells/uL. ANA, ANCA and APLA2RABand SFLC were negative. C3 and C4 were normal. Urine LAM was negative. Kidney biopsy revealed salmon-pink deposit with apple green birefringence under polarized light and which stained positive with Congo Red stain. Electron microscopy showed randomly arranged aggregates of uniformly thin fibrils within the mesangium and glomerular basement membrane. The immunofluorescence showed predominant deposition of AA amyloid in the mesangium. After multiple attempts to gain sputum for tuberculosis testing, we finally retrieved a sample with sputum induction which confirmed Mycobacterium Tuberculosis which was sensitive to rifampicin. Conclusions: This is a case is of nephrotic syndrome in a newly diagnosed HIV positive patient caused by AA amyloidosis secondary to pulmonary tuberculosis. It serves as a reminder to actively investigate and pursue the aetiology of unexplained kidney dysfunction in treatment naive, advanced HIV patients and not to reflexively attribute it to classic HIVAN. This approach could reveal treatable and/or curable aetiologies of the kidney dysfunction and spare patients of being subjected to long term kidney replacement therapy. No conflict of interest
