N2  - Introduction: Most tuberculosis (TB) in solid organ transplantation is due to reactivation of latent TB in the recipient. Donor-positive TB remains very rare, as active TB is a contraindication to organ donation. However, since donor screening is based on past medical history, epidemiological risk and radiological imaging, undiagnosed TB can be missed. Management of donor-positive TB in transplant recipients remains unclear with no definitive guidelines. Methods: We describe here a case of retrospectively-identified TB in a renal transplant donor. Results: The recipient was a 32-year old UK-born lady of South Asian heritage with end-stage kidney disease of unknown aetiology on maintenance haemodialysis. She was a non-smoker with a normal pre-transplant chest x-ray (CXR). The donor was an age-appropriate woman of European origin with diabetes, who had a spontaneous intracranial haemorrhage; she had no history to suggest active TB. The recipient was classified a low immunological risk as the calculated reaction frequency was 0%, though it was a 1-2-2 mismatch. The transplant procedure was uncomplicated with good renal function thereafter. Subsequently, a pre-transplantation biopsy of the donor lung revealed a thin-walled necrotic nodule with abundant acid-fast bacilli on microscopy, consistent with active TB. PCR testing confirmed the presence of mycobacterium tuberculosis, with no isoniazid or rifampicin resistance mutations. At follow-up, the recipient had no cough/breathlessness and a normal CXR. Early morning urine (EMU) TB PCR and a blood interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA) were negative. After multidisciplinary discussions, including all units that received organs, she started treatment with isoniazid 300mg daily, with pyridoxine, for 6 months. Over two months post-transplant the recipient remains asymptomatic with a stable renal function and normal WBC and LFT. Repeat TB EMU PCR and culture remain negative to date. Conclusions: The question of what to do in transplant recipients at risk of reactivation of latent TB is well-rehearsed. The situation in this patient is quite different. The donor had clear evidence of active TB at the time of donation. What then is the risk to the recipient and how should this be managed? The questions we considered were: (i) The burden of infective organisms - Donor disease was localized with no evidence of dissemination (although bone marrow or other tissue samples were not available for analysis). It was felt unlikely therefore that the recipient would have received a significant renal mycobacterium bacillus burden. (ii) How should possible reactivation be countered? The consensus was that single-agent chemoprophylaxis should be sufficient in view of the pauci-bacillary nature of the infection. Rifampicin co-administration could have afforded a shorter course but was avoided due to drug interactions, especially with tacrolimus. (iii) The risks of isoniazid, including hepatotoxicity and peripheral neuropathy, were considered – hence clinical and laboratory monitoring were set in place. (iv) Transparency and counselling - the strategy was discussed and agreed with the patient. Finally, although she remains negative to date, extended monitoring for TB disease was instituted with a plan for repeated cultures over an extended follow-up period. No conflict of interest
