N2  - Pneumonia is one the main causes of death worldwide, and it is mainly due to the increase of antibiotic microbiological resistance[1]. Photodynamic therapy (PDT), which uses the combination of light and a photosensitizer (PS) drug to cause damages in biological target, has emerged as a non-invasive clinical approach for different kind of treatment to which development of resistance is reported to be unlikely [2]. The purpose of this study was to verify if photodynamic inactivation (PDI) is able to being effective against Streptococcus pneumoniae, when phantom barriers are used as optical attenuators that mimic biological tissue, illuminating at 808 nm wavelength and using Indocyanine green as a PS. Five phantoms with optical properties of the skin were made using cosmetic base and Al2O3 particles to mimic absorption and scattering coefficients [3]. A 200 diode lasers source panel with an irradiance of 78 ± 10 mW/cm2 was used to perform the experimental assays. The optical transmission attenuation as increasing the phantom thickness was measured using an isotropic fiber and wide field imaging system. Dark, light and PDI groups were tested, for PDI groups a logistic model was used to model the bacterial death. We found that even with just 3.2% of the incident light, transmitted through the 37.10 mm thickness phantom, led to PDI effects that completely 7log10 reduction of S pneumoniae after 48 minutes of irradiation. The results of the present study suggest that ICG is a promising PS, and use combined with external illumination and NIR light may result in antimicrobial effective parameters even under extra-body irradiation.
