N2  - Background: Induced sputum (IS) is less invasive, simple, requires inexpensive equipment, and gives good sputum quality. The bacteria confirmed diagnosis was difficult in suspected PTB patients who have smear-negative or had no sputum. Methods: We conducted a cross-sectional descriptive study. The study recruited 309 subjects aged 15 years and older. Subject selection criteria: 1) Clinical symptoms of suspected PTB and/or x-ray lesions suspected of tuberculosis. 2) At least 2 smear-negative with spontaneous sputum or had no sputum. Exclusion criteria: 1) Contraindications with IS include medical conditions that went badly if coughing a lot or were at risk of dangerous bronchospasm. 2) Patients could not coordinate to cough actively. The IS procedures were done in an open-air ventilated with an ultrasonic nebulizer. The patient performed IS on the first day if the smear and Xpert MTB/RIF were negative they continued a bronchoscopy on the next day. Results: Of the 309 patients, IS was successful in 303 (98,1%) patients. Of the 303 IS successful patients, five were smear-positive, and the remaining 298 patients were suspected of smear-negative PTB. Among 298 suspected smear-negative PTB, 106 patients underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage. Xpert MTB/RIF of IS sample was positive in 39/298 (13.09%), of which there was 2/39 (5.13%) rifampicin resistance. Liquide culture of IS sample was positive in 43/298 (14.43%) patients. In 106 patients who performed both sampling methods, the result of liquid culture was: 9 patients positive with BAL, 8 patients positive with IS sample, and 7 patients positive with both samples. The Kappa coefficient was 0.81, p=0.0000, 2 methods had a very good consensus. Conclusions: IS had a very high success rate and helped 14.43% additional detection of PTB bacteria confirmed in suspected smear-negative PTB patients. Induced sputum: IS; Pulmonary tuberculosis: PTB.
