N2  - Background: Right-sided infective endocarditis (IE) accounts for approximately 5 – 10% of all IE cases. The vast majority involve the tricuspid valve (TV), with pulmonic valve involvement accounting for less than 10% cases. Tricuspid valve IE (TVIE) usually occurs in patients with a cardiovascular implantable electronic devices (CIED), central venous catheters or in congenital heart disease. It is most frequently observed in intravenous (i.v) drug abusers, especially in patients with concomitant immunodeficiency. Staphylococcus aureus is the predominant causative organism in TVIE. In-hospital mortality is approximately 7%. We present a case of isolated TVIE with no associated risk factors. Case report: A 68-year-old gentleman, a known hypertensive since 12 years presented with the history of fever with chills, cough with scanty white expectoration and loss of weight and appetite for 2 months. It was associated with dyspnea, NYHA class II (New York Heart association). He had no history of previous surgery, intravenous drug abuse or hospitalization. His saturation was 88% on room air and liver was palpable. There were bilateral basal crepitations on auscultation. Investigations revealed anemia and azotemia with microscopic hematuria. Ultrasound showed hepatomegaly with grade 1 fatty liver. Transthoracic Echocardiogram (echo) showed vegetation attached to anterior tricuspid leaflet with prolapse (0.8x1.2cm), severe Tricuspid regurgitation (TR), mild pulmonary hypertension, and mild mitral and aortic regurgitation with normal biventricular function. Computerized tomography (CT) chest showed features suggestive of septic emboli to lung. Transesophageal echo revealed multiple TV vegetations with severe TR. Blood cultures were positive for methicillin sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. Coronary angiogram showed triple vessel disease. Results: A diagnosis of TVIE with septic emboli to lung was made. Patient was treated with appropriate i.v antibiotics for 2 weeks followed by TV Replacement with bio-prosthetic valve and Coronary artery bypass grafting using three grafts. Post-operatively i.v antibiotics were continued for 2 more weeks. Echo showed normally functioning prosthetic valve with TV-MPG (mean pressure gradient) of 4 mm Hg, no TR and normal biventricular function. The patient was discharged after a week and continued on oral antibiotics for a month. The patient is on regular follow-up and is doing well. Conclusion: The diagnosis of right-sided IE is tricky owing to anomalous anatomic features of the right heart that may be difficult to distinguish from vegetations; septic emboli are pulmonary, as opposed to systemic, and clinically less obvious until they cause pulmonary infarcts and abscesses and early pulmonary radiographic findings may be mistaken for pneumonia. TV involvement in a patient with no predisposing conditions and a structurally normal heart, as occurred in our patient, is a clinical rarity in which the diagnosis can be quite challenging. Prompt diagnosis and treatment have favorable prognosis. [Formula presented] Transesophageal echo showing multiple tricuspid valve vegetations (arrow).
