N2  - Background: Extracellular vesicles (EVs) are nano-sized particles released from different cell types that play critical roles in intercellular communication and disease pathogenesis [1]. Neutrophil-derived EVs containing myeloperoxidase (MPO) have been identified in inflammatory conditions other than cystic fibrosis (CF). Because MPO is a key player in CF neutrophilic airway inflammation, the aims of this study were to isolate and characterize EVs from sputum of children with CF and analyze expression of MPO in sputum-derived EVs during treatment for CF pulmonary exacerbation (PEx). Methods: EVs were isolated from expectorated sputum of people with CF collected before and after 14 days of intravenous antibiotic therapy for PEx. EVs were characterized by nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA) and electron microscopy (EM) following international guidelines [2]. Western blot analysis of EV protein extracts was used to quantify expression of EV canonical proteins CD-63, CD-9, and flotillin-1 (FLOT1), as well as MPO and interleukin-8 (IL-8). MPO expression in EVs was quantified as MPO/FLOT1 ratio. IL-8 levels in sputum were measured by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. Results: Ten children (8 female) with a mean age of 13.5 (range 8.0–16.9) were included. Mean percentage predicted forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1pp) was 59.8 ± 9.6% before treatment for PEx and 71.8 ± 11.6% after. There were no differences in sputum-derived EV concentration (1.7e*11 ± 1.1e*11 particles/mL before vs PEx therapy. 2.0e*11 ± 1.4e*11 particles/mL after; p = 0.44) or size (151.2 ± 26.6 nm before PEx therapy vs. 145.7 ± 31.8 nm after; p = 0.47]. EV MPO/FLOT1 ratios were significantly lower before than after PEx (3.1 ± 1.3 vs 6.0 ± 1.12, p < 0.001, paired t-test). EV MPO/FLOT1 correlated with FEV1pp at all timepoints (r = 0.64, p < 0.01, Pearson) and changes in EV MPO/FLOT1 with changes in FEV1pp (r = 0.63, p < 0.05) with treatment. IL-8 proteinwas not detected in the EVs. Sputum IL-8 levels did not correlate with EV MPO/FLOT1. Therewas no difference in EV MPO content between children treated for Staphylococcus aureus (n = 5) or Pseudomonas aeruginosa (n = 5) infection. Conclusions: CF airways contain EVs that carry MPO as cargo. The increase in EV MPO expression (MPO/FLOT1) after treatment for PEx suggests greater release of MPO from EVs before initiation of PEx therapy than after. The contribution of EV-derived MPO to CF lung disease and airway inflammation warrants further investigation.
