N2  - Background: Aspergillus fumigatus is the dominant fungal pathogen colonizing the airways of up to 50% of adults with cystic fibrosis (CF). In addition to occurrence of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis in some patients, the presence of A. fumigatus has been associated with greater risk of pulmonary exacerbations and faster lung function decline. Detection of Aspergillus colonization and infection is therefore an important feature of routine microbiological surveillance in CF. Since the wide introduction of elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor (ELX/TEZ/IVA), people with CF typically describe lower sputum volumes and in many cases struggle to produce sputum samples for microbiological culture. Cough swabs are routinely used as an alternative in pediatrics and are known to have lower sensitivity for bacterial pathogens, but sensitivity for fungal infections is unknown. We aimed to determine whether cough swabs would offer a reliable alternative to sputum culture for detection of A. fumigatus in people with CF. Methods: This study was performed as a part of the Telmisartan to Reduce AIDS-Related Fibrotic and Inflammatory Contributors study (www.trific.org) Table 1. (abstract 495): Breakdown of positive results for patients who provided paired cough swab and sputum samples, according to whether the patient was receiving cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator modulator therapy.Adults with CF provided spontaneously expectorated sputum samples and cough swabs collected at the same visit. The sputum sample was prepared using the high-volume culture method and applied directly onto Sabouraud agar plate culture medium. The cough swab was applied directly onto the plate. Both samples were cultured at 30°C for 14 days. Results: Of 103 patients recruited to date, 31 (30%) were unable to expectorate any sputum; 24 (23%) had growth of A. fumigatus in either sample. Fifty percent of unmodulated patients and 30% of patients taking ELX/TEZ/IVA grew A. fumigatus (Table 1) To date, 63 patients have provided 76 paired cough swab and sputum samples; 14 patients have provided two sets of paired samples becausewe are sampling our patients longitudinally. Twenty-two of these patients (29%) had positive sputum cultures for A. fumigatus, but only five had positive cough swab cultures, of which four were also sputum culture positive. Based on this, sensitivity to detect A. fumigatus (identified by any culture method) was 96% for sputum and 22% for cough swabs. Conclusions: Cough swabs show a lower rate of detection of A. fumigatus than sputum. Molecular techniques may be more sensitive but are not validated or widely available.We conclude that cough swabs are unable to detect A. fumigatus reliably by culture and are not suitable for detecting or excluding fungal infection. We will continue to follow these patients longitudinally and investigate whether molecular techniques provide additional sensitivity.
