N2  - Background: Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infects the upper and lower airways in young children, causing cell shedding, mucus hypersecretion, and bronchiolitis, which can produce severe disease in cystic fibrosis (CF). Individuals with CF accumulate thick mucus and have impaired mucociliary clearance, resulting in high susceptibility to respiratory viruses. Highly effective CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) modulators have greatly improved the quality of life of people with CF, because they reverse the aberrant mucus properties and restore airway clearance. We examined the effects of RSV infection in disease-control and CF primary human bronchial epithelial cell cultures and the effects of elexacaftor/ tezacaftor/ivacaftor (ELX/TEZ/IVA) treatment on RSV mobility. Methods: Human bronchial epithelial cells from non-CF donors (NCF) and CF donors with the F508del mutation (CF) were grown on air-liquid interface and treated with vehicle or ELX/TEZ/IVA for 3 days before infection with green fluorescent protein (GFP)-tagged RSV. Because thick mucus adheres to CF airway surfaces, viral access to the cells was examined in the presence or absence of apical mucus. Viral mobilitywas quantified as percentage area of GFP signal in infected cultures (e.g., viral spread). Airway surface liquid (ASL) height was measured via confocal microscopy. Quantitative reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR) was used to quantify viral, CFTR, and epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) messenger ribonucleic acid (mRNA) expression. Biophysical properties of mucuswere assessed according to percentage solids and cilia dynamics via video tracking. Results: In RSV-infected NCF cultures, ASL height was significantly greater than control. In contrast, ASL height failed to increase in CF cultures in response to RSV infection. RT-qPCR showed no change in CFTR mRNA expression between mock and RSV-infected NCF cells, but β-ENaC and λ- ENaC expressionwere decreased, suggesting that ASL hydrationwas linked to ENaC downregulation. Analysis of GFP signal revealed significant greater viral mobility in NCF than CF cells. When NCF and CF were treated with ELX/TEZ/IVA, viral mobility was significantly greater than in the vehicle groups. ELX/TEZ/IVA treatment of CF cells resulted in lower percentage of mucus solids (wet/dry ratio) and greater mucociliary transport. Removal of mucus before infection increased viral mobility in NCF and CF cells, independent of ELX/TEZ/IVA treatment. Greater viral mobility correlated with higher viral mRNA levels in NCF and CF cells treated with ELX/TEZ/IVA than in CF cells without treatment, which can be explained by the fact that in vitro systems cannot clear and eradicate particles like the lungs can. Therefore, viral mobility without annihilation of the virus increases the odds of infecting new cells. More work needs to be conducted to generate an open system that will permit clearance of particles. Conclusions: Children with CF experience more severe disease from RSV infection than the general population, in part because their clearance of pathogens is impaired.We demonstrated that RSV infection of NCF cultures resulted in ASL hydration, which correlated with greater viral mobility. In contrast, RSV-infected CF cultures failed to hydrate the ASL, which correlated with less viral mobility. ELX/TEZ/IVA treatment restored viral mobility in CF cultures and further increased viral mobility in NCF cultures, suggesting that CFTR modulators may have positive effects on RSV infection for CF and NCF individuals. We anticipate that greater viral mobility will correlate with lower viral load in vivo, as suggested by greater mucociliary transport and lower percentage of mucus solids after ELX/TEZ/ IVA treatment, as shown in vitro
