N2  - Background: Bacteriophages are a promising treatment alternative for multidrug-resistant bacterial infections. People with cystic fibrosis (PwCF) are often chronically colonized with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. In clinical trials and compassionate use cases, phage therapy has proven effective at reducing P. aeruginosa titers and improving health outcomes in PwCF. Although effective at treating the infection, the consequences of high doses of foreign viruses on the mucosal surface of the airways has not been studied. We hypothesized that interactions between the lung epithelium and phages occur during therapy that could have unintended outcomes. Figure 1. (abstract 413): (A) Immunofluorescence images obtained from dissected human lung tissue; (B) MUC5B is highly expressed by goblet cells of submucosal gland ductal epithelial cells (SMGECs); (C) MUC5AC is highly expressed by airway goblet cells; (D) cystic fibrosis (CF) transmembrane conductance regulator function of SMGECs robustly improved upon treatment with elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor in ΔF508 homozygous CF patient (CF-cells were pretreated with VX-445 (2 μM) and VX-661 (2 μM) for 48 hours); (E-F) cell viability assay in 3D airway scaffold at days 0 and 7, respectively; (G) scanning electron microscopy image of the co-culture lung airway scaffold that consists of (H) human lung fibroblasts and (I) airway epithelial cells.Methods: To understand the effects of phage therapy in the CF airways, we used a panel of P. aeruginosa phages that are effective at killing clinical isolates in vitro and in clinical use. We used these phages to examine interactions with CFBE41o- cells, a CF airway epithelial cell line that, when grown at the air-liquid interface, polarizes and forms a pseudostratified epithelium. Cytokine secretion after phage treatments was evaluated in apical and basolateral media using an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. To determine factors that account for differences in cytokine induction, we quantified phage internalization in the airway epithelium using plaque assay. To visualize phage interactions with the respiratory epithelium and intracellular trafficking,we conducted confocal microscopy assays using fluorescently labeled phages. Results: Some P. aeruginosa phages in our panel induce pro-inflammatory cytokines, suggesting that phages differentially activate pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) and the innate immune response. In phage internalization assays, we detected that very low levels of phage enter airway epithelial cells and that these levels do not correlate with proinflammatory cytokine induction. Our imaging results indicated that phages that induce more pro-inflammatory cytokines associate better with the plasma membrane, suggesting that an extracellular PRR is responsible for phage detection. Current experiments are focused on identifying possible phage PRRs using various immunomodulatory ligand screens. Conclusions: The information obtained from our research will help identify phage pathogen-associated molecular patterns and will be crucial to personalize phage therapy to the immunological milieu that could maximize therapeutic benefit. Overall, our studies suggest that the airway epithelium recognizes phage particles and demonstrates the need for more studies when considering the use of phage therapy to ensure its safety in PwCF.
