N2  - Background: Analysis of the biochemical and biophysical properties of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) of children with cystic fibrosis (CF) has identified several markers of early disease. These markers include mucin and deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) concentration and the presence and abundance of insoluble, viscoelastic mucus flakes. The advent of highly effective modulator therapy (HEMT) in young patients raises the prospect of reducing marker concentration and abundance in BALF and reversing patterns of CF disease progression. Accordingly, we hypothesized that mucin and DNA concentration and flake abundance would decrease after HEMT treatment initiation. Methods: Ten patients who underwent annual surveillance bronchoscopy and BAL as part of the AREST-CF Synergy initiative were studied before (mean age 2.9) and after (mean age 4.3) initiation of ivacaftor. Nine control BALF samples from untreated people with CF (mean age 3.4) were also studied. Mucin concentration was determined via light scattering and refractometry. DNA concentration was determined via PicoGreen assay. BALF and flake biophysics were determined via particle-tracking microrheology (PTMR) using 1-μm-diameter fluorescent polystyrene spheres. All methods are described in [1]. Results: The relative proportion of viscoelastic signal associated with insoluble mucus flakes was lower in HEMT-treated subjects (49%) than untreated subjects (80%) ( p < 0.01), although this was also true for HEMTtreated subjects before treatment (54%) ( p < 0.05). This contributed to an apparent, although not statistically significant, difference in overall BALF complex viscosity (η*) between post-treatment (mean η* = 0.018 P. aeruginosa) and control (mean η* = 0.052 P. aeruginosa) subjects ( p = 0.11) as well as pre-treatment subjects (η* = 0.020 P. aeruginosa, p = 0.05). No biochemical differences were statistically significant, possibly because of the small sample sizes of each cohort. Conclusions: PTMR analysis detected a smaller viscoelastic signal in BALF from children with CF before and after HEMT than in controls with CF. This decrease in total signal contributed to an apparently lower average viscosity of BALF from pre- and post-treatment subjects than in controls. Analysis of these samples is not complete because four pre- and posttreatment subjects and five controls await processing. Although not finalized, these results demonstrate that HEMT may reduce flake burden and overall viscoelasticity of airway mucus in children with CF.
