N2  - Background: Biofilm viscoelasticity is correlated with susceptibility to mechanical and chemical stress, and the cystic fibrosis (CF) airway facilitates robust biofilm formation. Hyperconcentrated, viscoelastic mucus promotes chronic inflammation and infection, resulting in higher mucin and deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) concentrations. Muco-modulating therapies decrease viscoelasticity via cleaving or by disrupting interactions between the polymeric components (mucins and DNA). Similar to mucus, biofilm viscoelasticity is regulated by polymers including polysaccharides and DNA and depends upon the length scale. Macrorheology characterizes bulk properties relevant to structural rigidity, microrheology describes biofilm heterogeneity and the polymer network, and nanorheology quantifies nanoparticle diffusion through the biofilm. We hypothesize that biofilm viscoelasticity will vary with length scale and that macro-, micro-, and nanorheology are essential to understanding the host– pathogen relationship in chronic CF respiratory infections. Together these data provide a better understanding of biofilm mechanics and inform the design of better antibiofilm therapies. Methods: Pseudomonas aeruginosa biofilms were grown in human airway mucus and evaluated using cone and plate rheometry (C&P), particletracking microrheology (PTM), and a novel one-dimensional migration assay (1-DMA). The diameter and surface chemistry of the fluorescent tracer particles used in PTM and 1-DMAwere varied to evaluate a range of sizes and probe–biofilm interactions. Biofilms were also treated with DNase or dithiothreitol (DTT) to compromise structural integrity and then evaluated with all three assays. Susceptibility to tobramycinwas evaluated via disruption and colony counting. Results: Bulk measurements of P. aeruginosa biofilms with C&P agreed with previously published work and differ from bulk mucus rheology. Viscoelasticity measured via PTM varied with probe size and surface chemistry, although confocal microscopy suggested similar distribution of probes throughout the biofilm. The 1-DMA indicated that neutrally charged dextran probes had minimal interactions with the biofilm, whereas poly-lysine (cationic) caused entanglement. Treating biofilms with poly-lysine increased biofilm macrorheology. Probes smaller than 20 nm in diameter diffused through the biofilm with water. Cleavage of polymeric components with DNase or DTT decreased microscopic moduli and universally improved probe diffusion through the biofilm. Further, lower viscoelastic moduli correlated with greater susceptibility to tobramycin. Conclusions: Biofilm viscoelasticity is associated with resistance to mechanical and chemical challenge. Characterizing the length scale– dependent biophysical properties of biofilms will improve treatment design. The ideal antibiofilm agent would thoroughly penetrate the biofilm, decrease viscoelastic moduli, and improve antibiotic efficacy. Targeting the polymeric components in the biofilm with micro- or nanoscale treatments that do not interact with the mesh will improve biofilm degradation and subsequent clearance. Nanoscale agents that are biofilm-inert diffused with water, suggesting that small, noninteracting agents may penetrate and degrade the polymeric mesh efficiently and ultimately improve antibiotic action
