N2  - SESSION TITLE: Variety in Chest Infections Case Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/19/2022 12:45 pm - 01:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Rapidly growing mycobacterium (RGM) are organisms that grow in a subculture within one week and Mycobacterium abscessus is the most pathogenic of the group. The diagnosis of RGM infection is important to prevent further progression of the disease. Here we discuss a case of a 56-year-old female with productive cough with hemoptysis, weight loss, and night sweats who was diagnosed with M. abscessus. CASE PRESENTATION: A 56-year-old female with a history of non-small cell lung cancer status post left lower lobe lobectomy with current ongoing workup for recurrence, COPD, nocturnal hypoxemia, chronic hepatitis C infection status post-treatment presented with symptoms of worsening cough associated with brown colored sputum, night sweats for 2-3 weeks and episodes of hemoptysis. No history of recent travel outside the United States or sick contacts. Of note, the patient was getting tested with yearly PPD at her workplace. The patient recently relocated within the United States and was being evaluated for recurrence of her non-small cell lung cancer. During this time, her vitals were within acceptable limits. Labs appeared to be grossly normal. Most recent CT of her chest revealed multiple solid nodules spiculated in appearance; cavitary lung lesions in the left lung, right upper lobe and right lower lobe; mediastinal lymphadenopathy consistent with metastatic disease. MRI of the brain was negative for metastatic changes. The patient underwent biopsy for PD-L1 and NGS testing prior to initiation of cancer treatment. Biopsy specimen showed findings consistent with necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and positive for rare acid-fast bacilli on AFB special stain. Immunohistochemical stains showed reactive pneumocytes. GMS stain was negative for fungal organisms. To evaluate this further, three specimens for AFB smear and culture were sent at appropriate intervals. Sputum for MTB PCR was also sent. On day 5, AFB smears were negative for acid-fast bacilli and MTB PCR was negative. However, within 5 days of culture, the specimens started growing nontubercular mycobacterium and finally resulted in speciation with mycobacterium abscessus. Her tumor was found to be positive for PD-L1 and is scheduled to receive stereotactic radiation therapy followed by immunotherapy. The patient fulfilled the clinical, radiological criteria and microbiological criteria for Mycobacterium abscessus and is currently awaiting sensitivities to be initiated on antibiotic therapy. DISCUSSION: Diagnosis of RGM is through extrapolation of the clinical, radiological, and microbiologic criteria for diagnosing nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary diseases. Identification of clinically significant M.abscessus is important to prevent rapid progression. CONCLUSIONS: RGM tend to cause pulmonary infection in patients with underlying lung diseases. A high index of suspicion must be exercised for early identification and treatment. Reference #1: Griffith DE, Girard WM, Wallace RJ Jr. Clinical features of pulmonary disease caused by rapidly growing mycobacteria. An analysis of 154 patients. Am Rev Respir Dis. 1993;147(5):1271-1278. doi:10.1164/ajrccm/147.5.1271 Reference #2: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, et al. An Official ATS/IDSA Statement: Diagnosis, treatment and prevention of nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2007; 175:367. Copyright © 2007 American Thoracic Society. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Brian Bustos No relevant relationships by Pooja Shekar No relevant relationships by Henry Uradu No relevant relationships by Hilary Wagner
