N2  - SESSION TITLE: Procedures in Chest Infections Case Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/19/2022 12:45 pm - 01:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS) can cause infectious and noninfectious conditions to dramatically worsen or appear after initiation of antiretroviral therapy (ART). Up to 13% of patients develop IRIS upon initiation of ART. We present a patient with mediastinal lymphadenopathy who was found to have IRIS associated with two species of nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM). CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old African American female with AIDS who began ART 6 weeks prior to presentation was admitted after a gunshot wound to the left lower extremity (LLE). Her CD4 on admission was 83 cells/microliter with HIV viral load of 88 copies/milliliter. Later during her hospital stay, she started to spike intermittent fevers and became tachycardic. She did not have leukocytosis. Blood cultures, urine cultures, LLE wound cultures were done and she was started on antibiotics. She continued to have fevers and tachycardia despite negative cultures. Patient did not have a cough or shortness of breath but she underwent chest X-ray to rule out pneumonia for fever work up which revealed left perihilar disease. Patient underwent chest CT which showed bilateral mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy with compression of the left hilar bundle. This raised suspicion of IRIS lymphoma or sarcoidosis. IGRA was negative and sputum acid fast bacilli (AFB) staining was also negative. Pulmonology was consulted and endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) guided transbronchial needle aspiration (TBNA) of the mediastinal lymph nodes (LNs) was performed, which resulted in 1 LN with a granuloma, 1 LN with inflammation, and 2 normal LNs. Flow cytometry did not show evidence of lymphoma. Blood AFB cultures remained negative. Needle rinse fluid cultures from the LN with granuloma grew AFBs with follow up nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) negative for Mycobacterium tuberculosis. However, they tested positive for M. kansasii and M. avian complex by DNA probing. They eventually grew M. kansasii and M. avium complex and the diagnosis of NTM - IRIS was established. DISCUSSION: HIV patients who are coinfected with NTM may develop NTM - IRIS after ART initiation. It may present as mediastinal lymphadenopathy with constitutional symptoms like fever and tachycardia as in this patient and may mimic lymphoma or sarcoidosis. While IRIS lymphoma remains high on the differential in this case, NTM - IRIS is far more common. This case illustrates the importance of EBUS - TBNA and culture of needle rinse fluid in patients suspected to have NTM without which establishing the diagnosis may be difficult. CONCLUSIONS: NTM - IRIS while uncommon can have a variable presentation. High index of suspicion is necessary. Multiple species can be involved as in this case. EBUS - TBNA and cultures of LNs along with DNA probing and/or nucleic acid sequencing may aid in improving diagnostic sensitivity and accuracy. Reference #1: Müller M, Wandel S, Colebunders R, Attia S, Furrer H, Egger M. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome in patients starting antiretroviral therapy for HIV infection: A systematic review and meta-analysis. The Lancet Infectious Diseases. 2010;10(4):251-261. doi:10.1016/s1473-3099(10)70026-8 Reference #2: Phillips P, Bonner S, Gataric N et al. Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Immune Reconstitution Syndrome in HIV-Infected Patients: Spectrum of Disease and Long-Term Follow-Up. Clinical Infectious Diseases. 2005;41(10):1483-1497. doi:10.1086/497269 Reference #3: Jagielski T, Minias A, van Ingen J et al. Methodological and Clinical Aspects of the Molecular Epidemiology of Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Other Mycobacteria. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2016;29(2):239-290. doi:10.1128/cmr.00055-15 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Christina Chai No relevant relationships by Lea Fisher No relevant relationships by Namratha Seetharam Meda No relevant relationships by Harshkumar Patel
