N2  - SESSION TITLE: Complications of Thoracic Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 01:35 pm - 02:35 pm INTRODUCTION: We present a case of non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis with recurrent exacerbations caused by an unusual acid-fast organism. CASE PRESENTATION: A 73-year-old female with reflux disease and post-infectious/idiopathic bronchiectasis (Bronchiectasis severity index – 6) with prior history of recurrent exacerbations presented to a tertiary bronchiectasis center with a 1-month history of worsening cough, sputum production, and weight loss. Over the previous few months, sputum cultures had grown various organisms in different samples, including Staphylococcus aureus, Acinetobacter calcoaceticus, and "presumptive” Mycobacterium chelonae. A repeat lower respiratory culture of induced sputum detected an unidentified organism that was positive on acid-fast stain. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplification of the 16S ribosomal RNA (rRNA) gene identified it as Tsukamurella species. The patient was treated based on antimicrobial susceptibility, following which her symptoms resolved. There were no acid-fast organisms seen on her smear and culture thereafter. DISCUSSION: Tsukamurella species are gram-positive rod-shaped, partially acid-fast bacteria found in soil, water, and sludge (1). The literature on Tsukamurella infections is sparse, and it is generally considered to be a rare opportunistic pathogen causing catheter-related bloodstream infections. Prior cases implicating Tsukamurella in pneumonia and COPD exacerbations have been described (2). To date, only a handful of cases have been reported in bronchiectasis patients, although this paucity of cases may likely be due to misidentification issues. Genotypic techniques like 16S rRNA gene PCR testing are needed to accurately identify Tsukamurella, but these are not routinely performed in clinical laboratories. As such, it is frequently misidentified for pathogens with similar phenotypic properties such as nontuberculosis mycobacteria (NTM). This is particularly important to consider in patients with bronchiectasis, given that NTM are among the bacterial agents most frequently implicated in the disease. Despite sharing similarities on phenotypic testing, infections from NTM and Tsukamurella species may differ in ways that are clinically meaningful. For example, some respiratory Tsukamurella strains have shown resistance to macrolides and rifampin, which are commonly used in the treatment of pulmonary NTM infections (3). Therefore, identifying the causative pathogen accurately is imperative. CONCLUSIONS: Tsukamurella is an underrecognized pathogen that can be misidentified for NTM. A 16S rRNA gene PCR test should be considered in ambiguous situations. Reference #1: Safaei S, Fatahi-Bafghi M, Pouresmaeil O. Role of Tsukamurella species in human infections: first literature review. New Microbes New Infect. 2017;22:6-12. Published 2017 Oct 10. doi:10.1016/j.nmni.2017.10.002 Reference #2: Amara Elori EI, Méndez Ocaña R, Gil Brusola A, et al. Tsukamurella species isolation in bronchiectasis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease patients: A retrospective case series. In: Respiratory Infections. European Respiratory Society; 2018:PA5312. Reference #3: Savini V, Fazii P, Favaro M, et al. Tuberculosis-like pneumonias by the aerobic actinomycetes Rhodococcus, Tsukamurella and Gordonia. Microbes Infect. 2012;14(5):401-410. doi:10.1016/j.micinf.2011.11.014 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Kunal Jakharia No relevant relationships by Rachel Swier
