N2  - SESSION TITLE: Severe and Unusual Blastomycosis Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 12:25 pm - 01:25 pm INTRODUCTION: Histoplasmosis and Blastomycosis are two endemic mycosis that are usually asymptomatic but can result in very severe illness. Both pathogens are found worldwide, but most commonly reported in the North America, especially in the Midwestern states located around the Ohio and Mississippi river valleys. Pulmonary infection is the most common presentation as they are most commonly acquired by inhalation. The demographics that frequently are affected are middle age males and African Americas. CASE PRESENTATION: 18-year-old female with a past medical history of uncontrolled type II diabetes mellitus who presenting with acute hypoxic respiratory failure. She reported cough and dyspnea for several weeks. She was treated outpatient with Amoxicillin-Clavulanate. In the same time period, she wa treated for an ESBL UTI. Symptoms progressed despite antibacterial therapy. She denied smoking, e-cigarette use but did report smoking Marijuana on occasion. On further questioning it was revealed that several weeks prior to development of symptoms she worked at an animal hospital cleaning cages. On examination, vital signs were normal except for hypoxia requiring 8L supplement oxygen. Lung exam demonstrated coarse bilateral breath sounds. Chest CT with evidence of bilateral consolidations, with areas concerning for necrosis and central clearing. Blood and sputum cultures were obtained as well as urine studies for pneumococcus, legionella and histoplasma antigens. Broad spectrum antibiotics were initiated. Given the extend of the disease, along with the history, bronchoscopy was performed which showed thick yellow mucous throughout the airways. Bronchoalveolar lavage was taken from the right middle lobe. Transbronchial biopsies were performed in the right lower. All cultures remained negative. Tissue specimen showed areas concerning for organizing pneumonia, steroid therapy Was started. Urine histoplasmosis/blastomycosis antigen then resulted positive. The BAL specimen was sent for PCR for confirmation. The patient was discharged pending the results with plans for short term follow-up outpatient. The BAL fluid PCR confirmed Blastomyces. Attempts were made to contact the patient failed and she was treated based on this finding at an outline facility after she was re-admitted. Unfortunately she lost to follow. DISCUSSION: Histoplasmosis and Blastomycosis urine antigen testing are sensitive and specific in detect both organism though the urine antigen cannot differentiate between the two organisms. The diagnosis of histoplasmosis or blastomycosis is commonly made by urine antigen, positive respiratory culture or PCR, or by direct visualization of yeast in the tissue microscopy. The treatment for both organisms is with Itraconazole being used most often. Amphotericin B can be used in life threatening illness CONCLUSIONS: Fungal infections can mimic a lot of different lung pathologies and are difficult to diagnose Reference #1: 1. Baumgardner D. J. (2018). Use of Urine Antigen Testing for Blastomyces in an Integrated Health System. Journal of patient-centered research and reviews, 5(2), 176–182. https://doi.org/10.17294/2330-0698.1452 Reference #2: 2. Limper, A. H., Knox, K. S., Sarosi, G. A., Ampel, N. M., Bennett, J. E., Catanzaro, A., Davies, S. F., Dismukes, W. E., Hage, C. A., Marr, K. A., Mody, C. H., Perfect, J. R., Stevens, D. A., & American Thoracic Society Fungal Working Group (2011). An official American Thoracic Society statement: Treatment of fungal infections in adult pulmonary and critical care patients. American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine, 183(1), 96–128. https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.2008-740ST Reference #3: 3. Howard D. H. (1984). The epidemiology and ecology of blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis. Zentralblatt fur Bakteriologie, Mikrobiologie, und Hygiene. Series A, Medical microbiology, infectious diseases, virology, parasitology, 257(2), 219–227. DISCLOSURES: no disclosure on file for Anthony Roohollahi; No relevant relationships by Dany Tager
