N2  - SESSION TITLE: Unusual Presentations in Sarcoidosis SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/19/2022 11:15 am - 12:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Sarcoidosis is a multi-system disease of unknown etiology. Although mycobacterium infection must be ruled out, it has also been implicated as a potential trigger for sarcoidosis and vice versa. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old South East Asian man with a previous TB exposure 10 years prior was hospitalized with right lower lobe pneumonia. He was noted to have predominantly upper lobe bilateral reticulonodular opacities and mediastinal lymphadenopathy on CT chest. Patient's fever, cough and night sweats resolved with antibiotics. Chest CT to follow-up lymphadenopathy showed resolved consolidation and otherwise unchanged. Diagnostic work-up included normal pulmonary function tests, negative QuantiFERON. Bronchoscopy with biopsy was declined. Five years later, during primary care follow-up, a chest CT showed progression of bilateral upper lobe patchy nodular infiltrates, traction bronchiectasis and unchanged enlarged mediastinal and hilar lymph node. Patient was referred to pulmonary for evaluation and concern for sarcoidosis. Prior to bronchoscopy for definitive diagnosis of pulmonary infiltrates and lymphadenopathy, fungal serologies were negative and QuantiFERON test that was positive. First sputum AFB smear was positive, but TB-PCR returned negative. A second AFB smear was negative. The third AFB smear one week later and TB-PCR was. Two of three cultures were positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis complex. Oral isoniazid, pyridoxine, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol was started and his bronchoscopy with biopsy was held. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary sarcoidosis can present as chronic dyspnea with imaging characteristic for bilateral perihilar lymphadenopathy with upper lobe predominant perilymphatic pulmonary nodules. Bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound-transbronchial node aspiration is the cornerstone of histological diagnosis. Based on ATS guidelines, possible sarcoidosis can be diagnosed without histological confirmation in patients with localized asymptomatic infiltrates such as ours. However, it is a diagnosis of exclusion and other causes of granulomatous diseases need to be excluded. Given progression of upper lobe nodularity, our patient was tested for mycobacterium tuberculosis prior to his bronchoscopy. Studies have shown a relationship between mycobacteria triggering sarcoidosis in countries with high TB burden and mycobacterial antigens have been associated with granulomatous formation. It is possible that, his presumed sarcoidosis could have been placed him at risk for tuberculosis. A longitudinal cohort study by Wang et al. found an 8.08-fold higher risk of developing sarcoidosis in TB patients than in non-TB patients over 5 years. CONCLUSIONS: Our case not only reflects on this possibility but stresses the importance that sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of exclusion and of timely and appropriately performed confirmatory AFB smears to yield maximum sensitivity and specificity. Reference #1: Gupta, D., Agarwal, R., Aggarwal, A. N., & Jindal, S. K. (2012). Sarcoidosis and tuberculosis: the same disease with different manifestations or similar manifestations of different disorders. Current opinion in pulmonary medicine, 18(5), 506-516. Reference #2: Mootha VK, Agarwal R, Aggarwal AN, Gupta D, Ahmed J, Verma I, et al. The Sarcoid-Tuberculosis link: evidence from a high TB prevalence country. J Infect 2010;60(6):501-503 Reference #3: Crouser, E. D., Maier, L. A., Wilson, K. C., Bonham, C. A., Morgenthau, A. S., Patterson, K. C., … & Baughman, R. P. (2020). Diagnosis and detection of sarcoidosis. An official American Thoracic Society clinical practice guideline. American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine, 201(8), e26-e51. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Eva Carmona No relevant relationships by Irene Riestra Guiance
