N2  - SESSION TITLE: Severe and Unusual Blastomycosis Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 12:25 pm - 01:25 pm INTRODUCTION: Blastomyces dermatitidis is endemic to the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys. Infection occurs by inhalation of aerosolized conidia particles and clinical spectrum ranges from being asymptomatic to disseminated disease. We present a rare case of disseminated blastomycosis with initial cutaneous presentation that later revealed osteomyelitis and acute respiratory failure. CASE PRESENTATION: A previously healthy 44-year-old female presented with a 3-week history of left ankle pain, edema, and erythema and 30-pound weight loss. Her ankle pain was managed as a gout flare in the outpatient setting. She received indomethacin, colchicine, and steroids. Magnetic resonance imaging of the left ankle revealed osteomyelitis and phlegmon in the subcutaneous tissues. She was started on empiric antibiotics. 3 days later, she became febrile at 40°C and hypoxic, requiring hi-flow nasal cannula. She developed an open, nodular skin lesion over the left ankle that spread to all four extremities. Her antibiotics were broadened. Chest computed tomography showed innumerable bilateral pulmonary nodules. Multiple blood cultures were negative. 2 weeks after initial admission, a skin lesion biopsy and bronchial alveolar lavage sample were obtained. Pathology from the samples showed broad-based budding yeast consistent with Blastomyces dermatitidis. Blastomyces urine antigen was positive at 13.55 ng/mL. Mycobacterial tests and vasculitis panel were negative. Antibiotics were stopped, and she was given a 7-day course of liposomal amphotericin B then transitioned to a 12-month course of itraconazole. Her respiratory status, ankle pain, and skin lesions improved. DISCUSSION: Extrapulmonary blastomycosis occurs in 25-40% of cases. Cutaneous presentation makes up 40-80% of extrapulmonary cases and osteoarticular involvement represent 5-25% of cases. Commonly involved osteoarticular sites are vertebrae, ribs, long bones, and skull. Diagnosis of osteoarticular blastomycosis may be missed as it can mimic malignancy such as sarcoma, giant cell tumor, or Pott's disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. A 2007 retrospective study of 45 patients with disseminated osteoarticular blastomycosis showed 73% of cases were osteoarticular and cutaneous, 64% with osteoarticular and pulmonary involvement, and 44% with pulmonary, cutaneous, and osteoarticular involvement. Culture is the gold standard and most sensitive, but takes 2-4 weeks. Antigen enzyme immunoassay is faster with 82-88% sensitivity, but cross reacts with Histoplasma. Itraconazole is used for mild to moderate cases, but severe cases require 1-2 weeks of lipid amphotericin B before transitioning to itraconazole for at least 1 year. CONCLUSIONS: Our case highlights the importance of identifying extrapulmonary manifestations of disseminated blastomycosis, especially in an endemic area. Early recognition of this disease will decrease patient morbidity and mortality. Reference #1: 1. Miceli A, Krishnamurthy K. Blastomycosis. [Updated 2020 Aug 10]. In: StatPearls [Internet]. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing; 2020 Jan-. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441987/ Reference #2: 2. Oppenheimer M, Embil JM, Black B, Wiebe L, Limerick B, MacDonald K, Trepman E. Blastomycosis of bones and joints. South Med J. 2007 Jun;100(6):570-8. doi: 10.1097/SMJ.0b013e3180487a92. PMID: 17591310. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sher Khan No relevant relationships by Shu Xian Lee No relevant relationships by Leanne Mazzella No relevant relationships by Gary Monroe No relevant relationships by Rebecca Reece No relevant relationships by Brandon Shiflett No relevant relationships by Jenna Sizemore
