N2  - SESSION TITLE: Rare Pulmonary and Cardiac Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 01:35 pm - 02:35 pm INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium farcinogenes is the causative agent for bovine farcy and is an exceedingly rare human pathogen. We report a case of Mycobacterium farcinogenes infection in a 46-year-old woman who presented with hypoxic respiratory failure and diffuse bilateral pulmonary infiltrates concerning for cryptogenic organizing pneumonia. Further, bronchoalveolar lavage, Karius testing, peripheral blood culture, and autopsy specimens revealed Mycobacterium farcinogenes infection. CASE PRESENTATION: The patient was a 46-year-old female with a history of severe esophageal dysmotility, ultimately treated with total gastrectomy and partial esophagectomy, who presented with 20 lb weight loss, cough, fevers, and fatigue of one month's duration. CT scan of the chest (Figures 1 and 2) showed multiple lung masses, diffuse centrilobular nodules, and new infiltrates. Bronchoscopic examination revealed a milky white BAL with neutrophilic predominance without bacteria, fungal elements, viral inclusions, or evidence of malignancy. She had continued fevers and was placed on Piperacillin/Tazobactam and Posaconazole. A repeat bronchoscopy with EBUS and FNA was negative, with tissue pathology showing abundant acute inflammation, foamy alveolar macrophages, and negative AFB and PAS stains consistent with acute organizing bronchopneumonia. She received prednisone 60 mg daily with plans to taper and PJP prophylaxis with atovaquone. She had minimal symptomatic improvement at outpatient follow-up. A chest x-ray (Figure 3) showed continued bilateral infiltrates and she was readmitted with worsening hypoxic respiratory failure shortly after follow-up. She was placed on steroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, antifungals, and prophylactic pneumocystis pneumonia treatment. Bronchoscopy revealed purulent secretions with positive AFB staining. Speciation of BAL returned as Mycobacterium farcinogenes as well as high amounts of HSV1 and EBV. Infectious evaluation with Karius, showed elevated Mycobacterium fortuitum. Blood cultures returned positive for candida glabrata and acid-fast bacilli. Evaluation for an immunosuppressed state, including HIV screening and immunoglobulin levels, was unremarkable. She unfortunately, had progressive respiratory failure requiring intubation, shock and multiorgan dysfunction and transitioned to comfort care. An autopsy revealed a positive AFB stain on lung sections consistent with Mycobacterium farcinogenes pneumonia. DISCUSSION: Mycobacterium farcinogenes is the causative agent of bovine farcy, a chronic granulomatous disease found in zebu cattle. Cases of human infection are exceedingly rare, with only one prior report of pulmonary involvement noted in China (1) and two post-surgical infections having been reported (2,3). Our case represents a very rare, disseminated mycobacterium farcinogenes infection with a poor outcome. CONCLUSIONS: Mycobacterium is a very rare cause of human disease. Reference #1: Jing H, Tan W, Deng Y, Gao D, Li L, Lu Z, Graviss EA, Ma X. Diagnostic delay of pulmonary nontuberculous mycobacterial infection in China. Multidiscip Respir Med. 2014 Sep 11;9(1):48. doi: 10.1186/2049-6958-9-48. PMID: 25264489; PMCID: PMC4177178. Reference #2: Fatma Al Farsi, Badriya Al Adawi, Hashim Ba Tahir, Mohammed Al Mutani, Chamila Adikaram, Turkiya Al Siyabi, Ibrahim Al Busaidi,Mycobacterium farcinogenes osteomyelitis of the proximal tibia: A case report, IDCases, Volume 25, 2021, e01194, ISSN 2214-2509 Reference #3: T.-C. Wong, W.F. Chan, W.L. Tsang, S.H. Yeung, F.K. Ip Mycobacterium farcinogenes infection after total hip arthroplasty, J Arthroplasty, 20 (August (5)) (2005), pp. 684-687 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by AMIR LAGSTEIN No relevant relationships by John Parent No relevant relationships by Gayathri Sathiyamoorthy
