N2  - SESSION TITLE: Path to the Rescue SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/19/2022 12:45 pm - 1:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Herpes simplex virus (HSV) has rarely been identified as an etiology for an endobronchial mass (2). We present a probable case of HSV pseudotumor resulting in endobronchial obstruction and recurrent bacterial pneumonia. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71 year old man with a past medical history of tobacco use and recurrent pneumonia presented with one week of dyspnea, cough, and sputum production. On exam, he was tachypneic on high flow nasal cannula. Chest x-ray revealed a right lower lobe pneumonia and respiratory cultures grew group C streptococcus for which he was started on ampicillin-sulbactam. Labs were notable for a WBC count of 30.1 with neutrophilic predominance. Bronchsocopy revealed an endobronchial lesion in the bronchus intermedius. Result from forceps biopsy showed suspected squamous cell carcinoma (SCC). The patient was transferred to our facility for further work up and management. Interventional pulmonary was consulted and rigid bronchoscopy was performed for tumor debulking and staging. Pathology revealed pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia associated with herpes virus inclusions in ground glass nuclei in submucosal nests of atypical squamous cells with overlying squamous metaplasia. Immunostain for HSV was positive and a presumptive diagnosis of HSV pseudotumor was made. The patient recovered well post-procedure and had improved aeration on chest imaging. Outside records were reviewed. Nine months prior to presentation a CT chest demonstrated an endobronchial lesion in the bronchus intermedius during an admission for pneumonia. Four weeks prior to presentation a PET-CT showed unchanged size in the endobronchial lesion and absence of distant disease. The prior biopsy showed bland squamous and focally atypical proliferation with rare nests extending into the submucosa suggestive of invasion associated with ulcer debris. An immunostain for Herpes was negative, but due to small sample size sampling error could not be excluded. DISCUSSION: Diagnosis of HSV pseudotumor is made by characteristic cytologic and histologic findings. Cytology reveals multinucleated giant cells with basophilic ground glass nuclei. Confirmation is made with positive immunohistochemistry for HSV. In our case, the pathology presented a dilemma: either HSV pseudotumor or HSV infection superimposed on underlying SCC. The latter is less likely due to lesion stability on serial imaging, lack of carcinoma seen on large excision specimen submitted from debulking, and low FDG avidity on PET. We believe the chronic inflammatory process driven by HSV caused atypical appearing squamous cells and masqueraded as SCC. As we cannot exclude the possibility of a superimposed malignancy, the patient is scheduled for close outpatient follow up and surgical evaluation. CONCLUSIONS: HSV Pseudotumor should be considered on the differential diagnosis for non-malignant endobronchial lesions. Reference #1: Ramsey PG, Fife KH, Hackman RC, Meyers JD, Corey L. Herpes simplex virus pneumonia: clinical, virologic, and pathologic features in 20 patients. Ann Intern Med 1982;97:813–20. Reference #2: Upadya A, Tilluckdharry L, Nagy CD, Ravichandran P, Manthous C. Endobronchial pseudo-tumour casued by herpes simplex. European Respiratory Journal 2005; 25: 1117-1120. Reference #3: Simoons-Smit A. Kraan E, Beishuizen A, Schijndel RS, Vandenbroucke-Grauls C. Herpes Simplex Type 1 and respiratory disease in critically-ill patients: real pathogen or innocent bystander? Clinical Microbiology and Infection 2006. 12(11), 1050-1059. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sisir Akkineni No relevant relationships by David Berkowitz No relevant relationships by Jaishree Jagirdar No relevant relationships by Stephen Russell No relevant relationships by Matt Schimmel
