N2  - SESSION TITLE: Using Imaging for Diagnosis Case Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/19/2022 12:45 pm - 01:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pleural effusion from a pyogenic liver abscess can develop when diaphragmatic irritations, and pleural friction rubs lead to fluid accumulation in the pleural space. Pleural effusions from a pyogenic liver abscess is uncommon in immunocompetent individuals and is often managed as a right sided pneumonia based on the clinical presentation, lack of clinical intuition for the disease and chest x-ray findings. Elevated right hemidiaphragm, subsegmental atelectasis and right sided pleural effusion are characteristics seen on the coronal view of a chest tomography (CT) that can help with early diagnosis and management. CASE PRESENTATION: Patient is a 36-year-old male with no significant past medical history that presented with 4 days of dull diffuse abdominal pain and watery diarrhea. Patient denied nausea, vomiting, fever, change in diet, camping or recent travel. Patient also endorsed shortness of breath with non-productive cough. Physical exam and laboratory tests were significant for temperature of (100.8 oF), heart rate (105 bpm), decreased breath sound in the right lower lung base and diffuse abdominal pain, white blood cell count (27.08 with 77% neutrophil), alkaline phosphatase (275 IU/L), alanine transaminase of (76 IU/L), aspartate transaminase (79 IU/L), total bilirubin (2.1 mg/dl), direct bilirubin (1.2 mg/dl), serum C- reactive protein (10.3 mg/dl). Hepatitis panel, HIV serology and Entamoeba histolytica antibody, blood culture, sputum culture and urinalysis were not significant for any findings. CT of the chest and abdomen (figure 1a &b) showed right sided pleural effusion, atelectatic changes, elevation of the right hemidiaphragm, and hepatomegaly fluid collection (figure 1c). A French drain was placed in the perihepatic region and brownish purulent fluid was drained and collected. A pigtail was also placed in the right hemithorax. Streptococcus anginosus and Prevotella BIVA were cultured from the hepatic abscess. Ceftriaxone and metronidazole were started based on sensitivity. Pleural fluid was exudative and reported no growth. Patient showed clinical improvement and repeat imaging showed drainage of both hepatic abscess and pleural fluid. Patient was switched to Augmentin and discharged home. DISCUSSION: Pleural effusion from a pyogenic liver abscess in an immunocompetent host is a rare condition. However, if not diagnosed and managed early, it can be potentially fatal. Transdiaphragmatic extension of pyogenic liver abscess can lead to rare cases of pleural empyema and pericarditis which have close to a 100 percent mortality rate if not caught and treated on time (Cho et al 2018). CONCLUSIONS: Coronal view of a chest CT can help narrow down the differentials and lead to appropriate intervention, if characteristic findings such as centrally hypoattenuating liver lesion, hepatomegaly with elevated right diaphragm, and right sided pleural effusion with atelectasis are present. Reference #1: Cho, E., Park, S.W., Jun, C.H. et al. A rare case of pericarditis and pleural empyema secondary to transdiaphragmatic extension of pyogenic liver abscess. BMC Infect Dis 18, 40 (2018). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-018-2953-8 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mubariz Hassan No relevant relationships by Biola Ohiokpehai No relevant relationships by Alicia Thomas
