N2  - SESSION TITLE: Remarkable Imaging Cases SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 12:25 pm - 01:25 pm INTRODUCTION: Although incidence Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) infection has declined in the United States (US), the diagnosis remains higher on the differential amongst household contacts of persons with MTB disease who present with typical imaging findings and weight loss. Here, we present a case initially concerning for miliary tuberculosis with spondylitis (Pott's disease) that was ultimately found to be metastatic lung adenocarcinoma. CASE PRESENTATION: A 62-year-old male with a history of basal cell carcinoma of the skin and allergic rhinitis presented with progressively worsening non-productive cough and dyspnea. He had no recent travel history or suspicious environmental exposures. He denied fever, chills, or night sweats but reported ten pounds of weight loss over the prior six months. Family history was significant for treatment of pulmonary MTB infection in his mother. Initial laboratory results were unremarkable, including sedimentation rate and QuantiFERON TB testing. However, chest radiograph showed bilateral patchy airspace disease with nodular infiltrates. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest identified extensive bilateral pulmonary nodules with a miliary pattern, an area of consolidation with associated cavitation, and lucencies in the spine—findings concerning for miliary tuberculosis with spondylitis, given his exposure history. Bronchoscopy with transbronchial and endobronchial biopsies was performed and consistent with adenocarcinoma. Immunophenotype was consistent with a primary lung adenocarcinoma, and immunohistochemistry showed positivity for TTF1, Napsin-A, and CK7. Cytology showed =1% PD-L1 positivity and an EGFR mutation. Magnetic resonance imaging of the spine and brain confirmed extensive bony and intracranial lesions. He was initiated on osimertinib, carboplatin, and pemetrexed. Follow-up CT scan showed an interval decrease in the size of his miliary nodules, a right lower lobe mass, and intracranial metastases. DISCUSSION: Miliary MTB infection may present at several extra-pulmonary sites, including in the spine as tuberculosis spondylitis (Pott's disease). This poses a special diagnostic challenge in MTB high-risk populations, as miliary patterning of adenocarcinoma is a known but rare radiographic appearance. Similarly, spinal metastases closely mimic the appearance of tuberculosis spondylitis, so differentiating the two is difficult based on clinical history and imaging alone, and tissue is required to establish the diagnosis. CONCLUSIONS: Our case highlights that, while rare, lung adenocarcinoma may present with both a miliary pattern and spine metastatic lesions. Clinicians should also be aware that the radiographic appearance of tuberculosis spondylitis and spinal metastases from lung adenocarcinoma are similar. Metastatic lung cancer should remain on the differential for patients with miliary lung findings and spinal lesions. Reference #1: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. TB Incidence in the United States, 1953-2020. url: https://www.cdc.gov/tb/statistics/tbcases.htm (visited on 03/26/2022). Reference #2: Felix C Ringshausen et al. "A fatal case of spinal tuberculosis mistaken for metastatic lung cancer: recalling ancient Pott's disease". In: Annals of Clinical Microbiology and Antimicrobials 8.1 (2009), p. 32. issn: 1476-0711. doi: 10.1186/1476-0711-8-32. url: http://ann-clinmicrob.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1476-0711-8-32. Reference #3: Dawlat Khan et al. "Metastatic Adenocarcinoma of the Lung Mimicking Miliary Tuberculosis and Pott's Disease". In: Cureus (Jan. 2021). issn: 2168-8184. doi: 10.7759/cureus.12869. url: https://www.cureus.com/articles/43423-metastatic-adenocarcinoma-of-the-lung-mimicking-miliary-tuberculosis-and-potts-disease. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by David Halle No relevant relationships by Kamran Manzoor No relevant relationships by Eric Robbins No relevant relationships by Ari Sacks No relevant relationships by Karin Sloan
