N2  - SESSION TITLE: Problems in the Pleura Case Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/17/2022 12:15 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Parvimonas micra is fastidious anaerobic gram-positive cocci, and are commensals of the oral cavity. They are sometimes implicated in polymicrobial infections of the oral cavity, paranasal sinus infections, and intracerebral abscesses. Streptococcus constellatus is another gram-positive cocci that are commensal of the oral cavity and urogenital tract. Pulmonary infections from these pathogens, especially empyema, are exceedingly rare (1). CASE PRESENTATION: The patient is a 71-year-old male with dementia who was brought in from a nursing home with complaints of shortness of breath, productive cough, and altered mental status after testing positive for Influenza A one day prior. On presentation, the patient's blood pressure was 90/56 mmHg, heart rate was 108 per minute, temperature was 101 F, and oxygen saturation was 74% on room air. The patient was lethargic, barely localizing to painful stimuli. He was noted to have decreased breath sounds on the right lung base. Preliminary laboratory investigations showed neutrophilic leukocytosis of 14,000/mm3. The patient was treated with intravenous normal saline, oseltamivir, and empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics vancomycin and cefepime. Due to acute hypoxic respiratory failure and altered mental status, the patient was intubated, and mechanical ventilation was initiated. Computed tomography of the chest without contrast revealed a large loculated pleural effusion at the right lung base, and a left lower lobe consolidation. A right-sided pigtail catheter was placed and intrapleural t-PA–DNase was administered. The pleural fluid analysis demonstrated a transudative pleural effusion with a white blood cell count of 167 million/uL. Pleural fluid cultures grew Parvimonas micra and Streptococcus constellatus. Despite continued therapy, the patient's clinical status did not improve, and he failed multiple ventilator weaning trials. After extensive discussion, the family opted to proceed with palliative care, terminal extubation, and subsequent inpatient hospice where he passed away peacefully in the presence of the family. DISCUSSION: Empyema is a collection of pus in the pleural cavity. Often a sequela of pneumonia and parapneumonic effusion, they are treated with aggressive drainage, intravenous antibiotic therapy, and intrapleural t-PA–DNase if the collection is loculated. In a review of case reports, up to 71% of patients with Parvimonas micra infection had risk factors such as periodontal disease, oral procedures, or reduced systemic immunity through conditions such as diabetes (2). In our patient, infection with influenza A virus may have predisposed to the development of Parvimonas micra polymicrobial empyema and this is the first such reported case. CONCLUSIONS: Influenza infection hampers the natural immunity of the lungs and predisposes to the development of pneumonia and polymicrobial empyema, sometimes with commensal organisms. Reference #1: Rodriguez-Segade S, Velasco D, Marcos PJ. Empyema due to Aggregatibacter aphrophilus and Parvimonas micra Coinfection. Arch Bronconeumol. 2015 May 1;51(5):254–5. 2. Cobo F, Rodríguez-Granger J, Sampedro A, Aliaga-Martínez L, Navarro-Marí JM. Pleural effusion due to Parvimonas micra. A case report and a literature review of 30 cases. Rev Espanola Quimioter Publicacion Of Soc Espanola Quimioter. 2017 Aug;30(4):285–92. Reference #2: Cobo F, Rodríguez-Granger J, Sampedro A, Aliaga-Martínez L, Navarro-Marí JM. Pleural effusion due to Parvimonas micra. A case report and a literature review of 30 cases. Rev Espanola Quimioter Publicacion Of Soc Espanola Quimioter. 2017 Aug;30(4):285–92. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Rana Prathap Padappayil No relevant relationships by Khushee Shah No relevant relationships by DISHANT SHAH
