N2  - SESSION TITLE: Poster Cases in Asthma and Allergy SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/17/2022 12:15 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Radiological findings of severe bronchiectasis and mucus plugging are typically seen in asthmatic patients in the setting of ABPA (Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis). We present a case of severe eosinophilic (Type-2 high) asthma presenting with severe, non-ABPA related bronchiectasis and mucus plugging. There was remarkable clinical and radiological improvement after dupilumab initiation. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old female presented for a second opinion regarding her poorly controlled asthma and chronic cough. She had been diagnosed with chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis as well as asthma more than 10 years ago and her asthma had been under relatively good control for many years. Over the past year, she had developed a worsening dry cough as well as chest tightness and nocturnal wheezing. Her eosinophil count was significantly elevated at 1.97x109/L. Pulmonary function testing showed moderate fixed obstruction with air trapping. Exhaled nitric oxide (eNO) was markedly elevated at 106 parts per billion (<39) and total IgE was 358 kU/L (< 214). Chest CT scan showed extensive severe bilateral bronchiectasis with mucoid impactions in a distribution pattern not typical for ABPA (image 1). Extensive testing for bronchiectasis including sweat chloride and infectious pathogens were all negative. Given the high eosinophil count, uncontrolled asthma and CRSwNP; the patient was started on dupilumab along with continuation of nasal steroids, ICS-LABA and bronchial hygiene measures. Follow-up evaluation after 5 doses of dupilumab showed complete resolution of mucous plugging, improved bronchiectasis, normal spirometry and normal eNO. DISCUSSION: This case highlights the fact that significant bronchiectasis and mucous plugging can be present in Type 2-high (eosinophilic) asthma even in the absence of ABPA criteria. The initiation of dupilumab in this patient was associated with a very rapid clinical and radiological improvement. A complete resolution of endobronchial mucous plugging as well as reduction in the size of bronchiectasis areas occurred after just 5 doses of dupilumab. Te serum eosinophil count actually rose from 1.97 to 3.02 [10(9)/L] after 5 doses of dupilumab highlighting the fact that serum eosinophil counts are not reflective of tissue eosinophil activity. Whether resolution of mucous plugging could have occurred with bronchial hygiene measures alone is an open question, but these mucous plugs were not visible and could not be suctioned out even after aggressive bronchial washings and lavage during initial diagnostic bronchoscopy. CONCLUSIONS: We present an unusual case of severe varicose bronchiectasis with extensive mucoid impaction (likely with eosinophil derived products) that improved dramatically after IL4/IL13 inhibition with dupilumab. The encouraging response to dupilumab in non-ABPA asthmatic bronchiectasis requires further study. Reference #1: dupilumab efficacy and safety in moderate-to-severe unconrtolled sthma. Castro M et al. NEJM.2018 Jun 28; 378(26): 2486-2496. Reference #2: Efficacy and safety of treatment with biologicals (benralizumab, dupilumab, mepolizumab, omalizumab and reslizumab) for severe eosinophilic asthma. A systematic review for the EAACI guidelines-recommendations on the use of biologicals in severe asthma. Agache I et al. Allergy. 2020 May; 75(5): 1023-1042. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Vaibhav Ahluwalia No relevant relationships by Vibhor Ahluwalia No relevant relationships by Hasan Ahmad Hasan Albitar No relevant relationships by Vivek Iyer
