N2  - SESSION TITLE: Rare Cases with Masquerading Pulmonary Symptoms SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 01:35 pm - 02:35 pm INTRODUCTION: We present to you a case of reactivation of latent Tuberculosis mycobacterial infection presenting bilateral pleural effusions. CASE PRESENTATION: A 40 year old male with past medical history of hypertension and recent immigration from India presented with a complaint of non-productive cough ongoing for 3 months. The patient had previously failed outpatient management with empiric antibiotics and a course of steroids. An outpatient computed tomography scan of the chest revealed bilateral pleural effusions with pleural thickening. Patient was sent to the hospital for further evaluation. A right sided thoracentesis performed, with removal of 1.1 liters of serosanguineous fluid. Pleural fluid testing demonstrated a lymphocyte predominant exudative pleural effusion with glucose 115, lactate dehydrogenase 484, protein 5.5, and pH 7.353. Fungal and bacterial studies were negative. Additional serologic testing revealed negative HIV, positive anti-nucleated-antibody in a 1:320 speckled pattern, elevated levels of compliment 3 and 4, and a positive QuantiFERON gold, raising concern for a rheumatoid process or tuberculosis. Thoracoscopy with pleural biopsy was obtained to establish definitive diagnosis. Intra-operatively, 2 liters of serous fluid was evacuated from the right lung and sent for culture, which ultimately grew acid fast bacilli. Pleural biopsy revealed necrotizing granulomas and rare acid-fast bacilli on gram stain. Pleural fluid adenosine deaminase was also elevated. Reactivation of latent mycobacterium tuberculosis infection was diagnosed, and he was initiated on therapy with rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, and isoniazid. At two month follow up, patient had improvement of cough with nearly resolved right sided pleural effusion, but persistent small complex left sided pleural effusion. Patient also developed peripheral neuropathy, suspected to be secondary to isoniazid. DISCUSSION: Patient's initial tuberculosis exposure most likely occurred in India prior to his emigration to the United States. Reactivation of disease is more frequent in the first 5 years after exposure, but can occur more remotely. This case is unusual given both the time frame and uncommon initial presentation. Lower lung or sub-hilar involvement occurs in about 2-9% of tuberculosis cases, while bilateral pleural effusions in tuberculosis is even more rarely documented in the literature. CONCLUSIONS: Incidence of tuberculosis continues to decline in the US and on a global level, but it is still a major public health issue. Prompt identification and treatment of the disease can prevent additional exposures and improve patient outcomes. As this case demonstrates, identifying risk factors is vital in uncommon presentations of tuberculosis to allow for directed testing and early diagnosis. Reference #1: 1. Trauer, James M et al. "Risk of Active Tuberculosis in the Five Years Following Infection… 15%?.” Chest vol. 149,2 (2016): 516-525. doi:10.1016/j.chest.2015.11.017 Reference #2: 1. SEGARRA, F et al. "Lower lung field tuberculosis.” The American review of respiratory disease vol. 87 (1963): 37-40. doi:10.1164/arrd.1963.87.1.37 Reference #3: 1. Krysl, J et al. "Radiologic features of pulmonary tuberculosis: an assessment of 188 cases.” Canadian Association of Radiologists journal = Journal l'Association canadienne des radiologistes vol. 45,2 (1994): 101-7. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Anthony Greco No relevant relationships by Cecilia Molloy No relevant relationships by Joseph Moran No relevant relationships by Christopher Stancic
