N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pathology Identifying Chest Infections Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/17/2022 12:15 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: The differential for a cavitary lung lesion is broad including benign, infectious, malignant, and rheumatologic causes. Though radiographic features of a cavitary lesion can help narrow the differential, these features are not always consistent between disease states. Therefore, the presence of a cavitary lung lesion requires a thorough diagnostic approach. CASE PRESENTATION: A 23 year old male with no past medical history presented to the Emergency Department (ED) in the Fall due to worsening fever, cough, and vomiting. One month prior, he was diagnosed with strep throat and treated with Amoxicillin and improved. Four days prior to admission, he developed fever and cough and presented to Urgent Care. He was diagnosed with pneumonia and given Cefpodoxime and Azithromycin. However, his symptoms worsened and he presented to the ED. In the ED, he was febrile, tachycardic, and tachypneic. CBC demonstrated leukocytosis with 91% lymphocyte predominance. Physical exam was notable for pharyngeal erythema and tonsillar exudate. Contrast Chest CT revealed a solitary right lower lobe opacity with reverse halo sign. Heterophile mono screening was positive with elevated Epstein Barr Virus (EBV) IgM and quantitative EBV DNA level over 8,000. Peripheral blood smear was concerning for a T-cell lymphoproliferative disorder with positive gamma chain rearrangement. Infectious disease and Hematology were consulted due to concern for infection versus lymphoproliferative malignancy, including Lymphomatoid Granulomatosis (LG). The decision was made to biopsy the lung lesion. Biopsy revealed a dense lymphoid infiltrate. Flow cytometry revealed a CD8 predominant population of T-cells with lack of immature myelomonocytic cells not consistent with LG. The remainder of testing was negative and the patient recovered with supportive care. Three-month follow-up CT demonstrated complete resolution of the lesion. DISCUSSION: This otherwise healthy male presented with an atypical case of cavitary lung lesion in the setting of EBV infection. Given the severity of disease on presentation and radiographic findings, extensive testing was completed to rule out other causes of cavitary lung lesions including common bacterial, viral, and fungal infections, rheumatologic disorders, and testicular malignancy. With EBV infection, LG was also suspected. Diagnosing LG is critical given its angio-destructive pathogenesis, aggressive progression and high mortality. Given response to supportive treatment alone, benign T-cell appearance on flow cytometry, and complete resolution of the lesion, it was concluded this was an atypical presentation of EBV with a cavitary lung lesion. CONCLUSIONS: A cavitary lung lesion yields a broad differential. In the context of active EBV infection, LG must be considered. We present a unique case of active EBV infection causing a cavitary lung lesion with complete resolution three months following infection. Reference #1: Gadkowski LB, Stout JE. Cavitary pulmonary disease. Clin Microbiol Rev. 2008 Apr;21(2):305-33, table of contents. doi: 10.1128/CMR.00060-07. PMID: 18400799; PMCID: PMC2292573. Reference #2: Gafoor K, Patel S, Girvin F, Gupta N, Naidich D, Machnicki S, Brown KK, Mehta A, Husta B, Ryu JH, Sarosi GA, Franquet T, Verschakelen J, Johkoh T, Travis W, Raoof S. Cavitary Lung Diseases: A Clinical-Radiologic Algorithmic Approach. Chest. 2018 Jun;153(6):1443-1465. doi: 10.1016/j.chest.2018.02.026. Epub 2018 Mar 6. PMID: 29518379. Reference #3: Dunleavy K, Roschewski M, Wilson WH. Lymphomatoid granulomatosis and other Epstein-Barr virus associated lymphoproliferative processes. Curr Hematol Malig Rep. 2012 Sep;7(3):208-15. doi: 10.1007/s11899-012-0132-3. PMID: 22814713; PMCID: PMC6317897. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Alicia Brunelle No relevant relationships by Catherine Fiore no disclosure on file for Paulo Oliveira;
