N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lifelong Learning in Critical Care SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Original Inv PRESENTED ON: 10/17/2022 12:15 pm - 1:15 pm PURPOSE: Mycobacterium Lung Disease (MLD) is diagnosed by Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) testing done on the sputum. A significant proportion of sputum samples tested in our hospital for AFB revealed Mycobacterium Avium Complex (MAC) in patients with no evidence of MLD or underlying structural lung disease. This suggests contamination of sputum samples with MAC. The hospital is in the midst of construction which may be responsible for this contamination. Our aim was to determine the extent of this problem leading to false-positive results on AFB testing. METHODS: We retrospectively reviewed the medical records of patient’s sputum samples sent for AFB testing in 2018.The results are categorized as MTB or MAC on culture. Criteria used to identify MAC lung disease include a) subacute infectious illness with fever, cough with sputum, weight loss or night sweats b) cavitory disease on chest imaging especially in the upper lobes with evidence of transbronchial spread or small airway disease in the form of diffuse centrilobular nodules with ectasia. RESULTS: Of the 238 sputum samples tested for AFB,72 (30.2 %) were positive.15/72 (20.8 %) grew MTB. 57/72 (79.2%) grew MAC. Only 3/57(5.3 %) had features consistent with MAC lung disease and were treated. 54/57 (94.7 %) samples positive for MAC were considered false positive. Sputum samples tested for AFB 238 Positive for AFB 72/238 (30.2%) Positive for MTB 15/72 (20.8%) Positive for MAC 57/72 (79.2%) Positive for MAC lung disease 3/57 ( 5.3%) False positive for MAC 54/57 (94.7%) The most remarkable was that the majority of the patients who tested positive for MAC without evidence of MLD had features consistent with that of community-acquired pneumonia and responded to antibiotics. CONCLUSIONS: While MAC was identified in 79.2 % sputum samples that grew AFB, only 5.3 % revealed evidence of MAC lung disease resulting in a false positive rate of 94.7 % due possibly to contamination of the samples as a result of MAC unearth from soil due to construction at the hospital site. Majority of patients without MAC lung disease had clinical features consistent with community acquired pneumonia that responded to antibiotics indicating that AFB testing in these patients was not required. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: The growth of MAC in sputum is the result of disease colonization or contamination. As MAC is present in the environment, contamination of sputum samples can be expected, more so in the presence of construction. Our data indicates that in the majority of patients who tested positive for MAC, the test was not clinically indicated leading to false-positive results, unnecessary healthcare expenditure, and patient dissatisfaction with being isolated. Awareness of Mycobacterium Avium as a source of contamination in sputum samples sent for AFB testing, especially in a setting that allows its contamination such as construction will hopefully lead to the more appropriate use of this test. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Antony Anandaraj No relevant relationships by Rohan Banan No relevant relationships by Logan Gonsalves No relevant relationships by Padmanabhan Krishnan No relevant relationships by CHIBUZO OKOYE No relevant relationships by divya rajmohan No relevant relationships by Fawwaz Safi No relevant relationships by Sumedha Sonde
