N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pathology Identifying Chest Infections Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/17/2022 12:15 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) that represents a group of tumors arising from post-germinal center marginal zone B-cells and share a similar immunophenotype. Extra-nodal MZL arises in several epithelial tissues including, stomach, salivary glands, thyroid, and skin. We present a case of a female with a pulmonary MZL associated with chronic inflammation due to autoimmune and infectious etiology. CASE PRESENTATION: A 65-year-old female with a history of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), chronic bronchitis, recurrent Klebsiella pneumonia, and a 32-pack year smoking history, presented with chronic productive cough. Lung cancer screening with low dose computed tomography (CT) chest revealed a 2.3 x 1.6 cm lobulated irregular nodule in the right lower lobe. A positron emission tomography showed minimal FDG labeling of 1.5 SUV and no hilar or mediastinal adenopathy. Given high pretest probability of malignancy, navigational bronchoscopy and endobronchial ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration were done. Fine needle aspiration of the nodule and mediastinal lymph nodes were negative for malignancy and revealed acute on chronic inflammation. Bronchoalveolar lavage cultures grew Klebsiella pneumoniae and she received a course of antibiotics. A 6 month follow up CT chest showed interval growth of nodule to 3.3 cm x 2.6cm. Given concern for an indolent malignancy, a CT-guided needle biopsy was done, which revealed low-grade B-cell lymphoma, consistent with extra-nodal MZL of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT). A subsequent esophagogastroduodenoscopy showed no evidence of gastric MALT or Helicobacter pylori infection. A bone marrow biopsy showed less than 10% involvement. She received 4 cycles of rituximab with a good response and a decrease in the size of pulmonary nodules. DISCUSSION: The incidence of pulmonary MALT (PMALT) is rare, occurring in about 0.1% of pulmonary tumors.[1] Clinical course is indolent and most patients have no pulmonary or constitutional symptoms. Radiographic appearance is variable, and lesions can appear as unilateral or bilateral alveolar opacities and nodules. Extra-gastric MZL is generally associated with an underlying autoimmune disease, but SLE as seen in our patient, is only seen in 3% of cases.[2] Rarely, in healthy adults increased prevalence has been observed among patients with chronic inflammation.[3] Although Helicobacter pylori is a known causative microorganism in gastric MALT, no microorganism has been confirmed as a contributing agent for PMALT. Like observations noted in other cases, our patient developed PMALT while having ensuing inflammation from chronic bronchitis and recurrent bacterial infections, highlighting a possible association between them. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary MZL is a rare indolent cancer that can be associated with chronic inflammatory conditions like autoimmune disorders and recurrent infections. Reference #1: Cadranel J, Wislez M, Antoine M. Primary pulmonary lymphoma. Eur Respir J. 2002 Sep;20(3):750-62. doi: 10.1183/09031936.02.00404102. PMID: 12358356 Reference #2: Wöhrer S, Troch M, Streubel B, et al. MALT lymphoma in patients with autoimmune diseases: a comparative analysis of characteristics and clinical course. Leukemia. 2007;21(8):1812-1818. doi:10.1038/sj.leu.2404782 Reference #3: Inadome Y, Ikezawa T, Oyasu R, Noguchi M. Malignant lymphoma of bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue (BALT) coexistent with pulmonary tuberculosis. Pathol Int. 2001;51(10):807-811. doi:10.1046/j.1440-1827.2001.01272.x DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Varun Bhalla No relevant relationships by Jayashri Bhaskar
