N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Presentations of TB SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 12:25 pm - 01:25 pm INTRODUCTION: We highlight diagnostic challenges from a case of miliary mycobacterium tuberculosis (TB) presenting with a pleural effusion and liver and skin involvement. CASE PRESENTATION: A 77-year-old female Cambodian immigrant with no prior medical history presented with three-weeks of waxing and waning fevers, chills, dry cough, and one day of bilateral lower extremity diffuse, erythematous, and tender 1-2 cm macules. She required 2L by nasal cannula, with unremarkable labs apart from a mixed hepatocellular, cholestatic pattern of liver injury. Chest CT showed right upper lobe scarring with traction bronchiectasis extending into the apex from the hilum, scattered bilateral upper lobe calcified granulomas, and a 4.4 x 4.1 cm right major fissure loculated pleural effusion. Abdominal imaging showed an enlarged nodular liver. QuantiFERON was positive, however sputum samples were unobtainable and blood, urine, and gastric lavage samples were negative for acid-fast bacilli. The pleural effusion was inaccessible by thoracentesis and high-risk pleural biopsy was deferred. She had strongly positive serum anti-CCP titers, as well as anti-ANA, -dsDNA, and -SSA. Although active TB was unconfirmed, given high clinical suspicion, the decision was made to empirically treat with rifampin, ethambutol, and levofloxacin. Three weeks after initial presentation, a repeat chest CT showed innumerable bilateral diffuse ground glass micronodules in a miliary pattern. She continued directly observed therapy with symptomatic improvement, near resolution of the pleural effusion, and normalization of liver function tests 6 weeks after presentation. DISCUSSION: The prevalence of latent TB infection (LTBI) in the U.S. is estimated to be 5%, with 5-10% of untreated LTBI progressing to active TB. Approximately, one-fifth of active TB cases are extrapulmonary, often found in foreign-born immunocompetent immigrants in the U.S. These cases present atypically, with lymph node or pleural involvement, resulting in diagnostic challenges[1]. For example, the inability to capture sputum samples without cough, the limited yield of bronchoalveolar lavage without parenchymal disease, the difficulty in sampling the pleural effusion, and the presence of several rheumatologic autoantibodies may have introduced cognitive biases in diagnostic reasoning. However, anti-CCP antibody, while specific for rheumatoid arthritis, can be falsely elevated in patients with TB, limiting its positive predictive value in cases with low pre-test probabilities[2]. This case also demonstrates the importance of identifying subclinical TB, a newly defined intermediate state prevalent in foreign-born immigrants in high-income countries that offers treatment opportunity prior to reactivation[3]. CONCLUSIONS: Despite pragmatic challenges in diagnostic workup, empiric treatment for extrapulmonary TB through prioritized differential diagnosis can improve clinical outcome. Reference #1: Henson, T., et al., 9 Year-Analysis of Extrapulmonary Tb in a New York Community Hospital. Chest, 2020. 158(4). Reference #2: Kakumanu, P., et al., Patients with pulmonary tuberculosis are frequently positive for anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies, but their sera also react with unmodified arginine-containing peptide. Arthritis Rheum, 2008. 58(6): p. 1576-81 Reference #3: Lau, A., et al., The Radiographic and Mycobacteriologic Correlates of Subclinical Pulmonary TB in Canada: A Retrospective Cohort Study. Chest, 2022. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Guillermo Jimenez No relevant relationships by Karan Rai
