N2  - SESSION TITLE: Dirty Jobs: Occupational Lung Diseases SESSION TYPE: Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 11:15 am - 12:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Anthracosis is a type of pneumoconiosis caused by accumulation of carbon-based particles in the lungs from repeated exposure of air pollutants. Exposure to tuberculosis, biomass fuel, and mineral dust increases the risk for developing a more severe form called bronchial anthracofibrosis (BAF). Most cases of BAF are found in the developing world, usually in non-smokers with biomass exposure. The first reported native case in the USA was in 2014 which was found to be related to biomass exposure. We present a rare case of a Nevada nuclear test site worker with a history of COPD and silicosis, found to have FDG-avid lymphadenopathy who was ultimately diagnosed with BAF. CASE PRESENTATION: A 78-year-old man with past medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and silicosis was referred to the pulmonology clinic for endoscopic bronchial ultrasound (EBUS) in November 2021 for a left suprahilar mass [Fig1]. The patient presented in August 2021 for dyspnea and hemoptysis. The CT Scan was obtained which revealed a left suprahilar mass with perihilar adenopathy, chronic bilateral upper lobe mild interstitial thickening, and linear scarring. A PET CT was ordered revealing an avid hilar mass with adenopathy [Fig2]. The patient was a lifetime nonsmoker and his history was significant for previous work at the Nevada nuclear test site. Bronchoscopy revealed bilateral anthracotic pigmentation in the airways with mucosal thickening and hyperemia [Fig3]. Enlarged lymph nodes were noted on 11R, 4R, and 7. Bacterial cultures were negative, ruling out tuberculosis. Fine needle aspiration of the lesions and lymph nodes were negative for malignancy but significant for anthracotic histiocytosis consistent with pulmonary anthracosis. The patient was to follow-up with pulmonary function tests given his symptoms and a repeat CT chest in 12 months. DISCUSSION: Our case highlights a unique case of BAF in a non-immigrant patient with a history of silicosis who worked at a former Nevada nuclear test site. He had no previous history of coal exposure, farming, tuberculosis, or other commonly known risk factors. As demonstrated through this case, BAF can present with respiratory symptoms of COPD and PET CT imaging of hilar mass with adenopathy, suspicious for malignancy. Upon investigation with EBUS and biopsy, the patient was found to have diffuse mucosal anthracotic pigment with biopsy-proven anthracotic lymph nodes. Although BAF remains uncommon in the USA, physicians should keep this diagnosis in their differentials when considering patients who are non-smokers with signs and symptoms of COPD or malignancy. CONCLUSIONS: Cases of BAF in the developing world, although rare, can present with symptoms resembling COPD and a false-positive PET scan. It is important to keep this differential in mind in non-smoking patients showing a positive PET scan whilst ruling out cancer in FDG avid nodes. Reference #1: Kim YJ, Jung CY, Shin HW, Lee BK. Biomass smoke induced bronchial anthracofibrosis: presenting features and clinical course. Respir Med. 2009;103(5):757–65 Reference #2: Kuschner W. Abnormal FDG-PET findings in particulate-induced lung disease. Clin Med Res. 2010;8(3-4):125. doi:10.3121/cmr.2010.963 Reference #3: Sigari N, Mohammadi S. Anthracosis and anthracofibrosis. Saudi Med J. 2009;30(8):1063–6. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Andrew Manfra No relevant relationships by CHRISELYN PALMA No relevant relationships by Kushal Patel No relevant relationships by Arthur Oliver Romero
