N2  - Supraglottitis is an acute bacterial infection involving the epiglottis, arytenoids and aryepiglottic folds, which can cause acute airway obstruction. Awake fibreoptic intubation (AFOI) is the favoured technique in an anticipated difficult airway. Although they rarely fail [1], when they do, reasons for this may not be straightforward. Description An 84-year-old woman was admitted with breathlessness, cough, sore throat and hoarse voice. She was treated for pneumonia but subsequently deteriorated; developing biphasic stridor with increased work of breathing. She was given dexamethasone, nebulised adrenaline and transferred to theatre. Humidified nasal oxygen was commenced and front of neck structures marked. Nasoendoscopy identified supraglottitis with enough space to attempt endotracheal intubation. A plan was made for AFOI with an ear, nose and throat (ENT) consultant scrubbed for a tracheostomy in event of failure. Co-phenylcaine and 10% lidocaine, total dose 7 mg.kg-1, was used for topicalisation. A decision was made to avoid sedation in view of the threatened airway. Shortly after, she deteriorated, becoming anxious, tachycardic, tachypnoeic and hypoxic. AFOI was abandoned and emergency cricothyrodotomy was performed by the ENT team, then converted to a surgical tracheostomy. She recovered fully and was discharged from hospital after 12 days. Discussion The deterioration in our patient's condition following topicalisation is likely multifactorial, with issues relating to both the underlying pathology and AFOI. Topicalisation can reduce pharyngolaryngeal tone, causing dynamic obstruction on inspiration and precipitating upper airway collapse [2]; a picture exacerbated by high anxiety states. A spray-as-you-go technique has been advocated in this scenario as it can minimise time between topicalisation and intervention. Another consideration is local anaesthetic efficacy in infected tissues due to increased ionisation. Does this add to the secretion burden, thereby exposing patients to risks such as loss of tone and laryngospasm with limited benefit? The role of sedation here also remains debateable. There is a paucity of high-grade evidence to allow detection of fine discriminators. Additionally, emergency AFOI remains uncommon (1). We suggest that it is time to consider a central database to populate data to uncover any true patterns. An AFOI remains an essential technique but the manner in which it is performed may be more nuanced than we think.
