N2  - M. abscessus is the most difficult non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) to cure and erradicate. We made a retrospective study of patients with isolation of M. abscessus, following the ATS/IDSA 2020 criteria. We analyze epidemiological, clinical, therapeutic characteristics and a survival analysis in the northern area of Gran Canaria, about the last 20 years. In 462 patients some NTM was isolated. 13% corresponded to M. abscessus. 86.7% of the isolates were from the respiratory tract, of which 81 % fullfilled ATS/IDSA disease criteria. The mean age was 57.5 years, no gender difference. Radiological findings: Bronchiectasis 67%, nodules 45% and tree in bud 43%. The cardinal symptom was cough 80%. 60% received treatment and 56% obtained an immediate successful outcome. 40% did not receive treatment (due to loss follow-up or clinical stability), because in the first 10 years of the study, this possibility was still considered by international guides. The overall success rate was 36%. Patients with the same age and gender who were successful after the first treatment had a mortality risk 85.7% lower than the others. (HR=0.143 (0.028;0.725)). Most of the pharmacological guidelines were based on at least 3-4 drugs for long periods of time (6-12 months), including Clarithromycin; Amikacin; Linezolid; Quinolones and Carbapenems. 72% of patients required a treatment's change during their evolution, due to poor response or adverse effects. There was worse survival in COPD (p=0.003), Dyspnea(p=0.003) and poor respiratory function(p<0.05). None of our patients died for M. abscessus. Although the management of M. abscessus disease is difficult, with a close follow-up and correct pharmacological combinations, we can achieve favorable results.
