N2  - Introduction: Persistent bacterial bronchitis (PBB), Chronic Suppurative Lung Disease (CSLD) and non - cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis are entities of chronic endobronchial infection and major causes of childhood morbidity. Aim: To evaluate clinical characteristics, bronchoscopic findings and microbiology of children with chronic lower airway infection. Methods: Retrospective study of medical files from patients with endobronchial suppuration who underwent bronchoscopy in the pediatric Allergology and Pulmonology Unit of University Hospital Attikon in Athens. Results: 41 children were studied (mean age 7 years [6 months-16 years]). 19.5% were categorized as PBB, 43.9% as CSLD and 36.6% had bronchiectasis. Recurrent lower respiratory infections (41.5%) and chronic cough (39%) were the most common symptoms. 56.1% had preschool wheezing or asthma under systematic treatment, while only 12.2% had never received anti-asthmatic therapy. Bronchomalacia and / or tracheomalacia were detected in 51.2%. In 61% of total BAL specimens at least one pathogen was cultured in significant growth. Most frequent pathogens were: Staphylococcus aureus (22%), Haemophilus influenzae (17,1%), Streptococcus Pneumoniae (12,2%), Streptococcus Viridans (9,8%), Enterobacteria (7,3%), Moraxella Catarrhalis (4,9%). Bronchomalacia was correlated with isolation of any pathogen in significant growth (p-value=0,012). Conclusions: In children with chronic endobronchial suppuration: 1) Preschool wheezing / asthma is a frequent comorbidity, 2) airway malacia is common and bronchomalacia may be associated with higher bacterial load, 3) a possible upcoming role of S. aureus should be evaluated.
