N2  - Background: nCFnPCD-BE results in significant morbidity (Quint: ERJ: 2016). Long term outcomes in preschool children are unknown. We describe clinical characteristics and outcomes of preschool children with nCFnPCD-BE. Methods: Retrospective single centre review of children with computerised tomography (CT) confirmed nCFnPCDBE between 2000-2016. Demographics, co-morbidities, aetiology, microbiology, and imaging were recorded. Results: Fifteen children were identified with nCFnPCD-BE aged ≤ 6 yr. Mean age at diagnosis was 4 (1.9-6.3) years, 6M:9F. Etiology was identified as post infectious in 1 (6.7%), aspiration 1 (6.7%), obliterative bronchiolitis 3 (20%) and idiopathic in 2 (13.3%). 7 (46.6%) had isolated bronchiectasis, 8 (53.3%) had complex multisystem disease. There was no difference in age at diagnosis or severity of bronchiectasis between these groups (4.1 vs 3.9 years, p=0.9078). All patients had at least one cough swab sample per year. 3 (20%), all with multisystem comorbidities, grew Pseudomonas. CT was repeated in 13 (86.7%) at a mean interval of 6.2 years. 10/13 (76.9%) showed improvement in distribution and/or severity of bronchiectasis. 3 (23.1%) had worsening changes. Conclusion: Preschool nCFnPCD bronchiectasis is rare and children commonly have multisystem co-morbidities. Interval CT scan shows improvement in over 75% of children. Early age of diagnosis may positively impact outcome. The natural history of nCFnPCD-BE in preschool children is not clearly understood and further work is required to better understand this, its risk factors and factors affecting outcome.
