N2  - Background: Aspergillus fumigatus is the dominant fungal pathogen colonising airways of up to 50% of adult patients with CF. Fungal lung infections are a major complication of CF, contributing to pulmonary exacerbations, and accelerating lung function decline. Detection of Aspergillus colonisation/infection is therefore an important feature of routine microbiological surveillance in CF. Since the wide introduction of CFTR-modulators (ELE/TEZ/IVA) patients with CF typically describe reduced sputum volumes, and in many cases struggle to produce any sample for microbiological culture. Cough swabs are routinely used as an alternative in paediatrics, and are known to have reduced sensitivity for bacterial pathogens but sensitivity for fungal infections is unknown. Objectives: To determine whether cough swabs are a reliable alternative to sputum culture for the detection of Aspergillus fumigatus in patients with CF. Method: Adult patients with CF provided paired sputum samples and cough swabs, taken at the same time. Sputum sample was prepared using the high-volume culture method, and applied directly onto the Sabouraudagar (SAB) plate culture media. The cough swab was applied directly onto the plate. Both sampleswere then cultured at 30°C for 14 days. Results: Samples from 45 patients were analysed. 14 (31%) patients had positive sputum cultures for Aspergillus sp. From this cohort, only 1 paired cough swab (7%) also grew Aspergillus fumigatus. There were no positive cough swab samples without a paired positive sputum culture sample. Conclusion: Cough swabs showa low rate of detection of Aspergillus when it is known to be present in sputum. Molecular techniques may be more sensitive but are not validated or widely available. Cough swabs are therefore not good enough at detecting Aspergillus to provide a suitable, reliable alternative to a sputum culture, and cannot be used to rule out fungal infection.
