N2  - Background: Prompt detection of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) may prevent chronic infection in cystic fibrosis (CF), but challenges exist. Cough swabs are insensitive compared with sputum; young patients and many adults on CFTR modulators cannot expectorate sputum; standard culture intervals may miss infection, and costs preclude frequent surveillance. Medical Detection Dogs demonstrated excellent sensitivity and specificity in detecting Pa in culture broths (ERJ 2019;54:1900970).We are now developing a method suitable for frequent home screening. Gauze loaded into a mouthpiece provides a large surface area onto which the subject ‘huffs’; this is then rapidly screened for Pa volatiles by trained dogs. Methods: 10 clinical Pa and 30 non-Pa (neg control) isolates were cultured in triplicate, adjusted by optical density and diluted to generate high, medium and low concentrations. 20 μl drops of these (~1–5 × 106, 103 & 100CFU respectively) were placed on the gauze (n = 30 Pa, n = 90 neg) mimicking a ‘huff’. Sterile gauze (n = 45) served as blanks. Gauze was incubated overnight in Pa-selective broth. Sampleswere presented to three trained dogs in a randomised, double-blind study. Positive responses were indicated by the dog standing still; immediate trainer unblinding allowed rewards for correct responses. Data are means (95%CI). Results: Dogs ignored 99.3 (95.9,100)% blanks and 90.0 (85.8,93.0)% neg controls. They correctly identified Pa in 100 (95.9,100)% high, 97.8 (92.3,99.4)% medium and 63.3 (53.0,72.6)% low concentration samples. Conclusions: Trained dogs rapidly identify Pa from gauzes after overnight enrichment even when starting with very low bacterial numbers. Pilot testing of patient samples is now starting; results will be available at the conference. This technique could allowhundreds of samples to be screened per day thus providing a form of frequent home surveillance for increasing numbers of non-sputum-producing patients.
