N2  - Introduction: Treatments with inhaled antibiotics as a treatment for eradication of acute and chronic Pseudomonas infection of the airways in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF) lead to reduction in rate of lung function deterioration and decreased hospitalisation. The aerosol administration has been proven to be effective and safe. Objective: To identify the incidence of inhaled tobramycin solution hypersensitivity or allergic reactions in CF patients in the Center for Cystic Fibrosis at the Institute for Pulmonary Diseases in Children, North Macedonia. Methods: Fifty-nine patients (range 6–45 years, median 25.5 y) with acute and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection who inhaled tobramycin solution (on–off 28 days) were monitored by a CF clinical pharmacist for allergic reactions over a period of 3 years (2019–2021). Any suspicion of a possible allergic reaction was thoroughly investigated and reported. Results: Three patients (5.08%) had recurrent bronchospasm, persistent cough, severe headaches, redness of face, and feeling unwell despite premedication with a bronchodilator, and discontinued the treatment with tobramycin. Аll patients had a complete recovery after discontinuation of the therapy. Conclusion: Despite the good safety profile and benefits of inhaled tobramycin therapy, the treatment had to be discontinued due to hypersensitivity and allergic manifestations
