N2  - Objectives: Among CF patients, Pa infection is associated with lung function decline and increased mortality. Pa is the most prevalent infective organism and is difficult to eradicate. In addition, chronic anti-Pa antibiotic therapy lead to multi-drug resistant (MDR) Pa infections in adults. Lytic bacteriophages (‘phages’) offer an alternative/adjunctive approach for the treatment of MDR chronic Pa lung infections. Phages are natural, speciesspecific viruses that infect bacteria and amplify locally. Unlike antibiotics, phages can penetrate biofilm formed by Pa using specific depolymerising enzymes. The aim of the current study is to develop a phage therapy for the treatment of chronicPA pulmonary infections in CF patients. Methods: Clinical strains derived from the US and Europe CF patients’ sputum were used for phage isolation. Host range of isolated phages was established by plaque assay and activity within biofilmswas determined by viability assay and crystal violet staining. A breathing simulatorwas used to determine the delivered dose by nebulisation; droplet size distributionwas tested by laser diffraction. Results: A selected lytic phage combination targeted most of the strains in the panel and significantly reduced Pa embedded in the biofilm. Combination of phage with SoC antibiotic revealed synergy both in liquid culture and in biofilm. Breathing simulation results revealed phage viability following nebulisation, estimated delivered dose of viable phages contained in particles that are likely to reach the lower airways was 1.8×109 PFU/dose. Phages safety was demonstrated by ex vivo and animal models. Conclusions: A natural phage cocktail that demonstrated potent antimicrobial activity in vitro and within biofilm, broad host range against clinical Pa CF isolates and high viability by nebulisation was developed. A randomised study will be initiated to evaluate the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of this phage cocktail in adult CF patients with chronic Pa pulmonary infection
