N2  - Introduction: Tobipodhaler (TIP) is a dry powder inhaler for pseudomonal suppression designed to reduce burden of care by being portable and quick. Real-world data of TIP compared to the equivalent nebulised tobramycin over a 12-month period showed improved self-reported adherence (43% vs 83% respectively) and significant reduction in intravenous antibiotics required (Harrison et al. 2014). However, there has been no data looking at the longer term tolerability of TIP. Methods: Retrospective data from 171 patients who started TIP at the Royal Brompton Hospital (RBH) adult CF centre was collected using electronic medical records. Results: 171 pwCF (89 females, 52%) started TIP at RBH with a mean FEV12.28 L (77%) and FVC4.5 L (94%). Data recorded up until Nov. 2021 showed 71 (41.5%) stopped taking TIP with a mean length of use of 3 years (range 1 month - 9 years 5 months). Of the patients who discontinued TIP, 45.1% (32) stopped secondary to side effects, with chest tightness (46.5%) and poor adherence (19.7%) being the most common reason. The group that stopped TIP had a lower FEV1 than the group that continued (FEV174.0% vs FEV179.0%), however this was not statistically significant. Conclusion: 58.5% of pwCF who started TIP at RBH continue to use it as part of their anti-pseudomonal maintenance therapy. The primary reason for stopping TIP is documented as adverse side effects. In this data it was primarily chest tightness rather than cough or dysphonia as per previous TIP studies. Therewas no difference in the characteristics of the peoplewho stopped TIP and those who did not. The discontinuation rate is higher overall than the previous real-world data (14%) recorded over a 6-month period; however, 82.6% of the pwCF in this cohort were still using TIP at 6 months. The higher continuation rate may be linked to robust training on inhalation technique given by specialist physiotherapists. In conclusion, TIP is still a useful tool for pseudomonas suppression in CF.
