N2  - Objectives and Study: Describe the factors associated with survival in Mexican pediatric CF patients. Prospective cohort study from 1990 to 2018 of pediatric CF patients registered at the Hospital Infantil de México Federico Gómez. Methods: All patients who attended the CF Clinic were registered, anthropometric evaluation, pulmonary function evaluation with FEV1 and FVC, values more than 80% were considered as optimal lung fuction, also a psychosocial evaluation was performed. Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency was defined as requirement for regular pancreatic enzyme supplementation, undernutrition was considered BMI-2.0 SD or weight for height-2.0 SD, treatment goverment cover was defined as the full supply of pancreatic enzymes, dornase alfa, nebulized tobramycin, salbutamol, vitamins and oral antibiotics as nedded. Positive Pseudomona aeruginosa was defined from a sputum sample or cough swab within 7 years old. Data analysis, survival from date of first assessment to the last visit was calculated by the Kaplan Meier method. Pearson correlation coefficients were calculated to test relationships between predictor variables, the analyses were performed using SPSS 21. Results: We included 341 patients from 1990 to 2018, the estimated median age of survival is 20 years (95%IC 17.3-23), 55% of patients were diagnosed before 2 years, with no significant OR for survival. 91% of the patients have pancreatic exocrine insufficiency with a p.value <0.01 as predictor variable for survival. 44% of the patients had suboptimal values for FEV 1 and 26% of our patients had suboptimal values for FVC. Major impact on survival were detected in patients with positive P.aeuroginosa within 7 years and those with undernutrition. 61% of our patients didn't have treatment goverment cover with a clear effect on survival. Conclusions: To our knowledge, this study is the first report of survival in CF patients in Latin America. It's important to highlight the difference in median survival (MS) reported from USA in 2017 that was 43.6 years vs 46.2 in 2019, while the UK MS was 47 years in the same period, which makes a difference of more than 2 decades in the survival reported in our study. The predictor variables affecting survival agree with other reports, but not with the diagnosis before 2 years, this could be explained by 2 reasons, the implementation of CF diagnosisis by new born screenign started in 2015, so, we consider that the effect on survival is not enought yet, those patients with early diagnosis didn't have full treatment coverage. Many interventions have been performed since the beginning of the cohort. We want highlight the treatment coverage for the goverment from 2015 and the intervention of the nutrition team (NT) from 2006. The NT was integrated full time to the CF clinic, with the counting of fat grams, dynamic management of pancreatic enzymes, increase of energy density, homemade elemental diet, as well as the management of food portions, so we expect to see a greater effect on survival in the following years. However, we want to emphasize that the treatment with CFTR modulators it's not available in Mexico, therefore, the survival results of this study are strongly associated with the multidisciplinary approach and the continuous education of patients and parents, which has made an important difference above all quality of life of patients.
