N2  - Background: Chronic wet cough is a recognized entity in children. It can be a manifestation of several respiratory diseases. It also can suggest endobonchial infection, and if left untreated can lead to bronchiectasis. We aim in this study to correlate the bronchoscopic findings in children with chronic cough with the clinical and microbiological findings. Patients and methods: A retrospective chart review of all children with the complaint of chronic wet cough who attended the pulmonology clinic at Queen Rania Hospital for children between January 2013 to December 2016 was conducted. Children between 1-14 years of age were included. Inclusion criteria included chronic productive cough more than 4 weeks. Children who were known to have a chronic disease or were found to have an alternative diagnosis were excluded. A specially formulated data sheet was used. Microbiology results and bronchoscopy findings were included as well. Results: There were 80 patients (55% males and 45% females) who were included in the retrospective chart review. The median age was 3.6 years. Structural abnormality of the airway was seen in 83% (66 patients). The most common was bronchomalacia (50%). Normal bronchoscopy was detected in 12 children (15%). Inflamed airway mucusae was seen in 55% of cases, while purulent secretions were seen in 30%. Foreign body was discovered in 2% of cases. BAL culture was positive in 45% of cases (36 patients) especially in those with purulent discharge. The most common microorganisms detected were Streptococcus pneumoniae and Hemophilus influenza spp. Conclusion: Children who presented with chronic cough showed evidence of purulent bronchitis that correlated with positive bacterial growth in bronchoalveolar lavage. The possibility of a missed foreign body can be found as a cause of the symptoms.
