N2  - Objective: To investigate the etiology of necrotizing pneumonia (NP) in children and the clinical characteristics of NP caused by different pathogens. Methods: A retrospective, case-control study was performed in children with NP who were admitted in Children's Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine from January 2008 to December 2019. The data were divided into three groups based on the following years: 2008-2011, 2012-2015 and 2016-2019, and the distribution characteristics of the pathogens in the different periods were compared. The demographic and clinical information, laboratory data, etiological and radiological findings were analyzed. T-test or Mann-Whitney nonparametric test was used for comparison of numerical variables, and χ2 test was used for categorical variables. Results: A total of 146 children with NP were enrolled, the average ages were 6.71 years, including 70 boys and 76 girls. There were 17 cases during 2008-2011, 30 cases during 2012-2015, and 99 cases during 2016-2019. Among these patients, pathogens were identified in 106 cases and were unclear in the remaining 40 cases. The main pathogens were Mycoplasma pneumoniae (MP) (74 cases), Streptococcus pneumoniae (SP) (20 cases), Staphylococcus aureus (SA) (10 cases), adenovirus (4 cases), Streptococcus pyogenes (2 cases), Pseudomonas aeruginosa (1 case) and G + Graveney actinomycetes (1 case), and MP was the most common pathogen in all three periods. There was no significant difference in the proportion of MP in 2008-2011, 2012-2015 and 2016-2019 (52.9% (9/17cases) vs. 53.3% (16/30cases) vs. 49.5% (49/99cases), P = 0.67). The main clinical manifestations of MP necrotizing pneumonia were fever and cough, other manifestations included shortness of breath, chest pain, wheezing, hemoptysis, vomiting, abdominal pain, and so forth. Necrotizing pneumonia led to more extrapulmonary complications, with 68 cases (46.6%) presenting with extrapulmonary complications, most involving one system, and 23 cases (15.8%) involving two or more systems. After treatments, all patients were discharged without death. As to prognosis, 103 cases were followed up, and the results showed that 2 of them underwent surgical intervention and in the remaining patients, chest lesions were improved. Conclusions: The prevalence of pediatric NP shows an increasing trend during the past years. MP is the main pathogen of NP in China, and the most common clinical symptoms are fever and cough. NP is found to be severe and prolonged, yet reversible through proper therapy.
