N2  - “The more I learn, the more I realize how much I don't know”, quote by Albert Einstein. This still applies today in almost all fields. Thus, what do we know about Cystic fibrosis (CF)? • It is a genetic and chronic disease • The phenotype depends on class mutations, but other factors like modifier genes can also affect disease severity • Without early treatment, the disease may progress quickly • The basic treatment helps, however, a deterioration may occur • There are some “mild” (?) mutations associated with “mild” (?) disease; however, disease severity can still be significant • There are specific treatments that can change the natural course of the disease The CFTR gene encodes the CFTR protein, which regulates the fluid and electrolyte balance in epithelial tissues. Mutations in the CFTR gene result in defective CFTR channels, the underlying defect of CF disease. As a consequence, the chloride transport is defective leading to salt-water imbalance, and accumulation of thick sticky mucus. The pathophysiology of CF is therefore characterized by the progressive loss of lung function through a cycle of infection, inflammation, and tissue damage. The phenotype of the disease is variable, depending on the different CFTR mutation class, but other factors like modifier genes can have an impact on disease severity. Additionally, the level of care, nutritional status, exposure to cigarette smoke and other toxins, and the age at onset of lung infection can significantly influence the phenotype. The various organ systems, such as the lungs, the sinuses, the sweat glands, the reproductive system, and the gastrointestinal tract, are affected differently, depending on the respective CFTR activity. It is well known that inflammation is present in infants as young as 4 weeks, regardless of airway bacterial colonization, and abnormalities start very early in life and can be detected in CT scans. Furthermore, bronchiectasis is present in infants and preschool children diagnosed with CF after newborn screening, although most are asymptomatic1. Therefore, the importance of early treatment initiation, before symptoms and loss of lung function are evident, is crucial. In parallel to inflammation, CF airway infection starts early in life. In order to assess the hypothesis that early and continuous antibiotic treatments can decrease the airway bacterial burden after birth, 3 groups of pigs (wild-type, CF, and CF treated continuously with broad-spectrum antibiotics) were studied from birth until ∼3 weeks of age2. The results showed that CF airway disease begins immediately after birth, and early and continuous antibiotics improved chest abnormalities as tested by CT imaging, as well as airway mucus accumulation, but not airway inflammation or other aspects of CF lung disease development. According to the CFF data, before the age of 6 years, ∼20% of CF patients are colonized by Pseudomonas3. Chronic infection with Pseudomonas in the airways is associated with decline in pulmonary function, worsening nutritional status, more hospital admissions and shorter life expectancy. Therefore, the value of early detection and treatment of new Pseudomonas infection was shown to result in more successful eradication4. Pulmonary exacerbations (PEx), commonly seen in CF patients, are defined as acute clinical deterioration, including malaise, anorexia, dyspnea, fever, increased cough, change in sputum quantity/quality, worsening of nutritional status and/or decline in pulmonary function, requiring addition or change of antibiotics. The decision regarding the duration of antibiotic treatment for PEx is based mainly on clinical experience rather than by evidence-based medicine. Recently, the results of the STOP2 trial, a landmark study in the treatment of PEx, were published5. This large randomized clinical trial, including almost 1,000 patients with CF, compared different lengths of antibiotic courses. The authors showed that for adults with CF with early treatment improvement during PEx, forced expiratory volume in 1 second percent predicted) FEV1) after 10 days of intravenous (IV) antibiotics is not inferior to 14 days, and for patients with less improvement after 7 days, 3 weeks is not superior to 2 weeks. FEV1, measured by spirometry, is being used routinely to monitor lung disease status, to evaluate the response to therapies and as an outcome measure in clinical trials. Recently, we showed that regardless of their age, most CF patients with normal FEV1 have already physiological (shown by lung clearance index-LCI) and structural lung abnormalities (shown by CT scans), which were associated with more PEx and IV antibiotic treatment6. The basis of CF management comprises prevention and standard therapy, including nutrition -hypercaloric-high fat diet-, antiinfectives, anti-inflammatory agents, improving mucociliary clearance, early detection of CF-related complications, adherence to treatment and routine follow-up in specialized CF centers. However, all these therapies do not treat the underlying cause of CF. In 2012, ivacaftor, the first new drug addressing the causative CFTR function defect, was approved for clinical use in patients with CF carrying gating mutations7. Functionally, ivacaftor is considered a potentiator, being able to increase the function of a residual protein. Later on, the development of correctors that may improve defective protein trafficking was another significant success. The combination of a potentiator (ivacaftor) and a corrector (lumacaftor or tezacaftor) showed beneficial clinical effects8,9 and has been proven effective in patients with the F508del mutation. Following FDA approval of Trikafta (a combination of elexacaftor, tezacaftor, and ivacaftor), there are finally effective treatment options available for most CFTR mutation types, including for patients carrying residual function (RF) mutations. Patients with RF mutation have a reduced rate of lung function decline compared with those homozygous for F508del10; however, they still develop progressive lung disease, particularly during adolescence, and therefore, for patients with a RF mutation, intensive treatment including CFTR modulators are recommended. Finally, two critical global events transformed the CF world over the last years-the worldwide use of CFTR modulators, and the impact of the SARS-2-CoV pandemic. We learned from the latter that we must be creative and adapt to these challenges, in which virtual consultation sometimes is the best and the unique option to follow up on our patients. That is what Albert Einstein taught us, “the measure of intelligence is the ability to change”.
