N2  - Non-tuberculosis mycobacterium (NTM) pulmonary infections are becoming more frequently identified worldwide. Current guidelines for diagnosis require microbiologic, radiographic and symptomatic criteria, which can be challenging in those with underlying symptomatic and radiographically significant lung disease. We present a case of an untreated Mycobacterium aviumintracellulare (MAI) culture compounded by a positive culture for Mycobacterium xenopi in a patient with a history of emphysema and cavitary lung disease. A 73-year-old female with a history of bullous emphysema and tobacco use presented to the pulmonology clinic after a CT chest revealed a left upper lobe (LUL) cavitary lesion. She had negative HIV, aspergillus, QuantiFERON gold and beta-d-glucan testing. Subsequent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) returned positive for MAI from broth only. She was asymptomatic at that time with spontaneous regression of her lesion. Treatment was deferred and she was lost to follow up. She re-established 5 years later after developing worsened cough and dyspnea. CT chest illustrated an increase in the LUL cavitary lesion with multifocal infiltrates in the left lung and a small left lower lobe cavity, which became concerning for active MAI infection. Bronchoscopy with BAL of LUL revealed negative acid-fast bacillus smear and culture, bacterial cultures and cytology. QuantiFERON gold was also negative. A few months later, she required hospitalization after a perforated diverticulum with subsequent exploratory laparotomy. She then developed worsened dyspnea prompting a CT chest. This showed a new mass in the left lung. Repeat bronchoscopy yielded cultures growing M. xenopi. She was intolerant of the recommended treatment with azithromycin, ethambutol and rifampin. She then unfortunately developed a superinfection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and progression of her underlying NTM infection. She ultimately decided to pursue a comfort-based approach. NTM includes over 190 species excluding Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium leprae. NTM, when isolated, is difficult to interpret as it is can be a contaminant given its prevalence in the environment. Predisposing factors for true infections include those with structurally abnormal lungs or who are immunosuppressed. Treatment consists of a prolonged course of a multi-drug regimen including a macrolide or moxifloxacin-based antibiotic, ethambutol and rifampin. Unfortunately, in M. xenopi in particular, there is a high mortality rate ranging from 51 to 69 percent at five years. As NTM infections appear to be a rising cause of pulmonary infections in humans, it is pertinent to understand the presentation and treatment recommendations in order to prevent significant morbidity and mortality in these patients.
