N2  - Nodule characteristics on Chest computerized tomography (CT) such as size, location, and distribution pattern aid in the diagnosis of its etiology. Our case is a 44-year-old man with a positive QuantiFERON test and a Chest imaging finding of diffuse nodular densities. Initial assessment was active tuberculosis; however, he was found to have incidental adenocarcinoma of the colon T4bNoMx. Due to the pattern of densities on CT he underwent multiple biopsies of the nodules which were all negative for malignancies. Our Patient is a 44-year-old man with history of exposure to Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) who recently immigrated to the USA from Mauritania and found to have a positive QuantiFERON test. He endorsed chronic fatigue, right lower quadrant (RLQ) abdominal pain and unintentional weight loss of about 15 lbs. He denied any night sweats, chronic cough. Physical examination was significant for RLQ tenderness. Chest X ray showed a 5 mm nodule in the right middle lung lobe.CT chest showed multiple diffuse pulmonary nodules with mediastinal adenopathy. Sputum AFB done was negative. Due to the gastrointestinal complaint, he underwent colonoscopy which revealed a friable and necrotic mass in the ascending colon and biopsy showed mucinous adenocarcinoma with signet-ring and negative surgical margin pT4bpNopMx. The sub centimeter bilateral diffuse nodules on chest CT were characteristic of a metastatic pattern and a CT needle biopsy (CTB) was done. Biopsy result was negative for malignancy. Due to the suspected low yield of CTB, Endobronchial ultrasound with transbronchial needle aspiration was done. Histologic analysis was also negative for malignancy. Patient underwent laparoscopic right hemicolectomy and started on adjuvant chemotherapy. Further investigation of the suspected metastatic disease to the lungs with video assisted thoracoscopy (VATS) was done with wedge excision of anteromedial segment of left lung. Result of the excision biopsy confirmed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation. Microbiological studies were positive for mycobacterial PCR and fungal culture was negative. He was started on antimycobacterial Therapy. Specie identification later revealed mycobacterium avium intracellular organism. Follow up imaging 1 year later showed a stable lesion with no change in nodule characteristic. Diffuse nodular densities on CT should be interpreted with consideration of overall clinical condition of a patient. Though patterns may be characteristic of specific etiology, biopsy of lesion is the most specific investigation. EBUS with TBNA has a 93.8 % sensitivity2 and benefits vs risks of further diagnostic work up should be considered.
