N2  - RATIONALE : Non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) lung infections are increasing in prevalence with significant symptoms and poor quality of life. It is commonly identified in patients with pre-existing structural lung disease (e.g cystic fibrosis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bronchiectasis) but its clinical relevance in interstitial lung disease (ILD) is poorly understood. We conducted a retrospective observational study to identify clinical predictors of NTM in ILD patients. METHODS : Retrospective chart reviews were performed on ILD patients followed in New York University Langone Health ILD clinic between January 2016 to July 2021 who had acid-fast bacilli (AFB) sputum smear/culture sent for suspected NTM infection. Data collection included demographics, symptoms, ILD classification, pulmonary function testing (PFT), ILD pattern on imaging and medications. Symptoms obtained from medical records included dyspnea, chest pain, cough, expectoration, unintentional weight loss, fatigue, night sweats and hemoptysis. Continuous variables were described as mean with standard deviation and categorical variables as frequency and percentage. Two-sample t-tests, Chi-square tests, Mann-Whitney tests and Kruskall-Wallace tests were used to compare continuous and categorical variables. Univariate and multivariate binary logistic regression model were utilized to identify significant independent predictors of NTM in ILD patients. RESULTS : Descriptive statistics and results are highlighted in (Table 1). Among the 98 patients included, NTM was identified in 51 patients (47 patients were NTM negative). The mean age in the total cohort was 69 years, predominantly Caucasian (58.8%) with 66% (n=64) females. Sarcoidosis (20.6%) followed by Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (15.5%) were the most common etiology for ILD. Usual interstitial pneumonia (32.6%) and undifferentiated fibrosis (31.6%) were the most common ILD imaging patterns described and were equally found in both groups. Mycobacterium avium-complex (MAC) (86.2%; n=41) was the most common species identified. On regression analysis, weight loss (X2=12.2, p<0.001) and cavitation (X2=10.96, p<0.001) on imaging were present exclusively in NTM positive group demonstrating complete separation. We also identified age (OR=1.06, p=0.011) and bronchial wall thickening (OR-5.04, p=0.011) as significant predictors of NTM in patients with ILD. The presence of other imaging findings, causes of ILD, use of immunosuppression, or inhaled corticosteroids were not predictive. CONCLUSION S: Advanced age, weight loss, cavitary lung disease, and bronchial wall thickening on CT scan were significant predictors of NTM in ILD patients. Lung function (FVC), inflammatory markers, and underlying diseases were similar in the two groups. Routine testing for NTM may be suggested if these risk-factors are present, although further studies are needed to confirm these findings. (Table Presented).
