N2  - Introduction: Pneumothorax is a known complication following bronchoscopic procedures, including bronchoscopic lung volume reduction (BLVR). An isolated loculated pneumothorax resulting in air entrapment within a fissure is an uncommon process that does not typically occur unless there are adhesions or inflammation present. To our knowledge, there have not been other reported cases of contained intrafissural pneumothorax following BLVR. Case: A 68-year-old male presented for evaluation regarding BLVR for GOLD grade 4, group D, emphysema type COPD. Daily symptoms included dyspnea, cough and nonpurulent sputum production which limited his daily activities. Preoperative pulmonary function testing demonstrated forced vital capacity (FVC) to forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) ratio 28%, FVC 2.52L (65% predicted), FEV1 0.7L (23% predicted), hemoglobin-corrected diffusion capacity of carbon monoxide (DLCO) 33%, total lung capacity 131% predicted and residual volume 260% predicted. ABG demonstrated pH 7.43, PaO2 77mmHG, PaCO2 40.7mmHG. Preoperative six-minute walk distance (6MWD) was 169m. He was found to require 2L of nocturnal supplemental oxygen. StratX™ analysis demonstrated 93% left fissure completeness. Chartis™ assessment revealed no collateral ventilation. BLVR was performed with four Zephyr™ endobronchial valves placed in LB1 LB2, LB3 and the common lingular segments (LB 4/5) with visualization of appropriate venting and negative saline leak test. Procedure was completed without any immediate complication. Eight days postoperatively, he developed chest pain and chest X-ray was concerning for infected bullae. He was treated with antibiotics for Pseudomonas on sputum culture. Follow-up CT chest demonstrated complete collapse of the left upper lobe (LUL) and lingula and a new 10cm air density in the region of the left oblique fissure. Serial monitoring with CT imaging suggested moderate sized pneumothorax contained within the left fissure. CT imaging through 4 months demonstrated adequate collapse of the LUL with improvement of the fissural pneumothorax. 6MWD improved by 83m with subjective improvement in dyspnea. Spirometry did not significantly change with FEV1 of 0.61L and FVC 1.75L. Discussion: Pneumothorax has been described as a possible complication of BLVR in up to 26% of cases. Our patient developed a loculated pneumothorax within the major fissure, which has not previously been reported. Though many patients will require chest tube placement, hemodynamically stable patients without respiratory signs or symptoms with pneumothorax may be observed. In this case, imaging revealed adequate evidence of LUL volume reduction and improvement of pneumothorax without intervention.
