N2  - Introduction: Necrotizing Sarcoid Granulomatosis (NSG) is a distinct rare pulmonary granulomatous disease of unknown aetiology which is characterized by sarcoid-like granulomas, extensive necrosis, and vasculitis. It is still controversial if NSG is a separate entity or a variant of sarcoidosis.Case: 34-year-old African American man with no significant PMH presented to the ED for evaluation of progressive left-sided vision loss for 1 week associated with left lower extremity weakness. He also reported intermittent cough, weight loss (20lbs) and night sweats for the last 3 months. An ophthalmology exam revealed bilateral keratic precipitates, subepithelial corneal infiltrates, periphlebitis, and multiple clusters of intraretinal hemorrhages on fundal exam in the right eye. This was concerning for an infectious vs inflammatory process hence ganciclovir was started empirically. Labs were significant for elevated calcium, creatinine and low Vitamin D level. Workup for HIV, hepatitis B/C, HSV, CMV, toxoplasma, RPR, and Quantiferon testing was negative. MRI brain/spine revealed extensive parenchymal and leptomeningeal abnormalities in the intracranial compartment along with multiple foci of spinal cord signal abnormalities and contrast enhancement within the thoracic and lumbar spine. Lumbar puncture showed atypical cells however negative cytology. CT C/A/P revealed bronchiectasis and hilar retraction in the setting of pulmonary interstitial fibrosis, bulky mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly with hypodensities, and mesenteric, retroperitoneal, and pelvic lymphadenopathy. Further workup including ANCA, anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide, rheumatoid factor, Fungitell, galactomannan, Aspergillus antigen/antibody was negative. The patient underwent bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound with Transbronchial Needle aspiration and bronchial biopsies. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) cultures were negative for fungal, acid-fast bacilli (AFB), and other respiratory pathogens. Cytology showed benign respiratory epithelial cells with no atypical cells. Histological analysis of lung biopsy specimen showed benign lung parenchyma with well-formed necrotizing granuloma, with no fungal elements or acid-fast bacilli which is consistent with a diagnosis of NSG, in absence of malignancy, infectious or autoimmune disease. The patient was started on pulse dose steroids and reported symptomatic improvement within a week and is currently being followed as an outpatient.Discussion: As demonstrated by our case, diagnosis of NSG can be challenging and requires a high index of suspicion. NSG is a rare entity that should remain in the differential diagnoses of necrotizing granulomatous processes of the lung. NSG's definite diagnosis requires excluding a malignant, infectious, or autoimmune process that may also present with granuloma. NSG holds a favorable prognosis with a good response to steroid treatment.
