N2  - Introduction: Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disease characterized by non-caseating granulomas. The lungs are commonly involved, up to 95% of the time. Typical chest radiographic pattern of sarcoidosis is lymphadenopathy and bronchovascular nodularity. We report a rare case of miliary pattern of sarcoidosis. Case Report: A 26-year-old gentleman presented with a three-day history of dry cough, malaise, and fatigue. He had no other complaints including skin or vision complaints. He worked as a mechanic, denied occupational inhalational exposure, incarceration or travel, and had no pets, birds, or exotic hobbies. He had no sick contacts or exposures to tuberculosis. On initial encounter, his vital signs were stable, physical examination was benign and labs were unremarkable including a normal calcium level. Chest x-ray showed diffuse bilateral scattered nodularity, followed by computerized tomography that showed scattered diffuse nodular infiltrates consistent with a miliary pattern. A fluoroscopic evaluation ruled out aspiration, HIV was negative, blood and induced sputum cultures showed no growth. Acid fast bacilli (AFB) smear and culture were negative. Interferon Gamma Release Assay were negative. Histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, and aspergillosis serologies were negative. He was admitted with pulmonology consulted and the next day underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL), brushing, and transbronchial biopsy. Cultures showed no growth, both BAL and biopsy were negative for any AFB, including Non-Tuberculosis Mycobacterium (NTM), viral, fungal, pneumocystis. Cytology of BAL and brushing showed no evidence of malignancy or infectious process. BAL showed 63% macrophages and moderate lymphocytes. Biopsy showed non-caseating granulomas with evidence of respiratory bronchiolitis. A formal diagnosis of sarcoidosis was made. No evidence of cardiac sarcoid on echo. He was started on methotrexate and prednisone eventually and was given a follow up in sarcoid clinic. Discussion: The Siltzbach classification system defines five stages of sarcoidosis radiographically: stage 0, with no abnormality; stage 1, with only hilar lymphadenopathy; stage 2, with adenopathy and abnormal lung parenchyma; stage 3, with abnormal lung parenchyma only; and stage 4, with fibrotic changes. Miliary sarcoidosis is rare. When encountering case of miliary pattern on chest imaging, clinicians must first rule out other etiologies of infection or metastatic malignancy before diagnosing miliary sarcoidosis. Miliary nodules have a random distribution and there is no relation to pleural surface, small vessels, and interlobular septa. In our case, there was a predominant perilymphatic and subpleural pattern of the nodules which may be suggestive of a pseudo-miliary pattern.
