N2  - A former 39 week, then 8 week old Caucasian male was referred to the pediatric pulmonary clinic with a prenatal history of situs inversus (diagnosed at 20 weeks gestational age) and intermittent cough since ∼ 2 weeks of age. Birth weight was 4.22 kilograms. Apgars were 8 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes of life respectively. He had an unremarkable postnatal course without evidence of respiratory distress. Maine CF NBS was negative. He passed his newborn hearing screening. SNP microarray was negative. The cough did not occur during sleep. There was no known position that worsened the cough although it did occur immediately after eating and burping. Endorsed intermittent nasal congestion and stertor; no hoarseness, increased work of breathing, apnea, cyanosis, tone changes, choking, gagging, bottle propping or concern for foreign body aspiration. Weight gain is excellent. There has been no evidence of acute illness nor had he been prescribed antibiotics, systemic steroids or inhaled medications. Lung examination was without focality. Two view plain chest films revealed mild bronchial wall thickening and dextrocardia. Oropharyngeal bacterial culture grew Enterobacter cloacae, Citrobacter Freundii, Klebsiella Oxytoca and Haemophilus Influenzae. An echocardiogram revealed dextrocardia and small PFO. Genetic sequencing ultimately revealed 1 pathogenic variant in the DNA12 (c.1407C>A (p.Cys469∗)) and 1 variant of uncertain significance (c.84C>T(p.Thr285Met)) in the DNA12 gene (Invitae Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) Panel). Parental testing confirmed that each one is a carrier of the mutations he has. Furthermore, he had normal Heterotaxy and Congenital Heart Disease panels performed through Invitae. A modified barium swallow study with speech therapy is pending. He was initiated on daily acid suppression in the form of a H2 blocker and intranasal saline as needed with improvement in his cough. His parents elected to initiate manual chest physiotherapy twice a day in addition to as needed. He has close follow up in the general pediatric and pulmonary clinics. There is not one universal way to make the diagnosis of PCD. Instead, it may be made via genetic testing and/or via electron microscopy of the cilia. The yield of ciliary biopsy in this age group is low and he is too young to measure nasal nitric oxide (NO). At this point, he is a carrier for a known mutation which causes PCD. Time will tell if his clinical status changes and/or more information is gleaned from his known variant of uncertain significance that may alter his diagnosis.
