N2  - The leading cause of death in patients after renal transplant is sepsis or infection related disease. Moreover, upwards of 70% of patients will have any infection within 3 years of receiving a renal transplant and it has been previously estimated that 47% will develop a bacterial infection. Renal allograft recipients are immunosuppressed often with combination of Inosine-5'-monophosphate, mycophenolate, and mTOR inhibitor tacrolimus , and will always be at risk for opportunistic infection. In patients with immunosuppression, disseminated infection requires prompt and accurate diagnosis for precision treatment. A 57 year old male with history hypertension and end stage renal disease presented to the hospital with severe flank and abdominal pain for 2 days that was preceded by 2 weeks of cough with brownish sputum. He had undergone previous transplant in 2012, but, due to acute humoral rejection and transplant failure, had undergone deceased donor transplant in January 2020. His immunosuppression regimen included mycophenolate and tacrolimus. He was a non smoker with no exposure to tuberculosis and denied any hemoptysis, lymphadenopathy, fevers, chills, or drenching night sweats. His physical exam was noted for marked tender subcutaneous nodules over the periumbilical area and left flank with normal pulmonary findings and neurologic function. CT imaging of the chest, performed due to worsening cough, revealed a necrotic 22mm X 22mm lesion at the confluence of the right middle and upper lobes that communicated with the pleura. His abdominal lesions were biopsied and showed only necrotic material. Bronchoscopy with protected brush biopsy revealed a weakly positive acid fast organism, Nocardia farinica. MRI of the brain revealed 4 foci of restricted diffusion consistent with a diagnosis of disseminated nocardiosis. He was started on IV Amikacin for 6 weeks and an extended regimen of 12 months Trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole and Linezolid. Pulmonary infiltrates in immunocompromised individuals must quickly be identified with proper sampling since diagnostic yield by early bronchoscopic sampling, within the first 4 days of presentation, is almost 2.5 times greater than those performed late. Moreover, clinically relevant growth varies based on sampling technique and 1 X 103 CFU/ml growth in a protected brush specimen demonstrating 88% specificity previously . Presented here, although the patient had subcutaneous lesions that were easily accessible, they did not yield a diagnosis whereas a protected brush biopsy allowed the prompt diagnosis and treatment of a patient with disseminated disease at high risk for morbidity and mortality. (Figure Presented).
