N2  - Introduction:Legionella bacteria are aerobic, gram-negative pathogens that account for 2-15% of cases of community-acquired pneumonia requiring hospitalisation. Patients who are immunocompromised, especially due to hematologic malignancy and solid organ transplantation are at risk for infection. Vasculitides are a group of entities featuring vascular inflammation and injury, which can be triggered by infections and cytokine production and recruitment of neutrophils to small vessels. We present a renal transplant patient who developed pulmonary hemorrhage and acute renal failure with positive P-ANCA after contracting Legionella pneumonia. Case:A 30 yearold female with history of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis status post-renal transplant two years prior, on chronic immunosuppression, presented to the ER with worsening cough and intermittent hemoptysis for 1 month, associated with nausea and vomiting. On presentation she was hemodynamically stable and afebrile. Labs revealed hemoglobin 5.7, creatinine 17 and bicarbonate level 11. Chest computed tomography showed diffuse bilateral ground-glass-opacities and consolidations. Bronchoscopy demonstrated bloody secretions and lavage cultures grew Legionella pneumophila. She was given levofloxacin. Rheumatological workup revealed positive ANA, Anti- SSA, ANCA, MPO, PR3 and p-ANCA 1:160. Her renal failure worsened, necessitating hemodialysis, and subsequent renal biopsy revealed acute renal transplant rejection as well as focal interstitial hemorrhage and thrombotic microangiopathy. She received plasma exchange with intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) and thymoglobulin, pulse dose steroids, and rituximab. Discussion: Pulmonary hemorrhage can be caused by infection or autoimmune etiologies. Legionella infection can precipitate direct necrosis of alveolar epithelium via a tissue-destructive protease causing alveolar translocation of erythrocytes, leading to clinical findings of diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. It has also been associated with development of a more systemic autoimmunemediated vasculitis. This is supported by serum ANCA detected in Legionella infections, as in our patient. De novo ANCA-associated vasculitis after renal transplantation is exceptionally rare. Instead it is likely that this clinical picture of vasculitis in our patient was triggered by the concomitant Legionella infection. Legionella-induced vasculitis is a rare phenomenon in the literature, and morbidity has been linked to delayed diagnosis and treatment, which includes plasmapheresis, steroids, and IVIG. Timely and aggressive management of both the infection and autoimmune sequelae can help preserve precious renal function as in our patient with a solitary transplanted kidney. Our case highlights the importance of a broad differential with consideration for infectious workup in vasculitis, particularly in an immunocompromised patient with a pulmonary-renal syndrome.
