N2  - Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS) diagnosed after allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) is the only pulmonary complication formally associated with chronic-graftversus host disease (GVHD). Different phenotypes of BOS are currently being identified. The presence of bronchiectasis can be observed at diagnosis or during the follow-up of BOS. The aim of this study was to determine the characteristics and outcomes of BOS-associated bronchiectasis. We reviewed the data from a cohort of consecutive patients managed at Saint Louis Hospital for BOS between 1981 and 2019. The clinical, radiological and pulmonary function data obtained during the patient follow-up were analyzed. Bronchiectasis was assessed using the BRICS (Bronchiectasis radiologically indexed CT score). The strength of the association between patient characteristics and the occurrence of bronchiectasis was measured with odds ratios, estimated from univariate analyses. Multivariate analysis allowed adjustment for potential confounding factors. Overall survival was estimated using the Kaplan-Meier method. A total of 154 patients with BOS were included, including 43 (28%) patients with bronchiectasis at the time of their BOS diagnosis and 29 (19%) patients who developed bronchiectasis during follow-up. Patients with early bronchiectasis had more symptoms of cough (p=0.0008) and sputum (p=0.005) than the other patients. A higher age at transplant, a history of acute GVHD, and treatment with prednisone or mycophenolate mofetil since transplant were the factors associated with the presence of early bronchiectasis, with no evidence of impact on survival after a BOS diagnosis (p = 0.43). While there was no difference in the prevalence of pneumonia, bacterial bronchial colonization was more common in patients with early bronchiectasis (p=0.048). Female sex, a history of acute GVHD, less history of smoking and a lower forced expiratory volume in one second at BOS diagnosis were associated with the occurrence of late bronchiectasis by multivariate analysis. A female donor/male recipient sex mismatch and a related donor were protective factors for bronchiectasis, while treatment with prednisone since transplant and acute GVHD were deleterious factors for overall bronchiectasis. BOS-associated bronchiectasis was associated with specific factors depending on whether they were present at the time of diagnosis of BOS or occurred later in its course, suggesting different phenotypes of BOS.
