N2  - RATIONALE: Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia causes both direct cytotoxic and immune-mediated lung injury which can result in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Macrophages are professional phagocytes responsible for clearing pathogens in the lung. Both soluble factors, such as pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), and environmental cues, such as tissue stiffness, influence macrophage polarization and effector function. Flagellin is a highly immunogenic protein component of the bacterial flagellum recognized by macrophages through Toll-like receptor 5 (TLR5). Inflammation leads to changes in the biophysical properties of the lung which can be sensed by macrophages through mechanosensitive, Ca2+ permeable cation channels, such as Piezo1. We hypothesize that macrophages integrate the TLR5 and Piezo1 intracellular signal upon flagellin exposure, leading to augmented intracellular Ca2+ and macrophage effector function. METHODS: The role of flagellin on Piezo1 function was examined via a commercially-available florescent Ca2+ assay (FLIPR Calcium 5 Assay Kit, Molecular Devices) in murine bone marrow derived macrophages (BMDMs) and immortalized BMDMs (iBMDMs, NR-9456). BMDMs were stimulated ± flagellin from P. aeruginosa (250 ng/mL, 24 h, FLA-PA Ultrapure, Invivogen) and then treated ± indicated concentrations of Piezo1 agonist (Yoda1). A one-way ANOVA was used to compare intracellular Ca2+ as measured in percent radiofrequency units (%RFU) normalized to the untreated control (RPMI without flagellin). To determine if changes in intracellular Ca2+ led to changes in macrophage effector function, we performed a phagocytosis assay with heat inactivated fluorescent Escherichia coli particles (Vybrant Phagocytosis Assay, Molecular Probes) in iBMDMs. iBMDMs were stimulated ± flagellin, as above ± Yoda1 (10-20 μM, 4 h). RESULTS: For the first time, we report that flagellin sensitizes Piezo1 to the agonist effect of Yoda1 (Figure 1). Flagellin sensitizes the Piezo1 channel with a greater than a ten-fold shift in the Yoda1 dose-response curve (15 μM to 1μM at 2000% RFU) leading to a two-fold increase (2093 vs. 4670% RFU, p <0.0001) in intracellular Ca2+ concentration when compared with Yoda1 alone. Interestingly, flagellin induces twice as much intracellular Ca2+ as the known Piezo1 sensitizer, lipopolysaccharide (LPS). Flagellin modestly increases (14% at 20 uM Yoda1) phagocytosis of E. coli particles by iBMDMs in response to Yoda1. CONCLUSION: Flagellin augments Piezo1-mediated increases in intracellular Ca2+ and modestly induces macrophage phagocytic activity. These data reveal a novel interaction between flagellin signaling and Piezo1- dependant mechanosensitivity that affects key macrophage function. This convergent signaling likely participates in the macrophage functions that underlie immune-mediated lung injury during bacterial pneumonia.
