N2  - RATIONALE: Bronchiectasis are abnormal permanently dilated bronchi which lead to productive cough and other respiratory symptoms. Tuberculosis is a common cause of bronchiectasis. Data on this association are scarce. The objective of this study was to describe the profile of patients with post-tuberculosis bronchiectasis in our hospital. METHODS: This was a retrospective study with patients in follow-up at a hospital in the southern region of Brazil from January 2005 to December 2020. It was approved by the institution's Ethics Committee. Data regarding demographic and clinical characteristics was collected from standardized medical records. We included patients 14 years of age or older who had bronchiectasis on chest computed tomography and a history of pulmonary tuberculosis. We excluded patients with confirmed non-tuberculous mycobacteria infection. RESULTS: During the study period, 204 bronchiectasis patients were seen at the hospital, and 54 of them met inclusion criteria (TABLE 1). Only 15 (27.8%) had available microbiological evidence of tuberculosis infection. The sample consisted mostly of female patients (n = 32, 59.3%), with a mean age of 59.1 years. History of smoking was present in 21 patients (38.9%), with only 2 (3.7%) being current smokers. Sputum samples were obtained from 41 patients, with 33 (80.5%) having no chronic colonization, and 5 (12.2%) Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Of the 45 patients who underwent pulmonary function testing, 34 (75.6%) had impaired lung function, the most common defect being obstruction to airflow (n = 31, 68.9%). All patients had undergone chest computed tomography, which showed a predominance of upper lobe impairment (n = 31, 57.4%). The Bronchiectasis Severity Index (BSI) was available for 41 patients, with 14 (34.1%) being graded as mild, 13 (31.8%) as moderate and 14 (34.1%) as severe. In regards to treatment, the most common choice was the association of inhaled corticosteroids and longacting beta-2-agonists, prescribed to 40 patients (74.1%). Azithromycin was prescribed to 18 patients (33.3%). CONCLUSIONS: The profile of patients with post-tuberculosis bronchiectasis in follow-up at our hospital is heterogeneous. Most of them are female, older, and are non-smokers. Less than a third are colonized by some agent. More than two thirds have some type of ventilatory defect. More than half have upper-lobe impairment. Severity of the disease seems to be equally distributed from mild to severe. Treatment is varied, which could be due to the lack of standardized guidelines for this disease.
