N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary infections can mimic primary lung malignancy both with presenting symptoms and similar chest imaging. Distinguishing between these two etiologies presents a diagnostic challenge that often requires a tissue biopsy. Here we present the case of a patient with multiple cavitary lesions found to have a primary lung malignancy and concomitant invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. Case Presentation: A 63 year-old male with no significant past medical history presented to an outside hospital with several months of cough, hemoptysis and ten pounds of unintentional weight loss. His social history was notable for a 50 packyear smoking history and many decades of work on a farm in the southwest United States. He underwent a CT-chest which revealed a large right upper lobe cavitary lesion with internal necrotic debris and right lower lobe cavitary lesion with an associated 6.2cm mass (Figure 1 and Figure 2). Serial sputum and serologic studies were negative for mycobacterium tuberculosis. Initial sputum samples at the outside hospital were suggestive of coccidioidomycosis, so he was started on fluconazole and transferred to our institution for further evaluation of his cavitary lung lesions. He underwent flexible bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) and transbronchial biopsies of the right lower lobe mass as well as enlarged hilar and mediastinal lymph node stations (11L, 7, 4R, 11R). Pathology showed non-small cell lung cancer of the right lower lobe mass, while the lymph node biopsies of station 7, 4R and 11R showed reactive histiocytes without evidence of tumor cells. Station 11L showed lymphoid tissue without histiocytes or malignancy. Bronchoalveolar lavage from the right upper lobe grew aspergillus niger and an elevated aspergillus galactomannan index value of 7.46. Culture and serologic data did not support a diagnosis of coccidioidomycosis. He was started on voriconazole and discharged home with plan for PET-CT and ongoing management of primary pulmonary malignancy.Discussion:Co-existing pulmonary infections and lung malignancies present diagnostic and therapeutic challenges given similar presenting symptoms and imaging. In this case, we present a patient diagnosed with both invasive pulmonary aspergillosis as well as a primary lung malignancy during a single bronchoscopy. The presence of multiple enlarged lymph nodes on CT with pathology showing reactive tissue without malignant cells detected via EBUS further highlights the radiographic overlap these two disease processes can have and the need for tissue biopsy to better delineate their role in the patient presentation. (Figure Presented).
