N2  - Background: People with type 1 or type 2 diabetes have a higher risk of respiratory infections, and especially community-acquired pneumonia. A risk that increases even after 65 years. Aim: The interest of this study is to describe the bacteriological profile of community-acquired pneumonia in diabetic patients. Method: We report a retrospective descriptive study of cases of acute community-acquired pneumonia in diabetic patients at the Kerkennah Regional Hospital medical service from January 2014 to January 2020. Results: 40 patient medical records were studied. The average age was 54 years with a predominance of men in 59% of cases, the average consultation time was 6 days, the average hospital stay was 11 days. Clinical signs included cough with purulent sputum in all cases, chest pain in 80% of cases and fever in 87% of cases. Clinical examination found condensation syndrome in 87% of cases, the fluid effusion syndrome in 33% of cases and labial herpes in 20% of cases. Chest x-ray revealed alveolar- opacity in all cases associated with costophrenic recess in 30% of cases. Hyper-leukocytosis was found in 65% of cases, functional acute renal failure objectified in 25%. Cytobacteriological examination isolated a germ in 26 cases: 12 pneumococci, 10 gram-negative bacilli, 4 gram-positive cocci. Stage III, according to Fine's score, was present in 70% of cases. Bi-antibiotic therapy was necessary in 75% of cases and monotherapy in 25% of cases. The mean duration of treatment was 7 days. The course was good in 95% of cases. Discussion: Impaired immunity in diabetic patients results in more frequent but also more serious infections. This alteration is a consequence of the chronic hyperglycemia that characterizes their disease. In diabetes, the risk of pneumonia is globally multiplied by a factor of 2. Thus, pneumonia represents the 3rd reason for hospitalization for infection of diabetic subjects. In addition, the presence of polypathologies considerably increases the risk of pneumococcal infections. However, the germs involved do not differ from the general population since the bacteria most often isolated is pneumococcus with an estimated prevalence of between 30 and 40%. Diabetes is associated with increased severity of the disease in terms of the risk of hospitalization; mortality. The prognosis depends on the quality and the earliness of the treatment. Pneumococcal vaccination should be recommended for all people with diabetes.
