N2  - TYPE: Case Report TOPIC: Chest Infections INTRODUCTION: Tuberculosis (TB) may present as multiple pulmonary nodules mimicking lung metastases. CASE PRESENTATION: A 57-year-old female presented to the hospital with generalized weakness, failure to thrive, and shortness of breath. She had lost 10 pounds in the past 10 months. She had shortness of breath for 3 weeks, associated with cough productive of white–greenish sputum. She denied any fevers, chills, recent travel. On further evaluation- CXR showed a thick-walled cavitary lesion in the right lung apex, irregular wedge-shaped opacification in the left lung apex. CT Angiography chest showed multifocal pneumonia with multiple ground glass nodular foci throughout the lungs. Multifocal pneumonia or malignancy was considered. She was empirically treated with ceftriaxone, azithromycin. Work up for pneumonia was negative. As a part of the malignancy workup, EGD and colonoscopy were unremarkable. CT-guided lung biopsy was done with the suspicion of malignancy. The pathology report showed markedly necrotic lung parenchyma with ill-defined granuloma, many acid-fast bacilli on AFB stain with no evidence of malignancy. Unfortunately, the patient was lost to follow-up and thus could not peruse further workup. DISCUSSION: Bilateral multiple pulmonary nodules are suggestive of metastases, multiple nodules with an upper lobe predominance, Pulmonary Tuberculosis, or Mycobacterium Avium Complex should be considered in the differential diagnosis. CONCLUSIONS: Many asymptomatic cases of TB are detected incidentally on chest radiography, and patients are often negative for acid-fast bacilli staining and culture despite having active TB. It is important to know the imaging findings characteristic of pulmonary TB and its variant forms. DISCLOSURE: Nothing to declare. KEYWORD: Tuberculosis
