N2  - TYPE: Abstract TOPIC: Chest Infections PURPOSE: To characterize the population with pulmonary actinomycosis diagnosed from january 2015 to december 2019 in our centre. METHODS: Demographic and clinical data were collected by consulting the clinical process. RESULTS: 1: A 65-year-old-man, non smoker, oligophrenic, with chronic cough and changes on chest CT scan – a 4-cm-long linear calcified image and bronchiectasis in the RLL. The bronchofibroscopy revealed mucosal swelling in the RBT and a foreign body, that was removed. The bronchial biopsy showed bone tissue and colonies of microorganisms with Actinomyces morphology. He did doxycycline for 6 months with clinical and radiological improvement. 2: A 47 year-old-man, ex-smoker, with previous history of schizophrenia, admitted for right pleural effusion. He reported progressive weigh loss and presented extensive dental caries. The patient underwent chest drain insertion for purulent drainage. Subsequent chest radiographs showed pleural thickening preventing full lung reexpansion. The patient underwent lung descortication. Anaerobic cultures of pleural fluid reaveled Actinomyces israelii. He did amoxicillin-clavulanic acid for 3 months with good response. 3: A 73 year-old-man, ex-smoker, presented with hemoptysis. The chest CT scan showed a right basal pneumonia and the BAL revealed Actinomyces naeslundii in culture. He responded well to high doses of intravenous and then oral amoxicillin for 5 months. CONCLUSIONS: The sample size is a limitation of our study, which is in agreement with the fact that pulmonary actinomycosis is a rare bacterial infection. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Pulmonary actinomycosis often leads to a misdiagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis or lung cancer. An early diagnosis will prevent the psycological and physical morbidity, including thoracic surgery. DISCLOSURE: Nothing to declare.
