N2  - TYPE: Abstract TOPIC: Chest Infections PURPOSE: In 2019, the Philippines was amongst the seven countries accounting for the greatest burden (90%) of tuberculosis (TB) in the world. We sought to investigate the presence of other potentially pathogenic organisms and their clinical features in archived sputum samples from acutely unwell patients clinically diagnosed with TB and admitted to a TB ward in Manila and not on treatment. These patients however, had a negative Xpert® MTB/Rif assay and Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB). METHODS: A pilot retrospective molecular analytical cross-sectional study on 82 sputa samples was carried out. DNA was extracted. Positive controls prepared from blood culture isolates were extracted using the same DNA Quiagen kit following manufacturers protocol. Three multiplex Polymerase chain reactions (PCR) were designed for Hemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis), Streptococcus pneumoniae, mycoplasma pneumoniae, Legionella pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae, and Burkholderia pseudomallei, thailandensis. Shimadzu MultiNA Microchip Electrophoresis System was used for DNA analysis. Statistics analysis was done on STATA® 15. RESULTS: The prevalence of bacteria was 74/82(90.24%). Burkholdera pseudomallei 72/82(87.80%) and Hemophilus influenza 22/82(26.83%) were most prevalent. A cough of over 2-week was 51/82(76.12%). X-ray findings consistent with TB was 59/76(86.76%). The odds of a cough of over two weeks and weight loss were about six {OR 5.70(CI: 1.79, 18.09)}, p=0.0008 and four folds {OR 4.44(95%CI:1.56, 12.62)} p=0.0022 respectively greater in those with positive Xpert/AFB compared to those negative. CONCLUSIONS: Other bacterial infections can be found in patients suspected of PTB. A cough of over 2 weeks, fever, chest x-ray lesions, and weight loss can be present in other lung affections/diseases. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: diagnostics DISCLOSURE: Nothing to declare. KEYWORD: GeneXpert negative
