N2  - Background: Severe Combined Immunodeficiency(SCID) is a primary immunodeficiency disorder characterised by absent or dysfunctional T lymphocytes leading to defective cellular and humoral immunity requiring urgent Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation(HSCT). We report a case of SCID with disseminated BCG infection who developed cytokine release syndrome(CRS) and possible IRIS(Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome) following Haploidentical-HSCT with post-transplant cyclophosphamide(PTCy). Methods: Data was retrospectively retrieved from electronic medical record. Results: 5 month boy referred with fever, cough, generalised maculopapular rash for 15days, had pallor without hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy. History of previous male sibling death at 6 months of age due to pneumonia. Investigations:Hemoglobin-4.7gm/dL, TLC-6.37x103/uL, Absolute lymphocytes-0.98x103/uL, platelets-319x103/uL, bilateral patchy opacities in both lung fields and low immunoglobulin levels. Lymphocyte subset analysis revealed T-,B+,NK-. Genetic analysis showed hemizygous mutation in IL2RG(c.314A>G). Child received IV antibiotics, antifungal, anti-tubercular drugs, irradiated blood products and intravenous immunoglobulins. Urgent haploidentical HSCT from mother was planned. Conditioning was Fludarabine-40mg/m2/day for 4days, Cyclophosphamide-14.5mg/kg/day for 2days. He received peripheral blood hematopoietic stem cells with CD34- 15X106cells/kg and CD3- 805X106cells/kg. Within 2 hours of stem cell infusion, he developed respiratory distress, fever, shock and flaring of rash. Methylprednisolone was started in view of CRS. On day+2, he had sudden desaturation and bradycardia needing mechanical ventilation and inotropes. His inflammatory markers were elevated(Ferritin:3640ng/mL, IL-6:5000pg/mL, CRP:255mg/L). In view of high grade CRS, he received Inj Tocilizumab@8mg/kg on day+2 and day+4. He received PTCy@25mg/kg on day+3. The endotracheal secretion GeneXpert was positive for Mycobacterium supporting diagnosis of disseminated tuberculosis. He succumbed to severe acute respiratory distress syndrome and multiorgan dysfunction on day+5 post-transplant. Conclusions: In haploidentical HSCT of SCID, post-transplant course can be complicated by CRS and IRIS as these patients are inefficient in mounting any response to infused donor lymphocytes resulting in their unregulated growth.
