N2  - Objective To summarize the clinical features of 10 cases of Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia for clinicians to better understand this disease and avoid misdiagnosis in clinical practice. Methods The diagnosis of Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia was confirmed in 10 patients by metagenomic next generation sequencing (mNGS) of alveolar lavage fluid from January 2019 to July 2021. The clinical features, laboratory test results, chest CT scan, antimicrobial treatment and outcomes were analyzed retrospectively. Results The 10 patients included 7 males and 3 females (median age of 69.5 years). The main clinical manifestations were fever (9/10), cough (7/10), expectoration (6/10), dyspnea (6/10), nervous system symptoms (6/10), and malaise (5/10). The white blood cell count did not elevate in general. Decreased albumin (10/10), elevated lactate dehydrogenase (9/10), hypoxemia (9/10), elevated serum C-reactive protein (10/10) and procalcitonin (8/10) levels were the prominent laboratory abnormalities. Relatively slow pulse rate was found in half of the patients (5/10). Chest CT scan showed exudation and consolidation in all patients (10/10). Six patients were cured after moxifloxacin treatment, and two patients cured by doxycycline therapy. The remaining two patients died due to severe Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia even after treatment with moxifloxacin plus doxycycline, and tigecycline plus moxifloxacin. Conclusions Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia has some clinical features, including high fever, dyspnea, malaise, and relatively slow pulse rate. The use of mNGS can improve the accuracy of diagnosis and reduce themisdiagnosis of Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia. Most patients can be cured by appropriate antimicrobial therapy.
