N2  - Background: Home nebulizers routinely used to aerosolize inhaled therapies are frequently found to be contaminated with pathogenic microbes such as Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and Burkholderia cepacia. Previous work in our lab has demonstrated that these bacteria can be dispersed to the lower airway through contaminated nebulizers as measured by cascade impaction. Analysis of these data revealed that total number of colonyforming units (CFU) of bacteria aerosolized from the nebulizer surface into the cascade impactor varied with ambient relative humidity (RH) during nebulizer drying. In addition, optimal drying time of nebulizers has not beenwell quantified, and improper drying has been demonstrated to cause P. aeruginosa infection in CF patients. Therefore, we sought to clarify the relationship between drying time, ambient RH and bacterial dispersion from nebulizer surfaces on particles that may reach the lower airway. Methods: The refrigerated Next Generation Impactor (NGI) was used to measure bacterial dispersion on different aerosolized particle sizes generated by a Pari LC Plus nebulizer. CF respiratory bacterial isolates were inoculated to the Pari LC Plus, as we previously described elsewhere, and the nebulizers were dried for 2 or 24 hours in chambers set up with high (RH 60–70% with water bubbled into a closed container with forced air) or lowhumidity (RH levels of ≤22% achievedwith desiccant). Five mL of 0.083% albuterol (Nephron,West Columbia, SC) was nebulized through the NGI for 7 minutes, and then the original Pari LC Plus inoculum sites, the remaining albuterol solution, and the NGI collecting cups with aerosolized particles of various sizes were swabbed for bacteria and CFU recorded at 24 to 48 hours. Results: We found a positive correlation between higher ambient RH during drying and greater bacterial surface dispersal (P = 0.05). Two-hour nebulizer drying under low RH resulted in less aerosolization than drying at high RH, with geometric mean CFU values of 3.1 (95% CI, 1.0–9.5) in low RH and 118.9 (95% CI, 20.7–680.8) in high RH (P = 0.02; n = 5 each). Additional experiments extending the drying time overnight confirmed that this was sufficient to reduce dispersion. Only 3 of 15 samples had 1 CFU or less after 2 hours of drying, which increased to 11 of 15 samples at 24 hours (P = 0.009; OR of finding persistent organisms 0.091 (95% CI, 0.017–0.501). (Figure Presented) Conclusion: Greater RH increases the likelihood of dispersion of bacteria to the lower airway during nebulization. These data confirm that complete drying reduces but does not eliminate risk of bacterial detachment and dispersion to the lower airway during nebulization of medication. Our findings suggest that prolonged drying times may be helpful to prevent CF pathogen exposure during nebulization under conditions of high ambient RH, reinforcing the importance of following recommended cleaning and disinfection, including drying, practices to avoid contamination of the lower airway during routine nebulization.
