N2  - Introduction: Allergic bronchopulmonary mycosis (ABPM) is typically diagnosed in patients with underlying severe bronchial asthma or cystic fibrosis (CF). ABPM is differentiated from allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) by the production of IgE-specific antibodies to a mold other than Aspergillus fumigatus (A. fumigatus). Case Description: A 19-year-old Puerto Rican female with a prior history of bronchial asthma and PCD positive for biallelic homozygous RSPH4A (c.921+3_6delAAGT) genetic variant was consulted to our institution due to a 6-month history of worsening productive expectoration from baseline cough.A high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) chest scan showed bilateral centrilobular bronchiectasis with associated mucus plugs and development of a left lower lobe (LLL) infiltrate (Figure 1A). A new area of focal infection at the Left Upper Lobe (LUL). Additionally, nasal ciliary biopsy was performed and confirmed an abnormal ciliary axis orientation with significant ultrastructural abnormalities affecting the central complex.Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fungal culture was positive for Aspergillus species (not fumigatus, flavus or niger groups) as well as Geotrichum species in both the LLL and right middle lobe (RML). Skin prick and intradermal skin testing for Geotrichum and Aspergillus demonstrated immediate hypersensitivity reactions (3+) for both species, confirming a superimposed ABPM. Discussion: Even though the patient was immunocompetent, her history of multiple hospitalizations and 14 day antibiotics regimen for elimination of Pseudomonas aeruginosa and chronic inhaled steroid use may have contributed towards the opportunistic colonization by fungal species (14). Additionally, ABPA and ABPM are known to exhibit a high prevalence in patients with underlying chronic inflammatory lung diseases, such as PCD (6). CT Chest
