N2  - Background: Non Tuberculous Mycobacterial (NTM) lung disease is a chronic, underrated debilitating condition that can cause morbidity and mortality. Case Description: A 61-year-old female was referred due to malaise, shortness of breath, productive cough and occasional hemoptysis for at least 4 months. History of completed anti-tuberculosis (TB) therapy with good adherence three years prior to admission. She had already received TB relapse regiment for six weeks with no improvement in primary health care. Physical examination revealed low body mass index, respiratory distress, wheezing, coarse crackles prominently on basal right lung. Abnormal lab results showed monocytosis, positive sputum acid fast bacilli/AFB, and bronchiectasis at basal right lung. Gene X-pert® test showed no detection of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb). These lead to bronchiectasis with probable NTM infection. Budesonide and ipratropium bromide nebulization, oxygen, tranexamic acid, azithromycin, rifampicin and ethambutol were administered. Symptoms was decreased. Follow-up showed a conversion of sputum AFB after 3 months, and clinically improving. Azithromycin-rifampicin-ethambutol continued until 12 months. Discussion: NTM infection is underrated due to the dominance of tuberculosis infection in our rural area. The hint in our case was unresponsive to anti-TB therapy, positive sputum AFB but no Mtb detection of serial Gene X-pert®, geriatric population and bronchiectasis. Azythromycin based regiment (azyhtromycin-rifampicin-ethambutol) daily was chosen in this case. Those regiment completed to 12 months with good result. Due to lack of resources, sputum AFB with Gene Xpert® examination were applied in place of culture test. Conclusion: NTM infection should not be underestimated in endemic area of tuberculosis.
