N2  - Background and Aims: Pulmonary tuberculosis (PTB) can mimic lung cancer as both the diseases share similar presentation and radiological features. Hence there may be diagnostic dilemma, especially if a pivotal investigation gives a false-positive report. Methods: A 33-year-old male smoker presented with the complaints of fever and cough for 3 months, occasional scanty haemoptysis for 1 month and, loss of 5 kg weight over 2 months. A chest X-ray showed consolidation and cavitation in the right mid zone. Sputum for AFB was negative and MTB not detected in GeneXpert. There was no clinical improvement with empirical antibiotics. Due to persistent fever, haemoptysis, and gradual weight loss, he underwent bronchoscopic evaluation, showing grossly inflamed and narrowed RB6. Bronchoalveolar lavage was negative for AFB, GeneXpert, malignant cell, and no growth on bacterial and mycobacterial culture. Bronchial biopsy reported squamous cell carcinoma. On evaluation at our facility, he looked anxious, febrile, BMI19.1 kg/m2, no organomegaly, or palpable lymph nodes. PET-CT showed a FDG avid mass 10.1×6.7×6.5 cm in the right lower lobe, and few mediastinal lymph nodes. CTguided core biopsy was negative for granuloma or malignancy. EBUS-TBNA showed vascular station 7, blood tap; fibrotic station 4R, dry tap. Reactive hyperplasia on TBNA cytology. Repeat core biopsy revealed caseating epithelioid cell granuloma, consistent with tuberculosis. Results: His condition dramatically improved on category-I anti-tuberculous drug. Follow-up chest X-ray 1 month later showed complete resolution of pulmonary lesion. Conclusions: PTB may imitate lung cancer. A diagnosis is sometimes deceptive, since a false-positive report is often unavoidable. It is important to exercise the utmost caution, as an erroneous report may lead to delayed diagnosis of a potentially benign disease. It affects the progression of illness, increases the cost of healthcare, and casts the patient with agony and mental distress.
