N2  - Background: Pulmonary melioidosis (PM) is the most common clinical manifestation of melioidosis caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei. Definite diagnosis has based on the culture result to delay the initiation of appropriate antibiotics. Aims: To describe characteristics of PM which could be useful clues to detect it early. Methods: A cross-sectional study was conducted at the respiratory department of the tertiary hospital from January 01, 2020 to March 31, 2021. All adult patients with cultureconfirmed PM were included. Results: We found out 23 patients (mean age 48.3 ± 9.9, the rate of male gender 91.3%, and the rate of diabetes mellitus 82.6%). Fever and cough were common symptoms (95.7% and 82.6%, respectively) and duration of initial symptom was acute/sub-acute [median time 2 weeks, interquartile range (IQR) 2 - 4]. 34.8% of case showed abscess of soft tissue or joint simultaneously. Laboratory tests revealed the median of neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (9.4, IQR 7.1 - 17.0), the mean of blood C-reactive protein (208.0 ± 93.7 mg/l), the mean of blood sodium (126.4 ± 4.2 mmol/l). Cavitary pulmonary lesion (69.6%), parenchymal infiltration (21.7%), and pleural involvement (8.7%) were recorded on radiology. Sepsis was reported in 60.9% of PM patients. PM patients diagnosed with sepsis have age less than that without sepsis (44.6 ± 9.5 vs 54.1 ± 7.7, p = 0.020). Conclusions: PM is common in male patients with diabetes mellitus and often manifests as severe pneumonia with the high rate of sepsis, especially in patients with lower age.
