N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary melioidosis in children usually presents acutely with respiratory distress and progression to sepsis and shock. Whereas chronic presentations are observed mainly in adult populations. Here, we describe a case of pulmonary melioidosis in a Cambodian child who presented to our hospital with symptoms of unusually long duration. Case: A 9-year-old girl was referred to our hospital with a presumed diagnosis of smear negative pulmonary tuberculosis. She had a 3-month history of fever, wet cough, and weight loss. Physical examination was notable for bilateral crackles with chest in-drawing, mild digital clubbing and cachectic appearance. Chest radiography showed diffuse bilateral patchy infiltration and multiple pneumatoceles on left lung (Figure 1). Initial investigation showed WBC 43.4 × 109/L. Her sputum was recollected for AFB smear and throat culture for melioidosis. Throat culture grew Burkholderia pseudomallei, while blood culture and all three morning sputum were negative. She has been receiving intravenous ceftazidime for three weeks at the time of report. After two weeks in hospital, she had become afebrile, had no signs of respiratory distress and was gaining weight gradually. A four-week course of ceftazidime is planned with minimum 12 weeks of oral co-trimoxazole for eradication phase. Discussion: In Cambodia, chronic pulmonary melioidosis in children is uncommon and frequently misdiagnosed as pulmonary tuberculosis. The lack of access to microbiology tests and the limited knowledge of clinicians put patients at higher risk of morbidity and mortality. Increased awareness of melioidosis and quicker referral will increase patient safety.
