N2  - Introduction: Melioidosis is an endemic infectious disease in tropical countries caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei, a gram negative bacterium that survives in soil and on the surface of fresh water. Humans acquire the bacteria by direct inoculation or inhalation. Melioidosis presents with diverse clinical manifestations from mild, localized infection to severe sepsis and septic shock. Additionally, half of the cases are frequently associated with pneumonia which would rapidly progress to acute respiratory distress. We describe a challenging case of melioidosis pneumonia. Case: A 19-month-old boy presented with fever and cough for 7 days. On exam, he was clinically well with transmission sound on both lungs. His chest radiography revealed unspecific bilateral infiltrate (image1) and he had increased white cell count. Ceftriaxone was given. Unfortunately, he had ongoing fever throughout 5 days of intravenous antibiotic. The initial blood culture reported no growth after 5 days incubation. More tests were done including second blood culture, urine and throat swab culture for B. pseudomallei; an abdominal ultrasound looking for deep organ abscesses. A day later, his throat swab grew B. pseudomallei whilst the other tests were all negative. Ceftazidime was started then continued for 14 days, followed by 3 months eradication phase with cotrimoxazole. The child recovered and remains well upon subsequent follow up. Discussion: Pneumonia is one of the most common presentations of melioidosis in children admitted to our hospital. Despite pneumonia, an independent risk of death, and delayed effective antibiotic, the patient remained stable during hospitalization and recovered fully.
