N2  - TOPIC: Chest Infections TYPE: Medical Student/Resident Case Reports INTRODUCTION: We present a case of Cryptococcal pneumonia in a patient with newly diagnosed human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection. His hospital course was complicated by secondary pneumothorax. CASE PRESENTATION: A 28 year old homosexual male with no past medical history presented with fever, chills and a productive cough for one week. He endorsed a 20 lb weight loss over the last 6 months. He had no history of recent travel, incarceration or field work. He has sexual intercourse with males. His last sexual encounter was one year ago. He does not use contraception. He was found to have a normal blood pressure, heart rate of 108, respiratory rate of 18, temperature of 97.3 F, and oxygen saturation of 99% on room air. His physical exam was significant for cachexia, and diminished breath sounds in the right lower lobe (RLL). No oral or genital lesions were appreciated. His labs were notable for a hemoglobin of 5.3 (ref: 13.0- 17.0 g/dL), WBC of 2.5 (ref: 4.0- 10.0 10*3/uL), and platelet count of 144 (ref: 150.0- 450.0 10*3/uL). He was HIV positive with a PCR of 58,987 (ref: not detected), and CD4 count of 6 (ref: >600 cells/uL). His CSF, respiratory, and blood cultures resulted with Cryptococcus Neoformans. Cryptococcal antigen titer was >1:2560. Coccidioidomycosis IgM immunodiffusion was positive. Gene Xperts and AFB cultures were negative for Tuberculosis. CT scan of the chest revealed a large thick walled cavitary lesion with an air-fluid level in the posterior RLL associated with peribronchial thickening. Three weeks into the hospitalization, he developed a fever of 104.0 and persistent tachycardia in the 130s. He developed hemoptysis and hematochezia, which required several blood transfusions. Routine CT scan of the chest was performed at this time, and he was incidentally found to have a large right-sided hydropneumothorax. Surgery was consulted for chest tube placement which failed. He ultimately underwent a VATS procedure. DISCUSSION: Secondary pneumothorax is more common in HIV patients than the general population. It is frequently associated with underlying lung infection. The most common underlying infection is Pneumocystis pneumonia. Other etiologies include but are not limited to Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, Salmonella spp, staphylococcus aureus and streptococcus pneumonia.Our case is unique in that we presume that his cavitary lung lesion is secondary to Cryptococcal fungemia. Furthermore, his clinical signs of pneumothorax were subtle. He never developed respiratory distress and did not require supplemental oxygen. CONCLUSIONS: Pneumothorax is a relatively frequent complication of HIV that may be associated with increased morbidity and mortality. It is pertinent to maintain a high index of suspicion for secondary pneumothorax in a patient with a cavitary lung lesion. REFERENCE #1: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4203992/ REFERENCE #2: https://erj.ersjournals.com/content/10/6/1332 REFERENCE #3: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0720048X08002453 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Gretel D'Souza, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Bryan Garber; No relevant relationships by Sri Mandava, source=Web Response
