N2  - TOPIC: Pulmonary Manifestations of Systemic Disease TYPE: Medical Student/Resident Case Reports INTRODUCTION: Loeffler syndrome is a rare disorder consisting of transient, migratory pulmonary infiltrates and peripheral eosinophilia. Ascaris infection is the most common cause of parasitic Loeffler. (1) Hookworm-related cutaneous larva migrans (CLM) is characterized by a migrating rash and can affect travelers visiting tropical climates. The diagnosis is usually made clinically based on the characteristic rash in the setting of parasite exposure. (2) Extra-dermal manifestations of CLM are rare but have been reported. We report a case of CLM complicated by Loeffler syndrome. CASE PRESENTATION: A healthy 23-year-old woman presented with one month of pruritic rash, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and non-productive cough. One month prior, she traveled to Puerto Rico and spent time on sandy beaches. Five days after returning, she developed a serpiginous, raised, migratory rash on her back (Figure 1A). Her labs were notable for peripheral eosinophilia to 10.0%, (absolute eosinophil count (AEC) 0.95x10^3/µl), which peaked at 24.3% (AEC 2.57x10^3/µl).Computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed multifocal, patchy, ground-glass opacities with small areas of cavitation throughout all five lobes of the lungs (Figure 2A). Serologic markers for infectious etiologies, connective tissue disease, and autoimmunity were all negative. Bronchoscopy was performed and revealed no infectious etiology. Skin biopsy of the rash showed epidermal cavitation with extravasated red blood cells, consistent with burrows (Figure 1B). Repeat chest imaging (10 days after presentation) showed migrating pulmonary infiltrates (Figure 2B).A CT scan one month after initial presentation showed marked improvement in pulmonary infiltrates despite lack of directed therapy (Figure 2C). However, her rash and dyspnea persisted. She was then treated with 12 mg oral ivermectin for two days, which resulted in complete resolution of her rash, pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea. Follow-up labs one week after treatment showed resolution of peripheral eosinophilia (4.8%, AEC 0.42x10^3/µL). DISCUSSION: This case details a patient with CLM complicated by Loeffler syndrome. While CLM is most commonly confined to the skin, it can be part of a systemic presentation including peripheral eosinophilia, eosinophilic enteritis, or pulmonary infiltrates. As seen in this case, Loeffler syndrome is generally benign and self-limited, usually resolving spontaneously regardless of treatment. (1) Corticosteroid use has been reported for Loeffler syndrome, but the standard for symptom management is etiology specific. (3) In this case, all symptoms resolved after ivermectin therapy. CONCLUSIONS: Clinicians should consider CLM as a rare cause of Loeffler syndrome in patients with appropriate history. Treatment of the underlying parasitic infection can lead to rapid symptomatic relief. REFERENCE #1: Guerrant, R. L., Walker, D. H., & Weller, P. F. (2011). Tropical Infectious Diseases: Principles, Pathogens and Practice E-Book. Elsevier Health Sciences. REFERENCE #2: Heukelbach, J., & Feldmeier, H. (2008). Epidemiological and clinical characteristics of hookworm-related cutaneous larva migrans. The Lancet infectious diseases, 8(5), 302-309. REFERENCE #3: De Giacomi, F., Vassallo, R., Yi, E. S., & Ryu, J. H. (2018). Acute eosinophilic pneumonia. Causes, diagnosis, and management. American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine, 197(6), 728-736. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Imo Akpan, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Neda Bionghi, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Donald Dietz, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ariel Eber, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Christina Eckhardt, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by David Furfaro, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Christine Kim Garcia, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Michael Murn, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by DEBORAH THEODORE, source=Web Response
