N2  - TOPIC: Chest Infections TYPE: Medical Student/Resident Case Reports INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium xenopi, a non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM), rarely causes cavitary lesions in the immunocompetent patient due to its low pathogenicity. M. xenopi-related pulmonary disease manifests in the immunosuppressed patient, such as in patients with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) illness or structural lung disease like bronchiectasis or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Still, pulmonary diseases secondary to this organism are rare in the US. Here, we present a case wherein an immunocompetent patient presented with syncope and was incidentally found to have a pulmonary cavitary lesion on computed tomography (CT) imaging secondary to Mycobacterium xenopi and Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC). CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old male smoker with a past medical history of alcohol misuse disorder, bradycardia, and prostate cancer, presented to the ED after a syncopal episode. A CT scan elucidated an apical cavitary lesion in the right lung with associated interstitial stranding. HIV and QuantiFERON gold testing was negative. There was a high clinical suspicion initially for malignancy due to his tobacco use. Initial bronchoscopy grew Streptococcus pneumoniae and Moraxella catarrhalis on bronchoalveolar lavage culture and biopsy was negative for malignancy. He admitted to a history of night sweats, a 40-pound weight loss over two years, and chronic productive cough with occasional hemoptysis. He was treated with a 7-day course of levofloxacin. After six weeks, M. xenopi isolate resulted from the same culture. Given progression on imaging, follow-up of increased mural soft tissue thickening and nodularity of the cavitary mass, a repeat bronchoscopy was performed. There was no evidence of malignancy but there was presence of necrotizing granulomas with rare acid-fast organisms on pathology. With an established diagnosis now he was referred to an infectious disease specialist for treatment. Repeat sputum AFB smear and culture revealed co-infection with M. xenopi and MAC. He began antibiotic therapy with rifampin, azithromycin, and ethambutol. A CT scan done 9 months after initiation of antibiotic therapy showed significant reduction in the diameter of the cavitation with increased interstitial stranding. DISCUSSION: NTM often occur in the environment and contaminate laboratory specimens. Therefore, ATS guidelines require multiple clinical, radiological, and microbiological criteria to be met for the diagnosis of NTM-pulmonary disease. The recommended regimen for M. xenopi is: azithromycin, ethambutol, and rifampicin, to which MAC is also susceptible. The duration of treatment is 18-24 months with 12 months of negative sputum culture. CONCLUSIONS: NTM rarely manifests disease in the immunocompetent patient. In this case, co-infection of MAC and M. xenopi was found incidentally in the absence of severe structural disease in a patient with an extensive smoking history. REFERENCE #1: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, et al; ATS Mycobacterial Diseases Subcommittee; American Thoracic Society; Infectious Disease Society of America. An official ATS/IDSA statement: diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2007; 175:367–416. REFERENCE #2: Charles L Daley, Jonathan M Iaccarino, Christoph Lange, Emmanuelle Cambau, Richard J Wallace, Claire Andrejak, Erik C Böttger, Jan Brozek, David E Griffith, Lorenzo Guglielmetti, Gwen A Huitt, Shandra L Knight, Philip Leitman, Theodore K Marras, Kenneth N Olivier, Miguel Santin, Jason E Stout, Enrico Tortoli, Jakko van Ingen, Dirk Wagner, Kevin L Winthrop. Treatment of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Pulmonary Disease: An Official ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline. Clin Infec Dis. 2020 Aug 14;71(4):905-913. REFERENCE #3: Jason E Stout, Won-Jung Koh, Wing Wai Yew. Update on Pulmonary Disease due to non-tuberculous mycobacteria. Int J Infect Dis. 2016 Apr;45:123-34. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Marylene Duah, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Vivian Keenan, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Christine Ma, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Kimberly Movsesian, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Sukhbir Randhawa, source=Web Response
