N2  - TOPIC: Occupational and Environmental Lung Diseases TYPE: Medical Student/Resident Case Reports INTRODUCTION: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP) is an immune-mediated disease initiated by a variety of inhaled particles. Given the heterogeneous clinical, radiographic, and histopathological patterns seen in presentations diagnosis can be difficult, and delay in diagnosis is common. We present a case of bird fancier's lung secondary to bird feeder exposure in a 50-year-old female originally misdiagnosed as uncontrolled asthma. CASE PRESENTATION: A 50-year-old female never-smoker with a history of rheumatoid arthritis and asthma presented to the ED with acute on chronic dyspnea on exertion for 6 months. She was diagnosed with acute hypoxemic respiratory failure secondary to asthma exacerbation. Her symptoms improved with a 5-day course of prednisone. Chest CT at that time was read as diffuse emphysema with mosaic perfusion likely secondary to air trapping. Follow-up with a pulmonologist found chest CT findings to be more consistent with diffuse ground-glass opacities (GGOs). High-resolution CT of the chest (HRCT) exhibited diffuse GGOs with mild air trapping (Figure 1). Pulmonary function testing demonstrated a mixed restrictive and obstructive defect. Social history revealed the patient frequently cleaned bird feeders and used a hot tub daily for arthritic pain. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) demonstrated >50% lymphocytes, biopsy showed loose non-caseating granulomas, and cultures grew mycobacterium avium-complex (MAC). This clinical picture was consistent with nonfibrotic HP likely secondary to bird fancier's lung or hot tub pneumonitis. Treatment with moderate dose prednisone and avoidance of bird and hot tub exposure resulted in the resolution of her clinical, radiographic, and spirometric abnormalities. Hot tub exposure was rechallenged, and she did not have any reoccurrence of disease. DISCUSSION: Nonfibrotic HP commonly presents with dyspnea and cough over days to months. The "typical" pattern of nonfibrotic HP on HRCT is diffuse parenchymal infiltration with evidence of small airway disease, commonly seen as diffuse GGOs with air trapping. Diagnosis is established with bronchoscopy demonstrating BAL lymphocytosis, poorly formed non-necrotizing granulomas, and a lymphocyte-predominant cellular interstitial pneumonia or bronchiolitis. Our patient met criteria for HP. Her findings were more consistent with Bird Fancier's Lung than Hot-Tub Lung, as well-formed granulomas are observed in the latter. She had complete resolution of pulmonary abnormalities with antigen avoidance and oral corticosteroids (Figure 2) and continues to enjoy using her hot tub without disease reoccurrence. CONCLUSIONS: Nonfibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis is a rare pulmonary syndrome that responds effectively to antigen avoidance and in some cases, a course of corticosteroids. Prompt diagnosis relies on detailed assessment of pulmonary exposures, HRCT, and a thorough review of histopathological findings. REFERENCE #1: Raghu G, Remy-Jardin M, Ryerson CJ, et al. Diagnosis of Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis in Adults. An Official ATS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2020;202(3):e36-e69. REFERENCE #2: Selman M, Pardo A, King TE. Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2012;186(4):314-324. REFERENCE #3: Cappelluti E, Fraire A, Shaefer O. A Case of "Hot Tub Lung" Due to Mycobacterium avium Complex in an Immunocompetent Host. Arch Intern Med. 2003;163:845-848. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Matthew Koroscil, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Maria Rechtin, source=Web Response
