N2  - We report a case of complicated bacterial pneumonia in a previously healthy 3-year-old girl. She developed necrotizing pneumonia which resulted in a pleural effusion and multiple pneumothoraces, which is an uncommon and severe complication of pneumonia in children. This case highlights and raises awareness of management of necrotizing pneumonia in the pediatric population, who may have minimal signs of respiratory distress in the setting of significant lung impairment on imaging compared to adults. A previously healthy, fully immunized 3-year-old girl presented to the Geisinger Medical Center Emergency Department from her Pediatrician's office for further evaluation and management of respiratory distress. Prior to presenting, she was noted to have fevers of one-week duration along with worsening non-productive cough. Chest x-ray (CXR) obtained in the office revealed a large right-sided pleural effusion, and multiple large pneumothoraces with mediastinal shift. Despite these concerning features, the patient had minimal work of breathing and maintained her SaO2 on room air. She was initiated on vancomycin and ceftriaxone, and a CT chest w/ contrast confirmed findings of complex, necrotizing pneumonia and empyema. After discussion with subspecialists and Pediatric ICU teams, the decision was made to proceed with IR-guided pigtail catheter placement, with subsequent removal of 320 mL of purulent fluid and moderate re-expansion of her lung. The patient received three doses of Tissue Plasminogen Activator. Infectious workup was notable for parainfluenza 4, methicillin sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) on pleural fluid cultures, and negative blood cultures. Thus, her antibiotic regimen was narrowed to nafcillin monotherapy. Repeat CT chest obtained on hospital day 7 revealed persistent loculated effusion with air-filled cysts, which led to video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) procedure and chest tube exchange. New cultures were concerning for polymicrobial infection on Gram stain, which led to intensifying her antibiotic regimen with ampicillin-sulbactam and fluconazole, but ultimately only MSSA was isolated. On hospital day 13, patient's chest tube was removed after minimal output, with subsequent hospital discharge the following day. She continued an antimicrobial regimen of amoxicillin-clavulanate and fluconazole for 14 days as an outpatient. A follow-up CXR 6 weeks post discharge showed complete lung re-expansion with no residual evidence of pneumothorax or scarring. Necrotizing pneumonia is a rare and severe complication of pneumonia in the pediatric population. Urgent evaluation with advanced imaging is helpful for definitive management with chest tube with/without VATS procedure. Fortunately, children respond well to these interventions and antibiotics with little to no long-term deficits.
