N2  - Rationale: Cystic fibrosis (CF) lung disease is characterized by intermittent pulmonary exacerbations (PEx) leading to progressive lung function decline. Diagnostic criteria for PEx are poorly defined. FEV1 decline (ΔFEV1) is associated with PEx diagnosis but its utility compared to symptom reports is not well-defined. This study aims to 1) identify the concordance between different degrees of ΔFEV1 (5-9% vs ≥10% from baseline), clinical symptoms, and clinically diagnosed PEx and 2) evaluate the correlation between ΔFEV1 and PEx management. Methods: Retrospective chart review was performed using 315 outpatient clinical encounters with pulmonary function testing in 91 patients with CF ages 6-17 years at Riley Hospital for Children during 2019. Clinical symptoms, BMI percentile, baseline and current FEV1, and ΔFEV1 were collected from each encounter. ΔFEV1 defined as percent decrease from baseline FEV1. Outcomes, including clinician-defined PEx diagnosis and antibiotic management, were compared using χ2-tests. Results: Cohort patients had a mean (SD) age of 11.6 ± 3.4 years, BMI percentile of 46.6 ± 28.3, and baseline FEV1 of 98% ± 15.3%. 53% were on CFTR modulator therapy. The most common respiratory pathogens included methicillin-sensitive S. aureus (65%), methicillinresistant S. aureus (31%), and P. aeruginosa (27%). PEx were diagnosed in 37% of encounters (115/315). FEV1 (82.1% ± 19.8% v. 96.3% ± 15.7%) and BMI percentile (48.8 ± 28.5 v. 56.9 ± 27.9) were decreased in PEx, p<0.01 for both. Compared with non-PEx encounters, PEx had more ΔFEV1 5-9% (24% v. 16%) and ≥10% events (63% v. 12%) (p<0.001). More ΔFEV1 <5% events occurred in non-PEx encounters (37% v. 9%) (p<0.001). Increased cough (72%), sputum (48%), and fatigue (19%) were the most common symptoms in PEx, and did not differ substantially between ΔFEV1 5-9% and ΔFEV1 ≥10%. Baseline FEV1 (98.4% ± 13.8% v. 94.5% ± 16.4%) and BMI percentile (53.2 ± 26.9 v. 46.5 ± 29.2) were similar for ΔFEV1 5-9% PEx and ΔFEV1 ≥10% PEx. Antibiotic management (p<0.001) differed between ΔFEV1 5-9% and ΔFEV1 ≥10% PEx. Oral antibiotic use was similar between ΔFEV1 5-9% and ΔFEV1 ≥10% PEx (64% v. 65%) whereas IV antibiotics were prescribed more often in ΔFEV1 ≥10% PEx (22% v. 11%). Management without antibiotics was more common in ΔFEV1 5-9% PEx (25% v. 13%). Conclusions: Our study demonstrated that ΔFEV1 is associated with PEx diagnosis and management without significant differences in symptoms. This suggests FEV1 measurement is valuable in assessing for possible PEx in children with CF.
