N2  - Introduction: Particulate matter (PM) exposures are linked to various adverse health outcomes. Research on the effects of PM exposures on Mycobacterium tuberculosis (M. tb) infection and tuberculosis development is still rare. This study determined particle load in alveolar macrophages (AM) as a novel exposure matrix for correlation with ambient PM2.5 exposures estimated with an earlier published improved fine spatial land use regression (LUR) model based on México City air pollution network data. Earlier studies from our group showed that PM load in human AM is correlated with altered M. tb-induced production of protective cytokines in primary human bronchoalveolar and peripheral blood mononuclear cells. Methods: Bronchoalveolar lavages (BAL) were conducted on 54 healthy, non-smoking study subjects (male 25, female 29) recruited at the Instituto Nacional de Enfermedades Respiratorias in Mexico City. AM with and without PM were identified from digital bright field microscopy images of thin-layer cytospin slides following Accustain Wright staining. Proportions of AM containing PM (%AM with PM), the PM area within AM (median, μm2), and AM area size (μm2) were measured using an automated cell image analysis (CellProfiler 3.1.9, Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA). Ambient PM2.5 concentrations at the residence locations of the study participants were simulated with the LUR model. Simulated average ambient PM2.5 concentrations during a time period of 1-365 days before the BAL dates were correlated with the PM load in AM. Results: Average numbers of AM assessed per subject were 197±96 with AM sizes of 350±113 μm2. Of the studied AM, 62±16% (mean; range 23-90%) contained PM. The PM area within AM ranged from 0.01 μm2 to 5.52 μm2 and increased significantly with increasing %AM with PM (Spearman's ρ=0.917, p<0.001). Simulated daily average ambient PM2.5 concentrations (27.90±11.65 μg/m3) were correlated with %AM with PM (Spearman's ρ=0.239, p=0.084) and PM area within AM (Spearman's ρ=0.228, p=0.100). Every inter-quartile range increase of simulated ambient PM2.5 concentrations (IQR=15.12 μg/m3), increased %AM with PM and PM area within AM by 9.10% and 0.48 μm2, respectively (Figure). Conclusion: Persons exposed to higher ambient PM2.5 levels have increased proportions of AM containing PM and larger PM areas in AM. PM load in AM may serve as a biomarker of air pollution exposure for health studies. Our results suggest a measurable linkage between ambient PM exposure levels and altered cellular immune responses.
