N2  - Introduction: Genetic mutations in more than 45 human genes, including RSPH4A can lead to Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD). Mutations in RSPH4A gene alter the configuration of the ciliary ultrastructure. As a result PCD manifests as Neonatal Respiratory Distress (NRD), chronic oto-sino-pulmonary diseases, infertility, and others. The suggested diagnostic algorithm for PCD recognizes the presence of biallelic pathogenic variants in PCD-associated genes as a diagnosis of the disease. Nevertheless, a combination of single pathogenic or non-pathogenic variants with positive structural ciliary changes may also present a positive diagnosis. We describe a child with “classic PCD” symptoms, and two pathogenic heterozygous RSPH4A genetic variants. Case Description: A 12-year-old Puerto Rican male present with past medical history of NRD despite term gestation, daily wet-cough and recurrent oto-sino-pulmonary infections. Family history is positive for chronic sinusitis in the mother. On physical examination, the patient showed bilateral crackles at right middle lobe (RML) and left lower lobe (LLL). Bilateral nasal polyps (Figure A) and mild clubbing were also present. Chest X-ray showed patchy opacities suggesting chronic atelectasis (Figure B). Cylindrical and varicose bilateral bronchiectasis on RML and LLL were evident on Chest CT (Figure C). Spirometry showed a pseudorestrictive pattern. Sweat test was intermediate however, CFTR genetic test was negative. Serum immunoglobulin levels were normal and FeNO was decreased [<5 ppm]. Bronchoscopy revealed abundant thick and greenish secretions. Bronchoalveolar lavage resulted positive for Pseudomonas Aeruginosa. Nasal ciliary biopsy showed abnormal microtubules number, distribution and configuration [7+2, 9+0] (Figure D). Initially extended PCD genetic panel revealed one pathogenic variant at the RSPH4A gene [c.921+3-921+6delAAGT (Intronic)]. Additional two likely pathogenic variants were noted: RSPH4A [c.1103T>G (p.Val368Gly)] and DNAH8 [c.9839A>T (p.Gln3280Leu)]. Familial genetic studies showed that RSPH4A [c.1103T>G (p.Val368Gly)] variant was maternally inherited. No paternal RSPH4A or DNAH8 variants were detected. As a result of the family analysis, RSPH4A [c.1103T>G (p.Val368Gly)] PCD genetic variant was reclassified as pathogenic. Novelty and Importance: In this case report we were able to identify a non-biallelic pathogenic way of inheritance of PCD. Elucidation of new pathogenic variants of PCD and modes of inheritance are necessary for advancement in the diagnosis and management of patients of PCD.
