N2  - 41 year old female with a medical history of achondroplasia and tobacco use presented with right sided flank pain associated with acute onset shortness of breath, cough for approximately 3 weeks, intermittent bloody sputum, and 15 lbs weight loss over two months. No recent travel, incarceration, steroid use, sexually transmitted infections, alcohol abuse, diabetes mellitus, Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV), organ transplant, intravenous drug abuse, dental infections, or employment around animals. On presentation, she was tachycardic, febrile, otherwise unremarkable physical examination. Complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel were unremarkable. Computerized tomography (CT) of the abdomen without contrast demonstrated bilateral, large thick-walled cystic spaces in both lungs containing air fluid levels and no abdominal pathology. CT of the chest showed bilateral thick walled cavities containing air fluid levels findings consistent with bilateral pulmonary abscess. Sputum samples were positive for Pneumocystis jirovecii and Mycobacterium Fortuitum and Kansasii, or Non-tuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM). She tested positive for HIV with an absolute CD4 count of 26 cell/mcL. She was treated with broad spectrum antibiotics then de-escalated to Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole, Fluconazole, and Ampicillin/Sulbactam. CT chest completed 8 days after initial imaging demonstrated decrease in size of bilateral pulmonary abscesses. Clinical presentation and physical exam signs for Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) and NTM tend to be non-specific. As a result, the diagnosis is suspected once characteristic radiologic findings, bilateral reticular opacities, granular opacities, pneumothoraces 2 or septal thickening are identified in an immunocompromised host. NTM appears as a nodular disease with bronchiectasis with a predominance for upper lobe. In the immunocompromised findings include interstitial involvement , consolidations and pleural effusions4. Literature displayed a case of a single lung abscess in a 23 year old male intravenous drug user and CD4 cell count <200 who was diagnosed with PCP by lung biopsy and commitment cytomegalovirus infection 3. Our patient displayed no risk factors for abscess formation at the time of presentation. However, a study published in the International Journal of infectious Diseases shows significant hospital admissions in HIV patients due to respiratory disease of which 24% were attributed to PCP and 11% were attributed to NTM 5.Disseminated NTM carries 10% mortality 5 while infection with PCP carries a high of serious risk complications including pneumothorax and death with a mortality ranging between 5 to 40% 1. It is important to remain vigilant for atypical presentations of NTM and PCP as bilateral abscess as our case has demonstrated.
