N2  - INTRODUCTION Streptococcus pneumonia is the most common bacteria identified in adults with communityacquired pneumonia (CAP). However, its etiology has been changing in the past few decades(1). CASE PRESENTATION A 25-year-old female college student with a history of chronic allergic asthma since childhood was assessed for a 3-week history of fever and productive cough, with hemoptysis in the last days. Current medications included budesonide/formoterol DPI 160/4.5mcg BID for the past three years. She denied chronic use of oral corticosteroids, smoking, drugs or exposure to dusts or fumes. Physical exam revealed normal vital signs and basal crackles in the left lung. The chest x-ray showed a homogeneous opacity in the left lower lung consistent with bronchopneumonia. Initial WBC count was 14.5x109 cells/L with absolute neutrophil count 9.9x109 cells/L. Ziehl Neelsen and X-Pert tests for TB in sputum were negative. Negative HIV/p24 test. Treatment for CAP with cefixime was begun. Her condition deteriorated, experiencing shortness of breath, continued cough, fever, and hemoptysis, HR 125, RR 26, SpO2 88% on room air, absolute neutrophil count 12.35x109 cells/L, RCP 120 mg/L, and chest x-ray evidenced an increase of the pulmonary opacity on left lung field, consistent with progressive bronchopneumonia despite treatment. A bronchoscopy was performed without relevant findings, and culture of bronchial wash was positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa (ESBLs and AmpC), resistant to penicillins, cephalosporins and monobactams, sensitive to quinolones and aminoglycosides. Treatment was changed to imipenem-cilastatin and amikacin. Her cardiovascular condition remained stable, SpO2 improved to 95% with low flow oxygen. Symptoms gradually resolved in the first week of treatment and she was discharged from the hospital after completion of antibiotics for 21 days. DISCUSSION P. aeruginosa is a gramnegative aerobic bacterium that is implicated in hospital-acquired pneumonia as well as in patients with specific risk factors, but rarely causes pneumonia in healthy individuals(2). Although the prevalence of P. aeruginosa-CAP is low, it exhibits a rapid progression, high severity, and poor prognosis(3, 4). Limited information is available about clinical outcomes and microbiology of CAP in asthma. While the risky association between ICS and CAP has been well demonstrated in COPD(5, 6), it is not confirmed in asthma. Moreover, some observational studies have shown that ICS therapy is not a risk factor for CAP in asthma(7).
