N2  - Introduction: A complicated parapneumonic effusion is defined as one that is filled with bacteria or other microorganisms. When these microorganisms are pyogenic, an empyema develops. Empyema is generally a complication of pneumonia, but since the advent and rising use of antibiotics, the incidence is generally only seen in 2-3% of cases of pneumonia. Usually, patients that present with an empyema have a prolonged course of pulmonary symptoms, including, but not limited to: cough, fever, sputum production, dyspnea, and severe chest pain. It is rare to present asymptomatically or with minimal symptoms. Here we present a case of massive empyema without hypoxemia or respiratory distress. Description: A 62-year-old woman, active smoker (40 pack years), with a history of depression and alcohol abuse, presented to the ED with complaints of days of severe generalized body pain, ultimately more localized to the right side, and breathlessness. She reported a baseline cough, attributed to smoking, but denied fevers, chills, weight loss, or malaise. In the ED, she was tachycardic and tachypneic, saturating well on room air. Labs were notable for a WBC 25.5, BUN 32, D-Dimer 756, and a lactate of 6. CXR revealed a large right pleural effusion. CT angiogram: massive right hydro-pneumothorax with complete atelectasis of the right lung. She was started on broad-spectrum IV antibiotics with subsequent chest tube placement; purulent fluid collected from the tube grew Streptococcus intermedius. Discussion: A review of the literature reveals very little regarding the incidence of asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic empyema in the population. Speculation as to why certain effusions present with minimal symptoms includes: the absence of an overwhelming pleural inflammatory process, diminutive size, early discovery prior to symptom onset, and altered mentation preventing communication of symptoms. Interestingly, our patient had a massive empyema with complete atelectasis of the right lung, despite her initial symptoms being significant for generalized body pain only. This case brings to light a very unique presentation of empyema: no clear history of signs or symptoms of pneumonia or hypoxemia, despite the degree of infection and lung collapse.
