N2  - Introduction: Harlem Hospital Center has for generations served the African American community and now with a swiftly changing demographic profile serves a large and underserved immigrant population from Africa1. Along with understanding the unique health challenges amongst them, our hospital continues to serve this diaspora to the best possible way. We describe such a case where a significant difference was made in an immigrant's life. Body: 48 year old thin looking female who had recently immigrated from Africa, had come to the ER of our hospital with a chronic cough, weight loss and recently worsening shortness of breath. Apparently, she had this cough for few years, and had approached multiple physicians in her country but had not received answers. Her current chest x-ray showed diffuse interstitial markings. She was started on treatment for community acquired pneumonia and admitted. Her legionella urine antigen, HIV testing, sputum AFBs and serum LDH came out negative. Chest CT showed diffuse ground glass changes with mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy. Bronchoscopy was differed for now as she had improved clinically and was discharged with follow up to the Pulmonary clinic. Her repeat chest CT about 4 months later did not change as compared to the previous. Transbronchial biopsy with EBUS-TBNA was performed which did not show malignant cells but did not reveal additional information. A PET scan was performed which showed diffuse uptake in the mediastinal lymph nodes as well as in the liver and spleen. Secondary to a suspicion of lymphoma, a mediastinoscopy was performed which showed multiple non-caseating granulomas, stain and culture was negative for mycobacterium and no evidence of lymphoma. Discussion and Conclusion: Multidisciplinary discussion identified high probability of sarcoidosis. Patient was relieved to hear that she did not have malignancy. She was started on prednisone for a short time with improvement in symptoms. She continues to follow up with us in the clinic. Along with providing her with an answer we possibly were able to provide her with hope. This holds true to many of the underserved immigrants we continue to serve at our city hospitals, and emphasizes the difference our safety net medical system makes irrespective of their ethnicity, immigration status or social standing.
