N2  - INTRODUCTION: Broncholithiasis are calcified lymph nodes eroding into the bronchial lumen. Granulomatous disease is most common etiology with Tuberculosis most common etiology worldwide. Most common symptoms are cough, dyspnea, recurrent pneumonia or recurrent hemoptysis. We present a case of recurrent hemoptysis secondary to Broncholithiais eroding into the anterior segment of Left upper lobe.CASE:We present 62 years old never smoker male who presented with two episodes of hemoptysis with no other respiratory or infectious symptoms .each episode was around 2-3 tablespoons of blood. Upon presentation vital were stable and CXR was concerning for small, calcified nodule over the left upper lobe. CTA thorax was concerning for alveolar hemorrhage with calcified granuloma eroding into the anterior segment. Eroding near obstructing broncholith in LB3 with no signs of active bleeding was noted on bronchoscopic evaluation, Cultures including bacterial, AFB and fungal and cytology were negative on BAL.Further fungal and autoimmune workup were negative as well. Patient had no further bleeding episodes and was discharged with plan to have follow up with cardiothoracic surgery as outpatient. Patient was readmitted with recurrent episodes of hemoptysis in which he underwent Robotic left upper lobe lobectomy with mediastinal lymph node dissection. Pathology was consistent with Histoplasmosis. Post-operative course was uneventful, and patient recovered with no further episodes of hemoptysis on outpatient follow up.DISCUSSION:Broncholithiasis is with disease incidence of 0.1% to 0.2% (1). Etiology is due to lymph node erosion due to respiratory movement and cardiac pulsation (2). Similar to our patient Histoplasmosis is most common etiology in USA. Management options include Conservative management, bronchoscopic broncholithectomy or surgical resection. Treatment choice depends on symptoms, broncholith size, relationship to airway and proximity to adjacent thoracic structure. Our patient underwent robotic lobectomy due to the anatomical site and recurrence of his hemoptysis (3). Broncholith is a rare but important etiology of hemoptysis which requires high clinical suspicion especially in setting of calcified lymph nodes which require bronchsocopic evaluation of the airways to confirm diagnosis.
