N2  - Introduction-We present the case of a patient with an unusually high eosinophil count on both bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and pleural fluid analysis during video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) who developed bilateral pneumothoraces, each side a couple of years apart. Case Presentation-A 66-year-old gentleman with a past history of asthma presented to the hospital for a stroke and was found to have diffuse left sided infiltrates on chest x-ray (CXR). He was hemodynamically stable with an unremarkable physical exam. Routine labs showed a significant peripheral eosinophilia (27%). Computerized Tomography (CT) of the chest revealed ground glass opacities and a prominent left lower lobe cavitary lesion. A BAL performed during bronchoscopy revealed the presence of 74% eosinophils, without evidence of infection or malignancy. A day later, a CXR revealed a left pneumothorax requiring a chest tube. Due to a persistent air leak, a VATS wedge resection of the lingula and cavitary lesion was performed. Pathology revealed changes consistent with Nonspecific Interstitial Pneumonia, whilst analysis of the cavity revealed it was lined by organizing fibrosis. Both specimens had negative bacterial, fungal and AFB cultures and no evidence of malignancy. The patient was discharged. Pulmonary function tests revealed mild obstruction, without a bronchodilator response. Despite a prolonged prednisone taper, his peripheral eosinophilia persisted. He was diagnosed with chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. Two years later, he presented with two weeks of productive cough and associated shortness of breath. This time, he was hypoxemic and required two liters of oxygen. The patient had been off prednisone for several months by this time. A CT chest revealed significant right-sided diffuse ground glass opacities (Figure 1X). Three days later, a CXR revealed a large right-sided pneumothorax which required a chest tube. He was discharged with a Pneumostat in place along with a prolonged taper of prednisone. On outpatient follow up, resolution of pneumothorax was noted. He continued prednisone 10mg daily until Benralizumab was initiated, which allowed a successful decrease of prednisone. Discussion-Whilst chronic eosinophilic pneumonia is a rare disorder itself, the case we presented is rather unique due to the occurrence of bilateral pneumothoraces several years apart. It is interesting to note that his initial presentation was primarily left sided whilst the subsequent presentation was mainly on the contralateral right side. For a disease that is described as affecting the thoracic cavity bilaterally, this is a very peculiar presentation and the first of its kind described in the literature.
