N2  - Pulmonary sclerosing pneumocytoma is a benign tumor that mainly affects women in the sixth decade of life. It often presents as an asymptomatic solitary lesion. A 67-year-old non-smoking man, with no relevant personal medical history, was admitted to the hospital with a 5-day history of, fever (38.4°C) and left thoracic pain that worsened with a deep breath. He denied dyspnea, cough, sputum, hemoptysis, nocturnal hypersoduresis, and weight loss. Physical examination revealed a man who appeared healthy, with a blood pressure of 140/70mmHg; heart rate of 103 beats/minute; febrile 38.3°C; eupneic with oxygen saturation of 97% on room air. Pulmonary auscultation showed in the lower half of the left hemithorax absent lung sounds and dullness to percussion. With no other abnormal finding. Chest X-ray revealed an opacity in the lower left hemithorax, causing contralateral deviation of the trachea. Chest computed tomography (CT) found a consolidation with air bronchogram on the left lower lobe with moderate homolateral pleural effusion. Laboratory analysis revealed white blood cells 13.9x109/L (neutrophils 71.5%); hemoglobin 13.1g/dL; C-reactive protein 20.6mg/dL. Urinary antigen testing for Pneumococci and Legionella were negative. Blood cultures were negative. We assumed pneumonia with metapneumonic pleural effusion. He started on intravenous antibiotics with amoxicillin/clavulanic and azithromycin. Ultrasound-guided thoracentesis revealed a yellow citrus pleural fluid, with drainage of about 1800mL, pH 7.14. Pleural fluid analysis revealed an exudate. Cytology: 3090cells/mm3; neutrophils 2%; lymphocytes 89.9%; Adenosine deaminase 7.7UI/L. Cultures for bacteria and mycobacteria were negative. Blind pleural biopsies were performed. The patient showed clinical and analytical improvement. Imaging reassessment revealed a significant reduction of the pleural effusion and identified in the left lower lobe a mass with 4.5x4.1cm. Bronchoscopy revealed in the left lower lobe a mass, with 2.4x0.5cm, occluding the bronchus. Histological evaluation revealed a sclerosing pneumocytoma with a predominantly papillary pattern. Distal biopsies were also performed in the same segment, with no signs of malignancy. Posteriorly, pleural biopsies results revealed lung adenocarcinoma, PD-L1 negative. Positron emission tomography-CT scan confirmed the malignant process in the left lung with pleural, high cervical ganglionic, and left supraclavicular involvement. Brain CT showed secondary deposits of the adenocarcinoma. Given the advanced stage of the malignant disease, the recommended surgical approach for pneumocytoma has not been performed. The patient started chemotherapy and brain radiotherapy.We present a rare case of two synchronous tumors, malignant lung adenocarcinoma, and pneumocytoma, a benign tumor. Endobronchial pneumocytoma is extremely rare.
