N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary nontuberculous Mycobacterial infections can be complex and chronic processes with symptoms that overlap with many other pulmonary and systemic diseases including malignancy. In this case we look at the pitfalls of anchoring on this complex disease and the false negatives possible with CT guided biopsy. Case: An 84 year old female with history of pulmonary Mycobacterium avium intracellulare (MAI) infection, bronchiectasis who completed 2 years of MAI therapy, non insulin dependent diabetes, 30 pack year history of smoking initially seen in pulmonary clinic for followup of her MAI in October 2017. Previous CT scan of the chest showed mild bronchiectasis with numerous bilateral tree-in-bud nodular opacities, together with patchy geographic groundglass opacity in the right upper lobe (RUL) measuring at least 2.3 cm, and patchy lingular opacity. Follow up imaging approximately 1 year later in October 2017 showed persistence of tree and bud opacities throughout and interstitial changes in the lingula but right upper lobe round glass had evolved into a 2.2 cm nodule. PET CT scan showed that this RUL lesion was also FDG avid and the decision was made to biopsy. She underwent a CT guided biopsy in November that showed organizing pneumonia and interstitial inflammation along with reactive alveolar underlying cell hyperplasia on pathology review. Follow-up CT in March 2018 showed some interval increase in size of the RUL lesion but 3 month interval showed stability. With stability and limited symptoms the next CT was in January 6 months later; which was concerning for worsening infiltrate appearance of RUL and bilateral tree in bud opacities. Patient also was more symptomatic with increasing cough and sputum. 3 more AFB sputums were negative and the patient finally agreed to a bronchoscopy. Bronchial alveolar lavage cultures in May 2019 showed +MRSA and biopsy was positive for adenocarcinoma of lung origin. Discussion: MAI can be a complex chronic pulmonary disease with lung changes and symptoms that can mask other pathology. Our patient had residual lung changes including RUL lesion that had appropriate imaging follow up and CT guided biopsy- showing plausible pathology of organizing pneumonia and unfortunately falsely negative for malignancy as on the margin of the lesion. This case highlights the importance of not anchoring in one diagnosis and need for continued vigilance and additional imaging and diagnostic work up when there is concern for other serious concurrent pathology in patients with pulmonary MAI infection.
