N2  - The incidence of tuberculosis (TB) in the United States (U.S.) has declined by 85.4% between 1993 and 2019. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported 2.7 new cases per 100,000 persons in 2019, with 71.4% incidence among people born outside the U.S. Although laryngeal TB is rare, accounting for less than 1% of cases, it is often missed and highly contagious. We highlight the importance of keeping TB on the differential. A 27-year old man presented with 2 months of odynophagia, 10-pound weight loss, night sweats, hoarse voice, and cough with blood-tinged sputum. He reported no recent travel after emigrating from Ecuador 15-years prior to initial presentation. On exam, he was febrile to 101°F, had an erythematous oropharynx, enlarged submandibular lymph nodes, and coarse breath sounds in the right lower lung field. He had adequate oxygen saturation on room air without respiratory distress. Studies showed elevated sedimentation rate of 81 mm/hour and c-reactive protein of 77 mg/L. Serology for human immunodeficiency virus was negative. Chest radiograph showed diffuse reticular opacities, patchy consolidation and loss of volume in the right upper lobe. Laryngoscopy demonstrated thickened, erythematous epiglottis and arytenoids, and edematous but mobile vocal cords. Computed tomography of the neck and chest further characterized the grossly thickened epiglottis and aryepiglottic folds, and revealed fibrosis and bronchiectasis of the right upper lobe, nodular consolidations in the left upper lobe with localized tree-in-bud pattern (Figure 1). Sputum smear revealed acid-fast bacilli, confirmed to be Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) by MTB PCR assay. A diagnosis of secondary laryngeal tuberculosis of pulmonary origin was made. This was a previously healthy man born outside the U.S., with a sub-acute onset of symptomatology concerning for MTB, prompting isolation and diagnostic testing. Importantly, individuals in close contact were identified, quarantined, and treated. The patient was initiated on intravenous dexamethasone for laryngeal edema and antituberculosis therapy with resolution of symptoms. Laryngeal TB is a highly contagious manifestation of MTB infection with potential for significant morbidity at the individual and population level. Though previous studies suggest TB screening should encompass those born outside the U.S. up to 5 years after arrival, reactivation of TB has been reported at high rates in those living in the U.S. for over 10 years. Remote travel history should not dissuade the clinician from keeping MTB in mind when clinical suspicion is high. As MTB is a treatable disease, prompt identification and isolation is crucial.
