N2  - Introduction:Mycobacterium tuberculosis affects primarily the lungs. It remains prevalent in under-developed countries and is transmitted via aerosol particles. The progression of the disease can have several outcomes, determined by the host's immune response. We describe a patient with tuberculous empyema and the interventions that successfully paved his way to recovery. Case Presentation: A 19-year-old asthmatic male presented with two to three months of progressively worsening exertional dyspnoea and unintentional fifty-pound weight loss. He had lived in the Philippines for several months with relatives; otherwise, he was in his usual state of health. Chest radiography showed a large right pleural effusion with atelectasis and leftward mediastinal shift. A CT of the chest showed chronic empyema with pleural thickening with rind formation and contraction of the chest wall. A thoracostomy was performed, a chest tube placed, and partial re-expansion of lung was achieved. A second tube was placed due to recurrent empyema. The pleural fluid cytology revealed elevated adenosine deaminase and cell count consistent with tuberculosis. Further testing showed an 8.3 mm PPD and a negative QuantiFERON. Treatment with four first line antimicrobials was started - isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol. For diagnostic purpose, a bronchoscopy was performed, showing right upper, middle and lower lobe bronchial occlusion from external compression; the brushing samples were unremarkable. His hospital course was complicated by Pseudomonas pneumonia and persistent empyema with tension physiology requiring repeat chest tube insertion to allow deferevescence. After two months of anti-mycobacterial therapy, he underwent definitive treatment with Eloesser flap thoracostomy with decortication and partial pleurectomy. After initial post-operative recovery, he was discharged home. A follow up chest CT showed dramatic improvement of right lung aeration. Discussion:While tuberculosis affects multiple systems, pleural effusion is the second most common form of extrapulmonary tuberculosis, after lymphatic involvement. It may occur as a result of delayed hypersensitivity reaction, rupture of a subpleural focus into the pleural space, extension of infection from thoracic lymph nodes or a subdiaphragmatic focus via hematogenous spread. The initial treatment of empyema is antibiotics. For chronic empyema, Eloesser flap thoracostomy remains a viable option, creating a one-way valve to drain the infected cavity and facilitate expansion of the lung to fill the thoracic space. This case offers insight into complicated Mycobacterium Tuberculosis presentation, unusual in developed countries, and a multidisciplinary approach to successful treatment that saved a nineteen-year-old from a life-threatening infection and spared him from a pneumonectomy.
