N2  - A 69-year-old previously healthy Taiwanese woman presented to primary care with 1 month of productive cough, intermittent fevers and wheezing. She did not have shortness of breath or hemoptysis. She moved from Taiwan to San Fransisco in 1976 and is a lifelong non-smoker. Her family history was notable for a sister who died of lung cancer at age 37. On physical exam, she was afebrile with a normal cardiopulmonary examination. A chest radiograph revealed a right perihilar opacity, and a subsequent chest computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrated a solid 5.6x5.1x3.4cm right upper lobe mass with central necrosis. This study also showed multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, scattered low-density hepatic lesions, and a left-sided adrenal lesion. Pulmonary consultation resulted in recommendations for serial AFB sputum cultures, interferon-γ release assay (IGRA) testing, and a positron emission tomography (PET)-CT scan. The IGRA and acid-fast stains of her AFB cultures were negative. PET-CT showed fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)-avidity of the right perihilar mass, right superior and inferior paratracheal, subcarinal and left superior paratracheal lymph nodes; this study also revealed hypermetabolic hepatic and osseous lesions. Overall, her work-up was felt to be consistent with metastatic primary lung cancer. The patient initially underwent fine-needle aspiration biopsy of a hepatic lesion, with pathology demonstrating a necrotizing granuloma. Next, she had a flexible bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration, as well as biopsy via mediastinoscopy, of the perihilar mass, right inferior paratracheal and subcarinal lymph nodes. Pathology was negative for malignancy, again revealing necrotizing granulomas and inflammatory debris. Two weeks after her procedures, the initial AFB sputum culture was positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis. She was referred to infectious disease and was started on isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for disseminated tuberculosis. Her productive cough improved rapidly with antimicrobial therapy, and a follow-up CT scan demonstrated interval improvement in the right perihilar mass with stable mediastinal lymphadenopathy. In conclusion, we describe an atypical presentation of mycobacterium tuberculosis initially mistaken for metastatic lung cancer in the setting of an FDG-avid right perihilar mass, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, hepatic and osseous lesions. This case presents an opportunity to review the diagnostic work-up for pulmonary tuberculosis, including the application and testing characteristics of interferon-γ release assays. Additionally, we highlight a less common imaging finding and will review the range of radiographic presentations in pulmonary tuberculosis.
