N2  - Introduction Endobronchial tuberculosis (EBTB) is a tuberculous infection of the tracheobronchial tree with microbial and histopathological evidence. We present the case of an elderly South Asian man with the actively caseating EBTB, an extremely uncommon complication of pulmonary tuberculosis (PT) in this age group. Case presentation A 74-year old South Asian man with a history of hypertension and prediabetes presented with fever and cough for two weeks after failing an outpatient course of antibiotics. Review of systems revealed a 25-pound weight loss over the last 3 months. The patient was febrile and mildly dyspneic on admission. Respiratory examination revealed bronchial breathing and crackles in the right scapular, infrascapular and infra-axillary areas. Initial panel of laboratory tests were only significant for mild microcytic anemia. Chest radiogram revealed right middle and lower lung zone infiltrates worst in the right perihilar region. A computed tomography (CT) of the thorax revealed extensive heterogeneous alveolar opacities in the same distribution. A diagnostic bronchoscopy revealed thick, yellowish mucoid secretions in the right middle lobe with lesions resembling a pseudomembrane extending down to the right middle lobe bronchial subsegments. Endobronchial forceps biopsy from the above regions revealed numerous acid fast bacilli (AFB) positive bacilli within necrotic granulomatous inflammation tissue. Bronchial lavage, blood and sputum cultures were negative. The patient was diagnosed with endobronchial and parenchymal TB and treated with anti-tubercular therapy along with levofloxacin to cover for suspected superimposed bacterial infection. Discussion Endobronchial tuberculosis (EBTB) is a tuberculous infection of the tracheobronchial tree with microbial and histopathological evidence. CXR and sputum AFB smears have remarkably low sensitivities for diagnosis. Computed tomography (CT) and bronchoscopy along with microbiological investigations are the most useful diagnostic tools for the confirmation and evaluation of the tracheobronchial stenosis. EBTB is divided into seven subtypes based on bronchoscopic appearance; our patient fits into the actively caseating subtype which usually carries a grave prognosis, resulting in fibrostenosis in two-thirds of patients. Prevalence of EBTB is highest in young females under 50 years of age. Upon extensive review of literature up to October 2020, PubMed revealed only 4 published cases of EBTB in people aged above 50 years. The high rate of fibrostenotic complications along with a grave prognosis should alarm the physician to maintain a low threshold for diagnostic bronchoscopy in patients with known.
