N2  - Objective: Our project investigates the role of macrophages in the cystic fibrosis (CF) lung. Macrophages represent the first line of defense in the innate immune system, taking up pathogens from the airway surface and neutralising them via enzymatic digestion in phagosomes, which are called phagolysosomes after the phagocytic uptake of particles. Digestive enzymes such as proteases need an acidic pH to effectively kill pathogens. Several studies have shown that exactly this acidic pH is hampered in CF macrophages due to a defective CFTR channel in the lysosome membrane. Methods: For our studies we used either alveolar macrophages from the CF mouse model B6.129P2(CF/3)-CftrTgH(neoim)Hgu, harvested from the murine lungs by bronchoalveolar lavage or bone marrow derived macrophages differentiated from hematopoietic stem cells of the bone marrow. Additionally, we differentiated human-induced pluripotent stem cells from CF patients or healthy, non-CF subjects into macrophages. Using the pH sensitive dye LysoSensor Green DND-189 and confocal microscopy, we were able to detect differences in the pH of CF and non-CF lysosomes of mice and men. To further improve our technique and establish a reliable and user-independent read-out, we developed a software-based pipeline, which now allows a rapid and robust analysis of our image data. Results: Given this background and pipeline developed, we already could demonstrate successful protection of CF mice from severe pneumonia, which had been reconstituted with hematopoietic stem cells from healthy wild type mice. As a next step, we are going to investigate macrophages isolated from humanised mice, carrying the human CFTR gene instead of the murine counterpart. Conclusion: In summary, our studies shed light on the dysfunction of macrophages in the CF lung disease which is characterised by massive chronic and progressive inflammation. As a long-term goal, we aim to target macrophages as a cell-based immunotherapy.
