N2  - Objective To analyze the clinical characteristics and relevant risk factors of active tuberculosis in patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Methods The clinical data of 41 RA patients complicated with active tuberculosis were retrospectively analyzed. The contemporary inpatients (1:1 matching in terms of age and sex) with active tuberculosis who did not suffer from RA or other rheumatic diseases were selected as the control group for clinical characteristics analysis. Additional 101 randomly selected RA patients without active tuberculosis or other infection during the same period were used as the control group for analysis of relevant risk factors. All patients had complete medical history, physical examination, laboratory, and auxiliary examination data. Results The 41 RA patients complicated with active tuberculosis included 18 males and 23 females, with an average age of (67.7 ± 12.0) years. Compared with the patients with active pulmonary tuberculosis but without RA, the patients with both RA and active pulmonary tuberculosis had numerically more i nf eri or l obe l ung i nvol vement, ext rapul monar y involvement, and other bacterial infection (P > 0.05), while the positive rate of acid-fast bacillus culture, tuberculosis antibody, PPD test, and the prevalence of cough symptoms were significantly lower (P < 0.05). Univariate analysis showed that the patients with both active pulmonary tuberculosis and RA were older associated with more males, a history of smoking, joint deformity, coronary heart disease, glucocorticoid use, lower lymphocyte percentage, lower hemoglobin, lower albumin, higher C-reactive protein, higher erythrocyte sedimentation rate, higher white blood cell count, less joint swelling and tenderness, and lower prevalence of disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drugs treatment (P < 0.05) compared with the RA patients without active tuberculosis or other infection. Multivariate logistic regression analysis found that RA and active tuberculosis comorbidity is significantly associated with older age, history of smoking, lower lymphocyte percentage, higher percentage of glucocorticoid use, lower prevalence of joint tenderness and disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drugs treatment (P < 0.05). Conclusions RA patients complicated with active tuberculosis are associated with older age, history of smoking, joint deformity, glucocorticoid use, and lower lymphocyte percentage. It is not uncommon to find multi-lobe involvement and other bacterial co-infection in the RA patients complicated with active tuberculosis.
