N2  - Objective To explore the application of metagenomic next-generation sequencing (mNGS) in the diagnosis of Chlamydia psittaci pulmonary infection, so as to provide reference for the diagnosis and treatment of this disease. Methods The respiratory tract specimens were collected from 101 patients with pulmonary infection in Huashan Hospital, Fudan University from December 2019 to December 2020. The samples were analyzed by mNGS technique. CNKI, Wanfang, and VIP databases were retrieved with "parrot fever" and "sequencing" as the search terms. PubMed database was retrieved with "psittac" and "next generation sequencing" as search term. The clinical data of Chlamydia psittaci-positive patients were collected appropriately. The retrieval period is unlimited. A total of 23 cases of Chlamydia psittaci pulmonary infection were diagnosed by metagenomic sequencing. Results In this study, respiratory specimens of 101 patients with pulmonary infection were detected by mNGS. Chlamydia psittaci sequence was detected in 4 cases. A total of 27 cases (including 4 recent cases in this paper) of pneumonia due to Chlamydia psittaci were reported from 2019 to 2020. The main clinical manifestations were fever (100%), cough (63.0%), and productive cough (55.6%). The findings on lung CT images included consolidation (13/27, 48.1%) and pleural effusion (11/27, 40.7%). Only a single lobe was involved in 14 patients (51.9%). Before diagnosis, antimicrobial regimen did not cover atypical pathogens in 6 patients (22.2%). After diagnosis, 10 patients (37.0%) received monotherapy, with doxycycline or moxifloxacin being the most commonly used. The outcome of Chlamydia psittaci pneumonia was good. Overall, 26 patients (96.3%) improved after effective treatment. Conclusions mNGS has become one of the most important method for clinical diagnosis of psittacosis. Tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones are effective in treating pneumonia caused by Chlamydia psittaci.
