N2  - Objective To examine the clinical characteristics of pulmonary tuberculosis complicated with type 2 diabetes. Methods Of the patients treated for pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) in People's Hospital of Zengcheng District from January 2018 to December 2019, 94 were identified with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM). These patients were included as TB-T2DM group. Additional 94 TB patients without T2DM treated in the same period were included as TB-NT2DM group. The clinical data, including symptoms (fever, chest pain, cough, hemoptysis), chest CT findings, sputum smear results, serum procalcitonin, platelet count, WBC count, glycosylated hemoglobin, and hemoglobin, were collected retrospectively, and compared between the two groups. Chest CT lesion absorption and sputum negative conversion rate were compared between the two groups after 2-month and 6-month anti-tuberculosis treatment. Results TB-T2DM group showed significantly higher positive rate of sputum smear, increased serum procalcitonin, proportion of chest CT cavitary lesions, proportion of lesions involving more than two lobes, and average level of glycosylated hemoglobin than TB-NT2DM group (all P < 0.05). After anti-tuberculosis treatment for 6 months, the lesion absorption on chest CT was worse in TB-T2DM group than in TB-NT2DM group (P < 0.05). The clinical symptoms, platelet count, WBC count, hemoglobin, sputum negative conversion rate after 2-month and 6-month anti-tuberculosis treatment did not show significant difference between the two groups (P > 0.05). Conclusions The sputum smear-positive rate is higher in case of T2DM complication. The increased serum procalcitonin suggests susceptibility to bacterial infection, more extensive and severe pulmonary lesions, and poor absorption of TB lesions on chest CT after 6-month anti-tuberculosis treatment. These clinical characteristics of TB-T2DM patients are helpful for better management of patients.
