N2  - INTRODUCTION: We present a 16-year-old male with acute respiratory distress syndrome and BAL findings consistent with acute eosinophilic pneumonia (AEP), associated with the use of a disposable nicotine salt vaping device. METHODS: A 16-year-old male presented with difficulty breathing, pleuritic chest pain, dry cough, and fever. Upon admission he was distressed and hypoxic. He had respiratory alkalosis, white blood cell count of 24.7 thou/Cumm with a neutrophilic predominance, lactate of 2.47 mmol/L, and a C Reactive protein of 177.4 mg/L. Sars-Cov-2 nucleic amplification assay was negative. Upon admission to the PICU vital signs were stable on CPAP except for persistent tachypnea. Due to a clinical picture consistent with infective pneumonia, the patient was managed as such. During his stay he divulged a history of vape use and provided the team with a PuffBar Lush Ice disposable e-cigarette. Due to worsening of his acute hypoxic respiratory failure the patient was intubated and a bronchoscopy with BAL was performed which demonstrated an absence of purulent fluid or signs of bacterial infection. BAL samples showed no pathogenic organisms but had a marked Eosinophilia (39%) with concomitant lipid-laden macrophages. The patient was treated with Methylprednisolone 125mg every 6 hours for AEP and tapered over 14 days of therapy. Ventilator settings were weaned steadily following systemic steroids. He was intensely ventilated for 6 days with maximum oxygen index of 13.5, PIPs in the low 30's and a positive end expiratory pressure of 12cmH2O. He was discharged on day 16 of admission on steroid wean. Pulmonary function testing 2 weeks after discharge remained consistent with restrictive lung disease. RESULTS: EVALI is a well-established mechanism of lung injury and an increasingly prevalent cause of ICU admission around the country, it is predominantly associated with Neutrophilia and lipid laden macrophages on BAL. This case has an atypical cytology, consisting of an Eosinophilic predominance. This is the first report of Eosinophilic Pneumonia secondary to EVALI in a patient under 18 years of age, and it represents the second reported case of this phenomena in all patient populations. This incidence involves a poorly regulated nicotine salt disposable device, similar to the popular JUUL.
