N2  - Background: While Measles is a preventable disease, outbreaks frequently occur among population with low vaccination coverage such the indigenous group. An outbreak of measles among the indigenous group of north east Malaysia, known as the Batek tribe of Kelantan was declared in June 2019 involving 75 patients. We describe the clinical presentation and outcome of hospitalized patients during the outbreak. Methods and materials: This was a retrospective cohort study undertaken in Hospital Kuala Krai and Hospital Gua Musang, Kelantan. The patients among the Batek tribe diagnosed with measles during the outbreak were reviewed from notification line listing and hospitalized patients were identified via hospital registration. 30 consecutive medical records of laboratory confirmed measles either via measles serology and/or PCR >12 years old admitted from June 1 to August 31 2019 were retrieved. Relevant data were extracted and analysed. Results: Majority presented with fever (93.3%, n = 28) and cough (90%, n = 27). Conjunctivitis was reported in 11 patients (36.6%). Rash was detected in only 5 patients (16.6%) and Koplik spots were not reported in any patients. The complications of measles in our cohort were pneumonia (53.3% n = 16), pleural effusion (6.6% n = 2), pneumothorax (6.6% n = 2), tuberculosis reactivation (3.3% n = 1), and mediastinum abscess (3.3% n = 1). Other complications included blood stream infection, miscarriage, corneal perforation, heart failure, and acute kidney injury. There was high prevalence of iron deficiency anaemia and hypoalbuminaemia. We postulated that this reflected poor nutritional status of this cohort, and in turn conferred a higher rate of complications due to immunosuppression. Care was mainly supportive including antipyretics, fluids and treatment of bacterial superinfections. All patients received oral vitamin A 200,000 IU. Two patients required ICU care and mechanical ventilation and 1 patient developed acute kidney injury requiring hemodialysis. All patients survived and were discharged well. Conclusion: Measles, a highly contagious vaccine-preventable disease is still a threat among the indigenous population due to low vaccination coverage. In our cohort, the absence of typical rash posed challenges to the treating physicians in early diagnosis of measles.
