N2  - Background: Both pneumonia and sepsis in children still remain the highest burden in developing countries. The options for the treatment of pneumonia and sepsis by using antibiotics are limited because of the development of resistant S. pneumoniae and multidrug resistant strains (MDR). This study aimed to determine the bacterial isolates obtained from blood of under-five pneumonia children with sepsis and without sepsis along with the antibiotic sensitivity testing. Methods and materials: This is an unmatched case-control study where the children of either sex, aged 0–59 months with the history of cough or difficult breathing and radiologic pneumonia were enrolled. The children who were assessed for sepsis (n = 123) at admission were the study population. Children those without sepsis (n = 82) were the controls. Comparison was made between children with clinically defined sepsis and without sepsis. Results: Bacterial growth on blood culture was found in 19% patients, however, 7% patients did not show any bacterial growth. Among them 8% of bacterial isolates was coagulase-negative staphylococci which is followed by Acinetobacter species (2.4%). Only 1.7% cases were infected with Streptococcus pneumonia, Haemophylus influenza, Salmonella typhi and Klebsiella. In contrast with that, children without sepsis presented 2.4% growth of Salmonella typhi, 1.2% of Klebsiella and no growth of Streptococcus pneumonia and Haemophylus influenza was obtained. Species of E. coli (0.8%) and Enterococcus (0.8%) were also obtained in the cultures. Among them 100% sensitivity was obtained for imipenem, 87% for meropenem, 84% for ceftazidime, 77% for ciprofloxacin, 71% for both netilmicin and amikacin and 52% for gentamicin. The mortality rate (odds ratio [OR] = 3.02, 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.11-8.64, p < 0.027) is high among the children with sepsis. Conclusion: Laboratory characteristics in these children will help to have early diagnosis and treatment that will help to reduce mortality.
