N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Nocardial pneumonia is a rare but potentially serious infection. Its nonspecific signs and symptoms mimic many common conditions and present a diagnostic challenge. Concurrent pulmonary embolism (PE) is a complication that has been infrequently noted in previous literature. CASE PRESENTATION: A 63-year-old female presented to the emergency department with worsening productive cough for one week. Her medical history is notable for specific antibody deficiency that evolved into common variable immunodeficiency complicated by bronchiectasis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), for which she was maintained on intravenous immunoglobulin and prophylactic antibiotics. Chest x-ray showed multifocal infiltrates, and she was admitted with community acquired pneumonia and COPD exacerbation. Treatment included levofloxacin and prednisone taper, and she was found to have new supplemental oxygen requirement. Three weeks later she presented again, complaining of pleuritic chest pain. Computed tomography PE protocol showed pulmonary emboli with stable lung parenchymal findings, and she was started on apixaban. At clinic follow-up four weeks after initial presentation, she noted persistent cough despite completing her previous therapy. Sputum culture was obtained and acid-fast sputum culture grew Nocardia nova. She was started on trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and meropenem with good clinical response, and follow-up imaging showed improvement of multi-lobar pneumonia. DISCUSSION: The association of PE and nocardial pneumonia was noted in two transplant patients in 1982 but has not been widely described since. Prolonged untreated nocardial pneumonia in our patient may have contributed to the inflammatory milieu favorable for a thromboembolic event. PE was both a diagnostic confounder but also a possible consequence of unrecognized Nocardia infection. Nocardiosis is an uncommon gram-positive bacterial infection caused by aerobic actinomycetes in the genus Nocardia. Nocardiosis is typically regarded as an opportunistic infection, but approximately one-third of infected patients are immunocompetent. Identification of Nocardia isolates to the species level is important, as they correlate with particular antibiotic resistance patterns that may help inform therapy while awaiting susceptibility testing. CONCLUSIONS: This case report demonstrates the temporal association of thromboembolic disease and nocardial pneumonia. The overlapping features of PE and nocardial pneumonia created a diagnostic challenge. Additionally, Nocardia species identification guides treatment regimens before antibiotic susceptibility is finalized. Reference #1: Tazmaloukas AH, Ahlin T, Katzestein D, Sterling WH. Association of Pulmonary Embolism and Pulmonary Nocardiosis in Renal Transplant Recipients. Transplantation. 1982;33:569 Reference #2: Wallace RJ, Brown BA, Tsukamura M, et al. Clinical and laboratory features of Nocardia nova. Journal of Clinical Microbiology. 1991;29:24017-2411 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Robert Gilkeson, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Patrick Kramer, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by David Nguyen, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Kathryn Ruda Wessell, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Catalina Teba, source=Web Response
