N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Diffuse Lung Disease SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Bleomycin is used in the management of germ cell tumors and lymphoma. Lung toxicity is a known complication that usually presents subacutely due to the development of pneumonitis. Acute respiratory failure in the setting of organizing pneumonia, spontaneous pneumomediastinum, and pneumothoraces is a rare presentation. CASE PRESENTATION: 45-year-old male with a history of mixed germ-cell testicular cancer treated with 2 cycles of bleomycin(total of 150 units), cisplatin, and etoposide who presented with a 4-week history of worsening dyspnea and cough despite completing a course of Moxifloxacin. In the emergency room, the patient was hypoxic and tachypneic. On physical examination, He had normal JVP, bilateral rales on auscultation, and use of accessory muscles. Blood work revealed mild leukocytosis, anemia, and normal procalcitonin level. CT chest showed bilateral peripheral-predominant consolidation and ground-glass opacities suggestive of organizing pneumonia as well as evidence of extensive pneumomediastinum. Bronchoscopy showed hyperemia and edema of airways. Bronchoalveolar lavage was obtained and sent for bacterial gram stain & culture, fungal culture, also for Nocardia, Pneumocystis Jirovecii, Legionella, Aspergillus, HSV, and VZV testing. In addition, autoimmune disease serologies were also obtained. After extensive infectious and rheumatologic workup came back negative, the diagnosis of bleomycin-induced lung toxicity was made. The patient developed worsening hypoxia requiring high-flow oxygen. Repeat CT chest was obtained which revealed new bilateral pneumothoraces and subcutaneous emphysema. He was started on pulse doses of steroids and mycophenolate. Pneumothoraces were treated with bilateral chest tubes. His oxygen requirement improved and he was discharged with a plan for close follow up, however, the patient was noncompliant. DISCUSSION: Bleomycin is associated with lung-toxicity due to low levels of bleomycin hydroxylases in lung tissues. It is usually seen after a total dose of greater than 400 units and the elderly are more susceptible. The presentation can be variable ranging from an asymptomatic decrease in diffusing capacity of carbon monoxide to development of severe respiratory failure as seen in our case. Findings on imaging are also variable and can reveal multiple airspace opacities or increasing reticular markings in the periphery concerning fibrosing alveolitis. Diagnosis requires the exclusion of infectious as well as autoimmune pathologies. A biopsy is rarely needed but may be helpful when there is a concern for the involvement of lungs from primary malignancy. Treatment involves systemic steroids and discontinuation of bleomycin. There is limited data regarding the use of other immunosuppressive therapy and requires further research. CONCLUSIONS: Spontaneous pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum should be considered as a possible complication of bleomycin therapy. Reference #1: Blum RH, Carter SK, Agre K. A clinical review of bleomycin–a new antineoplastic agent. Cancer. 1973;31(4):903-914. Reference #2: Watson RA, De La Peña H, Tsakok MT, et al. Development of a best-practice clinical guideline for the use of bleomycin in the treatment of germ cell tumours in the UK. Br J Cancer. 2018;119(9):1044-1051. Reference #3: Bellamy EA, Husband JE, Blaquiere RM, Law MR. Bleomycin-related lung damage: CT evidence. Radiology. 1985;156(1):155-158 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Shahrukh Arif, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Firas Jafri, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Omair Tahir, source=Web Response
