N2  - SESSION TITLE: Fellows Diffuse Lung Disease Posters SESSION TYPE: Fellow Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Sarcoidosis is a systemic disease of unknown origin with granulomatous inflammation as the underlying pathologic mechanism. The prevalence of sarcoidosis in Indians is rare and often misdiagnosed as tuberculosis (TB). We present a case of acute hypoxic respiratory failure with diffuse lymphadenopathy as a rare presentation of sarcoidosis in an Indian patient. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old South Indian male with no known medical history presented with shortness of breath for less than a week with dry cough. His exercise tolerance was only 15 feet without any limitations in past. He was evaluated in the Emergency Department and noted to have oxygen saturation in 70s requiring high flow nasal cannula with 50% FiO2. He denied any fever, chills, weight loss, joint pain, sick contacts, history of TB or repiratory diseases. He is a never smoker and denies illicit substance or e-cigarette use and denies exposure to chemicals. A chest radiograph revealed interstitial edema and cardiomegaly. CT pulmonary angiogram revealed diffuse nodular/ground glass infiltrates and mediastinal/hilar adenopathy but no pulmonary embolism. His oxygen requirements increased to 90% FiO2 despite treatment with broad spectrum antibiotics. Extensive work up for autoimmune conditions, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, fungal serology, respiratory viral panel, brain natriuretic peptide, troponins and microbial cultures were within normal limits. Angiotensin converting enzyme levels were elevated at 83 U/L (normal 9-67 U/L). He was started on intravenous corticosteroids with dramatic resolution of symptoms and was discharged on oral prednisone and on room airn. Pulmonary function tests revealed moderate restrictive ventilatory defect and reduced diffusion capacity. Endobronchial Ultrasound Transbronchial Needle Aspiration of thoracic lymph nodes (LN) was performed which revealed epitheloid granulomas. Culture of LN was negative for fungi and bacteria including mycobacteria. Patient was continued on prednisone therapy with resolution of symptoms. DISCUSSION: The presentation and the acuity of symptoms in sarcoidosis are variable but generally tends to be more insidious. Less than one half of patients are asymptomatic with incidental abnormalities noted on routine chest imaging. In the other half of patients with symptoms, the onset of symptoms is insidious ranging from pulmonary, ophthalmic or cutaneous involvement. Very few cases have been reported for a hyper acute presentation of sarcoidosis. This is hypothesized to be due to cytokines released from the macrophages and activated T-cells within the ongoing inflammation in the pulmonary parenchyma. CONCLUSIONS: Even though unusual, acute presentation of sarcoidosis needs to be considered as a cause for acute hypoxic respiratory failure. Sarcoidosis is uncommon in Indians and the etiology for low prevalence remains unknown like those in other ethnicities. Reference #1: Kumar, R., Goel, N., & Gaur, S. N. (2012). Sarcoidosis in north Indian population: a retrospective study. Indian Journal of Chest Diseases and Allied Sciences, 54(2), 99. Sabbagh, F., Gibbs, C., & Efferen, L. S. (2002). Pulmonary sarcoidosis and the acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Thorax, 57(7), 655-656. Reference #2: Sabbagh, F., Gibbs, C., & Efferen, L. S. (2002). Pulmonary sarcoidosis and the acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Thorax, 57(7), 655-656. Reference #3: Costabel, U., & Hunninghake, G. W. (1999). ATS/ERS/WASOG statement on sarcoidosis. Sarcoidosis statement committee. American thoracic society. European respiratory society. World association for sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disorders. European Respiratory Journal, 14(4), 735-737. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Bredon Crawford, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by SherrieLynn Dixon-Williams, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ishan Lalani, source=Web Response
