N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Necrotizing pneumonia is a life-threatening complication of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) that exemplifies lung parenchyma liquification. We present a case of an immunocompetent female who had a fatal outcome despite starting appropriate early antibiotics. CASE PRESENTATION: A 69-year-old healthy female with no past medical history presents to an outside hospital with 5 days of cough, fevers, and shortness of breath. She had positive influenza A on a respiratory viral panel and bilateral multilobar pneumonia on a chest radiograph (CXR). She was admitted and treated for CAP. Overnight, she developed acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation and was transferred to our facility. On arrival, vitals were 103 F, blood pressure 84/40 mmHg, and heart rate 117 beats per minute. Labs showed leukopenia 1700/uL, lactate 5.9, and procalcitonin 62.17. CT chest (Figure 1) showed bilateral infiltrates. She was admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock and started on norepinephrine, vasopressin, broad-spectrum antibiotics (vancomycin, ceftriaxone, azithromycin), and oseltamivir. On hospital day 4, blood and tracheal aspirate cultures grew MSSA. Thus antibiotics were narrowed to nafcillin. The patient continued to have intermittent fevers, leukocytosis, but multiple blood cultures collected remained negative. Repeat CT chest (Figure 2,3) on day 6 showed multiple abscesses in the right lung consistent with necrotizing pneumonia. Thoracic surgery was consulted, and no surgery was offered. Clindamycin was added to the antibiotic regimen. Given the grave prognosis, the family decided to transition her to comfort measures, and she passed away on day 10 of her hospitalization. DISCUSSION: Panton-Valentine Leukocidin (PVL) is a cytotoxin produced by S.aureus that causes leukocyte destruction and tissue necrosis. The high mortality with PVL- S. aureus pulmonary infections in young, healthy populations has been well established (1). Literature supports the early use of antitoxin agents clindamycin, linezolid, rifampicin, and IV immunoglobulins to reduce mortality. The use of beta-lactams has been controversial as in vitro studies have suggested increased PVL toxin release with inadequate mean inhibitory concentration. This may be a consequence of inadequate penetration due to tissue necrosis and increased volume of distribution in sepsis (2,3). Beta-lactams like nafcillin in our case should be used cautiously in patients with suspected necrotizing pneumonia. CONCLUSIONS: Given the high mortality and low prevalence of PVL-MSSA, high clinical suspicion and early diagnosis are crucial to improve survival. We suggest early addition of antitoxin agents in patients with MSSA pneumonia presenting with leukopenia, septic shock or with preceding viral illness. Reference #1: Gillet, Vanhems, Philippe, Lina, Gerard, Daniel, et al. Factors Predicting Mortality in Necrotizing Community-Acquired Pneumonia Caused by Staphylococcus aureus Containing Panton-Valentine Leukocidin [Internet]. OUP Academic. Oxford University Press; 2007 [cited 2020Jun1]. Available from: https://academic.oup.com/cid/article/45/3/315/358582 Reference #2: Kreienbuehl L, Charbonney E, Eggimann P. Community-acquired necrotizing pneumonia due to methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus secreting Panton-Valentine leukocidin: a review of case reports [Internet]. Annals of intensive care. Springer; 2011 [cited 2020Jun1]. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3259061/#B5 Reference #3: Gillet Y;Dumitrescu O;Tristan A;Dauwalder O;Javouhey E;Floret D;Vandenesch F;Etienne J;Lina G; Pragmatic Management of Panton-Valentine Leukocidin-Associated Staphylococcal Diseases [Internet]. International journal of antimicrobial agents. U.S. National Library of Medicine; [cited 2020Jun1]. Available from: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21733661/ DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Arupreet Kochar, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ankit Kumar, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Lokendra Thakur, source=Web Response
