N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Pulmonary Manifestations of Systemic Disease Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Hermansky-Pudlak Syndrome (HPS) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by oculo-cutaneous albinism, bleeding diathesis and pulmonary fibrosis. [1,2] Pulmonary fibrosis is described to develop only in 3 subtypes, and usually manifests between the ages of 30 and 40. [1] We present a case of HPS with late-onset, rapidly progressive, fulminant pulmonary fibrosis, likely precipitated by an infectious process. CASE PRESENTATION: A 74-year-old non-smoking Latin-American man with an established diagnosis of HPS from family history and abnormal bleeding as an outpatient, initially presented to the hospital 6 months ago with dyspnea and a productive cough for one week. His CT scan of the chest showed right upper lobe consolidation with pleural-based calcifications, but otherwise normal lung parenchyma (Figure 1). He was diagnosed with bacterial pneumonia and started on piperacillin-tazobactam. He was discharged following clinical improvement and followed up in clinic with spirometry showing FVC 56% and FEV-1 140% with plans to repeat imaging but was lost to follow up. After 6 months, he presented to our hospital with persistent dyspnea and hypoxia. His subsequent CT chest revealed new findings of basilar predominant interstitial fibrosis with subpleural honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis consistent with usual interstitial pneumonia pattern (Figure 1). He was discharged with home oxygen with the plan to initiate antifibrotic therapy in the outpatient setting. DISCUSSION: Hermansky-Pudlak Syndrome is a rare disorder encompassing 10 genetically distinct subtypes, with Types 1, 2 and 4 being associated with pulmonary fibrosis. [1] It is hypothesized that the etiology of pulmonary fibrosis in HPS is due to epithelial cell dysfunction and fibroblast proliferation triggered by infection. [1] The case of our patient reinforces this hypothesis given his newly developed, rapid progressing lung fibrosis following a bacterial pneumonia infection. CT scans and pulmonary function tests can be helpful to detect early signs and progression of pulmonary fibrosis in HPS. Treatment of pulmonary fibrosis in HPS is limited to supportive and preventative measures and ultimately, lung transplantation. More recently, antifibrotic medications, pirfenidone and nintedanib, have also been used to slow the progression of fibrosis. CONCLUSIONS: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first case of HPS with new onset pulmonary fibrosis after the age of 70, with rapid progression within 6 months following an insult of bacterial pneumonia. This case highlights the importance of lifelong monitoring on all HPS patients, especially after pulmonary infection, to detect early pulmonary complications. Reference #1: Vicary GW, Vergne Y, Santiago-Cornier A, Young LR, Roman J. Pulmonary Fibrosis in Hermansky-Pudlak Syndrome. Annals of the American Thoracic Society. 2016. Reference #2: Doubková M, Trizuljak J, Vrzalová Z, et al. Novel genetic variant of HPS1 gene in Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome with fulminant progression of pulmonary fibrosis: a case report. BMC Pulmonary Medicine. 2019;19(1). DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Abhinav Bhalla, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Rostislav Gorbatov, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Young Im Lee, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Lina Miyakawa, source=Web Response
