N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Influenza has caused significant morbidity and mortality worldwide with bacterial co-infection increasing those rates. Lung biopsies done during the 1918 Influenza pandemic showed that streptococcus pneumoniae and streptococcus pyogenes were the most commonly isolated bacteria. (1) In fact, a synergistic effect has been shown with influenza and streptococcus species co-infections. (2) CASE PRESENTATION: A 31 year old male smoker with congenital esophageal atresia status post multiple reparative surgeries presented with three days of nausea, vomiting and decreased appetite, associated with fevers, chills, myalgias and productive cough. He did not receive influenza vaccination and had known contact with family with flu-like symptoms. Initial chest Xray showed early reticulonodular opacities and viral PCR confirmed Influenza A infection. He was admitted to the hospital and treated with intravenous fluids, ceftriaxone, azithromycin and oseltamivir. Two days into hospitalization, he experienced worsening pleuritic chest pain, sinus tachycardia and desaturated to 84% on 3L oxygen via nasal cannula. Computer tomography angiogram was done and showed a multilobar pneumonia with small left pleural effusion, no pulmonary embolism. He was placed on high-flow oxygen and upgraded to the intensive care unit for acute hypoxemic respiratory failure. Chest X-ray the following day remarkable for complete opacification of the left hemithorax without mediastinal shift. Lung ultrasound showed a large left loculated pleural effusion. Chest tube was placed and drained 800 cc of purulent fluid. Pleural fluid analysis was consistent with empyema. Both pleural fluid and blood cultures were positive for Streptococcus pyogenes. The patient was successfully extubated 48 hours later and chest tube removed after a total of 4 days and 1300 cc drainage; effusion radiologically resolved. Ultimately, he was discharged home on antibiotics with outpatient follow up. DISCUSSION: Tamayo et al reviewed 40 cases of S. pyogenes pneumonia and found a 30 day mortality rate of 20% with half of those fatalities occurring within 24 hours of admission.(3) Reviews of literature have shown a correlation in severity of illness with influenza A and B co-infections, perhaps due to the viral induced respiratory tract damage.(4) In mouse models, vaccination against influenza was shown to be associated with decreased mortality from superimposed S. pyogenes infections.(5) This may also be because influenza A inhibits IL-1B production in vivo, allowing for downstream suppression of the body’s protective mechanisms against bacterial infection. CONCLUSIONS: Streptococcus pyogenes is a rare cause of community acquired pneumonia but is often associated with mortality, even in patients without comorbidities.(3,4) Our patient’s lack of influenza immunity appears to be the nidus for his severe superimposed streptococcus pyogenes infection and empyema. Reference #1: Rudd JM, Ashar H, Chow V, Teluguakula N. Lethal Synergism Between Influenza and Streptococcus Pneumoniae. J Infect Pulm Dis. 2016 Dec (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5154682/) Reference #2: Morens D. M., Fauci A. S. (2007). The 1918 influenza pandemic: insights for the 21st century. J. Infect. Dis. 195, 1018–1028. 10.1086/511989 Reference #3: Tamayo, Esther et al. “Streptococcus pyogenes Pneumonia in Adults: Clinical Presentation and Molecular Characterization of Isolates 2006-2015.” PloS one vol. 11,3 e0152640. 30 Mar. 2016, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0152640 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sarah Bjork, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Trisana Cox, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Fastina Khan, source=Web Response
