N2  - SESSION TITLE: New Developments in the Diagnosis and Management of Mycobacterial and Bacterial Chest Infections SESSION TYPE: Original Investigations PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 PURPOSE: We hypothesized that nebulised amphotericin B would be effective in pulmonary aspergilloma patients in producing significant clinico-radiological improvement. This RCT was designed to verify the hypothesis The objectives were - to compare the effectiveness of Amp B (50mg/day) given daily for 7 days with oral itraconazole (400 mg/day) given for 6 months in reducing symptoms and/or radiological improvement(decrease in size of cavity or fungal ball by 50%) at the end of 6 months of therapy -to compare the side-effects observed in patients receiving Amp B and oral itraconazole METHODS: It was a parallel group randomized controlled trial (RCT) with a non-inferiority design conducted over 2 years. It was registered in Clinical Trial Registry India (reference number CTRI2018/11/016266). It was conducted over 2 years in a tertiary care hospital. Adult patients who were symptomatic due to pulmonary aspergilloma were recruited. RESULTS: A total of 32 patients, age and sex matched, were recruited in the 2 arms with the commonest complaints being hemoptysis (94%) and cough (78%). There was history of past tuberculosis in 91% of patients and IgG (specific for Aspergillus fumigatus) was positive in 63% patients. The 2 groups were well matched with respect to the number and size of fungal balls as well as their locations. At the end of 6 months there was no significant difference between the 2 groups, in terms of clinical improvement/stability (75% in oral itraconazole vs 90% in Amp B), radiological improvement/stability (75% vs 90%), severe bleeding requiring hospitalization (15% vs 13%, p=0.88). Amp B had higher incidence of minor side-effects (cough) as compared to oral itraconazole (gastric upset) (67% vs 12.5%, p= 0.01). Amp B was found to be 6 times more cost effective than oral itraconazole. CONCLUSIONS: Neb Amphotericin B is non-inferior to oral itraconazole in pulmonary aspergilloma for clinico-radiological improvement, though it is associated with higher incidence of minor side-effects. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Nebulized amphotericin B can be effective in symptomatic pulmonary aspergilloma patients. DISCLOSURES: no disclosure on file for j manikanta; No relevant relationships by Animesh Ray, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Surabhi Vyas, source=Web Response
