N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 PURPOSE: Legionella pneumonia incidence appears to be increasing, yet therapeutic options are limited. Omadacycline (OMC) is an aminomethylcycline, an oral and intravenous (IV) antibiotic derived from the tetracycline class, approved in the USA for community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP) in adults, including Legionella. OMC showed noninferiority to moxifloxacin (MOX) in CABP in the OPTIC Phase 3 study. We present a descriptive subanalysis of OPTIC participants with CABP and L. pneumophila as a causative pathogen. METHODS: Patients with suspected CABP were randomized 1:1 to receive IV OMC or MOX, with optional transition to oral medication, for a total of 7–14 days. L. pneumophila was detected via culture-independent methods (positive urinary antigen test or serology). Early clinical response (ECR) was assessed 72–120 h after receipt of the first dose; clinical success was assessed 5–10 days after last dose (post-treatment evaluation; PTE). ECR was defined as improvement in ≥2 CABP symptoms with no worsening of other CABP symptoms or use of rescue antibacterial treatment. Success at PTE was defined as resolution of signs and symptoms to the extent that further antibacterial therapy was not necessary. Safety was assessed by treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) and laboratory measures. RESULTS: Of 74 patients with L. pneumophila, 37 were treated with OMC and 37 with MOX. Baseline demographics (age, sex, ethnicity, BMI) were largely comparable in both groups. Notably, 0% and 2.7% of patients in OMC or MOX groups, respectively, had history of liver disease; however, liver enzyme elevation at baseline was noted in 35.1% for each group (patients with ALT or AST ≥2× ULN were excluded from the study). Mild-to-moderate COPD affected 5% and 1% patients; 0% and 5.4% had symptomatic asthma with wheezing; 0% and 2.7% had chronic cough with/without sputum production; 13.5% and 8.1% had prior lung infection; and mean (SD) PORT scores were 79.6 (13.1) and 82.5 (14.7) in OMC and MOX, respectively. OMC was comparable to MOX for ECR (OMC, 83.8% vs MOX, 86.5%; difference -2.7%; 95% CI, -20.0, 14.5) and success at PTE (OMC, 94.6% vs MOX, 97.3%; difference −2.7%; 95% CI, −15.5, 9.2). For safety, 37.8% and 32.4% patients had at least one TEAE; 21.4% and 58.3% were drug-related for OMC and MOX, respectively. There were no serious drug-related TEAEs or deaths. CONCLUSIONS: OMC showed comparable safety and efficacy profiles to MOX for treatment of CABP patients with L. pneumophila. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: OMC is a safe and effective monotherapy for patients with CABP caused by L. pneumophila. DISCLOSURES: Advisory Committee Member relationship with GSK Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/22/2020 by Antonio Anzueto, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Advisory Committee Member relationship with BI Please note: $1001 - $5000 Added 05/22/2020 by Antonio Anzueto, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Advisory Committee Member relationship with Mylan Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/22/2020 by Antonio Anzueto, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Advisory Committee Member relationship with AstraZeneca Please note: $1001 - $5000 Added 05/22/2020 by Antonio Anzueto, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Surya Chitra, source=Web Response Employee relationship with Paratek Pharmaceuticals Please note: >$100000 Added 04/03/2020 by Kyle Gunter, source=Web Response, value=Salary Employee relationship with Paratek pharmaceuticals Please note: >$100000 Added 04/03/2020 by mauricio Rodriguez, source=Web Response, value=Salary Employee relationship with Paratek pharmaceuticals Please note: >$100000 Added 04/03/2020 by mauricio Rodriguez, source=Web Response, value=Salary Removed 04/03/2020 by mauricio Rodriguez, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Salim Surani, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Alison Terry, source=Web Response
