N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Diffuse Lung Disease SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis (PLCH), a rare and uncommon interstitial lung disease, is often misdiagnosed due to a confluence of non-specific symptoms and lack of diagnostic testing. Here we present the case of a patient who fell outside the typical demographic and symptom profile and experienced a protracted treatment course as a result. CASE PRESENTATION: A 69 year old woman with a medical history of chronic hepatitis-C was admitted for cough and fever. CT Chest revealed numerous diffuse bilateral ground-glass and nodular opacities. Echocardiogram demonstrated an echogenic density in the mitral valve which was suspicious for vegetation. She was discharged with a 4 week course of ceftriaxone for presumptive cavitary pneumonia secondary to endocarditis and readmitted after a month for persistent cough and fever. Repeat CT Chest was unchanged and echocardiogram showed no clear evidence of vegetations. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed; cytology and culture were negative for malignancy and pathogens. She underwent video-assisted thoracoscopic wedge resection and pathology demonstrated features consistent with multifocal Langerhans cell histiocytosis. Antibiotics were discontinued and the patient was started on a 6 month prednisone taper with complete resolution of symptoms. DISCUSSION: PLCH is a myeloid neoplasm with inflammatory properties, resulting from activating mutations of the BRAF gene. Langerhans cells develop clusters around small bronchioles, forming nodules in the airways with organizing pneumonia at their edges, leading to destruction of the bronchiolar wall, interstitial fibrosis and cyst formation [1]. It’s a rare disease with a prevalence of 3-5% in patients with chronic interstitial lung disease [3]. The majority of cases are reported in young adults and occurs almost exclusively in smokers [2]. It’s presentation varies, ranging from non-specific symptoms like cough and dyspnea (67% cases) to abnormalities incidentally detected on imaging (25% cases) to spontaneous pneumothorax (10–20% cases). There are no diagnostic tests for PLCH; most testing is conducted to rule out other disease processes and histopathologic findings from transbronchial biopsies are diagnostic in only 17-50% of patients. The first step in management is smoking cessation [2]. Systemic glucocorticoids are used but no standard criteria exists; 0.5-1.0 mg/kg daily with a slow taper is documented in case reviews. Immunosuppressive agents have been used to treat progressive disease. CONCLUSIONS: A high index of suspicion is required in order to diagnose PLCH. Clinicians must be aware of its non-specific presentation and effort involved in establishing a diagnosis, so as to prevent significant physical and psychological morbidity. Reference #1: Roden AC, Yi ES. Pulmonary Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis: An Update From the Pathologists’ Perspective. Arch Pathol Lab Med. 2016;140(3):230-40. Reference #2: Tazi A. Adult pulmonary Langerhans’ cell histiocytosis. Eur Respir J. 2006;27(6):1272-85.Tazi A. Adult pulmonary Langerhans’ cell histiocytosis. Eur Respir J. 2006;27(6):1272-85. Reference #3: Gaensler EA, Carrington CB. Open biopsy for chronic diffuse infiltrative lung disease: clinical, roentgenographic, and physiological correlations in 502 patients. Ann Thorac Surg. 1980;30(5):411-26. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Violeta Alvarez-Retamales, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by John Gao, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Moses Hayrabedian, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Suhayb Ranjha, source=Web Response
