N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Pulmonary Vascular Disease Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: A Pulmonary Embolism (PE) is a blood clot in one of the pulmonary arteries or its branches, and can be associated with a variety of complications including pleural effusions, hemoptysis, pulmonary infarction, and pulmonary cavitation. While some of these complications are common, rarely are multiple seen at once. CASE PRESENTATION: A 53 year-old female with history of Necrotizing Autoimmune Myositis presented to the hospital with generalized myalgia and productive cough. She was treated with IV antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia, and was also found to be positive for parainfluenza virus type 1. On day 18 after admission she developed a lower extremity DVT while in rehab and was started on Enoxaparin. The next day she was noted to be hypoxic and tachycardic, a CTA showed bilateral pulmonary emboli in the lower lobes with a left sided pleural effusion. She was readmitted for IV anticoagulation, transitioned to oral Apixaban and returned to rehab on admission day 24, but then developed recurrent hemoptysis. Repeat CT scan 36 days after presentation showed evolving pulmonary infarctions in the lingula and left lower lobe, and development of a cavitary lesion in the left lower lobe. Bronchoscopy did not show any active sites of bleeding, and Bronchoalveolar Lavage (BAL) was negative for any infectious etiology. Her hospitalization was further complicated by Heparin-induced Thrombocytopenia and septic shock. She continued to deteriorate medically and was eventually admitted to hospice. DISCUSSION: This patient’s PE was complicated by the development of hemoptysis, pleural effusion, infarct, and cavitation. Pleural effusions are commonly seen with PE, with the prevalence ranging widely from 23-47% (1, 2). However, the other complications are much less common. Pulmonary Infarcts are seen in about 10-31% of patients suffering from PEs, with approximately 7% of infarctions proceeding to pulmonary cavitation (3, 4, 5, 6, 7). While data is limited, one study found hemoptysis in 5.7% of patients with PE (8). From our extensive literature search, cases involving all these complications related to PE occurring in the same patient have not been reported, but the serial order of these complications helps us better understand the physiology. Extensive work-up was performed and ruled out infectious or rheumatologic etiologies for these complications, and no source of bleeding was found suggesting the hemoptysis was likely secondary to the pulmonary infarction. We believe that PEs can lead to pulmonary infarct due to lack of oxygen delivery; the infarcted area becomes weak and friable, causing bleeding and eventual cavitation. CONCLUSIONS: PE is common but its complications are uncommon, and we observed all PE related complications like hemoptysis, cavitation, pulmonary infarct, and pleural effusion in a single patient. This case also exemplifies the chronological order of these complications. Reference #1: 1. Stein PD, Beemath A, Matta F, et al. Clinical characteristics of patients with acute pulmonary embolism: data from PIOPED II. Am J Med. 2007;120(10):871-879. 2. Elliott CG, Goldhaber SZ, Visani L, DeRosa M. Chest radiographs in acute pulmonary embolism. Results from the International Cooperative Pulmonary Embolism Registry. Chest. 2000;118(1):33-38. doi:10.1378/chest.118.1.33 3. Kirchner J, Obermann A, Stückradt S, et al. Lung Infarction Following Pulmonary Embolism: A Comparative Study on Clinical Conditions and CT Findings to Identify Predisposing Factors. Rofo. 2015;187(6):440-444. doi:10.1055/s-0034-1399006 4. Miniati M, Bottai M, Ciccotosto C, Roberto L, Monti S. Predictors of Pulmonary Infarction. Medicine (Baltimore). 2015;94(41):e1488. doi:10.1097/MD.0000000000001488 5. Islam M, Filopei J, Frank M, et al. Pulmonary infarction secondary to pulmonary embolism: An evolving paradigm [published online ahead of print, 2018 Mar 25]. Respirology. 2018;10.1111/resp.13299. doi:10.1111/resp.13299 Reference #2: 6. Rajagopala S, Devaraj U, D'Souza G. Infected Cavitating Pulmonary Infarction. Respiratory Care. 2011; 56 (5) 707-709; doi: 10.4187/respcare.00828 7. Libby LS, King TE, LaForce FM, Schwarz MI. Pulmonary cavitation following pulmonary infarction. Medicine (Baltimore). 1985;64(5):342-348. doi:10.1097/00005792-198509000-00006 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response Removed 04/27/2020 by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response Removed 04/27/2020 by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response Removed 04/27/2020 by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response Removed 04/27/2020 by Bhavinkumar Dalal, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Adam Foglesong, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Yotam Weiner, source=Web Response
