N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium abscessus complex (MabsC) is a group of rapidly growing Non-tuberculous Mycobacterium (NTM) that causes pulmonary, skin, soft tissue, and bone disease. It is mainly seen in white females and those with pre-existing lung disorders like COPD, Cystic Fibrosis (CF) (1). We present a case of MabsC with Mycobacterium Avium Complex (MAC) co-infection, in a patient with no prior history of Tuberculosis or CF, rapidly progressing to respiratory failure despite antibiotic therapy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 49-year-old female with a history of COPD and 30-pack-yr smoking, presented with dyspnea and productive cough for one week. She had similar complaints five years prior, managed as community acquired pneumonia (CAP) but was lost to follow-up. Five months prior to current presentation, a CT chest showed multiple nodules in left upper lobe, and a 4 cm cavity in right upper lobe (RUL). CT guided biopsy was negative for malignancy with BAL showing Mabs. subsp. Abscessus (MAA) identified by molecular analysis and MAC. Antibiotic susceptibility revealed resistance to commonly used drugs (Table 1). She was started on IV tigecycline, delafloxacin, linezolid, and azithromycin. 5 months later, on her current presentation, vitals were RR 30, HR 110, O2 sat 78% on RA with a BMI-17 kg/m2 and coarse breath sounds were heard on auscultation. Metabolic panel was unremarkable. Blood counts showed WBC 40000 cells/µL. ABG showed pH 7.16 pCO2 63 pO2 52. CT chest revealed thick walled cavities with largest measuring up to 11x6 cm in RUL and consolidation in right lower lobe (Images A-D). She was intubated in intensive care unit for respiratory failure and continued on her previous regimen along with CAP therapy. Sputum culture isolated MAA. Unfortunately, MabsC with MAC co-infection and superimposed pneumonia led to fulminant respiratory failure resulting in her demise. DISCUSSION: MabsC is known to cause chronic progressive pulmonary infection, often requiring extended therapy due to multi-drug resistance (MDR). MabsC causes pulmonary nodules but MAC co-infection and fibrocavitary lesions are seen in only 15% of cases (1,2). In the absence of lung disease, except bronchiectasis, MabsC causes slowly progressive disease. A low BMI and fibrocavitary disease is associated with progression (2). At cellular level, a plausible explanation for prolonged infection could be the NTM resistance to LL-37 (3) leading to immune evasion. LL-37, an antimicrobial polypeptide present in macrophages, is involved in innate immunity. Currently, a multi-drug regimen for a minimum of 12 months, with surgical resection in those with complications or failed antibiotic therapy is recommended by ATS(1). CONCLUSIONS: Early diagnosis and extended therapeutic regimens have shown clinical improvement and disease regression (4) in MDR MabsC infection. Understanding the cellular properties of NTM might help in developing new targeted therapies. Reference #1: David E, Timothy A, et al.(2007). An Official ATS/IDSA Statement: Diagnosis, Treatment, and Prevention of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Diseases. Am J Respir Crit Care Med, 4, 367. Reference #2: Griffith DE, Girard WM, Wallace RJ Jr. Clinical features of pulmonary disease caused by rapidly growing mycobacteria. An analysis of 154 patients. Am Rev Respir Dis 1993; 147:1271. Reference #3: Honda JR, Hess T, Malcolm KC, Ovrutsky AR, Bai X, Irani VR, etal. Pathogenic nontuberculous mycobacteria resist and inactivate cathelicidin: implication of a novel role for polar mycobacterial lipids. PLoS One 2015;10:e0126994. Crossref, Medline, Google Scholar DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by susanne ajao, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Richard Miller, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Rutwik Patel, source=Web Response Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with Gilead Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/31/2020 by jihad slim, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with ViiV Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/31/2020 by jihad slim, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with Abbvie Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/31/2020 by jihad slim, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with merck Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/31/2020 by jihad slim, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Speaker/Speaker's Bureau relationship with Jansen Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 05/31/2020 by jihad slim, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria No relevant relationships by Divya Mounisha Thimmareddygari, source=Web Response
