N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Hepatobiliary tuberculosis is an uncommon complication of Mycobacterium Tuberculosis. Diagnosis and management is based upon clinical suspicion of alarming gastrointestinal symptoms and appropriate studies in the setting of known tuberculosis infection. We present the case of a 64 year old female who presented with non-resolving pneumonia complicated by cholecystitis as a result of her active TB infection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 64 year old female initially presented to the emergency department for fever, productive cough, and dyspnea. She worked on a cannabis farm in Mexico previously and had been frequently hospitalized for recurrent pneumonia. Emergent chest radiograph demonstrated bilateral upper lung consolidation with underlying emphysematous changes. Further lab studies revealed elevated titers for mycoplasma pneumonia IgG, a positive quantiferon gold study, and detectable levels of mycobacteria tuberculosis DNA via sputum samples which confirmed diagnosis of tuberculosis. As such, she was immediately moved to a negative pressure room with air droplet precautions and started on treatment for active tuberculosis including rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, isoniazid, and pyridoxine (RIPE therapy). Following gradual improvement of her respiratory symptoms, the patient began to complain of right upper quadrant abdominal tenderness. An ultrasound of her gallbladder showed distention and wall thickening of gallbladder with pericholecystic fluid but no evidence of gallstone while a hepatobiliary scan showed delayed clearance of tracer activity from blood pool. Laboratory tests showed mild transaminitis (AST: 171, ALP: 160, total bilirubin: 4.6, direct bilirubin 2.8). Cholecysectomy was initially considered, but deferred due to her acute state. DISCUSSION: After finishing 18 days of RIPE therapy and negative acid fast bacilli sputum cultures after 15 days of RIPE therapy, patient was discharged successfully with follow up to CDC for continued monitoring of RIPE therapy. CONCLUSIONS: Acute acalculous cholecystis due to gallbladder tuberculosis is an uncommon presentation of extrapulmonary active mycobacterium tuberculosis. It is important to recognize and appropriately treat patients with mycobacterium tuberculosis with concurrent hepatobiliary complications. Hepatobiliary tuberculosis is manifested as local involvement with caseation in localized areas of the biliary tree or miliary spread of the bacteria to extrapulmonary organs with biliary tree involvement. Conventional RIPE therapy for tuberculosis is usually sufficient to treat both pulmonary and extrapulmonary manifestations of tuberculosis and surgery may not necessarily be required at each instance for cholecystitis. Diagnosis and management is based upon clinical suspicion and appropriate studies to prevent long term complications associated with acute infection and unnecessary surgical intervention in a critically ill patient. Reference #1: Chaudhary P. Hepatobiliary tuberculosis. Ann Gastroenterol: Q Publ Hellenic Soc Gastroenterol. 2014;27:207–211. Reference #2: Alvarez S.Z. Hepatobiliary tuberculosis. J Gastroenterol Hepatol. 1998;13:833–839. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Shiyu Wang, source=Web Response
