N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Disorders of the Mediastinum Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Fibrosing mediastinitis (FM) is a rare, delayed hypersensitivity reaction to a variety of infective or non-infective stimuli that result in a local invasive proliferation of fibrous tissue within the mediastinum. Here, we present a case of a 60-year-old asymptomatic male with right hilar calcified mass with compression of multiple mediastinal and hilar structures. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old male with a history of essential hypertension noticed shortness of breath and cough, while he was on vacation in North Carolina for which he did not seek medical attention. However, due to progressively worsening symptoms and new-onset fever, he presented to his physician’s office and was prescribed empiric antibiotics for the treatment of potential pneumonia. Two weeks after the treatment, he went to the emergency department (ED) with similar complaints, and at that time chest computed tomography (CT) showed dense consolidation in the right middle and lower lobes with calcified right hilar lymph node and small right pleural effusion (fig 1-left). Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) detected a posterior mediastinal mass measuring approximately 4.8x5.2 cm abutting the left atrium (LA) and right inferior pulmonary vein (PV). Bronchoalveolar lavage was negative for fungal, bacterial, or viral infection. Lymph node biopsy was negative for malignancy. Serum testing for Cryptococcal antigen, Histoplasma antibodies, Blastomyces antibodies, Aspergillus, and Beta D glucan was negative as well. Positron emission tomography with computed tomography (PET/CT) showed increased fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) uptake at the dystrophic calcified mediastinal soft tissue mass suggesting an area of active mediastinal inflammation, compatible with fibrosing mediastinitis (fig 1-right). After multidisciplinary discussions no subsequent intervention such as stenting of compressed structures was initiated, as the patient has remained to be symptom-free with good exercise tolerance. DISCUSSION: Although rare, FM should be among the differentials in patients with a mediastinal mass of unknown etiology. FM can present with varying disease severity ranging from asymptomatic mediastinal mass to acute cardiovascular decompensation or massive hemoptysis. The exact pathogenesis of FM remains unknown. The rampant mediastinal fibrosis has been ascribed to chronic inflammation in response to various antigens. The extent of the initial clinical symptoms and their relationship to the compromise of specific mediastinal structures should guide the therapeutic approach [1]. CONCLUSIONS: The clinical presentation of FM varies depending on which of the intrathoracic structure is compromised. Although the definitive treatment of FM is unclear, the treatment should be symptomatic in the setting of uncertain etiology with close follow up. Reference #1: Peikert T, Colby TV, Midthun DE, Pairolero PC, Edell ES, Schroeder DR, Specks U: Fibrosing Mediastinitis: Clinical Presentation, Therapeutic Outcomes, and Adaptive Immune Response. Medicine. 2011, 90:412-423. 10.1097/MD.0b013e318237c8e6 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Hafiz Muhammad Siddique Qurashi, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Tabinda Saleem, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Selin Sendil, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Hiren Shingala, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Keerthi Yarlagadda, source=Web Response
