N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Imaging Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome (MKS) is a rare disorder typically diagnosed radiographically by marked dilation of the trachea and main bronchi. Patients typically present with recurrent bronchopulmonary infections and bronchiectasis. We report a patient who presented with refractory pneumonia and hemoptysis with atypical radiographic features suggestive of MKS. CASE PRESENTATION: A 41-year old Hispanic male presented for evaluation of hemoptysis, productive cough, and night sweats. The patient had no medical history and denied tobacco use. He had recently been unsuccessfully treated for pneumonia. Physical exam revealed a cachectic male, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, clubbing, SpO2 of 98% on room air, and a blood pressure of 82/53 mmHg. A CT chest with contrast showed diffuse bilateral bronchiectasis, small right middle lobe consolidation, and large posterior tracheal ectasia/diverticula. Tracheal diameter was 23 mm, while the main bronchi measured 17 mm. Microbial studies, including tuberculosis and coccidioidomycosis, serum a1-antitrypsin, and rheumatic work-up were negative. Bronchoscopy showed several tracheal diverticula and mucopurulent secretions with blood throughout several lobes, main bronchi, and trachea. BAL microbial studies and cytology were negative, except for Pseudomonas aeruginosa culture growth. The patient was treated with an antibiotic, steroids, and close pulmonary follow-up. Although airway measurements did not meet the criteria for tracheobronchomegaly (TBM), the patient’s overall clinical presentation with tracheal diverticula and distal bronchiectasis support the diagnosis of MKS. The patient has since been prescribed a flutter valve for airway clearance and long-term suppressive therapy with azithromycin. DISCUSSION: Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome, also known as TBM, is a rare condition with its etiology widely debated. Congenital weakness of the muscular and elastic tissue of the tracheal and bronchial walls is the most accepted theory. Diagnostic criteria by CT are based on diameter: trachea >3 cm, right main bronchus >2.5 cm, and left mainstem bronchus >2 cm. Treatment is focused on pulmonary hygiene and antibiotics for exacerbations. Tracheal stenting and/or tracheobronchoplasty are reserved for severe cases. A suggestion to classify TBM in three types based on anatomical appearance has been made. Although our patient did not meet exact measurement criteria, his tracheal dilation with airway diverticula and bronchiectasis, as well as refractory bronchopulmonary infection with hemoptysis, support the diagnosis of Type 1 TBM. CONCLUSIONS: Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome is characterized by tracheal and bronchial dilation with bronchiectasis and recurrent pulmonary infections. Although diagnosis is based off CT findings, this patient demonstrates the need of high clinical suspicion when diagnostic criteria are not met. Less stringent criteria may be required to capture more cases of MKS. Reference #1: Krustins E, Kravale Z, Buls A. Mounier-Kuhn syndrome or congenital tracheobronchomegaly: A literature review. Respir Med. 2013;107(12):1822–28. Reference #2: Katz I, Levine M, Herman P. Tracheobronchiomegaly. The Mounier-Kuhn syndrome. Am J Roentgenol Radium Ther Nucl Med. 1962;88:1084–94. Reference #3: Celik, Burcin et al. “Mounier-Kuhn syndrome: a rare cause of bronchial dilation.” Texas Heart Institute journal vol. 38,2 (2011): 194-6. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ricardo Herrera, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jonathan Munoz, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by kinnari SHAH, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Syed Zaidi, source=Web Response
