N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Chest Infections Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: As of February 2020, there have been 2,807 reported E-cigarette or vaping product use associated lung injury (EVALI) hospitalizations or deaths in the United States1. It is well known that cigarette smoking increases susceptibility to respiratory infections, and there is growing information that the same may hold true with the use of e-cigarettes2,3. Here we discuss a case of Legionella in the setting of EVALI, in a patient who is generally considered to be low risk for this type of infection and had no identifiable inoculative source besides the reservoir of her vaping device. CASE PRESENTATION: A 19 year-old female presented to the emergency department for evaluation of four days of rapidly worsening shortness of breath and productive cough accompanied by several episodes of vomiting, with recorded temperatures of 102 Fahrenheit. She had a past medical history of obesity and endorsed vaping daily for one year, using predominantly tobacco vaping products but noted occasionally vaping marijuana. Patient denied any contact with pools, hot tubs, fountains, air-conditioning systems and had no exposure to birds, other animals or molds. Urine drug screen was positive for marijuana. Chest Computed Tomography indicated extensive patchy opacities concerning for multifocal pneumonia and the patient was admitted at that time and started on Rocephin and Azithromycin. On day 2 of admission, she was sent to the intensive care unit for increased work of breathing with tachypnea up to 64 breaths/min with oxygen saturations in the upper 80s that did not improve with open diffuser mask or BiPaP and eventually required intubation and soon after was placed on rotoprone bed for 4 days for improved respiratory support. On day 7 of admission, urine testing was positive for Legionella and patient was started on Levoquin. Patient was extubated on day 10 of her admission and was discharged on day 17. DISCUSSION: Given imaging findings and severity of the illness, the patient was diagnosed with Legionella as a superimposed infection on EVALI. However given the patient’s age and lack of environmental exposures, the diagnosis of Legionella itself was unexpected. There was concern that the exposure to Legionella may have been through the vaping device, as she noted vaping pens often leaks, leaving the cartridge compartment, and the cotton pads inside of the cartridge wet, and difficult to clean. Multiple laboratory sites were contacted in an attempt to test e-cigarette cartridge for Legionella, however no labs that were contacted were capable of running such tests. CONCLUSIONS: There have been other reports of severe pulmonary infections in patients who meet EVALI criteria and there is growing evidence supporting that vaping increases infection the risk for pulmonary infections 3-7. However there is limited data regarding if the vaping devices themselves can be a nidus for these respiratory infections. Reference #1: 1. Outbreak of Lung Injury associated with the Use of E-Cigarettes, or Vaping, Products. https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/basic_information/e-cigarettes/severe-lung-disease.html Reference #2: 2. Bagaitkar J, Demuth DR, Scott DA. Tobacco use increases susceptibility to bacterial infection. Tob Induc Dis. 2008;4(1):12. Published 2008 Dec 18. doi:10.1186/1617-9625-4-12 Reference #3: 3. Madison MC, Landers CT, Gu BH, et al. Electronic cigarettes disrupt lung lipid homeostasis and innate immunity independent of nicotine. J. Clin. Investig., 129 (10) (2019), pp. 4290-4304 4. Schier JG, Meiman JG, Layden J, et al. Severe Pulmonary Disease Associated with Electronic-Cigarette-Product Use - Interim Guidance. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep 2019; 68:787. 5. Layden JE, Ghinai I, Pray I, et al. Pulmonary Illness Related to E-Cigarette Use in Illinois and Wisconsin - Final Report. N Engl J Med 2020; 382:903-916 DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa1911614 6. Kooragayalu S, El-Zarif S, Jariwala S.Vaping Associated Pulmonary Injury (VAPI) with superimposed Myocplasma pneumonia infection. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmcr.2020.100997 7. El Chebib H, McArthur K, Gorbonosov M, Domachowske JB. Anaerobic Necrotizing Pneumonia: Another Potential Life-threatening Complication of Vaping?. Pediatrics Apr 2020, 145 (4) e20193204; DOI: 10.1542/peds.2019-3204 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Juliana Cackovic, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Roshni Ganesan; No relevant relationships by Ranjit Nair, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Prashanth Reddy;
