N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Occupational and Environmental Lung Disease SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Dyspnea is one of the most common reasons for presentation to the hospital. Antigen mediated lung diseases are an important cause of dyspnea however due to the wide variation in clinical presentation, the large number of possible antigens, and a lack of diagnostic tests identification can be difficult.1 Thus, a high index of suspicion must be maintained to prevent missed or delayed diagnosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 44-year-old nonsmoker with an insignificant past medical history originally presented to an outpatient urgent care with an acute onset of fever, dyspnea, productive cough, and myalgias. He was empirically treated for bacterial pneumonia with an oral antibiotic. After initial improvement, his symptoms progressed and he ultimately presented to the emergency room with fever and worsening dyspnea. He was admitted with acute hypoxic respiratory failure and sepsis secondary to presumed post-influenza pneumonia (standard chest radiograph showed left lower lobe infiltrate). He was fluid resuscitated and received broad spectrum antibiotics. With antibiotics his fever dissipated although he continued to remain hypoxic and was discharged on hospital day seven on three liters of oxygen via nasal cannula. At hospital follow up one week later he continued to endorse dry cough and severe dyspnea (being only able to ambulate household distances with oxygen prior to having to rest). Exam was significant for crackles in the bilateral lung bases along with hypoxia (oxygen saturation dropped to 83% with removal of his nasal cannula during rest). Further history revealed the patient owned seven parakeets which were kept in the same room that the patient slept. He also endorsed unintentional 12-pound weight loss over the past 3 months. CT imaging was pursued which showed diffuse ill-defined centrilobular micronodularity and ground glass opacities throughout both lungs. Based upon this new information serum hypersensitivity pneumonitis screen was performed and ultimately returned negative for the presence of six antigens (only bird antigen on the screen was pigeon serum). Based upon the patient meeting five out of six clinical criteria for a validated clinical predication rule a diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis was established (92% Likelihood).2 He was advised to avoid further exposure to his birds and outpatient pulmonology consultation was arranged. After a short course of prednisone and avoiding further exposure the patient's symptoms resolved. DISCUSSION: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is an important disease to keep on the differential for a patient presenting with dyspnea. CONCLUSIONS: Early diagnosis is of the upmost importance as with avoiding further exposure early lung disease can be potentially completely reversed. This case also illustrates the importance of taking a complete history and how routine use of validated clinical predication rules can help you make a diagnosis. Reference #1: 1. Riario Sforza, G.G., Marinou, A. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis: a complex lung disease. Clin Mol Allergy. 2017; 15, 6. Reference #2: 2. Lacasse Y, Selman M, Costabel U, Dalphin JC, Ando M, Morell F, Erkinjuntti-Pekkanen R, Muller N, Colby TV, Schuyler M, Cormier Y. Clinical diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2003;168(8):952. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Catherine Cheney, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Chris Morris, source=Web Response
