N2  - SESSION TITLE: Medical Student/Resident Obstructive Lung Disease Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Mounier-Kuhn Syndrome (MKS), known as congenital tracheobronchomegaly, is a rare disease defined by marked dilation of the trachea and main bronchi. It includes a vast spectrum of clinical manifestations, frequently as recurrent lower respiratory tract infections, associated with bronchiectasis and emphysema. Our patient presented with recurring pyogenic pneumonia in the setting of progressive bronchiectasis and bullous emphysema. CASE PRESENTATION: A 41-year-old African-American male presented to the hospital with an acute four-day history of worsening dyspnea and cough with purulent sputum. His medical history includes recurrent pneumonia, chronic respiratory failure with continuous home oxygen supplementation (2 L/min), bronchiectasis, and end-stage bullous emphysema. On physical examination, he was tachypneic and hypoxic with bibasilar crackles. His lab findings demonstrated leukocytosis and mild lactic acidosis. His computed tomography (CT) of the chest with contrast demonstrated tracheal dilation, right apical bullous emphysema and bilateral upper and lower lobe cystic bronchiectasis. Previous serology showed normal alpha-1 antitrypsin levels, negative antinuclear antibodies, negative cystic fibrosis mutation, and normal immunoglobulins. He was admitted for acute exacerbation of bronchiectasis with pneumonia and treated with corticosteroids and directed antibiotics for Pseudomonas aeruginosa in sputum. He underwent bronchoscopy which showed severe tracheomalacia, severe dynamic expiratory airway collapse, and copious purulent secretions. Thus, he was diagnosed with MKS. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) subsequently grew atypical Mycobacterium Avium-Intracellulare (MAI). In the future, he will be evaluated for double-lung transplantation. DISCUSSION: Pathologically, there is a defect of longitudinal elastic tissue and thinning of underlying smooth muscles, resulting in weakened airways. This can lead to outpouching of the mucosa between the tracheal rings culminating in airway diverticulosis or cystic appearance with retention of secretions. This is the mechanism behind recurrent lower respiratory tract infections and bronchiectasis. In our case, initially, the exact etiology of bronchiectasis was elusive given normal lab testing, however characteristic imaging and bronchoscopy findings confirmed MKS. Presence of MAI, needing prolonged antimicrobial therapy, has led to an unexpected setback in future double-lung transplantation. Moreover, extreme airway distortions causing discrepancies in donor and recipient airway diameters can make the anastomoses during transplant challenging. CONCLUSIONS: Patients with MKS are prone to infections with life-threatening pathogens. Aggressive supportive therapy is recommended to prevent such consequences, but lung transplantation is required in some cases. Techniques to optimize the airway anastomoses during the transplant have led to successful outcomes. Reference #1: Shin M, Jackson R, Ho K. Tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome): CT diagnosis. American Journal of Roentgenology. 1988;150(4):777-779. doi:10.2214/ajr.150.4.777 Reference #2: Menon B, Aggarwal B, Iqbal A. Mounier-Kuhn syndrome: Report of 8 cases of tracheobronchomegaly with associated complications. South Med J. 2008;101(1):83–87 Reference #3: Drain AJ, Perrin F, Tasker A, et al. Double lung transplantation in a patient with tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome). J Heart Lung Transplant. 2006;25(1):134-136. doi:10.1016/j.healun.2005.06.018 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Batool Abuhalimeh, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Shubham Adroja, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Urbee Haque, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Randall Harris, source=Web Response
