N2  - Background: Airway mucus is an important barrier at respiratory epithelial surfaces that acts to maintain lung health despite exposure to billions of particulates and pathogens in a given day. MUC5AC and MUC5B are evolutionarily conserved genes that encode structurally related mucin glycoproteins, the principal secreted macromolecules in airway mucus. Studies in mouse models have shown that Muc5B expression affects inflammation and macrophage-mediated responses in the lung. However, the origin of Muc5B secretion in the large cartilaginous airways differs between mice and humans, since mice are largely devoid of airway submucosal glands that secrete Muc5B. To better understand the roles of MUC5B and MUC5AC in lung function and mucociliary clearance (MCC), we evaluated pulmonary function and tracheal MCC in MUC5AC-KO and MUC5B-KO ferrets, a species that closely mirrors the anatomy of the human lung and cystic fibrosis disease state. Methods: Ferrets underwent PFTs (pulmonary function tests) using a custom-designed flexiVent system (SciReq) specially designed for ferrets. Parameters included measures of lung volumes and capacities along with compliance of the respiratory system. Our data suggest that FEV0.4 in ferrets approximates FEV1 in humans as FEV0.4:FVC ratio is >0.7 in healthy wild-type controls. At the time of sacrifice, mucociliary clearance (MCC) was measured using fluorescent microbeads (1 μm) on freshly excised trachea under baseline and submucosal gland (SMG)-stimulated conditions (IBMX/forskolin and carbachol). Age, sex, and size-matched controls provided comparison. Results: Loss of MUC5AC resulted in a greater FEV0.4:FVC ratio when compared with matched controls (p=0.0346) and increased over time as MUC5AC-KO animals aged (age vs FEV0.4:FVC, p=0.037). Lung compliance was reduced in MUC5AC-KO ferrets, as determined by inspiratory capacity (IC, p=0.0346) and quasi-static compliance (Cst, p=0.0224). Ferrets lacking MUC5B lose lung function as they age, but the agedependent trend is not quite significant for FEV0.4:FVC over time (p=0.0543 vs controls). Trachea harvested from MUC5B-KO ferrets have accentuated non-goal-oriented motion that resulted in reduced MCC compared with age-matched controls under baseline and SMG-stimulated conditions (p<0.05 for both). MCC experiments in MUC5AC-KO trachea are pending while animals age for other studies. Discussion: MUC5AC and MUC5B are required to maintain pulmonary function in the ferret lung. MUC5B is required for mucociliary clearance of small particles and may lead to airflow obstruction as animals age. Loss of MUC5AC leads to reduced pulmonary compliance. Ferrets lacking MUC5AC and MUC5B are being crossed with G551D-CF ferrets to inform the roles of mucin subtypes in CF lung disease.
