N2  - Objectives: Culture-based microbiology is used to inform treatment of lung infection in CF by growing key pathogens, such as P. aeruginosa (PsA), on selective growth media. Molecular microbiology, based on PCR, is known to be more sensitive and detects a wider range of organisms in cross-sectional comparisons. Here we compared molecular- and culturebased microbiology on longitudinal respiratory samples from PsA-free paediatric and adult patients Methods: Paediatric (n=44) and adult patients (n=45) with mild CF provided samples at regular intervals over a period of 3 years. Patients with at least 6 samples (paediatric; n=20, adult; n=20) were included in this analysis. All patients were considered to be free of chronic PsA at study entry. High-throughput sequencing (HTS) of the 16S rRNA gene was compared to routine microbial culture performed on samples collected at the same time. Patients with ≥50% isolation of PsA were deemed to be chronically infected. Results: 40 patients (Table) were included, comparing 346 paired samples (242 sputum samples, 104 cough swabs). 28 patients had no PsA on clinical microbiology at any point (13 adult, 15 paediatric), 11 patients had intermittent infection (7 adult, 4 paediatric) and 1 patient (paediatric) had chronic PsA infection. In contrast, 16 patients (9 adult, 7 paediatric) had chronic PsA by PCR, 20 were intermittently infected (10 adult, 10 paediatric) and only 4 patients (1 adult, 3 paediatric) remained free of PsA infection throughout. PCR sequencing also found Burkholderia spp as a chronic colonisation in 13 paediatric patients, and intermittently in 14 adult patients (none isolated by culture) and high rates of H. influenzae infection in both adults (5% chronic, 80% intermittent), and paediatric patients (45% chronic, 55% intermittent). Conclusion: HTS identified substantially greater levels of infection with PsA, Burkholderia spp and H. influenzae than conventional microbiological culture. Based on PCR, 16 patients (9 adults, 7 paediatric) with mild disease would be reclassified from non-PsA to chronically infected, and 13 patients (6 adults, 7 paediatric) would be reclassified from non-PsA tointermittently infected. This has significant implications for patient segregation and treatment options. Table.
