N2  - Introduction: Staphylococcus aureus is a common nosocomial pathogen in the environment that can survive on abiotic surfaces for prolonged periods of time. For patients with CF, S. aureus is frequently recovered from used nebulizers, suggesting the possibility of re-exposure with subsequent aerosolized treatments. Therefore, we studied S. aureus surface interactions with drying and rewetting to understand the risk of bacterial dispersion during inhaled therapies with contaminated nebulizers. Methods: Clinical strains of S. aureus were inoculated to a PARI LC Plus nebulizer, followed by aerosolization of albuterol into a Next Generation Impactor (NGI). Bacteria were then cultured from collection cups containing different aerosolized particle sizes. To quantify the dispersal ability of these strains in defined environments, we use confocal microscopy (Nikon W1) to quantify the drying pattern and detachment of these strains from a glass surface. Results: Preliminary data from microscopy demonstrate two CF clinical strains of S. aureus (CF07 and CF46) have low numbers of cells remaining on glass after in vitro rewetting (28%, and 0.02% remaining cells, respectively). In separate experiments these strains dispersed from the nebulizer surface into the NGI (Table). In contrast, for S. aureus strain CF05 which shows 88% of remaining cells, we observe no dispersal into the NGI, demonstrating a correlation between the in vitro detachment assay and the NGI quantification. These data together suggest that strains with weaker attachment to surfaces tend to detach with rewetting as occurs during nebulized therapy. Examination of additional strains is ongoing and bacterial properties that favor attachment or dispersion are ongoing. Conclusions: A subset of S. aureus clinical isolates do not adhere well to abiotic surfaces during drying and are easily dispersed when subjected to sheer stress. This implies patients who re-use S. aureus-contaminated nebulizers have increased exposure to bacteria in solution during therapy. Staphylococcus aureus Dispersion.
