N2  - Background: Induced sputum (IS) is a microbiological sampling technique that is being increasingly adopted in clinical practice for children with cystic fibrosis (CF). In comparison to other routine sampling techniques, IS is less invasive than bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and has been shown by culture methods to outperform cough swabs (CS) for pathogen detection. Here, we evaluate the performance of IS against BAL using microbiota analysis. Methods: 16S rRNA gene sequencing and analysis was performed on 30 matched sets of BAL-IS samples from paediatric patients, collected as part of the CF-SpIT study (UKCRN14615; ISRCTNR12473810). IS microbiota diversity and composition were evaluated against one-lobe, two-lobe and six-lobe BAL. Results: IS samples had higher bacterial diversity than BAL samples, but regional differences in the lung microbiota were seen dependent on the number of lobes sampled, which suggested compartmentalisation of the lower airway microbiota in certain patients. There was overlap between the microbiota of different sample types, with BAL detecting higher abundances of “typical” CF-associated genera and IS identifying higher levels of “non-typical” CF genera. On average, IS detected 83.4% of the top 5 and 79.1% of the top 10 genera captured by six-lobe BAL. The sensitivity of IS to detect specific CF pathogens present on six-lobe BAL at relative abundance >5% varied between 46-100%, with negative predictive values between 75-100%. IS also identified CF pathogens not detected on concurrent BAL samples. Conclusions: Microbiota analysis validated that IS samples both the upper and lower respiratory tract and can capture pathogens within lung compartments not identified by BAL.
