N2  - Rationale: Hyper-viscous, pathologically concentrated respiratory mucus is a hallmark of cystic fibrosis (CF) airway disease. We recently demonstrated that mucins are elevated in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) in preschool-aged CF patients (1), with roughly half of the mucins found in non-swelling gels termed mucus flakes. To establish mucin concentration and mucus flakes as biomarkers of CF airway disease progression, we measured the biophysical and biochemical properties of airway mucus flakes from subjects with CF over an age and disease-severity spectrum relative to healthy controls. Methods: BALF was collected from 11 healthy adults (age 29.8 ±7.6, FEV1% 91.5 ± 8.3), 46 preschool-aged CF patients at a time of clinical stability (age 3.3 ± 1.7, [1]) and 37 children with CF with more advanced disease undergoing clinically indicated bronchoscopy (age 10.5 ± 4.4 years, FEV1% 78.9 ± 14.5). Mucin and DNA concentrations were measured by refractometry and PicoGreen assay respectively. The MUC5B/MUC5AC ratio and PRR4 (marker of glandular mucus) were measured by immunohistochemical staining. The biophysical properties of mucus flakes were measured by particle tracking rheology, and flake size and coverage area (a proxy measurement for volume fraction) were measured by tilling fluorescence microscopy. Infection was defined as growth of pathogens >10,000 CFU/mL. Results: In healthy individuals, mucin and DNA concentrations were low, and mucus flakes were sparse. In preschool children with CF, mucins and DNA concentrations were increased relative to control, with a higher abundance of mucus flakes. In more advanced CF airway disease, mucin and DNA concentrations were further increased with increased flake size and coverage area. Further, mucus flakes in these samples had decreased MUC5B/MUC5AC ratio in immunohistochemical stains and decreased staining of PRR4, indicating that mucus flakes arise from the superficial epithelium rather than glands. The abundance of mucus flakes and their rheology correlated with neutrophil counts. The size and coverage area of flakes from these subjects showed a bimodal distribution, with samples from subjects with active airway infection having more, larger mucus flakes relative to uninfected samples. Conclusions: Mucin and DNA concentrations increase with disease progression and severity across the three cohorts, as does the amount, size, and rheology of mucus flakes. We hypothesize that flakes are part of normal airway physiology that are exaggerated by heightened mucus concentrations, altered mucus composition, inflammation, and infections that are associated with CF airway disease.
