N2  - Background: There are no approved therapies to treat chronic pulmonary S. aureus (including MRSA) in CF, which is a concern since it affects approximately 25% of patients and is associated with shortened life expectancy (1,2). We are developing RV94, a lipoglycopeptide therapy designed as an inhaled dry powder for once daily dosing, effective against bacteria in planktonic, intracellular, and biofilm stores, the latter of which have been linked to clinical relapse (3,4). RV94 is designed to demonstrate superior PK/PD for the treatment of pulmonary MRSA when compared to approved conventional antibiotics and lipoglycopeptides. Methods: RV94 was prepared by modifying vancomycin using standard chemistry techniques. MRSA ATCC 1556 (USA300) was used in intracellular, biofilm, and in vivo efficacy testing models. In vitro susceptibility testing versus planktonic MRSA was done by broth microdilution. Activity versus MRSA in protected stores was measured intracellularly in THP-1-derived macrophages and in biofilms using the standard MBEC™ assay. RV94 was formulated (90% by weight) into a dry powder for inhalation and PK evaluation of a single dose was performed on healthy rats using a nose-only inhalation chamber equipped with a Vilnius aerosol generator. Lung and plasma drug levels were measured using LC-MS/MS. In vivo efficacy studies were performed on rats made neutropenic with i.p. cyclophosphamide at 4 and 1 day prior to infection. MRSA challenge was administered intranasally at 8log10 CFU/mL. Single-dose treatments were administered by inhalation either prophylactically up to 7 days before challenge or therapeutically 24 h after challenge with necropsy at 48 h post-challenge for enumeration of lung titer. Results: A median MRSA MIC (12 isolates) for RV94 was 0.015 μg/mL, making it 60-fold more potent than vancomycin and 2-4-fold more potent than the lipoglycopeptides oritavancin and telavancin. RV94 demonstrated superior MRSA killing intracellularly which was 4-6-fold more potent than vancomycin, linezolid, and tigecycline. Against standard MRSA biofilms, RV94 was 10-fold more potent than vancomycin. In a single-dose PK experiment, RV94's lung t1/2 was 3.5 days and its AUC0-21 day was 800 μgday/g with nanogram levels measured in the plasma. When administered prophylactically and therapeutically in an acute efficacy model in rats, a single inhaled dose of RV94 yielded a statistically significant reduction in bacterial lung burden versus air sham control. Conclusions: RV94 inhalation powder is a new therapeutic being developed for chronic MRSA in CF that has demonstrated superior preclinical activity in comparison to conventional antibiotics against bacteria in planktonic and protected stores. PK/PD results in rodents support a convenient and effective pulmonary MRSA therapy with once daily (or less frequent) dosing that yields minimal systemic exposure.
