N2  - Introduction: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the main contributor to cystic fibrosis (CF) patient morbidity and mortality. Chronic infection in the form of biofilms is particularly difficult to eradicate due to protections by CF mucus and the biofilm matrix. The composition of the biofilm matrix largely determines its viscoelastic properties and decreases clearance by the innate immune response and conventional antibiotics. Nitric oxide (NO) is an endogenously produced antimicrobial agent that can both degrade the physical structure of the biofilm and simultaneously eradicate colonies. Exogenous NO can be delivered using macromolecular scaffolds such as cyclodextrin (CD), and NO-releasing CD has previously demonstrated broad-spectrum antibacterial action and a favorable cytotoxicity profile. The effects of NO exposure on biofilm mechanical properties and viability were compared to that of the conventional antibiotics, tobramycin and colistin. Methods: The antibiofilm efficacy of NO-releasing CD against P. aeruginosa biofilms grown in a variety of media was compared to that of tobramycin and colistin using parallel plate macrorheology, particle tracking microrheology, and colony counting. The growth media included tryptic soy broth (TSB), TSB supplemented with mucins (TSB+), artificial sputum media (ASM), and TSB supplemented with CF sputum filtrates (SFM). The concentrations of polysaccharides, proteins, and DNA in each biofilm matrix were quantified with standardized colorimetric and fluorometric assays. Results: Biofilms grown in DNA-containing media (ie, ASM and SFM) exhibited greater macroscopic viscoelastic moduli compared to those grown in TSB or TSB+. On a microrheological scale, biofilm rheology was statistically indistinguishable. The macroscopic viscoelastic moduli correlated with susceptibility to tobramycin and colistin, with biofilms grown in ASM demonstrating the greatest macroscopic viscoelastic moduli and greatest resistance to tobramycin and colistin. In contrast NO-releasing CD eradicated all biofilms at the same nontoxic concentration. Conclusions: Cyclodextrins have an outer diameter of >2 nm, which may facilitate improved biofilm penetration and localized delivery of NO. The large dose of NO delivered by CD is capable of degrading the polymeric components of the biofilm matrix and eradicating interior colonies. Tobramycin and colistin electrostatically interact with polysaccharides and DNA in the biofilm matrix, rendering them less effective. Superior antibiofilm action of NO-releasing CD may be attributable to the small size of both CD and NO.
