N2  - Background: Few institutions have established and evaluated a standardized approach for the treatment of Mycobacterium abscessus (MAB) infections in children with cystic fibrosis (CF). There are limited data on the safety and tolerability of clofazimine and inhaled liposomal amikacin (ILA) in children, including in combination with CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) modulator therapy. Methods: At a large CF pediatric center located in Houston, Texas, we established a multidisciplinary Non-Tuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM) working group of pediatric pulmonologists, infectious disease physicians, and clinical pharmacists to develop, implement and monitor a standardized approach for the treatment of MAB in children with CF. Treatment included an initiation phase of intravenous (IV) and oral drugs (> 2 (IV) antibiotics for > 2 months) followed by a prolonged continuation phase (including clofazimine and ILA). Antimicrobials administered were chosen based on MAB susceptibility testing. Treatment protocol enrollment was determined by the NTM working group and occurred during widespread use of CFTR modulator therapy. All children were monitored for medication side effects. Clinical, microbiologic, laboratory and radiologic outcomes were determined by medical record abstraction. Results: Seven children were enrolled in the MAB treatment protocol of whom 6 (85.7%) have completed > 6 months of treatment. Three (42.8%) were previously treated for MAB. The median age was 13.1 years (range, 9.9 - 17.5 years). Five (71.4%) were started on CFTR modulator therapy. At enrollment, 7 children had had antibiotic-refractory respiratory symptoms of: shortness of breath (2), increased cough/change in sputum production (7), significant decline in FEV1 (6), hemoptysis (1). Four children had systemic symptoms of: fever/chills (3), weight loss (3), and night sweats (2). All children had radiographic changes on advanced CT imaging (treein- bud [7/7, 100%] or inflammatory nodules [5/7, 71.4%]). Five children (71.4%) experienced improvement in their respiratory symptoms: resolution of shortness of breath (1), improved cough or sputum production (4), gradual improvement in FEV1 (5), resolution of systemic symptoms (4); (4/5 received a CFTR modulator). Five children (71.4%) had follow-up advanced CT scans; 3 (60%) had radiologic improvement; (2/3 received a CFTR modulator). Four children (57.1%) achieved microbiologic clearance of MAB; (2/4 received a CFTR modulator). Three children experienced side effects leading to a discontinuation or modification of aminoglycoside therapy including: acute kidney injury (3, 42.9%), high-frequency hearing loss (2, 28.6%), progressive hearing loss (1, 14.3%), and tinnitus (3, 42.9%). No child experienced serious side effects due to clofazimine. Conclusions: We successfully developed and implemented a standardized approach to the treatment of MAB in children with CF. Most children experienced clinical or radiologic improvement and over half (57%) experienced microbiologic clearance. Drug toxicity occurred with aminoglycoside therapy. Clofazimine was safe and well tolerated. It is speculated that treatment outcomes were positively impacted by co-administration of CFTR modulator therapy.
