N2  - The high frequency of certain CFTR mutations has suggested robust heterozygote advantage against an unidentified pathogen historically present in Europe. Organisms such as V. cholerae, Y. pestis, and M. tuberculosis have been implicated as causal selective pressures based on mechanistic or epidemiologic data - albeit with conflicting results. Tuberculosis was proposed as the most likely candidate for mutant CFTR enrichment based on the 17th century tuberculosis (TB) epidemic, together with historical and geographic data (Poolman and Galvani. 2007). A more recent study of tuberculosis in Brazil suggested protection among subjects with one copy of mutant CFTR (Bosch, et al. 2017). The current study was designed to further investigate a possible relationship between TB and cystic fibrosis carrier status. We obtained DNA samples from 129 individuals with active pulmonary tuberculosis diagnosed in Tbilisi, Georgia. Adult patients were enrolled between July 2009 and April 2012 from the National Center for Tuberculosis and Lung Diseases in Tbilisi as consecutive subjects. Eligibility was based on symptoms, signs, positive acid-fast bacillus smear and mycobacterial sputum culture. Relevant exclusion criteria included a previous diagnosis of tuberculosis and evidence of extrapulmonary mycobacterial infection. The spectrum and frequency of CFTR mutations vary among different populations within Europe. Although there are no reports defining the most frequent CFTR mutations in Tbilisi, 1677delTA (c.1545-1546delTA; p.Tyr515X) has been established as a very common allele (81.3%), followed by E92K (c.274G>A; p.Glu92Lys, 12.5%) among Chechen CF patients in a proximate geographic area (Petrova NV, et al. BMC Med Genet. 2019). We therefore investigated 1677delTA carrier frequency among patients with pulmonary tuberculosis in Tbilisi. Genomic DNA was purified from blood using the EZNA DNA kit (Omega Bio-tek), and digital droplet PCR (ddPCR) performed to evaluate 1677delTA (SNP ID; rs121908776). TaqMan SNP genotyping probe set (catalog no: 4351379, ID: C-151693870-20, Thermo Fisher) was used with droplet digital PCR (Biorad), and specificity of TaqMan probes validated/ optimized using a plasmid harboring CFTR cDNA encoding 1677delTA. We identified three 1677delTA carriers among the 129 subjects with TB. Based on these data, power analysis indicates a sample size of >2000 would be necessary to demonstrate a protective effect of the 1677delTA mutation for this patient population. The results provide evidence against a selective advantage mediated by the 1677delTA mutation specifically with regard to clinically significant pulmonary tuberculosis.
