N2  - INTRODUCTION: Pyogenic liver abscess (PLA) is a life-threatening condition. It is commonly resulted from biliary infection. But, less frequently, it can be spread from bowel infection. Here, we will report a case of PLA secondary to sigmoid diverticulitis with atypical symptom. CASE DESCRIPTION/METHODS: A 60 year-old male presented to hospital with 1 week of fever, and 3 days of cough. No abdominal pain was reported. Physical exam was unremarkable except temperature was 102 Fahrenheit. Lab data was significant for leukocytosis of 21,000/uL. With the suspicion of 'pneumonia', a Chest CT was ordered. a large left hepatic lobe lesion was noted. Further study with CT of abdomen and pelvis found a 10×8.4 cm intrahepatic lesion which was concerned for abscess, and sigmoid diverticulitis (Figure 1). Patient was treated with Piperacillin/Tazobactam empirically. A catheter drainage was placed. The abscess culture grew Streptococcus Intermedius. The antibiotic was changed to ceftriaxone and metronidazole based on the susceptibility. Patient was discharged home with the drainage and total of 14 days intravenous antibiotic. The drainage was removed in 7 days after he completed the antibiotic treatment. Follow up CT scan showed resolution of abscess and improvement of diverticulitis in 4 weeks (Figure 2). He had colonoscopy in 6 weeks, only diverticulosis was found. DISCUSSION: PLA is relatively uncommon in North America [1]. Clinical presentation includes fever and rigors, nausea and vomiting, right upper quadrant pain, anorexia, weight loss, weakness and malaise. But our patient presented with fever and cough, which may be due to the diaphragmatic irritation. With regard to liver biochemistry, an elevated ALP is observed in up to 90% of cases [2]. Escherichia coli and Klebsiella. pneumoniae are the most common pathogens. But our patient's abscess culture grew Streptococcus Intermedius which is a normal flora of oropharynx, and gastrointestinal tract [1]. Treatment of PLA composes of abscess drainage and antibiotics. Drainage with either needle aspiration or catheter placement if the abscess size is less than 5cm. But drainage with catheter placement is preferable if it is greater than 5cm [3]. Empiric broad spectrum parenteral antibiotics should be administered until the culture result is available. A follow up colonoscopy is needed to if the PLA is associated with diverticulitis. Overall, our patient recovered well from the infection, no malignancy was identified.
