N2  - Introduction: Chronic cough, a frequent referral to paediatric respiratory service, may indicate underlying disease. Since the introduction of new-born screening for cystic fibrosis, no studies have evaluated the incidence of growth faltering in children referred for chronic cough. Hypothesis: i. Children referred for chronic cough will not grow at the rate expected from parental growth parameters ii. Growth faltering will indicate presence of underlying chronic disease. Methods: As part of an ongoing prospective cohort study, children referred for chronic cough to a tertiary centre from February 2019 were recruited. Anthropometric measures for children and their parents were converted to standard deviation scores (SDS). Expected height SDS was calculated (1) and compared to the observed height SDS using paired t-tests. This analysis was repeated in a subgroup with protracted bacterial bronchitis (PBB). Results: We evaluated 35 children with chronic cough (mean age 4.04, 24 (68.6%) male), of whom 15 had PBB. One with bronchiectasis was excluded from analysis. The observed height SDS was significantly lower than expected in these children (p<0.05), especially in those with PBB (p=0.01) (Table). Conclusion: Children referred for chronic cough showed significantly poorer growth, which was more marked in children with PBB. Moreover, PBB was more likely in those with chronic cough who had poor growth. Reference: 1. Wright et al. Arch Dis Child 81(3): 257-260.
