N2  - Introduction: A 35-year-old man was referred to the Rhode Island Cystic Fibrosis Center following urology evaluation for infertility. The patient described decades of symptoms suggestive of Cystic Fibrosis (CF) without prior workup, and on further testing was confirmed to have severe systemic disease with Pseudomonas colonization. This case report describes hallmark characteristics of CF and highlights the importance of adult providers maintaining a degree of suspicion for this disease. This patient's significant disease burden at the time of diagnosis may have been avoided, or at least delayed, by earlier identification and initiation of treatment. Case: The patient presented after urologic evaluation for infertility revealed an absent vas deferens, prompting evaluation for CF given the known association between the disease and anatomic variant. He described ten years of daily cough, which was worse in the morning and productive of yellow-green mucus, as well as shortness of breath with ambulation. He reported five separate diagnoses of pneumonia over the past three years, for which he was treated with antibiotics but had no further testing. In addition, he had lifelong recurrent sinusitis with multiple prior polypectomies. The patient had always been thin and struggled to gain weight despite high fat and high calorie diets, which had been attributed to his known diagnosis of inflammatory bowel disease. Despite these symptoms, CF had not been previously considered in this patient. Pulmonary Function Testing (PFTs) completed during his initial visit demonstrated a severe reduction in Forced Expiratory Volume (FEV1), 64% of predicted, as well as a decreased Forced Vital Capacity (FVC), 82% of predicted. Spirometry did not demonstrate an improvement in response to bronchodilators, which would be seen in an asthma-like process. A sputum culture obtained at this visit was positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a common pathogen in the sputum of patients with CF, particularly with disease progression. Discussion: While most individuals with Cystic Fibrosis are diagnosed in infancy or childhood, according to the national CF Foundation Patient Registry, slightly less than 10% of CF patients are diagnosed in adulthood . Available data suggests that these 'late diagnosis' patients often have better lung function and decreased prevalence of pancreatic insufficiency, although they continue to have the same trajectory towards compromised lung function later in life and benefit from initiation of CF-directed therapy. This case demonstrates an adult male with multiple symptoms consistent with a diagnosis of Cystic Fibrosis, yet for whom the diagnosis was not considered until he had nearly pathognomonic findings on an infertility workup. The combination of nasal polyps, frequent pneumonias or lower respiratory tract infections, and difficulty with weight gain are symptoms which, when taken together, should prompt suspicion for Cystic Fibrosis at any age. Chest CT demonstrating bronchial wall thickening, bronchiectasis, and multiple areas of inspissated mucus in the airways.
