N2  - Microbiology samples are central aspects of selecting targeted antibiotic therapy to treat pulmonary exacerbations in CF. We evaluated a novel non-invasive approach using face-mask-sampling (FMS). The clinical utility of FMS in pulmonary tuberculosis has been demonstrated (1) and we hypothesized that this approach would detect lower respiratory tract pathogens at similar rates to sputum or broncho-alveolar lavage (BAL). Objectives: 1. To assess feasibility of FMS in children. 2. To evaluate diagnostic capabilities and processes to maximise yield. 3. To compare with conventional sampling. Methods: Prospective study of participants with stable disease (single sample) or with pulmonary exacerbations (samples at initiation, midway and end of IV therapy) wore duck-bill surgical masks fitted with a 3D printed sampling matrix (SM) for 15–30 minutes. Exposed SM was dissolved, cells pelleted and DNA extracted then subjected to PCR to determine bacterial load (16SrDNA) and specific non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) pathogen content. Wherever possible, cough swabs, sputum and BAL were also obtained. Results: 92 masks were obtained from 39 CF children (aged 4–17 years), 66 during exacerbations and 26 during stable state. Bacterial pathogens were detected in 5/91cough swabs, 16/55 sputum samples and 1/2 BAL samples. The masks revealed bacterial burdens from 102 to 105 16S copies per sample. Lower yield noted during delayed processing method was rectified with immediate processing. DNAse activation in dissolved SM may explain this. Four sputa were positive for NTM. NTM was detected in one paired mask. Conclusions: FMS is feasible in children with CF. Clinical utility will require extended studies relating FMS results to clinical outcomes, preferably in patients with a higher frequency of samples positive by conventional analyses. Further testing to optimise bacterial yield and to investigate isolation of other CF pathogens from FMS is planned.
