N2  - Objectives: Culture-based microbiology is used to inform treatment of lung infection in CF by growing key pathogens, such as P. aeruginosa (Pa), on selective growth media. Molecular microbiology, based on PCR, is known to be more sensitive and detects a wider range of organisms in cross-sectional comparisons. Here we compared molecular- and culture-based microbiology on longitudinal respiratory samples from Pa-free paediatric patients. Methods: 44 paediatric patients with mild CF provided samples at regular intervals over a period of 3 years. Patients with at least 7 samples were included in this analysis. All patients were considered free of chronic Pa at study entry. High-throughput sequencing (HTS) of the 16S rRNA gene was compared to routine microbial culture performed on samples collected at the same time. Patients with ≥50% isolation of Pa were deemed to be chronically infected. Results: 17 patients (9M/8F) were included, average age 10.4yrs and mean FEV1 98.7% predicted. Median follow-up was 850 days (2.3 years). 103 paired samples were compared, (40 cough swabs and 63 sputum samples). 14 patients (82%) had no Pa on clinical micro at any point, 1 patient had intermittent infection, and 2 patients had chronic Pa infection. In contrast, 6 patients (35%) had chronic Pa by PCR, 7 were intermittently infected, and only 4 (24%) remained free of Pa throughout. PCR sequencing also isolated Burkholderia spp. as a chronic colonisation in 2 patients (none isolated by culture) and high rates of H. influenzae infection (58% chronic, 30% intermittent). Conclusion: HTS identified substantially greater levels of infection with Pa, Burkholderia spp. and H. influenzae than conventional microbiological culture. Based on PCR, 10/17 (59%) of children with mild disease in a non-Pa clinic would be re-classified. This has significant implications for patient segregation and treatment options. Analysis of a corresponding longitudinal adult population is ongoing.
