N2  - Objectives: Locate the first time point of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) in the respiratory tract of infants with cystic fibrosis (CF), identify bacterial, viral or environmental patterns influencing an early detection, and evaluate the practical feasibility of culture-independent sequencing tools as potential routine diagnostics. Methods: Cough swabs were collected from healthy (n = 52) and CF-diagnosed children (n = 48) up to the age of six. Longitudinal sampling of twelve CF patients still continues, starting from the date of diagnosis (newborn screening), ending with the first detection of Pa. High-resolution shotgun metagenomic sequencing and a reference-based assembly approach were applied for taxonomic and functional investigations. Results: Pa was detected in 24% of CF-infants and in 21% of healthy children with the youngest child being three weeks old. In all cases, Pa was part of the rare biosphere and did not affect alpha diversity of abundant or rare species. A hierarchical clustering method revealed three profiles of microbial community composition. The first two groups were similar, including diseased and healthy children with and without Pa. The third group appeared separate during cluster analysis, containing only healthy and PA-negative children. The latter profile was characterised by an increased bacterial load of abundant (epsilon-squared effect size (e2) = 0.57) and rare species (e2 = 0.39), as well as increased alpha diversity of rare species (e2 = 0.20) and more rare species in general (e2 = 0.21). Conclusion: Healthy and diseased infants come in contact with Pa early on. The presence of Pa as a rare species in the respiratory tract is not necessarily disease-associated. A Pa-negative microbial signature was identified, which was characterised by increased bacterial load of abundant and rare species, increased alpha diversity and number of rare species, contributing to the functional diversity of the respiratory tract microbiome.
