N2  - Introduction: Currently (January 2020) there are 2075 mutations listed in CFTR1 database. However, the clinical consequences of the mutations should be sought in CFTR2 database which contains only 346 mutations classified as CF-causing. Due to the increasing number of mutations and insufficient knowledge about correlation between the genotype and phenotype, there is an urgent need to determine which mutations are pathogenic, non-pathogenic or lead to variable clinical consequences. Aims: The aim of this study is to characterise the clinical outcome of CF patients with 13 novel or rare mutations not included in CFTR2 database. Materials and methods: The study group included 13 patients born between September 2006 and May 2019, who had positive sweat tests (>60 mml/l) and clinical symptoms of cystic fibrosis or at least positive result of CF NBS (Newborn Screening). All patients had two CFTR mutations identified, including at least one mutation not listed in CFTR 2 database. Results: We identified 13 CF-causing mutations H199R, R289NfsX17, I618Rfs2, L468P, A1217E, Q359R, T682Kfs40, S1347PfsX13, T1036I, W356X, E33X, dup.16,17A and W1282R. The diagnosed patients presented with a wide spectrum of clinical symptoms. In one patient sweat tests values were initially borderline, but in subsequent tests they become positive. Thus, sweat tests were positive in all patients. During our follow-up, most of children presented respiratory symptoms. The most common were cough and exacerbation of bronchopulmonary disease. One patient was treated for atelectasis of the upper right lobe before the age of three months, despite the early start of physiotherapy. We reported positive pharyngeal swab for P. aeruginosa in 4 patients. Conclusions: Based on the clinical phenotype we concluded that all described 13 CFTR mutations are CF-causing. This observation points that each patient with a mutation of unknown clinical consequences in one CFTR allele requires attentive follow-up.
