N2  - TYPE: Abstract Publication TOPIC: Chest Infections PURPOSE: An outbreak of measles among the indigenous group of North East Malaysia known as the Batek Tribe of Kelantan was declared in June 2019 involving 120 patients. We describe the clinical presentation and outcome of hospitalized patients. METHODS: This was a retrospective cohort study undertaken in two district hospitals in Kelantan. Hospitalized patients diagnosed with measles during the outbreak were identified via notification line listing and hospital registration. 30 medical records of confirmed measles cases either via measles serology and/or PCR >12 years old admitted from June 1 to August 31 2019 were retrieved. Relevant data were extracted and analysed. RESULTS: Majority presented with fever and cough. Conjunctivitis was reported in 11 patients. Rash was detected in only 5 patients and Koplik spots were not reported in any patients. Most patients developed complications involving the respiratory system including pneumonia, pleural effusion, pneumothorax, tuberculosis reactivation, and mediastinum abscess. There was high prevalence of iron deficiency anaemia and hypoalbuminaemia. We postulated that this reflected poor nutritional status of this cohort, and in turn conferred a higher rate of complications due to immunosuppression. Care was mainly supportive including antipyretics, fluids and treatment of bacterial superinfections. All patients received oral vitamin A 200,000 IU. Two patients required ICU care and mechanical ventilation and 1 patient developed acute kidney injury requiring hemodialysis. All patients survived and were discharged well. CONCLUSIONS: Measles lead to immunosuppression and a myriad of pulmonary complications. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Public health efforts to reach indigenous people for healthcare awareness and education regarding preventable disease via vaccination is of utmost importance. DISCLOSURE: No significant relationships. KEYWORDS: Pneumonia, measles, secondary infection
