N2  - Background and Objectives: Procalcitonin may distinguish bacterial from viral infection. Procalcitonin is a continuous value, but is used as a binary value with an arbitrary threshold defining positivity. Interval likelihood ratios may offer an elegant statistic for applying biomarkers. Herein we retrospectively reviewed adult inpatients with a procalcitonin level and calculated interval likelihood ratios to evaluate the utility of procalcitonin in predicting bacterial illness. Methods: We performed a retrospective chart review and collected data from January 2017 to January 2019 at our tertiary care hospital. Inclusion criteria: all patients for whom procalcitonin was measured and outcomes tracked during inpatient stay by electronic medical record. Serious Bacterial Infection (SBI): a culture positive for a non-contaminant bacterial pathogen in blood, urine, sputum, or other body fluid, or any pneumonia patients with a lobar consolidation on chest x-ray with cough, with or without a positive culture, or bacterial urinary tract infection in patients presenting with dysuria, >5 wbc/HPF on urinalysis, with or without a positive culture. Data were reported as medians with Interquartile ranges (IQR 25%, 75%) or percentages with 95% Confidence Intervals (95%CI). Procalcitonin Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (ROC) and Interval Likelihood Ratios (ILR) was calculated as a predictor of SBI. Comparisons were by Fisher's Exact Test (alpha = 0.05, all 2-tails). Results: 544 patients were included in our study, with a median age 65.0 yrs. (50, 79.1 yrs.) 48.5% male with a SBI prevalence of 27.9% (95%CI, 24.3%- 31.9%). Patients with SBI vs. non-SBI had no significant differences in Mortality (18.4% vs. 17.1%, p=0.707) or ICU admission (32.9% vs. 29.1% p=0.406) rates. Statistically significant (p=0.024) higher rates of vasopressor use were found in SBI 30.3% (95%CI, 23.5%-35%) vs. non-SBI 21% (95%CI, 16.9%-25%) patients. Hospital initiated dialysis was more common (p=0.010) in SBI 35.5% (95%CI, 28.3%-43.4%) vs. non-SBI 24.2% (95%CI, 20.3%-28.7%) patients. Procalcitonin predicted SBI poorly, ROC produced an Area Under the Curve 0.60 (95%CI, 0.54-0.65) (p<0.001). Testing ILR did not define any procalcitonin ILR > 2.0. Conclusion: Procalcitonin interval likelihood ratios did not identify patients at a higher risk of serious bacterial infections.
