N2  - Objective To study the clinical characteristics of senile influenza patients in influenza epidemic season and provide evidence for the better prevention and treatment. Methods Clinical data of patients who were diagnosed with influenza from January to March 2019 were collected, including general information, underlying diseases, clinical symptoms, laboratory results. Nasopharyngeal swabs were collected for detection of influenza nucleic acid. Results A total of 64 old patients (65 years or older) with influenza and 39 non-old patients were collected. All patients did not receive influenza vaccine. Overall, 55 (85.9%) old patients had influenza A alone. Fever (≥38.5 ℃) was recorded in 65.6% of the old patients. Other symptoms were cough (95.2%), sputum (87.5%), fatigue (84.4%), chest tightness (46.9%), and runny nose (45.3%). Additionally, 46 (71.9%) patients were complicated with myocardial injury and 40 (62.5%) complicated with bacterial pneumonia. Laboratory tests showed that the medians (interquartile range) of C-reactive protein 38.5(23.4, 89.1) mg/L and procalcitonin 0.09 (0.05, 0.68) μg/L were slightly elevated. The median time to negative nucleic acid conversion was 6.0 (5.0, 8.0) days. A total of 6 (9.4%) patients died. Old patients had more severe clinical symptoms, complications, and laboratory test results than non-old patients. The old patients with fatal outcome had significantly higher risk of bacterial pneumonia, prior organ dysfunction, multiple organ failure, sepsis, and acid-base imbalance (P＜0.05). Conclusions The influenza vaccination coverage is still low at present time. Elderly influenza patients are in serious condition and prone to complications such as myocardial injury and bacterial pneumonia. Clinicians should pay attention to the organ functions of elderly patients in case of influenza and take comprehensive preventive measures to avoid death.
