N2  - Objective To analyze the clinical characteristics of pulmonary infection in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis and provide evidence for early prevention and clinical diagnosis and treatment. Methods The data of 357 patients smear-positive for tuberculosis treated in the Affiliated Hospital of Southwest Medical University from July 2016 to July 2019 were retrospectively analyzed to identify microbial infections other than tuberculosis. The pulmonary imaging findings, clinical characteristics, pathogenic bacteria distribution, and relevant risk factors for infection were examined. Results The common clinical manifestations included moderate to high fever, cough, purulent sputum, and atypical chest imaging findings in case of tuberculosis complicated with other pulmonary infection. The prevalence of colonizing bacteria (mainly Candida albicans) was high. The most common bacterial pathogen was K. pneumoniae. Hemoptysis, severe pulmonary tuberculosis, concomitant chronic lung disease, serum albumin ＜40 g/ L, body mass index (BMI) ＜18.5 kg/m2, and duration of antibiotic use ＞2 weeks were independent risk factors for other pulmonary infection in tuberculosis patients. Conclusions The clinical picture and chest imaging of pulmonary infection are not specific in tuberculosis patients. Early empirical antimicrobial therapy targeting gram negative bacteria is beneficial for controlling infection as soon as possible. Adequate tuberculosis control, nutritional support, and maintaining ideal body weight may be helpful to reduce other pulmonary infections.
