N2  - Rationale. Ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP) is a frequent nosocomial infection in Intensive Care Units and the problem is compounded by antibiotic resistance. There is a need for alternative strategies to combat and to prevent these types of infections. We have developed a rat model that mimics VAP, and recently highlighted the efficacy of bacteriophage therapy for the treatment of pneumonia due to methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) [1]. In the current study, we assessed the potential for bacteriophage prophylaxis in this experimental context. Methods. Nebulized phages were delivered using a modified vibrating mesh aerosol drug delivery system (Aerogen Solo technology, USA). Rats were treated with a cocktail of 4 distinct bacteriophages at 1 of 2 concentrations prior to ventilation; either 1010 plaque forming units (PFU, C1), or 1011 PFU (C2). Following 4 hours of ventilation, rats were infected with 1010 colony forming units (CFU) of MRSA strain AW7 via the endotracheal tube. Animals were monitored for up to 96 hours after infection and survival was assessed. At the time of euthanasia, the lungs and spleen were harvested and both bacterial and phage loads were determined. Results. Nebulised bacteriophages displayed good bioavailability in the lungs of uninfected animals (4x106 PFU/g for C1 treated rats, and 9x106 PFU/g for C2 treated rats). Rats that received bacteriophage prophylaxis prior to ventilation and MRSA challenge showed a significant higher survival rate compared to untreated controls (60% survival for C1 animals, 70% for C2 animals, and 0% for controls). Survival correlated with bacterial load within the lungs whereby untreated animals had the highest average burden (2x109 CFU/g), and bacteriophage prophylaxis reduced bacterial loads (C1 6x108 CFU/g, C2 5x108 CFU/g). Bacterial loads in the spleen, which were assessed as a measure of systemic spread, were not different between experimental groups. Conclusions. Nebulized bacteriophage prophylaxis reduced bacterial loads in the lungs and increased the survival of MRSA infected rats, underscoring its potential in the context of VAP.
