N2  - Rationale Risk of school-based Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) transmission in tuberculosis (TB) endemic countries is not known. We aimed to measure classroom air quality and conduct air sampling for MTB DNA, in parallel with TB symptom screening, in two South African high schools. Methods We studied 72 high school classrooms (1,836 learners) in Worcester, South Africa, between 2017-2018. We measured carbon dioxide (CO2) concentration (parts per million (ppm)) for the first lesson of the day. High ventilation risk for TB transmission was defined as median CO2 ≥1,000 ppm. We simultaneously filtered air through removable polycarbonate track etch membrane filters to sample MTB DNA, measured by droplet digital Polymerase Chain Reaction (ddPCR) assay (positive fluorescence threshold ≥5,000). Risk of MTB inhalation per lesson was estimated from filtered air flow and number of MTB DNA copies sampled. Learners with ≥1 TB symptoms, and who were able to expectorate, provided sputum for Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra testing. Results Median duration of sampling was 40 minutes (IQR: 35-54) and median number of occupants was 34 (IQR: 29-39). 40% of classrooms were categorised as high ventilation risk. A positive ddPCR assay was obtained in 13/72 (18.1%) classrooms. Across all classrooms the average risk of a classroom occupant inhaling 1 MTB DNA copy over 1 lesson (35 minutes) was 0.71 %, over 1 academic day was 2.7%, over 1 academic week was 13.5%, over 1 academic month was 53.9% and over 1 academic year was 100%. 1,836/2,262 (81.2%) learners in sampled classrooms consented to TB symptom screening. Median age was 16.2 years and 779 (45.8%) were male. 90/1820 (4.9%) students reported cough ≥2 weeks and 215/1832 (11.7%) reported any TB-related symptom. 58/90 (64.4%) students with cough agreed to provide sputum, but only 21 (36.2%) submitted an expectorated sputum sample. 1 symptomatic student was confirmed Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra positive (prevalence of undiagnosed TB disease 55 (CI: 0-341) per 100,000). Conclusion More than one-third of high school classrooms had high ventilation risk for TB transmission and MTB DNA was detected by ddPCR assay in a significant proportion of sampled classrooms. Cumulative risk of classroom exposure to MTB suggests that the school setting is a significant contributor to adolescent TB transmission. Although symptom- based TB screening had low diagnostic yield, the ddPCR data suggest missed undiagnosed TB cases among students. Further research is needed to optimise school-based TB screening.
