N2  - Background: In clinic, macrolide antibiotics is the most common treatment mean for children with Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, but the incidence and drug resistance of Mycoplasma pneumoniae is on the rise due to the clinical irregular treatment and the abuse of antibiotics. Therefore, it's urgent to analyze the distribution and drug resistance of pathogens infected by Mycoplasma pneumoniae in children in different areas for better guidance on medication. In the present study, we aimed to analyze the distribution and drug resistance of pathogens infected by Mycoplasma pneumo-niae in children. Methods: Five hundreds of children with respiratory tract infection admitted to Pediatrics of Shanghai Jiangjiang Hospital from January 2016 to January 2018 were included in this study. All children presented with pediatric fever, cough and other respiratory symptoms and met the diagnostic criteria for respiratory infections. On the day in hospital, 2 ml venous blood samples of each child were collected and the serum samples were isolated. Then, the Mycoplasma pneu-moniae antibody was detected by using the respiratory tract infection pathogen IgM test kit. In addition, all children's oral and pharyngeal secretion samples were collected and cultured in the medium, following by the drug sensitivity experiments, including doxycycline, spiramycin, erythromycin, roxithromycin, clarithromycin, azithromycin, clindamycin, guitarmycin and josamycin propionate. Results: A total of 78 cases of positive mycoplasma pneumo-niae were detected in the samples of 500 cases, with the detection rate of 15.6% (78/500). The positive rate of mycoplasma pneumoniae was lowest in infants aged 0 to 12 months and highest in infants aged 3 to 10 years. Among the children who showed mycoplasma pneumonia positive, 42 are male, with the detection rate of 15.3% (42/275); and 36 cases are female, with the detection rate of 16.0% (36/225). The difference of the positive detection rate of mycoplasma pneumonia between males and females showed no significant difference (P > 0.05). Among the 500 children, 95 patients showed upper respiratory tract infection, with 20 cases were positive to mycoplasma pneumonia and the detection rate of 21.1% (20/95); besides, there were 405 cases were diagnosed with lower respiratory tract infection, among which 58 cases were positive to mycoplasma pneumonia, with the detection rate of 14.3% (58/405). And, the difference of the positive detection rate of mycoplasma pneumonia between upper respiratory tract infection and lower respiratory tract infection showed no significant difference (P > 0.05). Moreover, the drug resistance experiment demonstrated that Mycoplasma pneumoniae has a higher sensitivity to josamycin propion-ate, gentamicin, doxycycline and clindamycin, and a lower sensitivity to other antimicrobial agents. Conclusion: The infection of Mycoplasma pneumoniae frequently occurs in 3-10 years old children. Mycoplasma pneumonia shows higher sensitivity to josamycin propion-ate, gentamicin, doxycycline, and clindamycin, which may be used to guide the pharmacy for Mycoplasma pneumonia, aiming to avoid the overuse of antibiotics.
