N2  - Diabetes increases the risk of developing Tuberculosis. The underlying mechanisms in this comorbidity are not well defined. The transcription factor HIF-1a that regulates metabolic responses to hypoxia, has been shown to be destabilized during hyperglycemia. On the other hand, HIF-1a is triggered by infections and also regulates innate and adaptive immune responses. We hypothesize that HIF-1a contributes to Diabetes-TB comorbidity. Here, whether HIF-1a controls the outcome of infection with M. tuberculosis (Mtb) in euglycemic (5 mM glucose) and hyperglycemic (25 mM glucose) conditions was studied both in macrophages and during Mtb infection of mice. The regulation of diverse stabilizers or inhibitors of HIF-1a in the response of primary macrophages (BMDM) to Mtb infection was studied in different glycemic conditions. Leptin receptor deficient db/db obese and hyperglycemic mice (db/db) were infected with Mtb via aerosol route. CFU counts, lung histopathology and the expression of HIF-1a regulated genes were analyzed. The infection of BMDM with mycobacteria increased levels of HIF-1a regulated genes (glut-1 and vegf). Deferoxamine (DFO), selected as a HIF-1a stabilizer, further increased glut-1 and vegf mRNA levels. Moreover, it increased expression levels of il1b and inos also shown to be controlled by HIF-1a after mycobacterial infection in BMDM. We observed that the expression of glut-1, vegf, il1b and inos were all reduced in Mtb infected or uninfected BMDM under hyperglycemic conditions when compared with those incubated in euglycemic conditions. The DFO stabilization of HIF-1a reverted the hampering consequences of hyperglycemia on transcript accumulation. Moreover, M. tuberculosis CFU levels diminished in BMDM infected with M. tuberculosis and treated with DFO both in high or low glucose levels. A tendency to higher M. tuberculosis levels in hyperglycemic conditions was observed. In line with these results, we found increased M. tuberculosis bacterial loads in (db/db) mice compared to control animals. ifnγ, inos, glut-1 and vegf mRNA were all reduced in lungs of infected db/db mice suggesting that HIF-1a destabilization is associated to increased susceptibility to infection. Our preliminary results suggest that hyperglycemia seems to increase M. tuberculosis in vitro and in vivo and that HIF-1a might play an important role in susceptibility. These results support that HIF-1a stability might underlie TB/diabetes comorbidity in humans.
