N2  - Introduction: Immunodeficiency should be considered in patients with chronic rhinosinusitis and recurrent infections. Case Description: A 63-year-old man with sarcoidosis, GERD status post fundoplication, and chronic rhinosinusitis presented with recurrent infections. He endorsed 25+ years of cough, nasal congestion, and sinus infections (5-6 per year) requiring antibiotics, nasal irrigation, and systemic glucocorticoids. In 2017, he underwent sinus surgery for uncontrolled symptoms. In March 2018, he developed pneumonia after knee surgery with four subsequent episodes confirmed on imaging. Pseudomonas and MRSA were isolated. He denied any history of immunodeficiency. Work-up for primary ciliary dyskinesia, cystic fibrosis, alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, and ANCA-associated vasculitis was negative. Serum immunoglobulins were unremarkable except for mildly elevated IgA at 374 (61-356 mg/dL). IgG subclass analysis revealed an IgG3 of 6.7 (18.4-106 mg/dL). He had protective titers against Tdap and non-protective pneumococcal titers. Post-vaccination pneumococcal titers were inadequate with response to 3 out of 12 (25%) serotypes. CH50 and AH50 were normal. MBL was low at 6.2 and <5.0 (≥7.8 ng/mL). Discussion: A diagnosis of specific antibody deficiency was made and IVIG was started due to severe, recurrent pneumonias. The patient did require escalating doses of IVIG due to ongoing infections and is currently on 700 mg/kg with improvement in the rate of infection. Defects in humoral immunity (e.g. hypogammaglobulinemia, immunoglobulin subclass deficiency, specific antibody deficiency) or innate immunity (MBL deficiency) have been associated with recurrent infections in patients with chronic rhinosinusitis. IVIG may be beneficial in immunodeficient patients with impaired vaccine response and recurrent infections despite antibiotic prophylaxis.
