N2  - Introduction: Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) affects up to 15% of patients with cystic fibrosis (CF), representing a significant clinical challenge. It is especially a concern in the pediatric population given adverse effects of steroids in this population. Here we report on the use of omalizumab in the treatment of steroid-resistant ABPA in a pediatric patient with CF. Case Description: JV is a 13-year old male with CF admitted to the hospital for respiratory exacerbation of CF with chronic cough and purulent sputum production of several months duration, and progressive decrease in pulmonary function tests. Sputum cultures confirmed colonization with Pseudomonas aerugiosa and Aspergillus fumigatus. CF exacerbation with ABPA was diagnosed based on acutely worsening clinical status, IgE of 3220 IU/mL, Aspergillus IgE of 64.8 kUA/L, and a positive (1,3) beta-D-glucan. Chest CT demonstrated progression of bronchiectasis. Outpatient treatment included airway clearance, oral and inhaled antibiotics, as well as prednisone and voriconazole, with no improvement after 1 month. Decision was made to try omalizumab as a steroid sparing measure. Patient demonstrated clinical improvement following his first dose of omalizumab 375mg subcutaneous injection. Symptoms and FEV1 improved in the week following treatment, and patient was discharged with ongoing monthly omalizumab treatments. Over four month follow up, patient reported continued improvement in cough, nasal congestion, and sputum production. Discussion: This cases illustrates the potential benefits of using omalizumab in treatment of ABPA in CF. Moreover, it is beneficial in pediatrics where steroids are associated with significant morbidity.
