N2  - Introduction: Chest masses in children are commonly located in the mediastinum. These include nonvascular or vascular masses, congenital anomalies, benign and malignant neoplasms, and infections. We present a case of a mediastinal mass from an unusual cause. Case Description: A 2 y.o. boy with G6PD-deficiency was admitted with fever, cough, rhinorrhea and nasal congestion. Hb was 8.6g/dL and lymphocyte count 2.6 k/μL. Respiratory PCR revealed RSV and adenovirus. CXR suggested pneumonia and Ceftriaxone was initiated. Several days later, symptoms persisted. Chest CT showed a mediastinal and hilar mass 8.1X8X5.6cm. Fungal serology was positive for histoplasma antibody yeast phase 1:16. Further history revealed a brother, with IBD, who died at 3y of pneumonia and autopsy showed necrotizing granulomas in the lungs, liver, peripancreatic lymph nodes, and spleen, and bronchopneumonia. Our patient had normal immunoglobulins and lymphocyte subsets. Bone marrow biopsy was negative for malignancy. DHR testing was low at 66.4 % PMA ox-DHR+ and confirmed on repeat. Genetic testing was positive for CYBB (c.164C>A) mutation, indicating XL-chronic granulomatous disease. Biopsy of mass was declined. He was discharged on trimethoprim (5mg/kg/d, divided BID) and itraconazole (10mg/kg/d, divided BID) for possible histoplasmosis. Both medications were continued at prophylactic dosage. At 2wk follow up, his symptoms were improving while IFN-g-1b (50mcg/m2, therapy 3 times a week) was initiated. Discussion: CGD is caused by a defective oxidative burst in phagocytes resulting in recurrent bacterial and fungal infections, frequently involving the lung, skin, lymph nodes, and liver. This case illustrates a rare presentation and the importance of molecular genetic testing.
