N2  - Background and Aims: Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) is generally free-living organism; but it could cause harm in some susceptible individuals. There is evidence that the incidence of NTM among hospitalized patients in Vietnam is on its raise. This study reports characteristics and clinical outcomes of patients hospitalized the Vietnam National Lung Hospital in whom NTM was isolated. Methods: We collected information on demographic characteristics, clinical data, NTM species, treatment outcome among 30 hospitalized patients with NTM islolates at the Vietnam National Lung Hospital during November 2018 to July 2019. The criteria to diagnose NTM-PD follow British Thoracic Society guidelines for the management of nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease. Results: Of 30 recruited patients, 63.3% were female. The mean patient age was 51.7 years (20-85). Of those, 90% presented with prolong cough, 39% had a history of tuberculosis, 40.7% had AFB positive at admission. The most common NTM species isolated were M. Abscessus (57%) and M. Intracellular (35.7%). We found four cases in which the diverse isolated strains in separate samples at the beginning of the diagnosis, during or after the completed treatment. In these cases, it is difficult to distinguish the co-infection, re-infection or recovery of the different of NTM strains. Of 17 cases with results from microbacterial culture after treatment initiation, 10 cases (58.8%) had monthly culture negative results. There are several challenges in management of NTM, including lack of standard guidelines in diagnosis and treatment of NTM, over emphasize of microbiological result of NTM positive and NTM strains isolation, lack of NTM drug susceptibility test, lack of health insurance reimbursement for repeating molecular test for diagnose and monitoring or long-term treatment drugs for NTM. Conclusions: M. Abscessus is the most common NTM isolated, following by M. Intracellulare. A patient could have diverse NTM isolates in different culture samples. Lack of standard guideline for diagnosis and treatment of NTM poses a significant challenge for effective management of the disease.
