N2  - Objective: To investigate the distribution of common pathogens of fungal infection in hospitalized patients with acute exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and the clinical characteristics of patients with COPD complicated with fungal infection. Methods: Clinical data of COPD patients with fungal infection were collected, to analyze the distribution of common pathogen, and clinical manifestations and laboratory tests were compared between patients receiving antifungal therapy and those without antifungal therapy. Results: A total of 830 hospitalized patients with acute exacerbation of COPD were collected, among which 50 patients met the clinical diagnosis of pulmonary fungal infection, including 40 males and 10 females, aged 53-89 years, with an average age of (71.34±8.76) years. Patients with COPD and fungal infection had varying degrees of dyspnoea, cough, and sputum. The positive rate of fungal culture was 6%, and a total of 52 pathogens were obtained, among which 29 strains of Candida albicans (58%) were the most common, followed by 17 strains of Aspergillus (34%). Twenty-seven patients received antifungal therapy and 23 patients did not. Patients receiving antifungal therapy had a longer hospital stay than those not receiving antifungal therapy, the duration of antibiotics use and glucocorticoid use was longer and the total dose of glucocorticoid use was higher. Oral mucosa leucorrhoea was more common in patients with candida infection receiving antifungal therapy than in patients without antifungal therapy. Conclusion: Candida albicans and Aspergillus are common pathogens of fungal infection in patients with COPD. Patients who received antifungal therapy were clinically assessed to be more severe than those who did not receive antifungal therapy.
