N2  - Background: Cystic Fibrosis is the most common multi system genetic disorder among the Caucasian population. It was thought to be extremely rare in Indian subcontinent but literatures show that it is not so uncommon as thought previously, rather undiagnosed or missed in majority of cases. Objective: To establish the diagnosis of Cystic Fibrosis by pilocarpine iontophoresis sweat chloride test for the first time in Bangladesh. Methods: This observational cross-section study was conducted for a period 02 years from January 2017 to December 2018 in a tertiary care hospital in Dhaka, Bangladesh and total 386 patients ( N-386) were enrolled in the study following inclusion and exclusion criteria strictly. Sweat collection was done by pilocarpine iontophoresis following Gibson and Cooke method and using indigenously prepared and validated equipment. Chloride estimation was done by Schales and Schales method. The minimum acceptable sweat volume was 100mg collected in 30 minutes. Sweat chloride concentration of more than 60 meq/L was considered as positive, 40-60 meq/L was equivocal and less than 40 meq/L was negative. Results: Sweat chloride test was positive in 35 patients ( 9.06%) among which 26 ( 74.28%) were male. Age distribution was 07 month to 32 year, mean age was 9.2 year. The common clinical presentations were repeated respiratory tract infections (94.28%), failure to thrive (91.42%), chronic cough (71.42%), recurrent pneumonia (34.28%), persistent pneumonia (11.04%), chronic malabsorption ( 22.85%), steatorrhea (8.57%), rectal prolapse (2.85%), azospermia (2.85%). Radiological evidence of bilateral bronchiectasis and growth of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in sputum culture were seen in 51.42% and 65.71% respectively. Conclusion: Cystic Fibrosis is more common in people of Bangladesh origin than previously recognized. A stronger and more structured system for diagnosis and effective management is required for this disease.
