N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infections are commonly seen in immunocompromised patients.[1] We present a case of disseminated MAC infection in an immunocompetent host related to underlying lobar pneumonia. CASE PRESENTATION: A 61-year-old male, non-smoker, with no medical history presents with right flank pain, fever, and cough. Chest tomography (CT) chest revealed a right middle lobe (RML) and upper lobe consolidation with hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy with an adjacent loculated pleural effusion (Fig.1). A thoracostomy tube was placed for the exudative effusion, with resolution after three days of tPA/DNAse therapy. Thoracostomy tube was removed and patient was discharged on Amoxicillin/Clavulanic acid. Serial outpatient CT imaging showed a persistent RML consolidation and hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy (Fig. 2) associated with a cough, intermittent fevers, and weight loss. Patient underwent endobronchial ultrasound bronchoscopy (EBUS) in which fine needle aspirates were obtained from stations 4R, 7, and 11S, and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was obtained from the RML. Biopsy showed histiocytic inflammation consistent with granulomatous process. Stains for malignancy, fungus, and acid fact bacilli (AFB) were negative. Subsequent clinic follow-up revealed recurrence of right flank pain, fevers, worsening weight loss; thus the patient was re-admitted to the hospital. Repeat imaging remained unchanged. The patient was admitted for sepsis secondary to pneumonia and started on broad spectrum antibiotics. BAL studies would eventually come back positive for AFB, raising the suspicion for disseminated MAC infection secondary to MAC pneumonia. Patient underwent a bone marrow biopsy which again showed histiocytic inflammation (Fig. 3), with negative AFB and fungal cultures. Finally, AFB blood cultures were positive for MAC. Testing for an underlying immunodeficiency was negative. Patient was started on treatment for MAC with clinical improvement and was discharged on Moxifloxacin, Rifampin and Clarithromycin. DISCUSSION: Lobar pneumonia is an uncommon presentation for pulmonary MAC infections, which commonly presents with fibrocavitary or bronchiectacic lung disease. Diagnosis and timely treatment of MAC may be challenging, as presentation can mimic malignancy. Given the ubiquitous nature of the organism, positive cultures should be correlated with clinical presentation.[2] In our case, the patient presented with disseminated infection, in the absence underlying immunodeficiency. First-line therapy includes a macrolide, ethambutol and a rifamycin. For macrolide-resistance or severe infection, alternative antibiotics include aminoglycosides and a quinolone.[1] CONCLUSIONS: Mycobacterium avium should be considered in the differential diagnosis in patients presenting with persistent pulmonary symptoms in immunocompetent patients. Reference #1: Griffith, David E, et al. “An Official ATS/IDSA Statement: Diagnosis, Treatment, and Prevention of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Diseases.” American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, vol. 175, no. 4, 2007, pp. 367–416. Reference #2: Timothy R. Aksamit, Julie V. Philley, David E. Griffith, Nontuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) lung disease: The top ten essentials, Respiratory Medicine, Volume 108, Issue 3, 2014, Pages 417-425. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Heather Boakye, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jacqueline Choa, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Mutsumi Kioka, source=Web Response
