N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is one of the causes of community acquired pneumonia. Para pneumonic effusions with Mycoplasma are very rare but have been reported (1). There is a very limited data regarding such effusions. CASE PRESENTATION: 79-year-old male with a past medical history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, presented to the hospital with cough for few weeks with associated shortness of breath and some blood tinged sputum. On physical exam, Vital Signs were normal (Table 1). Lungs were clear to auscultation with decreased breath sounds at the right base. The rest of the exam was unremarkable. Laboratory analysis was relevant for mild anemia, with hemoglobin and hematocrit values of 10.4 g/dl and 30%, respectively and an elevated creatinine of 1.52 mg/dl was also noted. The patient’s chest x-ray showed RLL pneumonia and a right sided pleural effusion. CT chest showed moderate right sided pleural effusion and bibasilar consolidation. He underwent thoracentesis with pleural fluid analysis which was consistent with exudative effusion (Table 2). Pleural fluid and blood cultures remained negative. His mycoplasma IgM came back positive at 5.61. He was thus treated with azithromycin. DISCUSSION: Mycoplasma is a common cause of community acquired respiratory tract infections. It mostly causes upper respiratory infections and bronchitis, however pneumonia can occur. Mycoplasma can present with a variety of symptoms, including extra pulmonary manifestations, hemolysis being the most common. Para pneumonic effusions with mycoplasma are rare but have been reported. There are two distinct patterns. One is reactive and is associated with transient chest disease and another is the one seen with persistent chest disease and where Mycoplasma genome can be detected by PCR from the pleural fluid(1). Most of these case reports are from pediatric patients. One study done with 6 patients showed that the patients with Mycoplasma pneumoniae related pleural effusion tend to be younger and had more common use of systemic steroids compared to the patients without effusion. The pleural fluid in such effusions is exudative with lymphocytic predominance instead of polymorphnuclear predominance which was seen in our patient too. The results of the study indicated that the presence of pleural effusion may be related to a more serious form of infection compared to those without effusion(2). CONCLUSIONS: Although mycoplasma pneumoniae is a self-limiting disease, presence of lymphocytic dominant effusion should raise concern for a more serious disease requiring prompt treatment. Reference #1: Narita M., Tanaka H. Two distinct patterns of pleural effusions caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. Pediatr. Infect. Dis. J. 2004 Nov;23(11):1069. [PubMed] Reference #2:, Seung-Ick Cha et al. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Shreya Gor, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Mohammed Shariff, source=Web Response
