N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM INTRODUCTION: Diffuse Large B Cell lymphoma is the most common subtype of Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma. Patients most often present with a rapidly growing mass in single or multiple, nodal or extranodal sites. The term Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) encompasses multiple species that are genetically similar and generally not differentiated in the clinical microbiology laboratory. Malignancy and infection with either viral organisms or mycobacterium tuberculosis are usually more commonly associated with pericardial effusion. CASE PRESENTATION: A 24 year-old immigrant from Ghana presented to the hospital with fatigue, cough and whitish sputum production for two weeks and a 15 lb weight loss over two months. He denied fever, chills, night sweats, shortness of breath or chest pain. Physical exam was remarkable for bilateral cervical adenopathy and decreased breath sounds in lung bases bilaterally. Chest X ray showed consolidation of right upper, perihilar and infrahilar areas. A CT chest revealed a heterogeneous mediastinal mass measuring roughly 11 x 8 x 10 cm with moderate right pleural effusion and small left pleural effusion. Echocardiogram demonstrated a large pericardial effusion, largest area anterior to the right ventricle measured up to 2.0 cm with early tamponade physiology. Pericardiocentesis with placement of Wayne catheter drained 400 ml sanguineous fluid that started growing Mycobacterium species. HIV 1-2, Tb- Quantiferon, EBV, HTLV, Hepatitis panel were negative. Patient was started on RIPE therapy and a CT guided biopsy of the medicastinal mass revealed Diffuse Large B cell Lymphoma(primary mediastinal) after which patient was started on R-EPOCH regimen. The final culture grew Mycobacterium avium complex and patient was monitored off antibiotics. DISCUSSION: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common lymphoma worldwide representing a diverse spectrum of disease. Recent gene expression profiling studies have classified diffuse large B-cell lymphoma into 2 main subtypes, germinal center B-cell and activated B-cell, with the germinal center type showing an overall better survival from data in pre-rituximab treatment era. With addition of rituximab there has been significant prognostic benefits irrespectively. The finding of Non Tuberculous Mycobacterium is clinically significant when repeatedly isolated from sites that are normally unsterile, except from blood cultures or sterile sites where a single isolation is enough(1). Pericardial cavity is a sterile cavity and identification of Non Tuberculosis mycobacterium warrants further attention as it is an unusual finding. Early resolution of symptoms and lack of other clinical findings raises a question whether antibiotics should be considered CONCLUSIONS: We report an exceptionally rare presentation of Diffuse Large B cell lymphoma. The identification of MAC in pericardial fluid has to be further studied to guide treatment appropriately Reference #1: Watt B., Rayner A. & Harris G. Mycobacterium In: Practical Medical Microbiology, Colle, J.G., Marmion, B.P., Fraser, A.G. & Simmons, A. (editors), I4th edition. Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone, 1996, 32 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by pragnan kancharla, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Lam Nguyen, source=Web Response
