N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM INTRODUCTION: Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are ubiquitous environmental organisms found in soil and water. Although pulmonary disease is the most common clinical manifestation of NTM, pleural involvement is rare. We present a case of empyema caused by mycobacterium abscessus (M. abscessus). CASE PRESENTATION: A 77-year-old female, non-smoker, with a background history of post-tuberculous bronchiectasis presented in June 2017 with fever, worsening cough and increased sputum purulence. She fulfilled clinical and microbiological criteria for pulmonary NTM infection with M. abscessus in 2012 but had declined treatment. Physical examination revealed bilateral basal crepitations with decreased breath sounds over right lower chest. Diagnostic imaging showed a right hydropneumothorax with areas of peribronchial thickening, mucus plugging, centrilobular nodules and consolidation in the right upper and lower lobes, and chronic right middle lobe bronchiectasis. Pleural fluid analysis after thoracocentesis revealed a lymphocytic exudative effusion with a pH of 7.17. M. abscessus was cultured from the pleural fluid and sputum. She was started on intravenous cefoxitin and amikacin for 6 weeks along with oral clarithromycin. Oral clofazamine was added subsequently based on culture sensitivity. She completed more than 1 year of oral antibiotics from last negative sputum culture. Her symptoms improved and there was no recurrence of pleural effusion. DISCUSSION: Pulmonary NTM occurs most commonly in structural lung disease such as bronchiectasis and prior tuberculosis as seen in our patient. However, pleural involvement is extremely rare. To our knowledge, only 3 cases of empyema caused by M. abscessus have been reported. The first was in 2002 by Fairhurst et al. in a lung transplant recipient. The second was a case of empyema necessitatis, and the third was a patient with liver cirrhosis who had M. abscessus empyema and concurrent bacteremia. In our case, empyema may have developed via a pulmonary fistula given the presence of hydropneumothorax although the actual fistulous communication was not visualized in the scan. M. abscessus is highly drug resistant and difficult to treat. The American Thoracic Society and Infectious Diseases Society of America recommend multidrug macrolide-based therapy in combination with intravenous agents based on drug susceptibility testing. Treatment of M. abscessus empyema in our patient was extrapolated from guidelines for pulmonary disease. Our patient received 6 weeks of intravenous agents, in addition to clarithromycin and clofazamine with good clinical response and sputum culture conversion. CONCLUSIONS: In summary, patients with structural lung disease and pulmonary M. abscessus have the potential to develop empyema and it should be considered in those who continue to deteriorate. Reference #1: Fairhurst RM, Kubak BM, Shpiner RB, et al. Mycobacterium abscessus empyema in a lung transplant recipient. J Heart Lung Transplant. 2002 Mar; 21(3):391-4. Reference #2: Jo KW, Kim JW, Hong Y, Shim TS. A case of empyema necessitatis caused by Mycobacterium abscessus. Respir Med Case Rep. 2012;6:1-4. Reference #3: Lai CC, Chao CM, Gau SJ, Hsueh PR. Thoracic empyema and bacteremia due to Mycobacterium abscessus in a patient with liver cirrhosis. J Microbiol Immunol Infect, 46 (2013), pp. 482-4. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by John Abisheganaden, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Sharlene Ho, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Yick-Hou Lim, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ziqin Ng, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ser Hon Puah, source=Web Response
