N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Cavitary lung lesion can be a presenting symptom of many diseases including acute to chronic infection, malignancy and chronic systemic illness. CASE PRESENTATION: A 74 year old male with 60 pack year smoking history and emphysema on home oxygen and daily prednisone (20 mg) presented to pulmonary clinic for evaluation of dyspnea on exertion of several month duration. In clinic, his oxygen saturation was 80% on 5 L home oxygen. He was sent to the emergency department (ED) for further assessment. In the ED, he remained hypoxic, requiring nonrebreather. Laboratory studies showed hemoglobin 14.7 (13.2-16.6 g/dL), white blood cell count 18.4 (3.4-9.6 x109/L), lactate 2.5 (0.5-2.2 mmol/L), and c-reactive protein 90.6 (≤ 8 mg/L). Chest radiograph showed a thick-walled nodular cavitary lesion in the left apex with parenchymal infiltrates. Given his increased work of breathing, he was placed on non-invasive positive pressure ventilation and transferred to the medical intensive care unit. He was placed in airborne isolation for tuberculosis rule out and underwent a CT chest which showed the cavitary lesion to be a nodular opacity with calcification in left upper lobe surrounding bullous disease, measuring 2.8 x 1.1 x 3.6 cm. His sputum smear was positive for acid fast organisms with negative mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR, raising concern for mycobacteria or nocardia infection. On day 6, sputum culture was positive for M. abscessus. He additionally completed a 14 day course of antibiotics for community acquired pneumonia, and with aggressive pulmonary hygiene, was transitioned to nasal pendent, and eventually discharged home on 4 L supplemental oxygen. He was tapered off prednisone and planned to follow up with infectious disease outpatient awaiting susceptibility of M. abscessus, which later showed to be resistant to multiple antibiotics, intermediate to amikacin and cefoxitin, and susceptible to clarithromycin. DISCUSSION: There has been an increase in nontuberculosis mycobacteria infections worldwide, with annual rates of 5 to 10 per 100,000 in North America. M. abscessus is a rapidly growing mycobacterium that can affect patients with structural lung disease, including bronchiectasis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cystic fibrosis. Pulmonary M. abscessus disease often has an insidious course with cough and imaging findings of upper lobe infiltrates. Diagnosis and susceptibility testing of M. abscessus can be time consuming and often results in multidrug-resistance, posing challenges in treatment of the disease. CONCLUSIONS: It is important to consider nontuberculosis mycobacteria infection in the differential diagnosis in patients with existing lung disease and/or immunosuppression who present with subacute worsening of dyspnea and cough. Reference #1: Parkar AP, Kandiah P. Differential Diagnosis of Cavitary Lung Lesions. J Belg Soc Radiol. 2016;100(1):100. Reference #2: Prevots DR, Marras TK. Epidemiology of human pulmonary infection with nontuberculous mycobacteria: a review. Clin Chest Med. 2014;36(1):13-34. Reference #3: Griffith DE, Girard WM, Wallace RJ. Clinical features of pulmonary disease caused by rapidly growing mycobacteria. An analysis of 154 patients. Am Rev Respir Dis. 1993;147(5):1271-8. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Diana Kelm, source=Admin input No relevant relationships by Zhenmei Zhang, source=Web Response
