N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Fellow Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Fellow Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Highly active antiretroviral therapy and chemoprophylaxis based on CD4+ T lymphocyte counts have decreased PCP incidence in HIV patients. In contrast, PCP incidence has increased in non-HIV immunocompromised patients; one reason might be less standardized initiation and duration of chemoprophylaxis. We present a complicated case of PCP in a non-HIV immunocompromised patient. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71-year-old man with untreated chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) presented to pulmonary clinic prior to ibrutinib initiation with fevers, dyspnea, and cough with purulent sputum. He had previously received oral antibiotics for recurrent sinus infections and pneumonia. Chest computerized tomography (CT) demonstrated basilar predominant ground glass opacities (GGOs). Leukocyte count was 96 K/uL (92% lymphocytes). Sputum culture grew pan-sensitive Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Respiratory viral panel was positive for rhinovirus. He received levofloxacin prior to bronchoscopy. Transbronchial biopsy (TBBx) revealed CLL and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) grew normal flora. He began ibrutinib. Over one month his fevers, cough, and dyspnea worsened. Chest CT detected bilateral upper lobe GGOs and basilar consolidations. Serum (1,3)-beta-D-glucan was elevated. After repeat bronchoscopy, he became hypoxemic and was admitted for intravenous antimicrobial treatment for PCP and bacterial co-infection. Steroids were not given. He left against medical advice and was given oral ciprofloxacin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX). TBBx and BAL confirmed few PCP organisms by silver stain. One week later he returned to clinic with left sided pleuritic chest pain. X-ray demonstrated large pneumatocele. He was admitted for emergent lingulectomy. Intraoperatively a lingular abscess was found. Pathology revealed PCP, CLL, and infarcted lung. He improved with prolonged TMP-SMX treatment and was discharged to home. DISCUSSION: GGOs in immunocompromised patients can represent atypical infections, pneumonitis, or malignant infiltration. Typical PCP symptoms are cough without purulent sputum, mild fever, and acute hypoxemic respiratory failure. Fungal detection in respiratory secretions definitively diagnoses PCP; because lower fungal burden is needed for infection in non-HIV immunocompromised patients than in HIV patients, false negative results can occur. The most frequent early PCP disease pattern is symmetric apical GGOs; if untreated, these can evolve and help to identify previously missed cases. Pneumatoceles are rare, have lower incidence in non-HIV patients, and can lead to spontaneous pneumothorax. Rapidly progressive cavitary disease can occur despite treatment. CONCLUSIONS: Non-HIV immunocompromised patients can develop PCP with less fungal burden, yielding false negative results of diagnostics. Complications of PCP can occur despite treatment. Serial imaging is advised in cases of inadequate response to treatment. Reference #1: Matsumara, Y., et al. Clinical characteristics of Pneumocystis pneumonia in non-HIV patients and prognostic factors including microbiological phenotypes. BMC Infectious Diseases 2011 Mar 25;11:76. Reference #2: Mansharamani, N.G., et al. Management and outcome patterns for adult Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia, 1985 to 1995: comparison of HIV-associated cases to other immunocompromised states. Chest 2000 Sep; 118(3):704-11. Reference #3: Roux, A., et al. Update on pulmonary Pneumocystis jirovecii infection in non-HIV patients. Medecine et maladies infectieuses 2014 May;44(5):)185-198. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Gabriela Magda, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Mandana Mahmoudi, source=Web Response No relevant relationships Added 03/17/2019 by Daniel Sterman, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Removed 03/17/2019 by Daniel Sterman, source=Web 