N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM INTRODUCTION: Nitrofurantoin-induced pulmonary toxicity is a rare side effect that presents with various clinical manifestations, imaging abnormalities, & pathologic findings. We describe a case of chronic pneumonitis in a patient on suppressive nitrofurantoin therapy presenting with dyspnea and hypoxemia. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old female with recurrent urinary tract infections (UTIs) presented to the emergency department with acute on chronic dyspnea. She was afebrile & normotensive; but hypoxemic with bilateral fine crackles on exam. Medication history was notable for daily nitrofurantoin, which was started 15 months ago for UTI prevention. A CT pulmonary angiogram ruled out embolus but showed diffuse peribronchovascular nodules & ground-glass opacities (GGOs) with upper lobe predominance. The patient was admitted. She underwent bronchoscopy with BAL that showed 43% lymphocytes and 2% eosinophils. Transbronchial biopsies & cultures were unremarkable. A respiratory PCR panel was negative for influenza A/B & other common pathogens including M.pneumoniae & C.pneumoniae. Autoimmune serologic testing was negative. Given history & diagnostic results, the patient was diagnosed with nitrofurantoin-induced pulmonary toxicity. Her nitrofurantoin was stopped. She discharged in good condition, with two liters/minute of supplemental oxygen therapy. She did not receive corticosteroids. At follow-up, the patient had improved dyspnea but persistent hypoxemia. Chest high resolution CT (HRCT) at 3 months & 9 months showed improvement in peribronchovascular nodules and GGOs. Repeat PFTs exhibited a >10% increase in DLCO. Repeat oxygen saturation testing was ordered at 14 months. She was instructed to avoid nitrofurantoin indefinitely. DISCUSSION: Most patients with nitrofurantoin-induced pulmonary toxicity are women, as they’re more likely to be repetitively exposed to the drug. Chronic pneumonitis is most commonly seen in women in their 70’s who are on long-term therapy. Symptoms are nonspecific & may include: dyspnea, dry cough, & fatigue. Fever on presentation is uncommon with chronic exposure but can be frequently observed during acute reactions. Diagnosis of chronic pneumonitis is based on history, exclusion of other common etiologies, & clinical response after discontinuation of the drug. HRCT is always abnormal & most commonly reveals bilateral areas of GGOs. Our patient demonstrated a peribronchovascular distribution likely consistent with organizing pneumonia, which has rarely been described in the literature. Clinical & objective improvement typically is seen in weeks to months; however, a majority of patients will have some residual lung disease. After 9 months, our patient had near resolution of HRCT abnormalities. CONCLUSIONS: While acute reactions are more common, it is important to remain vigilant for chronic manifestations as these have a higher risk of irreversible lung parenchymal injury. Reference #1: Mendez, Jose L., et al. “Chronic Nitrofurantoin-Induced Lung Disease.” Mayo Clinic Proceedings, vol. 80, no. 10, Oct. 2005, pp. 1298–1302.,. Reference #2: Santos, Jody M., et al. “Evaluation of the Risk of Nitrofurantoin Lung Injury and Its Efficacy in Diminished Kidney Function in Older Adults in a Large Integrated Healthcare System: A Matched Cohort Study.” Journal of the American Geriatrics Society, vol. 64, no. 4, Apr. 2016, pp. 798–805.,. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Caitlin Batzlaff, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Matthew Koroscil, source=Web Response
