N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM INTRODUCTION: Diffuse Alveolar Hemorrhage (DAH) is bleeding into the alveolar spaces of lungs due to disruption between alveolar and capillary basement membrane as a result of injury or inflammation of the interstitium, alveoli, arterioles or venules. We present a case of DAH in an adult male with stable respiratory status who is diagnosed with inactive Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) CASE PRESENTATION: 48 year old male with no significant PMH presents with a dry cough for two months, followed by an acute episode of hemoptysis for one day. He denied prior episodes, smoking history, no recent travel, joint pain, fever or rashes. Differentials included: infectious, inflammatory, autoimmune, or malignancy. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest showed multifocal opacities concerning for pneumonia and he was started on empiric antibiotics. On further laboratory investigations, he had negative c-anca, p-anca, ds-DNA, ANA, influenza, complements, AFP, B-HCG, LDH, procalcitonin, Streptococcus An, Mycoplasma IgM, Urine Legionella An. He was found to have an elevated ESR, CRP and Anti-CCP Ab. He remained afebrile, with a normal WBC count and no respiratory distress, but complained of recurrent hemoptysis during admission. Pulmonary was consulted and he underwent bronchoscopy, which was consistent with DAH and was subsequently started on IV steroids. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) for AFB smear, fungal smear and bacterial culture were negative. After 4 days of high dose steroids, he had no further episodes of hemoptysis. Repeat CT chest was normal indicating resolution of opacities, and a repeat ESR was within normal limits. Steroids were tapered down very slowly and he was eventually transitioned to an oral regimen, and discharged home to follow up with a rheumatologist and pulmonologist DISCUSSION: DAH is a very rare presentation of RA, let alone in a patient with inactive RA. With varying differentials, following should be ruled out: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, Goodpasture syndrome, collagen vascular disease, SLE, Microscopic Polyangiitis, isolated pauci-immune pulmonary capillaritis, drugs and other toxins, autoimmune diseases and infectious etiologies. Pulse therapy with steroids (methylprednisolone IV 500-2000mg in daily divided doses for 4-5 days) with gradual taper with or without other immunosuppressants (rituximab or cyclophosphamide) should be considered depending on cause and severity of disease. Supportive care with oxygen and bronchodilators can be helpful for symptom control. Patients may require intubation and mechanical ventilation if presentation is severe CONCLUSIONS: Diagnosing DAH can be challenging (especially inactive RA) as it presents with varying severity of symptoms from mild bloody sputum to pleural effusions or worsening respiratory distress leading to acute respiratory failure. Prompt suspicion with initial positive labs should guide examiner towards starting first line treatment with steroids Reference #1: Afaf Osman1, Panagis Galiatsatos2,3, Sonali Bose3, Sonye Danoff3. Rheumatoid arthritis causing diffuse alveolar haemorrhage: a novel therapeutic approach. BMJ Case Rep. 2017 Jun 14;2017. pii: bcr-2017-220509. Reference #2: Ruchi Dua and Jagdish Rawat1. Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage in a patient of rheumatoid arthritis. Lung India. 2014 Apr-Jun; 31(2): 194–195. Reference #3: Omer S. B. Alamoudi and Suzan Mansour Attar. Pleuropulmonary manifestation in patients with rheumatoid arthritis in Saudi Arabia. Ann Thorac Med. 2017 Oct-Dec; 12(4): 266–271. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Durotimi Ayodeji, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Armando Cabrera Alonso, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Livasky Concepcion Perez, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Nehal Patel, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Lissette Pola, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Prashanth Reddy Yella, source=Web Response
