N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium xenopi, a non-tuberculous mycobacterium, causes apical cavitary process in patients with obstructive pulmonary disease. One of the complications of Mycobacterium Xenopi infections is hemoptysis, that responded to treatment with tranexamic acid nebulizer. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old male with past medical history significant for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and mycobacterium xenopi with cavitary lesions and mass-like lesion within both upper lobes, and recently completing course of rifampin, isoniazid, ethambutol and levofloxacin, presented with hemoptysis for 2 days. His blood pressure was 140/87 mm Hg, pulse rate of 86/minutes, and respiratory rate of 20/minutes. His oxygen saturation was 86% on 3 liters of nasal oxygen per minute. Physical exam was remarkable for coarse breathing sounds bilaterally. CT chest showed heterogeneous mass-like lesion within the right upper lobe that measured approximately 5 x 8.7 cm with a similar but much smaller lesion within the left lung apex, with the largest cavitary portion at the more superior aspect at the lung apex measuring approximately 2.5 x 1.6 cm. CT angiogram chest did not show any active bleeding from bronchial arteries. Completed blood count along was unremarkable. The patient was initially treated with levofloxacin for possible bacterial pneumonia. Hemoptysis significantly responded to scheduled tranexamic acid 500mg/5 ml every 8 hours nebulizer treatment. This led to significantly reduced expectorated blood volume, and he did not require bronchoscopy bronchial artery embolization to stop bleeding. DISCUSSION: Mycobacterium xenopi is the second non-tuberculous colonization occurs from ingestion or inhalation of, or cutaneous exposure to, organisms in water, soil, or airborne particles. Its usual feature is an apical cavitary process in patients with obstructive pulmonary disease, and can lead to hemoptysis. M xenopi infections are difficult to treat and there is no standard treatment. Rifampin, ethambutol and sometimes isoniazid or fluoroquinolones are usually recommended in combination with clarithromycin. Tranexamic acid is widely used to treat or prevent excessive blood loss from major trauma, postpartum bleeding, surgery, tooth removal, nosebleeds, and heavy menstruation. Tranexamic acid is in the antifibrinolytic family of medications. Tentative evidence supports the use of tranexamic acid in hemoptysis. CONCLUSIONS: Overall, tranexamic acid is an under recognized treatment that may have temporizing value in managing patients with nonmassive hemoptysis. In the context that the available literature is sparse, large, well-designed clinical trials are needed to advance understanding of the role of TXA in hemoptysis. TXA inhalation was used safely and effectively to control bleeding in our patient with nonmassive hemoptysis. Reference #1: Schwabacher H. A strain of mycobacterium isolated from skin lesions of a coldblooded animal, Xenopus laevis and its relation to atypical acid fast bacilli occurring in man. J Hyg (Lond) 1959;57:56–67. Reference #2: British Thoracic Society. Management of opportunist mycobacterial infections: Joint Tuberculosis Committee guidelines 1999. Thorax 2000;55:210–8. Reference #3: Andre'jak C, Lescure FX, Douadi Y, et al. Non tuberculous mycobacteria pulmonary infection: management and follow-up of 31 infected patients. J Infect 2007;55:34–40. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Syed Azharuddin, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Shirin Majdizadeh, source=Web Response
