N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Electronic Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Original Inv Poster Discussion PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM PURPOSE: Delafloxacin (DLX) is an IV/oral anionic fluoroquinolone with no QT restrictions. DLX is approved in the US for treatment of serious skin infections including those due to MRSA and gram-negative pathogens including P. aeruginosa. DLX has no preclinical signals for QT prolongation and has no QT prolongation in validated challenge study (1). A Phase 3 trial of patients with community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP) was recently completed comparing DLX to moxifloxacin (MOX). METHODS: Multicenter, randomized, double-blind trial of adults with CABP with at least 2 clinical symptoms: cough, sputum, dyspnea, and chest pain; physical signs; and radiographic evidence of pneumonia. Patients were randomized 1:1 to DLX or MOX treatment for 5-10 days. Patients received a minimum of 3 days of IV treatment, then were switched to oral at MD discretion. Key clinical endpoints were the investigator assessment of response at Test of Cure (TOC) 5-10 days after end of treatment and all-cause mortality at Day 28. Clinical success was defined as complete or near resolution of signs and symptoms and no further antibiotics needed per investigator assessment. RESULTS: 859 patients were randomized. Patient characteristics: 58.7% male; mean age 60 (21% ≥ age 75); 25.4% and 1.4% were PORT class IV and V; 28.6% multi-lobar pneumonia; 447 (52%) with CrCl <90 mL/min. Bacterial pathogens were identified in 60.5% at baseline. Patients received treatment of an average 8.5 days (6.3 days of IV and 2.2 days oral). DLX was non-inferior to MOX with Clinical Success at TOC in the Intent-To Treat population, 90.5% (390/431) DLX vs 89.7% (384/428) MOX [0.8 (-3.3, 4.8)], and, in the Clinically Evaluable population 94.7 (376/397) DLX vs 94.7 (373/394) MOX [0.0 (-3.2, 3.3)]. All-Cause Mortality at Day 28 was 8 DLX and 6 MOX patients. 30.5% DLX and 26.2% MOX patients had ≥1 treatment-emergent adverse events (AEs). The most common DLX events were diarrhea and transaminase elevations, which were mild to moderate and did not lead to treatment discontinuation (DC). There were no QT-related adverse events reported in DLX patients; there was one MOX patient with reported QT prolongation. Serious AEs were reported in 23 DLX and 20 MOX patients. Treatment DC due to treatment-related AEs were seen in 9 DLX and 4 MOX patients. CONCLUSIONS: IV/oral DLX was comparable to IV/oral MOX for treatment of patients with CABP. DLX appears effective and well tolerated in patients with CABP. 1 Litwin J, Benedict, M, et al. A Thorough QT Study to Evaluate the Effects of Therapeutic and Supratherapeutic Doses of Delafloxacin on Cardiac Repolarization. Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy 2015; 59 (6): 3469-3473 CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: : DLX may be a useful treatment option for monotherapy in patients with CABP on either an inpatient or outpatient basis. The lack of QT restrictions may be a factor in antibiotic choice. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Rodolfo Alvarez-Sala, source=Admin input Employee relationship with Melinta Please note: >$100000 Added 03/03/2019 by Sue Cammarata, source=Web Response, value=Salary No relevant relationships by Juan P. Horcajada, source=Web Response Employee relationship with Melinta Therapeutics Please note: >$100000 Added 03/12/2019 by Laura LAWRENCE, source=Web Response, value=Salary No relevant relationships by Yang Li, source=Web Response Principal Investigator relationship with Melinta therapeutics Please note: $5001 - $20000 Added 03/15/2019 by Floarea-Mimi Nitu, source=Admin input, value=Grant/Research Support Employee relationship with Melinta Therapeutics, Inc. Please note: >$100000 Added 03/12/2019 by Megan Quintas, source=Web Response, value=Salary
