N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium Chimaera (MC), a species in the Mycobacterium Avium Complex, is a rare organism associated with heater-cooler units (HCU) and causes serious infections involving prosthetic valves and grafts in patients who undergo surgical procedures involving cardiopulmonary bypass. We present a case of MC in a patient with no such surgical history or exposure to HCU and presented with shortness of breath (SOB) and pleural effusion. CASE PRESENTATION: An 82-year-old Caucasian male with the past medical history of pulmonary sarcoidosis (PS) for 10 years on Methotrexate 2.5 mg and Prednisone 10 mg presented with SOB, yellow sputum, fatigue, and weight loss. He had slow progressive symptoms for a year but was unable to walk due to SOB at rest for one week. He smoked pipe at an early age and was a farmer and truck driver by profession. Physical examination was pertinent for bilateral fine crackles and decreased breath sounds at bases. Initial blood work including lactic acid and blood cultures were negative. Chest radiograph revealed possible pneumonia due to increased bilateral air-space disease from his baseline due to PS. He was initially started on ceftriaxone and azithromycin to treat possible community-acquired pneumonia. High-resolution CT showed stable findings of PS, however, a new 3 x 8 cm loculated lower left sided pleural effusion along with pleural thickening and calcifications was noted. He underwent CT guided pleural fluid aspiration of which gram stain showed beaded gram-positive bacilli with positive AFB smear. Intravenous sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim was given awaiting cultures for possible Nocardia versus mycobacterial species. The final culture showed mycobacterium chimaera for which clindamycin, amikacin, ethambutol, and rifampin was started. His condition improved over days and was discharged on antibiotics with outpatient follow up. DISCUSSION: MC is usually involved in serious infections with the presumed source from the prosthetic valve, aortic graft, left ventricular assisted device, or sternal wound after surgical procedures involving HCU during cardiopulmonary bypass. MC has low virulence and slow growing characteristics which makes it a challenging case. Most common presenting symptoms are fatigue, fever, sweats, SOB, weight loss, and cough. AFB culture is the main diagnostic modality to establish diagnosis while PCR is also helpful in some cases. More research is required to establish to optimal treatment of MC infection, however, it is usually treated with combination antimicrobial therapy and also the removal of the presumed source of infection. The crude mortality rate has been reported up to 50%. CONCLUSIONS: Our case was unique as he had no exposure to HCU and no prior surgical procedures involving cardiopulmonary bypass. More data is required to establish whether it is spreading to communities or has an affiliation with immunosuppressive conditions. Reference #1: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, et al. An official ATS/IDSA statement: diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2007;175(4):367-416. Reference #2: van Ingen J1, Kohl TA2, Kranzer K3, et al. Global outbreak of severe Mycobacterium chimaera disease after cardiac surgery: a molecular epidemiological study. Lancet Infect Dis. 2017;17(10):1033-1041. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Arul Chandran, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Siddique Chaudhary, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Mahin Khan; No relevant relationships by Hafiz KHAN, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Ahsan Wahab;
