N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary mucormycosis is an uncommon, but deadly opportunistic fungal infection that can be difficult to diagnose. Even with a combined-medical surgical approach to treatment, mortality rate is about 30%(1). We present a case that illustrates the challenges of diagnosing a disease that is increasing in incidence. CASE PRESENTATION: A 38-year-old man with a history of poorly controlled diabetes presented to an emergency department with a pH of 6.88, blood glucose > 500 and high ketoacids after being found down 2 days after onset of fever, chills, and cough, he was diagnosed with diabetic ketoacidosis and treated for community-acquired pneumonia. Over the next 5 days, despite typical treatment, his mental status deteriorated, he developed shock physiology as well as progressive hypoxemic respiratory failure requiring intubation. Chest radiography and blood gases were consistent with severe acute respiratory distress syndrome. He was managed with low-tidal volume ventilation, neuromuscular blockade, prone positioning, vasopressors, steroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and renal replacement therapy. Testing for infection was negative until hospital day 12, when Rhizopus was isolated from a bronchoalveolar lavage. Rhinoorbital exam was normal and computed tomography of the head did not demonstrate classical features of invasive mucormycosis. The decision was made to consider the isolated Rhizopus nonpathogenic. Subsequent course was marked by continued respiratory failure, persistent fevers and leukocytosis. During investigation, two additional respiratory cultures produced Rhizopus and repeat imaging revealed new left upper lobe cavitation. On hospital day 23, posaconazole was started for presumed pulmonary mucormycosis, which was followed by resolution of his fevers and leukocytosis. Before a lung biopsy could be acquired, the patient experienced an acute pulmonary hemorrhage and cardiac arrest resulting in death on day 28 of his hospitalization. On autopsy, a fungal abscess involving bronchial and vascular structures was noted. DISCUSSION: Incidence of pulmonary mucormycosis is increasing with the rise in uncontrolled diabetes(3). A definitive diagnosis is difficult and requires biopsy as there is no antigen detection test available(2), culture sensitivity is less than 50%(1), there can often be co-infection with other pathogens, and chest imaging findings are non-specific. These diagnostic difficulties combined with need for early treatment often results in poor outcomes. CONCLUSIONS: Our case illustrates the difficulty in diagnosing and treating pulmonary mucormycosis and illustrates importance of considering this infection outside the classic immunoincompetent host. Reference #1: Feng J, Sun X. Characteristics of pulmonary mucormycosis and predictive risk factors for the outcome. Infection. 2018 Aug;46(4):503-512. Reference #2: Hammer MM, Madan R, Hatabu H. Pulmonary Mucormycosis: Radiologic Features at Presentation and Over Time. AJR Am J Roentgenol. 2018 Apr;210(4):742-747. Reference #3: Arunaloke Chakrabarti, Ashim Das, Jharna Mandal, M. R. Shivaprakash, Varghese K. George, Bansidhar Tarai, Pooja Rao, Naresh Panda, Subhash C. Verma, Vinay Sakhuja; The rising trend of invasive zygomycosis in patients with uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, Medical Mycology, 2006 June:44, (4):335–342. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Brian Jones, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Frank Papik, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jason Pettus, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ryland Richards, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by David Zielke, source=Web Response
