N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM INTRODUCTION: Empyemas are grossly purulent and/or infected effusions in the pleural space. Bilateral empyema is exceedingly uncommon. We present a case of bilateral empyema from Streptococcus mitis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 57-year-old man with coronary artery disease presented to the hospital with septic shock and acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring intubation shortly after a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction and percutaneous coronary stent placement a few days prior. CT imaging demonstrated bilateral complex loculated effusions concerning for empyema and bilateral chest tubes were placed. Pleural fluid from both effusions were exudative and grew Streptococcus mitis. His antibiotics were narrowed to ampicillin-sulbactam and his chest tubes continued to drain fluid; however, he developed pneumothorax ex vacuo. Despite slow clinical improvement, his dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) and recent coronary procedure made him high risk for surgical management. After 3 weeks of recovery he underwent video-assisted thoracic surgery but was unable to achieve complete apposition of the pleural space and his pneumothorax ex vacuo persisted. He was discharged with plan for continued antibiotics and re-evaluation for decortication when no longer requiring DAPT. DISCUSSION: Empyema is most commonly caused by the same organisms that can cause pneumonia, but may be difficult to isolate (especially anaerobic bacteria). Depending on the etiology, the patient will have fever, productive cough, chest pain, night sweats and weight loss. Ultrasound imaging may demonstrate thickened pleura and septations and loculations, and help identify an appropriate target for thoracentesis. The effusion will be exudative and may be frank pus. The large majority of empyema are unilateral; the incidence of bilateral empyema has been reported to be as low as 0.6%. Streptoccocus mitis is a gram positive cocci that is part of the viridans group and is commonly found in the human oropharynx. Though it is not usually implicated as a cause of community-acquired pneumonia, it more commonly presents with parapneumonic effusions or empyema compared to pneumococcal pneumonia. Our patient did not have an anatomic variant or prior surgical procedure that could have led to a connected single pleural space, and presumably developed bilateral empyema from bilateral aspiration pneumonia in the setting of poor dentition and procedural sedation. Unfortunately, we did not achieve adequate apposition of his pleural spaces and he ultimately developed a pleural rind leading to a pneumothorax ex vacuo. CONCLUSIONS: Bilateral empyema is an uncommon entity but can develop with Streptococcus mitis pneumonia. In the right clinical setting, both effusions should be investigated with thoracentesis. Reference #1: Light RW. Disorders of the Pleura. In: Jameson J, Fauci AS, Kasper DL, Hauser SL, Longo DL, Loscalzo J. eds. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 20e New York, NY: McGraw-Hill. Reference #2: Keyes EL. Bilateral Empyema of the Pleural Cavities. Ann Surg. 1931;93(5):1050-63. Reference #3: Choi SH, Cha SI, Choi KJ, et al. Clinical Characteristics of Community-Acquired Viridans Streptococcal Pneumonia. Tuberc Respir Dis (Seoul). 2015;78(3):196-202. DISCLOSURES: no disclosure on file for Ian Britton; No relevant relationships by Victor Chun, source=Web Response no disclosure on file for Gary Ma; No relevant relationships by Rebecca Sell, source=Web Response
