N2  - SESSION TITLE: Wednesday Fellows Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Fellow Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/23/2019 09:45 AM - 10:45 AM INTRODUCTION: Common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) is the most commonly encountered immunodeficiency and has known pulmonary complications. Recurrent infection is often regarded as the most common pulmonary manifestation of primary immunodeficiences, but chronic lung diseases such as bronchiectasis, LIP, and granulomatous disease may also be seen. We share an interesting case of CVID presenting as chronic cough. CASE PRESENTATION: 65 year old male presents to pulmonary clinic for daily chronic cough with thick sputum production over the last 2 years. There were no relieving periods and no specific exacerbating factors. He had been trialed on multiple therapies to target common causes of chronic cough including omeprazole for treatment of GERD, montelukast, fluticasone nasal spray, and loratidine to treat allergic rhinitis, albuterol for reactive airway disease component, as well as multiple courses of antibiotics without any relief. Other medical history includes low-grade non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, OSA, HTN, DM, as well as recurrent ear infections. He is a life-long non-smoker and uses well water.PFTs were normal without evidence of obstructive pulmonary disease. CT imaging revealed bronchial wall thickening without focal airspace consolidation, mild basilar bronchiectasis, no pleural effusion or nodules. His workup included AFB, fungal, and bacterial cultures, RAST and IgE testing, as well as immunoglobulin levels.His immunoglobulin levels returned as IgG 358 (normal 576-1523), IgA 69 (normal 70-400), and IgM 12 (normal 26-251), IgE 9 (normal 10-180). It is worth noting that CVID is associated with lymphoma, most commonly B cell non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, which our patient had. Both immunology and oncology evaluations were concerned his hypogammaglobinemia was associated with his lymphoma rather than a primary immunodeficiency, although both clinically present the same. Ultimately, he was started on IVIg with dramatic improvement in his symptoms. DISCUSSION: As treatment options and life expectancy of patients with CVID and other immunodeficiencies continue to improve, the incidence of pulmonary complications is also likely to increase. It is important to understand and recognize the pulmonary manifestations of CVID that may be encountered in clinical practice. CONCLUSIONS: In this case we highlight the importance of a broad differential and thorough workup for a common pulmonary complaint manifested by a rather uncommon pulmonary disease. Reference #1: Bang TJ et al. Pulmonary Manifestations of Common Variable Immunodeficiency. J Thorac Imaging 2018; 33: 377-383. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Rachel Guthrie, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Rebecca Potfay, source=Web Response
