N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Cryptococcus is an encapsulated yeast typically destroyed by cell-mediated immunity but can become a fatal pathogen, especially in immunocompromised hosts. Presentation of cryptococcus ranges from asymptomatic pulmonary consolidation to disseminated systemic infection. We present a case of an immunocompetent host presenting with chest pain, cough, night sweats, and a mass-like infiltrate ultimately proven to be a large pulmonary cryptococcoma. While the prevalence of malignancy far outweighs that of pulmonary cryptococcosis, this case highlights the importance of including invasive fungal diseases in the differential diagnosis of lung masses even in immunocompetent hosts. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old woman with extensive cardiac history presented with substernal chest pain, nonproductive cough, and new onset drenching night sweats. Chest CT revealed a 4.5 x 3.0 cm mass-like right lower lobe infiltrate with ipsilateral hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy concerning for pneumonia versus neoplasm. She was trialed on two weeks of antibiotics and prednisone with short-term follow-up. Despite empiric therapy, symptoms worsened. Follow-up chest CT revealed increased mass-like consolidation (7.2 x 4.9 cm) with progressive lymph node enlargement concerning for malignancy with nodal metastatic disease. She ultimately underwent bronchoscopy with lavage, radial endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) guided biopsy of mass, and linear EBUS guided needle aspirate of lymph nodes. Mass biopsy revealed inflammatory changes and features consistent with organizing pneumonia whereas lymph node aspirate showed necrotizing granuloma. Fungal cultures subsequently grew C. neoformans. Further diagnostic work-up was negative, including: serum cryptococcal antigen, HIV serology, CD4 count, and screening for underlying chronic disease (diabetes, renal disease, hepatitis, autoimmune and hematologic malignancies). She was started on fluconazole 400 mg daily and had gradual symptom improvement. Interval chest CT revealed decreasing size of the right lung mass-like infiltrate (1.9 x 1.4 cm). DISCUSSION: While pulmonary fungal infections such as cryptococcus in immunocompetent hosts are rare, it is important to include them in the differential diagnosis of lung masses given the high morbidity and mortality of undiagnosed infections which typically respond readily to treatment. Pulmonary cryptococcus in both immunocompetent and immunocompromised hosts can appear as masses or nodules, however cavitation within nodules are more common in the latter. Although cryptococcal speciation can be difficult, it is important to differentiate C. neoformans from C. gattii as the latter is much more likely to cause CNS involvement. Therefore, clinicians should have a low threshold to perform lumbar puncture to evaluate for CNS infection. CONCLUSIONS: Cryptococcus although rare can present in immunocompetent hosts and can mimic lung cancer. Reference #1: Huang C-J, Yang M-C, Ueng SH. Large Cryptococcoma Mimicking Lung Cancer in an HIVNegative, Type 2 Diabetic Patient. J Thorac Imaging. 2005;20:115-117. Reference #2: Anandpara KM, Aswani Y, Hira P. Large Solitary Pulmonary Cryptococcoma Mimicking Lung Carcinoma in an Immunocompetent Patient. Malays J Med Sci. 2018;25(1):114-118. Reference #3: Singh R, Joshi D, Gupta A, Gangane N. Large Pulmonary Cryptococcoma and Cryptococcal Meningitis in an Immunocompetent Patient: A Case Report. Diagnostic Cytopathology. 2010;38(12):929-931. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Brian Boer, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Brittany Hance, source=Web Response
