N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: Legionella pneumophila and Mycoplasma pneumonia co-infections have been infrequently reported as a true co-infection. Fever is reported to be present in up to 74% of the patients presenting with Legionella pneumonia and up to 77% patients presenting with Mycoplasma pneumonia. Here we present an interesting case of Legionella and Mycoplasma co-infection presenting with atypical symptoms. CASE PRESENTATION: A 70-year-old African American woman presented with 2 week history dry cough and generalized body aches. She reported worsening shortness of breath and stated that her legs have been swollen for the past week. She has past history significant for coronary artery disease, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, pulmonary hypertension and COPD. On physical examination, she was afebrile with blood pressure 101/74 mm of Hg, pulse 70/min, respiratory rate 18/min and had O2 saturation of 84% on room air, that improved to 91% on 3 liters/min. Further examination revealed bilateral crepitations in lung bases, bilateral 2+ pedal edema, jugular venous distension and normal heart rhythm. Laboratory examination revealed normal electrolytes and white count, but was noted to have left shift, elevated serum creatinine 2.4 mg/dl, elevated pro-BNP of 7087 pg/ml and mildly elevated troponins. Chest x-ray showed cardiomegaly, increased interstitial markings and suspected dense area of consolidation in left retrocardiac region. Patient was admitted with impression of acute exacerbation of congestive heart failure and pneumonia and was started on furosemide, ceftriaxone and azithromycin and pneumonia workup was sent. Blood culture and influenza testing was negative. Mycoplasma IgM titer was reported elevated (4702 U/ml) and urine legionella antigen also came back positive. Echocardiogram showed ejection fraction 58%, diastolic dysfunction and severely elevated right ventricular systolic pressure (74 mm Hg). Ceftriaxone was stopped and patient was continued on Azithromycin to complete the course. DISCUSSION: Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumonia commonly present as fever and typical symptoms of pneumonia. Data shows that in up to 64% of the patients with mycoplasma pneumonia, at least one other pathogen was identified as the cause of pneumonia. While Mycoplasma pneumonia can present with mild symptoms, Legionella pneumonia usually present with moderate to severe symptoms. It is, nonetheless, extremely unusual for the co-infection of Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumonia to present without typical signs of pneumonia and rather as acute exacerbation of congestive heart failure. Fortunately, the treatment for both Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumonia is similar with azithromycin and levofloxacin among the preferred agents. CONCLUSIONS: Providers should watch out for the atypical presentation of co-infection of Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumonia. Reference #1: Isenman HL, Chambers ST, Pithie AD, et al. Legionnaires' disease caused by Legionella longbeachae: Clinical features and outcomes of 107 cases from an endemic area. Respirology 2016; 21:1292. Reference #2: Lieberman D, Schlaeffer F, Lieberman D, et al. Mycoplasma pneumoniae community-acquired pneumonia: a review of 101 hospitalized adult patients. Respiration 1996; 63:261. Reference #3: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Mycoplasma pneumoniae outbreak at a university - Georgia, 2012. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep 2013; 62:603. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Muhammad Adrish, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Anuhya Alapati, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Umair Ashraf, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Peter Bhandari, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Muhammad K Guhjjar, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jeirym Miranda, source=Web Response
