N2  - SESSION TITLE: Monday Medical Student/Resident Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Med Student/Res Case Rep Postr PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2019 02:30 PM - 03:15 PM INTRODUCTION: The Fleischner Society defines a pulmonary cavity as a gas-filled space, seen as a lucency or low-attenuation area, within a nodule, mass, or area of parenchymal consolidation which has a clearly define wall > 4 mm thick. [1] A cavity is the consequence of a myriad of pathological processes in the lungs from infections to autoimmune diseases and neoplasms. Nontuberculous (NTB) mycobacteria are a group of pathogens that have gained prominence for causing lung cavities and their high level of drug resistance. CASE PRESENTATION: An 80-year-old female with a past medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), sigmoid colon cancer status post a low anterior resection presented to the Emergency Department because of shortness of breath for 2 months along with a productive cough and a 7-lb weight loss. She was noted to be hypoxic on room air. A CT scan of the chest showed bilateral upper lobe pulmonary cavities with intra-pulmonary debris. Her work up, including 3 sputum samples for acid-fast bacilli staining and culture, testing for fungal antibodies and rheumatological serology returned negative. A bronchoscopy was performed with bronchial washings positive for Mycobacterium xenopi. She was evaluated by Infectious Disease and started on anti-tuberculosis treatment (ATT) including ethambutol, rifampin and azithromycin. Six months into her treatment, her sputum cultures returned positive for Mycobacterium avium as well. Her most recent sputum culture unfortunately showed a relapse of M.xenopi. DISCUSSION: Mycobacterial infection, both tubercular and nontubercular (NTM), often affect the upper lobes or superior segments of the lower lobes. Patients at risk of NTM include those with chronic lung disease such as COPD or bronchiectasis, those with abnormal body habitus such as scoliosis and pectus excavadum, and those who are immunocompromised such as HIV and on TNF-α blockers. This particular patient's risks factors for NTM infection were COPD and history of smoking. Patient with M.avium infections commonly have co-infections with other pathogens however co-infection with M.xenopi is relatively rare the bacteria is typically found in Canada, the UK, and Europe. Treatment typically includes an antituberculosis medication, quinolone, and clarithromycin. Therapy is continued until sputum is negative for 12 months. Recurrence rates are high. It is a highly virulent organism with one study reporting a 3-year mortality of 69.1%.[2,3] ] CONCLUSIONS: Patients with cavitary lesions need an extensive evaluation depending on their risk factors as well as chronicity of their illness. The incidence of NTM infections is rising owing in part to the ability to better diagnose these pathogens and diagnosis requires a high index of suspicion. Reference #1: Hansell DM, Bankier AA, MacMahon H, McLoud TC, Müller NL,Remy J. Fleischner Society: glossary of terms for thoracic imaging. Radiology. 2008;246(3):697-722. Reference #2: Andréjak C, Lescure FX, Pukenyte E, et al. Mycobacterium xenopi pulmonary infections: a multicentric retrospective study of 136 cases in north-east France. Thorax 2009; 64: 291–296. Reference #3: Griffith DE, Aksamit T, Brown-Elliott BA, et al. The official ATS/IDSA statement: Diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2007; 175:367-416. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Omer Bajwa, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Briana DiSilvio, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jennifer Leap, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Sheldon Rao, source=Web Response
