N2  - An unintended outcome of cystic fibrosis (CF) newborn screening (NBS) is the detection of infants with a positive NBS but indeterminate diagnostic testing. As CF NBS became more widespread in the USA in the early 2000s, a consensus conference was held in 2009, where the term CFTR-related metabolic syndrome (CRMS) was created to describe these clinical situations (1). In Europe, an analogous term, CF screen positive, inconclusive diagnosis (CFSPID) was developed (2,3). The definitions of CRMS and CFSPID were harmonized at the CF Diagnosis Conference in 2015 (4). The diagnosis of CRMS/CFSPID is given to an individual without signs or symptoms of CF who has a positive CF NBS with 1) normal sweat test results and 2 CFTR mutations with at least 1 mutation having an unclear significance; or 2) intermediate sweat test results and 0 or 1 CF-causing mutations. Initially, little was known about the outcomes of infants with CRMS/CFSPID. One small, single-center study reported that some infants with CRMS/CFSPID can develop clinical features concerning for CF (eg, respiratory culture positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa) and in some cases their sweat chloride concentration can rise into the range diagnostic of CF (5). However, recently several large studies conducted throughout the world have provided more insight into the clinical outcomes of these infants (6-10). Differences in study populations and design make it difficult to compare across studies, but several general findings are apparent. The ratio of CF to CRMS cases ranged from 1.8:1 to 5.3:1. In California, where extended gene analysis identifies numerous mutations with unclear phenotypic consequences, CRMS is actually identified more frequently than CF, with a CF: CRMS ratio of 0.8:1 (7). In general, CRMS infants are pancreatic sufficient, and their nutritional indices are normal. However, the prevalence of Pseudomonas-positive oropharyngeal cultures ranged from 10.7-78.4%, and some studies have also reported respiratory cultures yielding other CF-related pathogens, such as Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. In one study, 11% of CRMS infants were reclassified as CF after additional study of their CFTR mutations revealed that both were CF-causing (8). In a 14-year retrospective study, 48% of CRMS infants were subsequently diagnosed as having CF, but the clinical features upon which this decision was made were not specific for CF (eg, recurrent cough) (6). Studies that were helpful in monitoring these infants included repeat sweat testing and extended genetic analysis (4). The US CF Foundation Patient Registry (CFFPR) added CRMS as a diagnostic category in 2010. An analysis of the first 2 years of patient data performed in 2015 found that CRMS was a relatively common event following CF NBS and that a small proportion of infants (∼10%) developed clinical features concerning for CF (9). We recently reanalyzed the CFFPR from 2010-2018. The full results of this analysis will be presented at the 2019 NACFC.
