N2  - Cystic fibrosis (CF) lung disease is characterized by airway inflammation and infection that ultimately leads to bronchiectasis and lung function decline (1). Episodic increases in respiratory symptoms such as cough and sputum production are known as pulmonary exacerbations (PEx) and are a common cause of morbidity (2). Importantly, PEx in older children and adults have been associated with decline in lung function (3). Although PEx frequently occur in patients with CF (4), there is no standard definition for their diagnosis (5). Causes of PEx are likely multifactorial including new bacterial infections, change in the density of an individual's underlying CF pathogens, viral infections and environmental exposures (6). The lack of standardized definition, variable presentation and multifactorial etiology present additional challenges in the diagnosis and management of PEx. Although it has been well established that CF lung disease begins early in life (7,8), less is known regarding the characteristics of PEx and outcomes in young children (≤6 years). Brumback and colleagues sought to better characterize PEx in young children using data from a multicentered study of inhaled saline (9). They determined that PEx in this age range were common, were more often treated with oral antibiotics and that worse parent-reported outcomes at the time of an illness were associated with PEx (9). Young children are more likely to receive oral antibiotics compared to IV antibiotics for PEx treatment (4,9) but data regarding long-term outcomes following this treatment are limited. Results from two longitudinal studies of PEx in young children differed in findings regarding long-term effect on lung function (10,11). The difference in outcomes may be related to the use of prophylactic antibiotics within the cohort (11) and a relatively small number of patients studied (10). There are key gaps in our knowledge of PEx in infants and young children. Much of the prior PEx research focused on IV antibiotic treatment (3,12) and less is known about treatment with oral antibiotics (13,14). As oral antibiotics are commonly used, a better understanding of outcomes related to oral antibiotic treatment is needed. Additionally, some children fail to recover to baseline FEV1 after PEx treatment with both IV (12) and oral (10) antibiotics. Therefore, antibiotic failure in young children remains an important area of future study to prevent early lung damage and potential lung function decline. The diagnosis of PEx also remains challenging without standard criteria and further research is warranted to better characterize PEx symptoms in order to standardize definitions for use clinically and as an outcome measure in research studies. Objective measurements of lung function in this age range are limited as spirometry in preschool-aged children can be difficult to obtain and is effort dependent for patients to perform. Furthermore, spirometry may not be an accurate reflection of early CF lung disease as structural lung injury can be observed prior to changes in spirometry (15). Thus, measurements of lung function that are less dependent on patient effort and more specific for early lung function changes are needed. A study of multiple breath washout (MBW) in preschool children in the setting of PEx demonstrated significant worsening in values with onset of PEx and improvement following treatment (16). This suggests that MBW may be beneficial in PEx management but further studies are needed to define its role in clinical care. Finally, as CFTR modulators become available to young children, exacerbation frequency and severity will likely be impacted as observed in older individuals (17-19). However, PEx are unlikely to fully resolve with current CFTR modulators and therefore continued research to optimize treatment will remain an important area of study with the goal of preventing lung function decline.
