N2  - Introduction: Chronic cough (>8 weeks duration) is common in children and can be associated with serious underlying disease. There is a lack of evidence relating to the incidence of underlying disease and associated risk factors. Aims: To describe the natural history of children referred with chronic cough and determine parameters associated with unfavourable outcomes (e.g. bronchiectasis, CF, primary immunodeficiency disorders (PID)). Methods: We collected data from a historical cohort of children referred with chronic cough to a tertiary respiratory clinic between August 2017 and December 2018. Clinical notes, investigations and follow-up data were reviewed using a standardised proforma. Incidence and potential risk factors for unfavourable outcomes were determined. Children with pre-existing diagnoses of bronchiectasis, CF or PID were excluded. Results: Data were available from 90 children, 35 (39%) female, median age 4.1y (IQR 1.4-6.5), median time since cough onset 52 weeks (IQR 27-104). Of 46 tested for PID, 7 (15%) were positive (5 low IgA, 1 CD4+ lymphopenia, 1 both). There was no significant association between PID and nature of cough (wet vs dry) (p=0.37), positive bacterial cultures (p=0.30) or duration of cough (p=0.49). Tests for respiratory pathogens were positive in 5/16 (31%) children aged 5 years or older and 1/26 (4%) children aged under 5 years. Conclusions: The higher rate of isolation of respiratory pathogens in older children could be due to different sampling techniques. We speculate, however, that they may also have a different underlying disease process. The prevalence of PID in our cohort highlights the importance of screening in children with chronic cough.
