N2  - Our patient, a 49-year-old female, presented with productive cough, haemoptysis and dyspnoea on a 5-year history of recurrent chest infections, complicated by bronchiectasis. She was found to have an empyema and a bronchopulmonary fistula that required surgical resection and long-term antibiotics. The patient did not mount a fever, despite elevated inflammatory markers, and chest imaging demonstrated granulomas. At different time points, sputum-cultured organisms included Mycobacterium avium complex, Mycobacterium chelonae and Nocardia farcinica. Recurrent infections were not a feature until five years ago, and recent infections were limited to the lungs. Our patient had two bacille Calmette- Guérin (BCG) scars from childhood but did not report a history of BCG infection post-vaccination. There was no family history of recurrent infections and no consanguinity. Laboratory tests demonstrated normal lymphocyte subset numbers, immunoglobulin levels, and neutrophil oxidative burst, but absent interferon-gamma (IFN-y) response within the mitogen tube of the Quantiferon Gold assay. Further analysis of the mitogen tube demonstrated almost undetectable cytokine levels for those found in the IFN-y signalling pathway. Laboratory investigations revealed a serum factor against the IL-12 p40 subunit, not previously thought to be pathogenic in humans. This acquired defect may potentially explain a pathomechanism for patients who present with recurrent non-tuberculous mycobacterial or other intracellular bacterial infections, similar to the recently identified anti-IFN-y antibodies.
