N2  - A previously healthy 18-year old male presented with two months of fatigue and generalized malaise with ten pounds unintentional weight loss. For the preceding two weeks, he developed dyspnea with cough, hemoptysis, nausea, vomiting and drenching night sweats. He is sexually active with females only and did have travel to Central America in the previous eight months. The initial workup included testing for autoimmune disorders, infectious processes, and malignancy. Pertinent positives included an elevated bilirubin (1.5), CRP (35 mg/L), CMV IgG (3.31), IgM (>4), and CMV PCR (1853 copies/mL). HIV testing was negative and EBV serology revealed past infection. CT scan of the head, chest and abdomen revealed splenomegaly and abdominal lymphadenopathy. Upper endoscopy showed scattered ulcerations. Immunohistochemical staining was positive for CMV in the duodenum. He was treated with oral valganciclovir for four weeks and his condition improved. He was well for one month, but went on a spring break trip to Florida where he suffered the recurrence of cough, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis and hematemesis along with new onset diarrhea. Upon readmission, respiratory viral testing and stool culture were positive for adenovirus. AFB culture of the sputum grew Mycobacterium abscessus, ultimately deemed to be non-pathogenic in this patient. CT of the chest showed new bilateral, patchy infiltrates with subsequent bronchoscopy demonstrating severe diffuse airway inflammation. Final BAL cultures for bacteria, viruses and AFB were negative, but CMV PCR of fluid was positive. He improved on ganciclovir and was ultimately discharged on oral valganciclovir after his weight stabilized and appetite improved. By follow-up, his weight was back to baseline, cough and night sweats had resolved, and energy levels were improved. Immunological tests remained negative for any evidence of an abnormal immune system at baseline. CMV, a member of the herpesviridae family, rarely causes severe symptoms in immunocompetent humans, but infections with complications can affect both adults and children. It can be immune suppressing, and an inversion of the CD4/CD8 T-cell ratio has been described in CMV pneumonia previously. This occurred in our patient and may represent a transient T-cell abnormality during severe CMV infection. CMV enteritis is described in the immunocompetent host mainly in children under 1 year and in elderly adults. CMV pneumonia in immunocompetent hosts has been rarely described in the literature. A high index of suspicion for severe CMV disease with lung or gastrointestinal involvement needs to be held by the clinician for any patient presenting with systemic symptoms as seen in this patient. It is essential to rule out other processes including malignancy and autoimmune conditions. Treatment of severe CMV with oral valganciclovir has been reported only a few times in the literature with success, and more studies are needed in this area.
