N2  - Objective: The Cystic Fibrosis (CF) intravenous (IV) antibiotic service offers hospital and home treatment based on patient suitability. This review aimed to compare the safety and efficacy of home vs hospital services. Methods: Information was gathered from the medical records over a 6 month period, including age, reasons for IV's, microbiology, pulmonary function, length of course and time to next course. Results: A total of 97 IV courses were reviewed. 45 children had 57 courses of IVs that were started as inpatients 28 of which were completed at home. 25 patients had 40 courses of IV's started as outpatients. Mean age (years); Inpatients: 8.6, outpatients: 11.3. Approximately 45% of both groups isolated pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA). 20% of inpatients had a symptomatic proven viral infection vs 0% of home patients. Other indications for treatment included cough and decreased FEV1. 54% of home IVs were routine compared to 5% in hospital. Pulmonary function was measured for all inpatients and the majority of those at home. Stability or improvement in FEV1 was demonstrated in the majority of patients. Home patients received support from specialist nurses (1–3 visits) and physiotherapists (1–2 visits). All home IV patients had documented evidence of discussion about safety and none had any allergic reactions. 2 children started their IV course at home but were admitted due to deterioration. The Mean time (days) to next IV course: Hospital: 151, Home: 105. Conclusion: The CF unit offers a comprehensive, responsive service for children receiving IV antibiotics. Younger children and those acutely unwell are all admitted to hospital for their treatment. Children who have routine IV antibiotics generally receive them at home, reducing attendance at hospital, disruption to schooling, home life and activity. Currently there is no home dietetic service, however children are supported by nurses and physiotherapists with no suggested evidence of poorer outcomes.
