N2  - Objective: To evaluate our eradication strategy for adults with a new or reemergent P. aeruginosa (PsA) culture using tobramycin inhalation solution (TIS) in an adult CF centre. Methods: Retrospective cohort study of consecutive CF patients who were treated with intent to render sputum PsA negative after a new PsA culture in either sputum samples or cough swabs May 2017-Dec 2018. Patients were categorised into 2 groups; Group A: first-ever PsA culture, Group B: new P. aeruginosa culture and prior history of PsA-positivity but not on chronic inhaled antibiotics; all patients had a minimum of 6 months with 2 clear samples prior to re-growth. Treatment was 1 month of TSI. PsA culture-negativity was based on sputum taken within 1 month of completing TSI. Baseline clinical characteristics and sputum microbiology were taken from electronic patient records. By protocol, our microbiology laboratory alerts us to all new PsA cultures. Sputum results were collected from subsequent clinic appointments up to a minimum of 6 months after TSI treatment, to assess for ongoing negativity. Results: 27 newPsA cultures, 6 from cough swabs, (13 group A,14 group B) from 27 patients (11 female). Median age 32yrs (IQR 25–40) and median FEV1 80.5% (IQR 57.5–90.5). 20 patients (71%) had PsA successfully eradicated using TSI; 11 (85%) group A, 8 (53%) group B. Of the 20 patients who eradicated PsA, 6 (30%) regrew(2 Group A, 4 Group B). Overall patients who had their sputum rendered PsA negative at 1 month had a median time to re-culturing PsA of 9 months (range 5–18). Conclusion: Sputum can be successfully rendered PsA-negative in adults using one month of TSI. Treatment is more effective in first-ever detection versus re-emergence, with nearly a third of patients re-culturing PsA overall. Prospective clinical trials are needed to test eradication in adults with a new PsA culture, as this is an increasing clinical scenario.
