N2  - The CFTR potentiator ivacaftor has been approved by the FDA for CF patients with gating and residual function CFTR mutations. It has been shown that intestinal current measurements (ICM) are sensitive to detect in vivo activation of CFTR in CF patients with G551D mutations after initiation of ivacaftor therapy. However, data on its effects in CF patients with the splicing mutation 3849 + 10kbC > T are pending. Therefore, we are evaluating the degree of functional rescue and clinical benefit of ivacaftor treatment in a patient carrying this mutation. The patient is compound heterozygous for the CFTR mutations I507del and 3849 + 10kbC > T, resulting in CF with residual function anticipated with a milder progression in comparison to “classical” CF. However, there has been a significant worsening of CF lung disease since autumn 2015 with permanent productive cough and progressive worsening in chest MRI scans. In summer 2016, Pseudomonas aeruginosa was detected, which persisted despite intensive antibiotic therapy. Due to severity and progression of the symptoms, we decided to treat the patient with ivacaftor. Current experimental and clinical data indicate that the mutation 3849 + 10kbC > T is likely to respond to administration of a potentiator, which might lead to an improvement in CFTR function. This led to drug approval by FDA, but not EMA. To evaluate the effects of this therapy we are planning to monitor laboratory values, sputum, lung function, sweat test and clinical condition. Additionally, we are investigating sensitivity of other biomarkers to detect in vivo activation of CFTR in patients with this mutation. Therefore, nasal potential difference measurement (nPD) and ICM were performed before and will be reevaluated 3 months after start of therapy (approx. 03/2019). To our knowledge this will be the first case to evaluate, whether nPD and ICM are sensitive biomarkers to detect in vivo activation of CFTR in patients with a 3849 + 10kbC > T mutation under ivacaftor treatment.
