N2  - Objectives: Cough swabs (CS) are used to sample airway flora. They are limited by poor sensitivity. Ideally a child should cough, allowing airway contents to settle on the CS. In a prospective observational study we assessed how often children coughed during a CS and if there was a difference in microbiology between cough and non-cough swabs. Methods:. Children (<2y) with CF underwent CS by a physiotherapist, preceded by airway clearance. A cough-CS (CCS) was defined as a sample where coughing was observed during the sampling, with minimal contact with the oropharyngeal mucosa or tongue. A no-cough CS (NCCS) was defined as identical sampling technique with no cough observed. Results: 35 children had 89 CS in total (mean 2.5, SD 1.7) in a period of 8 months. 76 (85%) were CCS and 13 (15%) were NCCS. Results were available for 75/76 CCS (99%) and 13/13 (100%) NCCS. 37/75 (49%) CCS isolated at least 1 bacterial species, versus 5/13 (38%) NCCS (p = 0.35, Fisher's exact test). No difference in bacterial species was observed between groups. Conclusion: True CCS were obtained on the majority of occasions. Coughing during a CS did not affect the proportion of swabs in which bacteria were isolated in this study. This finding may be affected by the small number of NCCS. It does not appear that CCS or NCCS affected the species isolated, again this is limited by the small number of NCCS. No child with previous isolation of Pseudomonas aeruginosa on a CCS had a subsequent NCCS for comparison. A definitive study comparing paired samples would address these limitations. Data from this study demonstrate insufficient evidence to discount the possibility that a NCCS is less effective than a CCS.
