N2  - Background: Percussion (Perc) and Positive Expiratory Pressure (PEP) are airway clearance techniques (ACT) used in CF infants. Although there is minimal evidence, there has been a move towards using more PEP within the UK. The centres within our CF network (managing 320 children) practice both treatments.We hypothesised that Perc is non-inferior to PEP as measured by microbiology, admission rates and chest x-ray changes. Objectives: To review the clinical outcomes of 2 ACT's used in infants with CF Methods: Data from 48 infants diagnosed between January 2014 & December 2016 was analysed from across the West Midlands (South & Central) Paediatric CF Network for the 2yrs following their diagnosis. Children were grouped according to the ACT used. Group 1-PEP (n = 8), group 2-Perc (n = 27), group 3-mixed methods Perc > PEP (n = 7), & group 4-mixed methods PEP > Perc (n = 6). Cough swab results, chest x-ray Chrispin Norman score (CNS) & inpatient bed days for respiratory exacerbations were collected for the 4 groups. Results: No differences were noted between the 4 groups in terms of airway pathogens identified by cough swabs, inpatient bed days or CNS. When the Perc predominant group (2,3) were compared with PEP predominant group (1,4), no differences were found for isolation of P. aeruginosa (p = 0.67), S.aureus (p = 0.7) & H.influenzae (0.37) (Table 1). [Table Presented] Conclusions: In our cohort of CF infants, Perc is non-inferior to PEP. Randomised trials comparing both treatment methods are needed to promote uniform practice. Evaluating Parent experience is crucial.
