N2  - Objectives: Early detection of Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection is crucial to avoid or postpone the onset of chronic lung infection in CF patients. Herein, we analyzed the utility of a real-time PCR-based test for screening and early detection of P. aeruginosa, in comparison with microbiological respiratory (sputum or cough swab) culture. Methods: The accuracy of the real-time PCR test was compared with the microbiological culture in a cross-sectional analysis of 101 pediatric CF patients and 52 healthy controls, and a three-year follow-up of 38 patients without P. aeruginosa infection was conducted to assess the test's ability to early detect P. aeruginosa colonization/infection. The real-time PCR test used the P. aeruginosa gyrB (DNA gyrase) gene as the target primer and samples for this test were collected at least once a year. Results: In the cross-sectional analysis, P. aeruginosa was detected in 34 samples by microbiological culture and in 38 samples by real-time PCR. The agreement between the tests was 82% (κ = 0,612; p < 0,001). The realtime PCR test showed values of 79%, 84%, 71% and 89% for sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value and negative predictive value, respectively. In the longitudinal analysis, the test was able to detect P. aeruginosa in respiratory samples with a mean interval of 10.4 months (0.1–34.0) before culture in 13/20 patients who converted the culture results, and 10 (26%) patients converted the real-time PCR results without converting the culture results. Conclusion: A priori, despite some limitations (e.g. low sensitivity in the cross-sectional analysis) and need for further methodological evaluation (in progress), the test shows potential to early detect P. aeruginosa infection and to identify patients who are probably colonized/infected with P. aeruginosa but whose microbiological cultures are negative, and its inclusion in the diagnostic routine of our CF centre can be discussed in the near future.
