N2  - Objectives: More than 2000 mutations described in CFTR gene. Currently, the description of rare variants remains an important task. In the age of new CF targeted therapies, the full characterization of such variants could offered new therapeutic perspectives to patients. Methods: In this work presents a case of mild CF in an adult 31-year-old woman (dagestani nationality). Highly parallel sequencing assay and quantitative MLPA analysis were using for CFTR genotyping. Results: The patient was diagnosed with CF at the age of 3 years (bronchitis and moderately pancreatic insufficiency from the first weeks of life, 74.2 mmol/l sweat chloride concentration). In the future, up to the age of 30, she did not carry out any basic therapy, against the background of a stable state and not a clearly aggravated cough in the autumn-winter period! In 2017 fluorography changes were detected and M.intracellulare was diagnosed. In 2018 was performed sweat chloride retest - 94 mmol/l (≪Nanoduct≫); Pseudomonas aeruginosa was detected in the respiratory tract. Spirometry: FEV1 73%, FVC 86%. No pathological changes have been identified on ultrasonography of the abdominal cavity. There are single bronchiectasis in CT of the lungs. A molecular-genetic analysis revealed two rare (not previously described) CFTR variants: c.3815_3816insTTG (p.Val1272_Ser1273insTrp) and CFTRdele23. Conclusion: Thus, a phenotypically stable patient with no pronounced damage of target organs is presented. The pancreas is clinically intact. It is highly probable that the mutation c.3815_3816insTTG has mild features.
