N2  - Objectives: Identification of respiratory pathogens is essential in the management of cystic fibrosis (CF) to enable timely and efficacious treatment of respiratory exacerbations. The gold standard for identifying pathogens is sputum culture, however many children are unable or unwilling to expectorate. In non-expectorating children, cough swabs (CS) or cough plates (CP) can be used for surveillance cultures. Previous studies comparing the performance of these two types of sample are conflicting. Our objective was to investigate the diagnostic yield of CS and CP. Methods: Children with CF attending Shrewsbury and Telford NHS Trust who do not expectorate sputum provide both CS and CP samples at each outpatient visit. The results of the paired cultures from 2013–2018 were analysed. We excluded positive cultures for non-pathogenic organisms, which included: yeasts, candida and Bacillus species. Results: We identified 647 paired CS and CP samples from 38 patients. Mean (SD) age was 10.4 (8.5) years. The CS and/or the CP was positive for a respiratory pathogen on 116 (18%) of the paired samples. This included 66 (10%) CS and 85 (13%) CP samples. Only 27 (23%) of the paired samples with a positive culture identified the same pathogen(s) on both CS and CP. Number needed to screen (NNS): in this sample 13 children needed both CS and CP samples to identify one additional respiratory pathogen compared to CS alone. Conclusion: The rate of respiratory pathogen isolation was similar for CS and CP samples. Simultaneous use of CP and CS identified more pathogens than either sampling method alone. Further studies are required to investigate if similar improvements could be achieved by obtaining duplicate CS or CP samples.
