N2  - Objectives: The objective of the review was to identify whether performing a cough swab before and after inhaled 7% hypertonic saline, (as part of patients annual review investigations) identified differences in pathogen growth in cystic fibrosis patients under the age of 4 years old Methods: 7 patients (mean 24 months) with a range of 10–39 months attended for annual review investigations during the time of the pilot. Patients included were unable to expectorate sputum and were Pseudomonas Aeruginosa free. Patients already on nebulised hypertonic saline as part of their normal airway clearance regimen were excluded. A cough swab was completed following which patients were pre-treated with inhaled salbutamol and given nebulised 7% hypertonic saline via a jet nebuliser. Patients were monitored throughout for any adverse reaction. A further cough swab was then carried out. The swabs were sent for microbiology culture and sensitivity testing in a specialised cystic fibrosis laboratory Results: 4 patients grewnormal upper respiratory tract flora only on swabs pre and post hypertonic saline. 2 patients grew Candida Albicans on both pre and post cough swabs and 1 patient grew Staphylococcus Aureus on both pre and post cough swabs. Conclusion: No benefit was observed in carrying out induced cough swabs via a jet nebuliser in patients with cystic fibrosis under the age of 4 years who were unable to expectorate sputum. The cost and potential for an adverse reaction to the hypertonic saline means that this is a procedure that would not be beneficial as part of routine annual reviewinvestigations in this age group.
