N2  - INTRODUCTION: Tunisia is a country endemic for tuberculosis (TB) which is a major hindrance to public health. Tuberculosis is an infection that complicates immuno-depressive diseases such as renal failure in chronic dialysis, hence its higher incidence among chronic dialysis patients compared to the general population. It is characterized by the predominance of extra-pulmonary tuberculosis. The diagnostic difficulty is multifactorial. The different locations and their frequencies vary according to the studies. METHODS: We conducted a retrospective descriptive study collecting all cases of extra-pulmonary tuberculosis occurring in adults on chronic dialysis in the nephrology department at La Rabta hospital. The objective of this work is to analyze the diagnostic, therapeutic and evolutionary approach. RESULTS: Forty patients were collected, of whom 34 were in hemodialysis and 6 in peritoneal dialysis. The average age was 49.9 years (18-85 years) with a female predominance (sex ratio 0.73). Thirteen patients came from a rural area with low socio-economic level in 65% of cases. The history of cured pulmonary tuberculosis was noted in 5 patients. Twenty-three patients were vaccinated against the Koch bacillus previously. At the time of diagnosis, 7 patients were on immunosuppressive therapy. The mean interval between the onset of hemodialysis and the time of diagnosis was about 586 months. The clinico-biological picture was highly variable and nonspecific dominated by asthenia, anorexia and weight loss in 92.5% of cases, fever in 50% of cases, night sweats and lymphadenopathy in 35% of cases, chest pain in 22.5% of cases, persistent cough in 15% of cases and haemoptysis in one patient. Biology revealed unexplained biological inflammatory syndrome in 36 cases (90%), lymphopenia in 7 cases (17%), anemia in 38 cases (95%), Hyperferritinemia in 27 cases (65.5%), hypercalcemia in 6 cases (15%) and a relatively low parathyroid hormone in 12 cases (30%). The intradermal reaction to tuberculin was positive in 5 cases. The diagnosis was obtained on bacteriological arguments in 13 cases (32.5%): Mycobacterium tuberculosis was isolated in sputum in a patient, in urine in 4 patients, in the ascites fluid in 3 patients, in ganglionic puncture in 3 patients, and skin examination in 2 patients. Tuberculosis was confirmed histologically in 7 patients (17.5%). Because of the diagnostic difficulty in this particular population, tuberculosis was retained on presumed diagnosis in 22 patients (50%). The localization of tuberculosis was urogenital in 12 patients (30%), peritoneal in 13 patients (30%), pleuropulmonary in 5 patients (12.5%), lymph node in 14 patients (35%), pericardial in 3 patients (7.5%) and bone in 5 patients (12.5%). All our patients were treated with TB drugs.We noted well responses in 30 patients. CONCLUSIONS: The diagnosis of tuberculosis is often difficult in chronic dialysis patients. It is characterized by clinico-biological nonspecificity and the frequency of extrapulmonary localization hence the interest of repetition of bacteriological samples. The extra-pulmonary localization is variable according to the studies, in our series we note the predominance of the ganglionic, peritoneal and urogenital involvement. The prognosis is closely related to the early management of this particular field.
