N2  - Objective To analyze the clinical manifestations, diagnosis and treatment of patients with aspiration pneumonia due to near drowning. Methods The clinical data of 18 inpatients with near-drowning pneumonia from January 1, 2010 to November 30, 2017 at the First Affiliated Hospital of University of Science and Technology of China, Anhui Provincial Hospital were analyzed retrospectively. The related literature was reviewed. Results The common clinical symptoms of the 18 patients included cough, shortness of breath, and chest distress. Disturbance of consciousness was found in 8 patients. Two patients received cardiopulmonary resuscitation before admission to hospital. Eight patients recovered successfully after anti-infective therapy. Ten severe patients received invasive mechanical ventilation due to acute respiratory failure. 9 patients were detected bacterial infection, most of which were caused by Acinetobacter baumannii, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas luteola, Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and other gram-negative bacteria. Two patients were complicated with Aspergillus infection. Eventually, one patient (10%) died due to critical condition. Conclusions Most cases of aspiration pneumonia due to near-drowning were caused by gram negative bacilli, associated with high mortality if complicated with fungal infection in severe cases. Timely etiological diagnosis via bronchoscopy and appropriate antibiotic treatment would be helpful to improve patient outcome.
