N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium gordonae (M. gordonae) is one of the least pathogenic of the mycobacteria. It is usually a contaminant or colonizer in patients who are not infected with HIV. However, multiple reports are implicating it in lung disease regardless of immune status with a higher incidence in immunocompromised patients. (1) We hereby describe a case of pulmonary M. gorodonae in immunocompetent patient. Case presentation: Our patient is 70-year-old man known to have a history of stage 1 colon cancer treated with partial colectomy and Renal Cell Carcinoma in remission, presented with chronic cough and progressive weight loss. He was not on any chemotherapy or immunotherapy. He was prescribed two courses of azithromycin 3 months apart by his PCP due to persistent cough, weight loss and fever. His CXR showed mild patchy infiltrate noted in both lungs consistent with pneumonia. His cough worsened and developed night sweats with chills for which he received a 7 day course of Levofloxacin. His complete blood count, metabolic profile and pro-calcitonin were within normal. A Computed topography of his chest (Fig 1) showed several areas of bilateral nodular infiltrate and ground glass opacities. Bronchoscopy with lavage showed a neutrophil predominant BAL; and culture grew M. gordonae.He was started on triple therapy with ethambutol, Rifampin and Azithromycin with remarkable improvement in his cough, weight loss and resolution of his fevers. Follow-up imaging 6 months later showed resolution of bilateral lung infiltrates. Discussion: Risk factors for developing pulmonary infection secondary to M. gordoane haven't been well established in immunocompetent patients. Case series found that M. gordonae had a predilection for elderly males and chronic lung disease (COPD, history of Active Tuberculosis or Lung carcinoma).The sensitivity and specificity of a positive culture is 67% and 80% respectively with an average timeframe for positive culture detection from 9 to 24 days. (5) The most common High Resolution Computed Tomography findings are bilateral small nodules, cylindrical bronchiectasis, and branching centrilobular nodules. (6) Antimicrobial agents most consistently active in vitro include ethambutol, rifabutin, clarithromycin, linezolid, and the fluoroquinolones with reported resistance to Isoniazid. (7)Regardless of treatment duration and immune status; most of the patients are not cured and goal is to have the disease stabilized. (8) Conclusion: Mycobacterium gordonae is a rare cause of community acquired pneumonia in immunocompetent host. Differentiating infection from contamination is challenging and prolonged treatment is required for disease suppression.
