N2  - Context: Respiratory infections with nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are favored by immunosuppression and/or underlying lung disease. Their incidence is increased in recipients of allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) transplantation. We report our experience in the management of these patients. Methods: Monocentric retrospective study in the Respiratory Department of Foch hospital, located in Paris area, France Reviewing of clinical, radiological, biological and microbiological records of patients treated for pulmonary NTM infection between 2003 and 2017 among the 350 allogeneic HSCT recipients hospitalized in our department for pulmonary manifestations. The diagnosis of NTM infection was made according to the DSA/ATS 2007 criteria. Respiratory samples were systematically cultured on Löwenstein-Jensen and liquid Becton Dickinson medium. Results: 10 patients with pulmonary NTM infection: 7 M. avium complex, 1 M. abscessus, 1 M. kansasii and 1 unidentified mycobacteria. Sputum examination was positive for 7/9 patients, endobronchial aspiration in 3/6 cases. 3 lung biopsies were performed. Co-infection was frequent: aspergillus spp. n=3, pseudomonas aeruginosa n=3, nocardia spp n=1 Time of onset after transplant: median 984.5 days [428.7-1405]; more than two years in 7 patients. 9 patients had chronic Graft Versus Host- Disease (GVHD), 6 had pulmonary GVH. 8 patients were receiving immunosuppressors: corticosteroids n = 8, ciclosporin n = 3, mycofenolate mofetil n = 3, ruxolitinib n = 4. 5 patients were on long-term azithromycin treatment for a GVH-related bronchiolitis. One patient had received recent chemotherapy. 6/10 patients did not present any acute symptoms, 2 patients had fever, 3 suffered from cough, 2 presented an acute respiratory failure. 5/9 patients had abnormal respiratory functional tests: 3 obstructive respiratory pattern (FEV1 <50% predicted), 2 restrictive ventilatory disorder (Vital capacity <40% predicted). All patients had previous underlying chest CT-scan abnormalities. The radiological pattern observed during infection were: pulmonary nodules n = 4, bronchiolar micronodules n = 1, ground glass opacities n = 4, worsening of underlying interstitial lung disease n = 2 3 patients died (5, 36 and 214 days after infection diagnosis): 2 acute respiratory failure and 1 hematological malignancy relapse. The duration of antimycobacterial treatments among survivors was on average 15±5 months. Conclusion: Respiratory infections with NTM after allogeneic HSCT are frequently pauci-symptomatic and occur even at distance from the transplantation in a context of chronic respiratory disease. Ground glass opacities are a common radiological pattern. It may be life threatening and clinicians should be aware of NTM infection in allograft recipients with chronic pulmonary manifestations.
