N2  - Rationale : Pulmonary mycobacterium avium (MAI) infection has long thought to be a indolent disease with progressive dyspnea. However, morbidity may be substantial. This retrospective study attempts to characterize demographic findings, clinical characteristics and prognosis of pulmonary MAI. Method: We retrospectively reviewed all positive mycobacterial cultures between 2015 and 2017 in three academic teaching hospitals in New York City. All cases were reviewed their clinical symptoms and radiographic findings to determine whether patients met ATS/IDSA criteria for pulmonary MAI. Results: Of 2185 AFB culture positives, 1517 grew MAI (69 %), of which 109 patients (7.2 %) were identified with pulmonary MAI. Microbiological diagnosis was made in 76 cases with sputum culture and broncho-alveolar lavage (BAL) in 31. Average age was 68+15 years old and average BMI was 22+6. 21% were Caucasian, 15% African American, 34% Hispanic, and 31% Asian. Fifteen (14 %) patients had previous history of treated pulmonary tuberculosis. Common comorbidities included smoking (50%), COPD (29%), and asthma (13%), while 28% of patients had no comorbid pulmonary disease. The majority of patients (69 patients, 63 %) were worked up for MAI infection in the inpatient setting. Common presenting symptoms include cough (60 %), dyspnea (27 %), constitutional symptoms (26 %), hemoptysis (18 %), and interestingly, community acquired pneumonia (14%). Only 51% of cases presenting to the hospital were recognized as pulmonary MAI during hospitalization. The majority of patients (83%) were discharged to home, but 6% of patients died in hospital, all of whom died due to worsening respiratory failure and sepsis. Of the patients diagnosed in hospital setting, 33% had follow up with pulmonary clinic and 23% of patients received for MAI treatment. Treatment was successful in only 29% of known results; 29% of patient had to stop the treatment due to GI symptoms (1), death (1), or loss to follow up (2). Conclusions: Only minority of positive AFB culture accounts for MAI infection. Unlike traditional belief, the disease was more prevalent in male (55%) in our study cohort and BMI averaged within normal limits. Majority of patients had diagnosis of MAI infection as inpatients, while being treated for recurrent bacterial pneumonia, which suggests pulmonary MAI should be considered in the differential for recurrent pneumonia, but whether the rate of recurrence decreases as a result of MAI therapy has yet to be studied.
