N2  - INTRODUCTION: Empyema necessitans (EN) is a rare complication of a necrotizing pleural infection that dissects across the parietal pleura and invades surrounding tissues. Most cases of EN are related to mycobacterial or Actinomyces species infections, but streptococcal infections, especially with species from the S. anginosus group, have also been reported to cause EN.1- 3CASE REPORT: A 61-year-old man with asthma, nicotine dependency, and alcoholism presented with two weeks of productive cough, chills, dyspnea, and right-sided pleuritic pain. One month before he had been admitted to a local hospital for pneumonia with a pleural effusion managed with tube thoracostomy, however he left against medical advice. On admission, he had low-grade fever with otherwise stable hemodynamics and good oxygenation on room air. Right breath sounds were decreased, and there were soft diffuse rhonchi. Laboratory tests revealed white blood cell count 10.5 k/μL (85% neutrophils), hemoglobin 8.2 mg/dL, and negative HIV. Initial chest radiography demonstrated a large right pleural effusion (Figure 1, Panel A). Several hours after admission, he had acute worsening of dyspnea as well as facial and chest swelling with crepitus throughout. Repeat chest radiography (Figure 1, Panel B) demonstrated interval development of spontaneous subcutaneous emphysema, evacuation of the previously seen pleural cavity, and an increased soft-tissue density in the right lateral chest wall. The patient was emergently taken to the operating room for thoracotomy with decortication. Cultures from the pleural fluid grew Strepotococcus constellatus, and the patient had a good clinical response to a prolonged course of beta-lactam antibiotics.DISCUSSION: This case is a unique presentation of a common clinical entity that can have serious morbidity and mortality. Although pleural infections with streptococcal species are relatively common in patients with alcoholism and recurrent aspiration, EN from spontaneous progression through the chest wall is very rare. Our patient developed EN spontaneously within hours of presentation while awaiting chest tube placement. Streptococcus constellatus is a member of the anginosus group of streptococcal species, which is a group that has been been associated with necrotizing chest infections, although this appears to be the first specific report of S. contellatus in EN.4,5 Emergent pleural intervention is required for all types of empyema in order to avoid progression to EN, as well as other complications such as sepsis, multisystem organ failure, and death.Figure 1: Chest X-rays demonstrating initial presentation of empyema (panel A) and then spontaneous evolution of empyema necessitans (panel B). (Figure Preseted) .
