N2  - We report the presentation of a patient with pulmonary nodules found to reflect pulmonary syphilis infection. This entity is exceedingly rare with fewer than 15 cases in the literature since 1967. Case Report: A 74 y/o male was brought by family to the Emergency Department for progressive confusion. His only other symptom was four weeks of dry cough. Upon initial examination, he was disoriented with tangential speech. He was unstable upon ambulation with a wide based gait. Initial laboratory evaluation was notable for a modest elevation in his WBC to 11.2 (103 /μL) and creatinine elevation from baseline 0.6 to 1.2 (mg/dL). Given his acute neurologic changes, he underwent a lumbar puncture which revealed pleocytosis (18 nucleated cells) with lymphocyte predominance (85%), normal glucose, elevated protein: 54 mg/dL (normal < 45 mg/dL) and negative gram stain. The patient also underwent a CT scan of his chest, given his only other localizing symptom, which revealed two >1cm pleural-based nodules with minimal surrounding ground glass infiltrate. One of these nodules was biopsied and showed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation without an organism on typical staining. After biopsy, the patient's RPR came back positive at 1:128, and his CSF VDRL was also positive. He was diagnosed with secondary syphilis with pulmonary and CNS involvement and was initiated on IV penicillin therapy. Follow-up CT showed complete resolution of the nodules after 6 weeks of antibiotic therapy. Discussion: Pulmonary syphilis is a rarely reported condition, with a handful of cases. There are several challenges in establishing the diagnosis, including low incidence, relative lack of symptoms, reluctance to report exposures, variable clinical presentation, lack of direct evidence of T. pallidum in bronchopulmonary samples, and the widespread use of penicillinbased antibiotics. Recognizing these challenges, some have proposed clinical criteria, including a) historical and physical findings typical of secondary syphilis, b) serologic test results positive for syphilis, c) pulmonary abnormalities seen on imaging, d) exclusion of other etiologies of pulmonary disease, and e) response to appropriate anti-syphilis therapy. In this case, the patient had confirmed syphilis by serum testing. These factors, when taken together, lead to a diagnosis of pulmonary syphilis. The differential diagnosis of the necrotizing granulomatous inflammation includes fungal infections, mycobacterium tuberculosis, rheumatoid nodules, and granulomatous polyangiits (GPA). However, none of these processes respond to IV penicillin therapy alone. With a recent upswing in the incidence of syphilis, clinicians should have heightened awareness of this diagnosis. (Figure Preseted) .
