N2  - Background: The identification of Mycobacterium tuberculosis in respiratory specimen of a patient with pleural effusion is diagnostic of tuberculosis. A safe, noninvasive technique to get respiratory secretions in such patients is sputum induction. Majority of patients with no parenchymal involvement on chest radiograph have dry cough, thereupon sputum studies are typically overlooked.Rationale: The rationale of the study was to evaluate the diagnostic yield of Acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear, culture for mycobacterium tuberculosis and Xpert MTB/Rif assay in induced sputum specimen in suspected pleural tuberculosis patients. Methods: This was a cross sectional study in which 62 patients out of 106 have been evaluated in Jinnah post graduate medical center Karachi from April 2016 to April 2017. Patients with exudative lymphocytic pleural effusion which was confirmed to be tuberculous on histopathology underwent sputum induction. Samples of induced sputum were sent for AFB smear (Ziehl-Neelson staining), observed under fluorescence microscope, culture(Lowenstein Jensen medium) for mycobacterium tuberculosis and Xpert MTB/Rif assay. Result:A total of 62 patients were enrolled in the study, out of which 40 (64.51%) were males and 22 (35.48%) were females. Mean age of the study population was 33.52 ±11.0 years.The most commonly reported symptoms in order of decreasing frequency were fever in 48 (77.41%) patients, cough in 45(72.58%) and dyspnea in 39 (48.38%). Out of 62 patients right sided pleural effusion was seen in 30 (48.38%), left sided in 29 (46.77%) and bilateral pleural effusion in 3(4.83%) patients. A total of 14 (22.58%) pleural tuberculosis patients had evidence of tuberculosis in sputum samples; 4 (6.45%) were smear positive, 11 (17.74%) culture positive and 14 (22.58%) gene expert positive. Conclusion:This study concludes that testing sputum induction samples for mycobacterium tuberculosis gives another approach to diagnosing pleural tuberculosis especially in settings where invasive procedures are less accessible. This study also emphasizes the contagiousness of pleural tuberculosis and the need to screen household contacts of these patients.
