N2  - Introduction: Most commonly is seen in immunosuppressed patients with cystic lung disease or malignancy, Achromobacter has been reported in increasing frequency in recent years. We present a unique case of Achromobacter xylosoxidans pneumonia in the setting of sarcoidosis. Case: 69 year old female known with Stage IV pulmonary sarcoidosis, pulmonary embolism on warfarin presented with 1 day history of worsening headaches and productive cough. On vitals, her blood pressure was 159/47 mmHg, heart rate of 77 beats per minute, respiratory rate of 22 breaths per minute, temperature of 100.1 degrees, and she was saturating at 96% on room air. Examination revealed a drowsy female who was easily arousable with an otherwise normal neurological examination. Auscultation of the chest revealed coarse breath sounds bilaterally and decreased breath sounds in the right lower zone with egophony. Computerized tomography of the chest showed left sided fibrosis and lingular bronchiectasis, right lower lobe consolidation with a small right sided pleural effusion. She developed hypoxic respiratory failure requiring endotracheal intubation. Bronchoscopy revealed copious, thick, green secretions & bronchoalveolar lavage cultures resulted positive for Achromobacter xylosoxidans sensistive. Despite course of IV cefepime, she continued to be febrile with increasing leukocytosis, unable to be weaned from mechanical ventilation. Repeat bronchoalveolar cultures resulted positive for Achromobacter xylosoxidans, now resistant to cefepime. Antibiotic therapy was switched to meropenem, percutaneous tracheostomy was inserted & she is still being weaned from mechanical ventilation. Discussion: Achromobacter xylosoxidans is an aerobic, motile, oxidase and catalase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting, gram-negative bacillus. It was previously seen as a nosocomial infection but the prevalence of community acquired infection is increasing. Historically it has been detected in blood, CSF, stool, urine, sputum, peritoneal fluid, skin, ear discharge, wounds, abscesses, bone, joints, endocardium, and central venous catheters. A. xylosoxidans is most commonly found as a pulmonary source and presents as pneumonia in cystic fibrosis patients. It is an emerging opportunistic pathogen and in non-cystic fibrosis patients it is most commonly associated with immunosuppressed patients particularly those with solid organ malignancies and bronchiectasis of different etiologies, A. xylosoxidans is a virulent organism that is known to survive in sterile environments such as chlorhexidine and ultrasound gel. It's patterns of resistance have remained similar since the 1970s with resistance to aminoglycosides, aztreonam, tetracyclines, and certain penicillins and cephalosporins, as demonstrated in our case. If achromobacter is suspected, it is wise to broaden antibiotic coverage with a 4th generation cephalosporin or carbapenem. (Figure Preseted) .
