N2  - Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) has become a common problem in the era of HIV and antiretroviral treatment. Paradoxical responses to tuberculosis treatment predate the HIV pandemic and continue to occur, albeit in much smaller numbers, in HIV-negative individuals receiving treatment for active tuberculosis. A 34-year-old previously healthy man presented to the emergency department with 3 months of productive cough, fever and chills, night-sweats, and weight loss. He had never smoked and denied having any ill contacts. He emigrated from Guatemala 10 months before his presentation. He reported a similar but briefer episode 6 months prior which resolved after treatment with azithromycin and cefpodoxime. He was febrile, tachycardic, and cachectic. Blood work was remarkable only for anemia and hyponatremia. HIV was ruled out. His chest radiograph showed extensive interstitial and airspace opacities of the right upper lobe. Sputum was positive for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) and antitubercular treatment was initiated promptly. His fever resolved but recurred five days later. Repeat imaging appeared unchanged. Mycobacterial drug resistance and bacterial superinfection were ruled out. A decision was made to initiate systemic corticosteroids to treat tuberculosis-associated IRIS. Over the following days his fever receded and his respiratory symptoms improved. Tuberculosis associated immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) is characterized by the body's immune response against M. tuberculosis bacterium that can occur during or after treatment of TB. The syndrome manifests as an immune response due to the destruction of bacterium during treatment. TB-IRIS more commonly occurs in individuals with HIV, but can also rarely occur in HIV-uninfected individuals, usually presenting as extrapulmonary TB (1). Clinically, individuals may present with fevers, shortness of breath, increased lymphadenopathy, and worsened chest x-ray findings after appropriate therapy is started (2). Mortality in these patients is reported to be low (3). TB-IRIS in non-infected HIV patients has been reported, but a systemic analysis is lacking (4). Per report, 120 cases were reviewed, but only 21 were intrapulmonary TB, denoting the rarity of our case presentation. The other reviewed cases were extrapulmonary TB. To date, there are no diagnostic tests for IRIS. The diagnosis is dependent on high clinical suspicion. Its disease progression can mimic multidrug resistant (MDR) TB or a superimposed infection, which were ruled out in our patient. The diagnostic criteria established by Lanzafame was used to make our diagnosis (5). This case in particular demonstrates the importance of recognition and diagnosis of a rare, but manageable disease.
