N2  - Ashley L. Garrett, MD1, Alexis Christie, MD1, Holenarasipur R. Vikram2, MD, Prasad Panse, MD3, Lewis Wesselius, MD1 Mayo Clinic Arizona, Department of Medicine, Divisions of Pulmonary Medicine1, Infectious Disease2 and Department of Radiology3 Background: Bronchiectasis not associated with cystic fibrosis (non-CF) is a risk factor for airway infections with a variety of pathogens. Nocardia species have been isolated with increasing frequency from respiratory tract samples obtained from patients with non-CF bronchiectasis who are immunocompetent (IC), but it is not known whether there are distinct clinical features of this patient group. Methods: A retrospective review of all patients at our institution with positive respiratory tract samples for a Nocardia species (2007-2018) was performed. Medical records were reviewed to determine IC status and clinical features associated with each IC patient. Chest computed tomography (CT) imaging was then reviewed to determine presence of bronchiectasis. Results: Positive respiratory samples from a total of 206 patients were identified and 52 patients (25%) in this group were IC. CT imaging demonstrated bronchiectasis without significant other radiologic abnormality in 36 of 52 (67%) patients, with no bronchiectasis noted in 16 patients. Clinical features of IC patients with and without bronchiectasis were compared. IC patients with Nocardia and bronchiectasis were more frequently female (77% vs 45%), had fewer pack years of smoking (mean 6.4 vs 29.5, p<0.001) and were less likely to have evidence of extensive emphysema on chest CT (0% vs 31%, p< 0.001) compared with patients without BE. Respiratory symptoms were reported in 32/36 (89%) IC patients with bronchiectasis and included cough (77%) and dyspnea (20%). Nocardia was the only airway pathogen in 16 of these patients (44%), with the most common co-pathogen being Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare. Treatment directed at Nocardia resulted in rapid clinical improvement in 32/36 (89%) patients within a 6 month follow-up period. Conclusions: These findings indicate bronchiectasis is a risk factor for Nocardia airway infections in IC patient and these patients have clinical features similar to those in patients with bronchiectasis and nontuberculous mycobacteria infections. Nocardia warrants clinical consideration as a pathogen in this patient population and special stains and culture techniques to identify and isolate Nocardia sp. should be utilized on respiratory samples from these patients.
