N2  - Introduction In immunocompetent hosts non-typhoidal salmonella (NTS) infection is a common cause of gastroenteritis, with less than 5% of cases developing secondary bacteremia. In contrast, immunocompromised patients are at much higher risk of developing NTS bacteremia. A small proportion of those with NTS bacteremia can seed nearly any body site, including joints, bones, meninges, or the lungs. Rarely has direct inhalation of NTS been described as a cause of salmonella pneumonia. Case A 51-year-old woman with past medical history significant for alcohol induced hepatic steatosis and ascites presented with several days of worsening confusion, abdominal distention, and productive cough. She had recently cleaned the mulch of her indoor pet reptiles (four turtles and two iguanas). On admission, she was afebrile, with a heart rate of 101, blood pressure of 86/52, and normal oxygen saturation. Laboratory data was remarkable for leukocytosis with left shift, hyponatremia, and lactic acidosis. Chest x-ray demonstrated a left lower lobe infiltrate. She rapidly deteriorated, developing septic shock and refractory hypoxemia requiring prone ventilation. Repeat imaging demonstrated diffuse, patchy bilateral opacities. Blood, peritoneal, and urine cultures showed no growth. Streptococcus pneumoniae and legionella urine antigens were negative, and HIV and Hepatitis C serologies were nonreactive. Her sputum culture grew salmonella species. The Colorado Department of Health confirmed non-typhi Salmonella. The patient was treated with appropriate antibiotics and had a prolonged hospital course requiring tracheostomy. Discussion Invasive NTS is best described in HIV patients in Africa, where this infection is a more prevalent cause of bacteremia and is often fatal. One mechanism underpinning invasive infection in this population is a reduction of interleukin-17 (IL-17) producing T cells, resulting in decreased neutrophil recruitment to the site of infection. Similarly, animal studies of bacterial pneumonia have shown reductions in IL-17 in the lung of animals with alcohol exposure compared to controls. Given our patient's alcohol use, we postulate that she was at higher risk for developing NTS pneumonia. She was likely exposed to NTS from her turtles and iguanas, which are known to be colonized by salmonella. This case is unique given the absence of preceding gastrointestinal symptoms or bacteremia. Since NTS was only isolated in her sputum and she had recently cleaned the habitat of her reptiles, we believe that she developed primary NTS pneumonia as a result of inhalation of NTS rather than the typical route of infection via gastrointestinal absorption leading to bacteremia and secondary pneumonia.
