N2  - RATIONALE: Maintenance of lung homeostasis relies on the removal of bacterial pathogens, fungal spores and apoptotic cells by macrophage phagocytosis. In chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), this process is defective and may be associated with increased bacterial colonisation and chronic inflammation. Dynein motors, which transport cargo via the microtubule network, are key components of the phagocytic process. Dynein drives phagocytic cup expansion, and transports the phagosome, permitting lysosome fusion and foreign particle destruction. We hypothesise that there is aberrant activity of dynein motors in COPD macrophages, leading to defective phagocytosis. METHODS: Monocytes from non-smokers, smokers and COPD patients were incubated in granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) for 12 days to generate monocyte-derived macrophages (MDM). MDM were treated with media (control) or ciliobrevin D (10μM), a dynein inhibitor, for 30 min, prior to incubation for up to 4h with fluorescently-labelled Haemophilus influenzae. Phagocytosis was measured by fluorimetry, or cells were fixed and tubulin and dynein imaged using widefield microscopy. The image analysis programme ICY was used to quantify the intracellular dynein staining. RESULTS: COPD MDM (n=10) showed reduced phagocytosis of H. influenzae (2.8±0.6 RFUx103) compared to smokers (n=3) (4.4±0.5 RFUx103), and non-smokers MDM (n=7) (6.9± 1.1 RFUx103) (p<0.01). This defect may be attributed to impaired dynein activity, as ciliobrevin D had no effect on phagocytosis in COPD MDM (n=6), yet in non-smoker (n=5) and smoker (n=5) MDM, dynein inhibition caused a significant decrease in H. influenzae phagocytosis by 75% (p<0.0001) and 36% (p<0.01), respectively. Consistent with this finding, a decrease in dynein spot count was observed at 15min in MDM from non-smokers (n=3) (-31%) (p<0.05) and smokers (n=3) (-17%) exposed to H. influenzae and compared to baseline, suggesting dynein coalesces in the cell interior during phagocytosis. This decrease at 15min is reversed by ciliobrevin D in non-smoker (30%) (p<0.001) and smoker MDM (26%). In contrast there is no change over time in both untreated and ciliobrevin D treated MDM from COPD patients (no difference in spot counts), suggesting there is already reduced movement of dynein in these cells, resulting in reduced phagocytosis. CONCLUSON: Inhibition of dynein reduced phagocytosis and dynein movement in control macrophages but not in COPD cells. Taken together, these data suggest that there is a defect in dynein motor function in COPD macrophages. The motor protein network may provide a novel therapeutic avenue to improve phagocytosis in COPD patients.
