N2  - INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium abscessus is a rapidly growing mycobacterium that, in the setting of an active erm (41) gene, may have poor treatment outcomes. We present a case of rapidly progressive Mycobacterium abscessus subspecies abscessus in a patient with a new diagnosis of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). CASE REPORT: A 29-year-old woman with cystic bronchiectasis of unclear etiology and known Mycobacterium abscessus subspecies abscessus infection (with active erm gene suggesting inducible macrolide resistance) was evaluated in clinic for persistent fevers, weakness, and cough. Prior cystic fibrosis workup was negative, including sweat chloride testing and gene mutation panel. At an outside hospital, she had previously received one year of moxifloxacin, azithromycin, and intravenous amikacin for M. Abscessus. Prior sputum cultures grew Nocardia and M. avium complex (MAC), previously treated with sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim and azithromycin, rifampin, and ethambutol, respectively. Despite treatment, serial computerized tomographic (CT) scans of the chest demonstrated worsening bronchial dilation and endobronchial mucoid impaction (Figure 1). Sputum cultures remained positive for M. Abscessus and MAC, and she had progressive dyspnea. Although prior serum Immunoglobulin E (IgE) levels were not diagnostic of ABPA, serum aspergillus antigen and repeat total IgE levels were sent given her worsening imaging. Both study results were significantly elevated and diagnostic of ABPA. Given active co-infection with M. Abscessus, the patient was electively admitted to the hospital to begin intravenous amikacin, imipenem, and tigecycline for M. Abscessus, with a plan to initiate itraconazole and prednisone shortly after. An inpatient bronchoscopy was also performed and a bronchial cast was extracted. In the days after the bronchoscopy, she had continued cough with scant hemoptysis. One week later she developed massive hemoptysis that resolved spontaneously. CT angiography demonstrated large complex collection at the right lower lobe with multiple cystic components, air fluid levels, and worsening multifocal bronchiolar impaction. Interventional radiology performed a right lower lobe bronchial artery embolization. Thoracic surgery performed a robotic right lower lobe lung resection, lymph node dissection of bulky lymphadenopathy, and chest tube insertion. Surgical pathology demonstrated bronchiolitis with airway ulceration, granulomatous inflammation, and allergic mucin. Postoperatively, she was continued on the aforementioned antimicrobial regimen with improvement in symptoms and radiographic findings. DISCUSSION: M. Abscessus infection can cause relentlessly progressive pulmonary disease in patients with underlying cystic bronchiectasis. The coincidence of ABPA is rare but, when present, can significantly increase morbidity and mortality. A multidisciplinary approach to managing complications of these infections is necessary.
