N2  - Introduction About 10.4 million new cases of tuberculosis with 1.7 million deaths are recocrded every year. More than 95% of tuberculosis related deaths occur in low-income countries. Among the comorbidities associated with tuberculosis, chronic kidney disease is among those contributing to a high mortality rate in tuberculosis patients. Several studies have focused on characteristics of tuberculosis in patients with chronic kidney disease; studies aiming to determine the prevalence of tuberculosis in this group are scarce. The aim of this study was to determine the prevalence of tuberculosis and its associated factors in patients on chronic hemodialysis at the Douala General HospitalMethods: We conducted a cross-sectional study over a period of 6 months. Using a consecutive sampling, we included all consenting patients who had been on hemodialysis for at least 3 months and who were able to cough and produce sputum. The diagnosis of tuberculosis was done using a chest x-ray, sputum examination for acid fast bacilli, culture of mycobacterium tuberculosis, geneXpert and biological characteristics of ascitis or pleural fluid. Logistic regression was used to identify factors associated with tuberculosis. A p-value of less than 0.05 was considered significant. Results: In total, 153 chronic hemodialysis patients were included. The mean age of participants was 46.5 ± 13.2 years. The median time from onset of dialysis to diagnosis of tuberculosis was 41 [interquartile range 21-75] months. The diagnosis of tuberculosis was made in 20 chronic hemodialysis patients, giving a prevalence of 13.1%. Of these patients 8 (5.2%) were already known tuberculosis and 12 (7.8%) were newly diagnosed. Tuberculosis infection occurred late (> 1 year after the commencement of dialysis) in 80% of cases. A high proportion of pulmonary tuberculosis was found (70%) as compare with extra-pulmonary tuberculosis. The only associated factor with tuberculosis was HIV infection (P = 0.021).Conclusion: Tuberculosis is a common condition in chronic hemodialysis patients in Douala General Hospital. Patients on hemodialysis for more than one year are the most affected and HIV infection is the only associated factor.
