N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium xenopi is a rare and fatal infection in patients with impaired immunity and/or structural lung disease. Case Report: A 62-year-old woman presented to clinic with progressive dry cough and dyspnea on exertion for one year. Her exercise tolerance of one block (stable for 5 years), decreased to half a street block with no associated wheeze, fevers/chills, edema, weight loss, hemoptysis, or night sweats. She was a former smoker with COPD, HIV (CD4 of 350) and a prior non-biopsy proven diagnosis of sarcoidosis. On physical exam, she was afebrile with a normal blood pressure, respiratory rate, and pulse. Pulse-oximetry revealed an oxygen saturation of 93% on room air with decreased breath sounds throughout the left lung. Labs were within normal limits except for an ACE level of 81. Pulmonary function tests showed severe diffusion impairment (<30% predicted). Steroids were subsequently initiated for presumed active sarcoidosis. Chest imaging revealed extensive fibrotic and cystic disease with mediastinal lymphadenopathy. She underwent a bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsies (TBBXs) as well as an endobronchial ultrasound. M. xenopi was isolated from respiratory specimens (sputum, BAL fluid). Non-caseating granulomas were not identified on histopathology. Repeat BAL and TBBXs were performed three months later to rule out contamination, which showed persistent mycobacterial disease. Discussion: There are less than 1000 reported M. xenopi cases, yet the diagnosis is associated with high mortality (up to 69%). Identification of M. xenopi can pose a diagnostic dilemma: it is a slow-growing non-tuberculous mycobacterium and advanced molecular techniques are required for confirmatory testing. Radiographically, M. xenopi can mimic sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and malignancy. M. xenopi is a thermophile and the distinction between colonization and disease may be difficult upon isolation. The American Thoracic Society (ATS) requires three bronchial washes with a positive culture and or acid-fast bacilli stain (AFB) in varying computations to confirm the diagnosis. Our patient met diagnostic criteria for significant disease and treatment with a macrolide-containing regimen is recommended in symptomatic HIV patients to prolong survival. M. xenopi was not an environmental contaminant in our patient and early recognition was necessary to prevent delay in treatment. Conclusions: Sarcoidosis in our patient was initially diagnosed with radiographic findings and an elevated ACE level. However, in the absence of non-caseating granulomas on multiple tissue specimens, in an HIV patient, active mycobacterial and/or fungal infection should be investigated.
