N2  - INTerferon alpha-2b topical eye drops (IFNa2b) have been used off label for the treatment of ocular squamous cell carcinoma due to its limited ocular toxicity. IFN triggers an immunologic host response through regulation of its antimicrobial effects and production of cytokines. In contrast, adverse effects of these cytokines, through the production of IL-1 and IL-10 receptor antagonist, causes attenuation of IFN induced macrophage activation, which promotes, rather than control, mycobacterium infections. A 70-year-old Hispanic man with history of COPD had left conjunctival squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) successfully treated in 2015. IFNa2b eye drops (ED) were prescribed by Ophthalmology in 2017 due to recurrence of SCC. After one month of treatment he began to come to emergency room due to episodes of dry cough and shortness of breath. Chest imaging showed chronic bilateral apical pleural/parenchymal opacities that increased mildly in size. Chest CT scan showed development of a new cavitary lesion in the superior segment of left lower lobe. Acid Fast Bacillus Sputum (AFB) culture grew positive for MAI complex sensitive to clarithromycin. Laboratories results were negative for HIV disease, Tuberculin skin test and Quantiferon. IFNa2b therapy was discontinued since it was thought it was contributing to flu-like symptoms. Patient was started on azithromycin, ethambutol and rifampin. Follow up AFB sputum 6 months after starting therapy were negative and Chest CT scan at 8 months after treatment showed the left lower lobe cavitary lesion decreased in size. Patient is expected to complete triple antibiotic therapy for one year and has remain free of symptoms. Upon literature review, this is the first documented case of MAI infection as a result of this eye immunomodulator. This side effect should be considered when prescribing this medication.
