N2  - Background: In Mozambique Kaposi's sarcoma (KS) is the most frequent tumor observed in patients with HIV infection. Pulmonary involvement in Kaposi sarcoma (PSK) occurs in immunosuppressed patients who had preceding mucocutaneous lesions (MCKS) and leads to considerable morbidity. This study aims to determine the clinical and laboratorial pattern of MCKS with and without pulmonary involvement. Furthermore, we determine factors associated with severity of PKS and mortality. Methods: Individuals with MCKS and HIV infection admitted to Maputo Central Hospital (MCH) were recruited from 2016 through 2018. They completed a questionnaire with demographic data and medical history. Blood tests were performed for CD4 count, hemoglobin and platelet count; chest X-ray, bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage for AFB smear and culture, routine bacterial culture, cytology, fungi and HHV8 DNA levels. Results: 196 patients enrolled, 118 (60.2%) were male with age ranging from 18 to 75 years old (Median=34.0 IQR=30.0-41.0). 183 (93.4%) patients were on ART before KS diagnosis and in 91 (51.4%) ART initiation was less than 1 year prior and 21 (10.7%) more than 5 years. At the time of KS diagnosis, CD4 median was 160 (IQR= 40.5-293.5) and Hgb median was 9.7 g/dL (IQR 8.4-11.3). PKS was identified in 91 (46.4%) by bronchoscopy of which 48 (53.3%) had KS stage T1S1. Multiple logistic regression revealed factors associated with PKS development (Table 1): low education level (OR=2.0, 95% CI=1.10-3.77, p-value = 0.024), dyspnea (OR=2.73, 95% CI=1.04-7.18, p-value=0.041) and X-ray with reticular infiltrate (OR=8.83, 95%CI=2.34-33.32, pvalue= 0.001) and pleural effusion (OR=5.23, 95%CI=1.12-24.32, p-value=0.035). Higher Hgb level and older age appeared protective against development of PKS, respectively (OR= 0.67, 95% CI= 0.52-0.85, p-value= 0.001; OR=0.93, 95% CI=0.88-0.99, p-value=0.016). Detection of opportunistic infections by bronchoscopy included: bacterial pneumonia 60 (30.6%), pulmonary tuberculosis 37 (18.8%), pleural tuberculosis 9 (4.5%), Histoplasma capsulatum 15/47 (31.9%), pneumocystis jiroveci 12/47 (19.1%) and dual infection with pneumocystosis and histoplasma 9/47 (19.0%). Follow up at 3 and 6 months was achieved in 135 (68.8%), 44 (32.1%) deaths were reported and among these 35 (61.3%) before 6 months. Factors associated with mortality by multi-variate regression analysis included: educational level, chemotherapy for 1 to 5 years, KS stage, female sex, dry cough, chest pain and absence of interstitial infiltrate. Conclusions: Half of those with MCKS presented with PKS independent of CD4 level despite ART treatment. Multiple infections were detected with clinical presentations indistinguishable from PKS. A higher mortality was observed at 6-month of follow-up. (Table Presented).
