N2  - INTRODUCTION: Cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is relatively common in immunocompromised patients. CMV pneumonia in an immunocompetent young patient causing severe morbidity has been rarely reported. CASE: A 33-year-old male presented with chief complaints of high-grade fever, cold sweats and arthralgia for ten days. He had recently travelled back from India. The patient then developed a cough productive of whitish sputum and severe shortness of breath requiring oxygen by nasal cannula. Physical exam was remarkable for multiple fever spikes, coarse breath sounds and lymphadenopathy. Laboratory investigations were remarkable for normal WBC count, ALT 201 U/L, AST 122 U/L and ALP 62 U/L. Infectious workup including HIV was negative. Chest film showed diffuse nodular bilateral lung disease. CT chest revealed bilateral pleural effusions, left greater than right, with bilateral lower lobe infiltrates. The patient initially received antibiotics and antimalarials but continued to spike fevers. Subsequent viral serologies were significant for highly elevated CMV IgM titer > 240 and atypical lymphocytes on peripheral smear. CMV quantitative PCR was > 6930. Workup for tropical parasites and other viruses was negative. The patient was started on anti-CMV treatment with intravenous ganciclovir resulting in rapid resolution of symptoms and establishing the diagnosis of acute CMV infection. DISCUSSION: CMV is a well-documented pathogen in neonates, pregnant and immunocompromised individuals causing significant mortality and morbidity. However, the clinical burden is much less reported in immunocompetent patients. Owing to its wide tissue tropism, CMV can mimic a wide array of clinical manifestations including colitis, encephalitis, retinitis and pneumonia; posing a significant diagnostic dilemma to clinicians resulting in delayed treatment.However, deterioration of oxygenation status and extensive lung infiltrates in the absence of known bacterial or fungal pathogen should raise a clinical suspicion of CMV infection in patients with pneumonia. Quantitive nucleic acid amplification tests for blood CMV DNA, CMV pp65 antigenemia test on histology/immunohistochemistry are recommended diagnostic tools. Currently data is limited but oral valganciclovir or intravenous ganciclovir are recommended as first-line treatment for at least 2 weeks.CONCLUSION: CMV pneumonia in immunocompetent patients, though uncommon, should be considered amongst the differential diagnosis in a patient not responding to conventional antibiotics or those presenting with prodromal viral symptoms. Early diagnosis and treatment can significantly decrease morbidity and improve prognosis.
