N2  - Background: The patient is a 3yo female diagnosed with cystic fibrosis (CF) by newborn screening, with mutations of F508del/R117H 7T/9T. She was admitted in August 2017 with cough and dyspnea, treated with clindamycin. Previous cultures grew methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, repeat on admission grew Haemophilus influenzae. She was treated with antibiotics for persistent cough over the next 2 months with clindamycin and amoxicillin/clavulanate. She also treated with prednisolone and low dose inhaled beclomethasone. Without pulmonary function testing, it was unclear if her recurrent cough was secondary to CF or to asthma due to clinical response to albuterol. Testing for allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis was negative. In October 2017, she had a surveillance culture which grew Burkholderia cenocepacia (BCC), pan susceptible. She was admitted for IV antibiotics to attempt eradication. Methods: Treatment was planned to be 14 days of IV meropenem, IV ceftazidime and PO trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, followed by an outpatient regimen of PO levofloxacin and inhaled tobramycin. She was tolerating IV treatment but developed an erythematous, blanching rash on her legs and trunk which spread to her arms. Her CBC revealed an eosinophil count of 18.5%. Due to concern for possible hypersensitivity reaction, she was transitioned to her outpatient regimen. Eosinophil count trended down to 5.7% at discharge. She then completed her oral medications as well as 3 months of inhaled tobramycin. Surveillance cultures were obtained at the time of discharge and monthly for 3 months. We then obtained bronchoscopic bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) samples of all lobes of her lungs. Results: Follow up surveillance cultures over the next 3 months were negative for Burkholderia. Her BALs obtained 5 months after the initial positive culture were negative. She was too young for pulmonary function testing but her cough was resolved and her inhaled corticosteroid was discontinued without increased symptoms. She also showed marked improvement in weight gain, increasing from 51%tile for BMI in August 2017 to 81%tile in early 2018. Conclusion: In first isolation of Burkholderia cenocepacia, it is reasonable to attempt eradication of the organism due to its potential for increased morbidity and mortality in patients with cystic fibrosis. Utilizing a multi-drug approach as well as a prolonged outpatient course of inhaled therapies may be a starting point for developing a standardized protocol. Close follow up with surveillance cultures and a second attempt at eradication if BCC is found could be used in patients whose BCC does not eradicate on the first attempt.
