N2  - Introduction Progressive interstitial lung disease (ILD) associated with chronic liver disease is uncommon and we describe a liver transplant recipient that developed post-transplant cholangiopathy subsequently diagnosed with usual interstitial pneumonitis (UIP). Case Report A 57 year old male with alcoholic cirrhosis underwent liver transplant and months later developed post-transplant cholangiopathy that required retransplantation. While awaiting liver retransplantation, he was referred for dyspnea and chest CT findings of bilateral subpleural reticular infiltrates. At time of initial visit, he reported exertional dyspnea with moderate physical activity. He was a retired air traffic control agent, former smoker and denied asbestos exposure. He reported owning a cockatiel 9 months earlier and denied a family history of lung disease. Medications included prednisone, sirolimus, tacrolimus, dapsone and valganciclovir. Physical examination revealed room air SpO2 95%, faint inspiratory crackles and no clubbing. Repeat high resolution CT of the chest revealed bilateral subpleural reticular markings and traction bronchiectasis, more prominent in lower lobes (see figure). Upper GI series revealed no aspiration. Laboratory data revealed negative ANA titer, HIV and serum CMV PCR. Pulmonary function tests revealed a restrictive defect and six minute walk demonstrated exercise desaturation. Sirolimus was discontinued given concern for drug induced lung disease. A second liver transplant was successfully performed. Yet over six months, he described exertional dyspnea and nonproductive cough. Bronchoscopy respiratory cultures were unremarkable for bacteria, fungi or acid fast bacilli. Subsequently, a surgical lung biopsy identified histopathology findings of UIP. He was initiated on nintedanib 100 mg bid and has been referred for pulmonary rehabilitation and lung transplantation evaluation. <strike></strike> <strike></strike> Discussion ILD in patients with chronic liver disease is uncommon. Respiratory symptoms in a liver transplant recipient requires exclusion of infectious, neoplastic, medication and environmental conditions associated with ILD. Infectious etiologies, neoplastic and hypersensitivity pneumonitis were excluded after lung biopsy. While sirolimus is a cause of ILD in organ transplant recipients, the lack of improvement and histopathology did not support this as a cause. Primary biliary cirrhosis is associated with autoimmune conditions such as Sjogren's and scleroderma, however findings to support this condition were absent. Examination of the explanted liver allograft revealed no granulomas and lymphohistiocytic inflammation with bile inspissation. The cause of ILD is not evident and we speculate that it was subclinically present and manifested after liver transplantation. In patients with chronic liver disease, UIP is uncommon being associated with primary biliary cirrhosis, while in liver transplant recipients it is rare. (Figure Preseted).
