N2  - Introduction Nontuberculosis Mycobacteria (NTM) can lead to significant pulmonary disease in the Cystic Fibrosis (CF) population. We present a case on a 16-year-old female who attends a magnet high school that has a sustainable agricultural theme, where she spends significant time at a farm, around livestock, and around hydroponic agricultural greenhouses. Description She presented to clinic with history of worsening productive cough, green sputum, low grade fever, shortness of breath, fatigue and a decrease in FEV1. She was started on antibiotics with minimal improvement. Subsequent work-up including chest CT revealed tree-in-bud changes and flexible bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) was positive for Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare (MAI) complex. She began triple antibiotic therapy with improvement in FEV1 and symptoms. Subsequent chest CT demonstrated worsening of previous findings with a cavitary lesion, raising concern of additional NTM versus treatment failure. Antibiotics were adjusted, and follow-up chest CT demonstrated significant improvement. She remained on NTM therapy for an additional year. She was asymptomatic and had no positive sputum cultures for NTM. Since she is not a regular sputum producer, BAL was repeated, and culture was positive for MAI complex. After discussions with family and infectious diseases specialists, NTM therapy was discontinued. Three months after discontinuing therapy, she presented with similar symptoms, and a decrease in FEV1. Repeat chest CT showed no evidence of worsening disease. At the time of her resurgent symptoms her throat culture was positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). She was treated aggressively for her culture pathogens and subsequent cultures were negative for the organisms, however her fatigue continued and FEV1 decreased further. Repeat BAL was performed which revealed MAI; no other NTM, nor any other bacteria were detected. She was restarted on antibiotic therapy and at subsequent visits her fatigue resolved and her FEV1 improved to baseline. Discussion NTM are emerging group of multidrug resistant (MDR) pathogens that are difficult to diagnosis and treat. NTM can be found in the environment, predominantly in soil and water, although transmission may occur via patient to patient. Current recommendations call for acid-fast bacilli smears and culture for NTM as standard diagnostic method, which can be challenging in non-sputum producers and cough suppressors. Antibiotic management is complex and prolonged with an intensification and continuation phase. This case highlights challenges in distinguishing MAI flare vs new infection as well as maintaining strict vigilance to therapy in light of resistance to multiple drugs.
