N2  - Background Treatment of tuberculosis (TB) is long and difficult to monitor. Currently, monitoring relies on less sensitive smear microscopy, and culture methods which are very slow. We evaluated the novel Molecular bacterial load assay (MBLA) based on detection of the 16Sribosomal RNA of M. tuberculosis (M.tb) for implementability and real-time monitoring of TB treatment response in a clinical setting. Methods Therapy naive (Xpert MTB/RIF confirmed) TB positive patients were enrolled in Mbeya (Southwest Tanzania). Sputum samples were collected at baseline before initiation of standard anti-TB therapy, HRZE and thereafter at week 2, month 2, 5 and 6 of treatment. Samples were analysed for M.tb by MBLA compared to smear microscopy (SM), culture and clinical monitoring. Treatment response was determined as change in bacterial load (BL) measured by MBLA, SM and culture positivity, and resolution of clinical signs. Results 59 TB patients were enrolled for the study. Median age (range), 37 (18-65) years, 62.7% (37/59) male, 45.6% (27/59) HIV positive and 8.47% (5/59) were re-treatment cases. The mean BL (±SD) at baseline was 5.48±1.3 declining to 3.42±0.7 at month 2 and 3.51± 0.62 log10CFU/ml at month 6 of treatment. This corresponds MBLA positivity of 92.98%, 65.5% and 7.84% at baseline, month 2 and 6 respectively. In contrast, positivity of SM and culture were 78.95%, 9.62% and 0%, and 85.96%, 25% and 3.39% at baseline, month 2 and 6 respectively. The fall in test positivity reflected resolution of clinical signs (night sweat, chest pain and cough). While night sweat, and chest pain resolved earlier on in treatment, resolution of cough was slow and consistent with change in BL. Furthermore, the turn-around-time for MBLA test results was 24h compared to median (range) of 14.83 (4.33-42) days for liquid culture. Conclusion The MBLA exhibited higher sensitivity and shorter turn-around-time than standard-of-care tests, and most clinical signs, except cough for which resolution matched sputum BL clearance. This demonstrates the potential of MBLA to offer real-time accurate results to inform clinical decision making.
