N2  - Rationale: Aspiration of oropharyngeal gastric contents into the lower respiratory tract can occur from dysphagia and/or altered cough reflex. Bacteria and gastric secretions associated with aspiration can lead to infection or inflammation of the lower respiratory tract as well as clinical pneumonia. Gross aspiration is a major contributor to clinical pneumonia in adults. One method for assessing swallowing functionality and aspiration is a video fluoroscopic swallowing study (VFSS). The findings from this study can describe both the pattern and severity of aspiration. This study aims to assess the diagnoses and clinical course of patients who were evaluated for potential aspiration through VFSS testing. Methods: Retrospective chart review of all 258 patients in TJUH who were evaluated via VFSS from February 1st 2017 to March 31st 2017. Aspiration was identified by VFSS in 40 patients and we recorded the pattern/severity of aspiration, radiographic findings, underlying diagnoses and clinical course. Evidence of pneumonia was defined by clinical parameters (fever and elevated white blood cells) and radiographic evidence of typical pulmonary infiltrates (posterior dependent lung zones). Descriptive statistics were used to summarize patient characteristics and outcomes. Results: In forty of the 258 patients (16%) were noted to have some evidence of clinical aspiration. Sixty-two percent of patients aspirated thin liquids, 27.5% aspirated honey/nectar thick substances, and 10% aspirated pureed substances. Thirty-six percent of patients with abnormal swallows suffered pneumonia in the weeks or months following the detection of aspiration. Of this subset, 15% of patients died in one year following the abnormal VFSS. The average age of patients with abnormal VFSS was 66 years, while patients with clinical pneumonia were 73 years on average. The most common diagnoses for patients who encountered pneumonia included altered mental status with diminished cough reflex (due to dementia, seizure disorder, or encephalopathy) (31%), oropharyngeal cancer (19%) and cervical spine surgery or cervical fracture (13%). Conclusions: For patients who experience aspiration associated with abnormal VFSS, clinical pneumonia developed in more than 1/3rd over the next several months and 15% of patients died within one year. Aspiration is an important entity which influences morbidity and mortality for patients with altered mental status, oropharyngeal cancer, and cervical spine disease. Further understanding of this important phenomenon is required in order to improve outcomes.
