N2  - RATIONALE Acute exacerbations of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) are associated with high morbidity and high short-term mortality. The 2016 international working group report proposed subcategorization of acute exacerbations as either “triggered” or “idiopathic”. Acute exacerbation can be triggered by pulmonary infection, so significance of pneumonia is unknown in the management of IPF according to the 2016 diagnostic criteria. METHODS We retrospectively examined the medical charts of all patients with IPF who were admitted to our hospital for pneumonia in the past ten years. Pneumonia was defined as the presence of fever or a wet cough, an increased white blood cell (WBC) count or C-reactive protein (CRP) level, and the presence of a newly developed focal infiltrate on chest imaging. Patients with acute exacerbations according to the 2016 diagnostic criteria were excluded. The severity of pneumonia was evaluated using predictive rules according to an A-DROP 6-point scale (0-5) proposed by the Japanese Respiratory Society. RESULTS Fifty-three IPF cases (43 males and 10 females, mean age of 79±8) were hospitalized with pneumonia during the study period. They consisted of 27 community-acquired pneumonia and 26 nursing and healthcare associated pneumonia. Treatments for IPF before pneumonia included prednisolone (n=4), cyclosporin (n=2) and pirfenidone (n=1). Hospitalization due to pneumonia occurred more frequently in winter: 9 in spring, 5 in summer, 15 autumn and 24 in winter. A-DROP scores were as follows; 1/2/3/4/5=22/13/13/4/1, and WBC, CRP and KL-6 were 13217±5162/μL, 14.9±10.9mg/dL and 902±621U/mL, respectively. Good-quality sputum data as classificated Geckler of 4 to 6 were obtained in 25 cases and Heamophilus influenza, Streptococcus pneumonia, methicillinsensitive Staphylococcus aureus were isolated in 4,3, and 2, respectively. S. pneumonia was detected in 1 by blood cultures and 6 using pneumococcal urine antigen tests. Mechanical ventilation was needed in 7 cases (noninvasive ventilation in 3, intubation in 4). Median hospital stay was 15 days (3-145 days) and 30-day and hospital mortality rates were 15.1% and 20.8%, respectively. Thirty-day mortality of pneumonia was significantly associated with the A-DROP score (p<0.0001). It looks like that these mortality rates were lower than those of acute exacerbations of IPF diagnosed based on the 2016 diagnostic criteria in our hospital; 30-day and hospital mortality rates were 37.5% and 54.7%, respectively. CONCLUSION We found that outcomes in IPF patients with pneumonia was not so worse compared with those in acute exacerbations of IPF diagnosed based on the 2016 diagnostic criteria.
