N2  - Organizing pneumonia (OP) is a histopathologic diagnosis that is idiopathic, or associated with connective tissue disease (CTD), drugs, malignancy, radiation and hypersensitivity pneumonitis. We describe a case of OP causing profound hypoxic respiratory failure. A healthy 46-year-old woman presented with two-months of constitutional symptoms and non-productive cough. She had received antimicrobials without relief. Examination was notable for an oxygen saturation of 88% on 4 liters oxygen, inspiratory crackles on lung auscultation, normal cardiac exam and no edema or clubbing. Laboratory tests revealed a leukocytosis (14,000cells/mL) with 78% neutrophils and 7% eosinophils, and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate greater than 120mm/hr. Computed tomography (CT) showed dense lower lobe consolidations, bilateral pleural effusions, and mediastinal and hilar adenopathy (Figure 1). Bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) revealed 17% neutrophils, 2% lymphocytes, 6% eosinophils and 73% macrophages. The CD4:CD8 ratio of lymphocytes was 7:1. Viral, bacterial, fungal cultures, and acid-fast bacillus smears were negative. Transbronchial biopsies were inconclusive. Serologies for CTD (ANA, dsDNA, SSA, SSB, Scl-70, centromere, Jo-1, rheumatoid factor and CCP) were negative. She developed progressive respiratory failure requiring non-invasive ventilation. She was at high risk for pulmonary complications associated with video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) and was started on empiric corticosteroids (CS) with rapid improvement of symptoms and radiologic findings. Two months later, CS were tapered with relapse of disease. VATS lung biopsy showed OP. Upon further questioning, the patient reported use of down bedding, and subsequent hypersensitivity testing was positive for avian exposures. OP typically presents with cough and dyspnea, but can also cause acute respiratory failure. Thorough history taking with attention to environmental exposures, current and recent medications, as well as symptoms suggestive of CTD is important. Common radiographic patterns include patchy airspace consolidation with air bronchograms and ground-glass opacities that can be bilateral, peripheral, and migratory. Mediastinal adenopathy and pleural effusions are uncommon. Bronchoscopy is performed to exclude infection and malignancy. BAL findings are nonspecific with lymphocytosis, neutrophilia and eosinophilia. The CD4:CD8 ratio is decreased. Transbronchial biopsies are often inadequate for confirmation of OP and a surgical lung biopsy is necessary. In this patient, peripheral blood eosinophilia, elevated CD4:CD8 ratio, atypical CT findings, and non-diagnostic transbronchial biopsies delayed the diagnosis. Treatment of OP has not been studied in randomized trials. CS are standard of care. There is also a role for other immunosuppressive agents, particularly in patients with persistent disease, or reticulation and fibrosis on CT. (Figure Preseted).
