N2  - Background:COPD, commonly caused by chronic cigarette smoke exposure, is characterized by mucus hypersecretion, chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Airway mucus is a key player in lung homeostasis: it is a secreted hydrogel consisting of proteins, salt, and water, and it acts like a dynamic barrier, sequestering particulates, toxins, and pathogens, aiding their removal from the airway by mucociliary transport and/or cough clearance. In case of mucus hypersecretion, the mucociliary transport is ineffective, resulting in airway obstruction, inflammation, infection, and eventually FEV1 reduction. More than a hundred proteins are present in normal mucus, with MUC5AC and MUC5B being the major polymers that generate the gel-like properties required for mucociliary clearance: MUC5B,the predominant gel-forming mucin, is vital for mucociliary clearance, while MUC5AC plays a relatively minor role. Here we investigated individual mucin concentration in relation to cigarette smoking status and history, assessments scores, symptoms, progression and severity of COPD, to understand their role in COPD disease. Methods:respiratory secreted mucins MUC5AC and MUC5B were quantitated by stable isotope labeled mass spectrometry in 334 induced sputa from subjects in the SPIROMICS cohort (healthy control and gold stages 0 to 3). MUC5AC and MUC5B concentrations were related to GOLD stage classification, smoking status and history, CAT score, cough, exacerbation and small airway patency. Results:In our previous results (based on 150 subjects) we reported that both mucins are associated with COPD severity. Here we showed that MUC5AC and MUC5B are also associated with phlegm and cough production, smoking status, lung function decline and small airways patency reduction. Current smokers showed higher MUC5AC concentration compared to former smokers in the same disease severity stage. Parallel to MUC5AC concentration, current smokers with no COPD (but not ex-smokers) exhibited significantly decreased lung function. Longitudinal data over 4 years indicated MUC5AC-low healthy ex-smoker preserved their lung function, while FEV1 decreased for MUC5AC-high healthy current smokers. MUC5AC concentration was associated with increased CAT score, and it was significantly higher in COPD subjects with two or more exacerbations requiring HCU. MUC5AC concentration was inversely related to FEF25-75% and directly related to RV/TLV and PRM-FSAD values. However, MUC5AC increase did not correlated to TH2 inflammation and allergy markers. Conclusions:These data suggest that increased mucin MUC5AC is an important pathobiological factor for COPD initiation and progression driven by cigarette smoking, and therapies that target MUC5AC producing pathways may be a useful therapeutic approach.
