N2  - Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is a chronic, progressive, and inevitably fatal age-related disorder. Converging evidence implicates cellular senescence in the disease process. Selectively ablating senescent cells in vivo using dasatinib and quercetin (DQ) alleviates symptoms of IPF and frailty in mouse models. We conducted a two-center, open-label, pilot study of intermittent DQ for 3 weeks (9 dosing days; D:100mg/day, Q:1250mg/day) in patients with IPF (n=14) to evaluate 1) feasibility of approach, 2) preliminary estimates of safety, and 3) future trial instruments such as biochemical assays.The retention rate for completion was 100% with no discontinuation of DQ. Planned assessments were completed in 14/14 patients, except for one patient whose follow-up physical function assessment could not be performed. One serious adverse event (AE) was reported (possible bacterial multifocal pneumonia and pulmonary edema superimposed on IPF). Remaining adverse events reported were non-serious and graded mild-moderate in severity, with most frequent events reported as IPF/respiratory symptoms (16 total: cough, shortness of breath, runny nose), skin irritation and bruising (14 total, 11 related to skin biopsy and medical adhesives), gastrointestinal discomfort (12 total: nausea, constipation, diarrhea), headache (5), and general health (4)/tiredness (4). Physical function, as measured by 6-minute walk distance (6MWD), 4-meter usual gait-speed, five-repetition chair-rise time, and short physical performance battery were all significantly improved following DQ (p < 0.05), with clinically meaningful mean improvements. Pulmonary function, frailty index, clinical chemistries, and reported respiratory and quality of life were stable, and support overall safety of the DQ drug administration in patients with IPF. Despite limited sample sizes, trends were observed such that circulating senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) factors assayed after 3-weeks of intermittent DQ showed moderate correlations with markers of physical and pulmonary function (10 of 42 cytokine/chemokine Spearman rho > 0.50). For example, lower pro-inflammatory chemokine GRO-α was moderately associated with higher forced vital capacity (FVC), faster gait speed, and faster chair stands (rho > 0.50 all, statistical trends p < 0.10), and lower pro-inflammatory cytokines eotaxin, interferon gamma, monocyte-chemoattractant protein-1/CCL2, and CCL3 were all moderately associated with FVC (rho > 0.50 all). In conclusion, we report feasibility of approach as well as general safety and tolerability to short-term intermittent DQ in IPF patients. Moreover, we provide initial evidence of positive effects on mobility, including 6MWD, and on markers of cellular senescence and SASP factors. Collectively, these findings support trial advancement of DQ in IPF.
