N2  - Introduction: About half of COPD exacerbations are bacterial and respond to antibiotic therapy. In the clinical setting, tailoring the antibiotic based on speciation of bacteria from sputum is unhelpful in treatment decisions due to its long turnaround time, and identification of strain change requires longitudinal sampling. It would be useful to identify clinical symptoms and signs that help identify patients with well-defined bacterial exacerbation who will benefit from antibiotic treatment. Methods: We conducted a prospective longitudinal cohort study in COPD involving a total of 186 patients between 1994-2014 to maintain a cohort of approximately 50 patients. During each clinic visit, clinical information and sputum samples were obtained. We obtained status of chronic respiratory symptoms (dyspnea, cough, sputum production, viscosity, and purulence), vital signs, and lung examination findings. Samples of spontaneously expectorated sputum were obtained. Strains of Haemophilus in uenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Moraxella catarrhalis, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa were characterized by molecular typing. Strains of these pathogens not previously isolated from a participant were designated as new, whereas strains isolated previously were designated as preexisting strains. We defined an exacerbation as bacterial if associated with a new strain of the above four major pathogens on sputum analysis. The odds ratio of having a bacterial exacerbation when a clinical factor is present was calculated using generalized estimating equations (GEE). Results: Our patients were aged 67±9 years old, majority were white (88%) male (98%). The analysis involved 1376 exacerbations in 152 patients. Presence of increased cough (OR 1.74 95% CI: 1.03-2.92 p=0.04), sputum viscosity (thickness) (OR 1.52 95% CI 1.05-2.18 p=0.03), and sputum purulence (OR 2.3 95% CI 1.51-3.52 p<0.01) were associated with increased probability of a bacterial COPD exacerbation, while wheezing (OR 0.58 95% 0.44-0.78 p<0.01) was associated with a decreased probability of bacterial exacerbation (figure). Although antibiotic use in the previous 4 weeks decreased the probability of a bacteria exacerbation, the predictive ability of these clinical signs for a new strain exacerbation remained the same. Changes in overall sense of well-being, shortness of breath, amount of sputum production, and on examination, overall appearance, rales, and vital signs were not associated with bacterial COPD exacerbation. Conclusion: Increased cough, sputum viscosity and purulence indicate increase probability of bacterial exacerbation while presence of wheezing is a negative indicator for bacterial etiology. Our findings can help refine the Anthonisen criteria for antibiotic use and help guide decision to use antibiotics. (Figure Presented) .
