N2  - Introduction: Rasmussen aneurysm is an uncommon cause of hemoptysis; it refers to the formation of an aneurysm in the setting of cavitary lesion that extends into the adventitia and media of pulmonary arteries resulting in inflammation and thinning of the vessel wall, it is mostly associated with pulmonary Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection. We present a unique case of a Rasmussen aneurysm caused by pulmonary squamous cell carcinoma. Clinical Cas: A 55-yearold man with schizophrenia and tobacco use presented with acute onset weakness and dizziness. Physical exam was significant for decreased breath sounds at the right base. Chest radiograph revealed large right perihilar opacity. Additional history uncovered complaints of cough, and scant hemoptysis beginning six months prior. Personal history was significant for daily congregate daycare attendance, low socioeconomic status, and poor nutritional status. Chest computed tomography showed large pulmonary lesion with cavitary component and a 6.3cm x1.8cm x3.1cm aneurysm extending from the artery supplying the superior segment of the right lower lobe and narrowing of the right mainstem bronchus. Given high risk for aneurysm rupture and fatal massive hemoptysis, the patient underwent pulmonary artery coiling by interventional radiology. Quantiferon gold testing (QFT) results were indeterminate. Given pathognomonic findings on imaging and risk factors, anti-tuberculosis treatment was initiated prior to sputum AFB and MTB-PCR results were finalized. Inadequate sputum collection and narrowing of right mainstem bronchus prompted bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and endobronchial biopsies. Pathology came back showing squamous cell carcinoma. Repeat QFT was negative and anti-tuberculosis therapy was discontinued. The patient remained in the hospital for initial oncology evaluation. Nineteen days after admission patient experienced cardiopulmonary arrest, with peri-arrest echo significant for right ventricular dilation suggestive of pulmonary embolism. Resuscitation attempts were unsuccessful, and the patient expired. Discussion: Increasing awareness of Rasmussen aneurysm and differential diagnoses is crucial to prevent life-threatening complications such as vessel rupture and hemorrhage. First described by Waldemar Rasmussen in 1868, “Rasmussen aneurysm” is an aneurysmal dilatation of the segmental branches of pulmonary arteries. It is a rare complication of pulmonary tuberculosis with prevalence reported between 5-8%. Other associations with similar aneurysms include mycotic infections, congenital heart disorders, and vasculitic processes. An exceedingly rare etiology is lung malignancy, to our knowledge, there is only one case reported of metastatic squamous cell as the cause of Rasmussen aneurysm. Due to the aggressive nature of the disease, early recognition and prompt treatment can prevent death.
