N2  - Introduction: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a common respiratory disease which affects more than 5 percent of the population and is associated with high morbidity and mortality. COPD exacerbations are often associated with a respiratory infection. Tsukamurella species is a rare human pathogen which belongs to the aerobic actinomycetes and found in soil, water, and sludge. A few cases of Tsukamurella species infections in humans have been described. However, its association with COPD exacerbation has not been previously reported. We present a case of COPD with frequent exacerbations where Tsukamurella pulmonis was isolated from sputum culture. Case Report: An 80-year-old female, a former smoker with chronic hypoxic respiratory failure secondary to COPD and frequent exacerbations (Gold group D), presented with progressively worsening cough and green colored expectoration. She had recently completed a 14-day course of levofloxacin for Stenotrophomonas maltophilia isolated from sputum culture. CT chest showed emphysematous changes with multiple nodules. Due to her persistent symptoms, infection with non-tuberculous mycobacterium was suspected, and sputum AFB culture was ordered. However, this had resulted with Tsukamurella pulmonis and was confirmed with 16s rDNA sequencing analysis. Based on the available sensitivities, the patient was started on Azithromycin. She had significant clinical improvement after 2 months of therapy. Discussion: Tsukamurella pulmonis is an aerobic gram-positive rod-shaped bacterium which can demonstrate mild acid-fast staining. This has a potential to be misidentified with Rhodococus, Mycobacterium and Nocardia species. Therefore, molecular methods are necessary for accurate identification. This includes sequencing for 16S rRNA, groEL, rpoB, secA1 and ssrA genes. Few cases of Tsukamurella species in the literature report association with immunocompromised status, lung malignancies, indwelling catheter-associated infections, and prosthetic joint infections. No standard treatment guidelines have been established, however, Tsukamurella species are susceptible to amikacin, ciprofloxacin, imipenem, doxycycline, linezolid and sulfamethoxazole. A multi-drug combination therapy should be considered until susceptibility is available. Our case highlights the importance of evaluating for atypical organisms in patients with frequent COPD exacerbations not responding to conventional therapy. T. pulmonis and related species infections may be underdiagnosed due to the difficulty in identifying the organism by regular phenotypic tests.
