N2  - INTRODUCTION: Congenital bronchiectasis is a rare heterogeneous progressive disease characterized by permanent dilatation of bronchi manifesting in early infancy associated with a clinical presentation of productive cough, hemoptysis and recurrent respiratory infections. Due to varying etiologies, this under-recognized disease warrants consideration. Here, we present a case of congenital non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis with auto-pneumonectomy and compensatory lung growth with an acute pulmonary embolism and community-acquired pneumonia. CASE PRESENTATION: A 44-year-old African American male smoker with past medical history of congenital bronchiectasis on 4L nasal cannula at baseline and no infertility issues presented to our tertiary medical center with acute on chronic dyspnea occurring over past few days. Patient has suffered from recurrent hospitalizations for pneumonia with over three episodes in the past six months. Patient expressed symptoms of pleuritic chest pain, productive yellow-greenish sputum with scant hemoptysis, nightly low-grade fevers, chills, post-tussive emesis and palpitations. Patient denied any recent travel history, tick bites, sick contacts or weight loss. Physical examination was significant for afebrile, tachypnea, tachycardia, hypoxia, decreased right-sided breath sounds and no clubbing. Laboratory testing were notable for a leukocytosis, contraction alkalosis, troponin negative, and positive S. Pneumoniae urine antigen. Chest X-ray revealed right cystic bronchiectasis with volume loss and extreme mediastinal shift. CT chest angiogram showed pulmonary embolism in the left upper lobe with moderate clot burden, severe right cystic bronchiectasis with auto-pneumonectomy and hyper-lobar expansion of left lung with scattered bronchiectasis in left upper lobe and at left lower lobe including the lung base with hyperinflation. Immunoglobins, CFTR, sweat chloride, Aspergillus and HIV testing were all negative. Bronchoscopy showed normal mucosa throughout, but severely narrowed segments throughout the entire right tracheobronchial tree. Bronchoalveolar lavage was predominantly neutrophilic, but was was negative for AFB, fungal and bacterial cultures. Patient was discharged on amoxicillin-clavulanate and rivaroxaban with follow-up outpatient with our pulmonary clinic. DISCUSSION: The combination of severe right lung predominant cystic bronchiectasis with auto-pneumonectomy and compensatory lung expansion suggests a chronic process. Early intervention with pneumonectomy may have reduced morbidity. The differential diagnosis includes Congenital Pulmonary Airway Malformation, Chronic infection-induced bronchiectasis, Swyer-James syndrome, Postinfectious Bronchiolitis Obliterans, William- Campbell Syndrome, Pulmonary hypoplasia and Necrotizing pneumonia. CONCLUSIONS: Congenital non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis represents a rare progressive lung disease associated with high morbidity and mortality. There are numerous syndromes associated with congenital bronchiectasis and early intervention with pneumonectomy may reduce the rate of hospitalizations (Figure Presented) .
