N2  - Introduction: Primary infection with coccidioidomycosis causes symptomatic infection in 25-30% of patientsi. The majority of these cases present as self-limited pneumoniaii with less than 1% resulting in disseminated diseaseiii. Pericardial involvement is rare with few cases reported in the literature. Case: A 65-year-old Caucasian female reported four months of waxing and waning fatigue, drenching sweats, and non-productive cough despite multiple antibiotic regimens. She reported progressive fatigue and left SI joint arthralgia. Medical history was pertinent for rheumatoid arthritis on immunosuppressive therapy. Within the past year, she travelled throughout the US, including biking trips from the Pacific Northwest to New Mexico. Computed Tomography (CT) of the chest revealed innumerable bilateral miliary opacities and trace pericardial effusion. Bronchoalveolar lavage was performed with negative bacterial and fungal cultures. Serum fungal antigen testing, including coccidiodes, were negative. Labs revealed fungitell of 36 pg/mL, and hemoglobin of 10.5 g/dL. Empiric therapy for Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Brucella spp was initiated. She developed progressive dyspnea on exertion and lower extremity edema. Cardiac Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) revealed constrictive pericarditis. Cultures from bone marrow and SI joint aspiration grew Coccidioides immitus from both sites. Amphotericin B was started and incision and drainage of the left SI joint was perfomed. Right heart dysfunction progressed, ultimately requiring pericardiectomy. Pericardial tissue grew Coccidioides immitus. The patient improved and was discharged on lifelong posaconazole therapy. Discussion: Disseminated coccidioidomycosis is rare; pericardium is the sixteenth most frequently involved structureiv. Presentation may be nonspecific and difficult to diagnose without a high index of suspicion. Furthermore, a delay in diagnosis may increase mortality and morbidity as invasive interventions such as pericardiectomy may be required. This case highlights the importance of early recognition of extrapulmonary manifestations of coccidioidomycosis in a patient on immunosuppressive therapy.
