N2  - Introduction: Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) has been reported to cause various types of lung injury, but is not a commonly considered cause of bronchiectasis. Case presentation: A 45-yearold man presented to pulmonary clinic with a productive cough of 3 years duration. Prior to presentation, his symptoms had progressed to include exertional dyspnea and wheezing that was unresponsive to trials of inhaled fluticasone-salmeterol. He underwent total colectomy at age 19 for ulcerative colitis (UC), and subsequently required multiple abdominal surgeries for lysis of adhesions. The patient was a lifetime nonsmoker who occasionally used marijuana vaping devices. His arterial oxygen saturation was 94% while he breathed ambient air. Auscultation of his lungs revealed bilateral wheezing and rhonchi. Laboratory findings included a weakly positive perinuclear anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (p-ANCA), a positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) with a titer of 1:320, and negative cultures of sputum for bacteria and mycobacteria. Radioallergosorbent testing (RAST), immunoglobulin levels, and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing were negative. Pulmonary function testing showed a severe obstructive pattern with no significant bronchodilator response and a normal carbon monoxide diffusion capacity. Previous chest computerized tomographic imaging showed patchy bibasilar infiltrates and poorly defined basilar-predominant nodular opacities. Repeat imaging 3 months later at the time of our initial evaluation showed interim bilateral development of lower lobe tubular bronchiectatic changes with interspersed tree-in-bud opacities. Given his history, serologic workup, and imaging, UC-associated airway inflammation was felt to be the most likely cause of his respiratory pathology. On prednisone, respiratory symptoms and chest imaging abnormalities improved dramatically. He was subsequently transitioned to an inhaled corticosteroid. Discussion: The prevalence of pulmonary abnormalities in IBD patients is reportedly greater than 1 in 5 and may involve parenchyma or airways predominantly, or a combination of both. The onset of UC-related pulmonary disease can predate a diagnosis of bowel disease and can conversely begin long after total colectomy. Concurrent, active bowel inflammation is not a prerequisite for the development of lung disease. Clinicians should be alert to the possibility of pulmonary manifestations as a cause of respiratory symptoms in patients with underlying IBD (Figure Presented) .
