N2  - Introduction: Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA), formerly known as Churg Strauss syndrome, is a systemic vasculitis characterized by peripheral eosinophilia, asthma, pulmonary infiltrates and necrotizing medium and small vessel disease. Its annual incidence is estimated to be 0.24 cases per 100000. Radiographic appearance is variable, ranging from transient pulmonary infiltrates to ground glass and tree in bud opacities. Considering multisystem involvement and lack of pathognomonic radiographic findings, timely diagnosis remains a challenge. Case Presentation: 35-year-old Caucasian female with past history of thalassemia minor and ankylosing spondylitis presented to the hospital with worsening dyspnea on exertion and occasional wheezing accompanied by substernal pleuritic chest pain, fever, night sweats and 30 lbs weight loss for the past two months. Her review of systems was positive for fatigue, dry cough, recurrent sinusitis and lower extremity weakness. Laboratory work up was significant for leukocytosis (white count 20.5 K/CUMM), anemia (hemoglobin 10.1 gm/dl), peripheral eosinophilia (absolute eosinophil count 0.9 K/CUMM) and elevated sedimentation rate (34 mm/hr). Immunoglobulin E levels were normal. Pertinent negatives include a negative Anti-nuclear antibody, antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibodies (ANCA) panel, Quantiferon, fungal serologies, and testing for parasitic diseases. Computed Tomography (CT) Angiography of Chest revealed ground glass tree-in-bud opacities within the upper lobes concerning for respiratory bronchiolitis interstitial lung disease versus atypical pneumonia. . She was started on antibiotics with minimal improvement. Bone marrow biopsy confirmed trilineage hyperplasia with associated moderate eosinophilia. Bronchoalveolar lavage sample also had marked eosinophilia (47%) however bacterial and fungal cultures remained negative. PFTs showed an FEV1/FVC ratio of 85%, FEV1 63% predicted, and a positive methacoline challenge test. A follow up high resolution CT revealed subtle centrilobular ground-glass nodules along the bronchovascular structures in the apical and mid lung zones. A transthoracic biopsy revealed granulomatosis with polyangiitis and eosinophilia on histopathology. She was prescribed prednisone with improvement in her symptoms. Discussion: The American College of Rheumatology (ACR) requires four of the following for diagnosis of EGPA: peripheral eosinophilia (more than 10%), asthma, pulmonary infiltrates, paranasal abnormalities, neuropathy and extravascular eosinophilia. Our patient met the ACR criteria for EGPA. ANCA autoantibodies are positive in around 40% of cases and are associated with more aggressive renal and pulmonary involvement. On the contrary, ANCA negative patients, like ours, have a younger age at diagnosis, refractory asthma and cardiac involvement. Conclusion: EGPA is a rare multisystem disease with significant morbidity. Early diagnosis and treatment is crucial to prevent devastating long term consequences.
