N2  - INTRODUCTION: Pleural fluid eosinophilia (PFE) is not commonly encountered in clinical practice. When present, it provides a diagnostic clue to narrow the differential diagnosis. Most commonly, PFE is associated with blood or air in the pleural space, certain malignancies, and atypical infections. We present a rare case of a large unilateral eosinophilic pleural effusion caused by pulmonary coccidioidomycosis. Case: A previously healthy 32-year-old Hispanic male, who travelled frequently to Mexico and worked in construction, presented to the Emergency Department for shortness of breath and left-sided pleuritic chest pain. He reported a history of pneumonia that was diagnosed and treated in Mexico with ceftriaxone and moxifloxacin two weeks prior to presentation. He reported fever, night sweats and dry cough. Physical exam revealed decreased left sided breath sounds and dullness to percussion over the left hemithorax. His initial laboratory studies showed leukocytosis and peripheral eosinophilia. Chest radiograph showed a large left-sided pleural effusion with mediastinal shift and computed tomography of the chest additionally revealed up to 1.4 centimeter mediastinal, cardiophrenic, and axillary lymphadenopathy. Diagnostic thoracentesis was performed and pleural fluid studies revealed an eosinophilic exudate with low glucose and normal pH. Further laboratory data was significant for high peripheral IgE and elevated Coccidioidomycosis IgG and IgM serologies of 3.3 and 3.8 respectively. Blastomycosis and histoplasmosis serologies were negative. Bacterial and fungal cultures of the pleural fluid and serum were also negative. The patient was diagnosed with pulmonary coccidioidomycosis with associated PFE. He was started on amphotericin and eventually transitioned to fluconazole prior to discharge. At a follow up visit one month later, he reported overall improvement in symptoms with minimal cough and shortness of breath. Additionally, chest radiograph showed decreased size of pleural effusion with minimal pleural thickening. He is to complete a 3 to 6-month course of fluconazole. Discussion: Coccidioidomycosis predominantly occurs in the southwestern United States and presents similarly to community acquired pneumonia if the patient is symptomatic. An unusual but unique feature of this disease is an associated small unilateral pleural effusion. Lymphocytes are usually the predominant cell in the pleural fluid and eosinophils are almost always found but rarely at the level to diagnose PFE. Only a few case reports exist to establish a correlation between PFE and coccidioidomycosis. This case demonstrates that coccidioidomycosis should be considered in the differential diagnosis of pleural fluid eosinophilia.
