N2  - A broncholith is defined as the presence of calcified or osseous material in the lumen of tracheobronchial tree. It usually presents due to erosion by extrusion of a calcified adjacent lymph node into the bronchial lumen, sometimes associated with the findings of Tuberculosis or Histoplasmosis. The diagnosis and treatment is often delayed because patients present with vague symptoms of airway obstruction, hemoptysis and mucoid impaction with no clear cause identified. We present a case of recurrent hemoptysis and shortness of breath in a 58-year-old CPAP dependent male patient. A broncholith with evidence of calcified mediastinal lymphadenopathy was identified on a high resolution CT scan with contrast. Some compression of the lateral segment of the left lower lobe was found along with evidence of broncholith formation in the airway. The treatment strategy was discussed with the patient. After pre-anesthesia assessment, deep sedation and analgesia, a size 14 mm Storz scope was introduced into the trachea and advanced without difficulty using fluoroscopic guidance. A broncholith was found in the lateral basal segment of the left lower lobe. The calcified body was successfully removed using a cryoprobe. Some residual granulation tissue remained for which a bronchoalveolar lavage was performed and sent for cell count, bacterial and fungal cultures and Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) analysis. Symptoms resolved after removal of the broncholith with rigid bronchoscopy and cryotherapy. Owing to the fact that the patient was born and raised in the Ohio River Valley and Northern Kentucky, exposure to bats and Histoplasmosis could not be ruled out as the cause of the broncholith formation at the time of presentation in this otherwise immunocompetent individual. Broncholithiasis is a very rare cause of non-productive cough associated with hemoptysis, wheezing, shortness of breath and stone expectoration. The diagnosis of the condition is often delayed. The available treatment options and choice of therapy depend on patient demographics, severity of symptoms and the size and location of the broncholith. Uncomplicated and loose broncholithiasis, such as the one presented in this case, can be successfully treated with rigid bronchoscopic removal, with success rates ranging from 67-87%. However, complicated cases presenting with bronchial artery fistula or recurrent obstructive pneumonias secondary to bronchoesophageal fistulas require surgical management with segmentectomy, lobectomy or pneumectomy.
