N2  - Introduction: Endobronchial lesions represent large airway masses and mass-like lesions. They are relatively rare, constituting less than 0.4% of all tumors. Such condition can have variable respiratory symptoms and nonspecific presentations. Patients can be asymptomatic or can develop a clinically significant airway obstruction like malignant masses or obstructive airway disease. Pathologic examination is essential to establish diagnosis and bronchoscopy with biopsy remains the most efficacious diagnostic modality in such situations. Case presentation: A 57-year-old male with history of COPD who presented with dyspnea, productive cough, fever, chills and right-sided pleuritic chest pain. On examination he had diffuse bilateral wheezing with rhonchi. Blood tests showed leukocytosis. Chest X-ray demonstrated chronic signs of COPD in addition to right upper lobe infiltrates. A chest CT scan revealed enlarged mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes with post-obstructive pneumonia in the superior segment of the right upper lobe. The patient was started on antibiotics and corticosteroids for acute exacerbation of COPD and community acquired pneumonia. For better evaluation of post-obstructive pneumonia, he underwent bronchoscopy which showed right endobronchial mass in the superior segment of the right upper lobe. Bronchioloalveolar lavage (BAL) and endobronchial biopsies of the mass were done. Microscopic evaluation was significant for acute inflammatory cells with normal appearing bronchial cells, bronchoalveolar macrophages and squamous cells. No malignant cells were seen. Subsequently, transbronchial aspiration of enlarged subcarinal lymph nodes was done, however, no malignant cells were found. Patient had significant improvement with guideline directed medical therapy. The presence of a mass in this clinical setting was concerning for malignancy. Bronchoscopy was repeated demonstrating the persistence of the mass. BAL and endobronchial biopsies again did not show any malignant cells. Further evaluation was negative for tuberculosis and vasculitis. Upon outpatient follow up and repeated chest x-ray, patient had resolution of his clinical symptoms and acute radiographic findings. Discussion: Endobronchial lesions can present with a non-specific clinical picture and endobronchial biopsy with microscopic examination is essential. Differential diagnosis can vary widely including malignant and benign primary neoplasms, metastatic malignancy, inflammatory conditions (e.g., relapsing polychondritis, Wegner granulomatosis), infection (e.g., bacterial abscess, papillomatosis) and infiltrating disease such as amyloidosis. Situations where microscopic pathologic examination does not establish diagnosis pose a clinical challenge. Further studies are required to establish situations were benign findings are prevalent and risk of invasive testing outweighs benefits.
