N2  - Introduction: Burkholderia pseudomallei is a gram negative organism endemic to South-East Asia and Australia; infection is known as melioidosis. Melioidosis is usually reported in returning travelers who visit an endemic region. It has gained notoriety for its use as a bioterrorism weapon. To our knowledge, only 25 culture-proven case reports of melioidosis in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients have been reported in the literature. Only 1 case involved a patient from the United States (US). The following is a case of B.pseudomallei infection in a returning traveler from the US with CF. Case presentation: A 23-year-old female with history of CF (heterozygous dF508) with baseline FEV1 of 75% complicated by bronchiectasis, sinus disease, pancreatic insufficiency, history of multidrug resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa (MDR PsA) and methicillin susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) in sputum presented to the emergency room for acute onset chest pain, shortness of breath and cough productive of blood tinged sputum shortly after returning from Thailand. Vitals were notable for an oxygen saturation of 89% on room air and fever to 38.8C. She was placed on high flow nasal cannula and admitted to the intensive care unit (ICU). Given recent long flight and use of oral contraceptives, computed tomography angiography (CTA) of the chest was obtained to rule out acute pulmonary embolism (PE). Imaging demonstrated a right lower lobe nodular airspace consolidation along with small pneumomediastinum. She was started on Ceftazidime-avibactam, Doxycyline and Trimethoprimsulfamethoxazole due to prior susceptibility patterns. Sputum cultures were obtained on admission which were initially only positive for MSSA, PsA and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. Viral illness was ruled out with negative respiratory viral panel. Eventually, oxygen requirements improved and her airway clearance regimen was continued. On hospital day 11, however, respiratory cultures returned positive for Burkholderia pseudomallei. Infectious diseases recommended a three week course of intravenous (IV) Ceftazidime followed by a 2-3-month course of Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to help eradicate this organism. She was eventually weaned down to room air and was discharged on IV antibiotics. She continued to do well on follow-up and pulmonary function tests demonstrated no decrease in baseline FEV1. Discussion: Our patient's structural lung disease was not only a risk factor in acquisition of this infection but it will likely also make it difficult to eradicate this organism. Early identification and suspicion of disease especially in a traveler returning from an endemic area with structural lung disease, although challenging, may help prevent chronic infection.
