N2  - Parapneumonic pleural effusion develops in pleural space adjacent to bacterial pneumonia. A minority of patients develop infection in this effusion with resulting empyema. In such cases, fibrin peel form in the visceral pleural surface preventing the lung from full expanding during respiration. Direct visualization with thoracoscopy confirm the diagnosis of fibrinous pleural effusion. A sixty-six-year-old female patient with a medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and Crohn's disease presented with the right upper quadrant abdominal and right flank pain, with radiation to the right shoulder. Associated with chills, shortness of breath and bilateral lower limbs edema but without chest pain, fever, weight loss or cough. She was hypoxic with 85% oxygen saturation at room air and tachypneic with respiratory rate of 24 per minute. Chest examination revealed decreased breath sounds in the right middle and lower fields. Her abdomen was tender in the right upper quadrant with hypoactive bowel sounds but without rigidity. Laboratory work up was only significant for leukocytosis at 28.8 K/uL with left shift. Chest CT scan showed large loculated right sided pleural effusion with extensive volume loss and total right lung consolidation. A CT scan of the abdomen did not reveal bowel pathology. Patient developed pleuritic chest pain with increased oxygen needs and she was admitted to the medical ICU (MICU) with treatment of pneumonia with antibiotics and placed on BiPAP. Ultrasound guided thoracentesis was done to drain loculated right side pleural effusion with 325 ml of yellow turbid exudative fluid with 2384 cell found, 79% of them were segmented white blood cells, and other features of exudative effusion. The fluid was analyzed, without malignancy or infectious organisms isolated. A flexible bronchoscopy revealed crowding of right middle lobe and lower lobe bronchi. Cardiothoracic surgery team underwent video-assisted thoracoscopy showed significant coagulative thick fibrinous fluid throughout the chest extending to the middle and lower right-side lobes and the fissures. During the procedure, this coagulative fluid was removed, and total right-side decortication performed. After decortication, patient symptoms improved, and she was discharged from MICU to floor. During follow up after discharge, symptoms resolved fully. The novelty of this case is that complicated parapneumonic effusion developed with acute accumulation of fibrinous coagulative material inside the pleura and extended to the fissures. Analysis of the aspirated material twice did not reveal underlying infection though a high number of WBC's were isolated.
