N2  - Introduction Actinomyces are a group of anaerobes causing cervicofacial disease. In pulmonary actinomyces, the most notorious organism, A. israelii, is associated with severe morbidity and mortality. However A. odontolyticus is a rare pathogen from the group is associated with infections such as brain abscess, peritonitis and thoracopulmonary. We present a young male patient with necrotizing pneumonia and empyema caused by Actinomyces Odontolyticus in an active drug user. Case report 26 year old male with significant smoking history and reported IV drug abuse presented with productive cough, pleuritic chest pain and fever for 2 weeks unresponsive to oral antibiotics. Patient was afebrile, BP was within normal limits however oxygen saturation on room air was 94%. On examination: adequate dentition and decreased breath sounds on left lung. Labs showed WBC 31,200, Urine positive for opiates and workup for HIV, Hepatitis B and C and Flu swab test were negative. CT showed large multi-loculated left pleural effusion with findings suspicious of necrotizing pneumonia with abscess formation. He was started on Vancomycin, Zosyn and Ciprofloxacin. Thoracentesis showed exudate with pH <6.9 and high WBC. Blood culture was positive for Actinomyces Odontolyticus. Chest tube was placed, followed by fibrinolytic therapy. Condition improved and he was discharged home with oral antibiotics CT with contrast showing left sided necrotizing pneumonia with abscess and pleural effusion (left), interval improvement (right) Discussion Pulmonary Actinomycosis has a bimodal age distribution with male predominance. Incidence is greater in patients with poor oral hygiene, drug use, alcoholism, and underlying respiratory disorders. Pulmonary actinomycosis may present as lung abscess, necrotizing pneumonia, or lung gangrene depending on the severity of infection and causative organisms. CT scan shows different range of findings such as air space consolidation, cavitation, pleural thickening, small to moderate sized pleural effusions and lymphadenopathy. In the present case, consolidation with pleural involvement, and pulmonary infiltrates with air bronchograms were more suggestive of pulmonary actinomycosis. Treatment of anaerobic necrotizing pneumonia and empyema is challenging due to lack of appropriate guidelines regarding medical versus surgical management. Early detection, prompt antibiotic treatment, and monitoring with serial imaging provides excellent prognosis. However, if empyema develops, early thoracentesis and intercostals drainage are indicated. If the patient does not improve or further deteroriates while on adequate treatment, surgery is indicated. Conclusion Prompt diagnosis, antimicrobial therapy and chest drainage with frequent imaging scan monitoring of necrotizing pneumonia with empyema resulted in favorable outcome.
