N2  - Clinical Presentation: An 81-year-old male, ex-miner presented with prolonged dry coughs and a Myelodysplastic syndrome with history of asbestos exposure. Radiology scans showed a left upper lobe (LUL) mass and PET (Positron Emission Tomography) positive nodes in the chest. Transbronchial Needle Aspiration (TBNA) of the 4R and 7L lymph nodes was attended by a cytotechnologist. Both bronchial brushing and washing samples were obtained from the LUL. Cytohistological Findings: The bronchial brushing showed high cellularity with large aggregates of metaplastic squamous epithelial cells and granulation tissue fragments within a florid mixed acute and chronic inflammatory background. Specimens from the two nodes and the washing were negative for neoplasm. Whilst the cytology sample favoured florid immature squamous metaplasia with reactive cytological atypia rather than a neoplasm, a necrotic squamous carcinoma could not be definitely excluded. The concurrent core biopsy from the LUL was fibrotic and heavily inflamed with a mixed inflammatory infiltrate, and showed similar features to the cytological specimen. Some of the biopsy sample was sent to microbiology for testing. Culture was performed and Nocardia beijingensis was isolated and identified as a result. Discussion: Clinically, nocardiosis is a rare and potentially life threatening gram positive bacterial infection with a mortality rate as high as 60% [1, 2]. Nocardia is recognised as an opportunistic pathogen found in soil and vegetable matter, most commonly affecting immuno-compromised patients [3]. Non-specific symptoms and radiological features can make diagnosis difficult as it may mimic other infectious diseases like Tuberculosis, actinomycosis, fungal infection, and malignancy [1]. A high index of suspicion for nocardiosis must be maintained while assessing intense inflammatory cytologic material in immunosuppressed patients to allow a timely diagnosis and lead to appropriate treatment of the patients with nocardiosis.
