N2  - Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) kills around 1.5 million people each year. Albeit, only 10% of humans develop active disease after aerosol infection, the basis for differential susceptibility of humans remains unclear. To determine if human macrophages are heterogeneous in their ability to kill Mtb, cord blood derived macrophages were treated with either IFN-γ or IL-4 to prepare M1 and M2 phenotype respectively in vitro. Macrophages were infected with a 1:1 ratio of Mtb and days 3 and 5 post infection, macrophage lysates were plated for CFU counts. In addition, intracellular autophagy was measured in gfpMtb infected macrophages using cytoID stains to detect autophagosomes and LC3 lipidation through western blot of macrophage lysates. Mtb infected macrophages were overlaid using CD4 T cells specific for the Antigen-85B epitope to measure antigen presentation. M1 macrophages showed a bacteriostatic ability and restricted the growth of Mtb while IL-4 treated M2 macrophages showed nearly 1 log increase in the growth of Mtb. Thus M2 macrophages were permissive for growth of Mtb. Consistent with this observation, gfpMtb co-localized with autophagosomes more frequently in M1 macrophages suggesting a role for autophagy during growth inhibition. M1 macrophages also presented Ag85B to CD4 T cells better than M2 macrophages. Enhanced antigen presentation by M1 macrophages was also found to be dependent on IFITM3, Atg5 and Atg 16 genes as siRNA knock down of these specific genes resulted in reduced antigen presentation by M1 macrophages. These observations suggest that autophagy pathways within macrophage may govern their heterogeneity into M1 or M2 hence their innate ability to kill intracellular Mtb and trigger immune activation.
