N2  - PURPOSE: Endobronchial tuberculosis (EBTB) is TB infection of the tracheobronchial tree with microbial and histopathological evidence. Further investigations often needed to diagnose EBTB due to its complexity. We sought to profile our EBTB patients in terms of clinical, radiological, microbial, pathological and bronchoscopic features in Hospital Selayang. METHODS: This is a retrospective analysis of 17 patients with EBTB diagnosed in Hospital Selayang (HS) from 2015 till 2017. Demographic data, clinical, radiological, microbiological, histopathological and bronchoscopic findings were obtained from HS online system. Endobronchial lesions were classified according to Chung classification. Diagnosis were made based on histopathological examination (HPE) of endobronchial biopsy, and/or positive AFB stain/Mycobacterium Tuberculosis (MTB) culture of bronchial alveolar lavage (BAL) and/or endobronchial biopsy. RESULTS: The study population is predominantly female, mean age of 43 years old. Commonest symptom is cough (n=15, 88%). Commonest radiographic finding is consolidation (n=15, 88%). Commonest endobronchial lesion is caseous lesion (n=9, 53%). Commonest HPE is caseating granulomatous inflammation (n=8, 47%). BAL specimens grew Mycobacterium tuberculosis in 65% of cases. All patients showed good clinical response with TB treatment, and repeated bronchoscopy post treatment showed complete resolution in 35% of patients. CONCLUSIONS: Profiles of EBTB patients in our cohort are young female with cough, caseating HPE and good response to antiTB. Future research is required to validate this profiling in order to form a universal prediction model. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Clinical prediction via patient profiling will aid in early and accurate diagnosis of EBTB, hence expediting treatment and prevent complications.
