N2  - PURPOSE: In this paper, we report on seroepidemiology of Bordetella pertussis, Mycoplasma Pneumonia and Chlamydia Pneumonia in Iranian Hajj pilgrims who suffered from prolonged cough illnesses. These coughs, known as ‘Hajj cough’, are very common. METHODS: Antibodies against three bacteria which are known to cause severe prolonged coughs in general populations were measured in pilgrims before and after a Hajj journey. The concept of mixed infection was also examined. RESULTS: The study showed exposure to some pathogens exists at least at the laboratory level. Even in some instances, infections occurred, especially mixed infections, but these could not be a reason for ‘Hajj cough’ on their own. There may be some bacterial or viral infections superimposed on chronic respiratory diseases, and toxic or allergic conditions, such as atopy, but epidemiologic and even treatment studies will be difficult to do until easier and more reliable methods of diagnosis are available. CONCLUSIONS: Our results can possibly be generalized to the whole Hajj population because overcrowding is universal during the Hajj ceremony. The results of this study can open a new venue to promote future research in the field. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: We hypothesized that ‘Hajj cough’ is not just an atypical infection, and that atopy has its role too, which needs more investigation to confirm. CHEST19-0392
