N2  - We present the case of a 15-year-old girl, who went to emergency department due to fever, cough and purulent expectoration, initially treated as a catarrhal condition. She comes days later with slow thinking and language, neck stiffness, rough systolic murmur, vascular palmoplantar lesions and subungual slime hemorrhages. She developed a left hemiparesis, so a cranial CT was performed demonstrating a right hypodense area compatible with ischemic stroke. Urgent TTE was performed, which revealed a mitral valve with multiple vegetative lesions; one in relation to the posterior leaflet at P2-P3 level, and another large one in relation to the mitral anterior leaflet, with perforation and destruction of the valvular tissue. Large implants were also found at the left atrial wall. In the transesophageal echocar-diogram performed subsequently, abscess of the mitral annulus was observed. Lumbar puncture shown turbid cer-ebrospinal fluid, with cytochemical characteristics compatible with bacterial infection. Chest radiography revealed pulmonary condensation at the basal level of the right lung. Given the suspicion of bacterial endocarditis with mitral valve destruction, pneumonia and meningitis, IV antibiotic treatment was started. Given that the patient did not present severe neurological deterioration; and faced with the risk of hemorrhagic transformation, cardiac surgery was postponed. However, during admission she developed acute heart failure and hemodynamic instability, so he underwent surgery 16 days after admission, evidencing great vegetation and perforation of the anterior leaflet, vegetation at the basal level of the posterior leaflet with destruction of the mitral annulus at P2-P3 level. ATS No. 27 prosthesis was implanted, with the need to perform suture in the left atrial wall because of destruction of the annulus at that level. Sputum cultures, blood culture and cerebrospinal fluid culture were negative, but PCR (polymerase chain reaction assay) in these biological samples detected Streptococcus pneumoniae DNA. Austrian's Syndrome is the triad of pneumonia, meningitis and endocarditis caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae. S. pneumoniae is responsible for <3% of all cases of native valve endocarditis since the discovery of penicillin, and today is a rarity, usually affecting the aortic valve and secondly the mitral valve, being usually healthy native valves. Only 1 to 25% of patients with pneumococcal endocarditis have been reported to have the triad. Alcoholism and asplenia have been described as risk factors, factors that our patient did not present. The mortality of this syndrome remains high despite treatment (overall mortality approximately 65%), given that it manifests itself in an aggressive and acute manner, with early valvular rupture, formation of myocardial abscesses, peripheral embolisms, septic arthritis, glomerulonephritis, meningitis, etc. Surgical treatment is necessary in the majority of patients.
