N2  - Case report A 54 year old male with AIDS (CD4 116), G6PD deficiency and noncompliance presented with shortness of breath accompanied with productive cough, pleuritic chest pain and subjective fevers. He denied any weight loss, hemoptysis, night sweats, being homeless, incarcerated or being exposed to TB. He was found to be febrile to 102.9, tachycardic and hypoxic requiring non-rebreather. X-ray and CT chest were both significant for multifocal pneumonia. LDH was 259. CBC was significant for leukocytosis with bands. BMP showed hypokalemia and a sodium of 140. Procalcitonin was 1.3. EKG was significant for sinus tachycardia. Urine Legionella antigen was positive. Streptococcus pneumonia antigen, MRSA, respiratory panel, respiratory culture, AFB and blood cultures were negative. He underwent bronchoscopy which showed purulent secretions in left upper lobe and no obstructive lesions. AFB, PCP and fungal cultures were negative. He was started on vancomycin and cefepime, which was later changed to azithromycin to treat legionella pneumonia. He was also treated with prednisone and atovaquone empirically for PCP, which was discontinued once PCP culture was noted to be negative. Legionella is a Gram-negative intracellular pathogen. Infection usually occurs after inhalation of aerosols from contaminated water or soil. Incidence of Legionnaire's disease is 1.4- 1.8 cases per 1 00 000. Risk factors include age greater than 50 and immunosuppression. Our patient's risk factors included AIDS and age but no known Legionella exposure or other sick contacts. Patients usually present with respiratory symptoms; however associated symptoms may include nausea and vomiting. Hyponatremia is present in the majority of cases; however, our patient had a normal sodium. Diagnosis is confirmed via PCR, sputum culture or urine antigen testing. It must be noted that urine antigen testing only detects serotype 1 and thus, PCR or culture should be ordered if antigen testing is negative and there is a high suspicion for the disease. The urine antigen sensitivity and specificity for Legionella is seventy percent and ninety percent, respectively. Extrapulmonary infections such as cellulitis, septic arthritis, endocarditis, meningitis are rare but have been reported. Treatment includes use of Levaquin or Azithromycin.
