N2  - Learning Objectives: Streptococcus Pneumoniae is the most common bacterial cause of community acquired pneumonia in adults and children. Complications associated with Pneumococcal pneumonia commonly include bacteremia, parapneumonic effusion and rarely necrotizing pneumonia/lung abscess. We present a case of rapidly developing lung abscess associated with pneumococcal pneumonia. Methods: A 56-year-old lady presented with a four day history of productive cough, shortness of breath and fever. Her past medical history was significant for gouty arthritis and anxiety disorder. She had a 20 pack year smoking history and consumed about 16 units of alcohol weekly. At presentation her vital signs were: blood pressure 151/90mmHg, heart rate 125 beats/minute and temperature of 103.8 F; respiratory rate 28 breaths/min and oxygen saturation of 85% on room air. She was intubated for worsening hypoxemic respiratory failure. Lung auscultation revealed coarse crepitations in the right middle and lower zones. The remainder of her examination was unremarkable. Initial laboratory data including complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel were unremarkable. Chest computed tomography (CT) revealed extensive segmental and patchy consolidation involving both lungs. Urine streptococcal antigen was positive. She was started empirically on intravenous Ceftriaxone 1 g daily. On day 5, streptococcus pneumoniae was isolated from the initial blood cultures. She was extubated on day 8 of hospitalization. On day 9, repeat chest CT performed showed extensive cavitary and cystic transformation within the right upper lobe. She completed 12 weeks of therapy with oral cefuroxime. Repeat CT chest performed at outpatient follow-up 4 months later showed improvement in areas of necrosis. Results: Necrotizing pneumonia and lung abscesses are commonly caused by anaerobic bacteria and are rarely reported as a complication of pneumococcal pneumonia. Our patient presented with pneumococcal pneumonia and respiratory failure, with initial imaging consistent with bilateral consolidation without any evidence of cavitary changes. Repeat CT Chest only 9 days after first CT showed extensive cavitary and cystic changes. There are very few cases reported with a rapidly progressing necrotizing pneumonia. Our patient developed necrotizing pneumococcal pneumonia within nine days of presentation despite initial appropriate antibiotic treatment thus emphasizing on maintaining an index of suspicion for this rare complication of a common illness.
