N2  - Learning Objectives: The incidence of Tuberculosis (TB) in the United States was at 2.8 per 100,000 in 2017 of which 20% had extra-pulmonary disease. Disseminated tuberculous lymphadenitis in young immunocompetent and HIV negative patients is an under reported cause of generalized lymphadenopathy. Early diagnosis is imperative in the management and overall prognosis of the disease. Methods: A 32-year-old male from Africa with no past medical history presented with intermittent fatigue and multiple painless bilateral lumps in the neck, axilla and groin. Review of systems was negative for fever, cough and weight loss. He denied recent travel or sick contact. Physical exam was remarkable for bilateral nontender cervical, supraclavicular, axillary, and inguinal lymphadenopathy. Laboratory data including complete blood counts, liver function tests, serum ACE and LDH levels were normal. Sputum for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) and other infectious work up including Hepatitis B, C and HIV were negative, however the QuantiFERON test was positive. CT neck, chest and abdomen showed granulomatous disease of lung and multifocal adenopathy in the neck, chest, and abdomen. Excisional biopsy of the left axillary lymph nodes showed necrotizing caseating granulomas. Flow cytometry was negative for lymphoproliferative disorder. AFB smear was negative; however, the culture grew mycobacterium tuberculosis complex (MTC). The patient was started on a four-drug anti-TB regimen resulting in significant clinical improvement. Results: Disseminated TB lymphadenitis, although uncommon, can lead to severe complications if left untreated. Factors associated with disseminated forms of TB include poverty, female sex, pregnancy, malnutrition, advanced age, alcohol abuse, corticosteroid or other immunosuppressive therapy. Rarely TB dissemination may also occur in healthy, immunocompetent patients posing a clinical dilemma. The clinical picture is often variable. Treatment is essentially the same as for pulmonary infection, however the duration may vary in cases of immunosuppression and CNS involvement. Disseminated tuberculous lymphadenitis can rarely affect healthy individuals, posing a clinical dilemma and hence should always be included in the differential diagnosis of widespread lymphadenopathy.
