N2  - Learning Objectives: Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) is a severe drug hypersensitivity reaction carrying a mortality rate as high as 45% in critically ill patients. Diagnosis is often elusive and withdrawal of a medically necessary offending agent can prove difficult. We describe a case of a 54-year-old female admitted to the ICU with DRESS presumed secondary to drug therapies for active pulmonary tuberculosis. Methods: The patient presented after recent travel to Mexico with severe cough. She was found to have bilateral cavitary lesions and hilar lymphadenopathy on Chest CT. Sputum culture was positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis and she was started on RIPE (Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol) therapy. The patient was discharged to home where she noted fever, nausea, poor appetite and rash on her torso and limbs two weeks later. She presented again to the hospital and was found to have peripheral eosinophilia and transaminitis. RIPE was discontinued with improvement in rash and her liver function tests. Other causes of hepatitis were pursued and returned no significant findings. Her symptoms were attributed to Rifampin and the rest of her RIPE therapies were restarted, however within days, the rash again worsened and RIPE therapy was discontinued. Corticosteroids were initiated for treatment of DRESS along with cyclosporine. Acute liver injury rapidly progressed to acute liver failure. The patient became significantly altered and developed respiratory failure requiring intubation. Additionally, she demonstrated refractory shock and severe acidosis requiring renal replacement therapy and vasopressors. She was not a candidate for liver transplantation due to her active pulmonary tuberculosis. After several days of corticosteroids and organ supportive therapies, the patient continued to demonstrate multiple organ failure with no signs of improvement and her family elected to withdraw support. Results: The most severe site of visceral involvement in DRESS is the liver. Hepatitis is sometimes fulminant and is responsible for the majority of deaths associated with this syndrome. Early recognition and withdrawal of the offending drug is essential, and corticosteroids represent first line of therapy. This case presented particular challenge due to the necessity of the offending agents to optimally treat a tenacious, active infectious disease and because of the difficulty in identifying the offending agent in the setting of multiple drug therapy.
