N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary embolism (PE) may present as extremely varied clinical scenarios, oftentimes requiring a high index of suspicion for diagnosis. In patients already receiving vitamin K antagonists, although the INR on admission is not a clear indicator of the continuous quality of previous anticoagulation, therapeutic levels are unlikely to be associated with new thrombosis. Case presentation: A 76-year-old patient presented for progressive dyspnea on mild exertion and productive cough with initially black sputum, then mucopurulent. His past medical history was positive for congestive heart failure due to ischemic heart disease, permanent atrial fibrillation, and CRT-P resynchronization therapy implanted four months prior. He was anticoagulated with acenocoumarol, with a history of low time in therapeutic range (TTR) and a supratherapeutic INR on admission. After clinical examination, we suspected right inferior lobe (RIL) pneumonia. Chest x-ray showed atypical RIL interstitial infiltrates. Blood work revealed increased inflammatory biomarkers, but no leukocytosis or neutrophilia. No bacteria was isolated from the sputum samples. Due to these discrepancies, chest CT scan was performed, diagnosing right PE with pulmonary infarction and a filling defect in the right atrium (RA). Repeated transthoracic echocardiography showed no masses in the RA. However, transesophageal examination revealed a thrombus attached to the RA. Doppler ultrasound of the lower extremities showed no thrombosis as a source for the RA thrombus. Abdomen and pelvis CT ruled out solid malignancy as a substrate for hypercoagulability. We concluded that the RA thrombus developed as a complication of multiple factors: recent implantation of resynchronization therapy in a patient with heart failure and permanent atrial fibrillation, anticoagulated with acenocumarol with an inadequate TTR. Anticoagulation treatment was switched to rivaroxaban with favorable outcome. Discussion: There are data reporting the association of RA thrombosis with either atrial fibrillation, or heart failure, or very rarely with device leads. Our case has multiple particularities, previous antivitamin K therapy being one them, together with the supratherapeutic INR and the concomitant presence of a RA thrombus and PE presenting with signs and symptoms of pneumonia. (Figure Presented).
