N2  - Cystic fibrosis is the most common life-limiting autosomal recessive genetic disorder among white populations. The majority of cystic fibrosis cases are diagnosed in childhood; however, an increasing number of adults are being diagnosed with the condition. We report a case of a 65-year-old Irish woman presenting with a cough productive of green sputum on a background of a 5-month history of recurrent respiratory tract infections requiring multiple hospital admissions. Staphylococcus aureus, Scedosporium apiospermum, and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia were grown from bronchoalveolar lavage, raising suspicion for cystic fibrosis. Genetic testing subsequently revealed the presence of -F508 and R117H cystic fibrosis mutations. In this case, the microbiological results were key to arriving at a diagnosis of cystic fibrosis.
