N2  - Background: Recently, there has been an increase in the range of textiles with barrier effect to airborne bio-particles and micro-organism in healthcare sector. Generally speaking, the inhibition of the growth of microbes in textile material surface can be achieved through biocidal approach (destroy microbes) and biostatic approach (inhibits the growth of microbes). Currently, healthcare apparels will be disposed after use which would induce harmful effect to environment and high operation cost. The biostatic approach of treating textile materials would introduce antibacterial effect and the materials are reusable. With the biostatic approach, it will not induce possible mutation to pathogens leading to development of unknown new bacteria. In this project, antibacterial textile materials by biostatic approach with poly (ethylene glycol) (PEG) for healthcare products will be developed. PEG is a hydrated, non-toxic coating and FDA-approved product which is widely used in biotechnology and consumer applications. In order to applying PEG to textile materials, pad-dry-cure method would be studied to apply PEG with crosslinking agent on the textile material. The effectiveness of PEG coating would be evaluated with wear trial in Faculty of Dentistry, The University of Hong Kong located at the Prince Philip Dental Hospital. Methods: PEG with -NH2 end group (molecular weight of 400) was used due to the consideration of cost and its supplementary effect. Cotton fabrics were treated with PEG (with NH2 groups; molecular weight: 400) (10%) + 1,3-dimethyl-4,5-dihydroxyethylene urea (10%) + Magnesium chloride (2%) by Pad (pick-up 70%) → Dry (90°C, 5 minutes) → Cure (160°C, 45 seconds). The treated fabrics were then made into dental gown for wear trial (5 dentists and 5 dental nurses were invited to participate in the wear trial) and their antibacterial properties were evaluated. Results: The antimicrobial properties of the treated fabrics were evaluated by AATCC Test Method 100 and AATCC Test Method 147 and the results indicating that the treated fabrics have very good antibacterial effect to Klebsiella pneumonia and Staphylococcus aureus. In addition, the wear trial results revealed that the dental gown made with the treated fabrics would not hinder the operation of the dental professional but with good antibacterial and protective effect. Conclusions: PEG with NH2 group can be applied to the cotton woven fabric. Experimental results revealed that the PEG could be coated in the fabrics and the treated fabrics had certain degree of antibacterial function and could be used for making dental gown for daily use.
