N2  - Background: In this paper, plasma treatment would be used for enhancing antibacterial treatment for textiles. The pilot wear trial was conducted in Prince Philip Dental Hospital under the supervision with the staff in The Faculty of Dentistry, The University of Hong Kong. Dental environment is selected because it is associated with a significant risk of exposure to various micro-organism which is one of the occupational related health problems of dental professional. Infectious agents may be present in blood or saliva, as a consequence of bacteremia or viremia associated with systemic infections. Dental professional may be exposed to variety of micro-organisms via aerosols of saliva, gingival fluid and natural organic dust particle mixed with air and water during the dental surgical procedure. Recently, dental professional wear glove, face mask and protective clothes to prevent the explosion to micro-organism during work but the mirco-organisms may still have chance to adhere in the glove, face mask and protective clothes leading to unexpected infection. As a result, it is valuable to develop additional performance enhancements through plasma processing for opening up entirely new market for antimicrobial textiles to prevent the infection. Methods: 100% cotton poplin was used as the fabric substrate and monomer (antimicrobial agent) used was biguanide derivatives. The plasma treatment was conducted using a continuous type plasma machine with the treatment conditions: (a) Plasma treatment (plasma treatment → dip monomer solution → curing 160°C for 60 seconds); (b) Setting: Temperature: 30°C; speed: 0.8 m/min.; flow: 10 m3/min (air) and gap distance between electrodes to generate plasma: 6 mm and (c) 5% monomer with 80% pick-up. The treated fabrics were then made into dental gown for wear trial (10 dentists and 10 dental nurses were invited to participate in the wear trial) and the antibacterial properties of dental gown before and after wear trial were evaluated. Results: The antibacterial properties of the dental gown, before and after wear trial, were evaluated by AATCC Test Method 100 and AATCC Test Method 147 with the bacteria of Klebsiella pneumonia and Staphylococcus aureus. The experimnetal results indicating that the dental gown achieved very good antibacterial effect to Klebsiella pneumonia and Staphylococcus aureus before and after use. When compared, plasma treated cotton fabric could achieve better antibacterial effect than non-plasma treated cotton fabric. In addition, the wear trial results revealed plasmatreated fabrics can be used for making dental gown with better antibacterial function. Conclusions: The plasma treatment could help to give cotton fabric with good antibacterial results and the fabric can be used for preparing wear apparels for use in dental environment with good antibacterial results.
