N2  - The respiratory therapist (RT) plays an essential role in all aspects of nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) diagnosis and treatment. When the diagnosis of NTM is being considered, regular sputum cultures sent for acid-fast bacillus smear and liquid and solid culture are needed. These sputum cultures can be collected in various ways, and the RT should help ensure that the patient is able to produce samples for testing. Many patients are given a sputum tube to collect and bring a sample from home. These samples must be kept in the refrigerator if collected the previous night, and must be submitted for testing within 24 hours of production. Specialty labs, including the Advanced Diagnostic Laboratories at National Jewish Health, offer sputum mailer kits to allow patients to ship samples overnight from around the US for testing. This program has assisted in collecting multiple samples for patients that are not within close proximity to the hospital. At the Colorado CF Center we continuously re-educate patients on how to move mucus to the upper airway where it can be more easily expectorated. An example of this is huff coughing. In younger pediatric and also some adult patients, sputum induction is often required to collect an adequate sample. We use a series of 3-10% hypertonic saline (HTS) nebulizations to hydrate the mucus and help stimulate a cough. For the more difficult to induce patients, physical activity following the HTS treatment can be extremely helpful. Having the patient perform 20-30 jumping jacks following a dose of nebulized HTS has often proven to be effective. If sputum cultures do reveal the presence of NTM, all aspects of airway clearance therapy (ACT) must be optimized to assess the clinical significance of the infection. We review the importance of airway clearance, its physiological purpose, how often it should be performed, and various methods or devices that may be used. If NTM treatment is initiated, it becomes even more important that patients perform effective ACT. The anatomy of each patient and their response to respiratory treatments can vary widely, so ACT should be tailored to each patient's need and lifestyle. CT scans can be helpful in identifying areas of consolidation or disease that the RT and patient will want to pay extra attention to, and may be motivating for the patient. Often, patients with NTM disease have consolidation, atelectasis or collapse in the right middle lobe and/or lingula that is difficult to clear with a therapy vest. In some females, we find that breast tissue can expand the distance between the lungs and the therapy vest, reducing its efficacy. For patients with focal areas of disease we provide electric hand-held percussors, and instruct them how to give directed ACT either alone or with a partner, combined with postural drainage if possible. These techniques are important to review with patients and caregivers, as their effectiveness is reliant on the person administering the therapy. The Colorado Adult Program has been privileged enough to stock these devices within the hospital's central supply department. Using a durable medical equipment company for purchase is also an option, but can be timely and costly for patients. For many patients with NTM, the antibiotic therapy may include inhaled amikacin. Our standard administration and dosing is 500 mg daily with a Pari LC® Star Nebulizer cup. It is recommended that a filter attachment be connected to the nebulizer cup or that the patient go to a separate room if they are planning on performing the treatment around family or pets. The efficacy and safety of this medication depends on patients being properly informed on mixing and administering the drug. In addition to amikacin, some patients will be directed to continue therapies treating Pseudomonas aeruginosa. If the patient is on inhaled tobramycin, we recommend discontinuation during the use of inhaled amikacin in order to avoid the use of two aminoglycosides. Success of NTM treatment is generally defined by clearing the sputum of NTM organisms for 12 months on treatment. For patients that have been successfully treated, we continue to follow NTM cultures in additional to CF pathogen and fungal cultures at all clinic visits. Our NTM testing frequency exceeds the minimum requirements recommended by the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation. We do this because the rate of relapse and/or re-infection is high. NTM treatment requires the patient to be dedicated to their respiratory treatments, and it is the goal of the RT to guide, support and assist patients to be successful with this part of their care. While on treatment, patients may be seen as often as monthly for toxicity monitoring, review of clinical course and to check sputum cultures. At each visit, we try to review ACT and encourage patients to keep up the hard work. Adherence to extended inhaled therapies and ACT can be very challenging for patients to balance with their quality of life. The RT should be a source of positive reinforcement and understanding, which can include working with patients to obtain portable devices or helping to create a respiratory regimen that can fit into their day-to-day life.
