N2  - It is well established that lung disease in cystic fibrosis (CF) begins early in life (1,2). CF lung disease is characterized by airway inflammation and infection, ultimately leading to bronchiectasis and lung function decline. Pulmonary exacerbations defined by an increase in respiratory and systemic symptoms including increased cough, increased sputum production, decreased exercise tolerance and weight loss are common in pediatric patients. Pulmonary exacerbations are frequently treated with antibiotics to improve these symptoms with the goal of preventing lung function progression. Pulmonary exacerbations are a significant cause of morbidity in older children and adults with CF and are associated with more rapid lung function decline (3). However, less is known about the impact of pulmonary exacerbations and their treatment in infants and young children with CF. This is in part due to challenges with outcome measures in this age range. However, a few studies have demonstrated improvements in clinical, imaging and lung function outcomes suggesting that early and aggressive use of antibiotics may be beneficial in infants and young children with CF. Investigators for the Epidemiologic Study of Cystic Fibrosis (ESCF) evaluated treatment of pulmonary exacerbations in a large cohort of children with CF under 6 years of age (4). Children with a higher number of clinical signs and symptoms were more likely to receive treatment (4). However, even amongst those noted to have all characteristics, only approximately two-thirds (63%) received antibiotics (4). The children who received treatment were less likely to have crackles, cough, wheeze and a culture positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa in short-term follow-up (defined as the first follow-up visit within 6 months) compared to those with symptoms who were not treated (4). Several studies examined the impact of antibiotics for pulmonary exacerbations in infants and young children on structural lung disease, measured by chest CT and lung function. In one study, high resolution CT scans were performed in 21 infants pre- and post-IV antibiotic course and improvements in airway wall thickening, airspace disease and air trapping were noted (5). Infant pulmonary function testing (iPFT) was also performed before and after antibiotics. Among those with abnormalities in flow and air trapping on initial testing, 72.7% (8/11) and 61.5% (8/14) respectively demonstrated improvement (5). Another study evaluated iPFT in 11 infants pre- and post-antibiotic courses (10 received IV antibiotics, 1 treated with oral antibiotics) and demonstrated significant improvements in FEV0.5, FVC, FEV0.5/ FVC and significant decreases in FRCpleth, RV and RV/TLC (6). This suggests improvements in obstructive lung disease and decreased air trapping following completion of an antibiotic course. Lung conductance and lung compliance has also been shown to improve following IV antibiotic treatment in a cohort of 17 infants and toddlers with CF (7). Symptom improvement was also noted in the majority of the subjects as defined by symptoms and physical exam (6,7). These studies span decades, suggesting that despite changes in CF care over time, the use of antibiotics during an exacerbation results in acute improvement in symptoms, pulmonary function and structural disease in infants and young children. Although these studies demonstrate short-term improvement in lung function following treatment with antibiotics, it is less clear the impact antibiotics have on long-term lung function. In the ESCF study, there was no detectable difference in FEV1 percent predicted or weight-for-age z-score at age 7 years between the treated and untreated children. However, the authors note that a single pulmonary exacerbation and its treatment may not have a large impact on ongoing airway inflammation and infection (4). Similarly, in a longitudinal study of infants and preschool children with CF, pulmonary exacerbation frequency over two years was not associated with lower lung function and in fact was associated with higher FVC at 7 years (8). In contrast, other studies have demonstrated worse lung function at school age in those with more frequent exacerbations (9) or early respiratory infections (10), although the outcomes of these studies may have been affected by the use of prophylactic antibiotics. Therefore, larger, longitudinal evaluations of pulmonary exacerbations and their treatment are indicated to better determine long-term outcomes. However, given the improvement in symptoms and short-term outcomes in lung function and imaging demonstrated in previous studies, the aggressive use of antibiotics in infants and young children is indicated in the setting of pulmonary exacerbations.
