N2  - Cystic fibrosis (CF) is insidious: while most infants and young children with CF do not have regular respiratory symptoms, there is clear evidence that inflammation, infection, and even significant functional and structural airways disease are present from a very early age (1,2). While there is good evidence that lower airways disease is present from an early age, the appropriate timing of antibiotics in the management of young children with CF remains somewhat controversial. While some children clearly require antibiotic therapy based on longevity or severity of symptoms, early, aggressive antimicrobial management of relatively mild respiratory symptoms in children with CF may do more harm than good. Pulmonary exacerbations are loosely defined clinically as an increase in respiratory symptoms (most often cough) and decrease in pulmonary function measures, often associated with treatment with oral or intravenous antibiotics. Exacerbations have been shown repeatedly to play a role in the progression of CF lung disease, with each occurrence associated with a risk of lack of recovery to baseline lung function (3). Though pulmonary exacerbations are often attributed to changes in the bacterial milieu of the lower respiratory tract, respiratory viruses are often present, particularly in children, raising the question of whether a new viral organism, a shift in bacterial community dynamics, or both, is responsible for presence of new symptoms (4). Patients with viral infections at the time of pulmonary exacerbation have been shown to have lower likelihood of response to antibiotic therapy or immediate recovery of lung function (5,6), but not a clear difference in decline in lung function over the subsequent year (7). This suggests that the time course of recovery from a viral infection differs from typical expectations surrounding a pulmonary exacerbation in children with CF, and may also point to the futility of treating a viral infection with antibiotics in this population. Given that even young children without chronic respiratory disease can experience 8 to 12 respiratory viral infections per year, there is a tremendous potential for overlap in symptoms between viral infections and bacterially-mediated exacerbations, and for overtreatment of those viral infections with antibiotics in children with CF. Antibiotic resistance is a rising threat in health care, and a significant issue in patients with cystic fibrosis, who tend to receive repeated courses of antibiotics directed at the same organism over their lifetimes (8). Overuse and inappropriate use of antimicrobials are key factors in the development of antibiotic resistance on both an individual and epidemiologic level (9). Further, resistant organisms such as MRSA have been associated with a more rapid decline in lung function in children and young adults with CF (10,11). Outside of antibiotic resistance, overuse of antibiotics can cause significant morbidity and social stress. Hypersensitivity reactions such as anaphylaxis are common children with CF (12), and children are often labelled as having a drug allergy if a rash develops while taking antibiotics, though the rash may be virally mediated. Diagnosis of drug allergy can have long-term implications for options for future oral antibiotic treatment as well as the cost, availability, and safety profile of future IV antibiotics. Even relatively benign adverse effects, such as loose stools and gastrointestinal upset, can result in weight loss in patients with CF, who are at high risk for poor nutritional outcomes in childhood at baseline. Finally, the social stresses of aggressive antibiotic use should not be minimized - struggles to administer oral medications to young children, additional demands on an already over-crowded treatment schedule, and financial costs of insurance co-pays and missed parental work days (particularly in the case of hospitalization for IV antibiotics) are real considerations. Young children with CF are likely to have a viral infection present at the time of presentation with a pulmonary exacerbation (5). As children with viral infections are less likely to respond robustly to antibiotics, but not necessarily more likely to experience greater decline in lung function following exacerbation (5,6,12), the aggressive use of antibiotics in these circumstances makes little sense, and may lead to unnecessary side effects, development of hypersensitivity, and increased treatment burden and social cost. Thus, the measured use of antibiotics in children with more severe, or more prolonged respiratory symptoms outside of the norm for viral upper respiratory infections, may strike an appropriate balance in our youngest patients with CF.
