N2  - Methods: We are prospectively evaluating patients, with HRCT findings of bronchiectasis, after excluding cystic fibrosis. Etiology, clinical features, radiological findings, spirometric results and microbiological data are collected. Results: These are the interim results of an ongoing study since March 2017. At the time of submission of this abstract, 93 patients have been enrolled. Among enrolled patients, 76.3% patients are men. Etiology is found in 92.5% of patients. The most common cause is post TB bronchiectasis(63.4%), followed by post pneumonia(11.8%), COPD(5.4%), ABPA(4.3%), connective tissue diseases(2.2%) with 1.1%each of ciliary dyskinesia, Marfan's syndrome, hypogammaglobinemia, post-obstructive, and diffuse panbronchiolitis. No cause was found in 7.5% of patients. Cough is the predominant symptom (95.7%) followed by daily sputum production(78%), dyspnea (60.2%), and clubbing(35.5%). Hemoptysis is the commonest complication(24.7%) followed by respiratory failure(19.4%). On spirometry, an obstructive pattern is observed in the majority of the patients (63.4%). On HRCT, unilateral and bilateral upper lobe involvement was found (24.7%) each.A diffuse bilateral disease is found in (11.1%) of patients. Pseudomonas is the commonest pathogen (36%), followed by Moraxella(12.9%), Haemophilus influenza(8.6%), Aspergillus species(7.5%), Enterobacteriaceae (5.4%), and no pathogen was found in 17.2% patients. Conclusion: In our data, post TB bronchiectasis remained the most common cause of non-CF bronchiectasis, Pseudomonas is the most common pathogen, obstructive pattern of spirometry is found in majority and hemoptysis is the most frequent complication.
