N2  - Objective: to asses the results of the tolerance test to inhaled antibiotics in adults with chronic bronchial infection. Material and Methods: this is an observational transversal unicentre study of patients under 18 with chronic bronchial infection that have been prescribed an inhaled antibiotic. Data about the tolerability of the antibiotics were collected in the first our before doing the tolerability test by pulmonary function tests and a Likert questionnaire. The sample was stratified into three groups: colistin, gentamicin or ampicillin. It was considered poor tolerance if the questions referring to feeling chest pain, dyspnoea and wheezing had a value of 2 or 3 (quite or a lot). Regarding the questions referring to cough, pharyngeal irritation and nauseas or poor taste, patients marking 3 (a lot) were considered to have poor tolerance. Poor tolerance was also considered if the forced forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) after bronchodilation decreased ≥ 15% following administration of the antibiotic. Results: 35 patients were included. The median age was 70 (55-77) and 17 of them were males. 19 patients were or had been smokers and the pack-year index was 50 (35-80). 65.7% had bronchiectasis with a median score in the FACED score of 2 (1-4). 25% had chronic infection by P.aeruginosa and the FEV1 was 69 (52-95). The rate of patients who tolerated colistina, gentamicin and ampicillin were 100%, 84% and 89, 7% respectively. There were no significant differences between groups. No statistically significant differences were found in any of the items of the Likert questionnaire. Conclusions: The three antibiotics tested were well tolerated with no statistically significant differences.
