N2  - Introduction: It is generally believed, though not yet proved, that the causative bacteria in protracted bacterial bronchitis (PBB) grow not as planktonic forms but rather in biofilms. Aim: To show that bacteria isolated from BAL cultures from children with PBB, can form biofilms. Methods: Thirty children, 2-8 year-old were diagnosed with PBB on clinical grounds. They all suffered from wet cough for at least 3 months and improved temporarily with antibiotics. None of the children had bronchiectatic changes on C/T scans. All children underwent sweat chloride test and serum immunoglobulins measurement for the exclusion of cystic fibrosis and immunodeficiency. Flexible bronchoscopy (FB) was performed after a number of recurrences of cough had occurred. All children were antibiotic free for at least 2 weeks before FB. BAL was cultured and isolated bacteria were further processed for biofilm formation with the Calgary biofilm device. The crystal violet assay was used as a semi-quantitative method for assessment of biofilm formation. The absorbance of each well was measured at 630nm and compared to negative control wells. Results: The appearance of bronchial tree in FB was compatible with the diagnosis of PBB. Conventional, quantitative BAL cultures isolated ≥104 cfu/ml of bacteria in 26 children. Eighteen (69.2%) of these isolates (6 Staphylococcus aureus, 5 Haemophilus influenzae, 5 Moraxella catarrhalis, 1 Proteus mirabilis, 1 Pseudomonas aeruginosa) were able of formatting biofilms. Conclusion: Our data provide indirect evidence that PBB might be due to biofilm formation.
