N2  - Background: To date no studies investigating the secretion of fungi have been performed in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients. However, the often high fungal loads in colonized CF patients may suggest transmission of fungi through cough-secretion. Aims and objectives: We investigated the microbial spread of Aspergillus fumigatus, relative to typical bacterial CF pathogens, during coughing maneuvers in chronically colonized CF patients. Methods: Adult CF patients colonized with A. fumigatus coughed twice on 2 different “cough plates”, one containing fungal medium and one containing bacterial medium. Simultaneously a sputum sample was collected. If A. fumigatus was isolated on both the cough plate and sputum sample, Short-Tandem-Repeat (STR) genotyping was performed. Identical STR genotypes were deemed as proof of secretion. Results: Twenty cough-plate assessments were performed from 15 patients. A. fumigatus was cultured in 11 sputum samples (9 patients). Two out of 11 (18%) assessments with a positive sputum culture also showed growth of A. fumigatus on the cough plate. In one patient secretion was demonstrated with an identical STR genotype of the secreted fungus and all A. fumigatus cultured in the current and previous sputum samples. The other patient showed four unique A. fumigatus in the sputum culture of which none was identical to the fungus cultured on the cough plate. All CBA cough plates showed bacterial growth. The transmission frequencies of bacterial pathogens was: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, 60%; Staphylococcus aureus, 8.3%. Conclusion: This is the first study that shows secretion of A. fumigatus in a CF patient during coughing.
