N2  - Introduction: Chronic cough is a common presentation in respiratory paediatrics. Differentiating benign from serious underlying causes remains a challenge partly due to lack of descriptive and longitudinal research. Aims: Retrospective observational study to describe the presenting features and clinical course of chronic cough, identifying possible predictors of duration and aetiology. Methods: Children aged ≤12 years (y) referred to a paediatric respiratory clinic January 2010-January 2012 with chronic cough (>8 weeks) had their clinic letter and two-year follow-up records analysed for the following data: age, sex, preterm birth, exposure to pets and smoke, family history of atopy, co-morbidities, associated vomiting/choking, symptom free spells, pre- and post-clinic cough duration, diagnosis. Results: 222 children (128 males) were included (mean age 3.0y (range 2.4 months (m)-12.8y, SD 2.7y) with 84% <5y. In 75% of children cough resolved by 24m (by 26 and 20m in those with and without a diagnosis respectively). No cause of cough was found in 32% (71/222). Diagnoses included viral-induced wheeze (VIW)/asthma (21% (47/222)), recurrent LRTI (20% (44/222)); protracted bacterial bronchitis (PBB, 5% (11/222)), and bronchiectasis (2% (5/222)). Symptom-free spells (reported in 71% (158/222) were positively associated with PBB (p=0.03); preterm birth with VIW/asthma (p=0.03). No other parameters investigated (including reported cough duration prior to 1st consultation) were statistically significantly associated with total duration or aetiology. Conclusions: PBB should be considered in chronic cough with symptom-free spells. Other predictors of cough duration and aetiology need further exploration.
