N2  - History of present illness: Sixty-year-old man without comorbidities presented with persistent left upper quadrant abdominal pain. One month ago, the patient had dull aching left upper quadrant pain worsening while taking deep breath. He denied pain in other area. The pain was persistent all day without relief by any medications. He had no history of cough. He had no fever, no diarrhea, no vomiting. He lost 2 kg of his weight in this period. He went to private clinic and was prescribed oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (80/400) twice a day for 7 days without improvement. Past medical history: The patient had no underlying disease. He denied history of major trauma or major surgery. He denied use of alcohol and tobacco. He had no history of tuberculosis contact. Medications: None Allergies: None Epidemiological history: Patient is a farmer. He lives in northeast of Thailand, Southeast Asia. There was no history of traveling outside the area. Physical examination: He appeared well and was afebrile during admission (maximum temperature 37°C). Other vital signs were in normal limits. The abdominal examination demonstrated mild tenderness at left upper abdomen without sign of splenomegaly, his liver span was 9 cm without tenderness, shifting dullness was negative. Other physical examinations were unremarkable. Studies: His Hemoglobin was 11.3 g/dl (normal range 12.7- 16.9 g/dl). His white blood count was normal (8430/mcl). He had mild elevation of absolute eosinophil count (792, 9.4%). His renal function and liver function test were all in normal range. His fasting blood sugar was 92 mg/dl and HbA1C was 6.1%. Chest x-ray showed no definite infiltration. Hemocultures were obtained but showed no significant growth. Abdominal computed tomography was performed which demonstrated prominent size of spleen with multiple small hypodensity nodules, 0.5-1.6 cm in size and there was fluid collection 1.4x6.1 cm at lateral part of spleen suspected abscess forming. Liver showed normal size without space taking lesion. Diagnostic procedures and results: The fluid collection at lateral part of spleen were collected with ultrasound-guided aspiration. There were no bacteria found in pus gram stain, however pus culture identified Burkholderia psuedomallei which was sensitive to antibiotics ceftazidime and co-trimoxazole. Treatment and follow up: Patient was given 2-week course of intravenous 2 gram every 8 hours of ceftazidime. He remained afebrile and his abdominal pain gradually improved until completely resolved. Twenty-week of oral co-trimoxazole is planned for maintenance therapy. The Follow up imaging was planned at 6 weeks of treatment. Discussion: Melioidosis is an infectious disease caused by Burkholderia psuedomallei. It mainly occurs in southeast Asia and northern Australia. Important risk factors in developing this infection include thalassemia and uncontrolled diabetes mellitus. The typical clinical manifestations are bacteremia, visceral abscess, and community acquired pneumonia. There were only few case reports of isolated melioidotic splenic abscess and most cases have uncontrolled diabetes. We present an uncommon case of isolated splenic melioidosis in a patient who does not have any medical history. Most melioidotic cases are very well responded to intravenous ceftazidime. Final diagnosis: Isolated melioidotic splenic abscess.
