N2  - Background: Strongiloidasis is an unfrequent tissular parasitosis caused by the nematode Strongyloides stercoralis (SS), in endemic tropical and subtropical areas. Here we present the case an unusual clinical expression of this infection in a non immunosupressed patient in a non-endemic area of SS. Method: A 62-year-old male patient was referred to the Allergy outpatient clinic affected by eosinophilia. Pathological history: Born and living in Barcelona. Working in food distribution. No travels abroad in the last 5 years. Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps. Bronchial asthma diagnosed more than 10 years ago, treated with corticosteroids and long-agonists Hyperosinophilic Syndrome with clinical expression of chronic eosinophilic pneumonia (NEC) since 1996 (three episodes that required hospital admission and good evolution with long-term corticosteroids). Apparently stable to date. Last two hospitalizations in January and March 2017 due to acute respiratory insufficiency. Reason for consultation: Eosinophilia, in the context of fever of 39°C, chills, asthenia, dyspnea of progressive effort and cough with greenish expectoration, refractory to ambulatory antibiotic treatment with amoxicillin-clavulanic and quinolones. He had been admitted for acute respiratory insufficiency. He had no history of bacterial or fungal colonization. Concomitantly he had presented self-limiting diarrhea and nausea with vomiting. Results: Complementary tests: Chest CT scan: bilateral pulmonary consolidation opacities and a pattern of organized pneumonia. During post-entry control, eosinophil counts were reviewed from 2009, which ranged to maximum levels of 1200 eos/mm3, total IgE 7500 IU/mL. Prick test for inhalant allergens and foods were performed with a negative result. Immunoglobulins A, G, M levels were within the reference range. A conventional study of parasites was negative. IgG antibodies levels to parasites were tested resulting positive for SS. Stool culture and microscopic analysis were performed, confirming the presence of SS. Conclusion: Diagnostic, treatment and evolution: Diagnosed as a Löffler Syndrome (pulmonary infiltrates of eosinophilic origin) secondary to a parasitic hyperinfection of SS. He was treated with iver-mectin. No new events of eosinophilic pneumonia have occurred to the patient after finishing the treatment. Serology, stool cultures have become negative, and peripheral eosinophilic blood counts are within the reference range.
