N2  - Objectives: Sputum collection is recognised as a more sensitive microbiological marker than cough swabs (CS). Young children are unable to expectorate and suction allows a method for sputum sampling. This study reviewed sampling methods and microbiological growths of suction sample (SS) for this population in our CF centre. Parental experience was also evaluated. Methods: This study reviewed sampling methods utilised on children ≤3 yrs attending CF clinic at the Royal London Hospital. Patients who had SS all had a clinical indication of pathogen growth. Patients who had not had a SS were excluded. For patients who had a SS taken between March 2016 and December 2017, microbiological results were reviewed. Parental experience was explored via a closed-question Likert-scale questionnaire. Results: 20 children were identified aged ≤3 yrs. 12 were excluded as they had not had an SS. 8 patients (mean age 13 months) fulfilled the criteria for microbiological analysis. 62.5% of patients had a pathogenic growth from SS. 37.5% were a growth that had not been previously identified on CS; with one patient growing two new pathogens. 25% were a growth that had not been identified in the last 12 months. 6 families were included in the parental experience result, 2 were excluded due to lack of parental recall. 100% of respondents agreed or strongly agreed that they understood why SS was undertaken; that they wouldn't be concerned if another SS was required; and they felt SS was worthwhile. 50% of responders felt their child was severely distressed by SS, however 100% felt their child had recovered after 10 minutes. Conclusion: This data suggests that SS is worthwhile and acceptable to identify pathogens in young children who cannot expectorate. Children do find the procedure distressing, but settle quickly afterwards. (Table Presented).
