N2  - Introduction: Antibiotic allergies are more frequent in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients than in the general population. Hypersensitivity reactions restrict antibiotic selection and result in suboptimal therapy leading to lung function decline. Patients with IgE mediated reactions often require drug desensitization to provide effective management of pulmonary infections. Nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are recognized as important pulmonary pathogens causing significant morbidity in CF patients. Mycobacterium abscessus is the most commonly isolated rapidly growing NTM and is the third most common cause of complex lung disease. Eradication of this bacterium is challenging due to antibiotic resistance. Mycobacterium bolletii is a subspecies of the mycobacterium complex lung disease (MABC). This case reports a patient with hypersensitivity to aminoglycosides who failed an initial desensitization. The patient was successfully desensitized to intravenous amikacin and converted to inhaled amikacin for long term management. To date there are no published reports of desensitization with IV amikacin and subsequent inhaled administration. Case Report: An 11-year-old female with CF and MABC presented with worsening of pulmonary disease including cough, hemoptysis, fever and declining pulmonary functions. She had a history of hypersensitivity reactions to multiple medications, including tobramycin, amikacin, imipenem, esomeprazole, and lumacaftor /ivacaftor. Treatment for mycobacterium bolletii was initiated with azithromycin, linezolid, and tigecycline with the addition of inhaled amikacin post intravenous desensitization. The first intravenous desensitization protocol was a 17-step dose escalating procedure doubling the dose of medication at a fixed time interval of 20 min. Following the second dose, the patient complained of itching and a red non-urticarial rash was noted on her infusion arm. Diphenhydramine was administered, and the infusion interval was increased to 40 min. The desensitization was restarted at 0.004 mg after administering methylprednisolone, famotidine, and montelukast as pretreatment medications. After the fifth dose, the desensitization was stopped due to complaints of throat tightness and tachycardia. Nineteen days later, a second desensitization involving a 12-step doubling dose process with fixed dose advancement intervals of 20 min was successfully completed over 8 hours. Premedication prior to the procedure included montelukast, famotidine, diphenhydramine, and midazolam. Prior to desensitization a flexible fiberoptic laryngoscopy was performed to examine the airway and remained available throughout the process in the event of laryngeal symptoms. Intravenous amikacin was continued for 6 weeks. The transition to inhaled amikacin occurred in the outpatient allergy clinic. She was continued on a combination of inhaled and intravenous therapy for two days and prescribed an epinephrine auto injection kit. The patient and family were provided additional education on the importance of adherence to maintain desensitization and prevent anaphylaxis during treatment. Conclusions: This is the first report of desensitization to intravenous amikacin with transition to inhaled amikacin.
