N2  - Learning Objectives: CA-MRSA is an uncommon cause of community acquired pneumonia, but is associated with poor outcomes. Screening with nasal MRSA polymerase chain reaction(PCR) is a routine practice in ICUs and is highly sensitive in detecting the pathogen. However we report a case of severe necrotizing pneumonia and septic shock with negative MRSA PCR, that highlights the need to continue empiric therapy until definite results of sputum cultures. Methods: A 57-year-old female, smoker(30-pack-year) with moderate COPD presented with a 2-day history of productive cough with green sputum and dyspnea with no prior health care contact. Fever(102.9 F) and Leukocytosis was noted. CXR-RUL consolidation. She was admitted to ICU for septic shock from pneumonia, required vasopressor support after fluids. Vancomycin and Zosyn initiated. Nasal MRSA, viral PCR and blood cultures were negative. Sputum cultures grew MRSA. Vancomycin levels were consistently subtherapeuthic. She required vasopressors for 7 days Linezolid was initiated. CT showed progression with cavitation and pleural effusion that was drained. She was discharged on day 20. Results: CA-MRSA is an emerging pathogen that can cause severe infections with high mortality upto 56 %. Although typically involving the skin, CA-MRSA may cause necrotizing pneumonia in healthy adults. Nasal colonization with MRSA is associated with clinical MRSA infection and can be detected using the nasal PCR which has 88% sensitivity, 90.1% specificity, with a positive predictive value(PPV) of 35.4% and a negative predictive value(NPV) of 99.2%. It has a poor PPV but an excellent NPV in populations with low MRSA pneumonia incidence. Sputum culture is confirmatory, with sensitivity of 92%. Current guidelines recommend the use of vancomycin or linezolid with prolonged duration of therapy. High treatment failures upto 40% are reported if antibiotic therapy is discontinued prior to 21 days. Maintaining serum trough concentrations of vancomycin between 15-20 mg/L can be difficult. Empiric treatment should not be discontinued in high risk patients despite negative nasal PCR.
