N2  - Introduction:Atypical bacteria are agents that cause upper and lower airway infections, most cases are in pediatric and young adult patients. Of the atypical bacteria, the most isolated agent is Mycoplasma pneumoniae, being responsible for 15 to 32% of cases of Pneumonia. Case report: A 17-year-old female, without comorbidities, started with a history of cough, rhinorrhea, nasal obstruction, fever and dyspnea for 12 days, with significant worsening in the last 2 days. The pulmonary auscultation revealed crackling on lung bases and she was in acute hypoxemic respiratory failure, with bilateral pulmonary infiltrate predominant in bases on initial chest radiography, hemogram with discrete leukocytosis, and increase of acute inflammatory phase proteins. Initiated ceftriaxone and clarithromycin but, there was no improvement in the respiratory pattern, requiring invasive mechanical ventilation. Initial tomography revealed diffuse ground glass and areas of consolidation with bilateral air bronchograms. Made the diagnosis of Severe Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome, the patient was submitted to invasive mechanical ventilation for 5 days with PEEP titrated to 20cmH2O and high FiO2, after considerable clinical improvement, she was extubated and remained in a high-flow nasal catheter, alternated with noninvasive ventilation. Etiological investigation with molecular test for virus collected from nasal swab sample at admission was negative for all agents tested. Serological tests revealed IgM positive for Mycoplasma pneumoniae. She was discharged from the hospital with doxycycline and systemic corticosteroid, in addition to physical therapy. She evolved with limiting dyspnea, spirometry showing restrictive ventilatory disorder with CVF of 45% of predicted. After 2 months she had a clinical improvement and normalization of the spirometry. Figure 1: showed the tomographic evolution and the spirometry evolution. Conclusion :The class of antibiotics most frequently used to combat atypical bacteria are macrolides, with good response. Cases with macrolide resistance are infrequent, and in these tetracyclines may be an option. (Figure presented) .
