N2  - Introduction: Lipoid pneumonia is uncommon and presents with various radiographic manifestations that can mimic other diseases such as malignancy. Familiarity with clinical and radiographic features of lipoid pneumonia is therefore important. Characteristic findings can be diagnostic and allow us to avoid unnecessary invasive testing for histopathologic confirmation in appropriate clinical settings. Case Presentation: We present a 56-year-old gentleman with past medical history of diabetes mellitus type II and asthma that was admitted with progressive cough and dyspnea despite outpatient therapy with levofloxacin for suspected pneumonia. Physical examination was notable for tachycardia, hypoxia with SpO2 of 90% on 2 liters per minute via nasal cannula, and diffuse bilateral wheezes. Chest radiograph showed multiple hazy air space opacities. Follow up computed tomography (CT) scan of the chest showed multiple masses in the lower lobes of the lung along with lower lobe bronchiectasis and dilated fluid filled esophagus (Image 1 A). Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsies was performed during the admission due to lack of improvement and concern for malignancy. Bacterial, fungal, and mycobacterial cultures were negative. Pathology showed mild chronic inflammation without evidence of malignancy. However, BAL showed lipoid elements consistent with diagnosis of lipoid pneumonia. Patient was referred to gastroenterology for upper endoscopy and esophageal dilation. Subsequently, reported improvement in symptoms and repeat CT imaging during 3 and 12 month follow up visits showed stability of previously described masses (Images 1 B-C). Discussion: Lipoid pneumonia results from exogenous or endogenous accumulation of lipids in the alveoli. Our patient presented with acute on chronic exogenous lipoid pneumonia secondary to Achalasia and chronic aspiration. Clinical features of acute exogenous pneumonia mimic infectious pneumonia as in our patient while chronic exogenous lipoid pneumonia is typically asymptomatic or identified incidentally on imaging. Radiologic manifestations typically include bilateral consolidation or ground-glass opacities involving the lower and middle lobes. When consolidations are present, CT scans reveal fat attenuation. Chronic exogenous lipoid pneumonia classically presents as an adipose-confining mass with irregular borders as a result of chronic inflammation. However, the fat attenuation can be obscured by superimposed inflammation prompting invasive diagnostic evaluation as in this case (Image 1 B). Follow up CT scans in our patient showed fat attenuation consistent with BAL findings of lipoid elements and lipoid pneumonia. This case demonstrates the importance of recognizing the various radiologic manifestations of lipoid pneumonia.
