N2  - RATIONALE: Bronchiectasis is associated with rare conditions including Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (AATD), Common Variable Immunodeficiency (CVI) and Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD). The purpose of this study is to compare and contrast the clinical characteristics of bronchiectasis associated with these rare conditions. METHODS: The Bronchiectasis Research Registry (BRR) is a centralized database of patients with bronchiectasis identified at 13 clinical sites throughout the United States. At the time of the present analysis, 2170 patients had been enrolled in the BRR. Only patients with AATD, CVI and PCD were included in the study (n=155, 7.1%). Descriptive statistics were computed for main characteristics stratified by group. Values between the groups were compared using Kruskal-Wallis test, and Chi-squared/Fisher's exact test respectively. The significance level was set at 0.05. SAS 9.4 was used to perform the statistical analyses. RESULTS: The study sample (n=155) included patients with AATD (N=58, 37.4%), CVI (N=18, 11.6%) and PCD (N=79, 51.0%). The mean age of the patients in the PCD group (41.9±14.5 years) was significantly less than the mean age of the AATD (66.9±10.7) and CVI (66.7±10.5) (p<.0001). Significantly greater proportion of patients with PCD reported pulmonary exacerbations and hospitalizations in the past 2 years compared to AATD and CVI (p<.0001), although the mean number of exacerbations and hospitalization did not differ between the groups. More patients with PCD had respiratory symptoms such as productive cough, hemoptysis (p<.0001) and wheezing (p=0.003). All three groups showed a reduced mean FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.70. Patients with PCD showed a significantly lower pre-bronchodilator FEV1% and FVC% predicted, and FEV1/FVC than the other groups (p=0.03, <.0001, and 0.04, respectively). Patients with AATD had significantly greater proportion of positive mycobacterial cultures (NTM) compared to the other groups (62.8% vs 42.9% and 25%, respectively; p<0.01). Overall, the most commonly reported bacterial isolate from sputum was Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Patients with PCD had the greatest proportion of cultures positive for this microorganism compared to AATD and CVI patients (63.5% vs 33.3% and 25.0%, respectively; p<.0001). CONCLUSION: This is the first study to compare and contrast the clinical features in patients with AATD, CVI, and PCD associated with bronchiectasis. Our results demonstrate that patients with these rare conditions significantly differ in their clinical characteristics and outcomes, that warrant important management considerations.
