N2  - Introduction: Encephalitis is an inflammation of brain. Many viruses can cause encephalitis by two ways either directly infecting Brain cells as primary encephalitis or can spread to brain from another part of body causing secondary encephalitis called as post infectious encephalitis. Encephalitis is a rare complication of influenza virus infection. Only few cases were reported and published till now. Here we are reporting a case of 50 years Indian female presented with post infectious diffuse encephalitis secondary to influenza viral pneumonia. Case report: 50 years female Diabetic, Hypertensive, Hypothyroid presented with fever, SOB, Cough of 4 days duration. Her complete blood counts, LFT, Serum electrolytes, serum creatinine were within normal range. Chest X-ray showed bilateral patchy pulmonary opacities. 2D ECHO was normal. In view of progressive respiratory failure patient was given Mechanical ventilator (MV) support. Endotracheal secreations were negative for Grams stain, AFB smear, fungal smear and positive for influenza virus RT-PCR.CT chest showed bilateral consolidation with areas of ground- glass opacities. Later she developed renal failure requiring hemodialysis. On 7th day patient developed altered sensorium and quadriplegia. CT scan brain showed gyral hyperdensities with cortical swelling seen in bilateral occipito parietal cortex. MRI Brain showed multifocal areas of restriction of diffusion and increased signal intensity in bilateral frontotemporo parieto occipital regions suggestive of encephalitis. MRI of cervical spine was normal. Her EEG negative for seizure activity. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis showed normal protein and glucose with minimal white blood cells. CSF negative for bacterial infections, TB-PCR, HSV. During prolonged hospital stay patient was treated with Oseltamivir, IV steroids, Insulin, hemodialysis, tracheostomy and other supportive care. Later her renal failure improved and also weaned off from MV support. Spontaneous eye opening with no improvement of power in all four limb, at the time of discharge. Patient advised nursing care and physiotherapy at home. On follow-up visit she was responding to verbal commands with mild improvement of power. Discussion: Encephalitis is a rare complication of influenza virus infection. Diagnosis is often difficult because of absence of particular clinical presentation and also virus is rarely detected in CSF. Pathophysiology is also not well understood but the inflammation and brain damage is due to cytokines released from the virus infection has been proposed till now. (Figure presented) .
