N2  - Introduction: Eosinophilic pneumonia is a rare but treatable condition that should be considered as a differential diagnosis of respiratory failure. We report the following case, with the objective to recognize the importance of timely diagnostic testing and treatment of eosinophilic pneumonia. Case: 55-year-old gentleman presented with shortness of breath, cough and pleuritic chest pain ongoing for several weeks, with four prior hospitalizations for pneumonia over the past 6 months. Notable history includes ischemic cardiomyopathy and heart failure. On presentation, his oxygen saturation was 95% on room air and his lung exam revealed bibasilar crackles. Chest X-Ray showed diffuse bilateral perihilar patchy opacities. Troponins, beta-natriuretic peptide and D-dimer were negative. Labs revealed a leukocytosis of 17.4 k/uL. He was empirically treated for community acquired pneumonia, however his symptoms and leukocytosis remained unchanged after 2 days. Differential cell count showed prominent eosinophilia of 32% and a urine toxicology notable for opiates. Further investigation revealed peripheral eosinophilia on previous blood samples ranging from 12-40% along with a drug screen positive for cocaine 1 month prior to his presentation. A bronchoalveolar-lavage (BAL) revealed 40% eosinophilia, and was unremarkable for infection or malignancy. ANA, ANCA serologies and HIV testing were negative. A probable cause of his eosinophilic pneumonia was cocaine and heroin inhalation. He was started on prednisone and his eosinophilia significantly decreased within 48 hours. Discussion: Eosinophilic pneumonia is a rare disorder and presents with progressive, infectious-like respiratory symptoms over several weeks due to a pronounced expansion of eosinophils in the lung parenchyma and plasma. Radiographic findings are consistent with bilateral consolidative opacities with areas of ground-glass attenuation. The diagnosis of eosinophilic pneumonia was confirmed by a BAL that showed eosinophilia >25%, diffuse pulmonary opacities and symptoms ongoing for a month, which likely correlated to toxic inhalation. Both heroin and cocaine inhalation are known causes of eosinophilic pneumonia, however the prevalence is unknown given its rarity. Drug induced eosinophilic pneumonia have similar clinical, radiographic and histopathological criteria to idiopathic eosinophilic pneumonia and are often indistinguishable by these measures. The diagnosis is supported by a temporal correlation to toxin use. Treatment involves discontinuation of the offending agent and corticosteroids. This case underscores the importance of considering alternative diagnoses when initial treatment does not result in improvement. Given its indolent course, rarity and similarities to bacterial pneumonia, eosinophilic pneumonia can be overlooked and can pose a challenge to timely diagnosis and treatment.
