N2  - Rationale: Non cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (NCFB) is a chronic lung disease characterized by permanent dilation of the airways, recurrent infection, persistent cough, and sputum production. Patients with chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) lung infections have more frequent pulmonary exacerbations (PEs), hospitalizations, worsening quality of life, and higher mortality. The efficacy of the once-daily inhaled antibiotic, ARD-3150 (liposome encapsulated ciprofloxacin 135 mg/3 mL plus free ciprofloxacin 54 mg/3 mL given in 28-day on/off cycles) to reduce and prevent PEs was investigated in patients with NCFB and chronic PA lung infection in two double blind 48-week phase 3 trials: ORBIT-3 (NCT01515007) and ORBIT-4 (NCT02104245). Using survival analysis techniques (Kaplan-Meier (KM) plots and log-rank statistical analyses) for the primary endpoint of time to first PE, the KM plots suggested improved event-free periods associated with ARD-3150-treated patients in ORBIT-3 and ORBIT-4, similar to the Phase 2 study ORBIT-2. The hazard ratio was statistically significant in ORBIT-4. For chronic treatment, we wished to investigate the impact of ARD-3150 on subsequent occurrences of PE. Methods: ORBIT-3 (n=278) and ORBIT-4 (n=304) enrolled patients with NCFB, chronic lung infection with PA, and ≥2 PEs requiring treatment with antibiotics in the preceding year. PEs were identified by strict protocol-defined criteria. In the post hoc analysis, a serial KM and log-rank testing methodology was employed to investigate the impact of ARD-3150 vs placebo on each serial occurrence of PE (second, third, fourth, etc.). Results: In ORBIT-3, the serial KM analysis showed differences in favor of ARD-3150 compared to placebo, especially from the third serial PE onwards. In ORBIT-4, a difference in favor of ARD-3150 vs placebo was observed for most serial PEs throughout the study, starting with the first PE. In the pooled analysis, a difference in favor of ARD-3150 was seen starting with the first PE. Conclusions: Serial KM analyses of all PEs indicate that ARD-3150 exerts beneficial effects by extending the time to the next PE beyond the first event, throughout the duration of the treatment period.
