N2  - Introduction: Dapsone, also known as diamino-diphenyl sulfone, is used for various infectious diseases, dermatologic and autoimmune diseases. Its side effects include hemolytic anemia, skin rash, peripheral neuropathy and dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome. Rarely, dapsone can lead to eosinophilic pneumonia. Case: A 43-year-old female was admitted to our hospital due to cough and dyspnea on exertion for 3 weeks. She has been having behcet's disease for more than 2 years, with recurrent oral and perianal ulcer. Despite taking the colchicine and prednisolone, she had recurrent oral ulcer and dyspepsia as a side effect of medications. Rheumatology specialist changed the medications to dapsone 50mg per day. After taking dapsone for 3 weeks, she progressively felt cough and dyspnea. Chest radiograph revealed patch infiltrations in both upper lung fields. Chest CT showed consolidation and ground glass opacities in both upper lobes. Blood tests showed leukocytosis with marked eosinophilia (22,510/mm3, neutrophil 47%, lymphocyte 6%, monocyte 1%, eosinophil 45%). We conducted bronchoalveolar lavage for evaluation of pulmonary infiltrations. The lavage fluid analysis revealed elevated eosinophil counts (monocyte 12%, neutrophil 1%, lymphocyte 30%, eosinophil 56%), and there were no visible bacteria, acid fast bacilli or malignant cells. Based on her clinical symptoms, medication history, peripheral blood tests, imaging studies and bronchoalveolar lavage fluid analysis, we presumed eosinophilic pneumonia induced by dapsone as a diagnosis. Because she had severe cough and dyspnea with hypoxemia over the admission period, we administered methylprednisolone 30 mg per day. Four days later, cough and dyspnea with hypoxemia were improved gradually with marked reduction in the blood leukocytes count (total 4,300/mm3, eosinophil 7.2%). Chest radiograph also showed an improvement of infiltrations. After total six weeks steroid treatment including tapering process, she was fully recoverd and there was no recurrence. Discussion: The frequent side effects of dapsone include hemolytic anemia, methemoglobinemia, skin rash, peripheral neuropathy and dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome. Rarely, dapsone can cause eosinophilic pneumonia. Although eosinophilic pneumonia is a rare side effect of dapsone, when we encounter patients taking dapsone with pulmonary infiltration and peripheral eosinophilia we need to consider eosinophilic pneumonia as a diagnosis.
