N2  - Introduction: We present a patient who survived life-threatening community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) from legionella in the setting of MRSA colonization. Case Description: A 52-year-old male with rheumatoid arthritis on adalimumab presented to an ED with several days of malaise, fever, dyspnea and cough. Chest imaging showed dense consolidation of right middle lobe (RML) compatible with CAP. Labs were notable for leukopenia, hyponatremia, and elevation of transaminases. He was intubated for impending respiratory failure from increased work of breathing, and was admitted to the ICU. Given his immunocompromised status, broad spectrum antibiotics were started. A bronchoscopy was performed and bronchoalveolar (BAL) fluid from RML was sent for microbiology. Tracheal aspirate obtained earlier grew 3+ MRSA on semiquantitative culture. Antibiotics were narrowed to MRSA coverage. BAL was only notable for 100 CFU/ML of MRSA. He was extubated on day 2 of ICU admission, but deteriorated in respiratory status days after antibiotics were narrowed and was eventually reintubated. Two days later, urinary legionella antigen returned positive and he was started on moxifloxacin. Although his clinical course was complicated by septic shock and alcohol withdrawal, his respiratory status continued to improve and he was successfully extubated on day 11 of his ICU stay. A BAL culture on a selective medium returned positive for Legionella pneumophila 11 days after it was sent. After 2 weeks more, his hospital stay was complicated by cecal volvulus yet he was eventually discharged home. Discussion: Legionella is an under-recognized but relatively common cause of both community-acquired and hospital-acquired pneumonia. Recipients of biologic therapies, like our patient on adalimumab, have a higher risk of Legionella infection. Microbiologic diagnosis is challenging since culture requires a selective media and often takes many days to result. Urinary antigen testing, although not highly sensitive, aids in rapid diagnosis with excellent specificity. Antibiotic of choice is a respiratory fluoroquinolone or azithromycin. In our case, the clinical picture was confounded by positive MRSA culture from initial respiratory specimen. Given the fact that the BAL grew only 100 CFU/ML, he deteriorated even with coverage for MRSA, and his upper respiratory tract cultures grew MRSA on outpatient follow-up after resolution of pneumonia, it was thought this only represented colonization. Overall, our case demonstrates that legionella is an important pathogen of pneumonia and high clinical suspicion accompanied by prompt microbiologic testing, including urinary antigen and specialized culture, can clinch a diagnosis. (Figure presented) .
