N2  - Introduction Eosinophilia can occur in several different conditions. There have been recent reports of patients being started on immunosuppressive medications for presumed eosinophilic pneumonia that actually ended being secondary to coccidioidomycosis, often with a fatal outcome. Case report A 45 year-old previously healthy man from Los Angeles presented with 15 days of productive cough and fever that was unresponsive to antibiotics. Chest x-ray showed opacities in the right lung base. Chest computed tomography showed diffuse bilateral ground glass nodules and a cavitating consolidative opacity in the right lower lobe (Image). He was noted to have a peripheral absolute eosinophil count of 2000/mm3. Tuberculosis was ruled out and cultures and autoimmune work-up were unrevealing. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsies of the right lower lobe was performed. While BAL revealed eosinophilia (34%), an organizing pneumonia pattern with fibrinous exudates was seen on pathology; silver and acid-fast bacilli stains were negative. Serum beta-D glucan and urine histoplasma antigen testing were negative. However, serum coccidioidomycosis antibody titer returned positive at 1:32. A few days later, BAL grew Coccidioides immitis on culture. Fluconazole was started, resulting in resolution of symptoms within 2 weeks. Repeat coccidioidomycosis titer 2 months later was 1:2. Discussion Coccidioidomycosis, a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia in endemic areas of the southwestern United States, Mexico and parts of South America, has an increasing incidence. Taking a thorough travel history is very important given the unrestrained and frequent travel across the United States. Patients often present to clinical attention when they return to their non-endemic home regions, which provides diagnostic challenges. Radiographic features are non-specific. Pulmonary nodules and cavities are seen in <5% of the cases. Serology is the most commonly used tool for diagnosis and can be negative early in the course of the disease. Bronchoscopy with BAL and transbronchial biopsy increases diagnostic yield. Eosinophilia has been seen in up to 25-30% of cases. If treated as an eosinophilic pneumonia with steroids, patients could rapidly deteriorate. The decision to treat with anti-fungal therapy is usually based on severity of illness and risk factors for severe disease. Our case highlights the importance of having a high index of suspicion for this pathogen in endemic areas to achieve timely diagnosis and avoid the wrong treatment. (Figure Presented) .
