N2  - Histoplasmosis is the most common endemic mycosis in the United States with an estimated incidence of 3 million per year and the highest rate of hospitalization and death. Its clinical impact is most prominent in the immunocompromised population. Though up to 90% of people that acquire the infection through inhalation of mycelia spores are asymptomatic, histoplasmosis can present as acute, subacute, or chronic pulmonary disease, traditionally with bilateral patchy infiltrates and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. It can also manifest as progressive disseminated disease, mediastinal granulomas, mediastinal adenitis, and the devastating fibrosing mediastinitis. Here is a case that breaks all the typical rules of histoplasmosis with a unique presentation in an immunocompetent individual. A 23 year old man with no significant past medical history initially presented with left sided chest and back pain, and was found to have a left lower lobe consolidation and pleural effusion on CT Chest. He was treated with doxycycline with no relief in symptoms. He was referred to pulmonology a month later with dry cough, persistent left sided consolidation and increasing pleural effusion. He underwent thoracentesis and bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). Pleural fluid analysis yielded an exudative pleural effusion with negative bacterial, fungal, and AFB cultures, negative fungal antigens, as well as negative BAL fungal antigens and cultures. Serum and urine studies were negative except for positive histoplasma complement fixation titers (Y titer 1:16) and immunodiffusion assay with positive M band. Patient was started on itraconazole for total of 6 months with complete resolution of the left pleural effusion. The LLL consolidation mostly resolved with the exception of residual pleural-based nodular density showing calcifications. Acute or subacute histoplasmosis typically presents as diffuse or focal pulmonary infiltrates, often with mediastinal lymphadenopathy, but rarely serosal involvement unlike tuberculosis. Pleural effusion is an unusual manifestation of this disease, only described in a handful of case reports in which many of the patients were immunocompromised. There are cases of pleural effusions as complications of fibrosing disease in which case antifungal treatment alone would not resolve and surgical management was needed. Besides our patient's immunocompetent state, this case is unique in that the pleural effusion and pleural mass-like consolidation were the isolated presenting findings of histoplasmosis without signs of fibrosis and resolved with itraconazole. Histoplasmosis pleural involvement may not be as rare as once thought and should be considered in parapneumonic effusions not responsive to antibiotics. (Figure Presented) .
