N2  - INTRODUCTION: Idiopathic acute eosinophilic pneumonia (IAEP) is rare in children. Diagnosis is based on acute onset of respiratory symptoms and fever, rapid progression to hypoxemic respiratory failure, diffuse infiltrates on imaging, and >25% eosinophils on BAL without other etiology. We report a unique case of IAEP in a toddler with focal CXR findings and diffuse mucus plugging. CASE: A healthy, fully-immunized 17 month-old male was transferred from his pediatrician's office to the ED of our tertiary care pediatric hospital for 2 days of cough and respiratory distress. His older sibling had recently completed treatment for community acquired pneumonia. Medical history included one episode of bronchiolitis and mild infantile eczema. No medications. No travel or exposure history. Family history notable for father with childhood asthma and allergies. On arrival, he was in significant respiratory distress, febrile to 38.5C, tachypneic, with oxygen saturations in the 70s. Exam revealed increased work of breathing, diffusely coarse breath sounds, and markedly decreased aeration on the right. Venous blood gas showed pH of 7.261, pCO2 of 40.4 mmHg. Bicarbonate was 19 mmol/L. CXR showed right upper lobe pneumonia (Figure A). Echocardiogram was normal. IV fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics were started and he was admitted to the PICU on high flow nasal cannula. He deteriorated rapidly over the initial 4 hours requiring intubation and high-frequency oscillatory ventilation for hypoxic respiratory failure. Bedside flexible bronchoscopy revealed diffuse bronchitis with firm mucous plugs and casts bilaterally, most pronounced on the right (Figure B). Bronchoalveolar lavage showed abundant mucin with poorly formed Curschmann spirals, 35% eosinophils, 30% neutrophils, 30% macrophages, 5% lymphocytes. Peak absolute peripheral eosinophil count was 480 cells/mcL. IgE was 82 IU/mL. All infectious testing for viral, bacterial, fungal and parasitic pathogens was negative. Methylprednisolone 1mg/kg every 6 hours was initiated with rapid improvement in hypoxia. Serial bronchoscopies over the subsequent 3 days assisted with clearance of more mucous plugs. Support was steadily weaned, he was extubated on day 11 of admission and discharged home on day 21. He has been asymptomatic at outpatient follow-up visits. DISCUSSION: While rare in children, IAEP should be suspected in previously well patients with sudden respiratory failure. Prognosis is excellent with prompt corticosteroid therapy. Our case demonstrates a unique presentation of localized consolidation secondary to mucus plugging instead of typical diffuse opacities. Similar plugging occurs in patients with chronic eosinophilic pneumonia but has not been reported with IAEP.
