N2  - BackgroundCulturing M. tuberculosis from cough-generated aerosols is a quantitative method to measure infectiousness in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) -that is superior to sputum acid-fast bacilli (AFB). In previous studies, only a minority of AFB+ and culture+ individuals generated high (≥10) colony forming units (CFU) of M. tuberculosis in aerosols, a validated predictor of both qualitative and quantitative tuberculin skin test (TST) and interferon gamma release assay (IGRA) responses, and risk of progression to disease in exposed household contacts. In this study, we evaluate factors associated with aerosol production among pulmonary TB cases from three cohorts in two different settings. Methods We analyzed aggregated data from three studies with near identical inclusion criteria that were conducted in Uganda (2002- 2004 and 2009-2011) and Brazil (2013-2015) that included adult patients with sputum AFB+/ culture+ pulmonary TB. We recorded clinical and radiographic data, obtained three sputum samples for AFB smear microscopy and cultures, and used the validated cough aerosol sampling system (CASS) to culture cough-generated aerosols. Aerosol data was used to categorize patients as: aerosol negative (CFU=0), low aerosol (CFU=1-9) and high aerosol (≥10 CFU) producers. We performed logistic regression analysis to identify factors associated with aerosol production. Results236 TB patients were included, 152 (60%) were sputum AFB 3+ and 132/210 (63%) had advanced radiographic disease; 148/214 (69%) had received at least 1 day of TB therapy before CASS collection. 135 (57%) were aerosol negative, 43 (18%) were lowaerosol and 58 (25%) were high-aerosol producers. Aerosol production was unrelated to symptom duration (median 12 weeks, p=0.37) or advanced radiographic disease (55% vs 56%, p=0.55). In an unadjusted analysis, aerosol production was associated with less days of antituberculous therapy before aerosol sample collection (p< 0.001), sputum AFB smear grade (p=0.01), shorter days to positivity (DTP) in liquid culture media (p=0.02), larger sputum volume (p=0.03) and subjectively strong cough during CASS (p=0.04) . In an adjusted analysis, no TB treatment (OR 2.86, 95% CI 1.43-5.70; p=0.003) and DTP in liquid media (per 1-day DTP OR 0.94, 95% CI 0.88-0.99; p=0.04) were associated with aerosol production.Conclusion Only a minority of culture-confirmed pulmonary TB cases generate culturable M. tuberculosis in cough generated aerosols, the infectious moiety required for transmission. Our data suggest aerosolized M. tuberculosis rapidly becomes non-culturable after initiation of antituberculous treatment, and that aerosol production is only partly related to sputum bacterial load. Mechanistic studies will be needed to further elucidate these findings.
