N2  - Case Presentation: A 33-year-old woman with a history of sickle cell trait presented for progressively worsening shortness of breath, persistent dry cough and bilateral pleuritic chest pain. She had been hospitalized 4 months prior for MSSA bacteremia with cavitary pneumonia and had completed 6 weeks of antibiotic treatment. Transesophageal echocardiogram then was negative for endocarditis. The upper-lobe predominant bilateral cavitary lesions and consolidation seen on chest computed tomography (CT) were thought to be secondary to bacterial pneumonia. Clinical Course: On current presentation, the patient was afebrile with normal vital signs. Basic admission laboratory studies were also normal. Chest exam revealed diffuse wheezing. A repeat chest CT showed interval improvement in nodular consolidation, but an increase in number and size of lower lobe cavities. Immunoglobulin levels and vasculitic work-up were unremarkable. One out of three expectorated sputum samples from her previous hospitalization was found to be positive for Mycobacterium avium complex as well. She ultimately underwent bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial and endobronchial biopsies. Bronchoscopy findings were significant for non-caseating granulomas on biopsies, CD4:CD8 of 17:1, and a focal granuloma on BAL (Figure 1). Acid fast stains and Mycobacterium Tuberculousis PCR were both negative. Figure 1 Discussion: Sarcoidosis is a systemic granulomatous disease that has pulmonary involvement in greater than 90% of cases [1]. Pulmonary sarcoidosis typically presents with bilateral hilar adenopathy with or without pulmonary symptoms. Presenting with cavitary sarcoid, however, is atypical and less described in the literature. Incidence has been quoted from less than 1% of cases [2] to 2.2% of cases as shown in a recent retrospective study [3]. The pathophysiology of cavitation is not well understood. Proposed mechanisms include cavitation from coalescence of granulomas, while others suggest that granulomatous angiitis causes necrosis [3]. Another rare finding in our case is a focal granuloma on BAL, which, to our knowledge, has only been described one other time in the literature [4]. This patient's atypical presentation of cavitary sarcoidosis was further confounded by the fact that she earlier presented with a MSSA bacteremia and presumed pneumonia. It is possible that some of the consolidations were alveolar sarcoid that cavitated, which accounts for their failure to resolve or improve after antimicrobial treatment. In atypical presentations of sarcoid such as this, tissue diagnosis is paramount, as it is important not only to rule out other causes of granulomatous disease, but also to evaluate other causes of cavitary disease [5]. (Figure Presented).
