N2  - Introduction: chronic bronchial infection is a very prevalent illness with a great morbimortality. Its treatment is based on long-term antibiotics, being the inhaled ones the gold standard for patients with bronchiectasis. The lack of studies in this area does not allow to predict which patients will better tolerate inhaled antibiotics and to tell if there is any tolerance difference between drugs. Objective: to asses the results of the tolerance test to inhaled antibiotics in adults with chronic bronchial infection. Material and methods: this is an observational, transversal, unicentre study of patients under 18 with chronic bronchial infection that have been prescribed an inhaled antibiotic. Patients unable to understand the test protocol, with an exacerbation treated with antibiotics within 2 weeks before the inclusion on the study, pregnant, with allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis or allergic to any of the antibiotics tested, were excluded. Data about the tolerability of the antibiotics were collected in the first hour before doing the tolerability test by pulmonary function tests and a Likert questionnaire. The sample was stratified into three groups according to the antibiotic tested: colistin, gentamicin or ampicillin. It was considered poor tolerance if the questions referring to feeling chest pain, dyspnoea and wheezing had a value of 2 or 3 (quite or a lot). Regarding the questions referring to cough, pharyngeal irritation and nauseas or poor taste, patients marking 3 (a lot) were considered to have poor tolerance. Poor tolerance was also considered if the forced forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) after bronchodilation decreased ≥ 15% following administration of the antibiotic. Results: 28 patients were included. The mean age was 65.43±14.36 and half of them were males. 13 patients were or had been smokers and the pack-year index was 60.5±45.01. 67% had bronchiectasis being the FACED score 2.86±2.35. 25% had chronic infection by P. aeruginosa and the FEV1 was 74.15±27.3. The rate of patients who tolerated colistin, gentamicin and ampicillin were 100%, 80% and 90.5% respectively. There were no significant differences between groups (figure 1). Regarding the adverse events, it seemed that the colistin was the best tolerated, although no statistically significant differences were found in any of the items of the Likert questionnaire (table 1). Conclusions: • The three antibiotics tested were well tolerated with no statistically significant differences. • Although the rate of adverse events observed in all the groups was very low, colistin seemed to be the best tolerated.
