N2  - IINTRODUCTION: Bronchiectasis is a chronic respiratory disorder characterized by recurrent cough, sputum production and respiratory infections considered as exacerbations.A chest computed tomography (CT) is the method of choice for a definitive diagnosis of bronchiectasis.The FACED score allows predicting mortality, and it was recently validated to predict exacerbations. AIMS: To evaluate the diagnostic value of the FACED score to predict exacerbations in a multicenter cohort. METHODS: A retrospective study was conducted to analyze a cohort of patients with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis. Clinical, functional, imaging and bacteriological variables were reviewed. In addition to the FACED score, it was analyzed a composite variable of exacerbations (CVE), defined as two or more ambulatory exacerbations or at least one exacerbation that required hospital admission. RESULTS: 279 patients were included. The mean age was 53.9 years (SD ± 18.6) and 176 patients were women (63%). The most common etiology was the post-infectious cause in 49%, COPD in 8%, rheumatoid arthritis in 3.2%, hypogammaglobulinemia in 1.8% and unknown in 38%. Pathogens isolated were P.Aeruginosa 14%, H.Influenzae 11%, and Pneumococcus 5%, in order of frequency. In relation to the FACED variables, it was observed: CT compromise of >2 lobes in 54.5%, dyspnea mMRC IIIIV in 13%, age> 70 years in 20.8%, value of FEV1 <50% in 24%, colonization by P.Aeruginosa 14%. The mean number of exacerbations in the previous year was 1.5 (SD ± 1.2). The number of patients with CVE was 127 (47%). The FACED score was calculated with a mean of 1.84 points (SD± 1.7) A receiver operator characteristic (ROC) curve analysis for CVE was performed and revealed a correlation between the FACED score and the CVE , indicated the area under the curve (AUC) value of 0.61 (95% CI 0,544 - 0,678) (Fig 1) CONCLUSION: These are the first results of a multicentric national database of this pathology. We have found a greater number of bronchiectasis due to postinfectious causes than reported previously. This study shows the utility of the FACED score in the prediction of exacerbations.
