N2  - INTRODUCTION: Q fever is a zoonosis caused by the gram-negative coccobacillus Coxiella burnetii. Though rare, its occurrence is a worldwide consideration when in presence of classic clinical clues, such as exposure to potentially infected livestock. Nevertheless, diagnosis of Q fever pneumonia remains challenging, not least because its respiratory manifestations are often nonspecific and variable. To our knowledge, miliary pneumonia has not yet been reported as a radiographic presentation of Q Fever. CASE DESCRIPTION: A 56-year-old male presented to an emergency room for nonresolving respiratory symptoms and fever. Previous history was unremarkable, except for two antibiotic regimens in the last month for similar symptoms without concurrent pneumonic infiltrates on his chest X- Ray (CXR). Interestingly, this gentleman's occupation as a renderer involved manipulation of a variety of livestock carcasses. He had no risk factors for immunosuppression. Upon admission, patient had fever, non-productive cough, and dyspnea. He was non-hypoxemic, and reported no headache, myalgia, or arthralgia. The CXR showed subtle apical micronodular opacities. A CT scan ordered the same day revealed innumerable randomly distributed micronodules with apical predominance compatible with miliary pneumonia (Figure 1). Initial working differential diagnosis favored miliary tuberculosis, tularemia, or atypical pneumonia. Diagnostic workup for nonresolving and miliary pneumonias, including a bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage, was inconclusive. In particular, liver enzyme levels were normal and blood cultures were negative. Successive antibiotic regimens were piperacillintazobactam- ciprofloxacin for 5 days followed by amoxicillin-clavulanic acid for 5 days. Evolution was favorable, and patient was discharged on the sixth day of therapy. Serologic two-phase testing for Q fever had been ordered, which revealed a typical biphasic immune response to Coxiella burnetii. Decision was to administer a two-week regimen of doxycycline regimen to avoid complications. Echocardiography for endocarditis/myocarditis was negative. Follow-up chest CT scan at 6 weeks showed an almost complete resolution of opacities. DISCUSSION: Our report describes a unique case of Q fever presenting as miliary pneumonia. To our knowledge, this case report is the first to prompt consideration of Coxiella burnetii as the causative organism of miliary pneumonia. Suspicion and identification of this pathogen can impact management, such as in our case. Figure 1 - Initial Chest Computed Tomography (Figure Presented).
