N2  - BACKGROUND: Hyperviscosity syndrome (HVS) as a critical care emergency is a well defined topic, and its prompt recognition allows for appropriate treatments such as plasmapheresis. Plasma cell dyscrasias, leukemias, and polycythemias represent some of its predisposing etiologies. Typically symptoms include mucosal bleeding, retinopathy, and neurological changes but heart failure is rarely described as a primary manifestation. Here, we describe a case of a 56 year old male with multiple myeloma refractory to numerous treatments whose cardiac decompensation and subsequent death is believed to have been precipitated by a hyperviscous state. CASE: A 56 year old man with multiple myeloma was admitted for fevers, cough, shortness of breath and sub-massive hemoptysis. He recently started daratumumab for salvage therapy. His room air SaO2 was 86%, and CXR revealed bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Treatment began for bacterial pneumonia with hydration and IV antibiotics. Admission bloodwork was notable for severe proteinemia (total serum albumin 2.5 g/dL, serum protein 14.8 g/dL with IgG 8.48 g/dL on electrophoresis). Echocardiogram demonstrated normal LV size and thickness, mildly reduced function with an ejection fraction of 50%, with no impaired relaxation. Days later, the patient had precipitous respiratory decompensation and required emergent intubation for impending respiratory failure. Repeat imaging demonstrated severe bilateral pulmonary edema. Emergent bronchoscopy noted only frothy red sputum, consistent with pulmonary edema. Repeat echocardiogram six days after the initial study demonstrated severely decreased left ventricular systolic function with EF<10%, and moderately decreased right ventricular systolic function. Seizure like activity was also witnessed during this time, which was confirmed with EEG. The patient was believed to have viral myocarditis, and was treated with inotropes and aggressive diuresis; he did not receive plasmapheresis. During this time the patient also developed ischemic hepatitis and acute renal failure possibly engendered from his acute cardiogenic shock. He died three weeks after his admission. Only later in his course did a discussion of serum viscosity arise, which was checked and was elevated to 2.6 cpoise. DISCUSSION: This case underscores the important consideration of hyperviscosity syndrome in the critically ill myeloma patient. Prompt recognition of acute heart failure due to hyperviscosity syndrome could have been a critical determination to change management. While cardiopulmonary manifestations are recognized as a complication of HVS, it is not well described in the literature. This association represents a diagnostic challenge as no strict parameters are recognized and its incidence is potentially rare and under appreciated.
