N2  - RATIONALE: Aspergillus fumigatus is the most common fungal pathogen causing Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis(ABPA) in patients with CF. It affects 1-15% of CF population and is associated with high morbidity and mortality. Most often ABPA remains underdiagnosed or diagnosed at very late age since the symptoms of ABPA overlap with that of CF. Therefore, it becomes necessary to timely diagnose ABPA in CF to prevent further deterioration of health in CF patients. The aim of the present study was to determine the prevalence of ABPA in Indian CF pediatric population. METHODS: The study included pediatric patients with CF attending the pediatric pulmonolgy unit of PGIMER, Chandigarh. A total of 117 patients with CF were screened and routine testing of the patients was done for suspected ABPA cases during treatment of CF. BMI was calculated at the time of enrollment of the study subjects. The parameters studied were; laboratory: total IgE in serum, Af specific IgE in serum, skin prick testing or sputum/BAL culture for Aspergillus, radiological: Chest X-ray or HRCT). A panel of 72 common mutations was also analyzed alongwith clinical parameters. The diagnosis of ABPA in CF patients was confirmed by the attending physician based upon the tests conducted. RESULTS: Sixteen patients were identified with ABPA in CF. The patients presented with cough, wheeze, malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency and low BMI. Out of 16 ABPA with CF patients, 7 had Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection , 6 had Staphylococcus aureus infection and 3 were infected with both the organisms. Chest radiographs of most of the patients showed bronchiectasis. Some of the patients were analysed for most common mutations and it was found that 3 patients were deltaF508 heterozygotes, 1 was having S549N mutation, two were compound heterozygotes for deltaF508 and 3849+10kbC->T and deltaF508 and N1303K respectively while another one was homozygous for c.595C>T and one patient did not have any of the common mutation. The average sweat chloride values were more than 60 mEq/L. The average total IgE and Af specific IgE values were 4832.05± 4490.91 and 33.81±30.68 respectively. 10 patients had skin prick test positive for A. fumigatus. One patient was found to have CFRD and two patients were positive for pulmonary tuberculosis. CONCLUSION: The prevalence of ABPA in Indian CF children was found to be 13.7% and the most common mutation detected was deltaF508.
