N2  - Rationale: Pneumonia is a leading cause of death in the United States, and delays in timely and appropriate antibiotic selection increase infection-related mortality and impede antimicrobial stewardship. Recently, using a novel hand-held DNA sequencing platform (MinION, Oxford Nanopore), we demonstrated our ability to identify bacterial pathogens via real-time metagenomics in hours, far faster than via conventional culture-based protocols. We here present an optimized protocol that further accelerates time to bacterial identification using real-time metagenomics. Methods: We collected bronchoalveolar lavage fluid from patients undergoing clinical bronchoscopy. We aliquoted lavage fluid to permit direct comparisons of DNA isolation and enrichment strategies. Our primary comparisons were 1) DNA isolation techniques, 2) DNA incubation time, and 3) techniques for rapid enrichment of microbial DNA relative to host DNA. We determined yield of bacterial DNA by quantifying both total DNA concentration and bacterial DNA concentration using complementary assays (Qubit, Thermo-Fisher; droplet digital PCR, Bio-Rad). Results: We first compared DNA isolation strategies by testing a rapid, five-minute DNA isolation device (PureLyse, ClaremontBio) against a conventional DNA extraction protocol (DNeasy, QIAGEN). Though faster, the rapid PureLyse strategy eliminated significant amounts of bacterial DNA, resulting in insufficient concentrations for real-time metagenomics. We then tested decreasing incubation times to accelerate our existing DNA isolation protocol; we determined that 20 minutes of incubation was sufficient for maximal DNA yield with minimal loss of signal for metagenomic sequencing. Finally, we attempted to enrich the bacterial DNA signal by decreasing the relative concentration of host human DNA. Using a saponin treatment step, we successfully decreased background human DNA by 94%, increasing the relative proportion of bacterial DNA in each respiratory sample. Conclusions: We here present an optimized protocol for rapid identification (<8 hours) of bacterial pathogens in respiratory specimens using real-time metagenomics. We successfully decreased time to real-time metagenomic identification without reducing bacterial DNA concentration. The competing PureLyse protocol, although faster, did not yield a sufficient bacterial concentration to proceed with real-time metagenomic identification. Saponin is an effective lysis agent for decreasing background human DNA and enriching for microbial DNA. Further studies will be needed to 1) validate the modified protocol and 2) ensure successful bacterial identification and genome sequencing with the above modifications.
