N2  - RATIONALE: To report this novel finding (a special pattern of reversed halo sign) on HRCT indicating pulmonary involvement of Klebsiella pneumonia blood stream infection. METHODS: Retrospectively analysis the clinical data of 3 patients admitted in our hospital with multifocal reversed halo sign on HRCT. RESULTS: Case 1, A 66 years old male with a chief complaint of cough and fever for 10 days, with small amount of blood in sputum and dyspnea. History of type 2 diabetes mellitus for 15 years and poorly controlled. Body temperature of 39°, moist rales on bilateral lung fields, and mild tenderness on the right upper abdomen. His HRCT showed infiltration and multifocal reversed halo sign, distributed bilaterally and peripherally, the abdomen ultrasound showed heterogenous hypoechoes which indicated liver abscess. Tazobactam and Piperacillin was applied 4.5 q8h empirically at the first 3 days after admission, then blood culture and the sputum culture all showed Klebsiella pneumonia, ESBL(-). He developed into septic shock 2 days later despite of aggressive fluid resuscitation, intubation and mechanical ventilation, and died 5 days later. Case 2, A 50 years old male with a chief complaint of cough, expectoration and fever for 4 days. History of ankylosing spondylitis for 30 years. Body temperature 39.6°, with systemic mucocutaneous and scleral jaundice. Moist rales on bilateral lung fields, and the liver could be palpated in the subxiphoid region with dull tenderness. AST 82 U/L, ALT 39 U/L, TBIL 182.4 μmol/L, DBIL 167.1μmol/L, creatinine 120.5μmol/L. Chest HRCT showed infiltration and multifocal reversed halo sign with bilateral and peripheral distribution, and effusions in the abdominal cavity and pelvic cavity was found by abdominal CT. Imipenem was administered 0.5 q 6h empirically and his body temperature became normal then recovered. Klebsiella pneumoniae was isolated from the blood culture. Case 3, 54 years old male with a chief complaint of fever and short of breath for 1 week, with some bloody mucous in the sputum when coughing, body temperature was 39°, moist rales can be heard on bilateral lung fields. HRTCT showed multifocal, bilateral and peripheral pattern of reversed halo sign. Imipenem was applied 0.5 q 6h empirically and his body temperature became normal 2 days later. Klebsiella pneumoniae bacilli was isolated from blood culture.CONCLUSION: The multifocal, bilateral and peripheral pattern of reversed halo sign might suggest pulmonary involvement of blood stream infection of Klebsiella pneumoniae and an empirical application of carbapenem is necessary. (Figure presented) .
