N2  - Introduction: Organizing Pneumonia (OP) is a distinct pathological entity and infectious cause is one among the various etiologies. Systematic review of literature indicates that Nocardia causing OP is mostly reported in the immunocompromised population. We report a case of Pulmonary Nocardiosis causing OP in an immunocompetent individual Case Report: 56 year-old Caucasian male with past medical history of hypertension, smokeless tobacco and sulfa allergy presented with intermittent fevers, non-productive cough and 17 lbs. weight loss over 8 weeks. He had completed two courses of antibiotics as outpatient. CT chest (Figure-1 A) revealed large indeterminate left upper lobe & mediastinal mass causing vascular encasement with left hilar lymphadenopathy. Anti-microbiological work up for bacterial and fungal infections were negative. CT guided biopsy of the mass was negative for malignancy and infection. Due to intermittent lowgrade fevers, he had a broncho-alveolar lavage with endobronchial ultrasound guided aspiration (EBUS) of the mass and paratracheal lymph nodes. Intra-operative cryopathology of the node was suggestive of non-small cell cancer but cellblock showed no evidence of neoplasm. Lavage studies revealed branching gram-positive rods that were modified acid-fast positive suggestive of Nocardia but no malignant cells. CNS imaging showed no metastasis. PET scan showed increased uptake of mass (SUV)-21.6. Video assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) was performed and pathology showed OP and no evidence of malignancy. Tissue cultures were positive for Nocardia Abscessus. He was diagnosed to have Pulmonary Nocardiosis and started on oral Bactrim therapy after desensitization. Discussion: Due to the lack of clinical risk factors, size of the lung mass, cryopathology from lymph nodes suggesting non-small cell cancer, Increased PET uptake, VATS was performed. Wedge biopsy of the lung mass showed OP with histiocytes and connective tissue plugs in the alveolar ducts. No malignant cells were noted. Tissue cultures from lymph nodes grew Nocardia Abscessus. Work up for immunodeficiency including flow cytometry, Quantitative Immunoglobulins & chronic granulomatous disease was negative. Our case shows OP due to Nocardia in an Immunocompetent individual which is very unusual. Conclusion: Nocardia is an opportunistic pathogen with a tendency to disseminate in immunocompromised individuals. In an era of Immunosuppression, due to increasing solid organ transplants, Nocardia is recognized to cause OP. We report a case of Pulmonary Nocardiosis presenting as an OP in an Immunocompetent individual. It is important to consider Nocardia in the differential diagnosis of OP even in healthy individuals since delay in diagnosis and treatment leads to increased mortality.
