N2  - Introduction: The Global initiative for chronic obstructive lung disease has defined Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) as a state characterised by progressive airflow limitation that is not fully reversible and is associated with an abnormal inflammatory response of the lungs to noxious particles or gases. According to World health Organization, COPD is estimated to be the 3rd major cause by 2020. We conducted this prospective study to assess the bacterial etiology related to acute exacerbation COPD by examining sputum specimens from the subjects for culture and sensitivity. This can potentially advance our current knowledge of etiologies related to Acute Exacerbation (AE) of COPD. Rationale: To study sputum microbiological profile in patients admitted with acute exacerbation COPD with a special reference to bacterial infections & to find out the drug sensitivity pattern of the bacteria isolated in the sputum of these patients. METHODS: Prospective Study, Observational StudyPatients presented with AE of COPD presented to us enrolled in study after written informed consent. Detail clinical history & medical examination was done. Sputum was collected for culture & sensitivity study & data was analysed for isolates & antibiotic sensitivity. Results: This study presented here involved 100 patients with history of AECOPD. Out of them 90% were male & 10% female. Total 50.00% of patients had Gram Negative Bacilli infection where as 22.00% patients had Gram Positive Coci infection and in 28.00% patients no organisms were isolated. In 32% patients isolate seen was pseudomonas species. Organisms were most commonly sensitive to pipericillin & tazobactum, Amikacin, Azithromycin, ciprofloxacin. Conclusion :AECOPD have a major impact on the quality of life of patients with the condition. They are a major cause of hospital admission and health care utilization. Pseudomonas species was the commonest isolate followed by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumonie 12.00%, Klebsiella Pneumoniae 08.00%. Sputum culture is a good and simple tool to study the aetiology & complications due to bacteria in AECOPD. If done well, it can replace the costlier diagnostic methods like Immunodiffusion. Antibiogram helps in the correct treatment protocol during management of AECOPD. It also helps in screening resistant pathogens and better drug for treatment, thereby helping to decrease the mortality and morbidity. (Figure Presented) .
