N2  - Introduction: Streptococcus constellatus of the Streptococcus anginosus group, is a known normal flora of the human oral cavity and gastrointestinal tract, with the unique characteristic of forming abscesses most often in patients with obvious risk factors. We describe a rare case of Streptococcus constellatus septic pulmonary emboli complicated with multi-loculated empyema in a young, healthy male with no risk factors and no identifiable inoculation source. Description: A 39-year-old male with no significant medical history experienced a week of left upper-abdomen and lower-chest pain with multiple visits to emergency. Chest X-ray was normal and computed tomography angiogram incidentally revealed multiple bilateral pulmonary nodules. He denied recent injuries, illness, dental work, travel, fever, cough, or intravenous drug use. Patient declined admission and elected for outpatient follow up. Two days later, he returned with worsening pain, shortness of breath, anorexia, fever, and generalized malaise. Computed tomography of the abdomen revealed rapid growth of the pulmonary nodules and development of a loculated left pleural effusion with internal gas. A chest tube was placed and fluid culture grew Streptococcus constellatus. He did not meet criteria for infective endocarditis as trans-esophageal echocardiogram was normal and initial and repeat blood cultures showed no growth. Work-up for HIV, coccidiodomycosis, histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, Streptococcus pneumonia HIV-1/2, Hepatitis C, fungal and acid-fast cultures were negative. Empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics were de-escalated to clindamycin and ceftriaxone based on sensitivity data for Streptococcus constellatus. Over the next two days, patient showed no clinical improvement, worsening pain, persistent fever, and leukocytosis. Computed tomography of the chest showed interval development of a new loculated left basal pleural effusion, with persistence of the initial effusion and pulmonary nodules. A second chest tube was placed, but the new pleural fluid culture showed no growth. After 12 days of antimicrobial therapy, patient underwent video-associated thoracoscopic surgery and decortication. Pleural tissue biopsy, gram stain, and culture were negative. Patient was discharged on four weeks of intravenous ceftriaxone with follow-up and monitoring. Discussion: Streptococcus constellatus is a human commensal organism recently gaining awareness for its unique pathogenic properties. Pulmonary infection has been associated in the literature with significant comorbidities, underlying respiratory disease, or risk of aspiration. However, our case describes infection in a young, healthy, immunocompetent individual, likely due to self-inoculation from typical hygiene-associated gingival bleeding. It highlights the importance of early recognition and treatment, as delayed antibiotic therapy can lead to complications such as empyema, as seen in this case. (Figure presented) .
