N2  - Introduction: Electronic cigarette (e cigarette) is a battery-operated device that produce aerosol by heating a solution typically made up of propylene glycol, nicotine and flavoring agent. E cigarettes were first introduced in China, in year 2004 and since than use of these devices has grown exponentially, especially in recent years. Although current data suggest that they are far less harmful to tobacco smoking but their use is not without health risks. We present a case of 40year old female patient who presented with acute hypoxemic respiratory failure and was diagnosed with organizing pneumonia secondary to E Cigarette use. Case Report: 40year old African American female patient presented to the hospital with intermittent chest pain and worsening dyspnea for past few weeks. She admitted increased use of e cigarettes recently to help her quit smoking tobacco cigarettes. A chest radiograph showed scattered bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Arterial blood gas analysis was consistent with hypoxemia. Computed Tomography of Chest showed multifocal discrete and confluent ground glass opacities diffusely involving bilateral lung lobes (Figure 1A). Diagnostic bronchoscopy revealed normal tracheobronchial anatomy. Bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial biopsy were performed. Extensive workup including gram stain, fungal and bacterial cultures, viral studies, acid-fast stain, and immunoassays were negative making infectious etiology less likely. Over the course of few days, patient became more hypoxic and landed in to respiratory distress requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation. An open lung biopsy was performed and histopathological examination showed organizing pneumonia. Patient was started on high dose methylprednisolone. She responded rapidly to steroids and was extubated successfully. Patient was transitioned to oral prednisone and was discharged from hospital. She was seen as follow up in outpatient clinic and reported significant improvement in her symptoms. A follow up CT was done which showed resolution of ground glass opacities (Figure 1B). Discussion: The health risk associated with e cigarette use is largely unknown. Clinical studies evaluating the safety and risk profile of e cigarette use are limited and most the current data is obtained from in vitro studies. Although no definitive association has been established between toxins in e cigarettes and organizing pneumonia, this is the most likely etiology in our case. Only one case has been reported so far attributing the development of organizing pneumonia with use of e cigarette and further studies would be needed to establish a definitive causal relationship. (Figure Presented).
