N2  - Introduction Adenocarcinoma is the most common histologic type of lung cancer. It can present with wide-ranging radiologic findings such as nodules, infiltrates, and consolidation. There are variants of adenocarcinoma that maybe difficult to diagnose due to radiologic similarity to pneumonia Case Presentation 73 year-old male nonsmoker presented with 1 month history of productive cough, dyspnea and fatigue. Computed tomography (CT) of chest showed bilateral basilar dense consolidation with air bronchogram, predominantly in right lung. He was initially diagnosed with pneumonia and received appropriate antibiotics. Follow up imaging eight weeks later showed persistent bibasilar lung consolidation. Differential diagnosis at this point included malignancy, cryptogenic organizing pneumonia. and recurrent aspiration. Patient underwent flexible bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial biopsies of right lower lobe. Workup was negative for infectious etiology, however pathology showed mucinous adenocarcinoma. Patient declined treatment and was therefore referred to hospice care. Discussion Malignancy may often mimic alveolar infiltrative processes, it should be considered in cases of incomplete or slowly resolving lung infiltrates or consolidation as to avoid delay in diagnosis. Invasive mucinous adenocarcinoma (previously mucinous bronchioalveolar carcinoma) is a rare variant of invasive adenocarcinoma that accounts for 2-4% of adenocarcinoma cases. Invasive mucinous adenocarcinoma has a distinctive radiologic appearance, often predominantly solid, consolidation with air bronchograms, and lobar/multilobar distribution. There are reported cases of this variant of adenocarcinoma misdiagnosed as bacterial pneumonia, cryptogenic organizing pneumonia, chronic eosinophillic pneumonia and chronic aspiration. Therefore we recommend tissue sampling using either bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsies, or CT-guided transthoracic biopsy, or surgical lung biopsy in cases of non-resolving pneumonia without apparent diagnosis.
