N2  - Background: Intermittent sputum suction and aerosol therapy are necessary daily care for patients in RCC and RCW. In order to prevent aerosol transmitted diseases, such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB), to healthcare workers and nearby patients, timely evaluation and intervention are very important. So routine chest X-ray and sputum survey for acid-fast stain (AFS) and culture for MTB are essential when a new patient was hospitalized. Methods: From 2015 to 2016, all new patients hospitalized to RCC and RCW received chest image and sputum examination for three consecutive days, which is sent for AFS and culture for MTB (TB culture). If positive AFS result was notified, nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) will done further. MGIT solid and liquid medium were used for TB culture. If acid-fast positive bacteria are isolated, Capilia TB assay or BluePoint MycoID Plus kit is used to identified MTB or not. Results: In RCC, 122 new patients were included. One patient was suspected MTB in chest image study. AFS was positive in one patient, and NAAT was negative in further examination. TB culture was positive in five patients, four were finally diagnosed as NTM and one was confirmed MTB. The incidence of MTB was 0.82%. In RCW, there were 27 new patients. Chest image was no suspected MTB patient. AFS was positive in one patient, and NAAT test was negative. TB culture was positive in 4 patients, and three was diagnosed as NTM and one was confirmed MTB. The incidence of MTB was 3.7%. The mean ago was 78.7 year-old, mean duration from sputum collection to MGIT report was 24.1 days, another 5.7 days was needed for identification. Conclusion: In the 2 years survey, therewere 2 patients diagnosed MTB in RCC and RCW respectively. Routine chest X-ray, sputum analysis for AFS & TB culture enabled early diagnosis and therapy for MTB. It also shortened the accidental exposure duration of healthcare workers and other hospitalized patients and avoided MTB outbreak in the facilities.
