N2  - INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium szulgai is a rare pathogen, that when isolated, always implies disease status. Among nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM), M. szulgai represents <0.5% of all cases of NTM infection, with less than 100 reported cases. It mimics mycobacterium tuberculosis in the clinical presentation, but therapy requires a different management. Here, we present a non-HIV patient M. szulgai associated lung disease. CASE PRESENTATION: A 65-year-old male patient with chronic tobacco use and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease presents with increasing cough and sputum production, along with an unexplained weight loss and cachexia despite a strong appetite. His physical exam was negative except for scattered wheezes on auscultation, and chest xray (CXR) demonstrated a right upper lung opacity with superimposed inflammation (Fig A). Computer tomography (CT)/ Positron Emission Tomography of his chest showed a hypermetabolic dominant lung mass at the right apex communicating with right upper lobe bronchus and measuring 7.9 x 5.8 cm along with scattered nodular hypermetabolic consolidation throughout right upper and lower lobes. An endobronchial ultrasound with bronchoscopy was performed, and precarinal biopsies were obtained. Cytology was negative for malignancy but showed small fragment of non-necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with anthracosis (Fig B&C). Acid fast bacilli cultures returned confirming mycobacterium szulgai. HIV screen was negative. Ethambutol, rifampin and clarithromycin were initiated with close follow up in clinic. Repeated AFB cultures 3 months later are negative, and he is clinically improving. DISCUSSION: American Thoracic Society classifies M. szulgai as rarely isolated pathogen that should not be counted as an environmental contaminant. Clinical presentation of M. szulgai is similar to mycobacterium tuberculosis, with a predilection to pulmonary disease. Diagnosis is achieved through a combination of clinical, radiographic and microbiological evidence. Like all NTM pulmonary disease, infection with M. szulgai is classified into two forms: fibrocavitary disease and nodular bronchiectatic disease. Bronchiectasis with centrilobular nodules has been reported in M. Szulgai. In an immunocompetent patient, this disease resembles tuberculosis. Fortunately, this bacterium is highly susceptible with two drug anti-TB regimen, as well as macrolides or fluoroquinolones. Clarithromycin with ethambutol and rifampin has been suggested. Therapy may take up to 24 months. CONCLUSIONS: This case highlights the unusual finding of a NTM organism such as mycobacterium szulgai in an immunocompetent individual.
