N2  - INTRODUCTION: Hickam's dictam, the counterpart to Occams Razor, states “a patient can have as many diseases as they damn well please”1. We discuss a case in which a patient who carried a diagnosis of pulmonary and cutaneous sarcoid experienced “recurrent exacerbations”. CASE PRESENTATION: A 67 y/o man with sarcoidosis (Cutaneous and Pulmonary), right middle lobe syndrome and sleep apnea presented to his PCP with cough and dyspnea. He was admitted once and saw his PCP twice over 3 months with diagnoses of pneumonia and worsening sarcoidosis. He was treated with antibiotics and steroids and referred to his pulmonologist. Upon presentation to pulomonary he reported fatigue, weight loss, rhinitis, and regression of his sarcoid rash over 2 months. He denied cough, fevers, hemoptysis. On examination his BP was 133/66 pulse 98 Spo2 93% on RA with new scatterred ronchi and crackles in the LLL. A CT demonstrated new consolidation/mass in his LLL. He subsequently underwent Bronchoscopy, which was complicated by pneumothorax necessitating admission and a thoracostomy tube. His BAL grew MSSA, pseudomonas aeruginosa, and AFB stain was positive for mycobacteria. PCR was positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis. His BAL histoplasma antigen was positive at 0.9ng/ml. His transbronchial biopsy showed changes of chronic inflammation and fibrosis. He was initiated on pip/ tazo, rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol (RIPE) as well as an azole antifungal with great improvement. DISCUSSION: The patient resides in an area with endemic histoplasmosis. Selection of a therapeutic regimen for treatment of histoplasmosis was complicated by a strong CYP3A4 induction interaction between rifampin and the drug of choice for histoplasmosis, itraconazole2. The patient was initiated on posaconazole, which has less CYP interaction. To our knowledge there are minimal reports of concomitant posaconazole and rifampin therapy. Steady state serum posaconazole levels will be monitored by the hospital pharmacy and the patient was discharged on a increased dose of 300mg twice daily. CONCLUSIONS: Medical training often teaches us to come to a single diagnosis that best explains many symptoms - heuristic analysis - and in a more cost conscious enviroment we seek to reduce repeat testing. In this case we note that a patient may have an unusual presentation of multiple diseases simulating a single problem, and sometimes a repeat broad work up is necessarry. Our patient also highlights the unusual complications of concurrent RIPE and Azole therapy and the critical role of pharmacists on medical teams.
