N2  - INTRODUCTION: Diffuse panbronchiolitis (DPB) is a rare pulmonary syndrome characterized by bronchiolitis and chronic sinusitis that has been reported almost exclusively in patients of Asian descent. The exact pathogenesis of DPB is unknown, however a variety of genetic, environmental and systemic factors have been implicated. Few cases have been recognized outside of East Asia and it is even more rarely reported in patients of European ancestry. Here we present a case of DPB in a Caucasian male. CASE PRESENTATION: A 58-year old Caucasian male, never smoker, presented to clinic with a 5-year history of productive cough, exertional dyspnea and sinusitis. In the preceding 6 months, patient had 2 hospitalizations for “asthma exacerbation and atypical pneumonia” requiring antibiotics and steroid therapy. His medications included a budesonide/formoterol inhaler and albuterol. The patient had no history of childhood asthma, no concerning exposures, no known Asian ancestry or travel to Asia. Physical examination revealed diffuse bilateral inspiratory ronchi and rare expiratory wheezes. Pulmonary function tests (PFT) demonstrated FEV1 1.67L, FVC 2.67L, ratio 0.63 without significant bronchodilator response. Sputum culture grew H. Influenza. A high resolution computed tomography (HRCT) scan revealed widespread bronchiolectasis, terminal bronchial filling and constrictive bronchiolitis with lower lobe predominance (fig. 1). Sputum AFB's and an HIV test were negative, IgE levels were within normal limits. Given the patient's negative infectious work-up and classic imaging findings a presumptive diagnosis of DPB was made. After shared decision making, empiric macrolide therapy was begun rather than pursuing lung biopsy. After 4 months of therapy with daily azithromycin, the patient reported substantial improvements symptoms. Repeat PFT's and HRCT scan revealed marked improvement (fig. 2) with FEV1 of 2.44L, FVC 3.1L, ratio 0.78. DISCUSSION: The natural history of DPB is progressive respiratory failure marked by chronic and/or frequent episodes of superimposed bacterial infection. A study from 1983 demonstrated 5- and 10-yr survival rates of 62.1 and 33.2% without treatment compared to 10-yr survival rate of >90% with chronic macrolide therapy. It is unclear if the beneficial effect of macrolides in DPB is due to both the antimicrobial and anti-inflammatory effects, or the anti-inflammatory effects alone. CONCLUSIONS: DPB should be considered in the differential diagnosis in refractory cases of obstructive lung disease and bronchiolitis that show no improvement with conventional therapies. Although biopsy is generally recommended, given the safety of macrolide therapy it is feasible to start treatment empirically after workup with good outcomes.
