N2  - A 40 year-old man presented with 3 weeks of worsening cough, fever and weight loss. He was noted to be febrile to 38.6 °C with diffuse jaundice and mild right upper quadrant abdominal tenderness. His lungs had mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy and three cavitary lesions on CT scan. Acid fast bacili stain was positive, and Mycobacterium Tuberculosis was detected by NAAT. The patient confirmed that he had close contact with an active TB patient. Aspartate transaminase and alanine transaminase were both greater than 1,000 U/L with total bilirubin greater than 10 MG/DL. Hepatitis B surface antigen, core antibody IgM, and envelope antigen were positive with HBV DNA greater than 250,000 IU/ml. No liver abnormalities were seen on imaging. The patient had no previous record of his HBV status, and his only risk factor was recent unprotected intercourse. Due to the patient's elevated liver enzymes, RIPE therapy for TB was held, and treatment of acute hepatitis B was initiated with Tenofovir. Co-infection with TB and acute HBV is not commonly encountered in clinical practice. The treatment of TB in patient with underlying liver disease is difficult for many reasons. Many first line drugs including isoniazid, rifampin, and pyrazinamide can cause drug- induced hepatitis. In patient with underlying liver disease, the likelihood of drug-induced hepatitis may be greater and the disease more severe. As these drugs are usually used in combination, there is scarce data on individual drug toxicity rates in patients with underlying liver disease. However, one study found the incidence of drug-induced liver injury was 17% in patients on RIPE therapy with chronic liver disease. Likewise, the CDC recommends using fewer hepatotoxic drugs in patients with severe or unstable liver disease. In our patient, TB treatment was delayed until improvement of the acute viral hepatitis. Though the vast majority acute HBV cases resolve without treatment, it can take up to 6 months to do so. Additionally, while there are guidelines for TB treatment in patients with liver disease, given the extent of this patient's liver injury, the team was reluctant to introduce any hepatotoxic medications. Two weeks after initiation of Tenofovir therapy, the AST and ALT were less than two times the upper limit of normal, and RIPE therapy was started. The patient's liver function tests continued to improve while on RIPE and were normal at time of discharge.
