N2  - Introduction: The ubiquitous Prototheca sp, achlorophyllic algae, without chloroplast or pyrenoides, which behaves as saprophytes can be isolated from environmental, animal and food reservoirs. As a pathogen, it leads to clinical entities as olecranon bursitis and systemic or cutaneous infection, the most frequent one (1). The involvement in renal transplant recipients is rare and, to our knowledge, the current case is the fourth of cutaneous protothecosis reported in English literature. Case: A 79 years-old Caucasian male, renal transplanted six years before due diabetic nephropathy and chronic renal disease on a single kidney, was referred to our hospital because of cough and bloody sputum. He was medicated with cyclosporine, prednisolone and mycofenolato-mofetil since the transplant procedure and was suspected to have idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis. During hospitalization he developed edema on the back of the left hand and forearm that progressed to ulcer on the extensor surface of the forearm. He also presented deep violet erythema on the distal portion of left leg and ankle. Hand and forearm ultrasound exam revealed a liquid collection between the subcutaneous tissue and the myo-tendinous planes, suggestive of seroma or hematoma. A biopsy from the forearm ulcer and a skin scrap from the leg erythema were made and sent to microbiological and pathological studies. On both samples, the bacteriological exam was negative but in the mycological exam a yeast-like fungus identified as Prototheca wickerhammi was isolated; the pathological exam confirmed the mycological findings as numerous ovoid structures Grocott e PAS positive, were observed. Blood cultures were negative and the diagnosis of disseminated cutaneous protothecosis was established. The patient started treatment with liposomal amphotericin B and the lesions began to heal; on the twentieth day treatment he developed pneumonia, respiratory failure, septic shock and died. Microbiology: A biopsy specimen from the forearm ulcer and the skin scraps from the leg were cultured on Sabouraud with gentamicin and chloramphenicol; in both, white and cream yeast-like colonies were grown; microscopic exam of the colony showed sporangiospores within the sporangia, and morula-like aspect, consistent with Prototheca sp. The final identification of Prototheca wickerhammi was accomplished using the yeast card for Vitek® 2. Susceptibility testing was made employing the European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing broth microdilution reference method (EUCAST). The MIC results obtained were: amphotericin 1 mg/L, liposomal amphotericin B 0.5 mg/L, itraconazol 2 mg/L, posaconazole 2 mg/L, voriconazole 0.5 mg/L, and isavuconazole 0.125 mg/L; the MEC results for anidulafungin and caspofungin were 8 mg/mL each, similar to a previous report (2). Conclusions: The case emphasizes the increased frequency of unusual fungal infections affecting immunocompromised patients. Besides imunossupression, high doses of prednisolone, diabetes mellitus and aging were other important risk factors that contributed to this opportunistic infection in our patient. The high mortality rate of 75% affecting solid organs transplant recipients who also develop systemic protothecosis, not verified in the current case, emphasize the importance of the microbiology laboratory for rapid microorganism identification and early therapy.
