N2  - Advances in cystic fibrosis (CF) care have led to improved life expectancy; however, morbidity and mortality still occur due to progressive lung disease that begins in early life. Lung disease is often silent during infancy; however, air flow obstruction, air trapping (1), bronchiectasis, bronchial wall thickening (2), and elevated inflammatory markers including IL-8 and neutrophil elastase (NE) in bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid (3) are present in the youngest population. Elevated NE during early infancy is linked to later development of bronchiectasis (4). The contribution of bacterial infections to progression of CF pulmonary disease is well accepted. However, inflammation and structural lung disease can occur even before the presentation of respiratory symptoms (2). Underlying defects in the CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) protein lead to altered ion transport and abnormal airway surface liquid, as well as intrinsic effects on the immune system (5) that may contribute to airway inflammation. The role of viral infections and bacteria detected independent of traditional culture techniques is unclear. Improvements in viral detection techniques allow for an increased understanding of the contribution of respiratory viral pathogens to CF lung disease. Viral illnesses are common in children (6). Compared to healthy children, children with CF contract viral illnesses at a similar rate; however, these illnesses tend to be more severe, last longer, and are more likely to include lower respiratory tract symptoms (7). Recent investigations reveal that rhinovirus is the most commonly detected virus in infants with CF with accompanying respiratory symptoms including cough, wheeze and nasal congestion being most common (8). In school-aged children with CF, infection with respiratory viruses is associated with a decline in FEV1 (9) and in a small number of patients, infection with rhinovirus was identified as a risk factor for failure to recover FEV1 during a pulmonary exacerbation (10). Loss of lung function following viral infection has also been demonstrated in infants with CF (11). When compared to controls, infants with CF hospitalized for viral infections have greater inflammation in BAL fluid with higher neutrophil, IL-8 and NE concentrations (12). Viral illnesses in children with CF are associated with new acquisition of common bacterial pathogens including Haemophilus influenza, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Moraxella catarrhalis (13). Also, infants hospitalized with viral infections had earlier acquisition of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (12). Mechanisms by which viral infections may predispose the airway to bacterial infection include increased epithelial permeability, promotion of bacterial adherence and decreased bacterial clearance (14-16). Over the past decade, sequencing of the 16S ribosomal RNA (rRNA) gene reveals that the airways contain a diverse community of bacteria known as the microbiome (17). Studies of the microbiome have yielded insights into both health and disease processes. Imbalance of the microbiome, termed dysbiosis, is considered a key influence in a number of chronic diseases including asthma and COPD, as well as inflammatory bowel disease and obesity (18). Recent research suggests that the lower airway microbiota diversity in young children with CF decreases with age (19). This finding is consistent with studies revealing that the diversity of the airway microbiome is lower in older patients and in those with more severe lung disease (20). In contrast, a longitudinal analysis in infants with CF revealed that bacterial diversity in the airway and the gut increased over time as CF infants developed a core microbiota (21). In comparison to healthy controls, infants with CF have significantly different nasopharyngeal microbiota composition (22,23). Antibiotics are a significant influence on the microbiome. In infants, antibiotic use is associated with increases in gram-negative bacteria (23) and decreases in potentially beneficial commensal bacteria in the upper airway (22). In adults, antibiotics have been shown to be a strong driver of decreased diversity (24). The association of these early life microbiota changes to outcomes is poorly understood. A recent study revealed that the presence of bacterial pathogens was linked to reduced microbial diversity and increased airway inflammation (19); however, no association was noted between early airway microbial diversity and spirometry measured at 6 years of age. Furthermore, decreased microbial diversity in young infants receiving anti-staphylococcal antibiotic prophylaxis is associated with lower IL-8 levels and absolute neutrophil counts compared to those not receiving prophylaxis (25). Additional studies are needed to evaluate the impact of microbial diversity on early outcomes. Viral infections and alterations in the microbiome are related to underlying lung pathology in CF children and adults. Infancy represents a “window of opportunity” prior to the development of bronchiectasis; therefore, elucidating the impact of these early infectious insults remains imperative. An increased understanding of the role of viral infections and the airway microbiome in the pathogenesis of early CF pulmonary disease will allow us to define areas to target and guide therapeutics.
