N2  - The surface of the CF lung is dehydrated and poorly able to defend itself. In addition to the thick, viscous and mucin-rich mucus, once infection and the subsequent robust inflammatory response occur, additional components in airway surface secretions exacerbate the mucociliary clearance challenge: these include the polysaccharide matrix of bacterial biofilms, inflammatory cells and products, actin and extracellular DNA. Much of the conventional treatment approach to CF is focused on clearing the airway surface of these substances: physiotherapy techniques, mucolytics and airway hydration strategies. Despite the proven benefits to respiratory health from these treatments, incomplete airway clearance perpetuates the vicious cycle, leading ultimately to irreversible airway wall damage. New approaches are needed as adjuncts to those in clinical use. The alginate, Oligo G CF-5/20 is one such novel approach. Alginate is a low molecular weight linear polymer composed of (1-4)-linked α-L-guluronate (G) and β-D-mannuronate (M) residues. OligoG, the purified pharmaceutical derived from the brown seaweed Laminaria hyperborea by selective hydrolysis and fractionation, is a mixture of G-rich polysaccharides (>85% G). OligoG possesses an attractive array of properties, directed both at mucin structure and infecting bacteria, both clear targets for CF therapeutics. With regard to the former, gel-forming mucins are the major components of mucus; mucins are secreted from goblet cells and submucosal gland cells within which they are tightly packed in secretory granules. Once released, they expand rapidly, a process which is dependent on local environmental ionic composition, in particular on a minimal concentration of bicarbonate necessary to chelate calcium. In both the gut and the airway, failure of CFTR to secrete bicarbonate has been implicated in the pathophysiology of CF. OligoG has been shown to restore viscoelasticity of both murine gut and human airway mucus. In vitro studies also demonstrated the ability of OligoG CF-5/20 to modify bacterial surface charge and disperse Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) in biofilms; there is also evidence that the agent can potentiate the action of antibiotics against drug-resistant CF pathogens. It was hypothesized that when inhaled into the CF airway, these low molecular weight alginate oligosaccharides would interact with the structural assembly of mucus, normalising its rheological properties. Initial studies using molecular modelling and advanced imaging techniques confirmed its disruptive effects on mucin networks. Preclinical animal and human studies showed that OligoG CF-5/20 was safe for delivery via an inhaled route in healthy and diseased lungs. Further, the potential effect of OligoG CF-5/20 in the management of patients with chronic lung disease was studied by investigating its ability to modify the rheological properties of sputum from a series of CF patients. Recently, a phase II randomised, placebo-controlled, cross-over trial of inhaled OligoG was completed (clinicaltrials. gov NCT02157922). The primary objective was to demonstrate efficacy measured by FEV1; additional secondary efficacy endpoints included mucociliary and cough clearance (MCC) scans, lung clearance index (LCI), sputum rheology and microbiology, and quality of life questionnaires. Secondary objectives were to demonstrate the safety and tolerability of inhaled OligoG as a dry powder for inhalation, and evaluate patient compliance with treatment. Sixty-five stable adult CF patients from sites across Europe inhaled 30 capsules daily of dry-powder formulated Oligo G-5/20 (1050 mg total daily dose) via a MIAT Monodose inhaler. Inclusion criteria were FEV1 40%-100% predicted, and a medical history of positive cultures for PA. Concomitant use of inhaled acetyl cysteine, hypertonic saline and mannitol was not permitted. When considering the whole population, the primary (change in FEV1) and secondary endpoints were not met. However, interesting results were seen on subgroup analyses: younger patients and those with suboptimal adherence demonstrated apparent improvements in FEV1. MCC scans showed increased peripheral deposition of tracer after OligoG treatment, suggesting that airways were more patent despite showing no significant change in MCC itself. From the small group with matched pre- and post-treatment LCI values, the results were inconclusive. Microbiological analysis showed >1 log reduction in CFU count of total PA with OligoG treatment. Rheological analysis of sputum indicated more fluid-like properties after treatment, which became statistically significant by day 56. Overall, the safety signal was excellent. The study team hypothesise that the dose administered may have been excessive and that a lower dose warrants testing; additionally, administration of Oligo G for a longer time period may be needed for robust clinical benefit. These considerations will be incorporated into planning for future trials.
