N2  - Introduction: Tobramycin inhalation powder (TIP; Tobi® Podhaler ™, Novartis Pharma AG, Basel, Switzerland) and tobramycin inhalation solution are considered equally effective in terms of FEV1 improvement and reduction in sputum P. aeruginosa load. However, TIP is inhaled with a low to medium resistance inhaler, and the impact of inhalation flows on distribution of TIP throughout the lung is unknown. We hypothesized that: 1) TIP inhalation patterns vary among patients; 2) patients inhale TIP mostly through a fast inhalation maneuver; and 3) a slow inhalation using TIP results in a higher deposition in the small airways as compared to a fast inhalation. Aims: To assess TIP deposition using patient-specific spontaneous, low and high inhalation flows. Methods: Inspiratory flows of 12 cystic fibrosis (CF) patients on TIP maintenance therapy were recorded in the home situation. For every patient, 3 TIP flow profiles were measured: 1) spontaneous inhalation without prior instruction; 2) instructed slow inhalation; and 3) instructed fast inhalation. Fluid dynamic modelling (Fluidda NV, Kontich, Belgium): Bronchial trees were segmented using chest computed tomography scans of CF patients with mild, moderate and severe lung disease as determined by PRAGMA- CF (Perth Rotterdam Annotated Grid Morphometric Analysis for CF). Input data: TIP flow dependent particle characteristics (Novartis, data on file). The conservative cut-off value for an effective tobramycin dose was set at 10xMIC i.e. 40 μg/mL. Patient population included 8 males (median age 21, range 12-25 years) and 4 females (median age 37, range 13-45 years). Median FEV1 was 81% predicted (range 49-113%). Results: All simulations performed for TIP resulted in an effective dose of tobramycin above 10xMIC (range 3-141 10xMIC) in the small airways of all lobes. Mean tobramycin concentration in the small airways for all inspiratory flows was 1065 μg/mL (SD 523). The mean concentrations for spontaneous and instructed slow and fast inhalation maneuvers were 963 μg/mL (SD 471), 1323 μg/mL (SD 596) and 908 μg/mL (SD 395), respectively. Conclusions: Overall, our models show that for a clinical cut-off value of 10xMIC, all recorded inhalations of TIP resulted in a sufficient deposition in the small airways of all lung lobes. However, an instructed slow inhalation of TIP resulted in higher small airways concentrations relative to spontaneous and instructed fast inhalations, and therefore might be more effective to treat the small airways.
