N2  - Hemoptysis, or the coughing up of blood from the lungs, is a common event in cystic fibrosis (CF), and hemoptysis is a typical feature of chronic infection and inflammation of the airways. The amount of blood can range from streaking in the sputum (scant) to a large amount causing a life-threatening event (massive). The actual frequency of hemoptysis is not well established. Patient registries have tracked massive hemoptysis (1) where they have demonstrated an average annual incidence of 0.87% (or 1 in 115 patients) and overall 4.1% of patients will experience this serious complication in their lifetime. A retrospective chart review reported that 9.1% of patients experienced some degree of hemoptysis during a 5-year period (2) but it is not clear how well this is documented. Results from placebo-treated patients in clinical trials have reported much greater rates of hemoptysis: tobramycin inhaled solution (31% over 6 months) (3), dornase alfa (21% over 6 months) (4), and, more recently, ivacaftor (22% over 12 months) (5). More recently, a cumulative analysis of placebo-treated patients from multiple clinical trials demonstrated an 8% incidence over a duration of about 8 months (6); this lower rate may be because this analysis included a larger number of younger patients in whom the event is less common (but does occur). Although hemoptysis in CF airways disease is common and typical, it is not normal. The presence of hemoptysis is believed to indicate worsening of the airways condition and often treated as a sign of a pulmonary exacerbation (7). The etiology of the bleeding has been attributed to infection and inflammation with erosion into capillary or arterial walls such that the pressure within the blood vessel causes eruption of blood into the airways. In the setting of scant to mild hemoptysis, this may be due to injury of small vessels such as capillaries or even arterioles, while massive hemoptysis is attributed to eruption from larger bronchial arteries under systemic pressure. The presence of inflammation stimulates further growth of bronchial arteries such that they are larger and more numerous. Indeed, analyses of risk factors for any amount of hemoptysis have identified older age, greater impairment of lung function, and the presence of Pseudomonas as the most important factors (6). Interestingly, that analysis did not find the presence of Staphylococcus aureus to be associated with hemoptysis, whereas an analysis of patients with massive hemoptysis did find an increased risk associated with this pathogen (1). It is notable that although massive hemoptysis occurs in older patients with more advanced stage lung disease, these events do occur in younger, seemingly healthier patients. Approximately 25% of massive hemoptysis events occurred in patients <18 years of age, and 10% occurred in patients with normal lung function measured by spirometry (1). Similar findings have been reported in an analysis of pediatric patients where 6% of all pediatric patients had suffered at least one event of major hemoptysis and in this group there is no association with severity of lung impairment (8). There is a reduced risk of massive hemoptysis associated with CF therapies including inhaled antibiotics and dornase alfa (1) also consistent with the attribution of hemoptysis with infection and inflammation in the airways. More recent open-label studies of inhaled antibiotics (9-11), including tobramycin, aztreonam, and levofloxacin, demonstrated overall rates of hemoptysis that were less than what had been reported previously in placebo arms of similar trials (3). In general patients with hemoptysis will do well with treatment and bleeding resolves without complications for most. However, hemoptysis has been associated with untoward outcomes. There is a greater morbidity with an increased number of clinic visits and hospital days (1). There is also a greater mortality in patients with massive hemoptysis, much of which is attributed to the severity of lung disease, but there is a greater risk of death when controlling for lung impairment (1). In a study of the causes of mortality in French patients, respiratory disease remains the most common overall with respiratory complications occurring in 10.8%, most of which was attributed to hemoptysis (8.5% overall) (12). Such observations support earlier referral for possible lung transplantation in those patients with significant hemoptysis. The CF community is well-prepared to manage hemoptysis and there are guidelines published to offer consensus on best practices (7,13). In general the patient and family is taught to recognize that hemoptysis is a serious sign that should not be ignored and consultation with the CF center is advised. Management recommendations often depend upon the severity of the bleeding, but typically clinicians will treat as if a pulmonary exacerbation (ie, antibiotics). Indeed, in a recent observational study of hospitalized pulmonary exacerbations (which excluded patients with massive hemoptysis), about 13% of patients reported the presence of hemoptysis (14). For many patients with milder amount of bleeding, inhaled therapies and airway clearance therapies will be continued under the premise that they are part of the treatment of pulmonary exacerbations (15), whereas some therapies more likely to promote cough (eg, hypertonic saline, high-frequency compressive chest therapy) will be held in the setting of massive hemoptysis (1) assuming that such therapies might dislodge a clot and promote further bleeding. It is noted that these recommendations were based on consensus agreement and there are no clinical trials to guide decisions. Recent studies of inhaled mannitol, a therapy intended to promote cough and airways clearance, have not shown an increased rate of hemoptysis compared to placebo (16), even in children (17), although these studies were not testing the therapy in the setting of hemoptysis. For those patients with life-threatening bleeding or persistent bleeding despite other therapies, bronchial arterial embolization can be performed successfully and CF centers are advised to have access to an interventional radiology program with this skill set (13).
