N2  - Background: The calcium binding alginate oligosaccharide OligoG CF-5/20 derived from seaweed is proposed as a new class of CF drug. The agent has been shown to release adherent mucus from epithelial surfaces and disrupt bacterial biofilms in vitro, ex vivo and in vivo. OligoG is currently in clinical development for CF. Methods: A randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, cross-over phase-2 trial was performed in 65 adult CF subjects with 40-100% FEV1 values at 18 European sites. Dry powder OligoG (350 mg, TID) and placebo were administered during separate 28-day treatment periods, with a 28-day washout period following each treatment. Mucociliary and cough clearance (MCC) were assessed via gamma scintigraphy in a subgroup of patients (N=12) at three sites with established MCC capabilities (Copenhagen, Southampton and Glasgow). A study specific, standardized procedure (SOP) and common equipment were utilized at each site. MCC images were centrally analyzed at UNC-Chapel Hill in blinded fashion. Results: MCC data obtained at paired baseline visits were analysed to characterize the repeatability of key MCC outcomes in this multisite study. Correlation between the paired baseline MCC rates (Ave90Clr) was high (R2 = 0.73; p = 0.0004) as was the central:peripheral particle deposition ratio (R2 = 0.73; p = 0.0004), suggesting these data are robust and did not suffer from carry-over effects. While no changes in the Ave90Clr or cough clearance were observed after OligoG treatment, OligoG led to a more peripheral deposition pattern when compared to the preceding baseline (C:P 2.46 ± 1.15 vs. 1.98 ± 0.87; p = 0.09, Wilcoxon test). Discussion: The rate of mucus clearance was not affected by OligoG treatment despite a strong, but non-significant trend toward a more peripheral deposition pattern in this small study. Interpretation of these data are complicated by the fact that more peripheral deposition of particles, where intrinsic clearance rates are slower, biases against seeing an improvement in MCC. Deeper penetration of radiotracer is expected when airways obstruction by mucus has been reduced. Further, a therapeutic effect on cough-clearance would be difficult to demonstrate with the selected MCC SOP, which prioritizes measurement of cilia-driven clearance. Further analyses that correct clearance rates for observed deposition patterns are underway. In light of the other clinical data from the phase 2 trial, which suggested that particular patient subgroups (younger; received less than full prescribed dose) benefited more from OligoG treatment, and sputum rheology improved, additional exploration of optimal dose and effects on mucus clearance are warranted.
