N2  - Pulmonary infections caused by common and emerging fungal pathogens (including Aspergillus fumigatus, Exophiala dermatitidis and Scedosporium apiospermum) are an increasing clinical challenge in the management of cystic fibrosis (CF) associated lung disease. In the absence of a wholly effective and safe antifungal therapy and the compounding factor of drug resistance, there is an obvious unmet clinical need for novel, targeted treatments. NP339 (Novamycin®) is a broad spectrum antifungal peptide with a novel membrane-targeted mechanism of action and rapid time to kill. NP339 has been developed for systemic (intravenous or subcutaneous) administration to address disseminated fungal infection and for delivery direct to the airways and lungs by nebulisation. Efficacy has been demonstrated in vitro and in vivo against the respiratory pathogens associated with CF. Antimicrobial susceptibility testing based on broth microdilution was conducted against a panel of reference and clinical isolates of respiratory fungal pathogens. The combination effect of NP339 and conventional antifungals were assessed based on chequerboard methods by Burkhart et al (Brit J Dermatol. 2009;154:341-4). The in vivo activity of nebulised NP339 as a monotherapy (nebulised BD 1-10mg/mL for 10min) and in combination with intravenous and oral azoles and AmBisome, was assessed in preliminary murine models of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. Efficacy was determined by measuring the survival, weight and temperature for up to 6 d post-treatment. NP339 was active in vitro against reference and clinical isolates of respiratory fungi with an average MIC between 4-16mg/L. Combination studies demonstrated synergy or additivity in NP339/caspofungin and NP339/amphotericin B combinations. NP339 killed 90% of A. fumigatus spores and germlings within 2h of exposure at 16x MIC outperforming standard of care antifungal classes in its time to kill. Preliminary in vivo results from murine models of A. fumigatus infection suggest that nebulised NP339 reduces fungal lung tissue burden and when in combination with current standard of care intravenous amphotericin B and oral posaconazole improves survival outcomes and indicators of health such as weight and body temperature versus standard of care therapy alone. NP399 is a potential new antifungal candidate with relevance to CF; differentiated from current antifungal therapies in its rapidly fungicidal, membranolytic mechanism of action. Nebulised NP339 has potential utility as a monotherapy or as a combination product with standard of care antifungals; improving survival and other outcomes in invasive pulmonary aspergillosis models most likely by directly addressing lung fungal burden as systemic therapy reduces disseminated infection impact. NP339 has advantages over most standard of care antifungal therapies in its potential for delivery directly to the lung in aerosol form and systemically; ideally applied via both routes for maximal benefit NP339 is a promising candidate as a therapy for common and emerging fungal pathogens associated with CF respiratory infections such as aspergillosis, Exophiala and Scedosporium infections and its development as a much needed new antifungal therapy in this context will continue.
