N2  - Approximately 85-90% of patients with cystic fibrosis are born with or develop pancreas insufficiency in early childhood and require lifelong pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT) to minimize nutrient loss through maldigestion and malabsorption, especially of dietary fats. Porcine pancreatic enzyme supplements are most commonly used for PERT. They include pancreatin and pancrelipase, the latter is a more purified preparation that has higher lipase, amylase and protease activity. There are currently several formulations of pancrelipase based on enteric-coating and particle size. Enteric-coating is important to prevent breakdown of pancrelipase by gastric acid in the stomach and to allow for dissolution of the coating and release of pancrelipase in the duodenum where the pH is 5-5.5. Uncoated pancrelipase can be inactivated by gastric acid if no concurrent acid suppressive therapy is administered and are largely now used for treating abdominal pain in patients with chronic pancreatitis. Particle size governs the rate of gastric emptying into the duodenum, with smaller size particles emptying more rapidly and this has led to the development of microspheres and mini-microspheres. A recent Cochrane review of 13 trials of PERT in cystic fibrosis patients showed that enteric-coated microsphere formulations have a greater impact on clinical symptoms of exocrine insufficiency compared to non-enteric coated preparations. However, the limitations of prior trials include short duration of 4 weeks, crossover design and a lack of placebo group. PERT should be given during or after meals. There are 3 potential strategies for dosing of PERT. The optimal strategy is not known, partly due to the fact that there are many factors that affect normal pancreatic enzyme output including age, gender, and diet consumed. Therefore, mimicking normal physiology in patients with cystic fibrosis through exogenous administration of pancrelipase is not evidence based. Dosing based on the fat content of meals, with 500-4,000 units of lipase per gram of fat ingested, seems logical but is difficult for most patients to calculate. For weight-based dosing, the CF Foundation recommends 500-2,500 units of lipase per kilogram per meal and half of this for snacks in patients beyond infancy. The last strategy is based on administering a fixed dose of pancrelipase. Since approximately 480,000-960,000 units of lipase are secreted during a meal and 5-10% of total lipase output is required to prevent malabsorption, 50,000-100,000 units of lipase per meal would achieve this goal. There are no guidelines for the use of pancrelipase in patients receiving nutrition through an enteral feeding tube. Pancrelipase should not be inserted into enteral feeding tubes crushed or dissolved. A novel in-line cartridge with immobilized lipase for hydrolyzing fats prior to infusion into the enteral feeding has been approved by the FDA. A recent small trial evaluated a novel liquid microbial lipase and showed efficacy in CF patients with pancreatic insufficiency. Larger studies are required but this may prove to be be useful in pediatric patients and those requiring nutrition through enteral feeding tubes. In general, there has been interest in the development of non-porcine PERT. One such formulation, lipromatase, is a combination of a bacterial lipase, fungal protease and amylase. There has been efficacy shown in trials but this has not been FDA approved at the present time.
