N2  - Introduction: Wisconsin Newborn Screening (WI NBS) routinely tests the CFTR gene via next-generation sequencing (NGS) for infants with elevated immunoreactive trypsinogen (IRT) in the highest 4% or above 65, and targets data analysis of the ∼250 pathogenic variants listed in the CFTR2 database (www.cftr2.org). A female infant was found to have an elevated IRT of 183 and one pathogenic CFTR variant (F508del) identified on WI NBS. She had a history of ileal atresia, failure to thrive with pancreatic insufficiency and chronic productive cough. Full gene sequencing via NGS and deletion/duplication analysis at an outside commercial laboratory also did not detect a second pathogenic CFTR variant. Thus, the family was initially told that the child was a likely CF carrier, however sweat chloride testing at age 2 months was abnormal at 112 and 120. Sputum culture at the time grew Pseudomonas fluorescens and she had sputum production and cough. Repeat sweat chloride testing at age 7 months remained elevated at 109 and 113. Method: Prompted by abnormal sweat chloride testing results, WI NBS laboratory reanalyzed the NGS data by removing the restriction of a predetermined mutation panel to look for a second disease-associated variant. Both parents' DNA samples underwent CFTR gene mutation analysis to determine genetic phase. Results: The WI NBS lab successfully identified a second, rare variant c.49-50delTT which meets criteria for pathogenicity per ACMG scoring guidelines (PVS1, PS1, PM3, and PP4 criteria). The variant was Sanger-confirmed by the NBS lab, and later confirmed by the commercial laboratory. This rare mutation leads to a premature stop codon in exon 1 of the CFTR protein; a similar known pathogenic variant has been reported in the same position (c.50delT). Carrier screening of both parents revealed the variants (F508del and c.49-50delTT) in trans. The rare c.49-50delTT variant has not been previously reported. However, this is consistent with the child's clinical phenotypic diagnosis of CF. Upon effective use of this novel protocol of checking NGS data following abnormal CF screening, the state lab has proposed routine use of this improved method of CF NBS in appropriate infants. This has the potential to decrease time to diagnosis for questionably affected infants by both reducing time to full gene testing in a commercial lab or obviating the need to wait for 2-6 months for a repeat sweat test. Conclusion: The novel approach of performing immediate reflex analysis of full CFTR sequence data from NBS following abnormal or indeterminate sweat testing can provide timely and accurate diagnosis for affected infants. A rare pathogenic variant (c.49-50delTT) was successfully detected using this approach to NBS with associated symptoms described. The Wisconsin NBS Program has adopted this new protocol into routine screening for CF.
