N2  - Introduction: As options for personalized CF therapies rapidly expand, the number of interventional drug studies in children with CF has risen. Many of these studies are focused on demonstrating lack of harm; however, little is known about the baseline rate of adverse events (AEs) in pediatric CF subjects. Our objective was to determine the background (e.g. placebo group) rates of common and important or severe AEs in pediatric CF patients participating in therapeutic trials. Methods: We performed a retrospective analysis of data from two CF Foundation-sponsored studies - EPIC (Early Pseudomonas (Pa) Infection Control study) and AZ0004 (chronic azithromycin in children without recent Pa). As inhaled tobramycin is now standard of care, we included all EPIC subjects, but only the AZ0004 placebo group, in our analysis. The 5 most common respiratory, gastrointestinal, and other AEs were identified. Rates for these as well as those a priori identified as important/severe were calculated as AE rate per person year. Results: We analyzed data from 433 subjects (304 EPIC, 129 AZ0004), including 8,266 discrete AEs. Subjects were enrolled at a mean age (SD) of 5.7(±3.5) and 11.1(± 3.1) years in EPIC and AZ0004, respectively, with mean follow up time of 480 days(± 91) and 168 days(± 27), and mean FEV1 percent predicted of 96%(±17) and 99%(±14). Approximately 50% of subjects were female and 50% F508del homozygous in each study. Mean rate of AEs per participant year was similar in the two studies (18.1 in EPIC, 17.2 in AZ0004) (Table). Respiratory complaints had the highest incidence at 7.5 AEs/person year, with cough and nasal congestion the most common complaints; 79% of subjects followed for ≥120 days reported respiratory symptoms. Gastrointestinal (GI) symptoms were next most common at 2.2 AEs/person year. Pyrexia and headache were the most common other complaints. Serious AEs such as elevated liver enzymes, hemoptysis, dyspnea, distal intestinal obstruction syndrome, and decreased pulmonary function tests occurred at rates of <0.3 per person year. Discussion: We found an overall adverse event rate of 18 per person year in CF children (mean age 7.3 years (±4.2)). Most were mild and might be attributed to common childhood ailments, such as rhinorrhea, cough, pyrexia, rash, and abdominal pain. Unsurprisingly, respiratory AEs were most common, followed by GI. Serious AEs were quite rare. An understanding of background AE rates in children with CF is crucial for therapeutic trials in young children. (Table presented).
