N2  - Objectives: Primary-Demonstrate efficacy of OligoG dry powder for inhalation (DPI) as measured by FEV1, supported by secondary endpoints; Secondary-Demonstrate safety and tolerability of OligoG DPI. Methods: The calcium binding alginate oligomer OligoG CF-5/20, previously shown to release stagnant mucin and disrupt bacterial biofilms in model systems, was administered 30 capsules/day in a 28 days randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, cross-over phase II trial, conducted in 65 adult CF patients on a stable therapeutic regimen at 18 European sites. Efficacy parameters included spirometry, microbiology, Q of L, Lung Clearance Index, and Mucociliary and Cough Clearance (MCC w/gamma scintigraphy) in 12 patients. Results: Although spirometry data did not show statistically significant improvement in FEV1 after OligoG treatment as compared to placebo, there was a pattern of slightly reduced lung function at day 14 followed by a noticeable improvement lasting through treatment and to end of wash-out day 56. Less compliant patients showed clinically relevant improvement both at day 28 and 56. Together these results suggested benefits of lower dose and longer term. MCC data showed more peripheral deposition indicating more open airways. Preliminary microbial sub-group analysis indicated reduction in Pseudomonas aeruginosa with OligoG treatment. Majority of SAEs reported were pulmonary exacerbations of the CF disease, deemed unrelated to treatment. Some AEs, including dyspnea and increased sputum during OligoG treatment, pointed to an initial swelling of stagnant mucin in the airways at the high dose administered. Conclusion: The observed response could be caused by calcium chelation and hydration enabled by OligoG, followed by release of the mucus and improved lung function. A three months follow-up study is proposed to further explore the dose and efficacy vs compliance and convenience.
