N2  - Objectives: If treated early after colonisation, Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Psae) in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients may be eradicated with antibiotic treatment. Psae serum antibodies (SA) have a PPV >80% and a NPV >95%, and are measured in this centre if there is clinical deterioration in those with negative cough swab or sputum samples. Methods: The results of Psae SA in all patients without current Psae infection were reviewed for 2015. We compared the characteristics of those patients that had Psae SA measured and those that did not, and reviewed how Psae SA changed management. Results: Patients with previous lung transplantation or on Ivacaftor were excluded. 185 patients remained: 56/185 had Psae SA measured. Those with Psae SA provided fewer microbiological samples (median 5 vs 7), fewer sputum samples (median 0vs4)but more cough swabs (median 3 vs 1). Those with Psae SA had better lung health with higher FEV1 (median 92% vs 76%) and fewer IV days (median 0 vs 21). 74/185 patients had not cultured Psae in 2015. Only 43/74 had their Psae SA measured. 5/43 were highlighted for more intensive investigations. 5/43 had rising Psae SA without clinical deterioration. 1/43 patient was started on anti-pseudomonal antibiotics due to clinical deterioration and rising Psae SA without positive sputum. Conclusion: Psae SA offer an opportunity to detect and treat infection earlier. Attempting to grow Psae on cough swab samples is insensitive. Rising Psae SAwith negative sputum culture warrant further investigation. These results show we appropriately targeted Psae SA for those who were culture negative, provided cough swabs instead of sputum samples and have better lung health. However, Psae SA were tested haphazardly and a protocol is needed to standardise Psae SA testing. We have developed such a protocol whereby Psae SA would be measured in all non-chronic Psae patients at annual review, or if there is clinical deterioration with negative sputum samples.
