N2  - Objectives: Accurate identification of lower respiratory tract pathogens is of utmost importance in the clinical management of CF. The various analytic tests and methods have improved greatly in recent years, only the initial step of sample collection has undergone few if any changes. Sample collection and procession is crucial for the quality of the subsequent analytical steps. The aim of this study was to compare samples from 20 volunteers without exacerbation, taken from URT and LRT by different collection methods. Methods: 20 patients (8f, Ø25a, ØFEV1, 42.7%) Sample taking order; 1: Saliva swab cotton. 2: Saliva swab flocked nylon. 3: Oropharyngeal swab cotton. 4: Oropharyngeal swab flocked nylon. 5: Spontaneous sputum. 6: Post-cough oropharyngeal swab cotton. 7: Post cough oropharyngeal swab flocked nylon. Swabs were vortexed; sputum was parted and liquefied by: (i) NAC (ii) by DDT and (iii) by beads.100μL of each sample were applied to COS/BM Macconkey/BD, Chocolate II/BM, SAID/BM for 3 days, BCSA/BM for 5 days, OFPBL/BD and Sabouraud/BD for 10 days. All bacteria were determined by MALDI TOF/BM. Results: For Ps.a; the best recovery was sputum by beads and DDT, or pharyngal swab (100%) and the least was saliva/cotton (53.8%). St.awas best recovered in sputum with beads and DDTand oropharyngeal flocked swab (100%) and least from saliva (50%). Achromobacter was not found bycotton swabs, BCC and enterobacteriaceae and fungi were not found in saliva. Conclusion: This prospective ethical approved (E-36-15) pilot study is an essential first step to get the highest sample quality available without anesthesia. Sputum has the highest quality Ø (91.7-94.2%) directly followed by flocked cough swab 91.7% and flocked oropharyngeal 91.3%, least saliva 55.4%.
