N2  - Background: Leeds criteria (Lee TWet al. J Cyst Fibros 2003;2:29-34) isthe most commonly used definition for P. aeruginosa (Psae) status in a CF research setting. Studies using PCR techniques have shown that Leeds criteria may lack sensitivity for chronic Psae infection (da Silva Filho LV et al. Pediatr Pulmonol 2007;42:938-44). In an earlier abstract, we have shown the inter-rater reliability and face validity of clinicians' consensus for Psae status among adults with CF. Aim: Compare Leeds criteria against clinicians' consensus, to understand the diagnostic properties of Leeds criteria. Methods: Clinicians' consensus was achieved for all adults receiving care at the Sheffield Adult CF Centre in 2015 without lung transplantation and not on ivacaftor between three clinicians with CF experience blinded to the study aims and analyses plan. Sensitivity and specificity of Leeds criteria for chronic Psae were determined for the entire cohort. The group in which the Psae status differed between clinicians' consensus and Leeds criteria were compared against the group where there was agreement, to understand the clinical characteristics associated with disagreement between the two methods. Results: Compared against clinicians' consensus, Leeds criteria has a sensitivity of 0.75 (95% CI 0.66-0.83) and a specificity of 1.00 (95% CI 0.95-1.00) for chronic Psae. The group with disagreement in Psae status differed most from the group with agreement, in the number of cough swabs and sputum samples provided. Among those who provided more cough swabs than sputum samples (n = 77), Leeds criteria only has a sensitivity of 0.38 (95% CI 0.20-0.59) for chronic Psae. [Table Presented] Conclusion: Leeds criteria is highly specific for chronic Psae but lacks sensitivity especially among those who provided more cough swabs than sputum samples.
