N2  - Less intravenous but more inhaled antibiotics (IVATB) are recommended in CF. However, frequent uncontrolled relapses of pulmonary exacerbations may enhance pulmonary function decline. THC monitoring of FEV1 has been proved useful for the early diagnosis of Pex in adults but not in children. Objective: To determine the feasibility and impact of THC by portable lung function testing in children with CF. Methods: From 03/2015 to 12/2016, data was obtained from 3 home spirometric measurements (supervised by a physiotherapist) performed 3 times per week. Inclusion criteria were as follows: age 6-18 yrs, with chronic pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation requiring regularantibiotics courses, satisfactory spirometric curves. LFT data (FEV1, FEF25-75) and clinical symptoms (body weight, oxygen saturation, increase in cough, tiredness, fever, loss of appetite, haemoptysis) were prospectively transmitted to the regional respiratory network, which alerted the pediatric CF center whenever FEV1 decline was accompanied by clinical Pex criteria. Results: 20 paediatric patients were included, median age: 11,0 [8-17] yrs, median FEV1: 62% [43-111] of reference values. Per week they accom-plished 6,3 spirometric tests (70% observance), and 3423 spirometry results with clinical questionnaires were transmitted. The regional network alerted the CF center in 33 cases, with a need for IV ATB in 5 cases. THC led to the safe avoidance of 130 days (-35%) of IV ATB compared to the previous year. Conclusion: Spirometric THC combined with a Pex symptoms questionnaire in children demonstrated fairly strong observance. Compared to the previous "baseline" year, 1/3 of IV ATB could be avoided. The prospective evaluation of the utility of long-term THC for Pex management in children with CF is required.
