N2  - Objectives: Patients with cystic fibrosis are at great riskof infection by non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM). The aim of this study was to analyze the results of sputum samples from our CF patients for NTM. Methods: Anthropometric data, type of mutation, respiratory functional tests, use of antibiotic aerosol therapy. Culture and staining for mycobac-teria were collected in each annually visit and in case of clinical or spirometric deterioration not explained byother cause and were processed by the Laboratory of Microbiology of the Regional University Hospital of Málaga. Results: 49 patients were included, with a mean age of 31.45 years, a BMI of 22.05. 79.6% had pancreatic insufficiency and 34.7% had diabetes mellitus associated with CF. 47.9% carried the mutation of the F508/other. The mean post bronchodilator FEV1 was 2098.57 mL (59%); FVC 3150 mL (72.7%) and FEV1/FVC 64%. In 94% of the cases they presented some bronchial colonization and received treatment with antibiotic aerosol therapy 74%. In 3 cases the sputum smear was positive. M. abscessus was isolated in one case, M. avium intracelullare and M. avium in another case in an isolated culture, so it was considered contamination and finally in the third case M. chelonae was isolated in a single culture, so it was also considered microbiological contamination. In the first case the serial cultures were positive for M. abscessus and the antibiogram showed intermediate resistance to Ciprofloxacin and Moxifloxacin. The patient presented an evident clinical deterioration reason why initiated specific antibiotic treatment. Conclusion: A low prevalence was found in agreement with the rest of the series of environmental mycobacteria in adult CF patients. It is of vital importance to perform staining and culture for mycobacteria periodically and especially in those cases of pulmonary exacerbation not attributable to another habitual bacterial infection. Patients with repeated isolations should be closely monitored.
