N2  - Spontaneous bacterial empyema (SBE) is an infection of a hydrothorax in a cirrhotic patient that is not of parapneumonic origin. Chest tube thoracostomy is traditionally thought to be contraindicated in SBE and current recommendations are for antibiotic treatment without drainage. Excluding positive bacterial pleural cultures, few reported cases of SBE meet the additional diagnostic criteria of empyema for which chest tube thoracostomy is recommended. There is currently no literature to suggest when the benefits of chest tube thoracostomy might outweigh the risks in cases of SBE. Herein we describe the case of a patient with SBE with an inflammatory pleural fluid managed via chest tube thoracostomy. A 64 year old male with history of cirrhosis complicated by a hepatic hydrothorax presented to the hospital with dyspnea. He denied fevers, pleuritic pain, cough, or sputum production. Chest radiograph revealed an opacified right hemithorax. Sampling of the pleural fluid revealed a white blood cell count (WBC) of 4530 cells (95% polymorphonuclear), glucose <10 mg/dL, pH 7.09, protein 3.677 g/dL, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) 617 U/L, and gram positive cocci on staining. The patient underwent chest tube thoracostomy in addition to receiving antibiotics. Pleural fluid culture later revealed methicillin-susceptible staphylococcus aureus. Serial pleural fluid monitoring demonstrated improvement in pleural inflammation as the pH improved to 8.5 with similar improvements in glucose, WBC, and LDH. The chest tube was removed after 10 days when daily output decreased to 40 mL, and he was discharged home without experiencing any renal or electrolyte related complications. Chest tube thoracostomy is considered contraindicated in cirrhotic patients due to a perceived high morbidity and mortality secondary to associated electrolyte disturbances and renal failure. This perception is based on only small case series. Literature regarding SBE largely excludes patients who fit traditional empyema criteria consistent with significant pleural inflammation, specifically a low pH or glucose and elevated LDH. This patient denied pneumonia-like symptoms and therefore satisfied the SBE diagnostic criteria. His pleural fluid studies demonstrated significant pleural inflammation with a low pH, low glucose, and elevated LDH. This case illustrates the lack of literature guiding management of cirrhotic patients with an infected and inflammatory pleural fluid. Chest tube thoracostomy in addition to antibiotics may be indicated in SBE cases that meet traditional empyema criteria. Further study is needed to help guide clinicians on when the benefits of a chest tube in a cirrhotic patient may outweigh the risks.
