N2  - INTRODUCTION: Bacterial coinfection in influenza contributes to substantial morbidities and mortalities. Diagnosis of coinfection makes clinicians a great challenge. Effective and timely empiric antibiotics improve patients outcome. The aim of this study is to analyze the characteristics of bacterial respiratory coinfection in pandemic 2016 severe complicated influenza in Taiwan. METHODS: This is a retrospective observational study conducted by Taiwan Severe Influenza Research Consortium (TSIRC) which includes seven tertiary referral medical centers. Patients with virology-proven influenza infection admitted to intensive care unit between January and March 2016 were included for analysis RESULTS: Two hundred thirty-three patients were admitted to intensive care units in 7 medical centers in Taiwan from January to March, 2016 with virology-proven influenza. Among them, 218 patients received ventilator support. The mean age was 58.7 ± 14.2 years, 103 (62.4%) patients were male, mean body mass index was 25.9 ± 5.7 kg/m2, type 2 diabetes mellitus was in 70 (42.4%) patients, and mean Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Examination II score was 21.9 ± 8.7, fever and cough were the most comlained symptoms (77% and 78.2%, respectively), and mean partial pressure of oxygen in arterial blood versus fraction concentration of inspired oxygen was 122.7 ± 102.7. Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome presented in 89 (53.9%) patients. Most documented influenza subtype was type A, H1 in 122 (73.9%) patients. Bacterial coinfection with positive cultures from respiratory tract were found in 91 specimen. Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (12 patients, 7.3%) was the most commonly identified pathogen in community-acquired respiratory coinfection. In patients developing late-onset ventilator-associated pneumonia, Acinetobacter baumannii (20 patients, 12.1%), Pseudomonas aeruginosa (11 patients, 6.7%) and Klebsiella pneumoniae (10 patients, 6.1%) were the most encountered pathogens. Duration of mechanical ventilation was 15.8 ± 13.5 days. Duration of length of stay in intensive care unit and hospital was 17.6 ± 15.1 and 27.5 ± 20.7 days, respectively. All-cause in-hospital mortality rate was 38.2%. CONCLUSIONS: Among intensive care unit patients with 2016 pandemic severe complicated influenza, Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus was the most prevalent community-acquired pathogen. In later hospital-acquired pneumonia, Gram-negative bacilli, including Acinetobacter baumannii, were most frequently isolated.
