N2  - Introduction: Pleuroparenchymal Fibroelastosis (PPFE) is a rare type of progressive interstitial lung disease (ILD) characterized by pleural and sub-pleural parenchymal fibrosis with elastic fibers deposition predominantly in the upper lobes. PPFE is extremely rare in pediatrics and has only been described in transplant patients or in association with chemotherapy. Case: We report a case of a previously healthy 16 year-old male who presented with a 6-week history of progressively worsening dyspnea, non-productive cough and decreased exercise tolerance. Initial treatment with antibiotics for presumptive pneumonia failed to cause any improvement. He had no significant medical history, occupational exposures or tobacco use. Family history was significant for fatal pulmonary fibrosis of maternal grandmother. Review of systems revealed decreased appetite, weight loss and fatigue. Physical exam was remarkable for low body mass index, flattened chest and scattered crackles over upper lung fields. Infectious work-up and basic laboratory data were unremarkable including normal white cell count and C-Reactive Protein. Rheumatologic work up was moderately positive for anti-melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5 and weakly positive for anti-nuclear matrix protein NXP-2 autoantibodies. Serum IgE level was mildly elevated. Aspergillus antibodies and hypersensitivity pneumonitis panel were negative. High-resolution chest CT revealed pleural thickening, wedge shaped opacities, bronchiectasis and volume loss at upper lobes bilaterally, pneumomediastinum and small pericardial effusion. Pulmonary function testing demonstrated severe restrictive ventilatory defect with impaired carbon monoxide diffusion capacity. Lung biopsy obtained via video-assisted thoracoscopy showed sub-pleural fibrosis and elastosis with signs of organizing pneumonia, focal obliterative bronchiolitis and veno-occlusive type pattern, all consistent with PPFE. Post-operative course was complicated by pneumothorax and bacterial pneumonia. Genetic work-up including whole exome sequence was negative. In less than 3 months since initial presentation, patient became oxygen dependent at rest. He developed pulmonary arterial hypertension treated with Sildenafil. Due to rapid disease progression patient was listed for lung transplant. Discussion: This is the first case of an adolescent patient with rapid development of hypoxemic respiratory failure secondary to PPFE without apparent etiology. PPFE is a rare type of ILD that can be idiopathic, familial or associated with transplantation, chemotherapy, radiation and occupational exposures. In pediatrics, it has only been described in association with transplant and chemotherapeutic agents. This unique case suggests that clinicians should include PPFE diagnosis in their differential for pediatric patients with idiopathic interstitial pneumonias. Early evaluation for lung transplant should be considered due to poor response to medical management.
