N2  - Introduction: Empyema is the presence of infected pleural effusion with purulent fluid and positive gram stain or culture. It usually develops as a complication of a preceding pneumonia but can also occur rarely as an isolated infection. We present a case of Salmonella empyema in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis on immunosuppressive therapy. Case Description: 70 year old male patient with past medical history of Rheumatoid Arthritis on leflunomide and prednisone, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, Chronic sinusitis and Restless leg syndrome who was referred for evaluation of pleural effusion first seen on CT chest one month ago. He has been smoking one pack of cigarettes per day for the last fifty years. He has chronic cough productive of clear sputum and dyspnea on exertion. He denied fever, chills, hemoptysis, wheezing, weight loss and chest pain. He had diarrhea with abdominal pain and low grade fever eight weeks ago that resolved without treatment in few days. Chest X ray and CT chest showed severe emphysema and a large loculated right-sided pleural effusion. No lung infiltrate, consolidation or meditational lymph nodes. On physical exam, he was afebrile and hermodynamically stable with decreased air entry on the right side of the chest. He had no leukocystosis on his CBC (WBC 7.8, no left shift). Patient underwent thoracentesis as outpatient with drainage of one liter of milky, exudative pleural effusion. Analysis of pleural fluid showed LDH >2500 U/L(serum LDH 169), protein 3.5 g/dl(serum protein 7.2 g/dl), albumin 1 g/dl, glucose 23 mg/dl, Cholesterol 420 mg/dl, triglycerides 267 mg/dl, no chylomicrons, ADA 89 ul (normal less than 9), RBC 545000 cellul, WBC 575635 cellul(90% Neutrophils). Pleural fluid bacterial culture grew Salmonella while fungal and mycobacterial cultures came back negative. Cytology showed chronic and acute inflammatory cells with no malignancy. Patient was admitted and a CT-guided pigtail catheter was placed with drainage of 250 ml of pleural fluid over the next three days and resolution of pleural effusion on CT chest. He was treated with levofloxacin orally for 6 weeks with complete resolution of empyema on repeat CT chest. Conclusion: This is a rare case of empyema caused by Salmonella that seeded a pseudochylothorax during a diarrheal illness in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis. It adds to the handful of cases of Salmonella empyema that were described in literature. [Image Presented].
