N2  - Background: Epidemiological data suggest a minority (∼20%) of smear-positive TB patients are associated with a disproportionate burden (∼80%) of transmission. A TB substantial burden (∼50%) remains undiagnosed in the community. The cough aerosol sampling system (CASS) is a method for quantifying culturable Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacilli in a particle size-dependent manner. CASS readouts are associated with tuberculin-skin-test-conversion and development of active TB in household contacts. Objective: To assess determinants of CASS-positivity in patients with drug-susceptible (DS)- or drug-resistant TB. Methods: Newly-diagnosed adults with DS- or drug-resistant TB (n=440), including patients with extensively drug-resistant (XDR)-TB failing treatment, underwent 5 min CASS, sputum microbiology (smear microscopy, Xpert MTB/RIF, liquid culture, lipid staining for non-replicating bacilli (Nile Red), phenotypic first- and second-line drug susceptibility testing), lung function testing, chest radiography, and clinical examination. CASS agar plates were incubated for 8 weeks and patients with ≥10 CFU in the respirable range (≤4.7 μm) were classified as CASS-positive. A subset of CASS-positive patients underwent longitudinal repeat CASS. Genomic and transcriptomic sequencing data are not reported here. Results: Of 395 sputum-culture positive patients with phenotypic drug-susceptibility data, 309 (78%) were on treatment ≤48h. 32 of 305 smear-positives (15%) and none of the 90 smear-negatives were CASS-positive (7 smear-negatives had <10 CFU in the respirable range). CASS-positives had a median (IQR) of 29 (17-80) CFU. In multivariate regression analyses, any TB treatment (odds ratio 0.46, 95% CI 0.21-1.02), smear-positivity (6.36, 2.53-15.99), sputum viscosity graded as “very fluid” (2.67, 1.13-6.32), and fewer symptoms (TBscore; 0.78 per symptom, 0.68, 0.89) predicted the presence of any CFU in respirable cough aerosol. Patients on effective drugs were less likely to be CASS-positive (0.62 per effective drug defined by phenotypic susceptibility, 0.39-0.99). Cavitation, cough strength or frequency, Nile Red phenotype, and HIV were not independent predictors of CASS-positivity. In baseline CASS-positive patients sampled a second time, all DS-TB patients (median (IQR) days to repeat sampling 41 (38-69)) and MDR-TB patients (141 (49-160)) were subsequently CASS-negative; however, each of the 4 XDR-TB patients repeatedly sampled were consistently CASS-positive for over a year. 7/32 therapeutically destitute home-discharged patients living in the community with MDR- or XDR-TB were likely highly infectious Conclusion: 15% of smear-positive patients were CASS-positive, and this was associated with lack of effective treatment, non-viscous sputum, and lower symptoms scores. These data inform the need for screening biomarkers and strategies that can identify and actively target highly infectious undiagnosed patients in the community.
