N2  - Introduction: Presenting symptoms of hypoxic respiratory failure are nonspecific and the differential diagnosis is broad. Physicians tend to rely on the patient's clinical history, demographic data, and history of exposure to design both initial diagnostic and therapeutic plans. We present a case of an immigrant patient in her 3rd trimester of pregnancy with CD4 lymphopenia, hypoxic respiratory failure, and a positive Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) screening test. Case report A 34-year-old woman originally from Ethiopia with a history of chronic Hepatitis B and 30 weeks of pregnancy was transferred from an outside hospital with one week of worsening shortness of breath, tachypnea, dry cough, and intermittent low grade fevers. Workup in the outside hospital included: positive HIV-1/-2 screening test, undetectable HIV-1 viral load, CD4 count of 84, and computer tomography (CT) angiography of the chest was negative for PE but showed diffuse ground-glass opacities with air bronchograms. She reported no sick contacts or environmental exposures. Echocardiogram was unrevealing. She was in mild distress while breathing on high-flow nasal cannula (50L/min, FiO2 50%), with pulse oximetry of 95%, temperature of 37.2 C, and diffuse coarse crackles throughout were noted. Repeated HIV-1/-2 screening and HIV-1/-2 viral loads were negative, and CD4 and CD8 counts were 52 and 51 cells/ L, respectively (CD4/CD8 ratio 1.0). Blood cultures, pneumocystis PCR on induced sputum, three AFB smears and Mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR, respiratory pathogen panel, and fungal serologies were all negative and broad spectrum antibiotics were stopped after 7 days. Additional laboratory tests fulfilled the SLICC criteria for systemic lupus erythematosus (lymphopenia, ANA, anti-DSDNA, and moderate anti-IgG phospholipid antibodies) and lupus pneumonitis with false-positive HIV testing were diagnosed. Methylprednisolone 1000 mg intravenously daily for 5 doses was started, followed by oral Prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, Azathioprine 75 mg twice daily, and Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily. The patient had no complications of pregnancy and was eventually discharged home. Discussion: Acute lupus pneumonitis is rare (1-12%) among patients with known systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and is even less common to be the presenting manifestation of SLE. This case was challenging due to the patient's atypical presentation for SLE and indeterminate HIV testing. However, in HIV infection associated with low CD4 counts, the CD4/CD8 ratio will be low, and her normal CD4/CD8 ratio was a unique clue for her diagnosis. Acute lupus pneumonitis can be commonly misinterpreted as an infectious process. Delayed treatment can lead to hypoxic respiratory failure.
