N2  - Introduction: Urine Blastomyces antigen testing is a safe and readily available method to aid in the diagnosis of both pulmonary and systemic blastomycosis. Direct visualization or culture of the organism can be both invasive and time-consuming while the effectiveness of urinary antigen testing is supported by both a high sensitivity and specificity. Despite this, we have identified a case in which falsely positive Blastomyces antigenuria led to a delay in diagnosis. Case Summary: A 66 year old non-smoking woman without a significant medical history presents with fevers, cough, and dyspnea. The cough had been present for 8 months and was associated with white colored sputum. She also reported a 20 pound weight loss and night sweats. Vital signs were unremarkable on admission and her exam was significant for bilateral lower lobe rales. Chest radiograph showed bilateral lower lobe opacities and the patient was empirically started on antimicrobials. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest showed multifocal pulmonary parenchymal consolidation and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Sputum cultures obtained for AFB, bacteria, and fungal cultures were negative. Bronchoscopy noted white secretions of the left lower lobe, but BAL cultures and cytology were normal. She reported improvement in her symptoms and was discharged home to complete a course of antibiotics for pneumonia. At subsequent outpatient follow up, the patient reported continued cough and dyspnea. CT of the chest showed persistent bilateral pulmonary opacities. Blastomyces urine antigen from her prior hospitalization had returned positive and she was started on itraconazole for pulmonary blastomycosis. After 4 months of treatment the patient remained symptomatic with worsening of the pulmonary opacities. Lung biopsy of the left lower lobe and mediastinal lymph nodes was completed and pathology revealed stage IV lung adenocarcinoma with lepidic growth pattern. Further review of the positive urinary Blastomyces antigen revealed that while antigenuria was detected, it was below the level of quantification. Discussion: While urinary Blastomyces antigen testing has been reported to have high sensitivity and specificity, newer testing using quantificative methods can identify lower levels of antigenuria that are below the limit of quantification. Similar testing in urinary Histoplama antigen testing has shown that positive tests below the limit of quantification are associated with greater percentage of false positives. We suggest that low positive results of urinary Blastomyces antigen testing should be interpreted cautiously and further study is necessary to define the appropriate cutoff for positive antigenuria in order to prevent delayed or missed diagnoses.
