N2  - Background: Antibiotics are commonly prescribed to treat patients with an escalation of respiratory symptoms that herald an acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease(COPD). Consensus guidelines base indications for antibiotics largely on sputum characteristics with the class of antibiotic to be determined by a patient's tolerance profile and underlying disease severity. We sought to determine the temporal response of daily respiratory symptoms during the peri-exacerbation period when patients are prescribed antibiotic therapy by using an electronic daily diary. Methods: 170 consecutive patients were enrolled over 18 months into a COPD telemedicine based disease management program. Patients accounted for a total of 408 outpatient COPD exacerbations and 174 (42%) were administered antibiotics as therapy for the exacerbation. Patients had retrospective review of their daily symptoms as well as peak expiratory flow rates. Dyspnea (modified Borg), sputum quantity, color and consistency, peak flow rates and upper airways symptoms of sneezing, sore throat, cough and wheeze were recorded. Results: 170 patients with mean age of 66±7.19 yrs. and mean FEV1 40% ±17 of predicted without the presence of bacterial airway colonization were monitored for 18 months. 104 patients accounted for the 174 outpatient COPD exacerbations that were treated with combined prednisone and antibiotic therapy vs. 236 episodes treated with corticosteroids alone. A total of 5 classes of antibiotics were utilized for exacerbation treatment, fluoroquinolones 59%, macrolides 30%, and 11% received sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim, doxycycline or amoxicillin-clavulanate. Symptom recovery, defined as return to baseline respiratory symptom within 10 days, was less likely in patients who receiving antibiotics vs. corticosteroids alone (53% vs 69% p<0.0014). The comparison of the use of fluoroquinolones vs. macrolides had a trend towards prolonged, >10 days, symptom recovery (51% vs 35% p=0.06). 78% of patients with multiple exacerbations, 2 or greater, (n=38) received the same class of antibiotic across exacerbations predominately fluoroquinolones (59%) compared to macrolides (25%), without a difference in symptom recovery (48% vs 56% p<0.65). Conclusion: Antibiotic prescribing patterns to treat an acute exacerbation of COPD are variable. The use of macrolides was associated with a trend towards quicker resolution of exacerbation symptoms compared to other antibiotic classes. Development of better tools to determine the indication, likelihood of benefit and duration of treatment with antibiotics for the onset of a COPD exacerbation are warranted.
