N2  - Introduction: Pleural effusions commonly complicate numerous chronic conditions including congestive heart failure, chronic kidney disease, and cirrhosis. Chronic pleural effusions in these cases are often of limited clinical significance though the presence of persistent fluid in the pleural cavity can predispose to important complications. Here we present a case of spontaneous bacterial empyema caused by a unusual pathogen which developed as a complication of a chronic transudative effusion. Case Presentation: A 49 year old woman with a chronic left pleural effusion presented with fever and palpitations. She was admitted to the ICU for atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular rate and evaluation was done to identify a potential source of sepsis given her fever. She had no leukocytosis and complained of only a mild chronic cough that was productive of clear sputum. Chest radiograph on admission showed a large left pleural effusion which had increased in size, but had been evident for five years. The chronic effusion was attributed to congestive heart failure and suboptimal hemodialysis compliance, with prior thoracentesis showing transudative fluid by Light's criteria. Thoracentesis was performed during the hospitalization with removal of 550 mL of straw-colored fluid. Fluid studies were now exudative with >500 polymorphonuclear cells (PMN) and a low pH. Cultures from the thoracentesis grew Pasteurella multocida and tube thoracostomy was performed for the empyema. She was treated with a two week course of antimicrobials with improvement of her respiratory status. Repeat fluid cultures at day 5 showed no further bacterial growth and she was later discharged home. Discussion: First recognized in patients with hepatic hydrothorax, spontaneous bacterial empyema has more recently been described as a complication of chronic transudative effusions from other sources, including end stage renal disease. The diagnosis requires absence of radiographic evidence for pneumonia with a positive pleural fluid culture and >250 PMNs, or a PMN count >500 in culture negative fluid. Additionally, there must be prior evidence of a chronic transudative pleural effusion. Pasteurella multocida is an uncommon pathogen in humans, yet has been described to cause both pneumonia and empyema, particularly in the elderly and those with chronic pulmonary diseases. This bacteria resides in the oropharyngeal cavity of domestic pets which are an important transmission vector to humans. Later discussion with the patient revealed that she had a Chihuahua at home. Early identification and aggressive treatment of spontaneous bacterial empyema are essential to preventing morbidity and mortality in this underdiagnosed disorder.
