N2  - Introduction:The diagnosis of primary tuberculosis (TB) is challenging for any clinician. Physicians must maintain a high degree of suspicion and be vigilant in pursing the diagnosis because of its low prevalence in the United States and variable degrees of presentation. We present a case of Primary Progressive pulmonary TB imitating a severe pneumonia in the absence of immuno-suppression. History of Present Illness: A 21 year-old woman, recently emigrated from Guinea, presented to the emergency room with dry cough and left pleuritic chest pain for two weeks. She denied fever, chills, night sweat, weight loss, no recent sick contacts. Initial physical exam revealed regular tachycardia and saturation of 96% on room air. The only positive finding was bronchial breath sounds on auscultation of the left chest. Chest imaging showed homogenous left sided opacification with air bronchogram. Laboratory work returned with a borderline elevated WBC count without shift and a negative HIV test. Patient was initially treated for community acquired pneumonia and isolated for possible tuberculosis. A sputum induction was performed thrice with negative bacterial cultures and smears for acid fast bacilli (AFB). Symptoms did not subside, and patient developed low grade fevers at night despite 14 days of antibiotic treatment. Subsequent sputum cultures were positive for mycobacterium complex. Patient was started on RIPE therapy with clinical improvement. Discussion: Prevalence of TB has increased in the United States in the face of Globalization possible due to increased awareness and medical vigilance. Primary TB, as depicted in the case above, can present in markedly variable ways in comparison to secondary.The symptomatology between primary and secondary TB have some overlap, mostly constitutional symptoms fever, nightsweats, and weight changes; but primary TB can vary wildly in acuity, from subclinical to fulminant and out-of-proportion in severity.Symptomatology aside, distinguishing between primary and secondary is best depicted radiographically. Primary TB is represented with an initial nidus of infection and can, like its symptomatology range from nearly undetectable to full lobar consolidation. Secondary TB usually confines itself to the upper lobes and is not typically significant for lymphadenopathy or bronchiectasis. Cavitary lesions can also be a distinguishing factor, being relatively uncommon in primary TB present only in 10-30% of cases. Clinicians should always uphold a high suspicion for alternative diagnoses with faced with community acquired pneumonias that fail to respond appropriately to antibiotic therapy. One should have primary tuberculosis as a differential with significant incongruence between symptomatology and radiography. [Image Presented].
