N2  - Introduction: Empyema necessitans is a collection of inflammatory tissue extending from the pleural cavity into the thoracic wall, forming a mass in extrapleural soft tissues [1]. The most frequent etiology is mycobacterium tuberculosis [2]. Extra-pulmonary manifestations of mycobacterium tuberculosis are increasingly rare given its increased early detection and treatment. [4] We review a case of empyema necessitans in a young, immunocompetent patient with pulmonary tuberculosis. Case: A 23 year old male with no prior medical history presented with one month of productive cough and unintentional 10lb weight loss. 18 months prior the patient moved to the United States from Mexico, and worked on farms in Indiana and Nebraska. Vital signs on admission revealed tachycardia, tachypnea, and fever. Pulmonary exam demonstrated diffuse rales throughout all lung fields and a 4cm round, fluctuant mass on the left posterior chest wall. Admission labs were remarkable for a leukocytosis to 12, a microcytic anemia, and a total bilirubin of 2.0 (direct of 1.1). Chest X-ray revealed diffuse miliary infiltrates with consolidation of the left lower lobe. Subsequently, CT scan was performed (Figure 1). Sputum stained positive for AFB, and RIPE (Rifaximin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol) therapy was initiated resulting in rapid symptomatic improvement. Workup was negative for HIV and other immunodeficiencies. He remained in the hospital for one month on RIPE therapy due to persistently positive AFB in sputum cultures. Discussion: Early detection and antibiotic therapy of mycobacterium tuberculosis has dramatically reduced the incidence of extrapulmonary complications [4]. Empyema necessitans is an extremely rare complication of tuberculosis. Other causes of empyema necessitans includes Streptococcus pneumoniae, staphylcoccus species, polymicrobial infections, and gram negative bacilli [4]. The majority of cases of empyema necessitans present in immunocompromised patients [3]. The presented patient was evaluated for underlying immunodeficiencies, but none were found. Management of empyema necessitans from tuberculosis should be tailored to the patient, treated medically or paired with surgical drainage. Given rapid improvement after initiation of RIPE, it was decided to withhold further intervention. Drainage may be necessary if there is no further response to therapy. In summary, empyema necessitans is a rare complication of pulmonary tuberculosis. Most commonly seen in those with underlying immunodeficiencies. Management is medical or paired with surgical drainage. If managed without surgery, close follow up is necessary to monitor response and need for further intervention. [Image Presented].
