N2  - Background: Human Adenovirus (HAdV) is a non-enveloped, double stranded DNA virus with over 55 genotypes, organized into 7 subgroups labeled A-G1. HAdV infections are associated with self-limiting gastroenteritis and upper respiratory infections in children as well as infections in immunocompromised patients such as HIV patients and transplant recipients2,3. Cases of severe HAdV pneumonia in adults remain quite rare4. We present a case of severe HAdV pneumonia requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) in an adult on immunosuppressants. Case Presentation: A 25 year old female with a history of granulomatosis with polyangiitis on chronic methotrexate and prednisone presented with worsening cough and fever after failing empiric outpatient antibiotic therapy for community acquired pneumonia. Chest X-ray demonstrated right upper lobe opacification and a follow up CT chest later indicated worsening of bilateral opacifications. She had persistent hypoxemia and a PaO2/FiO2 ratio compatible with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), necessitating mechanical ventilation. Antibiotic coverage was broadened to intravenous vancomycin and meropenem with adjunctive intravenous corticosteroids. She developed worsening leukopenia and her chronic immunosuppressive therapy was reversed with leucovorin and adjunctive filgrastim. All testing, including bronchoalveolar lavage done during bronchoscopy, had been negative until a Respiratory Pathogen Array revealed HAdV. Despite aggressive supportive care with pressure-regulated volume controlled ventilation with an FiO2 of 100%, PEEP of 18, and left lateral decubitus positioning, the patient continued to clinically decline. She was placed on veno-venous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (VV-ECMO) for 63 days, subsequently decannulated, and made a full recovery. Discussion: HAdV pneumonia can be rapidly progressive and eventually fatal, making timely recognition and diagnosis crucial. Patients often present with high-grade fevers, cough, and dyspnea.1,2,5 Radiographically, viral pneumonias typically present with interstitial markings.5,6 Additional radiographic findings may include: ground-glass opacification, pleural effusions, and rapidly progressive bilateral interstitial infiltrates within 10 days of onset. Common serologic findings in patients with HAdV pneumonia include acute renal failure, progressive leukopenia, and elevated levels of creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and transaminases5-9. HAdV pneumonia should be considered in cases of severe community acquired pneumonia unresponsive to antibiotics. This would allow for timely initiation of antiviral therapy, as well as prevention of antibiotic and antifungal overuse. [Image Presented].
