N2  - Background: Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) can be found anywhere in the air, soil or water of the environment. NTM infections have seen an increase in prevalence among adults in the United States during the last decades. Studies have shown that patients with pre-existing non-cystic fibrosis (CF) bronchiectasis are highly susceptible to NTM pulmonary infections. However, further clinical and epidemiological data is limited. Methods: We conducted a retrospective, cross-sectional study at the Miami VA Healthcare System and the University of Illinois Medical Center to obtain epidemiological and clinical data from 2010 to 2015. International Classification of Diseases ninth revision (ICD-9) was used to establish pulmonary NTM (ICD-9: 031.0) and Bronchiectasis (ICD-9: 494.0). NTM pulmonary infection diagnosis was subsequently confirmed using the ATS microbiologic criteria of two positive sputum cultures and/or a positive bronchial wash or lavage culture. Radiological criteria was used to define bronchiectasis: Bronchial dilation, peri-bronchial wall thickening and cylindrical changes. Results: Two hundred seventeen patients diagnosed with NTM were enrolled in our study. Patients were separated into two groups: NTM with bronchiectasis (n=66, 30.4%) and NTM without bronchiectasis (n=151, 69.6%). Both groups were compared for clinical characteristics and underlying diseases. The presence of bronchiectasis was significantly higher in females (p= 0.002) and elderly patients (65 years or older) (p= 0.005). On the other hand, African American ethnicity and height equal or over 170cm was significantly higher in the non-bronchiectasis group (p= 0.04 and 0.02). Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) was the most common isolated NTM species in both groups (58.7% and 38.4%) however was found to be significantly higher in the bronchiectasis set (p=0.02). Mycobacterium abscessus was also found to be significantly higher in patients with bronchiectasis (27% vs. 13%, p=0.01), while Mycobacterium kansasii and fortuitum were significantly increased in those with non-bronchiectasis (p=0.03, p=0.04 respectively). COPD prevalence was higher in bronchiectasis subjects, however this was not significant (p= 0.93). New onset cough and sputum production was significantly higher in bronchiectasis group. Conclusions: NTM infections are common in bronchiectasis patients, particularly those 65 years or older. Gender variability was also noted, placing females at a higher risk. MAC and Mycobacterium abscessus are the most frequent NTM species isolated in bronchiectasis subjects. New onset cough stands as the most common presenting symptom. Furthermore, we highly encourage the routine screening for NTM in bronchiectasis patients. Additional studies should focus on understanding the role of NTM outcomes in bronchiectasis.[Graph Presented].
