N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium shimoidei (M. shimoidei) is a rare cause of pulmonary disease, with only 15 cases previously reported worldwide. In Queensland, Australia, all non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) isolates are notifiable, providing a unique platform to investigate the less common NTM species. Aim: This study aims to review the epidemiology, clinical characteristics, and outcomes of M. shimoidei isolates in Queensland, Australia. Methods: Retrospective case series of all M. shimoidei isolates in Queensland between 1 January 2000 and 31 December 2014. An additional review of the literature was undertaken. Results: Twenty-three patients in Queensland (16 male; mean age 66.2 ± 12.6) had a positive culture for M. shimoidei. Isolates predominantly occurred in southern and central Queensland. All cases were respiratory specimens with only four (17.4%) being smear-positive. Nine (39.1%) were considered clinically significant and seven (30.4%) possibly significant. Ten patients (43.5%) met the American Thoracic Society and Infectious Diseases Society of America (ATS/IDSA) criteria for NTM lung disease. The most common symptoms were cough (16; 69.6%); weight-loss (9; 39.1%); dyspnea (5; 21.7%); and fevers or sweats (4; 17.4%). Cavitary disease was evident in nine patients (39.1%); nodules in eight (34.8%); and consolidation in two (8.7%). The most common co-morbidities were obstructive airways disease (10; 43.5%) and bronchiectasis (6; 26.1%). Six patients (26.0%) received medical therapy; seven (30.4%) were actively observed; and ten (43.4%) received no treatment or follow up. Ten patients (43.4%) improved or remained stable; five (21.7%) died; and eight (34.7%) had an unknown outcome. Conclusion: This is the largest cases series of M. shimoidei and highlights the clinical significance and pathogenicity of this species. Increased recognition and improved understanding of M. shimoidei is warranted.
