N2  - Introduction and rationale of the study Data on clinical infections due to nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) and other pathogens in aetiology of tuberculosis (TB)-like symptoms in many resource-poor settings remains unknown. Passive case finding, use of less sensitive smear sputum microscopy and overburdened with diseases like malaria, tuberculosis and HIV/AIDS contribute to both unrealistic and mismanagement of TB cases in such settings. The rationale of this study was to determine the role of NTM and other bacterial pathogens in aetiology of TB-like syndromes amongst TB suspects in Northeastern, Tanzania. Methodology The present study was a cross-sectional study. 372 TB suspects with symptoms suggestive of TB attending to primary health care facilities were recruited through November 2012 to January 2013. Three sputum samples were collected for culture on BacT/Alert 3D system, Löwenstein-Jensen, Gottsacker and Coletsos media. Identification of Mycobacterium tuberculosis and NTM were done using GenoType® MTBC and GenoType®CM/AS respectively. Identification of other bacterial pathogens from TB and or NTM negative patients was done by conventional and 16SrRNA sequencing for Nocardia isolates. Determination of Staphylococcus. aureus (S. aureus) clonal complexes by using microarray method. Results Overall, 80/372 (21.5%) patients were diagnosed with TB, 36/372 (9.7%) had NTM of diverse species as shown in Table 1. 23/372 (6.2%) of the patiets were diagnosed with S. aureus, of these two isolates belong to clonal complex CC88-MRSA-IV for methicillin-resistant (MRSA). From 20/372 (5.4%) patients different Nocardia isolates were isolated including, N. cyriacigeorgica (n= 5, 25%), N. farcinica (n= 4, 20%), N. asteroides (n=3, 15%), N. falorosea and N. brevicatena (n= 2, 10%) each, N. nova and N. carnea (n= 1, 5%) each. Four patients had NTM/TB co-infection and seven had S. aureus/TB co-infection. 11/36 (30.6%) patients with NTM isolates were HIV seropositive. Conclusion Inefficient screening of TB patients which is largely based on the use of chronic cough for 2 weeks as a major criteria for suspected TB case may result to missing of other bacterial causes of lung infection. A prevalent increase of NTM may contribute to both unrealistic and mismanagement of TB cases in Tanzania. A diverse array of other bacterial pathogens like Nocardia species, and S. aureus which is associated with both community and hospital acquired pneumonia suggest that they may likely be the cause of TB-like syndrome in this population. Therefore, need to integrate NTM and other bacterial pathogens causing TBlike syndromes in diagnosis and management of TB is urgent.
