N2  - Background: Our primary objective was to describe the plasma and sputum concentration-time profiles and to characterize variability (including identification of relevant covariates) in the PK of Ciprofloxacin DPI 32.5mg b.i.d. in the plasma and sputum of NCFB patients. A secondary objective was to explore lung exposure (sputum concentrations of ciprofloxacin). Methods: A total of 1764 plasma samples obtained from 95 individuals (healthy subjects [N=17], COPD patients [N=39], NCFB patients [N=39]), after administration of ciprofloxacin 32.5mg q.d., b.i.d. or t.i.d., or 48.75mg b.i.d., were analyzed using population-PK methods. A two-compartment disposition and first-order elimination model including two routes of absorption were used to describe plasma kinetics; 84% of the absorbed dose was estimated to enter the central compartment via the slow absorption route. Intrinsic and extrinsic factors that potentially impacted PK were tested. Results: The model predicted a median peak steady-state plasma drug concentration (Cmaxss) of 0.12mg/L and an area under the curve at steady state (AUCss) of 0.78mg h/L between 0 and 12 hours post-dose for NCFB patients treated with Ciprofloxacin DPI 32.5mg b.i.d. Lean body mass, age, sex and FEV1 did not significantly impact PK parameters. Renal function had a statistically significant influence on plasma PK (predicted increase: 81% [AUC]; 51% [Cmax] in patients with severe renal impairment), however this was not considered clinically relevant due to the very low exposure. Clearance corrected for oral bioavailability decreased by 29% over the first few days of treatment. This may reflect a temporal change in bioavailability (systemic ciprofloxacin clearance is known not to change over time), but is not considered clinically relevant. A large proportion of the observed variability in plasma exposure remained unexplained. Sputum concentrations up to 2200mg/L (median 123mg/L, N=94) were measured. Unfortunately a relation between systemic exposure and concentrations in sputum could not be established. This was due to high variability of drug levels in sputum, which exceeded the solubility of ciprofloxacin. It was not possible to differentiate how much the variability was due to saturated total drug concentrations; drug bound to lung matter (proteins, bacterial debris, etc.); and/or undissolved particles that reside in the respiratory tract unless eliminated via (patho-)physiological pathways (expectoration, mucociliary clearance). Summary/conclusion: Low systemic drug exposure combined with very high, saturated lung concentrations, was realized with Ciprofloxacin DPI. This demonstrates that Ciprofloxacin DPI can be used effectively in NCFB patients without dosing adjustments for special populations, such as those with renal impairment.
