N2  - Rationale Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an inherited genetic condition resulting in a higher frequency of airway infections, causing irreversible damage to the lung and eventual respiratory failure. Increasing evidence suggests that CF neutrophils possess an intrinsic and pathological inflammatory defect. Hydrogen voltage-gated channel1 (HVCN1) is a transmembrane proton channel that regulates pH changes across the cell membrane. HVCN1 knockout murine studies have demonstrated increased neutrophil phagosomal pH, significantly higher primary granule degranulation and reduced neutrophil mediated bacterial killing. Due to the increased primary granule degranulation observed in CF neutrophils we hypothesised that CF cells may have reduced abundance of HVCN1. The aim of this work was to investigate whether HVCN1 levels were altered on the surface of CF neutrophils resulting in dysregulated pH and impaired phagosomal killing. Methods Ethical approval was obtained from Beaumont Hospital Dublin. Patients with CF homozygous for the F508 mutation or heterozygous for the G551D mutation receiving ivacaftor were recruited to this study. Neutrophil phagosomes were isolated post phagocytosis of IgG opsonised latex particles, followed by nitrogen cavitation and sucrose gradient ultracentrifugation. Western blot and FACs analysis for HVCN1 was performed on isolated CF F508, G551D, non-CF bronchiectasis (NCFB) and healthy control (HC) neutrophils. Altered phagosomal pH was assessed post phagocytosis of microbes labelled with the pH fluorescence probe SNARF1. Statistical analysis was carried out using GraphPad Prism,5. Results Protein abundance of HVCN1 was significantly reduced in whole cell lysates of CF F508 neutrophils compared to HC (p<0.05) and NCFB (p<0.05) (n=5). HVCN1 abundance on neutrophil membranes was significantly decreased in CF F508 neutrophils at rest and post stimulation (TNF-alpha/IL-8) compared to HC (p<0.05) and NCFB (p<0.001) (n=4). The CF F508 phagosomal pH demonstrated a significantly higher pH of 7.65 when compared to control phagosomal pH of 7.54 (p<0.05, n=5). The inhibition of HC neutrophil HVCN1 with ZnCl2 significantly increased primary granule degranulation compared to untreated cells. In concurrence significantly higher levels of the primary granule marker myeloperoxidase (MPO) (p<0.05) was observed in CF F508 phagosomes compared to HC (n=13). The abundance of HVCN1 protein both intracellularly (p<0.05) and on the neutrophil membrane (p<0.001) was restored to HC levels in G551D patients receiving ivacaftor. Similarly, intraphagosomal primary degranulation was significantly reduced in G551D patients receiving ivacaftor therapy. Conclusion Reduced abundance of HVCN1 in CF neutrophils generates an alkaline phagosomal pH, unphysiologically high for adequate MPO mediated oxidant production, ultimately leading to impaired bacterial killing in CF.
