N2  - Background Legionella species are ubiquitous in the environment. Infection from the inhalation of aerosols of the organism can cause Legionnaires' disease (LD), an atypical, severe pneumonia in individuals with underlying risk. Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 (Lp1) has been reported to account for more than 90% of all clinical isolates related to LD, but recent studies have indicated that the landscape of Legionella infection is more complex than previously thought. Additionally, Legionella is a slow growing organism with culture considered the diagnostic “gold standard”, with cultures routinely maintained for up to 10 days. While whole genome sequencing for the study of Legionella isolates has been investigated, culture-independent metagenomic methodologies may provide more discriminatory typing information in a routine diagnostic service and outbreak investigations. Methods To test for the sensitivity of detection of Legionella, LP Philadelphia strain was mixed with human DNA in 13 different tests at proportions across a range from increasing percentages of human DNA to decreasing percentages of LP DNA. An additional 9 artificial communities were prepared composed of the three Legionella strains, human DNA and 3 other bacterial species (Haemophilus influenzae, Veillonella dispar and Streptococcus pneumoniae). Illumina MiSeq was used for sequencing. After trimming and quality assessment, alignment and taxonomic classification of the metagenomic data was carried out using the k-SLAM program. Results The output or percentage of sequencing reads mapped to LP Philadelphia-1 and other microbes consistently at the input proportions. However in the case of sensitivity tests composed primarily of human DNA and spiked with small proportions of LP, the percentage of output or sequencing reads decreased as the sample became more dilute. This can be expected when microbes are present in low abundance. In these preliminary steps, LP Philadelphia-1 was detected when present at as few as 27 genome copies. Conclusion Metagenomic detection of Legionella was sensitive to low numbers of genomes present the mock communities and able to identify this organism against a background of human and mixed microbial DNA. We are taking the methodology forward in order to attempt to sequence Legionella directly from clinical and environmental samples and to compare this to current gold standard methods. The authors are affiliated with the National Institute for Health Research Health Protection Research Unit (NIHR HPRU) in Respiratory Infections at Imperial College London in partnership with Public Health England (PHE).
