N2  - Introduction: Anthracosis is the deposition of carbon-based particles in the airway due to a chronic environmental exposure. It can result in endobronchial and parenchymal changes, and more rarely, mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Case: We describe the case of a 76 year old, non-smoking Honduran female with history of diabetes and hypertension who was referred to our pulmonary clinic for years of persistent dry cough and chest discomfort. She had previously had a cardiac evaluation that was negative. She was treated with antibiotics by her primary care physician, but that did not improve her symptoms. She did not use an ACE inhibitor and denied symptoms of GERD, allergic rhinitis, and she did not have a history of asthma. At the time, she denied exposure to pollutants, pets, and other environmental insults. Her lung exam was positive for wheezing heard over the right mid-posterior chest. Pulmonary function testing was within normal limits. A non-contrast CT scan of the chest demonstrated multiple subcentimeter nodules, right middle lobe and left lingular scarring with bibasilar atelectasis and mild interseptal lobular thickening. A CT scan with contrast followed which additionally demonstrated multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes. A systemic condition such as sarcoidosis was suspected, and in fact, between her clinic follow-up visits, she had an episode of significant wheezing and cough that was treated with systemic corticosteroids at an urgent care with good effect. She underwent a bronchoscopy which demonstrated diffuse hyperpigmentation of the airways, and endobronchial ultrasound demonstrated prominent lymphadenopathy that were biopsied at multiple lymph stations. Endobronchial biopsy and transbronchial needle aspirates indicated pulmonary and mediastinal anthracosis. Acid-fast-stain was negative. After the procedure, the patient admitted to exclusive cooking over a wood flame for more than 40 years while in Honduras. She was treated with bronchodilators and will continue to follow-up in clinic. Discussion: Pulmonary anthracosis is a well-described disease with multiple possible clinical manifestations, but most often cough, dyspnea, and wheezing. It bears an association with Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection, though not in this particular case. Imaging with CT often allows for differentiation from lung cancer, as the mediastinal adenopathy is often calcified, which is not present here. There is no reliable treatment, though patients do often respond well to systemic steroids. This case ably demonstrates the importance of a detailed history and physical, with special emphasis on the oft undervalued social history. (Figure Presnted).
