N2  - Rationale: Among the 35 million individuals living with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), pulmonary complications are the most frequent cause of morbidity and mortality. Although bacterial and fungal pathogens are well described as etiologies of lung disease, the role of respiratory viruses remains poorly understood though emerging evidence suggests they play a significant role. We sought to describe the burden of respiratory viral illness in HIV-infected inpatients admitted to our tertiary care center. Methods: All HIV-infected inpatients from August 2015 to August 2016 were approached if they presented with respiratory symptoms, defined as cough, dyspnea, sore throat, rhinorrhea, wheezing, or stridor. Twenty-nine patients were enrolled. After obtaining informed consent, nasopharyngeal swabs and blood were collected. If the subject underwent bronchoscopy per the treating physician, excess bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) sample was collected. Demographic and clinical data were recorded for each subject. Multiplex PCR testing of all respiratory samples was performed which identifies 11 respiratory viruses - adenovirus, influenza A/B, metapneumovirus, parainfluenza 1-4, respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus and coronavirus. Results: Of 29 HIV-infected patients enrolled, 10 (34%) tested positive for respiratory viruses. Of these, four (40%) were positive for rhinovirus, and one (10%) each tested positive for adenovirus, coronavirus, parainfluenza 3, parainfluenza 4, influenza A and influenza B. Five of ten patients (50%) had CD4 counts greater than 200 and three (30%) had detectable viral loads. The majority (8/10; 80%) were on anti-retroviral therapy. Of the ten patients, three (30%) had coexisting bacterial pneumonia and three (30%) had ARDS. All ten patients (100%) had chest x-ray findings consistent with lower respiratory tract disease. Six (60%) had severe illness requiring intensive care - three (30%) required mechanical ventilation and two (20%) needed vasopressor administration. Ultimately, three patients (30%) required discharge to a higher level of care and one patient (10%) died during hospitalization. Conclusion: The role of respiratory viruses on the lung health of HIV infected patients is poorly defined compared to bacterial and fungal infections. In this study, respiratory viruses were identified in over a third of HIV-infected inpatients, representing a substantial disease burden. Moreover, these patients demonstrated significant disease severity, as the majority required intensive care and a third of patients were discharged to a higher level of care. Given these findings, there is a need for future studies of viral infections in HIV-infected individuals to elucidate mechanisms of susceptibility to reduce the burden of pulmonary morbidity in this vulnerable population.
