N2  - Rationale: The number of colony forming counts (CFU) of M. tuberculosis cultured in cough-generated aerosols is a novel quantitative method to study tuberculosis (TB) transmission. In initial studies in Uganda, household contacts of pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) cases with high (≥10 CFU) aerosols had a 6.9-fold increase in risk of tuberculin skin test (TST) conversion compared to contacts of low-aerosol (<10 CFU) and aerosol-negative patients. Further, aerosol CFU showed a strong dose-response relationship with TST size and interferon gamma release assay (IGRA) readouts in exposed household contacts. These qualitative and quantitative differences in TST/IGRA responses were not observed when using sputum acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear microscopy to stratify the exposure. However, the reproducibility of these findings in other settings is currently unknown. Objective: To evaluate the predictive value of M. tuberculosis cough aerosols on prevalent and incident TB infection in household contacts of pulmonary TB patients in an intermediate TB prevalence setting. Methods: Adult (≥18 years) sputum smear- and culture-positive pulmonary TB cases underwent a standard evaluation and we cultured M. tuberculosis from cough-generated aerosols. We evaluated eligible household contacts for prevalent and incident infection (8 weeks post-enrollment) with TST and IGRA. We analyzed rates of prevalent and incident infection in contacts using generalized estimating equations. Main results and measurement: 48 index TB cases and 230 contacts were enrolled. 29 (60%) TB cases were aerosol-positive, of which 16 (33%) were high aerosol producers. The proportion of contacts with a TST ≥10 mm was 75% for high aerosol, 65% for low aerosol, and 59% for no aerosol producers (p= 0.34). IGRA readouts at 8 weeks were significantly higher in contacts of high aerosol cases (median 4.6 IU/mL, IQR 0.02-10) when compared to those with low (median 0.8, 0.2-10) or no aerosol (median 0.1, 0-3.7; p = 0.01). Of 76 (23%) contacts with TST <10mm at baseline, 16 (7%) had TST conversion but we had limited power to detect differences between aerosol groups (p=0.52). Among the 16 TST converters IGRA readouts were significantly higher for contacts exposed to high aerosols (median 20 IU/mL, IQR 10-24), compared to contacts of aerosol-negative TB cases (median 0.13, 0.04-3; p =o.o1). Conclusion: Cough aerosols of M. tuberculosis are a more precise predictor of infection outcomes than sputum AFB smear microscopy. The reproducibility of our results further strengthens the argument of using cough aerosols to guide targeted preventive treatment strategies, a necessary component of current TB elimination projections.
