N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium szulgai is a rarely reported nontuberculous mycobateria (NTM). Its presence rarely represents disease, but in immunocompromised hosts or patients with underlying lung disease, it may evolve with symptomatic infection. If clinically suspected and isolated in sputum samples, treatment is recommended with triple therapy following sensitivity test. Case Report 55-year old male with interstitial lung disease secondary to Giant Cell Interstitial Pneumonitis (GIP) and gastroesophageal reflux, with no previous history of smoking or clinical relevant history of exposures, presented with progressive, 6-month productive cough and night sweats, with no fever or loss of weight. Clinical examination showed no significant alterations. Tuberculosis was suspected and acid-fast bacilli test (AFB) and culture of sputum was collected. AFB was positive, and culture showed growth of Mycobacterium szulgai. Sensitivity test was performed and the patient was promptly started triple therapy with Rifampicin, Ethambutol and Clarithromycin, with routine follow-up blood exams. Over the course of treatment , patient's symptoms improved significantly with no adverse events. Discussion NTM have been increasingly recognized as pathogens capable of causing pulmonary and extrapulmonary infections in both immunocompetent and immunocompromised patients, especially in patients with lung disease M szulgai disease symptoms may be indistinguishable from classic tuberculosis, but can admit unusual presentations in the context of underlying lung disease. NTM usually manifests with indolent symptoms, with long-term cough, weight loss and fever. M. szulgai is characterized by large in vitro susceptibility to most standard antituberculosis drugs, with isoniazid, rifampin and ethambutol being the most frequently tested. No standard recommendation for treatment of M szulgai currently exists, but in general, triple therapies are preferred, and longer treatment are usually needed.
