N2  - Rationale: In COPD subjects, poor dental health correlates with severity of disease assessed by FEV1, and it has been shown that dental care can decrease COPD exacerbations. Limited data exist regarding how dental health impacts the daily burden of symptoms COPD patients experience. This study is a prospective evaluation of how certain dental health indices are correlated with daily respiratory symptoms in COPD subjects. Methods: Subjects greater than 40 years old with stage 3 or 4 COPD and at least a 10 pack year smoking history were included into the study. Exclusion criteria included exacerbation within the last 4 weeks requiring steroids or antibiotics, or edentulous subjects. Healthy controls had no lung disease and were not currently smoking. At the initial visit, a dental exam was performed which included counting number of teeth and calculating the plaque index (PI). A questionnaire called the Oral Health Impact Profile (OHIP) was completed. COPD subjects documented symptoms of breathlessness (BORG), cough, sputum, and wheeze in a daily electronic COPD journal. Healthy controls did not keep a symptom journal. Correlations with dental indices and daily COPD symptoms were drawn with linear regression. COPD subjects were compared to healthy controls with t-test. Results: 14 COPD subjects were followed for of 57.9 (± 22.1) days for a total of 800 patient days. There were 6 healthy controls. Compared to controls, COPD subjects on average had less teeth (16.8 vs 28.3, p = 0.001) and worse OHIP scores (6.9 vs 1.8, p = 0.04), indicative of worse overall dental health. There was no difference in PI. Regarding symptoms in the COPD group, number of teeth positively correlated with percent days with cough (β= 3.22, R2 = 0.34, p = 0.03), and wheeze (β= 2.75, R2 = 0.24, p = 0.08). There was a positive correlation with number of teeth and BORG score (β= 0.135, R2 = 0.18, p = 0.13) as well as percent days with sputum (β= 2.25, R2 = 0.17, p = 0.14), but this did not reach statistical significance. There was no correlation between the OHIP or PI and daily symptoms. Conclusion: Compared to controls, COPD subjects have poor dental health. In our COPD cohort, a greater number of teeth is associated with increased daily respiratory symptoms, specifically cough and wheeze. In the setting of poor dentition, it's possible that more teeth may harbor more bacteria and inflammation leading to worse daily symptoms.
