N2  - Necrotizing sarcoid granulomatosis (NSG) is a rare systemic disease characterized by sarcoid-like granulomas, granulomatous pulmonary vasculitis and varying degrees of necrosis. We report a case of a woman who developed bilateral necrotizing granulomatous pulmonary masses and nodules with uveitis. A 35 year old female presented to a local hospital for 3 months of progressive dyspnea, dry cough and exertional pre-syncope. History was notable for 15-pack year smoking history and latent tuberculosis two years prior diagnosed by QuantiFERON testing for which she completed 6 months of isoniazid treatment. She also had a history of posterior uveitis five months prior to presentation when she presented with vision loss treated with vitrectomy and intravitreal corticosteroids. CT scan of the chest revealed multiple bilateral lung masses, the largest of which was 5.3 x 3.7cm, and scattered nodules [Figure]. Fine needle aspiration of mediastinal lymph nodes, and endobronchial brushings and biopsies of the left lower lobe (LLL) were non-diagnostic. Bronchoalveolar lavage was inflammatory (41% each neutrophils and lymphocytes). Percutaneous biopsy of the LLL mass was also non-diagnostic. Cultures for bacteria, acid-fast bacteria (AFB) and fungal organisms were negative. ANCA levels, anti-PR3 antibodies, and anti-MPO antibodies were undetectable as were antibodies against HIV and common fungal antigens. Wedge resection of the LLL mass showed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and granulomatous vasculitis with infarct-like necrosis. No hyperchromatic giant cells were identified nor was evidence of capillaritis. No microorganisms were identified on AFB or fungal stains and PCR for tuberculosis was negative. Flow cytometry was negative for monoclonal B-cell population. She had no signs of systemic vasculitis, synovitis or evolving indolent infection over the course of her workup leading to a presumptive diagnosis of NSG. It is uncertain whether NSG is a distinct entity or variant of sarcoidosis or pulmonary vasculitis; the incidence and prevalence is likewise unknown. As a diagnosis of exclusion, it is important to rule out other causes of necrotizing granulomatous inflammation, namely indolent infections (particularly tuberculosis) and pulmonary vasculitides. Whereas previous cases have demonstrated an association of NSG with pulmonary nodules and uveitis, to the best of our knowledge, multiple bilateral masses have not been reported. Our case adds to the small but growing body of literature reinforcing the need to consider NSG in the differential of patients with pulmonary nodules and masses, particularly in those with extrapulmonary manifestations of disease.
