N2  - Rationale Although cough is one of the most common symptoms of lung tumor, the frequency of lung tumor as a cause of chronic cough was less than 2%. Among metastatic lung tumor, pulmonary lymphangitis cartinomatosa presenting with cough as a primary symptom were often misdiagnosed and treated as a military tuberculosis or sarcoidosis, especially when they are young with the age between twenties and forties. Case A 36-year old man who had no smoking history referred by two months of chronic cough, which exacerbate at night. He had a medical examination at a local hospital and prescribed ICS/LABA and leukotriene antagonistas cough variant asthma. Though it haven't improved, he was referred to Nagoya City Hospital. He complained of cough at the first visit. However, no symptoms such as fever elevation, body weight, fatigue, anoxia, abdominal pain, obstipation, diarrhea, melena and extra pulmonary were presented. Chest X-ray appeared enhanced vascular shadow and permeability depression in valenceregion of lung. Chest CT appeared diffuse nodular shadow and thickening bronchus blood vessel in whole pulmonary area. Also it shows swelling of lymph node at median space. Although serum angiotensin converting enzyme, cold agglutination reaction,and Mycobacterium tuberculosis-specific interferon-γ release test showed normal range or negative, elevated serum levels of CEA and CA19-9 were observed on blood examination. We thought histological examination is necessary. Transbronchial biopsy was performed on bronchoscopy, resulting well to moderate differentiated adenocarcinoma. Immunohistochemical analysis showed the positive staining of CD20 and CDX2, and the negative staining of CD7, napsin A, TTF-1. As for the pathologic diagnosis, a cancer of gastrointestinal tract origin was suggested. On positron emission tomography examination, increased 18F-FDG uptake in intraperitoneal lymph nodes, mediastinal lymph nodes, and rectum was observed. After performing colonoscopy for a definitive diagnosis, primary site was found in rectum. The biopsy specimens from rectum tumor showed the same as by those from lung including immunohistochemical analysis. Therefore we diagnosed lymphangitis cartinoma owing to rectum carcinoma, with a time of 14 days from the first visit. Conclusion We experienced a rare case of pulmonary lymphangitis cartinomatosa owing to colorectal cancer presented solely with chronic cough.
