N2  - Background: Lactobacillus is commonly used as a probiotic agent. There are isolated reports of systemic infection due to lactobacilli. We present a patient who developed severe lactobacilli pneumonia while receiving lactobacilli supplementation. Case Report: Our patient is a 69 year old man with stage II adenocarcinoma of lung treated with concurrent chemo and radiation therapy 7 months prior to admission. Patient has history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), coronary artery disease (CAD), and type II diabetes. He was admitted to the hospital with acute COPD exacerbation and suspected pulmonary embolism treated with systemic steroids, ceftriaxone and levofloxacin and anticoagulation, after failing an outpatient treatment with amoxicillin-clavulanic acid and prednisone. Initial sputum culture isolated coagulase negative Staphylococcus and Candida albicans. During the hospital stay, he developed severe clostridium difficle colitis and was treated with oral vancomycin and IV metronidazole. He initially improved, but on 16 th day of his hospital admission, the he developed nausea, vomiting, fever, dypnea, cough, purulent sputum, lethargy and worsening hypoxemia with new bilateral lung infiltrates. He was started on intravenous vancomycin, imipenem and non-invasive positive pressure ventilation. A repeat sputum sample isolated lactobacilli species with no normal flora. Patient was treated with vancomycin and imipenem for a total of 10 days with clinical and radiological improvement. Discussion: We believe that his pneumonia was a consequence of aspiration of lactobacillus supplementation. He may have acquired it through micro-aspiration or through aspiration of gastrointestinal contents during an episode of vomiting. Lactobacillus is a well-known commensal, but it can occasionally cause systemic infections. Therefore, when isolated from sterile sites, it cannot be dismissed as a contaminant. The majority of lactobacilli infections have been reported in immunocompromised patients. Predisposing factors include hematological malignancy, diabetes mellitus, post organ transplant and use of broad spectrum antibiotics. Lactobacillus related systemic infections include bacteremia, endocarditis and localized infections. Lactobacillus pneumonia has a mortality rate as high as 30% but is confounded by associated comorbidities. The most common species are L. casei and L. rhamnosus since these are the most common ingredients in the probiotics. Most isolates are sensitive to ciprofloxacin, erythromycin and clindamycin with resistance found mostly to ampicillin, cefazolin and vancomycin. Our case illustrates a potential for serious systemic infection by lactobacilli used as a probiotic agent.
