N2  - Introduction Respiratory viral infections are important causes of morbidity and mortality in hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT) recipients. Several viruses, including respiratory syncytial virus, adenovirus, influenza, and parainfluenza have been extensively studied, however, information regarding human metapneumovirus (hMPV) in HSCT recipients is limited. HMPV can cause protracted and severe infections in immunocompromised hosts, particularly in solid organ and HSCT recipients. Incidence of hMPV in HSCT recipients has been reported to be 3-7%, with mortality up to 39%. However, risk factors, clinical course and treatment of hMPV are not well described. We undertook this study to better define these factors. Methods Respiratory viral panels from adult HSCT recipients between January 2014 and July 2016 were reviewed to identify cases of hMPV. For these cases, demographics, clinical features, risk factors, biomarkers, treatment, and outcome were examined. Results We identified 16 patients with positive hMPV isolated from nasopharyngeal or bronchoalveolar lavage samples. Median age of our cohort was 60.5 years (range 24-74 years) and male to female ratio was 2.2. Acute myeloid leukemia was the most common underlying hematological diagnosis (37.5%). Majority of our patients received allogenic peripheral blood stem cell transplants (75%) with related donors (50%). 8 of 16 patients had established cGVHD at the time of hMPV infection. Concurrent fungal and other viral infections were seen in 3 and 8 patients, respectively. Median day of detection of hMPV after transplant was 744.5 days (range 125- 1545 days). Majority of the infections occurred in spring (56.2%) or winter (43.7%). All patients initially presented with cough, usually non productive (68.7%). Abnormal imaging was seen in only 56.2% with findings ranging from diffuse airspace disease to pleural effusions. Potential risk factors examined were recent immunosupression (50%), presence of lymphopenia and neutropenia (37.5% and 12.5% respectively), reduced IgG levels (25%) and presence of pre-existing lung disease (37.5%). Treatment was mainly supportive. Two patients received IVIG and another two received ribavirin. Only 5 of 16 patients required hospitalization (range 4- 16 days), with all requiring supplemental oxygen. One patient required mechanical ventilation and ultimately succumbed due to respiratory failure. Complications seen were persistent infection, secondary bacterial infection, and hypoxic respiratory failure. Conclusion We observed that hMPV is uncommon in our patient cohort. Although most patients did not require hospitalization, hMPV can potentially cause severe life-threatening pneumonia in HSCT recipients. Co-infections are common. Severe disease was observed in patients with co-existing lung disease, immunosupression, and lymphopenia.
