N2  - RATIONALE: Pneumonia and influenza are among the top 10 causes of mortality in US children >1 year old. Particulate matter (PM) exposure increases risk of emergency department visits and hospitalizations for lower respiratory tract infection. Particles with aerodynamic diameter less than 2.5 μm (PM2.5) are of greatest concern. We assessed whether exposure to PM2.5 in children hospitalized with community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) was greater among children living in high-risk versus low-risk areas for CAP, in the Memphis Metropolitan area. METHODS: Children <18 years hospitalized with CAP were enrolled in the Etiology of Pneumonia in the Community (EPIC) study in Memphis, TN from 2010-2012; the Memphis hospital has 86% of the market share for pediatric CAP. PM2.5 levels in the study area from 2010-2012 were obtained from published high-resolution surface 1 km by 1 km satellite-derived PM2.5 data. Residential addresses for CAP cases were geocoded and quality checked using Geographical Information System (GIS). GIS and spatial cluster “hotspot” analysis with Turnbull's method was used to determine high- and low-risk areas for CAP for the study population at census-tract level after adjusting for the population at risk, and small numbers problem using a powerful Emprical Bayesian Filter (Figure 1). Logistic regression was used to assess if 1 km by 1 km PM2.5 exposure was associated with residence in high- versus low-risk CAP areas while controlling for age, sex, race/ethnicity, insurance type (public, private, other), tobacco smoke exposure, asthma, and pathogens detected (bacterial, viral, or codetection). RESULTS: Two hundred twenty of 810 (27.2%) patients were identified to be from high- or low-risk CAP areas; 126 (57.3%) were from high-risk CAP and 94 (42.7%) from low-risk CAP areas. Higher PM2.5 exposure [adjusted odds ratio (aOR) = 2.4, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.3-4.5) was associated with living in a high-risk area for CAP; while private insurance (aOR 0.1, CI 0.0-0.3) and bacterial detection (aOR 0.2, CI 0.1-0.5) were associated with decreased risk of living in a high-risk area for CAP. CONCLUSION: In our preliminary analysis, PM2.5 exposure, an indicator of air pollution, was associated with residence in a high-risk area for CAP. In addition, children with private insurance and bacterial etiology of infection were associated with decreased risk of residence in a high-risk area for CAP.
