N2  - Introduction: Thoracic Actinomycosis may range from subclinical illness to a progressively fatal disease. It is a relatively rare disease caused by A. israelii, which is a normal oral bacterium and usually found more in patients with poor oral hygiene. Clinical presentation mimics most chronic infectious pulmonary diseases such as tuberculosis, nocardiosis, histoplasmosis and may also mimic bronchogenic carcinoma. We are presenting a case of endobronchial actinomycosis plug with post obstructive changes. Case Report: A 45 year-old-man smoker with a past medical history of diabetes, asthma, COPD, severe obstructive sleep apnea and GERD presented with complaints of chronic productive cough and left sided chest pain secondary to remote unhealed rib fractures. A CT of the chest was performed and demonstrated multiple unhealed ribs fractures on the left with an incidental finding of post obstructive dilated right lower lobe bronchi filled with soft tissue density. Based on the CT chest findings a bronchoscopy performed to rule out an endobronchial lesion on the right lower lobe. The airway exam showed excessive mucopurulent secretions at the right lower lobe. Following secrestion suctioning a foreign body was revealed and removed from the posterior segment of the right lower lobe using a flexible forceps biopsy device (figure). Histologic analysis of the foreign body was performed and Gram and Gomori Methenamine Silver (GMS) special stains were applied, which demonstrated numerous Gram-positive filamentous organisms. The cytomorphologic features and special stain profile, paired with the bronchoscopic findings and culture analysis, were most consistent with the diagnosis of an Actinomycosis endobronchial plug. Discussion: Thoracic Actinomycosis diagnosed by microbiological cultures in only 50% of patients due to improper culture environment or overgrowth of other contaminating bacteria. In most published data the diagnosis of thoracic Actinomycosis was made histologically. The A. israelii is most commonly seen as a saprophyte inhabiting the tonsillar crypts but may also be pathogenic. Pulmonary lesions caused by actinomyces are more frequently associated with secondary or complicating infections of previous damaged lung tissue. If the organism is observed in a bronchial brushing or aspirate, its role as a pathogen is secure. Penicillin is the treatment of choice. The treatment course starts with I.V. Penicillin G for 2-3 weeks followed by oral until the lesions disappear. Surgical intervention is rarely needed with the exception of patients whom present with hemoptysis or empyema.
