N2  - Nocardia infection is a rare disorder caused by bacteria, which tends to affect the lung, brain, and skin. Pulmonary nocardiosis can present as a subacute or chronic pneumonia. Nocardia infections are rare among normal population and typically develop in immunocompromised patients. Pulmonary nocardiosis is difficult to be diagnosed and is often mistaken for other lung diseases. We report a case of pulmonary nocardiosis in a 77-year-old patient without a definable predisposing condition. A 77-year-old smoker with past medical of DM-2, myocarditis resulting in non-ischemic cardiomyopathy with EF 20%, CAD for which he was undergoing evaluation for CABG and multiple pulmonary nodules stable since 2014. Who presented to ER for evaluation of acute left side chest pain that began a day prior while the patient was taking a shower. Upon arrival to ER, nitroglycerin was administered without significant relief in symptoms. The chest pain was described as different from his usual angina chest pain, on this occasion was worsened by deep inspiration and cough. CXR (Fig.1) reveal a rounded opacity at the left upper lobe not seen on a CXR performed 4 weeks prior as part as part of the pre-operative work up. Chest CT (Fig. 2) for further characterization confirmed the presence of a large soft tissue rounded mass like density at the left upper lobe with surrounding ground glass halo. PET CT (Fig.3) was remarkable for a mild hypermetabolic sub-solid “halo sign” lesion on the left lung with central necrosis. CT guided Biopsy showed few aggregates of filamentous beaded like bacteria suspicious of Nocardia, which was confirmed by cultures. Abx therapy was provided with IV trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole with plan to transition to PO and IV meropenem. After completing 6 weeks of therapy patient underwent CABG successfully with complete revascularization. Eventually patient was switch to Minocycline due to hyperkalemia suspected to be secondary to TMP/SMX. Follow up CT (Fig. 4) 8 weeks after initiation of Abx therapy showed decreased size of mass like consolidation at the left upper lobe. This case highlights that pulmonary nocardiosis should be keep in mind in immunocompetent patients in a community setting. Nocardiosis is an infrequent infection which diagnosis is often delayed, high level of suspicion is, thus, required especially when patient fail to respond to abx therapy. As it is difficult to diagnose interdisciplinary approach is often required including microbiologists to include specific stains and cultures to investigate the presence of Nocardia spp.
