N2  - We report a case of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) Bejing strain laryngeal tuberculosis (TB) occurring in a prison inmate with limited English, leading to an outbreak resulting in 27 further active TB cases to date, highlighting significant deficiencies in the Irish TB Control Program. A 43 y.o. male smoker of Malaysian origin presented with productive cough and hoarseness for four months. Chest X-ray demonstrated hilar adenopathy, left upper lobe infiltrates, and biapical scarring suggestive of prior TB. Sputum was smear and culture positive for Mtb. HIV serology was negative. CT Thorax demonstrated bilateral thickening of the margin of the epiglottis, suggesting laryngeal TB. Endoscopic biopsy confirmed diffuse laryngeal infiltrates. Genotyping indicated pansensitive Mtb Bejing strain. Nine months of standard anti-tuberculous therapy was completed with no complications. At the time of presentation, the patient was an inmate in a correctional facility serving a 6 year sentence. Presentation was delayed due to very limited English, with a four month history of productive cough prior to diagnosis. During this four month period, the patient was housed in a shared cell with varying inmates, most of whom were foreign-born and with limited English. Furthermore, the correctional facility in question serves as a remand centre in the Irish Prison Service, resulting in high turnover of prisoners and potential contacts of the index case. Contact tracing was performed with limited success due to difficulties tracing this population. Over the course of five years, however, twenty-seven active TB cases were diagnosed amongst patients who had been inmates or prison guards in the facility during that time period, three of whom were HIV positive. Lineage tracing confirmed identical genotype to the index case in all 27 cases. This case describes a patient with highly infectious Mtb strain and a site of infection that is associated with increased transmission, with consequent active infection diagnosed in 27 further patients to date. Two potential opportunities for screening and prevention were missed in this case. Firstly, routine screening of immigrant populations from high incidence countries is not employed in Ireland. This patient originated from Malaysia, radiography demonstrated changes consistent with prior TB, and limited English resulted in delayed presentation when symptoms developed. Secondly, routine screening of prison populations is not employed. Prisons represent a closely shared living space with a high proportion of socially marginalized and immunocompromised patients. This case highlights a need for revision of the approach to TB screening in Ireland.
