N2  - Introduction Mycobacteria can be divided into non-Tuberculosis (NTB) and Tuberculosis (MTB) Complex. They are considered mutually exclusive. However, we describe here a patient found to have MTB and an exceptionally rare but virulent case of M.abscessus also. Case Report A 77-year-old male was admitted for a six month history of cough associated with occasional hemoptysis, chills, nighttime sweating, fatigue, and decreased appetite. The patient had a history of imprisonment in Vietnam prior to moving to the United States in 1995. His past medical history included gastritis, chronic Hepatitis B infection, and a negative PPD in 2008. Physical examination was significant for an emaciated body habitus. Blood work was unremarkable. Chest x-ray showed extensive right upper lobe destruction, and chest CT revealed parenchymal fibrosis and chronic cystic bronchiectasis of the right upper lobe. Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide and Ethambutol (RIPE) was started when the sputum smear was positive for acid fast bacilli (AFB). When smears continued to be AFB positive after several weeks of treatment nucleic acid amplification tests (NAAT) were sent. Initial cultures grew Mycobacterium abscessus, and the antibiotic regimen was changed to Amikacin, Cefoxitin and Azithromycin. However, NAAT analysis then reported the presence of MTB as well. RIPE therapy was then added back to the antibiotic regimen. The sputum samples were negative for AFB after two weeks of this therapy. He was discharged home on the sixtieth day of admission on the current regimen for 12 more weeks to be followed by six more months of an oral regimen. (Figure presented). Discussion The annual prevalence of M. Abscessus pulmonary infection is less than 1 per 100,000 people. It is extremely difficult to treat and considered a chronic incurable disease.1 It is similar to MTB in sputum staining, symptoms and radiographic findings. Although rare, the occurrence of MTB and NTB together has been reported. One study reported the incidence of NTB culture positive samples was 7.1% in patients with confirmed MTB.2 Their concurrence may be a cause for RIPE therapy failure. Conclusion This underscores the importance of utilizing molecular testing early to identify all possible pathogens in AFB positive sputum samples. ATS guidelines may need to be revised to include this possibility.
