N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is a rare but known cause of pericardial effusions in immunocompromised patients. Exact incidence and prevalence is unknown as it has only been described in case reports/series. Only two cases of MAC causing pericardial effusion in immunocompetent patients have been reported. We present a case of MAC-related pericardial effusion that was treated conservatively with oral antimycobacterial medications. Case description: 59-year old African-American lady, 25 pack year active smoker, presented with a three month history of cough productive of white sputum associated with 12 pound weight loss and decreased exercise tolerance. CT chest showed bronchiectasis, numerous pulmonary nodules with random distribution (largest measuring 2.3cm), a large pericardial effusion with pericardial enhancement, and a 2cm hypodense hepatic lesion. Transthoracic echocardiogram showed a large pericardial effusion, with no frank tamponade. On review of her prior echocardiogram from eight years ago, there was a small pericardial effusion noted. She underwent CT guided biopsy which revealed non-necrotizing granulomas. Serial sputum samples showed MAC. Autoimmune workup and HIV testing were negative. The patient was started on antibiotic therapy with rifampin, azithromycin, ethambutol. She refused pericardiocentesis. After three months of treatment, the patient reported near complete resolution of cough, gained 3 pounds and exercise tolerance improved back to baseline. Serial transthoracic echocardiograms demonstrated decrease in size of pericardial effusion. Discussion MAC infection can be associated with a wide variety of radiologic patterns. Over the past few years, there have been an increasing number of case reports that identified MAC as a cause of pericardial effusion. The majority of these cases occur among immunocompromised patients, particularly those with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). This case signifies that MAC can be associated with pericardial effusion among immunocompetent patients. It can have a prolonged course, as this case demonstrated slow progression over eight years. Moreover, this case shows that clinical response could be achieved with conservative management with antimycobacterial therapy.
