N2  - Introduction Tuberculosis (TB) is one of the most common infectious causes of death in adults worldwide, and yet remains a diagnostic challenge. Even with multiple available diagnostic studies, the bacteria can still elude our most sophisticated methods. Therefore, it is essential to maintain a high clinical suspicion even in the face of a negative workup. Case This case involves a 22-year-old Chinese man with a 5-year smoking history and recent travel to China who presented with right sided pleuritic chest pain, productive cough, daily subjective fevers, and night sweats for 15 days. On examination, patient was tachycardic and in no acute respiratory distress. He had absent breath sounds of the right lung. A chest X-ray revealed a large right sided pleural effusion. Sputum cultures and a serum interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA) were sent, but were not resulted during the hospital course. Sputum Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) smears were sent three times. The results of a thoracentesis were consistent with an exudative effusion with lymphocytic predominance. Pleural fluid AFB, cultures, and adenosine deaminase were sent. Interestingly, all of these studies returned negative. A chest computed tomography scan was negative for evidence of parenchymal TB. The patient underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) for AFB analysis and culture. These studies were also negative. He finally underwent pleural biopsy, which failed to yield a diagnosis. Despite multiple negative studies, the patient was started on Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol. Discussion Sputum cultures are the gold standard for laboratory diagnosis of Mycobacterium tuberculosis infections. However, cultures can take up to eight weeks to become positive. Delaying treatment until diagnosis is confirmed poses increased risks at individual and public health levels. In clinical practice, a combination of serial AFB smears, nucleic acid amplification tests, and bronchoscopy studies are used to aid in providing a timely diagnosis. Furthermore, when these tests are nondiagnostic, staining and cultures of biopsy specimens are able to diagnose the majority of TB cases. It is important to be aware of circumstances that may delay diagnosis. In this case, age, smoking status, and extra-pulmonary findings likely contributed to the low diagnostic yield of the studies. Still, given the high impact of this disease and the lack of evidence to suggest alternative etiologies, the clinical suspicion was enough to justify starting therapy. Eventually, the IGRA returned positive, ruling in the diagnosis of tuberculosis and confirming that clinical acumen can still trump technology.
