N2  - Aspergillus tracheobronchitis (ATB) is an acute infectious disease caused by the Aspergillus genus. It is an airway-limited subtype of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (IPA). It usually develops in immunocompromised patients, such as those with prolonged neutropenia, following solid-organ transplants or progressed acquired immune deficiency syndrome. However, it rarely develops in immunocompetent hosts and, because of difficulty in diagnosis and treatment, is often lethal. A previously healthy 67-year-old male with diabetes mellitus, hypertension and a history of smoking 47 packs-year, was hospitalized after showing dyspnea and a cough for 5 days. Data at hospitalization was as follows; WBC 10,700/μl, CRP 22.2 mg/dl, PaO2 56.5 mmHg, PaCO2 37.5 mmHg, pH 7.409. Nasal swab sample was negative for influenza A and B antigens. There was no obvious pneumonia shadow on the chest X-ray or computed tomography. Fungal hyphae were present in the sputum smear, but were thought to be contamination. The patient received empirical therapy with Ceftriaxon and Azithromycin. His condition drastically deteriorated and he was intubated on the third day following hospitalization. Bronchoscopy after intubation showed purulent sputum covering almost the entire tracheobronchial surface (Figure). Microbiological study of the bronchoscopy specimen showed Aspergillus fumigatus. No other pathogens were detected. Despite Voriconazole and Micafungin being administered to treat ATB, the patient died on the eighth day. The autopsy specimen revealed Aspergillus fumigatus invasion in the bronchus and pulmonary alveolus tissue. Antibody titer for influenza B at hospitalization measured by complement fixation (CF) test was 16. Paired serum couldn't be collected to confirm influenza infection. There are a few reports on ATB after influenza infection in immunocompetent hosts. In this case, the facts that the disease developed during the influenza B-endemic season and CF antibody titer was increased (although the diagnosis couldn't be confirmed) indicate the possibility of influenza infection before development of ATB---an influenza antigen test may appear negative 5 days after initiation of infection. In the influenza season, the possibility of IPA, and ATB in the case of no obvious shadow on the chest X-ray should be considered even in immunocompetent patients with minor risk factors, such as diabetes mellitus or smoking, even if a nasal swab were negative for influenza. Further, in the influenza season, fungal hyphae in sputum smear should be evaluated as a possible pathogen.
