N2  - Purpose: Patients with acute stroke are at risk for colonisation by respiratory pathogens due to a sudden change in oral hygiene routines. Pneumonia risk may also be increased due to aspiration of contaminated oropharyngeal contents and reduced cough reflex sensitivity. The relationships between oral bacteria, cough sensitivity and pneumonia remain unknown. This research investigates this relationship in a clinical stroke population. Method(s): One hundred and two patients with acute stroke and dysphagia underwent saliva sampling and citric acid cough reflex threshold testing at three time points: admission to hospital, discharge, and one month post-stroke. Molecular testing was used to measure the prevalence of pathogenic bacteria in saliva. Aspiration pneumonia events were also recorded. Result(s): A location effect was evident: saliva samples obtained on acute hospital wards had the greatest proportion of detectable oral pathogens [p = .04]. No relationships between cough sensitivity, oral bacteria and aspiration pneumonia were found; however, statistical power was low. Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Escherichia coli are not typically found in healthy mouths, yet were detected in 18 samples within 48 h of admission (P. aeruginosa, E. coli) and at discharge from hospital (K. pneumoniae). Conclusions (Including Clinical Relevance): This study adds to the limited evidence relating clinical, microbiological, and pneumoniarelated outcomes for patients with stroke and dysphagia. Results suggest a 48-hour critical window may be present for preventing colonization by respiratory pathogens in this patient population.
