N2  - INTRODUCTION. Electrokinetic potential (zeta) and cell surface hydrophobicity are two important factors engaged in bacterial cell adhesion to surface and further biofilm formation. Biofilm formation during Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection in cystic fibrosis is one of the main difficulties in controlling infection. Exploration of the mechanisms of biofilm formation is important from a medical point of view. AIM. Aim of the studies was to: 1) evaluate the role of zeta potential and hydrophobicity in adhesion and biofilm formation process, 2) to correlate those values with adhesion, biofilm and with source of isolated stains. MATERIALS AND METHODS. The 42 Pseudomonas aeruginosa strains were derived from BCCM/LMG Bacteria Collection, Gent, Belgium. Overnight broth cultures were prepared by inoculating 10 ml TSB with fresh growth from a pure streak plate. Cultures were grown for 16-18 h at 37°C, shaking at 160 r.p.m. For hydrophobicity measurements, cells were spread as layer on microscopic slides and dried before contact angle measurement. Electrokinetic potential of harvested cells was measured in fresh TSB medium as a solvent solution. Statistical analysis were performed on Statistica 12 software (StatSoft, Inc.). RESULTS. Strains from Pseudomonas aeruginosa reference panel differ according to adhesion to solid surface, biofilm formation, cell surface hydrophobicity and electrokinetic potential. Cell surface hydrophobicity measured as contact angle of water droplet indicates that all of tested stains have hydrophilic nature (< 45°). Electrokinetic potential varied among panel strains from -2.25 to -16 mV. Statistical analysis revealed negative correlation between adhesion to solid surface and electrokinetic potential (r = -0.3, p < 0.05). Interestingly, positive correlation between strains isolated from patients with cystic fibrosis and adhesion was found (r = 0.21, p < 0.05). CONCLUSIONS. Electrokinetic potential of Pseudomonas aeruginosa strains plays a significant role in adhesion, where adhesion is positively correlated with cystic fibrosis as a strain source.
