N2  - Learning Objectives: Group A streptococcal (GAS) bacteremia from necrotizing pneumonia is most common in the immunocompromised and young children. To our knowledge, this is a rare case of necrotizing pneumonia from GAS leading to bacteremia and other complications in an otherwise healthy adult male without any significant past medical history. Methods: A 34-year-old male cab driver presented with cough, malaise, pleuritic chest pain, and fevers. He arrived to the ED in respiratory distress and developed septic shock, requiring vasopressors. Serial chest CT showed severe necrotizing pneumonia with increasing cavitation and a large right-sided loculated pleural effusion. He was started on antibiotics and blood cultures confirmed Group A Streptococcus bacteria. Subsequent HIV testing was negative. He underwent thoracentesis yielding 500 mL of tea colored fluid. Despite percutaneous drainage, repeat CAT scan revealed a loculated pleural effusion and gas concerning for empyema. He underwent a VATS with decortication. He continued to spike high-grade fevers and developed a bronchopleural fistula and lung abscess, which subsequently ruptured. Finally, he underwent a successful right open thoracotomy with debridement of a right lower lobe abscess and tube drainage. Results: Necrotizing pneumonia and parenchymal necrosis are a serious complication of community-acquired pneumonia. Among adults, GAS bacteremia has been found to occur commonly in immunocompromised patients. Group B Streptococcus is the most common cause of necrotizing pneumonia in healthy individuals. Lower respiratory infection is the least common cause of GAS bacteremia with the usual culprit being skin and soft tissue infections. Pleural effusions and empyema are common complications, however, bronchopleural fistulas and abscess formation are rare. In fact, lung abscesses usually form due to anaerobic bacteria normally found in the gingiva. Treatment consists of antibiotics and/ or surgery. To our knowledge, there are no cases reported of an otherwise healthy adult developing a bronchopleural fistula and lung abscess due to GAS.
