N2  - Learning Objectives: Pulmonary mucormycosis, seen most commonly in immunocompromised patients including diabetics, causes a necrotic pneumonia that progresses rapidly and has a high mortality. Methods: We present a case of Rhizomucor pneumonia in a 52 year old female with HIV (CD4 116 Cells/mcL), diabetes mellitus, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who presented with cough productive of yellow sputum, fevers, and shortness of breath for 4 days. She had normal blood pressure and respirations with 92% oxygen saturation on room air. Her heart rate was 130 beats per minute and she was febrile to 102.5°F. Physical exam was notable for bilateral diffuse rhonchi. Complete blood count, basic metabolic panel, and arterial blood gas were normal. Computed tomography scan of the chest showed no pulmonary embolism and bilateral patchy infiltrates. She was started on treatment for healthcare associated and pneumocystis (PJP) pneumonia. She progressively developed hypoxic respiratory failure requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation, shock with multi-organ failure, and required renal replacement therapy. Bronchoscopy was performed with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) negative for PJP, showed Candida albicans,and a few extracellular yeast to be identified. Micafungin was added. She continued to decline and expired on day 25 of admission. 3 days after expiration, the extracellular yeast was identified as Rhizomucor. Results: Early suspicion and treatment of mucormycosis can lead to improved survival rates. Given low clinical suspicion, treatment is often delayed with a mortality rate up to 76%. In our patient we had concern for fungal infection after the patient continued to deteriorate despite adequate medical therapy however, antifungal treatment was limited given renal failure and did not cover for mucormycosis. This was a unique case because the causative pathogen isolated was Rhizomucor - a rare organism often not identified until post-mortem. Given the extraordinarily high mortality of pulmonary mucormycosis it must remain as a differential cause of pneumonia in critically ill immunocompromised hosts.
