N2  - INTRODUCTION: Cyrpotococcosis is mostly seen in patients with acquired immunodeficiency or those with underlying T cell mediated deficiency. However, case reports have shown that the 10-40% of cases are seen in immunocompetent patients. Nonetheless, severe disseminated Cryptococcosis remains a rare entity in those without any underlying immune dysfunction. We present a case of a previously healthy man who presented with disseminated Cryptococcosis. CASE PRESENTATION: 35 year old man with no medical history presented with fever, dyspnea and cough of 12 days duration. CT chest revealed pulmonary nodules in the periphery of the bilateral lung fields, ground glass opacity and areas of consolidation in the bilateral lower lobe. Pt was started on antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia. Bacterial cultures and TEE were negative. His hospital course was complicated by ARDS requiring ventilator support. Fungal sputum culture grew Cryptococcus. Serum Cryptococcus antigen came back with titer of 1:5. HIV was negative; CD4 count and immunoglobulin levels were normal. Pt was started on amphotericin B; however his Cryptococcus titer remained at 1:5. His remaining stay was complicated by tension pneumothorax requiring chest tubes and generalized leptomeningitis and cerebritis. After a prolonged ICU course, he was transitioned to comfort care. Lung autopsy revealed Cryptococcus neoformans in a healing granuloma. DISCUSSION: Cyrptococcos neoformans ranges from asymptomatic pulmonary colonization to life-threatening meningitis and disseminated infection. In the immunocompetent host, it commonly presents as an incidental pulmonary finding. In one case series, 76.9% of patients who present with pulmonary Cryptococcosis were initially misdiagnosed. Pulmonary Cryptococcosis in the immunocompetent individual can present with normal serological levels; however initial titer level greater than 1:4 is consistent with infection. Treatment response is monitored through serial measurements of titer. Several case series indicate the need for identifying imaging characteristics that could be specific for pulmonary Cryptococcosis. Qu et al presented on their case series that multi-clustered nodules localized to the periphery as well as initial ground glass opacities in the middle or lower lobe should alert the clinician to the possibility of a Cryptococcosis. CONCLUSIONS: Disseminated Cryptococcus is not isolated to HIV or immunocompromised patients. When assessing patients with subacute cinical course and no underlying comorbidities, maintaining a broad differential is crucial to making an accurate diagnosis. The diagnostic clinician needs to consider imaging characteristics as a clue to identify sentinel Cyrptococcal infection which could prevent the progression to disseminated disease.
