N2  - INTRODUCTION: Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection is a well-documented cause of bronchiolitis in children but also plays a significant role in adult respiratory infections. CASE PRESENTATION: An 89 year-old woman with a history of asthma and second-hand smoke exposure, diabetes, hypertension, hypothyroidism, and stroke presented with altered mental status, rhinorrhea, shortness of breath, productive cough, and fevers up to 100.4 °F for two days. Patient denied sick contacts. She was admitted to the ICU and given bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, and oseltamivir. Exam was notable for increased work of breathing and diffuse end-expiratory wheezes. There was a mild leukocytosis of 10.2 with 75% neutrophils. Chest X-ray was clear. The following day, she was electively intubated for worsening hypercapneic, hypoxic respiratory failure and somnolence. She was extubated two days later but required non-invasive positive pressure ventilation support afterwards for two days. All cultures were negative except the viral respiratory panel, which was positive for respiratory syncytial virus. DISCUSSION: RSV infections in adults may have a clinical impact comparable to that of influenza. RSV has been detected in between 6.1% and 10% of adults hospitalized with respiratory symptoms, 3.8% of those with community-acquired pneumonia, 0.7% to 8% of COPD exacerbations, and 7% of asthma exacerbations. Viral infections in COPD exacerbations are associated with a greater negative impact on lung function, prolonged hospital stay, and longer recovery time (13 days vs. 6 days) compared to bacterial infections. Mortality rates are similar to that of influenza, except in hospitalized patients, with mortality rates up to 80%. Though not completely understood, patients with asthma and COPD may be more susceptible to viral infections due to preexisting inflammatory state and impaired innate immune responses involving interferon production. CONCLUSIONS: RSV plays a larger role in adult respiratory infections than previously thought, especially in elderly patients, immunocompromised patients, and those with asthma and COPD, thought to be related in part to impaired immune responses to viral infection. Despite the increasing awareness, there remains a knowledge gap in adult RSV infection, particularly in the areas of treatment and prevention.
