N2  - INTRODUCTION: Anthracofibrosis, is dark pigmentation in conjunction with bronchial narrowing or obliteration, is often associated with active tuberculosis.1 Here, we report the rare case of a 72-year-old woman who presented with right middle lobe atelectasis who was found to have anthracostenosis without evidence of fungal, bacterial or mycobacterial infection with an extensive history of biomass fuel exposure. CASE PRESENTATION: 72 year-old woman who presented with dyspnea, dry cough & chest pain after failing antibiotics for pneumonia; she was evaluated for pulmonary embolism & was found to have right middle lobe collapse with bilateral hilar & mediastinal lymphadenopathy. The patient reported that over the past year she had worsening dyspnea on exertion without constitutional symptoms. Her pertinent past medical history includes recent clinical diagnosis of asthma with no prior pulmonary function tests & no improvement with albuterol inhaler. Finally, she is a never smoker, but reported exposure to cooking over a wood burning stove for 30 years in Mexico. Bronchoscopy revealed diffuse black pigmentation of the bronchial mucosa throughout both lungs. The apical & posterior segments of the right upper lobe as well as left upper lung segments were significantly stenotic as was the right middle lobe. Pathology exam demonstrated benign bronchial mucosa with focal anthracofibrosis & mild chronic inflammation and negative cultures. DISCUSSION: The term anthracofibrosis initially was presented in 1998 by Chung after experiencing “an increasing number of patients in whom dark anthracotic pigmentation in conjunction with bronchial narrowing or obliteration” in a high propensity of patients with active or old tuberculosis (TB).1 Since that time the literature has focused on tuberculosis as the mitigating factor leading to bronchial fibrosis until 2008 when Wynn presented a seven cases with a weak correlation to TB leading to the thought that anthracofibrobosis may often times have a benign picture.2 In our patient, extensive work up was performed to rule out malignancy and serious treatable pathology such as TB. Based on her extensive history of wood-smoke exposure it is believed she is a rare case of anthracostenosis that progressed to anthracofibrosis causing eventual collapse of her right middle lobe. Although wood or carbon itself is inert, the combustion of wood or other biomass results in a complex mixture of gases & compounds that have been shown to be fibrogenic, mutagenic, irritant, & toxic in in vitro studies.3 CONCLUSIONS: Chronic biomass fuel use and smoke inhalation leads to anthracofibrosis, atelectasis and lung volume loss. It is important to consider this as potential etiology of newly found unexplained atelectasis.
