N2  - Background: Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA) is a category of ANCA-associated vasculitis Limited GPA usually present as a granulomatous disorder of respiratory tracts without vascultitis features, which may prone to misdiagnosis Objectives: We reported a case of GPA which was misdiagnosis pulmonary fungal disease to improve the understanding of GPA Methods: A 22-year-old previously health woman was admitted to surgery department with a history of fever and cough for 2 months. 1,3-b-D-glucans was positive, while purified protein derivative tuberculin, TB infection of T cells spot test and bronchoalveolar lavage fluid pathogen examination were negative. Chest CT scan found pulmonary abscess in left upper lobe The lesion expanded obviously after 3 weeks of wide-spectrum antibacterial and antifungal therapy. Upper left pneumonectomy was taken. Patient was treated with anti-tuberculosis therapy nearly 1 month. The above symptom relieved but nasal congestion appeared Results: Nasal endoscope showed epistaxis. Paranasal sinuses MRI enhanced scan prompted ulcers in retropharyngeal wall. Chest CT reexamination found emerging shadow in lower left lobe and right lung. More detailed blood tests were taken. C-ANCA/PR3 were positive, while p-ANCA/MPO, ANA, ENAs were negative. Pathological tissue reexamination found granuloma and vasculitis. Moreover, gomori methenamine silver stain, acid-fast stain and gram stain were all negative. Diagnosis of GPA was established. The patient was treated with oral prednisone (1 mg/kg/d tapering) and pulse cyclophosphamide monthly. Dynamic reexamine X-ray found the pulmonary shadow diminished gradually in the follow-up visit Conclusions: GPA is complicated, easy to misdiagnosis and missed diagnosis disease. Clinicians need to improve the cognition.
