N2  - Colistimethate sodium (CMS) is a prodrug that undergoes rapid hydrolysis to colistin, which is a polymyxin antibiotic effective against many gram-negative bacteria. It is used to treat Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections in CF patients. Due to the complexity of CMS and its rapid hydrolysis, identification and quantitation of CMS are difficult and methods complex. To the best of our knowledge, there are no rapid HPLC methods reported that directly quantitate CMS. The aim of this study was to develop a rapid HPLC method for quantitating CMS and to validate the method for use with aerosol captured onto a filter and samples from a next generation impactor (NGI) for the testing of nebulizers to confirm the reproducibility of drug delivered. Filter samples were prepared in triplicate from an I-neb AAD System nebulizer, nebulizing into a breathing pattern. Drug captured onto the filter was extracted with buffer and centrifugation. NGI samples were prepared in triplicate by nebulizing CMS. Drug in the NGI was extracted with buffer. For all tests, residual drug in the I-neb was washed out with buffer so mass balances could be calculated. The filter and NGI tests were performed with CMS reconstituted with deionized water and also with CMS reconstituted with 0.9% saline. For CMS aerosol captured onto the filter, mean recoveries of CMS were in the range of 97-101%; for CMS nebulized into the NGI, both mean recoveries were 96%. Thus, the HPLC assay for CMS is suitable for the analysis of aerosol dose to filter and particle size analysis by cascade impaction. A rapid HPLC assay was successfully developed and validated over a working range of 0.05-7.00 mg/mL.
