N2  - In the aerobic respiratory chain of the Gram-positive Actinobacterium Corynebacterium glutamicum, a supramolecular association of cytochrome bcc complex and cytochrome aa3 oxidase couples menaquinol oxidation to reduction of dioxygen. We isolated the cytochrome bcc-aa3 supercomplex to high homogeneity and present its biophysical and structural characterization. The supercomplex was stable in size-exclusion chromatography. It contained all subunits and cofactors as shown by SDS-PAGE, BN-PAGE, redox difference spectroscopy and EPR spectroscopy. Redox midpoint potentials of the supercomplex are fined-tuned for integrated quinol oxidase activity that employs low potential menaquinol. The study provides further support for the suggested correlation between phylum-specific patterns of Qo motif, redox potentials and quinone species [1]. A dimeric homology model of the bcc-aa3 supercomplex was constructed. Highly uniform particles were resolved by electron microscopy supporting a distinct composition of the supercomplex. The data advance the understanding of the mechanism of the important bioenergetic complexes and could be of interest for optimizing efficiency of C. glutamicum in industrial production. Homologous complexes could be useful targets to combat drugresistant forms of actinobacterial human pathogens such as Corynebacterium diphtheriae and Mycobacterium tuberculosis.
