N2  - Introduction: Colobreathe® dry powder inhaler (CSDPI) has become an established therapy for CF patients infected with P. aeruginosa. This study aimed to assess our clinical experience with CSDPI over 6 months at a regional adult CF centre. Methods: Spirometry was performed before and after test dose. Side effects were recorded at test dose and on every patient contact. Every patient was instructed how to use CSDPI by a respiratory therapist or doctor. Within 3 days, we provided a telephone interview to detect early problems that may cause discontinuation. We offered further telephone interviews on demand and reassessed inhalation technique and side effects on every following patient contact. We included 27 patients. 15/27 were homozygous for dF508. Results: Early problems were cough, nausea, inhalation of pieces of the gelatine capsule and chest tightness. At 3-months review, 26/27 patients successfully used CSDPI and planned to continue. Medium FEV1 in this cohort had increased by 6.1% overall. Only one patient quit within 3 months due to chest tightness. In all other cases we were able to solve early problems by personal guidance. After 6 months, 22/27 patients still used CSDPI. In these patients, FEV1 increased by 5.6% overall. No severe exacerbation was detected in this cohort. Reasons for discontinuation were bad taste, chest tightness and cough. Conclusion: CSDPI is well tolerated and convenient to use. By personal guidance we were able to detect and solve early problems with CSDPI and thereby contributed to excellent adherence and a very low break off rate. During observation, no severe exacerbation occurred in our patients. A notable increase of FEV1 was detected.
