N2  - Objectives: Young Children with CF are often non-productive of sputum. Effective pathogen surveillance is challenging but critical, as respiratory infection and lung disease begins early in life. CF-SpIT is a prospective internally-controlled trial comparing induced sputum (IS) with cough swab (CS) and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL). Methods: 1. 124 paired CS and IS procedures were performed. 2. In 31 children where BAL was clinically indicated, pathogen yield was compared from concurrent CS, IS and extensive 6-lobe BAL samples. Results: 1. 103 (83%) IS procedures were successful, with similar success in children aged <6 yrs and >6 yrs. 3 times as many pathogens were identified on IS compared to CS (56 v 19 CHIsq-p = 0.0001). 30% of IS samples identified multiple pathogens. Added value from IS was greatest in children <6 yrs. 2. 30 pathogens were identified in the 31 children with serial CS, IS and BAL samples. 6 (20%), 21 (70%), 20 (67%) and 24 (80%) pathogens were identified by CS, IS, 2-lobe BAL and 6-lobe BAL respectively. 6 (20%) pathogens including examples of PsA and BCC were identified on IS alone. Test specific detection rates were comparable for IS and BAL. Conclusions: Paired sampling in this study clearly identifies the inadequacy of CS sampling, and the huge advantages of IS sampling even in very young children. IS identified additional pathogens when compared to 2-lobe BAL suggesting this approach samples a wider lower airway compartment. IS precludes the need for bronchoscopy in 70% of cases. It is well tolerated, non-invasive and compares favourably to current gold standard 2-lobe BAL. It should be considered standard of care prior to all bronchoscopy procedures.
