N2  - Introduction: Reliable identification of lower respiratory tract pathogens is crucial in the management of CF. In the ongoing study (NCT02363764), bacterial yield of nasal swab (NS), cough swab (CS) and (induced) sputum samples (ISS/SS) in both expectorating (EPs) and nonexpectorating (NEPs) CF patients is investigated. Pain experienced with the different sampling methods and patients' preference is also studied. Methods: All participating patients aged ≥6 years, completed a questionnaire on pain perceived during sampling [No/Yes; VAS-scale (10 cm)]. Furthermore, they were asked to rate their personal preference for each sampling method (score 1-3). Results: 84 CF patients [n(<18 y) = 44] were included in the current analysis; 14 pts (1 adult) required HS inhalation to induce sputum. Of these NEPs, only children mentioned pain perception, mostly with ISS (Table). As for EPs, CS was considered as the most painful sampling method overall, in children and in adults with mean VAS of 3.1, 3.5 and 2.5, resp. A different sampling method was preferred by NEPs vs. EPs; NEPs preferred NS over CS and ISS (50.0%, 42.9%, 7.1%, resp.), whereas EPs preferred SS over NS and CS (64.3%, 27.1%, 8.6%, resp.), which correlated with pain experience during sampling. Conclusion: Patients' preference of the different sampling methods correlated significantly with concomitant pain perception and expectorating status. However, the most reliable method must be used. (Table Presented).
