N2  - The introduction of newborn screening (NBS) for CF has revealed a number of individuals who have an inconclusive diagnosis (CFSPID; CRMS) with mild mutations and normal or borderline sweat tests. Consensus documents on the management of these children are unclear on the role of cough swabs. Objectives: We undertook regular cough swabs in this group to document the microbiological findings. Methods: From Sept 2006 to Oct 2014, we identified 10 NBS children with 2 CFTR mutations and borderline (1) or normal (9) sweat tests and reviewed the cough swab results. Results: Microbiological surveillance revealed that 2 patients grew Pseudomonas aeruginosa; 5 tested positive for Staphylococcus aureus (3 chronically). One patient grew Acinetobacter and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia. Two further patients were isolated with Haemophilus parainfluenza and Haemophilus parahaemolyticus. Conclusion: The NBS program has picked up infants in whom the diagnosis of CF is uncertain. Subsequent management is controversial particularly pertaining to routine cough swabs. In our CF centre regular cough swabs in this group of NBS children yields a significant number of positive results. As the long term pulmonary outcome for these children is uncertain, we suggest it is prudent to continue performing regular cough swabs and treating the organisms known to be associated with CF related lung disease.
