N2  - Tuberculosis (TB) continues to be a major global health problem, causing 1.5 million deaths in 2014. To curb TB, several TB vaccine candidates are currently in various stages of testing. We investigated the importance of vaccination route in immune response and protection against TB in a non-human primate (NHP) model. We vaccinated cynomolgus macaques with a protein subunit vaccine (H56) admixed with a novel adjuvant (CAF09) by three routes-intranasal, subcutaneous, and aerosol. Immunogenicity was measured by intracellular cytokine staining of blood and bronchoalveolar lavage samples during and after vaccination. NHPs were challenged with M. tuberculosis, and their immune recall responses assessed by blood and BAL samples. Lung inflammation was tracked during the course of infection by serial PET-CT scans, and extent of disease was assessed at necropsy. Frequencies of T cells producing pro-inflammatory cytokines increased in the blood and BAL following vaccinations in all vaccinated groups, but NHPs vaccinated by aerosol had an earlier recall of T cells to the airways after the third vaccination. After challenge, aerosol vaccinated NHPs had less inflammation by PET-CT compared to other vaccinated groups and unvaccinated controls. The aerosol cohort had similar gross pathology and total bacterial burden to controls at necropsy, but improved bacterial clearance and higher frequency of IFN-γ-producing T cells in the granulomas compared to controls. Although all vaccinated groups had TB disease, this study suggests that the route of vaccination affects the immune response and extent of disease, with the aerosol group having better outcomes. Further studies into the beneficial effects of aerosolizing TB vaccines are warranted.
