N2  - Purpose: To describe the effects of aerosolized antipseudomonals (AAP) on Pseudomonas spp (PS) recurrence, bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS), and acute cellular rejection (ACR) in lung transplant (LT) recipients (LTRs) colonized with PS. Methods: Single-center, retrospective cohort study of adult LTRs colonized with PS treated with either AAP for 28 days or greater vs no AAP therapy, indexed to 68 days post LT. Primary outcome was freedom from PS recurrence by positive bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) or sputum culture at 1 year. Secondary outcomes were freedom from BOS or BOS progression and ACR burden (defined by the novel composite rejection standardized score (CRSS)). Normality was assessed and univariate and multivariate parametric and nonparametric statistical tests were used to assess baseline characteristics and outcomes, where appropriate. Freedom from events were compared using the Kaplan Meier method with log-rank conversion and risk was assigned using multivariable Cox proportional hazards modeling. Results: 305 (106 AAP, 199 no AAP) LTRs were included. Median age in AAP and control cohorts were 50 (29.5 - 63.3) and 62 (53 - 67) years (p< 0.01). Median AAP duration was 194.5 (IQR 93.8 - 411.0) days. Tobramycin was the most commonly used AAP (45.3%). Time to positive PS culture was similar between AAP (median 371 (IQR 138 - 828) days) and controls (365 (IQR 140 - 685) days); p= 0.34. Freedom from PS recurrence at 1 year was similar in APP vs. controls was 59.4% vs 55.3%, p= 0.63. When controlling for cystic fibrosis (CF) diagnosis, there was a trend toward less recurrent PS with AAP (HR 0.731, 95% CI 0.523-1.022). Death-censored freedom from BOS or BOS progression in APP vs. control at 1 (17.0% vs 14.1%, p= 0.50) and 3 (37.7% vs 35.6%, p= 0.72) years were similar. Deathcensored freedom from high-grade BOS were also similar in aerosol vs. control at 1 (5.7% vs 6.5%, p= 0.76) and 3 (24.5% vs 19.1%, p= 0.27) years. CRSS was similar in APP vs control group at 1 year (0.38 vs 0.33, p= 0.4). Conclusion: APP use was not associated with less recurrent PS, BOS, or ACR in LTRs. When controlling for CF, treatment with AAP may be associated with protection against recurrent PS. Limitations include retrospective design and possible selection bias. Larger assessments are required; studies in CF specifically should be pursued.
