N2  - Background: Mounting evidence supports the notion of 'one airway, one disease'. Objective: To determine whether chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS) poses adverse impacts on Chinese adults with bronchiectasis. Methods: We enrolled 148 consecutive adults with clinically stable bronchiectasis. CRS diagnosed based on the 2012 EP3OS criteria. We systematically evaluated the bronchiectasis aetiology, radiology, lung function, sputum bacteriology, airway inflammatory biomarkers, Bronchiectasis Severity Index, cough sensitivity and healthcare resource utilization. All patients were prospectively follow-up for 1 year to examine the frequency of bronchiectasis exacerbations (BEs). Results: Forty-seven patients (31.8%) were diagnosed as having CRS. Bronchiectasis aetiologies did not vary statistically between CRS and no-CRS group. There was a trend towards non-statistically higher Bronchiectasis Severity Index [6.4 ± 3.4 vs 5.0 (6.0), P = 0.19], higher proportion of patients with BEs needing hospitalization before enrolment (48.9% vs 29.7%, P = 0.13), poorer FVC [78.2 ± 19.8% vs 82.2(16.8)%, P = 0.54] and FEV1 [68.2 ± 24.8% vs 74.8 (21.2)%, P = 0.29], a higher prevalence of Pseudomonas aeruginosa isolated (36.2% vs 26.7%, P = 0.27) or colonized in sputum (36.2% vs 21.8%, P = 0.12), greater capsaicin cough sensitivity [C2: 3.9 (123.0) μmol/L vs 11.7 (123.0) μmol/L, P = 0.81; C5: 62.5 (996.0) μmol/L vs 250.0 (973.0) μmol/L, P = 0.32] and a tendency of more frequent BEs during 1-year follow-up (P = 0.06 for log-rank test). Conclusion: Chinese adults with bronchiectasis appear to have a lower prevalence of CRS than that in western countries. Despite our overall negative findings, greater adverse impacts of CRS on bronchiectasis could not be precluded. Studies with greater sample sizes are of merit to resolve this issue.
