N2  - Objective: To determine the clinical features associated with correctly diagnosing community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) and to evaluate factor(s) that predict mortality during hospitalization. Methods: An audit was performed on all admissions to the Respiratory Ward in University of Malaya Medical Centre between January and April 2015 with a diagnosis of pneumonia. Patients were classified as having CAP based on the 2009 British Thoracic Society CAP guidelines after admission. Results: Only 121 (40%) of 305 patients admitted with a diagnosis of pneumoniaí were correctly diagnosed for CAP. Several clinical features which included symptoms of fever (P < 0.001), productive cough (P = 0.013), pleuritic chest pain (P = 0.028), hypoxia at presentation (defined by an SpO2 of < 94% or < 88% with underlying COPD) on room air (P < 0.001), temperature of >37.8°C (P = 0.021) and physical findings, e.g. crepitation, bronchial breathing or signs of pleural effusion (P < 0.001) on admission were significantly associated with CAP. Multivariate analysis of these clinical features identified hypoxia (adjusted OR, 0.43; 95% CI, 0.22-0.83; P = 0.012) and positive chest examination finding(s) (adjusted OR, 0.055; 95% CI, 0.03-0.011; P < 0.001) as independently associated with a correct diagnosis of CAP. The majority of patients [98/121 (81%)] were culture (blood, sputum or pleural fluid culture)- negative. Klebsiella pneumoniae and Streptococcus pneumoniae were the commonest pathogens isolated (7/23). Sixteen (13.2%) patients died during hospitalization. A retrospectively calculated CURB65 score >1 (adjusted OR, 4.41; 95% CI, 1.03-18.85; P = 0.046) and elevated total white blood cell count (TWCC) >11 x 109/L (adjusted OR, 1.14; 95% CI, 1.02-1.28; P = 0.027) were independent predictors of mortality. Conclusions: CAP was misdiagnosed in the majority of patients admitted for ëpneumoniaí. Careful history taking and careful physical examination improves the accuracy of diagnosis. Most of the specimen cultures taken were negative. Klebsiella pneumoniae and Streptococcus pneumoniae were the commonest pathogens identified. A CURB65 score >1 and elevated TWCC were predictive of mortality.
