N2  - Objective: To evaluate the differences between aspiration pneumonia (AsP) and community acquired pneumonia (CAP) and to determine the factor(s) that predict the clinical outcome of AsP. Methods: An audit was performed on all admissions to the Respiratory Ward of the University of Malaya Medical Centre between January and April 2015 with the diagnosis of ëpneumoniaí. The diagnosis of pneumonia was revised using 2009 British Thoracic Society CAP guidelines after admission. Results: There were 34 patients with AsP and 121 patients with CAP. About half of the patients with AsP did not have any symptom of respiratory tract infection. Two-thirds of patients with AsP [23/34 (67.6%)] had chest examination findings suggestive of pneumonia including crepitations and/or bronchial breathing. As with CAP, the majority of patients with AsP [26/34 (76.5%)] were culture (blood, sputum, urine and pleural fluid culture)-negative. Most of the bacteria isolated from the blood of AsP patients were commensals of the upper respiratory tract. Using productive cough (P < 0.001) and positive chest examination finding(s) (P = 0.009) as covariates, multivariate analysis showed that only positive chest examination findings was independently associated with a diagnosis of AsP (adjusted OR 3.25; 95%CI, 1.18-8.94; P = 0.022). Significantly more patients with AsP [23/34 (67.6%)] had a CURB65 score of 2-5 compared to CAP patients [58/121 (47.9%)] (P = 0.042). Length of hospital stay was longer for patients with AsP compared with CAP (mean 14.06 ± 12.24 days vs 9.95 ± 10.57days; P = 0.055). The inpatient mortality rate for AsP was 35.3% (12/34) versus 13.2% (16/121) for CAP (P = 0.003). Hypoxia as defined by an SpO2 of < 94% or < 88% with underlying COPD on room air at presentation was the only factor independently associated with mortality (OR 2.62; 95%CI, 1.35-5.08; P = 0.004). Conclusions: Symptoms of respiratory tract infection were less sensitive compared to physical examination findings in diagnosing AsP. AsP patients were more ill and had higher in-hospital mortality than CAP patients. Hypoxia at presentation was predictive of mortality in AsP patients.
