N2  - Objectives Cystic fibrosis is one of the most significant genetic disorders. It most commonly affects Caucasians with approximately 70,000 people suffering from it worldwide. The disease targets the respiratory tract and is characterized by the abnormal transport of chloride and sodium ions across the airway epithelium. This leads to the production of thick, viscous secretions, creating a perfect habitat for bacteria to grow. Polylactid-co-glycolid (PLGA)-based particles could serve as a drug releasing system targeting these viscous secretions and bacterial infections. To examine the biological impact of PLGA-particles on an epithelial surface, a 3D human airway mucosa model was used. Material and methods To generate the tissue models, a biological scaffold was seeded with human primary tracheobronchial fibroblasts and primary human tracheobronchial epithelial cells (hTEC). The models were incubated for four weeks, under airlift conditions, until the cells differentiated. Once developed, the models were treated with the PLGA particles for four hours. The ciliary motion and particle transportation across the epithelial surface were examined via a high speed camera. These samples were subsequently processed for electron microscopic analysis. The remaining tissue models were fixed and paraffin-embedded allowing for histological (H&E, alcian blue), immunohistochemical (anti-MUC5AC, anti-beta Tubulin, anti-Vimentin) and immunofluorescence (anti-beta Tubulin) staining to be performed. IL-6 and M30 ELISA tests were done with the supernatant of the tissue models at the second and fourth week, before and after particle incubation, to investigate the amount of inflammation and apoptosis, respectively. Results Light and electron microscopic analysis proved the tissue model showed a high resemblance to the in vivo situation. Alcian blue staining and anti-MUC5AC immunohistochemistry (IHC) verified the presence of mucus. Anti-beta Tubulin IHC and immunofluorescence showed the ciliated epithelium and anti-Vimentin IHC revealed fibroblasts had migrated into the scaffold. At the ultrastructural level, tight junctions, a basement membrane, microvilli, and kinocilia were observed. The PLGA-based particles attached to the epithelial surface could be seen in immunofluorescence and REM samples. These particles remained on the epithelial surface and mostly displayed a homogenous distribution. Moreover, using a high speed camera, the synchronized cilia movement was recorded. Lastly, IL-6 and M30 ELISA tests confirmed the particles do not induce any inflammatory reactions or trigger apoptosis. Conclusions The 3D tissue model of the airway mucosa displayed a high in vitro / in vivo correlation, facilitating investigations on interrelations with PLGA-based particles. Based on the preliminary data, it can be concluded the particles are not harmful to the 3D tissue model. Nevertheless further detailed studies need to be done to obtain more conclusive results. However, with the tissue model proving to be highly applicable for drug research, the number of experimental animals used in cystic fibrosis research can potentially be lowered.
