N2  - Introduction: The human pneumovirus, respiratory syncytial virus (hRSV), is an important cause of severe respiratory infection in young children worldwide. Extensive neutrophil accumulation in the lungs and occlusion of lower airways by DNA-rich mucus plugs are hallmark features of severe RSV infection. Activated neutrophils can release neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs), consisting of extracellular networks of DNA covered with antimicrobial proteins, as part of the first line defense against pathogens. NETs can trap and eliminate microbes, however abundant NET formation may also contribute to airway occlusion. In this study, we investigated whether NETs are formed in vivo during severe hRSV infection in infants, and two RSV animal models. Methods: The detection of NETs in the respiratory tract was performed in mechanically ventilated children with severe RSV disease and in two cognate host-pneumovirus models: bovine RSV (bRSV) infection in calves and mice infected with pneumonia virus of mice (PVM). NETs were visualized in 4% paraformaldehyde fixed cytology samples of broncho-alveolar lavage fluid (BALF) (hRSV patients) or in paraffin embedded lung tissue sections (animals) by DNA and immuno-staining using antibodies against citrullinated histone H3, elastase and myeloperoxidase (MPO). In the animal studies additional immunohistochemistry markers included CD3, D25 (RSV F-protein) and cytokeratin-8+18. Results: NETs were readily detected in BALF of hRSV-infected children and in lung tissue of bRSV-infected calves and PVM-infected mice. In the animal models NETs were localized in the lumen of small airways and in consolidated alveolar spaces. Dense mucus plugs with neutrophils and epithelial cells, resulting in small airway blockage, showed extensive positive staining of NETs and viral antigen. Conclusion: This is the first study that reports the release of NETs in the respiratory tract of patients with severe hRSV infection. Extensive NET formation in the airways and lungs appears a common feature during pneumovirus (hRSV, bRSV and PVM) infection. In pneumovirus animal models, NETs co-localize with the characteristic dense cellular- and virus-rich plugs in small airways. This suggests that NETs may trap viral particles, but also contribute to airway obstruction. We are currently performing further studies to investigate the role of NETs in pneumovirus disease.
