N2  - Introduction: Everolimus is a mammalian target of rapamycin inhibitor typically used in the treatment of various malignancies and in patients with solid organ transplantation. Everolimus, and more commonly sirolimus, have been documented in organ transplant recipients to cause pneumonitis and infectious pneumonia but less often organizing pneumonia. Our case demonstrates organizing pneumonia due to everolimus therapy for angiomyolipomas in a patient with tuberous sclerosis with TSC2 gene mutation and probable TSC-associated lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM). Case report: A 38 year-old female with a history of tuberous sclerosis, seizures, bilateral renal angiomyolipomas, stage 3 chronic kidney disease, hypertension, and seasonal allergies presented for initial evaluation of possible LAM. She had no respiratory complaints except occasional dry cough. Her home medications included everolimus, started about 6 month prior to presentation, carbamazepine, carvedilol, amlodipine, and folic acid. Physical exam was normal, including oxygen saturation and clear lungs bilaterally. Chest imaging showed few scattered cysts bilaterally. Pulmonary function testing revealed normal spirometry, normal lung volumes, and mildly decreased DLCO. Early or mild TSC-associated LAM was thought to be the cause of these findings. Her cough worsened over several months becoming persistent and intolerable, and repeat chest imaging was obtained revealing stable thin-walled cysts, with new multifocal consolidation and bronchiectasis (Image). Bronchoscopy was performed and transbronchial biopsies showed organizing pneumonia and chronic interstitial inflammation. BAL cultures were negative for infectious pathogens. She was started on prednisone, which has been gradually tapered, and everolimus was discontinued. She has had much improvement in her cough and follow up chest imaging is pending. Discussion: Lung disease due to everolimus is felt to be clinically under-recognized; however, some studies report rates as high as 25% of patients affected. A direct toxic effect or an autoimmune process have been proposed as potential mechanisms of lung injury, however the exact process is not completely understood. Some reported potential risk factors include higher medication doses, smoking, pre-existing lung disease, and renal disease. In our patient with probable mild TSC-associated LAM and stage 3 CKD, she possibly is high risk for everolimus-induced pulmonary toxicity. In all patients, it is essential to exclude infectious etiologies for abnormal pulmonary findings prior to making the diagnosis, and fortunately most patients will improve with reduction in dosage or discontinuation of everolimus with or without steroid therapy. (Figure Presented).
