N2  - Rationale: Chronic exposure to swine production facilities that harbor high levels of bioaerosols - air pollution comprised of microorganisms (bacteria, fungi and viruses), biologics (microbial components, plant and animal proteins), gases and other compounds - is associated with pulmonary dysfunction in facility workers. Airway oxidative stress, typified by higher levels of oxygen radicals than antioxidants, may contribute to tissue damage in patients suffering from agriculture-related chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and chronic bronchitis. Endogenous enzymatic antioxidants, such as superoxide dismutase (SOD) and catalase, work together to reduce oxygen radicals and are known to exist at low levels in asthmatic airways. The goal of this study was to evaluate morphology of and SOD expression in porcine airways to determine the impact of swine facility exposure on respiratory health. Methods: Freshly harvested tracheas from adult pigs reared indoors and outdoors were fixed, sectioned, stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) and Periodic acid Schiff (PAS). Tissue morphology and goblet cell density were determined. Superoxide dismutase (SOD) was detected via immunocytochemistry and immunohistochemistry in tracheobronchial tissues. The level of catalase activity and modulation of signal transduction was performed using porcine tissue protein extracts. Results: Tracheal epithelia of pigs reared indoors are densely packed with goblet cells with p-value = 0.0021 compared to outdoor-reared pigs, as determined by Student's paired T-test CI: 95%, with p-value<0.05 considered significant. The airways of outdoor-reared pigs had higher SOD2 expression than tracheas of indoor-reared pigs (p-value = 0.0088). Preliminary catalase activity assessment showed subtle differences between indoor and outdoor groups; however, we must investigate these findings further before a conclusion can be made. Proteome profiler arrays and western blot analysis of tracheal protein extracts revealed differential expression of SOD2 and other key oxidative stress molecules (e.g., heat shock proteins and lipid binding proteins) among indoor and outdoor pigs. Conclusions: Our observations demonstrate that higher density of goblet cells and differential expression of endogenous enzymatic antioxidants within the airway mucosa of pigs is linked to swine production system type. Moreover, these findings indicate that long-term exposure to indoor production environments, most likely attributed to bioaerosols therein, affect airway epithelium morphology and signaling dynamics. Maintenance of swine respiratory health is critical for overall animal health and production and endogenous antioxidant defenses play a key role in this process. The findings summarized here provide insight for understanding the impact of swine production environments on animal, worker and public health.
