N2  - Rationale: Occupational exposure to particles in bioaerosols from swine production facilities is a major cause of lung dysfunction in farmers and facility workers - many of which suffer from chronic lower respiratory diseases, such as asthma, chronic bronchitis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). The composition and complexity of bioaerosols depends heavily upon environment, but are generally mixtures of microorganisms (e.g., bacteria and viruses), microbial byproducts (e.g., endotoxin, peptidoglycan), plant and animal proteins, gases, metals and other inorganic compounds. Alveolar macrophages serve as a first line of defense again particles that deposit in the lungs. The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of combination exposure of swine facility dust extract and metallic nanoparticles (NPs) - representing aerosolized metals - on cell viability and oxidative stress in lung cells and macrophages in vitro. Methods: Swine facility dust extract [DE] was prepared by dissolving dust in saline, centrifugation and 0.2 micron filtration. Prior to cell exposures, NPs were characterized by scanning electron microscopy. Cultures of A549 and RAW 264.7 cells were exposed, separately and in combination, to DE [1%], magnesium oxide NPs [1-100 μg/mL] and DE+NP for 1-24 hours. Following exposures, cell viability, superoxide dismutase expression, intracellular hydrogen peroxide, superoxide and nitric oxide production was evaluated. Analysis of variance and Student's T-tests were utilized to evaluate differences among means with significance level set to p-value ≤ 0.05 for triplicate experiments. Results: Cell viability assays (Live/Dead assay) clearly indicated a dose-response relationship for exposure to NPs and DE+NP for both cell types. The expression of SOD was diminished in A549 cells exposed to DE+NP compared to control (p-value =0.0182). Additionally, there was increased superoxide production within mitochondria of DE, NP and NP+DE exposed macrophages as determined by MitoSox Red staining, which selectively detects superoxide, but not other radicals. In macrophages, NO production increased after 1 and 4 hours in cultures exposed DE and DE+NP compared to control, and after 4 hours for NPs only exposure. There was no difference in NO levels in lung cells. Conclusions: These findings demonstrate that agricultural bioaerosols and NPs induce oxidative stress in lung cells and macrophages. The results presented herein demonstrate that combinations of DE and NP reduced toxic effects of NPs alone, which may be explained by the tendency of NPs to aggregate and form larger particles. More studies are necessary to fully understand the role of metal-containing bioaerosols on pulmonary function.
