N2  - Background: A 63 year-old male current smoker with COPD presented with three days of progressive shortness of breath. Of note, the week prior to admission he had been cleaning his mother's moldy trailer and subsequently developed a non-productive cough and drenching night sweats. On initial presentation he was hypotensive and hypoxemic, and his chest x-ray revealed dense right lower lobe opacification. He was started on ceftriaxone and azithromycin for community-acquired pneumonia, and given intravenous fluid for hypotension. His hypoxemia rapidly progressed, and upon arrival at our institution, he required intubation and mechanical ventilation. His laboratory studies revealed leukocytosis with a left shift, elevated creatinine, and lactic acidosis. His intensive care unit course was characterized by refractory hypotension despite multiple vasopressors and vigorous fluid resuscitation. He had worsening oxygenation despite an FIO2 of 1.0, high PEEP, and neuromuscular blockade, along with worsening acidosis. He was switched to piperacillin-tazobactam and vancomycin when the gram stain from his sputum sample revealed gram negative rods. Unfortunately, his downward course continued, and he died 24 hours after admission. An autopsy confirmed the presence of pneumonia, and his sputum culture grew pan-sensitive Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Discussion: Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia is a rare cause of pneumonia in previously healthy individuals and only 12 cases were reported in the medical literature from 1968-2000 [1]. The presenting characteristics of the patients were: middle-age, active smoker, cough, pleuritic chest pain, fever, sputum production, and hemoptysis [1]. The mortality associated with community-acquired Pseudomonas pneumonia ranges from 30-60% and many have a fulminant hospital course (median time of death 11 hours) [1]. In studies of patients with pneumonia requiring ICU admission, Pseudomonas was the third most common causative agent [2]. In our patient, Pseudomonas aeruginosa coverage was started only after the sputum gram stain revealed gram-negative rods. In many reported cases, Pseudomonas pneumonia was fatal despite initial empiric treatment. We conclude that coverage for Pseudomonas aeruginosa should be considered in active smokers with or without known structural lung disease who present with hypoxemic respiratory failure and rapidly evolving septic shock.
