N2  - Mycoplasma pneumonia is a well-recognized pathogen known to be responsible 15% of all cases of community-acquired pneumonias (CAP). The overall rate of CAP in adults is approximately 6.11 cases per-1000-persons per-year, is higher for men and increases with age. Most cases occur during the winter and vary by geographic location. In 2005, pneumonia and influenza combined were the 8th most common cause of death in the United States. Mortality varies with geographic location (United States 7.3 % and Latin America 13.3 %) being highest for those patients who require hospitalization, with a 30-day mortality rate of 23%. A 32 year-old nonsmoker man presented to the emergency room after 5 days of general malaise, myalgia, fever, nausea, jaundice and one episode of non-bloody vomit. He also noted loss of appetite, sore throat, intermittent shortness of breath and dark brown color urine. Physical exam shows right upper quadrant abdominal pain, knife-like intermittent, 8/10 with epigastric irradiation. It was reproducible to superficial palpation which worsened with coughs and oral intake. Other symptoms such as chest pain, palpitations, diarrhea, constipation, changes in stool color, weight loss, melena, hematochezia, increased abdominal girth where all denied. He denied recent sick contacts, exposure to animal's or raw food intake. No history of alcohol consumption or intravenous drug abuse. Initial work up demonstrated elevated liver enzymes with a cholestatic pattern. A hepatobiliary scan was negative for cholecystitis or ducts dilation. Other causes of acute hepatitis were excluded. On third admission day, he presented mild cough, bibasilar crackles, desaturation of 90 % with new onset erythematous blanching and not pruritic rash over upper thorax area. Chest imaging confirmed bilateral pleural effusions, groundglass infiltrates and patchy pulmonary opacities at left lung base with peribronchovascular nodular patchy infiltrate in the right upper lobe. Bronchoscopy demonstrated foamy secretions seen over left lower lobe superior segment. No pathogen identified. Further studies ruled out other gastrointestinal etiologies related causes. Serological tests, IgG and IgM, returned positive consistent with mycoplasma pneumonia. Patient completed antibiotic therapy and discharged home. Several extrapulmonary manifestations have been documented in the literature including the skin, joints, central nervous system, cardiovascular system, and the gastrointestinal tract. Hence its occurrence could be the first sign of infection with Mycoplasma pneumonia, it appears after respiratory findings. However, cholestatic hepatitis, as our case illustrates is an unusual presentation previously documented in the pediatric literature. Ours is the first in an adult.
