N2  - Introduction: Cryptococcal infections are among the most common opportunistic infections found in patients with AIDS, even after the many advances made in HIV/AIDS treatment. The most common manifestation of cryptococcal disease is meningoencephalitis but pulmonary and disseminated disease are not uncommon. Pulmonary Cryptococcus infections have varied radiographic appearances including diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates, isolated lobar infiltrates and cavitary lesions, while very few cases have been reported of a miliary appearance on chest radiographs. Case Desription: A 55 year-old male with HIV/AIDS and CD4 count <20 presented with a few days of shortness of breath and cough. The patient was lethargic, unable to provide detailed history. His vital signs were all within normal limits and pulmonary exam was only notable for reduced respiratory effort. Chest x-ray and follow up CT scan both demonstrated a diffuse, miliary, nodular pattern concerning for Mycobacterium tuberculosis (TB) infection. The patient underwent bronchoscopy with broncheoalvelor lavage (BAL) prior to starting empiric treatment for miliary TB. Due to the patient's continued lethargy and significant immunocompromise, a serum cryptococcal antigen was ordered and returned positive. The patient's cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) was strongly positive for Cryptococcus neoformans and the patient was started on amphotericin B. Subsequently, the patient's BAL fungal culture and blood cultures also grew Cryptococcus demonstrating disseminated disease. Subsequent AFB cultures of sputum, BAL fluid, blood and CSF all failed to grow M. tuberculosis. A transbronchial biopsy was also performed showing fungal elements but no evidence of tuberculosis infection. The patient's antituberculous medications were stopped early in his course due to absence of laboratory evidence for TB and hepatotoxicity. The patient developed increased intracranial pressure during treatment requiring serial lumbar punctures which continued to grow budding Cryptococcus. Due to this apparent treatment failure, flucytosine was added to the patient's regimen and amphotericin was transitioned to liposomal formulation. With this regimen, the patient began to show clinical and radiographic improvement. Discussion: A miliary, nodular pattern on lung imaging is classically associated with Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection. However, this patient's workup presents strong evidence that Cryptococcus neoformans was indeed the causative organism for his presentation while Mycobacterium tuberculosis failed to manifest despite aggressive testing. There have been very few reported cases of miliary cryptococcal disease but this case demonstrates the importance of maintaining a broad differential diagnosis and avoiding presumptive diagnoses, especially in immunocompromised patients with the potential for multiple opportunistic infections. (Figure Presented).
