N2  - Introduction: Aspergillus species can produce a wide spectrum of pulmonary disorders. However empyema is an extremely rare presentation of this fungal infection. We report an unusual case of Aspergillus empyema thoracis in a middle-age woman with history of lung surgery. Case description: A 63-year old Hispanic woman with history of cryptogenic cirrhosis, diabetes mellitus and recurrent exudative pleural effusions of unknown etiology, requiring thoracotomy with decortication and right lower lobe wedge resection two years prior. Pleural fluid analysis, pleural biopsy and histopathological exam from resected lung were negative for malignancy and infection, including tuberculosis. The patient presented to our emergency department with three months of fatigue and cough. Cough was initially nonproductive, however became productive of yellow sputum 2 weeks prior to admission. She had completed a four day course of Levofloxacin prescribed during a previous emergency department visit at another institution. On the day of admission, patient reported one episode of hemoptysis, but denied other symptoms. Her vital signs were normal and physical examination was significant for decreased air entry over right lung field. Laboratory results showed mild pancytopenia including WBC 3.6K/uL and negative HIV test. CT chest revealed a moderate size right loculated hydropneumothorax with nodular pleural thickening (Figure-1). Patient subsequently underwent IR-guided chest tube placement. Analysis of pleural fluid was consistent with empyema. She was started on intravenous Meropenem and Vancomycin. Repeat CT chest four days later showed decrease in size of air-filled cavity, but revealed a new area of loculated fluid. Bacterial culture was negative, but cytology was significant for numerous fungal hyphea suggestive of Aspergillous species. Oral Voriconazole therapy was initiated and patient was immediately transferred for surgical intervention. Discussion: Aspergillus empyema is a rare entity caused by a rupture of an aspergilloma or a complication of a preexisting chronic empyema.1 In the past, majority of cases occurred in immunocompromised patients or those with history of tuberculosis, of which none were pertinent to this patient. A retrospective study of patients with fungal empyemas showed abdominal diseases, bronchopulmonary infections and chest surgeries as major risk factors. It also reported increasing incidence of this rare entity, especially in hospitalized patients.2 Although combination of antifungal therapy and surgery improves patients' outcomes, mortality rate remains high. Increase level of suspicion is required to ensure timely diagnosis and treatment of this severe disease. (Figure Presented).
