N2  - Introduction: Progressive inflammatory lung diseases such as necrotizing pneumonia, extensive bronchiectasis, fungal or mycobacterial infections may cause irreversible changes in the lung parenchyma and lead to destruction of the whole or a major part of a lung. This process has been termed “Destroyed lung” in literature but has rarely been reported in children. Case Presentation: A 14-year-old female, with no significant past medical history, presented to an outside emergency department with one week of dry cough, congestion and self limited epistaxis. Vital signs and pulse oximetry were normal. Decreased breath sounds were noted without respiratory distress. A chest radiograph was interpreted as a large right-sided pneumothorax with tension and underlying bullae, causing leftward mediastinal shift. She was transferred to our center where CT scan of the chest suggested numerous large bullae causing compressive atelectasis of the right lung and leftward mediastinal shift. The left lung appeared normal. Pediatric Surgery performed an urgent right-sided video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) with partial lung decortication. During the procedure, she was noted to have an entrapped right lung that minimally expanded with positive pressure ventilation, along with multiple thick septations and scar tissue in the thoracic cavity, suggesting a longstanding condition. Flexible bronchoscopy revealed no airway lesions. Serology, bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and pleural biopsy specimens were negative for active inflammation, bacterial, fungal and mycobacterial infections or malignancy. Given absence of active disease, treatment was not considered necessary. At follow-up visits in Pediatric Pulmonary clinic, one month and three months later, chest radiographs were unchanged from initial presentation and her lung did not re-expand. Spirometry demonstrated restrictive lung disease. She remains asymptomatic, actively involved in sports and is habituated to functioning with essentially one lung. Discussion: Our patient resided in Arizona for twelve years. She did not have a history of any significant past respiratory infections or risk factors for tuberculosis exposure. Imaging did not demonstrate bronchiectasis, lymphadenopathy, lung nodules or cavities. Diagnostic tests did not reveal a possible etiology. Coccidioides is known to be endemic in southwestern USA and can give rise to lung inflammation with an indolent course. This raised the possibility of a remote coccidioidomycosis infection in our patient which explains the involvement of only one lung with extensive destruction and fibrotic sequelae. Complete absence of symptoms and normal growth precludes any further workup (lung biopsy), or intervention (pneumonectomy) at this time. (Figure Presented).
