N2  - Introduction Human Rhinovirus (HRV) infection may cause immunomodulation in the lower respiratory tract, predisposing to co-infections with other organisms. We describe a case of disseminated coccidioidomycosis in a patient with concomitant HRV pneumonia. Case report A 23 year-old woman with lupus nephritis was admitted to the hospital with intermittent fever and dry cough for 3 weeks. She had not improved on levofloxacin. She was also taking prednisone for lupus nephritis. Physical examination revealed tachycardia, rales in right infrascapular region, tender left ankle swelling and subcutaneous nodules on forehead and elbow. Laboratory tests revealed leukocytosis (13,000 cells/cu.mm) with a neutrophilic predominance (96%) and lymphopenia. Chest x-ray showed a diffuse reticulo-nodular pattern. A computerized tomography (CT) scan of the chest demonstrated diffuse micronodules and ground glass opacities. Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) revealed HRV on Reverse Transcriptase-Polymerase Chain Reaction and Coccidioides immitis on culture. Tissue culture from skin nodules and blood cultures grew C. immitis/posadasii. She was started on fluconazole and amphotericin B, which improved her symptoms initially. However, on hospital day 5, she developed respiratory failure and the acute respiratory distress syndrome. Mechanical ventilation with high PEEP was unsuccessful and extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) was initiated. Unfortunately, she succumbed to her illness on day 7. Discussion HRV inhibits antigen specific T-cell proliferation, T-cell cytotoxicity, and potentially other processes that are dependent on ICAM-l/LFA-I binding. This enhances viral replication, and also contributes to increased susceptibility to other infections. Cell mediated immunity plays a critical role in protection against coccidioidomycosis. Studies in immunocompromised population indicate HRV pneumonia is associated with 60 % co-infection rate, which increases morbidity and mortality. Accordingly, corticosteroid use and viral pneumonia may have both contributed to the development of fungal pneumonia in our patient. Though aspergillosis has been described with HRV pneumonia, we did not find any case report of co-infection with coccidioidomycosis. Further investigation is required to determine the association between viral pneumonia and infection with endemic mycoses. Conclusion Immunomodulation by HRV has been implicated in bacterial co-infections, COPD and asthma exacerbations. Further research to determine exact role of HRV in fungal co-infections may help us understand the immune defect in disseminated coccidioidomycosis.
