N2  - Case: A 20-year-old female presented with a one-week history of malaise, rhinorrhea, and productive cough (yellow sputum). Her history was significant for premature birth, asthma (no intubations), anxiety, and seizures. She denied alcohol, tobacco, and drugs. Home medications include albuterol, levetiracetam, and topiramate. Her oxygen saturation was 79% on room air at admission; other vital signs were normal. Exam showed nontoxic appearance, bilateral crackles, decreased breath sounds, and bibasilar dullness to percussion. Laboratories showed leukocytosis (WBC 12,400/μL). Chest radiography revealed bilateral infiltrates, bibasilar consolidation, and cardiomegaly. Chest CT angiogram showed no pulmonary emboli, but confirmed extensive consolidation concerning for pneumonia (Fig. 1). Echocardiogram was essentially normal. ABG showed mild metabolic acidosis and hypoxemia. She failed bilevel positive-pressure ventilation and was intubated on hospital day 1, and a diagnosis of moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS; PaO2/FiO2 108 mm Hg) was made. Her respiratory status improved while on a proning protocol. She was extubated on hospital day 10, and was discharged home after 17 days of hospitalization. Out of a battery of tests for possible pathogens only human metapneumovirus returned positive. Chest CT one month later showed near-complete resolution of bilateral airspace opacities. Discussion: Human metapneumovirus (hMPV) is gaining notoriety as a cause of upper respiratory illness. First formally described in 2001, hMPV is a single-stranded, negative-sense RNA virus from the pneumovirinae family (along with respiratory synctivial virus [RSV]). hMPV infects type 2 alveolar and bronchiolar epithelial cells. Infection is characterized by alveolar and interstitial inflammation. Like RSV, hMPV is ubiquitous in children, with 100% seropositivity by age 5. Presentation is nonspecific: fever, cough, and rhinorrhea are common initial symptoms. Treatment is strictly supportive. Despite its link to mild respiratory illness, hMPV is not generally the cause of severe respiratory compromise in healthy adults. Poor baseline health has been associated with severe cases and current data suggest hMPV infection accounts for 4-12% of COPD, 7% of asthma, and 4% of pneumonia admissions in adults. This raises the question of whether or not we are missing a potential vaccine target that could significantly reduce morbidity and mortality of hMPV. hMPV must be considered a possible cause of respiratory failure, especially in patients with underlying pulmonary disease. In many hospitals, hMPV is not presently part of the adult viral respiratory panel and a pediatric panel should be ordered if there is a high index of suspicion for hMPV. (Figure Presented).
