N2  - RATIONALE: Subclinical bacterial infections may increase the risk for Chronic Lung Allograft Dysfunction (CLAD) or contribute to its progression. Aztreonam for Inhalation (AZLI) has been shown to improve lung function in cystic fibrosis patients and reduce Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) lung burden. We conducted a prospective multicenter study to assess the safety and impact of AZLI on lung function in lung transplant recipients with CLAD. METHODS: Adult, first lung transplant recipients from Duke University or UCLA with recent onset of CLAD completed 3, 28-day courses of AZLI administered in an alternating month on/month off fashion over 5 months. Lung function, assessed by FEV1, and safety data were prospectively measured in clinic at baseline and month 5. Nonparametric paired t-tests were used to evaluate within-subject changes to FEV1 from baseline to month 5. RESULTS: Thirty subjects were enrolled at Duke (n=18) and UCLA (n=12) from 4/2013 to 5/2014. At the time of analysis, data collection was complete for 28 subjects. The cohort was predominantly bilateral lung recipients (75%) transplanted for restrictive (54%), obstructive (32%), or cystic disease (14%) with median CLAD onset of 3.34 (IQR=2.36-7.02) years posttransplant. Thirty-two percent of subjects had a posttransplant history of lung PA. Upon starting AZLI, the median time in CLAD was 102 days (IQR=62, 212). At study baseline, the median FEV1 was 1.91L (IQR=1.61, 2.59). The within-subject change to FEV1 from baseline to month 5 was not statistically different. Median absolute difference in FEV1 was a loss of 0.02L (IQR= -0.15, +0.09), constituting a median percent decrease of 1.22% (IQR= -8.52%, +5.17%). Ten patients experienced a decrease in lung function of ≥5%, 8 remained stable (±4.99%), and 7 had an increase of ≥5%. Treatment related adverse events (AEs) were all mild in severity and occurred in 43% of subjects. Cough (36%) and dry mouth (7%) were the most frequent. CONCLUSION: AZLI in lung transplant recipients with recent onset of CLAD was generally well tolerated and in a majority of patients associated with stabilization of FEV1 over a five month period. While limited by an open-label, non-randomized study design, our results contrast with the expected progression of FEV1 loss in patients with CLAD and suggest further prospective studies of AZLI in the treatment of CLAD are warranted.
