N2  - Introduction: Recurrent exacerbation with increased airway inflammation is the hallmark of bronchiectasis. The prevalence of identifiable organisms from sputum during exacerbation of bronchiectasis is unknown. The identification of a pattern of clinical features associated with a specific organism during exacerbation of bronchiectasis may allow more timely appropriate treatment and use as a surrogate marker. Aims and objectives: To study the sputum microbiological and clinical profiles of patients admitted to hospital during acute exacerbation of bronchiectasis. Method: A retrospective study on all adult patients admitted to hospital with acute exacerbation of bronchiectasis in 2011. Patients' demography, sputum microbiology, spirometric lung function, clinical features during exacerbation of bronchiectasis, length of hospital stay (LOS), intensive care unit (ICU) admission and mortality were analysed. Results: We studied 189 patients with 320 episodes of exacerbation of bronchiectasis. There were 116 (62%) female patients. The mean (SD) age of the patient was 70.8(11.7) years. The median (IQR) of percent predicted FEV1 was 45(37-55) and the LOS was 5(4-7) days. 76% of the exacerbations showed no growth on sputum culture. The commonest organism cultured from the sputum was non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) (8.4%), followed by klebsiella pneumoniae (3.8%) and pseudomonas aeruginOSA (PsA) (3.1%). Cough was the commonest presenting complaint (78%), followed by increased sputum production (58%), dyspnoea (57%), fever (31%), haemoptysis (27%) and chest pain (13%). 2 patients developed spontaneous pneumothorax. Sputum growth of klebsiella pneumoniae was more likely associated with the presence of fever (p=0.0211), longer LOS (p=0.023) but less likely to have increased sputum production (p=0.0314) than patients with no growth. NTM was associated with haemoptysis (p=0.0408) but less likely with dyspnoea than patients with no growth (p=0.0141). Sputum growth of mycobacterium abscessus was associated with a longer LOS than patients with no growth (p=0.032). Body mass index (BMI) was significantly lower in patients with PsA than without (p=0.023). The presence of PsA and MRSA was significantly associated with mortality (p=0.044 and p=0.0233 respectively). There was no association between the presence of organisms in sputum, spirometric lung function and intensive care unit admissions. Conclusion: This study showed that NTM was the commonest organism isolated from sputum during exacerbation of bronchiectasis. There was a tendency of having fever, longer LOS but less expectoration during exacerbation in patients with klebsiella pneumoniae grew from sputum. Haemoptysis was common in patients with NTM. The presence of PsA and MRSA was associated with higher mortality.
