N2  - RATIONALE Sputum is heavily utilized for the diagnosis of pulmonary infections. However, this has many limitations. Sputum is often contaminated, necessitating decontamination in the laboratory. This may decrease the sensitivity for diagnosing infections such as tuberculosis. In addition, many patients are unable to produce sputum. Deton Corp. devised a novel, portable (fits in pocket), user-friendly “cough collector” device which bypasses the collection of sputum samples by collecting airborne bacteria from cough aerosol. (Figure presented) The patient coughs into the mouthpiece. The bag stores the cough volume while a one-way valve prevents back flow. Pump suction pulls the cough volume through the collector. Pseudomonas cells in cough droplets deposit on the collection surface through inertial impaction. Cough collection offers advantages over sputum: (1) Collected material is potentially free of the contaminants in sputum that make analysis difficult and less useful. This avoids harsh treatments needed to remove contaminating molecules and bacteria. (2) The cough collection can concentrate bacteria in a small volume of buffer or on a polycarbonate surface that support the viability of organisms; whereas sputum concentration and medium cannot be altered. (3) Cough collection can be performed in patients who cannot produce sputum. We conducted a pilot study using the cough collector in cystic fibrosis(CF) patients with this hypothesis: Cough aerosol samples are at least as reliable as sputum samples at detecting pseudomonas colonization of the airways. METHODS Patients with CF were recruited into the study if they were above 18years of age, did not have recent hemoptysis or pneumothorax, and were providing a sputum sample as part of their routine testing. Each patient was asked to cough into the sterile mouthpiece of the cough collector for several minutes, thus providing the “cough sample”. The polycarbonate surface on which aerosols are captured was removed for processing. The captured cells were lysed and the DNA was extracted. PCR amplification was then performed on the oprL gene specific to pseudomonas aeroginosa. This was then compared to the culture results of sputum samples obtained on the same day. RESULTS Out of 24 patients whose same day sputum grew pseudomonas aeroginosa, 23 provided cough samples that were positive for pseudomonas with PCR. Out of 6 patients whose same day sputum culture was negative for pseudomonas, 3 provided cough samples that were PCR-positive, 2 were negative, and 1 was indeterminate. CONCLUSION The cough collector demonstrated high sensitivity in detecting pseudomonas in the airways of CF patients. It is an exciting new way for detecting colonizing or infecting organisms. Another study is currently under way in patients suspected of having tuberculosis using molecular-based rapid diagnostics performed on cough samples.
