N2  - Clostridium sordellii, a ubiquitous anaerobic bacterium, rarely causes human infections. Most common infections are skin, soft tissue& female genital tract. 45 year old male with alcoholic cardiomyopathy(EF 10-15% s/p AICD), V.fib arrest, rheumatoid arthritis &atrial fibrillation presented with worsening dyspnea, leg swelling &orthopnea. No fever, cough, wheezing, chest pain, palpitations, trauma &reported diet &medication compliance(Furosemide, Carvedilol, Lisinopril, Spironolactone, Aspirin, Digoxin, Methotrexate, Prednisone). Examination showed afebrile cachectic man with BP107/80, pulse74, respiratory rate16 &saturation 97%on room air. Bibasilar crackles with reduced breath sounds, moderate pitting pedal edema till mid-calf, irregular pulse were present. Labs were troponin-I 0.11,ALT 111,AST 112,Na 134,K 4.6,BUN 22,creatinine 0.95,WBC 11.6(83/9/7),Hemoglobin 13.3,BNP 2290. Chest X-ray(CXR) showed cardiomegaly with pulmonary vascular congestion, consolidation in lung bases &blunting of the costo-phrenic angles. Treatment was started for CHF exacerbation &CAP. Serial CXR showed enlargement of left sided opacity with worsening effusion; &CT chest showed loculated left pleural effusion, partial collapse of lung base, scattered tiny pockets of gas within effusion, infiltrates in left lung base &scattered in right lung, &small thrombus in right ventricular apex. There was no definite lung abscess or necrotizing pneumonia. Pulmonology was consulted for persistent infiltrate, worsening effusion &possible empyema. Thoracentesis was performed &800cc clear straw colored fluid was aspirated. Analysis showed pH7.3, LDH358(Plasma 162), glucose94, albumin1.3(plasma 2.0), protein2.4(plasma 5.5). Cell count not done as sample was clotted. Cultures showed C. sordellii, which was considered contaminant, &patient was discharged on Levofloxacin for CAP. Patient came in 1 month later for a follow-up &didn't have any complains or abnormal physical examination findings but CTchest showed multi-loculated empyema in left oblique fissure &lower lobe with worsening infiltrate on left side, new infiltrate on right &RUL pulmonary embolism. WBC count with differential was unremarkable. Levofloxacin, Vancomycin &Piperacillin-Tazobactam were started&thoracic surgery was consulted but he was deemed high risk surgical candidate owing to cardiac status. Methotrexate &Prednisone were discontinued. IR inserted a pigtail in pleural cavity &pus drained slowly. Culture was positive for C.sordellii. Antibiotics were de-escalated to Piperacillin-Tazobactam &he clinically improved. Patient was discharged on Augmentin after removing pigtail &required 2months of oral antibiotics. English literature reports only 3previous cases of isolated pleuro-pulmonary infection caused by C. sordellii since 1977. Aspiration of oro-pharyngeal content or traumatic inoculation are usually the source of infection. Isolation of rare organism in pleural infection should always be clinically co-related with patient's pre-existing conditions, but it might be difficult to interpret when the patient does not have clinical signs or symptoms. (Figure Presented).
