N2  - INTRODUCTION: Clinical manifestations of lung cancer may overlap with those of pulmonary tuberculosis causing a delay in diagnosis and treatment. We present a patient evaluated for suspicion of malignancy and diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. CASE PRESENTATION: A 58year old woman, lifelong nonsmoker, presented with dry cough and significant weight loss for 5 months. She denied hemoptysis, dyspnea, fever, night sweats, recent travel, or sick contacts. She was initially treated with antibiotics for presumed community acquired pneumonia due to a left upper lung zone opacity visualized on a chest Xray. As this opacity was unresolving on a subsequent chest Xray, a computed tomography (CT) of the chest was performed revealing a 5 cm left upper lobe mass suspicious for malignancy (Image 1). Physical examination and laboratory tests on presentation were unremarkable. Bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound and transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) were then performed. Left upper transbronchial biopsies, brushings and bronchoalveolar lavage were performed. Complete mediastinal staging was also done using EBUS-TBNA. All specimens were negative for malignancy. Acidfast smears, bacterial, and fungal cultures were negative as well. A CT guided biopsy of the left upper lobe lesion was planned but acid fast cultures ultimately grew mycobacterium tuberculosis. She was subsequently started on antituberculosis therapy with resolution of her symptoms. DISCUSSION: The diagnosis and treatment of patients with pulmonary tuberculosis may be delayed due to atypical presentations and presumptive diagnosis of neoplasm as in our patient. This delay may result in an increase in patient mortality and infectivity in communities. In a retrospective study in which 2908 patients were referred for evaluation of lung cancer, 37 (1.3%) patients had been diagnosed with an infection, 10 (27%) of which with mycobacterial infection. Although infrequent, chronic smoldering pulmonary infections may mimic cancer in immunocompetent individuals due to their radiological appearance and should be kept in the differential especially when biopsies are negative for malignancy. Keeping this possibility in mind helps to establish the correct diagnosis early and reduce morbidity associated with delayed treatment. (Figure Presented).
