N2  - Introduction: Intravesicular instillation of bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG), an attenuated strain of Mycobacterius bovis, is used as treatment of bladder cancer. Described is a case of BCG-osis, a rare pulmonary side effect from BCG therapy. Case Description: A 73 year old never smoking man presented with four weeks of fever, dry cough, malaise and weight loss. He had a history of renal cancer status post left nephroureterectomy and received his third round of intravesicular BCG installation six weeks prior. He was treated with antibiotics for suspected pneumonia by his primary care physician. Due to lack of clinical improvement, he was admitted for further evaluation. On presentation he was afebrile, normotensive without hypoxemia. His physical exam showed diffuse bilateral crackles in all lung fields. Lab work including complete blood count, metabolic profile, autoimmune serologies, quantiferon gold and cultures were negative. His chest radiograph revealed bilateral miliary nodularity predominantly in the middle and lower lung zones. Computed tomography of the chest showed diffuse miliary nodular opacities with reticular changes at the bases. He underwent bronchoscopy. BAL cell count showed 400 nucleated cells with 81% lymphocytes and 19% macrophages. Transbronchial biopsies revealed patchy granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and no evidence of malignancy. Acid fast bacilli and Grocott's methenamine silver stains were negative. The patient was treated for pneumonia, stopped further BCG therapy, and discharged. Mycobacterial and fungal cultures were negative. At follow-up he was asymptomatic, but repeat imaging two months later showed minimal improvement, therefore he was treated with steroids. Since PET scan revealed metastatic disease in adrenal glands, bones and abdominal lymph nodes, he was ultimately started on isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol and levofloxacin. Discussion: BCG immunotherapy has been used in the treatment of bladder cancer since 1976. Pulmonary side effects occur in <1% of patients and are thought to be the result of infection with Mycobacterium bovis or a hypersensitivity reaction causing granulomatous pneumonitis. Chest imaging is variable, with case reports describing interstitial pneumonitis, ARDS, and miliary disease. As the pathogenesis is unclear, patients are often treated with anti-mycobacterial medications with or without corticosteroids. Although there is no treatment guidelines established to reduce the risk of recrudescence of symptomatic BCG-osis, given the patient's persistent radiographic abnormalities and metastatic disease with plans to start chemotherapy, he was placed on both steroids and antimicrobials. Physicians should be aware of BCG-osis as a potential pulmonary complication from BCG immunotherapy. (Figure Presented).
