N2  - Introduction: For the diagnosis of TB, WHO recommends that sputum smear microscopy is used initially. However, this is not a sensitive technique, and only 57% of new cases of pulmonary TB are smear-positive. In Brazil, 24% to 30% of pulmonary TB cases among adults are smear-negative pulmonary TB (TPBN). The mortality of these patients can reach 25% in populations with high prevalence of HIV infection, which may be largely a consequence of delayed diagnosis. In addition, although patients with positive sputum smears are considered more infectious, 10-20% of the transmissions of TB at the population level are attributable to cases of TPBN. The objective of this study is to describe the clinical, laboratorial and radiological characteristcs of patients with TPBN in a region with high prevalence of TB and HIV. Methods: A retrospective cross-sectional study. A search for cases by culture results that grew Mycobacterium tuberculosis will be held. These results will be provided at the Microbiology Laboratory of the hospital. Subsequently, the electronic medical records of these patients will be reviewed and those who have negative sputum smear, configuring a case of TPBN according to the WHO, will be included in the study. A standardized form will be filled out for each patient, with demographic, clinical, laboratorial and radiological data. Results: Until now 215 patients were included in the study. Of these, 131 (60.9%) were male and 170 (79.1%) were white. The mean age was 45.8 ± 17.4 years. One hundred and sixty-four (76.3%) patients were hospitalized and 51 (23.7%) were outpatients. Eighty-six (38.6%) patients were HIV positive and 39 (18.1%) had a previous history of TB. Cough (137 [63.7%]), weight loss (123 [57.2%]) and fever (104 [48.4%]) were the most common symptoms. The median duration of symptoms before diagnosis was 30 days (interquartile range 20.8 to 90 days). The most common radiological patterns were reticulonodular infiltrates (68 [35.4%]) and consolidations (54 [28.1%]). Conclusions: The preliminary data from this study show that most cases of TPBN on this sample were men, and white with a large percentage of HIV-positive patients. The duration of symptoms before diagnosis was long, corroborating previous data on delayed diagnosis of these patients.
