N2  - Rationale Tuberculous pleural effusion accounts for approximately 5 percent of disease due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis and is the second most common form of extra-pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) after lymphatic involvement. Methods The TB database of patients attending the TB clinic from 2009 to 2013 were reviewed. Data collected included pleural fluid analysis, TB culture of sputum, pleural fluid and bronchial washings. AFB results of pleural fluid, bronchial washings and sputum were recorded. Histopathology results of those referred for thorascopic pleural biopsy were also noted Results 28 patients were identified. 17 were male, 11 were female. All patients had symptoms suggestive of TB, i.e. cough, weight loss, night sweats. Some patients reported pleuritic chest pain. All 28 patients were HIV negative. All patients had positive mantoux tests and were referred for bronchoscopy and bronchial washings. They subsequently had a diagnostic thoracocentesis performed. Pleural fluid analysis revealed a mean ph of 7.32, protein 46 g/l, LDH 457 IU/L, and adenosine deaminase level of 54 IU/L. All patients were smear negative (sputum, bronchial washings and pleural fluid ). 20 patients had a documented lymphocytic exudative effusion (no malignant cells present). 9 of these patients cultured Mycobacterium tuberculosis on pleural fluid. 8 of the 28 were referred for thoracoscopic pleural biopsy. Histology revealed granulomatous inflammation suspicious for TB. One patient had a positive bronchial washing TB culture with negative sputum and pleural fluid TB culture. Conclusion Tuberculous pleural effusions are thought to result from a delayed hypersensitivity reaction to mycobacteria and mycobacterial antigens in the pleural space. As well as analysis of pleural ph, protein LDH and glucose, pleural fluid adenosine deaminase (ADA) level is most clinically useful and accessible. An ADA level should be sent along with the routine pleural fluid studies whenever TB is the suspected cause of the effusion. Even when the acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear and culture are negative, compatible routine studies along with an elevated ADA level may establish a presumptive diagnosis of tuberculous pleural effusion in the right clinical setting. Pleural biopsy is warranted in the setting of moderate to high suspicion for TB when pleural fluid evaluation is not diagnostic. Pleural biopsy offers the highest likelihood of a certain diagnosis, and a positive culture also provides information pertaining to drug sensitivities of the organism. Histologic examination and culture of pleural tissue is the most sensitive evaluation for pleural TB. The presence of pleural caseating granulomas on histologic examination is virtually diagnostic of TB pleural effusion. (Figure Presented).
