N2  - RATIONALE Mucus is a barrier to airway water loss and microbial invasion and serves to maintain homeostasis in the respiratory system. In patients with cystic fibrosis, the mucus secretions are thick and viscous, leading to abnormal mucus clearance, infection, inflammation, and airway remodeling. We present a longitudinal epidemiological study to examine biophysical and transport properties of CF sputa in relation to clinical parameters such as FEV1 and pathogen infection with the aim of developing a prognostic tool. METHODS We have collected expectorated sputum samples from cystic fibrosis patients from our institution over a period of several years at routine clinic visits. Sputum was then separated from saliva and samples were stored at -80oC prior to analysis. We present results for 3 patients enrolled in our study. After thawing, the rheometric properties of the CF sputa were analyzed using a dynamic rheometer (AR1500ex, TA Instruments). The elastic modulus G' and viscous modulus G' were measured across a range of angular frequency. We assessed transport properties of the CF sputum samples by measuring cough clearance (mm) using a Cough Simulation Device. We examined the relation between these biomechanical parameters in relation to FEV1 and pathogen infection. RESULTS We have shown a moderate to strong negative correlation between both the elastic modulus G' and viscous modulus G' with FEV1% predicted over time for all three subjects (n=8, 11, 13). We have also shown weak positive correlation with cough clearance and FEV1 and a weak negative correlation between both G' and G' with cough clearance. We did not find differences in the mean G' or G' for different types of pathogen infection (S. aureus, P. aeruginosa, etc). CONCLUSIONS In CF patients with decreased pulmonary function as evidenced by FEV1, their sputum becomes both more elastic and more viscous. There appears to be a negative correlation between both rheometric properties and FEV1; the implication is that as CF sputum becomes more elastic and viscous, it is more difficult to clear which perpetuates infection and inflammation and thus decline in lung function.
