N2  - Rationale. Exacerbations of COPD are a major unmet clinical need that result in extensive burdens on health care systems, particularly for patients characterized by a frequent exacerbation phenotype. Early detection and treatment of exacerbations can reduce exacerbation severity and improve clinical outcomes. We sought to evaluate serum biomarkers during the course of exacerbations to determine the relationship between inflammation and exacerbation frequency. Methods. Patients in the London COPD cohort record changes in respiratory symptoms on daily diary cards and are trained to recognize and present rapidly after the onset of exacerbation symptoms. Exacerbations were defined as an increase for two consecutive days in respiratory symptoms, with at least one major symptom (dyspnea, sputum purulence or volume) plus another major or minor symptom (wheeze, cold, sore throat, and cough). Exacerbation frequency was categorized as frequent (≥2/year, FE) or infrequent (0-1/year, IE) in the year prior to sampling. 1129 serum analytes were measured (Somalogic®) for samples collected during exacerbations (exacerbation presentation - day 56) and at baseline visits, with multiple exacerbations and baselines possible for a patient. Within-patient changes from baseline in serum analyte levels were tested in General Linear Models, stratified by exacerbation frequency group. Bacterial and rhinovirus (HRV) presence were determined by quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) analysis of spontaneously expectorated sputum. Results. In the FE group at exacerbation presentation, 6 analytes (serum amyloid A (SAA), hepcidin, C-reactive protein (CRP), LPS-binding protein (LBP), phospholipase A2 group IIA (PLAG2A), histone H1.2) were increased and 1 decreased (leukotriene hydrolase A4 (LTAH4)) at least 1.5-fold/baseline with p<0.05, with the dysregulation of SAA and LTA4H sustained through day 56. The increased analytes, except histone H1.2, were not significantly elevated in the IE group, with SAA and LBP changed significantly more in the FE group (p<0.05 vs. IE). In contrast, surfactant protein D was elevated at exacerbation presentation in the IE but not FE group. LTA4H was decreased and histone 1.2 increased similarly in both exacerbation groups. Within the FE group, SAA and hepcidin were elevated significantly more in patients with positive compared to negative sputum bacteriology (Figure 1), but were not associated with HRV positivity. Conclusions. Frequent exacerbators have a distinct pattern of inflammation at COPD exacerbation. Acute phase proteins, particularly SAA and hepcidin, were increased in FE patients at exacerbation presentation. Further research should consider novel therapies to target the increased inflammation seen at exacerbation in frequent exacerbators. (Figure Presented).
