N2  - Rationale: The isolation of Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) from respiratory cultures has been increasingly prevalent over recent decades. Despite ATS Consensus Guidelines released in 2007, the decision whether to treat can be complex in clinical practice. The aim of this study is to describe clinical characteristics of patients that required antibiotic treatment for MAC at a large cancer center. Methods: All non-HIV patients with MAC isolated from respiratory cultures between January 2008 and December 2011 were identified from medical records of Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center in New York City. Clinical characteristics, including gender, underlying cancer, symptoms, and radiographic findings of 100 consecutive patients were collected. MAC infection was defined as having associated clinical symptoms or cytopathological findings1. Results: Forty-two patients were identified with MAC infection. Of these, two had previously received antibiotics for MAC and were excluded from further analysis. Twenty patients had MAC identified from surgically-resected abnormalities initially thought to represent malignancy. Eight of the 20 remaining patients (40%) were treated for MAC with antibiotics. The percentage of patients receiving treatment for active cancer did not differ among those receiving antibiotics from those who did not. Positive acid fast staining was present among 50% of patients receiving antibiotics and 25% of those who did not. Common symptoms among those receiving antibiotics were dyspnea (6 patients), cough (5 patients), and hemoptysis (4 patients). Common radiographic abnormalities were nodules/masses (8 patients), bronchiectasis (5 patients), cavitary lesions (2 patients), and tree-in-bud (2 patients). In comparison to those not receiving antibiotics, patients receiving antibiotics tended to more likely have hemoptysis (50% vs. 17% of patients, p-value=0.07) and cavitary lesions (25% vs. 8% of patients, p-value=0.12), although perceived differences were not statistically significant. Two of the remaining 12 non-treated patients had severe symptoms and died from non-MAC infections shortly after MAC identification. Conclusions: The 2007 ATS Guidelines indicate that clinically significant symptoms are one of the major indications for treatment for MAC infection. More antibiotic-treated patients tend to have hemoptysis and cavitary disease on radiographs. Treated patients also tend to have more AFB positive smears, which has been suggested to be a surrogate marker for organism burden. Further studies, with appropriately larger sample sizes, will be necessary to determine if these conclusions are statistically valid.
