N2  - Introduction: Human Mesenchymal Stem/Stromal Cells (hMSCs) constitute a promising therapeutic strategy for sepsis and the Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome. MSCs modulate the immune response to reduce lung injury, and enhance the clearance of bacteria, in murine and rodent Escherichia coli pneumonia, and in the isolated human lung. The mechanisms by which MSCs exert beneficial effects are complex, and include their ability to modulate macrophage phenotype and function. A key function of macrophages is to phagocytose and clear invading microorganisms. Objective: To explore if hMSCs are capable of enhancing IgG mediated FcgR macrophages phagocytosis. Methods: Bone marrow hMSCs and peripheral blood monocytes were isolated from healthy donors. Monocytes were differentiated to macrophages over 5 days. Firstly, live E. coli was added to Mf. 15 minutes later, extracellular bacteria were washed. Intracellular E. coli, released from macrophages by lysis, were plated and colonies (CFU) counted at 24h. In the second assay we tested Fcg receptor mediated phagocytosis, by adding opsonized E. coli bioparticles (tagged with green fluorophore) to macrophages. Cells were fixed with 4% PFA, and streptavidin conjugate (tagged with red fluorophore) was added to enable visualization of extracellular bacteria. Images were captured using a confocal microscope. Counting of phagocytosed (green) or non-phagocytosed (red) E. coli was done at 7 randomly chosen fields/slide using Image-J software. Macrophages phagocytosis via complement receptor was assessed using opsonized sheep red blood cells, while phagocytosis via the scavenger receptor pathway was examined using carboxylate-modified flourospheres coupled with biotin. Results: Macrophages co-cultured with hMSCs engulfed significantly more E. coli than macrophages alone (control group) or macrophages co-cultured with fibroblasts. Macrophages co-cultured with hMSCs (on inserts or directly) engulfed approximately 2x more live bacteria than Mf alone (P<0.05). Confocal microscopy of PFA fixed macrophages demonstrated a significant increase in phagocytosed E. coli bioparticles (n=4) in macrophages co-cultured with hMSCs versus macrophage alone (300,299 ± 9229 particles in hMSC coculture group vs. 206,149 ± 11043 in macrophages alone, p < 0.001). Macrophages co-cultured directly in contact with hMSCs did not further enhance phagocytosis. Fibroblast co-culture had no effect on macrophages phagocytosis. Complement receptor mediated phagocytosis was only marginally enhanced by direct contact with MSCs only, while scavenger receptor mediated phagocytosis was markedly enhanced by direct contact with MSCs. Conclusion: Our study has shown that hMSCs enhance FcgR mediated and scavenger recptor mediated macrophages phagocytosis.
