N2  - Introduction ∗Sarcoidosis can present as a diffuse painless scrotal mass or can mimic acute epididymo-orchitis. Usually it appears with systemic manifestations of the disease. (3) Since it causes occlusion and fibrosis of the ductus epididymis, fertility may be affected. (4) ∗In the differential diagnosis of scrotal and epididymal masses, sarcoidosis is rarely considered. ∗We describe a case of systemic sarcoidosis presenting as a scrotal masses to emphasis their clinical aspects and discuss the differential diagnosis. Case 29 year old tobacco smoker white male from UK presenting with bilateral lower quadrant abdominal pain for 3 days, mild testicular pain for 2 days associated with night sweats, subjective fever, clear sputum cough in the last 1 week and 10 pounds weight loss in the last 2 months. Scrotal ultrasound showed four right hypoechoic lesions in the right testicle and varicocele, with normal arterial flow. CT chest revealed diffuse broncho lobular infiltrates and nodular densities in tree and Bud fashion in the apical segments of bilateral upper lobes and patchy infiltrates in the anterior and posterior segments of the upper lobe, paratracheal lymph nodes are noted. Patient was admitted for evaluation of tuberculosis, testicular cancer, lymphoma, and vasculitis related pathology. Laboratory data including Levels of Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), Beta-Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (βHCG), (ESR), Antinuclear antibody (ANA), Quantiferon, purified protein derivative (PPD), Acid fast bacilli (AFB), HIV RNA, Anti-citrullinated protein antibody (CCP), Rheumatoid factor (RF) all were within normal value or undetected. Transbronchial biopsy revealed noncaseating granuloma representing Sarcoid. KOH test showed no yeast or fungal elements. Angioconvert enzyme 135 (range 9-67) Patient was diagnosed with Sarcoidosis, improved within one week, was discharged home and followed up with in 2 weeks with no complaints. Discussion ∗Sarcoidosis can be the etiology for epididymitis, in some cases mycobacterium tuberculosis had been isolated in culture from either the lung or hilar lymph nodes. ∗Therapy for genitourinary sarcoidosis is controversial. Spontaneous resolution has been shown to occur. ∗The symptoms of pain and swelling are usually relieved by corticosteroid treatment. However, in most instances of symptomatic epididymal sarcoidosis, excisional biopsy is necessary for the cessation of pain. (5, 6) Conclusion ∗When intrascrotal masses are found in patients with confirmed sarcoidosis then surgical exploration is not mandatory unless the lesion continues to enlarge on corticosteroid therapy, then biopsy should be performed.(5).
