N2  - Pulmonary disease due to Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) occurs primarily in persons with underlying chronic lung disease. MAC infection also occurs in those with Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome (AIDS) but typically in disseminated form. Although MAC is frequently isolated from the respiratory tract of AIDS patients, it is uncommonly considered a pulmonary pathogen. The following case highlights the importance of maintaining MAC on the differential for pulmonary disease in AIDS. A 35 year-old-man diagnosed with AIDS 6 months prior to admission presents with 1 week of shortness of breath and chest discomfort. At the time of his original diagnosis his CD4+ cell count was 40 cells/microL and was treated for community acquired pneumonia. He was initiated on antiretroviral therapy (ART) and pneumocystis (PCP) and MAC prophylaxis. Review of symptoms revealed productive cough without hemoptysis and no fever, chills, weight loss or night sweats. The patient was well appearing with a normal pulmonary and cardiovascular exam. Vital signs were normal. Laboratory data revealed normal WBC, elevated ESR and CRP. CXR demonstrated a RUL opacity that was confirmed on thoracic computed tomography (CT) as a cavitary mass. The patient was isolated and three AFB sputum stains returned negative. Given the appearance of a cavitary mass, and concern for malignancy, a percutaneous biopsy was completed revealing granulomatous formation with necrosis and no malignant pathology. Within 7 days all three AFB cultures revealed MAC. Mycobacteria tuberculosis polymerase chain reaction testing (MTB-PCR) returned negative. Isolated pulmonary MAC is an uncommon presentation in AIDS. In a retrospective, multicenter study, conducted between 1987 and 1993, disseminated MAC was diagnosed in 200 people with AIDS. Five of the 200 also demonstrated pulmonary involvement to accompany the disseminated disease. Four patients were identified as having isolated pulmonary MAC based on the presence of a pulmonary infiltrate, two respiratory tract specimen or single lung biopsy cultures, and negative blood cultures. Review of literature reports 25 case reports of isolated pulmonary MAC in AIDS with features including pulmonary lymphadenopathy, infiltrates, endobronchial lesions and more rarely cavitary disease. There is also evidence that isolated pulmonary MAC is mostly a form of immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) so there may be debate on how to categorize the disease. One observational study from 1993 to 2004 revealed 51 cases of nontuberculous mycobacteria related IRIS. Fifteen cases were reported as isolated pulmonary MAC. Nevertheless, isolated pulmonary MAC is an important disease to recognize in AIDS. (Figure Presented).
