N2  - The cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) is a plasma membrane anion channel which, when mutated, causes the disease cystic fibrosis. Inflammation and infection conditions increase CFTR association and recruitment to the detergent-resistant membrane (DRM) fraction, suggesting a role for CFTR in the internalization of bacteria to help clear them from the lumen, and in the initiation of lipid raft-dependent epithelial cytokine and chemokine release. The presence of a CFTR population in the DRM fraction implies heterogeneity among CFTR molecules. Only one homogeneous population of CFTR channels was previously detected in the plasma membrane using single particle tracking, thus it remains uncertain if CFTR channels partition in lipid rafts microdomains under resting conditions and whether this influences their lateral mobility. Here, we quantified the distribution, the aggregation state and the mobility of CFTR both within and outside microdomains in the plasma membrane of primary human bronchial epithelial cells using live cell imaging, spatial image correlation spectroscopy (ICS) and a modified version of k-space image correlation spectroscopy (kICS). To identify the CFTR population which is in cholesterol-rich membrane microdomains, transport and partitioning dynamics were compared under control conditions and following cholesterol depletion or enrichment treatments. kICS analysis revealed two dynamically distinct populations of CFTR on the plasma membrane. One population showed confinement and had slow dynamics that were highly cholesterol dependent. The other, more abundant population, was less confined and diffused more rapidly. Cholesterol depletion reduced CFTR confinement, the fraction of CFTR in the confined population and the aggregation state of CFTR whereas these were all increased by elevating membrane cholesterol. The results show a clear dependence of CFTR distribution and dynamics on cholesterol and suggest that a fraction of CFTR partitions into lipid rafts under physiological conditions. Viral infection caused the nanoscale microdomains to fuse into large platforms, increased CFTR recruitment into these platforms and dramatically reduced its mobility. Fusion of lipid rafts into large platforms during infection may regulate CFTR channel activity or modulate its stability at the cell surface.
