N2  - The airway epithelium plays a critical role in lung function by forming a barrier between the external and internal environment, releasing inflammatory mediators in response to injury or pathogens, and clearing foreign particles through the mucociliary escalator. Disruption of this tissue is central to many diseases of the respiratory system, including cystic fibrosis (CF), asthma, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. The response of the lung epithelium to the environment is mediated by a network of transcription factors. Ets homologous factor (EHF) is an Ets family transcription factor expressed in the epithelial cells lining the respiratory tract. Its expression is increased in the bronchial epithelium in response to the presence of inflammatory mediators. EHF maps to the 5' end of an intergenic region that contains single nucleotide polymorphisms identified in a genome-wide association study to associate with lung disease severity in CF patients, suggesting it may play a role in modification of the lung phenotype in this population. EHF can act as a transcriptional activator or a repressor, however its targets in lung epithelial cells are largely uncharacterized. We utilized high-throughput methods to identify EHF targets genome-wide in a lung adenocarcinoma cell line (Calu-3) and primary human bronchial epithelial cells. Chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by sequencing (ChIP-seq) showed that the majority of EHF occupancy sites are intronic or intergenic and found in putative cis-regulatory elements. Peaks of EHF binding are frequently marked by histone modifications found at enhancers and have increased DNase hypersensitivity. EHF binds near genes enriched in pathways involved in epithelial junction formation, cell death, and cell motion. RNA-sequencing after EHF modulation showed significant alterations in the expression of genes involved in response to wounding, locomotory behavior, and adhesion. These changes in gene expression translated to differences in epithelial cell phenotype. EHF depletion slowed wound closure in a scratch assay and increased transepithelial resistance, a measure of epithelial barrier maintenance. These data suggest that EHF regulates genes in pathways critical for the lung epithelial response to injury, including wound closure, release of inflammatory mediators, and cell adhesion. Modulation of these pathways is a potential mechanism by which the 11p13 region can influence lung disease severity in CF patients.
