N2  - Background: CF patients are susceptible to airway infection by nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM), which are difficult or impossible to treat, and are associated with significant morbidity. NTM are ubiquitous environmental bacteria that are both aquatic and terrestrial, and are recovered routinely from water supply systems and soil. Currently, mechanisms of human acquisition and potential patient-to-patient transmission of NTM are unknown, but accumulating evidence supports the potential for direct or indirect transmission between CF patients. A lack of understanding of how NTM can be spread within the modern environment has limited the ability of CF centers to provide evidence-based guidance to reduce the risks of acquisition and transmission. Methods: We tested the hypothesis that NTM are physically associated with environmental particulates using environmental sampling and an in vitro model system. A preliminary sampling study was performed in locations across home, office and clinic environments with total exposure times ranging from 5-8 hours. A personal air-sampling pump fitted with an IOM sampler was carried by a CF clinician in four locations: 1) home-indoors, 2) home-outdoors, 3) office-lab, and 4) hospital-clinic including direct CF patient contact. Air sampling and high-throughput sequencing was used to characterize the bioaerosol microbiome in the clinical and home environmental samples. DNA was isolated from the filters, the V4 hyper-variable region of the 16s rRNA was amplified by PCR, and amplicons were sequenced on the Ion Torrent Personal Genome Machine. Sequence reads were compared to multiple 16S rRNA sequence databases with the QIIME analysis software using an open reference operational taxonomic unit (OTU) picking analysis pipeline that generates an annotated OTU count table (bacterial profile). The capacity of M. abscessus to attach to the clay mineral kaolin, a surrogate of an environmental particulate, was examined by microscopy; bacterial viability and growth was determined by serial dilution and culture. Results: A total of 22 bacterial genera were detected as airborne based on recovery from the filters. Mycobacterium was detected in the bioaerosols from all four environments, with greatest abundance in the clinical setting, followed by home-indoor. In vitro, M. abscessus adhered directly to kaolin particles, and growth of M. abscessus was enhanced in the presence of kaolin when compared to growth in medium alone. M. abscessus grown in the presence of kaolin, desiccated, and later reconstituted in medium, retained viability. Conclusions: NTM detection in air samples and M. abscessus association with dust-like particles indicates the potential for airborne NTM transmission. Adherence to fomites such as dust, skin cells and mites is a plausible mechanism by which Mycobacterium could be present across these varied environments. Confirmation of these findings increases our understanding of how CF patients are exposed to NTM, and may lead directly to improved strategies to decrease NTM infection and transmission.
