N2  - Objectives: Staphylococcus aureus (SA) can cause pulmonary exacerbations in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF). The treatment outcomes of methicillin-sensitive SA (MSSA) infections have not been adequately described in patients with CF. Methods: A query on our CF center's PortCF database was used to identify all instances of MSSA-positive respiratory cultures over a 5-year period (from 2009 to 2014). Patients treated with inpatient intravenous antibiotics were included and response to therapy was assessed using the change in total score of a modified Pulmonary Exacerbation Score (PES). This scoring system is a composite of clinical symptoms, signs and objective measurements (such as examination and radiologic findings and lung function data) and was calculated retrospectively from each patient's admission and outpatient follow-up clinical data. Results: From a total of 1471 specific instances of MSSA infections over 5 years, 110 episodes of inpatient treatment for pulmonary exacerbation from 33 patients met inclusion criteria. The average age at admission was 11 years (range 0.5 to 18 years), with predominance of females (65%). There was an average of 3 admissions per patient over the 5-year period (range 1-16) and 55% had MSSA infection alone. The most common presenting clinical features were increased cough (95%), new chest radiograph infiltrates (85%), decline in FEV1 by >10% (84%), new findings on chest auscultation (73%), increased sputum (56%), and weight loss >5% or no weight gain (49%). Baseline and post-treatment PES were 7.9 (range 3-14) and 1.8 (range 0-7), respectively, but the number of subjects in each antibiotic category was limited to make any conclusions about efficacy. The susceptibility profile of MSSA isolates showed resistance to clindamycin (38%), erythromycin (12%), and ciprofloxacin (5%). The most commonly used empiric antibiotic regimens for MSSA infections alone were ceftazidime+tobramycin, clindamycin, cefazolin, and vancomycin. MSSA co-infections (most commonly with Pseudomonas aeruginosa) were treated with various combinations of tobramycin with other antibiotics, such as cefepime, ceftazidime or meropenem. The antibiotics that led to the most significant change in PES for MSSA infections alone were ceftazidime and tobramycin, while for MSSA co-infections the use of ciprofloxacin, linezolid and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole led to the highest PES improvements. There are several limitations of this study, which include use of single center data, retrospective design, small sample size and inclusion of inpatient treatment episodes alone. Conclusions: MSSA infections in patients with CF-related pulmonary exacerbations requiring hospitalization were treated with wide-ranging combinations of antibiotics at a single center. The use of modified PES needs to be validated for assessment of response to therapy in patients with CF. Further large-scale studies are needed to better define antibiotic selection, treatment efficacy and outcomes in CF patients with MSSA infections.
