N2  - Introduction: An increasing number of studies report the isolation of non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) from the respiratory tract of patients with cystic fibrosis (CF), mainly Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) and Mycobacterium abscessus complex (MABSC). Mycobacterium bolletii (MB) is one of the three closely related “species” of the MABSC. There are few descriptions of MB infection in CF patients and the clinical relevance of the distinction from other MABSC subspecies is unknown. Objectives: The aim was to describe clinical, radiological and microbiological characteristics of MB lung infection in CF patients. Methods: Patients were identified during a national multicenter retrospective study, aiming to collect all MB strains identified in France since 1994. MB lung infection was defined according to the American Thoracic Society bacteriological criteria for NTM lung infection. Clinical data were collected from medical records and from questionnaires submitted to the primary doctor in CF centers; microbiological data from national microbiological centers. Results: Between 1994 and 2014, 16 patients were identified in 8 CF centers. At first MB isolation, 10 patients were below 18 years (mean age 17 years (1-49)), 13 presented with clinical symptoms (cough, sputum, fever, weight loss), and CT findings were suggestive for 11 (tree-in-bud, nodule, consolidation, cavitie). The inducible resistance to clarithromycin was almost constant (n=15), and MB strains were frequently sensitive to amikacin and cefoxitin. Treatment was attempted in 11 patients, based on multiple antibiotics including clarithromycin (n=10). It was associated with clinical and radiological improvement in respectively 8 and 2 patients. Eradication was obtained for 5 patients but spontaneous eradication was also observed in 3 of the 5 non-treated patients. FEV1 degraded by 15% (SD 13.6%) in 8 patients within the year after the diagnostic. Conclusion: MB is responsible for respiratory infections in CF patients at any age, with an heterogeneous presentation, and potentially associated with respiratory functional degradation. Treatment efficacy was variable. We should gain better knowledge on the strain characterization for adequate therapeutic strategy. We thank the clinicians who referred clinical cases: Burgel PR (Paris), Corvol H (Paris), Couderc L (Rouen), Gendry T (St Pierre, La Réunion), Huet F (Dijon), Houdouin V (Paris), Marchand S (Tours), Pin I and Quetant S (Grenoble), Pramil S (Rouen), Urban T (Angers).
