N2  - Introduction: Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) is an important gram-negative bacterium in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF), leading to increased morbidity and mortality. Reliable bacteriological detection and characterisation (antibiotic susceptibility, PFGE, NGS) is pivotal in aiding with the clinical management of such CF patients. Conventional bacteriological culture remains the cornerstone of detection from sputum of patients, however, it is not always possible to obtain fresh expectorated sputum from adult CF patients. The absence of a quality respiratory sputum specimen leads to a diagnostic dilemma microbiologically and necessitates employment of potentially lower quality respiratory specimens, including cough swabs. Whilst many CF centres may be forced into examining cough swabs, as a surrogate for sputum, uncertainty remains regarding the detection sensitivity of cough swabs for PA. Aim: To determine the reliability of cough swabs in adult CF patients in the detection of PA. Methods: In this study, we examined microbiologically approximately 300 adult patients attending the Northern Ireland Regional CF Centre at Belfast City Hospital, Northern Ireland, for the presence of PA, over the 3 year period 2012-2015. Patients were selected who were chronically infect-ed with PA, in accordance with the Leeds Criteria. Patients were furthered selected by interrogation of their historical microbiology culture results for PA, to include those who had at least one cough swab sent for processing for PA and where sputum culture results from the previous and subsequent culture to the cough swab had a positive PA finding. Additionally, the flanking sputum PA's antibiogram did not differ significantly, indicating the presence of the same clonal type of PA, before and after the cough swab was taken, in a chronically PA infected patient. Results:In total, there were 11 patients who fulfilled the inclusion criteria as outlined above and who had at least had one cough swab examined for the presence of PA. Overall, when compared to examination of sputum, the sensitivity of PA detection from cough swabs was 90.2%. Conclusions: An inability to obtain sputum from chronically PA-infected adult CF patients for microbiological investigation is a relatively rare event. However, there may be certain problematic patients where cough swabs may be the sole respiratory specimen available. In these patients, cough swabs yielded a very high (90.2%) PA sensitivity, but not absolute sensitivity (100%), compared to sputum culture. It is not possible to extrapolate these findings with chronically infected patients into other PA infection scenarios, including intermittent PA or early PA colonisation, as the microbiological dynamics in these scenarios differ to the chronic stage. Further work is now required with these latter PA infection scenarios. In the absence of PA from sputum cultures, cough swabs provide an alternative means to obtain bacteriological cultures required for confirmation of PA infection/colonisation status, as well as culture for susceptibility testing.
