N2  - Introduction: The safety and sustained efficacy of tobramycin inhalation powder hard capsules (TIP™) in treating P. aeruginosa (Pa) infection in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients (pts) was demonstrated in the 48-week (wk) ETOILES study (Virella-Lowell I, et al. Pediatr Pulmonol. 2014;49(S38):362-3). Here we present long-term data from 96 wk of TIP exposure in CF pts (48 wk in the ETOILES + 48 wk in the extension study). Methods: CF pts aged ≥6 years who had completed the core ETOILES study were eligible to enter this open-label, multicenter, single-arm extension study. All pts received TIP 112 mg bid via the T-326 Inhaler for 6 additional cycles (each cycle: 4 wk on-and 4 wk off-treatment; total 12 treatment cycles across core and extension periods). The primary endpoint was the safety of TIP in terms of the incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (AEs) across 12 treatment cycles. Key secondary and exploratory endpoints included relative change in FEV1% predicted, change in Pa sputum density and tobramycin MIC, rate of anti-Pa antibiotic use and frequency of pulmonary exacerbations (PE). Results: Among 96 pts who completed the core study, 45 pts entered (mean [SD] age: 24.5 [10.79] years; males: 55.6%), and 34 pts (75.6%) completed the extension study. AEs were reported by 39 pts (86.7%) across 12 treatment cycles; the incidence of AEs was comparable across core (n=35; 77.8%) and extension (n=36; 80%) phases. The most frequently reported AEs by preferred term were infective PE of CF (66.7%), nasopharyngitis (28.9%), hemoptysis (26.7%) and cough (24.4%). The majority of AEs were reported as either moderate (40%) or severe (24%). Drug-related AEs were reported by 33.3% pts. Serious AEs (SAEs) were reported by 42.2% pts with a higher frequency in the extension (35.6%) vs the core cycles (26.7%); infective PE of CF was the most common SAE (37.8%). One death due to PE (not drug-related) was reported in the off-treatment period of Cycle 12 during the extension cycle. Across 96 wk, a mean relative decrease of 7.5% in FEV1% predicted and a mean decrease of 0.6 log10 CFU/g in Pa sputum density (sum of all biotypes) was observed from baseline. A total of 66.7% of pts reported PEs that were mostly mild (47.2%) or moderate (42.7%) in severity. The rate of anti-Pa antibiotic use was comparable across core (66.7%) and extension (68.9%) periods. There was no evidence of increased antibiotic resistance from the core study to the end of the extension study based on tobramycin MIC50 values. Conclusion: The ETOILES core and extension study represent the longest (2 years) continuous exposure to TIP and provides a comprehensive assessment of its safety and efficacy in CF pts. Overall, TIP continues to be safe and well tolerated in pts with CF, with no increase in the frequency of AEs in the second year of treatment, and no new emerging safety signals. TIP efficacy was also sustained as observed by the decrease in sputum Pa density and rate of PEs, with no further increase in MIC. Study sponsored by Novartis Pharma AG.
