N2  - Bronchiectasis is a common disease in the AsiaPacific and affected patients suffer from chronic sputum production and recurrent exacerbation. Immunomodulatory agent such as low-dose macrolides have also been shown to have some efficacy. Bronchiecatsis is associated with mild to moderate airflow obstruction. The purpose of this study was to define the clinical characteristics of Non-CF bronchectasis and the efficacy of low dose macrolides for prevention of exacerbations in bronchiectasis. A retrospective study of all patients who were diagnosed with bronchiectasis (2007-2012) was conducted at the Busan Paik Hospital, Inje University, in Busan. Data regarding age, sex, sputum and bronchial washing Gram stain and culture, spirometry, chest CT, medications was obtained. 178 (55 men and 83 women) bronchiectasis patients (mean age 59.4+/-4.5 years) were studied. The most common symptoms were chronic sputum 41%, dyspnea 34%, hemoptysis 19%, cough 14%. Spirometry showed FEV1/FVC of 65.2% +/-12.27 (range 30.32-91.38), FEV1% pred 66.4 +/-10.42 (range 30.34-112.23)/. Airflow obstruction was detected in 102/178 (57%). The most frequent potential pathogenic microorganisms were P. aeruginosa 33%. 46/102 (45%) of patients with airflow obstruction were never smoker. 127 (35 men and 92 women) were followed up for more than 1 year. Mean length of low dose macrolides theatment 9 months and 60 patients had low dose macrolides treatment. There was a decrease in the exacerbation frequency from 0.78/month premacrolides to 0.32/month (P<0.001) post-macrolides. Airflow obstruction is common in bronchiectasis and low-dose macrolides decreased the rate of exacervations of non-CF bronchiectasis.
