N2  - Objective: We aimed to examine the efficacy of perioperative and short-term treatment in liver transplant (OLT) patients suffering from preoperative pneumonia, in terms of graft and survivals. Methods: We reviewed the medical records of 11 preoperative pneumonia patients who underwent OLT between 2011 and 2014. 10 patients received their grafts from deceased donors and 1 patient received living donor from her son. We analyzed demographic data, laboratory values, primary liver disease, diagnostic methods, postoperative outcomes and their treatment. Result: Follow-up time ranged from 1 to 42 months (median 18). The gender bias was 10:1 (male: female) and the median age 47.8 years. The cause of liver transplantation was hepatocellular carcinoma in HBV cirrhosis (n=3), acute hepatic failure with HBV infection (n=6), HBV cirrhosis (n=1) and HCV cirrhosis (n=1). The MELD score was 23.8±7.9 pre-operation. All the patients had symptoms of cough, sputum, the fever and the increase of leukocyte pre-transplantation. 8 patients were diagnosed with pulmonary inflammation by chest X-rays. Isolated pathogens from sputum smear and culture were: Aspergillus (n=1), Candida albicans (n=6) and Gram-positive bacilli (n=2). Sensitive antibiotics were administrated and perioperative immunosuppression was just basiliximab without steroid. All the patients would not receive immunosuppression therapy until their alanine aminotransferase increased by 20u previous resulting from rejection. Time without immunosuppressant (Tac and MMF) time was 28.9±15.2d. Tacrolimus concentration was maintained at a low level and the use of MMF was 500mg, twice daily. One patient died of MODS on 23d after surgery, the other recovered well and the average postoperative hospitalizations were 52.1d. Conclusion: The incidence of preoperative pneumonia was high in the waiting list, especially in acute hepatic failure patients. These patient were inclined to be postponed for liver transplantation for the high risk and mortality according to previous studies. However, we found that, in our center, patients with preoperative pneumonia would be safe to take liver transplantation by sensitive antibiotics treatment and delaying immunosuppressants use.
