N2  - Current tools for evaluating tuberculosis (TB) treatments are expensive and time-consuming. For instance, phase III trials entail treating hundreds of patients with test drug regimens and monitoring them for at least 2 years for relapses. This leads to an urgent need for noninvasive imaging biomarkers that would have the potential to accelerate TB drug development by providing real-time data on the activities of TB regimens and to detect relapse. It has been previously demonstrated that iodo-DPA-713, a synthetic ligand of translocator protein (TSPO), accumulates specifically in TB-associated inflammatory lesions, by selective retention within macrophages and phagocytic cells. In this study we utilized serial [125I]iodo-DPA-713 single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) as a novel imaging biomarker to monitor the activity of a bedaquiline (first TB drug to be approved in 40 years) TB drug regimen. C3HeB/FeJ mice, that develop well defined necrotic and hypoxic lesions, were aerosol infected with Mycobacterium tuberculosis and administered the standard (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide) or a highly active novel drug regimen (bedaquiline, pyrazinamide, clofazimine). Serial pulmonary [125I]iodo-DPA-713-SPECT was compared with [18F]FDG-PET, standard microbiology and cytokine analysis to monitor the bactericidal activity of drug regimens. Lesion-specific standardized uptake values (SUV) were calculated by drawing regions of interest (ROI) around three pulmonary lesions in each animal. Five mice were used per group and per time-point. A separate group of animals treated with RHZ for 12-weeks was assessed for relapse one and four months after completion of treatment. Pulmonary bacterial burden at the time of imaging (6-weeks post-infection) was 8.05 ± 0.02 log10 colony forming units (CFU). [18F]FDG-PET was able to differentiate between treated and untreated groups, but not between treatment groups. [125I]iodo-DPA-713-SPECT correlated well with the bacterial burden and correctly distinguished the two different treatment arms at both 4- and 8-weeks after starting treatments (P <0.05). Cytokine (IFNγ, IL-1β and TNFα) concentrations in lung homogenate correlated with imaging findings. Immunofluorescence studies confirmed the penetration of iodo-DPA-713 into fibrotic granulomas and cavitary lesions. There was also an excellent correlation between [125I]iodo-DPA-713-SPECT signal and bacterial burden during relapse (P=0.0006). Finally, [124I]iodo-DPA- 713-PET (proposed for human studies) had a higher signal to noise ratio compared with [125I]iodo-DPA-713-SPECT. These studies demonstrate that radioiodinated DPA-713, a novel imaging biomarker for TB-associated inflammation, can rapidly determine the bactericidal activities of novel TB drug regimens and can also detect relapse. Real-time, serial observational capabilities of radioiodo-DPA-713 imaging will augment the study of mycobacterial pathogenesis and monitoring of disease in real-time. Moreover, it could also be easily translated to clinical settings where rapid and accurate biomarkers for monitoring TB treatments and relapse are urgently needed.
