N2  - Purpose: CF LTx recipients usually grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) pre and post-LTx. Pa contributes to post LTx bronchiectasis, pneumonitis, BOS and CLAD and inhaled tobramycin decreases Pa load. Methods: From April 2014, a novel dry powder tobramycin inhaler (PTI) (Tobi Podhaler Novartis Pharma) was initiated in 43 LTx recipients with CF and proven Pa infection (n= 21) ± colonization (matt or mucoid). Patients excluded were those with bronchial hyperresponsiveness to PTI. Data including demographics, side effects, duration of therapy, spirometry, renal function, micro including sensitivities, re-admission requiring IV antibiotics, sputum production detected from bronchoscopy and tobramycin blood levels were recorded. Results: 43 LTx recipients: mean age 32 yrs (range 11 - 61), 67% male, median days post LTx = 575 (range 16 - 5538). 11 patients were started within 1st 3 months post LTx and 3/11 required readmission for IV antibiotics. All patients not readmitted had a reduction in secretions as per bronchoscopy report. 13 patients 3 months post LTx required IV antibiotics: median days = 13 (range 3 - 49). Median length of therapy was 28 days (range 7 - 114 days). 36/43 patients tolerated PTI not requiring cessation of treatment. Most commonly reported side effects included cough (30%), wheeze (7%) and sore throat (5%). Detectable blood levels were recorded in all 14 patients with levels measured; 2 requiring cessation due to deterioration of renal function. 36/43 patients reported an improvement or no change in sputum production. Pa isolated from bronchoscopy did not change in 13 patients and 4 patients did not isolate Pa post initiation. No significant change in FEV1 or renal function was seen in either cohort post TIP. Patients reported improved compliance and satisfaction compared with prior nebulised tobramycin therapy. Conclusion: PTI is a safe, effective, treatment option for patients with CF post LTx potentially resulting in lower readmissions for IV antibiotics, reduced Pa load and reduced secretions. The benefit was most evident in those 1st 3 months post LTx. Further studies are warranted to see long term patient and costing benefits.
