N2  - Aim: To describe the demographics and diagnoses of children with chronic wet cough who underwent bronchoscopy from 2008-2014 at RCH, Brisbane. Methods: Children referred for bronchoscopy were prospectively recruited into a larger cohort study. Common organisms found in BAL were considered clinically important when density of single organisms were ≥104 cfu/ml. Ascribed diagnoses were based on objective findings and/or standardised paediatric respiratory definitions. Results: 265 children with chronic wet cough were enrolled; 59.3% were male and 10.8% Indigenous. The median age was 26 months (IQR 15, 58). Median cough duration was 28 weeks (IQR 6, 58). Conclusion: Protracted bacterial bronchitis and bronchiectasis were the most common causes of wet cough in children accounting for 84.2% of cases. Of the four respiratory micro-organisms, H. influenzae was identified most frequently. Understanding the aetiology of wet cough in children is important for clinical care. (Table Presented).
