N2  - Introduction Health care settings are important sites for transmission of Mycobacteria tuberculosis. The prevalence of Tuberculosis (TB) in the laboratory environment is unknown due in part to poor occupational TB surveillance. A concern for a country with high prevalence of HIV and TB infections. Below we describe a possible TB outbreak in a microbiology laboratory. Cases Case 1: A 36 years old female laboratory worker presented with a history of malaise, fatigue, loss of appetite, weight loss and cough of more than two weeks duration. Chest X-ray was suggestive of pulmonary TB. Sputum Xpert MTB/Rif was positive. Mycobacterium tuberculosis isolated from sputum culture was susceptible to the first line antituberculous drugs on which she was put. Case 2: A 30 year old male co-worker with prior culture confirmed TB also presented with similar symptoms. Chest X-ray was nonspecific. Sputum culture and Xpert MTB/Rif were negative. Prior to the above cases, two co-workers had been diagnosed with pulmonary TB in the past five years. No isolates were available for further studies. The HIV status of these workers is unknown. An enhanced surveillance was initiated with active case finding and risk analysis was performed. We discuss the challenges encountered during investigation of possible outbreak. Discussion The case definition for TB outbreak is based on guidelines from low prevalent settings. There are no policies around TB outbreak investigation in workplace. Current Methods of surveillance are poorly sensitive. Molecular Methods to establish genetic linkage are not readily accessible. While primary containment and PPE are provided, infrastructures are below standards and fit tests are not widely available.
