N2  - With one third of the world population infected and more than 1 million yearly fatalities, tuberculosis (TB) presents a global health emergency. TB infection naturally occurs via an aerosol route, and has therefore been considered predominantly a disease of the lung. Central nervous system (CNS) TB is a rare, albeit highly devastating manifestation of extra-pulmonary TB. Now, with the rise of multi- and extremely-drug resistant bacteria CNS TB presents a novel threat. Additionally, this form of TB preferentially affects children in resource starved areas and immune-compromised adults. To date, research has focused on pulmonary, whereas no significant advancement has been made in understanding the mechanisms associated with CNS TB. One reason for this disparity is the lack of an available small animal model to study the immune pathology of TB in the CNS. In this study, we have used a small animal model of CNS TB that permitted us to investigate the immune responses following CNS Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) infection. We intracranially infected mice with H37Rv strain of Mtb and analyzed the cellular composition of the subsequent immunological response in the brain. As early as seven days post-infection (PI), we show significant infiltration of CD11c+ dendritic cells into the CNS. Additionally, Mtb-specific CD4+ (P25) T cells also accumulated in the brain and were activated in peripheral lymphoid tissues including the spleen and brain-draining lymph nodes. Adoptively transferred P25 T cells that recovered from the brain showed substantial proliferation. Compared to uninfected controls, P25 T cells present in the peripheral lymphoid organs of IC Mtb-infected mice also displayed increased proliferation, suggesting a systemic sampling of CNS Mtb antigen. The presence of IFNγ and IL17 producing Mtb-specific T cells has been link to protecting immune responses in pulmonary infection.We show that on day 7 PI a significant percentage of the CD4 T cells display activated (CD44HI) phenotype and produce IFNγ and to lesser extent IL17. On day 21 PI, both of these populations increased substantially in size. Most strikingly, starting on day 21 PI we detected Mtb-containing granulomatous lesions. These data will not only illuminate the unique pathogenesis of CNS TB but also aid in designing novel treatment options for mycobacterial infection of the CNS.
