N2  - Introduction/ Rationale Nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) are ubiquitous organisms that cause chronic pulmonary disease. Prevalence, natural history and outcomes of NTM disease have been poorly documented with factors predictive of disease development of those exposed to NTM and progression unknown. Particularly, the study of NTM pathogenesis and host immune responses in NTM lung disease has been hampered by the lack of a robust animal model. In this study, we aimed to develop a nonhuman primate model of pulmonary Mycobacterium avium (M. avium) disease to: (1) determine infectious dose of NTM and characterize disease progression in rhesus macaques and; (2) characterize the host innate and adaptive immune response to NTM. Methods Three adult female rhesus macaques (10-14 years of age) were infected with escalating doses (107, 108, 109) of M. avium strain via bronchoscope into the right middle lobe and were monitored for clinical symptoms (e.g. cough, sputum production, and weight loss). Chest X-rays were performed bi-monthly to evaluate radiographic characteristics of NTM disease (i.e infiltrate, nodules, lymphadenopathy, and bronchiectasis). In addition, peripheral blood was collected weekly and bronchial alveolar lavage (BAL) were collected bi-monthly after inoculation to characterize the immune response development and bacterial burden. The host innate and adaptive immune responses to NTM were characterized by measuring: lymphocyte proliferation and activation; antigen-specific T cell responses; cytokine secretion and innate immune cell activation. Bacterial load in BAL supernatant, blood cultures was measured by culture on bacterial plates. Results Bacterial burden was detected in the animal inoculated with the highest dose on day 14 post-infection. All animals generated T cell responses as indicated by an increase in the frequency of Ki67+ T cells and the appearance of IFNg/TNFa producing T cells, particularly in BAL specimens. Clinically and radiographical evidence of M. avium disease appeared only in the animal subjected to the highest (109) inoculum. This animal developed dense infiltrate within the right middle lobe apparent within two weeks after infection, further consolidation over the following 6 weeks (figure 1). At 8 weeks post-inoculum, the animal manifested minimal symptoms to include occasional cough with no fever or weight loss reported. Bronchoscopy has shown friable mucosa without purulence. Conclusions Our findings suggest that immune competent rhesus macaques could potentially serve as a robust animal model of NTM pulmonary disease. The availability of this model could facilitate efforts in therapeutic development, as well as understanding of the mechanisms underlying age-related development of this disease in humans.
