N2  - Statins, HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, are widely used as lipid lowering agents. Besides anti-cholesterolemic activity, many other beneficial effects of statin intake have been observed. Recently, many clinical studies have shown an association of statin intake with decreased mortality in pneumonia patients. Alveolar macrophages (AMs) are one of important components of the innate immune system, protecting against invading pathogens in the distal airways. The phagocytic ability of macrophages is highly susceptible to impairment mediated by oxidative stress, such as hyperoxia used during mechanical ventilation. Compromised phagocytic function of macrophages by hyperoxia has been shown to impair host immunity leading to increased susceptibility to bacterial infections. The aim of this study was to determine whether statins can rescue hyperoxia-compromised macrophage function. Three widely used statins: atorvastatin, lovastatin, and simvastatin, were tested for their effects on phagocytic function in hyperoxia exposed cells. Impact of statins on phagocytic function and antioxidant properties were studied in cultured macrophages like cell line, RAW 264.7 cells. Fluorescence labeled particles were used to assess phagocytic function while levels of antioxidants enzymes were studied using an antioxidant kit which assesses total antioxidant properties of cells. Here, we report that statins can significantly improve hyperoxia-compromised phagocytic function of macrophages. Corresponding to improved phagocytic function, statins can also enhance antioxidant capacity of macrophages which were reduced significantly upon hyperoxic exposure. However, statins had no observed effects on phagocytic activity of cells, which remained at room air conditions. These results indicate that statins can improve hyperoxia-compromised macrophage function via enhancing antioxidant capacity of macrophages cultured under hyperoxic conditions. Therefore, statins may help in maintaining innate immunity in patients on ventilators.
