N2  - Rationale: The current standard practice is to isolate patients suspected to have tuberculosis (TB) in a negative pressure room until three consecutive results of acid fast bacilli (AFB) smear microscopy are proven to be negative. Negative pressure rooms, however, are not always available especially in high prevalence TB settings. Our objective is to identify clinical predictors of AFB smear positivity in order to prioritize TB isolation for the most contagious patients. Methods: All patients diagnosed with TB in Montreal between 1996 and 2007 were evaluated. 27 demographic, clinical, and radiological characteristics were included in the analysis. Association between patients' characteristics and AFB smear status was assessed using multiple logistic regression. The most parsimonious regression model was selected using the Bayesian information criterion (BIC). From the regression model, the predicted probability of outcome for different clinical scenarios was estimated to construct a clinical algorithm. Priority for isolation was considered when the probability of smear positivity exceeds 20%. Analyses were performed using STATA and R software. Results: 1056 patients with pulmonary TB were included. Factors that were selected as important predictors of AFB smear positivity are cough (OR, 3.3 [95% CI: 2.1, 5.0]), systemic symptoms (3.0 [2.1, 4.5]), lung cavity on chest radiography (5.0 [3.0, 8.4]), and three or more lung lobes involvement on chest radiography (2.6 [1.4, 4.6]). A clinical algorithm with probability of different clinical scenarios is shown in the Figure. Conclusion: We constructed an algorithm using clinical and radiological characteristics to predict AFB smear positivity which may help prioritizing TB isolation.
