N2  - RATIONALE: Drug resistant tuberculosis (DRTB) is emerging as a threat to TB control programs world-wide. While the problem has been well recognized in adults, the same is frequently underestimated in children owing to limited awareness and difficulty in confirming the diagnosis. There is paucity of literature on profile and outcome of DRTB in children from India. METHODS: Hence, this retrospective record survey of the Pediatric Pulmonology clinic was conducted to determine the profile and outcome of DRTB in children from our tertiary care institute. Between 2007 and 2013, 23 children were suspected to have DRTB based either on drug susceptibility test (DST) detecting resistance to one or more anti-tubercular (AT) drug (9/23); or on contact with an adult suffering with culture proven DRTB (2/23); or on non-response to first line AT therapy (ATT) (6/23). In two children,cultures grew mycobacterium TB which was sensitive to first line ATT while in 4 atypical mycobacterium were grown,therefore these children (6/23) were excluded from the analysis. RESULTS: Amongst the culture proven DRTB (17), 2 were classified as mono-resistant TB, 1 as poly-resistant TB, 5 as multidrug resistant TB and 1 as extensively drug resistant TB based on the sensitivity pattern found. The median age of presentation was 114 month (SD ±27 months). 14 were female. The commonest presenting complaints were fever in 12, cough in 10 and lymph node swelling in 4. The median duration of onset of symptoms was 12 months (IQR 5-36 months) and of receiving ATT was 7 months (IQR 6-13 month), prior to presenting to our institute. Eight had pulmonary TB, 6 had disseminated TB, 2 had lymph node TB and one patient had TB meningitis. Twelve children completed therapy as per Revised National TB Control Program while outcome of five could not be determined as they were lost to follow-up. Many had complications in the form of bronchiectasis, draining sinus, pericardial effusion, hydrocephalus and psoas abscess;while 3 developed hearing loss as a result of therapy. CONCLUSIONS: Clinical features of DRTB are similar in all age groups. Past history of TB alongwith treatment with ATT and contact with adults suffering from DRTB are important risk factors for development of DRTB in children. All possible specimens should be used and every attempt tried for isolating the organism and getting its DST. Complications of the disease as well as the therapy are many. Increasing awareness is the key to diagnosis and therapy.
