N2  - RATIONALE: Engineered silver (Ag) and carbon (C) nanoparticles (NP) are increasingly used in industrial processes, consumer products and medical applications. The respiratory system represents the main target for incidental exposure. Individuals with pre-existing lung disease, such as cystic fibrosis (CF), are expected to be more susceptible to adverse effects of NPs than normal subjects. Safety testing thus needs to include studies in susceptible populations. METHODS: Realistic aerosol exposures were performed using the Nano Aerosol Chamber for In-Vitro Toxicology (NACIVT, www.nacivt.ch) that provides uniform and quantifiable particle deposition. Re-differentiated human bronchial epithelia (HBE) from normal and cystic fibrosis (CF) donors, cultured at a permanent air-liquid interface, were exposed to spark-generated Ag and C nanoaerosols. Particle size (20 nm), aerosol flow rate (25 mL/min/insert) and particle concentration (2X106 particles/cm3) were kept constant during exposure, while the exposure time (36 s, 360 s and 3600 s) was varied to deposit different particle doses (4X107, 4X108 and 4X109 particles/cm2). Control cells were left untreated in the incubator. Within 24h after aerosol exposure, cytotoxicity was assessed by measuring lactate dehydrogenase release and inflammation was evaluated by analysis of interleukin (IL)-6 and IL-8. RESULTS: Cellular responses were similar after exposure to both NP types, but more pronounced for Ag-NP than C-NP. Normal and CF HBE responded differently. Exposure to Ag-NP resulted in a significant increase of cytotoxicity by 15-17% in CF compared to normal and control HBE. Cytotoxicity in CF HBE was 5-7% higher than in normal and control HBE after C-NP exposure. IL-6 and IL-8 release increased with increasing particle dose. IL-8 levels (5000-9000 pg/mL) were not significantly different between normal and CF HBE. Exposure to Ag-NP increased IL-6 secretion significantly in CF up to 250 pg/mL compared to 50 pg/mL in normal HBE. Exposure to C-NP increased IL-6 secretion similarly, namely up to 150 pg/mL in CF and to 100 pg/mL in normal HBE. IL-6 release in CF controls was 6 times higher than in normal controls. CONCLUSIONS: This study supports epidemiological findings that persons with chronic airway disease are more vulnerable to adverse effects of ultrafine particles than normal subjects. Moreover, the data indicates constitutively increased IL-6 release by CF HBE, in the absence of bacterial infection, which might contribute to the greater sensitivity of HBE from CF donors to NP exposure. Particular attention should thus be given to susceptible populations in toxicological studies of engineered NP.
