N2  - Rationale: With the exception of sputum purulence, the clinical presentation and severity of exacerbations of COPD have not been shown to correlate with sputum microbiology. We hypothesized that exacerbations due to bacterial pathogens definitively associated with exacerbations (nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Pseudomonas aeruginosa) (Group 1) have distinct clinical features from those with no pathogen isolated (Group 2) or with other pathogens (Enterobacteriaceae, Staphylococcus aureus) isolated (Group 3) from sputum at exacerbation. Methods: Clinical information and sputum samples were prospectively collected from a cohort of COPD patients in an outpatient VA based research clinic, monthly and at exacerbation from 1994 to 2013. Symptoms and signs were collected and exacerbations were diagnosed in a standardized manner. Exacerbations were divided into 3 groups based on sputum microbiology as defined above. A clinical score was calculated from 10 symptoms and signs, with the score ranging from 10 at baseline to a maximum of 30. Anthonisen type of exacerbation and time for resolution were determined. Results: 1351 exacerbations were evaluated in 152 patients; 445 in group 1, 797 in group 2 and 109 in group 3. As shown in Table 1, Group 1 had a higher incidence of upper respiratory symptoms, cough and sputum changes. They also had higher body temperature and pulse rate, but were less often associated with wheezing. Group 1 exacerbations were more often Anthonisen type 1 and had a higher clinical score. Changes in dyspnea, respiratory rate and rales on lung examination were not different between the 3 groups. Group 1 and 2 exacerbations had a longer time to resolution compared to group 3. Conclusion: Significant differences in clinical features, severity and time for resolution between exacerbations related with definitive pathogens (H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, S. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa) compared to other exacerbations were seen. Whether these differences are magnified with characterization of these pathogens into new (infectious) and pre-existing (colonizing) strains needs to be determined. (Table Presented).
