N2  - Rationale: Both obstructive and restrictive patterns of airflow as detected by spirometry are associated with increased mortality in the general population. Little is known regarding the prevalence and clinical risk factors associated with different spirometric abnormalities among HIV-infected individuals. The multi-center Lung-HIV consortium has collected spirometric and clinical data from heterogeneous groups of HIV-infected participants, providing a unique opportunity to define the patterns of lung function abnormalities among HIV-infected individuals. Methods: Cross-sectional spirometric, demographic and clinical data from five US and two South African Lung-HIV study site were included in analysis. Categories of spirometric abnormalities were defined with pre-bronchodilator measurements as obstructed (FEV1/FVC<0.70), restricted (FEV1/FVC≥0.70 and FVC<80% predicted), undefined (FEV1/FVC≥0.70 and FVC≥80% predicted and FEV1<80% predicted) or normal (FEV1/FVC≥0.70 and FVC≥80% predicted and FEV1≥80% predicted). Kruskal-Wallis and Chi-squared test were used to test differences among groups. Multivariable logistic regression analysis was used to identify independent predictors of spirometric patterns. Results: A total of 928 US and 917 South African participants had spirometric and clinical data measured. Among US participants, 20% were obstructed, 9% restricted, 2% undefined and 69% had normal spirometry measurements. In multivariable analysis, older age, current or former smoking history, history of bacterial pneumonia and history of Pneumocystis pneumonia were independently associated with an obstructed pattern. Only older age was independently associated with a restrictive pattern. Among international participants, 5% were obstructed, 6% restricted, 2% undefined and 88% had normal spirometry. Older age and male sex were independently associated with an obstructed pattern, while white race, history of bacterial pneumonia and history of tuberculosis were associated with a restrictive pattern. Markers of HIV disease severity such as high viral load or low CD4 cell count were not associated with abnormal spirometry in either US or South African participants. Symptoms of cough, phlegm and wheeze were more prevalent in those with an obstructed compared to a normal airflow pattern. Conclusion: These data represent the first and largest examination of spirometry among HIV-infected individuals. The prevalence of obstructive and restrictive patterns was substantial, with abnormal spirometry present in over 30% of HIV-infected participants in the United States, and in 13% of HIV-infected participants from South Africa. Factors associated with obstruction and restriction differed between US and international HIV-infected individuals, reflecting the heterogeneity of disease in these populations. Future studies are needed to determine the relationship between these airflow patterns and clinical outcomes such as mortality.
