N2  - Rationale: Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is an opportunistic infection in patients with HIV infection, which may cause significant morbidity and mortality and can present in patients who are anti-retroviral therapy (ART) naïve as disseminated disease (positive MAC blood culture) or in ART-treated patients with atypical presentations (lymphadenopathy, pulmonary involvement) with an inflammatory component known as unmasking MAC-IRIS. In contrast, MAC in HIV(-) patients often presents as chronic bronchopulmonary disease with symptoms including cough and lymphadenitis. A comprehensive comparison of these two patient groups could help elucidate the pathogenesis of MAC infections. Methods: Data from HIV(+) ART-naïve patients with CD4 <100 cells/μL enrolled in a prospective study at NIH was reviewed to identify those with MAC infections. Patients were followed for up to 96 weeks after ART initiation. Similarly, HIV(-) patients enrolled in a natural history study of nontuberculous mycobacterial disease were reviewed to identify patients with MAC infections. Only patients who met ATS/IDSA microbiologic criteria for pulmonary disease were included in this analysis. Clinical, microbiologic, and laboratory data were reviewed for all patients. Results: Of 200 HIV(+) patients, 21 (10.5%) had MAC disease: 13 with pulmonary, 11 (84.6%) of which were M. avium, 2 (15.4%) were M. intracellulare. Of the 70 HIV(-) patients with MAC disease, 30 (42.9%) had M. avium, 51 (72.9%) had M. intracellulare, with 12 (17.1%) co-infected with M. avium and M. intracellulare, and 1 with an unknown species. Nine HIV(+) patients had a MAC diagnosis before ART initiation (5 disseminated; 4 isolated pulmonary disease). Eight cases of pulmonary unmasking MAC-IRIS presented at a median of 21 days (range 14-56) after ART initiation. On CT scan, 7 (53.8%) of the 13 HIV(+) pulmonary MAC cases had nodular disease, 6 (46.2%) had infiltrates, and 1 (7.7%) had ground glass opacities. Regarding treatment, all HIV(+) patients received antimicrobial therapy: 100% received Azithromycin (median duration 504 days), 92.3% Ethambutol (495 days), 84.6% Moxifloxacin (495 days), and 15.4% required corticosteroids (66 days). Three patients with pulmonary MAC died, with one death attributable to disseminated, non-IRIS MAC. In contrast, HIV(-) patients had a median of 2 courses of therapy (range 1-7) for a median of 327 days per course. Conclusions: MAC in HIV infection is more commonly related to M. avium and represents an opportunistic pathogen in younger patients without relapses after completion of therapy. HIV(+) patients demonstrate nodularity on CT scan, but not bronchiectasis as is typical in HIV(-) patients. (Table Presented).
