N2  - Introduction Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) is a sulfonamide antibiotic used in the treatment of a variety of bacterial infections. The adverse effects profile of this drug typically involves skin reactions ranging from uncomplicated rash to toxic epidermal necrolysis. Severe systemic reactions may include fulminant hepatic necrosis, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, and other blood dyscrasias. We present a case of diffuse alveolar damage (DAD) after initiation of TMP-SMX. Case presentation A 59 year old female was admitted with progressive dyspnea, subjective fever, and intermittently productive cough for several days. One day prior to admission she developed a diffuse pruritic rash and bilateral conjuctivitis. She was recently treated for a buttock abscess with incision and drainage and TMP-SMX. The physical examination showed tachypnea, tachycardia, and decreased breath sounds bilaterally. Laboratory data included WBC 8.0, bandemia of 29%, Hb 11.5 mg/dL, Plts 171, and lactic acid 2.4. Chest X-ray showed mild increase of interstitial markings. The working diagnoses were Toxic Shock Syndrome versus drug reaction. Treatment was started with broad spectrum antibiotics and steroids. On day 3 she was transferred to critical care due to tachypnea and hypoxemia. CT of the chest showed diffuse reticulation throughout lungs fields, with no bronchiectasis or honeycombing, consistent with interstitial lung disease (figure1). Lung biopsy showed acute lung injury with severe DAD and early organizing pneumonia (OP) (figure2). She received high dose steroids with slow taper upon discharge. At 4 weeks followup she was feeling well. Blood, sputum, and urine cultures were negative. Discussion Many drugs have been linked to drug induced interstitial lung disease (DILD), each with distinct radiological and histological patterns. Antibiotics predominantly cause patchy ground-glass opacities with centrilobular opacities and interlobar septal lines, with histological findings predominantly of DAD and OP. These findings may be seen in exacerbations of known interstitial lung disease, with the difference of pre-existing fibrotic lung tissue in the biopsy. This pathological pattern was not present in our patient, leading us to conclude that her condition was acute and related to TMP-SMX exposure. If recognized early, the prognosis of DILD is good. The diagnosis should be considered in any patient with history of drug therapy who presents with new onset of progressive respiratory complaints with no other explanation. Corticosteroid therapy is indicated in patients with significant acute respiratory failure with hypoxemia or when drug withdrawal fails to improve overall condition. Instructions should include avoidance of the drug since severe relapses have been described. (Figure Presented).
