N2  - INTRODUCTION: Cavitary lesions in the lungs remain dominated by infectious etiologies such as TB, Klebsiella pneumonia, Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumoniae. However, primary lung malignancies can also present with cavitations in about 7 to 11% of cases. CASE: A 42-year-old Hispanic male, non-smoker, presented to the ER, with 4-day exertional dyspnea, non-productive cough and night sweats, but no fever, weight loss, history of recent travel or exposure to sick contacts. He has no significant past medical history. His physical examination was unremarkable. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest revealed bilateral cavitary lesions, multiple lung nodules, lower lobe consolidation and moderate bilateral effusion. White blood count was normal, and sputum and blood cultures showed no growth. Tests for TB, Coccidiomycosis, Legionella, CMV, HSV, Coxiella and Histoplasmosis were all negative. Chlamydia IgG and Mycoplasma IgG were both elevated but IgM levels were low, indicative of past infection. A CT-guided biopsy followed by a transbronchial biopsy were non revealing including cultures. A VATS lung biopsy showed atypical lymphocyte infiltrate with predominant T-cells. Immunostaining was positive for T-cell markers CD2, CD3, CD5 and CD7; B-cell marker CD20; and histiocyte marker CD 68. PCR studies were not diagnostic for T-cell Lymphoma. The final diagnosis was Lymphoid Granulomatosis, owing to the characteristic angiocentric pattern of the atypical lymphocytes with granulomatous changes. The patient received a course of antibiotics while in the hospital. He was noted to show improvement and was discharged stable. Repeat CT of the chest during outpatient follow-up showed decreased pulmonary nodules and resolution of pleural effusion. DISCUSSION: Lymphomatoid Granulomatosis is a rare systemic angiodestructive lymphoproliferative disease that presents with pulmonary cavitations. Its prevalence is unknown and there is no identified racial predilection. It is often associated with Epstein-Barr virus infection, which leads to B cell proliferation and some benign T cell reaction. The most common symptoms include cough, dyspnea, fever and night sweats. About 50% of patients may develop papular lesions in the gluteal region or extremities, which was not seen in our patient. Lymph node, bone marrow and splenic involvement may be seen later in the course of the disease.
