N2  - RATIONALE. NTHi is commonly isolated from the sputum of patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). COPD patients develop small airway secretory IgA deficiency (SIgA) due to reduced expression of a transporter protein (polymeric immunoglobulin receptor or pIgR) needed to transport IgA across the epithelial lining. We hypothesized that NTHi exposure would accelerate small airway remodeling and emphysema in pIgR-/- mice (which are deficient in SIgA), and that this effect will be blocked by the phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor roflumilast. METHODS. WT or pIgR-/- mice (7-10 per group, all C57BL/6) were treated with an aerosol of 10 mg of killed NTHi lysates or vehicle (0.9% NaCl) once weekly for 4 months starting at 2 months of age using the InExpose system (Scireq). WT or pIgR-/- mice (10 per group, all C57Bl/6) were gavaged once daily with 100 mg of roflumilast (∼ 5 mg drug/kg animal weight) while undergoing identical treatment with NTHi. At 6 months of age the animals were euthanized, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) performed, and then the lungs perfused, inflated with 10% formalin, fixed overnight, and embedded in paraffin. Small airway wall thickness (as measured by subepithelial connective tissue volume density) and emphysema (as measured by mean alveolar septal perimeter) were quantified by morphometric analysis. RESULTS. Vehicle (saline)-treated pIgR-/- mice had an approximately twofold increase in BAL total cells (both macrophages and neutrophils) compared to WT animals, while NTHi treatment resulted in a 4-5 fold increase in total BAL cells in both WT and pIgR-/- mice. Roflumilast treatment resulted in significant decreases in BAL cells in both WT and pIgR-/- mice exposed to NTHi lysates. NTHi exposure significantly increased both small airway wall thickness and emphysema scores in WT and pIgR-/- mice compared to aerosolized saline, but pIgR-/- mice had more extensive small airway remodeling and emphysema. Roflumilast treatment effectively reduced small airway wall thickness and emphysema in WT and pIgR-/- mice exposed to NTHi lysates. CONCLUSION. SIgA/pIgR deficiency results in increased inflammation, small airway remodeling, and emphysema following repetitive NTHi treatment in a murine model. Roflumilast, which has been shown to block inflammatory cell trafficking to the lung, blunted lung inflammation and COPD-like remodeling in this animal model, suggesting that this drug could have a disease modifying effect in COPD.
