N2  - Rationale: Ceftaroline fosamil (CPT-F) is approved for the treatment of community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP) and acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI). CAPTURE is a multicenter retrospective study in patients treated with CPT-F for CABP and ABSSSI in the US. The elderly are a population at risk for significant morbidity and mortality due to CABP. Experience with CPT-F in the treatment of CABP in the elderly is presented here. Methods: Data on demographics, disease characteristics, antibiotic use, and outcomes of patients treated with CPT-F were collected at participating study centers in the US by randomized selection and chart review from August 2011 to February 2013. The evaluable population consisted of those who had a clinical outcome determined. Results: The CABP cohort comprised 398 (28%) of all evaluable patients. There were 50% males; the mean ( ± SD) age was 64 ± 18 years (range 19-99), and 196 (49%) were elderly (age ≥ 65 years). Among these patients, the most common symptoms on diagnosis were: dyspnea (74%), abnormal auscultatory findings (57%), cough (56%), and sputum production (35%). Common co-morbidities included structural lung disease (48%), history of pneumonia (29%), and congestive heart failure (27%). At the time of CPT-F administration, supplemental oxygen was used in 70% of patients and 30% were treated in an intensive care unit. Antibiotics before CPT-F therapy were administered in 81% of patients; the most commonly used classes included cephalosporins (40%), quinolones (28%), macrolides (27%), and glycopeptides (24%). The mean ( ± SD) duration of CPT-F therapy was 6.0 ± 3.8 days. Adjunctive antibiotic therapy was administered in 63% of patients and included macrolides (23%), quinolones (19%), and glycopeptides (8%). Pathogens were recovered in 34% of patients, the predominant being Staphylococcus aureus (19%) and Streptococcus pneumoniae (3%). Overall clinical success was reported in 80% (156/196) of the elderly. Disposition was to home (51%) or another healthcare facility (42%). Conclusions: CABP in the elderly is a severe disease. Patients in this study had significant co-morbidities and a high level of acuity. CPT-F offers a viable treatment option in these complicated patients, and its use in an ever-increasing elderly population warrants further investigation.
