N2  - Rationale: Ceftaroline fosamil (CPT-F) is FDA approved for the treatment of community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP) and acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections. There are little data on the use of CPT-F in the intensive care unit (ICU). CAPTURE is a multicenter retrospective study evaluating patients treated with CPT-F in the US. Methods: Data for demographics, disease characteristics, antibiotic use, pathogens, and outcomes were collected by chart review from August 2011 to February 2013. Patients with a clinical outcome determined were included in the evaluable population. Results: 398 patients with CABP were evaluable. Of these, 138 (35%) received care in the ICU. The mean age was 62 years (SD ± 17, range 19 - 96); 43% were 65 years or older and 59% were male. 78% of patients had a relevant medical history, including structural lung disease (42%), smoking (31%), and congestive heart failure (22%). At diagnosis, 2 or more signs or symptoms were noted in 88% of patients, including dyspnea (79%), abnormal breath sounds (59%), and cough (52%). CPT-F was used as 2nd-line therapy in 87% of patients. Prior antibiotics included glycopeptides, cephalosporins, quinolones, and macrolides. Concurrent antibiotics were used in 69% of patients, most commonly quinolones and macrolides. The mean duration of CPT-F therapy was 7 days (SD ± 5). Over 95% of patients were dosed with CPT-F twice daily. The mean ICU stay was 15 days (SD ± 20, range 1 - 157). Overall, clinical success was achieved in 68% of patients. Clinical success with CPT-F used as monotherapy was 72% and as concurrent therapy was 66%. Clinical success with CPTF used as 2nd-line therapy was 69% and as 1st-line therapy was 61%. Conclusions: Clinical success with CPT-F was high in patients treated for CABP in the ICU, whether it was used as 1st- or 2nd-line therapy, or as monotherapy. Clinical success rates were slightly lower when CPT-F was used concurrently with other agents, possibly because these patients were more complicated. At the end of CPT-F therapy, symptoms decreased, demonstrating a response to therapy. These data support the use of CPT-F as a treatment option in patients with severe CABP requiring ICU care.
