N2  - Rationale: Non cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis children (NCFBE) are increasing in prevalence, especially in developing countries, with significant morbidity. The aim of our study was to describe clinical features of case series of children with NCFBE at Hospital Italiano De Buenos Aires, Argentina. Methods: Case series study of 14 children with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis. The data included age, sex, clinical presentation, etiology, radiological findings, bacteriological cultures, pulmonary function tests and treatments. Results: A total of 14 patients were included (5 girls), median age at diagnosis 10 years (range: 9 months - 18 years) with a follow-up of 4 years (range 1 - 10 years) Common signs/symptoms were chronic cough, purulent sputum, recurrent pneumonia, wheeze, digital clubbing, chest deformity and hemoptysis. Underlying etiology of non-CF bronchiectasis patients were: postinfectious bronchiectasis (n=7), gastroesophageal reflux / chronic aspiration (n= 4) and immunodeficiency (n=3). Sweat test was on normal in all patients. High-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) scan was used to diagnose 100% patients. Radiological findings showed a bilateral disease in 5 children with more than 3 lobes involved in all of them. Left lower lobes were most commonly involved. The medical treatment in all patients was prompt antibiotic use in exacerbations, bronchodilators and physiotherapy. Inhaled steroids were given in 12/14 . Obstructive or restrictive spirometric pattern were found in 3/14 and 4/14 patients respectively. The annual exacerbation rate was 2 episodes/year (range 1 to 4.5). The most common bacterial pathogen involved in bronchial secretions was haemophilus influenzae. Azithromycin was used as prophylactic antibiotic treatment in 9/14 patients. There was clinical improvement after treatment in most of the patients. In spite of intensive medical treatment 2/14 required surgery due to recurrent hemoptysis Conclusions: Bronchiectasis remains a disease of concern to pediatricians, particularly in developing countries. Early diagnosis and treatment can decrease the long term morbidity and mortality associated with bronchiectasis. Although medical treatment is the mainstay of management, surgery should be considered in selected patients.
