N2  - Rationale Bronchiectasis is a chronic respiratory condition characterised by a vicious cycle of inflammation and infection of the airways that leads to permanent damage. It is characterized by excessive bronchial secretions, cough and recurrent exacerbations that require prolonged antibiotics treatment with or without hospitalization. The reasons for some patients with recurrent pulmonary exacerbations and hospitalisations are unknown. We studied the characteristics of patients with pulmonary exacerbation of bronchiectasis and recurrent hospital admissions. Methods A retrospective study on all adult patients admitted with acute pulmonary exacerbation of bronchiectasis between January 2011 and December 2011. Patients were divided into 2 groups: (1) non-recurrent admission group i.e. one hospital admission with pulmonary exacerbation of bronchiectasis, and (2) recurrent admission group. Patients' demography, smoking status, co-morbidities, spirometric lung function and sputum microbiology were analysed. Results 189 patients were included in this study. Of these, 118 (62%) patients had 1 admission and 71 (38%) had more than 1 admission. The median age (IQR) of the non-recurrent and recurrent admission groups was 73(62-81) and 71(62-79) respectively (p= 0.704). They were matched for sex, body mass index and smoking status. The mean (SD) predicted FEV1 and FVC of the recurrent admission group was significantly lower than the non-recurrent admission group (47(13) vs 55(24), p=0.032; 51(15.5) vs 59(23.5), p=0.033). Mental illness was more common in the recurrent admission group (16% vs 3%, p=0.0045). The prevalence of cardiovascular disease, diabetes mellitus, asthma, previous pulmonary TB, sputum isolation of pseudomonas aeruginosa and non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) was similar in both groups. 56% of the patients in the recurrent admission group had at least 1 hospital admission with pulmonary exacerbation in the preceding year compared to 20% of the patients with non-recurrent admission (p<0.0001). On subgroup analysis, 3 or more hospital admissions in the preceding year was associated with a significant risk of hospital admission (p=0.0003, OR19.2 95%CI(2.4-152.4). 14% of the patients in the recurrent admission group was on long term oxygen therapy (LTOT) versus 3% in the non-recurrent admission group (p=0.0051). Conclusions This study showed that patients with recurrent hospitalisations with pulmonary exacerbation were associated with a significantly reduced spirometric lung function. The presence of mental illness, on LTOT, and a history of hospitalisation with pulmonary exacerbation in the preceding year were more common in patients with recurrent admissions. This may suggest a recurrent exacerbation phenotype which will require further prospective study.
