N2  - Introduction: Molecular testing is important in lung cancer as targeted therapies are now available. Case reports show genetic changes are not mutually exclusive. This case reviews the treatment outcome for a patient with leptomeningeal disease (LM) secondary to non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with two simultaneous genetic alterations. Case Description: A 45 year old Laotian male, lifelong nonsmoker with history of latent tuberculosis presented with 1 month history of bi-temporal headache, fatigue, 20 pound unintentional weight loss following a trip to Laos. He acknowledged 2 month history of nausea, vomiting and diarrhea. He denied history of fever, chills, nightsweats, cough, hemoptysis or palpitations. He had received Bacillus Calmette-Guérin vaccine. Initially he was treated for bacterial and tuberculous meningitis. Infectious work-up for human immunodeficiency virus, hepatitis, coccidoidomycosis and syphilis were negative. Chest radiograph was suggestive of miliary disease. Computed tomography (CT) of the chest demonstrated “diffuse nodular appearance of the interstitium with 1-2 millimeter nodules throughout both lungs”. Transbronchial biopsy revealed “poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma”. Further testing demonstrated epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) deletion mutation at exon 19 (E746-A750del5) and anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) gene rearrangement at 2p23. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain showed “abnormal leptomeningeal enhancement”. His cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) showed “large vacuolated cells” which were similar in appearance to cells from the bronchial washings. He was started on Erlotinib 150 milligrams (mg) daily, and then increased to 600 mg every 4 days for better CSF penetration. He received 5 treatments of whole brain radiation with a cumulative radiation dose of 2000 centigray. Post-treatment MRI of the brain 7 months later showed “scattered areas of irregular leptomeningeal enhancement markedly improved”. Discussion: A PubMed search with the terms “EGFR”, “ALK” and “lung cancer” in humans and written in English identified 189 articles. Upon further evaluation, 7 articles reported 12 different cases of patients harboring both EGFR and ALK abnormalities. This is the first case report of a patient with 2 genetic alterations (EGFR and ALK) with LM. This case raises the question on whether patients with dual mutations have a predisposition to central nervous system disease. It has been reported that the survival of patients with LM with NSCLC is about 3 months. Our patient survived 7 1/2 months after the time of diagnosis. It is not clear whether targeted therapy crosses the blood-brain barrier; nevertheless, this case suggests that targeted therapy provided a mortality benefit. (Figure Presented).
