N2  - Cases of Lymphoma presenting with endobronchial lesions have been mostly attributed to Hodgkin's lymphoma. Rarely does non-Hodgkin's lymphoma present with endobronchial lesions. Samuels et al reported a case series of 5 patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma invading into the tracheobronchial tree in 1961. Kilgore et al (1983) reported a series of 4 such patients, and they postulated that endobronchial spread was due to direct invasion from mediastinal lymph nodes, and lymphatic spread to peribronchial connective tissue. Since then, there have been isolated case reports of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma presenting with endobronchial masses. We present a case of a patient with clinical features suggestive of a bronchogenic carcinoma but who was eventually diagnosed with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, as well as pulmonary and pleural tuberculosis. Mr L is a 73 year old gentleman with no past medical or smoking history. He presented in August 2013 with cough and hemoptysis for 1 week, without weight loss or night sweats. His chest X-ray showed a right lower zone mass, splaying of the main carina and a small right pleural effusion. Computed tomographic (CT) scan of the thorax revealed an irregular mass in the right lung with enlarged mediastinal, right hilar, and pericoeliac lymph nodes. There was also a right pleural effusion with thickened and enhancing pleural lining. He underwent a bronchoscopy, and an endobronchial lesion was seen in the left main bronchus (Figure 1) which was not apparent on the CT scan. Biopsies were taken from the lesion, and endobronchial ultrasound guided transbronchial needle aspirations of the mediastinal lymph nodes were performed. Both specimens demonstrated diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. A right thoracocentesis was also performed. The pleural fluid analysis was negative for malignant cells, but showed a raised adenosine deaminase of 54, and a positive polymerase chain reaction for tuberculosis (TB). He underwent chemotherapy for lymphoma, and anti tubercular treatment was commenced. His sputum culture was subsequently found to be positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis complex. Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma spreading into the tracheobronchial tree, although rare, has been reported, and needs to be considered in patients presenting with endobronchial lesions with or without lymphadenopathy. An added point of interest is the concurrent diagnosis of pleural and pulmonary tuberculosis, for which timely treatment was important, as this could have worsened dramatically with the commencement of chemotherapy.
