N2  - Introduction: Carboxyhemoglobin may now be measured transcutaneously (SpCO%) by a pulse CO-oximeter. Although usually thought to be of exogenous origin, carbon monoxide (CO) may be produced endogenously in multiple pulmonary or inflammatory conditions. While evaluating the Rainbow-SET Rad-57 Pulse CO-Oximeter (Masimo Inc., Irvine, CA) in a pediatric pulmonary service, a child with severe necrotizing staphylococcal pneumonia and sepsis had SpCO% monitored during her hospitalization and outpatient follow up. As an outpatient, clinical status and spirometry improved, but SpCO% continued to rise. Six months after discharge, acute surgical sequelae occurred, suggesting that the continued rise of SpCO% indicated ongoing inflammation despite her continued and consistent clinical improvement. Case Report: A previously healthy, athletic 12 year old Caucasian female was hospitalized in severe respiratory failure, shock, and acute renal failure. Chest radiograph showed bilateral opacities predominantly on the right. Blood and sputum cultures grew methicillin sensitive Staphylococcus aureus; viral studies showed Influenza B. She was treated with intensive cardio-pulmonary support for 3 weeks, chest tube drainage of a right pleural effusion and pneumothorax, hemodialysis for acute renal failure, and intravenous oxacillin, rifampin, and gentamicin for 4 weeks and was discharged after 6 weeks. Chest computerized tomography and subsequent radiographs showed right upper lobe opacification. SpCO% was initially 0, rose to 14 and declined to 0 three months after admission. Productive cough resolved; spirometry, appetite, and physical endurance showed steady improvement; nebulized dornase alfa, clarithromycin, night time oxygen, and chest physiotherapy were stopped; but SpCO% rose to 14 five months after discharge. Six months after discharge, productive cough, acute right sided chest pain, and dyspnea occurred after she wrestled playfully with her brother. Chest radiograph showed a right pneumothorax and air and liquid filled cysts. At surgery, pneumatoceles and bronchopleural fistulae emanated from the right upper lobe, which was resected. After right visceral and parietal pleural decortications, the right middle and lower lobes spontaneously re-inflated. During the next year, she had no acute illnesses or symptoms. SpCO% fell to 5 with continued improvement in spirometry and physical activity. Discussion: This report describes a late effect of necrotizing pneumonia when the patient was clinically improving. The progressive rise in SpCO% before the acute complication suggests that this may be an easily attainable measure of ongoing, subclinical inflammation. (Figure Presented).
