N2  - Introduction Acinetobacter baumannii (AB) has emerged worldwide as an important nosocomial pathogen. It is a relatively low-grade pathogen but it has become the pathogen causing life-threatening hospital acquired infections, particularly in intensive care units (ICU). Presently AB is important in the control of hospital acquired infections because it is detected frequently as a multiple drug and extreme drug resistant bacteria. To investigate the mode of transmission in the ICU, we performed colony PCR and Multiplex Ligation dependent Probe Amplification (MLPA) detection analysis to identify major multiple drug resistance (MDR) pathogens. Methods We collect air and surface samples in a 30 bed medical ICU from June to August 2012. The microorganisms obtained by air and surface-sampling were cultured in solid agar plate and tryptic soy broth media respectively. All colonies in a plate were collected and used for genomic DNA isolation. Then we performed PCR analysis for detection of microorganism. Liquid culture media were centrifuged to pellet the bacterial cells and the pellets were used for genomic DNA isolation. For multiplex detection of major MDR pathogens, predesigned MLPA probes were used. We select two patients as index cases for AB infection and compare cultural data with environmental sampling data. Results The diagnosis of two patients was septic shock with aspiration pneumonia and pneumonia respectively. Acinetobacter baumannii and Stenotrophomonas were identified on their sputum cultures. M. luteus, S.epidermidis, S.haemolyticus and Acinetobacter baumannii were identified from air-sampling using colony PCR analysis. Acinetobacter baumannii was also detected in air sample using MLPA detection method. Acinetobacter baumannii was detected on in environmental surface such as medical device, bed rail and computer mouse. Conclusion Acinetobacter baumannii was detected in air sample of medical ICU using MLPA detection method. It was also confirmed by colony PCR analysis. MLPA detection method can be used for identification of MDR pathogens. Although contact-spread is the principle route of transmission for Acinetobacter baumannii, airborne transmission may be the route of infection. Further investigation about airborne transmission of Acinetobacter baumannii will be needed for exact infectious epidemiology.
