N2  - AIM To describe the clinical manifestations of patients affected with influenza A like illness (H1N1 2013) during the endemic . INTRODUCTION Most cases of H1N1 influenza infection present as mild or subclinical pneumonia, but some present as severe community acquired pneumonia (CAP) and require admission to intensive care unit (ICU) usually. The main reason for admission to ICU is due to acute lung injury/acute respiratory distress syndrome (ALI/ARDS). It causes primary viral pneumonia with secondary bacterial infections and it is notoriously known to affect younger population. Pneumonia is one of the common and serious complications of H1N1. There is need to accumulate evidence regarding patient's intensive care parameters for effective management of newer strains of influenza viral infections. Hence an observed retrospective record analysis of confirmed H1N1 patients admitted to ICU of a tertiary care centre, from Jan 2013 to March 2013, was undertaken with the following aims. The study was designed: to study the profile and pattern of H1N1 patients admitted to ICU and to study the distribution and associated factors (patient care factors and laboratory parameters) with treatment outcomes (survivors vs. non-survivors). MATERIAL AND METHOD A retrospective study was conducted in patients with influenza-like illness receiving care at AIMSR BATHINDA Punjab India between JAN 2013 and March, 2013. The inclusion criteria were as follows 1) patients had influenza-like illness that was defined as fever, with cough and/or sore throat, 2) detection of influenza A H1N1 by real-time polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) from nasopharyngeal swabs or throat swabs. RESULTS Among 38 patients, 21 (55.0%) were male and the mean age was 50+/- 13.1 years (range 1-80). The presenting symptoms were cough (dry or productive) (38 patients, 100%), fever > or = 38.0 degrees C (32 patients, 84%), ARDS (5 patients, 13%), Consolidation(10 patients, 26%) and Shock(5 patients, 13%). Five patients had co-infection at admission CONCLUSION Younger people were more likely to be infected with influenza A H1N1 2013. The clinical manifestations were similar to the seasonal influenza. However, the mortality rate was much higher, particularly in patients who developed pneumonia. In this study, all patients who died had existing underlying medical conditions.
