N2  - Recreational abuse of nitrous oxide is associated with multiple complications, but there are no previous reports of respiratory failure and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) caused by nitrous oxide abuse in an adult. A 20-year-old man presented with a history of shortness of breath, cough, white sputum production, pleuritic chest pain, palpitations, fever, chills, and vomiting. Evaluation showed fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, bilateral decreased breath sounds, and oxygen desaturation. Laboratory studies showed leukopenia (white blood cell count, 1.9 ×109/L) and thrombocytopenia (platelet count, 80 ×109/L). Chest radiography showed bilateral infiltrates. Blood and sputum cultures showed methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Despite broad spectrum antibiotics, he developed severe hypoxic respiratory failure and ARDS complicated by bilateral tension pneumothorax and cardiopulmonary arrest. The patient's girlfriend reported that the patient had a history of inhaling nitrous oxide from a gas cylinder without a pressure-reducing valve, most recently 1 night before admission. After resuscitation, his pulmonary status improved gradually. Follow-up chest computed tomography showed residual thin walled cysts, cicatricial atelectasis with bronchiectasis in both upper lobes, and scattered nodular opacities. Follow-up pulmonary function tests showed normal inspiratory flow,moderately-severe restrictive airway disease, and moderately decreased diffusion capacity. In summary, severe respiratory failure and ARDS may occur from a combination of the effects of nitrous oxide abuse and community acquired MRSA pneumonia. (Figure Presented).
