N2  - Newborn screening for cystic fibrosis (CF) is now universal in the United States. As a result, most diagnoses are made in early infancy. Cohort studies where children are diagnosed through newborn screening, compared to children diagnosed with symptoms, have demonstrated better nutritional and pulmonary outcomes when early intervention is provided. These effects can persist for decades. Specific guidelines for the care of children with CF from birth until age 2 were developed several years ago (1); these have provided an important blueprint for the approach to asymptomatic infants with CF, a distinct shift from earlier symptom- based treatment pathways. There are also recent comprehensive guidelines for pulmonary maintenance treatments for individuals with CF greater than 6 years old (2). However, there are no existing guidelines for preschool children, ages 2 through 5. It has been well documented that pulmonary inflammation and infection may proceed in a clinically silent manner in infants and young children. The need for ongoing aggressive nutritional and gastrointestinal monitoring and intervention is critical in this age group. As a result, the CF Foundation convened a multidisciplinary group of experts to develop guidelines for children in the 2 through 5 year preschool age group. The intent of these recommendations is to complement the approach developed in the infant care guidelines. Monitoring for the development and progression of pulmonary disease in young children is important. Even in the absence of cough, dyspnea or overt respiratory symptoms, abnormalities are frequently detected by imaging or lung function testing. Radiographic studies may reveal gas trapping, bronchial wall thickening and bronchiectasis. Infant and preschool lung function testing have demonstrated airflow limitation and air trapping. Oropharyngeal cultures can detect pathogenic bacteria in asymptomatic children. The ongoing study of evolving modalities, such as multiple breath washout, may offer future promise of a more sensitive approach to the monitoring of pulmonary disease. Several questions regarding the surveillance of disease in this age group remain. These include the frequency and type of lung function measurements, imaging and the mode of detection of potentially pathogenic organisms in the respiratory tract. The management of pulmonary disease in this age group presents several challenges. There are very few randomized controlled trials for pulmonary therapeutics in infants and preschoolers with CF, as measureable endpoints have been difficult to define. Therefore, extrapolations from existing recommendations in older patients with attention to mechanism of action have served as the basis for current practice. While airway clearance has been recommended for all people with CF, there is little guidance for the use of dornase alfa, hypertonic saline, azithromycin, and high dose ibuprofen in the youngest patients (3). The role of prophylactic antibiotics for Staphylococcus aureus has continued to be the subject of international debate (4). The focus on optimal nutrition has been a goal for infant care in CF, as better nutritional outcomes in early childhood have been associated with superior pulmonary outcomes in adolescents and young adults with CF (5). Again, these youngest children present special challenges, as behavioral and developmental barriers can often affect successful adherence with prescribed feeding and supplementation regimens. The need for closer follow-up of children at nutritional risk or in nutritional failure has been recognized. In those infants and children who fail to reach nutritional goals, it is important to consider the incorporation of behavioral modification education and strategies into any algorithm. The early identification of symptoms of other gastrointestinal disorders may also prevent complications or a delay in appropriate treatment. Infants and young children who have evolving pancreatic insufficiency must be routinely monitored, as symptoms of malabsorption may not be obvious, even to astute clinicians. With the recent drafting of guidelines of care for preschool children, this symposium will discuss methods to achieve the desired effect of an early, aggressive approach for the surveillance and management of silent pulmonary disease, as well as the achievement of optimal nutritional status with attention to behavioral issues. The issues of adherence and treatment burden must be underscored, as they pose a significant barrier to effective treatment. Routine assessment of adherence, identification of risk factors for poor adherence, and the development of individualized care plans to improve adherence will be vital to the ultimate success of these early childhood guidelines.
