N2  - Role of NO in CF Lung Disease: The genetic disorder cystic fibrosis (CF), which affects ∼100.000 individuals in Europe and North America, is caused by mutations in the CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene (1). The causative microbes associated with pathogenesis of pulmonary infection vary, are of mixed origin, and sometimes are involved in secondary environmental opportunistic bacterial infections (2). Antibiotic therapy for patients with respiratory infections can be hampered by the presence of antibiotic-drug-resistant (AMR) bacteria, increased and tenacious sputum production, inflammation, or mismatched ventilation to blood flow; ultimately, these may lead to increased morbidity and mortality (3). Individuals with CF are especially vulnerable to such lung infections. Patients diagnosed with CF have chronic recurring pneumonias; therefore, antibiotics have to be administered during multiple treatment courses over the entire lifetime of the patients or death occurs due to AMR pneumonia or other related sequelae (1). Thus, there is an urgent and ever present need to develop alternative treatment strategies for patients with CF. Nitric oxide (NO) is a naturally occurring nano-molecule produced in mammals. NO has been shown to play important roles in vasodilatation, neurotransmission, angiogenesis, modulation of wound healing, and non-specific responses to infection (4). We hypothesize that inhaled NO may have utility treating patients with CF. De Winter-de Groot and Van der Ent reviewed the role of NO in the (patho) physiological processes in the lungs of CF patients (5). Exhaled and sinus production of NO in patients with CF is reportedly low. These reduced NO levels may result in bronchial obstruction, hinder first-line defense to bacterial and viral infections, decrease ion transport and increase neutrophil sequestration. Restoring NO levels may have a therapeutic benefit and improve pulmonary function in CF patients. Rationale for potential therapeutic use of NO in CF lung disease: Utilizing a variety of NO vehicles, we and others have shown that NO possesses antimicrobial activity in vitro against a wide variety bacteria, viruses, helminthes, and parasites. We identified the effective antimicrobial dose to be about 160 ppm. Beyond NO's potential antimicrobial quality, we offer 11 additional reasons that have provided sufficient rationale as to why NO should be studied further as an inhaled adjuvant therapy for CF. Briefly, there is a large body of data over the last decade, suggesting that NO: 1) is a smooth-muscle relaxant; 2) may not have the concomitant bronchoconstriction associated with other aerosolized medicaments; 3) may be a natural mucolytic by nitrosylating or preventing sulphur-sulphur bonds in mucus, thus thinning secretions-an essential component of bronchial toilet ; 4) has a biological life of only a few seconds and thus might not accumulate in the body; 5) possesses anti-inflammatory activity; 6) may improve oxygenation during infection due to its selective vasodilator attribute; 7) may be well tolerated because host cells have higher levels of thiols that neutralize nitrosative stressors ; 8) may enhance the acquired immune response; 9) as a gas, can be delivered homogenously throughout the lung, as compared to aerosol drug delivery; 10) is an approved drug for full-term infants with an excellent safety profile; and 11) is not an antibiotic. Safety, pilot and future studies using inhaled NO in CF lung disease: Based on a literature review of the metabolic action and pharmacokinetics of methemoglobin, we developed a strategy of intermittent inhalation of 160 ppm NO. To overcome the problem of methemoglobinemia secondary to continuous NO inhalation, we proposed, and validated, in vitro and in vivo, the concept that an intermittent exposure to 160 ppm NO for 30 minutes every four hours would provide the same antimicrobial effects as continuous NO delivery (6). In vitro, exposure of clinical multi-drug resistant Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli strains, which had been isolated from the lungs of nosocomial pneumonia patients, and of a lethal antibiotic-resistant strain of Pseudomonas aeruginosa, isolated from a deceased CF patient, resulted in >5 log10 reduction in bacterial load after multiple, 30-minute treatments with 160 ppm NO, applied every four hours. We completed a Phase I clinical study in ten healthy adult volunteers, in which we investigated the safety and the physiologic effects of the delivery of intermittent 160 ppm NO (30 minutes), every four hours, five times a day, for five consecutive days) (7). The treatments were well tolerated. No significant adverse events occurred and three minor adverse events not attributable to NO treatment were reported. Methemoglobin concentration rose by 0.9% during treatment, in keeping with published first-order pharmacokinetics, and increased slightly between baseline (Day 1) and Day 5. However, the statistical analysis revealed that this increase was not associated with repeated treatments on the same day. Several lung function parameters, including FEV1 and lung diffusion were not different from baseline, demonstrating that NO at that concentration does not impair lung function. Blood chemistry, hematology, serum nitrites/nitrates, prothrombin, and pro-inflammatory cytokine and chemokine levels did not show any differences from baseline. These data suggested that inhalation of 160 ppm NO for 30 min, five times a day, for five consecutive days, is well tolerated in healthy individuals. Based on our data, from in vitro, in vivo and from the human safety trial, we collaborated with the late Dr. Gerd Doering at the University of Tubingen to initiate a prospective, monocentric, open-labelled, clinical Phase I study in CF patients. Our preliminary findings in eight adult chronic CF patients receiving NO by inhalation (30 min, thrice daily, at a concentration of 160 ppm for two periods of five days each) demonstrated safety and provided encouraging secondary outcomes. Although not powered for efficacy, there was an excellent, multiple log-fold reduction in colony forming units (cfu) for all bacterial and fungal species tested, with some being eradicated. In addition, lung function as determined by FEV1 showed an absolute and relative- to-baseline increase (10.9±3.7% and 21.2 ± 6.1% (P=0.028), respectively). Use of inhaled NO in randomized, controlled trials in patients with CF is on-going.
