N2  - The gastrointestinal tract carries the task of receiving nutrient and non-nutrient intake and then via a complex and coordinated system, processes, digests, absorbs and expels breakdown products. Any alteration in the physiology of the GI tract can result in negative sequalae. The gut is a key center of interaction with both ingested and intrinsic microbiota, as most nutrients cannot be absorbed in their natural form and require “assistance.” CF is also characterized by many co-morbidities related to aberrant mucosa and chronic inflammation in both airways and GI tracts: the latter serves as the primary interface between the gut microbiota and endocrine, neural and immune systems (1). In both the CF and non-CF world, the presence of and the balance between “acid” and “base” is essential for a myriad of processes in the quest to “be healthy.” In CF, it is especially important to accomplish digestion and appropriate absorption. It is known that CFTR plays a key role in ductal HCO3- (bicarbonate) secretion (2-6), and that bicarbonate secretion is vital for the formation and proper function of bodily fluids in the central organs, including pancreatic juice in the exocrine pancreas. Normally, chloride excreted into the lumen is exchanged for bicarbonate, with sodium and water following; in CF, however, the abnormal CFTR limits this exchange resulting in a more viscous bicarbonate-depleted fluid. That said, the secretion of HCO3--rich isotonic fluid serves to raise the pH of the duodenal contents, in concert with bile from the liver. Bicarbonate, in general, serves many functions including a buffer for intracellular and extracellular fluid acid-base balance; CO2 transport by blood; and innate defense and mucosal protection. The acinar cells of the exocrine pancreas secrete fluids that are rich in digestive enzymes and chloride; fluids from here contain the highest concentration of HCO3-, and this product assists to neutralize gastric acid and provides an optimal pH environment for digestive enzyme function in the duodenum (4,7). The presence and amount of CFTR present, though, will determine the actual amount secreted. In CF, both duodenal and pancreatic secretion of HCO3- are diminished, and duodenal pH is inferior compared to non-CF (5,6). Impaired pancreatic function eventually results in increased gastric acidity, which in turn can cause GER/ heartburn, possible peptic ulcers, as well as impairment of oral pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT). This serves as just one of the many challenges in pancreatic insufficient (PI) CF as interventions are directed toward maximizing nutritional intake. It is well documented that gastric acidity is a protective mechanism, and assists the intestinal barrier, or “gut barrier,” in minimizing bacterial distribution: the establishment of enteric infection and possible bacterial overgrowth is considered to be directly related to reduction in gastric acid (6-9). Gastric acid, gut micro-flora, local gut immunity, intestinal motility and secretion, and the gut barrier all assist in keeping “bad” microbes in check (7). In fact, within the small intestine, gastric acid and peristalsis are the 2 major defenses against bacterial overgrowth. This gut barrier further serves 2 critical functions: nutrient absorption as well as defense from macromolecule penetration. In this capacity, it is also important for nutrient and energy metabolism (1). Many studies have demonstrated the higher the pH (i.e., more alkaline) the less bactericidal protection present (1,6,8,9), a significant fact in preventing gastroenteritis. There are many triggers that will affect this gut permeability, including medications, microbes, and nutrients (10,11). This, in turn, influences what and how nutrients are absorbed, and influences the interventions necessary, including pharmacological. As is evident, the acid/base “balancing act,” therefore, becomes of high priority: both are needed, and often at different times. Rapid neutralization of gastric acid in the proximal portion of the small intestine (mostly duodenal) is essential to facilitate nutrient absorption and digestion- i.e., the need for an “alkaline” environment-yet, “acid” is needed for many microbiome, and intestinal gut, protective activities (8-11). Optimal duodenal pH required to dissolve enteric coating of PERT is in the range of 5.5-6.0 (10), yet this range and gastric “suppression” has been shown to be NOT optimal, or conducive, for GI gut barrier function in protecting against microbial overgrowth and further ramifications (7,9,11). Any increase in pH above 4 may cause a state of hypochlorhydria and allow ingested bacteria to survive this “gastric trap”; this pH > 4 appears to be a “watershed line” for acquisition of various infections (7). For about 90% of CF, PERT becomes a lifelong treatment for PI and exemplifies the challenge between finding the right “mix” of acid and base as impaired bicarbonate secretion is part of the CFTR defect. Interventions, both pharmacological and nutritional, must be well thought-out in order to maximize the “acid/ base” of underlying CF pathophysiology, in the quest to optimizing overall health. PERT requires a more alkaline environment to efficiently release its microsphere encapsulated products, yet the gut barrier requires a more acid milieu to function and protect efficiently. While the CF patient is at a higher risk for GER due to chronic cough, delayed gastric emptying and high fat diet (7,8), most acid reducing medications are used to provide a more alkaline environment in an attempt to maximize non-bicarbonate- buffered PERT rather than true GERD, peptic ulcer disease, or duodenal hyperacidity. While seemingly beneficial, this suppressing of gastric acid can eliminate bactericidal properties of the gastric acid milieu and even compromise respiratory issues (7,10). Both a newer bicarbonate-buffered PERT, currently on the market, as well as non-porcine enzymes not currently available, offer a different approach to this issue. Numerous studies, including a 2012 Cochrane review (12) showed no real benefit with either the proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) or H2 blockers. Further studies, particularly with PPIs, have also shown that by increasing gastric pH, there is an increased risk of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth and gastroenteritis (7,9,13-16), pneumonia, C. difficile diarrhea, fractures due to reduced calcium absorption and B12 levels (7,13), altering of various immunomodulatory and anti-inflammatory effects (7), as well as concern over increasing hypersensitivity reactions (17). A 2014 study found that compared to non-CF children, CF children do not have more frequent reflux and actually had better bolus clearance following reflux; however, they had a significantly prolonged chemical clearance perhaps due to decreased buffering capacity of the saliva and/or the fact that gastric hypersecretion is common in CF (18). There are numerous avoidance factors such as foods, smoking, alcohol, medications, co-morbid conditions (7), that we can educate to, in addition to the questionable efficacy of both H2s and the PPIs, especially the seemingly long term concerns of PPI (7,19). On a positive note, by following some basic common sense, avoiding some practices and encouraging others such as a healthy diet, a “brief” buffering for maximizing PERT, and the use of probiotics (20,21), it appears possible that we may be able to minimize the “disappearing microbiota theory” that implicates many developments contributing to a shift in the balance between different species and types of microorganisms in the gut, which, in turn, alters the symbiotic relationship between our gut microflora and the health benefits that have been conferred in the past (22). It is known that intestinal inflammation is present, and bacterial overgrowth possible, in CF. A study reported in 2010 showed that a mixed probiotic supplement not only reduced pulmonary exacerbation rate, but may also have a preventive potential for pulmonary deterioration (21). Perhaps, after all, pH can be successfully modulated in optimizing overall health!.
