N2  - Airway submucosal glands (SMGs) secrete a variety of macromolecules into the airway surface liquid (ASL) that play an important role in the composition, volume and physical properties of ASL. Serous and mucous acinar cells within SMGs, along with secretory cells in the surface airway epithelia, are the major sources of small antimicrobials and large molecules such as mucins. These proteins protect the airways from bacterial infection and ensure proper mucociliary transport. In CF, these components are defective, rendering the airway prone to infection. To better understand the components of ASL that are contributed by the surface airway epithelium and submucosal glands, we used a proteomics approach to compare proteomes secreted by polarized human airway epithelial cells (hAEC) to that of secretions produced by bronchial submucosal glands collected under oil following stimulation. Three non-CF and 3 CF SMG samples were obtained from lung donors at the time of the lung transplant. The glandular secretions were obtained upon applying cholinergic (carbachol) secretagogue basolaterally. The droplets that are secreted from glands were collected from the apical surface under a microscope and represented the pure mucus from the glands. hAEC secretions from polarized human primary cells were obtained from non-CF and CF donor millicells (3 millicells per donor) upon washing the apical surface with PBS for 5 days and combining the secretions. LC-MS/MS analyses of SMG secretions were combined and results afforded a total of 743 proteins with a minimum 2 peptides and 0% false discovery rate (FDR) by Mascot Search Engine. There were 550 proteins in non-CF and 649 proteins in CF samples. Among these, 456 proteins were common in both genotypes. The most common proteins were mucin 5AC, a gel-forming mucin and complement C3 proteins. Pathway analysis of the data revealed numerous proteins that are linked to oxidation or inflammation. Of interest, mitochondrial dysfunction was the most significant pathway in both non-CF and CF samples. Analysis of ASL proteins yielded a total of 254 proteins with 222 in non-CF and 199 in the CF sample. Similar to SMG, the most abundant proteins were an epithelial secreted mucin Muc16 and complement C3. Pathway analysis yielded actin cytoskeleton signaling as the most significant pathway. Both SMG and ASL data contained proteins with known antimicrobial properties or that played a role in immunity. Such proteins included alpha1-antichymotrypsin, lipocalin, lysozyme, protein S100, proteases such as cathepsin S, D and B and antiproteases. There were 134 unique proteins in both non-CF and CF ASL that did not exist in SMG. Likewise, there were 608 unique proteins from non-CF and CF gland samples that were absent in ASL. Pathway analysis of both SMG and surface airway secretomes produced insights into airway functions involved in innate immunity and inflammation, and shed light on biologic differences in CF.
