N2  - The high mortality rate of individuals with CF is related to the development of bacterial infections in mucus plugs that form due to ineffective mucociliary clearance (MCC). The development of novel and more effective treatments for diseases such as CF and COPD will be aided by a better understanding of healthy MCC, however the exact mechanism of successful MCC is unknown. Traditional studies of MCC are done on horizontal cell cultures or in animal models, but these methods exclude the important external force of gravity. Our lab has developed a bioreactor and tilting microscope system for studying the effect of gravity on MCC in which we grow primary human bronchial epithelial cells (HBEcs) that ciliate and produce mucus. This system allows us to distinguish between materials that can and cannot be cleared against gravity in order to define which properties of mucus are necessary and sufficient for vertical transport to occur. The different polymer gels (simulants) are chosen because they share different rheological or biochemical (compositional) characteristics with mucus. In each experiment a well ciliated culture is placed on the microscope, which is then tilted to create a physiologically relevant air-liquid interface (ALI) region. The material to be tested, dosed with 2μm yellow-green beads, is added apically and the bead transport is later quantified using particle image velocimetry. The only material that we have seen successfully transport at ALI against gravity is 2.5% reconstituted HBE mucus, which resembles healthy lung mucus. Because we believe there is a transient interaction between mucus and cilia that aids in vertical transport, we developed an adhesion assay to examine this further. Beads are functionalized with a protein or moiety found in the mucus or on the cilia surface, and are placed in direct contact with cells. The channel is hooked up to a syringe pump at a ramped volumetric flow rate and the flow rate, and therefore the drag force, needed to remove the beads is determined. Using lectins specific for sugar moieties important in other adhesion phenomena, we have identified the presence of sialic acid and N-acetylglucosamine on the cell surface, as well as demonstrated the ability to block the interaction of these sugars with the lectin functionalized beads. The presence of these sugars, and the ability to block the interaction of these sugars, gives us targets for the mucus-cell surface interactions (in particular, carbohydrate-carbohydrate interactions), and therefore potential mechanisms for turning off successful clearance in the MCC assay. For example, by functionalizing 1.5% 250kDa guar gum, which matches the tanδ of healthy mucus, with sialic acid using alpha 2,6 sialytransferase, and comparing their ability to clear against gravity, we can determine whether the addition of compositional likeness to mucus can make a material that is rheologically similar but unable to clear obtain the ability to clear against gravity. Together, these assays will allow us to determine which characteristics are necessary and sufficient for healthy clearance to occur, therefore illuminating potential targets to regain transport in diseases such as CF and COPD.
