N2  - Objective: CF lung disease is characterized by persistent bacterial infection, triggering neutrophilic inflammation and release of neutrophil elastase. This overwhelms the antiprotease defenses in the airway, causing a persistent protease/antiprotease imbalance. Alpha-1 HC is a new formulation of alpha1-proteinase inhibitor (A1PI) formulated for aerosol administration. Inhaled A1PI, as a means to address the protease/antiprotease imbalance and reduce inflammation in CF airways, has been reported as early as 1991. Our study sought to evaluate the safety and tolerability of inhaled Alpha-1 HC compared to placebo in subjects with CF. Methods: We conducted a multi-center, sequential dose escalation, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study, testing the safety and tolerability of 100 or 200 mg of inhaled Alpha-1 HC compared to placebo administered once daily for 3 weeks in subjects with CF. Thirty adult subjects (15 subjects per dose cohort) were randomized in a 2:1 ratio of Alpha-1 HC to placebo within each dose cohort. Safety from the 100mg treatment cohort was evaluated by the CF Foundation data monitoring committee prior to escalating to 200mg. Sputum, blood and urine biomarkers of inflammation were also collected and analyzed. For statistical analysis, the placebo groups from the two cohorts were pooled. Results: Demographic and baseline characteristics were comparable between treatment groups (alpha-HC 100 mg, alpha-HC 200 mg, and placebo), with 20 female and 10 male subjects enrolled. The majority of subjects 19/30 (63.3%) had two copies of the F508del genetic mutation. Treatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in all 3 groups, primarily in the respiratory system. Seven (20.0%) of the 35 TEAEs in the 100 mg dose group, 3 (13.0%) of 23 in the 200 mg dose group, and 4 (14.3%) of 28 in the placebo group were drug-related. One serious adverse event (SAE) occurred in 1 subject within each treatment group. These were a pulmonary exacerbation in the 100mg and placebo groups, and abdominal pain in the 200mg cohort. No SAE was judged related to study drug or placebo. Sputum data indicated a dose-related increase in A1PI levels. No clinically meaningful changes from baseline were recorded for vital signs, laboratory parameters including immunogenicity, pulmonary function tests, or peak flow measurements. No subject experienced a viral infection. Conclusions: Both 100 mg and 200 mg doses of inhaled Alpha-1 HC administered daily for 21 days were safe and well-tolerated. Aerosolized Alpha-1 HC may provide a novel therapeutic approach to address CF airway inflammation by correcting the protease/antiprotease imbalance.
