N2  - Introduction: The paranasal sinuses are an ideal breeding ground for bacteria, and may represent a reservoir for colonisation leading to repeated lower respiratory tract (LRT) infection. While this risk is recognised in the pulmonary transplant setting it has received less attention outside this group. We have previously demonstrated increased sensitivity of paranasal sinus sampling compared with LRT sampling and that paranasal sinus eradication of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (P.aeruginosa) is viable in the outpatient setting. Evidence from the EPIC study suggests recurrent P. Aeruginosa infections can be predicted from raised anti-pseudomonal antibodies (PsAb). Aims: To investigate if there is a relationship between raised PsAb and growth of P. aeruginosa in sinonasal lavage. Methods: PsAb were measured using a standard ELISA against LPS antigen from strain AK1401 at the Centre for Infections Laboratory, of the Public Health Authority in Colindale, London. Antibody titre is defined in terms of optical density, where a level of less than 1 is defined as a normal level. Paranasal sinus lavage was performed as previously described. LRT samples included cough swabs (n = 54) and sputa (n = 7). Samples from 2012-2014 underwent retrospective review. The chi-squared test was used to assess the relationship between PsAb status (positive - PsAb >1,and negative - PsAb < 1) and P. aeruginosa status (positive and negative) in paranasal sinus samples. Results: A total of 61 paired paranasal sinus and LRT samples were collected in 61 unique patients, all with a corresponding PsAb level (Table) p = 0.000 using chi-squared statistical test. The mean age was 11 years (IQR 10 - 12). Of the 20 children with raised PsAb, 9 (45%) had negative LRT and paranasal sinus culture. Discussion: In this series, a statistical significance demonstrated that normal PsAb was associated with P. aeruginosa-negative sinus culture. We have previously shown a correlation between LRT and sinus sample microflora (P=0.02), with increased sensitivity in sinus sampling compared with LRT sampling. These findings suggest that paranasal sinus P. aeruginosa culture may be a useful marker for persistence of pseudomonal disease. Future work should explore the impact of targeted parallel upper and lower airway therapy on both sinus culture and pseudomonal serology. (Figure Presented).
