N2  - Background and Objectives: Tobramycin powder for inhalation (TIPTM) is a fast and convenient way of delivering tobramycin to the lungs of CF patients for the treatment of chronic P. aeruginosa (Pa) infections (1). Cough has been previously reported to be more prevalent with the inhalation of TIP than tobramycin inhalation solution (TIS) (1). As a part of the safety analysis in this 48-week study, post-inhalation events associated with TIP were specifically assessed. Methods: In this single-arm, open-label, multi-center, phase IV trial, CF patients aged ≥6 years with a baseline FEV1 ≥25 to ≤75% predicted and infected with Pa, received for 6 cycles (each cycle=28 days on- and 28 days off- treatment) of TIP (112 mg tobramycin) b.i.d. via T-326 Inhaler. The primary endpoint was the incidence of treatment emergent adverse events (AEs). A unique case report form was used for the first time in the TIP clinical program to capture events (defined as cough or “other”) that occurred within 5 min of inhalation of the study drug. Results: A total of 157 patients were enrolled in the study and 96 patients (61.1%) completed. Patients were older (mean (SD) of 27.8 (10.82) years of age) than in previous trials with TIP [mean (SD) of 13.4 (4.42), 26 (11.4) and 12.9 (4.3) years, in EVOLVE (2), EAGER (1) and EDIT (3) trials, respectively] and had a baseline FEV1% predicted mean (SD) of 50.2 (13.95). The incidence of post-inhalation cough was highest at the first administration (31.4% of patients) and then subsequently decreased over time to the range of 21-22% of patients during Cycles 4, 5, and 6. The mean duration of post-inhalation cough that was reported at various time points was lowest (0.3 min) at the end of Cycle 6 and highest (3.4 min) at the end of Cycle 2. Most patients were able to take the complete dose of 4 capsules. No action was needed to reduce post-inhalation events in over 85% of patients. The overall incidence of cough as an AE was lower (n=23.6%) than in the EAGER (1) study [TIP (n=48.4%) and TIS (n=31.1%)]. Cough AEs were not reported as serious adverse events in the ETOILES study. Cough led to study drug discontinuation in 5.7% of patients. There was no difference in clinically significant bronchospasm (airway reactivity, measured as a decrease in FEV1 of 20% or more post-inhalation) in this trial compared to the EAGER (1) study. Conclusion: Post-inhalation cough events were specifically captured during this study. They were generally of short duration and decreased over the course of the study, possibly due to experience with administration of TIP.
