N2  - Background: Correct cleaning of nebulizers after an aerosol therapy session is considered essential for the nebulizer to maintain its function and to avoid contamination. Inadequate or infrequent cleaning can have a negative impact on particle size characteristics, increase nebulization time and increase the risk for infection. Objective: To determine compliance with recommendations for cleaning, disinfection, and maintenance of nebulizers by supplier. Methods: Children with cystic fibrosis (CF) (6-18 years) of the Erasmus MC-Sophia CF centre using an Akita®, I-neb® or other jet nebulizer (Sidestream or Pari LC Plus) were randomly selected. Patients or their parents completed a questionnaire regarding nebulizer cleaning, disinfection and maintenance procedures. Procedures were considered correct if they followed the recommendations of the Erasmus MC-Sophia CF centre. Key elements for cleaning were dismantling the equipment, washing, rinsing and drying after each use. Key elements for disinfection were submersion once daily for 10 minutes in alcohol 70% followed by air drying. Key elements for maintenance were boiling the equipment once a week in water with detergent/vinegar. Results: Thirty-two patients (47% male) were included, median (range) age 11.5 (6-18) years, FEV1 102.5 (64-132) %pred. Nebulizer: Akita 15.6%, I-neb 62.5%, Sidestream 21.9%. Twenty-three patients (71.9%) cleaned and disinfected their equipment in accordance with our recommendations. Most mistakes were made in washing and in disinfection in alcohol for 10 minutes maximum. Maintenance procedures were correctly executed in 25% of the patients. All recommendations regarding nebulizer cleaning, disinfection and maintenance procedures were followed by 12.5% of the patients. Conclusion: Three quarters of patients comply with our cleaning and disinfection recommendations. However, recommendations regarding maintenance of the equipment are poorly followed which may have a negative effect on nebulizer performance. Adherence to cleaning and maintenance procedures at home should be regularly discussed with patients and parents to reduce the risk for bacterial contamination and dysfunctioning nebulizers. In addition, instructions on paper should be given to the patients and parents.
