N2  - The target of this retrospectivestudy is to illustrate the epidemiological characteristics, clinical features, underlying etiologic factors (EF), and outcome of children with bronchiectasis Methods Forty two children with bronchiectasis were admitted between January 2009 and December 2013. All patients underwent a comprehensive examination to identify EF. Data analysis in terms of age at onset, initial presenting symptoms, underlying etiology, distinct change in treatment, distribution of pulmonary involvement on computed tomography, and bronchal flora was performed RESULTS: Mean age at diagnosis was 9,29 years (2 -15). Persistent cough and bronchorrhea were the most common symptoms. 9% of patients complain about exercise dyspnoea, together with chest deformation and/or finger clubbing. Haemoptysis was noted in 28% of cases. Mean time to diagnosis from symptom onset was 3,33 years (range: 0 to 10). The underlying etiologies were identified in 66% of patients: primary ciliary dyskinesia (40%) previous severe pneumonia (16%), and immunodeficiency (11%) were the most common causes. Bacteriologic tests isolated H. influenza in 66% of cases, S. pneumonia in 30% of cases and Pseudomonas aeruginosa in 16% of cases. Only 4 patients required surgery. Regardless of the cause, the treatment of bronchiectasis associated physiotherapy and antibiotics for exacerbations. 11% of the patients developed chronic respiratory failure and two of them died CONCLUSIONS: All children with bronchiectasis should be comprehensively investigated because identifying underlying causes may have a major impact on the management as it argues against surgical resection.
