N2  - Background: Tuberculosis (TB) is curable with the treatment currently available. Unfortunately, clinical management of this disease is limited because of degradation of the drugs before reaching their target site, the toxic side effects, low permeability and poor patient compliance to treatment. Thus, the drawbacks of conventional chemotherapy necessitate the development of delivery systems which can release active compounds in a sustained and controlled manner as well as reduce dose amount and frequency. The objective of the study is to develop polymeric nanoparticles as drug delivery systems to enhance the bioavailability and biodistribution of TB drugs. Methods: The two first line anti-TB drugs (ATDs): Isoniazid (INH) and Rifampicin (RIF) were encapsulated in the Poly (lactide-co-glycolide) polymer using a novel multiple emulsion spray drying technique. The nanoparticles were characterised for size, size distribution, surface charge and morphology. In vitro release studies were performed in PBS. In vitro efficacy tests were done on THP-1 cells. Intracellular drug delivery studies were performed in CaCo-2 cells and U937 cells. Histopathology assays and in vivo drug release studies were performed on Balb/C mice. Results: Particles in the size range of 250-350 nm were produced. The particles demonstrated release of the drugs from nanocarriers in a slow manner over several days. Results indicated that the particles are taken up by the cells within 30 min proving that intracellular delivery of the drugs may be feasible. Furthermore, we have illustrated significant reduction of the bacterial growth index in THP-1 cells treated with encapsulated RIF when compared to that of cells treated with free Rifampicin. Fluorescently labelled PLGA nanoparticles administered to mice were further detected in all tissues analysed, histopathology assays showed no lesions in the tissues analysed. In addition drug release was observed in vivo over a period of 6 days and the MIC for RIF and INH was maintained over this period. Conclusion: The pharmacokinetic data presented in this study shows enhanced bioavailability and biodistribution of the drugs due to nanoencapsulation. The results obtained have shown the potential of polymeric nanoparticles as a drug delivery system for TB drugs with the potential to improve the TB treatment.
