N2  - INTRODUCTION & OBJECTIVES: BCG therapy, which is the standard treatment for non-muscle invasive bladder cancer with high risk of recurrence and progression, has potential life-threatening but uncommon adverse effects (AEs). Pulmonary toxicity especially miliary pulmonary tuberculosis due to intravesical bacillus Calmette-Guerin therapy is rare and serious complications but understanding of this complication and early establishment of specific treatment is the only way to improve prognosis. MATERIAL & METHODS: Among 845 patients treated for non-muscle bladder cancer with intravesical instillations of BCG in our department we have diagnosed 5 patients with pulmonary complications. Pulmonary complications in the form of miliary pulmonary tuberculosis developed at 3 patients , in the remaining 2 patients were only observed pneumonia with fever. RESULTS: Pulmonary complications occurs at 4 patients at the beginning of the application of therapy. The mean number of instillations before development of pulmonary disease was 3.25 (range 2-5). At one men developed 15 months after the last intravesical instillation, during open abdominal operation on urinary system. All patients developed high fever, shivers, profuse sweating, dry cough, fatigue. At patients with military dissemintation the chest radiogram showed diffuse military opacities. Thoracic CT confirmed the diagnosis of military pulmonary tuberculosis at 3 patients. In all cases sputum, blood, bronchoalveolar (BAL) and urine cultures were negative for common bacteria, mycobacteria and fungi. At one patient a PCR-based technique detected the presence of BCG bacilli in blood stream.After starting treatment with isoniazid, rifampicin, etambutol and at one patients also prednisone, the symptoms improved during the first week. After 6 months the antymycobacterial treatment was completed, 12 months follow up demonstrated asymptomatic patients. CONCLUSIONS: This reports shows the potentially severe side effects of intravesical BCG instillation. The early and accurate diagnosis, immediate antimycobacterial treatment led to patient's recovery. Because in most cases microscopic examinations and cultures for mycobacteria could be negative, miliary pulmonary dissemination should be taken into consideration in all patiens with history of intravesical BCG therapy when a compatible and typical clinical symptoms are present with radiological confirmations.
