N2  - The International Association for the Study of Pain considers a person's self report as the gold standard for pain assessment (1). Apart from the presence of pain, it is important to know the location, onset and duration, intensity, and quality or type of pain/sensation. Further, it is also of priority to ascertain any previous treatments for pain relief and associated symptoms and effects on activities (2). Pain is a combination of physiologic, behavioral, and psychologic interactions: therefore, one must take all of these into consideration in assessing appropriate pain. For pediatric patients younger than school age, this becomes more difficult and pain assessment is based more on behavioral analysis and utilizing a validated behavioral scale (3). According to the American Academy of Pediatrics policy statement: “a major aim of pain treatment is to eliminate pain associated suffering. Pain is an inherently subjective experience and should be assessed and treated as such. Pain has sensory, developmental, sociocultural and contextual factors.” Another concern is that children have and perform fewer adaptive skills when pain is present, and the experience of pain early in life may lead to long term consequences (4). Many of these revolve around sleep disturbances, normal growth and development, activities of daily living, and social development. As indicated above, it is known that many variables are interrelated when the complaint of pain is verbalized. There are chemical/physiological biomarkers such as interleukins, TNF, endorphins (ability to dampen the perception of pain), and serotonin that have either positive or negative effects. There are behavioral measures that include age, developmental status, cultural, and acquired. Anatomic and physiologic causes may include pancreatitis, constipation/DIOS or diarrhea, polyps, reflux, cough, RAD, and osteoporosis. While pain in acute and chronic conditions have been researched and written about for many years, there are few studies that have examined assessment and management of pain in the cystic fibrosis (CF) population. As we all know, CF affects the functions of many organs and thus, the pain and discomfort are potential consequences of the disease process (5). The goal of Sermet-Gaudelius et al (6) was to assess the prevalence of pain symptoms and impact on quality of daily life. They found the primary location for pain in children was abdomen, and back/head/chest for adults. The most common combination included headache with those systems. There was, surprisingly, a low percentage of pain meds used and, importantly, they found the transition from acute to chronic pain seemed to occur around 3 years of age. In 2005, Koh et al (7) reported that 46% of children in their study described pain at least weekly, usually of mild intensity and short duration. Britton and Saeed (8) found that pain is caused by a myriad of triggers, including: constipation, IBD, enteric pathogens, pancreatitis, peptic ulcer disease, biliary issues, and others have described postural deformities and the subsequent changes. In 2009, Stenekes et al (9) examined the frequency and selfmanagement of pain, dyspnea and cough in CF, with an age range of 7-60 years. A significant finding was that 84% reported pain with an average of two locations, head and abdomen being the most common. In examining pain in adult CF patients, one study concluded that pain is common, interferes with activities, and is associated with lower quality of life and an increased risk of both exacerbations and death (10). Among numerous findings, this study found that pain was reported within the past month (82%), with the most common site head; was associated with increased risk of pulmonary exacerbation; may lead to decreased mechanical airway clearance; and may be a marker of increased inflammation and “disease activity.” They also found pain may be more indicative of patients who are at risk of more rapid disease progression: i.e., pain seems to be more common in “sicker” patients with greater levels of disability. Another study concluded that pain is common in adults with CF, irrespective of clinical status, and may interfere with important physiotherapy treatments (11). Although pain intensity is generally mild, those with a negative emotional response to pain have significantly impaired health related quality of life. In 2009, Flume et al (12) reported a significant association between pain and impaired sleep quality. An even more recent study found that pain was negatively associated with pulmonary exacerbations, quality of life and treatment adherence (13). They further conclude that there is little standardization of CF pain assessment, and they recommend the development of guidelines on how to assess pain and provide adequate treatment for CF related pain. In assessing current pain scales and deciding which to utilize, several items need to be considered. The three approaches to pain assessment include cognitive (self report), behavioral (cry, posture, facial expression), and physiologic (heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen saturations, metabolic and endocrine increases) (14). It is imperative to remember that recognition of pain relies heavily on the expression and demeanor of the patient aswell as the interpretation of the caregiver; i.e., it is a 2-way street! Before choosing any pain scale, it is imperative to determine what the possible causes of the pain are; what clinical findings may help to determine the cause of the pain; and finally what diagnostic studies may be helpful. Pain scales are designed as either uni-dimensional (i.e., only one element and usually a sensory component) or, more commonly, multi-dimensional, which are useful in persistent pain conditions and explore more characteristics of the pain and its impact by offering a more detailed description of the person's pain experience. Initially, there are three simple questions which should be asked when deciding which scale to use: first: do I need to know location of pain; second, do I need to know intensity; and third, do I need to know both? Most of the uni-dimensional scales should suffice. Secondarily, though, if outcomes and follow-up are important, multi-dimensional scales should be considered. There are many scales that have been developed, researched and validated over the years. When choosing a scale for the assessment of pain in CF, a chronic life long condition, it is essential that the scale prove to be as consistent and reliable as possible. Perhaps the most commonly used include the uni-dimensional visual analog scale (VAS), the verbal rating scale (VRS) and the numeric pain scale. Each has their pros as well as cons; important to remember, though, is that their intent is to only focus on intensity of pain. A newer scale is the full cup test (FCT) where the child draws/ “fills up” the amount of pain they were experiencing. This was found to be easier to administer, easy to use, and more understandable for younger children than theVRS orVAS (15). Other examples of these more uni-dimensional scales include the popular Wong-Baker faces, Oucher, Eland color tool, Facial Action Coding system, and Poker chips. The multi-dimensional scales have been used when additional and more in-depth information is needed. The Goodenough drawincorporates both color and description: the child simply chooses the color that relates to their degree of pain, and then draws that color on the generic figure at the location of the pain.Other pediatric scales include the FLACC, COMFORT, CRIES, CHIPPS, CHEOPS, APPT (Adolescent Pain perception tool), and one of the newest is the EVENDOL (developed in France, ref 16).
