N2  - The polymeric mucins, MUC5AC and MUC5B, and the mucus formed from their secretion onto airway surfaces are important to the defense of the healthy lung: they function as the initial layer of protection for the underlying epithelium from inhaled aerosols and particulates, and invading pathogens. However, in each of the obstructive airways diseases, e.g., cystic fibrosis (CF), chronic bronchitis, asthma, and primary ciliary dyskinesia, mucous meta/hyperplasia leads to an overproduction of mucins and mucus, leading them to also play important roles in disease pathogenesis and pathology. Though our information is incomplete, the indications are that the infectious and inflammatory insults of the various obstructive diseases causing meta/hyperplasia differ, and the exact pathology of the mucus produced may be as well. Obstruction of the airways and the lumens of other organs in CF by a thick, tenacious mucus has been known since the disease was first described; indeed, in many parts of the world the disease is known as mucoviscidosis. Prior to the cloning of the CF gene, CFTR, mucus was held broadly to be a primary defect in the disease; however, after the cloning of CFTR and its identification as an ion channel, the mucus hypersecretion and obstruction in CF have been recognized clearly to be a secondary or higher order defect, resulting from a complex of inflammatory signaling events which are still being detailed (1). Less well known, however, is whether or not defects in the mucins, per se, contribute to mucus obstruction in CF. Alterations in the sulfation and sialation of mucins have been described in CF, but these changes to the O glycans populating the glycosylated repeat domains of the mucins have been shown to be caused by the inflammation associated with the disease, i.e., they are not specific to CF (2). In 2001, Paul Quinton suggested that the defect in bicarbonate transport via CFTR was a problem in CF (3), and in 2008 he reported a potential link between the CF bicarbonate defect and mucus (4,5). To date, the evidence marshaled to support this notion suggests that the mucus formed from newly secreted mucins in the absence of bicarbonate, whether due to a faulty CFTR in CF tissue, or in normal tissue experimentally by inhibiting CFTR or removing the bicarbonate ion, results in a thick, tenacious mucus that appears to adhere to the epithelium (6-8). In this presentation, we discuss the possibility that a defect in the post-secretory maturation of the mucin molecule is responsible for the link between bicarbonate and thick, sticky mucus. The peptide backbones of polymeric mucins are synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum as C-terminal, coupled dimers through cystine knot domains. After transit to, and passage through the Golgi, the dimers are glycosylated, following which they are oligomerized through disulfide bonding in the N-terminal D-domains, probably in the trans-Golgi network. Likely because mucus is primarily a tangled network formed from linear mucin polymers, it was thought originally that themucins were packaged, intracellularly, as linear molecules into secretory granules, this despite their massive size (e.g., MUC5B has amolecular mass of 100 - 200 MDa and is many micrometers in length).A recent study by Kesimer, et al. (9), however, suggests that the mucins are instead packaged in compact spherical forms, likely created and maintained through inter-chain disulfide bonds in the N- and/or C-terminals. The compact form of MUC5B, as isolated from saliva, sediments to the bottom of a density gradient, while the linear form resolves at the top of the gradient; an intermediate form, suggestive of a compact form in the process of opening up, is found in the middle fractions. That the compact and intermediate forms of MUC5B are maintained by covalent, likely disulfide, bonds, is suggested by their stability in 8 M guanidine hydrochloride, a highly chaotropic environment that disrupts hydrogen, van derWaals, and hydrophobic bonding, denaturing proteins (and breaking membranes and unraveling DNA). We have tested whether mucins undergo a post-secretory maturation process, as proposed by Kesimer et al. (9), using step-wise, sucrose density gradients (10, 17.5, 25, 35%) to separate mucins secreted from primary, highly differentiated, human bronchial epithelial (HBE) cell cultures as a function of time following agonist stimulation (ATPgS). Under control conditions, approximately 60% of MUC5B secreted from normal HBE cell cultures resolves at the bottom of the gradient, presumably as the compact form, immediately following secretion (10 min); ∼20% resolves at the top of the gradient. Over time, there is a progressive shift in this distribution, such that at 24 hr ∼60% of the mucin resolves at the top of the gradient, presumably in the linearized form. The mucins recovered from the accumulated mucus removed from the cultures in preparation for the experiment resolved predominately, in the linear form, at the top of the gradient. These data will be presented, along with other results suggesting a defect in mucin maturation in CF.
