N2  - Introduction: Since publication of the Infection Control Guidelines for CF in 2003 (1), new knowledge and new challenges in both CF and infection prevention and control have occurred which provided the rationale to update the Infection Prevention and Control (IP&C) Guidelines by the CF Foundation. While the source of many pathogens acquired by people with CF currently remains unknown, there is much evidence that people with CF can acquire pathogens from others with CF or from the contaminated healthcare environment. However, many individuals with CF are infected with unique strains suggesting acquisition from other sources such as the natural environment. Thus, the updated IP&C guidelines provide recommendations for the CF clinic, inpatient settings, and non-healthcare settings. The rationale behind developing recommendations for non-healthcare settings is to provide the CF community with education about the risks of acquisition of CF pathogens from others with CF in non-healthcare settings and from the natural environment to assist people with CF and their families to make prudent choices for activities outside of the healthcare setting. Methods: To update the guidelines, an interdisciplinary committee was formed and a systematic review of the literature was performed. The committee: (1) reviewed the existing literature, (2) integrated other relevant authoritative IP&C guidelines from the CDC, WHO and professional societies, (3) incorporated relevant recommendations from the 2003 Infection Control Guidelines, (4) developed updated recommendations, and (5) developed implementation strategies for IP&C practices, including strategies for the CF clinic, education, and identification of potential barriers to implementation. Committee members assessed the evidence gathered by the systematic review to develop, when possible, evidence-based recommendations statements and graded them using the US Preventive Health Services Task Force system (2). For topics not included in the systematic review or for which limited evidence was available, anonymous voting was performed and >80% approval was the threshold set for acceptance of each consensus-based recommendation. IP&C Implications of CF Microbiology: In the past decade, the epidemiology of CF pathogens has continued to evolve (3,4) with increasing evidence of CF-patientto- CF-patient transmission of newer pathogens. These include epidemic strains of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (5,6), new species of Burkholderia (3), methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (7-9), and Mycobacteria abscessus (10,11). Not only has there been increased evidence of patient-to-patient transmission, but acquisition of transmitted pathogens has been associated with increased morbidity and mortality (5,6,10,12,13). Furthermore, while the routes of transmission largely remain indirect contact with infectious secretions and infectious droplets, new evidence has emerged from both non-CF and CF literature, that droplet nuclei may play a role in transmission of respiratory pathogens. Notably, it is not possible to precisely quantify the risk of transmission of CF pathogens associated with specific activities. However, the overall rate of transmission is an area of active investigation, but molecular typing and genetic sequencing are required to detect and confirm transmission. Core IP&C Recommendations: The overarching principles of the IP&C recommendations are to prevent transmission of potential pathogens to people with CF by source containment and to minimize acquisition by the use of masks, hygienic principles, and maintaining distance between individuals with CF. The recommendations are intended to be implemented for all people with CF and all healthcare personnel involved with the care of people with CF. The following is a summary of the CF Foundation's core recommendations: 1. All healthcare personnel caring for people with CF, including the CF care team, inpatient staff, and staff in diagnostic and therapeutic areas (e.g., pulmonary function test laboratories, radiology, phlebotomy, operating room, physical medicine, environmental services), should receive education regarding Infection Prevention and Control for CF. 2. The CF care team should develop strategies to monitor adherence to infection prevention and control practices by healthcare personnel and to provide feedback. Feedback to the CF care team includes immediate feedback to an individual when a lapse in practice is observed and feedback and trending of overall adherence rates at regular intervals (e.g., quarterly). 3. People with CF and their families should receive education regarding Infection Prevention and Control for CF, using age appropriate tools and reading/language level appropriate to the target audience. 4. Healthcare personnel should assume that all people with CF could have transmissible pathogens in respiratory tract secretions. 5. People with CF, regardless of their respiratory culture results, should be separated by at least 6 feet (2 meters) from other people with CF in all settings, to reduce the risk of droplet transmission. This does not apply to members of the same household. 6. People with CF and their family members should perform hand hygiene (with either alcohol based hand rub or with an antimicrobial soap and water) when there is a potential for contamination or to prevent contamination of hands with pathogens. 7. All healthcare professionals should perform hand hygiene with either alcohol based hand rub or wash hands with an antimicrobial soap and water in relevant clinical situations. 8. People with CF should wear a surgical mask (also known as procedure or isolation mask) of appropriate size in a healthcare facility to reduce the risk of droplet transmission or acquisition of pathogens. The mask should be worn throughout the facility except in the exam room, during PFTs, or in the patient's room when admitted to the hospital. 9. People with CF should be instructed to contain their secretions, i.e., cough into a tissue, immediately discard soiled tissue into a trash receptacle, and perform hand hygiene after disposing of soiled tissues. 10. People with CF who do not reside in the same household should avoid activities and risk factors that have been associated with transmission of CF pathogens in non-healthcare and healthcare settings. Implementation Strategies: Implementation of IP&C strategies can be challenging and each CF center must incorporate the guidelines in a manner appropriate for their setting. Thus, whenever feasible, more than one suggestion for implementation has been provided by the committee. In addition, the committee developed tables with examples of opportunities for hand hygiene; use of personal protective equipment by healthcare professionals, peoplewith CF and their families; checklists for environmental cleaning or monitoring adherence to IP&C; strategies forCF clinic; and strategies to minimize the impact of isolation precautions on patients and their families. Furthermore, it is critical that CF centers continue to share best practices and study the impact of implementation of IP&C strategies on the epidemiology of CF pathogens and on patient outcomes.
