N2  - Mucociliary transport (MCT) is a pulmonary defense mechanism that removes bacteria, viruses, particulates and noxious material captured in mucus via coordinated cilia beating. Because airway plugging with mucus is commonly found in cystic fibrosis (CF) lung disease, it has been hypothesized that loss of CFTR function might disrupt MCT, thereby initiating disease. However, that hypothesis has been difficult to test for two reasons. First, it has not been possible to measure MCT in newborns. Although some human studies have reported decreased MCT in CF, the subjects were adults with longstanding CF airway disease. Therefore, those results may be confounded by infection and inflammation. To overcome this limitation, we generated a porcine CF model. These animals exhibit a host defense defect at birth and spontaneously develop airway disease that recapitulates clinical manifestations of CF airway disease. Second, testing the contribution of CFTR to MCT has been difficult because of limitations of current in vivo assays, which often lack resolution. To overcome this, we developed a highresolution CT-based assay. Advantages of this assay include temporal and spatial resolution capable of assessing speeds and 3-dimensional trajectories of individual particles over time, registration to the newborn pig airway anatomy, and the pigs breathe spontaneously through the nose to humidify airways. We found that both non-CF and CF newborn pigs exhibit similar particle distribution and trajectories with particles traveling towards the larynx with a component directed to the anterior (ventral) trachea. Under basal conditions, particles in newborn non-CF and CF pigs exhibited similar maximum speeds (non-CF: 8.7 ± 1.4 mm/min, N=15; CF: 9.9 ± 1.7 mm/min, N=11) and similar mean speeds (non-CF: 5.4 ± 1.0 mm/min; CF: 6.6 ± 1.8 mm/min). These data suggest that there may not be major differences in MCT between newborn CF and non-CF pigs under basal conditions. However, stresses that cause increased mucus secretion could initiate or exacerbate CF lung disease. Therefore, we are examining MCT following the delivery of mucus secretagogues, and we are currently analyzing the data.
