N2  - Background: The airway surface layer involves two components: 1) a mucus layer that traps inhaled pathogens and toxins; and 2) a periciliary layer that provides an environment for the ciliary beating to transport the mucus out of the lungs. The direction of the mucus transport is controlled by the direction of the coordinated ciliary beating, which is thought to be entrained in utero by fluid shear stress. The mechanisms of how fluid flow influences the direction of ciliary beating and how the beating of cilia from different ciliated cells is coordinated are poorly understood. Objective:We tested the hypothesis that the perfusion of laminar fluid flow across the lumen of epithelia acts as a signal to coordinate airway ciliary beating to the direction of flow. The goals of this project were to (a) develop a novel method of culturing well-differentiated human bronchial epithelial (HBE) cell cultures for perfusion of the luminal surface; (b) design and develop a system to produce planar oriented cilia in HBE cultures; and (c) investigate properties of the laminar fluid flow (i.e. flow rate, fluid shear rate, viscosity) that affect coordination of cilia-mediated mucus transport. Methods: A novel culturing protocol was developed to create confluent HBE cultures on a membrane substrate (Snapwell) such that the cultures were not surrounded by walls and to transfer them to a macrofluidic flow chamber in a perfusion system. The shear stress invoked onto the cultures was calibrated to be approximately 0.5 dynes/cm2 which was derived to be the maximum shear evoked on the airway epithelium in vivo during tidal breathing. The coordination of mucus transport was derived from particle velocimetry analysis. Results: We demonstrated that short term exposure of shear stress on the luminal surface of well-differentiated cultures with uncoordinated cilia beating yielded coordination of mucus transport in the direction of fluid flow. We found that this coordination could persist for several hours after removal from the fluid flow. However, the planar coordination was inevitably transient and ciliary beating eventually returned to their pre-shear, uncoordinated, state. Studies are being performed to investigate both short and long term exposures of shear stress on cultures as well as to determine when the coordination of cilia in a planar orientation may occur in vitro. Conclusions: We have developed a novel method for culturing HBE cells suited for entrainment in a flow chamber. We have also engineered a novel high-throughput, automated bioreactor system set in an incubator for culturing HBE while maintaining: a) an “air”-liquid interface necessary for growth; and b) continuous laminar fluid shear. Together, these methods will allow researchers to not only culture HBE cells with coordinated ciliary beating which may reflect human airway physiology, but also to understand the signals and parameters which facilitate cilia coordination.
