N2  - Objective: To expand the safety database for AZLI in children with CF and chronic PA infection, an open-label, single arm study of 3 intermittent 28-day AZLI treatment courses was performed, as a post-marketing commitment for the European Medicines Agency. Preliminary data are presented. Methods: Children age ≤12 years with CF and chronic PA infection, documented by 2 positive lower respiratory tract cultures for PA within 12 months prior to study entry and at screening, were enrolled. There were no restrictions on FEV1% predicted for inclusion/exclusion. Subjects were treated with 3 courses of AZLI 75 mg 3 times daily (28 days on/28 days off) via the PARI Investigational eFlow® Nebulizer System with a SmartMask® Baby, SmartMask Kids, or standard nebulizer mouthpiece for best fit. The primary endpoint was percent of subjects discontinuing AZLI due to safety or tolerability reasons. Secondary endpoints included exacerbations, hospitalizations, use of additional antipseudomonal antibiotics, adverse event (AE) rates, and changes from baseline in FEV1% predicted (in subjects >6 years), CFQ-R respiratory symptoms score (RSS) (in subjects >6 years), and PA sputum density. (ClinicalTrials.gov NCT0140423) Results: Sixty-one subjects were enrolled from 25 CF centers in the US and Europe. Baseline data: Median age 10.0 years (range: 1-12 y). Age groups: 1 year to <2 years: 2 (3.3%), 2 to <6 years: 7 (11.5%), 6 to ≤12 years: 52 (85.2%). Female: 31 (50.8%). Median body mass index (BMI): 16.2 (range: 13.3-20.8). Mean (SD) FEV1% predicted for subjects ≥6 years: 80.3 (19.5). Mean (SD) log10 PA CFU/g for subjects with samples evaluable for quantitative analysis (N=34): 5.81 (1.41). Primary endpoint: No subject discontinued AZLI for safety/tolerability reasons; 59 subjects completed the study. Two subjects voluntarily withdrew from the study (not due to adverse events). Secondary endpoints: There were 17 serious AEs, all reported as unrelated to AZLI; 54 subjects experienced treatment-emergent AEs. The most commonly reported AEs were cough (36/61 (59%)) and pyrexia (16/61 (26.2%)). Nine subjects (14.8%) were hospitalized for respiratory exacerbations; 22 subjects (36.1%) used additional IV/inhaled antipseudomonal antibiotics for treatment of acute exacerbations. Among children ≥6 years, the average on-treatment percent change from baseline in FEV1% predicted (adjusted mean [95% CI]) was 4.91 (1.44, 8.38) and the overall average change from baseline in CFQ-R RSS (mean [SD]) was 7.57 (14.5). Mean (SD) change from baseline in Log10 PA CFU/g sputum at the end of the third AZLI treatment course (N= 23 evaluable paired samples) was -1.2 (2.13). Conclusion: This study enriches the database for the safety and efficacy of AZLI in children ≤12 years of age with CF and chronic PA infection. AZLI was well tolerated, increased lung function and improved respiratory symptoms in this study population. These results are consistent with data from previous AZLI trials in this younger age group.
