N2  - Background: Tobramycin powder for inhalation (TIP™) composed of light porous particles delivered via the portable T-326 dry powder inhaler (TOBI® Podhaler®), has been specifically designed to reduce treatment time and improve ease of use in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients compared with nebulised delivery. To date, the longest studied treatment period with TIP has been 24 weeks (3 cycles of 28 days on, 28 days off treatment [Konstan MW, et al. J Cyst Fibros. 2011;10:54-61]). The recent double-blind randomised EDIT trial compared the efficacy and safety of TIP (112mg) with placebo for one cycle of treatment in CF patients aged 6-21 years with chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) infection. Following the core study, two extensions assessed the long-term safety and efficacy of TIP, up to a total duration of 1 year. Methods: Patients successfully completing the core 1-treatment-cycle EDIT trial were eligible to participate in the single-arm extension studies. All patients were assigned to TIP for two consecutive 24-week periods, irrespective of their previous core study treatment, providing a maximum exposure to 7 cycles of TIP treatment. Assessments included safety and tolerability, spirometry, change in sputum Pa density and change in Pa tobramycin minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC). Results: Sixty-two patients (30 TIP; 32 placebo; mean age 12.9 years; mean baseline FEV1 59.2% predicted) were enrolled in the core EDIT study. A total of 59 patients received TIP at any time during core or extension phases of the study. Fifty-three patients completed 3 cycles, 47 completed 6 cycles and 24 completed 7 cycles of TIP treatment. The most frequently reported adverse events in the safety population (N=59) were cough (n=9, 15.3%), hypoacusis (n=6, 10.2%) and respiratory tract infection (n=6, 10.2%). Most reports of cough were moderate and none led to discontinuation. Overall, adverse events (including cough) occurred at lower rates with additional cycles of exposure. No deaths were reported. Relative changes in FEV1% predicted from baseline ranged from 9.2 to 16.2% at Day 29 (end of dosing) of each cycle, and were sustained over the 1-year treatment period. Similarly the decrease from baseline in Pa sputum density ranged from -1.3 to -3.2 log10 colony forming units at Day 29 of each cycle. MIC50 was 1.0 μg/mL and MIC90 was 8.0 μg/mL at Day 29 of Cycle 7 (n=18).The number of patients treated with anti-Pa antibiotics was low (10 patients, 16.9%), with 6 patients (10.2%) treated with intravenous antibiotics. Over the 1- year treatment duration with TIP, 3 patients (5.1%) were hospitalised for respiratory events. Conclusions: The safety profile observed with TIP exposure for up to seven cycles was consistent with previous TIP studies. Extended exposure to TIP does not lead to an increase in adverse events over time. Further, TIP provides sustained improvements in lung function and reductions in Pa sputum density over one year, with no significant increase in emergence of resistant Pa strains.
