N2  - Introduction: Despite adoption of newborn screening (NBS) for CF in 2008, clinical outcomes for those patients diagnosed by NBS at The Children's of Alabama/University of Alabama (COA/UAB) pediatric CF center indicate a high rate of early Pseudomonas (Pa) acquisition and hospitalizations for pulmonary exacerbation (PE). In follow-up to an abstract our center presented in 2012 that analyzed predictors of patient outcomes at 6 months, this study seeks to identify predictors of patient outcomes at 12 months of age in a larger cohort. In this study, we sought to determine the association between parental knowledge (quantified by a 10-item survey), socioeconomic status (SES) (smoking, parental education, income, and insurance) and clinical outcomes (nutrition, infant lung function (iPFT), microbiology and PE rate). Methods: The COA/UAB pediatric CF center has an open IRB protocol to collect SES, parental knowledge and clinical data on infants diagnosed by NBS. From the clinical data, we developed a 10 point clinical score (higher score indicates milder disease) to combine assessment of nutrition (weightfor- length %, microbiology (acquisition and eradication of Pa) and respiratory symptoms (cough, PE, and hospitalization). iPFT results were an independent measure of clinical outcome at 12 months. We developed a 10 item multiple choice survey to assess parental knowledge of CF (higher score indicates increased knowledge) for parents of patients over 1 year of age. Other data collected included parental smoking, parental income and education, clinic attendance, and refill history. Linear regression and paired t-tests were used for the analysis of the relationship among variables. Statistical significance was assumed at p ≤ .05. Results: Data from 43 NBS infants at age 12 months treated at our center were analyzed. The average frequency of PE was 0.65 PEs/year, with 46% of the infants being hospitalized in the first year. The rate of Pa acquisition in the first year was 45%. An iPFT was performed in all 43 patients with mean FVC 107.9±2.6%, FEV0.5 102.5±3.0% and FEF25-75 of 95.7±4.4%. Clinical score of <8 was associated with a significantly diminished FEV0.5 (High Score: 108.9±4.0%; Low Score: 92.7±3.6%). Paternal education (college attendance) was associated with both diminished FEV0.5 (p=.02) and CF knowledge score (p=.001). Maternal education was not associated with outcomes or knowledge. Knowledge score was not correlated with clinical outcome. Discussion: Despite early diagnosis and treatment, outcomes for infants diagnosed by NBS at our center remain diverse, with high rates of Pa acquisition and hospitalization. Determination of factors that may predict clinical outcomes remain elusive with parental knowledge of CF having little correlation with clinical outcomes. However, we have identified that paternal educational achievement (separate from maternal education) has an influence on both caregiver knowledge and iPFT function, a finding that merits further investigation. We have also confirmed the utility of a 5-item clinical score (max 10 points) in identifying infants at risk of diminished iPFT at 12 months. Future study will determine the predictive value of the clinical score during infancy with long-term clinical outcomes.
