N2  - Introduction: Good's Syndrome is characterized by hypogammaglobulinemia, B cell depletion, variable defects in cellular immunity and thymoma. Immunodeficiency, recurrent infections can be the first symptom after thymectomy. Methods: Case report: A 39 year old female presented for immunologic evaluation with frequent sinus infections, allergic rhinitis, otitis, on prior immunotherapy in childhood, a prior history of pneumonia, current cough and sinus pain. Four years earlier she developed pneumonia with empyema followed a thymoma diagnosed by a CAT scan which was resected. On physical examination she had facial erythema, boggy nasal mucosa, enlarged turbinates, tympanic membrane scaring, frontal and maxillary sinus tenderness. Cardiopulmonary examination, skin and extremities were normal. Results: The patient was evaluated by otolaryngology earlier for acute otitis media, turbinate hypertrophy. A CAT scan revealed frontal, ethmoid, maxillary sinusitis. She was treated with antibiotics and prednisone for moderate to severe maxillary, sphenoid, ethmoid and frontal sinusitis She had blood tests from 2 years earlier that revealed IgG = 64mg/dl, IgA < 4mg/dl, IgM = 110 mg/dl, decreased subclasses, Mycoplasma pneumoniae IgG < 100 U/ml and no antibodies to S. pneumoniae, diphtheria and tetanus. She was evaluated by another immunologist 7 months earlier but did not follow up. Repeat IgG = 59 mg/dl, IgA= 9.9 mg/dl, decreased IgG2, IgG4, and sIgE slightly positive to oak and dust mite allergens. She continued to have bronchitis on 3 occasions, received antibiotics and finally was referred for IVIG with 35 g of Gammagard and has improved significantly due to repeated infusions. Conclusions: Good's syndrome is characterized by thymoma associated with immunodeficiency and a lack of specific clinical manifestations. The pathogenesis suggests cytokines from bone marrow are involved along with genetic defects. IVIG restores defective signaling and can reestablish immune homeostasis. IVIG at regular intervals is the most effective way to improve the clinical symptoms and reduce patient mortality. Increased awareness about the clinical and immunological profile of this syndrome may increase early recognition of this syndrome. CVID patients appear to have a dysregulated antimicrobial peptide response to pathogenic bacteria in the upper respiratory tract, which could be linked to an aberrant Th17 cell response.
