N2  - Introduction: Mycobacterium tuberculosis, a causative agent of chronic tuberculosis disease, is widespread among humans, domesticated and wild animals and is transmitted within and between species by aerosol route. Because of its high incidence, zoonotic potential, ability to spread rapidly and high mortality rates, it is utmost priority to diagnose and prevent the spread of the disease. This paper explains the various diagnostic tests (Tuberculin skin test, PrimaTB Stat-Pak, Ziehl Neelsen staining and chest radiograph) and successful treatment for tuberculosis in ten suspected chimpanzees from Tacugama Chimpanzee Sanctuary, Sierra Leone. Materials/Methods: Skin tests with mammalian old tuberculin and purified protein derivatives - avian and bovine tuberculin were performed intrapalpebrally at the sanctuary in various occasions. Simultaneous serological tests were carried out with PrimaTB Stat-Pak in blood serum or plasma. Relevant history was also assessed to confirm their stay with humans (2 weeks to 2 years) before coming to the sanctuary. Gastric lavage samples were collected for Ziehl Neelsen (ZN) staining methods from eight chimpanzees and tested in human hospital. Nine chimpanzees had their chest radiographs taken to confirm for the presence of lesions of TB. All ten chimpanzees were treated with prophylactic and full course of anti-tuberculosis drugs from 2 to 12 months. An annual check for tuberculosis tests had been established for the sanctuary staff members. Results: All ten chimpanzees were suspicious with either mammalian old tuberculin or avian and bovine purified protein derivatives skin tests. One positive, six negative and three suspicious results were observed in the Prima Stat Pak tests. 1 out of 8 gastric lavage samples turned out to be doubtful with rest reported as negative. Chest radiographs of two confirmed hilar adenopathy, one with consolidation of lungs and six showed no abnormality detection. Seven and three chimpanzees were treated with prophylactic and full course of anti-tuberculosis drugs respectively from 2 to 12 months. A multiple drug combination of isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol and pyrazinamide were used for the treatment. Sanctuary staff members were tested negative with ZN staining method. Discussion/Conclusion: Ante-mortem diagnosis of TB is quite challenging. Due to mixed results with different TB diagnostics and high risk of spreading of disease, the chimpanzees were isolated and treated individually with human medicine. Generally non-human primates are not recommended for treatment of TB but chimpanzees being endangered species are treated with TB under close monitoring. Antibiotic sensitivity tests could not be performed. After the completion of anti-tuberculosis medications, all ten chimpanzees were confirmed negative with tuberculin skin tests, Stat-Pak tests and chest radiographs. We could not confirm TB with culture and molecular detection methods which are generally impractical and inaccessible in field settings. Considering the importance, costs and ethical issues of animals, we successfully treated ten chimpanzees which are living together with other chimpanzees in the group.
