N2  - Objective: To determine the characteristics of patients with isolation of M. xenopi. Method: Retrospective study of patients with one or more isolates of M. xenopi in any clinical specimen in our hospital between January 2005 and July 2012. Results: 46 patients (mean age 62±18 years), 36(78%) males. 31(67%) patients were smokers or former smokers. 9(20%) were HIV positive. The most common comorbidities were bronchiectasis 14(30%), previous tuberculosis 14(30%) and COPD 12(26%) The most common symptoms were cough and expectoration 76%, fever 43% and chest pain 28%. The CT scan showed emphysema in 15(33%), bronchiectasis 13(28%) and cavitation 8(17%). 98% of the isolates were respiratory. In 17(37%) patients two or more isolates were obtained. 12(26%) patients were treated, only 9(20%) fulfilled ATS disease criteria. The patterns were variable with one patient requiring lobectomy. In 6 patients treated, cured. In Table I are observed factors related disease by M. xenopi. Conclusions: 1. Patients with isolation of M. xenopi were predominantly male with bronchiectasis and/or previous tuberculosis. 2. The most common chest CT presentation were bilateral infiltrates, lymphadenopathy and residual lesions. 3. The treatment regimens were not standardized and evolution was variable. 4. In patients with concomitant isolation of other atypical mycobacteria, therapy was started less frequently. (Table Presented).
