N2  - Objectives: Describe the clinical, laboratory and bacterial pathogens present in exacerbations of COPD in patients admitted to a general hospital. Methods: Cross-sectional study, quantitative and observational, conducted a year in which a form was completed by the inpatient information to hospital with exacerbation of COPD in relation to clinical status, disease history, use of antibiotics, laboratory tests and imaging. Sputum was collected and quantitative culture was realized, with a cutoff point for infection of 106 CFU/ml. Results: We evaluated 47 patients, 24 (52,2%) were male, mean age of 67,1 years. The combination of cough, dyspnea and sputum change was found in 38 (80,9%) participants. Revealed leukocytosis in 26 (55,3%) cases. There was growth of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in 5 (10,6%) of the sputum cultures. We obtained 44 (93,6%) cultures without criteria for infection, and three (6,4%) with microbiological criteria for bacterial infection. The Kappa index of agreement between the use of antimicrobials and the presence or absence of microbiologically confirmed infection was 0.006 (p<0.05). Conclusion: The microbial agent most commonly isolated was Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Presence of leukocytosis showed no loyalty to a framework of bacterial pathogens as evidenced by growth in culture. Poor agreement occurred between the use of antimicrobials and the presence or absence of infection.
