N2  - RATIONALE: Extra-pulmonary tuberculosis (EPTB) is an important clinical dilemma in India as well as worldwide. Multidrug resistance tuberculosis (MDR-TB) is a serious problem in clinical management, and has become an important public health issue. The aim of this study to know clinical and genotypic characteristics of MDR-TB from clinical isolates from EPTB cases in Northern India. METHODS: A total of 1206 specimens from patients of EPTB cases with varied presentation were studied during Sept 2007-Sept 2012. All the specimens were processed by Ziehl Neelson staining, culture, identification of Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex (MTBC) by IS6110-PCR and MPT64 kit, first line drug susceptibility using the 1% proportional method by BacT/ALERT 3D system. MDR-TB isolates were further characterized at genotypic level by sequenced for rpoB, katG and inhA gene for mutation analysis. RESULTS: 260 (21.5%) of 1206 specimens from clinically suspected of EPTB were culture positive for mycobacteria. Of these 260 cultures isolates, 110 (58%) cases were males and mean age were 39.6+17.2 years. The most common clinical manifestations were cough, fever, weight loss, night sweat, dyspnoea, lymphadenopathy and chest infiltrations were present. 192 (73.8%) were M. tuberculosis complex cases and 68 (26.2%) were MOTT. Of these 192 M. tuberculosis complex isolates, 112 (58.4%) were fully susceptible and 78 (41.6%) strains were resistance. 28 (14.5%) strains were confirmed MDR-TB by genotypic method. Genotypic and mutation analysis of MDR-TB isolates were; 20 (71.4%) most common point mutations at codon 531 (S531L) and 7 (25%) at 526 (H526Y) for rpoB; 23(82.2%) at 315 (S315T1) for katG gene and 4(14.3%) mutations at codon 209 (C15T) for inhA gene. CONCLUSIONS: We found a high prevalence of MDR-TB among EPTB has been increasing and their pattern has reached an alarming level in northern India. This study also provides valuable information on mutations occurring at rpoB, katG gene and promoter region of inhA in clinical isolates of M. tuberculosis in EPTB cases. The rapid diagnosis of MDR-TB may facilitate an effective treatment regimen and minimizes the risk of further resistance development. Keywords: Tuberculosis, Multidrug resistance tuberculosis, Extra pulmonary tuberculosis.
