N2  - RATIONALE: Since the late 1980's increase in tuberculosis (TB) in the United States associated with the HIV epidemic and a lapse in public health infrastructure, there has been a renewed effort to prevent TB transmission. Despite efforts to place patients on airborne isolation early and keep them on isolation for an appropriate duration, delays still occur and patients are still taken off isolation prematurely. We describe the characteristics of patients who had an error in isolation for TB at a tertiary care, public, teaching hospital. METHODS: Cases were defined as all patients culture-positive for Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) for whom an inpatient contact investigation was performed from 1/2006 to 7/2012. Errors in isolation were defined as no isolation, a delay in isolation, or premature discontinuation of isolation. Cases were identified through the Bellevue Hospital Center Infection Control Office from records of TB contact investigations and confirmed with health department records. Data collected included patient demographics, co-morbidities, social history, clinical presentation, microbiology and imaging. RESULTS: Nineteen patients were identified over the 6-year study period; 12 (63%) were men, 3 (17%) were homeless, 4 (25%) were active smokers, and 3 (18%) used intravenous drugs. Clinically, 9 (50%) presented with fever, 10 (59%) had cough, and 7 (44%) had weight loss. Five (29%) patients had type 2 diabetes mellitus and 1 (5%) patient was HIV-infected. Six (35%) patients had a concurrent malignancy: 2 had a primary lung cancer, 3 had a primary blood cancer (acute myelogenous leukemia or myelodysplastic syndrome) and 1 patient had a primary breast cancer. Admission chest radiographs were clear for 6 (32%), 9 (47%) had at least a pulmonary infiltrate and 1 (5%) had a cavity. Twelve (75%) patients were sputum smear negative, of which 3 (16%) were sputum culture negative for Mtb and diagnosed with TB from lung biopsy (1), or an extra-pulmonary source (2). CONCLUSION: Characteristics of TB patients have changed over time. Previously, HIV/AIDS has been shown to be the most common co-morbidity leading to errors in isolation; our study shows malignancy as a more common co-morbidity. It is important for clinicians to maintain vigilance for TB, particularly when patients present with illnesses that may alter the typical clinical presentation.
