N2  - Background: TB is a global health problem, nearly one third of the population is infected with Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Delay in diagnosis and treatment can lead higher mortality. The objetive was to assess the factors associated with delayed treatment of pulmonary TB and facilitate early detection. Method: observational cohort study of patients with pulmonary TB were treated in our Hospital in period of 7 years (February 2004-2011. Differents demographics data were collected (clinical-epidemiological, radiological, diagnostic methods, time from onset of symptoms to initiation of treatment). Late onset of treatment was considered when the patient had more than 90 days of symptoms. Statistical analysis: Categorical variables were analyzed using the chi2 test and t test for continuous variables. Statistical analyzes were performed using SPSS 15 (SPSS Inc, Chicago, IL) Results: Of 364 cases,285 (%) were pulmonary TB. In 200 cases the treatment was started early and in 85 cases was late. The average of days until the start of treatment was 115 days, median 60 days. Patients with delayed treatment were more often women (60% vs. 43%, p = 0.009), older than 60 years (27% vs. 10%, p <0.001). More frequent simptoms were dry cough (41% vs. 25%, p = 0.038) and absence of hemoptysis (89% vs. 77%, p = 0.019).When we compared early vs. late treatment, Smears-negative pulmonary TB (65% vs 35%, p = 0.048) and negative direct bronchial washing/bronchioloalveolar(58% vs. 44%, p = 0.037) were more frequent. Logistic regression was performed with confidence intervals of 95% (95% CI): age greater than 60 years RR 3.33 (95% CI, 1.63-6.84; p <0.001), female RR 1.98 (95% CI 1.15-3.44, P 0.009), absence of hemoptysis RR 2.45 (95% CI; 1,08-5.69, P = 0.019), negative direct of bronchoalveolar lavage RR 3.23 (95% CI, 1.00-11.26, P = 0.048), negative direct of sputum RR 1.78 (95% CI 1.03-3.08, P 0.037), presence of cough RR 2.1 (95% CI 1.18-3.72, P 0.006) Conclusion: In our cohort one third of the patients received late treatment. It was associated with ederly patients, absence of cough and hemoptysis, and smear-negative respiratory specimens.
