N2  - RATIONALE 1: Study the incidence of atypical mycobacterial infection in sputum positive patients with AIDS. 2: Comparative study of clinical features, tuberculin skin test and radiographic findings between AIDS patients with clinical disease due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis and atypical mycobacteria. MATERIAL AND METHODS A prospective study was carried out in 75 patients with AIDS who presented to a tertiary care hospital over 2 years with respiratory complaints and were sputum positive for acid fast bacilli by Ziehl-Nelson stain on 3 consecutive samples. Patients with other immunocompromised states such as diabetes, those with pre-existing structural lung disease such as bronchiectasis, liver function abnormalities and sputum positive tuberculosis already on anti-tuberculous medications were excluded from the study. Evaluation of each patient included a detailed history and physical examination, routine blood investigations, tuberculin skin test, and chest roentgenogram. The sputum examination was done by the DOTS method which included a spot sample, overnight sample and a repeat spot sample. Mycobacterial species identification was done using standard culture characteristics and chemical tests. RESULTS Majority of the patients were males in the age group of 21-50 years(73%). The most common presenting symptom in mycobacterial and atypical mycobacterial disease was fever and cough (100%). Mycobacterium tuberculosis was present in 71 of the 75 patients(94.67%). Atypical mycobacterial species was isolated in the remaining 4 patients(5.33%) with 3 having M. avium complex and 1 was M. kansasii. The tuberculin skin test was positive in 18 patients (24%) and all of these had Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection. All of the patients with atypical disease were tuberculin test negative. The absolute lymphocyte count of 15 of the 18 (83%) skin test positive patients was >2000 cell/mm3. Chest X-ray showed that approximately 70% of the patients had atypical findings such as lower lobe involvement(33.33%), more than one lobe involved(20%) and bilateral extensive disease(16%). The radiological findings were not specific for typical or atypical mycobacterial disease. CONCLUSION The study shows that Mycobacterium tuberculosis continues to be the major cause of pulmonary mycobacterial disease in HIV patients. The incidence of atypical mycobacterial disease is increasing following the AIDS epidemic from 1% initially to 5.33 % as noted in the present study. The tuberculin skin test is negative in atypical mycobacterial infection. Clinical and radiological differentiation is not possible between mycobacterium tuberculosis and atypical mycobacterial infection.
