N2  - Rationale: Ceftaroline (CPT) fosamil, a cephalosporin with broad spectrum activity was approved by the European Medicines Agency and the FDA for the treatment of community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP) and acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections. CAPTURE is a multicenter retrospective study describing patients treated with CPT for CABP and ABSSSI. Results of the Year 1 analysis for the CABP cohort are presented. Methods: Data were collected at participating study centers in the United States by randomized selection and review of patient charts and included demographic information, disease characteristics, antibacterial use, and outcomes. The evaluable population consisted of enrolled patients who received more than 4 doses of CPT and had available data to determine whether there was clinical cure, clinical improvement, or clinical failure. Clinical success was defined as clinical cure or clinical improvement and a change to oral agents or discontinuation of antibacterial therapy. Results: The CABP cohort comprised 290 (30%) of all patients in the study. Demographics included 54% males, 46% females, and the mean age was 63.7 years (SD ±,17.9, range 19 - 99). The most common symptoms on diagnosis included dyspnea (71%), cough (58%), abnormal auscultatory findings (46%), and sputum production (38%). Relevant medical history is presented in Table 1. At the time of CPT administration 38% of patients were treated in an intensive care unit (ICU) and supplemental oxygen was used in 69%. The evaluable population consisted of 273 (94%) of enrolled patients. Antibiotics prior to CPT therapy were administered in 84% and the most commonly used antibiotic classes included cephalosporins (36%), glycopeptides (34%), quinolones (32%), and macrolides (25%). The mean duration of CPT therapy was 6.3 days (SD ± 4.7). Adjunct antibiotic therapy administered in 63% of patients included quinolones (21%), macrolides (21%) and glycopeptides (13%). Clinical success was reported in 77% of patients: ICU clinical success was 67% and general hospital ward was 83%. CPT was discontinued in 6 patients due to an adverse event and there were 2 deaths. Patients were discharged to home (58%) or to another healthcare facility (37%). (Table presented) Conclusions: CPT achieved high rates of clinical success in the treatment of CABP including patients with significant co-morbidities and severe disease. There was a high rate of treatment in the ICU. CAPTURE data further support the clinical trials data of CPT as an important antimicrobial agent for treatment of complicated CABP infections.
