N2  - Rationale: Combination antibiotic therapy with a macrolide was associated with improved survival in patients with severe community acquired bacterial pneumonia (CABP). Macrolides have reduced mortality in chronic lung disease, particularly diffuse panbronchiolitis. These benefits are attributed to the immunodulatory activity of macrolides and their activity against atypical pathogens. However, global microbial resistance trends indicate that erythromycin, clarithromycin and azithromycin are losing effectiveness against the pneumococcus (30-40% resistance in the U.S.) and mycoplasma (>90% resistance in China and Japan). Solithromycin, a 4th generation fluoroketolide, retains activity against these macrolide-resistant organisms. Solithromycin has potent anti-inflammatory agent, demonstrated to decrease LPS-induced TNFα & IL-8 production by macrophages. To assess the contribution of both antibacterial and immunomodulatory activity, we conducted a study comparing the rapidly bactericidal respiratory fluoroquinolone, levofloxacin, with solithromycin, a potent but more slowly killing macrolide antibiotic, in outpatients with CABP. Methods: Adults with signs and symptoms of CABP and chest radiograph consolidation were randomized to receive oral solithromycin or levofloxacin for 5 days. Early clinical response (ECR) was assessed at 48-72 hours after first dose, and overall success at end of treatment (EOT) and test of cure (TOC) visits. ECR success required improvement in at least two cardinal symptoms (cough, chest pain, sputum production, dyspnea), without worsening in any other. Clinical success at EOT and TOC required resolution of baseline signs and symptoms. Safety was monitored through scheduled ECG & laboratory evaluations and adverse event reporting. Results: 132 patients, mean age 56, 51% male, were enrolled with CABP of PORT II (73%), III (20%) or IV (5%) severity. Baseline characteristics were generally balanced, although the solithromycin arm had more females (57% vs 42%) and more patients with history of COPD (25% vs 16%), two parameters associated with overall lower response rates for the entire study population. Despite this imbalance, solithromycin was comparable in efficacy to levofloxacin. (Table presented) An overview of treatment emergent adverse events is presented in Table 2. There were more AEs (including severe and serious) and more study drug discontinuations among levofloxacin recipients. Gastrointestinal AEs (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting) were reported more frequently among levofloxacin recipients. There were no episodes of significant QT prolongation. No patient had concomitant ALT and bilirubin elevation. (Table presented) Conclusions: The overall clinical success and tolerability profile of solithromycin versus levofloxacin supported initiation of an ongoing Phase 3 program in CABP. Comparable ECR rates in this trial suggest that solithromycin's immunomodulatory effects contribute to its overall efficacy profile.
