N2  - Rationale: Tobramycin Inhalation Powder (TIP) delivered via the T-326 Inhaler is a novel drug/device combination developed using PulmoSphereTM particle technology. The Establish tobramycin Dry powder effIcacy Trial (EDIT) was designed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of TIP (112 mg b.i.d) in cystic fibrosis (CF) patients with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Pa) infection. Methods: CF patients (6-21 years) with Pa infection and FEV1 of 25%-80% predicted who had not received inhaled anti-Pa antibiotics for the previous four months were recruited in a randomized, multi-center, double-blind, placebo-controlled core study with TIP versus placebo (28 days on + 28 days off treatment), with optional continuation in an extension study (E1) for three additional cycles on TIP. The primary objective of the core study was to evaluate efficacy as relative change in FEV1 % predicted from baseline to Day 29. The E1 study evaluated long-term safety of TIP. Clinical effects included outcomes such as relative change in FEV1 % predicted and decrease in sputum Pa density. Results: 62 patients (22 male, 40 female) were randomized; 95.2% (59/62) and 92.7% (51/55) patients completed the core and E1 studies, respectively. In the core, mean treatment differences (TIP-Placebo) for relative and absolute change in FEV1 % predicted were 5.9% (p=0.148) and 4.4% (p=0.0496), respectively. Relative change in FEV1 % predicted was statistically significant at all post-baseline time points in E1, with a highest mean increase of 17.3% (p<0.001). TIP decreased mean sputum Pa density by 1.2 log10 CFU versus placebo (p=0.002), which was maintained in E1. Six patients in the core and five in E1 reported new anti-pseudomonal antibiotics use. Eight TIP and eleven placebo patients in the core, and 26 patients in E1 reported treatment-emergent adverse events (AE). Commonly reported AEs (% core TIP/placebo, % E1) were cough (10.0/0, 9.1), dysphonia (3.3/3.1, 5.5), hypoacusis (10.0/6.3, 5.5) and respiratory tract infection (0/6.3, 9.1). AEs were mostly mild or moderate in severity. Two patients in the core (on placebo) and three in E1 reported serious AEs; none were related to study medication. One patient in core (on placebo) and two in E1 study were hospitalized due to respiratory events, no deaths were reported. Conclusion: TIP shows an acceptable safety and tolerability profile, with no new safety signals identified compared with previous phase III trials. Data from the core and E1 studies provide evidence of sustained efficacy and bacterial suppression over up to four cycles of TIP.
