N2  - RATIONALE: Overactivity of Complement anaphylatoxins (C3a and C5a) has been postulated to have an important role in the pathogenesis of COPD. We examined how anaphylatoxin levels vary within stable COPD patients, at exacerbation and longitudinally during exacerbation recovery and subsequent baseline in a well characterised COPD cohort. METHOD: All patients had FEV1≤80% predicted and FEV1/FVC ratio ≤0.7. Patients in the London COPD cohort are seen 3-monthly when stable and are specifically trained to recognise and present to the study team at exacerbation. Patients in the cohort record increased respiratory symptoms on daily symptom diary cards. Exacerbations were defined as an increase for two consecutive days in respiratory symptoms, with at least one major symptom (dyspnoea, sputum purulence or volume) plus another major or minor symptom (wheeze, cold, sore throat, and cough). Bacterial presence was determined by qPCR analysis of spontaneous sputum. Complement C3a and C5a levels were measured in serum of 50 stable COPD patients by a cytometric bead array method. A subgroup of 9 patients also had serum levels of C3a and C5a measured at exacerbation onset, at 4 time points during recovery and at a subsequent baseline. Demographics are shown in table 1. RESULTS: Baseline serum C5a levels were significantly lower in stable COPD patients compared to controls (mean C5a 17.6 ng/ml vs. 30.0 ng/ml (SD 18.3), p<0.001) (figure 1), but there was no difference for C3a (mean C3a 143.8 ng/ml vs. 151.6 ng/ml (SD 50.1), p=0.66). When stable, C5a levels were significantly lower in a subset of patients positive (9.83 ng/ml, n=6) vs. negative (18.8 ng/ml, n=11) for the presence of lower airway bacteria (p=0.005). There was a significant drop in C3a levels from the stable-state to one week post exacerbation onset (139.7 ng/ml vs 93.8 ng/ml, p=0.02); there was also a non-significant drop in C5a levels from the stable-state to one week post onset of exacerbation (21.6ng/ml vs 10.7 ng/ml, p=0.07). Both C3a and C5a levels remain reduced for over 5 weeks following exacerbation and eventually recover at subsequent baseline (figure 2). CONCLUSIONS: Complement C5a levels in serum are lower in stable COPD patients compared to healthy controls, and drop from stable-state levels during an acute exacerbation. Stable patients with bacteria identified in sputum may have lower serum C5a levels. These findings raise the possibility of increased sequestration of C5a from blood into lung tissue, in COPD patients during exacerbations or with lower airway bacterial colonisation.
