N2  - Rationale: The data on bacterial species and viruses associated with acute bronchitis are very limited. We investigated microorganisms commonly isolated from patients with acute bronchitis and also investigated their antibiotic resistances and clinical features Methods: A total of 31 hospitals (1 tertiary and 10 secondary hospitals and 20 primary clinics) participated in the study between July 2011 and October 2012. Adult patients of ≥ 18 age who had a cough illness with sputum were enrolled. Sputum specimens were collected from all enrolled patients and tested for 11 bacterial species (by cultural tests), 3 atypical bacteria (by PCR), and 10 viruses (by PCR) at a central laboratory center. Results: A total of 569 patients were enrolled and the median duration of sputum was 7.0 days (interquartiles [IQR], 4.0-7.0 days). Except for sputum and cough, rhinorrhea (35.9%) and sore throat (37.1%) were most common symptoms. Two-hundred six typical bacteria were isolated from 187 (32.9%) patients and 97 atypical bacteria were identified from 93 (16.3%) patients; 210 viruses were identified from 184 (32.3%) patients. Hemophilus influenzae (54/9.5%), Klebsiella pneumoniae (35/6.2%), and Streptococcus pneumoniae (34/6.0%) were the most common typical bacteria and Legionella spp. (50/8.8%) was the most common atypical bacteria; among the viruses, rhinovirus (119/20.9%) and adenovirus (22/3.9%) were the most common. Antibiotics were prescribed to 405 (71.2%) patients with macrolide as the most frequent one. H. influenza, K. pneumonia, and S. aureus showed a high resistance rate to ampicillin, and S. pneumoniae was highly resistant to azithromycin, cefaclor, and cefuroxime. Old age was significantly associated with typical bacteria (odds ratio [OR], 1.013; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.002 to 1.024) and chillness had a borderline significant association with atypical bacteria (OR, 1.943; 95% CI, 0.937 to 4.027). However, viruses were significantly associated with sore throat (OR, 2.162; 95% CI, 1.481 to 3.157) and rhinorrhea (OR, 1.544; 95% CI, 1.067 to 2.264). Conclusions; Bacterial organisms were present in more than 40 % of patients with acute bronchitis and viruses were also identified in 32.3% of the patients. Although the definite roles of these microorganisms are not fully understood, virus and atypical bacteria may be associated with different clinical manifestations, compared to typical bacteria.
