N2  - Background: HIV-infected patients are susceptible to a variety of bacterial, fungal, mycobacterial, and viral pneumonias that can be difficult to distinguish clinically. Furthermore, risk stratification is challenging because existing prognostic models have not been validated in HIV-infected patients. The serum biomarker procalcitonin is a helpful diagnostic and prognostic tool in HIV-uninfected patients with pneumonia. We sought to determine if procalcitonin has the same utility among patients with HIV. Methods: We conducted a prospective cohort study of HIV-infected patients hospitalized with pneumonia. We collected demographic, socioeconomic, and symptom based data via standardized questionnaires, and laboratory, radiographic, and outcome data via chart abstraction. We assigned final diagnoses based upon the impression of the treating physician. Furthermore, we collected serum at admission and measured procalcitonin using the Kryptor Immunoassay (VIDAS B•R•A•H•M•S PCT™). We used linear regression to test logged procalcitonin levels and logistic regression to test the proportion of cases with procalcitonin cut offs of 0.25ng/ml and 0.5ng/ml based upon the assigned final diagnosis. We also tested outcomes using logistic regression with logged procalcitonin as the predictor of interest. Results: We enrolled 65 patients into the cohort (January-September 2012). The mean age was 47 (±10.3) years, the median CD4 count was 150 cells/μl; 52.3% patients were Caucasian and 81.5% were male (Table 1). The majority of these patients were either homeless or marginally housed (56.9%); 74% were current or former smokers and 88.5% were illicit drug users. Patients commonly reported a history of underlying pulmonary disease and prior pneumonias (54.4% and 91.2%, accordingly). The most common presenting complaints were cough, fevers, chills, and dyspnea; bacterial pneumonia was the most common final diagnosis (46.2%). The median procalcitonin level was 0.11ng/ml (0.03ng/ml-174.1ng/ml). There was no statistically significant difference in procalcitonin level between patients with bacterial pneumonia and Pneumocystis pneumonia, PCP (0.23ng/ml vs. 0.12ng/ml, p=0.39) (Table 2). Commonly used procalcitonin levels of 0.25ng/ml and 0.5ng/ml did not differentiate bacterial and non-bacterial pneumonia and had poor prognostic utility. Furthermore, procalcitonin level did not differ between those who died, required intubation, or ICU admission and those who did not (0.41ng/ml vs. 0.21ng/ml p=0.63, 0.66ng/ml vs. 0.18ng/ml p=0.09, and 0.59ng/ml vs. 0.19ng/ml p=0.18, accordingly) (Table 2). Conclusions: The serum biomarker procalcitonin appears to lack diagnostic and prognostic utility among HIV-infected patients hospitalized with pneumonia. However, our study is limited by a small sample size and a low incidence of ICU admission, intubation, and death and further study with larger numbers is needed. (Table Presented).
