N2  - RATIONALE: Ventilator associated tracheobronchitis (VAT) and ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP) are common complications of mechanical ventilation; they prolong intensive care unit stays and can be fatal for critically ill patients. Intravenous administration of antibiotics often leads to sub-therapeutic concentrations in the respiratory tract. This clinical trial evaluated the pharmacokinetics and safety/tolerability of investigational amikacin/fosfomycin solution for nebulization, in mechanically ventilated patients with VAT or VAP. METHODS: In this first-in-human study, each adult patient received 3 escalating doses of a mixture of 50 mg/mL amikacin and 20 mg/mL fosfomycin; doses were separated by 24±2 hours. On Day 3, patients received 2 blinded, randomized treatments (amikacin/fosfomycin and volume-matched placebo), separated by 2 hours. All treatments were administered with a single patient, multi-treatment nebulizer (Investigational eFlow® Inline Nebulizer System; PARI Pharma GmbH, Starnberg, Germany), positioned in the tubing of the inspiratory limb between the ventilator and the patient. The nebulizer remained in-line until all treatments had been delivered. The nebulizer had a vibrating perforated membrane, and generated an aerosol with small droplets and narrow size distribution, optimal for deposition in the lower airways. Amikacin and fosfomycin concentrations were measured in tracheal aspirate and plasma samples obtained during the 24 hours after each dose. RESULTS: Initial pharmacokinetic results are presented herein for 4 patients in this ongoing study. Fifteen minutes after dosing, all amikacin sputum concentrations (μg/g sputum) were ≥98-fold higher than the minimum inhibitory concentration for 90% (MIC90; 32 μg/mL) of 1477 Pseudomonas aeruginosa isolates described in a recently published isolate collection; mean amikacin levels after the 6 mL dose were ≥453-fold higher than the MIC90 (Figure 1). All fosfomycin sputum concentrations were ≥68-fold higher than the published MIC 90 value for 148 isolates of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA; 32 μg/mL); mean fosfomycin levels after the 6 mL dose were ≥264-fold higher than the MIC90 (Figure 2). Six hours after dosing, all fosfomycin sputum concentrations remained ≥2.2-fold above the MIC90 for MRSA. Plasma concentrations were much lower; the highest observed amikacin plasma concentration was 1.4 μg/mL (less than the recommended peak concentration [35μg/mL] after a systemic dose) and the highest observed fosfomycin plasma concentration was 0.8 μg/mL. CONCLUSIONS: High sputum concentrations of amikacin and fosfomycin were achieved in mechanically ventilated patients with VAT or VAP after aerosolized administration with an inline nebulizer system. (Figure Presented).
