N2  - Rationale: Chronic infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PsA) is associated with worse outcomes in patients with non-CF bronchiectasis. The aim of this study was to evaluate the effect of nebulized Promixin in this patient population. Methods: Patients with bronchiectasis and chronic PsA infection were randomized to receive Promixin 1MU or placebo through the I-neb adaptive aerosol delivery (AAD) system twice daily for six months. Patients were enrolled within three weeks of completing exacerbation treatment and completed study therapy at the time of first exacerbation or after 26 weeks. The primary outcome measure was time to first exacerbation and secondary outcome measures included change in quantitative microbiology and QoL measured by the St George's Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ). Adherence was recorded by the I-neb. Results: 144 patients (61 males), mean (SD) age 59.3 (15.5) years and FEV1 57 (25) % predicted were randomized to Promixin (n=73) or placebo (n=71). The ITT analysis revealed no statistically significant difference in time to exacerbation (Promixin 165 days; Placebo 111 days) between treatment groups, although there were statistically significant improvements in QoL, estimated mean treatment difference -10.51, p=0.006; quantitative microbiology: the mean (SD) change in log10 PsA CFU in Promixin vs placebo treated patients was -1.7 (2.2) vs -0.3 (1.9), p=0.001 after 4 weeks and -1.6 (2.2) vs -0.5 (2.3), p=0.008 after 12 weeks treatment. The ability to determine patients' adherence via the I-neb, allowed a precise assessment of outcomes based on patients that took ≥80% of prescribed doses. Comparing the Promixin group (n=54) to the placebo group (n=54), the median time to first exacerbation was 168 vs 103 days, p=0.028 and both the estimated mean treatment difference in SGRQ and change in log10 PsA CFU remained statistically significant, p<0.02 and p=0.005 (after 4 and 12 weeks) respectively. Conclusion: There were statistically significant improvements in clinical, microbiologic and patient reported outcomes in adherent individuals prescribed Promixin in the context of non-CF bronchiectasis and chronic PsA infection.
