N2  - Rationale: Pulmonary nontuberculous mycobacteria (PNTM) infections in post-menopausal women with bronchiectasis have long been associated with a distinct body morphology: a tall and thin body habitus, mitral valve prolapse, pectus excavatum, and scoliosis. Reminiscent of Marfan syndrome (MFS), this morphology suggests a potential common etiologic pathway with connective tissue disorders. While bronchiectasis has previously been described in patients with MFS, other connective tissue diseases such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (EDS) have not been known to carry an increased risk. Estimations of bronchiectasis in a cohort of patients with various connective tissue diseases might further reinforce a shared causative mechanism. Methods: A retrospective chart review was conducted for patients with known connective tissue disease enrolled in a natural history protocol at the National Institute on Aging (NIA). Patients enrolled had a diagnosis of connective tissue disease established either clinically by their primary geneticist at the NIA or through genetic testing. Patient charts were reviewed for the following: the presence of respiratory symptoms, pertinent respiratory physical examination findings, chest radiology, and spirometry. Phone surveys were conducted in this same group to verify and obtain additional findings. Results: The study enrolled 103 patients (70 with vascular type EDS, 24 with MFS, 4 with classical type EDS, 3 with mitral valve prolapse, aortic root enlargement, and skin and skeletal alterations or MASS syndrome, and 2 with kyphoscoliosis type EDS). For the 66 patients with both a chart review and survey available, 28 patients (42.4%) reported having a chronic cough, 6 (9.1%) of whom also noted chronic sputum production. Fourteen patients (21.2%) reported having had at least one episode of hemoptysis. Forty-four patients had undergone chest computed tomography scanning with 4 cases (9.1%) of bronchiectasis identified. The 4 cases of bronchiectasis were equally distributed among the diagnoses vascular type EDS, classical type EDS, MFS, and MASS syndrome. Two of these 4 cases also developed PNTM, one with Mycobacterium abscessus and Mycobacterium avium and one with M. avium. Conclusions: Retrospective observations of a cohort of patients with various connective tissue disease diagnoses suggest a rate of bronchiectasis of 9.1%, higher than expected in the general population. In comparison, bronchiectasis in the general U.S. population has been estimated at 52.3 cases per 100,000 adults (0.05%). Moreover, we observed bronchiectasis in connective tissue diseases other than MFS. The ongoing associations between bronchiectasis, PNTM, and connective tissue phenotypes suggest a common pathophysiologic mechanism.
