N2  - INTRODUCTION Adenovirus causes upper respiratory infections (URI) and febrile illness in children which is usually self-limited. Major mode of transmission is found to be from droplets and stools. Fatal infections and multi-systemic disease in immunocompromised hosts have been reported in the literature. Respiratory involvement includes, but is not limited to pneumonia, ARDS (Acute Respiratory Distress syndrome), necrotizing bronchitis and bronchiolitis. CASE DESCRIPTION A 15 year old male presented with fevers, myalgias, and dry non-productive cough for 4-5 days. His physical exam was positive for expiratory wheezes, tachycardia and hypoxia. A chest X-ray (CXR) showed bilateral patchy airspace disease at the lung bases. Treatment with antibiotics, oxygen, inhaled bronchodilators and chest physiotherapy was started. For persistent fevers and worsening hypoxia, a chest computed tomography (CT) was obtained, showing multifocal alveolar pneumonia with mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy, hence antifungal therapy was added to his medication regimen, and serum testing for tuberculosis was performed which was negative. Despite this, patient went into cardiorespiratory failure, requiring intubation with mechanical ventilation and intravenous pressor support. Repeat CXR showed diffuse bilateral pulmonary infiltrates and paO2/FiO2 <250 consistent with ARDS. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing was positive from respiratory and blood samples for Adenovirus, consistent with a diagnosis of disseminated adenoviral infection (DAI). Single dose of intravenous cedofovir (5mg/kg) was given on day 6 followed by probenecid. This resulted in improvement in his clinical condition by day 10. Given his unusual presentation, an immune deficiency workup was performed and he was found to be immunocompetent. Repeat serum PCR for adenovirus was negative 1 month later. DISCUSSION Although DAI and ARDS are rare in immunocompetent patients, with very few cases reported in literature, it still carries a very high mortality of up to 60% in these cases. In immunocompetent patients, DAI can mimic bacterial infections, delaying appropriate treatment. CT chest findings most commonly associated with adenovirus infection are patchy ground glass opacities with or without consolidation, bronchiolitis obliterans or Swyer James syndrome. Although supportive treatment is the mainstay for DAI, cedofovir, a viral DNA polymerase selective inhibitor, should be started earlier in severe or refractory cases. Treatment should be followed by probenecid to prevent the nephrotoxic effects of cedofovir. Immunocompetent patients presenting with high fever, myalgia and multiorgan failure should be tested for adenovirus through serum testing as well as respiratory testing, and early therapy with antiviral agents should be considered due to high mortality of DAI.
