N2  - Introduction: Radiation-induced pulmonary fibrosis is a form of interstitial lung disease (ILD) associated with therapeutic irradiation of the lungs. Limited data are available regarding the frequency, features, and outcomes of this condition in children. Case Report: A 4-year-old girl presenting with constipation and abdominal distention was found to have bilateral renal masses. Staging workup and surgical biopsy confirmed Stage V Wilms' tumor with liver and pulmonary metastases. She was successfully treated with resection of renal and hepatic foci, chemotherapy (Vincristine, Actinomycin-D, Doxorubicin, Carboplatin, Cyclophosphamide, and Etoposide), and whole lung and abdomen irradiation (total of 1200 cGy lung irradiation over 18 days). Six months after completion of chemotherapy and 10 months after completing lung and abdominal radiation, she presented with progressive shortness of breath and increasing chronic dry cough. Chest CT (Figure 1) showed diffuse ground glass micronodular opacities, septal thickening, and a few lung cysts. An extensive infectious work-up was negative. Bronchoalveolar lavage showed no evidence of bacterial, viral, fungal, or pneumocystis pneumonia. Cytology was mildly inflammatory with 55% macrophages, 29% lymphocytes, and 16% neutrophils. An echocardiogram was normal. No mutations were identified upon sequencing of SFTPC and ABCA3. Based on the imaging findings and clinical context, a presumptive diagnosis of radiation pneumonitis was made. Empiric treatment with oral prednisolone (2 mg/kg/day) was initiated. She also received azithromycin three times per week but no other antimicrobial therapies. An overnight oximetry study and six minute walk test revealed hypoxemia with sleep and exercise (Table 1) and she was started on supplemental oxygen. Repeat chest CT three months later showed decreased ground glass opacities but marked increase in cystic remodeling changes concerning for progressive pulmonary fibrosis (Figure 2). However, her clinical status showed modest improvement, including resolution of resting tachypnea, no desaturation on six-minute walk testing, and increased functional status including return to preschool. The corticosteroid dose was gradually tapered with ongoing clinical stability and also subsequent decreased cystic remodeling changes on chest CT (not shown). Discussion: While extent of radiation exposure correlates with risk of radiation-induced lung disease, it remains difficult to reliably predict the disease course of radiation pneumonitis and there are no proven therapies. The radiographic progression and evolution of this case is striking and has not previously been well-described in children. Further, this case highlights the potential for discrepancy between clinical outcomes and imaging findings, highlighting the general importance of physiologic and functional assessments in children with ILDs. (Figure Presented).
