N2  - RATIONALE The Mounier-Kuhn syndrome, also called tracheobronchomegaly, is a rare disease. It consists of an anomalous trachea and main bronchi's diffuse dilation, generally accompanied by diverticula and bronchiectasis. The etiology is not completely understood, and it can be congenital or acquired. Some patients may experience minimal symptoms, if any, while others may present repeated pulmonary infections and eventual respiratory failure. METHODS Medical records from outpatients attending routine consultations at a tertiary Brazilian hospital were reviewed. An analysis of patients'characteristics, symptoms, pulmonary function, sputum colonization and radiological aspects was performed. RESULTS Nine cases (77.7% male) of this uncommon disease were identified, median age 54.2 years-old (range from 33 to 74). The median age of onset of symptoms was 37.4 years-old (range from 12 to 68). All patients were symptomatic: dyspnea, cough and sputum were found in all patients at different severity degrees. Wheezing was also a common finding and one patient have had hemoptysis. The median forced expiratory volume in the first second (FEV1) of these patients was 1.32 liters. The majority of patients (5 patients, 55.5%) were colonized by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and 2 patients had Aspergillus sp colonization. Other microorganisms identified were Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenza, Achromobacter sp and Serratia marcescens, in one patient each. Three patients (33.3%) had a previous diagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis. Three cases did not have any colonization identified. Chest computed tomography revealed the presence of diverticula and bronchiectasis with predominantly central location besides tracheobronchomegaly CONCLUSIONS Very few cases have been described in the literature, and most patients are men between the ages of 20 and 50 years. Our findings are in accordance with literature. The diagnosis depends on the demonstration of increased tracheal diameter, with the transverse tracheal diameter greater than 3 cm along with a right bronchial diameter of 2.4 cm and left diameter of 2.3cm. Treatment is considered when patient is symptomatic and includes bronchodilators, rehabilitation and management of exacerbations. Since bacterial colonization is such a common finding, treatment protocols should include diagnosis and management of this complication. The prevalence of this condition is very low, probably due to underdiagnosis considering the similarity of symptoms with other respiratory diseases. Therefore Mounier Kuhn should be considered in adult patients with recurrent infections, chronic sputum production, bronchiectasis and abnormal diameter of the trachea.
