KW  - bacterial infection
N2  - Rationale Despite evidence suggesting a role for bacterial infection in adult non cystic fibrosis (CF) bronchiectasis disease progression, the bacterial community present in the lower airways of these patients is poorly characterised. The aim of this study was to provide a detailed and comprehensive cross-sectional analysis of bacterial content of lower airway samples from adults with non-CF bronchiectasis using a culture-independent methodology. Methods Paired induced sputum and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) samples obtained from 41 adult non-CF bronchiectasis patients were analysed by 16S rRNA gene pyrosequencing. Meta-analysis of bacterial profiles was used to assess agreement between sample strategies, to define 'core' and 'satellite' bacterial populations for this patient group, and to correlate characteristics of the lower airway bacterial meta-community with clinical markers of disease. Results An average of 2753 16S rRNA gene sequences were obtained per sample, representing 140 separate bacterial species. Species detected included those associated with respiratory tract infections and opportunistic infections more generally. Analysis of species distribution and dispersal allowed the definition of a group of core species, consisting of bacteria detected frequently and in high abundance. In addition to species currently associated with infection in bronchiectasis, such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Haemophilus influenzae, and Streptococcus pneumoniae, the core species group also included bacteria that have been reported in other chronic respiratory infection contexts, but which would be unlikely to be reported through standard, culture-based, diagnostic surveillance. For example, members of the genera Veillonella, Prevotella, and Neisseria contributed substantially to bacterial community similarity. The comparative contribution of core and satellite groups suggests a low level of random species acquisition. Further, there was no significant difference in the bacterial community diversity in corresponding BAL and induced sputum samples. Bacterial diversity was significantly positively correlated with FEV1, (r2 = 0.20, F1,36 = 8.72, P = 0.006) and core bacterial community profile similarity (Bray-Curtis index) correlated significantly with FEV1 (P <0.0001), neutrophil count (P = 0.001), Leicester cough score (P <0.0001), and gender (P = 0.027). Conclusions Non-CF bronchiectasis is associated with a complex and defined microbiota, distinct from that reported in other respiratory contexts. The structure of the bacterial community present correlates with clinical measures of disease, and may provide additional markers of disease progression. The potential contribution of the bacterial diversity reported here to airway pathogenesis warrants further investigation.
