N2  - Idiopathic Pneumonia Syndrome (IPS) is defined as signs and symptoms of pneumonia, widespread alveolar disease without infectious source, and exclusion of cardiac or renal failure. It usually occurs within 120 days of hematopoetic stem cell transplant (HSCT), more commonly found with allogenic transplants, and encompasses a subset of pulmonary conditions including diffuse alveolar hemorrhage (DAH). Risk factors include old age, high-intensity conditioning, and graft versus host disease (GVHD). Mortality is 60-80%, and even higher if mechanical ventilation is required. We describe a case of a 64 year-old male with history of large B-cell lymphoma who presented 180 days after allogenic HSCT with progressive shortness of breath and nonproductive cough. His CT showed diffuse ground-glass opacifications involving all five lobes, as well as a right lower segment pulmonary embolus. He underwent bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage revealed progressively bloody lavage consistent with DAH. Infectious work-up including bacterial, fungal, AFB, CMV, HSV, Mycoplasma, Aspergillus, Nocardia, Legionella and Pneumocystis jeroveci cultures were all negative. Echocardiogram did not reveal congestive heart failure and renal function was normal. He underwent video-assisted thoracoscopy surgery with biopsies of right upper and lower lobes revealing acute diffuse alveolar damage, organizing pneumonia, and focal arterial thrombus. The clinical picture and biopsy results are suggestive of IPS with alveolar, endothelial and airway injury. He was treated with three days of high-dose methylprednisolone therapy followed by Prednisone taper. His course was complicated by subcutaneous emphysema and pneumomediastinum, a known complication of IPS. This case demonstrates several recognized and unrecognized features of IPS. Interestingly, our patient's presentation was out of the usual time frame for IPS. This may be attributed to cessation of immunosuppression therapy, as Tacrolimus was stopped two weeks before presentation. The mechanism of Tacrolimus involves inhibition of T-lymphocyte signal transduction. Models have been created to mimic IPS pathogenesis, however, it is still not well understood. What is known is that there is a link to T-cell mediated injury that was described by an allogenic HSCT Murine model of IPS. Moreover, the patient presented with DAH, which usually has mean time of onset within 30 days of transplant. Known pathogenesis of DAH involves T-cell mediated cytokine release. In this case, we hypothesize that Tacrolimus could have preventative effect of developing IPS in connection to T-cell response. (Figure presented) .
