N2  - INTRODUCTION Daptomycin is a relatively new antibiotic for the treatment of infections caused by methicillin-resistant Stapholococcus aureus (MRSA). Acute eosinophilic pneumonia (AEP) is a rare adverse reaction associated with daptomycin that requires prompt recognition for proper management. CASE REPORT A 28 year-old Caucasian male with previously-diagnosed polymicrobial osteomyelitis of the feet, presented to the emergency department with complaints of fever, dyspnea and dry cough. Daptomycin and piperacillin/tazobactam were started 4 weeks prior to this presentation for osteomyelitis caused by MRSA, Klebsiella and Streptococcus. One week prior to this presentation, the patient was diagnosed with hospital-acquired pneumonia and treated with levofloxacin but failed to return to his baseline pulmonary status. Upon evaluation, he was afebrile but tachypneic and severely hypoxic. He had course crackles bilaterally and rhonchi in the right upper lung. Initial labs revealed a leukocytosis to 15.4 x103/uL with 19% eosinophils. Chest CT showed diffuse peribronchovascular airspace disease, worse in the upper lobes. He underwent bronchoscopy requiring intubation and transfer to the ICU. Cell count from bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) revealed 75% eosinophils. Transbronchial biopsies showed acute fibrinous and organizing pneumonia with reactive alveolar and interstitial epithelial changes. He was diagnosed with drug-induced AEP secondary to daptomycin, which improved upon discontinuation of the offending agent and initiation of corticosteroids. At follow up 3 weeks later, the patient no longer required supplemental oxygen and his chest x-ray had improved. DISCUSSION: AEP is a potentially life-threatening process that has been reported after exposure to more than 300 different medications including antibiotics such as penicillins and cephalosporins, statins, and beta blockers. Daptomycin-induced AEP is increasingly recognized, however, definitive diagnosis presents a challenge given the overlap of clinical characteristics with alternative diagnoses (e.g. infectious pneumonia) and potential lack of awareness of this process. Physicians should be educated about the features that are characteristic of daptomycin-induced AEP (fever, dry cough, dyspnea, hypoxia and peripheral eosinophilia occurring within 1-3 weeks of exposure), and should have a low threshold for stopping daptomycin during the workup. Bronchoscopy with BAL and biopsy is the preferred method for diagnosis and commonly reveals >25% eosinophils on BAL, and histologic changes described above. Symptoms typically resolve quickly with cessation of daptomycin; corticosteroids may be an adjunct in therapy. However, some cases resolve slowly and relapses have been reported while tapering corticosteroids. CONCLUSIONS: Early recognition of daptomycin-induced AEP is key to proper management of the disorder and may prevent progression to respiratory failure.
