N2  - INTRODUCTION: Patients with bronchiectasis are frequently colonized with pseudomonas aeruginosa. Pseudomonas infection usually presents with multifocal airspace consolidation, small centrilobular nodules, or cavitations on computed tomography (CT). It does not usually present as large, isolated pulmonary nodules. CASE PRESENTATION: Our patient is a 76 year old woman who presented to the hospital with shortness of breath and was found to be in atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular rate. In addition, she complained of a dry cough. Past medical history included bronchiectasis secondary to childhood pertussis and respiratory tract colonization with pseudomonas aeruginosa. She was recently diagnosed with temporal arteritis and was undergoing treatment with 50 mg of prednisone. Pertinent physical exam findings included bilateral rhonchi, a prolonged expiratory phase, and scattered wheezes in addition to a rapid heart rate. Abnormal admission lab values included an elevated white blood cell count of 20,800 (k/mcl) with 94% neutrophils. Chest CT revealed several noncavitary pulmonary nodules ranging in size up to 2-3 cm. It also revealed brochiectasis that mostly involved the lingula and right middle lobe. The patient then underwent CT-guided core needle biopsy and fine needle aspiration (FNA) of two left upper lobe nodules to assess for neoplasm. Core needle histology revealed acute and chronic inflammation. FNA cultures revealed pseudomonas aeruginosa susceptible to ciprofloxacin. The patient was discharged on Ciprofloxacin. Follow up chest CT one month later showed decrease in the size and number of the pulmonary nodules. DISCUSSION: Multiple pulmonary nodules are a common presentation associated with a broad differential diagnosis. The most common etiology of multiple pulmonary nodules greater than 1 cm is metastatic solid organ malignancy. Other possible diagnoses include granulomatosis with polyangitis, amyloidosis, rheumatoid arthritis, pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, and pneumoconioses. Infectious etiologies include septic emboli, fungal and mycobacterial infections, and paragonimus westermani. Bronchiectasis and small centrilobular nodules (<1 cm) are frequently seen with atypical mycobacterial infections. Although pseudomonas aeruginosa in the lung can have various radiographic manifestations, the unusual presentation in our patient was the large size of the pulmonary nodules simulating metastatic disease. CONCLUSIONS: In the setting of bronchiectasis and large pulmonary nodules suspicious for metastatic disease, pseudomonas infection should be considered in the differential diagnosis.
