N2  - Introduction: Non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) infections are being increasingly recognized as a cause of significant morbidity in solid-organ transplant recipients. Mycobacterium avium complex infection (MAC) in the transplant population has mostly been described in disseminated and cutaneous forms. Pulmonary infection with MAC is rarely described in renal transplant recipients. We report a case of a renal transplant patient who was empirically treated for tuberculosis infection but subsequently diagnosed with pulmonary MAC infection. Case Report: A 59 year old Senegalese male presented with dyspnea, productive cough, and night sweats for one month. Medical history was significant for kidney transplant two years prior and recurrent venous thromboembolism. Chest imaging showed bilateral cavitating pulmonary nodules (Figure 1). The patient underwent left upper lobe wedge resection. Pathology showed areas of granulomatous inflammation and stains showed acid fast positive bacilli (Figure 2). Given high concern for tuberculosis, the patient was started on traditional anti-tuberculous quadritherapy. The final culture showed growth of MAC, for which he was subsequently treated. Computed tomography scan of the chest performed six months after initiation of therapy showed decreased lesion size. Despite growth of MAC from the lung biopsy, full duration of anti-tuberculous treatment was continued due to high suspicion of concurrent tuberculosis infection given the patient's immigration from an endemic region. Discussion: Organ transplant recipients are at increased risk of developing opportunistic infections due to immunosuppression from medications such as cyclosporine and sirolimus, which inhibit cell-mediated immunity that protects against mycobacteria. M. tuberculosis infection in renal transplant recipients is often seen in endemic countries, but NTM infection is less commonly reported. Causative agents in this population are usually reported to be M. kansasii and M. fortuitum. Pulmonary MAC in renal transplant recipients has been reported very rarely. The diagnosis of MAC in these individuals can be extremely challenging and requires a high degree of clinical suspicion. Furthermore, treatment of tuberculosis or NTM infection in transplant patients can be challenging due to interaction between immunosuppressive and anti-mycobacterial medications. For example, rifampicin is known to increase clearance and decrease the concentration of sirolimus, which may increase graft rejection. Conclusion: Early recognition and diagnosis of NTM infection is important to preventing progression, disseminated infection, and potentially adverse consequences in organ transplant recipients. Although the approach to diagnosis and treatment of NTM infection in transplant patients is the same as for the general population, close attention must be paid to clinical response to therapy.
