N2  - Introduction: Infection is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in lung transplant patients. Although ubiquitous in developing nations and present in a third of the world's population, tuberculosis has a much lower incidence in the developed world. The prevalence of tuberculosis infections in developed countries ranges from 1.2-6.4 percent among transplant recipients. Case: A 75 year old man presented with diarrhea and productive cough. He had a history of double lung transplant due to Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis sixteen months prior and prostate cancer. Medications included mycophenolate mofetil, tacrolimus, voriconazole and trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole. Chest radiograph revealed a new right lower lobe nodule, pictured below. He had no other symptoms and denied fevers, chills, night sweats or weight loss. There were no significant laboratory abnormalities or exam findings. The patient was discharged but returned ten days later with the same symptoms and worsening fatigue. Physical exam was again unremarkable. He was found to have a sodium of 114 and was admitted. CT scan revealed two right lower lobe pulmonary nodules (10 and 5 mm in diameter) and right-sided mediastinal lymphadenopathy. The patient's hyponatremia was thought to be secondary to SIADH and slowly returned to baseline with fluid restriction. He became febrile and developed a RML infiltrate. Work up for atypical pneumonia was negative. Sputum culture was positive for acid fast bacilli (AFB). The patient had a negative PPD prior to transplant and had no risk factors for tuberculosis. There was concern for disseminated mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) and the patient was started on treatment (azithromycin, rifabutin, ethambutol). A week later DNA probes from the AFB positive sputum and stool were positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis. Further review of the patient's social history revealed that he had contact with Mexican immigrants. The patient was treated with ethambutol, isoniazid, rifabutin and pyrazinamide. His symptoms resolved prior to discharge . Follow up imaging showed decreased size of the pulmonary nodules and resolved lymphadenopathy. Conclusions: Transplant recipients with tuberculosis commonly present with atypical symptoms and extra pulmonary manifestations. The medications used to treat tuberculosis can lead to decreased levels of calcineurin inhibitors. Therefore diagnosis and management of tuberculosis in lung transplant recipients can be challenging.
