N2  - RATIONALE: Protracted Bacterial Bronchitis (PBB) is a major cause of chronic wet cough in children and is now recognised in international chronic cough guidelines. Our original clinical description of PBB was based on a small cohort of children (n=45) and diagnostic criteria included presence of: (a) chronic wet cough 4 weeks, (b) identifiable lower airway bacterial infection on bronchoalveolar-lavage (BAL) and (c) response to antibiotics with resolution of cough within 2 weeks. The aim of this study was to further develop our understanding of PBB by examining a larger cohort of children, with specific focus on patterns of viral bacterial co-infection in children with PBB. METHODS: Children undergoing flexible bronchoscopy and BAL for clinical indication, excluding those with cystic fibrosis, were eligible for inclusion into this prospective cohort study. Only children fulfilling criteria for PBB were included in this analysis. All children had immune function testing, completed clinical questionnaires and had prospective follow-up with cough diaries. A subset, n=52, had nasopharyngeal aspirates (NPA) for extended viral studies by PCR. RESULTS: Amongst 96 children who fulfilled criteria for PBB, 71 (74%) were male, median age of 18.5 mo. Median cough duration was 28 wks and household tobacco smoke exposure proportion was 37.5%. At one-year follow-up, 54 of 65 (83.1%) had recurrent PBB (3 episodes/yr). Airway malacias (tracheo- and/or bronchomalacia) were present in 63%. Median CD56 & CD16 (Natural Killer) cells were elevated at 0.68 compared to the normal range (NR) of 0.05 to 0.52. Median total IgE =89 ug/L (IQR 32.8, 475) NR<240 and median %eosinophils in BAL =0. BAL %neutrophils were elevated at 39 (IQR 19, 65) and higher in the presence of positive virus detection (median =45, IQR 21, 69). Virus/es were detected on NPA in 44 of 52 (84.6%), with 18 (34.6%) positive for two or more viruses. Of those who had extended viral panel performed (on BAL and/or NPA), human rhinovirus (HRV) was detected in 28 of 52 (53.8%) and adenovirus in 13 (25%). H. influenzae and HRV was the most common viral-bacterial combination. CONCLUSION: PBB was more common in males and those exposed to tobacco smoke. Elevated IgE levels were rare. Very high rates of viral-bacterial co-detection and elevated numbers of NK cells in blood were observed. Viral-bacterial co-infection is likely to be an important feature of PBB in children.
