N2  - Introduction: Acute respiratory diseases are substantial cause of morbidity and mortality in childhood. Staphylococcus aureus is one of the microorganisms responsible of common hospitalization and death from pneumonia among children in developing countries. The aim of this study is to evaluate frequency and to identify epidemiological factors and clinical aspects of pleuro-pulmonary staphylococcal infection at Albert Royer Children Hospital. Methods: We conducted a retrospective review of medical records of all patients admitted at Albert Royer Children Hospital for pneumonia and/or empyema with positive bacterial culture isolating staphylococcus aureus from biological specimen (pleural fluid, tracheal secretion, blood culture). The diagnosis was suspected when existing cough, fever, tachypnea. Confirmation was obtained with radiographic and ultrasonographic with pattern of pneumonia and/or pleural effusion. We excluded tuberculosis cases, all cases with negative culture or incomplete medical record. Results: A total 24 cases of pleuro-pulmonary staphylococcal infections was diagnosed at Albert Royer Children Hospital from January 2010 to October 2012, representing 1.6% of total admission. The mean age is 29 months (2 months to 14 years) and 66% of the patients are under 18 months old. There is male predominance 62.5% versus 37.5% (sex-ratio: 1.6). Almost children have good health. Main morbidities are malnutrition 5 cases (20%), congenital heart disease 1 case (4%), Down syndrome 1 case (4%). No case of HIV infection or sickle cell anemia. The mean duration of hospitalization is 22 days (from 2 days to 63 days). Mortality rate is 8.3% (2 cases). Chest x-ray abnormalities are isolated empyema (20.4%), pleural effusion with pneumonia (71%), and pleural effusion with pneumothorax (8.4%). Main complications are pachypleuritis (5 cases), persistent atelectasia (1 case), aphonia (1 case). Eighteen (18) patients (75%) have chest tube drainage and 4 cases benefit of invasive ventilation. There is a good sensitivity: 91.4% of Staphylococcus aureus isolated colonies are methicillinsusceptible (MSSA). Conclusion: The high incidence of empyema at Albert Royer Children Hospital suggests increasing acute respiratory infections. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common organism responsible for severe pleuropulmonary bacterial infection. Despite good sensitivity of bacteria, lethality is considerable. Eight percent of Staphylococcus aureus are community acquired methicillin resistant (MRSA).
