N2  - Introduction: Bronchiolitis Obliterans (BO) is an infrequent form of chronic lung disease leading to the obstruction and/or obliteration of the distal airways. These pathologic processes are secondary to a severe insult that leads to a variable degree of inflammation. BO implies a chronic necrotizing and ultimately fibrosing and scarring process affecting the distal airways, which results in their progressive obliteration with resultant obstructive lung disease. BO is of special importance in pediatric recipients of allogeneic bone marrow or lung transplantations. However, BO following a severe lung infection is the most common form reported in children. Most reports of postinfectious bronchiolitis obliterans (PIBO) come from North and South America, Western and South-Eastern Europe, India, Korea, Taiwan, Malaysia, New Zealand, and Australia [1]. Epidemiology and aetiology: There are many reports of PIBO secondary to influenza, parainfluenza, measles, respiratory syncytial virus, varicella, and Mycoplasma pneumonia [1]. However, adenovirus (AV) is by far the most common cause of PIBO, and has a high mortality rate. Althought PIBO in children is considered a rare condition, the prevalence has been recently found to be higher than previously suspected, and their frequency tracks that of severe viral lower respiratory tract infections (LTRI) in young children, particularly to AV. Specific AV serotypes like AV3, AV7 and AV11, are the most common involved in South America; and AV7h strain was associated with more severe cases [2]. High prevalence of viral infections and low socio-economic status, which is correlated with poor sanitation, worse hygienic practices, indoor smoking and overcrowding, probably might all contribute to viral diseases trasmission [3]. The worldwide incidence of PIBO seems to be dropping since the beginning of the 21st century. It is likely that the changing epidemiology of viral infection, rather than improvement in socioeconomic conditions explain this change. The most important serotypes related to LRI in South Korea hospitals (from 1990- 2007), were AV3 and AV7 [4]. Although AV7h outbreaks were more frequent in the first years of the study period, the number of cases progressively diminished, and AV3 became more prevalent. Coincidently, the most severe cases of LRTI were due to AV7h [5]. Also, in the southern cone of South America, AV7h was the predominant and most damaging strain over a ten-year surveillance period (from 1985-1995) [2]. but after a progressive decline it was last detected in 2005. In a multicenter study (Argentina, Chile and Uruguay) during 1984-1994, from a total of 537 AV strains isolated in children, 63% were identified as subspecies B, 36% as subspecies C and 0.7% were subspecies E (AV4). The most frequently and serious AV isolated variants were 7, 2, 1 and 5 [6,7]. AV7 was the most frequently found (59% of cases). Five different genomic variants were identified (7b, 7c, 7h, 7i and 7j) [7]. Risk factors and prognosis: Colom et al. [8] in a case-control Argentinian study with 109 children who developed PIBO and 99 controls found after multivariate logistic regression analysis that AV infection and mechanical ventilation were the strongest risk factors for developing PIBO. Castro-Rodriguez et al. [9] reported a 5-year follow-up Chilean study of 18 children with AV pneumonia who developed PIBO and 20 controls. Patients who developed PIBO were more likely to be admited to intensive care, undergo mechanical ventilation, and need supplemental oxygen, as well as more likely to have been prescribed systemic corticosteroids and albuterol. Murtagh et al. [10] studied risk factors for PIBO in 415 Argentinian children hospitalized with AV positive LRTI. Of the 150 that developed PIBO, risk factors were hospitalization more than 30 days, multifocal pneumonia, and hypercapnea. Independent risk factors for death in the acute stage of disease were: mechanical ventilation, multifocal pneumonia, hypercapnia, coagulopathy, neurological symptoms and co-infection with measles. Mortality can be as high as 18% from acute AV infection, but, once PIBO is established, the mortality rate is low [9,10]. Ethnic factors have also been suggested as a predisposing factor. In a preliminary report on genetic profiles, an increased frequency of HLA-DR8-DQB1∗0302 haplotype was reported among amerindian population [11]. In contrast, 70% of the PIBO children followed-up in Porto Alegre-Brazil are Caucasian [12]. Different racial distributions are found in other centres, according to their local ethnic composition. Health-related quality of life data revealed scores comparable to healthy peers suggesting that coping may be very good in children with PIBO [13]. Cazzato et al. [14] described a decline in FEV1 of 1% per year in children, which correlated well with the inflammatory profile found in the BAL fluid. In contrast, imaging studies from Japan suggests that there is a clear progression of the lesions only during the first 3 months after the initial insult without signs of subsequent deterioration in the following 2 years [15]. Mattiello et al. [16] reported in Brazilian and Chilean children with PIBO, that those with the severest imaging findings in HRCT obtained within the first two years of the acute event had worse lung function a decade later. This suggests that those who show a functional decline are the ones with more severe disease, whose progression could also be explained by their inherent co-morbidities and complications, such as bronchiectasis or lobar collapse [17]. Pathogenesis and histopathology: PIBO is characterized by a pattern of constrictive bronchiolitis (bronchiolar walls are partially or completely replaced by inflammation and fibrosis leading to partial or total occlusion of the airway lumen). Other common findings include mucus stasis, airway distortion and dilation/bronchiectasis, and inflammatory exudates [1]. Characteristic lesions of PIBO are patchily distributed throughout both lungs without any definite pattern [1]. Some believe that the presence of inflammation, identified histologically or by bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) suggests that PIBO is a progressive disease. Two studies, found a significant neutrophilic profile in BAL fluid, associated with lymphocytosis [11,18]. A recent study [19] found evidence of a significant increase in oxidative stress in the BAL of children with PIBO post AV infection which supports the inflammatory activity suggested in the cellular studies. Surprisingly, adequate antioxidant activity was also present in BAL fluid, which suggests there is a counterbalance of the oxidative effects; this would suggest the disease is not progressive. A study revealed that lymphocyte predominance founded in some BAL patients with PIBO was different from post-transplant BO patients, which is well known to be progressive entity; in PIBO T cells CD3+ predominate (and the ratio CD4/CD8 is in the lower limit); while in post-transplant BO CD8+ T-cells predominate [17]. In contrast, elevated percentage of neutrophils and increase in IL-8 concentration was reported in BAL fluid of PIBO patients. Clinical manifestations: PIBO is characterized by the presence of signs and symptoms of severe and persistent small airway obstruction; however, there are no specific signs and symptoms of PIBO and there is a wide clinical spectrum [1]. The initial event (usually in a child under the age of 3) is a severe acute bronchiolitis or pneumonia with severe tachypnea, cyanosis, respiratory distress and multiorganic compromise; leading to high oxygen requirements, admission to the intensive care unit and sometimes mechanical ventilatory support. Progression to OB may occur, with increasing dyspnea, chronic wheezing, cough and sputum production. Children may have long-term ventilator or oxygen dependency at home. Wheezing, hypoxemia, and crackles can persist for months to years. Requirement of oxygen supplementation after discharge may have a median period of 33 months. Also frequent wheezing with symptom free intervals has been seen. Many patients progress to recurrent pneumonia, recurrent or chronic atelectasis, wheezing exacerbations and cor pulmonale, thoracic deformity and clubbing. Pneumonia and secondary bacterial infections contribute to the development of bronchiectasis that may become saccular. At school age exercise capacity is reduced and respiratory muscle function impaired. In the most severe cases the symptoms persist into adolescence, with chronic cough, bronchorrhea, wheezing and rarely a spontaneous pneumothorax. Lung function can be severely reduced and oxygen therapy still necessary with quality of life poor. These patients are candidates for lung transplantation. Imaging: There are no pathognomonic imaging features of PIBO, but radiology plays an important role in diagnosis and follow-up. The three commonly used imaging methods are high-resolution computed tomography of the chest (HRCT-C), conventional chest radiographs (CXR), and lung ventilation/perfusion scans (V/Q scans). HRCT-C is the most sensitive of the three, identifying parenchymal and airway abnormalities in large and small airways [21,22]. The most characteristic tomographic finding in PIBO is a mosaic pattern of lung attenuation, where there is reduced vascularity in the lucent areas due to abnormal ventilation, air-trapping and secondary hypoxic vasoconstriction [21,23]. Other tomographic findings include bronchiectasis, air trapping, bronchial wall thickening, atelectasis and mucus plugging [1]. Chest X-ray is the primary imaging modality used in PIBO, and the most common findings are peri-bronchial thickening and air trapping. Air trapping may be generalized or localized; straight diaphragms and retrosternal air are also indirect signs of air trapping. Atelectasis and bronchiectasis may also be present [24,25].(Table Presented).
