N2  - Malignant lymphoma arising in the pleura are rare, comprising 2.4% of the primary chest wall tumors. A 32-year-old obese female complained of dyspnea on effort and a cough for one month. On physical examination, she was clearly dyspneic, with no positive general signs. The trachea was deviated to the left side. The right side of the chest was moving less. She had no air entry to the right lung with a stony dullness percussion note. Her initial routine laboratory investigations were unremarkable except for anemia and a urinary tract infection. Chest radiograph demonstrated a large plural effusion and shadow on the right side. CT demonstrated a large right-sided pleural effusion with partial lung collapse, with tracheal and mediastinal shift to the left, and an anterior mediastinal soft tissue mass with a necrotic center. Laboratory findings did not demonstrate remarkable changes. In this case, several specimens of plural fluid were taken. The first analysis was positive for malignant blue round cell tumor and suggestive of Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma. A second analysis of pleural effusion did not detect empyema, tuberculosis, atypical acid-fast bacterium, or malignant cells. The third analysis of pleural fluid analysis was positive for Staphylococcus aureus. CT-guided biopsy of the plural fluid and mediastinal mass showed no evidence of malignancy. Surgery was performed for histological diagnosis. Under general anesthesia with double lumen endotracheal tube, right posteriorlateral thoracotomy was performed for complete resection of the pleura. No lymph node involvement was detected. Macroscopically, the pleura was thickened with no invasion of the chest wall or ribs. Microscopical examination of the 13×13 cm specimen showed infiltrating sheets of small dark cells with hyperchromatic nuclei and scanty cytoplasm. Scattered mitoses were seen, and there was a focal area of necrosis. Immunohistochemical staining was performed and cells were positive for CD3 and negative for CD20. The diagnosis was T-cell non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma of the pleura. The patient was discharged in good condition on the 7th postoperative day for follow up with the oncology centre. Reported here is a very rare case of primary malignant lymphoma originating in the soft tissue of the pleura.
