N2  - Description of Case A 69 year old, homosexual male, who has recently returned from Thailand presented to MCH with 4 weeks of high fever, chills and rigors off and on, cough with productive sputum. Patient reported significant weight loss and lack of appetite for past 2 weeks. Initial GCS was 15/15 with features of lower respiratory tract infection. Patient's past medical history includes Stage III malignant melanoma diagnosed in 2004 treated with surgery and chemotherapy.Tests still showed no evidence of metastasis at the latest follow up in November 2011 The initial differential diagnosis was malaria, immunodeficiency with a community acquired pneumonia and received treatment accordingly. The following day, the patient's conscious level reduced to 12/15. Major differential diagnosis were viral meningitis, encephalitis, cerebral malaria and immunodeficiency with opportunistic CNS infections. A CT brain with contrast showed no significant findings. Lumbar puncture showed normal opening pressure, numerous leucocytes with predominant lymphocytes. It showed high protein and normal glucose ratio. Investigations for malaria, HIV, hepatitis B and C, viral meningitis (HSV 1 and 2, varicella, influenza, enterovirus), cryptococcal infection, bacterial and fungal cultures for blood and CSF cultures were performed. The patient was empirically treated with IV acyclovir, IV ceftriaxone, IV azithromycin, IV Bactrim and IV Amphotericin before transferring to LGH ICU for further investigations and management. After 2 days, the patient deteriorated further and was intubated.The treatment spectrum was narrowed since HIV and malaria tests came back negative. Further investigation with MRI brain showed abnormal hyper intensity in bilateral thalami, hippocampi which lead to the suspicion of Japanese B encephalitis. EEG showed features of diffuse cerebral dysfunction, suggestive of encephalopathy. After 2 weeks, diagnosis was confirmed by positive serum IgMfor Japanese B encephalitis. The patient deteriorated and passed away in spite of supportive care after 4 weeks of hospitalisation. Issues of Interest in the Case List not more than 5 points 1. A definitive diagnosis of Japanese B encephalitis is difficult. Therefore the condition and it's preventive measures must be made available to the general public. 2. Mortality and morbidity rate is high although it has a low incident rate in Australia. 3. Only 55 published reports of travel associated Japanese B encephalitis in non-endemic countries. Overall incidence is one per one million travellers. 4. This is the first diagnosed case in Launceston General Hospital.
