N2  - AIM: To report two unusual cases of parainfectious optic neuritis in adults. BACKGROUND: Parainfectious optic neuritis may be an isolated finding or associated with intracerebral/spinal cord lesions following a viral or bacterial infection and occurs more commonly in children.1 METHODS: A review of 2 rare cases presenting to a tertiary neuroophthalmology unit. RESULTS: Both patients were immunocompetent and had evidence of raised antibody titres or positive serology to their respective causative pathogens: mycoplasma pneumonia and cytomegalovirus. The first case was that of a 37 year old lady with bifrontal headaches, bilaterally reduced visual acuity to hand movements and a prior history of a productive cough which resolved spontaneously without treatment. She had bilateral swollen optic discs and MRI imaging revealed an enhancing lesion in the left parietooccipital region. CSF analysis showed a lymphocytosis and there were increased left sided vascular markings on her chest x ray. The patient responded well to intravenous and oral steroid therapy. The second patient presented with headaches, a four week history of a viral illness, normal visual acuities and a unilateral papillitis. Raised antibody titres to CMV were found and MRI imaging was normal. No treatment was given and the patient made a spontaneous recovery. CONCLUSION: Two rare and unusual causes of parainfectious optic neuritis are discussed. The presenting features, disease severity, management and associated neurological features varied considerably in each. Both cases, however, were associated with a clear history of a recent prodromal illness. Questions pertaining to this should always be asked upon systems enquiry in a patient presenting with an (atypical) optic neuritis.
