N2  - Tuberculosis (TB) is a life threatening disease, caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Emergence of high degree of drug-resistance and prevalence of Mycobacterium other than tuberculosis (MOTT) necessitates the search for new anti-tubercular agents. In that context, silver nanoparticles (SNP) which have been known for their antimicrobial activity, were evaluated against the standard strain of M. tuberculosis and 26 clinical isolates including drug sensitive (DS), multi-drug resistant (MDR), extensive-drug resistant (XDR) and MOTT strains, through BACTEC 460TB radiometric analysis. All the clinical isolates got inhibited within the MIC range of 7.8 to 15.6 μg/ml and MOTT with MIC 25 μg/ml of SNP. Silver nanoparticles were synthesized by bioreduction of silver nitrate (AgNO3) solution with Cucumis sativus plant extract and characterized using UV-Vis absorption spectrophotometry, Photoluminescence, FTIR, XRD, TEM, EDS. A mixed phase, cubic and hexagonal SNPs with the average particle size of 15 nm were synthesized and evaluated for their antimycobacterial activity. © 2013 by American Scientific Publishers All rights reserved.
