N2  - Background: Pneumocystis jirovecii is an opportunistic fungal pathogen that causes pneumonia (PJP) in immunocompromised individuals. It's incidence is up to 15% in solid organ transplant recipients not receiving prophylaxis. Several outbreaks have been reported recently in centers where routine prophylaxis is not indicated. We describe an outbreak in a Brazilian centre, which performs routine chemoprophylaxis with trimethoprim/sulphamethoxazole (TMP/SMZ) for at least 6 months after transplantation. Methods: We describe epidemiological, clinical characteristics and outcome of a PJP outbreak that occurred in a single centre from June to November 2011. Data was retrospectively collected from medical records. In all cases, PJP was diagnosed by transbronchial or openlung biopsy. Results: Since 1998, more than 8,000 transplants were performed and less than 10 cases of PJP were reported in our centre. Fifteen cases of PJP occurred from June to November 2011.The mean age was 42 years, 8 were male, 14 received organ from deceased donor (11 kidney and 3 pancreas-kidney) and 1 living donor kidney transplant. The average time of transplantation was 27 months and mean creatinine clearance at the medical appointment prior to hospitalization was 38ml/min. Most patients (80%) received regimens containing mycophenolate. Four (27%) patients received treatment with methylprednisolone in the last 6 months and 7(47%) had received therapy with lymphocyte-depleting antibodies since the transplant. Except for one patient who had allergy to sulfa-containing drugs, all patients had received prophylaxis with TMP/SMZ (mean duration of 9 months). Clinical presentation consisted of dyspnea (100%), hypoxemia (100%), cough (87%), and fever (80%). Ground-glass infiltration was the predominant radiological pattern (93%). At the time of admission, 13 (87%) patients had lymphopenia, and 13 (87%) had renal impairment, most of then (77%) requiring dialysis. Treatment started 3 days after admission. All patients required intensive care unit admission, 11(73%) required mechanical ventilation and 6 patients (40%) died. Conclusion: The PJP is a life-threatening infection, even in patients who received adequate prophylaxis during the first months after transplantation. Patients under higher immunosuppression, those with lymphopenia and poor kidney function are probably more susceptible to late infection and should be considered for prolonged prophylaxis.
