N2  - Introduction: Idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia (ICL) is a rare disease with a clinical spectrum ranging from asymptomatic laboratory abnormality to life threatening opportunistic infections. There are a few reported cases of ICL associated with opportunistic organisms like mycobacterium, fungi, and parasites. We report a case of an HIV negative patient who presented with disseminated blastomycosis and found to have ICL. Methods: Chart review for CBC, Lymphocyte subset, Serology for HIV, Syphilis, Aspergillus, and Histoplasma, Chest Xray (CXR), MRI brain, Skin biopsy, Bronchoscopy with lung biopsy results. Results: 48-year old healthyAAM presented with a 2 month history of acute dyspnea, fatigue, generalized muscle weakness, and cough. He was initially treated with a course of moxifloxacin followed by a course of doxycycline for presumed left lower lobe pneumonia on CXR without improvement. The patient developed skin nodules and vesicles on the face which ulcerated during the treatment with meropenem, zithromax and vacomycin. Skin biopsy was suggestive of Sweet Syndrome and prednisone was started. Due to lack of clinical improvement with progressing pneumonia and worsening skin lesions, repeat skin and lung biopsies were performed which identified blastomycosis. While hospitalized, he developed diabetes insipidus and brain MRI showed a meningoencephalitic process due to blastomycosis. Laboratory tests revealed lymphocytopenia with CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts <100/microl with CD4/CD8 ratio of 1.33 ( total lymphocyte count prior to prednisone was 600), HIV RNAPCR and antibody tests negative, serology for Cryptococcus, Syphilis, Aspergillus, and Histoplasma also negative.The diagnosis of disseminated blastomycosis in a patient with ICL was made. He was treated with amphotericin B for 6 weeks and itraconazole for additional 12 months. Because of persistently low CD4 T cell counts, PCP prophylaxis was initiated. Conclusion: We describe a case of ICL in a patient presenting with disseminated blastomycosis. Monitoring lymphocyte counts is appropriate follow up in ICL as is antibiotic prophylaxis when indicated.
