N2  - Background: Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia (BOOP) is a clinicopathological entity characterized by: (i) a pneumonia-like clinical presentation and a history of rapidly progressive dyspnoea; (ii) visualization of patchy infiltrates on chest computed tomography; and (iii) histopathological evidence of intraluminal organization, especially in alveolar ducts. Case report: A 55 year old women presented with progressive moderate dyspnoea, dry cough, wheezing and malaise. She had a past history of Bronchial Asthma and was treated for the last 12 months with inhaler corticosteroid and bronchodilator, without clinical improvement. Lung function tests showed a moderate obstruction. Arterial blood gas analysis showed a Pa O2 of 8.9 k Pa (67 mmHg), a Pa CO2 of 4.9 k Pa (37 mmHg), and a pH of 7.44. White blood cell count 15 000 cells mm3 with 4% eosinophils and erithrosedimentation rate 65 mm3/h. The tuberculin skin test was negative. Chest CT scan confirmed the presence of patchy bilateral alveolar opacities with an air bronchogram. A transbronchial biopsy showed inflammatory alveolitis, with the presence of discrete buds of granulation tissue within alveoli. The serological tests for connective tissue disease and atypical pneumonias were negative. The tests for viral infections were positive for cytomegalovirus. Oral corticosteroid (prednisone) was commenced and after 2 weeks we observed a symptomatic improvement and a little clearing of the radiograph infiltrates. She remained well and her chest CT scan was normal at 6 months of treatment, while on a tapering dose of prednisone. Discussion and conclusion: BOOP was first described by Epler. It may be an idiopathic, or secondary to various causes, including reaction to different drugs, bacterial or viral infections, diseases of the connective tissue, radiation therapy, myelodysplastic syndrome and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection. We present a case of BOOP masquerading as severe persistent asthma. The patient's clinical presentation in the current study of subacute onset of cough and dyspnoea and her response to corticosteroid therapy were typical of BOOP. The most common chest radiograph abnormalities are those of diffuse, patchy, ground glass or alveolar densities. This disease usually runs a benign course, with complete response to steroid therapy in most patients. Chest CT scan is an important examination in the differential diagnosis of asthma.
