N2  - Introduction: In recent years, there has been a resurgence of tuberculosis (TB) in the United States as a result of immigration and the AIDS epidemic. We present a case of miliary TB presenting as dissemination to the colon. Case: 71-year-old male immigrant from India presented with generalized weakness, failure to thrive and a 10-lb weight loss over a six-month period. The patient denied hematemesis, melena or hematochezia. On review of systems, he reported early morning, productive cough. Laboratory data was remarkable for normocytic anemia with a hemoglobin level of 6.8 g/dL. Computed tomography scan showed numerous lung nodules in the right upper and middle lobes, colonic wall thickening at the hepatic flexure, and stranding surrounding the ileocecal area. Upper endoscopy was performed which did not identify a source of anemia. The patient then underwent colonoscopy which revealed large circumferential ulcerated areas of mucosa at the splenic and hepatic flexures with surrounding heaped-up mass-like lesions. The ileocecal valve appeared ulcerated and strictured; however, rest of the colonic and rectal mucosa appeared normal. Biopsies from all the three ulcerated sites showed necrotizing granulomas with acid-fast bacilli (AFB) consistent with mycobacterium species. Sputum smears were positive for AFB, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) was detected by PCR. The patient was diagnosed with miliary tuberculosis with dissemination to the colon. Discussion: The GI tract is an uncommon site for extrapulmonary TB. Any area of the GI tract can be involved; however, the ileocecal region is most commonly affected. The diagnosis of gastrointestinal TB (GI-TB) is difficult because clinical manifestations are nonspecific; and may include chronic abdominal pain or mass, weight loss, fever diarrhea, hematochezia, and obstruction. Endoscopy is the diagnostic modality of choice; however, it may be difficult to differentiate TB from Crohn's disease and malignancy endoscopically. Definitive diagnosis of GI-TB is established by identification of MTB in tissue by direct visualization, culture, or by a PCR assay. Treatment with standard antituberculosis medications is highly effective for GI-TB. In conclusion, diagnosis of GI-TB requires a high index of suspicion in high-risk populations including immigrants and immunosuppressed patients.
