N2  - INTRODUCTION: Pigs are monogastric omnivores. Of non-primate animals available for medical research they have most physiological similarity to humans. Important physiological differences need to be understood before data generated in pigs can be interpreted. Porcine models can be relatively cheap, allowing large numbers of experimental replicates to be included under rigorously controlled conditions, not possible with humans. Pigs can be electively necropsied in order to correlate clinical signs with pathological ones. Both porcine and human pneumonia are commonly caused by Mycoplasma spp., Haemophilus spp. and Streptococcus spp. with or without an initial viral infection. The organism encountered, transmission and clinical presentation is heavily influenced by environment, infection pressure and concurrent immune suppression. Porcine disease models provide means to correlate clinical signs with pathological measures at predetermined end points in the pathogenesis which is not possible with humans. MATERIALS AND METHODS: One hundred and fifty-eight pigs with severe respiratory disease were included. Their ante-mortem clinical data was correlated with pathological data and bacteriology when necropsy was performed at pre-determined stages of the disease course. Spearman's correlation coefficient was calculated and the significance of positive correlation has been set at the 5% level. RESULTS Clinical demeanour and respiratory scores were not found to correlate with the severity of pneumonic pathology occurring in the animal. The relationship between clinical demeanour score and rectal temperature was initially non- linear, however, once pigs become mildly ill approximated a linear relationship and the correlation became statistically significant (P = 0.05). Rectal temperature was positively associated with increasing lung consolidation (P = 0.02) and lung bodyweight ratio (P = 0.03). Cough scores were found to have little prognostic value and did not reliably reflect gross pathological changes. Respiratory scores showed positive association with lymph node size. Infection caused a decrease in individual feed and water intake. Clinical demeanour scores correlated well with respiratory scores (P = 0.001) and once pigs with mild or no clinical signs (scores < 1) were excluded there was a more linear relationship with rectal temperature. Respiratory and clinical scores correlated with lymph node enlargement (P = 0.02). Bacterial re-isolation correlated with lung lesions and lung/bodyweight ratios at 10 days (P = 0.05). Respiratory scores correlated with postmortem lung consolidation (P = 0.02) and rectal temperature (P = 0.02) but there was no significant direct association with bacteriological re-isolation. Lymphomegally of the thoracic and trachea-bronchial lymph nodes showed significant correlation with weight loss (P = 0.02). CONCLUSIONS Healthy pigs were often found to have rectal temperatures elevated above mild or moderately ill pigs. This was attributed to increased efforts to resist handling. Respiratory scores were correlated with post-mortem lung consolidation (P = 0.02) but not with bacteriological re-isolation. Virulence factors specific to an infecting microorganism determine the inflammatory pattern and the signs heard on auscultation. It is vital to understand the relationship between clinical signs and concurrent pathological changes before credible translation of outcomes between species can be made.
