N2  - Introduction: A third of all deaths in South Africa occur at home where the cause of death is often unknown. In a high antenatal HIV seroprevalence setting (29%) with extreme TB/HIV co-infection rates (>60%), we evaluated the prevalence of pulmonary infections in people dying at home. Methods: Adults dying of natural causes at home with no clear ante-mortem clinical diagnosis or recent (2 months) hospital admission were identified. A questionnaire was administered to a family member after obtaining consent for post-mortem examination. Bilateral Tru-cut® lung biopsies via the axilla and modified broncho- alveolar lavages (BAL)-through an anterior neck incision-were performed. Biopsies were examined histologically and submitted with BAL aspirates for mycobacterial culture and Xpert MTB/Rif. Results: Seventy-four families were approached. Fifty-five eligible decedents were included. All were Black and 23(43%) were women. The median age was 57.5 years; duration of symptoms was 63.5 days, most commonly cough. On histological evaluation of lung biopsies, 12 (22%) had mycobacterial granulomas, 10 of which were Zeihl Neelson positive. Of 11/12 histologically diagnosed mycobacterial cases were corroborated by culture, Xpert or PCR, which detected another eight cases, including three non-tuberculous mycobacteria. Other diagnoses (10 cases) were: Pneumocystis pneumonia, bacterial and interstitial pneumonia (adenovirus and rhinovirus isolated) and one small cell neuro-endocrine carcinoma. The remaining 33/55 cases showed no abnormalities. Conclusion: In this high HIV prevalence setting, 27/55 (49%) of home deaths had evidence of infectious pulmonary pathology, most of which was TB (36%). Whilst histology missed eight mycobacterial cases diagnosed by culture/xpert, it diagnosed important pathology not picked up by these modalities.
