N2  - Lung transplantation represents an effective treatment option for selected patients with end-stage lung disease. However, the main complication that significantly reduces long-term transplantation survival is obliterative bronchiolitis. 1 Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is highly prevalent in several lung diseases, including pulmonary fibrosis, systemic sclerosis, cystic fibrosis, COPD, and asthma.2 Furthermore, patients who undergo lung transplantation for end-stage lung disease are at increased risk of GERD: several clinical trials 3,4 have demonstrated proximal esophageal reflux to be present in about 60%-70% of patients after lung transplantation. Several mechanisms have been proposed: intraoperative vagal nerve injury, impaired cough reflex and mucociliary clearance, and delayed gastric emptying secondary to immunosuppressive drugs use.5 During the last decade, increasing data indicate GER as one of the main causes of bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS).6 Even though the mechanisms by which GERD may cause BOS are not well established, aspiration of gastric contents has been suggested to be a key factor in triggering an inflammatory injury to the transplanted organ. Several immunomodulating effects secondary to bile aspiration, including increased alveolar levels of neutrophils and lymphocytes, increased levels of some interleukines (IL-8, IL-1, IL-6, and IL-10) and tumor necrosis factor-α and β, reduced levels of surfactant proteins SP-A and SP-D, have been recently demonstrated.7 Furthermore, a significant association between bile and bacterial and fungal colonization in bronchoalveolar lavage have been reported.8 Finally, detectable pepsin levels in bronchoalveolar lavage liquid were found to be correlated with a quicker progression of BOS. 9 Recently, laparoscopic antireflux surgery (LARS) has been shown to be safe and effective in the treatment of patients with GERD after lung transplantation. While several studies 10,11 have reported on the positive impact of LARS on lung function, only few have objectively demonstrated its protective role against aspiration of pepsin and pulmonary immune changes after LARS in lung transplant patients.12,13 Based on preliminary data LARS seems to restore the physiologic inflammatory lung environment, by normalizing the percentage of neutrophils, lymphocytes and macrophages, and local levels of most proinflammatory chemokines. Nevertheless, further studies are needed to confirm these findings and identify the optimal timing of LARS that still remains controversial. In conclusion, because GERD is highly prevalent and it is an easily treatable risk factor for the onset and progression of BOS, all patients prior to lung transplantation should undergo a careful evaluation to rule out GERD. Reflux symptoms are often absent, in particular in patients on chronic therapy with proton pump inhibitors (PPIs); therefore, the diagnostic approach should be based on esophageal manometry, pH monitoring (24-H pH or pH-impedance studies), detection of pepsin in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and/or evidence of aspiration on trans bronchial biopsy.6 While LARS is increasingly gaining acceptance as a valid treatment option because it restores a physiologic antireflux barrier and stops GER, medical treatment with PPIs should be considered second-line therapy, as they affect the pH of the refluxate only, without reducing total number of reflux episodes, number of nonacid reflux episodes, and volume exposure or proximal extent of GER.
