N2  - Background: Children with lower respiratory tract infection (LRTI) often present with unspecific symptoms like fever, cough, and decreased nutritional intake. The usefulness of clinical and radiological findings for the decision to use anti-infective therapy is limited. Furthermore, due to high numbers of patients during the winter season the hygiene management in a hospital setting can be challenging. Thus, there is a great need to identify the causative pathogen of LRTIs. Methods: During an 8 week observation period in April and May 2012 a nasopharyngeal swab was taken from all children admitted to the pediatric department presenting with either signs of a respiratory tract infection or with fever of unknown origin. The sample was then analysed by a PCRbased multiplex assay that detects 15 viral and 6 bacterial pathogens known to be especially relevant with regard to community acquired LRTI. The overall positivity rate, the detection rate of the respective pathogens as well as the change during the observation time was analysed. Results: During the study period a total of 141 children were investigated by the multiplex PCR. The positivity rate per week ranged from 87 to 44% (mean 70%), decreasing from April to May. The mean frequency of single, double as well as triple pathogen detections was shown to be 58%, 11% and 1%, respectively. During April the RS-virus was the most prevalent pathogen, whereas during May Rhinovirus became the dominant pathogen detected. Other important viruses included Influenza, adenovirus and bocavirus. Other bacteria than Streptococcus pneumonia and Haemophilus influenza were not found during the observation period. Conclusions: The multiplex PCR used during this investigation proved to be robust and reliable. As expected RSV was one of the important pathogens detected in this cohort of hospitalized children with LRTI and FUO. Interestingly, Rhinovirus often considered to be of minor importance was found often during the second part of the study period. The high frequency of viral pathogens as well as the low detection rate of bacteria like Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydophila pneumonia might help to guide antibiotic usage. The broad pathogen coverage efficiently supported the appropriate hygiene management of the children.
