N2  - Introduction 3 year old Burmese male referred for abnormal chest X-ray in setting of constipation and abdominal pain. Case Description Burmese child referred to pulmonary clinic for abnormal chest X-ray during evaluation of constipation and abdominal pain. Work up notable for a large loculated pleural effusion identified as paragonimiasis. This case highlights issues concerning pulmonary parasitology, including hydatid disease of childhood due to echinococcosis and paragonimiasis. CC Constipation and abdominal pain HPI 3 year old Burmese male recently immigrated to the US presents for evaluation of an abnormal chest film during the evaluation of constipation and abdominal pain. History notable for 3 years of intermittent abdominal pain, irregular bowel movements and poor appetite and weight gain. With exception of occasional sore throat, the patient had no respiratory symptoms including shortness of breath, wheezing and cough. No previous treatment received. ROS Per HPI, otherwise Negative Immunizations Unknown Medications Acetaminophen Past Medical History Occasional respiratory viral illnesses Family History Paternal grandmother with “lung problems” Siblings treated for + PPD, patient not treated Social History Burmese, Immigrated to U.S. 12/2011 Lives with mother, father, 2 siblings No smoking Physical Exam Weight: <3rd %-ile VS: Normal Positive Findings: Soft systolic decrescendo murmur at left sternal border Shotty, bilateral, cervical lymphadenopathy Imaging Chest X-Ray: large soft tissue density located at the right base extending to the fourth rib. Ultrasound: large, unilocular, subpulmonic fluid collection with small, floating echogenic foci and large tubular structures layered dependently. Chest CT: large, loculated right-sided pleural effusion with hyper attenuating material layered dependently. Labs and Studies Bloodwork: peripheral eosinophilia, HIV negative, PPD+ Sputum: + MAC, Echinococcus IgG 1.016 +, Paragonimus ELISA + Surgical Pathology from video-assisted thorascopic surgery(VATS): necrotic cellular debris in proteinaceous transudate Pleural Fluid: bacterial, fungal, AFB and parasite stains all negative, LDH 2191, protein 7.640, glucose <5, Cytology: 60 WBC, 8% eos, 5% L, 88% monos Differential Diagnosis Tuberculosis, Echinococcosis, Paragonimiasis Discussion Hydatid disease of childhood with emphasis on echinococcosis and paragonimiasis including epidemiology abroad and in the US, types of human infection, the parasitic life-cycles and pathophysiology can be discussed. In addition, the rarity of these diseases in the US, the increased incidence with immigration as well as signs and symptoms of each infection, physical exam findings, suggested imaging and laboratory testing, and medical and surgical treatment options can be examined. Patient Outcome Treatment with praziquantel and albendazole. Resolution of eosinophilia and constipation with an improving chest film.
