N2  - Background: Pulmonary fibrosis is a known consequence of pulmonary tuberculosis (TB). Despite its clinical relevance, the mechanism of the pulmonary fibrosis in TB has not been described. Fibrocytes, monocytic cells of bone marrow origin that express matrix proteins including collagen-I and collagen-III, have been implicated in non-infectious fibrosis including bleomycin-induced pulmonary fibrosis in mice. Since monocytic cells are central to the immune pathology of TB, we hypothesized that fibrocytes contribute to pulmonary fibrosis in TB. Diabetes mellitus increases the risk of TB by ∼ 3 fold as well as TB severity based on radiographic extent of disease, and thus a ∼ 4 fold higher hazard of death. We hypothesized that diabetes increases the fibrotic response in TB disease, leading to pathological remodeling. Methods: C57BL/6 mice were made diabetic with streptozotocin and infected with a low aerosol dose of Mycobacterium tuberculosis Erdman. Pulmonary fibrosis was assessed by trichrome staining of paraffin-embedded lung sections from diabetic and non-diabetic mice with TB. Fibrocytes were quantified in peripheral blood and isolated whole lung leukocytes by flow cytometry with surface staining for CD45 and CD11b, plus intracellular staining for Collagen-I. Results: Trichrome staining of lung sections taken after 12 weeks of TB disease demonstrated significantly more collagen deposition in diabetic as compared to non-diabetic mice. Fibrocytes comprised 1.9% of CD45+ CD11b+ lung leukocytes in uninfected mice. In non-diabetic mice with 9 weeks of TB disease, fibrocytes comprised 1.8% of CD45+ CD11b+ lung leukocytes and 7.0% of peripheral blood leukocytes. In diabetic mice with 9 weeks of TB disease, fibrocytes comprised 3.0% of CD45+ CD11b+ lung leukocytes and 21% in peripheral blood. Conclusion: Our data support the hypothesis that diabetes is associated with increased pulmonary fibrosis in TB, and suggest that fibrocytes may play a role in this potentially damaging repair process. Fibrosis could be host-protective in limited TB disease by walling off granulomas, but in extensive and poorly controlled disease (as occurs in diabetes and with drug-resistant TB) fibrosis is a major factor contributing to morbidity and mortality. Combining antimicrobial therapy with adjuvant designed to inhibit fibrosis could improve outcomes in high risk patients. Our findings implicate diabetes as such a risk factor and suggest that targeted intervention directed against fibrocytes might limit damaging fibrosis in TB.
