N2  - Rationale: Development of antibiotic resistance and toxicity are the two major problems associated with the frequent, high-dose administration of intravenous antibiotics used for the treatment of chronically infected cystic fibrosis patients. In order to improve drug efficacy and patient compliance while minimizing the risk of toxicity, we have developed nebulizable nanoparticles capable of providing a sustained delivery of novel silver carbene complex (SCC) type antimicrobials. We have demonstrated the antimicrobial efficacy of these silver-loaded nanoparticle formulations both in vitro and in vivo in a murine acute lung infection model. Methods: Nanoparticle formulations (shell crosslinked knedel-like (SCKs) and acetalated-dextran (Ac-Dex)) were produced by standard procedures and characterized for their morphology, size distribution, silver loading, and silver release. In vitro antimicrobial efficacy was investigated by performing growth curves and determination of minimum inhibitory/bactericidal concentration (MIC/MBC) against a panel of clinical bacterial strains. To perform mouse infection model experiments, C57/BL6 mice were intranasally inoculated with 106 CFU of a mucoid, clinical strain of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA M57-15) and treated in a nose-only fashion with various nanoparticle formulations. In addition to survival, weight loss and clinical scores were also monitored. Results: Both SCK and Ac-Dex nanoparticles had a size distribution between 50-200 nm. All nanoparticle formulations exhibited a sustained release of silver and in case of the SCK nanoparticles, the silver release rate was found to depend on the distribution of silver within the particle (hydrophilic shell versus hydrophobic core). Both SCK and Ac-Dex nanoparticles inhibited the growth of bacteria under in vitro conditions; however, the nanoparticles showed higher MICs and MBCs than the free drug due to the sustained release rates of silver from the particles. In vivo studies demonstrated a 40% survival advantage and better clinical scores for animals treated with a single dose of nebulized core loaded SCC10-SCK nanoparticles compared with all other treatments including shell loaded SCK nanoparticle formulations (AgNO3-SCK and AgCOOCH3-SCK). Surprisingly, almost a 100 fold higher dose of nebulized un-encapsulated SCC1 delivered over 5 separate doses is required to achieve a similar survival outcome. Additionally, dual-loaded nanoparticles (SCC10-AgNO3-SCKs) did not improve the survival advantage compared with core-only loaded nanoparticles. In vivo studies with Ac-Dex nanoparticles are currently underway and preliminary results demonstrate efficacy similar to SCC10-SCK nanoparticles. Conclusions: Nebulized nanoparticle formulations capable of providing a sustained delivery of SCCs may provide an effective method for the treatment of pulmonary infections while minimizing toxicity and improving patient compliance.
