N2  - Rationale: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is the 3rd leading cause of death worldwide and mortality's increasing. Susceptibility to smoke appears variable and underlying genetic explanations remain unclear. Mutations in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator (CFTR) gene are implicated in a variety of non-CF related diseases. CFTR dysfunction has been implicated as a contributor to COPD pathogenesis, as cigarette smoke has been shown to inhibit CFTR activity in vitro and in vivo. As chronic bronchitis (CB) shares many pathologic similarities with CF including mucus hypersecretion, impaired mucociliary clearance and bacterial colonization, it follows that CFTR mutations may be associated with this phenotype. We hypothesized that Caucasian COPD patients with CB have a higher rate of CFTR mutations compared to the general Caucasian population. Methods: We performed a case control study to detect the prevalence of CFTR mutations in COPD patients with CB symptoms. COPD cases were selected from participants in the NIH COPD Clinical Research Network's Azithromycin in COPD Study who had blood stored for genetic analysis. Cases were Caucasians, age 40 to 80 with FEV1/FVC < 0.70, an FEV1 < 60% predicted and CB defined by productive cough amost days a week on the St. George Respiratory Questionnaire. The control population was 12,700 women who had CFTR analyzed for prenatal genetic testing. DNA testing was performed by the Baylor College of Medicine Genetics Laboratory using a CFTR related disorders mutation panel. This is an allele-specific genotyping technique performed by MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry and includes testing for mutations common in human populations and reflex testing of the 5T allele. Additional data collected on cases included age, sex, smoking status & pack-years, FEV1 %, and number of exacerbations in the last year. The sample size of 127 patients was 80% powered to detect a mutation rate in COPD patients of 10.5% (alpha 0.05). Results: The case patient characteristics are in Table 1. Five of the 127 (3.9%) case patients were heterozygous for CFTR mutations which was not significantly different from the control population (4.5%) (p=0.92). Conclusion: CFTR mutations weren't found to be more common in COPD patients with moderate to severe disease and CB compared to controls. CFTR mutations don't appear to increase the risk of COPD despite clear evidence that cigarette smoke reduces CFTR mediated anion transport. This suggests acquired CFTR deficiency is the principle contributor to the ion transport phenotype and is not affected by the presence of heterozygosity. (Table Presented) .
