N2  - Rationale: Pseudomonas aeruginosa causes chronic infections of the respiratory tract, particularly in cystic fibrosis (CF). The outer membrane protein F (OprF) of P. aeruginosa is a promising vaccine candidate; our prior studies have shown that mucosal immunization with the non-human primate AdC7OprF vector expressing a secreted form of OprF elicits robust and superior mucosal and protective immunity against P. aeruginosa compared to immunization with a human Ad5OprF vector. The present study focuses on the development of a 2nd generation AdC7 vaccine which has the RGD sequence incorporated in the Ad fiber (AdC7OprF.RGD) to enhance gene delivery to antigen presenting cells. Methods: AdC7OprF, AdC7OprF.RGD, Ad5OprF and Ad5OprF.RGD (each 1010 particle units) were administered to the respiratory mucosa of C57Bl/6 mice via the intratracheal route; systemic and lung anti-OprF IgG, as well as T cell responses, were analyzed up to 12 wk. Protective immunity was determined by quantification of P. aeruginosa in lungs following pulmonary challenge with agar-encapsulated P. aeruginosa (2x106 cfu). Results: Serum anti-OprF IgG was similar at 8 and 12 wk following immunization with AdC7OprF.RGD compared to AdC7OprF (p>0.08, all comparisons), but was up to 10-fold higher compared to immunization with Ad5OprF or Ad5OprF.RGD (p<0.01, all comparisons). To analyze cellular anti-OprF responses, splenic and pulmonary CD3+ T cells were isolated 8 wk following immunization and stimulated with either syngeneic splenic or pulmonary DC pulsed with OprF. Intracellular anti-OprF Th1 (IL-12, IL-17) and Th2 (IL-4, IL-5) cytokines in splenic and pulmonary CD4 T cells of mice immunized with AdC7OprF.RGD were similar to those immunized with AdC7OprF (p>0.06, all comparisons). Likewise, Th1 INF-γ responses in CD8 T cells from spleen and lung were similar following immunization with AdC7OpF or AdC7OprF.RGD. In contrast, immunization with Ad5OprF.RGD elicited an increased anti-OprF Th1 response compared to Ad5OprF. Interestingly, only immunization with AdC7OprF.RGD and AdC7OprF enhanced clearance of P. aeruginosa from the lungs after 12 wk, with AdC7OprF.RGD > AdC7OprF (p<0.01). Conclusions: These data suggest that addition of RGD to the fiber of an AdC7-based vector expressing OprF enhances longterm protective immunity against P. aeruginosa following mucosal immunization.
