N2  - INTRODUCTION: Common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) is the most common primary immunodeficiency diagnosed in adults. Pulmonary symptoms are common although under recognized, thus resulting in significant morbidity to these patients. Understanding the presentation and prevalence of respiratory manifestations among Manitoba population of CVID patients would be useful to improve clinical care. METHODS: A retrospective chart review identified 47 patients with a diagnosis of CVID. Data extracted included: documentation of respiratory complications at presentation and throughout the clinical course, measurement of lung function and abnormalities on thoracic computerized tomography (CT). We noted CT scan findings of nodules, bronchiectasis, lymphadenopathy, emphysema and consolidation documented as present or absent, as per the reviewing radiologist's final report. RESULTS: Of 47 patients, 28 (60%) had pre-diagnosis symptoms/complication recorded. These included: recurrent sinus infections (50%), recurrent pneumonia (39%), asthma (18%), chronic cough (21%), recurrent otitis media (14%), and bronchiectasis (14%). Many patients had more than one presenting symptoms. During the follow up additional 11 patients developed respiratory complications. These were: recurrent pneumonia (59%), asthma-like symptoms (36%), and symptomatic bronchiectasis (21%). Less common pulmonary manifestations included: eosinophilic pneumonia, cryptogenic organizing pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, and Mycobacterium avium infection. Lung function tests demonstrated mean FEV1 at 75±22.2 percent predicted, mean FVC at 82±17.8 % percent predicted, and mean FEV1/FVC ratio at 74±14.2%. Of 47 patients, 28 had a computerized tomography scan of their chest available for review. The findings included: bronchiectasis (61%), at least 1 pulmonary nodule (50%), mucous plugging (14%), small airway disease or tree-in-bud nodularity (14%), mediastinal or hilar lymphadenopathy (21%), airspace disease (18%), and emphysema (18%). CONCLUSIONS: Approximately 60% in our study population of patients with CVID presented with respiratory manifestations prior to diagnosis. Subsequent to the diagnosis, a majority developed respiratory symptoms or complications over the 3 year follow-up period. The predominant respiratory manifestations included recurrent pneumonia, asthma and bronchiectasis. However, pulmonary function tests performed at presentation were within normal range. The imaging of patients at presentation and follow-up showed significant abnormalities on CT scan such as high prevalence of bronchiectasis which was not apparent clinically. Our study highlights that patients with CVID present with non-specific and divergent respiratory involvement; high index of suspicion for an undiagnosed CVID is crucial for accurate diagnosis and appropriate management.
