N2  - Rationale: Routine culture of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) for tuberculosis has been recommended for countries with medium to high tuberculosis prevalence. In low prevalence countries the value of this practice is less clear. Therefore, we aimed to evaluate the value of routine BALF cultures for mycobacteria. Methods: This was an observational retrospective study in a tertiary care hospital. Currently it is standard of care to collect BALF for tuberculosis culture during bronchoscopy if any microbiological testing is ordered even if tuberculosis is not suspected. We retrieved all patients from our hospital information system who underwent bronchoscopy with BALF and had their BALF tested for tuberculosis culture during a period of 18 months. We collected clinical, serological and radiological data. Results: 416 males and 224 females were included into the study. Mean age was 57±16 years, 45% were considered to be immunosuppressed. Mycobacteria were detected in 16 cases (2.5%): 12 patients with Mycobacterium tuberculosis (1.9%) and 4 with nontuberculous mycobacteria (0.6%). Cough was the only sign that was more frequent in BALF culture positive patients (75 vs 34%). Other clinical and radiological factors did not discriminate between BALF culture positive and negative patients. The interferon-gamma-release assay (IGRA) was positive in 9 out of 10 tested patients with BALF positive cultures. Conclusion: Routine BALF culture for tuberculosis appear to be inappropriate in a low prevalence setting. IGRA may help clinicians in the decision to perform BALF culture for mycobacteria.
