N2  - Rationale: Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is characterized by recurrent respiratory infections and progressive lung damage. Early detection and vigorous treatment of airway infections is important in preserving lung function. Respiratory pathogens in CF are well known and have a clear age specific distribution. In contrast, data on respiratory microbiology in PCD patients are scarce (Li et al. 2005 ERJ). Identifying the key respiratory pathogens may help guide treatment in these patients. Methods: We performed a retrospective analysis of respiratory cultures in PCD patients of the VU University Medical Center and the Waterland hospital in 2010. Results of sputum, broncho-alveolar lavage and cough swab cultures were reviewed. Prevalence and age specific distribution of respiratory pathogens were assessed. The Fisher's exact test was used to determine differences in prevalence between children and adults. Results: Forty-five PCD patients were included, 31 children (mean age ± SD, 8.7 ± 3.9) and 14 adults (40.3 ± 14.3). Hundred and eighty respiratory cultures were performed (mean 4.0 per patient, range 1-13) and 102 cultures (56.7%) were positive for one or more bacterial pathogens. The most common pathogens isolated were H. influenzae (in 66.7 % of patients), S. aureus (28.9%), M. catarrhalis (26.7%), S. pneumoniae (24.4%), P. aeruginosa (20.0%) and S. maltophilia (8.9%). No significant differences between children and adults were found. The prevalence of P. aeruginosa was higher in adult than in PCD patients (35.7% versus 12.9%), however this difference did not reach statistical significance (p 0.11). H. influenzae remains the most common pathogen in adult PCD patients. Conclusion: H. influenzae was the most common respiratory pathogen in patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia. Our data may suggest an increasing incidence of P. aeruginosa infections with age, however to a lesser extent than reported in CF patients. This difference is probably related to differences in pathofysiology and the generally slower decline in lung function observed in PCD patients. These findings should be confirmed and further investigated in a longitudinal dataset.
