N2  - Background: Little is known about the relationship between early childhood wheezing and lung function in children with CF. We hypothesized that wheezing in early life is associated with lower lung function at age 6-7 years. Methods: We used data from the Epidemiologic Study of CF, a longitudinal observational study from 1994-2005. We studied children whose wheezing status was documented at least annually until age 6 years and who had spirometry recorded between 6-7 years. Wheezing phenotypes were defined as: no wheezing in the first 6 years of life (NW), transient wheezing (TW; wheezing <3 years old, but no wheezing after 3), late wheezing (LW; wheezing only after age 3 years), and persistent wheezing (PW; wheezing both before and after age 3 years). The best FEV1 percent predicted between ages 6-7 was chosen. Wheezing phenotype was related using chi-square analysis to demographic and clinical characteristics and using Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel to early acquisition of bacterial pathogens (positive culture at age 0-2 years). We used analysis of variance to relate lung function to wheezing phenotype. Results: 1302 children met the cohort inclusion criteria; 61% wheezed in the first 6 years of life. Male sex was significantly associated with wheezing of any type (P=.002). Sinusitis was significantly associated with PW (P=.004). The proportion of children with early Pseudomonas aeruginosa acquisition increased significantly from the NW to TW to PW groups (P=.007). Daily cough, daily sputum, and crackles were more likely to be present at some point before age 6 years in PW than NW (p<.01). CF children with NW had higher lung function at age 6-7 years than each of the other phenotypes (P<.05), with PW having the lowest lung function (Table). Conclusions: Childhood wheezing in CF is common and is associated with lower lung function at age 6-7 years. Patients with persistent wheeze have the lowest lung function. The prevalence of wheezing was higher than in non-CF children (Martinez et al. NEJM 1995), but the relationship between wheezing phenotype and lung function at age 6-7 years was similar to that in non-CF children (Morgan et al. AJRCCM 2005). Differences in clinical features of CF lung disease among wheezing phenotypes may reflect pathophysiologic mechanisms common to all children. (Table Presented).
