N2  - Fistula formation between mediastinal structures is commonly associated with malignancy in immunocompetent patients. Sometimes, however, another interesting etiology is discovered. A 63 year old Hispanic woman whose past medical history included only non-insulin dependent diabetes presented to the emergency department after 6 months of wheezing that failed to improve with outpatient asthma therapies. She reported new dyspnea over the previous week, several weeks of productive cough, subjective fevers, chills, and night sweats, as well as 30 pounds of weight loss. Additionally, she reported dysphagia for several years. She denied hemoptysis, chest pain, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, or edema, and she had no known tuberculosis exposure. Her family history included diabetes. She emigrated from Mexico 20 years ago and frequently traveled there when not working at a local candy factory. She had been a lifetime non-smoker without illicit drug use. Her physical exam revealed a well-developed woman who was afebrile without hypoxia on ambient air, with mild tachypnea, inspiratory and expiratory wheezing, and normal cardiovascular findings without lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. Basic labs were normal as was the initial chest x-ray, and testing for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) was negative. A CT of the neck and chest was performed to evaluate for any anatomical airway abnormalities and revealed the presence of a fistula between the right and left mainstem bronchi without lymphadenopathy (Figure 1). A bronchoscopy was performed that revealed fistulous orifices in both mainstem bronchi just distal to the carina that were filled with mucus and necrotic debris, and 2+ acid fast bacilli (AFB) were present on smear with Mycobacterium tuberculosis present on culture from the wash. Antituberculous therapy was initiated, and a barium swallow demonstrated only esophageal dysmotility explaining her chronic dysphagia while not revealing the presence of a tracheoesophageal fistula. At a 2 month follow-up the patient's symptoms had resolved, radiographic improvement was noted on repeat CT scan, and her AFB sputum cultures were negative (Figure 2). Involvement of the tracheobronchial tree, esophagus, great vessels, mediastinal space, or lymph nodes have all been described in fistula formation associated with M. tuberculosis, often in immunocompromised states such as HIV or solid organ transplantation. This is a unique case of a broncho-bronchial fistula between the mainstem bronchi in an immunocompetent patient due to M. tuberculosis, and it is the first reported case involving both sides of the tracheobronchial tree, also being successfully treated with antituberculous therapy. (Figure Presented).
