N2  - Introduction: Pneumatoceles are thin-walled, air-filled cystic lesions in the lungs generally observed soon after the development of pneumonia. They have been described most often as a complication of staphylococcal pneumonia in infants and children. However, pneumatoceles are also caused by other agents, including Streptoccus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenza, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, or by trauma and mechanical ventilation. Description: A 24-year-old male, with a history of lupus on prednisone presented with acute onset chest pain and shortness of breath after traveling on a long bus trip. The CTA scan showed acute pulmonary embolism in the right lower lobe. He was admitted and started on heparin drip. He also was found to be in acute lupus flare with elevated anti-DS DNA and marked proteinuria for which his prednisone was increased. His symptoms gradually improved and he was discharged home on coumadin. Two weeks later, he was readmitted with nonproductive cough, chills and right knee pain and swelling. A CXR showed a right lower lobe cavity. A subsequent CT scan confirmed the cavity measuring 5.9 x 8 cm with a small air-fluid level which was concerning for lung abscess or pneumatocele. He underwent arthrocentesis to rule out septic arthritis. His blood and synovial fluid grew Haemophilus influenzae for which he was started on ceftriaxone. The echocardiogram was negative for endocarditis. He remained clinically stable and afebrile without leukocytosis. No drainage or surgical procedure for the large lung cavity was performed and he was discharged home on amoxicillin/clavulanate. The serial CXRs in follow-up visits showed a gradual decrease in size of the cavity with complete resolution after 6 months. Discussion: A pulmonary pneumatocele can occur in association with acute pneumonia in infants and young children, and is almost transient as the lesions generally resolve spontaneously and completely without sequelae. Pneumatoceles are infrequent findings in adults with pneumonia. The usual medical care consists of the treatment of the underlying pneumonia, but pneumothorax and infected symptomatic pneumatoceles may require surgical intervention or catheter drainage. Our patient was a young male with lupus flare who had an acute pulmonary embolism. Subsequently he developed pneumonia complicated by pneumatocele formation, bacteriemia, and septic arthritis. His pneumatocele caused mild respiratory symptoms and resolved spontaneously. Pneumatoceles should be considered as a differential diagnosis for cavitary lung diseases. (Figure Presented).
