N2  - Rationale: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is an inflammatory airway and lung disease. Its underlying process is chronic inflammation, mainly due to tobacco smoke exposure and the presence of genetic factors. Three sub-phenotypes of COPD are identified based on clinical and pathological characteristics and the localization of the inflammation in the lungs: small airways disease (SAD, airways ø < 2mm), emphysema (parenchyma) and chronic bronchitis (CB, larger airways). These sub-phenotypes are present in different combinations in COPD-patients. In COPD, SAD and emphysema are predominantly responsible for the extent of airway obstruction. CB - characterized by chronic cough and/or chronic mucus hypersecretion (CMH) - is less directly associated with airway obstruction but constitutes a risk for more rapid lung function decline, hospitalization and mortality in COPD. We hypothesized that apart from the well known genes that are associated with development of COPD, additional genes will contribute to CMH. Methods: To compare the genome of COPD-patients (FEV1/FVC <0.70) with and without CMH, we conducted a genome wide association study (GWAs) in COPD-subjects - all males, 335 CMH-cases and 556 controls - of the Nelson study (a population-based lung cancer screening study from Groningen and Utrecht, the Netherlands). Data were analyzed using PLINK with correction for center and smoking. Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNP) showing strong associations (p-value <10-4) were evaluated by replication in 1080 COPD-subjects - male and female, 110 CMH cases and 970 controls - of the LifeLines cohort (a population based biobank from the Northern part of The Netherlands). Data were similarly analyzed with additional correction for sex. Subsequently, results of both cohorts were meta-analyzed. Results: There were no associations at the level of <10-7 in the NELSON population. We found 39 SNPs associated with CMH with p-values <10-4. Among those many were associated with genes related to tobacco use disorders. However, we could not replicate these SNPs in LifeLines where p-values were less significant. Conclusions: Data from this study suggest that CMH in patients with COPD is not genetically determined, which may be due to insufficient power of our study. However, an alternative explanation may be that the association of CMH with lung function decline in COPD may result from an additional environmental factor like increased bacterial colonization with CMH and a subsequent increase in exacerbation rate that is by itself linked to accelerated lung function loss.
