N2  - RATIONALE: Bronchiectasis is a respiratory disease characterized by chronic productive cough and infectious exacerbations. To evaluate the impact of the disease on health-related quality of life (HRQoL), tools such as the St George's Respiratory Questionnaire, designed for COPD, and the Leicester Cough Questionnaire, designed for chronic cough, have been used in patients with bronchiectasis. However, these are not bronchiectasis-specific and do not meet the recent FDA Guidance on Patient-Reported Outcomes (PROs; FDA, 2009). A novel bronchiectasis-specific PRO, Quality of Life Questionnaire - Bronchiectasis (QOL-B) was recently developed and validated by Quittner et al (Am J Respir Crit Care Med 179; 2009: A4310). We intend to use QOL-B to assess the changes in HRQoL in patients with bronchiectasis over a 5 year period, and correlates thereof. In this abstract, we present results obtained after one year of implementation. DESIGN: A prospective observational analysis of adult patients with bronchiectasis, who completed 2 QOL-Bs between 2009 and 2011, was performed. For 17 (89%) of these patients, the baseline QOL-B questionnaire was performed during their first visit to the Bronchiectasis Center. Responses to the 36-item questionnaire formed 8 independent scales: Physical Functioning, Role Functioning, Vitality, Emotional Functioning, Social Functioning, Treatment Burden, Health Perceptions and Respiratory Symptoms. A scaled score was calculated for each domain, ranging from 0 (worst possible score) to 100 (best possible score). The minimal importance difference for the Respiratory Symptoms Domain is 8. Paired t-test was used to calculate the differences. RESULTS: Baseline characteristics of the 19 patients who were followed over a mean period of 11.2 months are noted in Table 1. Common etiologies included: idiopathic (36%), alpha 1-antitrypsin deficiency (21%) and Mycobacterium avium intracellulare (15%). Nine patients (47%) were colonized with Gram negative rods at baseline. Various therapies including mucus clearance regimens, hypertonic saline and chronic azithromycin were instituted. On the 1st administration, 10 patients (52%) reported their health as being Good/Excellent as compared with 12 patients (63%) on 2nd QOL-B. Data obtained from the two measurements are depicted in Table 2. After 1 year of follow up, no patients reported worsening of their functioning and there was a statistically significant improvement in mean scores of four domains, as well as an improvement greater than the minimal important difference in the Respiratory Symptoms Domain. CONCLUSION: Treatment at a dedicated Bronchiectasis Center was associated with improved HRQoL in patients with bronchiectasis over an approximately one year period. (Table Presented).
