N2  - Introduction: Janus Kinase (JAK)-1 and JAK-2 initiate signal transduction pathways important to the hematopoetic and immune systems. Oral inhibitors of JAK-1 and JAK-2 are in phase III trials as treatment for myelofibrosis and may see broader application in a variety of myeloproliferative disorders. As a new drug class, there is limited information about possible adverse effects. We present a case of cryptococcal pneumonia associated with the use of ruxolitinib. Case: A 66 year-old man with history of polycythemia vera treated for the last 18 months with ruxolitinib was referred for evaluation of 6 weeks of dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and fevers not responsive to several courses of oral antibiotics. A recent prednisone taper resulted in marginal improvement. His social history was negative for unusual exposures including birds. Physical exam was unremarkable. A non-contrast chest computed tomography scan demonstrated multiple bilateral nodules with a slightly upper lobe and bronchovascular distribution (image 1). The largest nodule measured 10 mm and was minimally cavitated (image 2). Fungal cultures obtained via bronchoalveolar lavage of the anterior segment of the right upper lobe grew Cryptococcus neoformans. In addition, his serum cryptococcal antigen was positive with a high reactive titer (1:4). Discussion: Cryptococcus is an encapsulated yeast-like fungus found in bird droppings and soil. Cryptococcal infections are typically described in immunosuppressed patients and immunocompetent patients with heavy exposure to pigeon droppings. As an intracellular pathogen, cell-mediated immunity is most effective for controlling cryptococcal infections. Prior to the onset of his symptoms, our patient had no known impairment of his cellular immunity. Ruxolitinib reduces levels of interleukin 2, interleukin 6, interferon-gamma and tumor necrosis factor, all cytokines important in promoting cell-mediated immunity. A phase I/II clinical trial of ruxolitinib for myelofibrosis reported pneumonia in 10 of 153 subjects, only one of which was attributed to drug. We hypothesize ruxolitinib was responsible for the pathogenesis of cryptococcal pneumonia in our patient. With more experience using ruxolitinib, including data from ongoing phase III clinical trials, risk of opportunistic pulmonary infections may become a recognized adverse effect of this new class of medication. (Figure Presented).
