N2  - We describe a unique case of Mycobacterium Riyadhense, a non-tubercular mycobacterium, causing pulmonary disease. A 29 year old man, originally from Saudi Arabia, presented with complaints of weight loss, productive cough, and night sweats. A chest X-ray revealed a right upper lobe infiltrate, and he was treated with levofloxacin empirically. Although he initially improved, his symptoms progressed and three months later he returned for further evaluation. A chest X-ray now revealed bilateral upper lobe infiltrates and he was again treated with levofloxacin. He later had a CT chest which revealed a cavitary lesion in the right upper lobe and bilateral bronchiectasis. His sputum was positive for Acid-fast bacilli and a PPD was positive with a reaction of 25mm. A Quantiferon-TB gold test was also positive. Mycobacterium cultures were negative for M Tuberculosis complex by DNA probe and M Avium. He was empirically prescribed isoniazid 300 mg daily, rifampin 600 mg daily, pyrazinamide 1000 mg daily, and ethambutol 900 mg daily. Culture data identified M Riyadhense based on 16S ribosomal DNA sequencing. The patients symptoms improved and his radiographic abnormalities also improved during the course of treatment. His sputum cultures converted to negative for M Riyadhense after 4 months of treatment. Treatment with isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol was continued for 1 year following the conversion of his sputum cultures. Clinically his symptoms completely resolved. Mycobacterium Riyadhense was first described in Saudi Arabia after being isolated out of the maxillary sinus of a 19 year old man. It was initially identified as M Tuberculosis based on DNA probe. Subsequent analysis showed that this strain had distinct differences from M Tuberculosis. The patient was treated with 9 months of anti-tuberculosis treatment and improved. M Riyadhense is described as a non-chromogenic slow growing organism that produces rough white colonies after 28 days of incubation at 36 degrees Celsius and it shares RD1 region with esat-6 and cfp-10 genes with M tuberculosis. Phylogeneticlly, it is similar to M. kansasii and M. szulgai. Unlike Van Ingen's first reported case, this isolate was sensitive to amikacin and resistant to cycloserine. Our isolate was also resistant to levofloxacin, presumably due to use of levofloxacin as a single agent treatment prior to identification of the Mycobacterium. To our knowledge, this is the first case of M. Riyadhense causing pulmonary disease. Clinical resolution of the infection was achieved with treatment after 16 months of isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol.
