N2  - Introduction: Endobronchial tuberculosis (EBTB) has become a rare disease entity in developed counties. Given nonspecific symptoms and radiological findings in addition to low yield on routine mycobacterial staining, the diagnosis is delayed until bronchoscopic samples are obtained. We evaluated a case of unsuspected EBTB with an unusual presentation. Case Description: A 80 year female with remote cigarette smoking history was evaluated for progressive dry hacking cough over 9 months with gradual decline in exercise tolerance. She had lost 18 pounds with recent night sweats but no fever or chills. She previously worked as a nurse with prior negative purified protein derivative (PPD) test and denied occupational exposures or travel. The past medical history includes rheumatic fever. She had been treated for asthma with no significant improvement. Physical examination revealed no cervical or axillary lymphadenopathy but diffuse inspiratory and expiratory wheezing with a right lung basilar pleural rub. Chest radiograph showed right lower lobe consolidation with patchy nodularity. A thoracic computed tomography (CT) revealed significant narrowing of right middle lobe bronchus with associated atelectasis and right lower lobe consolidation with innumerable pulmonary nodules. Bronchoscopy demonstrated an endobronchial lesion in the bronchus intermedius causing approximately 50% obstruction and necrotic debris obstructing right middle and lower lobe bronchi. Endobronchial biopsies revealed granuloma formation with multinucleated giant cells and acid fast bacilli with positive mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR. Directly observed antituberculous drug therapy with rifampin, INH, pyrazinamide and ethambutol was initiated. Follow up CT after two months of therapy showed interval improvement in lower lobe consolidation and reduced bronchial narrowing. Given prior endoscopic findings, she underwent repeat bronchoscopy. The endobronchial lesion was reduced in size with minimal surrounding scarring. All distal airways were identifiable with reduced necrosis. She continues on conventional oral antituberculosis therapy. Discussion: We present a case of EBTB that presented as a mass lesion and resolved with conventional antituberculous therapy. EBTB may involve any part of tracheobronchial tree and the precise incidence of endobronchial lesions in tuberculous patients is underestimated given infrequent use of bronchoscopy. Early diagnosis is more difficult given nonspecific symptoms and lack of typical chest radiographic findings. EBTB can lead to subsequent complications including atelectasis, bronchiectasis and bronchial stenosis. Alternate treatments including steroids and inhaled therapies have been used to prevent complications of EBTB but their precise role remains to be defined. Stenotic complications have been treated with interventional bronchoscopic techniques. (Figure Presented).
