N2  - Rationale: Culture-independent microbiological techniques, such as 16S rRNA gene sequencing, detect a greater range of bacteria from specimens compared to standard culture techniques. This may be important in patients with bronchiectasis, where traditional cultures fail to yield microbial targets. Detection of uncultivable microorganisms, and the response of the microbiome as a whole to treatment may be of relevance in the pathogenesis, progression and management of respiratory disease. To investigate this we have serially assessed microbial communities in sputum over 6 months. Methods: 85 consecutive patients with non-CF bronchiectasis and a productive cough were recruited from the outpatient department at the Royal Brompton Hospital between December 2010 - May 2011. Fresh, paired sputum samples were collected monthly over 6 months for standard culture and 16S rRNA gene sequencing. Patients completed daily symptom and treatment diaries and had monthly spirometry. DNA was extracted from sputum and the 16S rRNA gene amplified using universal PCR primers. These amplicons were sequenced using a Roche 454 GS FLX Titanium pyrosequencer. Reads were analysed using the QIIME pipeline (Quantitative insights into microbial ecology). Results: The first 14 patients to complete the study included 10 with known Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonization. Idiopathic (5 patients) and post-infective (4 patients) were the most common aetiologies. 10 patients had exacerbations requiring antibiotics during the 6 month study. The FEV1 ranged from 21-100% predicted (mean 61%+/-23.3). Analysis of 16S rRNA gene sequences indicated that 119 different OTUs (operational taxonomic units) were present in total, with a mean of 65 per subject. The most common organisms were Pseudomonas spp. (20%), Haemophilus spp. (15%), two different Streptococcus spp. (13 and 12%), Moraxella spp. (6%), Stenotrophomonas spp. (4%) and Neisseria spp. (4%). There was significant inter-individual variability, and Principal Coordinate Analysis indicated that samples most strongly clustered by patient. We observed substantial variation of the diversity and composition of bacterial community structure in some subjects, which could be related to clinical events such as exacerbation or therapeutic intervention. Conclusions: Our study demonstrates that the bronchiectasis microbiome differs significantly between individuals, but in given patients is stable over a 6 month period. Our study also demonstrates variability in community composition that corresponds with status of clinical disease. Analysis of the whole community of microorganisms using pyrosequencing of the 16S rRNA gene is a powerful tool for assessing the pathogenesis and progression of bronchiectasis and is likely to provide novel approaches for disease management.
