N2  - The use of biologic therapy for psoriasis brings with it a need for vigilance for rare and unusual infections; we illustrate this with the following case. A 47-year-old man with a 32-year history of severe psoriasis and psoriatic arthropathy had been treated effectively with infliximab since 2005. In August 2011, he presented with a 1-week history of a nonhealing, nodular lesion on the upper left arm at the site of self-administered subcutaneous insulin injection. This was initially treated in primary care with flucloxacillin, with no improvement. Further questioning revealed recent planned weight loss, night sweats and a cough. An incisional biopsy was taken with a differential diagnosis of cutaneous tuberculosis or malignancy. Histology showed a suppurative granulomatous reaction and special stains (Ziehl-Neelson and Wade-Fite) revealed large numbers of acid-fast bacilli, consistent with mycobacterium infection. Although an atypical mycobacterium was suspected the patient's systemic symptoms meant antimicrobial treatment was commenced for suspected tuberculous infection and infliximab was discontinued. Four weeks after cessation of infliximab the patient had a severe flare of his psoriasis and arthropathy. Cultures remained negative after 6 weeks and the formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded tissue was sent for molecular diagnostics. Mycobacterium haemophilum was detected on polymerase chain reaction analysis. The patient's antibiotic regimen was changed accordingly from quadruple antituberculosis regimen to ciprofloxacin, clarithromycin and rifampicin. After 7 weeks treatment infliximab was recommenced. Mycobacterium haemophilum is an atypical mycobacterium, first recognized in 1978, which is thought to be widespread within the environment. To our knowledge no previous cases of M. haemophilum have been reported in patients on infliximab although there have been cases reported with cutaneous and subcutaneous manifestations in immunocompromised individuals. It is unique among mycobacteria in its fastidious growth requirements requiring media supplemented with ferric ions and optimal growth temperature of 30-32 °C, and therefore standard cultures are often negative. Tumour necrosis factor (TNF) antagonists such as infliximab interfere with granulomatous inflammation and inhibit phagocytosis of intracellular pathogens such as mycobacteria. The risk of mycobacterium tuberculosis is well documented in patients on TNF antagonist therapy, yet the incidence and presentation of atypical mycobacterium is less well understood. Key messages from this rare case are a high index of suspicion for rare infections and unusual presentations of common infections for all patients with psoriasis receiving biologic therapy, and that newer molecular diagnostics may be required for accurate diagnosis and thus appropriate treatment.
