N2  - Introduction: The issue of the meaning of pre-school respiratory symptoms, their relationship to asthma and the optimal treatment are the subject of numerous guidelines and consensus documents, but very little evidence and still less agreement between paediatricians. The aims of this presentation will be to: • Describe an initial and practical approach to the pre-school child referred for evaluation of respiratory symptoms • Discuss the concept of asthma, and the extent to which it applies in the pre-school years • Review the different concepts of pre-school wheeze, in particular episodic viral wheeze (EVW) and multiple trigger wheeze (MTW) • Demonstrate how different treatment stategies should be applied to EVW and MTW Preschool is here defined as age ≤5 years. Approach to the pre-school child referred for evaluation of respiratory symptoms: The child will be referred with any or all of noisy breathing, cough, breathlessness or respiratory distress. It is important to determine the nature of the noise; the word “wheeze” is used very non-specifically [1-4]. Most physicians would consider wheeze to be a polyphonic musical noise, in expiration sometimes with an inspiratory component. Parents, nurses and paediatricians differ on what is meant by wheeze [2]; thankfully, paediatricans are most often in agreement with objective recordings. A videoquestionnaire may help [3,4]. The extent of any respiratory distress and breathlessness should be assessed; noisy breathing and cough without breathlessness or respiratory distress is not caused by asthma. Chronic isolated cough is rarely due to asthma; and isolated chronic productive cough always merits careful evaluation. The commonest cause is persistent bacterial bronchitis [5], but this is a diagnosis of exclusion, and bronchiectasis with its myriad underlying specific causes must be excluded. Also important is to distinguish intermittent symptoms, usually associated with a viral cold, with continuous unremitting symptoms, which are of much more concern. For the vast majority of pre-school children, a good clinical history and a thorough physical examination should place the child into one of five categories [6]. Investigations are rarely needed, and if they are undertaken, should be carefully focussed rather than doing every test on every child. The five categories are as follows: • Normal child (the hardest diagnosis of all!) • Serious illness such as cystic fibrosis or tuberculosis • An asthma syndrome (see below)Minor symptoms which can mimic or exacerbate asthma syndromes (rhinit, gastrooesophageal reflux)Parental over-anxiety The approach to categorising the pre-school child with respiratory symptoms has been discussed in detail elsewhere [6]. Definition of asthma: relevance to the pre-school years: Papers and guidelines have defined asthma in numerous ways; symptoms which vary spontganeously over time and with treatment; variable airflow obstruction with time and treatment; bronchial hyperresponsiveness to a variety of specific and non-specific challenges; and airway inflammation. Obviously one key question is, to what does an asthma diagnosis lead? Too often asthma has been used as a synonym for lower airway steroid deficiency! For the purposes of pre-school asthma, for most clinical purposes 'asthma' is defined as breathlessness with either or both of cough and wheeze, varying over time and with the acute use of bronchodilator (usually inhaled short-acting β-2 agonists, occasionally anticholinergics). The definition does NOT imply airway inflammation is present, nor that inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are necessarily prescribed. The European Respiratory Task Force proposed that the term 'asthma' should not be used in pre-school children [7], because airway inflammation has been seen in older children and adults as intrinsic to the definition, whereas in pre-schoolers it is often not present and rarely in any case measured clinically. The terminology is probably less important than the action taken as a result of the application of the label. How should pre-school wheeze be described? The initial descriptions were from superb, prospective longitudinal studies. The Tucson group described transient early wheeze (wheeze between 0-3 years of age, not at 3-6 years), persistent wheeze (wheeze throughout the first six years of life) and late onset wheeze (no wheeze between 0-3 years, wheeze 4-6 years) [8,9]. These phenotypes were to some extent self-fulfilling, in that assessments were at soon after birth, and 3 and 6 years of age. The ALSPAC group studying a larger cohort with more frequent assessments used latent class analysis to determine six, somewhat overlapping phenotypes [10]. These epidemiological phenotypes have given insights into the mechanisms of disease but are not clinically useful, because they can only be applied retrospectively, and do not guide treatment. An alternative approach is to divise pre-school wheeze into atopic and non-atopic phenotypes. This approach is also flawed. Atopy may not be manifest early in life, and false negative skin prick tests are common early in life. Furthermore, it is increasingly being appreciated that atopy caan be graded in severity, and is not an all-or-none phenomenon [11]. Finally, just because a pre-school child has atopic skin inflammation, does not mean that respiratory symptoms are due to atopic (eosinophilic) bronchial inflammation. So it is unsurprising that a meta-analysis showed no relationship between atopic status and ICS response in pre-school children [12]. Given this background, after extensive evaluation of such evidence as exists, the European Task Force decided to categorise pre-school wheeze into EVW and MTW [7]. EVW is defined as wheeze and breathlessness, usually with cough, only in association with a usually clinically diagnosed viral infection. MTW is characterised by viral associated symptoms, but also symptoms between viral colds, triggered for example by exercise or allergen exposure. This approach has been criticised on the grounds that these phenotypes change over time [13], but this was always appreciated and does not discredit the pragmatic utility in determining present treatment (below). It is well known that EVW can evolve into MTW and school age atopic asthma, and indeed most asthma starts with early EVW. Furthermore, MTW can develop into EVW, either spontaneously or as a result of ICS treatment. The evidence that this is a useful categorisation, quite apart from guiding treatment is as follows: • The prognosis of pure EVW is different from MTW and mixed EVW-MTW; at age 14 years, those who never had MTW, but only pure EVW, were no more likely to be atopic or have respiratory symptoms than those who never had pre-school wheeze [14] • Children with MTW have worse obstructive physiology, distal gas mixing and higher exhaled nitric oxide than EVW [15] • Children with MTW, but not those with EVW, have mucosal eosinophilia and reticular basement thickening similar to older children with asthma [16] There is evidence from other studies, which have not necessarily discriminated carefully between EVW and MTW, that the neutrophil not the eosinophil is the key cell in viral induced symptoms [17-19]. The main justification for this approach is the implication for treatment. How should pre-schoolwheeze be treated? A really important principle is that no treatment, including intermittent [20,21] or continuous [22] ICS, prevents pre-school children with wheeze going on to develop asthma at schoool age. So treatment should be directed at present symtoms; even if we could predict which pre-school children will develop asthma, and the predictive indices have good negative but poor positive predictive values [23,24] we can as yet do nothing to prevent disease progression. It is also important to determine if treatment is actually necessary - merely having noisy breathing, with no other problems, rarely merits any action other than parental re-assurrance. Non-pharmacological therapy: Optimising the child's environment is essential. The main factor is passive smoking, which must be discouraged. The evidence for allergen avoidance in MTW is limited in the preschool years. Treatment of EVW: Therapy should be episodic, assuming any is needed at all. Iniitially inhaled short-acting b-2 agonists or anticholinergics should be tried. If these fail, the next options are intermittent montelukast (the PREEMPT regime)[25] or intermittent ICS. My first line choice would be intermittent montelukast, because of the potentially superior side-effect profile; however it is important to warn parents about the FDA black box warning about behavioural side-effects. If this approach fails, I would next use intermittent ICS. The best dose and duration of ICS is not clear. Fluticasone 750 mcg twice daily was shown to be superior to placebo in EVW [26], but at the cost of side-effects incuding growth suppression. Another study comparing intermittent with continuous nebulised budesonide showed no difference in outcomes [27], but with a smaller total steroid dose by the intermittent route. Unfortunately there was no placebo group, so it is unclear whether there was in fact any benefit. A study comparing intermittent inhaled short-acting b-2 agonists with montelukast and nebulised budesonide showed small and equal benefits with budesonide and montelukast compared with inhaled b-2 agonists [28]. So in summary, there seems to be a signal that intermittent ICS may be beneficial, but the dose is not known. My non-evidence based recommendation would be to use only a moderate dose, for example beclomethasone 400 mcg twice daily for no more than five days, in pre-school children with moderately severe EVW. The role of prednisolone has recently been re-evaluated.
