N2  - Accurate measurement of the global burden of tuberculosis (TB) in children is challenging due to the difficulty of making a definitive diagnosis as a result of non-specific clinical and radiological signs, paucibacillary disease, and lack of capacity for microbiologic diagnosis [1]. Diagnostic uncertainty has been compounded by the HIV epidemic in which chronic lung disease, anergy and non-specific clinical and radiological signs make definitive diagnosis even more challenging. Clinical scoring systems have wide variability with poor reliability for diagnosis [2]. While clinical diagnosis has relied on chronic symptoms such as prolonged coughing or failure to thrive, recent studies have shown that TB is a cause of acute pneumonia (rather than chronic disease). Amongst almost 1500 children under 2 years of age with suspected TB, traditional symptoms including chronic cough, weight loss or failure to thrive were not associated with culture confirmed TB [3]. Clinical or radiological signs do not enable TB to be distinguished from other pathogens. Radiological changes may be non-specific and interpretation is further complicated by wide inter- and intra-observer variation in the interpretation of the presence of mediastinal lymphadenopathy, one of the major radiological features for diagnosis [4,5]. Improved methods for diagnosis have focused either on measuring a host immune response or on better detection of M tuberculosis. Interferon-gamma release assays, which measure interferon production in response to exposure to M tuberculosis antigens, have similar sensitivity to tuberculin testing for detection of TB infection but higher specificity [6]. However, interferon measures do not offer substantial benefit over tuberculin skin test for the diagnosis of TB disease (and are more costly and require a blood test and specialized laboratory equipment) except in HIV-infected, very young or malnourished children. Current commercially available serological tests have poor sensitivity and specificity and should not be used - the World Health Organisation (WHO) recently issued a negative policy warning against their use [7]. Definitive microbiologic diagnosis and antimicrobial susceptibility has become increasingly important in children to accurately define the paediatric burden, to minimise pill burden, promote adherence, in the era of drug resistance and given the availability of rapid PCR based tests for M tuberculosis. Sputum induction is useful, safe and provides a good specimen for microbiologic confirmation even in infants [8,9]. In hospitalized children, a single induced sputum provides a similar culture yield to 3 gastric lavages [8]. Sputum induction is feasible in primary care settings and can be done as an ambulatory procedure [9]. The yield from nasopharyngeal specimens either from culture or PCR based testing is lower than that from gastric lavage [10]. Liquid culture is better than solid medium for culture confirmation. The availability of a rapid molecular diagnostic test (Xpert) is an important advance that can improve case detection in children and enable rapid detection of multi drug resistant TB. Xpert testing of 2 induced sputum specimens detect approximately 75% of children with culture confirmed disease [11]. The WHO has recently recommended that Xpert be the first line investigation in people suspected of having drug- resistant or HIV-associated TB [12]. Urine LAM has shown promise as a rapid diagnostic in a subgroup of HIV-infected severely immunocompromised adults, but there is no data in children [13]. Further research is needed to develop a rapid point of care, reliable and affordable diagnostic test for TB that can be widely used.
