N2  - Tuberculosis impacts children severely and severally causing serious and significant morbidity among children, particularly in the high burden developing countries. Despite this, childhood TB does not get any significant attention in the national strategies for TB control as these cases are often not infectious. The difficulties in establishing the diagnosis further make it hard to include children in national surveillance. Though significant improvements have been made in tuberculosis (TB) management under the World Health Organisation's (WHO) directly observed treatment, short course (DOTS) strategy, childhood TB continues to be relatively neglected. Lately, there has been a renewed interest in paediatric tuberculosis as it is acknowledged to constitute a substantial burden of the global TB caseload. This paper provides an overview of traditional and novel approaches used to diagnose and manage childhood TB in resource-limited regions. What is the likely burden of childhood TB? During the decade of nineties, WHO initially estimated that approximately 650,000 TB cases were occurring in children and later a prediction was made that by 2000 this figure would have increased to more than one million childhood cases, making up 10% of all cases [1]. Data from the WHO reflecting 2002 regional notification rates of smearpositive cases among children ranged from 0 per 100,000 persons in Europe to 6 per 100,000 in Africa and 43 and 45 per 100,000 in South Africa and Zambia, respectively [2]. This is certainly a gross underestimate as about 95% of children less than 12 years of age are AFB smear-negative. More recent estimates suggest that Childhood TB contributes approximately 15 to 20% of all cases, increasing to up to 40% in some high TB burden countries [3]. Controlling Infectiousness (read as curing positive sputum smear cases) has been central to all TB control activities and most children being sputum smear negative get excluded from the routine surveillance tools This happens despite the fact the young children carry the greatest burden of disease among all infected individuals. Not only are they most likely to develop disease once infected, they are also significantly more likely to develop extrapulmonary and severe disseminated disease as compared to adults. Two of the most severe manifestations of tuberculosis, miliary disease and tuberculous meningitis, are significantly more common in children. Miliary disease, an early complication of primary infection, occurs following haemotogenous dissemination and is often associated with crippling tubercular meningitis. It is also important to point out that in developing communities a large proportion of the population is aged less than 15 years and as many as 40% of tuberculosis notifications may be from children; tuberculosis may be responsible for 10% or more of childhood hospital admissions and 10% or more of hospital deaths [4]. Furthermore, with an annual risk of infection of 2-3%, close to 40% of the population may be infected by age 15 years. Children with latent tuberculosis infection (LTBI) are the reservoir of future disease in adulthood, perpetuating the epidemic. Childhood TB is also a sentinel event, indicating ongoing transmission of TB within communities. The HIV epidemic, which disproportionately affects young adults, many of whom are co-infected with TB, may also put their children at particularly high risk of infection. In wealthier, low-burden countries, infected children are often identified by contact tracing and given chemoprophylaxis, but limited resources and a much higher disease incidence prohibit this in most high burden settings. Even though the preventive Isoniazid (INH) therapy can substantively reduce the risk of disease amongst infected individual but this effect gets less profound in high prevalence situations since the benefit is not carried over for not so uncommon subsequent infections. While there are enough reasons to keep childhood TB as much in focus in TB control activities as other groups, yet the mismatch between available resources and need to decrease the high burden of the infectious cases often leaves them out. How is the diagnosis established for childhood TB conditions? Mycobacterial staining and culture in body specimens is the gold standard for diagnosis of TB at all age groups. However, the lack of sensitivity and specificity of these tests in childhood tuberculosis makes establishing diagnosis a challenging task. The difficulty in accessing the correct specimen in small children has made this important test less popular with most practicing paediatricians. The diagnosis of tuberculosis in children is more often based on ancillary evidence like exposure or contact history, prolonged symptoms, tuberculin skin test and chest radiography. Suspecting Tuberculosis: The symptoms of TB disease are chronic in nature and are non-specific. Typically fever and or cough for more than 2 weeks duration, unexplained weight loss and or anorexia, painless superficial lymphadenopathy are seen in cases with tuberculosis. The lack of specificity and frequency with which these symptoms occur without TB among children in poor countries makes the clinical diagnosis imprecise. A better characterisation of the symptoms can add further to the specificity of these and thus aid the diagnosis. Typically the cough and fever in TB are persistent and unremitting [5]. A reduced sensitivity in symptom-based TB diagnosis has been observed in younger children (<3 years of age) and those co-infected with HIV. TB disease may be characterized by acute symptoms in these settings. In another study when comparing the symptoms of children with culture proven TB to those who were initially suspected of having TB but shown to have other lung diseases it was shown that weight loss, chronic cough, duration of symptoms or weight less than the third percentile did not differ between the two groups. The only clinical factors which differentiated between the groups were contact with an adult index case and a positive tuberculin skin test [6]. The symptoms may also vary a little with the type and site of involvement. The most common symptoms of pleural effusion are chest pain, cough, fatigue, shortness of breath, and anorexia. The findings on physical examination can mimic bacterial pneumonia, with dullness to percussion, diminished breath sounds, and fever. Secondary (post-primary) pulmonary TB disease usually presents during adolescence and is most common in areas endemic for TB. Symptoms like weight loss, fever, productive cough, hemoptysis, and night sweats are commonly seen. Typically the findings on physical examination may be minimal. Chest Radiology: Chest roentgengraph is the commonest diagnostic tool used in evaluating patients for pulmonary tuberculosis. It is not only difficult to get a technically valid, well exposed and wellcentered chest skaigram in a young child, its interpretation can also be erroneous due the problems of subjectivity in reading and reader variability. Despite all its inadequacies, the chest roentgenogram remains the most widely used diagnostic test in clinical practice. The chest radiographic findings diagnostic of intrathoracic TB are enlarged mediastinal glands and complications resulting from the glands compressing airways or other structures. On plain radiographs, hilar lymphadenopathy is best seen on a posteroanterior radiograph as a lobulated density but can also be viewed in lateral films posterior to the bronchus intermedius as a “dough nut” sign. Lateral films can improve the accuracy of diagnosis by detecting hilar adenitis not clearly appreciated on frontal films. Lateral chest radiographs improve the diagnostic yield by 11% [7]. Fluoroscopy of the main airways helps differentiate the presence of hilar adenopathy (which remains immobile throughout respiration) from prominent pulmonary vasculature. In studies of culture-proven TB, adenopathy is visible in 49 to 60% of chest skiagrams. A calcified lesion found in the parenchyma of the lungs indicates an old granuloma. TB may present only with alveolar opacification making it difficult to distinguish from other causes of pneumonia. Common parenchymal changes seen in a child with TB include alveolar consolidation, pleural effusion, segmental hyperinflation, atelectasis, or a focal mass. Miliary TB has the only distinct radiologically diagnostic feature of TB disease- characterized by fine bilateral reticular shadowing, sometimes called a snowstorm appearance. Adolescents can present with adult-type reactivation disease. Radiographic features of post primary disease include patchy, poorly defined consolidation or inhomogenous alveolar opacities, particularly in the upper lobes; and, cavitations (about half of the patients) in the absence of lymphadenopathy. Post primary disease usually occurs in the apical, posterior segments of the upper lobes and apical segment of the lower lobe because the oxygen tension is highest in the upper lung zones. Bronchogenic spread is radiographically identified in 20% of cases of TB reactivation, manifested as multiple, ill-defined micronodules, involving the lower zones of the lungs, usually distant from the site of cavitation. As most of the radiological appearances are not specific to tuberculosis and the interpretation of the radiological findings suffers from both inter- and intra-individual variations, the over- and under-diagnosis is quite common. Computed tomography (CT) imaging can be helpful in a symptomatic child with a normal CXR or an asymptomatic TST or IGRA positive child with equivocal findings on CXR. Intravenous contrast medium is used to distinguish lymph nodes from normal blood vessels. However, CT scan of the chest is not routinely recommended due to cost, lack of specificity and high radiation.
