N2  - Hypertonic saline has been shown to reduce pulmonary exacerbations in CF patients 6 years and older; data supporting its efficacy in patients younger than 6 years are currently lacking. The inhaled saline for infants (ISIS) study was designed to assess the efficacy of hypertonic saline in children between the age of 4 and 60 months at baseline. This was a 30-center, randomized, double blind, controlled trial of 7% hypertonic saline (HS) vs. 0.9% isotonic saline (IS) inhaled twice daily for 48 weeks. The primary endpoint was the rate of protocol-defined pulmonary exacerbations. Additional efficacy measures included time to first pulmonary exacerbation; number of courses and total number of treatment days with oral, inhaled, and/or intravenous antibiotics for a pulmonary exacerbation or for any indication; change in weight, height, resting respiratory rate and oxygen saturation over the 48 week treatment period; CFQ-R respiratory domain score and parent report of daytime cough evaluated at each study visit. Infant pulmonary function testing was performed in a subgroup of patients. Safety outcomes included the rate of intolerance to the test dose of HS at enrolment, adverse events and withdrawal rates, and treatmentemergent respiratory cultures positive for CF pathogens. 321 participants were randomized, 158 to the HS group and 163 to the IS group; these individuals comprise the intent-to-treat population. The baseline characteristics of participants were similar in the two groups and withdrawal rates were low (9% in the HS group and 7% in the IS group). The study has been completed recently; resultswill be available at the time of the meeting.
