N2  - Background: The confusion of clinical and radiological pulmonary coccidioidomycosis with other respiratory diseases such as neoplasms, other fungal and bacterial diseases especially tuberculosis, requires the generation of information to guide medical personnel for the diagnosis and treatment on this disease. This fungal infection is acquired when susceptible hosts inhale arthroconidia that are the infectious propagules of the saprobia phase of this fungus; spherules containing endospores are the typical parasitic form of Coccidioides immitis and C. posadasii. However mycelial forms have been observed in the parasitic phase of this fungus considered dimorphic. Objective: To describe the clinical, mycological, radiological and epidemiological characteristics, of patients with coccidioidomycosis. Methods: Retrospective analysis of clinical data, mycological, radiological and epidemiological studies of 37 patients. The study focused on the presence of hyphae or spherules as parasitic forms. Inclusion criteria were: patients with clinical and laboratory diagnosis of coccidioidomycosis, incomplete records were excluded. Results: A differential picture produced by the presence of spherules (cough, fever, dyspnea, pneumonic image, nodules and micronodules) of that which occurs in patients with hyphae (chronic disease, weight loss, hemoptysis, cavitary lesion). 26.8% of patients were receiving amphotericin B and itraconazole, combinations of these with ketoconazole and fluconazole were manifold. Significant facts: history of exposure by residence, co-morbidity and prolonged hospital stay. Conclusion: The micro habitat in the cavity lesion in diabetic patients promotes coexistence of hyphae and spherules of Coccidioides, chronic conditions the latter one increases the burden of disease and the likelihood of hemoptysis. The different antifungal schemes showed no association with decreased hospital stay.
