N2  - Background: Pneumocystis pneumonia (PcP) which is caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii is an important agent of hospitalization and death in immunocompromised patients especially among HIVinfected persons. Here, we reported the first in depth molecular analysis of P. jirovecii epidemy among HIV-infected patients. Methods:Twomitochondrial large-subunitrRNA(mtLSUrRNA) and dihydropteroate synthase (DHPS) genes were investigated using nested Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) and Restriction Fragment Length Polymorphism (PCR-RFLP). Respiratory specimens collected from 126 Iranian HIV-positive patients referred to Iranian HIV/AIDS Research Center and National Research Institute of Tuberculosis and Lung Disease (during August 2010 to March 2011). The patients with cough, fever, pneumothorax, dyspnea and/or abnormal chest X-ray were introduced to this study. Results: One hundred and twelve patients were male (89%). The mean age of patients was 35.12±9.75 years and the median was 33 years. Median of CD4 T cell count of patients was 93 cells/μl. Thirty nine (31%) of patients were hospitalized.Twenty four samples (19.1%) were positive for M. tuberculosis. In spite of getting prophylaxis for PcP and HARRT treatment, the DNA of P. jirovecii were detected in 15 samples (11.9%). Just one patient (0.8%) was co-infected with both P. jirovecii and M. tuberculosis. In this category of patients no other microorganisms were detected by culture. The causes of pneumonia in the remaining 87 patients (69%) were the other bacterial and viral agents. The mortality rate of disease was 26.6% in these patients due to PcP. one of the isolate (6.6%) hadshownmutation in 55 codon which are associated with resistance to sulfa/sulfon drugs. The sequencing results of mt LSU rRNA gene showed that the genetic patterns of Iranian isolates were similar to Indian and Spanish isolates (99% identity). Conclusion: The study showed the rate of disease in Iranian HIV-infected patients is similar to Asian countries and some area in Africa. Sincewecould find just one isolate with mutation associated with drug resistance in DHPS gene, it is very difficult to explain the high rate of disease. The similarity of Iranian isolates to Indian Isolates is probably due to close geographical region.
