N2  - Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CF gene (CFTR), an epithelial chloride channel. In addition, CFTR regulates a sodium channel, ENaC, which in CF leads to sodium and water hyperabsorption from the airway fluids leading to thickening of the mucus layer, ciliary dysfunction, bacterial colonisation, inflammation and lung degeneration. SiRNA silencing of ENaC has been proposed as a CF therapeutic by preventing lung dehydration and the subsequent events of lung pathogenesis. ENaC is a multimeric sodium ion channel localised in the apical membrane of epithelia usually composed of three homologous subunits: α, β, γ but α is the major structural subunit and so was selected for silencing. We have developed a novel siRNA delivery formulation termed LPR. LPR consists of a liposome, DOTMA/DOPE (L) and a peptide (P), either K16GACYGLPHKFCG or K16GACSERSMNFCG, which self-assemble, electrostatically into nanocomplexes on mixing with siRNA (R), producing particles of less than 100 nm (by DLS) and a strongly cationic charge (approximately +60mV). The peptides contain a cationic siRNA binding domain (K16) and epithelial receptor-targeting motifs (underlined). The LPR formulation packaged siRNA with more than 90% efficiency as shown by quenching of picogreen fluorescently labelled siRNA. Transfections revealed targeting specificity of siRNA transfection, compared to non-targeted LPRs, and a critical synergy between lipid and peptide components with both lipid and peptide components essential for the formation of stable nanoparticles and efficient silencing. In this study we have shown LPR-mediated silencing of αENaC of >80% in normal and CF human airway epithelial cell lines by quantitative RT-PCR. Primary CF airway epithelial cells displayed >70% silencing with 50nM siRNA. CF cells were then grown in confluent, differentiated, air surface liquid interface cultures (ALI), producing mucus and cilia and transfected with αENaC siRNA, producing 40% silencing of expression. Mice were then with administered with 28 μg of siRNA in LPR formulations, by oropharyngeal instillation on three occasions at 48 h intervals. Mice were culled 48h after the final instillation and RNA was extracted from whole lung, and converted to cDNA. Three of six mice showed αENaC silencing of approximately 80%, while three other mice showed no effect. The causes of these differences in the qPCR data are unknown at present. Finally we have tested the stability of LPR to delivery by nebulisation with a jet nebuliser, showing by TEM, DLS and zeta analysis that the biophysical properties of the nanocomplex were unaltered. Comparisons of the siRNA transfection efficiency in cells showed that nebulisation did not affect functionality of LPRs. In conclusion, this study displays the potential of LPR formulations to evaluate the potential of ENaC silencing in cellular and in vivo models as a therapeutic strategy for CF.
