N2  - Objective: To investigate the clinical features of HIV/AIDS patients with the onset of pulmonary infection so as to improve the control rate of infection and reduce the early mortality. Methods: A total of 76 patients with a initial diagnosis of HIV/AIDS admitted to Shanghai Jiaotong University Affiliated Ruijin Hospital from January, 1998 to June, 2011 were included in the study. They were divided into two groups (group A: onset of pulmonary infection, 32/76; group B: without pulmonary infection, 44/76). The clinical data, such as, symptoms, pathogens, chest imagings, laboratory tests and short-term prognosis were collected and analyzed. Results: Fever (26 vs. 19, p < 0.01), weight loss (18 vs. 13, p < 0.05) and other systemic symptoms were significantly higher in patients of group A than those of group B. When the HIV/AIDS patients had the symptom of cough, they appeared as low respiratory tract infection (22 vs. 3, p < 0.01). The sputum cultures were collected from 27 patients in Group A. There were 21 positive results in the 27 cases, including 3 cases of G+ infection (14.2%), 3 cases of G- infection (14.2%), 7 cases of fungal infection (33.3%), 3 cases of mycobacterium tuberculosis infection (14.2%), 5 cases of multiple infection (23.8%). In group A, the chest imagings displayed as interstitial change in 10 cases (31.2%), patchy exudation in 12 cases (37.5%), lump or nodule in 2 cases (6.2%), miliary changes in 1 case (3.1%), cavitary change in 1 case (3.1%), other change in 6 cases (18.7%). Although the leukocytes (5.4 ± 4.3 vs. 4.8 ± 2.1 10∧3/ul P>0.05) and total lymphocyte count (0.78 ± 0.74 vs. 1.0 ± 0.39 p > 0.05) have not significant difference between the two groups, the CD4 absolute count (73 ± 116.5 vs. 85 ± 301/ul p < 0.05) and CD4/CD8 ratio (0.07 ± 0.10 vs. 0.28 ± 0.75 p < 0.01) in Group A were much lower than that in Group B. The short term (2 weeks) mortality of Group A was significantly higher than that of Group B (4 vs. 0 p < 0.05). Conclusion: These AIDS patients who suffered from pulmonary infection as the first symptom had more obvious systemic manifestation, worse general situation, higher short-term mortality and worse prognosis. So primary screening of HIV should be emphasized in patients with refractory pneumonia in order to improve the short-term prognosis.
