N2  - Background: Tuberculosis (TB) most commonly affects the lung, but in immunosuppressed patients, for example those infected by Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV), mycobacterium tuberculosis bacillus often disseminates and manifest in other organs. There has not been any report about the profile of extrapulmonary TB in HIV patients in Cipto Mangunkusumo Hospital. The purpose of this study is to obtain the demographic, clinical, and laboratory characteristics, and to assess the distribution of extrapulmonary TB in TB-HIV patients. Methods: We conducted a descriptive cross-sectional study by browsing through the medical records of TB-HIV patients who received treatment in POKDISUS Cipto Mangunkusumo Hospital between July 2008-December 2010. Results: Our subjects consisted of 145 males (84.3%) and 27 females (157%); the majority of the subjects were in the 18-40 years age group (91.9%). The educational status and ethnicity of patients showed that 77.3% finished mid-level education, and 31.4% of the subjects belonged to Batavian ethnic group. Intravenous drug user were found to be the main transmission risk factor, representing 60.5% of the subjects. Prolonged fever (63.4%) was the most common clinical manifestation followed by chronic cough (60.5%). Negative Acid Fast Bacilli smears were found in 34.9% of the subjects, while CD4+ counts of <50 cells/ll was found in 34.9% of the subjects. The most common type of extrapulmonary TB was lymphadenitis TB (54.6%), and followed by meningitis TB (12.7%). Conclusion: The prevalence of extrapulmonary TB in HIV patients is high in our study, especially those with advance immunosuppression. The possibility of extrapulmonary TB should be considered in this group of patients.
