N2  - Functional oligosaccharide (hereafter be shorted as oligosaccharide) is a kind of bioactive substances which exist widespreadly in nature. They cannot be digested and absorbed in hosts, but can be used by beneficial bacteria via selective absorption in the body, and then promote the probiotic activity. Oligosaccharides are not only involved in the intestinal immune, they can also adjust the entire immune function in vivo. In order to explore whether oligosaccharides improve the nutritional status and immune function of patients with COPD, thereby contributing potential effort to the comprehensive prevention and treatment of COPD, we study the effects of oligosaccharides on the nutritional status and the immune system in patients with COPD, analyze the role in prevention and protection against respiratory tract infection, which lay the foundation for clinical applications. Sixty patients with COPD meeting the enrolled criteria were randomly divided into group A (experimental group) and group B (control group), 30 patients in each group. Patients in the experimental group received 50 g milk powder containing oligosaccharides (containing prebiotics standard formula of galacto-oligosaccharide and oligofructose) per day for 4 months, while patients in the control group received same quantity of milk powder without oligosaccharides daily for 4 months as well. We recorded clinical manifestation of patients in both of groups (including symptoms of AECOPD like cough, sputum, shortness of breath, wheezing, fever and manifestations of digestive tract like diarrhea, vomiting). We respectively collected blood samples before and after intervention in each of group, analyzed immune parameters (T-cell subpopulations ratio, oxidation and phagocytosis of polymorphonuclear neutrophils) and the nutritional parameters (BMI, body weight, skeletal muscle content and prealbumin, albumin, transferrin, etc), as well as the pulmonary function (FVC, FEV1, FEV1 pred%, FEV1/FVC). Measurement data of normal distribution were indicated with mean ± SD, analysis of variance or student t tests were performed on the norm variablies; enumeration data were analyzed by chi-square test (χ2 test). In the experimental group, gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea, vomiting occurred in three cases at 4-month observation period. Symptoms of AECOPD like cough, sputum, shortness of breath, wheezing, fever also occurred in three cases, and two of which last more than 1 week, one case improved within a week. In the control group, gastrointestinal symptoms occurred in three cases. AECOPD occurred in 10 cases, four of which last more than 1 week, six cases returned to health within a week. The incidence of gastrointestinal symptoms in two groups had no significant difference (χ2 = 0,p = 1), the incidence of AECOPD had statistical significance (χ2 = 4.8118, p = 0.0283). After 4-month observation, we analyzed parameters from blood samples collected before and after the intervention for immune research in experimental group. CD3(+) T cells ratio (%) was from 67.16 ± 8.17 to 71.9 ± 8.49, CD4 (+) T cells ratio (%) from 41.27 ± 5.05 to 45.17 ± 6.06, CD8 (+) T cells ratio(%)from 21.99 ± 6.18 to 23.92 ± 7.67, CD4/CD8 from 2.01 ± 0.57 to 2.14 ± 0.91. Oxidation of polymorphonuclear neutrophils was from 140.23 ± 57.3 to 159.17 ± 58.7, phagocytosis of neutrophils from 83.99 ± 13.53 to 90.37 ± 7.58. We also analyzed nutritional status in experimental group. BMI (Kg/m2) was respectively 24.32 ± 3.71 and 24.7 ± 3.67 before and after the intervention, body weight (Kg) was 63.91 ± 11.54 and 64.92 ± 11.64, skeletal muscle content (SMM) (Kg) was 24.14 ± 5.56 and 24.21 ± 5.76, prealbumin (mg/l) was 241.53 ± 30.5 and 246.87 ± 43.16, albumin (mg/l) was 43.44 ± 1.98 and 43.5 ± 2.57, transferrin (g/l) was 2.33 ± 0.33 and 2.21 ± 0.29. In pulmonary function test, FVC (L) was 2.55 ± 0.69 before intervention and 2.57 ± 0.72 after intervention, FEV1 (L) was respectively 1.73 ± 0.6 and 1.71 ± 0.61, FEV1 pred% was 0.67 ± 0.21 and 0.65 ± 0.21, FEV1/FVC was 0.66 ± 0.13 and 0.65 ± 0.14. The proportion of CD3 (+) and CD4 (+) T lymphocytes and neutrophil phagocytosis were significantly enhanced after intervention in the experimental group (t = 2.2, p = 0.0315; t = 2.7, p = 0.009; t = 2.25, p = 0.0291). Nutritional parameters and pulmonary ventilation function had no significant differences (p > 0.05). On the other hand, we analyzed parameters for immune, nutrition, and pulmonary function in control group before and after the trial. All the data had no statistical significance before and after the trial (p > 0.05). After 4-month intervention, we analyzed the data in both groups by statistical methods. CD3 (+) T cells ratio in the experimental group and in the control group was respectively 71.9 ± 8.49 and 66.14 ± 10.12, that was obviously higher in the experimental group than that in the control group (t = 2.39, p = 0.0203). Neutrophil phagocytosis was respectively 90.37 ± 7.58 and 85.9 ± 7.61, which was also stronger in the experimental group (t = 2.28, p = 0.0266). The two groups had no significant statistical differences in other immune, nutritional parameters and pulmonary ventilation function (p > 0.05). We divided patients in experimental group into three levels according to pulmonary function, stage I as level I, stage II as level II, stage III-IV as level III. The changes of phagocytosis of neutrophils were 14.89 ± 17.18 in level I, 2.91 ± 7.85 in level II, and 2.83 ± 6.41 in level III. With the decline of lung function, effect of oligosaccharides on neutrophil phagocytosis decreased, positive correlation between the two (F = 3.62, p = 0.04). Patients in experimental group were classified into two subgroups by BMI stages. We set BMI ≤ 21 (Kg/m2) as group a, BMI > 21 (Kg/m2) as group b. After intervention, changes of albumin (mg/l) in group a was -2.53 ± 0.76, changes in group b was 0.45 ± 1.3. The level of albumin improved significantly in the group with higher BMI (t = 4.41, p = 0.0001). We concluded functional oligosaccharide can significantly improve immune function in patients with COPD, thus helping to minimize the exacerbation frequency of COPD. With the decline of lung function, effect of oligosaccharides on neutrophil phagocytosis decrease, and there is a positive correlation between them. Oligosaccharides can obviously enhance the level of albumin in the patients with COPD whose BMI > 21 mg/m2, which maybe improve the nutritional status in patients at higher BMI.
