N2  - Purpose: Introduction: Darling's disease (Histoplasmosis) is an opportunistic infection affecting patients with impaired cellular immunity. Progressive Disseminated Histoplasmosis (PDH) can rarely present as gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding. Case: A 40 year old woman from Honduras with AIDS (noncompliant with treatment) presented with fever, productive cough, vague abdominal pain for 1 week. She denied diarrhea, weight loss or recent travel. Examination revealed a febrile, pale, cachectic patient with normal systemic examination. Laboratory tests showed microcytic anemia, normal WBC, CD4 count of 121 cells/mm<sup>3</sup> and elevated transaminases. LDH level was markedly elevated (1216 IU/L). Chest radiograph showed bilateral reticulonodular infiltrates. She was started on broad spectrum antibiotics and intravenous (IV) sulfamethoxazole plus trimethoprim for suspected bacterial versus Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia. Despite treatment, patient remained febrile with rising LDH levels. Serial blood cultures including fungal cultures were negative. Abdominal CT revealed retroperitoenal adenopathy and mural thickening of cecal wall. On floors, she had recurrent melena causing hemodynamic instability, requiring transfer to ICU and treatment with proton pump inhibitor infusion and multiple blood transfusions. Upper GI endoscopy showed moderate gastritis and diffuse esophagitis without active bleeding or clots. Colonoscopy demonstrated multiple small ulcers and polyps in the sigmoid as well as 1.2 cm ulcer at ileocecal valve, polyps and whipworm in cecum. Subsequently urine Histoplasma antigen was positive. Colonoscopic biopsies showed non necrotizing granulomas. GMS and PAS stains were positive for numerous fungal organisms consistent with Histoplasma. Patient defervesced after 2 weeks of IV Amphotericin B. She was discharged on oral Itraconazole for planned duration of one year. Antiretroviral therapy was resumed. Discussion: Symptomatic Histoplasmosis has an annual incidence of 5% among HIV patients in endemic areas. GI involvement is very common in PDH (up to 70% at autopsy), but is rarely recognized during life (3-12%). PDH generally occurs with CD4 <150/mm<sup>3</sup> and frequently involves ileocecal region. Usual presentation is abdominal pain, diarrhea, colonic mass and rarely GI bleeding. Diagnosis is made by serology, culture and direct microscopy. Amphotericin is used for induction and itraconozole for suppressive therapy until mm<sup>3</sup>. Elevated LDH signifies severe disease. Mortality without treatment is 80% which is reduced to 25% with antifungal and antiretroviral therapy. Thus Histoplasmosis should be a consideration in immunosuppressed patients presenting with opportunistic infection and GI bleeding.
