N2  - Objectives: This review has the purpose to present the surgical experience of 437 patients undergoing to lung resection for the treatment of bronchiectasis, evaluating the impact on clinical postoperative complications and quality of life of patients. Methods: Between 1978 and 2010, the medical records of 437 patients who underwent a surgical lung resection for treatment of bronchiectasis in a single institution were reviewed. Inclusion criteria were patients with bronchiectasis, older than 16 years old, that had been treated with surgical resection. Patients with cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis who were transplanted were excluded. We evaluated sex, age, race, comorbidities, and quantifying the presence and location of symptoms of the disease preoperatively. In the postoperative period, evaluation of the presence of bacteria in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, type of pulmonary resection, presence of postoperative infection, presence of prolonged air leak and hospital stay were evaluated. Follow-up in the late postoperative period evaluated the presence and severity of symptoms, quality of life and the personal opinion of the patients regarding the surgical procedure and impact on their lives. Results: The average age was 38.45 years, ranging from 16 to 80 years, mean body mass index of 20.7, where 57% were women and measured FEV1 was 74%. This was accompanied by sinusitis in 91 patients, dyslipidaemia in 15, COPD in 21, hypertension in 35, diabetes in 14, asthma 26, Kartagener 2, 3 and HIV syndrome in two H1N1 pacientes. A tomography showed bronchiectasis in 77.3% of unilateral and bilateral in 22 patients 7%. The causative agent was bronchiectasis childhood pneumonia in 49.2%, tuberculosis in 17.2%, foreign body 3.2%, 1.1% tumour obstruction and idiopathic in 29.3%. Cough (347), sputum (245) and recurrent pneumonia (194) were the most frequent symptoms. Identification of pseudomonas in the BAL was associated with higher incidence of postoperative complications. Total resection of disease was performed in 95% 344 patients with unilateral excisions that included 6.4% of pneumonectomy, lobectomy in 61% and 32% for segmentectomy. Complications such as prolonged air leak, empyema, atelectasis, and bleeding were present in 16% of cases, with index reintervention of 4.8% and 0.4% mortality. In the late postoperative period 68% were asymptomatic, 24% had improvement of symptoms and 5% reported no improvement of symptoms. When questioned about the impact of surgery upon their lives, 87.4% of patients responded positively. Conclusions: Surgical treatment of bronchiectasis is safe, effective, presents very low morbidity and low mortality with a significant improvement in quality of life of patients.
