N2  - A 4-year-old Asian girl was referred with a 6-month history of a nonhealing erosion on her left cheek. No similar lesions were noticed elsewhere. Apart from a mild itch and related bleeding it was relatively asymptomatic. Other than a past history of chronic otitis media she was fit and healthy. After much persuasion the parents agreed to a skin biopsy. This revealed granulomatous inflammation with no caseation or organisms. There was no evidence of vasculitis. A similar lesion on the pulp of her right thumb erupted within weeks of having the biopsy. There was no associated pain or bleeding. It was also noted that her left elbow was swollen during examination. Although she was reluctant to admit to any pain, the size of swelling and reduction in the range of movement signified an underlying pathology. Further questioning revealed a dry cough. There was no evidence of fever, night sweats or lymphadenopathy. The rest of the examination was unremarkable. The X-ray of her left elbow showed changes consistent with chronic osteomyelitis and this prompted further investigations towards a diagnosis of tuberculosis. She had upper zone shadowing on chest X-ray and positive Mantoux and QuantiFERON® (Cellestis Int. Pty Ltd, Chadstone, Victoria, Australia) tests. Early morning sputum was negative for tuberculosis. Routine blood tests confirmed an ongoing inflammatory process. A cerebrospinal fluid analysis done to rule out meningeal involvement was negative. The skin swab and bone aspirate grew Mycobacterium tuberculosis confirming the diagnosis. Further investigations to rule out an immunodeficiency disorder were all negative. Antituberculous treatments were commenced and her skin lesions resolved with scarring. Despite completing her treatment with concomitant physiotherapy the improvement in her elbow joint mobility lagged behind. Possibilities are that she had pulmonary tuberculosis at first presentation with lupus vulgaris due to direct inoculation or subclinical tuberculous osteomyelitis and tuberculous chancres from haematogenous spread. Negative caseation, negative organisms and well-formed granulomas with a peripheral rim of lymphocytes could persuade one to consider sarcoidosis as an alternative diagnosis if not for the 'cold' swollen joint. Children with tuberculosis can be remarkably asymptomatic and be sputum negative compared with adults. This should be borne in mind and any granulomatous inflammation of skin should be treated with a high index of suspicion for tuberculosis as delay in diagnosis could lead to significant morbidity as evidenced by this case.
