N2  - Background: DRCFI is being developed to increase the concentration and duration of action of ciprofloxacin in the lung, the site of P. Aeruginosa infections, while reducing systemic drug exposure and thus the potential for side effects. The randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled ORBIT-2 trial was conducted in Australia and New Zealand. Following a screening period, 42 patients, were treated once-a-day for 28 days with either DRCFI or placebo, followed by a 28 day off-treatment period. This on- off sequence was repeated 3 times. Results: In the full analysis population, there was a significant mean reduction of 4.2 log10 units in the DRCFI group, reflecting an almost 16,000 fold decrease in bacterial load, versus a mean decrease of 0.1 log10 units in the placebo group (p = 0.004). Importantly, the Kaplan-Meier analysis showed a statistically significant (p < 0.05, log rank test) increase in the median time to first pulmonary exacerbation in the per protocol evaluation from 58 days in the placebo group to 134 days in the DRCFI group. DRCFI also had a superior pulmonary safety profile reflected in the number and severity of pulmonary adverse events. Furthermore, there was a statistically significant lower need to initiate antibiotics in the DRCFI group versus the placebo group. Conclusion: The sustained release profile of inhaled DRCFI and its effect in reducing PA sputum density and exacerbations in BE warrant further clinical development.
