N2  - Introduction: The epidemiology of ventilator-associated pneumonia(VAP) is well described for adults but few data are available regarding risk factors and outcomes for children. In adult, oropharyngeal secretions aspiration, aerosols inhalation, hematoge-nous spread, and bacterial translocation from the gastrointestinal tract are all considered to be mechanisms of VAP development. Objectives: To identify predisposing factors for VAP occurrence in children. Methods: Retrospective cohort study, conducted in a pediatric intensive care unit of a university hospital in Brazil. Data was collected from November, 2009 to February, 2010, after ethical approval. The sample comprised 86 children submitted to MPV. VAP diagnosis was established by the Clinical Pulmonary Infection Score. For the statistical analysis were applied Pearson Chi-square, t test and odds ratios (OR) with confidence interval of 95% (significance level 5%). Results: Of the 86 children, 30.2% developed VAP. The comparison between the children characteristics showed no statistically significant difference in age, gender, skin colour, nutritional status and VAP development in children. Statistically significant differences to VAP occurrence was identified to the follow aspects: intermittent postpyloric feeding (p= 0,010;OR= 4,766), nasoenteral tube presence (p= 0,004;OR= 4,091), emergency rein-tubation (p= 0,019;OR=1,678), elective reintubation (p= 0,022;OR= 4,00), duration of mechanical ventilation(p= 0,000). The use of neuromuscular blockade (OR= 4.524) and the previous presence of infectious diseases (OR=2.333) increased the chances for VAP occurrence, but without significant differences. Conclusion: Intermittent postpyloric feeding, nasoenteral tube presence, emergency or elective reintubation and duration of mechanical ventilation constituted risk factors for VAP development in children submitted to mechanical ventilation.
