N2  - Rationale: Contamination by bacterial or fungal organisms reduces the effectiveness of mycobacterial culture for diagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis (TB). Standard decontamination procedures reduce the growth of non-mycobacterial as well as mycobacterial organisms. We evaluated the effect of an anti-microbial oral rinse prior to expectoration on culture contamination rates. Methods: We enrolled a consecutive random sample of adults with cough for ≥2 weeks admitted to Mulago Hospital (Kampala, Uganda) between October 2008 and June 2009. We randomly assigned patients to oral rinse (60 seconds with chlorhexidine followed by 60 seconds with nystatin) vs. no oral rinse prior to initial sputum collection. Uganda National Tuberculosis Reference laboratory (NTRL) technicians blinded to the method of sputum collection processed all sputum specimens for smear microscopy (direct Ziehl-Neelsen technique) and mycobacterial culture (Lowenstein-Jensen media). NTRL technicians read cultures weekly for up to 8 weeks and considered cultures to be contaminated if there was a change in color or consistency of the culture media, development of liquid or water film over the culture media, or if non-mycobacterial colonies grew in the culture media. Results: Of 220 patients enrolled, 177 (80%) were HIV sero-positive (median CD4-count 37 cells/uL, IQR 13-171 cells/uL). Baseline characteristics were similar between patients assigned to the oral rinse (N=110) and no oral rinse (N=110) groups. The proportion of contaminated cultures was significantly lower in the oral rinse group compared to the no oral rinse group (4% vs. 15%, p=0.005). Oral rinse significantly reduced the proportion of contaminated cultures among HIV-infected (3% vs. 18%, difference 15%, 95% CI 6 to 23%, p=0.002) but not HIV-uninfected (6% vs. 4%, difference -2%, 95% CI -15 to 12%, p=1.00) patients. However, the proportion of smear-positive specimens (25% vs. 35%, p=0.10) and the proportion of culture-positive specimens (48% vs. 56%, p=0.24) were lower in the oral rinse compared to the no oral rinse group, though the differences were not statistically significant (Table 1). Conclusions: Oral rinse prior to sputum expectoration is a promising strategy to reduce mycobacterial culture contamination in areas with high HIV prevalence, if strategies can be devised to reduce the adverse impact of oral rinse on smear- and culture-positivity. (Table Presented).
