N2  - Introduction: Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is characterized by impaired mucociliary clearance with chronic sinopulmonary disease. While it is evident that disease manifestations begin early in life, little is known about disease characteristics in infancy/early childhood. Methods: Cross-sectional analysis of enrollment data from the Genetic Diseases of Mucociliary Clearance Consortium multicenter, longitudinal study of children with PCD <5 years of age, including medical history, respiratory culture and controlled-breathing chest CT. Results: of 48 subjects enrolled at 6 centers, we present data on 23 with confirmed PCD (N=21) or acilia (N=2) (22 confirmed by electron microscopy, 1 by genetics). Eleven subjects were female; eighteen Caucasian; mean age 2.6 years. Fourteen subjects (61%) had situs inversus totalis, one had abdominal situs inversus. Twenty-two were born at term; of those, 86% had neonatal respiratory distress, with mean 18.3 (range 2 - 105) days hospitalized and 15.5 (range 0 - 90) days oxygen requirement. All subjects had chronic daily cough, beginning at mean age 2.3 months, characterized as wet (91%) and productive (65%). Sixteen subjects (70%) had history of wheeze, beginning at mean age 3 months. All subjects had recurrent otitis media; mean age of first OM was 4.6 months. Eleven subjects (48%) had at least 6 OM before 2 years of age, 17 (74%) had at least one set of PE tubes placed, and 7 (30%) had at least transient hearing loss. All subjects reported chronic nasal congestion beginning at mean age of 1.3 months, though only 26% had received a diagnosis of sinusitis. All subjects had abnormal chest CTs, 22% with bronchiectasis, 65% with atelectasis/opacities, 35% with air-trapping/hyperinflation, and 35% with bronchial wall thickening/dilatation (not mutually exclusive). Respiratory cultures grew oropharyngeal flora (61%), Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and Moraxella catarrhalis (each present in 9%, not mutually exclusive). Five subjects (22%) had a history of Pseudomonas aeruginosa on previous culture. Conclusions: Among 23 children at mean age 2.6 years with confirmed PCD or acilia, all had chronic, daily cough and nasal congestion and recurrent otitis media, usually starting before 1 year of age, and abnormal chest CTs, 22% with bronchiectasis; 86% had neonatal respiratory distress. These findings demonstrate the significant disease burden in children with PCD <5 years of age and emphasize the need for further study to determine risk factors for disease progression and optimal disease management.
