N2  - RATIONALE: The clinical isolation of nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM) has been increasing in both HIV positive and negative individuals, with marked geographical difference in the species encountered. The treatment of NTM disease is lengthy and often involves complicated drug regimes. Therefore it is of great importance to differentiate between a clinically significant infection and sample contamination. The aim of this study was to assess the frequency and clinical significance of isolation of NTM in patients presenting to a large district general hospital, serving a predominantly rural region of the UK. METHODS: A retrospective review was conducted of the case notes of all patients from whom NTM were isolated between January 2008 and December 2009. The clinical significance of the isolates was determined using the diagnostic criteria for NTM disease published by the American Thoracic Society (ATS) in 2007. RESULTS: In total 35 patients had NTM isolated from clinical specimens, mostly from the respiratory tract (89%). The majority of patients were female (73%) with an average age of 63 years (range 5-86). Almost all patients had pre-existing pulmonary disease or were immunosuppressed. The most common presenting symptoms were cough and dyspnoea. 14 of 35 patients (40%) with NTM isolates met the ATS diagnostic criteria for clinical significance, this percentage differed by species (Fig 1). M. gordonae was the most prevalent species isolated but only 1 patient from 8 met the criteria for significant infection. In comparison M. abscessus was isolated from 3 patients, all with cystic fibrosis, who all had clinically significant NTM disease. Most patients were treated with multi-drug regimes for a minimum of 9 months. Conclusion: 40% of all patients with NTM isolates met the ATS criteria for clinically significant infection. This percentage differed markedly by species. Although the numbers in this patient group are small they would suggest that isolation of M. abscessus should warrant special attention as this usually reflects true NTM disease. The use of diagnostic and treatment guidelines can help improve outcomes but more studies are required to establish the prevalence and incidence of NTM, geographical differences in rates, and risk factors. Fig 1 (Table Presented).
