N2  - RATIONALE: Many studies have hypothesized that the prevalence of Nontuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM) infections has increased in the recent decades. In developed countries, NTM is more prevalent than tuberculosis (TB). Few epidemiologic studies have been published with data from developing countries. Data accounting for NTM infections are restrict to a few reference laboratory studies that fail to reflect the real epidemiology of the infection. Cystic fibrosis (CF) is a disease that results in chronic infection and inflammation and high risk of bacterial colonization and infection. With improved treatment, the bacterial epidemiology of lung infections has suffered changes with NTM infection occurring specially in older patients with better lung function. In order to study the prevalence and clinical relevance of NTM in CF patients in RJ, this study is underway with a focus in identifying NTM detection and its association with clinical outcomes. METHODS: We studied all children with CF from the IFF/CF Center in RJ. All children had clinical, functional (<6years) and radiological data collected. Sputum samples were collected in every visit (up to once a month) and hospital admission. Sputum samples were submitted to the Mycobacterium Laboratory for baciloscopy and culture. Identification was performed by using PRA-hsp65 method or sequencing of rpoB/hsp65 when required. Drug susceptibility tests were performed as recommended by the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. RESULTS: 64 children (34 female), age 2 to 22 (mean 11.7, 95%CI 10.6-12.8) were studied. Pancreatic insufficiency was present in 78%, 75% were in use of dornase alfa, 54% of inhaled corticosteroids and 15% antibiotic aerosol therapy. Moderate to severe obstructive lung disease was seen in 24.5%. Bacterial contamination of the airway by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphilococcus aureus and/or Burkholderia cepacia was seen in 83% (43.8%; 46.9% and 10.9% respectively). NTM isolation was observed in 13.7% (n=9/64) of the patients (7 with more than 1 isolate). Mycobacterium massiliense was the most common species found (n=4; 6.1%) followed by M.abcessus (n=2; 3%). M.asiaticum, M.bolleti, M.fortuitum, and M.timonense were detected once each. CONCLUSIONS: We found a similar prevalence of NTM in a CF population as described in the US, even though TB burden is higher in Rj and Brazil compared to the US. However, M.massiliense was the most prevalent species, suggesting the emergence of this pathogen in our population. Further study are underway to better characterize risk factors for colonization and NTM disease.
