N2  - RATIONALE: Interleukin-32 (IL-32) is a pro-inflammatory cytokine. A mouse homolog for IL-32 has not been found. We previously found that THP-1 cells silenced for IL-32 using siRNA technology were impaired in the killing of intracellular Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB). METHODS: To study the effects of IL-32 in vivo, we created the IL-32 transgenic mouse using the sufactant protein C promoter (SPC-IL-32gTg), resulting in the constitutive expression of IL-32 in the lungs. SPC-IL-32g DNA fragments were purified and microinjected into pronuclei of fertilized eggs isolated from C57BL/6 mice and transferred into the oviducts of pseudo-pregnant ICR foster mothers. After egg transplantation, transgenic offspring were identified by Southern blot and PCR analyses of genomic DNA isolated from tail biopsies of the founder litter. Two male founder mice were bred with WT C57BL/6 females. Subsequent identification of the SPC-IL-32g transgene was confirmed by PCR analysis of tail genomic DNA obtained from 4 week old mice. After four to five generations of in-breeding, a uniform colony of SPC-IL-32gTg mice was achieved with confirmed PCR positivity for the IL-32g transgene. RESULTS: As quantified by ELISA, IL-32g protein in the lungs of four SPC-IL-32gTg mice was 80-fold greater than the level seen in four WT mice (mean lung IL-32g level = 1,654±372 pg/mg in SPC-IL-32gTg vs. 20.8±16 pg/mg for the WT). The small amount of IL-32 detected in control animals is likely due to non-specific background signal. Measurement of lung volumes following instillation of +++ under 20 cm of hydrostatic pressure indicated the lungs of SPC-IL-32Tg mice were larger. The SPC-IL-32gTg mice also had higher lung compliance when compared to age-matched controls. Infection of mice with a low-dose aerosol of MTB W-Beijing HN878 showed the SPC-IL-32gTg animals were modestly protected against MTB infection compared to control mice. CONCLUSION: Mice with constitutive expression of human IL-32g in their lungs have an abnormal lung phenotype and appear to be less susceptible to MTB.
