N2  - Rationale: In contrast to the sterile airways of a healthy lung, respiratory pathogens are frequently isolated from the airways in patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) in the absence of an exacerbation. This bacterial “colonization” is associated with host inflammatory and immune responses. Effects of bacterial colonization on daily symptoms in patients with COPD were examined in this study. Methods: Patients enrolled in a longitudinal prospective observational cohort study of COPD participated in this sub-study. All patients recorded daily symptoms in the form of the Breathlessness, Cough, and Sputum Scale (BCSS) score in electronic diaries (Micro Diary, Micro Direct Inc., Lewiston ME). Sputum cultures were performed every two weeks to identify presence of bacterial pathogens. BCSS data obtained in the period around a COPD exacerbation were excluded from the analysis. The relationship of BCSS and colonization by bacterial pathogens in stable COPD was analyzed with generalized estimation equations (GEE) analysis using an unstructured correlation matrix. Results: A total of 41 patients with COPD recorded their daily symptoms for 12527 (68.4%) of 18304 possible days of recording. Bacterial pathogens were isolated from 321 of 993 (32.3%) sputum samples collected during stable COPD. Average BCSS was higher during the periods of colonization with one or more of the following pathogens: nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae (NTHI), Moraxella catarrhalis (MC), Streptococcus pneumoniae (SP), Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA), compared to periods without colonization with these pathogens (5.06 vs. 4.21, SE: 0.33, p = 0.01). For individual bacterial pathogens, patients were more symptomatic during colonization with NTHI and MC (5.24 vs. 4.48, SE: 0.34, p = 0.026 and 5.53 vs. 4.52, SE: 0.37, p = 0.007, respectively) when compared to absence of colonization by these pathogens. Conclusions: Even in the absence of clinical exacerbation, colonization by the four major pathogens in COPD was associated with a clinically and statistically significant increase in daily symptoms. We speculate that unreported exacerbations in COPD could be in part related to periods of bacterial colonization. Our findings are complementary to prior observations showing increased immune and inflammatory responses to bacterial colonization in COPD.
