N2  - Background. Few data exist to inform treatment of LTBI in contacts of patients with multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB). TMC207 (J) is a new diarylquinoline with potent bactericidal activity in mice and no known cross-resistance with existing TB drugs. Recently, addition of J to an optimized background regimen improved sputum conversion in MDR-TB patients. A study in a mouse model of active TB has also shown the combination of J, rifapentine (P), and pyrazinamide (Z) to be superior to JZ or PZ, indicating that JP or JPZ may provide an ultra-short-course treatment option for drug-susceptible LTBI. Therefore, we evaluated the efficacy of J alone and in combination with P and PZ in an experimental model of LTBI. Methods. Mice were immunized by aerosol with 103 CFU of Mycobacterium bovis rBCG30, then challenged 6 wk later with ∼15 CFU of M. tuberculosis H37Rv (Mtb). Daily (5 days/wk) treatment began 6 wk after challenge (on Day 0). Treatment groups included isoniazid (H), rifampin (R), RH, P, PH, J, PJ and PJZ. Another group received once-weekly PH. Doses (in mg/kg) were: H (10), R (10), P (10), J (25) and Z (150) daily or H (50) and P (15) weekly. Outcomes were lung CFU counts at 1 month and relapse rates after treatment completion. Relapse rates were compared by Fisher's Exact test, controlling for multiple comparisons. Results. Mtb CFU counts were 4.31±0.24log10 at Day 0 and remained stable over 6 months in untreated mice. See figure for CFU counts after 4 wk in treated groups. See table for relapse rates (blank cells = not done). Relapse results after 4, 5 and 6 months of treatment are pending. Conclusions. In this model of stable paucibacillary infection, J alone had sterilizing activity greater than that of R (p<0.001) and at least as good as RH and PH1/7, suggesting it may treat LTBI in 2-3 months. However, J is not as effective as P or PH (p<0.001) and the addition of J did not increase the sterilizing activity of P in this model, whereas PJZ was more active than PH or PJ (p<0.05). (Figure presented) .
