N2  - BACKGROUND Despite recent large outbreaks of pandemic (H1N1) 2009, mortality rate has remained low in Japan. Surveillance data showed that the mortality rates among groups aged 40-60 years was higher than those for seasonal influenza, and influenza pneumonia was observed more frequently in fatal cases. This study aimed to reveal the characteristics of influenza pneumonia among adult patients in Japan. METHODS In a retrospective study of adult patients with influenza pneumonia, questionnaires were sent to respiratory physicians working in departments of pulmonary medicine at 2491 hospitals across Japan. Questions pertained to age, sex, respiratory symptoms, physical findings, laboratory data, and radiological findings of such patients. RESULTS 994 physicians returned the questionnaire (response rate: 39.9%), and data concerning 346 influenza pneumonia cases were gathered. Primary influenza viral pneumonia was reported for 96 cases (27.7%), mixed viral and bacterial pneumonia for 135 (39%), secondary bacterial pneumonia for 55 (15.9%), and miscellaneous for 44 (12.7%). No underlying disease was reported for 147 of cases (42.5%). In 196 cases with suspected accompanying bacterial infection, Streptococcus pneumoniae as the major agent was detected in 67 cases (34%). Almost all cases (97%) were treated with anti-influenza viral drugs, which included oseltamivir (299cases, 89%), zanamivir (34 cases, 10%), and peramivir (2 cases, 1%). Primary influenza viral pneumonia was observed more frequently in young adult patients, while bacterial pneumonia was observed more frequently in older patients. The characterics of primary influenza viral pneumonia diagnosed by the respiratory physicians were non productive cough, dyspnea, low white blood cell count (<4,000/uL), and respiratory failure. Radiological findings included ground-glass opacity in bilateral lung field, diffuse alveolar damage pattern, and patchy consolidation like organizing pneumonia or eosinophilic pneumonia. Pneumonia severity determined by a pneumonia severity index or CURB-65 showed no relationship with mortality rate (average 8.2%). CONCLUSION Severe primary influenza viral pneumonia tends to be rare in outbreaks of seasonal influenza, but more than a few cases of “pure” viral pneumonia were observed in the recent pandemic. It is widely accepted that primary influenza viral pneumonia is severe and has a high mortality rate, yet we found no difference from other patterns of other types of pneumonia in Japan. Early treatment with anti-influenza viral drugs may contribute to decreasing the mortality rate of influenza pneumonia.
