N2  - Introduction: Acid suppression therapy, which converts acid to non-acid reflux, has been implicated as a cause of respiratory infections, however, the mechanism by which this occurs is unknown. We hypothesize that this increased rate of infection is due to overgrowth of bacteria in the pH-neutral stomach which, if refluxed, can seed the lung. No studies have explored the relationship between non-acid reflux burden and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PSa) infection and lung function in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF). Methods: We performed a retrospective chart review of all patients with CF who underwent impedance probe testing (pH-MII) between 2003 through 2010. We compared the reflux profiles between those patients with positive and negative oropharyngeal cultures for PSa to determine if reflux burden predicted PSa positivity. The symptom index (SI), symptom sensitivity index (SSI) and the symptom association probability (SAP) for cough were calculated for each pH-MII study. An abnormal pH-MII study was defined as >73 episodes per 24 hour period. An abnormal pH-probe study was defined as a pH<4 for >6% of the 24 hour study. We compared means using t-tests and proportions using chi square analyses. Results: Thirty one patients were included in the analysis. The mean age was of 13.7 ± 5.9 years. The mean percent-predicted forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1) was 70% ± 23%. Seventy-five percent of patients had positive respiratory cultures for PSa within the 6 months prior to their impedance probe. Ninety seven percent of patients were on PPI and of those 61% were on twice daily dosing. Despite acid suppression therapy, the mean percentage of time pH was <4 was 8.4±12.2%. Patients with a positive respiratory culture for PSa had a significantly higher total reflux burden which included greater amounts of acid and non-acid reflux episodes (Table). Interestingly, there was a significant negative correlation between non-acid reflux and FEV1 (r=-0.489, p=0.01), but there was no significant correlation between FEV1 and any other reflux parameter (p>0.4). There was no significant difference in the mean symptom indices (SI, SSI and SAP) for cough between PSa positive and negative patients (p>0.2). After adjusting for age and FEV1, total reflux burden remained an important contributor to PSa positivity (p=0.055). Conclusions: Total reflux burden may predispose patients to PSa infection and non-acid reflux burden, in particular, correlates with worse lung function in these patients. (Table presented).
