N2  - Introduction: A highly concentrated aerosolized solution of tobramycin (300 mg/4 ml) has been developed to reduce the length of nebulization (BRAMITOB®, Chiesi Farmaceutici, Italy). Its efficacy and safety was evaluated in a head-to-head comparison against the marketed gold standard product, TOBI® (300mg tobramycin/5 mL, Novartis), in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF) and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) infection. Methods: In a multinational, multicenter, open-label, non-inferiority, controlled trial, 324 CF patients aged ≥ 6 years, with chronic PA infection and with forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1) ≥ 40 and ≤ 80% predicted were randomized to 28-day twice daily treatment with BRAMITOB or TOBI , followed by 28-day period without treatment. The primary efficacy variable was the change from baseline of FEV1 % predicted at the end of the treatment phase. Secondary efficacy variables included other pulmonary function parameters and PA density and susceptibility. Safety was assessed through monitoring of adverse events, including ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity. Results: After 28 days of treatment there was a similar statistically significant increase in FEV1% predicted normal in both treatment groups over baseline: 6.99% and 7.51% in the BRAMITOB and TOBI groups, respectively. The least squares means of the change from baseline were 4.66 in the BRAMITOB group and 5.16 in the TOBI group [LS mean difference: -0.50; two-sided 95% CI: (-2.58, 1.59); ANCOVA]. Four weeks after treatment withdrawal, FEV1 % predicted decreased but still showed significant changes over baseline (5.47% for BRAMITOB and 5.37% for TOBI). Similar findings were obtained for forced vital capacity (FVC) and forced expiratory flow at 25-75% of FVC (FEF25-75 ). Both treatments significantly reduced PA density in sputum (mean reduction of 2.14 and 2.07 log10 CFU/g for BRAMITOB and TOBI, respectively) and rates of positive and negative microbiologic outcomes, e.g., eradication, re-infection and superinfection, were similar between treatment groups. Similar percentages of patients in both treatment groups reported adverse events (31.4% vs. 28.0%, in the BRAMITOB and TOBI groups, respectively). Cough was the most frequently occurring drug-related adverse event in the TOBI group (2.4%), compared to no patients in the BRAMITOB group. Based on serum creatinine and audiometric tests, no clinically relevant signs of nephrotoxicity or ototoxicity were observed in either treatment group. Conclusion: BRAMITOB showed comparable therapeutic efficacy to TOBI, both significantly improving pulmonary function and decreasing PA density in the lungs of CF patients. BRAMITOB and TOBI had similar safety profiles and both were well tolerated.
