N2  - Background: Opportunistic pneumonias remain a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in HIV-infected persons worldwide. Recent studies suggest that respiratory infections are caused by a complex community of microorganisms, including viruses, rather than by a single pathogen. Objective: To assess the prevalence and epidemiological associations of viral infections in HIV-infected individuals with pneumonia using culture-independent techniques. Design and methods: A cross-sectional cohort study was conducted at Mulago Hospital in Kampala, Uganda. Study participants were HIV-infected individuals with cough ≥2 weeks duration who had negative sputum acid-fast bacillus smears and underwent bronchoscopy to evaluate for an opportunistic pneumonia. Specimens were evaluated by a pan-viral DNA microarray and reverse transcriptase-polymerase chain reaction for respiratory viral infections. Results: Between October 2009 and April 2010, 42 HIV-infected individuals underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage. The median age of participants was 31 (IQR 27 to 39); and twenty-one subjects (50%) were female. The median CD4 count was 67 (IQR 28 to 153) cells/uL. A putative cause of viral pneumonia was identified in 2 subjects (Influenza A, Bocavirus). One or more viruses were identified in BAL fluid of 23 (55%) subjects: Cytomegalovirus (8); Epstein-Barr virus (8); Torque teno virus (7); Human simplex viruses (5); Bocavirus (1); Human Herpes Virus 6 (1); Influenza A (1). Identification of a virus was not associated with the use antiretroviral therapy at enrollment (6/10 on ART vs 14/32 not on ART (p=0.37)). Individuals with one or more viruses detected in their BAL fluid had a lower median CD4 count (43 (IQR 22 to 67) cells/uL) in comparison to that of individuals without viral infection (145 (IQR 41 to 296) cells/uL; p <0.005). Conclusions: Microarray analysis of BAL specimens in HIV-infected patients rarely identified a putative viral cause of opportunistic pneumonia in this cohort. However, a majority of subjects had evidence of viral infection or colonization in their lower respiratory tract and lower CD4 count was predictive of detecting a virus. Further studies utilizing high throughput sequencing are needed to verify these findings and are likely reveal the presence of additional viruses in the lower respiratory tract of HIV-infected patients. Longitudinal studies of viral infections in HIV-infected individuals are needed to better understand their potential role in the development and outcome of opportunistic pneumonia.
