N2  - Case Report: Organ transplantation with immunosuppression is frequently complicated by acute kidney injury (AKI). Calcineurin inhibitor toxicity, acute tubular necrosis, and hypersensitivity tubulo-interstitial nephritis are usual causes. Here we report a less common cause that requires specific treatment in this patient population. A 56 year-old man required lung transplantation for pulmonary fibrosis. Initial immunosuppression included anti-thymocyte globulin followed by tacrolimus, mycophenolate, and prednisone. Course was complicated by rejection with a humoral component, treated with steroids and rituximab. Four months after transplantation, he presented with fever, cough, diarrhea, and AKI. Renal function improved transiently with decreased tacrolimus doses. Despite extensive evaluation for fever and empiric therapy with multiple antimicrobial agents over a 6-week period, however, the renal function worsened (peak creatinine 4.3 mg/dl) and periodic fever persisted. Urinalysis by a nephrologist then demonstrated sterile pyuria and atypical tubular epithelial cells with intracellular inclusions. PCR tests for BK and JC virus DNA were negative. Kidney biopsy established the diagnosis. It demonstrated granulomatous pyelonephritis with gram-positive, pleomorphic, rod-shaped organisms in renal tubular epithelial cells. Electron microscopy and directed PCR testing identified the organism as a microsporidium, Encephalitozoon cuniculi. Retrospective examination of lung biopsy tissue from 2 months prior demonstrated the same organism. Of note, the patient worked closely with herding dogs, a known host for E. cuniculi. Treatment with albendazole was initiated, but the patient subsequently developed superimposed Aspergillus pneumonia, and died 4 weeks later. This case demonstrates that unusual causes of AKI, including infections, should be considered in transplant patients, since successful treatment requires early detection. Kidney biopsy was key in this patient, but proper staining and examination of urine sediment can lead to consideration of unusual pathogens like microsporidia.
