N2  - In 2003, evidence-based guidelines for Infection Control in CF were published to minimize transmission of potential pathogens from others with CF or from the contaminated healthcare environment by standardizing infection control practices [1]. These guidelines addressed practices for healthcare workers as well as CF patients and their families and included appropriate hand hygiene; minimizing person-to-person contact between CF patients including avoiding socialization; containing spread of infectious droplets by respiratory hygiene; cleaning and disinfecting of respiratory therapy equipment; and cleaning the healthcare environment. However, writing guidelines is only the first step in changing behaviors. It is critical to assess adherence to these guidelines, barriers to adherence to the guidelines, and strategies to overcome these barriers. In fact, there is much evidence that the CF community is adhering to the infection control guidelines, as increasing numbers of CF centers have implemented recommended clinical microbiology laboratory practices and written infection control policies, particularly for hospitalized CF patients [2, 3]. From 1995-2005, the incidence and prevalence of Burkholderia cepacia complex decreased, highly suggestive that patients infected with B. cepacia are being segregated from others with CF [4]. Patients have generally reported an understanding of the need for infection control and recognized that socialization could have adverse consequences [5]. Nevertheless, adherence to infection control can be improved, as evidenced by recent reports of patient-to-patient transmission that may have been fueled by socializing in the nonhealthcare setting as well as the CF clinic and hospital [6]. Our group has studied the barriers to adherence to the guidelines (including avoiding socialization) experienced by healthcare workers as well as by CF patients and their families. We have developed and administered anonymous infection control knowledge, attitude and practice surveys. Our study in healthcare workers detected numerous barriers to avoiding socialization and thus confirmed the complexity of this issue [7]; only 31% of 528 respondents from 25 randomly selected CF care centers in the USA were familiar with the guideline to avoid socialization and 32% did not agree with the recommendation. While 48% of respondents lacked confidence (self-efficacy) they knew how to discourage socialization among CF patients, 84% thought that avoiding socialization could benefit their patients' health (outcome expectancy). Only 61% of respondents stated they discussed avoiding socialization with >75% of their patients [6]. However, access to a copy of the infection control guidelines (considered a surrogate for education) was associated with increased familiarity with the recommendation to avoid socialization (OR: 5.58 95% CI 2.62-11.92); increased agreement with the guideline (OR: 2.94; 95% CI 1.88-4.59); increased self-efficacy (OR: 2.02; 95% CI 1.23-3.33) and increased outcome expectancy (OR: 2.37; 95% CI 1.29-4.36). In our study to assess barriers experienced by CF patients and families from 17 randomly selected CF care centers, we found that only 34% of 1399 respondents reported that their CF care team had told them to avoid close contact with others with CF (Saiman, unpublished data). While 83% knew that germs could be transmitted from CF patient-to-CF patient, only 64% agreed that close contact and 57% agreed that shaking hands with others with CF should be avoided. Respondents lacked confidence they could avoid close contact with another person with CF in the hospital (36%) or in CF clinic (58%). Those respondents who had discussed the guidelines more than once with their care team (considered a surrogate for education) were more likely to report they avoided socialization with other CF patients (AOR: 1.43; 95% CI: 1.13, 1.79); to have confidence they could do so (AOR: 1.53; 95% CI: 1.27, 1.85); and to believe that avoiding socialization in the hospital (AOR: 1.84; 95% CI: 1.26, 2.68; p-value: 0.0014) or clinic (AOR: 1.34; 95% CI: 1.00, 1.79) would improve their health. During interviews conducted with people with CF, additional potential barriers to adherence were described (Saiman, personal communication). Some patients stated they did not know who had CF so they couldn't avoid close contact. Others were afraid to alienate their CF friends if they declined to socialize. Others felt it was “safe” to sit more than 3 feet apart. Others felt it was “safe” to hug, but not kiss or it was “safe” to be together as long as neither person was coughing. Others felt that transmission precautions were difficult to endure as they felt like “lepers” and/or “were bored.” These findings offer insights into the educational needs of the CF community. Future efforts should focus on more education of staff, including inpatient hospital staff, regarding the dangers of socialization. Strategies could include: assessing implementation of the current infection control program, providing regular updates to accommodate new staff and ensure existing staff are practicing appropriately, performing observational studies of practices by engaging with the infection control and prevention team, and addressing knowledge gaps and misperceptions in real time. Further strategies include improving education and communication between staff and patients and their families by regularly providing verbal and written education, regarding the risks of socialization and anticipating the understandable lure of socialization. The development of a “tool box,” e.g., strategies, incentives, and enablers could facilitate such education. Efforts should be made to minimize the isolation of transmission precautions by encouraging visitors, child life, and other diversions. To minimize the psychological burden of not being able to socialize with others with CF, non-face-to-face methods of interaction via the internet can be developed, although it is important to anticipate the potential for such strategies to have the undesired effect of promoting subsequent socialization. We are currently performing a multi-center study of CFFONETM, a web-enabled, inter-active, cell phone intended to facilitate knowledge and adherence to treatment [8]. There is a supervised chat room feature within CFFONETM. In conclusion, the CF community has struggled with balancing the risks and benefits of socialization among CF patients. In the past, CF patients were encouraged to socialize and gain psychosocial support from one another via the shared experience of coping with their illness and the burdens of treatment. Now, faced with irrefutable evidence of patient-to-patient transmission of pathogens causing increased morbidity and mortality, socialization is shown to be dangerous and should be avoided. However, strategies to facilitate avoiding socialization are needed for the CF healthcare team as well as patients and families.
