N2  - Airway mucociliary clearance in health and cystic fibrosis In a healthy airway, the CFTR protein sits on the apical surface of the airway epithelial cell, where it helps to regulate the amount of sodium, chloride and water in the airway surface liquid. The depth of the airway surface liquid is maintained at approximately 7 microns, the height of the outstretched cilia. This allows the mucus layer to be moved along a watery surface by the tips of the cilia to carry inhaled pathogens out of the lungs to keep them free of infection. In the CF airway, however, the CFTR protein is absent or abnormal. Sodium and chloride transport is poorly regulated, and too much water is lost from the airway surface allowing the mucus layer to entangle in the cilia. Mucus and its trapped pathogens are retained in the lungs, chronically infecting, inflaming, obstructing and damaging the airways. Medications to improve mucociliary clearance Mucolytics disrupt the structure of mucus to promote airway mucociliary clearance. Apart from their mucolytic action, some have effects that are also mucoactive via other mechanisms, such as alteration of mucus secretion, hydration of the airway or stimulation of ciliary beating. Recombinant human deoxyribonuclease (dornase alfa) is an enzyme that has been shown to reduce the viscosity of sputum taken from people with CF bydigesting the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) released from neutrophils [1]. Although the effect on mucus pourability occurs within 30 minutes [2], no effect on mucociliary clearance per se has been detectable [3]. Therapy with dornase alfa over a one-, six- or twelvemonth period is associated with an improvement in lung function in CF [4]. Hypertonic saline is a mucoactive agent used clinically in concentrations of 3-7%. It reduces viscosity and spinnability of sputum [5], restores airway surface liquid volume [6], stimulates cough [7] and cilia [8], and disrupts biofilms [9]. The increase in mucociliary clearance stimulated by hypertonic saline has been demonstrated in radioactive aerosol studies [10-11]. Regular dosing has been shown to sustain a higher baseline mucociliary clearance rate [6]. Hypertonic saline significantly improves lung function and reduces exacerbations. It does not increase the acquisition of pathogens or cause any lasting increase in density of common CF pathogens in sputum. It significantly improves school and work attendance and quality of life in adults and adolescents with CF. Patients report that clearing their secretions is easier [6, 7, 12]. Mannitol also uses the same osmotic principle to stimulate airway mucociliary clearance to a similar magnitude to hypertonic saline [10], although it is a sugar rather than a salt. It significantly improves lung function and quality of life [13]. P2Y2 agonists are among the most recently investigated mucoactive agents that stimulate the transport of chloride onto the airway surface and inhibit the absorption of sodium, to transport water onto the airway surface. They enhance mucus secretion from goblet cells, and increase ciliary beat frequency and promote surfactant release. Denufosol is metabolically more stable than previously tested P2Y2 agonists, thus it may have long lasting effects in the airways of patients with CF [14]. Timing of inhalation of mucoactive therapies Timing of dornase alfa inhalation was examined in a recent Cochrane review [15], specifically examining before vs. after airway clearance techniques and morning vs. evening inhalation. Five studies (involving 127 randomised participants) met the inclusion criteria. All 5 studies used a cross-over design with intervention periods ranging from 2-8 weeks. The current evidence does not indicate that inhalation of dornase alfa after airway clearance techniques is more or less effective than the traditional recommendation to inhale nebulised dornase alfa 30 minutes prior to airway clearance techniques, with respect to lung function (FEV1, FVC), quality of life and most other outcomes. However, in children with well preserved lung function, inhalation of dornase alfa before airway clearance techniques is more beneficial for small airway function (as reflected by FEF25) than inhalation after airway clearance techniques. In one trial for the comparison of morning versus evening inhalation, no outcomes differed significantly. In an additional trial, allowing dornase alfa to dwell in the lungs longer (mean 11hr) before airway clearance improved FEF25- 75 and quality of life significantly more than a short dwell time (0.2hr). However, the primary outcome examined by this review (FEV1) was not affected by the timing of dornase alfa inhalation with respect to airway clearance, time of day, or dwell time. Timing of hypertonic saline inhalation has only been examined in one 3-arm study, comparing inhalation before, during or after airway clearance physiotherapy, in adults with CF [16]. The outcomes included lung function and subjective treatment efficacy, tolerability and satisfaction. There was no significant impact on lung function due to the timing of hypertonic saline inhalation with respect to airway clearance physiotherapy. Subjective perceptions of treatment efficacy and satisfaction were significantly lower with inhalation after airway clearance physiotherapy. Based on repeatability data, it is likely that an individual patient's preferred timing regimen remains constant over time. Aerosol deposition The distribution of deposition of inhaled drugs is influenced by airflow. In sitting, tidal ventilation is preferentially to basal regions [17]. Thus nebulisation in sitting may under-dose the apical regions [18]. Poor deposition in the upper lobes may contribute to the faster progression of CF lung disease in the upper lobes. It has been hypothesised that inhalation in alternate side lying will improve homogeneity of drug deposition by dosing each lung (including its apex) when it is dependent [19]. A recent study [20] has confirmed that lung deposition in sitting is preferentially to basal regions in health and CF. In this study, CF subjects had twice as much variation in regional deposition. Sidelying can improve apical deposition in healthy lungs but not when significant CF lung disease exists. However, there is no negative impact on deposition if patients with CF nebulise in sidelying. Conclusion Mucolytics are an increasingly important component of airway clearance physiotherapy. Given they also contribute to a significant treatment burden for people with CF, therapy should be prescribed to optimise delivery, efficacy, and treatment satisfaction. Understanding the physiology of mucus clearance in the CF lung will assist both the CF patient and care provider to optimise the airway clearance program, including inhaled therapies, on an individualised basis.
