N2  - CF is caused by mutations in the gene encoding the CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR), an epithelial anion channel involved in chloride and bicarbonate transport. In the airway, deficient CFTR activity results in absent or severely reduced chloride (Cl-) transport and excess sodium reabsorption through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). The characteristic bioelectric defect can be readily detected by the transepithelial nasal potential difference (NPD) measurement, and is useful as a diagnostic test and outcome measure in studies of therapies that modulate the ion transport environment in the airway. Potential difference measurements in the lower airway (LAPD) of CF subjects have also demonstrated the bioelectric defect, although the procedure is technically challenging and generally requires general anesthesia. As part of a biomarker development program, our center recently identified and validated an improved method for measuring NPD using an agar-filled non-perfusion nasal probe. The method significantly reduced artifacts due to external electrical interference and interruptions of the electrical circuit due to microbubble perfusion (1). The technique also provides increased sensitivity to Cl- conductance, and provides a clear path forward for longer catheters required for intrabronchial measurements. Cigarette smoking is the proximate cause of 85% of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), an acquired lung disorder that affects more than 200 million individuals worldwide. Many of the pathologic abnormalities in COPD are similar to those observed in CF lung disease, including the presence of chronic bacterial colonization, dysregulated inflammatory and protease balance, and mucus stasis. COPD patients with chronic bronchitis due to tobacco use are also afflicted with a productive cough and many suffer recurrent pulmonary exacerbations. In addition, a number of radionuclear studies have demonstrated that COPD patients have reduced mucociliary clearance, a finding also present in CF. A number of laboratories have shown whole cigarette smoke, cigarette smoke extract, and cigarette smoke condensate reduces CFTR activity in airway epithelial cells in vitro (2-4). Cantin and colleagues reported that patients who smoke tobacco and who do not have CFTR mutations exhibit reduced CFTR dependent Cl- transport as measured by NPD (2). Recently, our laboratory has also shown that neutrophil elastase, a prominent protease highly expressed in the COPD airway, also reduces CFTR function through a calcium dependent pathway. These studies raise the question as to whether patients with COPD might also exhibit an ion transport defect resembling CF, and whether this is also present in the lower airway. To test this hypothesis, we are examining patients with COPD who do and do not smoke tobacco in comparison to otherwise healthy smokers without airflowobstruction and healthy controls. NPD findings have confirmed reduced Clconductance (consistent with mild CF) in healthy smokers and COPD patients who smoke.Asubset of COPD patients who have quit smoking also demonstrated reduced Cl- conductance. Subsequently, we have developed a new technique for measuring LAPD in adult subjects under conscious sedation. Using a modification of the agar catheter suitable for bronchoscopic placement, we have successfully measured LAPD (including baseline PD, amiloride sensitive PD, and change following lowCl- plus isoproterenol) in a consistent anatomic location (lingula outlet, a 4th generation airway) under conscious sedation. Tracings are readily interpreted and appear otherwise similar to NPD tracings, and have been successfully obtained in 17 of 19 individuals studied to date. Transbronchial biopsies for CFTR protein quantification and BAL samples are also collected following the LAPD procedure. In the subjects studied to date, LAPD findings demonstrate a reduction in Cl- conductance in affected (COPD) individuals. No patients studied have harbored CFTR mutations based on a commercially available assay (74 mutation analysis). These findings indicate the utility and feasibility of lower airway potential difference measurements obtained under conscious sedation in adults. In addition, emerging results suggest that patients with COPD may exhibit reduced CFTR Cl- transport activity in the upper and lower airway. The role of CFTR dysfunction in COPD deserves further study as a potential contributor to disease pathogenesis.
