N2  - Introduction: The diagnosis of lower airway infection in children with Cystic Fibrosis who cannot expectorate sputum is difficult. It relies on techniques which are unreliable (cough swabs) or unpleasant (induced sputum). Although flexible bronchoscopy in children is invasive and requires a general anaesthetic, it has been shown to be safe with a low incidence of complications. Due to this, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) samples taken during flexible bronchoscopy are increasingly being used to gain a definitive microbiological diagnosis in children with CF. We wanted to review our increased use of bronchoscopy in these patients to assess the impact on their management. Methods: A retrospective case note review was undertaken. It included all paediatric patients with CF who had undergone flexible bronchoscopy between May 2007 and May 2009. Results: We performed 39 bronchoscopies on 31 children with CF. The median age at bronchoscopy was 8.5 years (IQR: 5.3-11.5 years) and the median time from diagnosis to first bronchoscopy was 7 years (IQR 4.5-8.7 years). The main indication for bronchoscopy was the need for a definitive microbiological diagnosis in patients who had a clinical deterioration. Positive BAL cultures were obtained from 31 bronchoscopies. A total of 56 organisms were isolated, only 15 of these had been identified by surveillance cultures in the same patient over the preceding 4 months. First culture of Pseudomonas aeruginosa was obtained in 2 patients and first culture of Staphylococcus aureus in 5 patients. Positive cultures could not be correlated with symptoms prior to bronchoscopy or with the amount of secretions seen during the bronchoscopy. Patient management was altered due to bronchoscopy findings in 32 patients. Physiotherapy was altered in 27 patients (targeted in 17 and intensified in 10), antibiotic courses were changed in 15 patients and Dornase Alpha was commenced in 8 patients. Conclusions: This study has confirmed that flexible bronchoscopy is an important tool in the management of CF patients. BAL samples isolated a number of organisms that had not been identified from surveillance cultures; including Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus. The bronchoscopy findings caused a change in the management in most patients.
