N2  - Objective: To investigate the clinical characteristics of tuberculosis (TB) infection in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) patients. Methods: Medical records of 1057 SLE patients hospitalized in Xiangya hospital from 2003 to 2009 were reviewed. The collected data including the patient's age, gender, SLEDAI, relevant laboratory data, duration between SLE diagnosis and TB diagnosis, prednisolone dose used in the previous year, use of methylprednisolone pulse therapy, TB location, clinical manifestations of TB, therapeutic regimens and duration of anti-TB therapy, were recorded. To identify potential risk factors for the development of TB infection in SLE, 50 SLE patients without TB infection from the same database were selected as controls. Results: There were 52 patients (4.9%) proven TB infection in 1057 SLE patients, and 3 of 52 patients (5.8%) died of TB meningitis. 36 of 52 patients were pulmonary TB while seven patients had concomitant pulmonary and extra-pulmonary TB. Of the nine extra-pulmonary TB cases, there were TB meningitis in seven patients, and soft tissue involvement in two patients. The most common manifestations of TB were fever and cough. There were only 10 patients with positive acid-fast bacillus smear or histopathologic finding of caseating granuloma on specimens, but no patient had a positive culture of Mycobacterium tuberculosis from an appropriate specimen. The interval between the onset of SLE and TB diagnosis ranged from 1 to 240 months. Compared to SLE patients without TB infection, patients with TB infection were all receiving methylprednisolone pulse therapy and higher mean daily dose of prednisolone (>30 mg/day) during the previous year. Furthermore, all patients with TB infection received anti-TB combination therapy. The duration of TB treatment ranged from 9 to 24 months, and the mean daily dose of prednisolone was increased during taking rifampicin. Conclusion: The morbidity and mortality of TB infection in SLE patients is significantly higher than in general population, and the clinical manifestation is atypical. High dose pulse intravenous methylprednisolone therapy and higher mean dose of prednisolone within the previous year were both risk factors for TB infection. Once TB infection suspected, careful test and early tentative anti-TB therapy are crucial to cure.
