N2  - Background: Outbreaks of drug-resistant Mycobacterium tuberculosis (TB) in South Africa (SA) have demonstrated the clear and urgent need to evaluate infection control (IC) in TB care settings. Clinical settings that integrate patients with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) and TB require intensive evaluation to prevent the spread of TB. Objectives: To systematically evaluate administrative, environmental, and personal protective IC infrastructure within HIV clinics situated within drug-resistant TB hospitals as part of a larger IC evaluation. This is the first country-wide evaluation of IC in these clinics. Methods: A cross-sectional descriptive study was conducted from June to September 2009. All drug-resistant TB hospitals were eligible. A structured interview was completed with key personnel from IC, occupational health, hospital administration and HIV providers (as available). Investigators visually inspected and conducted environmental inspections in each site. Results: All 24 drug-resistant TB hospitals were enrolled in the overall evaluation; 15 (63%) had an HIV clinic; the majority (80%) were managed solely by the hospital. The following administrative infrastructure was identified: no HIV center had a written TBIC plan; 3 (20%) had triage upon entry; 3 (20%) moved coughing patients ahead of others; 4 (27%) masked coughing patients; 2 (13%) scheduled patients with drug-resistant TB on separate days; 11 (73%) collected sputa outside; 6 (40%) displayed patient education on cough hygiene. The following environmental infrastructure was identified: 4 (27%) had ultra-violet (UV) light; none had a UV maintenance plan; 4 (27%) had windows opened appropriately; 7 (47%) had outdoor covered waiting areas; 3 (20%) had operational extraction fans. The following personal protective infrastructure was identified: 10 (67%) had N-95 respirators, none offered fit testing and 3 (30%) had respirators in use at the time of visit. Conclusions: These findings indicate limited IC infrastructure in administrative, environmental and personal protective measures. The poor infrastructure findings are likely to facilitate both patient to patient and patient to healthcare worker transmission of drug-resistant TB. Urgent attention is required to remediate these findings and must include evidence-based, hands-on IC training for HIV clinical staff and IC preventionists who cover the HIV clinic.
