N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Recognize Mycobacterium tuberculosis as a cause of pericarditis. LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: Describe the identification and treatment of tuberculous pericarditis. CASE: A 26 year old man was admitted for the evaluation of shortness of breath. History elicited a one month decline in exercise tolerance, progressive shortness of breath, increasing abdominal girth, and an 11 pound weight loss. Fever, cough, and night sweats were absent. The patient was native to the United States and occasionally traveled to the Dominican Republic. Physical exam was remarkable for sinus tachycardia, tachypnea, jugular venous distension, diminished breath sounds at the left lung base, ascites, and the absence of organomegaly. Serum chemistry, LFTs, and CBC were unremarkable. PPD and HIV testing were negative. Echocardiogram revealed a pericardial mass with constrictive physiology. CT scan demonstrated bilateral pleural effusions with enhancement of the right pleura, moderatepericardial effusion, ascites with enhancement of the peritoneum, nodularity of the greater omentum, and diffuse lymphadenopathy. Thoracentesis with pleural biopsy yielded an exudative effusion without evidence of pathogens, malignancy, or granulomatous disease. Omental biopsy revealed multiple necrotizing granulomas and acid-fast bacilli. Cultures ultimately grew Mycobacterium tuberculosis. A presumptive diagnosis of tuberculous pericarditis was made. In addition to anti-mycobacterial therapy-narrowed to isoniazid and rifampin once susceptibility testing was completed-a course of corticosteroids was initiated. The patient's symptoms resolved and subsequent echocardiography exhibited resolution of the pericardial mass. DISCUSSION: Mycobacterium tuberculosis accounts for up to 70% of large pericardial effusions in the developing world but less than 4% in industrialized nations. Tuberculous pericarditis has a mortality rate of 8-34% with antibiotic treatment and 80-90% without. Early diagnosis and initiation of treatment is essential to halt progression to constrictive pericarditis and disseminated disease. Definitive diagnosis is established by demonstration of tubercle bacilli or caseating granulomata in pericardial fluid or pericardial biopsy. Presumptive diagnosis is based on pericarditis with evidence of tuberculosis elsewhere in the body, a lymphocytic pericardial exudate with elevated biomarkers of tuberculous infection (adenosine deaminase activity, interferon gamma, or lysozyme assay), and/or good response to anti-tuberculosis therapy. A total of six months of anti-mycobacterial therapy is recommended: consisting of two months of four drug regimen (isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol) followed by four months of isoniazid and rifampin. There are three clinical presentations of tuberculous pericarditis: pericardial effusion (80% of cases), constrictive pericarditis (5% of cases), or effusive-constrictive pericarditis (15% of cases). In this case, the patient was diagnosed during the constrictive phase by echocardiogram. The treatment of constrictive pericarditis consists of standard anti-mycobacterial drugs for six months and pericardiectomy for persistent constriction in the face of drug therapy. Both the timing of pericardiectomy and use of steroids in constrictive pericarditis remain controversial. However, pericardiectomy is recommended for the following instances: 1. No improvement or deterioration in hemodynamic status after four to eight weeks of drug therapy 2. Development of pericardial calcifications suggesting chronic disease.
