N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Generate an appropriate differential diagnosis for neck abscesses and determine appropriate management. LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: Recognize scrofula and understand the appropriate therapeutic and precautionary procedures. CASE: A 31-year-old South Asian man, with an HIV(-) test within the past year, noted multiple right-sided neck masses for two months prior. The masses increased in size and number and became painful, at which point he sought medical care. The masses were incised and drained with resulting purulent material, and he began a 5-day course of cephalexin. His symptoms did not improve, and a 10-day course of trimethoprimsulfamethoxazole and ciprofloxacin was prescribed. Despite this, his symptoms worsened, and he presented again with subjective fevers, chills, sore throat, rhinorrhea, 3-month history of malaise and 20-lb weight loss. He reported no cough, dyspnea, dysphagia or changes in bowel habits. He worked as a truck driver, immigrated to the US from India >10 years ago and lived with his brother-in-law who emigrated from India a few months ago. He reported no history of tuberculosis (TB) or recent sick contacts, no IVDU. He was sexually active with barrier protection 100% of the time. CT scan of his neck revealed numerous fluid collections and necrotic lymph nodes in the right jugular chain, supraclavicular fossa and axilla. The following day he became febrile and had a fine-needle aspiration (FNA) of a node which eventually revealed Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection, however, he was discharged before final culture results and lost to follow-up. DISCUSSION: Scrofula is cervical tuberculous lymphadenitis, and approximately 95% of the adult cases are caused by M. tuberculosis, with half of those occurring in immunocompetent adults. The increased incidence of TB lymphadenitis in the US is thought to be due to increased emigration from endemic countries and the rising population of HIV-infected individuals. The differential diagnosis of isolated peripheral, particularly cervical, lymphadenopathy includes malignancy and infectious sources including HIV, mycobacterium and mononucleosis. Imaging can be useful for staging of cancers and often assists in defining the size and distribution of affected nodes for guiding FNA or biopsy. Diagnosis of TB lymphadenitis is based on culture of lymph node material and can be obtained via FNA which appears to be as effective as and less invasive than excisional biopsy, although data are limited. Surgical excision of the affected nodes is not recommended due to high rates of recurrence with formulation of fistulae and risk for seeding infection to other organs. Since TB lymphadenitis may present with existing pulmonary TB, patients should be placed in negatively-ventilated isolation rooms with respiratory precautions. A proper diagnosis of scrofula will further allow the internist to screen the patient's family through PPD testing with appropriate treatment to avoid long-term sequelae. All patients with scrofula should undergo HIV testing. Treatments are similar to those for pulmonary TB and consist of 6- 9 months of antitubercular chemotherapy including rifampin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide, with consideration of patient immune status, organism resistance profile and drug tolerability. At initiation of therapy a paradoxical but temporary increase in size or number of nodes occurs in up to 30% of patients and is likely due to immune response.
