N2  - Introduction Actinomycosis is a bacterial infection caused by filamentous, gram positive bacteria that are not acid fast. In humans, most frequently it is caused by Actinomyces israelii. Endobronchial actinomycosis is a rare condition and is usually associated with some underlying risk factor, but may also occur in the absence of any such risk factor. It presents with features that mimic a bronchial neoplasm but responds to treatment with antibiotics. Description A 68-year-old female non smoker with a background of atrial fibrillation was referred to respiratory clinic with a three month history of productive cough, dyspnoea, loss of weight and appetite and a chest x-ray showing opacification of the left upper and mid zones. General and respiratory system examination was normal. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate was raised. Sputum cultures yielded normal flora and no acid-fast bacilli was identified on smear or culture. CT scan of chest revealed a small central (15mm in diameter) soft tissue mass occluding the origin of the left upper lobe bronchus (staged T2N0M0). At bronchoscopy, a necrotic appearing growth was seen partially obstructing the upper lobe bronchus. Biopsy specimen showed inflamed bronchial mucosa but there was no evidence of malignancy. Cultures sent for acid-fast bacilli and tuberculosis was negative. Bronchoscopy was repeated and granular yellowish pus was seen discharging from behind the mass. Cultures confirmed Actinomyces. She was given a course of oral coamoxyclav and was treated as an outpatient. A repeat CT scan of chest and bronchoscopy after three months of oral antibiotics showed reduction in the size (10mm) of mass with partial resolution of surrounding consolidation. As she continued to improve clinically no further treatment or intervention was necessary. A repeat CT chest six months later showed complete disappearance of the mass. Bronchoscopy a year on showed normal looking bronchial mucosa and no mass was visible. Discussion This case has many unique features. Differentiating from a neoplasm was important as this prevented an unnecessary surgery with its accompanying risks. It is important for physicians to remember this possibility although it may be uncommon. The patient was treated with oral antibiotics without the need for parenteral antibiotics. This enabled treatment to be carried out entirely at home without the need for hospitalisation. Regular follow up was done with CT scan and bronchoscopy and radiological and bronchoscopic clearing took almost a year although clinical improvement was evident within three months.
