N2  - In pediatric patients, the hypothesis of tuberculosis (TB) should be investigated in the presence of slowly progressing symptoms that do not improve with conventional treatment, or in the case of contact with patients with TB, irrespective of the presence of symptoms. Objectives: To describe the implantation of a TB control program between July 2002 and January 2009 in a population of 10,000 children aged 0 to 10 years living in the shanty town of Paraisópolis. The Sociedade Beneficente Israelita Brasileira Albert Einstein maintains a not-for-profit healthcare service in this community and this program is part of a strategy to reduce morbidity related to respiratory diseases. Methods: Systematic investigation of patients with chronic or recurrent respiratory manifestations showing an unfavorable outcome after treatment or with a history of contact with TB. Chest X-rays and the tuberculin skin test (TST) were performed in these situations. Chest computed tomography (CT), bacilloscopy and culture might be performed according to clinical criteria. The guidelines proposed by the Brazilian Ministry of Health were adopted for diagnosis and management. A system for surveillance was created. The supervised administration of medications five times per week was adapted to the child's daily routine. Results: A total of 483 patients with a mean age of 4.38 years were studied. Of these, 69.9% reported contact with TB or adult with chronic cough. One-hundred (20.7%) cases of TB were identified and 82 (16.9%) patients received prophylactic treatment. Recurrent wheezing was the clinical presentation showing the highest chance of a diagnosis of TB [odds ratio = 2.14 (1.187-3.416)]. TST greater than 10 mm indicated the need for therapeutic intervention, with a sensitivity of 77% and specificity of 83%. A total of 438 chest X-rays (36.1% abnormal) and 59 CT scans (64.4% abnormal) were performed. Bacilloscopy was positive in 1.17% of 767 gastric aspirate samples collected. Mycobacterium was isolated from 7.21% of 721 cultures of gastric aspirate samples. The scoring system proposed by the Ministry of Health for the diagnosis of TB was found to be useful in clinical practice. Individual treatment adapted to the age and daily routine of the child and his family did not compromise therapeutic success and resulted in high compliance rates. Conclusion: The implantation of a TB control program is mandatory for populations in which the prevalence of the disease is high. The strategy adopted resulted in a high rate of therapeutic intervention and resolution of cases.
