N2  - Introduction: A diagnosis of Cystic Fibrosis (CF) is based on consistent clinical symptoms combined with evidence of CFTR protein-channel dysfunction confirmed by an abnormal sweat chloride concentration or nasal potential difference. Although an elevated sweat chloride levels remains the gold standard for confirming a diagnosis of CF, diagnostic ambiguity remains problematic in some pancreatic sufficient individuals cases screened through newborn screening. Case summary: A 15 year old Caucasian male presented to our clinic with a 3 week history of productive cough, shortness of breath, intermittent fevers, chills, and night sweats. Past medical history indicated that he was born full-term in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. An IRT (immunoreactive trypsinogen) performed in 1993 when patient was 6.5 weeks old, as part of newborn screening, was elevated at 189ng/ml. Sweat chloride testing at that time was 31.3mEq/L and 28.4mEq/L with an adequate amount of sweat obtained. CFTR mutation analysis showed one delta F508 mutation. There was no family history of CF. There was exposure to cigarette smoke in the home. Physical exam: Growth parameters: weight 55.3 kg 31st %, height 178.2 cm 76%, and BMI 17.4, 4th %. Cachectic appearance; other positive findings included boggy nasal turbinates with no apparent polyps, distant breath sounds bilaterally, slightly diminished in the apices; no wheezes or crackles were appreciated; abdomen was scaphoid without hepatosplenomegaly, extremities showed grade 3 clubbing. Lab data: A sputum culture grew mucoid Pseudomonas Aeruginosa, and few Staphyloccus Aureus. A chest X-ray showed bronchiectasis. Repeated sweat chloride test was elevated: 77.4 mEq/L and 91.3 mEq/L. Pulmonary Function Testing -FVC 102 % of predicted, FEV1 86%, FEV1/FVC ratio 73, FEF25-75% was 58% of predicted. Lung Volumes: TLC 138% of predicted, RV 218%, RV/TLC ratio 32. There findings were consistent with airway obstruction with significant air trapping. Repeat genetic testing for CF showed: delta F508 and 3849+10kbC>T mutations. Discussion: We suspect that the initial newborn screening evaluation as well as concurrent findings of pancreatic sufficiency, asthma and sinusitis delayed the CF diagnosis. Using current diagnostic criteria for CF, this patient would have been correctly classified as having CF with pancreatic sufficiency. We call attention to this case to discuss the current recommendations for evaluation and follow up of an elevated IRT upon newborn screening and the current diagnostic criteria for CF in the context of new cutoff values for a positive sweat chloride test for newborn screening (< 6 months, > 6 months of age).
