N2  - Agency for Health Protection and Promotion (OAHPP), Toronto, Canada, 5Ontario Agency for Health Protection and Promotion (OAHPP), Toronto, Canada Rationale: Mycobacterium avium (MA) and Mycobacterium intracellulare (MI) are often grouped together as the MA-MI complex (MAC). MA and MI can now be differentiated by molecular methods. Limited and conflicting data are available regarding the value of this distinction. We sought to investigate the relative frequency of MA versus MI, and differences in disease presentation in Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Methods: At the clinic level, data were retrospectively collected for 127 patients from two out-patient clinics at two academic teaching hospitals in Toronto. All patients reviewed had positive cultures for MA or MI between January 2008 and June 2009. At the population level, basic demographics were collected from the Ontario Public Health Laboratory for all 1433 patients in Ontario with MA or MI isolates in 2008. Results: In the clinics, MA was isolated from 114 patients (90%), and MI from 13 (10%). The average age of patients was 60 years or greater in both groups, and the majority were female (63% MA, 85% MI, p=0.21). Most had underlying pulmonary disease (MA 79%, MI 85%, p=0.91). The majority had cough (MA 93%, MI 77%, p=0.15), but more patients with MA had sputum production. (74% MA, 31% MI, p=0.004). Most patients had obstruction on pulmonary function testing (MA 61%, MI 62%, p=0.98). MA and MI patients had a similar proportion of nodular-bronchiectasis and fibrocavitary disease on CT imaging. A similar proportion in each group had a specimen that was positive on AFB smear (MA 47%, MI 46%, p=0.93). Many patients had at least one culture positive for a different mycobacterial species (MA 34%, MI 54%, p=0.28). Long-term pharmacotherapy was prescribed for 48% of patients with MA, versus 31% with MI (p=0.37). At the population level MA was isolated in 87% (1251) and MI in 13% (182). MA patients were younger (mean(sd) 66(16) years versus 71(13), p<0.0001). Both groups were mostly female, but the MA group contained a lower fraction of females (53% versus 67%, p=0.0004). Patients in the MA group less often fulfilled ATS microbiologic criteria for disease (49% versus 58%, p=0.03). Conclusions: The vast majority of pulmonary MAC isolates and clinical cases of pulmonary MAC disease in Ontario are due to MA. Patients with MA isolates tend to be younger, have a lower likelihood of being female, and less often fulfill ATS microbiologic criteria than patients with MI. Detailed clinical analysis was limited by the paucity of MI cases.
