N2  - Introduction: Tuberculosis (TB) is a very contiguous disease, with high Morbidity and Mortality of a Worldwide Epidemic. In many industrialized countries, tuberculosis has failed to decline, cases and deaths are increasing. Drug resistance is a serious problem, especially in the United States. And because of vague clinical presentation and high potency of the disease, the numbers of cases and mortality is expected to continue rising, 8 million new cases a year, with 1.6 million TB related death a year, in 2007. Case Report: I am reporting a case of a 25 year old male, with no significant history, presented to our hospital with non productive cough and fever for one month. Was seen in Emergency Room (ER) 3 weeks before presentation with negative chest X ray (CXR), was diagnosed with viral bronchitis and discharged home. On current ER visit His chest x ray (CXR) showed a moderate left pleural effusion and left basilar opacity. So was started on CAP coverage and admitted to the hospital. PPD which was placed was positive. AFB Sputum, Gram stain, cultures were all negative. CAT scan of thorax showed large left sided pleural effusion, increasing in size, with secondary compressive atelectasis of the left lung. Pleurocentesis was done, and chemical analysis of fluids showed an exudative lymphocytic fluid, ADA assay was 10.4, but all pleural fluid culture, cytology, and pathology were negative. The patient was started on anti-TB medications. And thoracic surgery took the patient for Left thoracoscopy with partial pulmonary decortication and pleural biopsies, which showed Granulomas, with Immunostain and Ziehl-Neelsen stain, are negative for mycobacterium. So he was discharged on anti-TB medications, for full active TB treatment. Discussion: Pleural TB is second most common extrapulmonary TB site after lymph node involvement, and it is a rare presentation of tuberculosis in USA, more common in third world countries. Rarely associated with parenchymal lesion, only 1/3 of the cases. Usually diagnosed with a diagnostic thoracentesis with mononuclear cell dominant exudate fluid, AFB rarely seen, and only 1/3 of case has positive cultures, usually pleural biopsy shows Granulomas with high yield of positive culture up to 70% of cases.and usually treated as active TB.
