N2  - Background: Mycobacterium microti, a naturally occurring organism affecting mainly rodents, llamas and other small mammals, rarely causes tuberculosis in humans. Here we describe an immunocompetent patient with severe and prolonged pulmonary tuberculosis identified as M.microti and treated successfully with conventional antituberculous therapy. CASE REPORT A previously healthy 36-year-old Caucasian male was referred after an incidental finding of a left apical opacity on a chest radiograph. The patient had a persistent cough for several months, without any symptoms of weight loss, loss of appetite or haemoptysis. He had no history of contact with hazardous substances, tuberculosis or foreign travel. He was a smoker of 30 cigarettes a day. A bronchoscopy was planned as an outpatient and follow-up arranged. Subsequently the patient presented to the accident and emergency department with haemoptysis. A chest radiograph revealed progression of the left apical shadowing previously noted. A bronchoscopy with lavage was negative for acid-fast bacilli (AFB). CT scanning demonstrated loss of volume of the upper lobe of the left lung with extensive fibrotic and bronchiectatic changes thought to be secondary to chronic infection. Symptoms recurred 8 months later and sputum sample tested positive for AFB. Quadruple therapy was commenced but compliance was poor. Mycobacterium microti was isolated. Patient moved away and failed to keep his follow up appointments. 2 months later he was referred back with worsening cough and further sputum samples continued to grow M.microti. A repeat CT scan showed marked deterioration since previous imaging. and quadruple therapy was re-started and continued for a total of 6 months. Significant improvements were noted both symptomatically and radiologically. The persistent cough was dramatically reduced and no further episodes of haemoptysis had occurred. In addition, there was marked resolution of the left upper lobe lesions on the chest radiograph. He currently remains asymptomatic and well. CONCLUSION This is a rare case of human M.microti infection, occurring in the absence of an immunological abnormality or prior exposure to animals. The isolation and growth of M.microti is particularly difficult. In addition, hidden hereditary defects in interleukin receptor function may help explain why an organism thought to be avirulent in humans, has been largely overlooked. As demonstrated here, M.microti disease responds well to treatment with traditional antituberculous therapy. Future studies based on molecular identification would further enhance the understanding of this emerging pathogen and its epidemiology in human tuberculosis infection.
