N2  - Introduction: Rifampin (R) and pyrazinamide (Z) are the key sterilizing drugs in TB treatment. In the mouse model, RZ is more active than RZ + isoniazid (H), indicating antagonism by H. This antagonism is potentially important because the removal of H's negative influence is primarily responsible for the beneficial effect of substituting moxifloxacin for H in the first-line RZH regimen in mice, a therapeutic strategy that is now being studied in a Phase 3 clinical trial. To better understand the antagonism, we compared the activity of H, RZ and RZH in mice infected with isogenic mutants of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) with increasing resistance to H. Our hypothesis was that the antagonism of H is dependent upon the anti-Mtb effect of H such that, paradoxically, decreasing bacterial susceptibility to H would result in greater activity of RZH. Methods: Mice were aerosol-infected with 4.05-4.44 log10 CFU of 1 of 4 isogenic Mtb strains: H37Rv parent, inhA T(-8)A mutant, katG W149R mutant, and katG M1A mutant for which H MICs are 0.03, 0.25, 4 and 16 μg/ml, respectively. Treatment began 17 days later (Day 0), consisting of H 10mg/kg alone (Cmax, 7.5μg/ml), R 10mg/kg plus Z 150mg/kg, or RHZ. Negative controls went untreated. Five mice/group were sacrificed after 1, 2, 4 and 8 weeks of treatment for lung CFU counts. Results: Except for the katG M1A mutant, all strains were lethal for untreated mice, indicating their virulence. Monotherapy with H prevented death in all mice infected with H37Rv and the inhA T(-8)A mutant. Five of 20 mice infected with the katG W149R mutant died despite treatment with H. CFU counts from Days 0 and 56 are displayed in the table. (Table Presented) Conclusions: Against the highly H-resistant katG mutants, RZ and RZH had similar activity, as expected. Paradoxically, RZH was less active than RZ as the strain's susceptibility to H increased. The antagonistic effect of H was plainly evident only against strains with no or low-level H resistance, indicating that functional KatG is required to activate H in order to produce the effect. These data exclude a pharmacokinetic interaction as the cause of the antagonism.
