N2  - Introduction: A consortium of US academic institutions supported by the COPD Foundation created the Bronchiectasis Research Registry as a consolidated database of non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis patients to support collaborative research and facilitate future therapeutic clinical trials in bronchiectasis. Methods: The Registry, maintained by the Coordinating Center at UNC in Chapel Hill, consists of a secure, web-based data management and interactive reporting system described in detail in a separate abstract. Using the Registry's standard reporting feature, the database was queried for demographic and clinical variables that may be of interest for clinical trial enrollment criteria. Results: Clinical information was available for standard query on 283 patients. Symptoms reported from 269 patients included daily cough in 206 (77%), productive cough in 165 (62%) and hemoptysis in 70 (26%). Of 247 patients with PFT's, pre-bronchodilator mean % predicted FEV1 was 68±SD21% and mean FVC was 76±19%. Almost half had dilated airways and/or thickened airway walls involving 3 or more lobes on chest CT scan and 14% required supplemental oxygen. Respiratory cultures grew bacteria from 95% of the 230 patients cultured: oropharyngeal flora from 190 (87%); Pseudomonas aeruginosa from 68 (31%), and Staphylococcus aureus from 19 (9%). Overall, 74 (35%) of 211 patients cultured grew mycobacteria; 76% of these met the ATS diagnostic criteria. Species included M. avium complex from 55 (73%), M. abscessus from 8 (11%), and M. kansasii from 1 patient. Most patients reported airway clearance therapies, including inhaled bronchodilators (61%), bronchial hygiene measures (60%), and aerobic exercise (52%). Antibiotic use varied and included use of antibiotics for acute exacerbations only (49%), continuous suppressive oral antibiotics (29%), and rotating oral antibiotics (7%). Aerosolized antibiotic use was reported in 11%. Anti-inflammatory therapy included inhaled steroid use in 46% and oral steroids in 13%. Gastric acid suppression was utilized by 42%. Conclusions: This bronchiectatic patient population represents an ideal source for evaluation of new therapies: these patients suffer from high rates of pulmonary symptoms, with high frequency of bacterial and mycobacterial infection. Most are currently using pharmaceutical and non-pharmaceutical therapeutic measures for disease management.
