N2  - Introduction: Bronchiectasis is defined as abnormal dilatation of bronchi associated with bronchial wall thickening, distortion and inflammation which leads to chronic mucous production. Previously termed as an orphan disease non-CF bronchiectasis is now increasingly recognized as a major cause of respiratory morbidity in the adult population. Methods: A retrospective study of patients who were diagnosed with non-CF bronchiectasis (1999-2006) was conducted. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of our institute. Data regarding age, sex, sputum, tracheal and bronchoalveolar washing/lavage Gram stain and cultures, PFTs were obtained on 189 patients during the study period. Results: There were 139 (73%) women and 50 (27%) men with a diagnosis of bronchiectasis with an average age of 61.4 years. Most patients presented with cough in 93 % (177), and sputum 84% (160). Other symptoms included dyspnea 49% (92), hemoptysis 20% (38) with 8% of patients with a history of sinusitis. From the time of onset of symptoms on an average it took over 3.3 years before the diagnosis was established. Eighty eight percent of the patients were diagnosed by CT scan and in 12% the diagnosis was based on a CXR. 67 patients (35%) recalled a childhood infection of which 46 were diagnosed with Pneumonia, 12 with TB and 6 with Pertusis. Only 2 patients had a family history of bronchiectasis. Twenty three percent of patients had normal PFTs whereas 36% showed an obstructive defect, 29% had restriction, and 12% had combined obstructive and restrictive defect. Analysis of sputum, tracheal and bronchial washings showed that 9% were colonized with P.aeruginosa and S.Pneumoniae, 4% with H.Influenzae and 9% had Stomatocccus Mucilaginous isolated. Atypical mycobacteria were isolated in 12% of patients of whom 8% were found to have MAI. Conclusion: In conclusion our data shows that the diagnosis is frequently delayed in many patients. Our data also shows that patients with bronchiectasis may have a predominant restrictive defect on lung function test. In addition we found an interesting pattern of microbial pathogens from the lower respiratory tract of patients with non-CF bronchiectasis compared to previously reported pathogens in these patients. A significantly high percentage of patients were found to have S.mucilaginous which is very interesting as these bacteria are reported to form biofilms like P.aeruginosa. S. mucilaginous is often under-reported as a pathogen due to difficulties in obtaining samples for culture as well as misidentification due to its biochemical characteristics.
