N2  - Bronchiectatic patients have chronic cough, sputum production, and recurrent pulmonary infections with progressive pulmonary function decline. Pseudomonas species are one of the most prevalent organisms causing infection. Non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (NCFB) patients with Pseudomonas infections have a worse quality of life, more extensive bronchiectasis and an accelerated decline in lung function. Pseudomonas species have the ability to develop antibiotic resistance after few exposures, making treatment difficult. Objective: To examine bacteria recovered from respiratory cultures in NCFB patients and evaluate resistance patterns of Pseudomonas. Method: Retrospective analysis was done on NCFB patients from 2001 to 2007. Pseudomonal resistance to quinolones, carbapenems, aminoglycosides, extended-spectrum beta-lactams (ESBL), aztreonam, and cephalosporins was evaluated. Isolates were labeled resistant to a class if they demonstrated resistance to at least one antipseudomonal antibiotic in the above classes. Those with intermediate sensitivity were labeled as resistant. Findings: Pseudomonas species were the most common organism recovered in 16/36 (44.4 %) of patients that grew nonmycobacterial bacteria. The next most common organisms were Staphylococcus aureus, Hemophilus pnumoniae, and beta-hemolytic Streptococcus. Pseudomonas species had a 54.1% rate of resistance to quinolones in 2001-2004 compared to 30.7% resistance in 2005-2007 (p=0.012). Carbapenem resistance was 48.6% in 2001-2004 and 16.9% in 2005-2007 (p=<0.001). Aminoglycoside resistance was 32.4% in 2001-2004 and 63.6% in 2005-2007 (p=0.001). The resistance to ESBLs was 56.8% in 2001-2004 and 23.4% in 2005-2007 (p=0.001). When only piperacillin/tazobactam was analyzed the resistance was 23.3% and 3.8% in 2001-2004 and 2007 respectively (p=0.01). Cephalosporin resistance was 54.1% in 2001-2004 and 38.3% in 2007 (p=0.079). Aztreonam resistance was 42.9% and 33.3% (p=0.463) respectively. Conclusion: Pseudomonas was the predominant bacteria isolated from the respiratory tract of NCFB patients. These organisms demonstrated significant antibiotic resistance. This data suggests that empiric therapy with an oral quinolone should not be recommended. Empiric antibiotic therapy for bronchiectasis should include a carbapenem or ESBL with consideration of dual anti-pseudomonal therapy. The decreasing resistance rate for most antibiotics was likely due to fewer patients in the earlier group with more severe disease and more frequent antibiotic exposure. Increasing aminoglycoside resistance may be due to increased use of aminoglycosides at our institution treating multidrug resistant Acinetobacter in returning troops. The views expressed in this abstract are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the official policy or position of the Departmentof the Navy, Department of Defense, nor the U.S. Government.
