N2  - Illness from the novel H1N1 influenza has been widespread in California since its recognition in April 2009. Nationwide, hundreds of children have required inpatient care, but the characteristics of this population, co-morbidities, severity and outcomes have not been described in the US. We report here on all children admitted to Pediatric services at our large regional medical center in northern California with documented Influenza A/H1N1 from the time this illness was first recognized to the present (5/29/09 to 10/30/09). RESULTS: 46 children required admission, 21 (46%) to our PICU and 25 to acute care beds. 29 were males and 17 females. The average age was 5.9 years. Only 35% of children were white not Hispanic, in a region where this group constitutes 56% of the population. 28% of families had limited English proficiency. 54% of children received health insurance benefits from state/public sources. Average weight was 50th percentile. Six (13%) of children presented with fever and seizures or mental status change, and only 3 of these also had respiratory symptoms. 11 of 21 required positive pressure ventilator support, including 2 in which NIV was exclusively used. The average LOS was 7.3 days, but ranged from 1 to > 60 days. All but 3 were treated with systemic oseltamivir. 35 had pulmonary radiographic abnormalities documented, and 35 had respiratory symptoms ranging from cough and rhinnorhea to ARDS on admission: this latter child had burn injuries and was treated with a prolonged course of ECLS with good outcome. 3 children required VATS for empyema or other pleural disease. Asthma was the single most commonly reported past medical problem, in 10 children, but 25 others had a range of other significant co-morbidities, including seizure disorders, cystic fibrosis, renal transplantation, sickle cell disease, developmental disorders, chromosomal abnormalities, leukemia, thyrotoxicosis, Prader-Willi, Wilms' tumor, spina bifida, incarcerated inguinal hernia, colitis and heart block with pacer malfunction. Nine children (20%) had no co-morbidities. Secondary bacterial infection was identified in 13%. Data collection and analysis is ongoing. CONCLUSIONS: Compared to case series of adults with H1N1, more boys than girls require hospital admission. Non-white Hispanics are disproportionately represented in this hospitalized series. The majority of children requiring hospitalization with H1N1 related illnesses have underlying diseases. There is a broad spectrum of illness, and only respiratory failure on admission predicted a more severe course of illness in our patient population.
