N2  - Introduction: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), or extrinsic allergic alveolitis, is a rare, immune-mediated form of interstitial lung disease in children. Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is an acid-fast bacillus (AFB) that is ubiquitous in soil and water and is known to cause HP in adults. We report a case of a 20-month-old child with HP due to MAC from the home shower head. Case Report: A previously healthy 20-month-old Caucasian girl presented with progressive tachypnea and non-productive cough of two months duration. Eight weeks prior to presentation, she had been treated with antibiotics for clinical pneumonia. Despite this treatment, she experienced progressive tachypnea, respiratory distress, hypoxemia, and 3lb weight loss over a two-month period. She was admitted to our hospital for evaluation and management. On physical examination she was afebrile with RR 60-80/min and O2 saturations 88-92%. She had nasal flaring, subcostal, and supraclavicular retractions. However, no wheezing or crackles were noted. Chest x-ray (CXR) showed bilateral diffuse haziness (Figure 1A). Chest CT showed diffuse ground-glass opacities with small segments of preserved lung in the left upper lobe and bilateral bases (Figure 1B). Initial infectious disease, cardiac and immunologic evaluations were negative. A lung biopsy showed granulomatous interstitial pneumonia with granulomatous bronchiolitis and interstitial lymphoplasmacytic inflammation positive for acid fast bacilli (Figure 2A, 2B). AFB were present on repeat sections, as well as within macrophages and giant cells. Immunohistochemistry and special stains were negative. Lung tissue culture grew MAC. Water sources in the home (shower head, two humidifiers) grew MAC. Random amplified polymorphic DNA PCR testing revealed that the shower head strain and the lung tissue strain were identical. The patient was treated with pulse methylprednisolone weekly for three weeks. All symptoms resolved with treatment and avoidance of the water source. Repeat CXR 24 days after initial treatment was normal. The family moved to avoid any further exposure. The patient has remained asymptomatic. Discussion: We describe a previously healthy child with HP secondary to MAC isolated from both lung tissue and home shower head. To our knowledge, this is the first case of HP secondary to MAC reported in a child this young. While rare, HP has characteristic signs and symptoms that are important for clinicians to identify to ensure prompt treatment and recovery. The patient improved dramatically with pulse corticosteroids and avoidance. Notably, she was not treated for MAC and has remained asymptomatic since avoiding the major source of exposure. (Figure presented).
