N2  - Rationale: The novel 2009 H1N1 pandemic resulted in a peak of greater than 800 emergency care visits a day at the Children's Medical Center in Dallas (CMCD) in September, but resulted in only 200 admissions; 15 went to the Pediatric Intensive Care Unit (PICU). The characteristics of patients admitted to the PICU were reviewed to determine risk factors for poor outcome. Methods: A retrospective chart review of 15 patients admitted to the PICU in September 2009 was performed. Patient demographics, presenting symptoms, pre-existing medical conditions, medications and hospital course were compiled. Results: The patients were 73% male with a median age of 8 years (2 pts < 1yo, others 6-11yrs). The presenting symptoms of the school-aged children included fever (92%), increased WOB (77%), cough (69%) and GI symptoms (23%). In contrast, the infants presented with apnea, lethargy and hypothermia (50%; no fever). The majority of school-aged children admitted to the PICU had pre-existing conditions including asthma (63%), neurological conditions (23%), prematurity, cardiac diseases and immunodeficiency; 23% were previously healthy. Both infants were born prematurely. The majority (53%) of all patients were diagnosed with influenza on rapid screen. The initial chest radiograph findings among school-aged children included pneumonia (62%) and atelectasis (15%); no abnormalities were noted in 23%. The infants had chronic lung changes and atelectasis. Only 54% of the school-aged patients required invasive ventilatory support; 23% required non-invasive ventilation and 23% required oxygen therapy alone; 50% of infants required invasive ventilation. Thirty-three percent of all patients required inotropes and 80% of those had positive bacterial cultures. Endotracheal tube cultures were positive for MRSA (25%), pneumococcus (13%), MSSA (13%) and gram-negative rods (13%). Two patients died (both M: <1 and 9yo); one patient was placed on ECMO (10 yo M) and survived after a protracted course. Most patients (93%, 14/15) received antibiotics with the exception of the 9 yo patient who died. No steriods were given to patients that were not known asthmatics (except the infant who died). Only one patient, the 9 yo who died, received the first dose of oseltamivir > 24 hours after diagnosis. All received 5 or more days of oseltamivir. Conclusions: H1N1 causes severe respiratory disease resulting in ICU admission in ∼2% of the patients that presented to the CMCD emergency department with influenza-like illness. Risk factors for PICU admission included male gender and asthma. Super-infections with gram-positive organisms resulted in hemodynamic lability and poor outcome.
