N2  - Introduction Amyloidosis is characterized by the deposition of abnormally folded proteins in extra-cellular tissue creating insoluble protein fibril aggregates in normal tissue. Under crossed polarized light, amyloid produces apple-green birefringence following Congo red staining. Amyloidosis may be confused radiographically with carcinoma, pulmonary edema, diffuse lung fibrosis or tuberculosis. Although infrequent in occurrence, this case exhibits amyloidosis primarily affecting the lung manifesting as cavitary nodules. Case Presentation A healthy 25 year-old Taiwanese graduate student presented with a history of positive PPD test leading to an abnormal chest x-ray exhibiting an abnormal parenchymal opacity in the left mid-lung. The patient denied cough, sputum production, fevers, chills, anorexia, night sweats and weight loss, as well as past tuberculosis exposure and toxic or chemical exposures. Past medical history was significant for depression and family history was unremarkable for pulmonary disease, malignancy or auto-immune conditions. Blood work revealed a positive ANA and elevated atypical P-ANCA. Quantiferon-gold testing was negative, as were sputum and blood cultures. Chest CT scan revealed bilateral cavitary lung nodules without adenopathy, which was concerning for tuberculosis. A bronchoscopy demonstrated normal appearing airways; lavage fluid had no growth in viral, fungal, bacterial and AFB cultures. A TB PCR test was negative and cytology revealed no malignant cells. Open lung wedge biopsies demonstrated nodular aggregates of eosinophilic material and peri-vascular amyloid deposition strongly positive on Congo red staining, in addition an apple green birifringence to polarized light. These results were diagnostic for nodular parenchymal pulmonary amyloidosis. Because of its benign course and the patient's asymptomatic state, no treatment was elected aside from close follow-up. Discussion Localized pulmonary involvement in amyloidosis (without systemic lesions) is rare and is generally either tracheobronchial or parenchymal in location(1). Parenchymal pulmonary amyloidosis is generally asymptomatic, presenting as nodules varying in size and number, unilateral or bilateral (2). Nodules may resemble indolent or cavitating lung tumors or infections (2). Once diagnosed (usually by resection of one nodule to exclude malignancy), further treatment is generally unnecessary. This case presentation demonstrates that pulmonary amyloidosis can present asymptomatically with cavitary lung nodules.
