N2  - Introduction Despite universal immunization with PCV, sporadic cases of invasive pneumococcal infection occur. We describe a patient with fulminant disease manifesting as empyema and pericardial effusion with tamponade. Case SR is 2 year old girl with obesity and mild asthma. She presented with 6 days of fever, cough, tachypnea, vomiting and abdominal pain. She was admitted to the PICU for severe respiratory distress requiring BiPAP. CXR revealed opacification of the left hemithorax consistent with consolidation and pleural effusion. Thoracosotomy tube was placed revealing purulent fluid- pH 6.6, glucose =2 gram/dl, WBC= 12,500. Microscopy had many lancet shaped gram positive diplococci although the culture did not grow. She progressed requiring intubation, fluid resuscitation and inotropic, vasopressor and cortisol support. Her renal function worsened with elevated BUN and decreased urine output. CVVH was initiated for fluid overload. Concurrently her platelet count fell to <10,000 k/uL and her LDH =944 U/L suggestive of hemolytic uremic syndrome. However, there was no other evidence of hemolysis and her renal function quickly improved. On HD#6 she was extubated and although tachypneic (RR=40) and tachycardic (HR=130), she was oxygenating well on FiO2= 0.40. On HD#7, she developed worsened tachycardia, increased CVP and hepatomegaly. Electrocardiogram demonstrated electrical alternans (Fig1). Echocardiogram showed a pericardial effusion with fibrinous strands, present in pockets with sizes between 22mm (posterolateral) and 3mm (inferior) (Fig2). With right atrial diastolic collapse and the small amount of fluid visualized, constrictive pericardial effusion with tamponade was diagnosed. The small inferior effusion made percutaneous drainage difficult. An emergent surgical pericardotomy was performed where 150cc of purulent fluid under tension was evacuated. She immediately improved. She was extubated on HD#11 and chest and pericardial tubes were removed. On HD#13 she was breathing room air. Discussion In the modern antibiotic era, pyogenic pericarditis is rare. Mortality remains high (40%) due to tamponade, constriction, and sepsis. Pericarditis usually is a complication of infection originating elsewhere in the body. Most often, the bacteria infect the pericardium by contiguous extension from the lung, pleura or mediastinum. Less commonly, infection spreads hematogenously or through penetrating wounds. Although purulent pericarditis may have typical signs/symptoms of tamponade, this physiology can result from relatively small amounts of thick fluid. Purulence can also lead to loculation and fibrin deposition making percutaneous drainage incomplete; thus, predisposing to constrictive pericarditis. As demonstrated by our case surgical drainage and debridement is often required for pyogenic pericarditis. (Graph presented).
