N2  - Melioidosis is a disease caused by Burkholderia pseudomallei. It is endemic in Southeast Asia and Australia with reported cases in the U.S. In the Philippines, we only have a few reported cases probably due to lack of awareness and unavailability of serologic tests. We have a case of a 45-year old Diabetic female on metformin 500 mg 3x/day, who presented with 2 weeks history of fever with productive cough and progressive dyspnea. Physical examination showed respiratory distress, BP 120/70, CR 130s regular, RR 38, Temp 38.8. She had intercostal, subcostal and supraclavicular retractions, crackles on both lung fields. Laboratory examinations Hgb 115 g/L, Hct 0.34, WBC 15.8, segmenters 0.62, bands 0.24, Creatinine 1.1 mg/dL, BUN 29.7 mg/dL, Arterial Blood Gas showed pH 7.37, pCO2 26.9, PO2 86.9, HCO3 15.2, PO2 A-a 598, at 40% FiO2. Chest x-ray showed inhomogenous density occupying almost the whole left hemithorax. She was started on piperacillin-tazobactam, clindamycin, azithromycin. She was eventually intubated and hooked to mechanical ventilator. Repeat chest x-ray post intubation 4 hours after admission showed progression of infiltrates on the left hemithorax. Vancomycin was added; however patient continued to deteriorate and subsequently succumbed. Endotracheal gram stain showed gram negative bacilli, culture grew Klebsiella pneumoniae, blood cultures isolated Burkholderia pseudomallei from two sites. Melioidosis is fast becoming a global health issue. Given its high mortality rate for septicemic patients, early diagnosis is the key. We believe that for patients with co-morbidities especially diabetes mellitus presenting with infection, melioidosis should always be considered. Chest x-ray on admission 6/10/09 4:30 pm (Figure presented) .
