N2  - Introduction: Nocardia is a ubiquitous microorganism found in soil, organic matter and water. Most common predisposing conditions include solid organ transplant (29%), long term steroid therapy (63%), human immunodeficiency virus (19%), chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (23%) and alcoholism (6.5%). Nocardiosis is known for its ability to spread to any organ, with special tropism for lung, central nervous system, and skin. Pulmonary Nocardiosis accounts for a 14-40% of mortality, preceded by disseminated with 64% and central nervous system (CNS) with 100%. Case Report: 51 year old male non-smoker with medical history of hypertension and chronic alcoholism. He presented to the hospital for evaluation of a two weeks evolution of a right upper quadrant pain, right side chest pain that radiates to the ribs, right shoulder and back described as sharp, 10/10 of intensity, worsened by inspiration, cough and right lateral decubitus position. Patient also refers general malaise, fatigue, anorexia, 20 pounds weight loss, night sweats, unquantified fever, and visual/auditory hallucinations. He denied pets and exposure to tuberculosis. An abdominal and chest computer tomography (CT) (figure 1) reports right chest wall and right paraspinous musculature neoplasia with associated collection and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. He was admitted to internal medicine ward with leukocytosis and hyponatremia with diagnosis of suspected primary lung malignancy with a post-obstructive pneumonic infection. Intravenous antibiotic therapy started with vancomycin and piperacillin/tazobactam. Pulmonary Service was consulted for reccomendations, lung malignancy vs. infectious etiology considered and a percutaneous CT guided needle biopsy was recommended. Pathology of the aspiration reported a gram positive filamentous branching bacteria (figure 2), weakly acid fast positive from isolated pleural aspirate, consistent with a Nocardia infection. Infectious diseases consulted and recommended start trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole to cover Nocardia species. Due to hallucinations a head CT scan and brain MRI were done, reported negative, but patient was started on imipenem for CNS involvement Chest drainage was started, with later resolution. Follow up imaging studies post antimicrobial therapy with some improvement. Pt transferred to rehabilitation ward to continue receiving long term therapy for the condition. Novelty and importance of the specific case: This case represents an unusual presentation of pulmonary nocardiosis in a patient with alcoholism as identifiable risk factors, since it represents less than 10% of the reported cases. Pulmonary nocardiosis should be considered in the differential diagnosis of a thoracic/pleuritic mass with associated mediastinal lymphadenopathy, initially considered a primary lung malignancy with metastatic diseases in order to adequately perform swift and appropriate therapeutic decisions with the purpose of reducing mortality. (Figure presented) Abdominal CT Scan sho wing right side pleural involvement of pulmonary nocardiasis (Figure presented) .
