N2  - Introduction: Septic shock due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis was reported rarely, mostly among patients with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection. We report a non-HIV patient with fatal septic shock due to M. tuberculosis, and present her hemodynamic data. Case report: A 43-year-old female engineer presented with fever for 2 weeks, cough and weight loss (2 kg) for one year. She had pulmonary TB 4 years ago and was treated for 6 months. She never smoked or drank. On physical examination her temperature was 38.5âf, respiratory rate 26/min, pulse rate 120/min, and blood pressure 109/42 mmHg. She had bilateral crackles. Laboratory test revealed leukocytosis (24,980/mm3), neutrophilia (92%) and anemia (hemoglobin 10 g/dL). Blood gas analysis while breathing 5 L/min oxygen showed respiratory alkalosis (pH: 7.49, PCO2: 34 mmHg, PO2: 114 mmHg). Chest radiograph and CT (Figure) showed destroyed right lung with large cavities and mottling infiltrates at left upper lobe. Her sputum stained positive (4+) for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) and later grew M. tuberculosis. Isoniazid, ethambutol, rifampicin, and pyrazinamide were started on day 1. We also started intravenous ceftazidime and levofloxacin immediately, and streptomycin 3 days later, but fever persisted and hypoxemia deteriorated. Blood, urine, sputum cultures for bacteria and fungi yielded no growth, and HIV antibody test was negative. Hypotension and hypoxemic respiratory failure developed on day 8. She was intubated and transferred to ICU. Hemodynamic data recorded by pulse contour cardiac output method showed profile suggestive of septic shock (Table), with normal central venous pressure, cardiac index, index of end-diastolic volume, intrathoracic blood volume, and reduced systemic vascular resistance. Her shock once improved, but on day 13 her anemia worsened and creatinine increased suddenly. On day 19 high fever, profound shock and thrombocytopenia developed. Direct Coombs' test was positive. Bone marrow aspirate showed hemolytic anemia and thrombocytopenia. Multiple organ failure developed. She died on day 21. Blood culture for mycobacteria reported negative. Sputum M. tuberculosis isolates reported sensitive to isoniazid, ethambutol, rifampicin, streptomycin and ofloxacin. Discussion: Disseminated TB can present without miliary tubercles and lead to septic shock with multi-organ failure. Chest radiograph and CT usually show diffuse alveolar/interstitial infiltrates and cavities. In in-vitro study mycobacterial product lipoarabinomannan stimulated peritoneal macrophages to release tumor necrosis factor and cause shock. Our patient died despite of early anti-TB therapy. Her heavy mycobacterial load and complicating hemolytic disease contributed to the fatality. (Table presented).
