N2  - Introduction: Unilateral hyperlucency of lung due to congenital, developmental, bronchial and vascular abnormalities of ipsilateral lung and pulmonary vasculature is reported in literature. Occasionally the underlying etio -pathogenesis described is recurrent bronchitis and bronchiolitis secondary to childhood viral or bacterial respiratory infections as reported by Swyer-James and later by MacLeod in early 1950s. 1, 2. We report a case of healthy young adult with history of cocaine abuse and similar radiological features of Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome without any evidence of childhood respiratory disease or infections. Case: 19-year-old male, on active military duty was referred to pulmonary clinic with a recent episode of fever, fatigue, headache and unilateral right sided hyperinflation on CXR. No associated history of shortness of breath, chest pain, cough, wheezing or hemoptysis. No history of trauma or exposure to sick contacts. Past medical, surgical and family history was unremarkable. He smoked 1-pack per day for 2 year and reported cocaine abuse for past 5 years. Physical exam revealed normal vital signs, RR of 24 per minute, oxygen saturation of 98% on room air. No clubbing or cyanosis. On auscultation, there were decreased breath sounds on right side and no wheezing, ronchi or crackles. Pulmonary function tests revealed moderate obstructive defect with FEV1: FVC of 68% predicted, normal DLCO and total lung capacity of 113% predicted. Follow up CT chest (Panel A) showed right sided unilateral emphysema. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy ruled out airway obstruction and endobrochial lesions. BAL for Tuberculosis, atypical mycobacterium and Enzyme ImmunoAssay for blastomycosis, Cooidomycosis and Histoplama antigens were negative. Influenza A, B nasal swabs and HIV testing were all negative. His serum Alfa1 antitrypsin was normal and urine for legionella antigen was negative. Transthoracic ECHO demonstrated normal study. A Ventilation perfusion scan showed matched defects in the right lung with diminished activity on both ventilation and perfusion images (Pannel B). Patient was referred to thoracic surgery and advised to avoid any activities that would expose him to sudden atmospheric pressure changes. Discussion: Unilateral hyperlucency of lung in adults may be related toxic inhalation from smoking cocaine that appears similar to Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome . However, differential and unilateral radiological appearance of hyperlucency needs further studies.
