N2  - Introduction: Recombinase-Activating Gene (RAG) mutations are responsible for 3-10% of Severe Combined Immuno Deficiency (SCID), with classical lymphocyte phenotype T-B-NK+ (absent T & B cells; normal NK cells). Among SCID patients, those with no detectable B cells are more seriously affected. Here we report a case of SCID secondary to RAG-1 compound heterozygous mutation, with atypical lymphocyte phenotype and extensive lung involvement. Case Description: A previously healthy 1-1/2 year old female child of non consanguineous parents was referred to our hospital with cough, decreased oral intake, and decreased urine output secondary to Herpes gingivostomatitis. Upon admission, she was found to have fever and respiratory distress with crackles and wheezes at lung exam. Rashes were not found on skin. A Chest X-ray showed patchy infiltrates. She was diagnosed with bacterial pneumonia, and was placed on IV antibiotics. A chest CT revealed a large pneumatocele with cavitation (Fig-1). Bronchoalveolar lavage grew Staph.aureus, and adenovirus was detected by PCR. Family history was noncontributory, and her immunizations were uneventful. Immunological evaluation revealed low absolute T-cell count, profound CD4 lymphopenia and almost absent naïve T- cells (Table-1). There was no response to candida skin test, indicating an absence of DTH response. However, she had normal B and NK cells, and normal levels of immunoglobulins. Patient persisted with cough and wheezing, and had multiple hospital admissions due to recurrent pneumonias with poor response to prolonged courses of antibiotics, inhaled steroids, and other bronchodilators. Chest CT scans were consistent with lung cavitations, infiltrates, and lung abscess that required CT-guided drainage (Fig-1). Herpes simplex, adenovirus, EBV, P.aeruginosa, and Staph.aureus were isolated in alveolar lavage. Her infectious disease history also involved oral candidiasis, Herpes simplex-1, herpes zoster, and Clostridium difficile. Further evaluation identified RAG-1 compound heterozygous mutation (Fig 2). Her IL7Ra gene sequence was normal. She underwent hematopoietic stem cell transplantation from an HLA matched unrelated donor. Six months after transplantation, she has not had any respiratory recurrences. Discussion: The presence of profound lymphopenia with lung cavitations, infiltrates, and abscess in this child was a diagnostic challenge. It has raised multiple differentials due to normal B cell line and abscess formation suggesting some degree of preserved immunity. Interestingly, our patient had RAG mutation with an unusual lymphocyte phenotype T-B+NK+, and normal levels of immunoglobulins. Severe pulmonary disease may present in RAG-1 heterozygous patients. Bone marrow transplantation should be considered early after diagnosis to reduce morbidity and mortality. (Figure presented).
