N2  - Background: During human latent tuberculosis infection, Mycobacterium tuberculosis likely resides within the nutrient-starved environment of caseous lung granulomas. The stringent response alarmone (p)ppGpp is synthesized by RelMtb in response to nutrient starvation, thus enabling tubercle bacilli to restrict growth and shut down metabolism in a coordinated fashion. In this study, we investigated the virulence of a relMtb-deficient Mycobacterium tuberculosis mutant in the guinea pig model. Methods: Guinea pig groups were each aerosol infected with ∼200 bacilli of Mycobacterium tuberculosis H37Rv wild-type, ΔrelMtb and relMtb complement strains. At Days 1, 14, 28, and 56 after infection, body, lung, and spleen weights were measured, lung and spleen homogenates were plated to assess bacillary load, lung sections were fixed for histological analysis, and whole lungs were homogenized for host expression analysis. Quantitative RT-PCR was used to study the effect of (p)ppGpp deficiency on expression of key cytokine and chemokine genes in guinea pig lungs. Results: The relMtb-deficient mutant showed impaired initial growth and long-term survival relative to the wild-type strain. Loss of Rel Mtb was associated with the striking absence of tubercle lesions grossly and of caseous granulomas histologically. The attenuated phenotype of the relMtb-deficient mutant was associated with reduced expression of genes encoding the proinflammatory cytokines IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha in animal lungs 28 days after infection. Conclusions: The Mycobacterium tuberculosis stringent response is critical for long-term persistence in guinea pigs. Enhanced clearance of ΔrelMtb in guinea pig tissues is associated with the induction of more robust host immune responses. Loss of RelMtb led to relatively lower induction of proinflammatory cytokine genes in the lungs, mildly attenuated DTH responses, and the absence of caseous granulomas and tubercle lesions. Our data clearly show that mutants demonstrating a persistence phenotype in mice fail to induce true caseous granulomas in an animal model with human-like pathology.
