N2  - Introduction: Pandemic by Novel Influenza A/ H1N1 virus was declared by WHO on June 11th, 2009. In spite of a growing number of compatible cases since May 7th -when the first case was diagnosed - pandemic was acknowledged to have reached our country only on July1st, 2009. Until July 8th only one central Lab was performing diagnostic tests to a selected and restricted number of cases. Little was known on epidemiology and outcome in solid organ transplant recipients. Presenting symptoms and outcome are described herein. Methods: Data were prospectively collected and analyzed later for adult kidney transplant recipients (KTx) that presented with symptoms compatible with novel Influenza A/H1N1 infection between June 17th and July 6th, 2009. Demographics, immunosuppressive regimen, clinical presentation, antimicrobial therapy and outcomes are reported. No confirmatory diagnosis was available for our cases. Results: Among 842 KTx on follow-up, 11 (1.31%) presented with compatible symptoms. Age: 44.7y (36-55); time from KTx: 58.3 months (2-165); Maintenance immunosuppression included cortico-steroids (P), calcineurin inhibitors (CNI), Sirolimus (SRL); Micofenolate Mofetil or Mycofenolic acid (MMF/MyF) in different combinations. Triple therapy in 9: (P+SRL+MMF/MyF 5; P+CNI+MMF/MyF 3; P+SRL+CNI1) and double therapy in 2. Presenting symptoms: fever >38°C =11; cough=11; myalgias=10; rhinorrhea =8; headache = 6; sore throat =5; dyspnea=5; digestive symptoms=2. Clinical exam and labs showed pulmonary involvement in 8; creatinine > 3mg/dl in 3; lymphopenia in 6; concomitant bacterial pneumonia in 3; hypoxemia (<90 O2 sat) in 3 who were admitted to the ICU and needed ventilatory support. All 11 were started on oseltamivirat at first visit. Admitted cases also received IV broad spectrum antibiotics. Delay from presenting symptoms to first visit was less than 24hs in 8 and from 3 to 5 days for the 3 admitted cases. Of these 2 died within 7 days of admission. Conclusions: Early initiation of antiviral treatment seems to be a key factor for better outcome. Although lack of confirmed diagnosis can weaken the validity of this experience it has to be considered that as per local Health Authorities 93% of flu cases diagnosed in June and July 2009 were to be attributed to Novel Influenza A/ H1N1 virus infection.
