N2  - Description of Case: An eighteen month old girl, with a previous history of MRSA otitis media, presented with a four day history of cough, high grade fever and breathlessness. On initial presentation, she was febrile, cyanosed and in respiratory distress. Chest Xray revealed right upper lobe pneumonia. She was commenced on Ceftriaxone and rescuscitated with intravenous fluids. However, the pneumonia and respiratory distress worsened following which the antibiotic cover was broadened to Penicillin, Gentamicin, Azithromycin and Vancomycin. While on broad spectrum antibiotics, she developed a large right pleural effusion with multiple loculations suggestive of complex parapneumonic effusion. She underwent surgical drainage with VATS and thoracoscopic decortication. Blood and empyema fluid cultures were negative. However, she continued to have spiking temperature and developed multi-loculated pleural effusion again. She underwent further VATS, decortication and drainage of loculated abscess. On day six, pleural tissue culture grew MRSA, which was resistant to Penicillin and Flucloxacillin, but sensitive to Clindamycin, Vancomycin and Doxycycline. Clindamycin was started based on antibiotic sensitivity. CT chest now showed densely consolidated right lung and necrotic right middle lobe and she still had spiking fever and rigors. Multiple cultures showed no growth. She finally underwent open thoracotomy, decortication and debridement of necrotic abscess. Pleural cultures again grew MRSA. She was weaned and successfully extubated. Fever resolved, inflammatory markers improved and intercostal drains were removed. She was discharged home after a prolonged, stormy hospital stay. This case demonstrates the considerable morbidity associated with CAMRSA pneumonia. During the presentation, we go on to describe the burden of community-acquired MRSA pneumonia. Results of a retrospective analysis of patients hospitalized with CAMRSA pneumonia from 2002-2008 are also presented. Clinico-pathological characteristics and an audit of current practices of management of these patients are described in detail. Issues of Interest in the Case: CAMRSA pneumonia is associated with considerable morbidity. CAMRSA pneumonia should be suspected in all patients with severe community-acquired pneumonia. There is a significant delay in initiation of appropriate anti-microbial treatment.
