N2  - Introduction: Aerosolized bronchodilators are frequently used in mechanically ventilated patients. Previous studies suggested that aerosol therapy may promote bacterial colonization of ventilator circuit and subsequent ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP). Hypothesis: We hypothesized that aerosolized bronchodilators would be associated with increased risk for ventilator-associated pneumonia. Methods: This prospective observational study was conducted in a 30-bed ICU during a 1-year period. All patients requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation for >48 h were included. VAP definition included the presence of new or progressive radiographic infiltrate associatedwith two of the three following criteria: 1) temperature > 38.5°C or < 36.5°C, 2) leukocyte count >10000/μL or < 1500/μL, 3) purulent tracheal aspirate. In addition, a positive tracheal aspirate (≥106 cfu/mL) or bronchoalveolar lavage (≥104 cfu/mL) culture was required. Only first episodes of VAP were taken into account. No patient received prophylactic aerosolized antibiotics. Treatment with aerosolized bronchodilators was at the discretion of attending physicians. Risk factors for VAP were determined using univariate and multivariate analysis. Chi-square test or Fischer's exact test were used to compare qualitative variables. Continuous variables were compared using Student t-test or Mann-Whitney U test. All predictors showing a p <0.1 association with VAP in univariate analysis were incorporated in the multivariate logistic regression analysis. Results: 439 patients were included. 137 (31%) developed VAP, including 13% early-onset VAP. Pseudomonas aeruginosa (42%), Acinetobacter baumannii (13%), and Enterobacter species (9%) were the most frequently cultured bacteria in VAP patients. Univariate analysis identified (p<0.05) SAPS II (54±16 vs 48±20), transfer from other wards (71% vs 60%), aerosolized salbutamol (49% vs 34%), i.v. salbutamol (24% vs 12%), corticosteroid use (78% vs 62%), stress ulcer prophylaxis (94% vs 86%), red blood cell transfusion (62% vs 40%), and duration of mechanical ventilation before VAP (18±16 vs 13±12 d; in patients with and without VAP; respectively) as risk factors for VAP. Multivariate analysis identified aerosolized salbutamol (OR [95% CI] 1.7 [1.1-2.6]; p=0.012), SAPS II (1.01 per point [1.01-1.02], p=0.031), and red blood cell transfusion (2.05 [1.3-3.1], p=0.001) as independent risk factors for VAP. Conclusions: Aerosolized bronchodilators are independently associated with increased risk for VAP in critically ill patients.
