N2  - Persistent bacterial bronchitis (PBB) sometimes truncated to protracted bronchitis is a paediatric condition clinically defined as (a) the presence of isolated chronic (>4 weeks) wet/moist cough, (b) resolution of cough with antibiotic treatment and (c) absence of pointers suggestive of an alternative specific cause of cough. This condition has long been recognized by paediatric pulmonologists but has only been adequately characterized (by broncho-alveolar lavage and clinically) recently. PBB has been officially recognized by the cough guidelines of the Thoracic Society of New Zealand and Australia, the British Thoracic Society and was referred to in the American College of Chest Physicians guidelines. Children with PBB are typically young (<5 years of age, median age 3 years). They have a chronic wet cough and some parents may report a 'wheeze' which is actually a ruttle or rattles (reflective of airway secretions) and not a true wheeze. Systemic effects are generally minimal or non-specific such as tiredness or lack of energy. Like children with chronic cough, children with PBB have significant morbidity. Parents typically have seen multiple medical practitioners for their child's chronic cough in the last 12-months. In PBB the child's cough resolves only after a prolonged course (at least 10-14 days) of appropriate antibiotics. The diagnosis of PBB should only become definite when the response to treatment is dramatic, i.e. the child becomes asymptomatic. When a typical course (5 days) of antibiotics is used, the cough either relapses within 2-3 days, or slightly subsides but does not resolve completely. Airway neutrophilia is present and common respiratory pathogens are found in the endobronchial infection; Haemophilus Influenza, Streptoccus Pneumoniae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Their chest x-rays may be reported as 'normal' but usually show peribronchiolar changes. Hyperinflation is rare and if present should raise a concern for asthma alone or asthma with PBB. The mechanisms underpinning the development and the natural history of PBB are unknown but innate immunity dysfunction is implicated. Recurrent episodes of PBB and/or wet cough not resolving to simple therapies should prompt further evaluations of other causes of chronic wet cough (aspiration, chronic suppurative lung disease and bronchiectasis). Management of PBB is essentially the same as that for bronchiectasis. Managing PBB is important as it is curable and it is likely that non-treatment may lead to development of CSLD in some children, such as at-risk populations (eg indigenous children).
