N2  - Background/Purpose: Asian dust particles have known toxic effect to human respiratory organs and aggravated the respiratory diseases such as COPD, asthma. Many studies have reported that transition metals, bacterial LPS and many microparticles induced profibrotic cytokines and growth factors leading to airway remodelling. We investigated whether Asian dust particles (ADP) induce oxidative damage in mouse lung, and evaluated histopathologic changes in mouse lungs by ADP. Methods: We collected particles during Asian dust storm periods, and measured size of particles using MultisizerTM3. ADP were diluted with 20 μL PBS at each concentration (0.1, 0.2, 0.4 mg). Male 57BL/6 mice, 4 weeks ages, were divided 4 groups; saline 20 μL, 0.1 mg of ADP with 20 μL PBS, 0.2 mg, and 0.4 mg. Each concentration of ADP was instilled direct intratracheally three times a 12 weeks. After 12 weeks later, mice were sacrificed. We observed histopathologic findings of mouse lung using H&E stain and Mason-trichrome stain (M-T stain), Immunohistochemical (IHC) stain for 8-oxoguanine, also measured ROS in PBMC. Mean size of ADP was 6.25 μm. Results: On the finding of H&E stain, mouse lung with ADP for 12 weeks had peribronchiolar cuffing of lymphocytic infiltration and centrilobular emphysema at the H&E. However on the finding of M-T stain, there is no significant changes compare with control. On the finding of 8-oxoguanine, there is significant statistic change compared with control at mouse lung with ADP for 12 weeks (p < 0.0001). On the finding of ROS, there is no significant statistic change. Conclusions: These results suggest that long-term exposure with ADP for 12 weeks induced bronchoalveolar inflammation, emphysematous change and accompanied oxidative stress in mouse lung.
