N2  - Background/Purpose: To assess the prevalence of tracheobronchial infections (TBI), organisms isolated and their antibiotic susceptibility in a respiratory ICU. Methods: Patients admitted to the ICU of Vallabhbhai Patel Chest Institute were followed up prospectively. Haemogram, Chest X rays and cultures from sputum and Endotracheal tube were taken at the time of admission and later from the suspected sites of infection. Tracheobronchial infections (TBI) were defined according to criteria of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (Defned as no clinical or radiological evidence of pneumonia, and patient has at least two of the following symptoms with no other recognized cause: fever (380 C, cough, new or increased sputum production, rhonchi) and at least one of the following either positive culture obtained by deep tracheal aspirate, sputum specimens or positive antigen test in respiratory secretions. Results: 122 patients (95 males and 27 females) were studied. COPD, Bronchial asthma and interstitial lung disease were the commonest preexisting illnesses. 84 patients had history of multiple admissions in the hospital and 110 patients had history of multiple courses of steroids in the past. A total of 45 patients (36.88%) were found to have TBI infections, 13 patients (10.65%) had UTIs and 7 patients (5.73%) had pneumonias. Out of 45 patients in TBI group, 23 had community acquired TBI (CATBI) and 11 had hospital acquired TBI (HATBI) and ICU acquired TBI (ICUATBI) each. Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter were common in CATBI and Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas were the predominant organisms in HATBI and ICUATBI respectively. In CATBI, 80% these isolates were resistant to aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones, 50% resistant to 3rd generation cephalosporins but resistance to carbapenems was 13.5%. In HATBI and ICUATBI, both Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas were 100% resistant to fluoroquinolones and 3rd and 4th generation cephalosporins, 85% resistant to aminoglycosides but Acinetobacter was 25% and Pseudomonas was 27.5% resistant against carbapenems. Conclusions: TBI infections were the commonest infections in our ICU which is in contrast with other studies where Pneumonias and UTIs were the commonest infections. Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter were the predominant organisms isolated in community as well as nosocomial tracheobronchial infections. High level of resistance was seen in these isolates. Carbapenems are very valuable drugs in such category of patients.
