N2  - Background/Purpose: Chyliform pleural effusion, often called pseudochylothorax or cholesterol effusion, is a high-lipid effusion that is not chylous. The chyliform pleural effusion is formed on the basis of a chronic pleural effusion surrounded by thickened and fibrotic pleurae. The most common cause of this pleural reaction is tuberculous pleurisy, but it has also been described in association with rheumatoid arthritis. The presence of a fat-fluid level within the pleural space is unique to pseudochylous effusion. Pseudochylothorax is a very rare case, there has been only 174 cases reported since 1999. First case: A 68-year-old woman came to the clinic with breathlessness, cough, and chest pain for 2 months. Chest x-ray shows homogenous consolidation on lower field of both lungs with impression of bilateral pleural effusion. Thorax CT scan shows bilateral pleural effusion with collapsed left lower lobe and right lymphadenopathy hilus with collapsed right middle lobe. Pleural tapping was conducted on lower field of both lungs and macroscopic analysis shows milky pleural fluid. Fat profile examination of pleural fluid shows increasing trigliseride dan cholesterol. PCR mycobacterium tuberculosis analysis of pleural fluid shows positive result. Medication with tuberculosis drug on this patient for 5 months showed clinical and radiological improvement. Second case: A 57-year-old woman with cough and breathlessness for 2 months. Chest x-ray shows homogenous consolidation with meniscus sign on right lung and impression of right pleural effusion. Aspiration of 2000 cc of pleural fluid was conducted and macroscopic analysis shows milky fluid. Fat profile of pleural fluid shows increasing cholesterol dan trigliseride. PCR mycobacterium tuberculosis analysis shows positive result. Tuberculosis drug therapy on this patient for 4 months showed clinical and radiological improvement.
