N2  - Objectives: We identified previously HLA-A2-restricted CTL responses against mycobacterial Rv2780 and Rv3846 antigens. Anti-Rv2780 CTLs were detected in non-vaccinated, tuberculin skin test-positive asymptomatic individuals but not in active tuberculosis patients, while anti-Rv3846 CTLs were present in both groups, suggesting that responses against Rv2780 may correlate with protection. To test this hypothesis, we developed and tested a more effective vaccination strategy in an HLA-A2 transgenic mouse model, amenable to bacterial challenge. Methods: HHDII mice expressing transgenic beta2-microglobulin/HLA-A2/Db monochain were immunized in inguinal lymph nodes (LN) with Rv2780 or Rv3846- expressing plasmids followed by boosts with peptides identified as CTL epitopes. Mice were then challenged with M. tuberculosis aerosols. Bacterial counts in lungs and spleen, as well as phenotypic and functional analysis of Teff and Treg, served as measures of infection and elicited responses. Results: Infection of HHDII and parental wild-type C57BL/6 strain resulted in similar bacterial load curves with slightly higher CFU for HHDII mice. Intracellular IFNg staining confirmed effective immunization and expansion in inguinal LN of Ag-specific Teff. Over 12-week period post-infection, these CTLs could be readily detected in mediastinal LN. Remarkably, only the Rv2780-specific CTLs appeared to migrate to and expand in lungs while retaining Th1-like characteristics. In contrast, the Rv3846-specific CTLs were largely undetectable in lungs. Furthermore, vaccination against Rv2780 reduced the bacterial load in lungs by 1.0-1.2 log and in spleen by 0.6-0.7 log, while anti-Rv3846 vaccination was ineffective. CD4+/CD25+/FoxP3+ Tregs were found in LN and lungs of vaccinated and challenged animals. Their presence in lungs increased by 30-50% with progression of infection 12 weeks after challenge. Conclusion: Data demonstrated that HHDII mice expressing HLA-A2 monochain provide in vivo model for testing the role of CTL responses directed to mycobacterial antigens, identified originally in human patients and healthy contacts. In addition, the results support further evaluation of Rv2780 as a potential vaccine component eliciting protective CD8+ T-cell responses.
