N2  - Objectives: Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) has emerged as a global threat and cause of serious healthcare-associated infections in humans. Since 1958, vancomycin (a complex hydrophilic tricyclic glycopeptide with a molecular weight of 1485.73) has remained an important anti-staphylococcal agent but its use has been limited by its poor pharmacokinetic profile and a recent creeping of minimum inhibitory concentrations in clinical MRSA strains. Liposome formulation has been successfully applied to several antimicrobial and anticancer drugs to enhance their respective pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic profiles. The purpose of this study is to encapsulate vancomycin into various liposomal formulations and study their pharmacokinetics in mice. Methods: Thin-film hydration method followed by sonication and extrusion was employed to prepare vancomycin-encapsulated large unilamellar liposomes. A Sepharose-4B size exclusion column was used to separate the liposomes from free vancomycin. The liposome fractions were pooled and lyophilized with sucrose as the cryoprotectant. Natural and synthetic lipids with and without charge and surface-modifying agents were included to prepare liposomes with different properties. The encapsulation efficiency and the particle size of the liposomes were determined. A High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) method for the analysis of vancomycin in mouse plasma was developed. Pharmacokinetic analysis was conducted using selected liposomal vancomycin formulations in CF-1 mice at an intravenous dose of 2 mg/kg. Results: Six liposomal formulations of vancomycin were successfully prepared. Liposomes in Formulations 2, 3, and 5 were negatively charged while those in Formulation 6 were positively charged. Liposomes in Formulations 1 and 4 were neutral. The size of liposomes ranged from 98 to 135 nm. The encapsulation efficiency varied between 8 53%. To analyze vancomycin in mouse plasma, norvancomycin was used as the internal standard and the detection of vancomycin and norvancomycin was made at 214 nm. Pharmacokinetic study showed the half-life of intravenous vancomycin in a phosphate buffer solution to be 25 minutes. Liposome encapsulation with conventional lipids (diasteryl phosphatidyl choline and cholesterol) modestly prolonged the half-life of vancomycin to 30 minutes. In striking contrast, liposomes with surface modification by PEG2000 significantly improved drug exposure in systemic circulation with a half-life of >90 minutes. Conclusions: Vancomycin, albeit a hydrophilic molecule, can be encapsulated in liposomes prepared using various compositions of phospholipids. By changing the lipid composition of the liposomes, the in vivo pharmacokinetic profiles of liposomal vancomycin can be altered. PEG2000 modification of the liposomal surface significantly increased drug exposure and reduced drug clearance. Our study may provide a viable approach to combating infections involving MRSA strains with creeping vancomycin minimum inhibitory concentrations.
