N2  - Objective Aeorosolized aminoglycosides have been used to treat ventilator associated pneumonia, acid-fast bacilli pulmonary infections in non-CF patients, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection in CF patients. We report an 18 to 48 month follow-up of two adolescents who had symptomatic infections due to M. abscessus who received maintenance therapy with aerosolized amikacin. Methods: Case 1: 11 year old girl with CF colonized with S. aureus and H. influenzae developed increased cough, fever, failure to thrive, and deterioration in pulmonary function tests (PFTs) associated with new infiltrates on lung computed tomography (CT) in 2003. Three sputum specimens grew M. abscessus. She improved following a 4 week course of intravenous cefoxitin, amikacin and 6 months of clarithromycin. She clinically relapsed with positive cultures 10 months later and was retreated with the same regimen. She has been maintained on oral clarithromycin and aerosolized amikacin (300 mg bid) for 4 years. Apart from a clinical relapse due to Aspergillus, she remained clinically well with an FEV1 of 85%. Sputum cultures are intermittently positive for M. abscessus. Case 2: An 16 year old girl with CF without sputum was colonized with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. In 2006, she developed more cough, failed to gain weight, had deteriorating PFTs and new infiltrates on CT scan. Bronchoscopy specimens grew M. abscessus and Stenotrophomonas. She improved with a 4-week intravenous course of cefoxitin, amikacin, clarithromycin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Over the ensuing 18 months she received aerosolized amikacin and clarithromycin and has remained well, with an FEV1 of 81%. All subsequent 5 cultures for M. abscessus have been negative. In both cases, amikacin levels have been undetectable. Audiological and renal function testing have been normal. Conclusion: This drug delivery modality represents a novel approach to maintenance therapy of mycobacterial infections, particularly M. abscessus, considered to be a more virulent nontubercuous mycobacteria. The addition of aerosolized amikacin to clarithromycin therapy was not associated with significant side effects in these 2 patients even up to 48 months. We believe that this therapeutic intervention may have value in preventing relapses and decline in pulmonary function in this infection.
