N2  - The aim of this study was to analyze etiology, antibiotic sensitiveness and treatment of pneumonia in children over 3-year old, as well as drugs hypersensitivity reactions. Material and method: In the period 2006-2008, 750 children at the age 3-16 years were treated in our Department for acute respiratory diseases and undifenition conditions with pneumonia. Diagnosis was made by means of clinical picture, some biological parameters, chest X-ray findings and microbiological investigations. Results: Before hospitaliziation 91% of the patients were treated with antibiotics. Eighty-nine percentage of them have had anamnestic data for cough, 67%-fever and 9%-drugs hypersensitivity reactions. Chest X-ray showed consolidations in 51%. Etiologic agents were investigated in 85% of the patients and a positive identification was made in 32% of them. The most commonly isolated bacteria were Streptococcus pneumoniae 48%, Haempohilus influenzae 34%, and Moraxella catarrhalis 9%. Analyze of antibiotic sensitiveness confirmed that S. penumoniae was sensitive on Penicillin 91% and Ampicillin 86%. H. influenzae was sensitive on Penicillin 72% and Ampicillin 76%. M. catarrhalis was sensitive on Penicillin 69% and Ampicillin- 84%. Isolated bacterial agents showed sensitivity on Amoxycillin-clavon acid in above 96% and Cephalosporines (III generations) in above 99%. The treatment consisted of antibiotics, parenteral hydration and respiratory physiotherapy. Cephalosporins were applied as initial treatment in 51%, ampicillin in 30% and macrolides in 15%. Drugs hypersensitvity reactions was confirmed in 4.0%. (ampicillin-2%, cephalosporins and macrolides-1%). Duration of treatment was 7-14 days. A positive response to treatment was obtained in 98% of the patients, with delayed course in 2%. Conclusion: Pneumonia constitutes a major health problem in our country and very often there are diagnostic and therapeutic problem (particularly if the children have drugs hypersensitivity reactions).
