N2  - Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacterial biofilms, present in 80% to 90% of cystic fibrosis patients, are the leading cause of mortality among the population. Mucoid strains of P. aeruginosa form an extracellular matrix to resist the host's immune system and antibiotics. Since antibiotic treatment is minimally effective, our goal is to develop dry powder aerosols to co-deliver antibiotic and dispersion compounds to break up and eradicate bacterial colonies. Spray-dried powder aerosols of Ciprofloxacin HCl (antibiotic), glutamic acid (dispersion compound), and L-leucine (excipient) were characterized for powder density, geometric size, and morphology by tap density, laser diffraction, and scanning electron microscopy, respectively. An in vitro model of mucoid-producing P. aeruginosa cultured on glass coverslips was stained with a live/dead assay and imaged by confocal microscopy to quantify bacterial dispersion and death. Dry powders were formulated with 10% excipient and 90 wt% antibiotic and dispersion compounds, in varying ratios, and overall solid concentrations of 0.1625% - 0.5%. All samples had aerodynamic properties appropriate for tracheobronchial and alveolar deposition (3.50 - 4.77μm). Biofilm age, antibiotic and dispersion concentrations, and duration of treatment had significant effect on bacterial killing. This co-delivery system offers a new treatment strategy for bacterial biofilms which may improve the elimination of persistent infections in the lungs.
