N2  - Pertussis (wooping cough) is a well-known childhood respiratory disease and is caused primarily by Bordetella pertussis. The disease is quite relevant among adults and adolescents in whom the disease symptoms are often atypical. Several objectives had this pilot study: evaluation of spread of pertussis infection in Bulgaria by PCR, evaluatation of the effectiveness of the infants' vaccination programme, collection of locally relevant information of the transmission of pertussis from adults to infants, and assessment the role of PCR for detection of Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia (Chlamydophyla) pneumoniae and Legionella spp. as causatives of infants' respiratory infections misdiagnosed by the general practitions as pertussis infection. 344 nasopharyngeal samples were collected from infants with pertussis symptoms (cough lasting at least 7 days) and contact adults. 'In house' PCR tests were developed for each bacterium diagnosed. Detection of B. pertussis-specific DNA by PCR is a promising new technique for rapid and reliable diagnosis of pertussis. Our test offers simultaneous detection of three major atypical pneumonia causing pathogens by using gradient PCR methodology. Pertussis continues to circulate in Bulgaria even the achieved high vaccine coverage of infants and children. PCR is rapid and simplifies the laboratory diagnosis of pertussis. Atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma and Legionella may be misdiagnosed with pertussis.
