N2  - Mycoplasma pneumoniae is one of the most common pathogens causing community-acquired respiratory tract infections. The particle agglutination (PA) test and the complement fixation (CF) test are used for the diagnosis of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infections. However, we often experience low titer results in acute-phase Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. Culture, PCR, and IgM-specific ELISA are not very useful tools for examination in the acute phase, due to the time required and cumbersome procedures. The validity of the Meridian ImmunoCard Mycoplasma (IC) test, a 10-minute card-based enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay of IgM antibodies against Mycoplasma pneumoniae, was evaluated. We performed the IC test, PA test, and CF test on adult patients of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection (13 cases) and bacterial pneumonia (4 cases), and on healthy adults (9 cases). We obtained 69.2%, 15.4%, 8.3% of sensitivity and 92.8%, 92.8%, 100% of specificity, respectively, in the acute phase. The results of the IC test surpassed those of the PA test (p<0.01) and the CF test (p<0.005) in sensitivity, and were equal to them in specificity. Our results indicate that the IC test is a good tool for the detection of acute-phase Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection.
