N2  - The prognosis of cystic fibrosis mainly depends on the characteristic pulmonary alterations. As a consequence of the production of viscous bronchial secretions, the lung is colonized in early age by different microorganisms among which Pseudomonas aeruginosa is of highest relevance. In the last years, the progredient loss of lung function has been effectively treated and the prognosis has become significantly better. Inhalation of antibiotics plays a central role for the prophylaxis of pulmonary infective exacerbations. Although several antibiotics have been investigated, most experience is existent for tobramycin and colistin. Both substances are characterized by a high effectiveness against P. aeruginosa and a high toxicity in systemic treatment. Aerosol therapy allows the administration of therapeutic doses much higher than the minimal inhibitory concentration (MIC) without relevant systemic toxicity. However, up to now, published data for antimicrobial susceptibility and development of bacterial resistance against these substances after inhalative administration are sparse and conflicting. Our study summarizes a large number of prior investigations focussing on microbiological susceptibility. In addition, we isolated strains of P. aeruginosa from sputum of 33 patients and tested bacterial susceptibility for an observation period of up to 397 days. We observed high sensitivity of the isolated strains of P. aeruginosa for both antibiotics, especially colistin. However, different strains isolated from the same sputum sample showed important differences in respect to their susceptibility. There were also considerable variations of the MIC within the observation period. Neither tobramycin nor colistin showed a persistent increase of MIC within the observation period. In ample agreement with prior studies, our data suggest that inhalative administration of tobramycin or colistin may be an effective therapy for the reduction of pulmonary colonization and prophylaxis of infectious exacerbations due to P. aeruginosa. However, because of the risk of development of bacterial resistance, susceptibility should be regularly tested by determination of MIC, especially in patients treated for a longer time. © 2005 Dustri-Verlag Dr. Karl Feistle.
