N2  - A young lady of 20 years of age presented with swelling of face and legs, high coloured reduced urine volume and dull back pain for one and half month. She did not give any history of bleeding from any site of the body, any trauma, sore throat or skin infection. There was no history of fever, cough, weight loss, joint pain, mouth ulcer, skin rash and contact with tuberculosis. She did not complain of hearing and visual problem. Her blood pressure was normal, no lymph-adenopathy with normal skin condition. In systemic examination mild renal tenderness was noted and nothing abnormality detected in other systemic examination. Investigations revealed persistent haematuria an dpyuria. Urine for culture showed no growth of bacteria. Urinary total protein was 1.5 gm/24 hours with normal serum total protein, serum albumin, and globulin & cholesterol level ASO titre was 200 IU, skin tuberculosis test was 5 mm, HBsAg was negative. Her renal function test was normal, X-ray chest P/A view and ultra-sonogram of kidney ureter bladder region was normal. She was initially treated with antibiotics, diuretics and vitamins. She improved clinically in termed of oedema, haematuria and pyuria but back pain persisted. She refused renal biopsy. However, urine for AFB examination showed moderate number of pus cells and large number of acid and alcohol fast bacilli, morphologically resembling mycobacterium tuberculosis. She was then treated with four anti tubercular drugs namely rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide & ethambutal. On follow up, her back pain subsided and subsequent urine sediment showed absence of haematuria, pyuria and AFB bacilli. In conclusion, early diagnosis of real tuberculosis is very difficult, but examination of urine for AFB may provide sometimes rewarding result.
