N2  - Legionella pneumophila is the etiologic agent of Legionnaires' disease, a very serious pneumonia with a mortality rate up to 20%. More than 40 other Legionella spp. representing 64 serogroups are described. Legionellae are found in aquatic environments and soil where they are living in complex ecosystems, predominantly intracellular in amoebae and protozoa. This parasitizing life cycle makes the bacterium capable of multiplying in human macrophages. Transmission to humans takes place by inhalation of contaminated aerosols or by aspiration. Person-to-person transmission has never been found. Diagnosis of Legionnaires' disease is based on isolation of the organism or its DNA from respiratory secretions, on detection of antigenic substances in urine or on antibody detection in serum. The newer quinolones and macrolides are the antibiotics of choice for Legionnaires' disease. Prevention is theoretically possible by discovering and elimination of the environmental reservoir of the Legionella bacteria. Therefore, a nation-wide source finding project started in August 2002.
