N2  - Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection can lead to cavitary lung disease or nodular bronchiectasis in immunocompetent patients and is often responsible for disseminated disease in HIV-infected patients. Common symptoms in patients with MAC pulmonary infections include fever, cough, weight loss, hemoptysis, and fatigue. The sputum smear stained for acid-fast bacilli is useful in making the diagnosis. However, the presence of MAC in a sputum culture may simply represent colonization, so it is important to look for compatible symptoms and radiographic findings. A multidrug regimen including azithromycin or clarithromycin is the standard of care. If cultures remain positive after 6 months, sensitivity testing should be done to identify drug resistance. Treatment is usually effective, but existing regimens require prolonged administration of multiple drugs with monitoring for toxicity.
