N2  - Background. The atypical pneumonia syndrome is marked by high fever, excessive cough, and relatively few physical signs. The main etiological factors of atypical pneumonia syndrome are viruses. Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia sp. are the frequent causes of atypical pneumonia in children. Objectives. The aim of the study was to evaluate the incidence of pneumonia due to these two pathogens among 2081 children and infants admitted to the IInd Department of Pediatrics, Gastroenterology and Nutrition, Wroclaw Medical University in the period of 1985-1999. Material and methods. The analysis comprised 2081 children treated for pneumonia in the IInd Department of Pediatrics and Gastroenterology in the years 1985-1999. The diagnosis of pneumonia was based on clinical symptoms, results of physical examination and confirmed by an abnormal chest X-ray. The diagnosis of M. pneumoniae pneumonia were stablished by a complement fixation assay (an antibody titer greater than 1:320 or a four-fold rise of titer within two weeks). Since 1988, diagnostic tests for chlamydia were employed using specimens obtained by oropharyngeal swabbing with "type of swab"; for Chlamydia trachomastis: the immunoenzymatic method - Chlamydiazyme test, the conventional method of 72-hour culture on McCoy cells, the direct immunofluorescence technique - Chlamyset, and for Chlamydia pneumonia: C. pneumoniae FITC Research Reagent. The diagnosis of C. trachomatis pneumonia was based on the positive results of at least 2 out of 3 performed tests. Results. Mycoplasma pneumoniae was an etiologic agent of pneumonia in 107 (5.1%), whereas Chlamydia in 194 cases (9.3%). The overall percentage of atypical etiology of pneumonia in children ranged from 4.9% (in July) to 18.6% (in November), but differences were not statistically significant. Atypical infections of the lower respiratory tract occurred in epidemiological cycles with increased periods of mycoplasma activity lasting for two- to three-years followed by a seven- to eight-year interval without Mycoplasma pneumoniae, and Chlamydia for a three- to four-year period followed by four- to six-year period of low activity. Conclusions. 1. Both M. pneumoniae and Chlamydia sp. are frequent etiologic agents causing pneumonia in children, and recurring in several-year epidemiological cycles. 2. Differences in the seasonal pattern of incidence of atypical pneumonia in children caused by M. pneumoniae and Chlamydia sp. were observed but they were not statistically significant.
