N2  - A M. intracellularis rough strain (serotype 7) has been isolated from sputum of a sixty year-old patient. This patient was a political prisoner in Germany between 1942-1945 and had contracted pulmonary tuberculosis with a cavity in the upper lobe of the right lung. A strain of mycobacterium susceptible to antituberculous drugs was isolated from his sputum in 1973. Since 1979, the isolation of M. intracellularis has been accompanied by clinical signs of pulmonary mycobacteriosis, i.e. persistance of the cavity in spite of antituberculous treatments, reapparition of cough with sputum, general weakness, intradermoreaction with specific sensitin gives a strong positive reaction, contrasting with a weak reaction with PPD (purified protein derivative) tuberculin. As the antibiogram of the strain shows a susceptibility to cotrimoxazole and erythromycin, the patient underwent a therapy with the combination sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim and erythromycin, for 4 months. At the end of this treatment, he seems to have recovered completely. The radiological aspect of the lungs remained unchanged. Although the source of contamination remains unknown, one thinks on the basis of bibliographical data, that it can be found in human beings, or sometimes in animals (a pig, a bird) or in nature (a pond).
