N2  - Nine cases (8 pulmonary disease and 1 pleural empyema) of respiratory disease due to Mycobacterium fortuitum were found in 5 of 13 national sanatoria in Japan during the period from January 1967 to August 1980. It was supposed that the respiratory disease due to M. fortuitum occupied about 1 percent of all atypical mycobacterioses and the patients were found all over the country. The patients previously reported in Japan were all over 40-year-old and the patients found in national sanatoria were all over 50-year-old, while patients under 20 years of age have been found not rarely in Europe and America. There was no difference in the number of patients by sex (5 men and 4 women). Subjective symptoms such as fever, cough, sputum and hemoptysis were observed at the onset of the illness in the majority of the patients. The disease was divided into two types: the primary infection type without underlying respiratory disease and the secondary infection type with preexisting disease. Three of the 9 cases belong to the primary infection type. In the remaining 6 cases which belong to the secondary infection type, pulmonary tuberculosis, pleurisy and pneumoconiosis were observed as the underlying diseases, but there was no association of achalsia which was found frequently in European and American patients. In the primary infection type sputum culture negative conversion occurred in all cases by antituberculous drug therapy alone and the appearance of the chest X-ray improved, although all strains isolated from the patient were completely resistant to all antituberculous drugs. On the contrary, in the majority of the secondary infection cases the excretion of organisms from sputum continued for extended periods. No patient underwent surgical treatment. Two patients have died during this period, but both patients died of a disease other than atypical mycobacteriosis.
