N2  - In spite of recent studies contesting the reliability of sputum smear examinations for detection of acid-fast bacilli, this report supports the majority of workers who feel that microscopy is still a valuable tool in the management of mycobacterial disease (particularly tuberculosis). Although some have argued that there is little advantage to fluorochrome staining procedures as apposed to ZN, others feel that fluorescent stains offer several advantages. Results here presented suggest a slight advantage of F- over ZN-staining or at least an advantage of F-stains in detecting positive smears, with perhaps some loss in reliability of negative smear reports. One advantage of F-stained smears is that they may be scanned at a much lower magnification than is possible with ZN-stained smears, and this results in a considerable saving of time for the microscopist. The bacillary morphology of fluorescing particles viewed at lower magnifications (especially if they are few in number) must be confirmed at a higher power. The lower the scanning magnification, the more important the need for confirmation. A good combination seems to be scanning at 250X with confirmation at 450X to 630X. The observation of as few as 3 fluorescing AFB per smear is just as reliable as this same observation in a ZN-stained smear. A large number of variables have been reported to affect the final results of acid-fast smear examinations, some of which have been confirmed here: The staining method; the study coordinator was not privy to the staining procedures used by all participants in this study, hence nothing can be said about this. The larger the number of AFB in the specimen, the better the correlation of smear and culture results. The high correlation coefficients shown in Figures I and II indicate almost perfect correlation of numbers of AFB in smear with extent of culture positivity, while Tables 3 and 4 indicate that the lower the number of AFB used for a positive smear report, the larger the proportion of Absolute False Positive specimens. The greater the number of patients on treatment, the greater the number of false positive smear results. Table 3 shows the number of absolute false positive smears to be 10 times higher in treated as compared to newly diagnosed patients. The microscopist may correct for this to some extent by raising from 3 to 10 the number of AFB required for a positive smear. It has been suggested that all fluorochrome stained smears be overstained with the ZN stain, at least until the microscopist gains confidence in his ability to identify acid-fast bacilli. It should be noted in the voluminous data of Tables 2, 3 and 7, that none of the microscopists were confirming the acid-fastness of their fluorescing bacilli with a fuchsin overstain. The data suggest that their confidence in the interpretation of smears was justified.
