N2  - Droplets play the most important role. The penetration into the depth of the lung is dependent on the size of the particles; it must be far below 100 micron. Results of experiments are shown; it is demonstrated that coughed up particles are in 50% suspended in air after 30 min. particles produced by talking stay suspended only in 6%. Cough is the main source of spreading of tubercle bacilli. The mycobacteria, in contrast to many other microorganisms, stand nebulisation and suspension and the resultant drying and shrinking quite well. The socalled atypical mycobacteria have the same physical principles; but they multiply less after inhalation. It can be noted that the survival of tubercle bacteria, which are nebulised with isoniazid is not reduced. Essential factors for infectiosity of tuberculosis are numbers of microorganisms and frequency of cough. After a few days already these 2 parameters are essentially improved. It can be demonstrated that the initial infectiosity is reduced to 1/200 after a treatment of 14 days (either with isoniazid together with rifampicin or isoniazid together with ethambutol and streptomycin).
